window.DAILY_REVIEW_BANK = {"e:7:12":{"id":"e:7:12","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 12","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eGrant the data analyst the \u003ccode\u003eREFERENCES\u003c/code\u003e privilege on the procedure.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eGrant the data analyst the \u003ccode\u003eUSAGE\u003c/code\u003e privilege on the schema containing the procedure.\u003c/span\u003e","Grant the data analyst the \u003ccode\u003eEXECUTE\u003c/code\u003e privilege on the procedure.","Grant the data analyst the \u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eUPDATE\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eDELETE\u003c/code\u003e privileges on the table used by the procedure."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA data engineering team has developed a stored procedure in Amazon Redshift to streamline data processing tasks. The procedure benefits from the Redshift query optimizer to ensure efficient execution.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA data analyst, already a user of the Redshift database, needs permission to run this stored procedure for data analysis purposes.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eHow can the team grant this access while adhering to the principle of least privilege?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eA \u003cstrong\u003estored procedure in Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e is a piece of SQL code that has been pre-written and can be re-executed multiple times. Stored procedures allow encapsulating logic for data transformation, validation, and business rules.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHere are some permissions related to stored procedures in Amazon Redshift:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEXECUTE\u003c/strong\u003e permission allows users to run a stored procedure. This is the main permission required to allow users to execute procedures. \u003ccode\u003eGRANT EXECUTE\u003c/code\u003e is used to provide this permission.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eINSERT, UPDATE, \u003c/strong\u003eand\u003cstrong\u003e DELETE\u003c/strong\u003e permissions on underlying tables or views allow read/write access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUSAGE\u003c/strong\u003e permission on schemas is required to allow creating and managing procedures within that schema.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eALTER\u003c/strong\u003e permission on the procedure allows modifying the procedure definition after creation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eREFERENCES\u003c/strong\u003e permission allows using the procedure name in SQL statements like CALL.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6906281dae9bbd281f373cb42b3b97284e9e097ca8fd59b53d674f56f1cbf8af.jpg\" alt=\"Stored procedure in Amazon Redshift\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo provide the data analyst permission to call the stored procedure, the data engineering team can create a role in Amazon Redshift and grant EXECUTE permission on a stored procedure to the role using the \u003ccode\u003eGRANT\u003c/code\u003e statement. Add the data analyst to the role to allow them to execute the stored procedure and perform data analysis tasks while restricting access to the procedure only. This gives the data engineering team control over database permissions and access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eGrant the data analyst the \u003ccode\u003eEXECUTE\u003c/code\u003e privilege on the procedure.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eGrant the data analyst the \u003ccode\u003eREFERENCES\u003c/code\u003e privilege on the procedure\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. The \u003ccode\u003eREFERENCES\u003c/code\u003e privilege is primarily used in the context of creating a foreign key constraint and does not apply to the execution of stored procedures. It would not enable the data analyst to execute the stored procedure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eGrant the data analyst the \u003ccode\u003eUSAGE\u003c/code\u003e privilege on the schema containing the procedure\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While \u003ccode\u003eUSAGE\u003c/code\u003e on a schema grants the ability to access objects within that schema, it does not specifically allow for the execution of stored procedures. \u003ccode\u003eUSAGE\u003c/code\u003e might be necessary in addition to \u003ccode\u003eEXECUTE\u003c/code\u003e for certain scenarios, but on its own, it does not grant permission to execute stored procedures.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eGrant the data analyst the \u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eUPDATE\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eDELETE\u003c/code\u003e privileges on the table used by the procedure\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. These privileges just allow users to perform table data manipulation operations, such as adding, modifying, or removing data. However, they do not provide permission to execute stored procedures. These privileges are unrelated to the execution of stored procedures and would not enable the data analyst to run the stored procedure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/stored-procedure-security-and-privileges.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/stored-procedure-security-and-privileges.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_GRANT.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_GRANT.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:9":{"id":"e:8:9","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 9","correct":[0],"answers":["AWS Glue for Ray","AWS Lambda","Amazon EMR (Elastic MapReduce)","AWS Glue DataBrew"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is working on a project that involves processing large datasets using Python. The project requires a service that can handle distributed processing over multiple nodes with the least amount of operational overhead. The service should also be capable of handling both batch and real-time data processing tasks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following AWS services would be the most suitable for this task?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue for Ray\u003c/strong\u003e is a job type in AWS Glue, a serverless data integration service. It’s designed to scale AI and Python workloads. AWS Glue for Ray allows you to scale your Python workloads in a distributed cluster in a multi-node environment. This is particularly useful when you have large datasets that can’t fit on a single instance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eRay is an open-source unified compute framework that originated as a project at RISELab in UC Berkeley. It’s designed to scale AI and Python workloads. If your application is written in Python, you can scale it with Ray in a distributed cluster. Ray is Python native and can be combined with the AWS SDK for pandas to prepare, integrate, and transform your data for running your data analytics and ML workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/23962111c258c93490315c443d1bae8880b40a86d4f8687654883f329c07ac85.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use AWS Glue for Ray with Glue Studio Notebooks, SageMaker Studio Notebook, or a local notebook or IDE of your choice. This makes it a flexible and powerful tool for scaling Python workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue for Ray consists of two major components:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eRay Core – The distributed computing framework.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eRay Dataset – The distributed data framework based on Apache Arrow.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen running a Ray job, AWS Glue provisions the Ray cluster for you and runs these distributed Python jobs on a serverless auto-scaling infrastructure. This means you don’t have to worry about managing the underlying infrastructure, and you can focus on writing and running your Python workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDevelopers like Python for its ease of use and rich collection of built-in data processing libraries. They want to use familiar Python primitive type for processing large datasets. AWS Glue for Ray helps data engineers process large datasets using Python and popular Python libraries. AWS Glue for Ray uses\u0026nbsp;Ray.io, an open-source unified compute framework that helps scale Python workloads from a single node to hundreds of nodes. AWS Glue for Ray is serverless, so there is no infrastructure to manage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue for Ray.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Lambda\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. It\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis a serverless computing service that lets you run your code without provisioning or managing servers. While it’s great for running small, independent tasks in response to cloud events (like changes to data in an S3 bucket or updates to a DynamoDB table), it may not be the best choice for processing large datasets using Python. AWS Lambda has a maximum execution time of 15 minutes per request, which might not be sufficient for processing large datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAmazon EMR (Elastic MapReduce)\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. EMR is a cloud-based big data platform for processing large datasets using popular frameworks such as Apache Spark and Hadoop. While it’s powerful and flexible, it requires a fair amount of setup and management, which might not be ideal for a Python-based data processing task.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because AWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that makes it easy to clean and normalize data for analytics and machine learning. It’s a great tool for data cleaning and exploration, but it’s not specifically designed for processing large datasets using Python.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-for-ray-scaling-your-data-integration-workloads-using-python/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-for-ray-scaling-your-data-integration-workloads-using-python/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:47":{"id":"e:2:47","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 47","correct":[1,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eFor cost optimization, configure S3 lifecycle rules to transition archived data to Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFor cost optimization, configure S3 lifecycle rules to transition archived data to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eWrite an AWS Lambda function that identifies and exports old data from RDS to S3. Trigger the Lambda function through Amazon CloudWatch RDS Events\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS Step Functions to create an automation workflow. The workflow will call DataSync to sync the database files to the Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) to set up a task that runs periodically to migrate data older than 3 months from your RDS instance to an S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA large SQL Server database on RDS contains historical transaction data. A process is needed to automate the following requirements: regularly identify data older than 3 months, and export the old data to an S3 bucket for long-term, cost-effective storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options can be used to automate this export from SQL Server to S3 and perform lifecycle management in Amazon S3 to build a solution with the least operational overhead? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) to set up a task that runs periodically to migrate data older than 3 months from your RDS instance to an S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFor cost optimization, configure S3 lifecycle rules to transition archived data to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) is a managed migration and replication service that helps you move your databases and analytics workloads to AWS quickly and securely. The source database remains fully operational during the migration, minimizing downtime to applications that rely on the database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) to set up a task that runs periodically (e.g. weekly) to migrate data older than 3 months from your RDS instance to an S3 bucket. DMS supports homogeneous migrations between SQL Server and S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor cost optimization, configure S3 lifecycle rules to transition archived data to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d9d3995c4e49124cba20dea02fbf8ce91a2f929eb9acf80e132267e61f07c13d.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d9d3995c4e49124cba20dea02fbf8ce91a2f929eb9acf80e132267e61f07c13d.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eWrite an AWS Lambda function that identifies and exports old data from RDS to S3. Trigger the Lambda function through Amazon CloudWatch RDS Events\u003c/strong\u003e - An RDS event indicates a change in the Amazon RDS environment. RDS events cannot be triggered for data changes. Amazon RDS records events that relate to the following resources: DB instances, DB parameter groups, DB security groups, DB snapshots, RDS Proxy events, and Blue/green deployment events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure AWS Step Functions to create an automation workflow. The workflow will call DataSync to sync the database files to the Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e - This is unnecessarily complex. Also, the step function itself will need a trigger.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFor cost optimization, configure S3 lifecycle rules to transition archived data to Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering\u003c/strong\u003e - The S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class is designed to optimize storage costs by automatically moving data to the most cost-effective access tier when access patterns change, without operational overhead or impact on performance. S3 Intelligent-Tiering is not suited for long-term archival of data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/questions/QUA-X-OBSIS36vgqSoS1srbw/efficiently-archiving-transactional-data-in-rds-mssql-to-s3\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/questions/QUA-X-OBSIS36vgqSoS1srbw/efficiently-archiving-transactional-data-in-rds-mssql-to-s3\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/dms/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/dms/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:38":{"id":"e:3:38","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 38","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon DynamoDB global table in the provisioned capacity mode\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon DynamoDB table in the on-demand capacity mode\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon DynamoDB table with a global secondary index\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon DynamoDB table in the provisioned capacity mode with auto-scaling enabled\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA ride-sharing company wants to use an Amazon DynamoDB table for data storage. The table will not be used during the night hours whereas the read and write traffic will often be unpredictable during day hours. When traffic spikes occur they will happen very quickly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following will you recommend as the best-fit solution?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up Amazon DynamoDB table in the on-demand capacity mode\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon DynamoDB has two read/write capacity modes for processing reads and writes on your tables:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eOn-demand\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eProvisioned (default, free-tier eligible)\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon DynamoDB on-demand is a flexible billing option capable of serving thousands of requests per second without capacity planning. DynamoDB on-demand offers pay-per-request pricing for read and write requests so that you pay only for what you use.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe on-demand mode is a good option if any of the following are true:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou create new tables with unknown workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou have unpredictable application traffic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou prefer the ease of paying for only what you use.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIf you choose provisioned mode, you specify the number of reads and writes per second that you require for your application. You can use auto-scaling to adjust your table’s provisioned capacity automatically in response to traffic changes. This helps you govern your DynamoDB use to stay at or below a defined request rate to obtain cost predictability.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eProvisioned mode is a good option if any of the following are true:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou have predictable application traffic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou run applications whose traffic is consistent or ramps gradually.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can forecast capacity requirements to control costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/a1cc3e16891f8f76f9a8b5f66051e5658cade5fe6bf2d929c51fea97ab05b36f.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/a1cc3e16891f8f76f9a8b5f66051e5658cade5fe6bf2d929c51fea97ab05b36f.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/amazon-dynamodb-auto-scaling-performance-and-cost-optimization-at-any-scale/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/amazon-dynamodb-auto-scaling-performance-and-cost-optimization-at-any-scale/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWith on-demand, Amazon DynamoDB instantly allocates capacity as it is needed. There is no concept of provisioned capacity, and there is no delay waiting for Amazon CloudWatch thresholds or the subsequent table updates. On-demand is ideal for bursty, new, or unpredictable workloads whose traffic can spike in seconds or minutes, and when underprovisioned capacity would impact the user experience. On-demand is a perfect solution if your team is moving to a NoOps or serverless environment.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe given use case clearly states that when the traffic spikes occur they happen very quickly, thereby implying an unpredictable traffic pattern, therefore the on-demand capacity mode is the correct option for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up Amazon DynamoDB table in the provisioned capacity mode with auto-scaling enabled\u003c/strong\u003e - As mentioned in the explanation above, you should use the provisioned capacity mode (even with auto-scaling) only when you have predictable application traffic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you create Amazon DynamoDB table, auto-scaling is the default capacity setting, but you can also enable auto-scaling on any table that does not have it active. Behind the scenes, as illustrated in the following diagram, DynamoDB auto scaling uses a scaling policy in Application Auto Scaling. To configure auto-scaling in DynamoDB, you set the minimum and maximum levels of read and write capacity in addition to the target utilization percentage. Auto-scaling uses Amazon CloudWatch to monitor a table’s read and write capacity metrics. To do so, it creates CloudWatch alarms that track consumed capacity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up Amazon DynamoDB table with a global secondary index\u003c/strong\u003e - A global secondary index (GSI) is an index with a partition key and a sort key that can be different from those on the base table. A global secondary index is considered \"global\" because queries on the index can span all of the data in the base table, across all partitions. A global secondary index is stored in its own partition space away from the base table and scales separately from the base table. GSI cannot be used to handle an unpredictable load on a DynamoDB table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up Amazon DynamoDB global table in the provisioned capacity mode\u003c/strong\u003e - Global tables build on the global Amazon DynamoDB footprint to provide you with a fully managed, multi-Region, and multi-active database that delivers fast, local, read and write performance for massively scaled, global applications. Global tables replicate your DynamoDB tables automatically across your choice of AWS Regions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eGlobal tables eliminate the difficult work of replicating data between Regions and resolving update conflicts, enabling you to focus on your application\u0027s business logic. In addition, global tables enable your applications to stay highly available even in the unlikely event of isolation or degradation of an entire Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon DynamoDB global tables:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1dd10b3e8a0ef24ffd9f0cbec943adf36630ffcdf99a121484ed3640c8b907a9.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1dd10b3e8a0ef24ffd9f0cbec943adf36630ffcdf99a121484ed3640c8b907a9.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/global-tables/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/global-tables/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon DynamoDB global table cannot be used to handle an unpredictable load on a Amazon DynamoDB table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/HowItWorks.ReadWriteCapacityMode.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/HowItWorks.ReadWriteCapacityMode.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/SecondaryIndexes.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/SecondaryIndexes.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/amazon-dynamodb-auto-scaling-performance-and-cost-optimization-at-any-scale/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/amazon-dynamodb-auto-scaling-performance-and-cost-optimization-at-any-scale/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/global-tables/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/global-tables/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:16":{"id":"e:6:16","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 16","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eUse columns that are frequently used in \u003ccode\u003eJOIN\u003c/code\u003e conditions as the sort key.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eIdentify the largest dimension table and alter its distribution key to \u003ccode\u003eEVEN\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/span\u003e","Scale up the size of the node experiencing high CPU utilization.","\u003cspan\u003eIdentify the column that is most commonly referenced in the \u003ccode\u003eWHERE\u003c/code\u003e clause of \u003ccode\u003eSELECT\u003c/code\u003e statements. Then, assign this column as the new primary key\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses an Amazon Redshift cluster with KEY distribution for its data warehouse. This cluster has multiple nodes, but one node is using more CPU than the others, which have normal CPU usage during business hours.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDue to a limited budget, the data engineering team seeks a solution to distribute the workload evenly across the cluster without increasing the number of nodes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich adjustment should the data engineering team make to achieve the desired distribution?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eData distribution in Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e refers to how data is stored across the nodes and slices within a cluster. When you load data into a Redshift table, the rows are distributed and stored across these slices according to the table\u0027s distribution style.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eKEY distribution styles:\u003c/strong\u003e The rows are distributed based on the values in a specified distribution key column. Rows with the same value are stored together. This helps optimize queries that filter or join on the distribution key column.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe different key concepts in Amazon Redshift are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDistribution key:\u003c/strong\u003e This column is used to distribute the rows across nodes in the cluster. It is specified when creating a table using the \u003ccode\u003eDISTKEY\u003c/code\u003e clause.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSort key:\u003c/strong\u003e This column is used to order or sort the rows stored on each node. It is specified using the \u003ccode\u003eSORTKEY\u003c/code\u003e clause. Multiple columns can be used as sort keys.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePrimary key:\u003c/strong\u003e This uniquely identifies each row in the table. It consists of the distribution key as the partition key and can optionally include a sort key.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/bcaa621abee81fcb85f0c9e9705b8cc501cf4830f6b793a3b6ae014e0cc27f17.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team should adjust the distribution key to correct the load imbalance across the cluster nodes. The aim is to achieve a more balanced distribution of queries and storage across all nodes to prevent any single node from becoming a bottleneck. A well-chosen \u003ccode\u003eDISTKEY\u003c/code\u003e ensures that the data is distributed uniformly across all slices of each node, which helps in achieving an even distribution of query load. If the current \u003ccode\u003eDISTKEY\u003c/code\u003e is leading to high CPU usage, it suggests that it is not evenly distributing the data due to its low cardinality or because it doesn\u0027t align well with the query patterns. Choosing a column with a more evenly distributed range of values for the \u003ccode\u003eDISTKEY\u003c/code\u003e can solve this issue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Identify the largest dimension table and alter its distribution key to \u003ccode\u003eEVEN\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse columns that are frequently used in \u003ccode\u003eJOIN\u003c/code\u003e conditions as the sort key\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Sort keys optimize query performance by determining the order of data stored and are mainly used to improve the speed of range-restricted queries, not to balance CPU load across nodes. Therefore, adjusting sort keys would not address the issue of uneven distribution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eScale up the size of the node experiencing high CPU utilization\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because scaling up a single node is not possible in Redshift, as the cluster\u0027s nodes are uniform. Moreover, the aim is to balance the load without changing the node count or their configurations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Identify the column that is most commonly referenced in the \u003ccode\u003eWHERE\u003c/code\u003e clause of \u003ccode\u003eSELECT\u003c/code\u003e statements. Then, assign this column as the new primary key\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This would only improve query performance, not necessarily solve the uneven distribution of workload across the cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Distributing_data.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Distributing_data.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-best-dist-key.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-best-dist-key.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-53":{"id":"p:1-53","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 53","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSchedule AWS Glue ETL jobs to read the Kinesis data stream at intervals, transform the records in micro-batches, and write processed output to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose to read directly from Kinesis Data Streams, enable record format conversion, and deliver the transformed files to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSchedule Apache Spark jobs on an Amazon EMR cluster to process records from Kinesis Data Streams, maintain checkpoint state, and write transformed output to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink application to consume the Kinesis data stream, apply stateful transformations, and write processed results to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Tác vụ Glue theo lịch thiên về xử lý lô. Đề cần xử lý luồng liên tục, giữ trạng thái và tính tổng hợp cửa sổ trượt.","Format conversion của Firehose đổi định dạng bản ghi, không thực hiện đầy đủ phép biến đổi có trạng thái và rolling aggregate giữa nhiều sự kiện.","Spark trên EMR có thể xử lý trạng thái nhưng cần thêm quản lý cụm và tác vụ. Flink được quản lý phù hợp hơn tiêu chí vận hành.","Managed Service for Apache Flink xử lý luồng có trạng thái, cửa sổ thời gian và làm giàu dữ liệu. Đọc Kinesis rồi ghi S3, không phải tự quản lý cụm Flink."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA smart building company receives thermostat, humidity, and occupancy readings from thousands of connected devices. The data arrives in an Amazon Kinesis data stream, and the company must transform the data before storing the results in an Amazon S3 bucket. The transformation logic must maintain state across events, calculate rolling aggregates, and enrich records before storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-15":{"id":"p:4-15","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 15","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUpdate the RDS DB instance security group to allow inbound MySQL traffic from the security group that is attached to the EC2 instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eModify the IAM role that is associated with the RDS DB instance to allow network connections from the EC2 instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eModify an ec2_authorized_hosts parameter in the RDS DB parameter group to include the EC2 instance private IP address and restart the DB instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Systems Manager Session Manager document that forwards JDBC traffic from the EC2 instance to the RDS DB instance and attach the document to the RDS DB parameter group\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Security group của RDS cần cho phép lưu lượng MySQL, thường cổng 3306, từ security group của EC2. Đây là quyền mạng cần thiết để ứng dụng kết nối JDBC qua địa chỉ database trong VPC.","IAM role không mở kết nối TCP tới cổng MySQL. Việc kết nối vẫn phụ thuộc đường mạng, security group, endpoint và thông tin xác thực database.","Không có tham số RDS MySQL tên ec2_authorized_hosts dùng để cấp quyền mạng như mô tả. Quyền truy cập mạng được cấu hình bằng security group.","Session Manager document không được gắn vào DB parameter group để cho phép JDBC. Với hai tài nguyên trong cùng VPC, cấu hình security group đúng là giải pháp trực tiếp."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is building a non-production reporting application that runs on an Amazon EC2 instance. The application must connect to an Amazon RDS for MySQL DB instance by using Java Database Connectivity (JDBC). The EC2 instance and the RDS DB instance are in the same VPC and use subnets from the same private subnet tier.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-7":{"id":"p:4-7","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 7","correct":[2,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCross-job data access could be the reason for slow-running queries. Use a separate AWS Glue Spark cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCheck the IAM permissions on the service role created for the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePartition the data in the S3 bucket based on year, month, and day\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure larger Glue job workers by changing the instance type of the workers\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSetup AWS Glue Streaming to efficiently handle streaming data in near real-time\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Đề chỉ mô tả một Glue job, không cung cấp dấu hiệu tranh chấp dữ liệu giữa nhiều job. Tách thêm cụm không xử lý trực tiếp nguyên nhân quét nhiều dữ liệu hoặc thiếu tài nguyên.","Thiếu quyền IAM thường gây lỗi truy cập hoặc job thất bại. Nếu job vẫn chạy nhưng mất nhiều thời gian, kiểm tra quyền không phải biện pháp tăng hiệu năng được hỏi.","Phân vùng S3 theo năm, tháng và ngày giúp job chỉ đọc những phần dữ liệu cần xử lý. Với dữ liệu cập nhật hằng ngày, cách này có thể giảm đáng kể lượng dữ liệu phải quét.","Worker lớn hơn cung cấp thêm tài nguyên tính toán và bộ nhớ cho tác vụ. Đây là một hướng cải thiện khi job bị nghẽn tài nguyên; nên chọn kích thước dựa trên số liệu thực tế.","Glue Streaming phục vụ dữ liệu luồng liên tục. Chuyển sang streaming không tự khắc phục việc job xử lý dữ liệu S3 theo ngày đang chạy chậm."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company stores its data in a single Amazon S3 bucket that is updated daily. An AWS Glue job processes this data which is consumed by Amazon QuickSight to generate an interactive dashboard. Of late, the dashboard queries have been getting slower and an initial inspection has revealed that AWS Glue jobs are running longer than expected.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs a data engineer, which of the following actions will you take to improve the performance of the AWS Glue job? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:31":{"id":"e:5:31","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 31","correct":[0,3],"answers":["Identify and optimize high CPU usage queries using Amazon RDS Performance Insights.","Schedule weekly manual reboots of the RDS DB instance.","Add more tables and indexes by altering the database structure.","Transition to a DB instance with greater CPU and memory resources.","Cache the database queries to reduce loading time."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn e-commerce company is experiencing a significant increase in sales during the holiday season. Due to this surge, database queries processing customer orders are taking longer than usual. This is causing delays in the checkout process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe company’s primary database is hosted on Amazon RDS for SQL server, and the workload is write-intensive. To provide customers with a better checkout experience, the company needs to address the high CPU utilization of their RDS instance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich possible solutions can help solve the issue effectively? (Select TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon RDS Performance Insights\u003c/strong\u003e is a useful feature that gathers and analyzes important performance metrics from an Amazon RDS database instance, such as database load and SQL queries executed. It presents these metrics in an easy-to-understand visual format over a period of time to help you analyze your database load and identify any potential bottlenecks. To use Performance Insights, you must enable it when creating an RDS instance. By default, it provides seven days of metric history.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b4030e9436233b722131c44b55a188b9f4970979be244fbd20a60e5212751a1a.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAn instance upgrade in RDS is the process of upgrading the underlying database engine or version of an existing RDS database instance. It is important to upgrade instances for the following reasons:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNewer versions provide bug fixes, security enhancements, performance improvements, and new features introduced by the database vendor. This helps keep the database secure and optimized over time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eUpgrading helps maintain support status as database vendors stop supporting older versions after a certain time period.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFuture-proofing applications by upgrading to a version compatible with planned features in newer RDS instances or the database engine.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/93ac5fa7263998c4449d32e3f353187c2b64d27ba4dc1a748de5066b8f0544d1.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe company should first optimize the problematic queries identified using Performance Insights. However, since this is a write-intensive workload during peak season, upgrading the RDS instance to a larger size would also be needed to scale up the database capacity and meet the demands of processing higher order volumes. Together, these solutions can help improve database performance and provide customers with a seamless checkout experience.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Identify and optimize high CPU usage queries using Amazon RDS Performance Insights.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Transition to a DB instance with greater CPU and memory resources.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdd more tables and indexes by altering the database structure\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While indexing can help improve read query performance, it’s not beneficial for write-intensive workloads. In fact, excessive indexing can lead to increased overhead during insert, update, and delete operations, potentially aggravating CPU and write latency issues.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSchedule weekly manual reboots of the RDS DB instance\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Regularly rebooting the database instance does not directly impact long-term CPU utilization trends. While it might temporarily alleviate some issues, it does not address the root cause of high CPU usage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCache the database queries to reduce loading time\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Caching is generally more beneficial for read-heavy workloads. Since the database in question is write-intensive, caching would have a negligible impact on reducing CPU utilization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PerfInsights.UsingDashboard.AnalyzeDBLoad.AdditionalMetrics.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PerfInsights.UsingDashboard.AnalyzeDBLoad.AdditionalMetrics.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Overview.DBInstance.Modifying.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Overview.DBInstance.Modifying.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/rds-aurora-postgresql-high-cpu\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/rds-aurora-postgresql-high-cpu\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon RDS Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-relational-database-service-amazon-rds/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-relational-database-service-amazon-rds/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:38":{"id":"e:5:38","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 38","correct":[3],"answers":["Use an Amazon S3 data lake solution to store all data. Use Amazon Athena for querying the data.","Use an Amazon DynamoDB solution to store all data. Implement DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) for fast query performance. Use DynamoDB auto scaling to handle varying query loads.","Deploy an Amazon Redshift cluster with a dense compute instance type, keeping all data on local Amazon Redshift storage. Perform elastic resize on the cluster as necessary.","Deploy an Amazon Redshift cluster with an RA3 instance type to store the latest data. Archive the historical data in an Amazon S3 bucket and create external tables with Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query this data. Enable Amazon Redshift Concurrency Scaling."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA company needs to run complex queries on a 150 TB dataset. Data analysts will focus on analyzing data from the current year on a regular basis. The company will also need to access data from the past 5 years, but only on a monthly basis. \u003c/span\u003e \u003cspan\u003eA cost-effective storage solution must be provisioned that allows for quick responses to queries. \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich solution would best address the company’s needs?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift is a fully managed data warehouse solution that allows for fast query performance due to its columnar storage technique and ability to parallelize queries across multiple nodes. RA3 instances with managed storage allow you to scale compute and storage independently, making them suitable for large-volume workloads like the one described in this scenario. RA3 nodes enable you to store the most recent data, which requires rapid responses to business analysts’ queries, while managing costs by only paying for the storage you actually use.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift Spectrum allows you to run SQL queries directly against exabytes of data in Amazon S3. With Redshift Spectrum, you can extend the analytic power of Amazon Redshift beyond the data stored on local disks in your data warehouse to query vast amounts of unstructured data in your Amazon S3 “data lake”.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/14afa3011ee91dd2ce1f9cb018646ce3e67aeac41d0356f49a7ee42b3cfee256.png\" alt=\"Concurrency Scaling\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFinally, Amazon Redshift Concurrency Scaling allows the system to handle a varying number of incoming queries. As concurrency increases, Amazon Redshift automatically adds query processing power in seconds to process queries without any delays. Once the workload demand subsides, this extra processing power is automatically removed, so you pay only for the time when Concurrency Scaling clusters are in use.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eDeploy an Amazon Redshift cluster with an RA3 instance type to store the latest data. Archive the historical data in an Amazon S3 bucket and create external tables with Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query this data. Enable Amazon Redshift Concurrency Scaling.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy an Amazon Redshift cluster with a dense compute instance type, keeping all data on local Amazon Redshift storage. Perform elastic resize on the cluster as necessary\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect. Keeping all data on local Amazon Redshift storage is more expensive, given the infrequent access to historical data. Additionally,\u0026nbsp;dense compute\u0026nbsp;instances are optimized for performance rather than storage capacity, making them less suitable for handling large datasets.\u0026nbsp;Elastic resizing, on the other hand, is typically used when you need to add or remove nodes and change the node types of a cluster to accommodate changes in query workload. This is not the case in the given scenario, where the main requirement is to handle different access patterns for current and historical data efficiently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse an Amazon S3 data lake solution to store all data. Use Amazon Athena for querying the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because while Amazon S3 is a cost-effective storage solution and Amazon Athena allows for direct querying of data in S3, this setup might not provide the rapid response times required for business analysts’ queries, especially when analyzing large datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse an Amazon DynamoDB solution to store all data. Implement DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) for fast query performance. Use DynamoDB auto scaling to handle varying query loads\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because Amazon DynamoDB is simply a NoSQL database service, not a data warehouse solution. It’s designed for high-performance applications that need to read and write data quickly, not for complex analytical queries on large datasets. Also, storing 150 TB of data in DynamoDB would be very expensive.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/concurrency-scaling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/concurrency-scaling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/features/concurrency-scaling/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/redshift/features/concurrency-scaling/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/working-with-clusters.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/working-with-clusters.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:37":{"id":"e:4:37","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 37","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 standard storage for maximum performance, Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering for intelligent, durable storage, and Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive for archival storage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon EC2 instance store for maximum performance, AWS DataSync for durable data storage and Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive for archival storage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon EBS for maximum performance, Amazon S3 for durable data storage, and Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive for archival storage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon EC2 instance store for maximum performance, Amazon S3 for durable data storage, and Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive for archival storage\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media company is evaluating the possibility of moving its IT infrastructure to the AWS Cloud. The company needs at least 10 terabytes of storage with the maximum possible I/O performance for processing certain video files on its servers. The company also needs close to 450 terabytes of very durable storage for storing media content and almost double of it, i.e. 900 terabytes for archival of legacy data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich set of services will you recommend to meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon EC2 instance store for maximum performance, Amazon S3 for durable data storage, and Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive for archival storage\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAn instance store provides temporary block-level storage for your instance. This storage is located on disks that are physically attached to the host computer. An instance store is ideal for the temporary storage of information that changes frequently, such as buffers, caches, scratch data, and other temporary content, or for data that is replicated across a fleet of instances, such as a load-balanced pool of web servers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can specify instance store volumes for an instance only when you launch it. You can\u0027t detach an instance store volume from one instance and attach it to a different instance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSome instance types use NVMe or SATA-based solid-state drives (SSD) to deliver high random I/O performance. This is a good option when you need storage with very low latency, but you don\u0027t need the data to persist when the instance terminates or you can take advantage of fault-tolerant architectures.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Standard offers high durability, availability, and performance object storage for frequently accessed data. Because it delivers low latency and high throughput, Amazon S3 Standard is appropriate for a wide variety of use cases, including cloud applications, dynamic websites, content distribution, mobile and gaming applications, and big data analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Glacier is a secure, durable, and low-cost storage class for data archiving. You can reliably store any amount of data at costs that are competitive with or cheaper than on-premises solutions. To keep costs low yet suitable for varying needs, Amazon S3 Glacier provides three retrieval options that range from a few minutes to hours. You can upload objects directly to Amazon S3 Glacier, or use S3 Lifecycle policies to transfer data between any of the Amazon S3 Storage Classes for active data (S3 Standard, S3 Intelligent-Tiering, S3 Standard-IA, and S3 One Zone-IA) and S3 Glacier.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3 standard storage for maximum performance, Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering for intelligent, durable storage, and Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive for archival storage\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EC2 instance store volumes provide the best I/O performance for low latency requirements, so Amazon S3 standard storage is not the right fit for I/O performance requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class is designed to optimize costs by automatically moving data to the most cost-effective access tier, without performance impact or operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive is Amazon S3’s lowest-cost storage class and supports long-term retention and digital preservation for data that may be accessed once or twice a year. It is designed for customers — particularly those in highly-regulated industries, such as the Financial Services, Healthcare, and Public Sectors — that retain data sets for 7-10 years or longer to meet regulatory compliance requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon EBS for maximum performance, Amazon S3 for durable data storage, and Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive for archival storage\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS) provides block-level storage volumes for use with EC2 instances. Amazon EBS volumes are particularly well-suited for use as the primary storage for file systems, databases, or for any applications that require fine granular updates and access to raw, unformatted, block-level storage. For high I/O performance, instance store volumes are a better option.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon EC2 instance store for maximum performance, AWS DataSync for durable data storage and Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive for archival storage\u003c/strong\u003e -AWS DataSync is an online data movement and discovery service that simplifies and accelerates data migrations to AWS as well as moving data to and from on-premises storage, edge locations, other cloud providers, and AWS Storage services. AWS DataSync cannot be used for durable data storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:48":{"id":"e:4:48","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 48","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Athena Query Editor to access Apache Spark from Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Spark-enabled Athena workgroup\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpdate Athena query settings to enable Apache Spark\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eActivate Apache Spark in the underlying Amazon Athena Engine version\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has developed Athena SQL queries for Extract, Transform, and Load (ETL) tasks by using Create Table As Select (CTAS). The company now needs to replace SQL with Apache Spark for its new analytics solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich configuration changes are needed to begin using Apache Spark on Amazon Athena ?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Spark-enabled Athena workgroup\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena makes it easy to interactively run data analytics and exploration using Apache Spark without the need to plan for, configure, or manage resources. Running Apache Spark applications on Athena means submitting Spark code for processing and receiving the results directly without the need for additional configuration. You can use the simplified notebook experience in the Amazon Athena console to develop Apache Spark applications using Python or Athena notebook APIs. Apache Spark on Amazon Athena is serverless and provides automatic, on-demand scaling that delivers instant-on compute to meet changing data volumes and processing requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo get started with Apache Spark on Amazon Athena, you must first create a Spark-enabled Athena workgroup. After you switch to the workgroup, you can create a notebook or open an existing notebook.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate Athena query settings to enable Apache Spark\u003c/strong\u003e - This option acts as a distractor. There is no such thing as Athena query settings that can be used to enable Apache Spark.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eActivate Apache Spark in the underlying Amazon Athena Engine version\u003c/strong\u003e - Athena occasionally releases a new engine version to provide improved performance, functionality, and code fixes. Engine versioning is configured per workgroup. You can use workgroups to control which query engine your queries use and whether to let Athena automatically upgrade your workgroups. There is no such option to directly activate Apache Spark in the underlying Amazon Athena Engine version.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Athena Query Editor to access Apache Spark from Athena\u003c/strong\u003e - Athena query editor is used to run queries. In fact, after the initial setup, Athena opens in the query editor when accessed from the Athena console. This is no specific configuration to use Apache Spark on Amazon Athena query editor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/notebooks-spark-getting-started.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/notebooks-spark-getting-started.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/notebooks-spark.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/notebooks-spark.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/engine-versions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/engine-versions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:39":{"id":"e:7:39","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 39","correct":[0,1,2],"answers":["Create a MySQL or PostgreSQL Aurora  database.","Modify security groups to allow JDBC connections.","Set JDBC configuration values in hive-site.xml.","Configure the AWS Glue Data Catalog.","Set up cross-account access for EMR Serverless and AWS Glue Data Catalog.","Ensure that you do not write to the same metastore table concurrently."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA large-scale data analytics company is migrating their big data platform to Amazon Web Services (AWS). They have chosen to use Amazon Elastic MapReduce (EMR) for processing large datasets and Amazon Aurora as a Hive metastore.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe company’s data engineers need to configure Amazon EMR to use Amazon Aurora as a Hive metastore. This setup will allow the metadata to persist even after the EMR cluster is terminated, enabling cost savings and improved query performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following steps need to be taken? (SELECT THREE.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon EMR uses an external database to store metadata about the tables, schema, and other Hive data structures. The company’s data engineers would need to create an Amazon Aurora database (either MySQL or PostgreSQL) that will serve as the Hive metastore. This is a crucial step because the Hive metastore is a central repository of Hive metadata. It persists metadata for Hive tables and partitions in a relational database, and provides high availability, and ease of use.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ea518a13e89bb402f9570db7c72a5b59b14a2bdc4bf585a8d0b3d560f890e110.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHive uses JDBC to connect to the metastore database. The data engineers would need to modify the security groups of both the Amazon EMR cluster and the Amazon Aurora database to allow these connections. This is important because the security group acts like a virtual firewall that controls the traffic for one or more instances. When you launch an instance, you can associate one or more security groups with the instance. You add rules to each security group that allow traffic to or from its associated instances.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSet JDBC configuration values in hive-site.xml. This involves creating a configuration file called\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ehiveConfiguration.json\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;containing edits to\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ehive-site.xml\u003c/code\u003e. This file specifies the JDBC connection parameters that Hive uses to connect to the metastore database. The data engineers would need to set the appropriate JDBC configuration values in this file to point to the Amazon Aurora database. This is necessary because the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ehive-site.xml\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;file is where Hive reads the configuration for the metastore connection, among other settings. The JDBC connection parameters that Hive uses to connect to the metastore database are defined in this file.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Create a MySQL or PostgreSQL Aurora database\u003c/strong\u003e.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Modify security groups to allow JDBC connections\u003c/strong\u003e.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Set JDBC configuration values in hive-site.xml\u003c/strong\u003e.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eConfigure the AWS Glue Data Catalog\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because it is not relevant when using Amazon Aurora as the Hive metastore. AWS Glue Data Catalog is an alternative to using an external Hive metastore, but it’s not used in conjunction with Aurora for this purpose.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up cross-account access for EMR Serverless and AWS Glue Data Catalog\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because it is also not relevant when using Amazon Aurora as the Hive metastore. This would be necessary if you were using the AWS Glue Data Catalog and needed to access it from a different AWS account, but it’s not applicable in this scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnsure that you do not write to the same metastore table concurrently\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because this is just a cautionary step to avoid data conflicts when sharing metastore information between two clusters. While it’s good practice to avoid concurrent writes, it’s not a step in the process of configuring Amazon EMR to use Amazon Aurora as a Hive metastore. It’s more of a usage guideline after the setup is complete. The data engineers would need to be aware of this guideline when using the Hive metastore, but it’s not a step they would need to take to set up the metastore.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-hive-metastore-external.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-hive-metastore-external.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-metastore-external-hive.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-metastore-external-hive.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/migrate-and-deploy-your-apache-hive-metastore-on-amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/migrate-and-deploy-your-apache-hive-metastore-on-amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these Amazon Aurora and Amazon EMR Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-aurora/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-aurora/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-23":{"id":"p:2-23","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 23","correct":[1,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge rules to start AWS Glue ETL jobs when new S3 objects arrive, chain additional EventBridge rules for later stages, and manage complex dependencies across rules\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Step Functions to coordinate the AWS Glue ETL jobs, load stages, and dependency logic, and configure retry and error handling behavior in the state machine\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS CodePipeline to coordinate the ETL workflow and infrastructure deployments, and define the AWS Glue ETL job dependencies as pipeline stages\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to create a DAG that coordinates the AWS Glue ETL jobs and Redshift load tasks on a managed Airflow environment\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue workflows to create a visual graph of the ETL jobs, triggers, and dependencies that run the multi-step data processing pipeline\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["EventBridge phù hợp khởi động pipeline theo sự kiện. Nối nhiều rule để quản lý phụ thuộc phức tạp thiếu mô hình workflow và retry tập trung như hai đáp án đúng.","Step Functions điều phối các bước Glue và nạp Redshift, thể hiện phụ thuộc, retry và bắt lỗi trong state machine. Không cần tự quản lý máy chủ điều phối.","CodePipeline phục vụ quy trình phát hành phần mềm/CI-CD. Không phải công cụ phù hợp nhất cho điều phối ETL dữ liệu nhiều bước.","MWAA điều phối được ETL nhưng cần môi trường Airflow được quản lý. Theo tiêu chí serverless của bộ đề, Step Functions hoặc Glue workflows phù hợp hơn.","Glue workflows biểu diễn job, trigger và quan hệ phụ thuộc dưới dạng đồ thị. Phù hợp điều phối nhiều bước trong hệ sinh thái Glue mà không dựng scheduler riêng."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company is building a serverless, multi-stage data processing pipeline in AWS. The pipeline reads raw claims data from an Amazon S3 data lake, transforms the data by using several AWS Glue ETL jobs, and loads the curated results into an Amazon Redshift database. A data engineer must orchestrate the workflow, manage dependencies between stages, and keep the pipeline serverless.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solutions will meet these requirements? (Choose TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:23":{"id":"e:2:23","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 23","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the reservation session data in an Amazon RDS for MySQL cluster and configure RDS Optimized Reads for the database instances\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the reservation session data in an Amazon S3 Apache Iceberg table and enable S3 Transfer Acceleration for low-latency object access\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the reservation session data in an Amazon ElastiCache for Redis cluster, configure the application to write all transactional records directly to the cache, and create daily snapshots for durability\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the reservation session data in an Amazon DynamoDB table and configure DynamoDB Accelerator to cache frequently accessed items\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA travel booking company needs to store semi-structured reservation session data for a customer-facing application. The application writes session records only when customers create or modify itineraries, but it reads the records frequently during search, checkout, and support workflows. The database layer must be serverless, require minimal administration, and return frequently accessed records within milliseconds.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the reservation session data in an Amazon DynamoDB table and configure DynamoDB Accelerator to cache frequently accessed items\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon DynamoDB is a serverless, fully managed NoSQL database that supports key-value and document data models. This makes it a good fit for semi-structured transactional data such as reservation session objects, customer selections, and itinerary metadata.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDynamoDB provides single-digit millisecond performance at scale without requiring the company to manage database servers, storage allocation, patching, or cluster capacity in the same way as traditional databases. The application writes infrequently but reads frequently, which aligns well with DynamoDB access patterns when the table design uses appropriate partition keys and access patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) improves read performance for read-heavy workloads by adding a fully managed in-memory cache in front of DynamoDB. Because the application frequently retrieves the same session data and requires millisecond retrieval, DAX can reduce read latency and offload repeated reads from the DynamoDB table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the reservation session data in an Amazon ElastiCache for Redis cluster, configure the application to write all transactional records directly to the cache, and create daily snapshots for durability\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon ElastiCache for Redis can provide very low-latency access for cached data. However, using Redis as the primary transactional data store for all session records increases durability, recovery, and data modeling risk compared with DynamoDB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also requires the company to manage cache-specific design considerations such as memory sizing, eviction behavior, cluster configuration, and snapshot recovery. DynamoDB with DAX provides a more appropriate primary database plus cache pattern with lower operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the reservation session data in an Amazon RDS for MySQL cluster and configure RDS Optimized Reads for the database instances\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon RDS for MySQL can store transactional data in a relational database. However, an RDS cluster requires more database administration than a serverless NoSQL database for this use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eRDS Optimized Reads can improve query processing that uses temporary objects on supported instance classes. It does not provide a serverless document database model or an in-memory acceleration layer for frequently accessed semi-structured records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the reservation session data in an Amazon S3 Apache Iceberg table and enable S3 Transfer Acceleration for low-latency object access\u003c/strong\u003e - Apache Iceberg tables in Amazon S3 are designed for analytical table workloads, especially data lake queries, schema evolution, and large-scale analytics. They are not designed for millisecond transactional reads from a customer-facing application.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eS3 Transfer Acceleration does not solve the database access pattern. It improves data transfer into or out of S3 over long distances, but it does not provide low-latency item retrieval, transactional key-value lookups, or caching for frequent application reads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Introduction.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Introduction.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/DAX.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/DAX.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/evaluate-dax-suitability.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/evaluate-dax-suitability.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless/latest/devguide/starter-dynamodb.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless/latest/devguide/starter-dynamodb.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/reduce-latency-and-cost-in-read-heavy-applications-using-amazon-dynamodb-accelerator/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/reduce-latency-and-cost-in-read-heavy-applications-using-amazon-dynamodb-accelerator/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:6":{"id":"e:1:6","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 6","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDistribute the dynamic content through Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDistribute the dynamic content through Amazon EFS\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDistribute the static content through Amazon EFS\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDistribute the static content through Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team has deployed a microservice to the Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS). The application layer is in a Docker container that provides both static and dynamic content through an Application Load Balancer. With increasing load, the Amazon ECS cluster is experiencing higher network usage. The team has looked into the network usage and found that 90% of it is due to distributing static content of the application.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat do you recommend to improve the application\u0027s network usage and decrease costs?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDistribute the static content through Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use Amazon S3 to host a static website. On a static website, individual web pages include static content. They might also contain client-side scripts. To host a static website on Amazon S3, you configure an Amazon S3 bucket for website hosting and then upload your website content to the bucket. When you configure a bucket as a static website, you must enable website hosting, set permissions, and create and add an index document. Depending on your website requirements, you can also configure redirects, web traffic logging, and a custom error document.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDistributing the static content through Amazon S3 allows us to offload most of the network usage to Amazon S3 and free up our applications running on Amazon ECS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDistribute the dynamic content through Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - By contrast, a dynamic website relies on server-side processing, including server-side scripts such as PHP, JSP, or ASP.NET. Amazon S3 does not support server-side scripting, but AWS has other resources for hosting dynamic websites.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDistribute the static content through Amazon EFS\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDistribute the dynamic content through Amazon EFS\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) provides a simple, scalable, fully managed elastic NFS file system for use with AWS Cloud services and on-premises resources. Using Amazon EFS for static or dynamic content will not change anything as static content on EFS would still have to be distributed by the Amazon ECS instances.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/WebsiteHosting.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/WebsiteHosting.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:22":{"id":"e:4:22","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 22","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eChange the application architecture to create a new S3 bucket for each customer and then upload each customer\u0027s files directly under the respective buckets\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the application architecture to create a new S3 bucket for each day\u0027s data and then upload the daily files directly under that day\u0027s bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the application architecture to create customer-specific custom prefixes within the single bucket and then upload the daily files into those prefixed locations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the application architecture to use the S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data-storage service uses Amazon S3 under the hood to power its storage offerings which allow the customers to upload and view the files immediately. Currently, all the customer files are uploaded directly under a single S3 bucket. The data analytics team has started seeing scalability issues where customer file uploads are failing during peak access hours with more than 5000 requests per second.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents the MOST resource-efficient and cost-optimal way of resolving this issue?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange the application architecture to create customer-specific custom prefixes within the single bucket and then upload the daily files into those prefixed locations\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) is an object storage service that offers industry-leading scalability, data availability, security, and performance. Your applications can easily achieve thousands of transactions per second in request performance when uploading and retrieving storage from Amazon S3. Amazon S3 automatically scales to high request rates. For example, your application can achieve at least 3,500 PUT/COPY/POST/DELETE or 5,500 GET/HEAD requests per second per prefix in a bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThere are no limits to the number of prefixes in a bucket. You can increase your read or write performance by parallelizing reads. For example, if you create 10 prefixes in an Amazon S3 bucket to parallelize reads, you could scale your read performance to 55,000 read requests per second. Please see this example for more clarity on prefixes:\nif you have a file f1 stored in an S3 object path like so \u003ccode\u003es3://your_bucket_name/folder1/sub_folder_1/f1\u003c/code\u003e, then \u003ccode\u003e/folder1/sub_folder_1/\u003c/code\u003e becomes the prefix for file f1.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSome data lake applications on Amazon S3 scan millions or billions of objects for queries that run over petabytes of data. These data lake applications achieve single-instance transfer rates that maximize the network interface used for their Amazon EC2 instance, which can be up to 100 Gb/s on a single instance. These applications then aggregate throughput across multiple instances to get multiple terabits per second. Therefore creating customer-specific custom prefixes within the single bucket and then uploading the daily files into those prefixed locations is the BEST solution for the given constraints.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eOptimizing Amazon S3 Performance:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/39eaecfe254e3b96907ff73fb49b41f9bec7daddab0304291800b3340e051deb.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/39eaecfe254e3b96907ff73fb49b41f9bec7daddab0304291800b3340e051deb.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/optimizing-performance.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/optimizing-performance.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange the application architecture to create a new S3 bucket for each customer and then upload each customer\u0027s files directly under the respective buckets\u003c/strong\u003e - Creating a new S3 bucket for each new customer is an inefficient way of handling resource availability (S3 buckets need to be globally unique) as some customers may use the service sparingly but the bucket name is locked for them forever. Moreover, this is really not required as we can use S3 prefixes to improve the performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange the application architecture to create a new S3 bucket for each day\u0027s data and then upload the daily files directly under that day\u0027s bucket\u003c/strong\u003e - Creating a new S3 bucket for each new day\u0027s data is also an inefficient way of handling resource availability (S3 buckets need to be globally unique) as some of the bucket names may not be available for daily data processing. Moreover, this is really not required as we can use S3 prefixes to improve the performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange the application architecture to use the S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class\u003c/strong\u003e - Glacier Deep Archive is designed to provide durable and secure long-term storage for large amounts of data at a price that is competitive with off-premises tape archival services. Data is stored across 3 or more AWS Availability Zones and can be retrieved in 12 hours or less. This option is a distractor because Glacier Deep Archive would not allow customers to retrieve the objects immediately.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/optimizing-performance.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/optimizing-performance.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:61":{"id":"e:3:61","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 61","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) to ingest incoming data. Configure an AWS Lambda function to read events from the SQS queue and upload the events to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to ingest data. Configure an AWS Lambda function to cleanse/transform the data written into the Firehose delivery stream which is then delivered to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Configure a stream for incoming raw data. Kinesis Agent can be used to write data to the stream. Configure an Amazon Kinesis Data Analytics application to read the raw data and transform it to the necessary format before writing it to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka. Create a topic for the initial raw data. Use a Kafka producer to write data on this topic. Use the Apache Kafka consumer API to create a consumer application (that can be hosted on Amazon EC2 instance) that reads data from this topic, transforms the data as needed, and writes it to Amazon S3 for final storage\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company wants to store all of its consumer data on Amazon S3. Before storing the data, the company must clean it by standardizing the formats of a few of the data columns. A single data record might range in size from 500 KB to 10 MB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of these options represents the right solution?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka. Create a topic for the initial raw data. Use a Kafka producer to write data on this topic. Use the Apache Kafka consumer API to create a consumer application (that can be hosted on Amazon EC2 instance) that reads data from this topic, transforms the data as needed, and writes it to Amazon S3 for final storage\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (Amazon MSK) is a fully managed service that makes it easy for you to build and run applications that use Apache Kafka to process streaming data. Apache Kafka is an open-source platform for building real-time streaming data pipelines and applications. With Amazon MSK, you can use native Apache Kafka APIs to populate data lakes, stream changes to and from databases, and power machine learning and analytics applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eApache Kafka stores records in topics. Data producers write records to topics and consumers read records from topics. Each record in Apache Kafka consists of a key, a value, and a timestamp.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon MSK is preferred over Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and Kinesis Firehose because of the limitation on record size. The maximum record size of an Amazon MSK is 100 MB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to ingest data. Configure an AWS Lambda function to cleanse/transform the data written into the Firehose delivery stream which is then delivered to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - The maximum size of a record sent to Kinesis Data Firehose, before base64-encoding, is 1,000 KiB. Hence, Firehose cannot be used for this requirement where data can be of the order of 10MB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Configure a stream for incoming raw data. Kinesis Agent can be used to write data to the stream. Configure an Amazon Kinesis Data Analytics application to read the raw data and transform it to the necessary format before writing it to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - For Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, the maximum size of a data blob (the data payload before Base64-encoding) within one record is 1 megabyte (MB). Hence, Kinesis Data Streams cannot be used for this requirement where data can be of the order of 10MB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) to ingest incoming data. Configure an AWS Lambda function to read events from the SQS queue and upload the events to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon SQS offers a reliable, highly scalable hosted queue for storing messages as they travel between applications or microservices. It moves data between distributed application components and helps you decouple these components. Amazon SQS provides common middleware constructs such as dead-letter queues and poison-pill management. It is not the best fit as a real-time data stream processing solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/limits.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/limits.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/limits.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/limits.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://www.amazonaws.cn/en/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://www.amazonaws.cn/en/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:18":{"id":"e:5:18","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 18","correct":[0],"answers":["Query the dataset by using dot and bracket notation to access nested data in JSON columns.","\u003cspan\u003eQuery the dataset by using SQL aggregate functions such as \u003ccode\u003eCOUNT\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eSUM\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eAVG\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eMIN\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eMAX\u003c/code\u003e, et cetera\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eQuery the dataset by using SQL pattern matching operators like \u003ccode\u003eLIKE\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eSIMILAR TO\u003c/code\u003e, or \u003ccode\u003ePOSIX\u003c/code\u003e expressions.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eQuery the dataset by using string functions such as \u003ccode\u003eSUBSTRING\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eCHAR_LENGTH\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eUPPER\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eLOWER\u003c/code\u003e, et cetera\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial company recently migrated its cryptocurrency data warehouse on-premises to AWS and has chosen Amazon Redshift as its data storage solution. The company’s collection of digital currency information can either be in a semistructured or nested data format stored in Redshift as a \u003ccode\u003eSUPER\u003c/code\u003e data type. The data engineering team’s task is to query the large dataset stored in the Redshift cluster with less operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would meet the requirements with the LEAST amount of effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift supports querying nested data in JSON columns using dot and bracket notation. This allows you to access and query nested data efficiently, which is especially useful when dealing with large datasets. For example, if you have a JSON column\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003edata\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;with nested fields\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003efield1\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;and\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003efield2\u003c/code\u003e, you can access the nested data using the following syntax:\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003edata.field1\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;or\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003edata[\u0027field1\u0027]\u003c/code\u003e. This approach provides the least operational overhead, making it the most suitable for the given task. It allows you to access the required data directly without having to process the entire JSON object, thereby reducing the computational resources required for the query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d00935a803832d408729c1fb8022e3f94321a1c69e4ffd23cdae57d5da7bd2c4.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eQuery the dataset by using dot and bracket notation to access nested data in JSON columns.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eQuery the dataset by using string functions such as\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eSUBSTRING\u003c/code\u003e,\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eCHAR_LENGTH\u003c/code\u003e,\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eUPPER\u003c/code\u003e,\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eLOWER\u003c/code\u003e, et cetera\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While string functions can be used to manipulate and query data, they may not provide the least operational overhead when dealing with large datasets. These functions are more suited for specific data manipulation tasks rather than efficient querying of large datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;Query the dataset by using SQL pattern matching operators like\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eLIKE\u003c/code\u003e,\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eSIMILAR TO\u003c/code\u003e, or\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ePOSIX\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;expressions\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because the SQL pattern matching operators are primarily used for finding specific patterns within a particular table or database only. This is not commonly used to query semistructured or nested datasets. Moreover, they can be computationally expensive and may not provide the least operational overhead when querying large datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eQuery the dataset by using SQL aggregate functions such as\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eCOUNT\u003c/code\u003e,\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eSUM\u003c/code\u003e,\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eAVG\u003c/code\u003e,\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMIN\u003c/code\u003e,\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMAX\u003c/code\u003e, et cetera\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because SQL aggregate functions are mostly used to perform calculations on a set of values and return a single value. While they are useful for data analysis tasks, they may not provide the least operational overhead when querying large datasets. These functions are more suited for data analysis tasks rather than efficiently querying large datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/query-super.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/query-super.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/copy-parameters-data-format.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/copy-parameters-data-format.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:18":{"id":"e:3:18","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 18","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse federated queries feature of Amazon Redshift to retrieve and efficiently query the streaming data for analytics\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDirect the Kinesis Data Streams output into a Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream. Leverage the direct integration of Kinesis Data Firehose with Amazon Redshift Serverless to deliver the streaming data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to retrieve and efficiently query the streaming data for analytics\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the streaming ingestion feature of Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn e-commerce company uses Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to process real-time clickstream data from its website. The company needs to perform near real-time analytics of the streaming data in conjunction with the previous day\u0027s data. The streaming data should be stored in Amazon Redshift Serverless service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the streaming ingestion feature of Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eStreaming ingestion provides low-latency, high-speed ingestion of stream data from Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka into an Amazon Redshift provisioned or Amazon Redshift Serverless materialized view. It lowers the time it takes to access data and it reduces storage cost. You can configure streaming ingestion for your Amazon Redshift cluster or for Amazon Redshift Serverless and create a materialized view. After that, using materialized-view refresh, you can ingest hundreds of megabytes of data per second. This results in fast access to external data that is quickly refreshed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUse cases for Amazon Redshift streaming ingestion involve working with data that is generated continually (streamed) and must be processed within a short period (latency) of its generation. This is called near real-time analytics. Sources of data can vary and include IoT devices, system telemetry data, or clickstream data from a busy website or application.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWith streaming ingestion, you bypass several steps that involve other service(s) needed between Kinesis Data Streams and Redshift. The advantages of this configuration are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cp\u003eYou don\u0027t have to send data to an Amazon Data Firehose delivery stream, because with streaming ingestion, data can be sent directly from Kinesis Data Streams to a materialized view in a Redshift database.\u003c/p\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cp\u003eYou don\u0027t have to land streamed data in Amazon S3, because streaming ingestion data goes directly to the Redshift materialized view.\u003c/p\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cp\u003eYou don\u0027t have to write and run COPY commands because the data in the materialized view is refreshed directly from the stream. Loading data from Amazon S3 to Redshift isn\u0027t part of the process.\u003c/p\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7d27763ceaaf365128975e2a9b07832d027356d989698594a0ca85f5aaba91fb.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7d27763ceaaf365128975e2a9b07832d027356d989698594a0ca85f5aaba91fb.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse federated queries feature of Amazon Redshift to retrieve and efficiently query the streaming data for analytics\u003c/strong\u003e - By using federated queries in Amazon Redshift, you can query and analyze data across operational databases, data warehouses, and data lakes. With the Federated Query feature, you can integrate queries from Amazon Redshift on live data in external databases with queries across your Amazon Redshift and Amazon S3 environments. This option acts as a distractor since Redshift federated queries cannot be used to process streaming data from external sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDirect the Kinesis Data Streams output into a Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream. Leverage the direct integration of Kinesis Data Firehose with Amazon Redshift Serverless to deliver the streaming data\u003c/strong\u003e - This option introduces an unnecessary hop in the form of Kinesis Data Firehose to the solution. The ideal solution should have the LEAST operational overhead, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to retrieve and efficiently query the streaming data for analytics\u003c/strong\u003e - Using Amazon Redshift Spectrum, you can efficiently query and retrieve structured and semistructured data from files in Amazon S3 without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables. This option acts as a distractor since Redshift Spectrum cannot be used to process streaming data from external sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-create-sql-command.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-create-sql-command.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:26":{"id":"e:4:26","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 26","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003ePlace each department’s tables and views in dedicated schemas and manage permissions at the schema level\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore department data in separate S3 prefixes, create external schemas for each prefix, and manage all access through bucket policies\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRename every table and view to include the department name and manage permissions by following the naming convention manually\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePlace each department’s tables and views in separate databases and manage permissions at the database level for each department\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA manufacturing company uses a shared Amazon Redshift cluster as its central analytics warehouse. The engineering department and the finance department each maintain their own tables and views in the same warehouse.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company’s security policy requires each department to have separate permissions for its own analytics objects. Analysts also frequently run SQL queries that join engineering objects with finance objects in the same query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePlace each department’s tables and views in dedicated schemas and manage permissions at the schema level\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDedicated schemas are the best fit because Amazon Redshift schemas provide a logical way to group database objects such as tables and views. The company can create one schema for engineering and another schema for finance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company can grant schema-level permissions and object privileges to the appropriate department users or roles. This makes permission management simpler than granting access separately on every individual table and view.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis design also supports cross-department SQL queries with low overhead. Analysts can reference objects by schema-qualified names in the same Redshift database, which avoids the additional complexity of separate databases, separate clusters, or custom permission conventions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePlace each department’s tables and views in separate databases and manage permissions at the database level for each department\u003c/strong\u003e - Separate databases can isolate objects and permissions. Amazon Redshift also supports cross-database queries in supported configurations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that this adds more complexity than schemas for objects that users often query together. Dedicated schemas in the same database provide simpler organization and permission management for this shared warehouse pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRename every table and view to include the department name and manage permissions by following the naming convention manually\u003c/strong\u003e - A naming convention can make it easier for users to identify which department owns each table or view. This can improve readability and governance documentation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not enforce access control. Redshift permissions must be granted on database objects, schemas, roles, or users. Names alone do not prevent unauthorized access or simplify grants.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore department data in separate S3 prefixes, create external schemas for each prefix, and manage all access through bucket policies\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 prefixes and bucket policies can control access to data lake objects. External schemas can make S3 data queryable from Redshift Spectrum.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option does not match the requirement as directly because the company is managing Redshift warehouse tables and views. Moving governance to S3 bucket policies adds unnecessary data lake access management complexity for native Redshift objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_Schemas_and_tables-schema-based-privileges.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_Schemas_and_tables-schema-based-privileges.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_GRANT.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_GRANT.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_CREATE_SCHEMA.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_CREATE_SCHEMA.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/cross-database-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/cross-database-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:49":{"id":"e:7:49","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 49","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eKEY\u003c/code\u003e distribution style for the \u003ccode\u003esales\u003c/code\u003e table based on the \u003ccode\u003eproduct_id\u003c/code\u003e column and the \u003ccode\u003eALL\u003c/code\u003e distribution style for the \u003ccode\u003eproducts\u003c/code\u003e table to replicate it across all nodes in the cluster.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eEVEN\u003c/code\u003e distribution style for both \u003ccode\u003esales\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003eproducts\u003c/code\u003e tables to distribute the rows evenly across all nodes in the cluster.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eKEY\u003c/code\u003e distribution style for both \u003ccode\u003esales\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003eproducts\u003c/code\u003e tables based on the \u003ccode\u003eproduct_id\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003eid\u003c/code\u003e columns respectively to distribute the rows across the nodes in the cluster.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eALL\u003c/code\u003e distribution style for both \u003ccode\u003esales\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003eproducts\u003c/code\u003e tables to replicate all rows across all nodes in the cluster.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multinational corporation has recently started using Amazon Redshift for its data warehousing needs. The company\u0027s database contains a large \u003ccode\u003esales\u003c/code\u003e table that stores all the transactions and a smaller \u003ccode\u003eproducts\u003c/code\u003e table containing product details. The \u003ccode\u003esales\u003c/code\u003e table is frequently joined with the \u003ccode\u003eproducts\u003c/code\u003e table for generating various reports.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe management team has noticed that some queries are taking longer than expected to return results, especially those involving joins between the \u003ccode\u003esales\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003eproducts\u003c/code\u003e tables. The task is to optimize the performance of these queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich distribution style should be recommended to optimize the performance of the join queries?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift is a data warehousing service that makes it simple and cost-effective to analyze all your data using standard SQL. One of the key features of Redshift is its ability to handle large amounts of data and complex queries by distributing data and query execution across multiple nodes. The method by which Redshift distributes the data across the nodes is called the distribution style. There are three types of distribution styles in Redshift:\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eKEY\u003c/code\u003e,\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eALL\u003c/code\u003e, and\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eEVEN\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b7975eb8291af7b781cd8aa89255bab1fd8f8eb4c5fa666e32cb5c936ce1468c.gif\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eKEY\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;distribution style distributes the data across the nodes based on the values in one column. The rows with the same value in the distribution key column are located on the same node. This is beneficial when two tables are frequently joined on the distribution key column, as it allows for fast, local joins. The\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eALL\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;distribution style replicates the entire table on every node. This is beneficial for small tables that are frequently joined with large tables, as it allows for fast, local joins. However, it uses a lot of storage space and can slow down data loading and update operations. The\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eEVEN\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;distribution style (the default) distributes the rows evenly across all nodes without considering the join patterns. This could lead to a lot of network I/O during the join operations, which could slow down the query performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the given scenario, the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003esales\u003c/code\u003e table is a large table that stores all the sales transactions and the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eproducts\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;table is a smaller table that contains product details. The\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003esales\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;table is frequently joined with the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eproducts\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;table to generate various reports. Therefore, using the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eKEY\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;distribution style for the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003esales\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;table based on the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eproduct_id\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;column and the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eALL\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;distribution style for the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eproducts\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;table to replicate it across all nodes in the cluster would be beneficial. This would ensure that the rows with the same product ID are located on the same node, allowing for fast, local joins and that the entire\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eproducts\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;table is available on every node.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cspan\u003e \u003cstrong\u003eUse the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eKEY\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;distribution style for the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003esales\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;table based on the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eproduct_id\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;column and the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eALL\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;distribution style for the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eproducts\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;table to replicate it across all nodes in the cluster.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eEVEN\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;distribution style for both\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003esales\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;and\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eproducts\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;tables to distribute the rows evenly across all nodes in the cluster\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eEVEN\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;distribution style simply distributes the rows evenly across all nodes in the cluster without considering the join patterns. This could lead to a lot of network I/O during the join operations, which could slow down the query performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;Use the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eKEY\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;distribution style for both\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003esales\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;and\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eproducts\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;tables based on the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eproduct_id\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;and\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eid\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;columns respectively to distribute the rows across the nodes in the cluster\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect. While this approach can ensure related data resides on the same node, it does not consider the size disparity between the tables. For very large and smaller tables, replicating the smaller table across tables\u0026nbsp;can further reduce the need for cross-node data movement during joins, optimizing performance more than matching \u003ccode\u003eKEY\u003c/code\u003e distributions alone.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eALL\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;distribution style for both\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003esales\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;and\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eproducts\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;tables to replicate all rows across all nodes in the cluster\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect. Using the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eALL\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;distribution style for both tables would result in a lot of storage space being used, as every row from both tables would be replicated on every node. This could also slow down the data loading and update operations. This distribution style is typically recommended for small dimension tables that are frequently joined with large fact tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Distributing_data.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Distributing_data.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_choosing_dist_sort.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_choosing_dist_sort.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-engineerings-advanced-table-design-playbook-distribution-styles-and-distribution-keys/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-engineerings-advanced-table-design-playbook-distribution-styles-and-distribution-keys/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:14":{"id":"e:2:14","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 14","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDeploy Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to analyze the telemetry stream, detect unusual sensor patterns, and invoke alerts when machine readings deviate from expected ranges\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure CloudWatch alarms for key Kinesis Data Streams and Data Firehose metrics, including IncomingBytes, OutgoingBytes, and DeliveryToS3.Success\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lambda function that runs every day, calls AWS APIs to inspect the Kinesis stream and Firehose delivery stream, and sends SNS notifications\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable AWS CloudTrail data events for the S3 bucket and review object-level API activity to determine whether Data Firehose delivered telemetry files successfully\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA smart factory company runs a streaming data pipeline on AWS to process telemetry from industrial IoT devices. The pipeline uses Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest machine readings and Amazon Data Firehose to deliver the processed records to an Amazon S3 bucket. A data engineer must monitor the health of the pipeline and receive alerts when ingestion throughput, downstream consumption, or delivery to Amazon S3 shows abnormal behavior. The solution must require the least operational effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure CloudWatch alarms for key Kinesis Data Streams and Data Firehose metrics, including IncomingBytes, OutgoingBytes, and DeliveryToS3.Success\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon CloudWatch is the native monitoring service for both Kinesis Data Streams and Amazon Data Firehose. Kinesis Data Streams publishes metrics such as IncomingBytes and OutgoingBytes, which help the data engineer understand whether data producers and consumers are behaving as expected.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Data Firehose also publishes CloudWatch metrics for delivery behavior, including DeliveryToS3.Success for Amazon S3 destinations. Monitoring this metric helps the company confirm whether Firehose successfully delivers records to the S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCloudWatch alarms provide the least operational effort because the company can configure thresholds and notifications directly on managed service metrics. The data engineer does not need to build custom polling code, manually inspect logs, run SQL reconciliation jobs, or deploy a stream processing application only for pipeline health monitoring.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Lambda function that runs every day, calls AWS APIs to inspect the Kinesis stream and Firehose delivery stream, and sends SNS notifications\u003c/strong\u003e - A Lambda function could call AWS APIs, inspect stream status, and publish notifications through Amazon SNS. This design can work if the company builds all required checks and alert logic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this approach creates custom monitoring code that the team must deploy, test, schedule, and maintain. CloudWatch already receives the relevant metrics from Kinesis Data Streams and Data Firehose, so custom daily checks add unnecessary operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to analyze the telemetry stream, detect unusual sensor patterns, and invoke alerts when machine readings deviate from expected ranges\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink is well suited for real-time stream analytics and stateful event processing. It can detect anomalies in the actual telemetry values if the business needs operational analytics on machine behavior.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the requirement is to monitor the health of the streaming pipeline, not to analyze sensor data patterns. Deploying a Flink application only to monitor pipeline service health is more complex than using CloudWatch alarms on service metrics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable AWS CloudTrail data events for the S3 bucket and review object-level API activity to determine whether Data Firehose delivered telemetry files successfully\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS CloudTrail can record API activity, and S3 data events can provide object-level visibility for supported S3 actions. This can help with auditing access to objects in a bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, CloudTrail is not the best tool for monitoring streaming pipeline health. It does not provide a simple alarm-based view of Kinesis ingestion throughput, consumer output, or Firehose delivery success.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/monitoring-with-cloudwatch.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/monitoring-with-cloudwatch.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/monitoring-with-cloudwatch-metrics.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/monitoring-with-cloudwatch-metrics.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/AlarmThatSendsEmail.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/AlarmThatSendsEmail.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/monitoring-with-cloudwatch-logs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/monitoring-with-cloudwatch-logs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:2":{"id":"e:1:2","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 2","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the same S3 dataset instead of running the repeated queries in Amazon Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EMR to run a scheduled Spark preprocessing job that prepares summary files in Amazon S3 before the data engineer runs Athena queries\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable Amazon Athena query result reuse for the recurring queries in the same workgroup and configure an appropriate maximum age for reused results\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue crawler to refresh the Data Catalog before every dashboard update and require Athena to rerun each query after the crawler completes\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA subscription analytics company stores several terabytes of product usage events in an Amazon S3 data lake in the us-west-2 Region. A data engineer runs complex Amazon Athena queries many times each day to refresh executive dashboards. The queries use the same filter values and parameters, and the source data changes only a few times per day. The company needs to improve query response times and reduce query cost when previous results are still fresh enough to use.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Amazon Athena query result reuse for the recurring queries in the same workgroup and configure an appropriate maximum age for reused results\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena query result reuse allows Athena to return the result of a previous query instead of scanning the source data again. This feature is useful when the same query runs repeatedly and the source data does not change frequently enough to require a fresh scan every time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAthena checks for a matching previous query execution in the same workgroup. The query result must still be within the configured maximum age, and the user must have access to the referenced tables and the previous result location. If those conditions are met, Athena can bypass query execution and return the stored result.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution directly addresses both requirements. It improves performance because Athena can return a prior result faster, and it reduces cost because Athena avoids scanning the same S3 data repeatedly when the reused result is acceptable.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9d501d31dbc6539f19ec32fa9e16bd9a7879184b4a3bd4634fbb8e423f0cc80c.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9d501d31dbc6539f19ec32fa9e16bd9a7879184b4a3bd4634fbb8e423f0cc80c.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/reduce-cost-and-improve-query-performance-with-amazon-athena-query-result-reuse/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/reduce-cost-and-improve-query-performance-with-amazon-athena-query-result-reuse/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/06d2166b137f2e5275861a64ee13738eee354d05aec330fc2277fefda323ea39.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/06d2166b137f2e5275861a64ee13738eee354d05aec330fc2277fefda323ea39.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/reduce-cost-and-improve-query-performance-with-amazon-athena-query-result-reuse/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/reduce-cost-and-improve-query-performance-with-amazon-athena-query-result-reuse/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon EMR to run a scheduled Spark preprocessing job that prepares summary files in Amazon S3 before the data engineer runs Athena queries\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR can preprocess data at scale, and Spark can create optimized datasets for later querying. However, this option introduces an additional processing layer even though the main issue is repeated Athena queries with the same parameters.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePreprocessing can help when data layout, file format, partitioning, or compaction needs improvement. It does not directly reuse previously computed Athena results for identical queries, so it adds more operational overhead than necessary.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue crawler to refresh the Data Catalog before every dashboard update and require Athena to rerun each query after the crawler completes\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue crawlers update metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. They help discover schema changes and partitions so query engines such as Athena can understand the dataset structure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eRunning a crawler before every dashboard refresh does not prevent Athena from scanning the same data again. It can increase cost and runtime without solving the repeated-query optimization requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the same S3 dataset instead of running the repeated queries in Amazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift Spectrum can query data stored in Amazon S3 from Amazon Redshift. This can be useful when a team already uses Redshift and needs to join Redshift data with external S3 data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, moving the workload to Redshift Spectrum does not directly optimize repeated Athena queries that use the same parameters. It also introduces a different query environment rather than using Athena\u0027s native result reuse capability.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/reusing-query-results.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/reusing-query-results.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-query-optimization-techniques.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-query-optimization-techniques.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/reduce-cost-and-improve-query-performance-with-amazon-athena-query-result-reuse/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/reduce-cost-and-improve-query-performance-with-amazon-athena-query-result-reuse/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:30":{"id":"e:1:30","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 30","correct":[1,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eBasic Schema Copy\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Snowball Edge\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has a license-based, expensive, legacy commercial database solution deployed at its on-premises data center. The company wants to migrate this database to a more efficient, open-source, and cost-effective option on AWS Cloud. The CTO at the company wants a solution that can handle complex database configurations such as secondary indexes, foreign keys, and stored procedures.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following AWS services should be combined to handle this use case? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Database Migration Service helps you migrate databases to AWS quickly and securely. The source database remains fully operational during the migration, minimizing downtime to applications that rely on the database. AWS Database Migration Service supports homogeneous migrations such as Oracle to Oracle, as well as heterogeneous migrations between different database platforms, such as Oracle or Microsoft SQL Server to Amazon Aurora.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eGiven the use case where the CTO at the company wants to move away from license-based, expensive, legacy commercial database solutions deployed at the on-premises data center to more efficient, open-source, and cost-effective options on AWS Cloud, this is an example of heterogeneous database migrations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor such a scenario, the source and target database engines are different, like in the case of Oracle to Amazon Aurora, Oracle to PostgreSQL, or Microsoft SQL Server to MySQL migrations. In this case, the schema structure, data types, and database code of source and target databases can be quite different, requiring a schema and code transformation before the data migration starts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThat makes heterogeneous migrations a two-step process. First use the AWS Schema Conversion Tool to convert the source schema and code to match that of the target database, and then use the AWS Database Migration Service to migrate data from the source database to the target database. All the required data type conversions will automatically be done by the AWS Database Migration Service during the migration. The source database can be located on your on-premises environment outside of AWS, running on an Amazon EC2 instance, or it can be an Amazon RDS database. The target can be a database in Amazon EC2 or Amazon RDS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHeterogeneous Database Migrations:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/fb598a76a65c18f6926b6369fa6e417c69174f582d71bed79ee163ac874f75de.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/fb598a76a65c18f6926b6369fa6e417c69174f582d71bed79ee163ac874f75de.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/dms/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/dms/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Snowball Edge\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Snowball Edge Storage Optimized is the optimal choice if you need to securely and quickly transfer dozens of terabytes to petabytes of data to AWS. It provides up to 80 TB of usable HDD storage, 40 vCPUs, 1 TB of SATA SSD storage, and up to 40 Gb network connectivity to address large-scale data transfer and pre-processing use cases. As each Snowball Edge Storage Optimized device can handle 80TB of data, you can order 10 such devices to take care of the data transfer for all applications. The original Snowball devices were transitioned out of service and AWS Snowball Edge Storage Optimized are now the primary devices used for data transfer. You may see the Snowball device on the exam, just remember that the original Snowball device had 80TB of storage space. AWS Snowball Edge cannot be used for database migrations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy for customers to prepare and load their data for analytics. AWS Glue job is meant to be used for batch ETL data processing. Therefore, it cannot be used for database migrations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eBasic Schema Copy\u003c/strong\u003e - To quickly migrate a database schema to your target instance you can rely on the Basic Schema Copy feature of AWS Database Migration Service. Basic Schema Copy will automatically create tables and primary keys in the target instance if the target does not already contain tables with the same names. Basic Schema Copy is great for doing a test migration, or when you are migrating databases heterogeneously e.g. Oracle to MySQL or SQL Server to Oracle. Basic Schema Copy will not migrate secondary indexes, foreign keys or stored procedures. When you need to use a more customizable schema migration process (e.g. when you are migrating your production database and need to move your stored procedures and secondary database objects), you must use the AWS Schema Conversion Tool.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/dms/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/dms/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/dms/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/dms/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/dms/schema-conversion-tool/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/dms/schema-conversion-tool/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-28":{"id":"p:1-28","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 28","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eBuild the orchestration logic inside the AWS Glue job, invoke the three Lambda functions from the job script after the transformations finish, and inspect the pipeline flow by using Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow environment, build a DAG that starts the AWS Glue job and invokes the three Lambda functions after the job succeeds, configure CloudWatch Logs for task history, and use Airflow plus CloudWatch dashboards to reconstruct the full pipeline execution path\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Step Functions state machine that starts the AWS Glue job, invoke the three Lambda functions from the AWS Glue job code before the job exits, and view the pipeline flow in the AWS Glue console\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Step Functions workflow that starts the AWS Glue job and invokes the three Lambda functions after the job succeeds, and view the workflow graph and execution path in Step Functions Workflow Studio\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Gọi Lambda từ mã Glue làm logic điều phối gắn chặt vào ETL. CloudWatch Logs Insights không cung cấp đồ thị toàn bộ luồng thực thi như Step Functions.","Airflow có thể điều phối nhưng thêm môi trường và cách giám sát phức tạp cho chuỗi Glue–Lambda đơn giản. Step Functions ít công vận hành hơn trong tình huống này.","Cho các lời gọi Lambda nằm bên trong Glue khiến chúng không thành các bước độc lập trên sơ đồ Step Functions. Glue console không hiển thị đầy đủ chuỗi cần theo dõi.","Step Functions chạy Glue, chờ thành công rồi gọi ba Lambda ở các trạng thái tiếp theo. Workflow Studio thể hiện đồ thị và đường thực thi trong cùng giao diện."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA digital advertising company is building a serverless data pipeline in AWS. The pipeline must start an AWS Glue ETL job to prepare campaign performance data. After the AWS Glue job completes successfully, the pipeline must invoke three AWS Lambda functions to validate metrics, update reporting metadata, and notify downstream systems. A data engineer needs to view the full workflow sequence and execution path in one interface.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:53":{"id":"e:4:53","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 53","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a scheduled AWS Glue trigger that starts the workflow every 10 minutes, and configure the first job to stop when the daily file is missing\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure S3 Event Notifications to invoke a Lambda function for each upload, and have the function start only the first AWS Glue job in the workflow\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an on-demand AWS Glue trigger, run a Lambda function every 10 minutes to check the S3 bucket, and start the workflow when the file exists\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an event-based AWS Glue trigger, log S3 PutObject events to CloudTrail, and use EventBridge to forward matching events to the AWS Glue workflow\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team runs an AWS Glue workflow that processes a large supplier inventory file and loads the transformed records into Amazon Redshift. The file arrives in an Amazon S3 bucket once each day, but the upload time changes because the supplier generates the file after its own reconciliation process finishes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe AWS Glue workflow uses many workers because the company wants the file processed as soon as possible after arrival. The team needs to avoid delays from periodic polling or scheduled checks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an event-based AWS Glue trigger, log S3 PutObject events to CloudTrail, and use EventBridge to forward matching events to the AWS Glue workflow\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAn event-based AWS Glue trigger is the best fit when an AWS Glue workflow must start as soon as a file arrives at an unpredictable time. AWS Glue workflows can consume EventBridge events and use those events as start triggers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor Amazon S3 object arrival events, the S3 PutObject data event must be available through CloudTrail and EventBridge. The EventBridge rule can match PutObject events for the source bucket and forward the matching event to the AWS Glue workflow.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis avoids periodic polling and scheduled job starts. The workflow begins based on the object arrival event, which helps the company process the large daily file as quickly as possible after upload while keeping the orchestration serverless and managed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6ff22bdc66e7c0453ba737e830ba916015deaac93e3f8323e4e8e4d1e70e4ee0.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6ff22bdc66e7c0453ba737e830ba916015deaac93e3f8323e4e8e4d1e70e4ee0.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e65ce0b70cdeba3f2bd8deafa3428dac8b9d7764a1e011662a8b2f1d6c4af580.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e65ce0b70cdeba3f2bd8deafa3428dac8b9d7764a1e011662a8b2f1d6c4af580.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an on-demand AWS Glue trigger, run a Lambda function every 10 minutes to check the S3 bucket, and start the workflow when the file exists\u003c/strong\u003e - A Lambda function can check for objects in an S3 bucket and start an AWS Glue workflow by using AWS APIs. This approach can work when a team needs custom precheck logic before starting a workflow.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that polling every 10 minutes can delay processing after the file arrives. The requirement is to process the file as quickly as possible, so an event-based Glue trigger with EventBridge is a better fit.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a scheduled AWS Glue trigger that starts the workflow every 10 minutes, and configure the first job to stop when the daily file is missing\u003c/strong\u003e - A scheduled AWS Glue trigger can start a workflow or job at fixed intervals. This can be useful when input data arrives on a predictable schedule.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because the file arrives at unpredictable times. Running the workflow every 10 minutes creates unnecessary starts and still introduces delay, while an event-based trigger reacts to the actual S3 PutObject event.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure S3 Event Notifications to invoke a Lambda function for each upload, and have the function start only the first AWS Glue job in the workflow\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Event Notifications can invoke Lambda functions when objects are created in a bucket. Lambda can then call AWS APIs to start downstream processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis design adds custom code and starts only the first job instead of the workflow. The company already has an AWS Glue workflow, so an EventBridge-based Glue workflow trigger is more direct and preserves workflow-level orchestration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/starting-workflow-eventbridge.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/starting-workflow-eventbridge.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/about-triggers.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/about-triggers.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-serverless-event-driven-workflow-with-aws-glue-and-amazon-eventbridge/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-serverless-event-driven-workflow-with-aws-glue-and-amazon-eventbridge/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-63":{"id":"p:2-63","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 63","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects from S3 Standard to S3 Standard-IA after 45 days, transition them to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 150 days, and expire them after 400 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the Archive Access tier enabled and configure an S3 bucket policy to delete objects that are older than 400 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Inventory and run a daily Lambda function to transition objects between storage classes and delete objects after 400 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects from S3 Standard to S3 One Zone-IA after 45 days, transition them to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 150 days, and expire them after 400 days\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Lifecycle bám ba giai đoạn truy cập rồi xóa sau 400 ngày. Muốn khôi phục trong một giờ từ Glacier Flexible Retrieval cần chọn kiểu retrieval phù hợp; đối tượng rất nhỏ cũng cần xem điều kiện kích thước và chi phí transition.","Bucket policy không tự xóa object theo tuổi. Việc hết hạn phải dùng Lifecycle; các tầng archive cũng cần đối chiếu thời gian khôi phục.","Inventory và Lambda tự điều phối chuyển lớp làm tăng mã, chi phí và vận hành cho hàng triệu object mỗi ngày. Lifecycle thực hiện mục tiêu này trực tiếp.","Deep Archive không đáp ứng khôi phục trong một giờ. One Zone-IA còn thay đổi mức dự phòng mà đề không yêu cầu đánh đổi."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media analytics company needs to reduce storage costs for an Amazon S3 bucket that receives 8 million event objects every day. The objects range from 4 KB to 6 MB. The objects must remain immediately accessible for the first 45 days. Users access the objects infrequently from day 46 through day 150. From day 151 through day 400, the objects must remain retrievable within an hour. The company can delete the objects after 400 days.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:27":{"id":"e:5:27","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 27","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eRefactor the solution to send the cryptocurrency log files to Apache Hive on an Amazon EMR cluster. Launch a table from the log files by using a custom regular expression (regex). Set up an external table on Amazon S3 in Hive with the file format set to Apache Parquet and schedule a \u003ccode\u003eHiveQL UNLOAD\u003c/code\u003e query to persist the log files to the external Amazon S3 table.\u003c/span\u003e","Modify the solution to forward the cryptocurrency log files to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and install the Kinesis Client Library on an Auto Scaling group of Amazon EC2 instances. Configure the EC2 instances to fetch the stream records and automatically convert the log files to Apache Parquet. Store the processed log files in Amazon S3.","Use the Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) to forward the log files to an Amazon S3 bucket which will automatically transform the logs into Apache Parquet format.","Set up the solution to send the cryptocurrency log files to Amazon Data Firehose. Set up the Data Firehose to trigger a Lambda function that converts the log files to Apache Parquet format and delivers the files to the centralized S3 bucket."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial company has several AWS accounts that fetch market data from several cryptocurrency exchanges. The company is planning to launch a centralized logging ingestion system that automatically converts the incoming application log files to Apache Parquet format and stores the logs in an Amazon S3 bucket for easier processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineer has been tasked to ensure that the log files must be delivered in near real-time to provide accurate crypto market statistics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options can meet this requirement LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Data Firehose\u003c/strong\u003e can convert the format of your input data from JSON to\u0026nbsp;Apache Parquet\u0026nbsp;or\u0026nbsp;Apache ORC\u0026nbsp;before storing the data in Amazon S3. Parquet and ORC are columnar data formats that save space and enable faster queries compared to row-oriented formats like JSON. If you want to convert an input format other than JSON, such as comma-separated values (CSV) or structured text, you can use AWS Lambda to transform it to JSON first.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/60f431bced12b50a7a6a0dfefa75c73a8124122f2e1b652d2bcdc18614532928.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Data Firehose is a fully managed service for delivering real-time streaming data to destinations such as Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3), Amazon Redshift, Amazon OpenSearch Service, Amazon OpenSearch Serverless, Splunk, and any custom HTTP endpoint or HTTP endpoints owned by supported third-party service providers, including Datadog, Dynatrace, LogicMonitor, MongoDB, New Relic, Coralogix, and Elastic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eSet up the solution to send the cryptocurrency log files to Amazon Data Firehose. Set up the Data Firehose to trigger a Lambda function that converts the log files to Apache Parquet format and delivers the files to the centralized S3 bucket.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) to forward the log files to an Amazon S3 bucket which will automatically transform the logs into Apache Parquet format\u003c/strong\u003e \u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow is primarily used as a managed orchestration service for Apache Airflow and not for converting log files into Apache Parquet format.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eModify the solution to forward the cryptocurrency log files to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and install the Kinesis Client Library on an Auto Scaling group of Amazon EC2 instances. Configure the EC2 instances to fetch the stream records and automatically convert the log files to Apache Parquet. Store the processed log files in Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e \u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Take note that the scenario explicitly mentioned that the solution should have the least operational overhead in terms of managing the AWS resources. Having an Auto Scaling group of Amazon EC2 instances entails a lot of management and upkeep, which do not conform to the aforementioned requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRefactor the solution to send the cryptocurrency log files to Apache Hive on an Amazon EMR cluster. Launch a table from the log files by using a custom regular expression (regex). Set up an external table on Amazon S3 in Hive with the file format set to Apache Parquet and schedule a \u003ccode\u003eHiveQL UNLOAD\u003c/code\u003e query to persist the log files to the external Amazon S3 table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Just like the previous option, using an EC2-based Amazon EMR cluster requires maintenance and certain operational tasks. Using Amazon EMR could be a possible solution if the option mentioned that it uses the Amazon EMR Serverless type.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/record-format-conversion.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/record-format-conversion.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/data-transformation.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/data-transformation.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/what-is-this-service.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/what-is-this-service.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Data Firehose Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/#kinesis-data-firehose\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/#kinesis-data-firehose\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:42":{"id":"e:1:42","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 42","correct":[0,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eIf your instance has a public IPv4 address, it retains the public IPv4 address after recovery\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDuring instance recovery, the instance is migrated during an instance reboot, and any data that is in memory is retained\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIf your instance has a public IPv4 address, it does not retain the public IPv4 address after recovery\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eA recovered instance is identical to the original instance, including the instance ID, private IP addresses, Elastic IP addresses, and all instance metadata\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eTerminated Amazon EC2 instances can be recovered if they are configured at the launch of instance\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team at a social media company wants to use Amazon CloudWatch alarms to automatically recover Amazon EC2 instances if they become impaired. The team has hired you to provide subject matter expertise.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following statements would you identify as CORRECT regarding this automatic recovery process? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eA recovered instance is identical to the original instance, including the instance ID, private IP addresses, Elastic IP addresses, and all instance metadata\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIf your instance has a public IPv4 address, it retains the public IPv4 address after recovery\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can create an Amazon CloudWatch alarm to automatically recover the Amazon EC2 instance if it becomes impaired due to an underlying hardware failure or a problem that requires AWS involvement to repair. Terminated instances cannot be recovered. A recovered instance is identical to the original instance, including the instance ID, private IP addresses, Elastic IP addresses, and all instance metadata. If the impaired instance is in a placement group, the recovered instance runs in the placement group. If your instance has a public IPv4 address, it retains the public IPv4 address after recovery. During instance recovery, the instance is migrated during an instance reboot, and any data that is in memory is lost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTerminated Amazon EC2 instances can be recovered if they are configured at the launch of instance\u003c/strong\u003e - This is incorrect as terminated instances cannot be recovered.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDuring instance recovery, the instance is migrated during an instance reboot, and any data that is in memory is retained\u003c/strong\u003e - As mentioned above, during instance recovery, the instance is migrated during an instance reboot, and any data that is in memory is lost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIf your instance has a public IPv4 address, it does not retain the public IPv4 address after recovery\u003c/strong\u003e - As mentioned above, if your instance has a public IPv4 address, it retains the public IPv4 address after recovery.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ec2-instance-recover.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ec2-instance-recover.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-2":{"id":"p:4-2","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 2","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAvro\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eORC\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eParquet\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eXML\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Avro lưu theo hàng, thuận tiện cho trao đổi dữ liệu và thay đổi schema. Đề yêu cầu định dạng cột để tối ưu truy vấn nên Avro không phải lựa chọn phù hợp.","Theo đáp án của bộ đề, ORC phù hợp vì lưu theo cột, nén hiệu quả và hỗ trợ kiểu dữ liệu phức tạp như mảng, ánh xạ và cấu trúc lồng nhau. Lưu ý: Parquet cũng hỗ trợ dữ liệu lồng nhau; chỉ những yêu cầu trong câu hỏi chưa đủ để khẳng định ORC luôn tốt hơn Parquet.","Parquet cũng là định dạng cột và có hỗ trợ kiểu dữ liệu phức tạp. Bộ đề ưu tiên ORC trong tình huống này, nhưng không nên ghi nhớ rằng Parquet không hỗ trợ dữ liệu lồng nhau; lựa chọn thực tế còn phụ thuộc công cụ truy vấn và dữ liệu.","XML là định dạng văn bản có cấu trúc, không phải định dạng lưu theo cột. Nó thường tốn dung lượng và công phân tích hơn ORC trong bài toán phân tích dữ liệu lớn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team at an e-commerce company is looking at setting up a data lake on Amazon S3 that uses a columnar storage format that is optimized for fast retrieval of data. The team wants to ensure that the underlying data storage format also supports complex data types.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following data file formats would you recommend for the given use case?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:37":{"id":"e:1:37","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 37","correct":[0,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering =\u0026gt; Amazon S3 Standard\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Standard-IA =\u0026gt; Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 One Zone-IA =\u0026gt; Amazon S3 Standard-IA\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Standard =\u0026gt; Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Standard-IA =\u0026gt; Amazon S3 One Zone-IA\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe IT department at a company is conducting a training workshop for new data engineers. As part of an evaluation exercise on Amazon S3, the new data engineers were asked to identify the invalid storage class lifecycle transitions for objects stored on Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCan you identify the INVALID lifecycle transitions from the options below? (Select two) ?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs the question wants to know about the INVALID lifecycle transitions, the following options are the correct answers -\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering =\u0026gt; Amazon S3 Standard\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3 One Zone-IA =\u0026gt; Amazon S3 Standard-IA\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFollowing are the unsupported life cycle transitions for S3 storage classes -\nAny storage class to the Amazon S3 Standard storage class.\nAny storage class to the Reduced Redundancy storage class.\nThe Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class to the Amazon S3 Standard-IA storage class.\nThe Amazon S3 One Zone-IA storage class to the Amazon S3 Standard-IA or Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage classes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3 Standard =\u0026gt; Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3 Standard-IA =\u0026gt; Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3 Standard-IA =\u0026gt; Amazon S3 One Zone-IA\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHere are the supported life cycle transitions for S3 storage classes -\nThe S3 Standard storage class to any other storage class.\nAny storage class to the S3 Glacier or S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage classes.\nThe S3 Standard-IA storage class to the S3 Intelligent-Tiering or S3 One Zone-IA storage classes.\nThe S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class to the S3 One Zone-IA storage class.\nThe S3 Glacier storage class to the S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 supports a waterfall model for transitioning between storage classes, as shown in the diagram below:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e7652e3afc14c21c6ee69edb8464b28f90d891e7d5d39ae32955fc6f1ea68e5e.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e7652e3afc14c21c6ee69edb8464b28f90d891e7d5d39ae32955fc6f1ea68e5e.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:10:15":{"id":"e:10:15","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 15","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue crawlers to infer the Teradata schema, create matching Amazon Redshift tables, and run Glue jobs for the migration\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS DMS Schema Conversion to convert the Teradata schema, and use AWS DMS to migrate the warehouse data to Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS SCT to convert the Teradata schema to Amazon Redshift, and use AWS DMS to migrate the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS DMS to migrate the Teradata data first, and rely on automatic schema conversion during the Amazon Redshift load process\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics company runs a large on-premises Teradata data warehouse that stores merchandising, loyalty, and store operations data. The company wants to migrate the warehouse to Amazon Redshift so analysts can use a managed cloud data warehouse for reporting and forecasting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer must convert the Teradata schema to an Amazon Redshift-compatible schema and then migrate the warehouse data. The company wants to complete the migration with the least operational effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS SCT to convert the Teradata schema to Amazon Redshift, and use AWS DMS to migrate the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Schema Conversion Tool is the correct schema conversion tool for this scenario because the migration is from Teradata to Amazon Redshift. AWS SCT supports converting data warehouse schemas from Teradata to Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS SCT can assess the source schema, convert compatible objects, identify items that need manual action, and help create an Amazon Redshift-compatible target schema. This reduces the manual effort required compared with rewriting the Teradata schema by hand.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAfter the target schema exists, AWS DMS can migrate data to Amazon Redshift for supported migration paths. This combination uses AWS-managed migration tooling for the schema and data movement work, which provides the least operational effort among the available choices.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ac5e1666bf1fcd13841f91abed0940651e7011d4315b5057ed46d8e77f879af6.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ac5e1666bf1fcd13841f91abed0940651e7011d4315b5057ed46d8e77f879af6.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/assess-and-convert-teradata-database-objects-to-amazon-redshift-using-the-aws-schema-conversion-tool-cli/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/assess-and-convert-teradata-database-objects-to-amazon-redshift-using-the-aws-schema-conversion-tool-cli/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b4a5f571ff879cda63fe625170cb90e22f788479dd125fbecfd408f1d4f06f97.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b4a5f571ff879cda63fe625170cb90e22f788479dd125fbecfd408f1d4f06f97.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/assess-and-convert-teradata-database-objects-to-amazon-redshift-using-the-aws-schema-conversion-tool-cli/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/assess-and-convert-teradata-database-objects-to-amazon-redshift-using-the-aws-schema-conversion-tool-cli/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS DMS Schema Conversion to convert the Teradata schema, and use AWS DMS to migrate the warehouse data to Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS DMS Schema Conversion is a managed schema conversion capability within AWS DMS. It can convert supported source schemas to target engines such as Amazon Redshift for the conversion paths that DMS Schema Conversion supports.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that the current DMS Schema Conversion supported conversion paths do not list Teradata to Amazon Redshift. For a Teradata data warehouse migration to Redshift, AWS SCT is the appropriate schema conversion tool.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS DMS to migrate the Teradata data first, and rely on automatic schema conversion during the Amazon Redshift load process\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS DMS can migrate data from supported sources to supported targets, and Amazon Redshift can act as a DMS target. This is useful after the target schema is available.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because AWS DMS data migration does not replace the need to convert the Teradata schema to an Amazon Redshift-compatible schema. The target schema should be converted and created before the data migration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue crawlers to infer the Teradata schema, create matching Amazon Redshift tables, and run Glue jobs for the migration\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue crawlers can infer schemas from supported data sources and update metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. AWS Glue jobs can also transform and load data into target systems.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not provide the correct data warehouse schema conversion capability. Crawlers do not convert Teradata database objects, SQL dialects, stored procedures, or warehouse schema definitions into Amazon Redshift-compatible objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/SchemaConversionTool/latest/userguide/CHAP_Welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/SchemaConversionTool/latest/userguide/CHAP_Welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/assess-and-convert-teradata-database-objects-to-amazon-redshift-using-the-aws-schema-conversion-tool-cli/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/assess-and-convert-teradata-database-objects-to-amazon-redshift-using-the-aws-schema-conversion-tool-cli/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_SchemaConversion.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_SchemaConversion.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Target.Redshift.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Target.Redshift.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/Welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/Welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-39":{"id":"p:1-39","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 39","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a Global Secondary Index (GSI)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate DynamoDB Streams\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Local Secondary Index (LSI)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMigrate away from DynamoDB\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["GSI có thể thêm sau khi bảng đã tồn tại. Dấu hiệu chỉ tạo lúc tạo bảng trong đề không mô tả GSI.","Streams ghi lại thay đổi item. Nó không tạo chỉ mục để truy vấn bằng khóa sắp xếp thay thế.","LSI dùng cùng partition key và sort key khác, phải được khai báo lúc tạo bảng. Đây là dấu hiệu quyết định của câu hỏi.","DynamoDB có LSI đáp ứng điều kiện. Không cần di chuyển sang hệ thống khác."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eYou are a data engineer at an IT company that recently moved its production application to AWS and migrated data from PostgreSQL to AWS DynamoDB. You are adding new tables to AWS DynamoDB and need to allow your application to query your data by the primary key and an alternate key. This option must be added at the outset when you are first creating tables, otherwise, changes cannot be done once the table is created.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following actions would you suggest?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:10:16":{"id":"e:10:16","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 16","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EFS file system, enable audit logging for the Redshift cluster, and configure Redshift to write audit logs to the file system\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EBS volume, enable audit logging for the Redshift cluster, and configure Redshift to store connection and user activity logs on the volume\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon S3 bucket, enable Redshift database audit logging and user activity logging, and configure the cluster to deliver logs to the bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Config rules to evaluate Redshift cluster configuration changes, and store compliance evaluation results as the complete database audit trail\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company runs an Amazon Redshift provisioned data warehouse for regulated reporting. Auditors require the company to retain records of database connection attempts, user changes, and SQL activity that users perform in the warehouse.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer must configure the Redshift environment so the audit logs are stored outside the data warehouse for compliance review and long-term retention.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon S3 bucket, enable Redshift database audit logging and user activity logging, and configure the cluster to deliver logs to the bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift database audit logging can record database-level activity for compliance and security monitoring. The audit logs include connection logs, user logs, and user activity logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster, the company can configure audit logging to deliver log files to an Amazon S3 bucket. Storing the logs in S3 keeps the audit records outside the data warehouse and supports long-term retention and independent review.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe user activity log requires the associated database parameter to be enabled. Therefore, enabling Redshift audit logging, enabling user activity logging, and choosing an S3 bucket as the destination meets the requirement to store user activity and connection activity logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon EFS file system, enable audit logging for the Redshift cluster, and configure Redshift to write audit logs to the file system\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EFS provides shared file storage that can be mounted by supported compute services. It is useful for workloads that need a managed network file system.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option does not meet the requirement because Amazon Redshift audit logging does not write audit logs to an EFS file system. Redshift supports audit log delivery to destinations such as Amazon S3 for provisioned clusters.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Config rules to evaluate Redshift cluster configuration changes, and store compliance evaluation results as the complete database audit trail\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Config can evaluate resource configuration against rules and record configuration changes. It is useful for checking whether Redshift clusters meet governance requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not provide a complete audit trail of database connections and user SQL activity. AWS Config evaluates configuration compliance, while Redshift database audit logging captures the required user and connection activity logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon EBS volume, enable audit logging for the Redshift cluster, and configure Redshift to store connection and user activity logs on the volume\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EBS provides block storage for EC2 instances. EBS volumes can support applications that run on EC2 and need persistent local block storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because Amazon Redshift is a managed data warehouse service. The data engineer does not attach an EBS volume to a Redshift cluster to store audit logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/redshift-audit-logging\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/redshift-audit-logging\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:46":{"id":"e:4:46","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 46","correct":[0,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Advisor to review workload-based table design recommendations and implement the relevant distribution and sort key changes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the EVEN distribution style for billing_s1 and the ALL distribution style for billing_s2 so every node stores a full copy of the smaller table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the KEY distribution style for both billing tables and choose a high-cardinality join column as the distribution key\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the KEY distribution style for both billing tables and choose a low-cardinality status column as the distribution key\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the concurrency scaling configuration so Amazon Redshift can add temporary query capacity for the slow revenue aggregation query\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA subscription software company runs a series of SQL transformation queries against an Amazon Redshift cluster. After acquiring another business unit, the company updates a revenue aggregation query to join subscription billing tables from both business units. The first table is named billing_s1 and contains 12 billion records. The second table is named billing_s2 and contains 850 million records. After the change, the query runs much slower than before. A data engineer must improve the join and aggregation performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solutions will meet these requirements? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the KEY distribution style for both billing tables and choose a high-cardinality join column as the distribution key\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUsing the KEY distribution style for both large billing tables can improve join performance when the tables join on the distribution key. Amazon Redshift places rows with the same distribution key value on the same node, which can reduce or eliminate data redistribution during the join.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe distribution key should use the column that participates in the join and should have high cardinality or a uniform value distribution. A high-cardinality key reduces the chance that too many rows land on the same slices, which helps avoid data skew and uneven processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution directly addresses the performance problem introduced by joining two very large tables. By colocating related rows from billing_s1 and billing_s2, Amazon Redshift can process the join more efficiently before performing the revenue aggregation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDeciding Distribution Style:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b7975eb8291af7b781cd8aa89255bab1fd8f8eb4c5fa666e32cb5c936ce1468c.gif\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b7975eb8291af7b781cd8aa89255bab1fd8f8eb4c5fa666e32cb5c936ce1468c.gif\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-engineerings-advanced-table-design-playbook-distribution-styles-and-distribution-keys/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-engineerings-advanced-table-design-playbook-distribution-styles-and-distribution-keys/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Advisor to review workload-based table design recommendations and implement the relevant distribution and sort key changes\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift Advisor analyzes the workload and provides recommendations to improve performance and reduce operating costs. Advisor can recommend changes such as distribution keys, sort keys, compression encodings, and other table design improvements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis is useful because the workload changed after the company added a join between two large sales tables. Advisor can review the actual query patterns and table characteristics instead of relying only on manual assumptions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution complements table redesign because it helps the data engineer identify practical optimizations that match the cluster workload. Implementing relevant Advisor recommendations can improve query performance with less guesswork.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the KEY distribution style for both billing tables and choose a low-cardinality status column as the distribution key\u003c/strong\u003e - A KEY distribution style can improve join performance when the selected key supports join collocation. However, the selected distribution key must distribute rows evenly across compute slices.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA low-cardinality column, such as a status or region column with only a few values, can create data skew. Some nodes would process far more rows than others, which can make the join slower instead of faster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the EVEN distribution style for billing_s1 and the ALL distribution style for billing_s2 so every node stores a full copy of the smaller table\u003c/strong\u003e - The ALL distribution style copies a full table to every node. This can improve joins when the copied table is small enough to replicate efficiently across the cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, billing_s2 contains hundreds of millions of records. Replicating such a large table to every node can increase storage, load time, maintenance cost, and query overhead. ALL distribution is not appropriate for a large fact table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the concurrency scaling configuration so Amazon Redshift can add temporary query capacity for the slow revenue aggregation query\u003c/strong\u003e - Concurrency scaling can help Amazon Redshift handle more simultaneous queries by adding temporary capacity when queues build up. It is useful when many users or jobs compete for cluster resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the issue is that one modified join query is slow. Concurrency scaling does not fix data redistribution, skew, or poor table design for the join between two large tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-best-dist-key.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-best-dist-key.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_choosing_dist_sort.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_choosing_dist_sort.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/advisor-recommendations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/advisor-recommendations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-engineerings-advanced-table-design-playbook-distribution-styles-and-distribution-keys/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-engineerings-advanced-table-design-playbook-distribution-styles-and-distribution-keys/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-29":{"id":"p:4-29","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 29","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to create a DAG that runs every hour, checks each data store for new records, processes the records, and handles errors in Python operators\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun an Apache Spark application on Amazon EMR every hour to check Amazon RDS, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon OpenSearch Service for new records, process the results, and handle errors in the Spark code\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue workflows to run a crawler every hour against Amazon RDS, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon OpenSearch Service, then start AWS Glue ETL jobs to process all records that the crawlers discover\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge Scheduler to invoke a Lambda function every hour to check each data store, send processing messages to Amazon SQS when the function finds new data, and use a second Lambda function to process the messages with retries and a dead-letter queue\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["MWAA hỗ trợ lịch chạy và xử lý lỗi nhưng cần môi trường Airflow và quản lý DAG. Khối lượng nhỏ, chạy mỗi giờ không cần mức phức tạp này.","Khởi chạy Spark trên EMR để xử lý tối đa 8 MB mỗi đợt gây dư thừa tài nguyên và công vận hành. Lambda phù hợp hơn cho tác vụ nhỏ theo sự kiện.","Crawler khám phá cấu trúc dữ liệu chứ không phải công cụ phát hiện bản ghi mới trên mọi hệ thống này. Chuỗi crawler và Glue ETL cũng nặng hơn nhu cầu xử lý nhỏ theo giờ.","EventBridge Scheduler gọi Lambda kiểm tra nguồn; SQS tách khâu phát hiện và xử lý, đồng thời hỗ trợ retry và dead-letter queue. Thông điệp nên chứa khóa hoặc vị trí dữ liệu, không nhét toàn bộ 8 MB vào một message; Lambda sau đọc dữ liệu cần xử lý."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company needs a fully automated solution to check for new records in several operational data stores and process any records that the solution finds. The solution must run every hour and must work with Amazon RDS, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon OpenSearch Service. Each processing run will handle up to 8 MB of data at a time. The company wants to minimize cost and operational overhead while using reliable error handling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:33":{"id":"e:3:33","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 33","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate one AWS DMS task to send all Oracle table data to Amazon S3, create another AWS DMS task to stream refund_requests changes to Kinesis Data Streams, and use Lambda to call the REST API\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Oracle export utilities on a scheduled EC2 instance to copy all tables to Amazon S3, then run a containerized service on Amazon ECS to poll refund_requests and call the REST API\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS DMS task to replicate the Oracle database to Amazon Redshift, use the Redshift Data API to query refund_requests changes, and unload all warehouse data to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS DMS to replicate the Oracle database to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL instance, then run Lambda queries against the refund_requests table and call the REST API\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA travel booking company uses an Amazon RDS for Oracle database to store reservation, payment, customer, and support case data for an ecommerce platform. The company needs to make data from all database tables available for future analytics in a cost-effective storage layer. The company also needs to process changes from a table named refund_requests in near real time and send each relevant change to an external fraud review service through a REST API.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate one AWS DMS task to send all Oracle table data to Amazon S3, create another AWS DMS task to stream refund_requests changes to Kinesis Data Streams, and use Lambda to call the REST API\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Database Migration Service can replicate data from an Oracle source to Amazon S3. Amazon S3 is a cost-effective storage layer for future analytics because the company can store all database table data in a data lake and later query or process the data with analytics services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS DMS can also stream source database changes to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. The data engineer can configure table mappings so a separate DMS task captures changes from only the refund_requests table and sends those changes to a Kinesis data stream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lambda can consume records from Kinesis Data Streams and call the external REST API for near real-time processing. This solution is cost-effective because it uses Amazon S3 for broad analytical storage and a streaming path only for the table that needs immediate downstream processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8de2dc02ddaa92fccca14eaf3b43db59eb1a65bf039296d4f2c2a7d92ba511d9.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8de2dc02ddaa92fccca14eaf3b43db59eb1a65bf039296d4f2c2a7d92ba511d9.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/stream-change-data-to-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-with-aws-dms/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/stream-change-data-to-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-with-aws-dms/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ae4bb4d64070c6c812a70a672ca48aa2f564726f5cd63fa6175413efb32464b5.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ae4bb4d64070c6c812a70a672ca48aa2f564726f5cd63fa6175413efb32464b5.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/stream-change-data-to-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-with-aws-dms/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/stream-change-data-to-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-with-aws-dms/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS DMS to replicate the Oracle database to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL instance, then run Lambda queries against the refund_requests table and call the REST API\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS DMS can replicate data from Oracle to PostgreSQL, and Lambda can query a relational database if the function has the required network access and credentials. This could make the source data available in another database engine.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, creating another RDS database for all tables is less cost-effective than using Amazon S3 for future analytics storage. The Lambda query approach also requires polling or custom logic to identify new refund_requests changes near real time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS DMS task to replicate the Oracle database to Amazon Redshift, use the Redshift Data API to query refund_requests changes, and unload all warehouse data to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS DMS can load data into Amazon Redshift, and Redshift can support analytics over structured data. The Redshift Data API can run SQL statements without requiring a persistent database connection.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option adds an Amazon Redshift warehouse when the requirement only says future analytics storage for all tables and near real-time REST API processing for one table. Querying Redshift for refund_requests changes and unloading data back to S3 adds unnecessary cost and operational complexity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Oracle export utilities on a scheduled EC2 instance to copy all tables to Amazon S3, then run a containerized service on Amazon ECS to poll refund_requests and call the REST API\u003c/strong\u003e - Oracle export utilities and EC2-based automation could copy database contents to Amazon S3. A containerized service could also poll the source database or a derived store for refund_requests changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this approach requires the company to manage EC2 instances, export scripts, container services, polling state, retries, and failure handling. AWS DMS, Kinesis Data Streams, Lambda, and Amazon S3 provide a more managed and cost-effective architecture for the stated requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Target.S3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Target.S3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Target.Kinesis.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Target.Kinesis.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Source.Oracle.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Source.Oracle.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Task.CDC.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Task.CDC.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kinesis.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kinesis.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/stream-change-data-to-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-with-aws-dms/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/stream-change-data-to-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-with-aws-dms/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:6":{"id":"e:5:6","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 6","correct":[1],"answers":["Convert stored data in S3 to a more efficient format, such as ORC, to reduce query execution times.","Enable Amazon Athena\u0027s query result caching feature, setting the cache duration to 24 hours.","Activate Amazon QuickSight\u0027s in-memory calculation to minimize the number of Athena queries.","Implement a lifecycle policy in S3 to transition data to S3 Intelligent Tiering after 30 days."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAn e-commerce company stores customer profile data in Amazon S3. An AWS Glue crawler runs monthly using an on-demand trigger to update the metadata in the Glue Data Catalog.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eBusiness analysts use Amazon Athena to query customer data. The results of the query are visualized on Amazon QuickSight dashboards, which are refreshed daily.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eThe company wants to reduce the cost of running SQL queries in Athena.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich solution will fulfill these requirements with the LEAST amount of operational overhead?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e is a user-friendly query service that allows you to analyze data in Amazon S3 by using common SQL queries. Athena operates in a serverless environment, which means that you do not have to manage any infrastructure, and you only pay for the queries that you execute.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon QuickSight\u003c/strong\u003e is a fully managed, serverless BI service for visualizing data and getting business insights. Users can easily connect QuickSight to their data sources and build interactive dashboards and reports.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e is a fully managed ETL service that automatically crawls data, infers schemas, and stores metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Developers can write ETL scripts with Glue to prepare and move data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue crawlers\u003c/strong\u003e connect to data sources, extract schemas using classifiers, and populate the AWS Glue Data Catalog with metadata about tables, partitions, and file formats. Crawlers can run periodically to detect new or changed data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe \u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Data Catalog\u003c/strong\u003e is a centralized metadata repository that stores information about data assets across various data sources. It provides a unified interface to query schemas, formats, and source details.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/19a029e51d330abe1de2a37283031ae0ce94292850456e822765958e76f4c084.png\" alt=\"Amazon Athena\u0027s query result caching feature\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBy enabling reused query results in Athena with a maximum age of 1 day (24 hours), Athena can reuse prior query results from the same day rather than re-running queries each time the QuickSight dashboard refreshes. This avoids unnecessary data scans and reduces costs. The analysts still get updated reports based on the latest full dataset without provisioning any additional infrastructure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Enable Amazon Athena’s query result caching feature, setting the cache duration to 24 hours.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement a lifecycle policy in S3 to transition data to S3 Intelligent Tiering after 30 days\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because this action would not reduce the operational overhead associated with running queries in Athena or impact the cost of query executions directly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConvert stored data in S3 to a more efficient format, such as ORC, to reduce query execution times\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This approach is a valid way of reducing the amount of data scanned by Athena queries, thus reducing the cost of queries. However, an initial effort is typically required to convert the data format, and maintaining the process for new data may involve ongoing operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eActivate Amazon QuickSight’s in-memory calculation to minimize the number of Athena queries\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;While this option can primarily reduce the need to run new Athena queries for some types of data analysis, it primarily affects how QuickSight\u0026nbsp;processes the data after it has been retrieved. This option might lower the overall expenses of the solution, but it does not directly decrease the costs associated with Athena, which is the primary concern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/reusing-query-results.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/reusing-query-results.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/reduce-cost-and-improve-query-performance-with-amazon-athena-query-result-reuse/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/reduce-cost-and-improve-query-performance-with-amazon-athena-query-result-reuse/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these AWS Services Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-quicksight/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-quicksight/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:23":{"id":"e:6:23","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 23","correct":[0],"answers":["Create a state machine in AWS Step Functions to manage the execution sequence of the Glue job and Lambda functions.","Configure an Apache Airflow workflow on Amazon EC2. Design a directed acyclic graph (DAG) where the sequence begins with the invocation of the Lambda function and proceeds to the execution of the AWS Glue job.","Create an AWS Glue workflow that orchestrates the sequence of initiating the Glue job and triggering the Lambda functions.","Configure an Apache Airflow workflow on Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS). Design a directed acyclic graph (DAG) where the sequence begins with the invocation of the Glue job followed by the execution of the Lambda functions."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is building a pipeline in which an AWS Glue job first transforms data from a MySQL database and loads it into an S3 bucket. Following this, three Lambda functions process the data in parallel. The data engineer is looking for a way to orchestrate this workflow with the least amount of operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich would fulfill these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Step Functions allows the coordination of multiple AWS services into serverless workflows. By designing a state machine, AWS Step Functions can manage the execution order, dependency tracking, and error handling of the tasks, which in this scenario include an AWS Lambda function and an AWS Glue job. This service provides a visual interface to monitor the steps of the workflow, significantly reducing the management overhead compared to managing infrastructure or writing custom orchestration code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/cf9225b9bdb57314d0a5d9b030489e59b4ce29eb312dba0e80e326b805b97297.png\" alt=\"\"\u003eIn this scenario, the data engineer can create task states in Step Functions for the Glue job and Lambda functions. Step Functions has AWS SDK integration, allowing direct Glue API calls to run a Glue job for data transformation and to invoke multiple Lambda functions for data processing. You can configure a parallel state to execute the Lambda functions independently of each other concurrently. This approach significantly reduces operational complexity and accelerates the overall process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCreate a state machine in AWS Step Functions to manage the execution sequence of the Glue job and Lambda functions.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an Apache Airflow workflow on Amazon EC2. Design a directed acyclic graph (DAG) where the sequence begins with the invocation of the Lambda function and proceeds to the execution of the AWS Glue job \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. This approach introduces significant operational overhead by requiring the management of Apache Airflow on EC2 instances, which is not necessary when using AWS Step Functions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCreate an AWS Glue workflow that orchestrates the sequence of initiating the Glue job and triggering the Lambda functions \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because Glue workflow is primarily designed for orchestrating Glue jobs and crawler schedules rather than coordinating external services like Lambda functions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an Apache Airflow workflow on Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS). Design a directed acyclic graph (DAG) where the sequence begins with the invocation of the Glue job followed by the execution of the Lambda functions\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Deploying Apache Airflow on Amazon EKS introduces complexity primarily associated with Kubernetes management. Although Amazon EKS can simplify the management of containerized applications, using it just for workflow orchestration of external services is an overkill. Therefore, this option does not satisfy the requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-multiple-etl-jobs-using-aws-step-functions-and-aws-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-multiple-etl-jobs-using-aws-step-functions-and-aws-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Step Functions Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:30":{"id":"e:8:30","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 30","correct":[1],"answers":["Recreate the Redshift cluster and activate concurrency scaling in the settings during creation.","Activate concurrency scaling at the workload management (WLM) queue level in the Redshift cluster.","Optimize the distribution keys and sort keys to improve query performance.","Enable result caching in Redshift to reuse query results."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn organization has recently launched a new product line globally. This has led to a significant increase in data queries on their Amazon Redshift cluster. The company’s data engineering team must adjust its Redshift infrastructure to manage concurrent queries effectively.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich step would meet the requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eThe\u0026nbsp;concurrency scaling\u0026nbsp;feature in Amazon Redshift automatically adds and removes capacity by adding concurrency scaling to handle demands from thousands of concurrent users, thereby providing consistent SLAs for unpredictable and spiky workloads such as BI reports, dashboards, and other analytics workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/14afa3011ee91dd2ce1f9cb018646ce3e67aeac41d0356f49a7ee42b3cfee256.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift workload management (WLM) enables users to flexibly manage priorities within workloads so that short, fast-running queries won\u0027t get stuck in queues behind long-running queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift WLM creates query queues at runtime according to\u0026nbsp;\u003cem\u003eservice classes\u003c/em\u003e, which define the configuration parameters for various types of queues, including internal system queues and user-accessible queues. From a user perspective, a user-accessible service class and a queue are functionally equivalent. For consistency, this documentation uses the term\u0026nbsp;\u003cem\u003equeue\u003c/em\u003e\u0026nbsp;to mean a user-accessible service class as well as a runtime queue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you run a query, WLM assigns the query to a queue according to the user\u0027s user group or by matching a query group that is listed in the queue configuration with a query group label that the user sets at runtime.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWith manual WLM, Amazon Redshift configures one queue with a\u0026nbsp;\u003cem\u003econcurrency level\u003c/em\u003e\u0026nbsp;of five, which enables up to five queries to run concurrently, plus one predefined Superuser queue with a concurrency level of one. You can define up to eight queues. Each queue can be configured with a maximum concurrency level of 50. The maximum total concurrency level for all user-defined queues (not including the Superuser queue) is 50.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eActivate concurrency scaling at the workload management (WLM) queue level in the Redshift cluster.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRecreate the Redshift cluster and activate concurrency scaling in the settings during creation\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eis incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Recreating the Redshift cluster is a significantly disruptive process that involves downtime and potential data migration challenges. Concurrency scaling can be activated on an existing cluster without the need for recreation. \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eOptimize the distribution keys and sort keys to improve query performance\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. This option would\u0026nbsp;only help reduce the amount of data scanned and the overall execution time of an individual query. It won\u0027t affect the performance of running concurrent queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable result caching in Redshift to reuse query results\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Result caching primarily benefits scenarios where identical or very similar queries are frequently executed. It won\u0027t help with the issue of scaling to manage a large number of unique, concurrent queries, which is the challenge described in the scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_workload_mngmt_classification.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_workload_mngmt_classification.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/scale-read-and-write-workloads-with-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/scale-read-and-write-workloads-with-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:37":{"id":"e:3:37","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 37","correct":[2,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eTransform the data in each partition to Apache ORC format\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCompress the files in gzip format to improve query performance against the partitions\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Athena partition projection based on the S3 bucket prefix\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePerform bucketing on the data in each partition\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an AWS Glue partition index and leverage partition filtering via the GetPartitions call\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is encountering slow query performance while executing Amazon Athena queries on datasets stored in an Amazon S3 bucket, with AWS Glue Data Catalog serving as the metadata repository. The data engineer has identified the root cause of the sluggish performance as the excessive number of partitions in the S3 bucket, leading to increased Athena query planning times.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat are the two possible approaches to mitigate this issue and enhance query efficiency (Select two)?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an AWS Glue partition index and leverage partition filtering via the GetPartitions call\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you create a partition index, you specify a list of partition keys that already exist on a given table. The partition index is sub list of partition keys defined in the table. A partition index can be created on any permutation of partition keys defined in the table. For the above sales_data table, the possible indexes are (country, category, creationDate), (country, category, year), (country, category), (country), (category, country, year, month), and so on.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eLet\u0027s take a sales_data table as an example which is partitioned by the keys Country, Category, Year, Month, and creationDate. If you want to obtain sales data for all the items sold for the Books category in the year 2020 after 2020-08-15, you have to make a GetPartitions request with the expression \"Category = \u0027Books\u0027 and creationDate \u0026gt; \u00272020-08-15\u0027\" to the Data Catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIf no partition indexes are present on the table, AWS Glue loads all the partitions of the table and then filters the loaded partitions using the query expression provided by the user in the GetPartitions request. The query takes more time to run as the number of partitions increases on a table with no indexes. With an index, the GetPartitions query will try to fetch a subset of the partitions instead of loading all the partitions in the table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6abd850ed404dbc789c4078458678687ecf02c16653144e050f6d8144f3b7503.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6abd850ed404dbc789c4078458678687ecf02c16653144e050f6d8144f3b7503.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/partition-indexes.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/partition-indexes.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up Athena partition projection based on the S3 bucket prefix\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eProcessing partition information can be a bottleneck for Athena queries when you have a very large number of partitions and aren’t using AWS Glue partition indexing. You can use partition projection in Athena to speed up query processing of highly partitioned tables and automate partition management. Partition projection helps minimize this overhead by allowing you to query partitions by calculating partition information rather than retrieving it from a metastore. It eliminates the need to add partitions’ metadata to the AWS Glue table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn partition projection, partition values, and locations are calculated from configuration rather than read from a repository like the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Because in-memory operations are usually faster than remote operations, partition projection can reduce the runtime of queries against highly partitioned tables. Depending on the specific characteristics of the query and underlying data, partition projection can significantly reduce query runtime for queries that are constrained by partition metadata retrieval.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/a0257a3a7d6d24757f7f0b078bfc12e86c53a81de8dc04cc825e0893ef6f947a.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/a0257a3a7d6d24757f7f0b078bfc12e86c53a81de8dc04cc825e0893ef6f947a.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTransform the data in each partition to Apache ORC format\u003c/strong\u003e - Apache ORC is a popular file format for analytics workloads. It is a columnar file format because it stores data not by row, but by column. ORC format also allows query engines to reduce the amount of data that needs to be loaded in different ways. For example, by storing and compressing columns separately, you can achieve higher compression ratios and only the columns referenced in a query need to be read. However, the data is being transformed within the existing partitions, this option does not resolve the root cause of under-performance (that is, the excessive number of partitions in the S3 bucket).\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCompress the files in gzip format to improve query performance against the partitions\u003c/strong\u003e - Compressing your data can speed up your queries significantly. The smaller data sizes reduce the data scanned from Amazon S3, resulting in lower costs of running queries. It also reduces the network traffic from Amazon S3 to Athena. Athena supports a variety of compression formats, including common formats like gzip, Snappy, and zstd. However, the data is being compressed within the existing partitions, this option does not resolve the root cause of under-performance (that is, the excessive number of partitions in the S3 bucket).\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePerform bucketing on the data in each partition\u003c/strong\u003e - Bucketing is a way to organize the records of a dataset into categories called buckets. This meaning of bucket and bucketing is different from, and should not be confused with Amazon S3 buckets. In data bucketing, records that have the same value for a property go into the same bucket. Records are distributed as evenly as possible among buckets so that each bucket has roughly the same amount of data. In practice, the buckets are files, and a hash function determines the bucket that a record goes into. A bucketed dataset will have one or more files per bucket per partition. The bucket that a file belongs to is encoded in the file name. Bucketing is useful when a dataset is bucketed by a certain property and you want to retrieve records in which that property has a certain value. Because the data is bucketed, Athena can use the value to determine which files to look at. For example, suppose a dataset is bucketed by customer_id and you want to find all records for a specific customer. Athena determines the bucket that contains those records and only reads the files in that bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eGood candidates for bucketing occur when you have columns that have high cardinality (that is, have many distinct values), are uniformly distributed, and that you frequently query for specific values.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSince bucketing is being done within the existing partitions, this option does not resolve the root cause of under-performance (that is, the excessive number of partitions in the S3 bucket).\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/partition-indexes.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/partition-indexes.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/ctas-partitioning-and-bucketing.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/ctas-partitioning-and-bucketing.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-12":{"id":"p:4-12","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 12","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse the SELECT command in Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS) query in Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS) and INSERT INTO statements in Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the UNLOAD statement in Athena\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Kết quả SELECT thông thường của Athena được ghi dạng CSV. Chỉ chạy SELECT không cho đầu ra Parquet theo yêu cầu.","CTAS có thể ghi dữ liệu dạng Parquet, nhưng đồng thời tạo bảng mới trong Athena. Điều đó vi phạm điều kiện không tạo thêm bảng.","CTAS vẫn tạo bảng mới; thêm INSERT INTO không loại bỏ bước đó và còn tăng thao tác. Yêu cầu chỉ xuất kết quả truy vấn thì dùng UNLOAD đơn giản hơn.","UNLOAD xuất kết quả SELECT tới S3 và cho phép chọn định dạng như Parquet mà không tạo bảng mới. Đây là cách đáp ứng đồng thời cả hai điều kiện của đề."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn application needs the output of SELECT query from Amazon Athena to be stored in an Amazon S3 bucket in Apache Parquet format. The data engineer has been advised against creating any additional tables in Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow can this be achieved with the LEAST possible effort?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-59":{"id":"p:3-59","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 59","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSwap out Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon SQS Standard queues\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSwap out Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon SQS FIFO queues\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Enhanced Fanout feature of Amazon Kinesis Data Streams\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSwap out Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["SQS Standard chia công việc giữa consumer, không bảo toàn mô hình mỗi ứng dụng đọc cùng một stream độc lập.","SQS FIFO xử lý thứ tự hàng đợi nhưng không giải quyết chia sẻ băng thông đọc giữa các Kinesis consumer hiện có.","Enhanced fan-out tách thông lượng đọc cho từng consumer đã đăng ký theo shard. Giảm độ trễ do nhiều ứng dụng cùng tranh năng lực đọc chung.","Firehose là dịch vụ chuyển dữ liệu tới đích, không thay thế nguồn stream cho các ứng dụng consumer độc lập."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team is using Amazon Kinesis Data Streams (KDS) to process IoT data from the field devices of an agricultural sciences company. Multiple consumer applications are using the incoming data streams and the data engineers have noticed a performance lag for the data delivery speed between producers and consumers of the data streams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you recommend for improving the performance for the given use-case?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:10":{"id":"e:1:10","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 10","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable Short query acceleration (SQA) to concurrently run queries and thereby improve the concurrency scaling of the cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable workload manager (WLM) queue as a concurrency scaling queue. Set the \u003ccode\u003eConcurrency Scaling mode\u003c/code\u003e value to \u003ccode\u003eauto\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRe-configure the cluster to DC2 nodes and enable workload manager (WLM) queue as a concurrency scaling queue\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage custom parameter groups for the Amazon Redshift cluster to turn on the concurrency scaling of the cluster\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses Amazon Redshift as their data warehouse service in the cloud. The performance of the Redshift cluster needs improvement and the company decided to scale read and write capacity to meet the user demand. The cluster runs on RA3 nodes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs a data engineer, which solution will you use to turn on the concurrency scaling of the cluster?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable workload manager (WLM) queue as a concurrency scaling queue. Set the \u003ccode\u003eConcurrency Scaling mode\u003c/code\u003e value to \u003ccode\u003eauto\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWith the Concurrency Scaling feature, you can support thousands of concurrent users and concurrent queries, with consistently fast query performance. When you turn on concurrency scaling, Amazon Redshift automatically adds additional cluster capacity to process an increase in both read and write queries. Users see the most current data, whether the queries run on the main cluster or a concurrency-scaling cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou route queries to concurrency scaling clusters by enabling a workload manager (WLM) queue as a concurrency scaling queue. To turn on concurrency scaling for a queue, set the Concurrency Scaling mode value to auto.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen the number of queries routed to a concurrency scaling queue exceeds the queue\u0027s configured concurrency, eligible queries are sent to the concurrency scaling cluster. When slots become available, queries are run on the main cluster. The number of queues is limited only by the number of queues permitted per cluster. As with any WLM queue, you route queries to a concurrency scaling queue based on user groups or by labeling queries with query group labels. You can also route queries by defining WLM query monitoring rules. For example, you might route all queries that take longer than 5 seconds to a concurrency scaling queue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage custom parameter groups for the Amazon Redshift cluster to turn on the concurrency scaling of the cluster\u003c/strong\u003e - In Amazon Redshift, you associate a parameter group with each cluster that you create. A parameter group is a group of parameters that apply to all of the databases that you create in the cluster. These parameters configure database settings such as query timeout and date style. Custom parameter groups cannot be used to turn on the concurrency scaling of the cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Short query acceleration (SQA) to concurrently run queries and thereby improve the concurrency scaling of the cluster\u003c/strong\u003e - Short query acceleration (SQA) prioritizes selected short-running queries ahead of longer-running queries. SQA runs short-running queries in a dedicated space so that SQA queries aren\u0027t forced to wait in queues behind longer queries. If you enable SQA, you can reduce workload management (WLM) queues that are dedicated to running short queries. In addition, long-running queries don\u0027t need to contend with short queries for slots in a queue, so you can configure your WLM queues to use fewer query slots. SQA does not affect write operations, so this option is incorrect for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRe-configure the cluster to DC2 nodes and enable workload manager (WLM) queue as a concurrency scaling queue\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift supports concurrency scaling for write operations on only Amazon Redshift RA3 nodes. Concurrency scaling for write operations isn\u0027t supported on other node types.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/concurrency-scaling-queues.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/concurrency-scaling-queues.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/concurrency-scaling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/concurrency-scaling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:12":{"id":"e:1:12","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 12","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge rule that matches failed AWS Glue job state change events. Configure the rule to invoke an AWS Lambda function that parses the event and publishes a notification to an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon CloudWatch Logs for the AWS Glue jobs. Create an Amazon EventBridge rule that detects new log entries, invokes an AWS Lambda function to inspect the logs, and publishes a message to an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic when failure patterns appear\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge rule that matches failed AWS Glue job state change events. Use the EventBridge rule metric in Amazon CloudWatch to create an alarm, and configure the alarm to notify an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eModify every AWS Glue ETL script to publish a custom Amazon CloudWatch metric after each transformation stage, create a CloudWatch alarm for the custom metric, and configure the alarm to notify an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic when the metric value indicates a failed pipeline run\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics company runs eight AWS Glue ETL jobs in the eu-west-1 Region to transform point-of-sale data for executive dashboards. The dashboards are business critical, and the data engineering team must notify the data platform stakeholders whenever any of the AWS Glue pipeline jobs fails.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge rule that matches failed AWS Glue job state change events. Use the EventBridge rule metric in Amazon CloudWatch to create an alarm, and configure the alarm to notify an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue emits service events to Amazon EventBridge for job state changes. A rule can match AWS Glue job state change events and filter on failed states, which allows the monitoring mechanism to react to job failures without polling the AWS Glue API or continuously scanning logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon EventBridge publishes CloudWatch metrics for rules, including metrics that indicate when a rule has matched events. By creating a CloudWatch alarm on the relevant EventBridge rule metric, the team can trigger an alarm when the failure-matching rule fires. CloudWatch alarms can publish notifications to Amazon SNS topics, and SNS can fan out those notifications to email subscribers, incident management integrations, or other endpoints.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has low operational overhead because it uses managed event detection, managed metrics, managed alarm state evaluation, and managed notification delivery. The team does not need to write or maintain Lambda code, parse CloudWatch Logs, run scheduled checks, or add custom instrumentation to every AWS Glue job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ff87e0b1874aa57242a58fce656da32fe5e83fea0caa742e4834492b615de6c6.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ff87e0b1874aa57242a58fce656da32fe5e83fea0caa742e4834492b615de6c6.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/automating-awsglue-with-cloudwatch-events.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/automating-awsglue-with-cloudwatch-events.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge rule that matches failed AWS Glue job state change events. Configure the rule to invoke an AWS Lambda function that parses the event and publishes a notification to an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic\u003c/strong\u003e - This option correctly uses EventBridge to match AWS Glue job failure events, but it adds an AWS Lambda function between EventBridge and Amazon SNS. That function would require code, deployment, IAM permissions, error handling, retries, monitoring, and ongoing maintenance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBecause the requirement is to alert stakeholders when pipelines fail, the solution should use managed event and alarm capabilities wherever possible. Adding Lambda increases operational overhead and is not necessary for basic failure notification.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eModify every AWS Glue ETL script to publish a custom Amazon CloudWatch metric after each transformation stage, create a CloudWatch alarm for the custom metric, and configure the alarm to notify an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic when the metric value indicates a failed pipeline run\u003c/strong\u003e - Publishing custom CloudWatch metrics from every AWS Glue script could support customized monitoring, but it requires code changes across all ETL jobs. The data engineering team would also need to standardize metric names, handle failed script paths that might not publish metrics, and maintain this instrumentation over time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach has significantly more operational overhead than using the native AWS Glue and EventBridge integration. Native service events already provide the job failure signal that the team needs, so custom per-job metric publishing is unnecessary for this requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon CloudWatch Logs for the AWS Glue jobs. Create an Amazon EventBridge rule that detects new log entries, invokes an AWS Lambda function to inspect the logs, and publishes a message to an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic when failure patterns appear\u003c/strong\u003e - This option can detect failures if the Lambda function correctly parses AWS Glue logs, but it creates unnecessary operational complexity. The team must maintain parsing logic, handle different failure message formats, manage Lambda permissions, and monitor the notification code path.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue already emits job state change events to EventBridge, including failed job states. Using those state change events is more direct and requires less maintenance than analyzing logs after they are written.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/automating-awsglue-with-cloudwatch-events.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/automating-awsglue-with-cloudwatch-events.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/ref/events-ref-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/ref/events-ref-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-monitoring.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-monitoring.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/Notify_Users_Alarm_Changes.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/Notify_Users_Alarm_Changes.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automate-alerting-and-reporting-for-aws-glue-job-resource-usage/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automate-alerting-and-reporting-for-aws-glue-job-resource-usage/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:31":{"id":"e:7:31","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 31","correct":[1],"answers":["Use AWS Glue Schema Registry to strip any unused columns.","\u003cspan\u003eOrganize the S3 files into a \u003ccode\u003e/year/month/day\u003c/code\u003e structure. Then, create a partitioning schema via the \u003ccode\u003ePARTITIONED BY\u003c/code\u003e clause in the \u003ccode\u003eCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE\u003c/code\u003e command.\u003c/span\u003e","Divide the dataset into multiple compressed Gzip files, each approximately 1 GB in size, before storage.","Execute the query in an Athena workgroup and apply a specified per-query control limit."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail chain compiles daily transaction records from multiple store locations in a Comma-Separated Values (CSV) format and uploads them to Amazon S3. A data engineer uses Amazon Athena to generate an analysis of these records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOnly a subset of columns is typically queried, yet Athena scans the entire table each time. The data engineer must create a solution to reduce the volume of data involved in a query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich approach would meet the requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e is a user-friendly service that uses standard SQL queries to analyze data stored in Amazon S3. With Athena, you can analyze data in S3 without the need to load it into a separate database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePartitioning data in Amazon S3 and utilizing Amazon Athena for querying can significantly reduce the amount of data scanned, lowering costs. Partitioning enables Athena to scan only the relevant partitions of data for a query rather than the entire dataset. This method is highly recommended for large datasets stored in S3, where queries often target a subset of the data based on specific columns like date, region, or department.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/740b2cadf01db881a98b3e591ad21d58f371827eb13c971003de8eb42bb40428.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBy organizing the CSV files in an S3 bucket with a \u003ccode\u003e/year/month/day\u003c/code\u003e structure, you can create partitions based on these fields. When creating an external table in Athena, The \u003ccode\u003eCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE\u003c/code\u003e statement defines the table structure, columns, and data types. The \u003ccode\u003eEXTERNAL\u003c/code\u003e keyword specifies that the table is external to Athena and points to the data location in S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003ePARTITIONED BY\u003c/code\u003e clause can be used to define a partitioning schema. This divides the table horizontally based on partition columns like date, product, etc.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen queries use partition columns in the filter criteria, Athena can prune partitions to skip irrelevant data locations and reduce the amount of data scanned.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cstrong\u003e \u003cspan\u003eOrganize the S3 files into a \u003ccode\u003e/year/month/day\u003c/code\u003e structure. Then, create a partitioning schema via the \u003ccode\u003ePARTITIONED BY\u003c/code\u003e clause in the \u003ccode\u003eCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE\u003c/code\u003e command.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse AWS Glue Schema Registry to strip any unused columns\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. AWS Glue Schema Registry is primarily used for managing and controlling data schemas. It does not have the capability to remove or strip columns from the actual data stored in Amazon S3 or other data sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eExecute the query in an Athena workgroup and apply a specified per-query control limit\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. Per-query control limits just set a boundary on the total amount of data that can be scanned. It won\u0027t reduce the amount of data processed by Athena. If the limit is set too low, it may result in incomplete or incorrect query results, as the query may be terminated prematurely before scanning the necessary data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eDivide the dataset into multiple compressed Gzip files, each approximately 1 GB in size, before storage\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. While compression can reduce storage costs, Athena still needs to scan the entire compressed dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/ctas-partitioning-and-bucketing.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/ctas-partitioning-and-bucketing.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/create-table.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/create-table.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:43":{"id":"e:5:43","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 43","correct":[1],"answers":["Check the VPC flow logs and settings of the security group cluster to ensure no connectivity issues prevent the Amazon EMR cluster from connecting to any downstream or upstream services.","Check the IAM permissions associated with the state machine’s IAM role. Ensure it has the required permissions to run Amazon EMR jobs.","Check the EMR metrics published in Amazon CloudWatch to gain insights into the status of the EMR cluster.","Set up auto-scaling policies for the EMR cluster to adjust its size and compute capacity in response to the volume of data being processed."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team has a data pipeline that uses Amazon EMR for big data processing and stores intermediary results in Amazon S3. Part of the workflow involves transferring processed data to Amazon Redshift for analytics and business intelligence purposes. The team uses AWS Step Functions to orchestrate this process. However, when the Step Functions state machine is executed, the EMR jobs fail.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following should be taken to troubleshoot and resolve this issue?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAWS Step Functions need appropriate IAM permissions to interact with other AWS services, such as Amazon EMR. If the Step Functions state machine lacks the necessary permissions to initiate or manage EMR clusters, the jobs would fail to start or execute properly. Checking and updating IAM permissions and resources specific to the EMR clusters could resolve issues related to access and authorization.\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/12650467e2ceb00a167695aa6bf3f31bbd9c9c4d5400db32d1557f790075e5f8.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCheck the IAM permissions associated with the state machine’s IAM role. Ensure it has the required permissions to run Amazon EMR jobs.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cstrong\u003e \u003cspan\u003eCheck the EMR metrics published in Amazon CloudWatch to gain insights into the status of the EMR cluster \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect.\u0026nbsp;This option would only help in understanding the behavior of the cluster during its operation rather than solving an issue related to the triggering of jobs by Step Functions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCheck the VPC flow logs and settings of the security group cluster to ensure no connectivity issues prevent the Amazon EMR cluster from connecting to any downstream or upstream services\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. While connectivity issues can indeed cause failures, this option is less likely if the primary cause is the failure of EMR jobs when triggered by Step Functions. Connectivity problems would typically manifest as network-related errors or failures in interactions between services, not necessarily causing the EMR job itself not to run when initiated by Step Functions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up auto-scaling policies for the EMR cluster to adjust its size and compute capacity in response to the volume of data being processed\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because it addresses a concern (scaling of Amazon EMR clusters) that is unrelated to the primary issue described. The main problem is the failure of the AWS Step Functions state machine to initiate Amazon EMR jobs. This failure is an orchestration issue, not a scaling or resource capacity issue within the EMR clusters. Auto-scaling configurations would not rectify or address the inability of Step Functions to start EMR jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/procedure-create-iam-role.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/procedure-create-iam-role.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-security.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-security.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Step Functions Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:9":{"id":"e:7:9","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 9","correct":[1],"answers":["Load the data to an external table using Amazon Redshift Spectrum.","Create a temporary staging table to hold the data for transformation.","\u003cspan\u003eIngest data in bulk using the \u003ccode\u003eUNLOAD\u003c/code\u003e command.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eLoad data into the Amazon Redshift cluster with the \u003ccode\u003eS3DistCp\u003c/code\u003e command.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer has developed an Extract, Load, Transform (ETL) process that loads data from an S3 bucket into an Amazon Redshift cluster. The current process uses the \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e command to ingest data to the Redshift cluster. The company wants to improve the efficiency of its current process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich additional step should be implemented as part of the new ETL process?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eA \u003cstrong\u003eCOPY\u003c/strong\u003e command is the most efficient way to load a table. You can also add data to your tables using INSERT commands, though it is much less efficient than using COPY. The COPY command is able to read from multiple data files or multiple data streams simultaneously. Amazon Redshift allocates the workload to the cluster nodes and performs the load operations in parallel, including sorting the rows and distributing data across node slices.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use temporary staging tables to hold the data for transformation. These tables are automatically dropped after the ETL session is complete. Temporary tables can be created using the \u003ccode\u003eCREATE TEMPORARY TABLE\u003c/code\u003e syntax, or by issuing a \u003ccode\u003eSELECT … INTO #TEMP_TABLE\u003c/code\u003e query. Explicitly specifying the \u003ccode\u003eCREATE TEMPORARY TABLE\u003c/code\u003e statement allows you to control the DISTRIBUTION KEY, SORT KEY, and compression settings to further improve performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/43831a2b389e436182fbf98a90fdd4e37b30060bc95c3c9d65e43f4e281a8635.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA temporary table acts like a normal table but has a lifetime of a single SQL session. The proper use of temporary tables can significantly improve the performance of some ETL operations. Unlike regular permanent tables, data changes made to temporary tables don’t trigger automatic incremental backups to Amazon S3, and they don’t require synchronous block mirroring to store a redundant copy of data on a different compute node. Due to these reasons, data ingestion on temporary tables involves reduced overhead and performs much faster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a temporary staging table to hold the data for transformation. \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLoad the data to an external table using Amazon Redshift Spectrum\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Redshift Spectrum just offers a way for querying data directly on Amazon S3. It won\u0027t help improve the efficiency of loading data into Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIngest data in bulk using the \u003ccode\u003eUNLOAD\u003c/code\u003e command\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because the UNLOAD command literally \u0027unloads\u0027 or moves the result of your Redshift query to Amazon S3 and not the other way around.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLoad data into the Amazon Redshift cluster with the \u003ccode\u003eS3DistCp\u003c/code\u003e command\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because the S3DistCp tool is primarily used in Amazon EMR, not in Redshift, to load data from Amazon S3 to HDFS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Loading-data-from-S3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Loading-data-from-S3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-8-best-practices-for-high-performance-etl-processing-using-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-8-best-practices-for-high-performance-etl-processing-using-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_COPY.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_COPY.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-62":{"id":"p:3-62","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 62","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable Amazon Macie for the S3 bucket, configure a sensitive data discovery job, and use an AWS Glue job to load the discovered files into Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue job to load the S3 files into Amazon Redshift, identify sensitive columns, create Redshift masking policies, and apply dynamic data masking\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue job to read the S3 files, detect and permanently obfuscate sensitive values, and load only masked data into Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena views to hide sensitive columns in the S3 files, and configure Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the views instead of loading data into Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Macie phát hiện PII trên S3 nhưng không tự áp quyền che giá trị khi người dùng truy vấn bảng Redshift.","Glue nạp dữ liệu; Redshift dynamic data masking thay cách hiển thị cột nhạy cảm theo chính sách và quyền. Người được phép vẫn có thể xem dữ liệu gốc theo thiết kế.","Che vĩnh viễn trước khi nạp làm mất giá trị gốc trong Redshift đối với cả người được phép. Không linh hoạt như masking theo quyền khi truy vấn.","Athena view/Spectrum không thực hiện yêu cầu nạp dữ liệu vào bảng Redshift và áp chính sách che tại đó như đề."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company receives daily account activity files in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company must automatically load the files into an Amazon Redshift table every night. The files include sensitive fields such as tax identifiers, email addresses, and phone numbers. Users must be able to query the Redshift table, but unauthorized users must not be able to view the sensitive fields as plaintext.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-57":{"id":"p:4-57","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 57","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a local secondary index on CourierID so the courier dashboard can query completed deliveries by courier without scanning the base table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a global secondary index that uses CourierID as the partition key and DeliveryStatus as the sort key\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a global secondary index that uses CustomerID as the partition key and DeliveryStatus as the sort key\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a DynamoDB Accelerator cache for the table and configure the dashboard to retrieve completed courier deliveries from the cached base table queries\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["LSI giữ nguyên partition key DeliveryID của bảng gốc, nên không thể đổi sang CourierID. LSI cũng phải được tạo cùng bảng, không thêm tùy ý vào bảng hiện có.","GSI với CourierID làm partition key và DeliveryStatus làm sort key cho phép Query đơn của một tài xế với trạng thái đã hoàn tất. Không cần scan hàng tỷ item của bảng gốc.","Partition key CustomerID hỗ trợ tìm theo khách hàng, trong khi dashboard cần tìm theo tài xế. Index này không khớp mẫu truy cập cần tối ưu.","DAX tăng tốc các lần đọc có thể cache, nhưng không tạo một đường truy vấn mới theo CourierID. Trước hết cần index phù hợp để tránh scan."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA food delivery company stores records for all deliveries in an Amazon DynamoDB table. The table includes attributes such as DeliveryID, CustomerID, CourierID, DeliveryStatus, PickupTime, and DropoffTime.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe table currently contains several billion items. The table uses DeliveryID as the partition key and PickupTime as the sort key. The company wants to build a courier dashboard that shows each courier the deliveries that the courier has completed, based on DeliveryStatus. The solution must retrieve the required data without scanning the entire table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-49":{"id":"p:2-49","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 49","correct":[0,1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet iceberg as the value for the \u003ccode\u003e--datalake-formats\u003c/code\u003e AWS Glue job parameter\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet the \u003ccode\u003e--enable-auto-scaling\u003c/code\u003e AWS Glue job parameter to true\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an empty \u003ccode\u003e--conf\u003c/code\u003e key for the AWS Glue job and rely on AWS Glue to infer all Apache Iceberg Spark settings automatically\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a DynamoDB table for Iceberg metadata tracking and configure the AWS Glue job to use DynamoDB as the primary Iceberg catalog\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable the \u003ccode\u003e--job-bookmark-option\u003c/code\u003e parameter with job-bookmark-enable so AWS Glue tracks Iceberg table commits and enables Iceberg support\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["--datalake-formats iceberg bật thư viện/hỗ trợ framework Iceberg cho Glue job. Vẫn cấu hình catalog và quyền cần thiết cho workload cụ thể.","--enable-auto-scaling=true cho Glue tự điều chỉnh worker trong giới hạn cấu hình, giảm việc chỉnh năng lực thủ công cho dữ liệu lớn.","Một giá trị --conf rỗng không bật Iceberg hoặc tự khai báo mọi cấu hình cần thiết.","DynamoDB không tự trở thành primary Iceberg catalog theo cách mô tả. Đề sử dụng hỗ trợ Iceberg của Glue và cấu hình catalog phù hợp.","Job bookmarks theo dõi tiến trình xử lý nguồn được hỗ trợ. Nó không bật Iceberg hay thay thế cơ chế commit/snapshot của Iceberg."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is building a 140-TB transactional data lake in Amazon S3 by using Apache Iceberg tables. The data engineer wants to run AWS Glue Apache Spark jobs that use the Iceberg framework to process the data lake. The jobs must support large-scale processing while reducing the need to manually tune worker capacity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:7:26":{"id":"e:7:26","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 26","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eLoad the updated data into a temporary table using the \u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e command. Run an AWS Glue job that applies the Drop Duplicates transformation to remove any duplicate records in the temporary table.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e command to load the updated data into a temporary table. Then, run the \u003ccode\u003eUPSERT\u003c/code\u003e command to insert new records and update existing ones in the main table.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eCreate a temporary table for the updated data. Use the \u003ccode\u003eMERGE\u003c/code\u003e operation on the temporary table and the main data warehouse. When no match is detected, use the \u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e operation to insert values from the temporary table into the main data warehouse. When a match is detected, use the \u003ccode\u003eUPDATE\u003c/code\u003e operation to update the main data warehouse with values from the temporary table.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eLoad the updated data into a temporary table using the \u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e command. Then, use the \u003ccode\u003eDELETE\u003c/code\u003e command to remove any records in the main table that also exist in the temporary table. After that, use the \u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e command to insert new records from the temporary table into the main table.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is tasked with regularly updating their Amazon Redshift data warehouse. Every month, the data engineer receives a large batch of new data. This data often contains updates to existing records, and the goal is to ensure that the data in Redshift is always up-to-date while minimizing the impact on query performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich strategy should be chosen to ensure efficient data ingestion?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift is a cloud-based data warehouse that you can use to perform data analysis. One of the features of Amazon Redshift is the support for the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMERGE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;operation, which uses a temporary staging table. This operation is particularly useful when you have a large batch of updated data that often contains updates to existing records, and you want to ensure that the data in Redshift is always up-to-date while minimizing the impact on query performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1a40ec95d994eff98c367e27960b40ba475811b06bda457f6cfb73ecfc604f64.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMERGE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;operation works by comparing the records in two tables based on a specified match condition. When the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMERGE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;operation detects a match between the records in the two tables, it performs an\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eUPDATE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;operation. This means that the existing records in the main table are updated with the values from the matching records in the temporary table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOn the other hand, when the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMERGE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;operation does not detect a match, it performs an\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;operation. This means that the new records from the temporary table are inserted into the main table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis strategy of using the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMERGE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;operation ensures efficient data ingestion because it minimizes the number of separate operations that need to be performed. Instead of having to perform\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;and\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eUPDATE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;operations separately, you can perform them both at once using the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMERGE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;operation. This also minimizes the impact on query performance, as the database doesn’t have to process separate\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;and\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eUPDATE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;commands.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a temporary table for the updated data. Use the \u003ccode\u003eMERGE\u003c/code\u003e operation on the temporary table and the main data warehouse. When no match is detected, use the \u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e operation to insert values from the temporary table into the main data warehouse. When a match is detected, use the \u003ccode\u003eUPDATE\u003c/code\u003e operation to update the main data warehouse with values from the temporary table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is correct because\u0026nbsp;it involves creating a temporary table and using the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMERGE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;operation. When no match is detected, the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;operation is used to add new records from the temporary table to the main data warehouse. When a match is detected, the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eUPDATE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;operation is used to update the existing records in the main data warehouse with the new data. This ensures that the data in Redshift is always up-to-date while minimizing the impact on query performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLoad the updated data into a temporary table using the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;command. Run an AWS Glue job that applies the Drop Duplicates transformation to remove any duplicate records in the temporary table\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis\u0026nbsp;incorrect.\u0026nbsp;While loading the data into a temporary table and using an AWS Glue job to remove duplicates might ensure that the data in the temporary table is unique, this strategy does not account for handling updates to existing records in the main table. Additional steps would be required to compare and merge this data back into the main table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;command to load the updated data into a temporary table. Then, run the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eUPSERT\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;command to insert new records and update existing ones in the main table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect because\u0026nbsp;Amazon Redshift does not natively support the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eUPSERT\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;command. However, you could achieve similar functionality using a combination of Amazon services and big data frameworks. For instance, Amazon EMR, a managed service that runs big data frameworks including Hudi, supports record-level operations, including\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e,\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eUPDATE\u003c/code\u003e,\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eUPSERT\u003c/code\u003e, and\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eDELETE\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLoad the updated data into a temporary table using the \u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e command. Then, use the \u003ccode\u003eDELETE\u003c/code\u003e command to remove any records in the main table that also exist in the temporary table. After that, use the \u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e command to insert new records from the temporary table into the main table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;This approach involves deleting existing records that match those in the new batch before inserting the new data. This strategy can be very disruptive and might lead to data unavailability or integrity issues if not managed carefully.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/maximize-data-ingestion-and-reporting-performance-on-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/maximize-data-ingestion-and-reporting-performance-on-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_MERGE.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_MERGE.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-47":{"id":"p:2-47","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 47","correct":[0,1,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eThe security group of Amazon RDS should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon EC2 instances in the Auto Scaling group on port 5432\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe security group of the Amazon EC2 instances should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Application Load Balancer on port 80\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe security group of the Application Load Balancer should have an inbound rule from anywhere on port 443\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe security group of the Application Load Balancer should have an inbound rule from anywhere on port 80\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe security group of the Amazon EC2 instances should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon RDS database on port 5432\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe security group of Amazon RDS should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon EC2 instances in the Auto Scaling group on port 80\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["RDS PostgreSQL nhận kết nối từ ứng dụng EC2 trên cổng 5432. Giới hạn nguồn bằng security group của EC2, không mở rộng cho mọi địa chỉ.","ALB kết thúc HTTPS rồi gửi HTTP tới ứng dụng. EC2 cần cho phép inbound cổng 80 từ security group của ALB.","Người dùng truy cập HTTPS vào ALB trên cổng 443. Theo ứng dụng công khai của đề, ALB nhận lưu lượng này từ Internet.","Đề yêu cầu HTTPS vào ALB, nên cổng cần thiết là 443. Cổng 80 chỉ liên quan nếu bổ sung chuyển hướng HTTP, không phải đáp án yêu cầu.","Chiều kết nối bị đảo: EC2 khởi tạo kết nối tới RDS. Cần rule inbound ở RDS từ EC2, không phải inbound ở EC2 từ RDS trên 5432.","PostgreSQL lắng nghe cổng 5432 trong tình huống này, không phải cổng HTTP 80."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn HTTP application is deployed on an Auto Scaling Group, is accessible from an Application Load Balancer (ALB) that provides HTTPS termination and accesses a PostgreSQL database managed by Amazon RDS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow should you configure the security groups? (Select three)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:25":{"id":"e:1:25","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 25","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLoad data from Amazon S3 to Amazon Redshift using AWS Glue\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon S3 Event notifications to trigger an AWS Lambda function to copy the data from the S3 bucket to Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 Replication to copy the data from the S3 bucket to Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to copy data from S3 bucket to Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has integration with a third-party service to export its data to an Amazon S3 bucket. After each export, the incremental data from the S3 bucket needs to be transferred to Amazon Redshift and this data should be available to filter on specific keys.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich AWS service/solution is a good fit to address this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLoad data from Amazon S3 to Amazon Redshift using AWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWith AWS Glue, you can discover and connect to more than 70 diverse data sources and manage your data in a centralized data catalog. You can visually create, run, and monitor extract, transform, and load (ETL) pipelines to load data into your data lakes. Also, you can immediately search and query cataloged data using Amazon Athena, Amazon EMR, and Amazon Redshift Spectrum.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue provides serverless data integration service and is the best fit of the options given.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor further deep-dive on AWS Glue, Amazon S3, and Redshift, refer to the following example:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference Example:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e77953b542c68805a4b116eadf50316323c4300ffccb61c70597494ff377a8f8.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e77953b542c68805a4b116eadf50316323c4300ffccb61c70597494ff377a8f8.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/build-an-etl-service-pipeline-to-load-data-incrementally-from-amazon-s3-to-amazon-redshift-using-aws-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/build-an-etl-service-pipeline-to-load-data-incrementally-from-amazon-s3-to-amazon-redshift-using-aws-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to copy data from S3 bucket to Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - Using Amazon Redshift Spectrum, you can efficiently query and retrieve structured and semistructured data from files in Amazon S3 without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables. The given use case needs to move incremental data from S3 to Redshift, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon S3 Event notifications to trigger an AWS Lambda function to copy the data from the S3 bucket to Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - Using the AWS Lambda function to copy data from S3 to Redshift is not effective since Lambda functions have a maximum execution time of 15 minutes which does not suit incremental data transfers from Amazon S3 to Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon S3 Replication to copy the data from the S3 bucket to Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon S3 Replication is an elastic, fully managed, low-cost feature that replicates objects between S3 buckets. So, you cannot use it to transfer data into Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/build-an-etl-service-pipeline-to-load-data-incrementally-from-amazon-s3-to-amazon-redshift-using-aws-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/build-an-etl-service-pipeline-to-load-data-incrementally-from-amazon-s3-to-amazon-redshift-using-aws-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/questions/QUsYL4rbe8RAmdqWrE4WWabQ/transfer-from-s3-to-redshift-and-filter-specific-key\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/questions/QUsYL4rbe8RAmdqWrE4WWabQ/transfer-from-s3-to-redshift-and-filter-specific-key\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-46":{"id":"p:4-46","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 46","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) FIFO (First-In-First-Out) queue, and make sure the telemetry data is sent with a Group ID attribute representing the value of the Desktop ID\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) FIFO (First-In-First-Out) queue, and send the telemetry data as is\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) standard queue, and send the telemetry data as is\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, and send the telemetry data with a Partition ID that uses the value of the Desktop ID\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Dùng MessageGroupId bằng Desktop ID để giữ thứ tự các thông điệp của cùng máy. Các group khác nhau có thể được xử lý đồng thời, nên số consumer có thể mở rộng theo số máy có dữ liệu chờ.","FIFO cần MessageGroupId. Nếu không chia group theo máy, bạn không tạo được các luồng có thứ tự độc lập để xử lý song song theo yêu cầu.","SQS Standard chỉ cung cấp thứ tự theo nỗ lực tốt nhất, không bảo đảm thứ tự tuyệt đối trong từng máy. Yêu cầu này cần FIFO với group phù hợp.","Kinesis dùng partition key để ánh xạ dữ liệu vào shard, nhưng mức song song consumer chịu cấu trúc shard. SQS FIFO message group phù hợp trực tiếp hơn với việc xử lý độc lập theo từng desktop."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eYou are establishing a monitoring solution for desktop systems, that will be sending telemetry data into AWS every 1 minute. Data for each system must be processed in order, independently, and you would like to scale the number of consumers to be possibly equal to the number of desktop systems that are being monitored.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat do you recommend?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-46":{"id":"p:1-46","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 46","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eKeep the CSV logs in Amazon S3, compress each daily file with gzip, partition the files by transaction date, and query the compressed files with Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConvert the CSV logs to Avro files, partition the files by transaction date in Amazon S3, and use Athena partition projection for the daily partitions\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConvert the CSV logs to one Apache Parquet file per day, partition the data by transaction date in Amazon S3, and use predicate pushdown filters\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConvert the CSV logs into multiple ORC files, partition the data by transaction date in Amazon S3, and use columnar pushdown filters in Athena\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Gzip giảm dung lượng CSV nhưng dữ liệu vẫn lưu theo hàng. Truy vấn vài cột chưa tận dụng được lợi thế đọc theo cột.","Avro lưu theo hàng; partition projection chỉ giảm công quản lý partition. Không thay thế lợi ích lưu theo cột khi chỉ đọc một phần trường.","Parquet và partition theo ngày đúng hướng, nhưng gom cả ngày vào một tệp có thể hạn chế song song. Bộ đề chọn nhiều tệp ORC để phân phối công việc tốt hơn.","ORC lưu theo cột; nhiều tệp phù hợp giúp xử lý song song. Partition theo ngày và đẩy bộ lọc xuống bước đọc giảm dữ liệu cần quét."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn online grocery company stores daily checkout activity logs in CSV format in Amazon S3. Analysts use Amazon Athena to query only a small subset of columns from the logs on the same day that each log arrives. As order volume grows, Athena query runtimes are increasing, and the data engineering team needs to optimize the dataset for the shortest query times.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:39":{"id":"e:5:39","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 39","correct":[0],"answers":["Deploy on an EC2 instance that uses an instance store-backed Amazon Machine Image (AMI). Attach a secondary Amazon EBS volume for storing the temporary data. Leave the default settings upon creation.","Deploy on an EC2 instance with an Amazon Machine Image (AMI) that uses an EBS volume as the root. Leave the default settings upon creation.","Deploy on an EC2 instance with an EBS-backed Amazon Machine Image (AMI). Attach a secondary Amazon EFS volume for storing the temporary data. Leave the default settings upon creation.","Deploy on an EC2 instance that uses an instance store-backed Amazon Machine Image (AMI). Leave the default settings upon creation."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is deploying an application that requires low latency and high IOPS to AWS. The application writes temporary state data to disk during processing. The team must ensure that this data persists through instance terminations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow can the data engineer meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIn an EC2 instance that uses an instance store-backed Amazon Machine Image (AMI), the instance store serves as the root volume. EC2 Instance Store Volumes provide block-level storage directly attached to the host computer. This close integration with the host hardware enables the instance store to deliver exceptionally high IOPS, with certain instance types achieving millions of IOPS that exceed what EBS volumes can offer. This makes instance stores particularly suited for high-performance computing tasks, real-time processing of big data, and other I/O-intensive applications where speed is critical.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, one of the inherent limitations of instance store volumes is their ephemeral nature; the data stored on them is lost if the instance is stopped, terminated, or if the underlying physical machine fails. This characteristic makes instance store volumes unsuitable for storing data that needs to persist beyond the lifecycle of a single instance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6ad005940a6640eee61bdb16337195223c1a472e587aba76a9a538b7557a064a.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo overcome this limitation and ensure data persistence, you can attach an additional Amazon EBS volume to the EC2 instance. EBS provides persistent block storage that can be detached from one instance and attached to another, or retained independently of any instance lifecycle. By configuring the application to write data that needs to persist to the EBS volume, you can protect this information against instance termination or failure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy on an EC2 instance that uses an instance store-backed Amazon Machine Image (AMI). Attach a secondary Amazon EBS volume for storing the temporary data. Leave the default settings upon creation.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy on an EC2 instance that uses an instance store-backed Amazon Machine Image (AMI). Leave the default settings upon creation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Data on instance store volumes is ephemeral and will be lost if the instance is stopped, terminated, or fails. This does not meet the requirement for data persistence through instance terminations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eDeploy on an EC2 instance with an Amazon Machine Image (AMI) that uses an EBS volume as the root. Leave the default settings upon creation \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. Although EBS volumes are persistent and can survive instance terminations, by default, the root volume for an EBS-backed AMI is terminated as well when the instance terminates.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy on an EC2 instance with an EBS-backed Amazon Machine Image (AMI). Attach a secondary Amazon EFS volume for storing the temporary data. Leave the default settings upon creation \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. While EFS is a valid solution for a storage solution, it’s generally not optimized for scenarios requiring high IOPS and low latency compared to EBS volumes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/AmazonEBS.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/AmazonEBS.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/InstanceStorage.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/InstanceStorage.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon EBS Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-ebs/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-ebs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:9:4":{"id":"e:9:4","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 9 Â· CÃ¢u 4","correct":[0],"answers":["Use Amazon Athena Federated Query to perform SQL queries across the data stored in S3, Aurora MySQL, HBase on EMR, and DynamoDB without data movement.","Set up Amazon QuickSight to directly query and visualize the data from various sources.","Configure an Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the data from multiple sources and store the results in S3.","Employ AWS Glue to extract, transform, and load (ETL) data from all sources into Amazon S3, and then query using Amazon Athena."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company is using AWS for extensive data processing and analysis. The company has its primary datasets stored in an Amazon S3 data lake. However, the company also maintains several databases containing critical financial data in various formats and locations, including an Amazon Aurora MySQL database, an HBase on Amazon EMR, and a DynamoDB database. The company needs to create comprehensive financial reports by querying and combining data from these multiple sources without moving or duplicating the data into S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat AWS service can the data engineering team use to efficiently perform these queries across different data sources?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena Federated Query is a powerful feature that allows SQL queries to be executed across multiple data sources without the need for data movement. This service is particularly useful for scenarios where data resides in different storage systems and formats, as it enables querying data in place. With Athena Federated Query, you can extend the scope of your SQL queries to not just Amazon S3 but also to other data sources such as Amazon Aurora MySQL, HBase on Amazon EMR, and Amazon DynamoDB. This capability is crucial for creating comprehensive reports from various data sources without the overhead of extracting, transforming, and loading (ETL) the data into a single repository.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/06027e707ae43fa46c21e4b12dbafdf57222234cc6b2100a5583c683fa81231b.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena Federated Query to perform SQL queries across the data stored in S3, Aurora MySQL, HBase on EMR, and DynamoDB without data movement.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the data from multiple sources and store the results in S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Redshift Spectrum is used for querying data in S3 using Redshift, and it does not inherently provide the capability to query across various databases like Aurora MySQL or DynamoDB without moving the data to S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEmploy AWS Glue to extract, transform, and load (ETL) data from all sources into Amazon S3, and then query using Amazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This approach involves moving and duplicating data into S3, which contradicts the requirement to query data from its original locations without movement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up Amazon QuickSight to directly query and visualize the data from various sources\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While QuickSight can connect to different data sources for visualization, it does not have the native capability to perform complex SQL queries across multiple data sources like Athena Federated Query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eResources:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-a-data-source.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-a-data-source.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:31":{"id":"e:6:31","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 31","correct":[1],"answers":["Utilize Amazon Redshift to aggregate and analyze the data, ensuring quality and completeness before feeding it into the model.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker workflows, incorporating a SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking step for data preparation.","Run Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler to carry out exploratory data analysis (EDA) and stage the prepared data.","Implement Amazon SageMaker Processing to process the input data and output to Amazon S3."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer at a healthcare analytics firm is tasked with enhancing patient outcome predictions using a machine learning (ML) model. The model integrates diverse datasets, including patient records, treatment histories, and clinical trial data. As the model is set to be deployed in a production environment, the engineer must guarantee the integrity and reliability of the data for ML-driven predictions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAdditionally, the engineer needs to streamline the data preparation process for the model\u0027s deployment. Although the team currently uses AWS Glue DynamicFrame for data transformation, a more robust approach is required to track data lineage and ensure data integrity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will effectively meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking offers a way to create and store information about the various steps in an ML workflow. This feature is integral for establishing model governance and audit standards, which are essential for ensuring the accuracy, completeness, and trustworthiness of the data used in ML decisions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9429bef7a95175cfd8cc1a4b8c0fe3072a302384ddb3e256cb0aa59cd6e14281.jpg\" alt=\"SageMaker Pipelines execution graph for an Abalone machine learning workflow\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon SageMaker workflows, incorporating a SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking step for data preparation.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon Redshift to aggregate and analyze the data, ensuring quality and completeness before feeding it into the model\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While Amazon Redshift is simply a powerful data warehousing service that provides capabilities for data aggregation and analysis, it does not directly address the specific need to automate data preparation or ensure data lineage tracking for ML model deployments.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler to carry out exploratory data analysis (EDA) and stage the prepared data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. SageMaker Data Wrangler is primarily effective for conducting EDA and preparing data for ML models, but it does not inherently ensure the data\u0027s accuracy, completeness, and trustworthiness for ML decisions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement Amazon SageMaker Processing to process the input data and output to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Amazon SageMaker Processing is useful for various data operations, including processing and validation, but it does not inherently provide assurances about the data\u0027s accuracy, completeness, and trustworthiness for ML decisions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/lineage-tracking.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/lineage-tracking.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/pipelines-lineage-tracking.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/pipelines-lineage-tracking.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon SageMaker AI Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:64":{"id":"e:2:64","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 64","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a DynamoDB global secondary index on the sort key, scan the index from the AWS Glue job, and convert all matching index records into a DynamicFrame\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the AWS Glue DynamoDB ETL connector to read the table, configure a filter for the customer partition keys, and load the filtered output as a DynamicFrame\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun DynamoDB Query operations from the AWS Glue job by using the partition key values in the key condition expression, then create a DynamicFrame from the returned items\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun a DynamoDB query from the AWS Glue job by using only the sort key in the key condition expression, then convert the results to a DynamicFrame\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics company uses an AWS Glue PySpark job to enrich order data with selected customer profile records from an Amazon DynamoDB table. The job already knows the partition key values for the customer records that it needs to retrieve. The existing transformation logic requires the retrieved customer records to be available as an AWS Glue DynamicFrame. The company must ensure that the AWS Glue job reads only the required customer records from DynamoDB while consuming the fewest possible read capacity units.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun DynamoDB Query operations from the AWS Glue job by using the partition key values in the key condition expression, then create a DynamicFrame from the returned items\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA DynamoDB Query operation is the most efficient way to retrieve known items when the application already knows the partition key values. The AWS Glue PySpark job can call DynamoDB Query for the required partition key values and retrieve only the items that match the key condition expression.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach minimizes read capacity unit consumption because DynamoDB reads the targeted item collection for the specified partition key instead of reading the entire table. If the table also has a sort key, the job can optionally add a sort key condition to narrow the result set further.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAfter the job retrieves the required DynamoDB items, the job can convert the returned records into a Spark DataFrame or RDD and then create an AWS Glue DynamicFrame. This satisfies the existing processing requirement while avoiding a table scan or post-read filtering pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the AWS Glue DynamoDB ETL connector to read the table, configure a filter for the customer partition keys, and load the filtered output as a DynamicFrame\u003c/strong\u003e - The AWS Glue DynamoDB ETL connector can read data from a DynamoDB table into an AWS Glue DynamicFrame. This is useful when a job needs to ingest a full table or large portions of a table for ETL processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, using a filter after reading data does not provide the same RCU efficiency as a DynamoDB Query that uses the partition key in the key condition expression. To consume the minimum RCUs, the job must target the known partition key values during the DynamoDB read operation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun a DynamoDB query from the AWS Glue job by using only the sort key in the key condition expression, then convert the results to a DynamicFrame\u003c/strong\u003e - A sort key can help narrow query results when the query already identifies the partition key. This is useful for retrieving a subset of items within the same partition.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, DynamoDB Query requires a partition key equality condition. A query that uses only the sort key in the key condition expression is not valid for directly retrieving records from the base table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a DynamoDB global secondary index on the sort key, scan the index from the AWS Glue job, and convert all matching index records into a DynamicFrame\u003c/strong\u003e - A global secondary index can support alternate access patterns when the application needs to query by attributes other than the base table partition key. This can be useful when the required lookup pattern is not supported by the table key design.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the job already knows the base table partition key values. Scanning a secondary index by sort key is less efficient than querying the table directly by partition key, and it still risks reading many unnecessary index records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/APIReference/API_Query.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/APIReference/API_Query.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Query.KeyConditionExpressions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Query.KeyConditionExpressions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-api-crawler-pyspark-extensions-dynamic-frame.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-api-crawler-pyspark-extensions-dynamic-frame.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-dynamodb-home.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-dynamodb-home.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-36":{"id":"p:3-36","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 36","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Config managed rules to evaluate S3 bucket compliance, create compliance alarms, and notify the security team when the bucket becomes noncompliant\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable CloudTrail data events for the S3 bucket, send the events to CloudWatch Logs, and create CloudWatch alarms for policy-violation patterns\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse S3 server access logging, deliver logs to another S3 bucket, and manually query requester fields when the security team investigates violations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Macie to classify sensitive objects in the S3 bucket and send findings to the security team when new sensitive files appear\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Config kiểm tra cấu hình bucket, không ghi từng lần người dùng truy cập object rồi bị từ chối.","CloudTrail data events cung cấp người gọi, hành động và thông tin lỗi ở cấp object. CloudWatch Logs filter/alarm nhận diện mẫu vi phạm rồi gửi cảnh báo.","Server access log có thể hỗ trợ điều tra nhưng truy vấn thủ công không đáp ứng yêu cầu thông báo tự động khi vi phạm.","Macie phát hiện dữ liệu nhạy cảm trong object, không thay thế nhật ký hành động bị từ chối theo policy của người dùng."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA pharmaceutical research company stores processed clinical analytics data in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company uses IAM roles to give research, compliance, and analytics teams different levels of access to the bucket. The company has a strict data access policy that defines which users and roles can read, write, or delete objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe security team wants to receive a notification whenever a user violates the data access policy. Each notification must include the identity of the user who performed the unauthorized S3 object-level action.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:8:18":{"id":"e:8:18","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 18","correct":[0],"answers":["Launch an Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to analyze streaming data. Use a sliding window query for time-based aggregations to continuously aggregate data.","Configure an Apache Kafka with custom Java applications for real-time data processing.","Enable the Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink Studio for SQL-based real-time analytics. Aggregate data using a tumbling window query for time-based aggregations.","Utilize Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with AWS Lambda for real-time data processing over large, distributed data streams."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company wants to conduct real-time analysis of streaming data from various sources. The system must be fault-tolerant to ensure reliability and continuous operation. The company intends to continuously aggregate data from the most recent 5-minute period to gain actionable insights.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options would meet the requirements with the LEAST development effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eWith Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink, you can use Java, Scala, Python, or SQL to process and analyze streaming data. The service enables you to author and run code against streaming sources and static sources to perform time-series analytics and feed real-time dashboards and metrics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can build applications in Managed Service for Apache Flink with the language of your choice using open-source libraries based on\u0026nbsp;Apache Flink. Apache Flink is a popular framework and engine for processing data streams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6f0f7247f234bcff91d1525b597c5850cda33bdb16a22321859cda8708b88828.png\" alt=\"Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eManaged Service for Apache Flink provides the underlying infrastructure for your Apache Flink applications. It handles core capabilities like provisioning compute resources, AZ failover resilience, parallel computation, automatic scaling, and application backups (implemented as checkpoints and snapshots). You can use the high-level Flink programming features (such as operators, functions, sources, and sinks) in the same way that you use them when hosting the Flink infrastructure yourself.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor handling large volumes of streaming data with requirements for real-time analysis, fault tolerance, and the capability to perform diverse aggregation tasks over time windows, Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink is a robust solution. This service is specifically designed for processing and analyzing streams of data in real time, offering the scalability, flexibility, and fault tolerance needed for such tasks. Apache Flink\u0027s support for different types of windowing queries, including sliding windows, and tumbling windows. Sliding window aggregations allow for analyzing data across overlapping time windows, which is ideal for continuous monitoring and analysis no longer than one hour. This window query is suitable for continuous, real-time analysis over the most recent period. Moreover, the tumbling windows\u0026nbsp;are fixed-sized, non-overlapping intervals of time. Each event in a data stream belongs to exactly one window. This window is ideal for scenarios where you need to perform an operation over a chunk of data collected within a regular interval, and each interval is considered independent. For example, counting the number of events every minute.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch an Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to analyze streaming data. Use a sliding window query for time-based aggregations to continuously aggregate data.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an Apache Kafka with custom Java applications for real-time data processing\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because managing a Kafka cluster and developing custom applications to process the data is more complex and time-consuming than using a managed service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable the Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink Studio for SQL-based real-time analytics. Aggregate data using a tumbling window query for time-based aggregations\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because the tumbling window primarily aggregates data over non-overlapping, fixed-sized time intervals. This does not fulfill the requirement to aggregate over the most recent 5-minute periods continuously.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with AWS Lambda for real-time data processing over large, distributed data streams\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Although AWS Lambda can typically process data from Kinesis Data Streams, this setup is not as straightforward and complicated to implement compared to a dedicated streaming service like Flink. Moreover, Lambda functions have an execution duration limit per request that is not suitable for continuous long-term processing of streaming data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/managed-flink/latest/java/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/managed-flink/latest/java/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kinesisanalytics/latest/dev/sliding-window-concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/kinesisanalytics/latest/dev/sliding-window-concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/managed-flink/latest/java/how-fault.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/managed-flink/latest/java/how-fault.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these Comparison of AWS Services Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/comparison-of-aws-services/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/comparison-of-aws-services/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:1":{"id":"e:5:1","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 1","correct":[1],"answers":["Enable Athena Query Result Reuse to avoid scanning the underlying data again when a reusable result is available.","Enable Athena Partition Projection to determine the relevant partitions dynamically according to the query conditions.","Use Athena User-Defined Functions (UDFs) to apply customized processing logic within SQL statements.","Utilize an Athena Create Table As Select (CTAS) query to consolidate and restructure the data into a smaller number of larger partitions."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is using Amazon Athena to analyze three years’ worth of data totaling over 50 GB. The data is stored in Amazon S3 and is highly partitioned by date. However, as the size of data grows, the queries take longer to complete and often time out. The table is configured with an Athena data source that points to the S3 location containing the partitioned dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow can a Data Engineer resolve this issue with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data directly from Amazon S3 using Standard SQL. In partition projection, Athena calculates partition values and locations using the table properties that you configure directly on your table in AWS Glue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eCreating a complex structure can slow down query processing due to the overhead of loading and parsing partition metadata; as mentioned, the data is partitioned by date across three years. By enabling partition projection, Athena can bypass the traditional metadata loading process. Instead, it will directly calculate which partition to access based on the query criteria using rules and patterns specified in the table definition.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/51262ee8c3ad176dc7f0401ade30b1825fd0328e4c297fed89f605fc9a9a37b2.png\" alt=\"Users querying 50 GB of data in an Amazon S3 bucket via an Amazon Athena partitioned table\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis method will drastically reduce the initiation time of the query. It will narrow the data scanned to only relevant partitions, leading to better query performance and eliminating timeouts with minimal operational overhead. This method will also negate the need for manually updating or maintaining partition metadata, offering a streamlined approach to handling large-scale, partitioned datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Athena Partition Projection to determine the relevant partitions dynamically according to the query conditions.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Athena User-Defined Functions (UDFs) to apply customized processing logic within SQL statements \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because Athena User-Defined Functions (UDFs) offer the flexibility of custom query processing. However, they do not simply inherently improve query performance on large datasets, especially when dealing with partitioned data. Additionally, UDFs can only add more complexity and operational overhead, resulting in more performance issues.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize an Athena Create Table As Select (CTAS) query to consolidate and restructure the data into a smaller number of larger partitions\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because Athena’s CTAS feature allows for creating optimized tables based on query results. However, this solution needs additional steps to manage the generated tables that do not directly solve the issue of slow queries due to the need to scan highly partitioned data. This solution will not improve the query performance with the least operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Athena Query Result Reuse to avoid scanning the underlying data again when a reusable result is available \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because Query Result Reuse in Amazon Athena stores query outcomes, enabling faster retrieval for identical future queries by avoiding data re-scanning. This solution can speed up repeated queries on static data, offering cost and time savings. However, it doesn’t solve the initial slow query times caused by navigating a highly partitioned dataset. This solution will not improve the query performance with the least operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/athena-cloudtrail-data-timeout\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/athena-cloudtrail-data-timeout\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheatsheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:44":{"id":"e:6:44","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 44","correct":[0],"answers":["Configure the new data to be stored in the S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) storage class. Use Amazon Athena for ad-hoc querying. Implement lifecycle rules for data archival to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 3 years and deletion after 10 years.","Set the S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class for new data with a configuration towards Deep Archive Access for longer-term storage. Utilize Amazon Athena for ad-hoc querying. Configure S3 Intelligent Tiering to purge data after 10 years.","Use Amazon Redshift for data storage of the new data, then configure Redshift Spectrum to directly query data on S3. Implement lifecycle rules for data archival to the Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class. Set up another lifecycle rule to delete data longer than 10 years.","Configure Amazon RDS to store the newly added data. Create an Amazon Lambda function to periodically export the snapshots to the S3 Standard class. Set up lifecycle policies for transferring snapshots to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 3 years and deleting data after 10 years."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team manages 10 TB of data in an Amazon S3 bucket. The team must meet new data lifecycle and retention policies. Under the new guidelines, all newly stored data must be readily available for random, occasional SQL queries. Data older than 3 years requires secure storage but must be accessible within 8 hours for compliance audits. Additionally, data exceeding a 10-year retention period should be permanently deleted. The team initially explored using a tumbling window for real-time analytics but determined that occasional SQL queries made a simpler, cost-effective approach more appropriate.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will fulfill this requirement most cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e offers different types of storage classes that users can choose based on the specific needs of their workload. Depending on their workload\u0027s data access, resiliency, and performance requirements, these classes are tailored to provide the best storage options at the lowest possible costs. S3 storage classes are versatile and suitable for nearly any use case - including those with high-performance demands, data lakes, residency requirements, unknown or changing access patterns, or archival storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOne of these classes is the Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA). Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) storage class is designed for data that is accessed less frequently but, when needed, requires rapid access. S3 Standard-IA offers the high durability, throughput, and low latency of S3 Standard but with a lower storage cost and a slightly higher retrieval fee. This storage class makes it an ideal solution for long-term storage and backups and a data store for disaster recovery files. Amazon Athena allows users to analyze data stored in Amazon S3 with SQL queries. It\u0027s interactive and serverless, so there\u0027s no infrastructure to manage. Users are charged only for the queries they run, making it an incredibly cost-effective solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/cb434042f543ea5c8acbd790bd85ffead1c710a64cb5da3986acee1cc869ec41.png\" alt=\"S3 lifecycle rule deleting noncurrent objects\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBy implementing lifecycle rules in Amazon S3, you can easily manage your data throughout its lifecycle. S3 Lifecycle configuration rules allow you to transition objects to more cost-effective storage classes, such as S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval or S3 Glacier Deep Archive, for long-term storage and archiving purposes. Moreover, you have the option to set up rules that will automatically delete objects after a specific period, which helps you comply with data governance and regulatory requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, previously known as S3 Glacier, is a cost-effective storage solution for archiving data. You can configure retrieval times from minutes to hours, which is suitable for accessing data for compliance audits within a 10-hour window. On the other hand, the S3 Glacier Deep Archive is designed for data that is accessed less frequently but needs to be retained for decades as part of digital preservation or regulatory compliance. It is the most affordable long-term archiving storage option available on AWS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure the new data to be stored in the S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) storage class. Use Amazon Athena for ad-hoc querying. Implement lifecycle rules for data archival to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 3 years and deletion after 10 years.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet the S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class for new data with a configuration towards Deep Archive Access for longer-term storage. Utilize Amazon Athena for ad-hoc querying. Configure S3 Intelligent Tiering to purge data after 10 years\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Although using Amazon Athena to query data meets one of the requirements, transitioning to Deep Archive Access makes it impossible for data to be accessible within an 8-hour window. S3 Glacier Deep Archive is intended for long-term data storage that is accessed once or twice a year, with retrieval times typically ranging from 12 to 48 hours.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift for data storage of the new data, then configure Redshift Spectrum to directly query data on S3. Implement lifecycle rules for data archival to the Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class. Set up another lifecycle rule to delete data longer than 10 years\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Redshift is primarily a data warehousing solution for large-scale analytical workloads, not occasional SQL queries on recently ingested data. While Redshift Spectrum allows querying data in S3, maintaining a Redshift cluster incurs hourly charges, making it a less cost-effective solution. Moreover, although S3 Glacier Deep Archive is cheaper than Glacier Flexible Retrieval, it only allows retrieval times ranging from 12 to 48 hours.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon RDS to store the newly added data. Create an Amazon Lambda function to periodically export the snapshots to the S3 Standard class. Set up lifecycle policies for transferring snapshots to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 3 years and deleting data after 10 years\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Amazon RDS is primarily a database service for relational databases and is not optimized for cost-effective data storage and retrieval for compliance and lifecycle management in the way S3 and its associated services are. Moreover, managing snapshots and exports introduces operational overhead that does not directly contribute to the goals of easy SQL analysis and compliance with lifecycle policies.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/backup-recovery/amazon-s3-glacier.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/backup-recovery/amazon-s3-glacier.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these\u0026nbsp;Amazon S3 Glacier and Amazon Athena Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-glacier/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-glacier/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:25":{"id":"e:2:25","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 25","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an SNS FIFO topic, subscribe the team\u0027s email address to the topic, and create a Lambda function that publishes messages when AWS Glue job state changes to FAILED\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an SQS standard queue, subscribe the team\u0027s email address to the queue, and create an EventBridge rule that sends failed AWS Glue job events to the queue\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an SNS standard topic, subscribe the team\u0027s email address to the topic, and create an EventBridge rule that sends AWS Glue FAILED job state change events to the topic\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a CloudWatch alarm on AWS Glue job failure metrics, configure the alarm to write notifications to Amazon S3, and ask the business team to review the S3 objects\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is supporting an AWS Glue workflow that validates and transforms daily claims data. The workflow sometimes fails when the input files contain data quality issues. A business operations team needs to receive an email notification whenever an AWS Glue job in the workflow fails in the future.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an SNS standard topic, subscribe the team\u0027s email address to the topic, and create an EventBridge rule that sends AWS Glue FAILED job state change events to the topic\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon EventBridge can receive AWS Glue job state change events. The data engineer can create an EventBridge rule that matches events from AWS Glue where the job state is FAILED.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon SNS standard topics support email subscriptions. After the business operations team confirms the email subscription, SNS can deliver notifications when EventBridge sends matching failed job events to the topic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution meets the requirement because it uses managed event routing and managed email notification delivery. The company does not need to write custom polling code, manually inspect job logs, or build a separate workflow just to notify users when an AWS Glue job fails.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1990ad72c10ab161a5e89e4effd022b3fc3e002d04a920c276ad70b6bb20381a.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1990ad72c10ab161a5e89e4effd022b3fc3e002d04a920c276ad70b6bb20381a.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-email-notifications.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-email-notifications.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an SQS standard queue, subscribe the team\u0027s email address to the queue, and create an EventBridge rule that sends failed AWS Glue job events to the queue\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon SQS standard queues can decouple producers and consumers and can store messages for asynchronous processing. EventBridge can also send events to supported targets such as SQS queues.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because SQS does not directly subscribe email addresses and send email notifications. The company would need another consumer, such as Lambda, to read queue messages and send email, which adds unnecessary operational work.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an SNS FIFO topic, subscribe the team\u0027s email address to the topic, and create a Lambda function that publishes messages when AWS Glue job state changes to FAILED\u003c/strong\u003e - A Lambda function could inspect AWS Glue job state changes and publish a notification message. SNS FIFO topics are useful when strict message ordering and deduplication are required for application-to-application messaging.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe issue with this approach is that SNS FIFO topics do not support direct email delivery to customer-managed endpoints. A standard SNS topic is the appropriate topic type for email notifications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a CloudWatch alarm on AWS Glue job failure metrics, configure the alarm to write notifications to Amazon S3, and ask the business team to review the S3 objects\u003c/strong\u003e - CloudWatch alarms can notify teams when metrics cross configured thresholds. They are useful for operational monitoring and alerting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis design sends information to Amazon S3 instead of emailing the business team. It also requires users to manually review files, while the requirement asks for an email notification whenever the workflow fails.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/automating-awsglue-with-cloudwatch-events.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/automating-awsglue-with-cloudwatch-events.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/ref/events-ref-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/ref/events-ref-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-email-notifications.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-email-notifications.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/fifo-message-delivery.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/fifo-message-delivery.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-60":{"id":"p:2-60","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 60","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse the AWS Glue DynamoDB ETL connector to read the table, configure a filter for the customer partition keys, and load the filtered output as a DynamicFrame\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun a DynamoDB query from the AWS Glue job by using only the sort key in the key condition expression, then convert the results to a DynamicFrame\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun DynamoDB Query operations from the AWS Glue job by using the partition key values in the key condition expression, then create a DynamicFrame from the returned items\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a DynamoDB global secondary index on the sort key, scan the index from the AWS Glue job, and convert all matching index records into a DynamicFrame\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Đọc bảng qua ETL connector rồi lọc không giảm RCU tương đương Query theo khóa ngay tại nguồn. Đề yêu cầu chỉ đọc các khách hàng đã biết khóa.","DynamoDB Query yêu cầu partition key trong key condition. Chỉ có sort key không đủ để query bảng gốc.","Gọi Query với các partition key đã biết để đọc đúng nhóm item, rồi chuyển kết quả thành DynamicFrame. Tránh scan toàn bảng để tiết kiệm RCU.","Tạo GSI rồi scan vẫn đọc thừa dữ liệu và thêm chi phí chỉ mục. Không cần khi đã biết partition key của item cần lấy."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics company uses an AWS Glue PySpark job to enrich order data with selected customer profile records from an Amazon DynamoDB table. The job already knows the partition key values for the customer records that it needs to retrieve. The existing transformation logic requires the retrieved customer records to be available as an AWS Glue DynamicFrame. The company must ensure that the AWS Glue job reads only the required customer records from DynamoDB while consuming the fewest possible read capacity units.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:12":{"id":"e:5:12","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 12","correct":[1],"answers":["Use AWS Glue’s FindMatches ML Transform to detect sensitive data.","Use AWS Glue’s Sensitive Data Detection feature.","Use AWS Glue’s built-in classifiers for detecting sensitive data.","Use Amazon Macie to detect sensitive data."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA Data Engineer is tasked with integrating a large volume of customer data into an Amazon S3 data lake. The data is currently stored in an on-premises data center. In compliance with strict company policies, it’s mandatory to detect and redact any Personally Identifiable Information (PII) from these records before they are ingested into the data lake.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options best meets these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue’s Sensitive Data Detection feature is specifically designed to detect sensitive data such as PII. It uses pattern matching and machine learning to automatically recognize personally identifiable information (PII) and other sensitive data at the column or cell level.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/78d2615adff76e9e0e9fb8be72c61f6800afdf1420809124504fe19ac26e00b0.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can choose how you want the data to be scanned. AWS Glue provides two options for scanning data – detecting PII in each row or detecting the columns that contain PII data. When you choose to detect fields that contain PII, you can reduce costs and improve performance by sampling a portion of rows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can also choose the PII entities to detect. If you choose Detect PII in each cell, you can choose from one of three options: All available PII patterns, Select categories, or Custom.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you choose to detect fields that contain PII, you can specify the percentage of rows to sample and the percentage of rows that contain the PII entity in order for the entire column to be identified as having the PII entity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Detect PII transform provides the ability to detect, mask, or remove entities that you define or are pre-defined by AWS. This enables you to increase compliance and reduce liability.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Use AWS Glue’s Sensitive Data Detection feature.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue’s FindMatches ML Transform to detect sensitive data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because AWS Glue’s FindMatches ML Transform is primarily used for finding duplicate records, not for detecting sensitive data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue’s built-in classifiers for detecting sensitive data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because AWS Glue’s built-in classifiers are primarily used for inferring the schema of data, not for detecting sensitive data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Macie to detect sensitive data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because, while Amazon Macie is indeed a fully managed data security and data privacy service that uses machine learning and pattern matching to discover and protect sensitive data, it does not inherently have the capability to redact or remove the detected sensitive data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/detect-and-process-sensitive-data-using-aws-glue-studio/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/detect-and-process-sensitive-data-using-aws-glue-studio/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-26":{"id":"p:2-26","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 26","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eRun an AWS Batch array job that divides the CSV file into chunks, validates the chunks in parallel, and uses custom code to track failed records across all child jobs\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDeploy an Amazon EMR cluster with Apache Spark to process the CSV file in parallel and configure custom Spark logic to stop the job when failed validations exceed 12%\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Glue job that reads the CSV file from Amazon S3, repartitions the data in Spark, validates the records, and uses a custom accumulator to stop processing when the failed-record percentage exceeds 12%\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse an AWS Step Functions Distributed Map state to process the CSV records in parallel and set ToleratedFailurePercentage to 12\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Batch chạy song song được, nhưng cần tự chia dữ liệu, tổng hợp lỗi và quản lý ngưỡng toàn cục. Nhiều công hơn Distributed Map.","EMR cần quản lý cụm và tự viết logic tỷ lệ lỗi. Đề muốn cơ chế được quản lý, có ngưỡng thất bại sẵn.","Glue Spark có thể kiểm tra dữ liệu nhưng accumulator và cơ chế dừng theo tỷ lệ phải tự xây. Distributed Map cung cấp trực tiếp tùy chọn này.","Distributed Map đọc các phần tử của dữ liệu S3 và chạy child workflow song song. ToleratedFailurePercentage=12 làm Map thất bại khi tỷ lệ lỗi vượt ngưỡng đã cho."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA payment processing company receives a daily CSV file in an Amazon S3 bucket. The file contains several million settlement records that must be validated before the company updates a transaction database. The data engineering team needs a serverless solution that processes the records in parallel. The workflow must stop the entire processing run if more than 12% of the records fail validation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-28":{"id":"p:2-28","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 28","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse a Condition element with the s3:ResourceAccount condition key to restrict bucket ownership and add an explicit Deny statement outside the approved business-hours window\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse a Condition element with the s3:prefix condition key to restrict all object access and use aws:CurrentTime with DateGreaterThanEquals and DateLessThanEquals operators to restrict the access window\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse a Resource element with S3 bucket ARN patterns for each classification prefix and use a Condition element with aws:RequestTag to restrict access during the approved business-hours window\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse a Resource element with S3 object ARN patterns that include wildcards for each approved prefix and use a Condition element with aws:CurrentTime and DateGreaterThan and DateLessThan operators\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["s3:ResourceAccount kiểm tra tài khoản sở hữu, không phân biệt các prefix độ nhạy trong cùng bucket. Cần ARN đối tượng theo prefix.","s3:prefix chủ yếu giới hạn danh sách khi ListBucket, không bảo vệ mọi thao tác trên từng object. Quyền object cần Resource ARN thích hợp.","ARN bucket không biểu diễn các object dưới prefix; aws:RequestTag kiểm tra tag yêu cầu chứ không phải thời gian hiện tại.","Resource ARN dạng bucket/prefix/* giới hạn đối tượng được phép. Điều kiện aws:CurrentTime với toán tử ngày giới hạn khoảng thời gian; nếu cần lặp giờ làm việc hằng ngày phải thiết kế thêm, không hiểu một khoảng ngày cố định là lịch lặp."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA pharmaceutical company uses AWS Glue jobs to process research datasets stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company organizes the datasets by S3 prefixes that represent sensitivity levels such as public, internal, confidential, and regulated. The security team must allow each AWS Glue job role to access only the prefixes that match the job\u0027s approved data classification. The team must also restrict access to an approved business-hours window from 8 AM to 6 PM.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich elements must the company include in a custom IAM policy to meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-48":{"id":"p:3-48","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 48","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Python and pandas in an AWS Glue Studio notebook to prepare the datasets and ask data engineers to add the remaining pipeline transformations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Studio with data preparation recipe transformations and allow data engineers to extend the same visual ETL pipeline with additional transformations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon SageMaker Canvas and SageMaker Data Wrangler to prepare a new dataset, then ask data engineers to rebuild the remaining pipeline in AWS Glue\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena SQL views to standardize the raw datasets, then allow data engineers to copy the view results into curated S3 prefixes for downstream transformations\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Python/pandas cần viết mã, không đáp ứng người phân tích muốn chuẩn bị dữ liệu không lập trình.","Recipe transformation trong Glue Studio cho phép chuẩn bị trực quan và giữ trong cùng pipeline ETL. Kỹ sư có thể nối thêm phép biến đổi nâng cao mà không xây lại từ đầu.","Canvas/Data Wrangler có thể chuẩn bị dữ liệu nhưng phải chuyển và dựng lại phần còn lại trong Glue, thêm công tích hợp.","Athena views cần SQL và thêm bước sao chép kết quả; không phải trải nghiệm no-code trực quan trong cùng pipeline Glue."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company is building a new data lake ingestion pipeline for supplier, inventory, and sales data. A business analyst must clean, standardize, and prepare incoming datasets before the data engineering team adds advanced transformations and orchestration. The analyst needs a solution that supports no-code data preparation and can remain part of the AWS Glue pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:63":{"id":"e:4:63","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 63","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to push the data into an Amazon OpenSearch Service. Visualize the data by using OpenSearch (Kibana) dashboards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (MSK) to read the data in near-real-time. Develop a custom application for the dashboard by using D3.js\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to push data into Amazon S3. Use Amazon QuickSight to build the dashboards from S3 data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue streaming ETL to store the data into Amazon S3. Use S3 Analytics for analyzing the data\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn e-commerce company wants to develop a click analytics dashboard to see near-real-time user click patterns. The clicks are currently ingested from various devices through Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. The dashboard must be refreshed automatically every ten seconds to display the most updated data. The company is looking for an easy-to-implement solution that can be put into production as soon as possible.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution would you recommend for the given requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to push the data into an Amazon OpenSearch Service. Visualize the data by using OpenSearch (Kibana) dashboards\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can load streaming data into your Amazon OpenSearch Service domain from many different sources. Some sources, like Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose and Amazon CloudWatch Logs, have built-in support for OpenSearch Service. Others, like Amazon S3, Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, and Amazon DynamoDB, use AWS Lambda functions as event handlers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Firehose manages all underlying infrastructure, storage, networking, and configuration needed to capture and load your data into Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, or Amazon OpenSearch Service. OpenSearch (Kibana) dashboards support auto-refresh of data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue streaming ETL to store the data into Amazon S3. Use S3 Analytics for analyzing the data\u003c/strong\u003e - By using Amazon S3 analytics Storage Class Analysis you can analyze storage access patterns to help you decide when to transition the right data to the right storage class. This new Amazon S3 analytics feature observes data access patterns to help you determine when to transition less frequently accessed STANDARD storage to the STANDARD_IA (IA, for infrequent access) storage class. S3 Analytics helps monitor S3 storage patterns and is not useful for running real-time analytics on data stored on S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (MSK) to read the data in near-real-time. Develop a custom application for the dashboard by using D3.js\u003c/strong\u003e - While Apache Kafka has several advantages, it is not easy to set up and takes more development time. Moreover, custom dashboard development by using D3.js will take additional time. Hence, this is not an option for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to push data into Amazon S3. Use Amazon QuickSight to build the dashboards from S3 data\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon QuickSight is a cloud-scale business intelligence (BI) service that you can use to deliver easy-to-understand insights to the people who you work with, wherever they are. Amazon QuickSight connects to your data in the cloud and combines data from many different sources. QuickSight has options to refresh data on a Daily, Weekly, or Monthly basis. For the enterprise edition only, you have an option to choose Hourly. Hence, QuickSight is an incorrect option since the expected refresh rate cannot be met.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://www.amazonaws.cn/en/kinesis/data-firehose/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://www.amazonaws.cn/en/kinesis/data-firehose/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/refreshing-imported-data.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/refreshing-imported-data.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:55":{"id":"e:4:55","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 55","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable DynamoDB TTL on the table and update the pipeline code to write the correct expiration timestamp into the configured TTL attribute\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd a lifecycle configuration to the DynamoDB table that removes items when the ingestion timestamp is older than the retention period\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a daily AWS Glue job that exports the DynamoDB table to Amazon S3, filters metadata records by ingestion timestamp, and writes only active records back into DynamoDB\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a DynamoDB stream and configure a Lambda function to evaluate item changes and delete records that exceed the retention period\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media analytics company is building a new data pipeline that writes job metadata to an Amazon DynamoDB table. Each metadata item includes information such as source file name, ingestion timestamp, processing status, and output location. The data engineer must ensure that metadata items older than a configured retention period are automatically removed from the table with the least configuration effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable DynamoDB TTL on the table and update the pipeline code to write the correct expiration timestamp into the configured TTL attribute\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDynamoDB Time to Live is the correct feature for automatically removing items that are no longer needed. The data engineer enables TTL on the table and chooses a specific attribute that stores the expiration time for each item.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe pipeline must write the TTL attribute value when it creates or updates each metadata item. DynamoDB expects the TTL value to be a Unix epoch timestamp in seconds. When the timestamp expires, DynamoDB automatically identifies the item as eligible for deletion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution requires the least configuration effort because it uses a native DynamoDB lifecycle feature. The company does not need to build scheduled scans, custom cleanup code, stream processors, or table rewrite jobs to remove old metadata items.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a daily AWS Glue job that exports the DynamoDB table to Amazon S3, filters metadata records by ingestion timestamp, and writes only active records back into DynamoDB\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue can read and transform data from many sources, and it can support large-scale processing workflows. A Glue job could theoretically rebuild a DynamoDB dataset that excludes expired records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, exporting, filtering, and rewriting the table is excessive for simple item expiration. This design adds unnecessary ETL cost and operational complexity compared with enabling DynamoDB TTL.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdd a lifecycle configuration to the DynamoDB table that removes items when the ingestion timestamp is older than the retention period\u003c/strong\u003e - Lifecycle configuration is a common concept for services such as Amazon S3, where objects can transition storage classes or expire based on object age. This option sounds operationally simple.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, DynamoDB does not use an S3-style lifecycle configuration to delete table items by age. DynamoDB TTL is the native feature for item-level expiration and automatic deletion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a DynamoDB stream and configure a Lambda function to evaluate item changes and delete records that exceed the retention period\u003c/strong\u003e - DynamoDB Streams can capture item-level changes and invoke Lambda functions for downstream processing. This is useful for reacting to inserts, updates, deletes, or TTL-driven deletions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, a stream reacts after item changes occur. It does not automatically evaluate all stored items every day and delete old ones. Using a stream and Lambda for this requirement adds custom logic when TTL can delete expired items natively.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TTL.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TTL.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/time-to-live-ttl-before-you-start.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/time-to-live-ttl-before-you-start.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:41":{"id":"e:7:41","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 41","correct":[0],"answers":["Enable auto Concurrency Scaling mode and set up a distinct Workload Manager (WLM) queue for the long-running query.","Expand the number of nodes in the Redshift cluster to accommodate the increased load.","Activate Short Query Acceleration (SQA) to give priority to short-running queries.","Adjust the WLM settings to terminate the long-running query if it exceeds two hours of execution time."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA large e-commerce company has an Amazon Redshift data warehouse that contains about 300 TB of consumer data. The Redshift cluster is updated every 6 hours by importing newly gathered consumer activity data. Read-only queries are executed throughout the day to generate product patterns used to curate the company’s website homepage. However, there is a particular long-running query that runs for more than two hours every hour, causing other short-running queries to get queued up or take a longer time to execute.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would be the most cost-effective solution to optimize query execution?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift’s Concurrency Scaling feature allows you to automatically add additional cluster capacity to handle an increase in concurrent queries. This feature is designed to support thousands of concurrent users and concurrent queries with consistently fast query performance. When you turn on concurrency scaling, Amazon Redshift automatically adds additional cluster capacity to process an increase in both read and write queries. This means that users see the most current data, whether the queries run on the main cluster or a concurrency-scaling cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/14afa3011ee91dd2ce1f9cb018646ce3e67aeac41d0356f49a7ee42b3cfee256.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBy configuring a separate WLM queue for the long-running query, you can ensure that this query does not block other queries from executing. Amazon Redshift workload management (WLM) enables users to flexibly manage priorities within workloads so that short, fast-running queries won’t get stuck in queues behind long-running queries. When you run a query, WLM assigns the query to a queue according to the user’s user group or by matching a query group that is listed in the queue configuration with a query group label that the user sets at runtime.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach is cost-effective because you are charged for concurrency-scaling clusters only for the time they’re actively running queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable auto Concurrency Scaling mode and set up a distinct Workload Manager (WLM) queue for the long-running query.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eExpand the number of nodes in the Redshift cluster to accommodate the increased load\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. Increasing the number of nodes can improve performance by providing more resources to handle the load. However, this approach might not be the most cost-effective solution, as it involves additional costs for the extra nodes. It’s also worth noting that simply adding more nodes may not solve the problem if the issue is related to query optimization or workload management.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eActivate Short Query Acceleration (SQA) to give priority to short-running queries\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. While SQA might improve the execution time of other, shorter queries, it doesn’t directly address the issue with the long-running query. The long-running query would still take the same amount of time to execute and could still cause performance issues when it’s running.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAdjust the WLM settings to terminate the long-running query if it exceeds two hours of execution time\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. While terminating long-running queries can free up resources for other queries, it might not be the best approach if the long-running query is critical for your business operations. It’s also important to note that terminating a query does not address the root cause of why the query is taking a long time to run. A better approach might be to optimize the query or adjust the workload management settings to better handle long-running queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/concurrency-scaling-queues.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/concurrency-scaling-queues.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/cm-c-implementing-workload-management.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/cm-c-implementing-workload-management.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/scale-read-and-write-workloads-with-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/scale-read-and-write-workloads-with-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:27":{"id":"e:3:27","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 27","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Standard-IA 30 days after object creation. Delete the files 5 years after object creation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Standard-IA 30 days after object creation. Archive the files to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive 5 years after object creation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval 30 days after object creation. Delete the files 5 years after object creation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 One Zone-IA 30 days after object creation. Delete the files 5 years after object creation\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe content division at a digital media agency has an application that generates a large number of files on Amazon S3, each approximately 10 megabytes in size. The agency mandates that the files be stored for 5 years before they can be deleted. The files are frequently accessed in the first 30 days of the object creation but are rarely accessed after the first 30 days, however, immediate accessibility is always required. The files contain critical business data that is not easy to reproduce.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution is the MOST cost-effective for the given use case?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Standard-IA 30 days after object creation. Delete the files 5 years after object creation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Standard-IA class is for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. Amazon S3 Standard-IA offers the high durability, high throughput, and low latency of S3 Standard, with a low per gigabyte storage price and per GB retrieval charge.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5d4d73ba8b829486bba876b32a0211071d313b1827ac37ecda7849d7e5bdf6dc.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5d4d73ba8b829486bba876b32a0211071d313b1827ac37ecda7849d7e5bdf6dc.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the given use case, you can set up an Amazon S3 lifecycle configuration and create a transition action to move objects from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Standard-IA 30 days after object creation. You can set up an expiration action to delete the object 5 years after object creation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1948a8626e6be6766687c924d4bbcea318f2996c396c6c49b5aa33c6260003e2.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1948a8626e6be6766687c924d4bbcea318f2996c396c6c49b5aa33c6260003e2.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ef2a4775362c3b9aa48a71effa3344ab51572a5ce362d9f682815b87a494787b.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ef2a4775362c3b9aa48a71effa3344ab51572a5ce362d9f682815b87a494787b.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval 30 days after object creation. Delete the files 5 years after object creation\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval storage class has the best case retrieval time of the order of minutes, so this option is incorrect for the given requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Standard-IA 30 days after object creation. Archive the files to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive 5 years after object creation\u003c/strong\u003e - The files can simply be deleted 5 years after object creation instead of archiving the files to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive. There is no need to incur the cost of archival.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 One Zone-IA 30 days after object creation. Delete the files 5 years after object creation\u003c/strong\u003e - Unlike other Amazon S3 Storage Classes which store data in a minimum of three Availability Zones (AZs), Amazon S3 One Zone-IA stores data in a single AZ and costs 20% less than Amazon S3 Standard-IA. Amazon S3 One Zone-IA is a good choice for storing secondary backup copies of on-premises data or easily re-creatable data. The given scenario clearly states that the business-critical data is not easy to reproduce, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/glacier/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/glacier/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:9":{"id":"e:3:9","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 9","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSwap out Kinesis Data Streams with SQS Standard queues to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSwap out Kinesis Data Streams with SQS FIFO queues to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSwap out Kinesis Data Streams with Kinesis Data Firehose to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Enhanced Fanout feature of Kinesis Data Streams to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has developed an end-to-end AWS cloud-based Internet-of-Things (IoT) solution that provides customers with integrated IoT functionality in devices including baby monitors, security cameras, and entertainment systems. The company is using Kinesis Data Streams (KDS) to process IoT data from these devices. Multiple consumer applications are using the incoming data streams and the data engineers have noticed a performance lag in the data delivery speed between producers and consumers of the data streams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you suggest to improve the performance for the given use case?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Enhanced Fanout feature of Kinesis Data Streams to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams (KDS) is a massively scalable and durable real-time data streaming service. KDS can continuously capture gigabytes of data per second from hundreds of thousands of sources such as website clickstreams, database event streams, financial transactions, social media feeds, IT logs, and location-tracking events. By default, the 2MB/second/shard output is shared between all of the applications consuming data from the stream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou should use enhanced fan-out if you have multiple consumers retrieving data from a stream in parallel. With enhanced fan-out developers can register stream consumers to use enhanced fan-out and receive their own 2MB/second pipe of read throughput per shard, and this throughput automatically scales with the number of shards in a stream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9122fdc5243e0b0e1a6301fa8442a95d47238d6994001d134abef92870349861.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9122fdc5243e0b0e1a6301fa8442a95d47238d6994001d134abef92870349861.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/72c1ee4584659e05a2873b2abacfca92d44c80e5dc1e9be067767c900d26f95d.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/72c1ee4584659e05a2873b2abacfca92d44c80e5dc1e9be067767c900d26f95d.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSwap out Kinesis Data Streams with Kinesis Data Firehose to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications\u003c/strong\u003e -  Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose is the easiest way to reliably load streaming data into data lakes, data stores, and analytics tools. It is a fully managed service that automatically scales to match the throughput of your data and requires no ongoing administration. It can also batch, compress, transform, and encrypt the data before loading it, minimizing the amount of storage used at the destination and increasing security. Kinesis Data Firehose can only write to S3, Redshift, Elasticsearch or Splunk. You can\u0027t have applications consuming data streams from Kinesis Data Firehose, that\u0027s the job of Kinesis Data Streams. Therefore this option is not correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSwap out Kinesis Data Streams with SQS Standard queues to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSwap out Kinesis Data Streams with SQS FIFO queues to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Simple Queue Service (SQS) is a fully managed message queuing service that enables you to decouple and scale microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications. SQS offers two types of message queues. Standard queues offer maximum throughput, best-effort ordering, and at-least-once delivery. SQS FIFO queues are designed to guarantee that messages are processed exactly once, in the exact order that they are sent. As multiple applications are consuming the same stream concurrently, both SQS Standard and SQS FIFO are not the right fit for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d695b6151765b5ea65fc9cde6f4c19438dad59e0bc8a89f5857549c74e4ada05.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d695b6151765b5ea65fc9cde6f4c19438dad59e0bc8a89f5857549c74e4ada05.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:29":{"id":"e:8:29","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 29","correct":[0],"answers":["AWS Database Migration Service","AWS Data Transfer Terminal","AWS Transfer Family","AWS DataSync"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multinational corporation is planning to migrate its on-premises PostgreSQL database to Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL. Since the company runs critical operations on the database, the migration must be completed with minimal downtime. The company also wants to ensure data consistency by validating the migrated data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich AWS service would be the most suitable for this scenario?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)\u003c/strong\u003e is a web service you can use to migrate data from your database that is on-premises, on an Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) DB instance, or in a database on an Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) instance to a database on an AWS service. These services can include a database on Amazon RDS or a database on an Amazon EC2 instance. You can also migrate a database from an AWS service to an on-premises database. You can migrate between source and target endpoints that use the same database engine, such as from an Oracle database to an Oracle database. You can also migrate between source and target endpoints that use different database engines, such as from an Oracle database to a PostgreSQL database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/a93e0cfe80cbdb73df5be18cde5fe7cdb2380b9fe695b10958faf025247a3596.png\" alt=\"https://media.tutorialsdojo.com/public/AWS DMS-11JUN2026.png\"\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use AWS DMS to migrate databases to the AWS Cloud quickly and securely. Your source database remains fully operational during the migration, which minimizes downtime for applications that rely on the database. You can reduce the migration time by using AWS DMS to create a task that captures ongoing changes after you complete an initial full-load migration through a process called\u0026nbsp;change data capture (CDC).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Database Migration Service.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Data Transfer Terminal\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because this service is only intended for large-scale data transfers into Amazon S3, not for live database migrations or replication. It does not provide features for minimizing downtime or validating relational database consistency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Transfer Family\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because Transfer Family typically provides managed support for file transfer protocols (FTP, SFTP, FTPS). It is useful for moving files into and out of AWS, but it is not suitable for migrating relational databases with replication and validation requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS DataSync\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because it is merely a data transfer service. It can help automate and accelerate data movement\u0026nbsp;between on-premises storage systems and AWS storage services. However, it does not support database migration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-on-premises-oracle-database-to-amazon-rds-for-oracle.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-on-premises-oracle-database-to-amazon-rds-for-oracle.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-on-premises-oracle-database-to-amazon-rds-for-postgresql-by-using-an-oracle-bystander-and-aws-dms.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-on-premises-oracle-database-to-amazon-rds-for-postgresql-by-using-an-oracle-bystander-and-aws-dms.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Database Migration Service Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-database-migration-service/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-database-migration-service/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:50":{"id":"e:2:50","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 50","correct":[0,1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eYou have multiple concurrent jobs with job bookmarks, and the max concurrency isn\u0027t set to 1\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe job.commit() object is missing\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eYou have added a transformation context parameter to the DynamicFrame referenced within the job, which is causing the job to crash\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eJob bookmarks do not work for Amazon S3 input sources\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue keeps track of job bookmarks by storing the metadata in Amazon S3 configured during job creation. Deleting the S3 bucket can result in job reprocessing\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn AWS Glue job is scheduled to be run every Sunday. The Glue job copies data from certain folders in an S3 bucket to Redshift. To prevent reprocessing of old data, job bookmarks have been enabled on the AWS Glue job. However, the ETL job is reprocessing data that was already processed in an earlier run.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat could be the underlying issue and how should it be fixed to stop reprocessing of data? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eYou have multiple concurrent jobs with job bookmarks, and the max concurrency isn\u0027t set to 1\u003c/strong\u003e - Ensure that the maximum number of concurrent runs for the job is 1. When you have multiple concurrent jobs with job bookmarks and the maximum concurrency is set to 1, the job bookmark doesn\u0027t work correctly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe job.commit() object is missing\u003c/strong\u003e - Ensure that your job run script ends with the following commit: job.commit(). When you include this object, AWS Glue records the timestamp and path of the job run. If you run the job again with the same path, AWS Glue processes only the new files. If you don\u0027t include this object and job bookmarks are enabled, the job reprocesses the already processed files along with the new files and creates redundancy in the job\u0027s target data store.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTroubleshooting reprocessing error in job bookmarks:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/dbf37fc5bea42c52e08d60957a7001cde25508b5861be789d0de7eeb780ddaec.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/dbf37fc5bea42c52e08d60957a7001cde25508b5861be789d0de7eeb780ddaec.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-troubleshooting-errors.html#error-job-bookmarks-reprocess-data\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-troubleshooting-errors.html#error-job-bookmarks-reprocess-data\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eJob bookmarks do not work for Amazon S3 input sources\u003c/strong\u003e - This statement is incorrect. Job bookmarks can be configured even when the input source is Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eYou have added a transformation context parameter to the DynamicFrame referenced within the job, which is causing the job to crash\u003c/strong\u003e - Transformation context is an optional parameter in the GlueContext class, but job bookmarks don\u0027t work if you don\u0027t include it. So this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue keeps track of job bookmarks by storing the metadata in Amazon S3 configured during job creation. Deleting the S3 bucket can result in job reprocessing\u003c/strong\u003e - This statement is incorrect. AWS Glue keeps track of job bookmarks with the job itself. If you delete a job, the job bookmark is deleted.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-troubleshooting-errors.html#error-job-bookmarks-reprocess-data\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-troubleshooting-errors.html#error-job-bookmarks-reprocess-data\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-continuations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-continuations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-58":{"id":"p:2-58","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 58","correct":[3,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDeploy Amazon Rekognition to analyze the structured financial records for PII and redact sensitive text before use in downstream services. Integrate this with a Lambda function to batch-process the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon Detective to investigate potential unauthorized access to sensitive fields and generate detailed visualizations of user behavior on the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable Amazon Macie on the S3 bucket to automatically discover and classify sensitive data such as PII and financial data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to identify and mask sensitive fields such as account numbers and national IDs using built-in transformations. Store the masked results in a new S3 bucket for downstream analytics\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDefine granular IAM policies that restrict access to sensitive S3 prefixes or objects based on user roles and job functions\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Rekognition phân tích hình ảnh/video, không phải công cụ chuẩn để che PII trong hồ sơ tài chính dạng bảng.","Detective hỗ trợ điều tra bảo mật, không trực tiếp biến đổi hoặc che các trường dữ liệu nhạy cảm trong tệp.","Macie phát hiện và phân loại PII trong S3, nhưng riêng bước này không che giá trị hoặc áp đặt toàn bộ quyền truy cập. Hai đáp án được chọn giải quyết cả biến đổi lẫn quyền.","DataBrew dùng phép biến đổi có sẵn để nhận diện, che các trường nhạy cảm và ghi một bản đã xử lý cho phân tích. Giảm mã tự viết.","IAM policy chi tiết giới hạn các prefix/object theo vai trò công việc. Kết hợp với bản dữ liệu đã che để ngăn người không có nhiệm vụ đọc dữ liệu gốc."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA fintech startup processes transaction logs and customer account records that are stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. The bucket is encrypted using server-side encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS). Some of the stored data contains sensitive financial attributes such as customer bank account numbers, credit scores, and national ID numbers. To ensure data privacy and regulatory compliance, the company must automatically detect, mask, and control access to sensitive fields during the analytics workflow. Analysts need masked versions of the data for modeling, while certain pre-processing engineers require controlled access to unmasked data for enrichment tasks. The company wants a low-maintenance and scalable solution that ensures end-to-end protection of sensitive data within the pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of solutions should the company use to fulfill these requirements? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-55":{"id":"p:4-55","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 55","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a second set of Lambda functions with the same 128 MB memory setting and distribute S3 events across both function groups by using separate event notification prefixes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure the Lambda functions to run in the company\u0027s VPC so the functions can use private networking for S3 and AWS Glue access\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the memory allocation of the Lambda functions to 512 MB so the functions receive more proportional CPU capacity\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the Maximum Retry Attempts setting for the Lambda functions so failed invocations have more opportunities to complete successfully\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Nhân đôi số function chỉ chia bớt số sự kiện giữa các nhóm. Mỗi lần xử lý vẫn chỉ có 128 MB và mức CPU tương ứng, nên đoạn làm sạch dữ liệu vẫn chậm.","Đưa Lambda vào VPC thay đổi kết nối mạng, không cấp thêm CPU. Nó không xử lý nguyên nhân tính toán chậm trong mỗi invocation.","CPU của Lambda tăng theo mức bộ nhớ cấu hình. Nâng từ 128 MB lên 512 MB cấp thêm khả năng tính toán cho đoạn xử lý; nên đo thời gian và chi phí sau thay đổi.","Tăng retry chỉ thử lại các lần thất bại. Nó không làm mã xử lý nhanh hơn và có thể tăng số lần chạy nếu nguyên nhân timeout vẫn giữ nguyên."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media streaming company stores subscriber activity files in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company runs an analysis pipeline whenever a subscriber cancels a premium plan. The pipeline uses Lambda functions that respond to S3 events, clean the activity data, and start an AWS Glue workflow for downstream processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe Lambda functions are configured with 128 MB of memory, 512 MB of ephemeral storage, and a timeout of 20 seconds. The functions complete successfully, but monitoring shows that CPU usage is frequently close to 100 percent, which slows the pipeline. The company wants to improve function performance and reduce total pipeline runtime.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-40":{"id":"p:4-40","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 40","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAdd a created_at timestamp to each DynamoDB item, run a scheduled Lambda function to scan for 18-month-old records, and copy matching records to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable DynamoDB Streams, configure TTL to delete items after 18 months, use Lambda to archive TTL-deleted items to S3 Glacier Deep Archive, and configure S3 Lifecycle expiration\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd a created_at timestamp to each DynamoDB item, run a scheduled Lambda function to scan for expired records, write the records to S3 Glacier Deep Archive, and use the function to delete archived objects\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable DynamoDB Streams, configure TTL to delete items after 18 months, use Lambda to archive all stream events to Amazon S3 Standard, and retain the objects indefinitely\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Quét bảng định kỳ tiêu thụ năng lực đọc và cần mã theo dõi các bản ghi đã chuyển. Phương án còn thiếu cơ chế tự xóa bản lưu trữ sau 6 năm.","TTL đánh dấu bản ghi hết hạn sau 18 tháng; DynamoDB Streams với ảnh cũ của bản ghi cho phép Lambda lưu các sự kiện xóa do TTL sang S3 Deep Archive. Lifecycle xóa bản lưu sau 6 năm. TTL xóa bất đồng bộ, không bảo đảm đúng chính xác thời điểm hết hạn.","Tự quét, sao chép và xóa cả ở DynamoDB lẫn S3 tạo nhiều logic vận hành. TTL, Streams và S3 Lifecycle cung cấp các cơ chế được quản lý cho các bước này.","Lưu mọi stream event tạo cả bản sao insert và update không cần thiết cho mục tiêu này. Giữ vô thời hạn cũng vi phạm yêu cầu xóa sau thời gian lưu trữ 6 năm."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial technology company writes transaction audit records into an Amazon DynamoDB table throughout the day. The company must keep each record available in DynamoDB for 18 months. After 18 months, the company must archive the records for another 6 years, and then permanently delete the archived records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements in the MOST operationally efficient way?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:35":{"id":"e:3:35","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 35","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has hired you to help with redesigning a real-time data processor. The company wants to build custom applications that process and analyze the streaming data for its specialized needs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will you recommend to address this use-case?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams is useful for rapidly moving data off data producers and then continuously processing the data, be it to transform the data before emitting to a data store, run real-time metrics and analytics, or derive more complex data streams for further processing. Kinesis data streams can continuously capture gigabytes of data per second from hundreds of thousands of sources such as website clickstreams, database event streams, financial transactions, social media feeds, IT logs, and location-tracking events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eKinesis Data Streams Overview:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/82bd94cc900d62610f42fd3b01a02e17d4b9fff9e0a797aefd3cdcd76aef70f6.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/82bd94cc900d62610f42fd3b01a02e17d4b9fff9e0a797aefd3cdcd76aef70f6.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) is a highly available, durable, secure, fully managed pub/sub messaging service that enables you to decouple microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications. SNS cannot be used to decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor as described in the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) offers a secure, durable, and available hosted queue that lets you integrate and decouple distributed software systems and components. SQS cannot be used to decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor as described in the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose is the easiest way to load streaming data into data stores and analytics tools. Kinesis Firehose cannot be used to process and analyze the streaming data in custom applications. It can capture, transform, and load streaming data into Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, Amazon Elasticsearch Service, and Splunk, enabling near real-time analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Firehose Overview\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/86656f260eb689aa4bb4bc87cbcb28cd537002c059d19be4f303f141c4d132e2.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/86656f260eb689aa4bb4bc87cbcb28cd537002c059d19be4f303f141c4d132e2.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:8":{"id":"e:5:8","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 8","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eRun the \u003ccode\u003eUNLOAD\u003c/code\u003e SQL command on the primary Redshift cluster to unload all the data to an S3 bucket every 2 weeks. Then, create an Amazon Glue job to load data from the S3 bucket to the second Redshift cluster.\u003c/span\u003e","Generate manual snapshots of the primary Redshift cluster every 2 weeks. Then, restore these snapshots to the second Redshift cluster, ensuring that its configuration mirrors that of the primary Redshift cluster.","\u003cspan\u003eRun the \u003ccode\u003eUNLOAD\u003c/code\u003e SQL command on the primary Redshift cluster to unload all the data to an S3 bucket every 2 weeks. Then, use the Amazon Redshift spectrum from the secondary Redshift cluster to access the data stored in Amazon S3.\u003c/span\u003e","Use Amazon Redshift Serverless for the second Redshift cluster. Then, establish a data share from the primary Redshift cluster to the second Redshift cluster."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is currently provisioning an Amazon Redshift cluster as its primary data warehouse. They plan to do intensive data analysis on a separate Amazon Redshift cluster that utilizes the same live data from the primary Redshift cluster. The second Redshift cluster will only be used for 2 hours every 2 weeks for the new intensive data analysis procedure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich would be the MOST cost-effective solution for meeting these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshifts data sharing\u003c/strong\u003e provides two powerful capabilities:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Read-only access to data across multiple Amazon Redshift clusters.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Read live data without manually moving or copying data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFurthermore, this feature supports seamless sharing between provisioned and serverless clusters. This feature enables users to ensure consistent data availability across different teams and systems, improving collaboration and decision-making. Additionally, integrating serverless clusters in data sharing brings a layer of scalability and cost-effectiveness, which is particularly beneficial for testing environments.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/43b646ca478481d93f8fc6c06c8637335e9883e9c5049268ec776b807425127d.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshift Serverless\u003c/strong\u003e enables the use of Redshift capabilities without the need to provision and manage Redshift clusters, which is ideal for workloads that are intermittent and short-lived, such as the bi-weekly, 2-hour intensive data analysis procedure as said in the scenario. By utilizing Redshift Serverless for the second cluster, the company only pays for the compute resources used during the analysis period, avoiding the costs associated with running a dedicated Redshift cluster 24/7. Additionally, \u003cstrong\u003eestablishing a data share\u003c/strong\u003e from the primary Redshift cluster to the second serverless cluster allows for direct access to the live data without the need for data duplication or complex data pipeline setups, such as manual data unloads to S3, Glue jobs for data loading, or snapshot restoration processes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Serverless for the second Redshift cluster. Then, establish a data share from the primary Redshift cluster to the second Redshift cluster.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun the \u003ccode\u003eUNLOAD\u003c/code\u003e SQL command on the primary Redshift cluster to unload all the data to an S3 bucket every 2 weeks. Then, create an Amazon Glue job to load data from the S3 bucket to the second Redshift cluster\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect primarily because this approach would require significant data transfer and processing time, which is not at all efficient for an infrequently used cluster (only 2 hours every 2 weeks). Additionally, this solution incurs extra costs for data storage in S3 and the use of Amazon Glue. Lastly, this method does not support access to live data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun the \u003ccode\u003eUNLOAD\u003c/code\u003e SQL command on the primary Redshift cluster to unload all the data to an S3 bucket every 2 weeks. Then, use the Amazon Redshift spectrum from the secondary Redshift cluster to access the data stored in Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Just like the previous option, it involves unnecessary complexity and data movement. While Redshift Spectrum allows querying data in S3 without loading it into Redshift, regularly unloading to S3 is inefficient for this use case since data analysis on the second Redshift cluster occurs infrequently. Additionally, this method incurs additional costs for S3 storage and potentially for Spectrum queries. Lastly, just like the previous option, this method does not support access to live data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eGenerate manual snapshots of the primary Redshift cluster every 2 weeks, then restore these snapshots to the second Redshift cluster, ensuring that its configuration mirrors that of the primary Redshift cluster \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect primarily because provisioning another production cluster means two times the cost. Furthermore, snapshotting and restoring a cluster is a very heavy operation, especially for short and infrequent usage (only 2 hours every 2 weeks). Lastly, just like the previous option, this method does not support access to live data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/data_sharing_intro.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/data_sharing_intro.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/serverless-console-comparison.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/serverless-console-comparison.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-25":{"id":"p:3-25","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 25","correct":[0,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSetup a lifecycle policy to transition the raw zone data into Glacier Deep Archive after 1 day of object creation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lambda function based job to delete the raw zone data after 1 day\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSetup a lifecycle policy to transition the curated zone data into Glacier Deep Archive after 1 day of object creation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Glue ETL job to write the transformed data in the curated zone using CSV format\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Glue ETL job to write the transformed data in the curated zone using a compressed file format\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Raw zone cần giữ lâu cho tuân thủ nhưng không dùng trực tiếp để truy vấn hằng ngày. Sau xử lý có thể chuyển Deep Archive để tiết kiệm và vẫn giữ đủ thời hạn.","Xóa sau một ngày vi phạm yêu cầu giữ dữ liệu nguồn ít nhất năm năm.","Curated zone được Athena truy vấn ad-hoc, nên chuyển Deep Archive sẽ làm dữ liệu không sẵn sàng đọc trực tiếp trước khi khôi phục.","CSV không tận dụng lưu theo cột và thường quét nhiều hơn. Bộ đề chọn định dạng nén phù hợp để giảm dung lượng và chi phí truy vấn.","Ghi đầu ra curated bằng định dạng nén phù hợp, ưu tiên định dạng cột cho phân tích. Athena đọc ít byte hơn khi truy vấn các trường cần thiết."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn IT company needs to set up a data lake on Amazon S3 for a healthcare client. The data lake is split into raw and curated zones. For compliance reasons, the source data needs to be kept for a minimum of 5 years. The source data arrives in the raw zone and is then processed via an AWS Glue-based ETL job into the curated zone. The data engineering team runs ad-hoc queries only on the data in the curated zone using Athena. The team is concerned about the cost of data storage in both the raw and curated zones as the data is increasing at a rate of 2 TB daily in each zone.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options would you implement together as the MOST cost-optimal solution? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:57":{"id":"e:1:57","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 57","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDeploy Apache Kafka MirrorMaker 2.0 in each Region, configure custom replication flows between all regional Kafka clusters, and enable the SSL security protocol for encrypted cross-Region traffic\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Database Migration Service with Amazon MSK endpoints in each Region, create replication tasks for the trading topics, configure TLS for the endpoints, and run the tasks continuously to synchronize streaming data between the regional clusters\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams in each Region to ingest trading events, use Amazon Data Firehose to deliver records into regional Amazon MSK clusters, and configure AWS KMS encryption with IAM roles for access control\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon MSK Replicator to configure fully connected replication relationships among the Amazon MSK clusters in all three Regions, enable TLS encryption, and use IAM authentication for cluster access\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multinational brokerage firm runs a streaming platform for equity order events in the us-west-2 Region, the eu-central-1 Region, and the ap-southeast-1 Region. The firm needs near real-time bidirectional synchronization of trading events across all three Regions. The solution must encrypt data in transit, preserve event ordering for each Kafka partition, support multi-Region disaster recovery, and keep replication latency under 500 milliseconds during normal operating conditions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon MSK Replicator to configure fully connected replication relationships among the Amazon MSK clusters in all three Regions, enable TLS encryption, and use IAM authentication for cluster access\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon MSK Replicator is the best fit because it provides a fully managed way to replicate data across Amazon MSK clusters in the same AWS Region or in different AWS Regions. The company can create replication relationships among the regional MSK clusters so that events written in one Region become available to consumers in the other Regions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/12ab136a0f0f70470ee40db12958a9c5ab0e44e985ebe21b97f664e8de3585ad.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/12ab136a0f0f70470ee40db12958a9c5ab0e44e985ebe21b97f664e8de3585ad.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eMSK Replicator reduces operational effort because AWS manages the replication infrastructure, scaling, and cross-cluster replication workflow. The company does not need to deploy and maintain Apache Kafka MirrorMaker 2.0, build custom Kafka Connect connectors, or operate separate replication fleets in each Region. MSK Replicator also supports multi-Region streaming architectures for resiliency and disaster recovery.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon MSK supports encryption in transit with TLS, and MSK Replicator works with MSK clusters that use IAM authentication for access control. Kafka preserves ordering within a topic partition, so the design can maintain the expected Kafka ordering model while using a managed cross-Region replication capability for near real-time disaster recovery.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/dcfbe736425fed30e9b989ba93acf92307f7cec515b064554532bfacf1637fa4.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/dcfbe736425fed30e9b989ba93acf92307f7cec515b064554532bfacf1637fa4.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-amazon-msk-replicator-fully-managed-replication-across-msk-clusters-in-same-or-different-aws-regions/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-amazon-msk-replicator-fully-managed-replication-across-msk-clusters-in-same-or-different-aws-regions/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams in each Region to ingest trading events, use Amazon Data Firehose to deliver records into regional Amazon MSK clusters, and configure AWS KMS encryption with IAM roles for access control\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and Amazon Data Firehose are managed streaming and delivery services, but this option introduces an unnecessary translation path between Kinesis, Firehose, and Amazon MSK. Firehose is primarily a delivery service for destinations such as Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, Amazon OpenSearch Service, and compatible HTTP endpoints, not a managed bidirectional MSK cluster replication service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution also does not provide a simple way to preserve Kafka topic and partition semantics across all MSK clusters. It adds extra services and integration logic without meeting the requirement for least operational effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy Apache Kafka MirrorMaker 2.0 in each Region, configure custom replication flows between all regional Kafka clusters, and enable the SSL security protocol for encrypted cross-Region traffic\u003c/strong\u003e - Apache Kafka MirrorMaker 2.0 can replicate data between Kafka clusters and can support cross-Region disaster recovery patterns. However, the company must deploy, configure, scale, monitor, patch, and troubleshoot MirrorMaker 2.0 infrastructure in multiple Regions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach creates more operational effort than using Amazon MSK Replicator. The requirement asks for the least operational effort, so a fully managed MSK replication capability is preferable to operating open-source replication components directly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Database Migration Service with Amazon MSK endpoints in each Region, create replication tasks for the trading topics, configure TLS for the endpoints, and run the tasks continuously to synchronize streaming data between the regional clusters\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Database Migration Service can move data from supported sources to supported targets, including some streaming targets. However, it is not the preferred service for fully interconnected, near real-time Kafka topic replication among regional MSK clusters.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option would require multiple replication tasks and careful operational handling across all Regions. It does not provide the same purpose-built MSK-to-MSK replication capability, Kafka metadata handling, and managed multi-Region Kafka disaster recovery pattern as MSK Replicator.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-cross-region.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-cross-region.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-active-active.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-replicator-active-active.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-amazon-msk-replicator-fully-managed-replication-across-msk-clusters-in-same-or-different-aws-regions/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-amazon-msk-replicator-fully-managed-replication-across-msk-clusters-in-same-or-different-aws-regions/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-msk-replicator-and-mirrormaker2-choosing-the-right-replication-strategy-for-apache-kafka-disaster-recovery-and-migrations/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-msk-replicator-and-mirrormaker2-choosing-the-right-replication-strategy-for-apache-kafka-disaster-recovery-and-migrations/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-27":{"id":"p:3-27","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 27","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate EventBridge schedules that invoke Lambda functions to copy S3 objects and create RDS snapshots, then use IAM deny policies to prevent backup deletion during retention periods\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Backup plan with backup schedules, windows, lifecycle values, and a backup vault, assign the S3 and RDS resources, and configure AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Versioning and Same-Region Replication on the buckets, configure RDS automated backups, and create manual DB snapshots for retention periods that exceed automated backup limits\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Versioning and Cross-Region Replication on the buckets, configure RDS automated backups, and copy additional RDS snapshots to a second Region for long-term retention\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Tự viết backup bằng Lambda/EventBridge thêm mã và xử lý lỗi. IAM Deny có thể bị thay đổi bởi người đủ quyền, không thay thế khóa retention như Vault Lock compliance.","AWS Backup plan quản lý lịch, cửa sổ và vòng đời cho S3/RDS. Vault Lock compliance bảo vệ recovery point theo retention sau khi khóa có hiệu lực, giảm nguy cơ xóa trái quy định.","Versioning, replication và snapshot giúp có bản sao nhưng chưa áp đặt bảo vệ bất biến cho toàn bộ thời hạn lưu backup.","Sao chép sang Region khác tăng khả năng phục hồi thảm họa, nhưng không tự ngăn người có quyền xóa bản sao trước thời hạn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare company stores audit data in Amazon S3 buckets and patient application data in Amazon RDS DB instances. The security team must apply technical controls that create backups for both services and retain the backups for required retention periods. The backups must be protected from accidental or malicious deletion until the required retention periods expire.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-10":{"id":"p:1-10","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 10","correct":[1,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eTransform .csv files to JSON format by fetching the required key-value pairs only\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe S3 bucket should be configured in the same AWS Region where the Athena queries are being run\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRemove partitions and perform \u003ccode\u003edata bucketing\u003c/code\u003e on the S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe S3 bucket should be configured in the same Availability Zone where the Athena queries are being run\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eTransform .csv files to Parquet format by fetching only the data fields required for predicates\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["JSON vẫn là định dạng theo bản ghi, không mang lại ưu thế chỉ đọc các cột cần dùng như Parquet. Chuyển CSV sang JSON chưa giải quyết tốt chi phí quét và hiệu năng.","Đặt dữ liệu S3 cùng Region với Athena giúp tránh độ trễ và chi phí truyền dữ liệu liên vùng không cần thiết. Đây là tối ưu không làm thay đổi cách phân vùng hiện có.","Đề nói rõ phải giữ cách phân vùng theo ngày. Xóa phân vùng để thay bằng bucketing vi phạm điều kiện đó; bucketing và partitioning cũng không phải cùng một khái niệm.","Athena và S3 trong tình huống này được cấu hình ở cấp Region, không phải chọn cùng một Availability Zone. Đây là lựa chọn gây nhiễu về phạm vi dịch vụ.","Parquet lưu theo cột, giúp Athena đọc những cột cần thiết và tận dụng lọc dữ liệu để giảm lượng quét. Có thể giữ nguyên partition theo ngày, đồng thời cải thiện tốc độ và chi phí."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA university has tie-ups with local hospitals to share anonymized health statistics of people. The data is stored in Amazon S3 as .csv files. Amazon Athena is used to run extensive analytics on the data for finding correlations between different parameters in the data. The university is facing high costs and performance-related issues as the volume of data is growing rapidly. The data in the S3 bucket is already partitioned by date and the university does not want to change this partition scheme.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs a data engineer, how can you further improve query performance? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-22":{"id":"p:4-22","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 22","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up access to the historical data via Athena. The analytics team can run historical data queries on Athena and continue the daily reporting on Redshift. In case the reports need to be cross-referenced, the analytics team needs to export these in flat files and then do further analysis\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the Redshift COPY command to load the S3-based historical data into Redshift. Once the ad-hoc queries are run for the historic data, it can be removed from Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Redshift Spectrum to create Redshift cluster tables pointing to the underlying historical data in S3. The analytics team can then query this historical data to cross-reference with the daily reports from Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Glue ETL job to load the S3-based historical data into Redshift. Once the ad-hoc queries are run for the historic data, it can be removed from Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Athena đọc được dữ liệu S3, nhưng xuất kết quả hai hệ thống ra tệp rồi đối chiếu thủ công làm tăng công việc. Spectrum cho phép join dữ liệu lịch sử với dữ liệu Redshift ngay trong SQL.","COPY nạp được dữ liệu S3, nhưng mỗi lần cần truy vấn lại phải nạp rồi xóa. Điều đó tăng thời gian và dung lượng tạm trong Redshift không cần thiết.","Redshift Spectrum dùng external table tham chiếu dữ liệu ở S3. Nhóm phân tích có thể join dữ liệu lịch sử đó với bảng trong Redshift mà không phải nạp lại toàn bộ lịch sử.","Glue ETL để nạp rồi xóa lịch sử tạo thêm job và thao tác vận hành. Spectrum truy vấn tại chỗ trên S3 đơn giản hơn cho các truy vấn không thường xuyên."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn IT company has built a custom data warehousing solution for a large shipping company by using Amazon Redshift. The solution helps the shipping company to analyze the international/domestic cargo transportation details and operational records for the ships. As part of the cost optimizations, the shipping company now wants to move any historical data (any data older than a year) into Amazon S3, as the daily analytical reports consume data for just the last year. However, the data engineers at multiple divisions of the shipping company want to retain the ability to cross-reference this historical data along with the daily reports. The shipping company wants to develop a solution with the LEAST amount of effort and MINIMUM cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich option would you recommend for this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:14":{"id":"e:1:14","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 14","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon OpenSearch Ingestion Pipelines to pull data from S3, process the log files, and index them into Amazon OpenSearch Service. Use OpenSearch Dashboards for real-time visualization. Enable scheduled queries with AWS Glue for historical analysis and reporting\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue to process the uploaded S3 data files. Configure S3 Event Notifications to trigger AWS Lambda for near real-time orchestration. Use Amazon Athena for on-demand querying of transformed data stored in S3. Use Amazon QuickSight to visualize the results through an interactive dashboard\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse a provisioned Amazon EMR cluster running Spark to ingest and process the compressed files from S3. Trigger workflows using Amazon EventBridge rules and Step Functions. Store processed data in Amazon RDS and use Amazon Managed Grafana to visualize the vehicle inventory dashboards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream incoming vehicle log files from S3 and transform them on-the-fly with AWS Lambda. Store transformed data in Amazon Redshift. Use Redshift Query Editor V2 for ad hoc queries and Amazon QuickSight for reporting dashboards\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company operates a near real-time inventory tracking system for vehicle depots across multiple geographic regions. Third-party vendors upload multiple logs of vehicle arrivals and departures in the form of small compressed files (less than 10 KB) to a central Amazon S3 bucket. The company needs to immediately process new uploads to keep a dashboard up to date. The dashboard must be refreshed near real-time to reflect the latest vehicle inventory across regions. A data engineer is tasked with designing a cost-effective, low-latency, and scalable solution that automates the processing and transformation of the uploaded data, enables ad hoc querying for business analysts, and supports visual reporting through dashboards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will best meet these requirements in the most cost-effective and scalable manner?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue to process the uploaded S3 data files. Configure S3 Event Notifications to trigger AWS Lambda for near real-time orchestration. Use Amazon Athena for on-demand querying of transformed data stored in S3. Use Amazon QuickSight to visualize the results through an interactive dashboard\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution combines AWS Glue for serverless ETL with S3 Event Notifications that trigger AWS Lambda, allowing near real-time ingestion and processing of small, compressed files immediately after they are uploaded. Amazon Athena provides cost-effective, on-demand SQL querying directly against S3 without the need for infrastructure provisioning. Amazon QuickSight connects to Athena for real-time dashboards, making this architecture scalable, serverless, and cost-efficient, especially given the small file sizes and frequency of uploads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse a provisioned Amazon EMR cluster running Spark to ingest and process the compressed files from S3. Trigger workflows using Amazon EventBridge rules and Step Functions. Store processed data in Amazon RDS and use Amazon Managed Grafana to visualize the vehicle inventory dashboards\u003c/strong\u003e - While this solution uses EMR and Step Functions for data processing and orchestration, provisioning an EMR cluster introduces high fixed costs and overhead, which is not ideal for processing small 5–10 KB files. Additionally, Amazon RDS is not optimal for analytical workloads or schema evolution based on incoming log files. Grafana is suitable for operational metrics, but using it for tabular business dashboards increases complexity and may require additional plug-ins or integrations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream incoming vehicle log files from S3 and transform them on-the-fly with AWS Lambda. Store transformed data in Amazon Redshift. Use Redshift Query Editor V2 for ad hoc queries and Amazon QuickSight for reporting dashboards\u003c/strong\u003e - Kinesis Firehose is designed for streaming high-throughput data, not for event-based ingestion of small files dropped periodically into S3. Triggering transformations through Lambda per file is inefficient in this context. Additionally, Redshift is a costlier option for variable, low-volume usage patterns, especially when Athena can directly query S3 at a fraction of the cost and without provisioning. This architecture introduces unnecessary complexity for the company’s use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon OpenSearch Ingestion Pipelines to pull data from S3, process the log files, and index them into Amazon OpenSearch Service. Use OpenSearch Dashboards for real-time visualization. Enable scheduled queries with AWS Glue for historical analysis and reporting\u003c/strong\u003e - OpenSearch is designed for full-text search and log analytics rather than structured, relational data analysis. While ingestion pipelines can process data from S3, the system is optimized for search-based analytics over large unstructured data (e.g., logs), not small files with structured schema. Also, OpenSearch Dashboards lacks deep business intelligence features like multi-dimensional KPIs, visual pivoting, or tabular reporting. This makes it suboptimal for the company’s analytics dashboard needs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/starting-workflow-eventbridge.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/starting-workflow-eventbridge.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-what-is-emr.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-what-is-emr.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/redshift/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:45":{"id":"e:5:45","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 45","correct":[1],"answers":["Use Amazon QuickSight to visualize and analyze the data in your AWS data lake.","Create AWS Glue data quality rules and define rulesets to evaluate your table.","Implement AWS Lake Formation security policies to automate data access management in your AWS data lake.","Utilize Amazon Athena for querying and assessing the data quality in your AWS data lake."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA corporation with a data lake managed by AWS Lake Formation seeks to automate its manual, time-consuming data quality checks. They need a solution that integrates with AWS, reduces the effort from days to hours, and allows them to define rulesets for table evaluation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution would be recommended for this scenario?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Data Quality\u003c/strong\u003e is a feature of AWS Glue that provides a managed, serverless experience to help you evaluate and monitor the quality of your data. It is built on top of the open-source DeeQu framework.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Data Quality works with Data Quality Definition Language (DQDL), which is a domain-specific language that you use to define data quality rules. It offers non-coders an easy way to set up data quality rules. These personas include data stewards and business analysts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ddb55fc6501b6a767b680d143993a2761a59740a805c5c7439cf34aea6100b53.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIt uses machine learning to detect anomalies and hard-to-detect data quality issues, and it provides a Data Quality score that gives an overview of the health of your data. This allows corporations with large AWS data lakes to automate their manual, time-consuming data quality checks\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate AWS Glue data quality rules and define rulesets to evaluate your table.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement AWS Lake Formation security policies to automate data access management in your AWS data lake\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because,\u0026nbsp;while AWS Lake Formation does provide security policies, it doesn’t directly automate data quality checks. It’s more about managing access to the data. The actual data quality checks would still need to be implemented separately.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon Athena for querying and assessing the data quality in your AWS data lake\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because, while AWS Athena can be used for assessing data quality, it doesn’t inherently automate the process or allow for the definition of rulesets for table evaluation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon QuickSight to visualize and analyze the data in your AWS data lake\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because while Amazon QuickSight is a business intelligence service that can be used to visualize and analyze data, it doesn’t directly provide functionality for data quality checks. It’s more about data visualization and analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/getting-started-with-aws-glue-data-quality-from-the-aws-glue-data-catalog/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/getting-started-with-aws-glue-data-quality-from-the-aws-glue-data-catalog/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:22":{"id":"e:7:22","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 22","correct":[0,1],"answers":["Increase the shard count of the Kinesis data stream.","Implement exponential backoff and retry logic in the application.","Activate the enhanced monitoring feature for the stream.","Enable the enhanced fan-out functionality on the stream.","Use a larger EC2 instance type for the Kinesis Data Streams application."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses an Amazon Kinesis data stream to manage its real-time data processing workloads. Recently, their consumer application on an EC2 instance started encountering \u003ccode\u003eProvisionedThroughputExceededException\u003c/code\u003e errors.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe company must resolve this issue to ensure that the read data calls to the stream consistently succeed and that their real-time data processing is not disrupted.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich actions could be taken to resolve the error? (Select TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams\u003c/strong\u003e is a real-time data streaming service offered by Amazon Web Services (AWS). It enables the collection, processing, and analysis of large streams of data records in real-time. A Kinesis data stream is a collection of data records that are continually and sequentially added to over time, much like a stream. Each data record in the stream is composed of a sequence number, a partition key, and a data blob, which is an immutable sequence of bytes. It is capable of handling real-time data from hundreds of thousands of sources at a massive scale. It’s commonly used for scenarios such as collecting and processing log and event data, real-time analytics, and real-time metrics and reporting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c59e1d966f1ed91fe21ba9dcf469b48032c30e88b1c7421ac56a648947158db5.png\" alt=\"\"\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams uses shards, which are essentially independent streams of data within a Kinesis data stream. Each shard has a fixed rate of data it can handle — 1 MB/sec data input and 2 MB/sec data output. If the data rate exceeds the capacity of the current shards, you will receive a\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eProvisionedThroughputExceededException\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;error. Therefore, increasing the number of shards in your data stream can increase the throughput of your data stream and resolve this error. This is a process known as resharding, which involves splitting or merging shards to adjust the total capacity of the stream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen a\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eProvisionedThroughputExceededException\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;error occurs, it means that the application is reading data too quickly from the stream. Implementing a backoff strategy, where the application waits for a short period before trying the request again, can help manage the rate of data retrieval and avoid exceeding the read-throughput limit. This is a common strategy for handling rate-limited APIs and services, and it’s particularly important when dealing with real-time data processing workloads where maintaining a steady flow of data is crucial.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e-\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the shard count of the Kinesis data stream.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e-\u0026nbsp;Implement exponential backoff and retry logic in the application.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eActivate the enhanced monitoring feature for the stream\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because while enhanced monitoring provides additional metrics for the stream, it does not directly resolve the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eProvisionedThroughputExceededException\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;error. This error is related to the stream’s capacity, not its monitoring.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable the enhanced fan-out functionality on the stream\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;While enhanced fan-out can improve the consumer-side experience by providing dedicated throughput per consumer, it does not directly address the overall capacity quotas of the data stream. It\u0027s more about optimizing the consumption pattern rather than increasing the stream\u0027s capacity to accept writes or aggregate reads without exceeding provisioned throughput.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse a larger EC2 instance type for the Kinesis Data Streams application\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because the size of the EC2 instance does not directly affect the capacity of the Kinesis data stream. The\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eProvisionedThroughputExceededException\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;error is related to the stream’s capacity, not the size of the EC2 instance running the application.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/kinesis-using-sdk-java-resharding.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/kinesis-using-sdk-java-resharding.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/troubleshooting-consumers.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/troubleshooting-consumers.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Kinesis Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-35":{"id":"p:2-35","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 35","correct":[1,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eImplement ElastiCache in front of the RDS DB instance to reduce the CPU load on the RDS instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eActivate Enhanced Monitoring to view CPU utilization information for the RDS MySQL DB instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable RDS Performance Insights and review the appropriate dashboard to visualize the database load and filter the load by waits, SQL statements, hosts, or users\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena to analyze the SQL statements being run on the RDS instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eActivate Amazon CloudWatch Events and review the event data that has the SQL statements behind the CPU spikes\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Thêm cache có thể giảm tải nhưng chưa cho biết truy vấn hoặc tiến trình nào gây CPU tăng. Đề đang yêu cầu điều tra nguyên nhân.","Enhanced Monitoring cung cấp số liệu hệ điều hành chi tiết hơn để quan sát CPU và tiến trình, bổ sung metric CloudWatch cơ bản.","Theo bộ đề, Performance Insights phân tích tải database theo waits, SQL, host và user để tìm tác nhân gây tải. Đây là công cụ chẩn đoán chứ không chỉ giảm tải tạm thời.","Athena không tự quan sát các SQL đang chạy trong RDS và mối liên hệ với CPU. Cần công cụ giám sát database/OS.","EventBridge/CloudWatch Events không tự cung cấp danh sách SQL gây CPU tăng. Sự kiện vận hành khác với thông tin phân tích tải truy vấn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team at an e-commerce company is doing a root-cause analysis for a recent spike in CPU utilization for an RDS MySQL DB instance that caused the application to perform poorly. The standard metrics available in Amazon CloudWatch are not enough to guide the investigation. The company has hired you as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to determine what caused the CPU spike.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following steps would you recommend to provide more details about the underlying processes and queries resulting in an increase in the CPU load? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:45":{"id":"e:2:45","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 45","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eHost a custom fine-tuned large language model on Amazon SageMaker AI, train the model on the institute\u0027s course books, and generate feedback with section references\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain for keyword search across course materials, run Amazon Comprehend entity detection on each essay, pass matching textbook paragraphs to an Amazon Bedrock model, and use custom prompt templates to generate multilingual feedback and manually formatted citations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base from the institute\u0027s course materials and configure a multilingual foundation model to generate feedback with source citations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Comprehend to identify the essay language and topics, use Amazon Kendra to search course materials, and use an AWS Lambda function to assemble feedback with references\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA language learning institute is building an AI tutoring application that reviews student writing assignments. The application must provide individualized feedback that is grounded in the institute\u0027s approved course books and study guides. The application must support submissions in several languages, return feedback in the learner\u0027s selected language, and include citations that point to the relevant sections of the course materials.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base from the institute\u0027s course materials and configure a multilingual foundation model to generate feedback with source citations\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases provides a managed retrieval augmented generation capability. The institute can ingest approved course books and study guides into a knowledge base, and the application can retrieve the most relevant source chunks when a student submits an essay.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe RetrieveAndGenerate capability combines retrieval from the knowledge base with foundation model response generation. It can return generated responses and citations that reference the retrieved source chunks. This helps the application ground feedback in the institute\u0027s approved materials instead of relying only on the model\u0027s general knowledge.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational overhead because Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases manages the ingestion, chunking, embedding, storage integration, retrieval workflow, and citation support. The institute can select an appropriate multilingual foundation model and instruct it to respond in the student\u0027s selected language without building and maintaining a custom vector database, custom retrieval layer, or fine-tuned model hosting platform.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5c2139f8c6837374412c2ab12862b33f8a5d32d004af82277e99a696ab31d76e.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5c2139f8c6837374412c2ab12862b33f8a5d32d004af82277e99a696ab31d76e.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/kb-how-it-works.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/kb-how-it-works.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Comprehend to identify the essay language and topics, use Amazon Kendra to search course materials, and use an AWS Lambda function to assemble feedback with references\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Comprehend can detect language, extract key phrases, and identify entities or sentiment. Amazon Kendra can search enterprise content and return relevant documents or passages.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option requires the institute to build the feedback generation and citation formatting workflow with Lambda. It does not provide the same managed RAG workflow and integrated generated-response citations that Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases provides.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHost a custom fine-tuned large language model on Amazon SageMaker AI, train the model on the institute\u0027s course books, and generate feedback with section references\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon SageMaker AI can host custom machine learning models and can support model training and fine-tuning workflows. However, fine-tuning a model on textbooks does not automatically provide reliable citations to specific textbook sections in generated responses.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach also adds significant operational overhead. The institute would need to manage model training data, fine-tuning jobs, hosting endpoints, scaling, evaluation, and citation generation logic instead of using a managed knowledge base with retrieval and citations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain for keyword search across course materials, run Amazon Comprehend entity detection on each essay, pass matching textbook paragraphs to an Amazon Bedrock model, and use custom prompt templates to generate multilingual feedback and manually formatted citations\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon OpenSearch Service can support search use cases, and Amazon Comprehend can identify useful signals from essay text. This architecture could help retrieve candidate textbook passages.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the institute would still need to build custom retrieval orchestration, prompt construction, multilingual response handling, and citation formatting. This makes the solution more operationally complex than using Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases with native retrieval and citation support.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/knowledge-base.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/knowledge-base.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/kb-how-it-works.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/kb-how-it-works.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/kb-how-retrieval.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/kb-how-retrieval.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/kb-test-retrieve-generate.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/kb-test-retrieve-generate.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/build-a-contextual-chatbot-application-using-knowledge-bases-for-amazon-bedrock/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/build-a-contextual-chatbot-application-using-knowledge-bases-for-amazon-bedrock/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/evaluate-and-improve-performance-of-amazon-bedrock-knowledge-bases/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/evaluate-and-improve-performance-of-amazon-bedrock-knowledge-bases/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-52":{"id":"p:3-52","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 52","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to process the data streams as well as decouple the producers and consumers for the real-time data processor\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["SQS phân phối thông điệp để xử lý công việc. Không cung cấp mô hình nhiều consumer độc lập đọc/phát lại cùng luồng theo nhu cầu này.","SNS fan-out thông báo, không phải kho stream có vị trí đọc và retention để xây ứng dụng phân tích tùy chỉnh.","Kinesis Data Streams giữ luồng và cho consumer tự xử lý theo logic riêng. Tách producer khỏi consumer, hỗ trợ đọc lại trong thời gian lưu.","Firehose ưu tiên biến đổi/giao dữ liệu tới các đích được hỗ trợ. Không phải giao diện luồng linh hoạt cho nhiều consumer tùy chỉnh như Kinesis Data Streams."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has hired you to help with redesigning a real-time data processor. The company wants to build custom applications that process and analyze the streaming data for its specialized needs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will you recommend to address this use-case?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-49":{"id":"p:4-49","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 49","correct":[0,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Spark UI and AWS Glue metrics to inspect executor-level data skew, shuffle spill, and task distribution\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the worker type to a larger AWS Glue worker family to vertically scale disk and memory for skewed tasks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable the \u003ccode\u003e--write-shuffle-files-to-s3\u003c/code\u003e job parameter and apply salting to distribute skewed keys more evenly\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the number of AWS Glue workers horizontally so Spark automatically removes data skew from all shuffle stages\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRewrite the AWS Glue job as a Python shell job and process the dataset in a single-threaded script to avoid Spark shuffle spill\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Spark UI và Glue metrics giúp xác định executor nào tràn đĩa, lượng shuffle spill và phân bố task. Đây là bước chẩn đoán để phân biệt thiếu tài nguyên chung với một số khóa gây lệch tải.","Worker lớn có thêm RAM và đĩa, nhưng một khóa quá lớn vẫn có thể dồn dữ liệu vào một task. Nâng máy không xử lý gốc data skew và có thể tăng chi phí.","Ghi shuffle lên S3 giảm áp lực đĩa cục bộ; salting chia một khóa lớn thành các nhóm nhỏ để phân phối tải. Cần cấu hình hỗ trợ S3 shuffle tương ứng phiên bản Glue và xử lý gộp kết quả đúng sau khi thêm salt.","Thêm worker không tự chia lại dữ liệu của cùng một khóa lớn. Data skew vẫn có thể dồn tải vào ít task dù tổng số worker tăng.","Chuyển tập dữ liệu lớn sang script đơn luồng làm mất khả năng xử lý phân tán. Nó không phải giải pháp hiệu quả cho join và aggregation lớn đang cần Spark."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer maintains an AWS Glue Apache Spark ETL job that transforms a large set of marketplace transaction records. The job occasionally fails during wide transformations such as joins and aggregations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe error message says, \"No space left on device.\" The data engineer needs to identify whether data skew or excessive shuffle spill is causing the issue and implement a cost-effective fix that reduces local disk pressure during the job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of steps will meet this requirement MOST cost-effectively? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-61":{"id":"p:2-61","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 61","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon EFS to provide a fast, cost-effective, and sharable storage service\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class to optimize the Amazon S3 storage costs\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access, to reduce the costs on Amazon S3 storage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class to reduce the costs on Amazon S3 storage\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Chuyển sang EFS thay đổi mô hình lưu trữ và truy cập, không phải cách tối ưu đơn giản cho object S3 hiện có.","Intelligent-Tiering tự điều chỉnh tầng truy cập theo cách sử dụng, giảm việc phải duy trì nhiều quy tắc Lifecycle thủ công. Chọn tầng truy cập phù hợp yêu cầu độ trễ.","One Zone-IA giảm dự phòng đa AZ. Không phải lựa chọn ưu tiên cho dữ liệu nhạy cảm, thiết yếu nếu không có điều kiện cho phép mất và tạo lại.","Deep Archive có thời gian khôi phục dài, không đáp ứng yêu cầu truy cập độ trễ thấp."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses Amazon S3 buckets to store sensitive customer data that is business-critical. The data is kept segregated and well organized to ensure low latency. Recently, the data engineering team noticed that the lifecycle policies on the Amazon S3 buckets have not been applied optimally, resulting in higher costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCan you recommend a solution to reduce storage costs on Amazon S3 while keeping the team\u0027s involvement to a minimum?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:37":{"id":"e:5:37","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 37","correct":[1],"answers":["Leverage AWS Glue ETL jobs to write custom scripts for checking and enforcing the data quality constraints on the dataset.","Use AWS Glue DataBrew to create a ruleset that defines data quality rules for the specific requirements of the \"Stock Quantity\" and \"Product Category\" columns.","Implement AWS Lambda functions to trigger on data updates, validating and correcting the data as per the specified rules.","Utilize AWS Glue DataBrew recipes for data transformation, adding steps to validate and rectify data quality issues in the dataset."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team in a retail company needs to ensure the consistency and accuracy of its product inventory data. They must validate that the “Stock Quantity” column always contains positive integer values and the “Product Category” column should not have any null or empty values.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich AWS service and method would be the most effective for implementing these data quality checks with minimal operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew offers a streamlined solution for data quality management, particularly beneficial in scenarios requiring precise and automated data validation. Its ability to define specific data quality rules within a ruleset makes it an optimal choice for scenarios like ensuring inventory data accuracy. In the context of the given scenario, DataBrew can effectively validate that the “Stock Quantity” remains a positive integer and that the “Product Category” column is free of null or empty values. This functionality is crucial for maintaining the integrity of inventory data in retail, where accurate stock monitoring and categorization are vital.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/57f8b074ca6b1eaa25d44fc27f3d4d3975d99cd3fc2c5e791ef4f4037d92c65e.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to create a ruleset that defines data quality rules for the specific requirements of the “Stock Quantity” and “Product Category” columns.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage AWS Glue ETL jobs to write custom scripts for checking and enforcing the data quality constraints on the dataset \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. This approach, while feasible, demands more complex scripting and operational management, making it less efficient compared to the streamlined, rule-based system of DataBrew.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement AWS Lambda functions to trigger on data updates, validating and correcting the data as per the specified rules\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it involves additional development and operational complexities. This method is not as straightforward for predefined data quality checks as using DataBrew’s ruleset feature.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize AWS Glue DataBrew recipes for data transformation, adding steps to validate and rectify data quality issues in the dataset\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Recipes in DataBrew are more suited for data transformation tasks and may not offer the direct and focused approach of a ruleset for specific data quality validations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eResources:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-rules.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-rules.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-42":{"id":"p:2-42","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 42","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lambda function for the transformation, configure S3 Event Notifications to invoke the function on object creation, and publish the output to an SNS topic with an email subscription\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun an ECS task that polls the S3 bucket for new report files, executes the transformation code on a schedule, and sends email messages through operating system commands\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Glue job that runs every hour, scans the S3 bucket for new report files, transforms matching files, and writes email-ready output to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDeploy an EMR cluster with EMRFS to read the S3 files, schedule transformation steps, write output to another S3 bucket, and configure S3 notifications to an SNS topic\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["S3 gọi Lambda khi có tệp mới; xử lý dưới 45 giây phù hợp Lambda. Sau đó publish kết quả phù hợp giới hạn thông điệp tới SNS có đăng ký email.","Polling bằng ECS thêm container, lịch chạy và cách gửi mail cần quản lý. Sự kiện S3 đã đủ để kích hoạt công việc nhỏ.","Chạy Glue mỗi giờ không phản hồi ngay khi tệp xuất hiện và chưa gửi kết quả email. Nặng hơn Lambda cho tác vụ nhỏ.","Dùng cả cụm EMR cho mỗi tệp xử lý rất nhanh tạo chi phí và vận hành không cần thiết. Thông báo S3 cũng chỉ báo sự kiện, không tự chứa kết quả biến đổi."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer needs to run a lightweight transformation whenever a user uploads a report file to an Amazon S3 bucket. Each transformation completes in less than 45 seconds. After the transformation finishes, the data engineer must receive the output in an email message. Users upload approximately one report file every hour throughout the day.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements in the MOST operationally efficient way?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:4":{"id":"e:4:4","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 4","correct":[0,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eYou can only use DynamoDB for checkpointing KCL\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) can also be used for checkpointing KCL\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMultiple KCL applications can share a DynamoDB table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEach KCL application must use its own DynamoDB table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMultiple KCL applications can share a DynamoDB table if Amazon Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) is configured to save transit data and metadata\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company runs a real-time data processing application that uses Kinesis Client Library (KCL) to help consume and process data from the real-time data streams. The development team has raised a query on the viability of using the same DynamoDB table for different KCL applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following are correct statements for KCL while consuming Kinesis Data Streams? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEach KCL application must use its own DynamoDB table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUsers can\u0027t use different KCL applications with the same DynamoDB table for the following reasons:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cp\u003eScan operations are used to obtain leases from a DynamoDB table. Therefore, if a table contains leases of different KCL applications, each application could receive a lease that isn\u0027t related to the application itself.\u003c/p\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cp\u003eShard IDs in streams are used as primary keys in DynamoDB tables during checkpointing. When different KCL applications use the same DynamoDB table and the same shard IDs are used in the streams, inconsistencies in checkpoints can occur.\u003c/p\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eYou can only use DynamoDB for checkpointing KCL\u003c/strong\u003e - Users can only use DynamoDB as a checkpointing table for the KCL. A DynamoDB table is required as a checkpointing table for the KCL because the KCL behavior and implementation are interconnected with DynamoDB in the following ways:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe KCL includes \u003ccode\u003eShardSyncTask.java\u003c/code\u003e, which guarantees that shard leases in a stream are included in the DynamoDB table. This check is conducted periodically in the KCL.\u003c/p\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe KCL includes \u003ccode\u003eDynamoDBLeaseTaker.java\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003eDynamoDBLeaseRenewer.java\u003c/code\u003e, which are components that manage and update leases in the KCL. \u003ccode\u003eDynamoDBLeaseTaker.java\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003eDynamoDBLeaseRenewer.java\u003c/code\u003e work with \u003ccode\u003eDynamoDBLeaseRefresher.java\u003c/code\u003e to make frequent API requests to DynamoDB.\u003c/p\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cp\u003eWhen the KCL makes checkpoints, requests from \u003ccode\u003eDynamoDBCheckpointer.java\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003eDynamoDBLeaseCoordinator.java\u003c/code\u003e are made to DynamoDB.\u003c/p\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMultiple KCL applications can share a DynamoDB table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) can also be used for checkpointing KCL\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMultiple KCL applications can share a DynamoDB table if Amazon Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) is configured to save transit data and metadata\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThese three options are incorrect. Each KCL application must use its own DynamoDB table and only DynamoDB can be used for checkpointing KCL.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/kinesis-kcl-apps-dynamodb-table/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/kinesis-kcl-apps-dynamodb-table/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:48":{"id":"e:3:48","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 48","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Versioning and Cross-Region Replication on the buckets, configure RDS automated backups, and copy additional RDS snapshots to a second Region for long-term retention\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Backup plan with backup schedules, windows, lifecycle values, and a backup vault, assign the S3 and RDS resources, and configure AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate EventBridge schedules that invoke Lambda functions to copy S3 objects and create RDS snapshots, then use IAM deny policies to prevent backup deletion during retention periods\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Versioning and Same-Region Replication on the buckets, configure RDS automated backups, and create manual DB snapshots for retention periods that exceed automated backup limits\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare company stores audit data in Amazon S3 buckets and patient application data in Amazon RDS DB instances. The security team must apply technical controls that create backups for both services and retain the backups for required retention periods. The backups must be protected from accidental or malicious deletion until the required retention periods expire.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Backup plan with backup schedules, windows, lifecycle values, and a backup vault, assign the S3 and RDS resources, and configure AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Backup provides a centralized way to create and manage backups across supported AWS services, including Amazon S3 and Amazon RDS. A backup plan can define backup schedules, backup windows, lifecycle settings, and the backup vault where recovery points are stored.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode helps protect recovery points from deletion before the configured retention period expires. This is important when the company must defend backups against accidental deletion, malicious activity, or overly broad administrator permissions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational overhead because it uses one managed backup control plane for both S3 and RDS. The company does not need to separately manage S3 version protection, RDS snapshot workflows, custom Lambda functions, replication rules, and retention enforcement logic for each service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/eb705e41f09e44a79c27f959c7f0b830aee49d92385b8bf9d44942d69ec761fc.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/eb705e41f09e44a79c27f959c7f0b830aee49d92385b8bf9d44942d69ec761fc.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/protecting-data-with-aws-backup-vault-lock/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/protecting-data-with-aws-backup-vault-lock/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate EventBridge schedules that invoke Lambda functions to copy S3 objects and create RDS snapshots, then use IAM deny policies to prevent backup deletion during retention periods\u003c/strong\u003e - EventBridge and Lambda can automate custom backup workflows. IAM deny policies can reduce the risk that some principals delete backup resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this approach requires custom code, scheduling, error handling, monitoring, and ongoing maintenance. IAM policies alone are not as strong as AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode for retention-period deletion protection.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable S3 Versioning and Same-Region Replication on the buckets, configure RDS automated backups, and create manual DB snapshots for retention periods that exceed automated backup limits\u003c/strong\u003e - Same-Region Replication can copy S3 objects within the same Region and can help support resilience or data duplication needs. RDS automated backups and manual snapshots can provide database recovery points.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, replication is not a complete backup governance solution. This option still lacks centralized backup scheduling, lifecycle management, and Vault Lock compliance controls across S3 and RDS backups.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable S3 Versioning and Cross-Region Replication on the buckets, configure RDS automated backups, and copy additional RDS snapshots to a second Region for long-term retention\u003c/strong\u003e - Cross-Region Replication can improve resilience for S3 objects, and copied RDS snapshots can support recovery in another Region. This can be useful for disaster recovery requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the scenario asks for backup generation, retention, and deletion protection with the least operational overhead. Managing replication and copied snapshots separately adds complexity and does not provide the same centralized immutable backup enforcement as AWS Backup Vault Lock.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/whatisbackup.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/whatisbackup.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/about-backup-plans.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/about-backup-plans.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/s3-backups.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/s3-backups.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/rds-backup.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/rds-backup.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/vault-lock.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/vault-lock.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/protecting-data-with-aws-backup-vault-lock/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/protecting-data-with-aws-backup-vault-lock/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-43":{"id":"p:1-43","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 43","correct":[0,1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering =\u0026gt; Amazon S3 Standard\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 One Zone-IA =\u0026gt; Amazon S3 Standard-IA\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Standard =\u0026gt; Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Standard-IA =\u0026gt; Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Standard-IA =\u0026gt; Amazon S3 One Zone-IA\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Câu hỏi yêu cầu chọn chuyển lớp KHÔNG hợp lệ. Lifecycle không chuyển từ Intelligent-Tiering ngược về Standard theo đường chuyển lớp được nêu trong đề.","Chuyển One Zone-IA sang Standard-IA không phải đường Lifecycle được hỗ trợ trong sơ đồ chuyển lớp của đề. Vì đang tìm phương án không hợp lệ nên phải chọn.","Standard có thể chuyển sang Intelligent-Tiering bằng Lifecycle. Đây là đường hợp lệ nên không chọn ở câu hỏi tìm trường hợp không hợp lệ.","Standard-IA có thể chuyển sang Intelligent-Tiering. Vì đây là chuyển lớp hợp lệ nên không phải đáp án cần chọn.","Standard-IA có thể chuyển sang One Zone-IA bằng Lifecycle. Lựa chọn này hợp lệ, trong khi đề hỏi các chuyển lớp không hợp lệ."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe IT department at a company is conducting a training workshop for new data engineers. As part of an evaluation exercise on Amazon S3, the new data engineers were asked to identify the invalid storage class lifecycle transitions for objects stored on Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCan you identify the INVALID lifecycle transitions from the options below? (Select two) ?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-33":{"id":"p:1-33","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 33","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eFor both the S3 paths s3://INPUT-FOLDER1 and s3://INPUT-FOLDER2, the crawler creates two tables each, each table having columns of one schema respectively\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFor the S3 path s3://INPUT-FOLDER1, the crawler creates one table with columns of both the schemas. For the S3 path s3://INPUT-FOLDER2, the crawler creates two tables, each table having columns of one schema respectively\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFor both the S3 paths s3://INPUT-FOLDER1 and s3://INPUT-FOLDER2, the crawler creates one table with columns of both the schemas\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFor S3 path s3://INPUT-FOLDER2, the crawler creates one table with columns of both the schemas. And for S3 path s3://INPUT-FOLDER1, the crawler creates two tables, each table having columns of one schema respectively\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Theo hành vi crawler được mô tả trong lời giải, thư mục thứ nhất được gộp thành một bảng. Do đó kết luận cả hai thư mục đều tạo hai bảng không đúng đáp án bộ đề.","Lời giải của đề xác định thư mục có tỷ lệ 8/2 tạo một bảng gộp cột, còn thư mục 7/3 tạo hai bảng. Đây là kết quả theo cấu hình và quy tắc gom schema mà đề giả định.","Thư mục thứ hai được bộ đề xác định là tạo hai bảng riêng, không gộp một bảng. Cần phân biệt hai tỷ lệ schema thay vì kết luận giống nhau.","Phương án đảo ngược kết quả: đáp án bộ đề là thư mục 1 một bảng, thư mục 2 hai bảng. Không phải chiều ngược lại."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eConsider the following scenario on Amazon S3: A folder INPUT-FOLDER1 has 10 files, 8 files with schema SCH_A and 2 files with schema SCH_B, and another folder INPUT-FOLDER2 has 10 files, 7 files with the schema SCH_A and 3 files with the schema SCH_B. The schemas are defined as follows:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSCH_A:\n{ \"id\": 1, \"first_name\": \"John\", \"last_name\": \"Doe\"}\n{ \"id\": 2, \"first_name\": \"Li\", \"last_name\": \"Juan\"}\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSCH_B:\n{\"city\":\"Dublin\",\"country\":\"Ireland\"}\n{\"city\":\"Paris\",\"country\":\"France\"}\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is the outcome, when the crawler crawls the Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) path s3://INPUT-FOLDER1 and s3://INPUT-FOLDER2 separately?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-23":{"id":"p:3-23","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 23","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a backup process to persist all the data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive in the Production Region. Set up cross-Region replication of this data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive in the DR Region to ensure minimum possible costs in both Regions\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a backup process to persist all the data to an S3 bucket A using the S3 standard storage class in the Production Region. Set up cross-Region replication of this S3 bucket A to an S3 bucket B using S3 standard storage class in the DR Region and set up a lifecycle policy in the DR Region to immediately move this data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a backup process to persist all the data to an S3 bucket A using the S3 standard storage class in the Production Region. Set up cross-Region replication of this S3 bucket A to an S3 bucket B using S3 standard-IA storage class in the DR Region and set up a lifecycle policy in the DR Region to immediately move this data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a backup process to persist all the data to a large Amazon EBS volume attached to the backup server in the Production Region. Run nightly cron jobs to snapshot these volumes and then copy these snapshots to the DR Region\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Theo cơ chế trong bộ đề, không thể dùng dữ liệu đã vào Deep Archive ở nguồn như một đường replication trực tiếp đơn giản. Phải thiết lập luồng sao chép phù hợp trước bước chuyển lớp.","Ghi bản backup vào S3 Standard ở vùng chính, replication sang bucket vùng DR, rồi Lifecycle chuyển bản DR sang Deep Archive. Giữ hai bản ở hai Region với chi phí lưu lâu dài thấp.","Standard-IA có mức tính phí lưu tối thiểu 30 ngày. Chuyển ngay tiếp sang Deep Archive tạo phí không cần thiết so với đi qua Standard.","EBS gắn với máy chủ sao lưu và cron snapshot tạo nhiều công quản lý. Không kinh tế và trực tiếp bằng S3 replication/Lifecycle theo đề."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA digital media company has hired you to improve the data backup solution for applications running on the AWS Cloud. Currently, all of the applications running on AWS use at least two Availability Zones (AZs). The updated backup policy at the company mandates that all nightly backups for its data are durably stored in at least two geographically distinct Regions for Production and Disaster Recovery (DR) and the backup processes for both Regions must be fully automated. The new backup solution must ensure that the backup is available to be restored immediately for the Production Region and should be restored within 24 hours in the DR Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents the MOST cost-effective solution that will address the given use-case?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:8":{"id":"e:2:8","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 8","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to delete outdated records from the sales_orders table and insert the latest records from the daily_updates table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon Redshift stored procedure that deletes matching stale records from the sales_orders table and inserts the latest records from the daily_updates staging table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena federated queries to read outdated records from the Amazon Redshift sales_orders table, delete those records, and insert the latest rows from the daily_updates table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon Redshift materialized view that joins the sales_orders table with the daily_updates table and configure downstream applications to query the materialized view instead of refreshing the base table\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn online grocery company stores same-day purchase transactions in Amazon Redshift. The company has a sales_orders table that contains the existing order records. The company also has a daily_updates staging table that contains newly created orders and changed order records from the last processing window.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company must remove outdated records from the sales_orders table and load the latest versions from the daily_updates table. Several reporting applications query the sales_orders table throughout the day and must display current order information.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon Redshift stored procedure that deletes matching stale records from the sales_orders table and inserts the latest records from the daily_updates staging table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift stored procedures can encapsulate SQL logic that runs inside the database. A stored procedure can include data manipulation language operations such as DELETE and INSERT, which makes it suitable for refreshing an existing Redshift table from a staging table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe stored procedure can delete rows from the sales_orders table that match records in the daily_updates staging table. After the delete step completes, the procedure can insert the latest records from the staging table into the sales_orders table. This pattern keeps the downstream applications pointed to the same sales_orders table while ensuring that the table contains current records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution also keeps the data movement inside Amazon Redshift. The company does not need to unload data to Amazon S3, use Athena for table maintenance, or introduce external processing services for a standard Redshift staging-table update pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2bfa6157468dab4ea93ed3ba2adede0aa2d87bd5c38fa82d32b9cf798691ad6b.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2bfa6157468dab4ea93ed3ba2adede0aa2d87bd5c38fa82d32b9cf798691ad6b.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/bringing-your-stored-procedures-to-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/bringing-your-stored-procedures-to-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena federated queries to read outdated records from the Amazon Redshift sales_orders table, delete those records, and insert the latest rows from the daily_updates table\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Athena federated queries allow Athena to query data from sources other than Amazon S3 through data source connectors. However, Athena federated queries are not the right mechanism to perform transactional maintenance on an Amazon Redshift table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option adds an unnecessary query layer outside Redshift. The requirement is to update a Redshift table from a Redshift staging table, and Redshift can perform the delete and insert operations directly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to delete outdated records from the sales_orders table and insert the latest records from the daily_updates table\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift Spectrum allows Redshift to query external data in Amazon S3. It is useful when users need to analyze data in a data lake without loading all the data into Redshift tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis requirement involves two Redshift tables and requires changes to the managed sales_orders table. Redshift Spectrum does not provide the appropriate mechanism to delete stale rows from an internal Redshift table and insert current rows from a staging table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon Redshift materialized view that joins the sales_orders table with the daily_updates table and configure downstream applications to query the materialized view instead of refreshing the base table\u003c/strong\u003e - A materialized view can improve query performance for precomputed query results. However, this option does not remove stale rows from the sales_orders table or insert the latest rows into the table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe requirement specifically states that downstream applications need the sales_orders table to display up-to-date information. Redirecting applications to a materialized view changes the access pattern and leaves the base table unresolved.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_updating-inserting-using-staging-tables-.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_updating-inserting-using-staging-tables-.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/stored-procedure-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/stored-procedure-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_CREATE_PROCEDURE.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_CREATE_PROCEDURE.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/bringing-your-stored-procedures-to-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/bringing-your-stored-procedures-to-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:8":{"id":"e:1:8","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 8","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a CloudWatch Logs data protection policy that uses managed data identifiers for PII and attach the policy to the CloudWatch Logs log group\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun an Amazon Macie sensitive data discovery job to identify PII and configure Macie to redact matching values from the CloudWatch Logs log group\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Lambda subscription filter on the CloudWatch Logs log group to read every log event, detect PII with custom regular expressions, replace the sensitive values, and write the sanitized events to a separate log group\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCall AWS Glue sensitive data detection APIs from the ETL job and use the detected entities to mask values before writing log entries\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company runs an AWS Glue ETL job in the ap-southeast-2 Region to process patient appointment records. The job writes operational messages and error details to an Amazon CloudWatch Logs log group. Some log entries can contain personally identifiable information (PII), and a data engineer must mask the PII in the log group without modifying the ETL application code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a CloudWatch Logs data protection policy that uses managed data identifiers for PII and attach the policy to the CloudWatch Logs log group\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCloudWatch Logs data protection policies provide a native way to help protect sensitive log data. A data protection policy can use managed data identifiers to detect categories of sensitive information, including PII, and can apply a de-identify operation to mask matching values in log events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution applies directly to the CloudWatch Logs log group where the AWS Glue job writes logs. The data engineer does not need to change the AWS Glue script, add custom log processing code, or move logs to another service before masking can occur. The policy can protect sensitive values in the log events that users view in CloudWatch Logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis is the best solution because the requirement focuses on masking PII in a CloudWatch Logs log group. CloudWatch Logs data protection policies are purpose-built for this scenario and provide a managed control for sensitive data detection and masking at the log group level.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/08e6d4244aadb50c1d6edc2608c05ae4d2b1b48ba305f25dab76a744b2fef32c.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/08e6d4244aadb50c1d6edc2608c05ae4d2b1b48ba305f25dab76a744b2fef32c.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/protect-sensitive-data-with-amazon-cloudwatch-logs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/protect-sensitive-data-with-amazon-cloudwatch-logs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/016b411f4e683694bb251213487827a5ac1e8756244ab2ab149761ed59d41142.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/016b411f4e683694bb251213487827a5ac1e8756244ab2ab149761ed59d41142.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia -\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/protect-sensitive-data-with-amazon-cloudwatch-logs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/protect-sensitive-data-with-amazon-cloudwatch-logs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun an Amazon Macie sensitive data discovery job to identify PII and configure Macie to redact matching values from the CloudWatch Logs log group\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Macie discovers sensitive data in Amazon S3 buckets. Macie is useful for identifying sensitive data stored in S3 objects, generating findings, and supporting data security workflows for S3-based data lakes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eMacie does not attach to a CloudWatch Logs log group to mask PII in log events. This option uses the wrong service for the target location because the requirement is to mask sensitive values in CloudWatch Logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Lambda subscription filter on the CloudWatch Logs log group to read every log event, detect PII with custom regular expressions, replace the sensitive values, and write the sanitized events to a separate log group\u003c/strong\u003e - A Lambda subscription filter could process log events and write sanitized copies to another destination. However, this approach requires custom code, custom detection patterns, error handling, deployment management, and ongoing maintenance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also does not directly mask the original CloudWatch Logs log group where the sensitive events are written. It creates a second sanitized log stream or log group, which does not satisfy the requirement as directly as attaching a data protection policy to the original log group.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCall AWS Glue sensitive data detection APIs from the ETL job and use the detected entities to mask values before writing log entries\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue sensitive data detection capabilities can help detect sensitive data during data processing workflows. However, adding detection logic inside the ETL job requires application changes and places the responsibility for masking behavior inside the job code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe requirement asks for masking PII in the CloudWatch Logs log group. CloudWatch Logs data protection policies address that requirement directly, while custom ETL logic does not provide a centralized masking policy for the log group itself.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/mask-sensitive-log-data.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/mask-sensitive-log-data.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/mask-sensitive-log-data-start.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/mask-sensitive-log-data-start.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/cloudwatch-logs-data-protection-policies.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/cloudwatch-logs-data-protection-policies.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/protect-sensitive-log-data-types-pii.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/protect-sensitive-log-data-types-pii.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/protect-sensitive-data-with-amazon-cloudwatch-logs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/protect-sensitive-data-with-amazon-cloudwatch-logs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/how-amazon-cloudwatch-logs-data-protection-can-help-detect-and-protect-sensitive-log-data/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/how-amazon-cloudwatch-logs-data-protection-can-help-detect-and-protect-sensitive-log-data/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:10:7":{"id":"e:10:7","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 7","correct":[0,2],"answers":["Arrange the data within the S3 bucket into partitions by year, month, and day.","Enhance the Glue Jobs query performance by changing the execution class to Flex.","Modify the Glue Job worker type configuration to a larger instance type.","Modify the storage class of the S3 bucket to S3 Express One Zone.","Incorporate the DynamicFrame schema class within your Apache Spark-based Glue Jobs."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAn organization collects daily user interaction and app performance logs from its mobile app. Initially, raw logs are transformed by AWS Glue for Spark Jobs and then stored in an Amazon S3 bucket.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCurrently, the data in the S3 bucket is stored without any partitioning, causing the Glue jobs to scan the entire dataset during each run.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eThe company’s Data Engineer has noticed a gradual slowdown in the Amazon QuickSight Dashboard’s query performance over time. Upon investigation, the engineer determined that the long duration of the Glue Jobs is the root cause.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhat combination of actions should the Data Engineer consider to improve query performance? (Select TWO.)\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e is a serverless data integration service that makes it easy for analytics users to discover, prepare, move, and integrate data from multiple sources. With AWS Glue, you can discover and connect to more than 70 diverse data sources and manage your data in a centralized data catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou define jobs in AWS Glue to accomplish the work that’s required to extract, transform, and load (ETL) data from a data source to a data target.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can improve performance just by reducing the amount of data loaded into your Spark cluster for each data source. Consider loading only the data that you need.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo reduce the amount of data loaded into your job when reading from Amazon S3, consider \u003cstrong\u003efile size, compression, file format, and file layout (partitions)\u003c/strong\u003e for your dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Keep the file size of inputs and outputs within a moderate range (for example, 128 MB). Files that are too small or too large can cause issues.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– If your S3 objects are in the hundreds of megabytes, consider compressing them.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Use a columnar format. Apache Parquet and Apache ORC are popular columnar data formats. Parquet and ORC store data efficiently by employing column-based compression, encoding and compressing each column based on its data type.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– By storing your S3 data to objects in different paths based on how the data will be used, you can efficiently retrieve relevant data. In this scenario, the data is currently stored without partitions, causing AWS Glue jobs to scan the entire dataset on each run. By partitioning your data, you can restrict the amount of data scanned by each downstream analytics application, improving performance and reducing cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/394ded2aeb4ef21b987ebae45e83c71dd30006921aa73c0d7320d759abb58172.png\" alt=\"Work Type instance\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIf your job is taking too much time, but executors are consuming sufficient resources, and Spark is creating a large volume of tasks relative to available cores, consider scaling cluster capacity. You can scale cluster capacity by using the following options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Enable AWS Glue Auto Scaling\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Scale-out: Increase the number of AWS Glue workers\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Scale up: Use a larger worker type\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Arrange the data within the S3 bucket into partitions by year, month, and day.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Modify the Glue Job worker type configuration to a larger instance type.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eModify the storage class of the S3 bucket to S3 Express One Zone\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Although using this storage class can improve latency, it will still not resolve the slow query performance. With this approach, you will deploy your Amazon S3 bucket in the same availability zone as your resources, which will improve the latency. However, it is not mentioned in the scenario if there is a latency issue. This will not resolve the slow performance issue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncorporate the DynamicFrame schema class within your Apache Spark-based Glue Jobs\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While this can potentially optimize the jobs’ performance, it is not guaranteed to solve the issue. DynamicFrames are only well-suited for working with semi-structured data formats. It can handle data that includes nested structures or varying schemas more efficiently than traditional data representations. However, the scenario did not mention the complexity of the data characteristics. If your data is already well-structured and does not contain nested structures or varying schemas, the benefits of DynamicFrames may be less pronounced. More is needed if the issue is due to resource constraints or loading a large volume of data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnhance the Glue Jobs query performance by changing the execution class to Flex\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because Flex execution is simply more suitable for non-urgent or non-time-sensitive data integration workloads, such as testing and one-time data loads. The jobs are running daily, making this solution unsuitable. With Flex, AWS Glue jobs run on spare compute capacity instead of dedicated hardware. The start and run times of jobs using Flex can vary. This solution is for optimizing cost, not for enhancing performance. It could even abruptly stop data processing when AWS takes back the resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/reduce-data-scan.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/reduce-data-scan.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/scale-cluster-capacity.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/scale-cluster-capacity.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:63":{"id":"e:3:63","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 63","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a backup process to persist all the data to a large Amazon EBS volume attached to the backup server in the Production Region. Run nightly cron jobs to snapshot these volumes and then copy these snapshots to the DR Region\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a backup process to persist all the data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive in the Production Region. Set up cross-Region replication of this data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive in the DR Region to ensure minimum possible costs in both Regions\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a backup process to persist all the data to an S3 bucket A using the S3 standard storage class in the Production Region. Set up cross-Region replication of this S3 bucket A to an S3 bucket B using S3 standard storage class in the DR Region and set up a lifecycle policy in the DR Region to immediately move this data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a backup process to persist all the data to an S3 bucket A using the S3 standard storage class in the Production Region. Set up cross-Region replication of this S3 bucket A to an S3 bucket B using S3 standard-IA storage class in the DR Region and set up a lifecycle policy in the DR Region to immediately move this data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA digital media company has hired you to improve the data backup solution for applications running on the AWS Cloud. Currently, all of the applications running on AWS use at least two Availability Zones (AZs). The updated backup policy at the company mandates that all nightly backups for its data are durably stored in at least two geographically distinct Regions for Production and Disaster Recovery (DR) and the backup processes for both Regions must be fully automated. The new backup solution must ensure that the backup is available to be restored immediately for the Production Region and should be restored within 24 hours in the DR Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents the MOST cost-effective solution that will address the given use-case?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a backup process to persist all the data to an S3 bucket A using the S3 standard storage class in the Production Region. Set up cross-Region replication of this S3 bucket A to an S3 bucket B using S3 standard storage class in the DR Region and set up a lifecycle policy in the DR Region to immediately move this data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eS3 Replication enables automatic, asynchronous copying of objects across Amazon S3 buckets. There are two types of Replications:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCross-Region Replication (CRR) is used to copy objects across Amazon S3 buckets in different AWS Regions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSame-Region replication (SRR) is used to copy objects across Amazon S3 buckets in the same AWS Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/174c54c5dd1c5ff4aeebb5fe39c61b33ecd95918d08e799619b0a9f770c60baf.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/174c54c5dd1c5ff4aeebb5fe39c61b33ecd95918d08e799619b0a9f770c60baf.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/replication.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/replication.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the given use-case, you can set up cross-Region replication from S3 bucket A using the S3 standard storage class in the production Region to S3 bucket B using the S3 standard storage class in the DR Region and further create a lifecycle policy to transition this data in bucket B from standard storage class to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePlease note the allowed transitions for the S3 Lifecycle Policy:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/444648786eb1f2dd1e8938b2f38de01be1af24785dcd6692fe4aeb1fa9373039.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/444648786eb1f2dd1e8938b2f38de01be1af24785dcd6692fe4aeb1fa9373039.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBy default, Amazon S3 stores object replicas using the same storage class as the source object. You can also specify a different storage class for the replicas. This allows you to use something like an S3 Standard-IA for the replica bucket, however, S3 standard IA has a minimum storage duration charge of 30 days thereby making it costlier than using S3 Standard storage class for the given scenario because the data would be moved to Glacier Deep Archive via a Lifecycle policy immediately.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/bfe699ba2ecabe1d5eb86180f5a9ee7a96d294807142f6cc727d5a04f2f080c9.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/bfe699ba2ecabe1d5eb86180f5a9ee7a96d294807142f6cc727d5a04f2f080c9.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a backup process to persist all the data to an S3 bucket A using the S3 standard storage class in the Production Region. Set up cross-Region replication of this S3 bucket A to an S3 bucket B using S3 standard-IA storage class in the DR Region and set up a lifecycle policy in the DR Region to immediately move this data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive\u003c/strong\u003e - As mentioned in the explanation above, S3 standard IA has a minimum storage duration charge of 30 days thereby making it costlier than using S3 Standard storage class for the given scenario, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a backup process to persist all the data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive in the Production Region. Set up cross-Region replication of this data to Amazon Glacier Deep Archive in the DR Region to ensure minimum possible costs in both Regions\u003c/strong\u003e - One of the key requirements of the given scenario is to ensure that the backup is available to be restored immediately for the Production Region. However, Glacier Deep Archive has a first-byte latency of hours while restoring data, hence this option is not correct for the given use-case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a backup process to persist all the data to a large Amazon EBS volume attached to the backup server in the Production Region. Run nightly cron jobs to snapshot these volumes and then copy these snapshots to the DR Region\u003c/strong\u003e - One of the key requirements of the given scenario is to ensure that the backup is durable but the data in an EBS volume is only replicated within its Availability Zone so it is not highly durable. However, the EBS snapshots are stored on Amazon S3, which is durable. The issue with this option is that it introduces the additional cost of an EBS volume and also does not optimize the storage cost in the DR Region as it does not leverage Glacier Deep Archive for the backup data storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/replication.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/replication.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:16":{"id":"e:3:16","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 16","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Studio with data preparation recipe transformations and allow data engineers to extend the same visual ETL pipeline with additional transformations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Python and pandas in an AWS Glue Studio notebook to prepare the datasets and ask data engineers to add the remaining pipeline transformations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena SQL views to standardize the raw datasets, then allow data engineers to copy the view results into curated S3 prefixes for downstream transformations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon SageMaker Canvas and SageMaker Data Wrangler to prepare a new dataset, then ask data engineers to rebuild the remaining pipeline in AWS Glue\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company is building a new data lake ingestion pipeline for supplier, inventory, and sales data. A business analyst must clean, standardize, and prepare incoming datasets before the data engineering team adds advanced transformations and orchestration. The analyst needs a solution that supports no-code data preparation and can remain part of the AWS Glue pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Studio with data preparation recipe transformations and allow data engineers to extend the same visual ETL pipeline with additional transformations\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Studio with data preparation recipe transformations is the best fit because it gives analysts a no-code visual experience for preparing data. The analyst can define data cleaning and standardization steps without writing new code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach also keeps the preparation work inside the AWS Glue ecosystem. The data preparation recipe can be part of a visual ETL job, and data engineers can add additional AWS Glue transformations before or after the preparation step.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational effort because it avoids handoffs that require engineers to reimplement rules from documents or notebooks. It also avoids using a separate ML-focused preparation environment when the final pipeline must continue in AWS Glue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Python and pandas in an AWS Glue Studio notebook to prepare the datasets and ask data engineers to add the remaining pipeline transformations\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue Studio notebooks can support interactive development, and Python with pandas can prepare data if the analyst has the required coding skills. This can be useful for engineering-driven experimentation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the requirement says the analyst needs a solution that does not require writing new code. Python and pandas do not meet the no-code requirement and would increase dependence on engineering support.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon SageMaker Canvas and SageMaker Data Wrangler to prepare a new dataset, then ask data engineers to rebuild the remaining pipeline in AWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon SageMaker Canvas and data preparation capabilities can help users prepare datasets for machine learning workflows. This can be useful when the primary goal is model development or ML-driven analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the company is building an AWS Glue data lake pipeline. Preparing data in a separate SageMaker workflow and then rebuilding the remaining ETL process in AWS Glue adds avoidable handoff and integration overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena SQL views to standardize the raw datasets, then allow data engineers to copy the view results into curated S3 prefixes for downstream transformations\u003c/strong\u003e - Athena SQL views can standardize some datasets by projecting columns, renaming fields, or applying SQL expressions. They can be useful for query-time transformations over data in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, SQL views do not provide the same no-code preparation experience for analysts. This design also requires extra engineering work to materialize view results into curated S3 locations before the rest of the pipeline can proceed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-studio-data-preparation.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-studio-data-preparation.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-studio-data-preparation-recipe-transform-tutorial.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-studio-data-preparation-recipe-transform-tutorial.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/edit-jobs-transforms.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/edit-jobs-transforms.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/integrate-your-data-and-collaborate-using-data-preparation-in-aws-glue-studio/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/integrate-your-data-and-collaborate-using-data-preparation-in-aws-glue-studio/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/author-data-integration-jobs-with-an-interactive-data-preparation-experience-with-aws-glue-visual-etl/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/author-data-integration-jobs-with-an-interactive-data-preparation-experience-with-aws-glue-visual-etl/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-13":{"id":"p:4-13","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 13","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon QuickSight to restrict access to a dataset by configuring row-level security (RLS) on it based on the PII needs of each team. To do this, you create a query or file that has one column named \u003ccode\u003eUserName\u003c/code\u003e or \u003ccode\u003eGroupName\u003c/code\u003eand then add one column to the query or file for each field that you want to grant or restrict access to\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate IAM roles that have different levels of granular access for different PII requirements. Assign the IAM roles to IAM groups. Use an identity-based policy to row and column level access to the IAM groups\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up AWS Lake Formation and create data filters based on the access permissions needed to each user group. Grant the data filter permissions to different IAM roles. Assign the IAM roles to users based on PII access needs. Use Athena to query the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 Access Points to create unique access control policies for each access point to easily control access to shared datasets. Create different Access Points to different user groups with different PII access requirements\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Row-level security của QuickSight giới hạn dữ liệu trong phạm vi dataset và dashboard của QuickSight. Đề cần quản trị truy cập data lake cho nhiều nhóm nói chung, nên Lake Formation phù hợp hơn.","IAM policy không lọc được từng hàng và cột bên trong dữ liệu S3 theo cách này. Ngoài ra, IAM role không được gắn vào IAM group như mô tả; người dùng cần được phép đảm nhận role.","Lake Formation data filters giới hạn dữ liệu ở mức hàng, cột hoặc kết hợp cả hai cho các công cụ tích hợp như Athena. Gán quyền cho các role phù hợp giúp mỗi nhóm chỉ truy vấn phần PII cần thiết.","Access Point kiểm soát quyền truy cập object hoặc tập object. Nó không tự lọc từng trường PII hay từng hàng trong dữ liệu mà ứng dụng đọc."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses Amazon S3 as its data lake solution, where it stores all of its data including personally identifiable information (PII). This data is used by multiple teams and user groups. As a data engineer, you have been tasked to ensure that user teams and groups can access only the PII that they require.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow will you implement a solution for this requirement with the LEAST operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:7:47":{"id":"e:7:47","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 47","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eCreate a local secondary index (LSI) on \u003ccode\u003ePRODUCT_ID\u003c/code\u003e in a new DynamoDB database.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eCreate a new DynamoDB table called \u003ccode\u003eDEPARTMENT_ID\u003c/code\u003e with a more general partition key.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eCreate a new DynamoDB table with a Global Secondary Index (GSI) on \u003ccode\u003ePRODUCT_NAME\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eCreate a new DynamoDB table using a more specific partition key, for instance, \u003ccode\u003ePRODUCT_ID\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA Data Engineer at a large e-commerce company is managing a DynamoDB table that stores product data from various categories. The partition key currently in use is \u003ccode\u003eCATEGORY_NAME\u003c/code\u003e. However, this has led to some partitions receiving more requests than others, causing imbalanced I/O and potential throttling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following strategies should the engineer implement to balance the throughput and prevent “hot” partitions?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eIn Amazon DynamoDB, data is distributed across multiple partitions based on the partition key. Each partition can handle a certain amount of read and write capacity. If the workload is not evenly distributed across partitions, some partitions (known as “hot” partitions) may receive a higher volume of traffic than others, leading to throttling and inefficient use of provisioned capacity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d4f667b3958aeea2db1a18c0c7131cc67a48d33855cb255590cf58a99bc1cb9c.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe partition key currently in \u0026nbsp;use is\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eCATEGORY_NAME\u003c/code\u003e. However, if the data within a category is heavily accessed, it can lead to imbalanced I/O and potential throttling. This is because all items with the same\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eCATEGORY_NAME\u003c/code\u003e are stored in the same partition, and if that category is popular, it can lead to a “hot” partition.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe solution is to create a new DynamoDB table using a more specific partition key, such as\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ePRODUCT_ID\u003c/code\u003e.\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ePRODUCT_ID\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;is likely to have a higher cardinality (i.e., more unique values) than\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eCATEGORY_NAME\u003c/code\u003e, which means the data will be spread across more partitions. This results in a more even distribution of data and requests, reducing the likelihood of “hot” partitions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBy using\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ePRODUCT_ID\u003c/code\u003e as the partition key, each product would have its own partition. This means that even if one product is very popular, the high traffic will be isolated to that product’s partition and will not impact the performance of other partitions. This strategy ensures efficient use of your provisioned I/O capacity and prevents throttling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a new DynamoDB table using a more specific partition key, for instance,\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ePRODUCT_ID\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a local secondary index (LSI) on\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ePRODUCT_ID\u003c/code\u003e in a new DynamoDB database\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect.\u0026nbsp;An LSI does not change the underlying partition key or the distribution of data across partitions. It merely provides an alternative way of querying\u0026nbsp;by allowing you to create an index on non-key attributes and query the data based on those attribute.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a new DynamoDB table called\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eDEPARTMENT_ID\u003c/code\u003e with a more general partition key\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. The issue with this approach is that departments could still have an imbalanced number of products, leading to potential \"hot\" partitions. For example, some departments might have a large number of products, while others might have fewer products. This could result in a similar situation as the current\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eCATEGORY_NAME\u003c/code\u003e partition key, where certain partitions receive a disproportionate amount of requests\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a new DynamoDB table with a Global Secondary Index (GSI) on\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ePRODUCT_NAME\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because GSIs simply allow you to create a new view of the data where you can use a different partition key and sort key. However, creating a GSI on\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ePRODUCT_NAME\u003c/code\u003e would not address the issue of imbalanced I/O on the main table. GSIs are best used for improving the flexibility of queries, not for balancing throughput on the main table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/bp-partition-key-uniform-load.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/bp-partition-key-uniform-load.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/bp-partition-key-design.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/bp-partition-key-design.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon DynamoDb Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-63":{"id":"p:4-63","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 63","correct":[0,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eWrite the transformed data in ORC format and use AWS Glue Data Catalog to track schema metadata for Athena queries\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eWrite the transformed data in Avro format and use AWS Glue Data Catalog to track schema metadata for Athena queries\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eWrite the transformed data in Apache Parquet format and use AWS Glue Data Catalog to track schema metadata for Athena queries\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eWrite the transformed data in ORC format and store schema definitions in separate JSON files in Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eWrite the transformed data in Apache Parquet format and use an external DynamoDB table to track schema changes for Athena queries\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["ORC lưu theo cột và nén hiệu quả, giúp Athena đọc ít dữ liệu khi truy vấn một số cột. Glue Data Catalog cung cấp schema và metadata mà Athena dùng để truy vấn.","Avro phù hợp tuần tự hóa dữ liệu và thay đổi schema nhưng lưu theo hàng. Với truy vấn phân tích chọn cột, ORC hoặc Parquet thường giảm dữ liệu đọc tốt hơn.","Parquet lưu theo cột và hỗ trợ nén, thích hợp cho các truy vấn Athena trên dữ liệu phân tích. Glue Data Catalog là nơi quản lý schema tích hợp trực tiếp.","ORC là lựa chọn định dạng phù hợp, nhưng tệp JSON mô tả schema rời rạc không tự trở thành metadata mà Athena sử dụng. Cần catalog hoặc cơ chế tích hợp tương ứng.","Parquet phù hợp, nhưng DynamoDB không tự đóng vai trò catalog schema cho Athena. Dùng Glue Data Catalog tránh phải tự xây lớp quản lý metadata."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media analytics company runs Apache Spark jobs on an Amazon EMR cluster to process streaming playback events from connected devices. The Spark jobs transform the data and write the curated output to an Amazon S3 bucket. Analysts use Amazon Athena to query the curated data for viewing trends, device usage, and content engagement patterns. The company needs to choose data formats and metadata management approaches that provide the shortest Athena query times.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solutions will meet these requirements? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:53":{"id":"e:5:53","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 53","correct":[1,4,5],"answers":["Use the fewest rows possible in your queries.","Use Apache Parquet formatted data files.","Use multiple files in text-file format for parallel processing.","Use gzip compression. Keep your file sizes small, not exceeding 64MB.","Use partitions to limit the scanned data.","Use the Amazon Redshift database for frequently used smaller dimension tables."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company collects data from IoT Devices and stores it on an Amazon S3 bucket. The company wants to use Redshift Spectrum is used to query data from Amazon S3 and joins it with tables in an Amzon Redshift cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich actions will speed up the queries? (Select THREE.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eA \u003cstrong\u003edata lake\u003c/strong\u003e built on AWS uses \u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3 \u003c/strong\u003eas its primary storage platform. Amazon S3 provides an optimal foundation for a data lake because of its virtually unlimited scalability and high durability. You can seamlessly and non-disruptively increase storage from gigabytes to petabytes of content, paying only for what you use.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e is a fully managed, petabyte-scale data warehouse service in the cloud. Amazon Redshift achieves efficient storage and optimum query performance through a combination of massively parallel processing, columnar data storage, and very efficient, targeted data compression encoding schemes. This section presents an introduction to the Amazon Redshift system architecture.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eUsing \u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshift Spectrum\u003c/strong\u003e, you can efficiently query and retrieve structured and semistructured data from files in Amazon S3 without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables. Redshift Spectrum queries employ massive parallelism to run very fast against large datasets. Much of the processing occurs in the Redshift Spectrum layer, and most of the data remains in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d705f5623721c2b7845d33e77c6d16f6bff7d6e2657f617536a5276b44da4d0f.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eSample Architecture of Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, and Redshift Spectrum\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe following are ways to improve Redshift Spectrum performance:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Use Apache Parquet formatted data files. Parquet stores data in a columnar format, so Redshift Spectrum can eliminate unneeded columns from the scan. When data is in text-file format, Redshift Spectrum needs to scan the entire file.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Use multiple files to optimize for parallel processing. Keep your file sizes larger than 64 MB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Use the fewest columns possible in your queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Put your large fact tables in Amazon S3 and keep your frequently used, smaller dimension tables in your local Amazon Redshift database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– The Amazon Redshift query planner pushes predicates and aggregations to the Redshift Spectrum query layer whenever possible.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Write your queries to use filters and aggregations that are eligible to be pushed to the Redshift Spectrum layer.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Use partitions to limit the data that is scanned.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Use AWS Glue’s statistics generator to compute column-level statistics for AWS Glue Data Catalog tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Use Apache Parquet formatted data files.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Use the Amazon Redshift database for frequently used smaller dimension tables.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Use partitions to limit the scanned data.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse gzip compression. Keep your file sizes small, not exceeding 64MB\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because keeping the individual files small can impact performance, as the overhead of opening and processing each file will affect the performance. Keeping file sizes above 64 MB ensures that each file contains sufficient data to maximize throughput and minimize overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the fewest rows possible in your queries\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Although limiting the rows can improve query performance, it is more important to restrict the columns instead. Redshift Spectrum scans data stored in Amazon S3 in a columnar fashion, reading only the columns relevant to the query. By selecting only the necessary columns, you minimize the computational overhead. Limiting rows can improve performance but will be less pronounced.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse multiple files in text-file format for parallel processing\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While it is true that using multiple files can optimize performance by parallel processing, it is still inadequate as the Redshift Spectrum will have to scan all the data in text-file format. It is better to use columnar format files like Apache Parquet files, as the Redshift Spectrum can eliminate unneeded columns when scanning.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-spectrum-external-performance.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-spectrum-external-performance.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-33":{"id":"p:2-33","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 33","correct":[1,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eJob bookmarks do not work for Amazon S3 input sources\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eYou have multiple concurrent jobs with job bookmarks, and the max concurrency isn\u0027t set to 1\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eYou have added a transformation context parameter to the DynamicFrame referenced within the job, which is causing the job to crash\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe job.commit() object is missing\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue keeps track of job bookmarks by storing the metadata in Amazon S3 configured during job creation. Deleting the S3 bucket can result in job reprocessing\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Glue job bookmarks hỗ trợ nguồn S3 theo các điều kiện/định dạng được hỗ trợ. Khẳng định không hoạt động với S3 là sai.","Các lần chạy đồng thời có thể đọc trạng thái bookmark chưa được lần khác cập nhật. Đặt max concurrency=1 theo tình huống đề để tránh xử lý chồng lấn.","Transformation context giúp liên kết trạng thái bookmark với nguồn. Không thể kết luận việc có tham số này là nguyên nhân lỗi; thiếu nó mới có thể làm bookmark không đúng.","job.commit() lưu trạng thái bookmark sau xử lý. Nếu không commit, lần sau có thể đọc lại dữ liệu đã xử lý.","Bookmark do Glue quản lý gắn với job, không phải metadata đơn giản nằm trong bucket tùy chọn như phát biểu. Xóa bucket đó không phải nguyên nhân theo mô tả này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn AWS Glue job is scheduled to be run every Sunday. The Glue job copies data from certain folders in an S3 bucket to Redshift. To prevent reprocessing of old data, job bookmarks have been enabled on the AWS Glue job. However, the ETL job is reprocessing data that was already processed in an earlier run.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat could be the underlying issue and how should it be fixed to stop reprocessing of data? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:7":{"id":"e:3:7","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 7","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into Amazon Redshift on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into an Amazon EMR-based Spark Cluster on an hourly basis and use SparkSQL to run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into Amazon RDS on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena to run SQL based analytics against Amazon S3 data\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team at an e-commerce company has set up a workflow to ingest the clickstream data into the raw zone of the Amazon S3 data lake. The team wants to run some SQL-based data sanity checks on the raw zone of the data lake.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat AWS services would you recommend for this use-case such that the solution is cost-effective and easy to maintain?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena to run SQL based analytics against Amazon S3 data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data directly in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. Amazon Athena is serverless, so there is no infrastructure to set up or manage, and customers pay only for the queries they run. You can use Athena to process logs, perform ad-hoc analysis, and run interactive queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena Benefits:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4c19f1e2fad379ac423472029092d84b6892af3d365b13d272696f88e9e9bba9.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4c19f1e2fad379ac423472029092d84b6892af3d365b13d272696f88e9e9bba9.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into Amazon Redshift on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift is a fully-managed petabyte-scale cloud-based data warehouse product designed for large-scale data set storage and analysis.\nAs the development team would have to maintain and monitor the Amazon Redshift cluster size and would require significant development time to set up the processes to consume the data periodically, so this option is ruled out.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into an Amazon EMR-based Spark Cluster on an hourly basis and use SparkSQL to run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR is the industry-leading cloud big data platform for processing vast amounts of data using open-source tools such as Apache Spark, Apache Hive, Apache HBase, Apache Flink, Apache Hudi, and Presto. Amazon EMR uses Hadoop, an open-source framework, to distribute your data and processing across a resizable cluster of Amazon EC2 instances.\nUsing an Amazon EMR cluster would imply managing the underlying infrastructure so it’s ruled out because the correct solution for the given use-case should require the least amount of development effort and ongoing maintenance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into Amazon RDS on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/strong\u003e - Loading the incremental data into Amazon RDS implies data migration jobs will have to be written via a AWS Lambda function or an Amazon EC2 based process. This goes against the requirement that the solution should involve the least amount of development effort and ongoing maintenance. Hence this option is not correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-54":{"id":"p:3-54","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 54","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift row-level security policies to filter table rows for each user based on department, region, and assigned business unit\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Lake Formation LF-Tags to manage Redshift database object permissions for all internal users who query native Redshift tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift role-based access control to grant database privileges to roles and assign the roles to users\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift column-level privileges to grant or deny access to sensitive columns for each user and reporting group\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["RLS lọc hàng trong bảng, chỉ giải quyết một phần nhỏ. Đề cần quản lý quyền trên nhiều loại đối tượng theo chức năng công việc.","LF-Tags không thay thế RBAC cho mọi đối tượng native Redshift. Phạm vi Lake Formation khác quản lý role nội bộ database.","Redshift RBAC gom các đặc quyền vào role rồi gán role cho người dùng. Dễ quản lý quyền theo phòng ban/chức năng hơn cấp riêng từng người.","Quyền cấp cột hữu ích cho dữ liệu nhạy cảm nhưng không thay thế cơ chế role quản lý schema, bảng và các đối tượng trên diện rộng."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial analytics company runs an Amazon Redshift data warehouse that more than 150 employees access each day. Users connect by assuming different IAM roles that represent departments such as finance, risk, operations, and executive reporting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company needs to manage access to schemas, tables, and other database objects based on each user’s job function, assigned permissions, and the sensitivity of the underlying data. The security team wants a scalable permission model that avoids managing privileges separately for every individual user.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:8:28":{"id":"e:8:28","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 28","correct":[3],"answers":["Use Amazon S3 Bucket Policies","Use AWS IAM Roles","Use Service Control Policies (SCPs)","Use Amazon Athena Workgroups"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer uses Amazon Athena to run SQL queries against data stored in Amazon S3. They need a solution to enforce settings across all queries to ensure they use the same query results location in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich approach can the team use to enforce these settings?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Athena Workgroups\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;allows you to separate your query execution across different teams, workloads, and applications or even run them in isolation from other queries. Workgroups provide a way to track query-related metrics, set limits to manage costs and enforce settings across all queries within the workgroup. Each workgroup has its own query history, metrics, data usage, and performance statistics, allowing for better resource allocation and cost management.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6511228ea637ff9910557a7abbbc811844de2e9f87bb7aea9fb2dc43138dd995.gif\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWorkgroups also help you simplify the onboarding of new users to Athena. By selecting\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003eOverride client-side settings\u003c/strong\u003e, you enforce a predefined configuration on all queries within a workgroup. Users no longer have to configure a query results output location or S3 encryption keys. These settings default to the parameters defined for the workgroup where those queries execute. Additionally, each workgroup maintains a unique query history and saved query inventory, making queries easier for you to track down.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena Workgroups.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon S3 Bucket Policies\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because this primarily focuses on managing permissions on the S3 bucket itself. It does not provide a way to enforce settings across all Athena queries in a specific environment.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS IAM Roles\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because this only applies in managing access to AWS resources, not Athen queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Service Control Policies (SCPs)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because SCPs are only designed to manage permissions in AWS Organizations and do not have the capability to enforce settings like query results location in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/separating-queries-and-managing-costs-using-amazon-athena-workgroups/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/separating-queries-and-managing-costs-using-amazon-athena-workgroups/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/workgroups-procedure.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/workgroups-procedure.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:51":{"id":"e:7:51","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 51","correct":[0],"answers":["Set up a VPC connection in QuickSight. Create inbound and outbound rules in the security groups for both services to allow traffic between them.","Create an outbound rule in the security group of the Redshift cluster to allow traffic to Quicksight’s IP address. Establish a VPN connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight and modify the network ACLs to allow traffic between them.","Create an inbound rule in the security group of the Redshift cluster to allow traffic from Quicksight’s IP address. Establish a Direct Connect connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight and modify the route tables to allow traffic between them.","Set up a VPC connection in QuickSight. Establish a VPC peering connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight’s VPC. Create inbound and outbound rules in the security groups for both services to allow traffic between them."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAn e-commerce company stores customer data in an Amazon Redshift cluster hosted in a private VPC. To safeguard sensitive data such as customer contact details, Amazon QuickSight enforces column-level security (CLS) during report generation.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eThe company aims to create visual charts of sales rankings by product category and customer demographics. To achieve this, the company\u0027s data engineer must configure QuickSight to connect to the Redshift cluster securely.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eHow can the data engineer meet the requirements in the LEAST amount of effort?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eYou can create a VPC connection in Amazon QuickSight to securely access private databases, such as an Amazon Redshift cluster, directly within your VPC. This setup is ideal for scenarios where your databases must be accessible only from certain IP addresses or within a secure environment, ensuring your data remains isolated from the public internet. When QuickSight connects to your VPC, it creates elastic network interfaces (ENIs) to facilitate a secure channel of communication directly to your resources, like a Redshift cluster, without traversing the public internet.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can connect Amazon QuickSight to the same VPC where your Amazon Redshift cluster is located. Once connected, you\u0027ll need to configure the security groups associated with both QuickSight and the Amazon Redshift cluster. The security groups act as virtual firewalls, controlling the traffic that can enter and leave the network interfaces.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the Redshift security group\u0027s configuration, the inbound rule must allow TCP traffic on port 5439 (the default port number of Redshift) from the QuickSight security group, enabling QuickSight to initiate connections to the Redshift cluster. The outbound rule should permit all TCP traffic to the QuickSight security group, allowing Redshift to send responses back to QuickSight.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c5b3cd0611879e227eb94eb58ce4877b7642d1617642e7162ff74abcae20851a.png\" alt=\"Amazon Redshift Security Group\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor QuickSight\u0027s security group, the inbound rules need to allow incoming responses from Redshift’s port range, and the outbound rules must permit initiating queries to the Redshift cluster on port 5439.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/08c668c073ee98f2a0349dee97348afac4fd638d73df55c65600e9efef1ce3ff.png\" alt=\"Amazon QuickSight Security Group\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up a VPC connection in QuickSight. Create inbound and outbound rules in the security groups for both services to allow traffic between them.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an outbound rule in the security group of the Redshift cluster to allow traffic to Quicksight’s IP address. Establish a VPN connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight and modify the network ACLs to allow traffic between them\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Establishing a VPN connection is not necessary in this scenario. The use of VPNs is more applicable to scenarios where you are connecting on-premise resources to AWS resources, not for connecting AWS services within the same region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an inbound rule in the security group of the Redshift cluster to allow traffic from Quicksight’s IP address. Establish a Direct Connect connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight and modify the route tables to allow traffic between them\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Similar to the other incorrect option,\u0026nbsp;this only introduces complexity that isn\u0027t necessary for connecting QuickSight to Redshift. Direct Connect is primarily used to create a dedicated, private connection between your on-premises data center and AWS. QuickSight, being a fully managed AWS service, does not require Direct Connect to access Redshift within AWS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up a VPC connection in QuickSight. Establish a networking VPC peering connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight’s VPC. Create inbound and outbound rules in the security groups for both services to allow traffic between them\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While this method is technically viable, VPC peering just adds unnecessary complexity to the given requirement. Since QuickSight can connect to Redshift through a simpler VPC connection setup, introducing VPC peering is not the most straightforward approach. It would be useful in scenarios where a Redshift cluster and QuickSight need to communicate across different VPCs\u0026nbsp;without a shared VPC connection or when cross-account VPC communication is required.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/working-with-aws-vpc.html#private-connection-to-vpc-using-quicksight\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/working-with-aws-vpc.html#private-connection-to-vpc-using-quicksight\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/quicksight-redshift-private-connection\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/quicksight-redshift-private-connection\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-quicksight-deployment-models-for-cross-account-and-cross-region-access-to-amazon-redshift-and-amazon-rds/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-quicksight-deployment-models-for-cross-account-and-cross-region-access-to-amazon-redshift-and-amazon-rds/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon QuickSight Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-quicksight/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-quicksight/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:44":{"id":"e:1:44","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 44","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure network ACLs on the VPC subnets to allow selected workloads to reach the S3 bucket and deny all other S3 traffic\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAttach a security group to the S3 bucket that permits inbound HTTPS connections only from the CIDR range of the analytics VPC\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an S3 bucket policy that allows access only when requests originate from the company VPC or the approved S3 VPC endpoint\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Block Public Access on the bucket and rely on the setting to restrict object access only to workloads deployed in the VPC\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media analytics company stores time-series playback metrics from streaming applications in an Amazon S3 bucket. Several analytics workloads that run in the company\u0027s VPC need to read the metrics, but the security team must prevent access from workloads that run outside that VPC. A data engineer must enforce this restriction at the S3 bucket level.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an S3 bucket policy that allows access only when requests originate from the company VPC or the approved S3 VPC endpoint\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAn S3 bucket policy is the appropriate control because Amazon S3 access is governed by identity-based policies, resource-based policies, and related access controls. The bucket policy can use condition keys to restrict access based on the network origin of the request.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor workloads inside a VPC, the usual pattern is to route S3 access through an S3 VPC endpoint and use conditions such as aws:SourceVpc or aws:SourceVpce in the bucket policy. This ensures that requests must come from the approved VPC path or endpoint before Amazon S3 allows access to the bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution meets the requirement because it enforces the restriction at the S3 bucket level. It prevents workloads outside the allowed VPC access path from reading the time-series data, even if those workloads otherwise have broad S3 permissions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAttach a security group to the S3 bucket that permits inbound HTTPS connections only from the CIDR range of the analytics VPC\u003c/strong\u003e - Security groups control traffic for elastic network interfaces and supported VPC resources, such as Amazon EC2 instances, load balancers, and some database resources. They are useful for controlling inbound and outbound traffic for resources inside a VPC.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, an S3 bucket is not a VPC resource that can have a security group attached to it. The data engineer must use an S3 bucket policy and VPC endpoint-related condition keys instead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable S3 Block Public Access on the bucket and rely on the setting to restrict object access only to workloads deployed in the VPC\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Block Public Access is a critical baseline control that helps prevent public access through bucket policies, access point policies, ACLs, and related settings. It is useful for reducing accidental exposure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, blocking public access is not the same as allowing access only from a specific VPC. Private IAM principals outside the VPC could still access the bucket unless the bucket policy explicitly restricts the network origin.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure network ACLs on the VPC subnets to allow selected workloads to reach the S3 bucket and deny all other S3 traffic\u003c/strong\u003e - Network ACLs control traffic entering and leaving VPC subnets. They can be useful as stateless network controls for subnet-level traffic filtering.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, network ACLs do not attach to Amazon S3 buckets and cannot directly enforce which principals or workloads can access a bucket. S3 bucket policies with VPC endpoint conditions are the correct control point.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/example-bucket-policies-vpc-endpoint.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/example-bucket-policies-vpc-endpoint.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/vpc-endpoints-s3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/vpc-endpoints-s3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/privatelink-interface-endpoints.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/privatelink-interface-endpoints.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/establishing-a-data-perimeter-on-aws-allow-access-to-company-data-only-from-expected-networks/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/establishing-a-data-perimeter-on-aws-allow-access-to-company-data-only-from-expected-networks/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:35":{"id":"e:1:35","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 35","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate one Amazon SageMaker Studio domain for each research team and configure each domain to isolate the team environment from all other teams\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a centralized Amazon S3 bucket for all regulated datasets, replicate every dataset from the source accounts into the bucket, grant every research project read access through a shared IAM role, and use Amazon S3 server access logs to review dataset consumption\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon SageMaker Assets to publish datasets to an asset catalog, allow research teams to discover and request access to the datasets, and use governed approval workflows to track dataset access\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon SageMaker Feature Store with cross-account resource policies to share all datasets automatically with each research team without requiring subscription requests\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA pharmaceutical research company uses Amazon SageMaker AI for model development and experimentation. The company has multiple research teams that work in separate AWS accounts and use regulated clinical trial datasets. The teams need a governed way to discover available datasets across accounts, request access to relevant datasets, preserve existing access controls, and maintain an auditable record of dataset access for compliance reviews.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon SageMaker Assets to publish datasets to an asset catalog, allow research teams to discover and request access to the datasets, and use governed approval workflows to track dataset access\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon SageMaker Assets provides controlled and regulated access to assets such as models and data tables across an organization. Users from different AWS accounts can create and share assets with minimal administrator overhead, which aligns with the requirement for cross-account dataset discovery and access governance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe asset catalog lets teams publish datasets so other authorized users can discover the assets, review metadata, and request access. Asset owners can approve or reject access requests, and approved users can then access the underlying data sources according to the governed workflow. This model supports sensitive datasets because access is not granted broadly or statically to every project group.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational overhead because it uses a managed SageMaker Assets workflow for publishing, discovery, subscription requests, approvals, and access tracking. The company does not need to build a custom portal, write custom approval logic, duplicate datasets into a central bucket, or manually maintain broad IAM roles for each project group.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\n\u003cstrong\u003eCreate one Amazon SageMaker Studio domain for each research team and configure each domain to isolate the team environment from all other teams\u003c/strong\u003e - Separate SageMaker Studio domains can isolate project environments, but isolation does not solve the requirement to discover datasets across AWS accounts. This approach prevents the collaborative sharing model that the company needs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe requirement asks for governed discovery, access requests, access control, and compliance tracking. Creating disconnected Studio domains provides environmental separation, but it does not provide an asset catalog or managed subscription workflow for cross-account dataset access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a centralized Amazon S3 bucket for all regulated datasets, replicate every dataset from the source accounts into the bucket, grant every research project read access through a shared IAM role, and use Amazon S3 server access logs to review dataset consumption\u003c/strong\u003e - A centralized S3 bucket can simplify data placement, but replicating all regulated datasets into one bucket creates unnecessary data duplication and governance complexity. It can also increase storage cost and introduce additional operational work to synchronize data from multiple source accounts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe shared IAM role also conflicts with the need for precise access control by project group. Server access logs can provide object access records, but they do not replace a managed asset catalog with discovery, request, approval, and governed access workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon SageMaker Feature Store with cross-account resource policies to share all datasets automatically with each research team without requiring subscription requests\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon SageMaker Feature Store helps teams create, store, and share ML features. It is useful for feature engineering workflows, but it is not a general governed catalog for discovering and requesting access to multiple dataset types across project groups.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also grants access automatically without approval workflows. That design does not meet the requirement to maintain access controls and track access for compliance purposes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/sm-assets.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/sm-assets.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/sm-assets-user-guide.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/sm-assets-user-guide.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/discover-data.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/discover-data.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/grant-access-to-glue-asset.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/grant-access-to-glue-asset.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:10:17":{"id":"e:10:17","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 17","correct":[0],"answers":["Configure the new data to be stored in the S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) storage class. Use Amazon Athena for ad-hoc querying. Implement lifecycle rules for data archival to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 3 years and deletion after 10 years.","Set the S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class for new data with a configuration towards Deep Archive Access for longer-term storage. Utilize Amazon Athena for ad-hoc querying. Configure S3 Intelligent Tiering to purge data after 10 years.","Use Amazon Redshift for data storage of the new data, then configure Redshift Spectrum to directly query data on S3. Implement lifecycle rules for data archival to the Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class. Set up another lifecycle rule to delete data longer than 10 years.","Configure Amazon RDS to store the newly added data. Create an Amazon Lambda function to periodically export the snapshots to the S3 Standard class. Set up lifecycle policies for transferring snapshots to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 3 years and deleting data after 10 years."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team manages 10 TB of data in an Amazon S3 bucket. The team must meet new data lifecycle and retention policies. Under the new guidelines, all newly stored data must be readily available for random, occasional SQL queries. Data older than 3 years requires secure storage but must be accessible within 8 hours for compliance audits. Additionally, data exceeding a 10-year retention period should be permanently deleted. The team initially explored using a tumbling window for real-time analytics but determined that occasional SQL queries made a simpler, cost-effective approach more appropriate.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will fulfill this requirement most cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e offers different types of storage classes that users can choose based on the specific needs of their workload. Depending on their workload\u0027s data access, resiliency, and performance requirements, these classes are tailored to provide the best storage options at the lowest possible costs. S3 storage classes are versatile and suitable for nearly any use case - including those with high-performance demands, data lakes, residency requirements, unknown or changing access patterns, or archival storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOne of these classes is the Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA). Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) storage class is designed for data that is accessed less frequently but, when needed, requires rapid access. S3 Standard-IA offers the high durability, throughput, and low latency of S3 Standard but with a lower storage cost and a slightly higher retrieval fee. This storage class makes it an ideal solution for long-term storage and backups and a data store for disaster recovery files. Amazon Athena allows users to analyze data stored in Amazon S3 with SQL queries. It\u0027s interactive and serverless, so there\u0027s no infrastructure to manage. Users are charged only for the queries they run, making it an incredibly cost-effective solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/cb434042f543ea5c8acbd790bd85ffead1c710a64cb5da3986acee1cc869ec41.png\" alt=\"S3 lifecycle rule deleting noncurrent objects\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBy implementing lifecycle rules in Amazon S3, you can easily manage your data throughout its lifecycle. S3 Lifecycle configuration rules allow you to transition objects to more cost-effective storage classes, such as S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval or S3 Glacier Deep Archive, for long-term storage and archiving purposes. Moreover, you have the option to set up rules that will automatically delete objects after a specific period, which helps you comply with data governance and regulatory requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, previously known as S3 Glacier, is a cost-effective storage solution for archiving data. You can configure retrieval times from minutes to hours, which is suitable for accessing data for compliance audits within a 10-hour window. On the other hand, the S3 Glacier Deep Archive is designed for data that is accessed less frequently but needs to be retained for decades as part of digital preservation or regulatory compliance. It is the most affordable long-term archiving storage option available on AWS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure the new data to be stored in the S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) storage class. Use Amazon Athena for ad-hoc querying. Implement lifecycle rules for data archival to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 3 years and deletion after 10 years.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet the S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class for new data with a configuration towards Deep Archive Access for longer-term storage. Utilize Amazon Athena for ad-hoc querying. Configure S3 Intelligent Tiering to purge data after 10 years\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Although using Amazon Athena to query data meets one of the requirements, transitioning to Deep Archive Access makes it impossible for data to be accessible within an 8-hour window. S3 Glacier Deep Archive is intended for long-term data storage that is accessed once or twice a year, with retrieval times typically ranging from 12 to 48 hours.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift for data storage of the new data, then configure Redshift Spectrum to directly query data on S3. Implement lifecycle rules for data archival to the Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class. Set up another lifecycle rule to delete data longer than 10 years\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Redshift is primarily a data warehousing solution for large-scale analytical workloads, not occasional SQL queries on recently ingested data. While Redshift Spectrum allows querying data in S3, maintaining a Redshift cluster incurs hourly charges, making it a less cost-effective solution. Moreover, although S3 Glacier Deep Archive is cheaper than Glacier Flexible Retrieval, it only allows retrieval times ranging from 12 to 48 hours.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon RDS to store the newly added data. Create an Amazon Lambda function to periodically export the snapshots to the S3 Standard class. Set up lifecycle policies for transferring snapshots to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 3 years and deleting data after 10 years\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Amazon RDS is primarily a database service for relational databases and is not optimized for cost-effective data storage and retrieval for compliance and lifecycle management in the way S3 and its associated services are. Moreover, managing snapshots and exports introduces operational overhead that does not directly contribute to the goals of easy SQL analysis and compliance with lifecycle policies.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/backup-recovery/amazon-s3-glacier.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/backup-recovery/amazon-s3-glacier.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these\u0026nbsp;Amazon S3 Glacier and Amazon Athena Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-glacier/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-glacier/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:64":{"id":"e:5:64","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 64","correct":[1,4],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eVACCUM\u003c/code\u003e command to clean the table and release the lock.\u003c/span\u003e","Run a query to identify sessions that are holding locks.","\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eEXPLAIN\u003c/code\u003e command to analyze and optimize the query execution plan to prevent locks.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eREINDEX\u003c/code\u003e command on the table to rebuild indexes and attempt to clear the lock.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eUse \u003ccode\u003epg_terminate_backend\u003c/code\u003e to terminate the session that is holding the lock.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has created an ETL Pipeline using Amazon Glue that loads data from S3 into a Redshift table. The Glue Job uses the \u003ccode\u003eTRUNCATE\u003c/code\u003e command to refresh the table before inserting new data. Things are running without issues until they are receiving jobs that are getting stuck. Upon investigation, the table is under an \u003ccode\u003eAccessShareLock\u003c/code\u003e, caused by unexpected queries from one of their applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the steps can a Data Engineer use to resolve this problem? (SELECT TWO)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eLocking is a protection mechanism that controls how many sessions can access a table at the same time. Locking also determines which operations can be performed in those sessions. Most relational databases use row-level locks. However, Amazon Redshift uses table-level locks. You might experience locking conflicts if you perform frequent DDL statements on user tables or DML queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift has three lock modes:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAccessExclusiveLock:\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;Acquired primarily during DDL operations, such as \u003ccode\u003eALTER TABLE\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eDROP\u003c/code\u003e, or \u003ccode\u003eTRUNCATE\u003c/code\u003e. AccessExclusiveLock blocks all other locking attempts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAccessShareLock:\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;Acquired during \u003ccode\u003eUNLOAD\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eSELECT\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eUPDATE\u003c/code\u003e, or \u003ccode\u003eDELETE\u003c/code\u003e operations. AccessShareLock blocks only \u003ccode\u003eAccessExclusiveLock\u003c/code\u003e attempts. AccessShareLock doesn’t block other sessions that are trying to read or write on the table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eShareRowExclusiveLock:\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;Acquired during \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eINSERT\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eUPDATE\u003c/code\u003e, or \u003ccode\u003eDELETE\u003c/code\u003e operations. \u003ccode\u003eShareRowExclusiveLock\u003c/code\u003e blocks \u003ccode\u003eAccessExclusiveLock\u003c/code\u003e and other \u003ccode\u003eShareRowExclusiveLock\u003c/code\u003e attempts but doesn’t block \u003ccode\u003eAccessShareLock\u003c/code\u003e attempts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3f03418a8fcab1e1714e806317594394526d62965aa11338f1933269d0e1b61d.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn this scenario, \u003ccode\u003eTRUNCATE\u003c/code\u003e is frequently executed on the table. DDL statements like \u003ccode\u003eTRUNCATE\u003c/code\u003e attempts an \u003ccode\u003eAccessExclusiveLock\u003c/code\u003e on a table. If a process tries to acquire\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eAccessExclusiveLock\u003c/code\u003e on a table and there are ongoing queries that holds \u003ccode\u003eAccessShareLock\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eTRUNCATE\u003c/code\u003e would have to wait until the \u003ccode\u003eAccessShareLock\u003c/code\u003e is released, causing the Glue job to hang.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo solve a locking problem, identify the session (PID) that is holding the lock and then terminate the session. If the session doesn’t terminate, reboot your cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct options are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Run a query to identify sessions that are holding locks.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Use \u003ccode\u003epg_terminate_backend\u003c/code\u003e to terminate the session that is holding the lock.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eVACCUM\u003c/code\u003e command to clean the table and release the lock\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. \u003ccode\u003eVACCUM\u003c/code\u003e command Re-sorts rows and reclaims space in a specified table or all tables in the current database. While it improves query performance, it does not directly address or release active locks caused by rogue queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eEXPLAIN\u003c/code\u003e command to analyze and optimize the query execution plan to prevent locks\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. This is because The \u003ccode\u003eEXPLAIN\u003c/code\u003e command is designed to help maximize query execution plans. However, this command focuses more on the preventive side, helping to identify performance issues before they occur. This option does not offer a solution for releasing the lock.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eREINDEX\u003c/code\u003e command on the table to rebuild indexes and attempt to clear the lock\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. \u003ccode\u003eREINDEX\u003c/code\u003e rebuilds indexes to ensure efficient data retrieval. In contrast, this improves the performance of the database. I do not directly help with the issue of releasing the locks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/queries-troubleshooting.html#queries-troubleshooting-query-hangs\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/queries-troubleshooting.html#queries-troubleshooting-query-hangs\u003c/a\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/prevent-locks-blocking-queries-redshift\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/prevent-locks-blocking-queries-redshift\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Redshift Cheatsheet:\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003c/strong\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-46":{"id":"p:2-46","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 46","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an Express workflow in Step Functions and implement a Wait for Callback pattern so each external validation step can return its result\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Lambda functions to implement the workflow and use EventBridge rules to invoke each validation stage after the preceding stage finishes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Standard workflow in Step Functions and implement a Wait for Callback pattern to wait for each validation stage to finish\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Express workflow in Step Functions and split each transaction into multiple executions so no single execution exceeds the workflow duration limit\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Express workflow có thời gian chạy ngắn và không hỗ trợ mẫu waitForTaskToken như Standard. Không phù hợp chờ xác minh ngoài hệ thống nhiều giờ.","Tự nối Lambda/EventBridge cần tự quản lý trạng thái, timeout và kết quả từng bước. Standard workflow cung cấp cơ chế điều phối rõ ràng hơn.","Standard workflow hỗ trợ luồng dài và chờ callback bằng task token. Đặt timeout phù hợp giới hạn 18 giờ để kết thúc nếu hệ thống ngoài không trả kết quả.","Chia nhỏ thành nhiều Express execution không tự giải quyết callback và trạng thái toàn bộ giao dịch. Tăng công điều phối không cần thiết."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company needs to implement a transaction review workflow. Each payment request must pass through several dependent validation stages, including fraud review, balance verification, compliance screening, and final approval. Some validations call external systems and can take several hours to return a result.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company must approve or reject every payment request within 18 hours. The complete workflow must run for less than 18 hours and must wait for each validation result before moving to the next stage. The company wants the solution with the least operational cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-44":{"id":"p:2-44","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 44","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 Standard storage class\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval storage class\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access storage class\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access storage class\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["S3 Standard phù hợp dữ liệu tạm chỉ giữ 24 giờ và được đọc nhiều. Không có mức tính phí thời gian lưu tối thiểu dài như các lớp IA/archive được nêu.","Glacier Instant Retrieval có thời gian tính phí lưu tối thiểu 90 ngày. Giữ chỉ 24 giờ vẫn không kinh tế dù truy cập nhanh.","Standard-IA có thời gian tính phí tối thiểu và phí truy xuất, không phù hợp dữ liệu tạm đọc thường xuyên trong một ngày.","One Zone-IA cũng có thời gian tính phí tối thiểu và phí đọc. Rẻ mỗi GB không có nghĩa rẻ cho dữ liệu tồn tại chỉ 24 giờ."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA leading video streaming service delivers billions of hours of content from Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) to customers around the world. Amazon S3 also serves as the data lake for its big data analytics solution. The data lake has a staging zone where intermediary query results are kept only for 24 hours. These results are also heavily referenced by other parts of the analytics pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following is the MOST cost-effective strategy for storing this intermediary query data?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:56":{"id":"e:2:56","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 56","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue jobs to transform the payment activity data into Apache Parquet files in Amazon S3, catalog the files in AWS Glue Data Catalog, and assign each data team a separate Athena workgroup for analysis\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon OpenSearch Service domains with indexes to search and analyze the data, and assign each data team a separate domain to configure storage and query performance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Aurora clusters with Aurora I/O-Optimized configuration to store the data, and assign each data team a separate Aurora cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to process the data, and assign each data team a separate stream to consume\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial technology company is building an application that must search and analyze payment activity data in near real time. The application must support up to 1,200 search and analytics requests each second while keeping query latency low. The company wants each data product team to own its environment and tune storage, indexing, and query performance based on that team\u0027s budget and workload requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon OpenSearch Service domains with indexes to search and analyze the data, and assign each data team a separate domain to configure storage and query performance\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon OpenSearch Service is designed for search, log analytics, real-time application monitoring, clickstream analysis, and other low-latency search and analytics workloads. It uses indexes so applications can retrieve and analyze data quickly without scanning large raw datasets for every request.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe requirement includes up to 1,200 requests each second with low query latency. OpenSearch Service domains can be sized and tuned for indexing throughput, search throughput, storage, shard strategy, and node configuration. This makes the service a strong fit for high-throughput near real-time search and analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAssigning each data team a separate OpenSearch Service domain gives each team ownership of its cost and performance settings. Each team can tune instance types, storage, indexes, shards, replicas, retention policies, and query patterns without affecting other teams that have different workload profiles.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ecc8741aba35dfda0cf31bb4980312b21ca59e45a90cd3f9f64e2f288e939b1a.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ecc8741aba35dfda0cf31bb4980312b21ca59e45a90cd3f9f64e2f288e939b1a.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/bp.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/bp.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d2a190554ee0098d36f83308361d63680d21dbd1c8f3ce7816190f688a678719.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d2a190554ee0098d36f83308361d63680d21dbd1c8f3ce7816190f688a678719.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/bp.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/bp.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue jobs to transform the payment activity data into Apache Parquet files in Amazon S3, catalog the files in AWS Glue Data Catalog, and assign each data team a separate Athena workgroup for analysis\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue, Parquet, the AWS Glue Data Catalog, and Athena can create an efficient serverless analytics architecture for S3 data lakes. This approach can reduce scanned data and improve query cost for batch or interactive analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this architecture is not optimized for high-frequency, low-latency application search. It also depends on file transformation and query execution workflows rather than indexing data for near real-time search and analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to process the data, and assign each data team a separate stream to consume\u003c/strong\u003e - Kinesis Data Streams and Managed Service for Apache Flink can process streaming data in real time. This combination is suitable for stream processing, windowed aggregations, stateful transformations, and continuous analytics pipelines.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option does not provide a search index that applications can query with low latency at high request rates. It processes streaming events, but it does not directly satisfy the application\u0027s need for near real-time search and analysis across indexed data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Aurora clusters with Aurora I/O-Optimized configuration to store the data, and assign each data team a separate Aurora cluster\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Aurora can support transactional and relational workloads with high performance. Aurora I/O-Optimized can help workloads that have high I/O usage and require predictable database pricing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, Aurora is not the best fit for near real-time search and analytics across large volumes of indexed application data. The requirement focuses on search and analysis with low query latency, which aligns more closely with OpenSearch Service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/bp.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/bp.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/indexing.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/indexing.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/run-log-analytics-for-a-fraction-of-the-cost-with-the-new-engine-for-amazon-opensearch-service/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/run-log-analytics-for-a-fraction-of-the-cost-with-the-new-engine-for-amazon-opensearch-service/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:25":{"id":"e:6:25","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 25","correct":[0,1,2],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eA \u003ccode\u003eDeleteMessage\u003c/code\u003e API call was made to Amazon SQS.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003emaxReceiveCount\u003c/code\u003e for a message was exceeded.\u003c/span\u003e","A purge operation was performed on the queue.","An application receives a message without deleting it.","The visibility timeout expires on a message.","The configuration for a queue is edited."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is developing an application that involves sending data for transformation to an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) queue. Multiple Amazon Elastic Container Service (ECS) tasks will poll and consume messages from the queue. The engineer must ensure that each message is processed once before being deleted.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich events can lead to the removal of messages from an SQS queue? (Select THREE.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eIn Amazon SQS, several events can lead to the removal of messages, which is crucial for ensuring the queue\u0027s efficiency and reliability. A DeleteMessage API call is a direct method to remove a message from the queue, typically after it has been processed by a consumer. This ensures that messages are not processed more than necessary. Reaching the maxReceiveCount for a message is another way messages are removed; this occurs when a message has been received a specified number of times but not deleted, indicating processing issues and usually results in the message being sent to a dead-letter queue. Furthermore, performing a purge operation on the queue instantly clears all messages, useful for resetting or troubleshooting the queue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/34ece88cf927a9f2cb5943bf4f08cc447640540200b78240c3cd13e4b6d79282.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTherefore, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- A \u003ccode\u003eDeleteMessage\u003c/code\u003e API call was made to Amazon SQS.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- The \u003ccode\u003emaxReceiveCount\u003c/code\u003e for a message was exceeded.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- A purge operation was performed on the queue.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAn application receives a message without deleting\u003c/span\u003e \u003cspan\u003eit\u003c/span\u003e \u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. Merely receiving a message does not remove it from the queue. Instead, it triggers a visibility timeout, during which the message is not visible to other consumers but remains in the queue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe visibility timeout expires on a message\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. The expiry of the visibility timeout makes the message available to other consumers again but does not remove it from the queue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe configuration for a queue\u003c/strong\u003e \u003cstrong\u003eis edited\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Editing a queue\u0027s configuration can change its behavior and settings but does not directly lead to message removal from the queue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eResources\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/working-with-messages.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/working-with-messages.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-dead-letter-queues.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-dead-letter-queues.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-using-purge-queue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-using-purge-queue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sqs/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:35":{"id":"e:4:35","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 35","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon CloudWatch anomaly detection on custom vibration metrics and configure AWS Glue crawlers to identify records that have missing timestamp values\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Glue DataBrew project to profile the telemetry data, define completeness rules for event_timestamp, and configure numeric range validation for vibration values\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Glue DynamicFrame with a custom validation operator and use Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler transforms to validate timestamps and vibration ranges\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Quality rules with ML-based anomaly detection to learn unusual vibration behavior and detect records that do not include event timestamps\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA heavy equipment manufacturer uses AWS Glue jobs to prepare IoT telemetry data for predictive maintenance reporting. The telemetry dataset includes vibration readings, operating temperature, machine identifiers, and event timestamps. A data engineer must add automated data quality checks that identify vibration readings outside the expected range of 0 to 85 mm/s and records that are missing event_timestamp values. The solution must require minimal coding and must automatically flag the specified issues.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Glue DataBrew project to profile the telemetry data, define completeness rules for event_timestamp, and configure numeric range validation for vibration values\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew is the best fit for this requirement because it provides a visual, low-code way to profile datasets and define data quality rules. The data engineer can create a DataBrew project for the telemetry dataset and inspect the profile results to understand missing values, value distributions, and numeric characteristics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDataBrew rulesets can define validation checks for specific columns. In this scenario, the engineer can define a completeness rule for event_timestamp and a numeric range rule that checks whether vibration values remain between 0 and 85 mm/s.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution meets the requirement with minimal coding because DataBrew provides built-in profiling and rule-based validation capabilities. The company does not need to write a custom scanner, build a separate transformation framework, or use machine learning anomaly detection for fixed business rules.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Quality rules with ML-based anomaly detection to learn unusual vibration behavior and detect records that do not include event timestamps\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue Data Quality can define rule-based checks, and anomaly detection can help identify unusual data behavior. This can be useful when the data team needs to monitor changing data patterns over time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the requirement is to validate fixed conditions: missing timestamps and vibration values outside a known range. The original solution pattern expects AWS Glue DataBrew because it provides visual profiling and configurable rules for these checks with minimal coding.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon CloudWatch anomaly detection on custom vibration metrics and configure AWS Glue crawlers to identify records that have missing timestamp values\u003c/strong\u003e - CloudWatch anomaly detection can help identify unusual trends in published metrics. It can be useful for operational monitoring when the application emits metrics continuously.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option does not validate each source record against a fixed numeric range. AWS Glue crawlers discover schema metadata, but they do not identify individual records that are missing timestamp values.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Glue DynamicFrame with a custom validation operator and use Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler transforms to validate timestamps and vibration ranges\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue DynamicFrames can support custom transformations during ETL processing. SageMaker Data Wrangler can help prepare and transform data for machine learning workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option introduces unnecessary custom logic and an additional ML preparation tool. The requested checks are straightforward data quality validations that DataBrew can configure through profiling and rules.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-rules.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-rules.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-rules-create.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-rules-create.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-available-checks.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-available-checks.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/jobs.profile.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/jobs.profile.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/enforce-customized-data-quality-rules-in-aws-glue-databrew/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/enforce-customized-data-quality-rules-in-aws-glue-databrew/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:40":{"id":"e:7:40","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 40","correct":[0],"answers":["Use AWS Glue Data Catalog as the external metastore for Hive.","Use EMR File System (EMRFS) as the external metastore for Hive.","Use EMR notebook to extract metadata from your Hive metastore, then load the metadata and update the AWS Glue Data Catalog.","Use Amazon Athena to query data directly from S3 using standard SQL."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is planning to migrate its centralized metadata store for Apache Hive to AWS. The metadata store exposes S3 objects as tables that can be used by all data analytics teams running their own Amazon EMR clusters. Each team has its own metadata for running different SQL queries using Hive.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution would be the most suitable for this task?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Data Catalog\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is a fully-managed service that serves as a centralized metadata repository. It can be used as a drop-in replacement for the Apache Hive Metastore, providing a unified metadata repository across various services. This is crucial when you have multiple data analytics teams each running their own Amazon EMR clusters and using their own metadata for running different SQL queries using Hive.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f1753e710dc6fc149972429ded3dfa191e063d803326c81f4d523700b1bf97f7.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOne of the key features of AWS Glue Data Catalog is its ability to crawl data sources to discover schemas and populate your Catalog with new and modified table and partition definitions. This means that it can automatically keep the metadata up-to-date, reducing the need for manual management.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFurthermore, AWS Glue Data Catalog maintains schema versioning. This means that it keeps track of all changes to the metadata, allowing you to easily revert to a previous version if necessary.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog as the external metastore for Hive.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse EMR File System (EMRFS) as the external metastore for Hive\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because EMRFS is an Amazon S3-compatible file system that you can use with Amazon EMR. It is not designed to serve as an external metastore for Hive.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse EMR notebook to extract metadata from your Hive metastore, then load the metadata and update the AWS Glue Data Catalog\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because EMR notebooks are primarily used for creating and sharing documents that contain live code, equations, visualizations, and narrative text, not for extracting metadata from Hive metastore.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eU\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003ese\u003c/span\u003e Amazon Athena to query data directly from S3 using standard SQL\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because Amazon Athena allows you to query data directly from S3 using standard SQL, bypassing the need for a traditional data warehouse. However, this does not provide a centralized metadata store solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/migrate-and-deploy-your-apache-hive-metastore-on-amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/migrate-and-deploy-your-apache-hive-metastore-on-amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/serverless-etl-aws-glue/aws-glue-data-catalog.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/serverless-etl-aws-glue/aws-glue-data-catalog.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:20":{"id":"e:8:20","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 20","correct":[0,1],"answers":["Create an AWS Glue connection for the Amazon RDS DB instance and the Amazon Redshift cluster.","Set up an AWS Glue ETL job to transform the data and an AWS Glue trigger to start the ETL job daily after the RDS instance is updated at midnight.","Set up an AWS Glue ETL job to transform the data and an AWS Glue trigger to start the ETL job every hour.","Create an AWS Glue connection for the Amazon RDS instance and the Amazon Redshift cluster, and then use Amazon EMR to transform the data.","Use AWS Glue to create a data lake that stores the raw data from the Amazon RDS instance. Query the data lake using Amazon Athena."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company manages a large amount of data stored in various AWS services. They need to create a data pipeline that extracts data from an Amazon RDS DB instance, transforms the data using AWS Glue jobs, and then loads the transformed data into an Amazon Redshift cluster. The data in the DB instance is updated daily at midnight, and the transformed data should be available in the Redshift cluster by 2 AM. The transformation job takes about an hour to complete.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions would best meet these requirements? (Select TWO).\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue\u0026nbsp;connection\u0026nbsp;is a Data Catalog object that stores login credentials, URI strings, virtual private cloud (VPC) information, and more for a particular data store. AWS Glue crawlers, jobs, and development endpoints use connections in order to access certain types of data stores. You can use connections for both sources and targets and reuse the same connection across multiple crawler or extract, transform, and load (ETL) jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2324355da0b44ba6cf05f756525e4159b09165bd90a4ddf47a18e3f74c43668d.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn AWS Glue, you can create Data Catalog objects called triggers, which you can use to either manually or automatically start one or more crawlers or extract, transform, and load (ETL) jobs. Using triggers, you can design a chain of dependent jobs and crawlers.\u0026nbsp;When\u0026nbsp;\u003cem\u003efired\u003c/em\u003e, a trigger can start specified jobs and crawlers. A trigger fires on demand, based on a schedule, or based on a combination of events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA trigger can exist in one of several states. A trigger is either\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eCREATED\u003c/code\u003e,\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eACTIVATED\u003c/code\u003e, or\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eDEACTIVATED\u003c/code\u003e. There are also transitional states, such as\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eACTIVATING\u003c/code\u003e. To temporarily stop a trigger from firing, you can deactivate it. You can then reactivate it later.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Create an AWS Glue connection for the Amazon RDS DB instance and the Amazon Redshift cluster.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Set up an AWS Glue ETL job to transform the data and an AWS Glue trigger to start the ETL job daily after the RDS instance is updated at midnight.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eSet up an AWS Glue ETL job to transform the data and an AWS Glue trigger to start the ETL job every hour\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because it\u0026nbsp;sets up the ETL job to run every hour. This doesn’t align with the requirement that the data in the RDS instance is updated daily at midnight. Running the ETL job every hour would result in unnecessary processing and could lead to inconsistencies if the ETL job runs while the RDS instance is being updated.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Glue connection for the Amazon RDS instance and the Amazon Redshift cluster, and then use Amazon EMR to transform the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because it\u0026nbsp;uses Amazon EMR to transform the data. Amazon EMR is a cloud-native big data platform for processing large datasets, which might not be necessary for this use case. AWS Glue, on the other hand, is a fully managed ETL service that is more suitable for this use case. It can easily connect to the RDS instance and Redshift cluster, transform the data, and load the transformed data into the Redshift cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue to create a data lake that stores the raw data from the Amazon RDS instance. Query the data lake using Amazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. This option focuses more on ad-hoc querying of untransformed data rather than executing scheduled ETL jobs to transform and load data into Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-connections.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-connections.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/about-triggers.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/about-triggers.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:16":{"id":"e:4:16","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 16","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAdd Global Secondary Index as well as Local Secondary Index to the DynamoDB table to ensure maximum performance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpdate the capacity mode from provisioned to on-demand so that the table\u0027s capacity automatically adjusts to the traffic variations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eImplement AWS Application Auto Scaling to adjust provisioned capacity according to usage patterns. Set higher capacity during anticipated peak times and reduce it during periods of low activity\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eImplement AWS Application Auto Scaling to adjust provisioned capacity to meet the maximum demand seen in the last 12 months\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses an Amazon DynamoDB table in provisioned capacity mode to store data for an application that experiences predictable demand. Activity spikes sharply every Monday morning, while weekends see very low usage. The company needs a solution that ensures consistent application performance during these peak periods in a cost-effective manner.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is the best solution to meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement AWS Application Auto Scaling to adjust provisioned capacity according to usage patterns. Set higher capacity during anticipated peak times and reduce it during periods of low activity\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWith provisioned capacity, you pay for the provision of read and write capacity units for your DynamoDB tables. Whereas with DynamoDB on-demand you pay per request for the data reads and writes that your application performs on your tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWith provisioned capacity, you can also use auto-scaling to automatically adjust your table’s capacity based on the specified utilization rate to ensure application performance, and potentially reduce costs. To configure auto-scaling in DynamoDB, set the minimum and maximum levels of read and write capacity in addition to the target utilization percentage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon DynamoDB auto scaling uses the AWS Application Auto Scaling service to dynamically adjust provisioned throughput capacity on your behalf, in response to actual traffic patterns. This enables a table or a global secondary index to increase its provisioned read and write capacity to handle sudden increases in traffic, without throttling. When the workload decreases, Application Auto Scaling decreases the throughput so that you don\u0027t pay for unused provisioned capacity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWith Application Auto Scaling, you create a scaling policy for a table or a global secondary index. The scaling policy specifies whether you want to scale read capacity or write capacity (or both), and the minimum and maximum provisioned capacity unit settings for the table or index. The scaling policy also contains a target utilization—the percentage of consumed provisioned throughput at a point in time. Application Auto Scaling uses a target tracking algorithm to adjust the provisioned throughput of the table (or index) upward or downward in response to actual workloads so that the actual capacity utilization remains at or near your target utilization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ee727ad04dc4ce74aac0cb49c1d582089b255f3ded5457c19f06ddbfdb49099d.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ee727ad04dc4ce74aac0cb49c1d582089b255f3ded5457c19f06ddbfdb49099d.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/AutoScaling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/AutoScaling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate the capacity mode from provisioned to on-demand so that the table\u0027s capacity automatically adjusts to the traffic variations\u003c/strong\u003e - With on-demand capacity mode, DynamoDB charges you for the data reads and writes your application performs on your tables. You do not need to specify how much read and write throughput you expect your application to perform because DynamoDB instantly accommodates your workloads as they ramp up or down. With provisioned capacity mode, you specify the number of reads and writes per second that you expect your application to require, and you are billed based on that. Furthermore, if you can forecast your capacity requirements you can also reserve a portion of DynamoDB provisioned capacity and optimize your costs even further.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIt is important to note that DynamoDB auto scaling modifies provisioned throughput settings only when the actual workload stays elevated or depressed for a sustained period of several minutes. This applies to scaling up or down the provisioned capacity of a DynamoDB table. In the case that you have an occasional usage spike auto scaling might not be able to react in time. This sometimes can be mitigated from DynamoDB burst capacity where DynamoDB reserves a portion of the unused provisioned capacity for later bursts of throughput. The burst capacity is limited though and these extra capacity units can be consumed quickly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis means that provisioned capacity is best for you if you have relatively predictable application traffic (the given use case mentions that the application experiences predictable demand), run applications whose traffic is consistent, and ramps up or down gradually.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement AWS Application Auto Scaling to adjust provisioned capacity to meet the maximum demand seen in the last 12 months\u003c/strong\u003e - There is no need to have the Application Auto Scaling to adjust provisioned capacity to meet the maximum demand seen in the last 12 months, as it is a wasteful use of resources and also a much costlier solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdd Global Secondary Index as well as Local Secondary Index to the DynamoDB table to ensure maximum performance\u003c/strong\u003e - This option has been added as a distractor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon DynamoDB supports two types of secondary indexes:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eGlobal secondary index (GSI) — An index with a partition key and a sort key that can be different from those on the base table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eLocal secondary index (LSI)—An index that has the same partition key as the base table, but a different sort key.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon DynamoDB provides fast access to items in a table by specifying primary key values. However, many applications might benefit from having one or more secondary (or alternate) keys available, to allow efficient access to data with attributes other than the primary key. GSI and LSI can improve performance by improving data access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the use case needs a solution that can adjust to variations in traffic on a predictable basis. GSI and LSI cannot address this requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/AutoScaling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/AutoScaling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/AutoScaling.Console.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/AutoScaling.Console.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/serverless-applications-lens/capacity.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/serverless-applications-lens/capacity.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/SecondaryIndexes.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/SecondaryIndexes.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-2":{"id":"p:3-2","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 2","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003ePREPARE\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eOPTIMIZE\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEXPLAIN\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEXPLAIN ANALYZE\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["PREPARE tạo câu lệnh có thể thực thi sau, thường có tham số. Nó không báo chi phí thực thi từng bước.","OPTIMIZE tối ưu bố cục tệp của bảng Iceberg theo hỗ trợ engine, không phân tích kế hoạch runtime của truy vấn bất kỳ.","EXPLAIN cho biết kế hoạch dự kiến, nhưng không cung cấp đầy đủ số liệu chạy thực tế từng phép toán như EXPLAIN ANALYZE.","EXPLAIN ANALYZE thực thi truy vấn và trả kế hoạch kèm thống kê runtime. Dùng để tìm bước tốn tài nguyên; vì có chạy truy vấn nên vẫn có chi phí quét."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses Amazon Athena for ad-hoc analysis of customer data in its data lake hosted on Amazon S3. A data engineer at the company wants to understand the detailed breakdown for each of the SQL query’s run plan and the computational cost of each operation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following Amazon Athena statements can be used to accomplish this task?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:8:5":{"id":"e:8:5","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 5","correct":[0],"answers":["Set up a target data stream within the audit AWS account. Create an IAM role and a trust policy to authorize CloudWatch Logs to send data into the stream. Establish a subscription filter in the production AWS account.","Set up a target data stream within the production AWS account. Within the production AWS account, create an IAM role with cross-account permissions for Kinesis Data Streams located in the audit AWS account.","Set up a target data stream within the audit AWS account. Create an IAM role and a trust policy to authorize CloudWatch Logs to send data into the stream. Establish a subscription filter in the audit AWS account.","Utilize AWS CloudTrail within the audit AWS account to directly access and analyze the logs located in the production AWS account."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has a production AWS account and a separate audit AWS account. The company’s policy requires all logs from the production account to be sent to the audit account for real-time analysis and long-term storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe production account uses Amazon CloudWatch Logs to aggregate logs from Amazon EC2 instances. A data engineer must set up a system to stream these logs to an Amazon Kinesis Data Stream in the audit account.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options would meet the company’s requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eYou can use subscriptions to get access to a real-time feed of log events from CloudWatch Logs and have it delivered to other services such as an Amazon Kinesis stream, an Amazon Data Firehose stream, or AWS Lambda for custom processing, analysis, or loading to other systems. When log events are sent to the receiving service, they are base64 encoded and compressed with the gzip format.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo begin subscribing to log events, create the receiving resource, such as a Kinesis Data Streams stream, where the events will be delivered. A subscription filter defines the filter pattern to use for filtering which log events get delivered to your AWS resource, as well as information about where to send matching log events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/468e5eb4de352a0b5e0f48d099db76f6e638b2d296e55c96058fdb1e04793f85.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can create subscriptions at the account level and at the log group level. Each account can have one account-level subscription filter. Each log group can have up to two subscription filters associated with it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up a target data stream within the audit AWS account. Create an IAM role and a trust policy to authorize CloudWatch Logs to send data into the stream. Establish a subscription filter in the production AWS account.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up a target data stream within the production AWS account. Within the production AWS account, create an IAM role with cross-account permissions for Kinesis Data Streams located in the audit AWS account\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;The logs need to be sent to the audit AWS account, not stored in the production account. Additionally, while it does create an IAM role with cross-account permissions, it does so in the wrong account. The IAM role and trust policy need to be created in the audit AWS account to allow CloudWatch Logs to put data into the stream\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up a target data stream within the audit AWS account. Create an IAM role and a trust policy to authorize CloudWatch Logs to send data into the stream. Establish a subscription filter in the audit AWS account\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because it sets up the subscription filter in the audit account instead of the production account. The subscription filter needs to be in the production AWS account to capture the logs and send them to the audit account. While it correctly sets up a data stream and IAM role in the audit AWS account, the misplacement of the subscription filter makes this option incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize AWS CloudTrail within the audit AWS account to directly access and analyze the logs located in the production AWS account\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;AWS CloudTrail can track user activity and API usage, but it\u0027s not capable of streaming logs between accounts. Therefore, this option does not meet the company’s requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReference:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/Subscriptions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/Subscriptions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/SubscriptionFilters.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/SubscriptionFilters.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon CloudWatch Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-cloudwatch/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-cloudwatch/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:43":{"id":"e:2:43","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 43","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConvert the sort-merge join to a broadcast join so Spark sends one DataFrame to all executors before joining\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConvert the Spark DataFrames to AWS Glue DynamicFrames and perform a DynamicFrame join so AWS Glue can avoid local shuffle storage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable the AWS Glue Spark shuffle manager so the job writes shuffle files and spill data to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Glue crawler for both source datasets and update the Data Catalog before the job runs so Spark can reduce shuffle disk usage\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is tuning an AWS Glue Apache Spark ETL job that joins two large customer activity datasets. The job uses a sort-merge join between two equally sized Spark DataFrames.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDuring the shuffle stage, the job fails with the following error: No space left on device. The data engineer must resolve the failure without changing the join to a small-table optimization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will resolve the error?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable the AWS Glue Spark shuffle manager so the job writes shuffle files and spill data to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe job fails because a sort-merge join of two large DataFrames can create a large shuffle. Spark writes shuffle and spill data to local disk on the workers, and the job can fail with a No space left on device error when the local disk is exhausted.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe AWS Glue Spark shuffle manager can use Amazon S3 for Spark shuffle storage. By writing shuffle files and spill data to S3, the job is no longer limited only by the local disk capacity of the AWS Glue workers during the large shuffle stage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution directly addresses the root cause of the error. The issue is not the DataFrame API, catalog metadata, or timeout behavior. The job needs a shuffle storage configuration that can handle the volume of intermediate data produced by the large sort-merge join.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/079afe72d2d0a1a3741f9668480a11b1f22f9e336d87b3ce583bd271aca651f4.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/079afe72d2d0a1a3741f9668480a11b1f22f9e336d87b3ce583bd271aca651f4.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-amazon-s3-shuffle-in-aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-amazon-s3-shuffle-in-aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0842aad8cb085d67098fde31c7fc78f71125539dcd26c2996091a9cbd06837d9.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0842aad8cb085d67098fde31c7fc78f71125539dcd26c2996091a9cbd06837d9.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-amazon-s3-shuffle-in-aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-amazon-s3-shuffle-in-aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConvert the Spark DataFrames to AWS Glue DynamicFrames and perform a DynamicFrame join so AWS Glue can avoid local shuffle storage\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue DynamicFrames provide AWS Glue-specific abstractions for semi-structured data and ETL transformations. They can be useful when working with schema flexibility, choice types, and AWS Glue transformations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that converting DataFrames to DynamicFrames does not remove the need to shuffle data for a large join. A DynamicFrame join over two large datasets can still require substantial shuffle processing and can still hit disk limits.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Glue crawler for both source datasets and update the Data Catalog before the job runs so Spark can reduce shuffle disk usage\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue crawlers can discover schemas and update the AWS Glue Data Catalog. This helps ETL jobs and query engines understand table metadata and source layouts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCatalog updates do not solve local disk exhaustion during a Spark shuffle. The failure occurs while Spark executes the sort-merge join, so the appropriate fix is to move shuffle storage to Amazon S3 by using the AWS Glue Spark shuffle manager.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConvert the sort-merge join to a broadcast join so Spark sends one DataFrame to all executors before joining\u003c/strong\u003e - A broadcast join can improve performance when one side of the join is small enough to fit in executor memory. Spark sends the smaller dataset to all executors and avoids a large shuffle for that side.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because the question states that both DataFrames are large and equally sized. Broadcasting a large DataFrame can exhaust memory and is not the correct optimization for this join.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark-shuffle-manager.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark-shuffle-manager.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/optimize-shuffles.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/optimize-shuffles.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/glue-no-spaces-on-device-error\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/glue-no-spaces-on-device-error\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-amazon-s3-shuffle-in-aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-amazon-s3-shuffle-in-aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/best-practices-to-scale-apache-spark-jobs-and-partition-data-with-aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/best-practices-to-scale-apache-spark-jobs-and-partition-data-with-aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-37":{"id":"p:4-37","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 37","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Quality rules with ML-based anomaly detection to learn unusual vibration behavior and detect records that do not include event timestamps\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon CloudWatch anomaly detection on custom vibration metrics and configure AWS Glue crawlers to identify records that have missing timestamp values\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Glue DataBrew project to profile the telemetry data, define completeness rules for event_timestamp, and configure numeric range validation for vibration values\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Glue DynamicFrame with a custom validation operator and use Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler transforms to validate timestamps and vibration ranges\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Anomaly detection học mẫu bất thường, còn bài này có điều kiện cố định: giá trị từ 0 đến 85 và timestamp không được thiếu. Glue Data Quality cũng làm được bằng quy tắc tường minh, nhưng lựa chọn này lại dùng ML không cần thiết.","CloudWatch theo dõi metric chứ không kiểm tra đầy đủ từng dòng dữ liệu ETL. Glue crawler khám phá schema và cũng không xác thực timestamp của từng bản ghi.","DataBrew cung cấp profiling và quy tắc kiểm tra trực quan. Kiểm tra độ đầy đủ của event_timestamp và khoảng giá trị của vibration đáp ứng hai điều kiện đã biết với ít mã tùy chỉnh.","Tự viết operator rồi kết hợp thêm Data Wrangler tạo nhiều bước và mã cần bảo trì. Các quy tắc kiểm tra trực quan của DataBrew đơn giản hơn cho điều kiện cố định này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA heavy equipment manufacturer uses AWS Glue jobs to prepare IoT telemetry data for predictive maintenance reporting. The telemetry dataset includes vibration readings, operating temperature, machine identifiers, and event timestamps. A data engineer must add automated data quality checks that identify vibration readings outside the expected range of 0 to 85 mm/s and records that are missing event_timestamp values. The solution must require minimal coding and must automatically flag the specified issues.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:7:7":{"id":"e:7:7","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 7","correct":[0],"answers":["AWS Glue","AWS Lambda","Amazon Data Firehose","AWS Batch"],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA multinational corporation is working on a large-scale data processing project. The project will continuously consume streaming data from an Apache Kafka cluster, transform the data in-flight, and deliver it into an Amazon S3 bucket. The tasks involved in this project are complex and long-running, often taking over 15 minutes to complete.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich AWS service would be the best choice to handle these long-running tasks? \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy for users to prepare and load their data for analytics. It\u0027s designed to handle complex tasks that often take over 15 minutes to complete, which aligns with the requirements of the scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/db5b79b838cd1a7d75258f897436ba1e65fbe4b3658af3c1a2122abb5d3ed823.png\" alt=\"AWS Glue\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can create streaming extract, transform, and load (ETL) jobs that run continuously and consume data from streaming sources like Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, Apache Kafka, and Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (Amazon MSK). The jobs cleanse and transform the data and then load the results into Amazon S3 data lakes or JDBC data stores.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the scenario where a streaming ETL is required, AWS Glue is the best choice because it\u0027s designed for continuous streaming data processing. It can handle long tasks by transforming and loading data into Amazon S3 in real-time, which matches the project\u0027s needs for handling streaming data from Apache Kafka and delivering it efficiently to S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAWS Lambda\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because it is a serverless compute service that runs your code in response to events and automatically manages the underlying compute resources for you. However, AWS Lambda functions have a maximum execution time of 15 minutes. This makes it unsuitable for tasks that often take over 15 minutes to complete, as specified in the scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Data Firehose\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it is a fully managed service that delivers real-time streaming data to destinations such as Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, Amazon OpenSearch Service, and Splunk. While it typically supports data transformation through AWS Lambda, the complexity and duration of the tasks described in the scenario exceed the capabilities of simple transformations, which is the 15-minute execution limit of AWS Lambda functions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAWS Batch\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because it primarily enables developers, scientists, and engineers to easily and efficiently run hundreds of thousands of batch computing jobs on AWS.\u0026nbsp;However, it cannot directly consume data from a streaming source. It is more suited for scenarios where workloads are not continuous streams but rather discrete batches of data that can be processed independently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automate-alerting-and-reporting-for-aws-glue-job-resource-usage/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automate-alerting-and-reporting-for-aws-glue-job-resource-usage/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:18":{"id":"e:7:18","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 18","correct":[0],"answers":["Migrate all data from Apache Hive to an Amazon S3 bucket to create a centralized data source data and utilize AWS Glue Data Catalog for metadata storage. Leverage Amazon EMR and Athena for data processing.","Consolidate all data in HDFS by moving the data from Amazon S3. Transition Athena queries to Presto on Amazon EMR. Continue utilizing Amazon EMR for data processing.","Centralize all data in Amazon S3. Configure Amazon EMR to integrate with AWS Glue Data Catalog and adopt Spark SQL for Athena queries. Maintain the use of Amazon EMR for ongoing data operations.","Move all data stored in Amazon S3 to HDFS. Convert Athena queries to Spark SQL, and use EMR File System (EMRFS) for data processing tasks."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has expanded its AWS infrastructure, utilizing Amazon S3 for data storage and Amazon Athena for query execution. With growth, the complexity and volume of data have increased, leading to the adoption of Amazon EMR for advanced data processing using Apache Spark and machine learning libraries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe data collection is cataloged on both a Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS) via an Apache Hive metastore and a custom AWS Glue Data Catalog. The data engineering team faces the challenge of designing a durable data store that utilizes both storage systems.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo achieve a centralized data management strategy, which approach should the data engineering team adopt with the LEAST migration effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eThe AWS Glue Data Catalog serves as a centralized metadata repository, containing references to data employed as both origins and destinations for extract, transform, and load (ETL) tasks within AWS Glue. This catalog facilitates more efficient management of data sources, further enhanced by its compatibility with Amazon EMR, Amazon Athena, and other AWS services. Leveraging Amazon EMR, which supports various processing frameworks such as Apache Spark, expands capabilities for advanced data processing tasks, including batch processing, streaming, and machine learning.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA database in the AWS Glue Data Catalog is a container that holds tables. You use databases to organize your tables into separate categories. Databases are created when you run a crawler or add a table manually. The database list in the AWS Glue console displays descriptions for all your databases.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/807e46d5bf9e6d8e71ce7ba2d51e402ead54403ee39356869e338960fb691349.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe latest versions of Amazon EMR\u0026nbsp;allow you to configure Apache Hive to use the AWS Glue Data Catalog as its metastore. This configuration is recommended when you require a persistent metastore or a metastore shared by different clusters, services, applications, or AWS accounts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eCompanies might want to create AWS Glue Data Catalog tables manually and then keep them updated with AWS Glue crawlers. Crawlers running on a schedule can add new partitions and update the tables with any schema changes. This also applies to tables migrated from an Apache Hive metastore.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMigrate all data from Apache Hive to an Amazon S3 bucket to create a centralized data source data and utilize AWS Glue Data Catalog for metadata storage. Leverage Amazon EMR and Athena for data processing.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConsolidate all data in HDFS by moving the data from Amazon S3. Transition Athena queries to Presto on Amazon EMR. Continue utilizing Amazon EMR for data processing\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect\u0026nbsp;because it simply overlooks the primary concern of data durability. HDFS, when used within EMR, is considered an ephemeral storage that could potentially lead to data loss in the event of cluster termination.\u0026nbsp;Additionally, this approach entails significant migration effort to shift from Athena to Presto, increasing complexity without addressing the need for a centralized data catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCentralize all data in Amazon S3. Configure Amazon EMR to integrate with AWS Glue Data Catalog and adopt Spark SQL for Athena queries. Maintain the use of Amazon EMR for ongoing data operations\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect\u0026nbsp;because it mainly suggests\u0026nbsp;migrating Athena queries to Spark SQL. While integrating Amazon S3 with AWS Glue Data Catalog and Amazon EMR is beneficial, this move is not necessary as Athena can directly support SQL queries on data stored in S3 without the need for conversion to Spark SQL, making this step unnecessary and potentially adding complexity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMove all data stored in Amazon S3 to HDFS. Convert Athena queries to Spark SQL, and use EMR File System (EMRFS) for data processing tasks\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because it primarily relies on HDFS for centralized data storage, which does not offer the same level of durability and scalability as Amazon S3. This option also requires converting Athena queries to Spark SQL, which adds unnecessary migration effort and complexity without providing a clear benefit over using Athena for direct queries on data stored in Amazon S3. Moreover,\u0026nbsp;EMRFS simply provides the convenience of storing persistent data in Amazon S3 for Amazon EMR and not for running data processing tasks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-hive-metastore-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-hive-metastore-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon S3 Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:28":{"id":"e:7:28","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 28","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eIncrease the \u003ccode\u003edelaySeconds\u003c/code\u003e setting for the SQS queue.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eDecrease the \u003ccode\u003evisibilityTimeout\u003c/code\u003e setting for the SQS queue.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eIncrease the \u003ccode\u003emaxReceiveCount\u003c/code\u003e setting for the SQS queue.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eAdjust the \u003ccode\u003ereceiveMessageWaitTimeSeconds\u003c/code\u003e setting for the SQS queue.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA data engineer uses an on-premises application to process messages from an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) queue. The application has retry logic implemented to address message processing failures.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eHowever, due to a network outage, many messages failed to process and ended up in the dead-letter queues (DLQ). The engineer wants to mitigate the impact of similar network disruptions in the future.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich solution should the engineer implement to meet the requirement?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon SQS\u003c/strong\u003e is a fully managed message queuing service that provides a virtual queue acting as a buffer between the components of an application. It enables you to decouple and scale microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eDelaySeconds\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e setting refers to the delay period for a delay queue. This sets how long any messages sent to the queue remain invisible to consumers after being added to the queue. The default is 0 seconds, and the maximum is 15 minutes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eVisibilityTimeout\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/code\u003e is the period of time during which Amazon SQS prevents other consuming components from receiving and processing a message after it has been received from a queue (but not yet deleted). This helps prevent a message from being received and processed more than once.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMaxReceiveCount\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/code\u003e refers to the number of times a message can be received from the queue before being deleted. When the limit is reached, the message will be sent to the dead letter queue. The default Maximum received is 10.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReceiveMessageWaitTimeSeconds\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/code\u003e is an optional parameter that sets the duration (in seconds) for waiting for a message to arrive in the queue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7192849758d8a034e8fd7ad67d196fa7296d66c604dfbee3a7e03bef518746de.png\" alt=\"Dead-letter queue\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo reduce the number of premature messages sent to the DLQ, the engineer should increase the \u003ccode\u003eMaxReceiveCount\u003c/code\u003e setting for the SQS queue (In the AWS Management Console, it is the same with the option labeled \"Maximum receives\"). By increasing the \u003ccode\u003eMaxReceiveCount\u003c/code\u003e, it allows the on-premises application to retry processing messages more times before they are sent to the DLQ. This gives the application more opportunities to successfully process messages even during brief network issues.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eIncrease the \u003ccode\u003eMaxReceiveCount\u003c/code\u003e setting for the SQS queue.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the \u003ccode\u003eDelaySeconds\u003c/code\u003e setting for the SQS queue\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because delaying message delivery won\u0027t effectively handle transient failures. It merely postpones the delivery of messages without providing additional attempts for message processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDecrease the \u003ccode\u003eVisibilityTimeout\u003c/code\u003e setting for the SQS queue\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Lowering this timeout can typically lead to messages becoming available to other consumers too quickly before the initial processing attempt can be retried, exacerbating the problem.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdjust the \u003ccode\u003eReceiveMessageWaitTimeSeconds\u003c/code\u003e setting for the SQS queue\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While long polling can primarily reduce the number of empty responses from SQS by waiting for messages to arrive, it does not influence the retry behavior or the movement of messages to the DLQ.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-dead-letter-queues.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-dead-letter-queues.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/working-with-messages.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/working-with-messages.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon SQS Cheat Sheet: \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sqs/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-49":{"id":"p:3-49","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 49","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS DMS to continuously replicate the RDS for MySQL database into Amazon Redshift and run consolidated dashboard queries in Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Athena federated queries to query the RDS for MySQL database, unload historical Redshift tables to Amazon S3 each day, and join both datasets through Athena views\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift federated queries to directly query current-day RDS for MySQL transactions and join the results with existing Redshift historical tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eExport current-day RDS for MySQL data to Amazon S3 on a schedule, load the files into Redshift staging tables, and join the staged data with historical tables\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["DMS tạo bản sao liên tục nhưng cần task, schema đích và giám sát replication. Không cần thiết khi chỉ muốn JOIN dữ liệu sống với lịch sử.","UNLOAD lịch sử mỗi ngày tạo bản sao S3 và đường Athena bổ sung. Redshift đã có dữ liệu lịch sử và khả năng federated query.","Redshift Federated Query đọc dữ liệu MySQL được hỗ trợ tại nguồn rồi JOIN với bảng lịch sử nội bộ. Giảm bản sao/pipeline đồng bộ cho trường hợp này.","Export và nạp theo lịch tăng độ trễ, lưu trữ tạm và bảo trì. Không trực tiếp bằng truy vấn liên nguồn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA grocery delivery company stores current-day order transactions in an Amazon RDS for MySQL database. The company stores historical fulfillment and sales analytics in Amazon Redshift. Reporting teams need daily dashboards that combine live order activity with historical demand patterns, delivery trends, and customer segments. The company needs near real-time insights while minimizing data transfer costs and maintenance overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-47":{"id":"p:3-47","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 47","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS DMS to replicate the Oracle database to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL instance, then run Lambda queries against the refund_requests table and call the REST API\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate one AWS DMS task to send all Oracle table data to Amazon S3, create another AWS DMS task to stream refund_requests changes to Kinesis Data Streams, and use Lambda to call the REST API\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS DMS task to replicate the Oracle database to Amazon Redshift, use the Redshift Data API to query refund_requests changes, and unload all warehouse data to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Oracle export utilities on a scheduled EC2 instance to copy all tables to Amazon S3, then run a containerized service on Amazon ECS to poll refund_requests and call the REST API\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Sao chép mọi bảng sang một RDS khác đắt hơn S3 cho lưu trữ phân tích tương lai. Polling bảng hoàn tiền thêm logic và độ trễ.","Một DMS task đưa mọi bảng tới S3 để lưu kinh tế; task khác gửi CDC bảng refund_requests tới Kinesis. Lambda tiêu thụ sự kiện để gọi API ngoài, tách rõ hai nhu cầu.","Đưa toàn bộ dữ liệu vào Redshift rồi UNLOAD ra S3 thêm bước và chi phí. Truy vấn tìm thay đổi cũng không trực tiếp bằng luồng CDC.","EC2 export và ECS polling cần vận hành máy chủ/container, lịch và mã theo dõi thay đổi. DMS với Kinesis/Lambda giảm các công việc đó."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA travel booking company uses an Amazon RDS for Oracle database to store reservation, payment, customer, and support case data for an ecommerce platform. The company needs to make data from all database tables available for future analytics in a cost-effective storage layer. The company also needs to process changes from a table named refund_requests in near real time and send each relevant change to an external fraud review service through a REST API.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:8:16":{"id":"e:8:16","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 16","correct":[1],"answers":["Configure an AWS Database Migration Service replication with the Analytics team\u0027s cluster as the source and the Reporting team\u0027s as the target.","Configure Amazon Redshift Data Sharing with the Reporting team\u0027s cluster as the consumer.","In the Analytics team\u0027s cluster, create a materialized view of the required tables and give the Reporting team access.","In the Reporting team’s cluster, create an Amazon Redshift Spectrum table using the unloaded copy of data from the Analytics team\u0027s cluster to an Amazon S3 bucket."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company operates two Amazon Redshift clusters. The company’s Analytics Team uses the first cluster to derive critical business insights, while the Reporting Team uses the second cluster for report generation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Reporting team is tasked with publishing near real-time reports on an Amazon QuickSight dashboard. To achieve this, they require joining data from their cluster with specific datasets in the Analytics Team’s cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will satisfy these criteria with the LEAST impact on the Analytic’s team cluster?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift is a fully managed, petabyte-scale data warehouse service in the cloud. The Amazon Redshift service manages all of the work of setting up, operating, and scaling a data warehouse. These tasks include provisioning capacity, monitoring and backing up the cluster and applying patches and upgrades to the Amazon Redshift engine.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift can also securely share read access to live data across Amazon Redshift clusters, workgroups, AWS accounts, and AWS Regions without manually moving or copying the data with \u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshift data sharing\u003c/strong\u003e. Since the data is live, all users can see the most up-to-date and consistent information in Amazon Redshift as soon as it’s updated.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshift data sharing Use cases:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Supporting different kinds of business-critical workloads\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Enable seamless collaboration across teams and business groups for broader analytics, data science, and cross-product impact analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Delivering data as a service\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Sharing data between environments\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Licensing access to data in Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/137b817b78e2fd7ce40087a7cfa2c339c76fd0124ca1d2b21fce261b97867f61.png\" alt=\"https://media.tutorialsdojo.com/public/Redshuft Data Sharing .png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift data sharing allows you to securely and easily share data for read purposes across different Amazon Redshift clusters without the complexity and delays associated with data copies and data movement. Data can be shared at many levels, including schemas, tables, views, and user-defined functions, providing fine-grained access controls that can be tailored for different users and businesses that all need access to the data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWith this feature, you can seamlessly share live data between clusters. This option suits the requirement in the scenario, as the shared data will be made available to the consumer as quickly as possible. It will ensure transactional consistency and provide up-to-date and consistent data views to the Reporting team.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon Redshift Data Sharing with the Reporting team\u0027s cluster as the consumer.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an AWS Database Migration Service replication with the Analytics team\u0027s cluster as the source and the Reporting team\u0027s as the target\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While this method can replicate data from one Redshift cluster to another, this approach involves moving data continuously, which can add unnecessary load and complexity to the Analytics team\u0027s cluster. This could potentially impact the performance of the analytics operations, especially if the data volumes are large.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIn the Analytics team\u0027s cluster, create a materialized view of the required tables and give the Reporting team access\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Creating a materialized view in the Analytics team\u0027s cluster could potentially impact the cluster\u0027s performance since it requires additional storage and computational resources to maintain the view, especially if the data changes frequently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e In the Reporting team’s cluster, create an Amazon Redshift Spectrum table using the unloaded copy of data from the Analytics team\u0027s cluster to an Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Even though this will allow the Reporting team to query the same data from the Analytics cluster, it still will not satisfy the requirement, as unloading data to an S3 bucket could lead to delays in data availability for real-time reporting. Moreover, the unload operation imposes an additional workload on the Analytics team\u0027s cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-amazon-redshift-data-sharing-preview/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-amazon-redshift-data-sharing-preview/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/data_sharing_intro.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/data_sharing_intro.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/datashare-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/datashare-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:32":{"id":"e:2:32","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 32","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval storage class\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access storage class\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access storage class\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 Standard storage class\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA leading video streaming service delivers billions of hours of content from Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) to customers around the world. Amazon S3 also serves as the data lake for its big data analytics solution. The data lake has a staging zone where intermediary query results are kept only for 24 hours. These results are also heavily referenced by other parts of the analytics pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following is the MOST cost-effective strategy for storing this intermediary query data?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 Standard storage class\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Standard offers high durability, availability, and performance object storage for frequently accessed data. Because it delivers low latency and high throughput, S3 Standard is appropriate for a wide variety of use cases, including cloud applications, dynamic websites, content distribution, mobile and gaming applications, and big data analytics.\nAs there is no minimum storage duration charge and no retrieval fee (remember that intermediary query results are heavily referenced by other parts of the analytics pipeline), this is the MOST cost-effective storage class amongst the given options.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval storage class\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval delivers the fastest access to archive storage, with the same throughput and milliseconds access as the S3 Standard and S3 Standard-IA storage classes. S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval is ideal for archive data that needs immediate access, such as medical images, news media assets, or user-generated content archives.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe minimum storage duration charge is 90 days, so this option is NOT cost-effective because intermediary query results need to be kept only for 24 hours. Hence this option is not correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access storage class\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon S3 Standard-IA is for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. S3 Standard-IA offers high durability, high throughput, and low latency of S3 Standard, with a low per GB storage price and per GB retrieval fee. This combination of low cost and high performance makes S3 Standard-IA ideal for long-term storage, backups, and as a data store for disaster recovery files.\nThe minimum storage duration charge is 30 days, so this option is NOT cost-effective because intermediary query results need to be kept only for 24 hours. Hence this option is not correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the intermediary query results in Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access storage class\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon S3 One Zone-IA is for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. Unlike other S3 Storage Classes which store data in a minimum of three Availability Zones (AZs), S3 One Zone-IA stores data in a single AZ and costs 20% less than S3 Standard-IA.\nThe minimum storage duration charge is 30 days, so this option is NOT cost-effective because intermediary query results need to be kept only for 24 hours. Hence this option is not correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo summarize again, S3 Standard-IA and S3 One Zone-IA have a minimum storage duration charge of 30 days (so instead of 24 hours, you end up paying for 30 days). S3 Standard-IA and S3 One Zone-IA also have retrieval charges (as the results are heavily referenced by other parts of the analytics pipeline, so the retrieval costs would be pretty high). Therefore, these storage classes are not cost-optimal for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:33":{"id":"e:4:33","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 33","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon MSK to capture the events, use Amazon EMR to process the data, and use federated queries to access the data from Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon DynamoDB Streams to capture the events, use AWS Glue to process the records, and use a zero-ETL integration to load the data into Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to capture the events and use Amazon Redshift streaming ingestion to load the data directly into Redshift materialized views\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to capture the events, invoke AWS Lambda functions to transform the stream records, write the transformed events to Amazon S3 through Amazon Data Firehose, and schedule Amazon Redshift COPY jobs for analytics loading\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA global fashion retailer is building a real-time analytics platform for its ecommerce website and mobile application. The company must capture product-view, cart, checkout, and recommendation-click events. The platform must process hundreds of megabytes of events every second and make the data available in Amazon Redshift with the lowest possible query latency for analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to capture the events and use Amazon Redshift streaming ingestion to load the data directly into Redshift materialized views\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams is designed to capture high-throughput streaming data from sources such as clickstreams, application logs, and user activity events. It can support the ingestion scale required for hundreds of megabytes of ecommerce event data every second when the stream is configured with sufficient capacity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift streaming ingestion can read directly from Kinesis Data Streams into Amazon Redshift materialized views. This avoids staging the streaming data in Amazon S3 before loading it into Redshift, which reduces ingestion delay and helps make fresh clickstream data available for analytics quickly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution provides the least query latency for analytics because Redshift can query the materialized views that receive streaming data. The architecture removes intermediate batch loading steps such as Firehose-to-S3 delivery and Redshift COPY jobs, so analysts can work with recently ingested events with lower end-to-end latency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f3ded058c2136947f0f9d8ea6751e080a5db0199117650df00b3180e6d032731.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f3ded058c2136947f0f9d8ea6751e080a5db0199117650df00b3180e6d032731.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d8c8644f331394de057c4c819cb0ed3cbe6c2b2343253dd6cb604bdaad41ea79.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d8c8644f331394de057c4c819cb0ed3cbe6c2b2343253dd6cb604bdaad41ea79.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to capture the events, invoke AWS Lambda functions to transform the stream records, write the transformed events to Amazon S3 through Amazon Data Firehose, and schedule Amazon Redshift COPY jobs for analytics loading\u003c/strong\u003e - This option uses Kinesis Data Streams for ingestion and Lambda for transformation, which can be valid parts of a streaming architecture. It also uses Firehose and Amazon S3 to stage the transformed data before loading it into Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe staging and scheduled COPY steps increase analytics latency. The requirement asks for the least query latency, so Redshift streaming ingestion into materialized views is a better fit than a Firehose-to-S3-to-COPY pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon MSK to capture the events, use Amazon EMR to process the data, and use federated queries to access the data from Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon MSK can capture high-throughput streaming events, and Amazon EMR can process large-scale data. These services can support complex streaming and batch processing architectures.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the option does not load the streaming data directly into Redshift for low-latency analytics. Federated queries are not the right mechanism to minimize query latency for freshly ingested clickstream data in Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon DynamoDB Streams to capture the events, use AWS Glue to process the records, and use a zero-ETL integration to load the data into Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - DynamoDB Streams captures item-level changes from a DynamoDB table. It is useful for change data capture from DynamoDB workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe source data in this scenario is clickstream activity from an ecommerce website and mobile application. DynamoDB Streams is not the correct ingestion service for capturing the original high-volume event stream, and AWS Glue plus zero-ETL does not provide the lowest-latency streaming path into Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/introduction.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/introduction.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/new-for-amazon-redshift-general-availability-of-streaming-ingestion-for-kinesis-data-streams-and-managed-streaming-for-apache-kafka/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/new-for-amazon-redshift-general-availability-of-streaming-ingestion-for-kinesis-data-streams-and-managed-streaming-for-apache-kafka/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:46":{"id":"e:3:46","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 46","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate AWS Glue crawlers for the S3 data lake, schedule crawler runs daily, and enable Amazon DataZone to infer data quality scores from crawler schema changes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS Glue ETL jobs with an Evaluate Data Quality transform, define rules inside each job, and enable data quality for an Amazon DataZone AWS Glue data source\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate DQDL rulesets for AWS Glue tables, schedule the rulesets daily, and enable data quality for an Amazon DataZone Amazon Redshift data source\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a DQDL ruleset for each AWS Glue table, schedule the ruleset to run, and enable data quality for an Amazon DataZone AWS Glue data source\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company uses Amazon DataZone as its business catalog and governance portal. The company stores curated transaction data in an Amazon S3 data lake and catalogs the datasets in the AWS Glue Data Catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer needs to make AWS Glue Data Quality scores visible to business users in the Amazon DataZone portal. The data quality checks must run on a recurring schedule for specific AWS Glue tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a DQDL ruleset for each AWS Glue table, schedule the ruleset to run, and enable data quality for an Amazon DataZone AWS Glue data source\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Data Quality supports rulesets that use Data Quality Definition Language, or DQDL. The data engineer can define rules for specific AWS Glue Data Catalog tables and schedule the rulesets to run on a recurring basis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon DataZone can import AWS Glue Data Quality results for AWS Glue assets. To make the scores visible in the Amazon DataZone portal, the engineer must configure the Amazon DataZone project with an AWS Glue data source and enable data quality for that data source.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution aligns with the requirement because the company stores data in Amazon S3, catalogs the data in AWS Glue Data Catalog, and wants AWS Glue Data Quality scores to appear in Amazon DataZone. The scheduled AWS Glue Data Quality rules generate the scores, and Amazon DataZone publishes those scores to the business catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure AWS Glue ETL jobs with an Evaluate Data Quality transform, define rules inside each job, and enable data quality for an Amazon DataZone AWS Glue data source\u003c/strong\u003e - The Evaluate Data Quality transform can evaluate data quality rules inside AWS Glue ETL jobs. This can be useful when a team wants data quality checks embedded directly in a transformation pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that the requirement is to publish AWS Glue Data Quality scores for AWS Glue Data Catalog assets to Amazon DataZone. A scheduled DQDL ruleset applied to the Glue table and enabled through an Amazon DataZone AWS Glue data source is the more direct fit.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate DQDL rulesets for AWS Glue tables, schedule the rulesets daily, and enable data quality for an Amazon DataZone Amazon Redshift data source\u003c/strong\u003e - DQDL rulesets scheduled against AWS Glue tables are appropriate for generating AWS Glue Data Quality scores. This part of the option matches the data quality requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because it configures Amazon DataZone with an Amazon Redshift data source. Amazon DataZone data quality integration through the portal is for AWS Glue inventory assets, so the data source should be AWS Glue rather than Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate AWS Glue crawlers for the S3 data lake, schedule crawler runs daily, and enable Amazon DataZone to infer data quality scores from crawler schema changes\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue crawlers can discover schemas and update AWS Glue Data Catalog metadata for datasets in Amazon S3. They are useful for keeping table definitions current.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCrawler runs do not generate AWS Glue Data Quality scores. Schema discovery is not the same as executing DQDL rulesets, and Amazon DataZone cannot infer data quality scores only from crawler schema changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/datazone-data-quality.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/datazone-data-quality.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/create-glue-data-source.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/datazone/latest/userguide/create-glue-data-source.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-getting-started.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-getting-started.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-datazone-now-integrates-with-aws-glue-data-quality-and-external-data-quality-solutions/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-datazone-now-integrates-with-aws-glue-data-quality-and-external-data-quality-solutions/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:10":{"id":"e:4:10","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 10","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSend application logs to CloudWatch Logs log groups, stream the log events to OpenSearch Service with subscription filters, and create OpenSearch dashboards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse CloudWatch Logs anomaly detection to identify unusual log patterns and use those anomaly results as the primary query and visualization layer\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eExport CloudWatch Logs log groups to Amazon S3 on a schedule by using Lambda, then query the exported log files with Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eExport CloudWatch Logs to Amazon S3 every few minutes with a scheduled Lambda function, catalog the exported files with AWS Glue, and build Athena queries with QuickSight dashboards\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company runs several customer-facing applications on AWS. Each application already writes operational and application logs, and the operations team wants to analyze errors, latency patterns, and user activity across the applications. The team must query and visualize the logs in near real time so engineers can troubleshoot issues quickly from dashboards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSend application logs to CloudWatch Logs log groups, stream the log events to OpenSearch Service with subscription filters, and create OpenSearch dashboards\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon CloudWatch Logs can collect application logs in log groups. CloudWatch Logs subscription filters can stream matching log events to supported destinations, including Amazon OpenSearch Service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon OpenSearch Service is designed for search and log analytics use cases. After the log events are indexed in OpenSearch Service, the operations team can create queries, visualizations, and dashboards in OpenSearch Dashboards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution meets the near real time requirement because logs can stream from CloudWatch Logs to OpenSearch Service soon after ingestion. It also gives engineers a managed search and visualization layer for troubleshooting application behavior across multiple workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eExport CloudWatch Logs log groups to Amazon S3 on a schedule by using Lambda, then query the exported log files with Athena\u003c/strong\u003e - Exporting CloudWatch Logs data to Amazon S3 and querying the files with Athena can support historical log analysis. Athena is useful when teams need serverless SQL over archived logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that scheduled exports and Athena queries do not provide a near real time log search and dashboard experience. This approach introduces delay and is better suited for periodic analysis than rapid troubleshooting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse CloudWatch Logs anomaly detection to identify unusual log patterns and use those anomaly results as the primary query and visualization layer\u003c/strong\u003e - CloudWatch Logs anomaly detection can help identify unusual patterns in log events. It can be useful for surfacing unexpected behavior without writing every detection rule manually.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option does not provide the complete query and visualization solution required by the company. Anomaly detection identifies patterns, but OpenSearch Service with dashboards is a better fit for interactive near real time log analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eExport CloudWatch Logs to Amazon S3 every few minutes with a scheduled Lambda function, catalog the exported files with AWS Glue, and build Athena queries with QuickSight dashboards\u003c/strong\u003e - This option uses several AWS analytics services that can support log analysis over data stored in Amazon S3. Athena can query log files, AWS Glue can catalog the data, and QuickSight can visualize prepared datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option is incorrect because it creates a batch-oriented pipeline with multiple moving parts and export delays. The requirement is near real-time querying and visualization of application logs, which is better addressed by streaming CloudWatch Logs to Amazon OpenSearch Service with subscription filters.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/CWL_OpenSearch_Stream.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/CWL_OpenSearch_Stream.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/Subscriptions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/Subscriptions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/SubscriptionFilters.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/SubscriptionFilters.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/dashboards.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/dashboards.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/create-an-opensearch-dashboard-with-amazon-opensearch-service/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/create-an-opensearch-dashboard-with-amazon-opensearch-service/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-57":{"id":"p:2-57","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 57","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an SQS standard queue, subscribe the team\u0027s email address to the queue, and create an EventBridge rule that sends failed AWS Glue job events to the queue\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an SNS FIFO topic, subscribe the team\u0027s email address to the topic, and create a Lambda function that publishes messages when AWS Glue job state changes to FAILED\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an SNS standard topic, subscribe the team\u0027s email address to the topic, and create an EventBridge rule that sends AWS Glue FAILED job state change events to the topic\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a CloudWatch alarm on AWS Glue job failure metrics, configure the alarm to write notifications to Amazon S3, and ask the business team to review the S3 objects\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["SQS không hỗ trợ đăng ký trực tiếp địa chỉ email để gửi thông báo. Cần SNS standard topic cho đích email.","SNS FIFO không hỗ trợ email subscription như SNS Standard. Lambda bổ sung cũng không cần thiết cho việc định tuyến sự kiện lỗi đơn giản.","EventBridge lọc Glue Job State Change có trạng thái FAILED rồi chuyển tới SNS Standard. Đội vận hành đăng ký và xác nhận email để nhận thông báo.","Ghi thông báo vào S3 rồi yêu cầu người dùng tự xem không đáp ứng yêu cầu nhận email mỗi khi job lỗi."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is supporting an AWS Glue workflow that validates and transforms daily claims data. The workflow sometimes fails when the input files contain data quality issues. A business operations team needs to receive an email notification whenever an AWS Glue job in the workflow fails in the future.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:27":{"id":"e:2:27","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 27","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS Config remediation actions to automatically restore objects after users delete them from the S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable versioning on the S3 bucket and configure MFA Delete for the bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Replication to copy objects to another bucket and leverage the replica bucket to prevent accidental deletions in the source bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an S3 bucket policy that denies requests that create delete markers for objects in the bucket\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company stores sensitive claims-processing files in an Amazon S3 bucket. The files support downstream compliance investigations and must be protected from accidental deletion by application users and administrators. A data engineer must implement controls that help preserve deleted objects and add stronger protection against permanent deletion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable versioning on the S3 bucket and configure MFA Delete for the bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Versioning helps protect objects from accidental deletion. When a user sends a simple delete request to a versioning-enabled bucket, Amazon S3 does not permanently delete the object. Instead, Amazon S3 inserts a delete marker and preserves the previous object version.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eMFA Delete adds stronger protection for destructive actions. When MFA Delete is enabled, the bucket owner must provide MFA authentication to permanently delete an object version or change the bucket versioning state. This adds friction and helps prevent accidental or unauthorized permanent deletion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution directly meets the requirement because it combines recoverability with additional protection against permanent removal. Users can recover previous versions after accidental deletion, and MFA Delete reduces the risk that someone permanently deletes versions without the required MFA validation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/04f1b4d2ce3d330225e0c8aa3c9f5d33d901ae84ac052fe78f59cbbe98b2d7e1.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/04f1b4d2ce3d330225e0c8aa3c9f5d33d901ae84ac052fe78f59cbbe98b2d7e1.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/zero-downtime-amazon-s3-versioning-architectural-patterns-for-mission-critical-workloads/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/zero-downtime-amazon-s3-versioning-architectural-patterns-for-mission-critical-workloads/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an S3 bucket policy that denies requests that create delete markers for objects in the bucket\u003c/strong\u003e - A bucket policy can deny delete actions, but the option focuses on denying the creation of delete markers. Delete markers are created by Amazon S3 when a simple delete request occurs in a versioning-enabled or versioning-suspended bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option does not provide the correct native mechanism to preserve objects from accidental deletion. Versioning and MFA Delete provide purpose-built protection, while denying delete marker creation is not the proper control pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable S3 Replication to copy objects to another bucket and leverage the replica bucket to prevent accidental deletions in the source bucket\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Replication can copy objects to another bucket for durability, cross-Region resilience, or account separation. A replica bucket can help with recovery strategies when configured carefully.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, replication alone does not prevent accidental deletion in the source bucket. It also does not provide the same direct protection against simple deletes and permanent version deletion that S3 Versioning with MFA Delete provides.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure AWS Config remediation actions to automatically restore objects after users delete them from the S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Config can evaluate resource configurations and can invoke remediation actions when resources drift from desired configuration. This is useful for detecting and correcting noncompliant bucket settings.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, AWS Config remediation is not the best mechanism to prevent users from deleting S3 objects. It does not provide object-version preservation or MFA-based protection for permanent object version deletion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/DeletingObjects.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/DeletingObjects.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/MultiFactorAuthenticationDelete.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/MultiFactorAuthenticationDelete.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/UsingMFADelete.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/UsingMFADelete.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/versioning-workflows.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/versioning-workflows.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/zero-downtime-amazon-s3-versioning-architectural-patterns-for-mission-critical-workloads/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/zero-downtime-amazon-s3-versioning-architectural-patterns-for-mission-critical-workloads/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-38":{"id":"p:2-38","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 38","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure an S3 bucket policy that denies requests that create delete markers for objects in the bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Replication to copy objects to another bucket and leverage the replica bucket to prevent accidental deletions in the source bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable versioning on the S3 bucket and configure MFA Delete for the bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS Config remediation actions to automatically restore objects after users delete them from the S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Chặn delete marker riêng lẻ không cung cấp đủ cơ chế lưu phiên bản và bảo vệ xóa vĩnh viễn như đề yêu cầu.","Replication tạo bản sao nhưng không tự ngăn xóa ở bucket nguồn. Cần cơ chế lưu version và bảo vệ thao tác xóa.","Versioning giữ các phiên bản cũ khi xóa thông thường tạo delete marker. MFA Delete tăng bảo vệ cho các thao tác xóa version vĩnh viễn hoặc đổi trạng thái versioning liên quan.","AWS Config không tự phục hồi nội dung object đã mất nếu không có bản sao/version. Nó chủ yếu theo dõi cấu hình tài nguyên."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company stores sensitive claims-processing files in an Amazon S3 bucket. The files support downstream compliance investigations and must be protected from accidental deletion by application users and administrators. A data engineer must implement controls that help preserve deleted objects and add stronger protection against permanent deletion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-55":{"id":"p:2-55","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 55","correct":[3,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Web Application Firewall (AWS WAF) ACL and use an IP match condition to allow traffic only from those IPs that are allowed in the Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new AWS WAF ACL with the Amazon S3 bucket policy\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a new NACL that allows traffic from the same IPs as specified in the current Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new NACL with the Amazon CloudFront distribution\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a new security group that allows traffic from the same IPs as specified in the current Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new security group with the Amazon CloudFront distribution\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an origin access control (OAC) and associate it with the Amazon CloudFront distribution. Set up the permissions in the Amazon S3 bucket policy so that only the OAC can read the objects\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS WAF ACL and use an IP match condition to allow traffic only from those IPs that are allowed in the Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new AWS WAF ACL with the Amazon CloudFront distribution\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["WAF web ACL không gắn vào S3 bucket policy. Phải gắn vào tài nguyên được WAF hỗ trợ như CloudFront.","NACL gắn với subnet VPC, không gắn vào CloudFront distribution.","CloudFront không nhận security group như EC2. Muốn lọc địa chỉ IP người xem hãy dùng WAF.","OAC kết hợp bucket policy giới hạn đọc object qua CloudFront distribution được phép, giúp ngăn người dùng truy cập trực tiếp S3 để bỏ qua kiểm soát.","Gắn WAF web ACL vào CloudFront và tạo IP allowlist theo dải địa chỉ nhà cung cấp. Cơ chế này thay vai trò lọc IP trước đây tại EC2."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team at a company is moving the static content from the company\u0027s logistics website hosted on Amazon EC2 instances to an Amazon S3 bucket. The team wants to use an Amazon CloudFront distribution to deliver the static content. The security group used by the Amazon EC2 instances allows the website to be accessed by a limited set of IP ranges from the company\u0027s suppliers. Post-migration to Amazon CloudFront, access to the static content should only be allowed from the aforementioned IP addresses.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich options would you combine to build a solution to meet these requirements? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:10:1":{"id":"e:10:1","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 1","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up an AWS Step Functions state machine to run the Lambda function and then the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an AWS Glue workflow to run the Lambda function and then the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to set up an Apache Airflow workflow that is deployed on an Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) cluster. The workflow definition should have the first task as the Lambda function and the second task as the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to set up an Apache Airflow workflow that is deployed on an Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) cluster. The workflow definition should have the first task as the Lambda function and the second task as the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company needs to set up a data pipeline that includes an AWS Lambda function and an AWS Glue job. You have been hired as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to build a solution that has the least management overhead and is fully integrated with AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat do you recommend?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an AWS Step Functions state machine to run the Lambda function and then the AWS Glue job\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Step Functions is a serverless orchestration service that lets you integrate with AWS Lambda functions and other AWS services to build business-critical applications. Step Functions is based on state machines and tasks. In Step Functions, a workflow is called a state machine, which is a series of event-driven steps. Each step in a workflow is called a state. A Task state represents a unit of work that another AWS service, such as AWS Lambda, performs. A Task state can call any AWS service or API.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWith Step Functions\u0027 built-in controls, you examine the state of each step in your workflow to make sure that your application runs in order and as expected. Depending on your use case, you can have Step Functions call AWS services, such as Lambda, to perform tasks. You can create workflows that process and publish machine learning models. You can have Step Functions control AWS services, such as AWS Glue, to create extract, transform, and load (ETL) workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Step Functions state machine represents a simple way to orchestrate a workflow on AWS, as it has minimal management overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to set up an Apache Airflow workflow that is deployed on an Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) cluster. The workflow definition should have the first task as the Lambda function and the second task as the AWS Glue job\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to set up an Apache Airflow workflow that is deployed on an Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) cluster. The workflow definition should have the first task as the Lambda function and the second task as the AWS Glue job\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow is a managed orchestration service for Apache Airflow that you can use to set up and operate data pipelines in the cloud at scale. Apache Airflow is an open-source tool used to programmatically author, schedule, and monitor sequences of processes and tasks referred to as workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon MWAA offers support for using containers to scale the worker fleet on demand and reduce scheduler outages using Amazon ECS on AWS Fargate. Operators that invoke tasks on Amazon ECS containers, and Kubernetes operators that create and run pods on a Kubernetes cluster are supported.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUsing Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to set up an Apache Airflow workflow that is deployed on an Amazon ECS cluster or Amazon EKS cluster represents a solution with a very high management overhead. So, both these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an AWS Glue workflow to run the Lambda function and then the AWS Glue job\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue workflow is only for orchestrating complex ETL operations using AWS Glue, so you cannot have a Lambda function as a component of the workflow. So, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/what-is-mwaa.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/what-is-mwaa.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:16":{"id":"e:1:16","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 16","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Object Lock in compliance mode on the destination bucket before uploading the audit log objects\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Macie to scan the uploaded log objects, compare sensitive data findings with the source server inventory, and mark matching files as successfully validated\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lambda function that calculates SHA-256 checksums after upload, stores the results in DynamoDB, and validates objects during a later scheduled job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the AWS SDK to calculate a CRC32 checksum during upload, send the checksum with each request, and let Amazon S3 store checksum metadata\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company uploads application audit logs from data center servers to an Amazon S3 bucket for long-term analysis. The company must confirm that each uploaded log object in Amazon S3 matches the original log file from the source server. A data engineer must implement an integrity validation approach for the upload process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the AWS SDK to calculate a CRC32 checksum during upload, send the checksum with each request, and let Amazon S3 store checksum metadata\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 supports checksum-based integrity validation during object uploads. The data engineer can use the AWS SDK to calculate a supported checksum, such as CRC32, and include the checksum value in the upload request.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 calculates a checksum for the received object and compares it with the checksum that the client provided. If the values do not match, Amazon S3 can reject the upload, which helps confirm that the object received by S3 is the same as the object sent from the source server.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution directly validates data integrity during transfer and stores checksum information with the S3 object metadata for later verification. It avoids building a separate validation service when Amazon S3 and the AWS SDKs already provide managed checksum support.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/50c2fec1d85a07325cf87cac47d22cf2bc623e3e8715bee24c8d48c809394b7e.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/50c2fec1d85a07325cf87cac47d22cf2bc623e3e8715bee24c8d48c809394b7e.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-default-data-integrity-protections-for-new-objects-in-amazon-s3/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-default-data-integrity-protections-for-new-objects-in-amazon-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable S3 Object Lock in compliance mode on the destination bucket before uploading the audit log objects\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Object Lock in compliance mode can help prevent objects from being overwritten or deleted for a defined retention period. This is useful for write-once-read-many retention and regulatory protection.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, Object Lock does not prove that the uploaded object matches the original source file. It protects an object after storage, but it does not perform checksum comparison during upload.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Lambda function that calculates SHA-256 checksums after upload, stores the results in DynamoDB, and validates objects during a later scheduled job\u003c/strong\u003e - A Lambda function could calculate SHA-256 checksums after objects arrive in Amazon S3. DynamoDB could store checksum values for later comparison and reporting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this approach adds custom code, state management, scheduling, retries, and reconciliation logic. It also validates objects after upload rather than using native S3 checksum validation during the upload operation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Macie to scan the uploaded log objects, compare sensitive data findings with the source server inventory, and mark matching files as successfully validated\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Macie can discover sensitive data in Amazon S3 and generate findings for data such as personally identifiable information. It is useful for data discovery and security visibility.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, Macie does not compare source file checksums with S3 object checksums. Sensitive data findings cannot prove that uploaded log objects are identical to the original source files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/checking-object-integrity.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/checking-object-integrity.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/checking-object-integrity-upload.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/checking-object-integrity-upload.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdkref/latest/guide/feature-dataintegrity.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdkref/latest/guide/feature-dataintegrity.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-default-data-integrity-protections-for-new-objects-in-amazon-s3/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-default-data-integrity-protections-for-new-objects-in-amazon-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-36":{"id":"p:1-36","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 36","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EMR to run a scheduled Spark preprocessing job that prepares summary files in Amazon S3 before the data engineer runs Athena queries\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable Amazon Athena query result reuse for the recurring queries in the same workgroup and configure an appropriate maximum age for reused results\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue crawler to refresh the Data Catalog before every dashboard update and require Athena to rerun each query after the crawler completes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the same S3 dataset instead of running the repeated queries in Amazon Athena\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Tiền xử lý bằng EMR có thể tối ưu dataset nhưng thêm một tầng công việc. Bài toán chính là chạy lại truy vấn giống nhau khi kết quả cũ vẫn còn dùng được.","Query result reuse cho phép tái sử dụng kết quả hợp lệ trong workgroup, với tuổi tối đa phù hợp. Tránh tính lại và quét lại dữ liệu cho các truy vấn lặp đủ điều kiện.","Crawler cập nhật metadata, không tái sử dụng kết quả truy vấn. Chạy crawler rồi luôn chạy lại Athena còn thêm thời gian và chi phí.","Đổi sang Spectrum không trực tiếp xử lý nguyên nhân là lặp lại cùng truy vấn. Athena đã có tính năng dùng lại kết quả đáp ứng nhu cầu."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA subscription analytics company stores several terabytes of product usage events in an Amazon S3 data lake in the us-west-2 Region. A data engineer runs complex Amazon Athena queries many times each day to refresh executive dashboards. The queries use the same filter values and parameters, and the source data changes only a few times per day. The company needs to improve query response times and reduce query cost when previous results are still fresh enough to use.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:14":{"id":"e:5:14","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 14","correct":[3],"answers":["Store the data on Amazon EBS volumes and configure the Lambda functions to access the stored data.","Migrate the data to the local storage of each Lambda function for quick and seamless access.","Migrate the data to Amazon FSx for Windows File Server and configure each Lambda function to connect to the FSx file share for access.","Migrate the data to Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) and configure each Lambda function to mount the EFS for access."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company plans to migrate its on-premises data to the AWS Cloud. This on-premises data is mainly used for data analytics and is on an NFS file share. The company is also evaluating AWS Transfer Family as part of its broader strategy for moving file-based data into the AWS environment. Post-migration, the company utilizes Amazon Lambda functions to analyze the data further. This data should still be in an NFS file system shared with all Lambda functions to access across all concurrent runs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution would satisfy these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Elastic File System\u003c/strong\u003e is a scalable file storage service you can integrate with AWS Lambda or other compute options. Amazon EFS is a serverless solution that allows your compute resources in AWS to share data without provisioning or managing storage capacity and performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ad32c8e85e76e8620519176e9a105d13e4b91205c2d50084d4c903aea21a040c.png\" alt=\"Amazon EFS creation, compute mounting, data migration, and protection workflow\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon EFS offers a seamless, scalable, and fully managed NFS file system that perfectly aligns with the requirements for shared access and concurrent processing capabilities needed for AWS Lambda functions. While other storage solutions offer access sharing, Amazon EFS is the best solution for the scenario since the company must host the migrated data in an NFS file system.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMigrate the data to Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) and configure each Lambda function to mount the EFS for access.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMigrate the data to the local storage of each Lambda function for quick and seamless access \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Remember, the local storage in Lambda functions is ephemeral; Lambda erases any data stored in its local storage as soon as the invocation ends. Additionally, this local storage does not support the NFS protocol.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the data on Amazon EBS volumes and configure the Lambda functions to access the stored data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect primarily because you cannot mount an EBS volume on a Lambda Function. EBS volumes offer block storage, and you can only mount EBS volumes on EC2 instances, which do not support the NFS protocol.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMigrate the data to Amazon FSx for Windows File Server and configure each Lambda function to connect to the FSx file share for access\u003c/strong\u003e \u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Although it is possible for multiple Lambda functions to access an Amazon FSx for Windows File Server, Amazon FSx for Windows File Server only supports the SMB protocol, not NFS. It would help to look at other Amazon FSx file system types that support the NFS protocol, such as FSx for NetApp ONTAP and FSx for OpenZFS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/efs/latest/ug/whatisefs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/efs/latest/ug/whatisefs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/fsx/when-to-choose-fsx/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/fsx/when-to-choose-fsx/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-filesystem.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-filesystem.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon EFS Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-efs/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-efs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-7":{"id":"p:2-7","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 7","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eMap state\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eParallel state\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConcurrent state\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChoice state\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Map lặp cùng một quy trình xử lý trên từng phần tử đầu vào và cho phép chạy đồng thời. Phù hợp nhiều tệp đều cần cùng các phép biến đổi.","Parallel chạy các nhánh workflow được định nghĩa riêng, không phải cơ chế trực tiếp lặp cùng một bước cho danh sách tệp biến thiên.","Concurrent không phải loại state được nêu trong bộ trạng thái Step Functions cho yêu cầu này. Mức đồng thời là cấu hình của Map.","Choice rẽ nhánh dựa trên điều kiện. Nó không tự lặp và xử lý nhiều tệp song song."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is configuring an AWS Step Function for a banking system workflow. The workflow must process large amounts of data files in parallel and it should simultaneously apply transformations for each file.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich Step Functions state is the right fit for this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:18":{"id":"e:4:18","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 18","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Lake Formation permissions to restrict access to incomplete records and apply LF-Tags to separate valid records from invalid records\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue ETL jobs with SQL transforms to check required columns for null values and run separate SQL queries for referential integrity\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse SageMaker Data Wrangler to generate a Data Quality and Insights report, and review the report before writing each dataset to storage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue ETL jobs with an Evaluate Data Quality transform, IsComplete rules for required columns, and ReferentialIntegrity rules for related datasets\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is building a new data pipeline that prepares customer activity data for executive dashboards. Business users report that some dashboard records are incomplete because required fields are missing and some records do not match the related reference data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer must add data quality checks before the pipeline writes the curated output to storage. The checks must detect null values in required columns and validate referential integrity against related datasets with the least operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue ETL jobs with an Evaluate Data Quality transform, IsComplete rules for required columns, and ReferentialIntegrity rules for related datasets\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Data Quality can run inside AWS Glue ETL jobs by using the Evaluate Data Quality transform. This allows the pipeline to evaluate records before writing the curated output to storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe IsComplete rule checks whether a required column is complete, which means the column does not contain null values. This directly addresses the requirement to detect missing values in important columns before downstream reports consume the data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe ReferentialIntegrity rule validates relationships between a primary dataset and a reference dataset. Combining IsComplete and ReferentialIntegrity rules in the AWS Glue ETL job provides managed, rule-based validation with less operational overhead than maintaining custom SQL checks or Python validation code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDQDL rule type reference:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/437dd4cd42d9ed2c4782775c852f03abe8fc6556261439b756824e55dda89064.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/437dd4cd42d9ed2c4782775c852f03abe8fc6556261439b756824e55dda89064.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse SageMaker Data Wrangler to generate a Data Quality and Insights report, and review the report before writing each dataset to storage\u003c/strong\u003e - SageMaker Data Wrangler can help analyze and prepare data, and its Data Quality and Insights report can provide useful statistics about a dataset. This can help with exploratory data preparation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that this option does not provide the most direct managed validation step inside an AWS Glue ETL pipeline before storage. The requirement calls for repeatable null checks and referential integrity checks in the pipeline, which AWS Glue Data Quality supports directly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue ETL jobs with SQL transforms to check required columns for null values and run separate SQL queries for referential integrity\u003c/strong\u003e - SQL transforms in AWS Glue can detect null values and compare datasets. A data engineer could write SQL logic to check required fields and validate relationships.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because it requires custom SQL validation logic and ongoing maintenance for each rule and dataset relationship. AWS Glue Data Quality provides purpose-built DQDL rules for these checks with less operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Lake Formation permissions to restrict access to incomplete records and apply LF-Tags to separate valid records from invalid records\u003c/strong\u003e - Lake Formation can manage permissions for data lake resources and can use LF-Tags for scalable access control. This is useful for governance and authorization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not solve the data quality requirement. Access permissions do not detect null values, validate references, or prevent incomplete records from being written during the ETL process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/tutorial-data-quality.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/tutorial-data-quality.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-api-crawler-pyspark-transforms-EvaluateDataQuality.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-api-crawler-pyspark-transforms-EvaluateDataQuality.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types-ReferentialIntegrity.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types-ReferentialIntegrity.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/getting-started-with-aws-glue-data-quality-for-etl-pipelines/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/getting-started-with-aws-glue-data-quality-for-etl-pipelines/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:55":{"id":"e:5:55","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 55","correct":[1,2,5],"answers":["Decrease the number of shards in the Kinesis Data Stream.","Register the Lambda function as a consumer to the Kinesis Data Stream with enhanced fan-out.","Add additional shards in the Kinesis Data Stream.","Submit a support ticket to raise the default Lambda provisioned concurrency limit.","Use the provisioned capacity mode of the Kinesis Data Stream.","Configure different parallelization factor settings to identify the optimal performance setting."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company ingests large amounts of data through an Amazon Kinesis Data Stream and stores it into an Amazon S3 data lake. Additionally, the company uses AWS Lambda to consume and process these incoming data. However, the ingestion process undergoes a highly variable and unpredictable volume.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis variability significantly increases the \u003ccode\u003eIteratorAge\u003c/code\u003e metric during peak times when the data stream experiences high volume. A data engineer must design a solution to improve performance when processing data from Kinesis Data Streams with Lambda.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solutions will meet these requirements? (Select THREE.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is a service designed for real-time data streaming and processing. It captures and stores large volumes of data, allowing applications to process and analyze data immediately instead of waiting until all data is collected. Kinesis Data Streams are composed of shards, each with a capacity limit for read and write operations.\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Lambda\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003ecan directly process data from Kinesis Data Streams. When a Kinesis Data Stream triggers a Lambda function, it processes a batch of records from the stream. Lambda functions can scale automatically with the number of shards in a Kinesis stream; each Lambda invocation processes data from a single shard. The \u003cstrong\u003eParallelization Factor\u003c/strong\u003e in AWS Lambda determines how many concurrent invocations per shard are made. Adjusting this factor can optimize the processing of records and improve throughput. Lastly, The \u003cstrong\u003eEnhanced Fan-Out\u003c/strong\u003e feature of Amazon Kinesis provides a dedicated throughput of 2 MB/sec for read operations per shard for each registered consumer, as opposed to the shared throughput of 2 MB/sec across all consumers (e.g. EC2 Instances and Lambda Functions) in standard mode.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3a8e7ef9c167ead38e6f87af81bd6b8d0001bd648ba23b89b2c7fdb517cd88f1.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the context of handling variable and unpredictable data volumes with Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and AWS Lambda, \u003cstrong\u003eadding more shards\u003c/strong\u003e to the data stream increases its capacity to handle larger volumes of data by enabling higher throughput and parallel processing capabilities, which is crucial for managing peak loads. \u003cstrong\u003eAdjusting the parallelization factor\u003c/strong\u003e allows for fine-tuning the number of records processed concurrently by Lambda, optimizing performance by balancing the workload across more function instances, which enhances throughput during high-volume periods. \u003cstrong\u003eEnhanced fan-out\u003c/strong\u003e provides a dedicated throughput for Lambda consumers, ensuring faster and more reliable data delivery with reduced latency, which is essential for real-time processing needs. Together, these strategies effectively address the challenges of processing large and fluctuating data volumes, ensuring that the system can scale to meet demand while minimizing processing delays, as reflected by lower \u003ccode\u003eIteratorAge\u003c/code\u003e metrics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Add additional shards in the Kinesis Data Stream.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Configure different parallelization factor settings to identify the optimal performance setting.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Register the Lambda function as a consumer to the Kinesis Data Stream with enhanced fan-out.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the provisioned capacity mode of the Kinesis Data Stream\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect mainly because switching to this capacity mode does not increase performance. Simply using provisioned capacity mode without increasing the number of shards does not inherently address the issue of handling high volume and variability in data ingestion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eSubmit a support ticket to raise the default Lambda reserved concurrency limit\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. The context above did not state anything about the Lambda function nearing its service limit. While it can ensure that a certain level of Lambda resources is always available for your function, it doesn’t inherently improve the processing of high volumes of data from Kinesis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Decrease the number of shards in the Kinesis Data Stream\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Like the previous option, the context did not state anything about the Lambda function nearing its service limit. While it can reduce the function’s startup time, it doesn’t directly improve the processing capability for high volumes of data from Kinesis Data Streams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/building-consumers.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/building-consumers.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/lambda-iterator-age\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/lambda-iterator-age\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Kinesis Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:61":{"id":"e:4:61","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 61","correct":[0,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a destination database in Amazon Redshift from the zero-ETL integration so analysts can query the replicated Aurora data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS DMS replication task from Amazon Aurora MySQL to Amazon Redshift and configure table mappings to replicate only the required reporting tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift query editor v2 to query the Amazon Aurora MySQL database directly from the reporting dashboards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate materialized views in Amazon Redshift for all 90 Aurora MySQL source tables and refresh the materialized views on a fixed schedule before each reporting cycle\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a zero-ETL integration from the Amazon Aurora MySQL cluster to Amazon Redshift and configure data filters to include only the required reporting tables\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company generates operational reports from 24 tables in an Amazon Redshift data warehouse. The source data resides in an Amazon Aurora MySQL database that contains 90 tables. Currently, the company refreshes the entire Aurora dataset into Amazon Redshift every 30 minutes, which delays reporting jobs and consumes unnecessary resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a zero-ETL integration from the Amazon Aurora MySQL cluster to Amazon Redshift and configure data filters to include only the required reporting tables\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Aurora zero-ETL integration with Amazon Redshift is designed to make transactional data from Aurora available in Amazon Redshift without building and operating custom ETL pipelines. This reduces the operational work required to move data from Aurora MySQL to Redshift for reporting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company does not need all 90 source tables. Aurora zero-ETL integrations support data filtering, which lets the company include or exclude specific tables from replication. Selecting only the required reporting tables reduces unnecessary replication and storage in Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option directly addresses the current inefficiency. Instead of refreshing all Aurora data every 30 minutes, the company can use a managed integration that replicates only the tables that the reports require.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a destination database in Amazon Redshift from the zero-ETL integration so analysts can query the replicated Aurora data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAfter the zero-ETL integration is created, Amazon Redshift needs a destination database that uses the integration. This database exposes the replicated Aurora data inside Redshift so analysts and reporting workloads can query it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCreating the destination database completes the Redshift-side setup for the integration. Without this step, the source Aurora data can be configured for replication, but Redshift users will not have the database object that makes the replicated tables available for queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis step works with the zero-ETL integration to provide a managed, low-overhead reporting architecture. The company can continue using Amazon Redshift for reports while avoiding the hourly or half-hourly full refresh process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS DMS replication task from Amazon Aurora MySQL to Amazon Redshift and configure table mappings to replicate only the required reporting tables\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS DMS can replicate data from relational database sources to supported targets, and table mappings can limit the tables that a task replicates. This can work for migration or custom replication scenarios.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, AWS DMS requires the company to configure and manage replication instances or serverless replication settings, tasks, endpoints, table mappings, monitoring, and troubleshooting. Aurora zero-ETL integration with Amazon Redshift provides a more managed option with less operational overhead for this reporting use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift query editor v2 to query the Amazon Aurora MySQL database directly from the reporting dashboards\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift query editor v2 lets users run SQL queries and manage query workflows in Amazon Redshift. It can help users work with Redshift databases and query available data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, query editor v2 does not create a managed replication path from Aurora MySQL to Redshift. It also does not replace the need to create a zero-ETL integration and a Redshift destination database for the replicated data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate materialized views in Amazon Redshift for all 90 Aurora MySQL source tables and refresh the materialized views on a fixed schedule before each reporting cycle\u003c/strong\u003e - Materialized views in Amazon Redshift can improve query performance by storing precomputed query results. They are useful when queries repeatedly aggregate or join Redshift data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, materialized views do not move selected Aurora MySQL tables into Redshift. Creating materialized views for all 90 source tables also conflicts with the requirement to avoid unnecessary refresh work and process only the reporting tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.creating.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.creating.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.filtering.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.filtering.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/zero-etl-using.creating-db.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/zero-etl-using.creating-db.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/zero-etl-setting-up.create-integration-aurora.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/zero-etl-setting-up.create-integration-aurora.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-41":{"id":"p:2-41","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 41","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Batch to run three containerized Spark jobs on managed compute environments, and use DynamoDB to track when the first two jobs finish\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue workflows to run the first two AWS Glue Spark jobs in parallel, and start the third AWS Glue job after both jobs finish\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Step Functions to orchestrate multiple Lambda functions, and configure the third Lambda function to run after the first two functions finish\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EMR to run each Spark process, send completion messages to an SQS queue, and invoke the third process with a Lambda function\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Batch và DynamoDB có thể xây cơ chế này, nhưng cần container, thư viện Spark và logic theo dõi hoàn thành riêng.","Glue workflows chạy hai Spark job song song rồi dùng phụ thuộc để kích hoạt job thứ ba sau khi cả hai thành công. Phù hợp dữ liệu lớn và tác vụ cần nhiều RAM.","Lambda không đáp ứng tác vụ dài và yêu cầu ít nhất 15 GiB RAM của đề. Step Functions không thay đổi giới hạn tài nguyên Lambda.","EMR, SQS và Lambda thêm nhiều thành phần điều phối/giám sát. Glue workflow cung cấp mô hình phụ thuộc có sẵn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA digital media company processes 650 GB of audience engagement and campaign delivery data every day. The company stores incoming CSV files in Amazon S3, and the schemas are registered in AWS Glue Data Catalog. The company needs to convert the CSV files to Apache Parquet format and write the output to another S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe pipeline must run two long-running conversion and enrichment processes concurrently. Each process requires at least 15 GiB of memory. A third correlation process must begin only after the first two processes finish successfully. The company wants the solution with the least operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:57":{"id":"e:4:57","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 57","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure a single per-query limit by configuring all the mentioned thresholds into a single limit configuration\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure multiple per-query limits by utilizing the per-query cost control limit feature on Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure a single per-workgroup limit by configuring all the mentioned thresholds into a single limit configuration\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure multiple per-workgroup limits by utilizing the workgroup-wide data usage control limit on Athena\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA consulting firm uses Amazon Athena to analyze data in Amazon S3 using SQL. A rapid expansion plan has resulted in the company doubling its data engineers in a year resulting in high usage costs for Athena. Preliminary investigation suggests that most of the day-to-day queries run for only a few seconds fetching limited data. The firm wants to define different thresholds on hourly or daily aggregates on data scanned by the queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate, how will you configure a solution for this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure multiple per-workgroup limits by utilizing the workgroup-wide data usage control limit on Athena\u003c/strong\u003e - Athena allows you to set two types of cost controls: per-query limit and per-workgroup limit. For each workgroup, you can set only one per-query limit and multiple per-workgroup limits.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe workgroup-wide data usage control limit specifies the total amount of data scanned for all queries that run in this workgroup during the specified time period. You can create multiple limits per workgroup. The workgroup-wide query limit allows you to set multiple thresholds on hourly or daily aggregates on data scanned by queries running in the workgroup.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIf the aggregate amount of data scanned exceeds the threshold, you can push a notification to an Amazon SNS topic. You can also create an alarm and an action on any metric that Athena publishes from the CloudWatch console.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eData Usage Control Limits in Athena:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f08d217661fbcd9b8c02a6de9d083121b9ad40c849fdd0c604bbdd631b698337.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f08d217661fbcd9b8c02a6de9d083121b9ad40c849fdd0c604bbdd631b698337.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/workgroups-setting-control-limits-cloudwatch.html#configure-control-limit-per-query\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/workgroups-setting-control-limits-cloudwatch.html#configure-control-limit-per-query\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure multiple per-query limits by utilizing the per-query cost control limit feature on Athena\u003c/strong\u003e - The per-query control limit specifies the total amount of data scanned per query. If any query that runs in the workgroup exceeds the limit, it is canceled. You can create only one per-query control limit in a workgroup and it applies to each query that runs in it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure a single per-query limit by configuring all the mentioned thresholds into a single limit configuration\u003c/strong\u003e - This is an invalid statement, given only as a distractor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure a single per-workgroup limit by configuring all the mentioned thresholds into a single limit configuration\u003c/strong\u003e - As discussed above, multiple per-workgroup limits have to be defined to cater to hourly and daily aggregate thresholds of data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/workgroups-setting-control-limits-cloudwatch.html#configure-control-limit-per-query\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/workgroups-setting-control-limits-cloudwatch.html#configure-control-limit-per-query\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-36":{"id":"p:2-36","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 36","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate the replication instance in the same Region and VPC as the target DB instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate the replication instance in the same Region and VPC as the source DB instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate the replication instance in the same Availability Zone and VPC as the target DB instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate the replication instance in the same Availability Zone and VPC as the source DB instance\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Cùng Region/VPC của đích giảm khoảng cách nhưng chưa chặt bằng cùng AZ với đích. Bộ đề hỏi vị trí tối ưu nhất trong các lựa chọn.","Đặt cạnh nguồn không tối ưu đường ghi vào đích theo khuyến nghị của lời giải này.","Đặt replication instance cùng AZ và VPC với database đích để giảm độ trễ đường ghi và lưu lượng liên AZ tại đích. Đây là cấu hình được bộ đề chọn.","Cùng AZ với nguồn cải thiện đường đọc nhưng không phải vị trí ưu tiên theo tình huống migration trong lời giải gốc."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn IT company is using AWS DMS to migrate its Amazon RDS for Oracle DB instance configured in a VPC in the us-east-1 Region to another VPC in the us-west-1 Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhere would you place the DMS replication instance for the MOST optimal performance?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:22":{"id":"e:3:22","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 22","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate one shared project for all divisions in the SageMaker Unified Studio domain, use project membership to separate assets and metadata, configure AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, and instruct project owners to manually track dataset access approvals outside the domain\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure each division as a domain unit with owner permissions, restrict projects so only owners can access assets, prevent sharing across domain units, configure AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, and map user profiles to the corresponding domain units\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure each division as a domain unit, manage permissions for assets, projects, and metadata within each unit, use AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, map user profiles to the appropriate domain units, and enable cross-division sharing through access requests that domain unit owners approve or deny\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure each division as a separate SageMaker Unified Studio domain, isolate administrative policies across domains, configure AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, and delegate administration at the domain level\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA global manufacturing company is creating a new Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio domain for its analytics and AI teams. Each division must independently manage its own projects, assets, metadata, and permissions. Some approved datasets must be discoverable and shareable with other divisions through a governed request and approval process. The company also requires centralized user authentication and mapping between identities and the appropriate organizational units.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure each division as a domain unit, manage permissions for assets, projects, and metadata within each unit, use AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, map user profiles to the appropriate domain units, and enable cross-division sharing through access requests that domain unit owners approve or deny\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDomain units in Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio support organizational separation within a domain. A company can represent divisions or business units as domain units so that each unit can manage permissions, projects, assets, metadata, and governance responsibilities within its own scope.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSageMaker Unified Studio supports governed asset sharing through discovery and access request workflows. Users can discover assets across domain units, request access to specific datasets, and rely on domain unit owners or authorized approvers to approve or deny those requests. This meets the requirement for sharing selected datasets without removing governance controls.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS IAM Identity Center provides centralized authentication for Identity Center-based SageMaker Unified Studio domains. User profiles map identities to the domain so the company can associate users with the appropriate domain units and apply the right governance policies. This combination satisfies isolated business-unit control, governed sharing, and centralized identity management.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2420d98570c2c6ee92575f027768543335328f56312e17369ebefc0428862482.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2420d98570c2c6ee92575f027768543335328f56312e17369ebefc0428862482.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/using-amazon-sagemaker-unified-studio-identity-center-idc-and-iam-based-domains-together/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/using-amazon-sagemaker-unified-studio-identity-center-idc-and-iam-based-domains-together/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0b80d922fbe20ee4b2cc054781ed57f829f71472fcbaf705477481f87addaf48.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0b80d922fbe20ee4b2cc054781ed57f829f71472fcbaf705477481f87addaf48.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/using-amazon-sagemaker-unified-studio-identity-center-idc-and-iam-based-domains-together/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/using-amazon-sagemaker-unified-studio-identity-center-idc-and-iam-based-domains-together/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure each division as a separate SageMaker Unified Studio domain, isolate administrative policies across domains, configure AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, and delegate administration at the domain level\u003c/strong\u003e - Separate domains can isolate environments, users, and administrative boundaries. However, the requirement asks for each division to have isolated control within a new SageMaker Unified Studio domain, not for each division to run its own separate domain.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also makes governed sharing across divisions harder because the divisions do not operate as domain units inside the same organizational structure. Domain units provide the intended model for business-unit separation and delegated governance within a domain.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure each division as a domain unit with owner permissions, restrict projects so only owners can access assets, prevent sharing across domain units, configure AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, and map user profiles to the corresponding domain units\u003c/strong\u003e - This option correctly uses domain units and IAM Identity Center. However, it prevents sharing across domain units, which conflicts with the requirement to share specific datasets with other business units upon approval.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company needs both isolation and governed collaboration. Restricting projects exclusively to owners blocks the subscription and access request workflow that enables controlled sharing of approved datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate one shared project for all divisions in the SageMaker Unified Studio domain, use project membership to separate assets and metadata, configure AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, and instruct project owners to manually track dataset access approvals outside the domain\u003c/strong\u003e - A single shared project does not provide isolated control over each division\u0027s assets, projects, and metadata. Project membership can control access within a project, but it does not represent the intended business-unit governance boundary.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eManual approval tracking outside the domain also weakens governance and auditability. The company needs a built-in access request and approval process that domain unit owners can manage, not an external manual process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/working-with-domains.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/working-with-domains.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/user-management.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/user-management.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/catalog-iam-manage-access.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/userguide/catalog-iam-manage-access.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/security-accesss-control-patterns.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker-unified-studio/latest/adminguide/security-accesss-control-patterns.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/using-amazon-sagemaker-unified-studio-identity-center-idc-and-iam-based-domains-together/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/using-amazon-sagemaker-unified-studio-identity-center-idc-and-iam-based-domains-together/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:50":{"id":"e:3:50","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 50","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSwap out Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon SQS Standard queues\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSwap out Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSwap out Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon SQS FIFO queues\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Enhanced Fanout feature of Amazon Kinesis Data Streams\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team is using Amazon Kinesis Data Streams (KDS) to process IoT data from the field devices of an agricultural sciences company. Multiple consumer applications are using the incoming data streams and the data engineers have noticed a performance lag for the data delivery speed between producers and consumers of the data streams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you recommend for improving the performance for the given use-case?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Enhanced Fanout feature of Amazon Kinesis Data Streams\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams (KDS) is a massively scalable and durable real-time data streaming service. KDS can continuously capture gigabytes of data per second from hundreds of thousands of sources such as website clickstreams, database event streams, financial transactions, social media feeds, IT logs, and location-tracking events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBy default, the 2MB/second/shard output is shared between all of the applications consuming data from the stream. You should use enhanced fan-out if you have multiple consumers retrieving data from a stream in parallel. With enhanced fan-out developers can register stream consumers to use enhanced fan-out and receive their own 2MB/second pipe of read throughput per shard, and this throughput automatically scales with the number of shards in a stream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams Fanout:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7ac4f17ae6a0d4df8447f443d1226f1bd0f26454469d132927c873ee1b824003.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7ac4f17ae6a0d4df8447f443d1226f1bd0f26454469d132927c873ee1b824003.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSwap out Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose\u003c/strong\u003e -  Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose is the easiest way to reliably load streaming data into data lakes, data stores, and analytics tools. It is a fully managed service that automatically scales to match the throughput of your data and requires no ongoing administration. It can also batch, compress, transform, and encrypt the data before loading it, minimizing the amount of storage used at the destination and increasing security. Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose can only write to Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, Amazon Elasticsearch or Splunk. You can\u0027t have applications consuming data streams from Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, that\u0027s the job of Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Therefore this option is not correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSwap out Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon SQS Standard queues\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSwap out Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon SQS FIFO queues\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) is a fully managed message queuing service that enables you to decouple and scale microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications. Amazon SQS offers two types of message queues. Standard queues offer maximum throughput, best-effort ordering, and at-least-once delivery. Amazon SQS FIFO queues are designed to guarantee that messages are processed exactly once, in the exact order that they are sent. As multiple applications are consuming the same stream concurrently, both Amazon SQS Standard and Amazon SQS FIFO are not the right fit for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eExam Alert:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou should understand the differences between the capabilities of Amazon Kinesis Data Streams vs Amazon SQS, as you may be asked scenario-based questions on this topic in the exam.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/cbea91ecf27d8ab376916c5f7ca0cfa2f140d0f17408c9ae8e94f2c25f3a6a54.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/cbea91ecf27d8ab376916c5f7ca0cfa2f140d0f17408c9ae8e94f2c25f3a6a54.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-4":{"id":"p:4-4","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 4","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up an S3 interface endpoint to read data from the S3 bucket. Leverage the S3 bucket policy to control access for each application that accesses the data only via the interface endpoint\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an S3 Access Point to read data from the S3 bucket. Leverage the S3 Access Points policy to dynamically redact PII based on the needs of each application that accesses the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an S3 gateway endpoint to read data from the S3 bucket. Leverage the S3 bucket policy to control access for each application that accesses the data only via the gateway endpoint\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an S3 Object Lambda endpoint. Use the S3 Object Lambda endpoint to read data from the S3 bucket. Implement redaction logic within an S3 Object Lambda function to dynamically redact PII based on the needs of each application that accesses the data\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Interface endpoint tạo đường truy cập S3 riêng trong mạng. Bucket policy kiểm soát được ai đọc object, nhưng không tự sửa nội dung để che từng trường PII theo người đọc.","S3 Access Point giúp tách chính sách truy cập cho từng ứng dụng. Policy chỉ cho phép hoặc từ chối truy cập; bản thân nó không chạy mã để xóa hoặc che PII trong nội dung trả về.","Gateway endpoint giải quyết đường mạng tới S3 và có thể giới hạn truy cập bằng policy. Nó không biến đổi nội dung object nên không đáp ứng yêu cầu che PII động.","Theo kiến trúc trong bộ đề, S3 Object Lambda gọi hàm Lambda khi ứng dụng đọc object. Hàm có thể che các trường PII tùy nhu cầu của ứng dụng rồi trả bản đã xử lý, trong khi dữ liệu gốc vẫn được giữ nguyên."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company utilizes an Amazon S3 bucket to store datasets accessed by various applications, including a financial services application that generates datasets containing personally identifiable information (PII). There is also an internal application that does not need access to this PII. To adhere to regulations, the company must avoid unnecessary sharing of PII. A data engineer is tasked with finding a solution that can dynamically redact PII depending on the specific needs of each application accessing the dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution can achieve this with minimal operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:10:19":{"id":"e:10:19","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 19","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eAttach the \u003ccode\u003eAmazonSageMakerFullAccess\u003c/code\u003e managed policy to the SageMaker’s execution role.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eAttach the \u003ccode\u003eAwsGlueSessionUserRestrictedServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e managed policy to the SageMaker’s execution role.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eEnsure the data engineer\u0027s IAM user has permission to perform the \u003ccode\u003ests:AssumeRole\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003esagemaker:AddAssociation\u003c/code\u003e actions.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eAdd the \u003ccode\u003eAWSGlueServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e managed policy to the IAM user of the data engineer.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is trying to use AWS Glue Interactive Sessions in Amazon SageMaker Studio for data preparation tasks. The data engineer has successfully created a SageMaker IAM role with trust policies for both SageMaker and Glue services. The interactive session is being incorporated into an ML workflow that prepares data for subsequent analytical tasks. Despite this setup, the data engineer is being denied access when attempting to initiate an Glue Interactive Session.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow can the data engineer resolve the issue?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon SageMaker Studio is an integrated development environment (IDE) for machine learning that provides every tool needed to build, train, and deploy machine learning models. SageMaker offers a user-friendly interface for executing ML workflows, making its tools accessible across various integrated development environments (IDEs). Additionally, It conducts operations on your behalf by leveraging other AWS services. You need to assign permissions to enable SageMaker to utilize these services and access the corresponding resources. This permission is achieved through an AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) execution role, granting SageMaker the necessary permissions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5f9a876af9a6e759a735c721d871167ff49a330cf58f56bb6561f9c382016743.png\" alt=\"Amazon SageMaker console Setting up an IAM role using Role Manager\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Interactive Sessions provides a programmatic and visual interface for building and testing ETL scripts for data preparation. To use it in Amazon SageMaker Studio, the SageMaker execution role needs to have the necessary permissions for AWS Glue. This can be achieved by attaching the \u003ccode\u003eAwsGlueSessionUserRestrictedServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e managed policy to the SageMaker execution role.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003eAwsGlueSessionUserRestrictedServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e policy grants the required permissions for creating and managing AWS Glue Interactive Sessions, as well as accessing the AWS Glue Data Catalog and other related resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAttach the \u003ccode\u003eAwsGlueSessionUserRestrictedServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e managed policy to the SageMaker’s execution role.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Attach the \u003ccode\u003eAmazonSageMakerFullAccess\u003c/code\u003e managed policy to the SageMaker’s execution role.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;This policy just grants all actions across Amazon SageMaker, which is not required for the specific task of using AWS Glue Interactive Sessions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnsure the data engineer\u0027s IAM user has permission to perform the \u003ccode\u003ests:AssumeRole\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003esagemaker:AddAssociation\u003c/code\u003e actions\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ests:AssumeRole\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;is required to assume an IAM role, this permission is not specifically needed for using AWS Glue Interactive Sessions. Additionally, the \u003ccode\u003esagemaker:AddAssociation\u003c/code\u003e permission is only used for associating resources with a SageMaker notebook instance or studio, but it\u0027s not directly related to AWS Glue Interactive Sessions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAdd the \u003ccode\u003eAWSGlueServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e managed policy to the IAM user of the data engineer\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect because this policy is typically used to grant AWS Glue the permissions it needs to execute jobs and does not apply to the SageMaker execution role\u0027s permissions for AWS Glue Interactive Sessions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/getting-started-glue-sm.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/getting-started-glue-sm.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/sagemaker-roles.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/sagemaker-roles.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these AWS Glue and Amazon SageMaker AI Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:32":{"id":"e:6:32","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 32","correct":[2],"answers":["Synchronize a year’s worth of data on an Amazon RDS read replica. Move the older historical data to an Amazon S3 bucket for long-term storage. Create an AWS Glue Data Catalog and use Amazon Athena to join the historical and current data for report generation.","Use AWS Glue to perform ETL and incrementally load a year’s worth of data into an Amazon Redshift cluster. Run the regular queries against this cluster. Create an AWS Glue Data Catalog of the data in Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena to join the historical and current data to generate the reports.","Migrate historical data to Amazon S3. Set up a daily transfer of current data from Amazon RDS to Amazon Redshift. Use Redshift for regular queries and Redshift Spectrum to join the historical data in S3 with current data.","Configure an Amazon Aurora database to handle real-time data. Archive older data to an S3 bucket. Utilize Amazon QuickSight for analytics, combining current data in Aurora with historical data for comprehensive reporting."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA game retail company manages user purchase data in a MySQL database on Amazon RDS. They frequently query and analyze the most recent three months of data, amounting to several terabytes. The company also needs to store older data outside the database, yet access it for quarterly trend reports, which involves joining it with recent data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options will provide optimal performance and a cost-effective solution based on the requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshift Spectrum\u003c/strong\u003e stands out as a highly efficient solution for querying data across Amazon S3 and Amazon Redshift. It leverages the benefits of low-cost storage and open data formats, along with Redshift\u0027s advanced query optimization and fast access to local disks. Redshift Spectrum\u0027s serverless architecture aligns with Amazon Athena\u0027s, negating the need for resource provisioning and management.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/16f0325ad19dfed538cd42a4ec50e189cca36f8f333835d5878f4e59acabd972.gif\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn this scenario, two distinct query requirements are identified:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eRegular analytics on the most recent three months of data.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003ePeriodic queries for quarterly reports, encompassing both historical and recent data.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo meet the first requirement, a regular transfer of current data from the MySQL database to Amazon Redshift is established, taking advantage of Redshift\u0027s powerful analytics capabilities. For the second requirement, given the infrequent access to historical data, storing it in S3 and querying via Redshift Spectrum is a more cost-effective approach than keeping it within Redshift. This method avoids data migration costs and utilizes Redshift\u0027s allocated resources for optimized query performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThus, the optimal solution is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMigrate historical data to Amazon S3. Set up a daily transfer of current data from Amazon RDS to Amazon Redshift. Use Redshift for regular queries and Redshift Spectrum to join the historical data in S3 with current data.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSynchronize a year’s worth of data on an Amazon RDS read replica. Move the older historical data to an Amazon S3 bucket for long-term storage. Create an AWS Glue Data Catalog and use Amazon Athena to join the historical and current data for report generation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This approach is inefficient as it requires maintaining a full year\u0027s worth of data on an RDS read replica, which can be costly and doesn\u0027t leverage the optimized analytical capabilities of a dedicated analytics service like Amazon Redshift. Additionally, while Amazon Athena is effective for querying S3 data, it necessitates data movement between RDS and S3, which can be time-consuming and adds complexity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue to perform ETL and incrementally load a year’s worth of data into an Amazon Redshift cluster. Run the regular queries against this cluster. Create an AWS Glue Data Catalog of the data in Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena to join the historical and current data to generate the reports\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. In this method, there is an unnecessary duplication of effort and resources. Loading recent data into a Redshift cluster and then moving it again to S3 for Athena queries is inefficient. Redshift Spectrum would be a more streamlined and cost-effective solution since it can query data directly in S3 without the need for additional data movement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an Amazon Aurora database to handle real-time data. Archive older data to an S3 bucket. Utilize Amazon QuickSight for analytics, combining current data in Aurora with historical data for comprehensive reporting\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While Amazon Aurora is a powerful database for transactional workloads, it is not optimized for the kind of large-scale analytical processing described in the scenario. Moreover, using Amazon QuickSight for analytics across Aurora and S3 introduces complexity in managing data across different storage solutions and may not offer the same level of performance as Redshift, especially for large-scale data joining and analytical queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences: \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-getting-started-using-spectrum.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-getting-started-using-spectrum.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshift Overview:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003ciframe src=\"./📊 Your Progress - Tutorials Dojo_files/jlLERNzhHOg(1).html\"\u003e\u003c/iframe\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:23":{"id":"e:8:23","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 23","correct":[3],"answers":["Implement ETL operations on the data in S3 using AWS Glue and then query the transformed data using Amazon Athena.","Leverage an open-source data lake format like Apache Hudi with an Amazon EMR cluster to capture and manage changed data.","Use the AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) to replicate data changes to S3.","Leverage an open-source data lake format like Apache Iceberg with Amazon EMR Serverless to capture and manage changed data."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAn e-commerce company aims to consolidate customer data from various sources into a data lake stored as CSV files on Amazon S3. A data engineer must ensure changes in the data sources are replicated in the data lake using Change Data Capture (CDC) operations. The solution must be designed cost-effectively and support ACID (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability) properties to ensure data integrity.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich of the following options would meet the requirements?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon EMR Serverless\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is a deployment option for Amazon EMR that offers a serverless runtime environment. This feature simplifies the management of analytics applications that utilize modern open-source frameworks, such as Apache Spark and Apache Hive. With EMR Serverless, you don\u0027t need to configure, optimize, secure, or manage clusters to run applications using these frameworks.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/af483e5d9a2a172a35d9f93845c9fc823ac3b7c291cf06880c4ff426de468df3.png\" alt=\"Amazon EMR Serverless\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eApache Iceberg\u003c/strong\u003e is a distributed, community-driven data table format that is licensed under Apache 2.0 and is fully open-source. It simplifies data processing for large datasets stored in data lakes. Data engineers prefer Apache Iceberg because it is fast, efficient, and reliable at any scale, while also maintaining a record of how datasets change over time. Additionally, Apache Iceberg easily integrates with popular data processing frameworks, including Apache Spark, Apache Flink, Apache Hive, and Presto.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA \u003cstrong\u003edata lake\u003c/strong\u003e is a centralized repository that enables you to store all your structured and unstructured data at any scale. A data transaction refers to a series of data exchanges conducted as a single operation. For instance, when a customer withdraws money from a bank account, the bank performs multiple data exchanges simultaneously in one data transaction. This includes verifying that the account has enough balance, confirming the customer\u0027s identity, and debiting the withdrawal amount from the account.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA transactional data lake is a type of data lake that not only stores data at scale but also supports transactional operations. It ensures that the data is accurate and consistent and allows you to track changes to data and its structure over time. These characteristics are collectively known as Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability (ACID).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Atomicity\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;guarantees that each transaction is a single event that either succeeds or fails completely; there is no half-way status.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Consistency\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;ensures that all data written is valid according to the defined rules of the data lake, ensuring that data is accurate and reliable.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Isolation\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;ensures multiple transactions can occur at the same time without interfering with each other, ensuring\u0026nbsp;that each transaction executes independently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Durability\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;means that data is not lost or corrupted once a transaction is submitted. Data can be recovered in the event of a system failure, such as a power outage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage an open-source data lake format like Apache Iceberg with Amazon EMR Serverless to capture and manage changed data.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eIceberg supports ACID transactions, scalable CDC operations, and schema evolution directly on Amazon S3. Combined with EMR Serverless, it offers a cost-effective, cloud-native architecture that eliminates the need to manage infrastructure while ensuring data integrity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement ETL operations on the data in S3 using AWS Glue and then query the transformed data using Amazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because, while it is a valid approach for data transformation and analysis, it might not be the most cost-effective for the given scenario. AWS Glue and Amazon Athena are powerful services, but they might just be unnecessary for simply capturing changes in the data (CDC).\u0026nbsp;Additionally, they do not inherently support ACID properties.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage an open-source data lake format like Apache Hudi with an Amazon EMR cluster to capture and manage changed data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Although Apache Hudi does support CDC and ACID semantics, using EMR can be more expensive and operationally complex compared to serverless or open-source alternatives.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) to replicate data changes to S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While AWS DMS can be used for CDC, it may not be as cost-effective for large-scale data lakes as using an open-source format like Apache Iceberg. The scenario specifically stated that the solution must be designed to be cost-effective. Additionally,\u0026nbsp;it does not provide ACID properties for the data lake, which are crucial for ensuring data integrity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/what-is/apache-iceberg/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/what-is/apache-iceberg/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-Serverless-UserGuide/emr-serverless.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-Serverless-UserGuide/emr-serverless.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-Serverless-UserGuide/using-iceberg.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-Serverless-UserGuide/using-iceberg.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon S3 Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-64":{"id":"p:1-64","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 64","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Object Lock in compliance mode on the destination bucket before uploading the audit log objects\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lambda function that calculates SHA-256 checksums after upload, stores the results in DynamoDB, and validates objects during a later scheduled job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Macie to scan the uploaded log objects, compare sensitive data findings with the source server inventory, and mark matching files as successfully validated\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the AWS SDK to calculate a CRC32 checksum during upload, send the checksum with each request, and let Amazon S3 store checksum metadata\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Object Lock ngăn sửa/xóa trong thời gian giữ, nhưng không chứng minh dữ liệu tải lên giống tệp nguồn. Cần checksum để kiểm tra toàn vẹn.","Tính hash sau khi tải lên chỉ phản ánh bản đang lưu nếu không so với hash nguồn. Thêm Lambda, DynamoDB và tác vụ định kỳ cũng phức tạp hơn kiểm tra lúc upload.","Macie phát hiện dữ liệu nhạy cảm, không so sánh toàn vẹn từng byte giữa tệp gốc và đối tượng đã tải lên.","SDK tính CRC32 của dữ liệu gửi và kèm checksum trong request; S3 kiểm tra rồi lưu metadata checksum. Nhờ đó xác nhận dữ liệu nhận khớp dữ liệu nguồn trong quá trình upload."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company uploads application audit logs from data center servers to an Amazon S3 bucket for long-term analysis. The company must confirm that each uploaded log object in Amazon S3 matches the original log file from the source server. A data engineer must implement an integrity validation approach for the upload process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-32":{"id":"p:4-32","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 32","correct":[1,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eMultiple KCL applications can share a DynamoDB table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEach KCL application must use its own DynamoDB table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eYou can only use DynamoDB for checkpointing KCL\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) can also be used for checkpointing KCL\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMultiple KCL applications can share a DynamoDB table if Amazon Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) is configured to save transit data and metadata\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Các ứng dụng KCL độc lập phải có trạng thái lease và checkpoint riêng. Dùng chung bảng trạng thái có thể khiến chúng bị coi như cùng một ứng dụng và tranh quyền xử lý shard.","Mỗi ứng dụng KCL độc lập cần bảng trạng thái DynamoDB riêng. Nhiều worker của cùng một ứng dụng mới phối hợp thông qua bảng chung của ứng dụng đó.","Trong cơ chế KCL được hỗ trợ ở bài này, DynamoDB lưu lease và checkpoint để theo dõi shard và vị trí đã xử lý. Đây là backend trạng thái chuẩn của KCL.","KCL không cung cấp tùy chọn thay bảng checkpoint bằng RDS như mô tả. Tự xây consumer với backend khác là một thiết kế khác, không phải cấu hình KCL tiêu chuẩn.","Lưu dữ liệu trung gian vào S3 không tách được trạng thái lease của các ứng dụng KCL. Mỗi ứng dụng vẫn cần bảng trạng thái riêng."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company runs a real-time data processing application that uses Kinesis Client Library (KCL) to help consume and process data from the real-time data streams. The development team has raised a query on the viability of using the same DynamoDB table for different KCL applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following are correct statements for KCL while consuming Kinesis Data Streams? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:25":{"id":"e:4:25","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 25","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable Multi-AZ for the RDS database and run the analytics workload on the standby database\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFor Disaster Recovery purposes, create a Read Replica in another Region as the Master database and point the analytics workload there\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Read Replica in the same Region as the Master database and point the analytics workload there\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMigrate the analytics application to AWS Lambda\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA global CRM company has recently migrated its technology infrastructure from its on-premises data center to AWS Cloud. The data engineering team has provisioned an RDS PostgreSQL DB cluster for the company\u0027s flagship CRM application. An analytics workload also runs on the same database which publishes near real-time reports for the senior management of the company. When the analytics workload runs, it slows down the CRM application as well, resulting in a bad user experience.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you recommend as the MOST cost-optimal solution to fix this issue?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Read Replica in the same Region as the Master database and point the analytics workload there\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon RDS Read Replicas provide enhanced performance and durability for RDS database (DB) instances. They make it easy to elastically scale out beyond the capacity constraints of a single DB instance for read-heavy database workloads. For the MySQL, MariaDB, PostgreSQL, Oracle, and SQL Server database engines, Amazon RDS creates a second DB instance using a snapshot of the source DB instance. It then uses the engines\u0027 native asynchronous replication to update the read replica whenever there is a change to the source DB instance. Read replicas can be within an Availability Zone, Cross-AZ, or Cross-Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCreating a Read Replica within the same Region is the correct answer. As we want to minimize the costs, we need to launch the Read Replica in the same Region, because we have to pay for inter-Region data transfer, whereas the transfer of data within a single Region is free.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5063fcb7f9bf248f4b55e9963759acaac4c20fa13254b7ba0ee6d7612a0c34c2.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5063fcb7f9bf248f4b55e9963759acaac4c20fa13254b7ba0ee6d7612a0c34c2.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/rds/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/rds/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eComparison for Multi-AZ vs Read Replica for RDS:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1140a0023bbea205fe81ca25cc0157ba663115c9d6a80752854fb50db6aa2dd2.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1140a0023bbea205fe81ca25cc0157ba663115c9d6a80752854fb50db6aa2dd2.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/rds/features/multi-az/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/rds/features/multi-az/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Multi-AZ for the RDS database and run the analytics workload on the standby database\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployments provide enhanced availability and durability for RDS database (DB) instances, making them a natural fit for production database workloads. When you provision a Multi-AZ DB Instance, Amazon RDS automatically creates a primary DB Instance and synchronously replicates the data to a standby instance in a different Availability Zone (AZ). Multi-AZ spans at least two Availability Zones within a single region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eEnabling Multi-AZ helps make our database highly-available, but the standby database is not accessible and cannot be used for reads or writes. It\u0027s just a database that will become primary when the other database encounters a failure. So this option is not correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMigrate the analytics application to AWS Lambda\u003c/strong\u003e- AWS Lambda lets you run code without provisioning or managing servers. You pay only for the compute time you consume.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eRunning the application on AWS Lambda will not help, as it will still run against the main database and slow down the application.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFor Disaster Recovery purposes, create a Read Replica in another Region as the Master database and point the analytics workload there\u003c/strong\u003e - This option is not correct because we have to pay for inter-Region data transfer for the Read Replica, whereas the transfer of data within a single Region is free. Disaster Recovery is not within the ambit of the requirements mentioned for the given use case. The correct solution needs to optimize on cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/rds/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/rds/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/rds/features/multi-az/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/rds/features/multi-az/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/rds/features/read-replicas/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/rds/features/read-replicas/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:6":{"id":"e:6:6","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 6","correct":[2,3,4],"answers":["QuickSight does not have a configured connection to Athena.","Athena data tables have not been properly cataloged, leading to potential access issues within QuickSight.","QuickSight is not authorized to access the S3 bucket containing the data.","QuickSight is not authorized to decrypt the data in the S3 bucket, preventing data access.","The data engineer\u0027s IAM user does not have the required read and write access to the S3 bucket.","Amazon Athena\u0027s query performance optimization settings are not properly configured."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer wants to use Amazon QuickSight to visualize the results of Amazon Athena queries, which are delivered in an S3 bucket. However, the data engineer encountered a permission-related error message when they attempted to access the QuickSight dashboard.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat could be the underlying reasons for this error? (Select THREE.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eThe following are common reasons for getting \u003cstrong\u003eAccess denied\u003c/strong\u003e errors when you use Amazon Athena as a data source in Amazon QuickSight:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Your QuickSight account doesn\u0027t have the required permission to access the Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Your data file is encrypted with an AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) key.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- You don\u0027t have the necessary AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) permissions policy assigned to you.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- The Amazon S3 bucket doesn\u0027t exist, or the IAM role used to query the data doesn\u0027t have the required S3 permissions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- You don\u0027t have the necessary service control policies (SCPs) assigned to you. (For QuickSight accounts that use AWS Organizations)\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Your QuickSight user or group doesn\u0027t have AWS Lake Formation permissions. (For Athena accounts that use Lake Formation)\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/14fca410b711a042813fefb20420c4646f404640215cfc293839f00ab035af3b.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eQuickSight uses a service role to communicate with other AWS services like Amazon Athena and Amazon S3 on your behalf. If you are being denied access to a QuickSight dashboard, it\u0027s most likely due to insufficient permissions related to the QuickSight service role. For the data engineer to successfully run queries in Athena, their IAM user must have the necessary permissions to access the S3 bucket where Athena stores the query results, as well as permissions to decrypt the data if the S3 bucket is encrypted. IAM provides managed policies that you can use to avoid guessing required permissions and save time when granting access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e- \u003cstrong\u003eQuickSight is not authorized to access the S3 bucket containing the data.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e- \u003cstrong\u003eQuickSight is not authorized to decrypt the data in the S3 bucket, preventing data access.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e-\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe data engineer\u0027s IAM user does not have the required read and write access to the S3 bucket.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eQuickSight does not have a configured connection to Athena \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because it simply addresses the configuration for data querying between QuickSight and Athena, not the permissions issue directly. Therefore. this option is not related to the error.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAthena data tables have not been properly cataloged, leading to potential access issues within QuickSight \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. This option primarily focuses on the organization and accessibility of data within Athena. While cataloging data tables is crucial for data querying, the permissions error encountered in QuickSight is tied to access rights, not data organization. Proper cataloging would not resolve a permissions denial error when attempting to access data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Amazon Athena\u0027s query performance optimization settings are not properly configured\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan\u003e is incorrect because it only addresses Athena\u0027s query performance optimization rather than permissions. Athena\u0027s query performance optimization settings pertain to the efficiency and speed of data processing and have no direct impact on permissions or access rights.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/quicksight-access-denied-athena-data\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/quicksight-access-denied-athena-data\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/troubleshoot-athena-insufficient-permissions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/troubleshoot-athena-insufficient-permissions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these Amazon Quicksight and Amazon Athena Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-quicksight/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-quicksight/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:20":{"id":"e:5:20","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 20","correct":[0,2],"answers":["Use Amazon S3 Infrequent Access storage class. Query the records in place on Amazon S3 using Amazon Athena.","Create a lifecycle rule to migrate the records to Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval after 5 years. Create another rule to delete the records automatically after 15 years.","Create a lifecycle rule to migrate the records to S3 Glacier Flexible retrieval after 5 years. Create another rule to delete the records automatically after 15 years.","Use Amazon S3 Standard Storage class for newly created records. Use Amazon Athena for querying records in place on S3.","Use S3 Intelligent-Tiering to manage the records automatically based on access patterns."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA medical company manages patient records in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company is planning to implement a data lifecycle and retention policy for compliance purposes. The policy must follow the requirements below:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNewly created records must allow immediate access for occasional queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eRecords older than 5 years must be securely archived yet retrievable for legal and medical review within 24 hours.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eRecords older than 15 years must be securely deleted from S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution satisfies these requirements in the MOST cost-effective way? (SELECT TWO)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eS3 Standard-Infrequent Access is designed for data accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. It offers a lower storage price point than S3 Standard, making it an economical choice for storing data that are not accessed daily but are important enough to require rapid access when requested.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena allows users to perform SQL queries directly on data stored in S3 without moving the data to a separate data analysis platform.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/218da53bffc7ce458d88f74a6a3329e2ca50b36d215a1bacb4ac40d3d481c100.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAs records age, their access frequency typically decreases. Migrating older records to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after five years can significantly reduce storage costs while keeping the data accessible.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eCreating a lifecycle rule for data archiving and data retention will help reduce storage costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct options are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e– Use Amazon S3 Infrequent Access storage class. Query the records on Amazon S3 using Amazon Athena.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e– Create a lifecycle rule to migrate the records to S3 Glacier Flexible retrieval after five years. Create another rule to delete the records automatically after 15 years.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon S3 Standard Storage class for newly created records. Use Amazon Athena for querying records in place on S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While using S3 Standard Storage is a valid storage solution, it’s less cost-effective than S3 IA for storing data that are queried occasionally.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a lifecycle rule to migrate the records to Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval after 5 years. Create another rule to delete the records automatically after 15 years\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Although you can archive data to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval and access it within milliseconds, the requirement in the scenario permits retrieval within a 5-hour window. With this grace period, you can opt for a much cheaper storage solution, like S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse S3 Intelligent-Tiering to manage the records automatically based on access patterns\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. S3 Intelligent Tiering is more suitable for storing data with unknown access patterns. In the scenario, the data lifecycle and access patterns are clearly defined, eliminating the need for S3 Intelligent Tiering. Additionally, S3 Infrequent Access (IA) incurs monitoring and automation charges, potentially leading to additional costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out our cheat sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/s3-standard-vs-s3-standard-ia-vs-s3-one-zone-ia/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/s3-standard-vs-s3-standard-ia-vs-s3-one-zone-ia/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:1":{"id":"e:8:1","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 1","correct":[0],"answers":["Create a multi-region trail in AWS CloudTrail in the Prod account and direct the logs to an S3 bucket in the Security account.","Use AWS Config in the Prod account and send notifications of changes to an SNS topic in the Security account. Create an aggregator to collect config data from multiple regions.","Enable versioning on the S3 bucket in the Prod account and replicate the versions to an S3 bucket in the Security account.","Use AWS Lambda in the Prod account to copy any new objects in the S3 bucket to an S3 bucket in the Security account."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company operates with two AWS accounts - one for Production (Prod) and another for Security. The company stores data in various S3 buckets across multiple AWS regions. They are enforcing a strict policy requiring that any action taken on an S3 bucket in the Prod account be logged in the Security account.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would meet the requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eYou can have CloudTrail deliver log files from multiple AWS accounts into a single Amazon S3 bucket. For example, you have four AWS accounts with account IDs 111111111111, 222222222222, 333333333333, and 444444444444, and you can configure CloudTrail to deliver log files from all four of these accounts to a bucket belonging to account 111111111111.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/a5d7b64f003eb512b03cd28fc4fcd2e518493bc4eca36e6bd98f4d0566dd68a8.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS CloudTrail records actions taken by a user, role, or an AWS service in AWS. If a bucket is designated as a CloudTrail log bucket, CloudTrail logs API calls made on this bucket. Creating a multi-region trail in the Prod account and directing the logs to an S3 bucket in the Security account ensures that every action taken on S3 buckets, regardless of the region, is captured and stored securely in a centralized location.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a multi-region trail in AWS CloudTrail in the Prod account and direct the logs to an S3 bucket in the Security account.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse AWS Config in the Prod account and send notifications of changes to an SNS topic in the Security account. Create an aggregator to collect config data from multiple regions\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. While AWS Config can track changes in configurations and resource states over time, it is not designed specifically for logging every action taken on S3 buckets (such as data access or modification actions). Thus, it may not capture all types of actions as required by the policy.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eEnable versioning on the S3 bucket in the Prod account and replicate the versions to an S3 bucket in the Security account\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. This option will simply help in maintaining a backup of the data and protecting against accidental deletions or overwrites. It does not log user actions or API calls as required by the company\u0027s policy.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse AWS Lambda in the Prod account to copy any new objects in the S3 bucket to an S3 bucket in the Security account\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect because this would only replicate the data after actions have been taken, without providing any information regarding the details of the actions themselves, such as who accessed the data or when it was accessed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-receive-logs-from-multiple-accounts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-receive-logs-from-multiple-accounts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/sharing-aws-cloudtrail-log-files-between-accounts/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/sharing-aws-cloudtrail-log-files-between-accounts/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS CloudTrail Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-cloudtrail/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-cloudtrail/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:24":{"id":"e:8:24","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 24","correct":[1],"answers":["Deploy the service on AWS Lambda functions with SnapStart. Integrate the functions with API Gateway.","Create AWS Lambda functions with provisioned concurrency for the service and configure API Gateway to invoke the functions.","Deploy the service on Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS). Configure API Gateway to route requests to an EKS cluster.","Implement the service for API Gateway on an Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) cluster."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is developing a Python-based data processing service. The service must be exposed as private REST APIs using Amazon API Gateway. The data engineer must ensure the service can handle a steady load with quick, low-latency responses.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow can the service be deployed in the LEAST amount of effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eIn Lambda,\u0026nbsp;concurrency\u0026nbsp;is the number of in-flight requests that your function is currently handling. There are two types of concurrency controls available:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cdiv\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReserved concurrency\u003c/strong\u003e – This represents the maximum number of concurrent instances allocated to your function. When a function has reserved concurrency, no other function can use that concurrency. Configuring reserved concurrency for a function incurs no additional charges.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eProvisioned concurrency\u003c/strong\u003e – This is the number of pre-initialized execution environments allocated to your function. These execution environments are ready to respond immediately to incoming function requests. Configuring provisioned concurrency incurs additional charges to your AWS account.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8ae0c2aa8360a2dd31fcfec2883be4e2797560c9ddb4e81b6db8e27728f54cff.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you invoke a Lambda function, the invocation is routed to an\u0026nbsp;execution environment\u0026nbsp;to process the request. When a function has not been used for some time, when you need to process more concurrent invocations, or when you update a function, new execution environments are created. The creation of an execution environment takes care of installing the function code and starting the runtime. Depending on the size of your deployment package, and the initialization time of the runtime and of your code, this can introduce latency for the invocations that are routed to a new execution environment. This latency is usually referred to as a “cold start”.\u0026nbsp;For most applications, this additional latency is not a problem. For some applications, however, this latency may not be acceptable.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you enable Provisioned Concurrency for a function,\u0026nbsp;the Lambda service\u0026nbsp;will initialize the requested\u0026nbsp;number of execution environments\u0026nbsp;so they can be ready to respond to invocations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate AWS Lambda functions with provisioned concurrency for the service and configure API Gateway to invoke the functions.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy the service on AWS Lambda functions with SnapStart. Integrate the functions with API Gateway\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because SnapStart is designed to improve cold start performance primarily for functions that are not invoked frequently. From the given scenario, it requires high and steady load handling with quick execution, SnapStart may not provide the same level of readiness and performance consistency as provisioned concurrency, which is explicitly designed to keep functions warm and ready to execute.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eDeploy the service on Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS). Configure API Gateway to route requests to an EKS cluster\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Setting up an EKS cluster involves managing Kubernetes, which is complex and requires a significant amount of setup and operational management, even with AWS managing the Kubernetes infrastructure. This option would require much more effort compared to the serverless options.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eImplement the service for API Gateway on an Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) cluster\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While Amazon ECS is a fully managed container orchestration service, it still requires more setup and management effort compared to AWS Lambda. Thus, it\u0027s not the least effort compared to AWS Lambda.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/provisioned-concurrency.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/provisioned-concurrency.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/new-provisioned-concurrency-for-lambda-functions/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/new-provisioned-concurrency-for-lambda-functions/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:61":{"id":"e:1:61","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 61","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the database access credentials in an SSE-S3 encrypted text file on S3. Configure the application web servers to retrieve the credentials from S3 on system boot. Write custom code to change the database access credentials stored on the encrypted file after 90 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Secrets Manager to store the database access credentials with the rotation interval configured to 90 days. Set up the application web servers to retrieve the credentials from the Secrets Manager\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the database access credentials in a KMS encrypted text file on EFS. Configure the application web servers to retrieve the credentials from EFS on system boot. Write custom code to change the database access credentials stored on the encrypted file after 90 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the database access credentials as the EC2 instance user data. Configure the application web servers to retrieve the credentials from the user data while bootstrapping. Write custom code to change the database access credentials stored in the user data after 90 days\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company is migrating its infrastructure from the on-premises data center to AWS Cloud. The company wants to deploy its two-tier application with the EC2 instance-based web servers in a public subnet and PostgreSQL RDS-based database layer in a private subnet. The company wants to ensure that the database access credentials used by the web servers are handled securely as well as these credentials are changed every 90 days in an automated way using a built-in integration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions would you recommend for the given use case?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Secrets Manager to store the database access credentials with the rotation interval configured to 90 days. Set up the application web servers to retrieve the credentials from the Secrets Manager\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Secrets Manager enables you to easily rotate, manage, and retrieve database credentials, API keys, and other secrets throughout their lifecycle. Users and applications retrieve secrets with a call to Secrets Manager APIs, eliminating the need to hardcode sensitive information in plain text. Secrets Manager offers secret rotation with built-in integration for Amazon RDS, Amazon Redshift, and Amazon DocumentDB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBenefits of Secrets Manager:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/710ef69a30f008bb572e9bf5de162581ff40fb8b05b4292b47a7c91a13434db6.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/710ef69a30f008bb572e9bf5de162581ff40fb8b05b4292b47a7c91a13434db6.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/secrets-manager/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/secrets-manager/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the database access credentials as the EC2 instance user data. Configure the application web servers to retrieve the credentials from the user data while bootstrapping. Write custom code to change the database access credentials stored in the user data after 90 days\u003c/strong\u003e - The given use-case mandates that the database secrets are rotated every 90 days using a built-in integration in an automated way. Writing custom code to change these credentials after 90 days violates this key requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the database access credentials in an SSE-S3 encrypted text file on S3. Configure the application web servers to retrieve the credentials from S3 on system boot. Write custom code to change the database access credentials stored on the encrypted file after 90 days\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the database access credentials in a KMS-encrypted text file on EFS. Configure the application web servers to retrieve the credentials from EFS on system boot. Write custom code to change the database access credentials stored on the encrypted file after 90 days\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA key requirement of the use case is to automate the database access secrets rotation every 90 days using a built-in integration. Both these options involve writing custom code to change the database access credentials after 90 days. In addition, storing database access credentials in an external file (even if encrypted) is not a best practice, so both these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/secrets-manager/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/secrets-manager/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-42":{"id":"p:3-42","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 42","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse a provisioned Amazon EMR cluster after business hours to process the previous day\u0027s files, apply the transformations, and load the curated data into Amazon Redshift Serverless\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EMR Serverless after business hours to transform the previous day\u0027s files, store the curated output in Amazon S3, and require partners to query the files directly with Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse an AWS Glue Flex job after business hours to process the previous day\u0027s files, apply the transformations, and load the curated data into an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse an AWS Glue Flex job after business hours to process the previous day\u0027s files, apply the transformations, and load the curated data into Amazon Redshift Serverless\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["EMR provisioned cần quản lý vòng đời và dung lượng cụm. Glue Flex phù hợp tác vụ đêm không yêu cầu khởi động tức thì.","EMR Serverless/Athena có thể là kiến trúc hợp lệ khác, nhưng không khớp đường phục vụ báo cáo Redshift mà bộ đề chọn cho trường hợp này.","Glue Flex tiết kiệm cho ETL, nhưng Redshift provisioned có năng lực cụm cần quản lý. Bộ đề chọn Serverless cho nhu cầu sử dụng theo giờ làm việc.","Glue Flex giảm chi phí ETL khi chấp nhận thời gian khởi động linh hoạt; Redshift Serverless phục vụ SQL cho dữ liệu đã chuẩn bị theo nhu cầu. Phù hợp hai giai đoạn sử dụng trong đề."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn insurance company uses an Amazon S3 bucket as a central landing zone for files from several policy administration systems. The company must clean, standardize, and transform the previous day\u0027s data before making the curated data available to external reporting partners. The reporting partners need to run SQL queries against the prepared data during normal business hours. The company wants the most cost-effective solution for the overnight transformations and the daytime SQL query workload.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-49":{"id":"p:1-49","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 49","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue jobs to run SQL statements that validate known data issues and write failed records to a separate Amazon S3 location\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to profile the processed datasets and manually configure quality rules from the profile results after each schema change\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon CloudWatch anomaly detection on AWS Glue job metrics and create alarms to identify unusual record counts and failed validation runs\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Quality in AWS Glue jobs to define rules for known issues and enable anomaly detection for unexpected quality patterns\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["SQL tự viết kiểm tra được lỗi đã biết, nhưng không tự cung cấp phát hiện mẫu bất thường chưa dự đoán trước. Cần thêm logic thống kê và bảo trì.","Profiling và cấu hình thủ công sau mỗi thay đổi schema vẫn cần nhiều can thiệp. Đề muốn kết hợp quy tắc với phát hiện bất thường tự động.","Anomaly detection trên metric vận hành Glue không kiểm tra trực tiếp chất lượng giá trị trong dataset. Cần công cụ chất lượng dữ liệu.","Glue Data Quality dùng quy tắc cho lỗi đã biết và anomaly detection cho biến động ngoài dự kiến. Tích hợp vào Glue ETL để kiểm tra trước khi đưa dữ liệu cho nhóm phân tích."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA subscription analytics company runs a data platform on AWS. The platform uses AWS Glue Data Catalog for metadata management and AWS Glue ETL jobs to process customer usage data before analytics teams consume the results. The company has identified recurring data quality issues, including missing account identifiers and invalid usage totals. The company also wants the validation process to detect unexpected quality issues, such as unusual changes in record counts or abnormal column statistics. The solution must minimize operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:11":{"id":"e:2:11","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 11","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUpload the compressed file using multipart upload\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpload the compressed file using multipart upload with Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration (Amazon S3TA)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpload the compressed file in a single operation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFTP the compressed file into an Amazon EC2 instance that runs in the same region as the Amazon S3 bucket. Then transfer the file from the Amazon EC2 instance into the Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data analytics company measures what the consumers watch and what advertising they’re exposed to. This real-time data is ingested into its on-premises data center and subsequently, the daily data feed is compressed into a single file and uploaded on Amazon S3 for backup. The typical compressed file size is around 2 gigabytes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following is the fastest way to upload the daily compressed file into Amazon S3?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpload the compressed file using multipart upload with Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration (Amazon S3TA)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Transfer Acceleration (Amazon S3TA) enables fast, easy, and secure transfers of files over long distances between your client and an S3 bucket. Transfer Acceleration takes advantage of Amazon CloudFront’s globally distributed edge locations. As the data arrives at an edge location, data is routed to Amazon S3 over an optimized network path.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eMultipart upload allows you to upload a single object as a set of parts. Each part is a contiguous portion of the object\u0027s data. You can upload these object parts independently and in any order. If transmission of any part fails, you can retransmit that part without affecting other parts. After all parts of your object are uploaded, Amazon S3 assembles these parts and creates the object. If you\u0027re uploading large objects over a stable high-bandwidth network, use multipart uploading to maximize the use of your available bandwidth by uploading object parts in parallel for multi-threaded performance. If you\u0027re uploading over a spotty network, use multipart uploading to increase resiliency to network errors by avoiding upload restarts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpload the compressed file in a single operation\u003c/strong\u003e - In general, when your object size reaches 100 megabytes, you should consider using multipart uploads instead of uploading the object in a single operation. Multipart upload provides improved throughput - you can upload parts in parallel to improve throughput. Therefore, this option is not correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpload the compressed file using multipart upload\u003c/strong\u003e - Although using multipart upload would certainly speed up the process, combining it with Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration (Amazon S3TA) would further improve the transfer speed. Therefore just using multipart upload is not the correct option.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFTP the compressed file into an Amazon EC2 instance that runs in the same region as the Amazon S3 bucket. Then transfer the file from the Amazon EC2 instance into the Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e -  This is a roundabout process of getting the file into Amazon S3 and added as a distractor. Although it is technically feasible to follow this process, it would involve a lot of scripting and certainly would not be the fastest way to get the file into Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/transfer-acceleration.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/transfer-acceleration.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/uploadobjusingmpu.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/uploadobjusingmpu.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:47":{"id":"e:5:47","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 47","correct":[2],"answers":["Use Redshift Federated Query to access and query data from Timestream and Redshift tables together.","\u003cspan\u003eExport data from Amazon Timestream to Amazon S3. Then, use the \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e command to load it into the Amazon Redshift table. Query the newly imported data alongside existing tables within Redshift.\u003c/span\u003e","Use AWS Athena Federated Query to execute SQL queries that span both Amazon Timestream and Redshift tables.","Use Redshift Spectrum to query data in Timestream and join it with the Redshift table."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA manufacturing company manages IoT devices that collect and store records on Amazon Timestream and Amazon Redshift. These databases contain a vast amount of crucial information that needs to be analyzed using SQL. However, the analysis process requires the company to combine or join data from both the Timestream and Redshift tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution meets the requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena Federated Query allows you to query data from various sources using AWS Lambda-based connectors. The connectors integrate with data sources like Amazon CloudWatch Logs, DynamoDB, and relational databases like MySQL, enabling SQL queries to span different data locations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can create custom connectors using the Athena Query Federation SDK, enhancing query flexibility and data access management. The connectors return data in Apache Arrow format, supporting multiple programming languages for broad compatibility.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/73bd5966bc1d5f3da1113dbd5556488d705898b36676e39763b2e571d8ba98ed.png\" alt=\"Amazon Athena\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAthena Federated Query allows SQL queries across multiple data sources, including Amazon Timestream and Redshift. It uses AWS Lambda-based connectors to execute complex queries without data movement or replication. You can analyze IoT device data alongside corporate data stored in Redshift efficiently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the best option is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Athena Federated Query to execute SQL queries that span both Amazon Timestream and Redshift tables\u003c/strong\u003e.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Redshift Federated Query to access and query data from Timestream and Redshift tables together\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because Redshift Federated Query allows you to query external databases directly. However, Amazon Timestream is not currently supported as a source since Timestream is a specialized time-series database requiring specific query capabilities not addressed by Redshift’s federated querying features. These features are more suitable for traditional relational databases accessed via JDBC.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse Redshift Spectrum to query data in Timestream and join it with the Redshift table\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because Redshift Spectrum extends Redshift queries to S3 data, allowing for the analysis of large volumes of data stored in S3 without importing it into Redshift. However, it does not facilitate querying data from Amazon Timestream. Therefore, it is impossible to directly join data from Timestream and Redshift tables as required for this IoT data analysis scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e \u003cstrong\u003eExport data from Amazon Timestream to Amazon S3. Then, use the \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e command to load it into the Amazon Redshift table. Query the newly imported data alongside existing tables within Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because this approach\u0026nbsp;introduces significant operational overhead. It requires manual steps to export, store, and then load the data, making the process cumbersome and time-consuming. With Amazon Athena Federated Query, the data stays within their corresponding database and you can get to query them.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-a-data-source.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-a-data-source.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/timestream/latest/developerguide/what-is-timestream.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/timestream/latest/developerguide/what-is-timestream.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheatsheet:\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:28":{"id":"e:5:28","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 28","correct":[2],"answers":["Use AWS Glue STANDARD execution for the job.","Use a smaller data processing unit (DPU).","Use AWS Glue Flex for the job.","Use spot instances for the job."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer maintains multiple ETL jobs within AWS Glue. Among these jobs, there’s a specific non-urgent task that processes large amounts of data. The data engineer must optimize the cost of running this job without compromising its performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following actions would be the most cost-effective?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFlex\u003c/strong\u003e allows you to optimize your costs on your non-urgent or non-time sensitive data integration workloads such as testing and one-time data loads. With Flex, AWS Glue jobs run on spare compute capacity instead of dedicated hardware. The start and run times of jobs using Flex can vary because spare compute resources aren’t readily available and can be reclaimed during the run of a job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ecdca33bb35099139fb1ff7fadd11e891888c6d71427e56a47bfd3f8a30850f4.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eRegardless of the run option used, AWS Glue jobs have the same capabilities, including access to custom connectors, visual authoring interface, job scheduling, and Glue Auto Scaling. With the Flex execution option, customers can optimize the costs of their data integration workloads by configuring the execution option based on the workloads’ requirements, using the standard execution option for time-sensitive workloads and Flex for non-urgent workloads. The Flex execution class is available for AWS Glue 3.0 Spark jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse AWS Glue Flex for the job.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse a smaller data processing unit (DPU)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Using a smaller DPU might reduce costs, but it could compromise the job’s performance, given the large amount of data being processed. DPUs provide processing power and memory for AWS Glue jobs. If the DPU size is too small, the job might not have enough resources to process large amounts of data efficiently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse AWS Glue STANDARD execution for the job\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect because while STANDARD execution could be used for the job, it’s less cost-effective than AWS Glue Flex for non-time-sensitive jobs. STANDARD execution uses dedicated resources to run your jobs, which can provide consistent performance. However, this comes at a higher cost compared to AWS Glue Flex, which uses spare computing capacity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse spot instances for the job\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. Spot instances allow users to purchase unused EC2 capacity at reduced rates, which can lead to significant savings. However, AWS Glue jobs do not directly support the use of EC2 spot instances since Glue manages its own compute resources and does not expose underlying EC2 configurations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-flex-jobs-cost-savings-on-etl-workloads/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-flex-jobs-cost-savings-on-etl-workloads/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-6":{"id":"p:3-6","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 6","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up S3 Access Point to read data from the S3 bucket. Leverage the S3 Access Points policy to dynamically filter data based on the country of the analyst making the read request\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a separate bucket for each country\u0027s customer data. Provide access to each analyst based on the country that the analyst serves\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure the S3 bucket as a data lake location in AWS Lake Formation and leverage the Lake Formation row-level security features to enforce the company\u0027s access policies\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMigrate the data to AWS Regions that are close to the countries where the customers are. Restrict access to each analyst based on the country that the analyst serves\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["S3 Access Point kiểm soát quyền đối tượng/đường truy cập, không tự lọc các dòng bên trong tệp theo quốc gia của analyst.","Chia nhiều bucket cần tách và duy trì bản dữ liệu theo quốc gia. Lake Formation lọc hàng trên tập dữ liệu dùng chung với ít công hơn.","Lake Formation data filter/row-level permissions giới hạn các hàng theo thuộc tính quốc gia khi truy vấn qua dịch vụ tích hợp. Không cần nhân bản dataset cho mỗi nước.","Đổi Region lưu trữ không tự cấp quyền theo quốc gia của từng hàng dữ liệu. Vị trí hạ tầng và điều kiện truy cập là hai việc khác nhau."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company stores its customer data in an Amazon S3 bucket. The data is accessed by employees from various countries for analytics purposes. The governance team needs to implement a solution ensuring that data analysts can only access customer data from their respective countries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution would satisfy these requirements with minimal operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:2":{"id":"e:4:2","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 2","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eRun GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO NAMESPACES \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027 AND \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027; and run GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun GRANT SHARE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027; and run GRANT SHARE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO NAMESPACE \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027; and run GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO NAMESPACE \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics organization has three AWS accounts. A central data platform account owns an Amazon Redshift cluster that publishes a datashare named inventoryshare. Two separate analytics teams in two different AWS accounts need access to the same datashare. A data engineer must grant access by using the Amazon Redshift SQL interface from the producer cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich command or commands will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027; and run GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift data sharing lets a producer cluster grant datashare access to consumers in other AWS accounts. For cross-account sharing, the producer grants USAGE on the datashare to each consumer AWS account ID.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe correct SQL pattern uses the ACCOUNT keyword with each consumer account number. Because two separate AWS accounts need access, the data engineer can run one GRANT USAGE statement for the first consumer account and another GRANT USAGE statement for the second consumer account.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution matches the producer-side SQL interface requirement. After the producer grants access, each consumer account can associate with the datashare and create a database from the datashare according to its consumer-side configuration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/567ab29ac38fec85b01207bc0ae457b7fd577410c102db07d16ce8a57d37983d.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/567ab29ac38fec85b01207bc0ae457b7fd577410c102db07d16ce8a57d37983d.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/security-considerations-for-amazon-redshift-cross-account-data-sharing/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/security-considerations-for-amazon-redshift-cross-account-data-sharing/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/30b3a2a7035dc55ec02776a898267b1d112b466877cba2aae21ffc53e0afa18e.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/30b3a2a7035dc55ec02776a898267b1d112b466877cba2aae21ffc53e0afa18e.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/security-considerations-for-amazon-redshift-cross-account-data-sharing/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/security-considerations-for-amazon-redshift-cross-account-data-sharing/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2bd4c8f63ccc77df605d76c5cd605d74949c8deedec63a8646985cef011ddc69.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2bd4c8f63ccc77df605d76c5cd605d74949c8deedec63a8646985cef011ddc69.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/security-considerations-for-amazon-redshift-cross-account-data-sharing/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/security-considerations-for-amazon-redshift-cross-account-data-sharing/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO NAMESPACES \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027 AND \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/strong\u003e - Namespaces are valid consumers in specific datashare scenarios, such as granting access to an Amazon Redshift namespace. However, this option uses NAMESPACES with AWS account IDs and combines the values with AND.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe requirement says the two teams use separate AWS accounts. Cross-account authorization should use ACCOUNT with each consumer account number, not NAMESPACE with account IDs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun GRANT SHARE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027; and run GRANT SHARE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift supports the SHARE permission on a datashare for users or roles that need to grant usage to a consumer. That is different from granting a consumer account access to use the datashare.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe consumer AWS accounts need USAGE on the datashare. Granting SHARE to ACCOUNT is not the correct producer-side SQL command for cross-account consumer access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO NAMESPACE \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027; and run GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO NAMESPACE \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/strong\u003e - The NAMESPACE keyword identifies a Redshift namespace GUID, not a general AWS account ID. This option incorrectly places consumer AWS account IDs in the namespace position.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIf the producer needs to share with separate AWS accounts, the command must use ACCOUNT. Using NAMESPACE with account IDs will not correctly authorize the two external accounts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_GRANT.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_GRANT.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/datashare-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/datashare-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/writes-adding-data-consumer.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/writes-adding-data-consumer.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/writes-managing-permissions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/writes-managing-permissions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/security-considerations-for-amazon-redshift-cross-account-data-sharing/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/security-considerations-for-amazon-redshift-cross-account-data-sharing/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-4":{"id":"p:3-4","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 4","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eRun sam deploy with the --s3-bucket option to upload the Lambda code, and then manually add the S3 trigger in the console\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate the Lambda function in the SAM template, use EventBridge Scheduler to scan the source bucket, and deploy the schedule with sam sync\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd the source S3 bucket and S3 event source to the SAM template, run sam build, and then run sam deploy --guided\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd the source S3 bucket and S3 event source to the SAM template, run sam build, and deploy by using aws cloudformation deploy\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Thiết lập trigger thủ công ngoài SAM tạo lệch giữa template và tài nguyên triển khai. --s3-bucket chủ yếu chỉ nơi chứa artifact triển khai.","Polling theo lịch không đáp ứng xử lý ngay mỗi lần upload bằng sự kiện S3. sam sync cũng không thay cho định nghĩa event source đúng.","Khai báo bucket nguồn và sự kiện S3 trong SAM, build mã rồi deploy --guided. Toàn bộ trigger và quyền liên quan nằm trong quy trình hạ tầng dưới dạng mã.","CloudFormation có thể triển khai template phù hợp, nhưng phương án được nêu thiếu bước đóng gói/tải artifact SAM cần thiết. sam deploy xử lý luồng build/deploy thuận tiện hơn theo đề."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics team needs to deploy a serverless pipeline that processes inventory files. Store systems upload CSV files to a source Amazon S3 bucket, and each upload must invoke an AWS Lambda function. The Lambda function converts the CSV content to JSON and writes the transformed output to a separate destination S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer has already created an AWS Serverless Application Model template that defines the Lambda function. The data engineer wants AWS SAM to package and deploy the complete pipeline, including the S3 upload trigger.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will package and deploy this serverless data pipeline?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-19":{"id":"p:4-19","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 19","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to capture the events, invoke AWS Lambda functions to transform the stream records, write the transformed events to Amazon S3 through Amazon Data Firehose, and schedule Amazon Redshift COPY jobs for analytics loading\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to capture the events and use Amazon Redshift streaming ingestion to load the data directly into Redshift materialized views\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon MSK to capture the events, use Amazon EMR to process the data, and use federated queries to access the data from Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon DynamoDB Streams to capture the events, use AWS Glue to process the records, and use a zero-ETL integration to load the data into Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Chuỗi Lambda, Firehose, S3 rồi COPY theo lịch thêm nhiều bước và thời gian chờ. Khi ưu tiên độ trễ từ sự kiện đến phân tích thấp nhất, đọc stream trực tiếp vào Redshift phù hợp hơn.","Redshift streaming ingestion đọc trực tiếp Kinesis Data Streams vào materialized view. Bỏ bước đệm S3 và COPY theo lịch giúp sự kiện mới sẵn sàng cho truy vấn sớm hơn.","MSK và EMR có thể xử lý dữ liệu luồng, nhưng federated query của Redshift không phải cách truy vấn trực tiếp kết quả Kafka hoặc EMR như mô tả. Kiến trúc này cũng thêm thành phần xử lý.","DynamoDB Streams ghi lại thay đổi của bảng DynamoDB, không phải điểm tiếp nhận clickstream độc lập. Kết hợp Glue và zero-ETL theo mô tả không tạo đường nạp trực tiếp có độ trễ thấp nhất cho bài toán này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA global fashion retailer is building a real-time analytics platform for its ecommerce website and mobile application. The company must capture product-view, cart, checkout, and recommendation-click events. The platform must process hundreds of megabytes of events every second and make the data available in Amazon Redshift with the lowest possible query latency for analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:34":{"id":"e:2:34","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 34","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum for all analytical queries against data in Amazon S3, and continue running ETL updates on the Amazon Redshift dimension tables with transaction isolation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDeploy separate Amazon Redshift clusters for ETL and analytics workloads, use Amazon Redshift data sharing to expose live data to the analytics cluster, and use cross-database queries when users need consistent access across shared databases\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the workload management concurrency slots on the existing Amazon Redshift cluster, assign ETL queries and reporting queries to separate queues, and keep both workloads running against the same updated dimension tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon Redshift federated queries to access operational source databases directly, create read replicas for the source systems, and route analytical queries to the read replicas instead of Redshift tables\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics company runs several concurrent reporting queries on Amazon Redshift sales and inventory tables throughout the day. Business users require consistent reporting snapshots four times each day. The company also runs ETL jobs that update product, customer, and store dimension tables while analysts run dashboard and ad hoc queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company has found that concurrent reporting workloads create lock contention during ETL updates. During peak business hours, dashboards experience query timeouts and some ETL transactions encounter deadlocks. The company needs to isolate the reporting workload from the ETL workload while maintaining consistent access to current data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will fix this issue?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy separate Amazon Redshift clusters for ETL and analytics workloads, use Amazon Redshift data sharing to expose live data to the analytics cluster, and use cross-database queries when users need consistent access across shared databases\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift data sharing allows one Redshift cluster or workgroup to share live data with another Redshift cluster or workgroup without copying or moving the data. The ETL cluster can act as the producer, and the analytics cluster can act as the consumer that serves dashboard and ad hoc query workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis design isolates compute resources for ETL and analytics. ETL operations can update dimension tables on the producer cluster, while analysts run reporting workloads on a separate consumer cluster. This reduces lock contention, query timeouts, and deadlock risk that occur when both workload types compete on the same cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe solution also supports consistent access to current shared data. Redshift data sharing provides access to live data, and users can query shared databases from the consumer environment. Cross-database queries can help users access data across databases when the reporting model requires that pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1c98a57840908866e9daa6ff3e2b04d2dee40b1565eb56d8537fbb1db851723a.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1c98a57840908866e9daa6ff3e2b04d2dee40b1565eb56d8537fbb1db851723a.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-data-sharing-best-practices-and-considerations/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-data-sharing-best-practices-and-considerations/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum for all analytical queries against data in Amazon S3, and continue running ETL updates on the Amazon Redshift dimension tables with transaction isolation\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift Spectrum allows Redshift to query external data stored in Amazon S3. This can be useful for data lake analytics, especially when teams need to query large external datasets without loading all data into Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option does not directly fix lock contention on the Redshift tables that ETL jobs update. The analytical workload still depends on a separate S3 data path, and the company would need additional processes to keep S3 data synchronized with Redshift tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the workload management concurrency slots on the existing Amazon Redshift cluster, assign ETL queries and reporting queries to separate queues, and keep both workloads running against the same updated dimension tables\u003c/strong\u003e - Workload management can help control query concurrency and allocate resources across queues. It can improve fairness and reduce resource pressure when workloads share a Redshift cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option keeps ETL and reporting queries on the same cluster and the same updated tables. It may reduce resource contention, but it does not eliminate table-level lock contention or provide the stronger workload isolation that separate clusters with data sharing provide.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon Redshift federated queries to access operational source databases directly, create read replicas for the source systems, and route analytical queries to the read replicas instead of Redshift tables\u003c/strong\u003e - Federated queries let Amazon Redshift query data in external operational databases. Read replicas can help isolate read workloads for certain relational database sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option moves reporting access toward source systems rather than fixing contention in the Redshift analytics environment. It also does not maintain the Redshift-based curated dimension table model that the ETL process updates for reporting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/datashare-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/datashare-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/gsg/datasharing.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/gsg/datasharing.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/cross-database-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/cross-database-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-data-sharing-best-practices-and-considerations/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-data-sharing-best-practices-and-considerations/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:13":{"id":"e:8:13","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 13","correct":[1,4],"answers":["Utilize Amazon DynamoDB to store data and Amazon QuickSight to create visualizations and perform ad-hoc analysis of this data.","Utilize Amazon S3 to store data and Amazon Athena for running analysis.","Utilize Amazon Redshift to store and analyze a large amount of data and AWS Glue to prepare the data (ETL operations) for analytics.","Utilize AWS Glue Data Catalog to centralize data access and permissions.","Utilize Lake Formation to centralize data access and permissions."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team is working on a new project that involves processing and analyzing a large amount of data. The data is collected from various sources and is expected to grow exponentially.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe team needs a solution that can handle this scale of data and provide efficient querying capabilities. They also need to ensure centralized data governance and access control.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of AWS services would best meet the team’s requirements? (Select TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) is storage for the internet. You can use Amazon S3 to store and retrieve any amount of data at any time, from anywhere on the web.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena is a serverless, interactive analytics service built on open-source frameworks that support open-table and file formats. Athena provides a simplified, flexible way to analyze petabytes of data where it lives. Analyze data or build applications from an Amazon Simple Storage Service (S3) data lake and 30 data sources, including on-premises data sources or other cloud systems using SQL or Python. Athena is built on open-source Trino and Presto engines and Apache Spark frameworks, with no provisioning or configuration effort required.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation is a managed service that makes it easy to set up, secure, and manage your data lakes. Lake Formation helps you discover your data sources and then catalog, cleanse, and transform the data. You can use Lake Formation to secure the data and ingest it into an Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) data lake.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0ede9de7a9e531200693688d368d4d377b4abd2baa2fd73bb32c98532d8e71a5.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 is a scalable object storage service that can handle large amounts of unstructured data, which is ideal for the team’s requirement of handling an exponentially growing amount of data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena is a serverless interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. It can handle large-scale datasets, which aligns with the team’s need for efficient querying capabilities.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation is a service that makes it easy to set up, secure, and manage data lakes. It provides centralized data governance and access control, which is one of the team’s requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Utilize Amazon S3 to store data and Amazon Athena for running analysis.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Utilize Lake Formation to centralize data access and permissions. \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon DynamoDB to store data and Amazon QuickSight to create visualizations and perform ad-hoc analysis of this data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect. While DynamoDB is excellent for handling large amounts of data, it’s primarily a NoSQL database service used for key-value and document data structures. It might not be the best choice for complex analytical queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon Redshift to store and analyze a large amount of data and AWS Glue to prepare the data (ETL operations) for analytics\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because while Amazon Redshift is indeed a powerful data warehousing solution, it’s primarily designed for structured data and might not be the best fit for handling unstructured or semi-structured data that could come from various sources in this scenario. AWS Glue, while great for ETL operations, doesn’t provide the storage or querying capabilities needed for this scenario. It’s more about data preparation rather than data storage or querying.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize AWS Glue Data Catalog to centralize data access and permissions. \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect. Glue Data Catalog just serves as a centrailize\u0026nbsp;metadata store. By itself, it\u0027s not capable of\u0026nbsp;managing data access permissions or governance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/s3/\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these Amazon S3, Amazon Athena, and AWS Lake Formation Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:35":{"id":"e:5:35","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 35","correct":[0],"answers":["Create an Amazon S3 Object Lambda access point for the S3 bucket. Then, configure the access point to invoke an S3 Object Lambda function that redacts PII.","Use Amazon Macie to discover, monitor, and protect sensitive data.","Use Amazon Athena to query the data stored in Amazon S3 and retrieve only non-PII data from the objects.","Create an S3 event notification to trigger an AWS Lambda function that redacts PII as new data is uploaded. Share the redacted copies with the partners."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA multinational corporation has a requirement to share certain data stored in an Amazon S3 bucket with external partners. However, the data contains Personally Identifiable Information (PII), which needs to be redacted before sharing. The company wants to retrieve redacted versions of the data from S3.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich option would satisfy the requirements?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eWhen using Amazon S3 for shared datasets for multiple applications and users to access, it’s important to restrict privileged information, such as personally identifiable information (PII), to only authorized entities. For example, when a marketing application uses some data containing PII, it might need to first mask PII data to meet data privacy requirements. Also, when an analytics application uses a production order inventory dataset, it might need to first redact customer credit card information to prevent unintended data leakage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c9d886d3a2b10c4d00d45741262db718546154a122d43e786c77292321bec871.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn this scenario, the corporation can use S3 Object Lambda to dynamically redact PII from objects upon retrieval from S3. By creating an S3 Object Lambda access point attached to their bucket, the corporation ensures that when data is requested, the Lambda function intercepts the retrieval process to modify the content, specifically to redact PII, before the data reaches the requester. This method means that the original data remains intact within the S3 bucket, and there is no need to maintain separate copies of the data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon S3 Object Lambda access point for the S3 bucket. Then, configure the access point to invoke an S3 Object Lambda function that redacts PII.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena to query the data stored in Amazon S3 and retrieve only non-PII data from the objects\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because Amazon Athena is a query service that allows you to analyze data stored in Amazon S3 using SQL. It does not have the capability to dynamically redact PII data from objects in S3 when they are retrieved. Athena can primarily query and retrieve data, but it cannot modify or redact the data itself.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Macie to discover, monitor, and protect sensitive data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because Amazon Macie is a security service that uses machine learning to automatically discover, classify, and protect sensitive data like PII. While it can identify and monitor access to PII, it does not have the capability to dynamically redact PII from the objects when they are retrieved. Macie is primarily focused on discovery and classification.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCreate an S3 event notification to trigger an AWS Lambda function that redacts PII as new data is uploaded. Share the redacted copies with the partners \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because as this approach involves processing data as it’s uploaded, not dynamically at retrieval time. It would require storing a separate, redacted copy of the data, which doesn’t align with the requirement for dynamic redaction upon retrieval. This method only handles redaction at upload, not at the time of retrieval.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/tutorial-s3-object-lambda-redact-pii.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/tutorial-s3-object-lambda-redact-pii.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/protect-pii-using-amazon-s3-object-lambda-to-process-and-modify-data-during-retrieval/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/protect-pii-using-amazon-s3-object-lambda-to-process-and-modify-data-during-retrieval/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon S3 Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:52":{"id":"e:1:52","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 52","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eRun Amazon Athena SQL queries against AWS CloudTrail log files stored in Amazon S3 buckets. Use Amazon QuickSight to generate reports for managerial dashboards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS CloudTrail to stream event data to Amazon Kinesis. Use Amazon Kinesis stream-level metrics in the Amazon CloudWatch to trigger an AWS Lambda function that will trigger an error workflow\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Trusted Advisor publishes metrics about check results to Amazon CloudWatch. Create an alarm to track status changes for checks in the Service Limits category for the APIs. The alarm will then notify when the service quota is reached or exceeded\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon CloudWatch metric filter that processes AWS CloudTrail logs having API call details and looks at any errors by factoring in all the error codes that need to be tracked. Create an alarm based on this metric\u0027s rate to send an Amazon SNS notification to the required team\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eWhile consolidating logs for the weekly reporting, a development team at an e-commerce company noticed that an unusually large number of illegal AWS application programming interface (API) queries were made sometime during the week. Due to the off-season, there was no visible impact on the systems. However, this event led the management team to seek an automated solution that can trigger near-real-time warnings in case such an event recurs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents the best solution for the given scenario?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon CloudWatch metric filter that processes AWS CloudTrail logs having API call details and looks at any errors by factoring in all the error codes that need to be tracked. Create an alarm based on this metric\u0027s rate to send an Amazon SNS notification to the required team\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS CloudTrail log data can be ingested into Amazon CloudWatch to monitor and identify your AWS account activity against security threats and create a governance framework for security best practices. You can analyze log trail event data in CloudWatch using features such as Logs Insight, Contributor Insights, Metric filters, and CloudWatch Alarms.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS CloudTrail integrates with the Amazon CloudWatch service to publish the API calls being made to resources or services in the AWS account. The published event has invaluable information that can be used for compliance, auditing, and governance of your AWS accounts. Below we introduce several features available in CloudWatch to monitor API activity, analyze the logs at scale, and take action when malicious activity is discovered, without provisioning your infrastructure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the AWS Cloudtrail logs available in Amazon CloudWatch Logs, you can begin searching and filtering the log data by creating one or more metric filters. Use these metric filters to turn log data into numerical CloudWatch metrics that you can graph or set a CloudWatch Alarm on.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eNote: AWS CloudTrail Insights helps AWS users identify and respond to unusual activity associated with \u003ccode\u003ewrite\u003c/code\u003e API calls by continuously analyzing CloudTrail management events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eInsights events are logged when AWS CloudTrail detects unusual \u003ccode\u003ewrite\u003c/code\u003e management API activity in your account. If you have AWS CloudTrail Insights enabled and CloudTrail detects unusual activity, Insights events are delivered to the destination Amazon S3 bucket for your trail. You can also see the type of insight and the incident time when you view Insights events on the CloudTrail console. Unlike other types of events captured in a CloudTrail trail, Insights events are logged only when CloudTrail detects changes in your account\u0027s API usage that differ significantly from the account\u0027s typical usage patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure AWS CloudTrail to stream event data to Amazon Kinesis. Use Amazon Kinesis stream-level metrics in the Amazon CloudWatch to trigger an AWS Lambda function that will trigger an error workflow\u003c/strong\u003e -  AWS CloudTrail cannot stream data to Amazon Kinesis. Amazon S3 buckets and Amazon CloudWatch logs are the only destinations possible.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun Amazon Athena SQL queries against AWS CloudTrail log files stored in Amazon S3 buckets. Use Amazon QuickSight to generate reports for managerial dashboards\u003c/strong\u003e - Generating reports and visualizations help in understanding and analyzing patterns but is not useful as a near-real-time automatic solution for the given problem.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Trusted Advisor publishes metrics about check results to Amazon CloudWatch. Create an alarm to track status changes for checks in the Service Limits category for the APIs. The alarm will then notify when the service quota is reached or exceeded\u003c/strong\u003e - When AWS Trusted Advisor refreshes your checks, Trusted Advisor publishes metrics about your check results to Amazon CloudWatch. You can view the metrics in CloudWatch. You can also create alarms to detect status changes to Trusted Advisor checks and status changes for resources, and service quota usage (formerly referred to as limits). The alarm will then notify you when you reach or exceed a service quota for your AWS account. However, the alarm is triggered only when the service limit is reached. We need a solution that raises an alarm when the number of API calls randomly increases or an abnormal pattern is detected. Hence, this option is not the right fit for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudwatch-alarms-for-cloudtrail.html#cloudwatch-alarms-for-cloudtrail-authorization-failures\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudwatch-alarms-for-cloudtrail.html#cloudwatch-alarms-for-cloudtrail-authorization-failures\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-insights-events-with-cloudtrail.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-insights-events-with-cloudtrail.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awssupport/latest/user/cloudwatch-metrics-ta.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/awssupport/latest/user/cloudwatch-metrics-ta.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:3":{"id":"e:6:3","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 3","correct":[0],"answers":["Use AWS Secrets Manager to store the credentials. Grant the Glue job’s IAM role permission to retrieve secret values.","Use AWS Parameter Store to store the credentials. Grant the Glue job’s IAM role permission to retrieve parameters.","Create a configuration file containing the credentials and upload it to an Amazon S3 bucket. Update the bucket policy to grant the Glue job access to the file.","Pass the credentials as job parameters when invoking the AWS Glue job."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer at a large organization is setting up an AWS Glue job to transform and load data from various sources into an Amazon Redshift data warehouse. To ensure security and access management, the engineer needs to store and access the necessary credentials securely.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is the most secure and best practice for the engineer to manage these credentials?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Secrets Manager is a robust service designed specifically for managing access to applications, services, and IT resources. It provides a secure and scalable method to store and manage database credentials, API keys, and other secrets. This service is particularly useful in scenarios where there is a need to securely handle sensitive information such as credentials. It allows you to protect access to your applications by enabling you to easily rotate, manage, and retrieve these secrets throughout their lifecycle.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5acd7d3f382ab699091e40b50bae48861234da1711937ca14f9bd70d692b7add.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen setting up an AWS Glue job, it is important to manage credentials securely. AWS Secrets Manager integrates seamlessly with other AWS services and provides a centralized service to manage secrets. By storing credentials in AWS Secrets Manager, they are kept secure and can be programmatically retrieved when needed. This eliminates the need to hardcode sensitive information in plain text, which is a common security bad practice.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4545f876ee14cfeae0958a33e0873c41a66e26757a9311c67eb4888c4cb27f93.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn addition, AWS Secrets Manager allows for fine-grained access control to secrets using AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) policies. This means that you can control which AWS Glue jobs or other AWS resources have permission to access these secrets. This level of control is important in maintaining the principle of least privilege, a key security best practice.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cspan\u003e \u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Secrets Manager to store the credentials. Grant the Glue job’s IAM role permission to retrieve secret values.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Parameter Store to store the credentials. Grant the Glue job’s IAM role permission to retrieve parameters\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Systems Manager Parameter Store is also a viable option, but it is generally recommended for storing configuration data rather than sensitive credentials. It’s not the best choice for managing credentials in this scenario. AWS Secrets Manager is more suited for this purpose as it offers additional security features tailored for credential management, like automatic secrets rotation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a configuration file containing the credentials and upload it to an Amazon S3 bucket. Update the bucket policy to grant the Glue job access to the file\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because storing sensitive information like credentials in a plain text configuration file in an Amazon S3 bucket is not a secure practice. Even though you can control access to the S3 bucket using IAM roles, it does not provide the same level of protection and management features as AWS Secrets Manager.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePass the credentials as job parameters when invoking the AWS Glue job\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because hardcoding sensitive information like credentials as job parameters can lead to security risks. These parameters are also logged in AWS CloudTrail, making it a less secure option.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/security-iam.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/security-iam.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Secrets Manager Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-secrets-manager/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-secrets-manager/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Secrets Manager vs Systems Manager Parameter Store:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-secrets-manager-vs-systems-manager-parameter-store/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-secrets-manager-vs-systems-manager-parameter-store/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:29":{"id":"e:2:29","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 29","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate the replication instance in the same Region and VPC as the target DB instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate the replication instance in the same Availability Zone and VPC as the target DB instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate the replication instance in the same Region and VPC as the source DB instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate the replication instance in the same Availability Zone and VPC as the source DB instance\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn IT company is using AWS DMS to migrate its Amazon RDS for Oracle DB instance configured in a VPC in the us-east-1 Region to another VPC in the us-west-1 Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhere would you place the DMS replication instance for the MOST optimal performance?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate the replication instance in the same Availability Zone and VPC as the target DB instance\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) is a cloud service that makes it easy to migrate relational databases, data warehouses, NoSQL databases, and other types of data stores. AWS DMS is a server in the AWS Cloud that runs replication software. You create a source and target connection to tell AWS DMS where to extract from and load to. Then you schedule a task that runs on this server to move your data. AWS DMS creates the tables and associated primary keys if they don\u0027t exist on the target.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/dc969e3126391bce2679400ccfa3e1dfd5bc655d127cf726280ac066074076c5.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/dc969e3126391bce2679400ccfa3e1dfd5bc655d127cf726280ac066074076c5.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/Welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/Welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhile migrating an Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) for Oracle source database to a different AWS Region, AWS suggests that you create the replication instance in the VPC of the target AWS Region. This is sufficient to get your solution working. However, to further optimize your solution, you should consider creating your replication instance in the same Availability Zone and VPC as the target DB instance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6187dcbefd6e035edafc7350ec223be22e5fba7ec52eb2be9289e4ddcb985499.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6187dcbefd6e035edafc7350ec223be22e5fba7ec52eb2be9289e4ddcb985499.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-amazon-rds-for-oracle-database-to-another-aws-account-and-aws-region-using-aws-dms-for-ongoing-replication.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-amazon-rds-for-oracle-database-to-another-aws-account-and-aws-region-using-aws-dms-for-ongoing-replication.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate the replication instance in the same Region and VPC as the source DB instance\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate the replication instance in the same Region and VPC as the target DB instance\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate the replication instance in the same Availability Zone and VPC as the source DB instance\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThese three options contradict the details mentioned in the explanation above, hence these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/Welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/Welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-amazon-rds-for-oracle-database-to-another-aws-account-and-aws-region-using-aws-dms-for-ongoing-replication.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-amazon-rds-for-oracle-database-to-another-aws-account-and-aws-region-using-aws-dms-for-ongoing-replication.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:17":{"id":"e:5:17","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 17","correct":[1],"answers":["Implement Python Shell Jobs in AWS Glue for custom ETL script execution in Python.","Use AWS Glue Studio for authoring, running, and monitoring ETL jobs via a graphical interface.","Use AWS Glue DataBrew for visual data preparation.","Employ the AWS Glue Data Catalog to organize and manage metadata for data assets."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company is looking to improve its data management practices. This company deals with a vast array of data types, including patient records, medical imaging, and research information. The goal is to consistently compile and arrange this data to make it readily available for analysis by team members with varying levels of technical skills. This includes requirements for both script-based customization and a visual interface for ease of use.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following can meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Studio offers a graphical interface that simplifies the creation, execution, and monitoring of ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) jobs in AWS Glue. This visual interface is especially valuable for teams with varying levels of technical expertise, enabling users to visually compose data transformation workflows. Additionally, AWS Glue Studio supports scripting for those who prefer or require custom code in their ETL processes. Running on AWS Glue’s Apache Spark-based serverless ETL engine, it provides the necessary power and scalability for handling large and complex datasets, such as patient records, medical imaging, and research information.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOne of the key advantages of AWS Glue Studio is its comprehensive functionality that streamlines the authoring, execution, and monitoring of ETL jobs. This capability is vital for making the data preparation process efficient and accessible to all team members. The platform allows users to inspect schema and data results at each step of the ETL job visually, enhancing understanding and decision-making efficiency. The addition of scripting capabilities ensures that AWS Glue Studio can cater to a wide range of technical requirements, from those preferring a graphical interface to those needing to write custom scripts for complex transformations. This flexibility and ease of use make AWS Glue Studio suitable for all team members, ensuring comprehensive participation in data processing tasks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/46f5ee9b60d2889cb6ba8a4beb7c0c2e891b44844d237859788d987f79223770.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Use AWS Glue Studio for authoring, running, and monitoring ETL jobs via a graphical interface.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement Python Shell Jobs in AWS Glue for custom ETL script execution in Python\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While Python Shell Jobs in AWS Glue enable custom script execution, they primarily focus on scripting capabilities without providing a graphical interface for ETL job management, making them less ideal for teams seeking an intuitive, visual tool for ETL processes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew for visual data preparation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While AWS Glue DataBrew provides an excellent platform for visual data preparation, focusing on cleaning and normalization, it lacks the feature for writing custom scripts for transformation jobs. This limitation makes it less suitable for scenarios that require specific, custom transformations beyond what can be achieved through its graphical interface.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEmploy the AWS Glue Data Catalog to organize and manage metadata for data assets\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. The AWS Glue Data Catalog plays a crucial role in metadata management and data discovery, but it doesn’t offer tools for the hands-on ETL job process, such as authoring, execution, and monitoring, which are critical for the described requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eResources:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/en_us/glue/latest/ug/what-is-glue-studio.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/en_us/glue/latest/ug/what-is-glue-studio.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2020/09/announcing-aws-glue-studio/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2020/09/announcing-aws-glue-studio/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/serverless-etl-aws-glue/aws-glue-etl.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/serverless-etl-aws-glue/aws-glue-etl.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:33":{"id":"e:8:33","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 33","correct":[0],"answers":["Utilize AWS Glue Data Catalog partition indexes and Athena partition projection based on the S3 bucket prefix.","Optimize query performance by refining data partitioning and using bucketing strategies in Amazon S3","Make use of the Amazon EMR S3DistCP to consolidate smaller files into larger ones and reorganize data by more efficient partitioning schemes.","Improve data formats by converting to columnar formats like Apache Parquet."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer at a large e-commerce company is tasked with improving the performance of ad-hoc queries run on customer behavior data stored in Amazon S3 using Amazon Athena. The data is partitioned by date, and over time, the number of partitions has grown significantly, leading to an increase in Athena query planning time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following should the data engineer implement to reduce the Athena query planning time?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue partition indexes are an important configuration to reduce overall data transfers and processing, and reduce query processing time. In the AWS Glue Data Catalog, the GetPartitions API is used to fetch the partitions in the table. The API returns partitions that match the expression provided in the request. If no partition indexes are present on the table, all the partitions of the table are loaded and then filtered using the query expression provided by the user in the \u003ccode\u003eGetPartitions \u003c/code\u003erequest. The query takes more time to run as the number of partitions increases on a table with no indexes. With an index, the \u003ccode\u003eGetPartitions \u003c/code\u003erequest tries to fetch a subset of the partitions instead of loading all the partitions in the table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c5c3b65ca826849941cd4da742f07347012a8a92b62ad518591a1cb2df276f48.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use partition projection in Athena to speed up query processing of highly partitioned tables and automate partition management. In partition projection, Athena calculates partition values and locations using the table properties that you configure directly on your table in AWS Glue. The table properties allow Athena to \u0027project\u0027 or determine the necessary partition information instead of having to do a more time-consuming metadata lookup in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Because in-memory operations are often faster than remote operations, partition projection can reduce the runtime of queries against highly partitioned tables. Depending on the specific characteristics of the query and underlying data, partition projection can significantly reduce query runtime for queries that are constrained on partition metadata retrieval.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize AWS Glue Data Catalog partition indexes and Athena partition projection based on the S3 bucket prefix.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Optimize query performance by refining data partitioning and using bucketing strategies in Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While refining data partitioning and using bucketing strategies can optimize query performance, it does not address the issue of Athena query planning time related to managing a large number of partitions. This approach may still result in a large number of partitions that need to be managed and queried, which can adversely affect Athena query planning time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMake use of the Amazon EMR S3DistCP to consolidate smaller files into larger ones and reorganize data by more efficient partitioning schemes\u003c/strong\u003e \u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. S3DistCP is only a tool that can be used to combine files and minimize the number of objects that Athena scans. This can result in faster query execution times. However, it does not directly reduce the amount of time Athena takes to manage partition metadata during query planning. Its primary benefit is data organization and file size optimization, rather than direct improvement of Athena\u0027s partition management.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImprove data formats by converting to columnar formats like Apache Parquet\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Converting data to a columnar format can improve query performance, but it does not address the issue of longer query planning time in Athena due to a large number of partitions. While columnar storage enables faster retrieval of columns, it doesn\u0027t directly impact the management or retrieval of partition metadata, which causes longer planning time in Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/glue-best-practices.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/glue-best-practices.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/improve-query-performance-using-aws-glue-partition-indexes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/improve-query-performance-using-aws-glue-partition-indexes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these AWS Glue and Amazon Athena Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:11":{"id":"e:6:11","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 11","correct":[3],"answers":["On-demand flows","Event-triggered flows","Full transfer schedule-triggered flows","Incremental transfer schedule-triggered flows"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses a variety of SaaS applications and AWS services. The data engineering team has been tasked with integrating and automating data flows between Salesforce and Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe team needs to transfer new Salesforce event data to Redshift every day but also requires the ability to manually trigger the data flow for debugging and testing purposes. The data transferred should only include records that have been updated or newly created since the last successful flow run, similar to how the team\u0027s existing Glue ETL pipelines use AWS Glue job bookmarks to process only new and updated data from other sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich Amazon AppFlow Flow Trigger should be used?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eWhen you select incremental transfer, Amazon AppFlow transfers only the records that have been added or changed since the last successful flow run. This is achieved by selecting a source timestamp field to specify how Amazon AppFlow identifies new or changed records. For example, if you have a Created Date timestamp field, you can instruct Amazon AppFlow to transfer only newly-created records (and not changed records) since the last successful flow run.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/32294cbf595193ed869c86e8055104cf40d5b070b9b83a7b5131279abdffcef2.png\" alt=\"Amazon AppFlow data integration workflow from SaaS sources to cloud destinations\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe first schedule-triggered flow will pull 30 days of past records at the time of the first flow run. To transfer records created or modified over a different time range than the past 30 days at the time of the first flow run, you can set up the flow to be triggered on demand. You can then use the filter option to pull records over the desired time range. After the on-demand flow runs and pulls the initial set of records, you can edit the flow to be triggered on schedule so that subsequent flow runs transfer incremental data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOptionally, you can add a time offset to the time range for the incremental transfer. The flow run will import records that were created or changed between the previous flow run and the specified offset prior to the current flow run. This feature can be used to accommodate any latencies in the source systems in timestamping changes to records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncremental transfer schedule-triggered flows.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eOn-demand flows \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect.\u0026nbsp;These flows are manually run as needed. This option would not be suitable for the given scenario because the requirement is to transfer new Salesforce event data to Redshift every day automatically, not manually.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEvent-triggered flows\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Amazon AppFlow runs these flows based on a specified change event in the source application. This option is not suitable for the given scenario because the requirement is to transfer data every day, not just when a change event occurs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFull transfer schedule-triggered flows\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Amazon AppFlow runs these flows based on the schedule that you specify during the flow setup. When you select full transfer, Amazon AppFlow transfers a snapshot of all records at the time of the flow run from the source to the destination. This option is not suitable for the given scenario because the requirement is to transfer only the records that have been added or changed since the last successful flow run, not all records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/flow-triggers.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/flow-triggers.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/1.0/APIReference/API_ScheduledTriggerProperties.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/1.0/APIReference/API_ScheduledTriggerProperties.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon AppFlow Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-appflow/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-appflow/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:10:9":{"id":"e:10:9","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 9","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon MSK to capture the events, use Amazon EMR to process the data, and use federated queries to access the data from Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon DynamoDB Streams to capture the events, use AWS Glue to process the records, and use a zero-ETL integration to load the data into Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to capture the events and use Amazon Redshift streaming ingestion to load the data directly into Redshift materialized views\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to capture the events, invoke AWS Lambda functions to transform the stream records, write the transformed events to Amazon S3 through Amazon Data Firehose, and schedule Amazon Redshift COPY jobs for analytics loading\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA global fashion retailer is building a real-time analytics platform for its ecommerce website and mobile application. The company must capture product-view, cart, checkout, and recommendation-click events. The platform must process hundreds of megabytes of events every second and make the data available in Amazon Redshift with the lowest possible query latency for analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to capture the events and use Amazon Redshift streaming ingestion to load the data directly into Redshift materialized views\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams is designed to capture high-throughput streaming data from sources such as clickstreams, application logs, and user activity events. It can support the ingestion scale required for hundreds of megabytes of ecommerce event data every second when the stream is configured with sufficient capacity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift streaming ingestion can read directly from Kinesis Data Streams into Amazon Redshift materialized views. This avoids staging the streaming data in Amazon S3 before loading it into Redshift, which reduces ingestion delay and helps make fresh clickstream data available for analytics quickly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution provides the least query latency for analytics because Redshift can query the materialized views that receive streaming data. The architecture removes intermediate batch loading steps such as Firehose-to-S3 delivery and Redshift COPY jobs, so analysts can work with recently ingested events with lower end-to-end latency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f3ded058c2136947f0f9d8ea6751e080a5db0199117650df00b3180e6d032731.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f3ded058c2136947f0f9d8ea6751e080a5db0199117650df00b3180e6d032731.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d8c8644f331394de057c4c819cb0ed3cbe6c2b2343253dd6cb604bdaad41ea79.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d8c8644f331394de057c4c819cb0ed3cbe6c2b2343253dd6cb604bdaad41ea79.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to capture the events, invoke AWS Lambda functions to transform the stream records, write the transformed events to Amazon S3 through Amazon Data Firehose, and schedule Amazon Redshift COPY jobs for analytics loading\u003c/strong\u003e - This option uses Kinesis Data Streams for ingestion and Lambda for transformation, which can be valid parts of a streaming architecture. It also uses Firehose and Amazon S3 to stage the transformed data before loading it into Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe staging and scheduled COPY steps increase analytics latency. The requirement asks for the least query latency, so Redshift streaming ingestion into materialized views is a better fit than a Firehose-to-S3-to-COPY pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon MSK to capture the events, use Amazon EMR to process the data, and use federated queries to access the data from Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon MSK can capture high-throughput streaming events, and Amazon EMR can process large-scale data. These services can support complex streaming and batch processing architectures.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the option does not load the streaming data directly into Redshift for low-latency analytics. Federated queries are not the right mechanism to minimize query latency for freshly ingested clickstream data in Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon DynamoDB Streams to capture the events, use AWS Glue to process the records, and use a zero-ETL integration to load the data into Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - DynamoDB Streams captures item-level changes from a DynamoDB table. It is useful for change data capture from DynamoDB workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe source data in this scenario is clickstream activity from an ecommerce website and mobile application. DynamoDB Streams is not the correct ingestion service for capturing the original high-volume event stream, and AWS Glue plus zero-ETL does not provide the lowest-latency streaming path into Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/introduction.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/introduction.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/new-for-amazon-redshift-general-availability-of-streaming-ingestion-for-kinesis-data-streams-and-managed-streaming-for-apache-kafka/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/new-for-amazon-redshift-general-availability-of-streaming-ingestion-for-kinesis-data-streams-and-managed-streaming-for-apache-kafka/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:23":{"id":"e:5:23","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 23","correct":[0],"answers":["After 2 months, transition to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA), then transition to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 10 months and finally delete the data after 7 years.","After 2 months, transition to S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA), then transition to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 10 months and finally delete the data after 7 years.","After 2 months, transition to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA), then transition to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 10 months and finally delete the data after 7 years.","After 2 months, transition to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA), then transition to S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA) after 10 months, and finally delete the data after 7 years."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn organization generates 100MB of log data every day. The data is initially stored in Amazon S3 Standard. The organization has a policy to save costs where possible while maintaining high availability. The access pattern for this data is such that it is frequently accessed for the first 2 months, occasionally accessed for the next 10 months, and rarely accessed thereafter. However, the corporation wants to retain this data for 7 years for compliance reasons.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich S3 Lifecycle policy should be implemented?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA)\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is one of the storage classes in Amazon S3 designed for data that is not accessed frequently but requires rapid access when needed. It offers the high durability, high throughput, and low latency of Amazon S3 Standard, but with a lower cost per GB stored and a per GB retrieval fee. This makes S3 Standard-IA suitable for long-term storage, backups, and as a data store for disaster recovery files. It’s designed to sustain the concurrent loss of data in two facilities, providing high durability and availability.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f4376defdf42ce223c2c87cd737695803e26326b65cc17d223c682fb2926dcdd.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is Amazon S3’s lowest-cost storage class and is ideal for long-term archiving of data. It’s designed for customers who want to retain data sets for 7-10 years or more to meet regulatory compliance requirements. This storage class is designed for long-term archiving of data that will be retained for decades and can tolerate retrieval times of up to 12 hours. This aligns with the organization’s requirement to retain the data for 7 years for compliance reasons.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAfter 2 months, transition to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA), then transition to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 10 months and finally delete the data after 7 years. \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eThis solution\u0026nbsp;aligns with the access pattern of the data and the organization’s policy. S3 Standard-IA is suitable for data that is less frequently accessed but requires rapid access when needed. S3 Glacier Deep Archive is the most cost-effective storage class for long-term archiving of data that is rarely accessed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAfter 2 months, transition to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA), then transition to S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA) after 10 months, and finally delete the data after 7 years\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because S3 One Zone-IA stores data in a single Availability Zone, which makes it less resilient compared to other storage classes that store data across multiple Availability Zones. This could potentially compromise the organization’s high availability requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAfter 2 months, transition to S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA), then transition to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 10 months and finally delete the data after 7 years\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because S3 One Zone-IA is not highly available and has a higher storage cost than S3 Standard-IA for the occasional access period. Moreover, storing data that will be rarely accessed in S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval is more expensive than S3 Glacier Deep Archive.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAfter 2 months, transition to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA), then transition to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 10 months and finally delete the data after 7 years\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval is not the most cost-effective choice for data that is rarely accessed thereafter.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon S3 Cheat Sheet:\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:59":{"id":"e:4:59","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 59","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eMigrate the ETL jobs to AWS Glue Spark jobs and use AWS Glue workflows to orchestrate and automate the pipeline\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to schedule DAGs that invoke Spark-based ETL jobs across the data platform\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Step Functions and EventBridge to orchestrate AWS Glue jobs and Amazon EMR jobs across the existing ETL workflow\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePackage the Spark jobs in containers, run them as Amazon ECS tasks on Fargate, and use Step Functions to coordinate the containerized stages\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media analytics company is modernizing its extract, transform, and load workflows. The current architecture uses Amazon EMR with Apache Spark for high-volume audience analytics transformations and AWS Glue for smaller ETL jobs. The workflows write curated datasets to an Amazon S3 based data lake. The company wants to move to a fully managed serverless architecture that can continue to use Spark for data processing. The new solution must orchestrate multiple ETL jobs, automate execution, and reduce manual operational work.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMigrate the ETL jobs to AWS Glue Spark jobs and use AWS Glue workflows to orchestrate and automate the pipeline\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue is the best fit because it provides a serverless ETL environment that can run Spark-based processing jobs. The company can migrate the existing Spark transformation logic from Amazon EMR to AWS Glue Spark jobs and continue writing curated output to Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue workflows can orchestrate multiple jobs, crawlers, and triggers as a single ETL pipeline. The workflow can manage dependencies between jobs, run jobs on demand, and automate execution on a schedule.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution meets the requirement because it uses fully managed and serverless AWS Glue capabilities for both Spark execution and ETL orchestration. The company avoids managing EMR clusters, Airflow environments, EC2 instances, or custom container orchestration while preserving Spark-based processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to schedule DAGs that invoke Spark-based ETL jobs across the data platform\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow is a managed orchestration service that can schedule and coordinate complex workflows through Airflow DAGs. It can be useful when a company needs a broad workflow scheduler across many systems.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, MWAA is primarily an orchestration layer and does not by itself provide the serverless Spark execution environment required for the ETL transformations. The company would still need to operate or integrate with a separate Spark processing service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Step Functions and EventBridge to orchestrate AWS Glue jobs and Amazon EMR jobs across the existing ETL workflow\u003c/strong\u003e - Step Functions and EventBridge can orchestrate jobs and event-driven workflows across AWS services. This approach can be useful for complex workflows that coordinate many different service actions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option still includes Amazon EMR jobs from the existing architecture. EMR on clusters introduces cluster management tasks and does not meet the requirement to move the ETL workflow to a fully managed serverless solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePackage the Spark jobs in containers, run them as Amazon ECS tasks on Fargate, and use Step Functions to coordinate the containerized stages\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon ECS on Fargate can run containers without requiring the company to manage EC2 instances. Step Functions can coordinate multiple containerized stages in a workflow.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this design requires the team to package and maintain container images, manage Spark runtime dependencies, configure task networking, and build orchestration around the containers. AWS Glue provides a more direct serverless Spark ETL and workflow orchestration model.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-23":{"id":"p:1-23","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 23","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate one Amazon SageMaker Studio domain for each research team and configure each domain to isolate the team environment from all other teams\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a centralized Amazon S3 bucket for all regulated datasets, replicate every dataset from the source accounts into the bucket, grant every research project read access through a shared IAM role, and use Amazon S3 server access logs to review dataset consumption\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon SageMaker Assets to publish datasets to an asset catalog, allow research teams to discover and request access to the datasets, and use governed approval workflows to track dataset access\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon SageMaker Feature Store with cross-account resource policies to share all datasets automatically with each research team without requiring subscription requests\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Tách Studio domain giúp cô lập môi trường nhưng không cung cấp danh mục dữ liệu liên tài khoản và quy trình xin quyền dùng chung.","Sao chép tất cả dữ liệu rồi cấp một vai trò đọc chung làm mất kiểm soát truy cập theo nhu cầu. Log truy cập không thay thế quy trình phê duyệt quyền.","SageMaker Assets cung cấp danh mục để tìm dữ liệu, yêu cầu quyền và phê duyệt chia sẻ có quản trị. Phù hợp với cộng tác liên tài khoản và yêu cầu kiểm toán.","Feature Store phục vụ lưu và chia sẻ đặc trưng cho ML. Tự động chia sẻ mọi dữ liệu mà không có yêu cầu/phê duyệt trái với quản trị truy cập trong đề."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA pharmaceutical research company uses Amazon SageMaker AI for model development and experimentation. The company has multiple research teams that work in separate AWS accounts and use regulated clinical trial datasets. The teams need a governed way to discover available datasets across accounts, request access to relevant datasets, preserve existing access controls, and maintain an auditable record of dataset access for compliance reviews.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:24":{"id":"e:3:24","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 24","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse the purge table option\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDelete then re-create the table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eScan and delete items using batch mode\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eScan and delete items individually\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA sports analytics firm uses AWS DynamoDB to store information about user\u0027s favorite sports teams and allows the information to be searchable from their home page. The data engineering team at the firm has a requirement wherein all 1 million records in a DynamoDB staging table should be deleted and then re-loaded at 3:00 AM each night.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich option is an efficient way to delete with minimal costs?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDelete then re-create the table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe DeleteTable operation deletes a table and all of its items. After a \u003ccode\u003eDeleteTable\u003c/code\u003e request, the specified table is in the \u003ccode\u003eDELETING\u003c/code\u003e state until DynamoDB completes the deletion. This is the most cost-efficient option.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eScan and delete items using batch mode\u003c/strong\u003e - Scan is a very slow operation for 10 million items and this is not the best-fit option for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eScan and delete items individually\u003c/strong\u003e - Scan is a very slow operation for 10 million items and this is not the best-fit option for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the purge table option\u003c/strong\u003e - This is a made-up option and has been added as a distractor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/dynamodb/delete-table.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/dynamodb/delete-table.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-21":{"id":"p:1-21","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 21","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLeverage Kinesis Data Analytics to detect anomalies on a data stream from Kinesis Streams by running SQL queries which compute an anomaly score for all transactions and then store all fraudulent transactions in Amazon S3. Use Amazon QuickSight to visualize the results from Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eTransform the data from JSON format to Apache Parquet format using an AWS Glue job. Configure AWS Glue crawlers to discover the schema and build the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Leverage Amazon SageMaker to build an anomaly detection model that can detect fraudulent transactions by ingesting data directly from Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Kinesis Data Firehose to detect anomalies on a data stream from Kinesis Streams via a Lambda function which computes an anomaly score for all transactions and stores all fraudulent transactions in Amazon RDS. Use Amazon QuickSight to visualize the results from RDS\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eTransform the data from JSON format to Apache Parquet format using an AWS Glue job. Configure AWS Glue crawlers to discover the schema and build the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Leverage Amazon Athena to create a table with a subset of columns. Set up Amazon QuickSight for visual analysis of the data and identify fraudulent transactions using QuickSight\u0027s built-in machine learning-powered anomaly detection\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Phương án này phải tự viết SQL tính điểm bất thường trên luồng. Đề muốn tính năng phát hiện bất thường có sẵn và ít phát triển hơn.","SageMaker cho phép xây dựng mô hình riêng nhưng cần chuẩn bị, huấn luyện, kiểm thử và triển khai. QuickSight đã có tính năng phát hiện bất thường phù hợp yêu cầu.","Tự viết Lambda tính điểm bất thường và lưu vào RDS thêm mã và hạ tầng không cần thiết. Nó cũng chưa tận dụng tốt việc chỉ phân tích 10 trong 100 cột.","Glue chuyển JSON thành Parquet và catalog hóa; Athena chọn các cột cần phân tích; QuickSight dùng khả năng phát hiện bất thường có sẵn. Giảm dữ liệu quét và tránh tự xây mô hình ML."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company is looking for a solution that detects anomalies in order to identify fraudulent transactions. The company utilizes Amazon Kinesis to transfer JSON-formatted transaction records from its on-premises database to Amazon S3. The existing dataset comprises 100-column-wide records for each transaction. To identify fraudulent transactions, the solution needs to analyze just ten of these columns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate, which of the following would you suggest as the lowest-cost solution that needs the least development work and offers out-of-the-box anomaly detection functionality?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:5":{"id":"e:3:5","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 5","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon S3 Event notification to trigger Amazon EventBridge. The EventBridge event will trigger an AWS Lambda function to copy the Orders bucket object metadata to the Audit bucket. The EventBridge event will then log the event into AWS CloudTrail logs\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon S3 Event notification to trigger an AWS Lambda function on the \u003ccode\u003eNew object created\u003c/code\u003e event for all the objects in the Orders bucket. The Lambda function will write the data event to the Audit bucket. The IAM role assigned to the Lambda function should have full access privileges on both the S3 buckets\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a trail to log data events using the AWS CloudTrail console. Configure the trail to receive data events from the Orders bucket by specifying an empty prefix and the option to log \u003ccode\u003eWrite\u003c/code\u003e data events. Configure the Audit bucket as the destination bucket for the trail\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a trail to log data events using the AWS CloudTrail console. Configure the trail to receive data events from the Orders bucket by specifying the prefix as \u003ccode\u003eAll-objects\u003c/code\u003e and the option to log \u003ccode\u003eWrite\u003c/code\u003e data events. Configure the Audit bucket as the destination bucket for the trail\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn e-commerce company stores a copy of its order details in an Amazon S3 bucket (Orders bucket). The company wants to log all writes to the Orders bucket into another Amazon S3 bucket (Audit bucket) that is in the same AWS Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will address this requirement with the LEAST operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a trail to log data events using the AWS CloudTrail console. Configure the trail to receive data events from the Orders bucket by specifying an empty prefix and the option to log \u003ccode\u003eWrite\u003c/code\u003e data events. Configure the Audit bucket as the destination bucket for the trail\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAn event in CloudTrail is the record of an activity in an AWS account. This activity can be an action taken by an IAM identity, or service that is monitorable by CloudTrail. By default, trails and event data stores log management events, but not data or Insights events. Additional charges apply for data events. Data events provide visibility into the resource operations performed on or within a resource. These are also known as data plane operations. Data events are often high-volume activities.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eData event types available for Amazon S3 are Amazon S3 object-level API activity (for example, GetObject, DeleteObject, and PutObject API operations) on buckets and objects in buckets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIf you have to log write events on all objects in an S3 bucket then you specify an empty prefix along with the option to log Write data events for the current requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a trail to log data events using the AWS CloudTrail console. Configure the trail to receive data events from the Orders bucket by specifying the prefix as \u003ccode\u003eAll-objects\u003c/code\u003e and the option to log \u003ccode\u003eWrite\u003c/code\u003e data events. Configure the Audit bucket as the destination bucket for the trail\u003c/strong\u003e - If you want the data events for all objects in the S3 bucket then the prefix should be left empty. If a prefix is defined, the CloudTrail will only look for objects per the defined prefix. Hence, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon S3 Event notification to trigger an AWS Lambda function on the \u003ccode\u003eNew object created\u003c/code\u003e event for all the objects in the Orders bucket. The Lambda function will write the data event to the Audit bucket. The IAM role assigned to the Lambda function should have full access privileges on both the S3 buckets\u003c/strong\u003e - Using the AWS Lambda function adds unnecessary complexity to the solution. You can simply leverage AWS CloudTrail integration with Amazon S3 as the most elegant solution to meet this requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon S3 Event notification to trigger Amazon EventBridge. The EventBridge event will trigger an AWS Lambda function to copy the Orders bucket object metadata to the Audit bucket. The EventBridge event will then log the event into AWS CloudTrail logs\u003c/strong\u003e - Using Amazon EventBridge adds unnecessary complexity to the solution. You can simply leverage AWS CloudTrail integration with Amazon S3 as the most elegant solution to meet this requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html#logging-data-events-examples\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html#logging-data-events-examples\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-47":{"id":"p:4-47","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 47","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate separate IAM roles for data engineers in each AWS Region and require each engineer to assume the Region-specific role that matches the engineer\u0027s work location\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate one IAM role with administrator permissions, configure the pipeline code to set the AWS Region from the engineer\u0027s profile, and rely on SDK configuration to enforce locality\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate one IAM role for pipeline access, create Region-specific IAM policies with aws:RequestedRegion conditions, and pass the correct policy as a session policy during AssumeRole\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate individual IAM policies for each data engineer, allow access only to one AWS Region, and require the identity broker to select the engineer\u0027s assigned role\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Role riêng cho từng Region có thể áp dụng giới hạn, nhưng làm tăng số role phải quản lý. Broker đã xác thực vị trí nên có thể cấp session policy thích hợp trên một role chung.","Cấu hình Region trong SDK chỉ là lựa chọn endpoint và có thể thay đổi bằng mã. Nó không phải rào chắn phân quyền; role administrator còn cấp quyền quá rộng.","Broker gọi AssumeRole với session policy giới hạn aws:RequestedRegion theo vùng được phép. Quyền hiệu lực là phần giao giữa role và session policy, nên session policy thu hẹp quyền cho phiên; điều kiện Region không thay thế mọi biện pháp kiểm soát di chuyển dữ liệu liên vùng.","Policy và role riêng cho từng kỹ sư tạo nhiều đối tượng phải cập nhật và kiểm toán. Áp dụng session policy theo Region qua broker phù hợp hơn với yêu cầu giảm quản trị."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multinational financial services company must comply with data residency regulations that require each Region\u0027s data to remain accessible only from that same AWS Region. A data engineer is building a pipeline that creates analytics resources in the AWS Region where the engineer works. The pipeline must access data only from that Region. The company uses Active Directory for identity and authentication. A custom identity broker verifies that employees are signed in to Active Directory and then calls the AssumeRole API operation to obtain temporary AWS credentials for each employee.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet the locality requirements with the LEAST administrative effort?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:46":{"id":"e:1:46","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 46","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSchedule AWS Glue ETL jobs to read the Kinesis data stream at intervals, transform the records in micro-batches, and write processed output to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink application to consume the Kinesis data stream, apply stateful transformations, and write processed results to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSchedule Apache Spark jobs on an Amazon EMR cluster to process records from Kinesis Data Streams, maintain checkpoint state, and write transformed output to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose to read directly from Kinesis Data Streams, enable record format conversion, and deliver the transformed files to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA smart building company receives thermostat, humidity, and occupancy readings from thousands of connected devices. The data arrives in an Amazon Kinesis data stream, and the company must transform the data before storing the results in an Amazon S3 bucket. The transformation logic must maintain state across events, calculate rolling aggregates, and enrich records before storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink application to consume the Kinesis data stream, apply stateful transformations, and write processed results to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Managed Service for Apache Flink is designed for real-time stream processing. It can consume records from Kinesis Data Streams and run Apache Flink applications that perform transformations, aggregations, joins, enrichment, and other stream processing operations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe requirement specifically calls for complex and stateful transformations. Apache Flink is well suited to stateful processing because it can maintain application state, use event-time processing, run windowed aggregations, and checkpoint state for fault tolerance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational overhead because Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink manages the underlying stream processing infrastructure. The company can focus on the transformation logic instead of operating an EMR cluster, building custom state management with Lambda, or maintaining a self-managed Flink environment.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/472767dcfc47191ccc1f06ba1ee4bb0db31dfc88af94d5bfcc37a98861f4d454.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/472767dcfc47191ccc1f06ba1ee4bb0db31dfc88af94d5bfcc37a98861f4d454.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/build-modern-data-streaming-analytics-architectures/working-with-streaming-data-on-aws.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/build-modern-data-streaming-analytics-architectures/working-with-streaming-data-on-aws.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2d9092918e2e5854ba1ae7259b9f2ca6ccd37b3bd51ec6ab950a25d3e5a72af8.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2d9092918e2e5854ba1ae7259b9f2ca6ccd37b3bd51ec6ab950a25d3e5a72af8.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/build-modern-data-streaming-analytics-architectures/working-with-streaming-data-on-aws.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/build-modern-data-streaming-analytics-architectures/working-with-streaming-data-on-aws.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSchedule AWS Glue ETL jobs to read the Kinesis data stream at intervals, transform the records in micro-batches, and write processed output to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue ETL jobs can process and transform data, and AWS Glue streaming jobs can handle streaming sources in some architectures. Glue is useful for many extract, transform, and load workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, scheduled Glue ETL jobs are not the best fit for complex stateful transformations on a continuously arriving Kinesis stream. This option introduces batch scheduling behavior and does not provide the same purpose-built stateful stream processing model as Apache Flink.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose to read directly from Kinesis Data Streams, enable record format conversion, and deliver the transformed files to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Data Firehose can deliver streaming data to Amazon S3 and can perform lightweight transformations with Lambda or format conversion. It is a low-maintenance delivery service for common streaming ingestion pipelines.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, Firehose is not intended for complex stateful stream processing. Rolling aggregates, event correlation, and stateful enrichment require a stream processing engine such as Apache Flink.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSchedule Apache Spark jobs on an Amazon EMR cluster to process records from Kinesis Data Streams, maintain checkpoint state, and write transformed output to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR can run Apache Spark jobs that process streaming data. Spark can support stateful streaming workloads when the team designs the application, checkpointing, and cluster configuration correctly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, running scheduled Spark jobs on EMR adds cluster management, scaling, patching, and job orchestration overhead. Managed Service for Apache Flink provides a managed stream processing environment that better matches the near real-time and stateful transformation requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/managed-flink/latest/java/get-started-exercise.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/managed-flink/latest/java/get-started-exercise.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/build-modern-data-streaming-analytics-architectures/working-with-streaming-data-on-aws.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/build-modern-data-streaming-analytics-architectures/working-with-streaming-data-on-aws.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/architectural-patterns-for-real-time-analytics-using-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-part-1/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/architectural-patterns-for-real-time-analytics-using-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-part-1/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:33":{"id":"e:1:33","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 33","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge to detect new objects in the S3 data lake and start an AWS Glue workflow. Use AWS Glue DataBrew to identify and mask sensitive data, and write the prepared output to a curated S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an AWS Lambda function to poll the S3 bucket every 15 minutes, use custom regular expressions to identify sensitive fields, mask matching values in the function code, and write the transformed data to a separate S3 prefix\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest the customer files. Use Amazon Comprehend to detect and mask sensitive values, and write the processed records to Amazon DynamoDB tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon S3 Event Notifications to invoke an AWS Glue job when new files arrive. Use Amazon Macie to detect and mask sensitive data, and load the processed output into Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multinational online marketplace receives customer profile files a few times each week in an Amazon S3 data lake in the us-west-2 Region. The analytics team must use the files only after the company automatically detects new arrivals and masks sensitive customer attributes. The company wants to keep the processed data in Amazon S3 for downstream analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge to detect new objects in the S3 data lake and start an AWS Glue workflow. Use AWS Glue DataBrew to identify and mask sensitive data, and write the prepared output to a curated S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew provides a managed visual data preparation capability that can identify personally identifiable information and apply masking transformations. DataBrew can use PII recipe steps to mask, replace, encrypt, or otherwise protect sensitive values during the data preparation workflow.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon EventBridge can detect object-level events from Amazon S3 and start an AWS Glue workflow. This event-driven design fits the requirement because the company receives customer data files only occasionally and needs the processing workflow to start automatically when new data arrives.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational overhead because it uses managed services for event detection, workflow orchestration, sensitive data detection, masking, and storage. The company does not need to manage streaming infrastructure, operate EMR clusters, build a custom masking application, or move the curated analytics dataset out of Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3089a59ca7bc696033da2b3754c54c0c11e99dd175087b39d38b9bd9f133af8f.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3089a59ca7bc696033da2b3754c54c0c11e99dd175087b39d38b9bd9f133af8f.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-data-pipeline-to-automatically-discover-and-mask-pii-data-with-aws-glue-databrew/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-data-pipeline-to-automatically-discover-and-mask-pii-data-with-aws-glue-databrew/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon S3 Event Notifications to invoke an AWS Glue job when new files arrive. Use Amazon Macie to detect and mask sensitive data, and load the processed output into Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon S3 Event Notifications can detect new object creation and invoke downstream processing. However, Amazon Macie discovers and reports sensitive data in Amazon S3, but it does not serve as a direct data transformation engine that masks sensitive values and writes curated records for analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also stores the processed output in Amazon Redshift even though the requirement focuses on making masked data available from the S3 data lake. The design adds unnecessary movement into a data warehouse and uses Macie for a masking function that it does not provide.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest the customer files. Use Amazon Comprehend to detect and mask sensitive values, and write the processed records to Amazon DynamoDB tables\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Kinesis Data Streams is designed for streaming records, not for automatically detecting occasional customer files that land in an S3 data lake. Using Kinesis would require the company to redesign ingestion around streams instead of object arrival events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Comprehend can detect entities and personally identifiable information in text, but this option does not provide the simplest managed file preparation workflow for S3 datasets. DynamoDB is also not an appropriate target for curated analytical files from a data lake.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an AWS Lambda function to poll the S3 bucket every 15 minutes, use custom regular expressions to identify sensitive fields, mask matching values in the function code, and write the transformed data to a separate S3 prefix\u003c/strong\u003e - A Lambda polling design can identify new files and apply custom masking logic, but it requires the team to build and maintain detection patterns, masking code, retries, monitoring, and failure handling. Polling also adds unnecessary delay and cost compared with event-driven processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option does not use the managed PII detection and masking features available in AWS Glue DataBrew. For the least operational overhead, the company should avoid custom code for a standard sensitive data preparation requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/personal-information-protection.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/personal-information-protection.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions-reference.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions-reference.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/starting-workflow-eventbridge.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/starting-workflow-eventbridge.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventBridge.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventBridge.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-data-pipeline-to-automatically-discover-and-mask-pii-data-with-aws-glue-databrew/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-data-pipeline-to-automatically-discover-and-mask-pii-data-with-aws-glue-databrew/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:10:11":{"id":"e:10:11","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 11","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable Multi-AZ for the RDS database and run the analytics workload on the standby database\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFor Disaster Recovery purposes, create a Read Replica in another Region as the Master database and point the analytics workload there\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Read Replica in the same Region as the Master database and point the analytics workload there\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMigrate the analytics application to AWS Lambda\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA global CRM company has recently migrated its technology infrastructure from its on-premises data center to AWS Cloud. The data engineering team has provisioned an RDS PostgreSQL DB cluster for the company\u0027s flagship CRM application. An analytics workload also runs on the same database which publishes near real-time reports for the senior management of the company. When the analytics workload runs, it slows down the CRM application as well, resulting in a bad user experience.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you recommend as the MOST cost-optimal solution to fix this issue?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Read Replica in the same Region as the Master database and point the analytics workload there\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon RDS Read Replicas provide enhanced performance and durability for RDS database (DB) instances. They make it easy to elastically scale out beyond the capacity constraints of a single DB instance for read-heavy database workloads. For the MySQL, MariaDB, PostgreSQL, Oracle, and SQL Server database engines, Amazon RDS creates a second DB instance using a snapshot of the source DB instance. It then uses the engines\u0027 native asynchronous replication to update the read replica whenever there is a change to the source DB instance. Read replicas can be within an Availability Zone, Cross-AZ, or Cross-Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCreating a Read Replica within the same Region is the correct answer. As we want to minimize the costs, we need to launch the Read Replica in the same Region, because we have to pay for inter-Region data transfer, whereas the transfer of data within a single Region is free.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5063fcb7f9bf248f4b55e9963759acaac4c20fa13254b7ba0ee6d7612a0c34c2.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5063fcb7f9bf248f4b55e9963759acaac4c20fa13254b7ba0ee6d7612a0c34c2.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/rds/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/rds/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eComparison for Multi-AZ vs Read Replica for RDS:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1140a0023bbea205fe81ca25cc0157ba663115c9d6a80752854fb50db6aa2dd2.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1140a0023bbea205fe81ca25cc0157ba663115c9d6a80752854fb50db6aa2dd2.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/rds/features/multi-az/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/rds/features/multi-az/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Multi-AZ for the RDS database and run the analytics workload on the standby database\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployments provide enhanced availability and durability for RDS database (DB) instances, making them a natural fit for production database workloads. When you provision a Multi-AZ DB Instance, Amazon RDS automatically creates a primary DB Instance and synchronously replicates the data to a standby instance in a different Availability Zone (AZ). Multi-AZ spans at least two Availability Zones within a single region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eEnabling Multi-AZ helps make our database highly-available, but the standby database is not accessible and cannot be used for reads or writes. It\u0027s just a database that will become primary when the other database encounters a failure. So this option is not correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMigrate the analytics application to AWS Lambda\u003c/strong\u003e- AWS Lambda lets you run code without provisioning or managing servers. You pay only for the compute time you consume.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eRunning the application on AWS Lambda will not help, as it will still run against the main database and slow down the application.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFor Disaster Recovery purposes, create a Read Replica in another Region as the Master database and point the analytics workload there\u003c/strong\u003e - This option is not correct because we have to pay for inter-Region data transfer for the Read Replica, whereas the transfer of data within a single Region is free. Disaster Recovery is not within the ambit of the requirements mentioned for the given use case. The correct solution needs to optimize on cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/rds/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/rds/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/rds/features/multi-az/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/rds/features/multi-az/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/rds/features/read-replicas/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/rds/features/read-replicas/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:62":{"id":"e:5:62","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 62","correct":[2],"answers":["Convert the .csv files to a JSON format and use Snappy compression to reduce the size of the data.","Convert the .csv files to a columnar format like Avro and use Snappy compression to reduce the size of the data.","Convert the .csv files to a columnar format like Apache Parquet or ORC and use Snappy compression to reduce the size of the data.","Use Gzip compression on the .csv files to improve data retrieval speed."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAn e-commerce company stores massive transaction data as .csv files in an Amazon S3 bucket. The data is processed using Amazon Athena to generate various reports. However, as the volume of data grows, the processing time increases. The company’s data engineer must expedite the data processing time.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich of the following solutions meets the requirements?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eApache Parquet\u0026nbsp;and\u0026nbsp;ORC\u0026nbsp;are columnar storage formats that are optimized for fast retrieval of data and used in AWS analytical applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eColumnar storage formats have the following characteristics that make them suitable for using with Athena:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cdiv\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003e– Compression by column, with compression algorithm selected for the column data type\u003c/em\u003e\u0026nbsp;to save storage space in Amazon S3 and reduce disk space and I/O during query processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003e– Predicate pushdown\u003c/em\u003e\u0026nbsp;in Parquet and ORC enables Athena queries to fetch only the blocks it needs, improving query performance. When an Athena query obtains specific column values from your data, it uses statistics from data block predicates, such as max/min values, to determine whether to read or skip the block.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003e– Splitting of data\u0026nbsp;\u003c/em\u003ein Parquet and ORC allows Athena to split the reading of data to multiple readers and increase parallelism during its query processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo convert your existing raw data from other storage formats to Parquet or ORC, you can run\u0026nbsp;CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS)\u0026nbsp;queries in Athena and specify a data storage format as Parquet or ORC, or use the AWS Glue Crawler.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e8b95e6a258ad29c7da26e7cc7afb46fd99e70ad7ac60df4a7e8096baca578a5.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSnappy is a compression/decompression library that provides reasonable compression rates while still maintaining good performance during decompression. When used in conjunction with a columnar format like Parquet or ORC, Snappy can help to significantly reduce the size of the data, which in turn improves read efficiency and reduces data processing time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConvert the .csv files to a columnar format like Apache Parquet or ORC and use Snappy compression to reduce the size of the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConvert the .csv files to a columnar format like Avro and use Snappy compression to reduce the size of the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because, while Avro is a useful data serialization system, it is not as efficient as Parquet or ORC for columnar data storage. Avro is primarily used for data serialization, and it doesn’t provide the same level of query performance as Parquet or ORC for columnar data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse Gzip compression on the .csv files to improve data retrieval speed\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Although Gzip compression can reduce file size for quicker data retrieval, it is not the most efficient way to speed up data processing in Athena. Gzip is a row-based compression, meaning Athena would still need to decompress and read through entire files to process queries, which does not significantly improve processing time compared to columnar formats.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConvert the .csv files to a JSON format and use Snappy compression to reduce the size of the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;\u0026nbsp;This option might reduce the overall data size, but JSON is not an optimized format for query performance in\u0026nbsp; Athena. JSON is a row-oriented data format that does not support the efficient columnar data retrieval that is possible with formats like Parquet or ORC.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-output-compression.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-output-compression.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/create-table-as.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/create-table-as.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-35":{"id":"p:1-35","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 35","correct":[0,1,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eApplications that need to perform many kinds of queries, using a variety of different attributes for their query criteria should use Global Secondary Indexes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIf the writes are throttled on the Global Secondary Indexes, then the main table will be throttled, even though the Write Capacity Units on the main tables are fine\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eA Local Secondary Index maintains an alternate primary key for a given partition key value\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eA Global Secondary Index contains a selection of attributes from the base table which are organized by a primary key that is different from that of the table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eGlobal secondary indexes support both data models - eventually consistent or strongly consistent reads\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFor greater query or scan flexibility, you can create up to twenty Local Secondary Indexes per table\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["GSI cho phép dùng khóa phân vùng và khóa sắp xếp khác bảng gốc. Nhờ đó hỗ trợ nhiều kiểu truy vấn theo các thuộc tính khác nhau.","Ghi bảng gốc cũng phải cập nhật GSI liên quan. Nếu GSI thiếu năng lực ghi, việc ghi bảng có thể bị throttling dù bảng chính còn đủ WCU.","LSI giữ nguyên partition key và cung cấp sort key khác. Cách diễn đạt thay khóa chính trong lựa chọn không chính xác theo phân biệt mà đề kiểm tra.","GSI chứa các thuộc tính được chiếu từ bảng gốc và tổ chức theo bộ khóa riêng. Đây là cách tạo thêm đường truy cập dữ liệu.","GSI chỉ hỗ trợ đọc eventually consistent. Không thể yêu cầu strongly consistent read trên GSI.","Giới hạn được hỏi trong bộ đề là tối đa 5 LSI cho một bảng, không phải 20. Đừng nhầm giới hạn LSI với GSI."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is working on modeling data for a DynamoDB production database. To address certain access patterns for the application, the data engineer is in the process of creating secondary indexes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options should the data engineer consider for these requirements? (Select three)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:23":{"id":"e:4:23","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 23","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAdd a created_at timestamp to each DynamoDB item, run a scheduled Lambda function to scan for 18-month-old records, and copy matching records to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd a created_at timestamp to each DynamoDB item, run a scheduled Lambda function to scan for expired records, write the records to S3 Glacier Deep Archive, and use the function to delete archived objects\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable DynamoDB Streams, configure TTL to delete items after 18 months, use Lambda to archive all stream events to Amazon S3 Standard, and retain the objects indefinitely\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable DynamoDB Streams, configure TTL to delete items after 18 months, use Lambda to archive TTL-deleted items to S3 Glacier Deep Archive, and configure S3 Lifecycle expiration\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial technology company writes transaction audit records into an Amazon DynamoDB table throughout the day. The company must keep each record available in DynamoDB for 18 months. After 18 months, the company must archive the records for another 6 years, and then permanently delete the archived records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements in the MOST operationally efficient way?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable DynamoDB Streams, configure TTL to delete items after 18 months, use Lambda to archive TTL-deleted items to S3 Glacier Deep Archive, and configure S3 Lifecycle expiration\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDynamoDB Time to Live, or TTL, can automatically delete items from a table when the timestamp in a designated TTL attribute expires. This allows the company to retain each transaction audit record in DynamoDB for 18 months without running custom deletion scans.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDynamoDB Streams can capture item-level changes, including TTL-driven delete events. A Lambda function can consume those stream records and archive the deleted item images to Amazon S3 with the S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class for long-term retention.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution is operationally efficient because it uses native lifecycle behavior in DynamoDB and S3. DynamoDB TTL handles table retention, DynamoDB Streams and Lambda handle the archive path, and S3 Lifecycle expiration deletes the archived records after the 6-year archive period without custom cleanup code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2bc2412ae2f5c79a67c70be78c04125f40452d4e671c8dd307f957c45a5cfc0c.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2bc2412ae2f5c79a67c70be78c04125f40452d4e671c8dd307f957c45a5cfc0c.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/automatically-archive-items-to-s3-using-dynamodb-time-to-live-with-aws-lambda-and-amazon-kinesis-firehose/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/automatically-archive-items-to-s3-using-dynamodb-time-to-live-with-aws-lambda-and-amazon-kinesis-firehose/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdd a created_at timestamp to each DynamoDB item, run a scheduled Lambda function to scan for 18-month-old records, and copy matching records to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval\u003c/strong\u003e - A scheduled Lambda function could scan the DynamoDB table for old records and move matching items to an archive location. This approach can work for small datasets or simple maintenance jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, table scans add custom operational logic and can consume read capacity as the table grows. This option also does not include an automated lifecycle mechanism to delete archived data after the required archive period.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdd a created_at timestamp to each DynamoDB item, run a scheduled Lambda function to scan for expired records, write the records to S3 Glacier Deep Archive, and use the function to delete archived objects\u003c/strong\u003e - This option stores archived data in S3 Glacier Deep Archive, which is suitable for long-term retention when immediate access is not required. It also uses timestamps to determine which records are old enough to archive.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, using Lambda to scan the DynamoDB table and later delete archived objects creates unnecessary custom code. DynamoDB TTL and S3 Lifecycle provide managed lifecycle controls that reduce operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable DynamoDB Streams, configure TTL to delete items after 18 months, use Lambda to archive all stream events to Amazon S3 Standard, and retain the objects indefinitely\u003c/strong\u003e - DynamoDB Streams and TTL are appropriate components for identifying records that leave the table after the retention period. Lambda can process stream records and write archived data to Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option archives all stream events, not only TTL-deleted records. It also stores the archive in S3 Standard and retains objects indefinitely, which fails the requirement to delete archived records after 6 years.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TTL.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TTL.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/time-to-live-ttl-streams.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/time-to-live-ttl-streams.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/automatically-archive-items-to-amazon-s3-using-dynamodb-ttl.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/automatically-archive-items-to-amazon-s3-using-dynamodb-ttl.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/automatically-archive-items-to-s3-using-dynamodb-time-to-live-with-aws-lambda-and-amazon-kinesis-firehose/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/automatically-archive-items-to-s3-using-dynamodb-time-to-live-with-aws-lambda-and-amazon-kinesis-firehose/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-10":{"id":"p:3-10","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 10","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSpecify the smaller table on the left side of the join and the larger table on the right side\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpdate the Athena workgroup settings for the given query, so it has access to the latest version of Athena engine\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSpecify the larger table on the left side of the join and the smaller table on the right side\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpdate the Athena workgroup settings for the given query, so it has access to the most stable version of Athena engine\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Trong mô hình hash join mà đề xét, bảng bên phải được dùng làm phía build. Đặt bảng lớn bên phải dễ tốn bộ nhớ.","Đổi engine có thể ảnh hưởng tối ưu nhưng chưa trực tiếp sửa cách JOIN hai bảng khác biệt kích thước được nêu.","Đặt bảng lớn bên trái và bảng nhỏ bên phải để phía build của phép JOIN vừa bộ nhớ hơn theo cách lập kế hoạch mà đề mô tả. Optimizer thực tế cũng có thể sắp xếp lại JOIN.","Khái niệm phiên bản ổn định nhất không phải thao tác tối ưu cụ thể cho điểm nghẽn JOIN này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn Amazon Athena query is lagging in performance. The query has a join on two tables using the equality condition. You have been hired as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to optimize the query. Upon analysis, you have noticed that one of the tables is large with millions of rows of data and the other table is small.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat do you recommend to address the given requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:54":{"id":"e:2:54","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 54","correct":[2,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eBucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has low cardinality and evenly distributed values\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eBucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has high cardinality and sparsely distributed values\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eBucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has high cardinality and evenly distributed values\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePartitioning CTAS query results works well when the number of partitions you plan to have is limited and the partitioned columns have low cardinality\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePartitioning CTAS query results works well when the number of partitions you plan to have is limited and the partitioned columns have high cardinality\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company wants to store data from Athena CTAS (\u003ccode\u003eCREATE TABLE AS SELECT\u003c/code\u003e) query results in Amazon S3. A data engineer wants to understand the distinction between partitioning vs bucketing for storing data via such CTAS queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate, which of the following options would you identify as the right fit for choosing bucketing vs partitioning? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePartitioning CTAS query results works well when the number of partitions you plan to have is limited and the partitioned columns have low cardinality\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eBucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has high cardinality and evenly distributed values\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBy partitioning your data, you can restrict the amount of data scanned by each query, thus improving performance and reducing cost. You can partition your data by any key. A common practice is to partition the data based on time, often leading to a multi-level partitioning scheme. For example, a customer who has data coming in every hour might decide to partition by year, month, date, and hour. Another customer, who has data coming from many different sources but that is loaded only once per day, might partition by a data source identifier and date.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you run a CTAS query, Athena writes the results to a specified location in Amazon S3. If you specify partitions, it creates them and stores each partition in a separate partition folder in the same location. Having partitions in Amazon S3 helps with Athena query performance because this helps you run targeted queries for only specific partitions. As a best practice, you should partition data by those columns that have similar characteristics, such as records from the same department, and that can have a limited number of possible values (low cardinality), such as a limited number of distinct departments in an organization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has high cardinality and evenly distributed values. For example, columns storing timestamp data could potentially have a very large number of distinct values, and their data is evenly distributed across the data set. To choose the column by which to bucket the CTAS query results, use the column that has a high number of values (high cardinality) and whose data can be split for storage into many buckets that will have roughly the same amount of data. Columns that are sparsely populated with values are not good candidates for bucketing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou should also note that you can partition and use bucketing for storing the results of the same CTAS query. These techniques for writing data do not exclude each other. Typically, the columns you use for bucketing differ from those you use for partitioning.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c4726e2184840cb11b127fad8bada0f9d6ad45b7015dda0a6f1862dd7b27018c.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c4726e2184840cb11b127fad8bada0f9d6ad45b7015dda0a6f1862dd7b27018c.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/bucketing-vs-partitioning.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/bucketing-vs-partitioning.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePartitioning CTAS query results works well when the number of partitions you plan to have is limited and the partitioned columns have high cardinality\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eBucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has low cardinality and evenly distributed values\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eBucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has high cardinality and sparsely distributed values\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThese three options contradict the explanation provided above, so these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/bucketing-vs-partitioning.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/bucketing-vs-partitioning.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:27":{"id":"e:6:27","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 27","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003ecurrent_schema()\u003c/code\u003e is a compute node–only function in Redshift and can\u0027t be run with a query that references a Redshift \u003ccode\u003esales_data\u003c/code\u003e user table that resides on the leader nodes.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003ecurrent_schema()\u003c/code\u003e is a leader node–only function in Redshift and can\u0027t be run with a query that references a Redshift \u003ccode\u003esales_data\u003c/code\u003e user table that resides on the compute nodes.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eThe\u003ccode\u003ecurrent_schema()\u003c/code\u003e is a compute node–only function in Redshift and can\u0027t be run with a query that references a Redshift \u003ccode\u003euser_id\u003c/code\u003e column that resides on the compute nodes.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003ecurrent_schema()\u003c/code\u003e is a leader node–only function in Redshift and can\u0027t be run because it does not reference a table.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has a data warehouse solution running in an Amazon Redshift cluster in AWS. The company’s data engineer runs the following query in Redshift:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eselect current_schema(), userid from sales_data where order_date = \u00272025-01-01\u0027 limit 1;\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eUpon running the query, there was an error in the output that says:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eINFO:  Function \"current_schema()\" not supported\u003c/code\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003ccode\u003eERROR:  Specified types or functions (one per INFO message) not supported on Redshift tables.\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is the MOST likely root cause of this issue?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift has two types of nodes: \u003cstrong\u003eleader nodes\u003c/strong\u003e and \u003cstrong\u003ecompute nodes\u003c/strong\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCompute Nodes\u003c/strong\u003e: These nodes store user-created tables and process queries. They handle data storage, filtering, and computations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeader Node\u003c/strong\u003e:\u0026nbsp;This node manages query execution, distributes work to compute nodes, and stores system tables. It also manages external communication and aggregates the results from the compute nodes. Certain functions, including\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ecurrent_schema()\u003c/code\u003e, are \u003cstrong\u003eleader node–only functions\u003c/strong\u003e, meaning they can only be executed on the leader node.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis node manages query execution, distributes work to compute nodes, and stores system tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSystem Tables\u003c/strong\u003e: Redshift has several system tables that store metadata and information about the cluster, databases, schemas, tables, and users. These tables are typically prefixed with \"PG_\" or \"STV_\".\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUser-Defined Tables\u003c/strong\u003e: These are the tables created by users to store their data in Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/798db4aabb6a36bc73703b758eb7a9a367f50614ab0d82065335421d6823f434.png\" alt=\"Redshift Compute Leader Node\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe sales_data table in the query is a user-created table, which resides on the compute nodes. Redshift does not allow leader node–only functions, like current_schema(), to be executed in queries that reference user-created tables on the compute nodes, as this would require the function to run on compute nodes, which is not supported.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTherefore, the error occurs because the current_schema() function, which can only run on the leader node, is used in a query that references the sales_data user table, which is stored on the compute nodes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003ecurrent_schema()\u003c/code\u003e is a leader node–only function in Redshift and can\u0027t be run with a query that references a Redshift \u003ccode\u003esales_data\u003c/code\u003e user table that resides on the compute nodes.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003ecurrent_schema()\u003c/code\u003e is a compute node–only function in Redshift and can\u0027t be run with a query that references a Redshift \u003ccode\u003esales_data\u003c/code\u003e user table that resides on the leader nodes\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because the \u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003e\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003ccode\u003ecurrent_schema\u003c/code\u003e is actually a leader-only function and not for compute node-only.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003ecurrent_schema()\u003c/code\u003e is a compute node–only function in Redshift and can\u0027t be run with a query that references a Redshift \u003ccode\u003euser_id\u003c/code\u003e column that resides on the compute nodes\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Similar to the previous option, the \u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003e\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003ccode\u003ecurrent_schema\u003c/code\u003e is actually a leader-only function and not for compute node-only. Additionally, the error message in the question refers primarily to the \u003ccode\u003esales_data\u003c/code\u003e table, not a \u003ccode\u003euser_id\u003c/code\u003e table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003ecurrent_schema()\u003c/code\u003e is a leader node–only function in Redshift and can\u0027t be run because it does not reference a table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect simply because the issue is not due to the absence of a table reference but because of the leader node function being used with a table from the compute nodes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_SQL_functions_leader_node_only.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_SQL_functions_leader_node_only.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_sql-functions-leader-node.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_sql-functions-leader-node.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:60":{"id":"e:4:60","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 60","correct":[1,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to upload data from multiple files in Amazon S3 into a single Amazon Redshift table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eWhen loading multiple files into a single table, use a single COPY command\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eWhen loading multiple files into a single table, use a single S3DistCp command\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage temporary staging tables during the data loading process\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eWhen loading multiple files into a single table, use multiple COPY commands\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn IT company is revamping its ETL process and wants to transfer data from Amazon S3 to an Amazon Redshift cluster. The company wants to load the data in bulk onto Amazon Redshift using the best possible high-performance solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate, which of the following steps would you recommend for this requirement? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eWhen loading multiple files into a single table, use a single COPY command\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift is a fully managed, scalable data warehouse that enables secure analytics at scale. Amazon Redshift is an MPP (massively parallel processing) database, where all the compute nodes divide and parallelize the work of ingesting data. Each node is further subdivided into slices, with each slice having one or more dedicated cores, equally dividing the processing capacity. When you load data into Amazon Redshift, you should aim to have each slice do an equal amount of work. When splitting your data files, ensure that they are of approximately equal size – between 1 MB and 1 GB after compression. The number of files should be a multiple of the number of slices in your cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen loading multiple files into a single table, use a single COPY command for the table, rather than multiple COPY commands. Amazon Redshift automatically parallelizes the data ingestion. Using a single COPY command to bulk load data into a table ensures optimal use of cluster resources and the quickest possible throughput.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/74c4b511037bc4bf499e3156615cda2ba81a677e09155a9a3f3b437163c253c5.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/74c4b511037bc4bf499e3156615cda2ba81a677e09155a9a3f3b437163c253c5.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-8-best-practices-for-high-performance-etl-processing-using-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-8-best-practices-for-high-performance-etl-processing-using-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage temporary staging tables during the data loading process\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you are doing bulk ETL for Redshift, AWS recommends that you use temporary staging tables to hold the data for transformation. These tables are automatically dropped after the ETL session is complete. This allows efficient and fast transfer of these bulk datasets into Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eWhen loading multiple files into a single table, use a single S3DistCp command\u003c/strong\u003e - Using S3DistCp, you can efficiently copy large amounts of data from Amazon S3 into HDFS where it can be processed by subsequent steps in your Amazon EMR cluster. S3DistCp cannot be used with a Redshift cluster, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eWhen loading multiple files into a single table, use multiple COPY commands\u003c/strong\u003e - As mentioned in the explanation above, when loading multiple files into a single table, use a single COPY command for the table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to upload data from multiple files in Amazon S3 into a single Amazon Redshift table\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift Spectrum enables you to run Amazon Redshift SQL queries on data that is stored in Amazon S3. With Redshift Spectrum, data is not uploaded into Redshift tables. Instead, the native Amazon Redshift cluster makes the invocation to Amazon Redshift Spectrum when the SQL query requests data from an external table stored in Amazon S3. You can handle multiple requests in parallel by using Amazon Redshift Spectrum on external tables to scan, filter, aggregate, and return rows from Amazon S3 into the Amazon Redshift cluster. All these operations are performed outside of Amazon Redshift, which reduces the computational load on the Amazon Redshift cluster and improves concurrency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-8-best-practices-for-high-performance-etl-processing-using-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-8-best-practices-for-high-performance-etl-processing-using-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/10-best-practices-for-amazon-redshift-spectrum/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/10-best-practices-for-amazon-redshift-spectrum/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:17":{"id":"e:2:17","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 17","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSELECT * FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history WHERE column_name NOT IN (\u0027customer_account_id\u0027, \u0027source_system_key\u0027);\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSELECT * NOT IN customer_account_id, source_system_key FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history;\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSELECT * EXCLUDE customer_account_id, source_system_key FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history;\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSELECT * EXCEPT customer_account_id, source_system_key FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history;\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses Amazon Redshift as its enterprise data warehouse. A data engineer must query a table named analytics.fulfilled_order_history, which contains 120 columns. The query must return all columns except the columns named customer_account_id and source_system_key.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich Amazon Redshift SQL statement will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSELECT * EXCLUDE customer_account_id, source_system_key FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift supports the EXCLUDE column_list clause with SELECT *. This syntax returns all columns from the table except the columns that the query explicitly names in the EXCLUDE list.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineer needs to query a wide table with 120 columns and omit only customer_account_id and source_system_key. SELECT * EXCLUDE customer_account_id, source_system_key avoids listing the other 118 columns manually.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis statement directly meets the requirement because it uses valid Amazon Redshift SQL syntax and applies the exclusion at the projection level. The query returns the same rows from analytics.fulfilled_order_history while removing only the two specified columns from the result set.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSELECT * NOT IN customer_account_id, source_system_key FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history;\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift does not use SELECT * NOT IN to exclude columns from the SELECT list. NOT IN is a predicate that SQL commonly uses in WHERE clauses to compare values against a list.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis statement treats column exclusion as if it were a row-filtering construct. It does not provide valid Redshift syntax for returning all columns except selected columns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSELECT * EXCEPT customer_account_id, source_system_key FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history;\u003c/strong\u003e - EXCEPT is not the correct Redshift syntax for excluding columns from SELECT *. In SQL, EXCEPT generally represents a set operation that returns rows from one query that do not appear in another query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option resembles syntax that exists in some SQL dialects for column exclusion, but the Amazon Redshift syntax for this requirement is EXCLUDE column_list. Therefore, this statement will not meet the requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSELECT * FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history WHERE column_name NOT IN (\u0027customer_account_id\u0027, \u0027source_system_key\u0027);\u003c/strong\u003e - A WHERE clause filters rows, not columns. The expression shown attempts to compare a metadata-like column_name value to a list of column names, but the query still does not change the set of columns returned by SELECT *.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eEven if the table had a column named column_name, this statement would filter rows based on values in that column. It would not exclude customer_account_id and source_system_key from the result set.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_EXCLUDE_list.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_EXCLUDE_list.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_SELECT_synopsis.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_SELECT_synopsis.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_SELECT_list.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_SELECT_list.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:49":{"id":"e:5:49","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 49","correct":[0,3],"answers":["Create a job that automatically copies the last three months of data to Amazon Redshift and unloads data older than three months to Amazon S3. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to include the 6 months of data for semi-annual analysis.","Implement an S3 lifecycle policy that moves data over 6 months old to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval.","Create a job that automatically copies the last three months of data to Amazon Redshift and unloads data older than three months to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to include the 6 months of data for semi-annual analysis.","Implement an S3 lifecycle policy that moves data over 6 months old to S3 Glacier Deep Archive.","Implement Amazon QuickSight to analyze all the data stored in the S3 Data lake. Configure the QuickSight to go directly to the S3 data lake to enable real-time analytics of all the data."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA manufacturing company uses Amazon S3 as a data lake for collecting records from multiple sources. The company’s sales team frequently analyzes data from the past three months.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMoreover, the company generates a semi-annual report that includes data from the past six months. Even though there will be no further access to data older than 6 months, the company must retain it to comply with its data retention policy.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the solutions will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively? (Select TWO).\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift is a fully managed cloud data warehouse that makes analyzing all your data simple and cost-effective. Using Amazon Redshift Spectrum can efficiently query and retrieve structured and semistructured data from files in Amazon S3 without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift Tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Glacier storage classes are purposely built for data archiving that provides the highest performance and the lowest cost archive storage in the cloud.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c0c9e3fb63098cf4ff06c2a04a1e554b1a256586324b6b48405406465a0499b9.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThere are two query requirements in the scenario:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e1. Regular queries against the most recent 3 months of data\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e2. Occasional queries for the semi-annual report\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo solve the first requirement, we create an automated job that loads current data from the data lake to Amazon Redshift. We then use Amazon Redshift for running analytics.\u0026nbsp;As for the 2nd requirement, since any data past 3 months would be rarely accessed, we can unload it to S3 instead of keeping it in the Redshift cluster to save on storage costs. Redshift Spectrum eliminates the need to move data to Redshift in order for it to be accessible within a cluster. Hence, you’d be able to use Redshift Spectrum to run queries directly against data in an S3 bucket. Redshift Spectrum uses the same resources you used on the Redshift database, and it only charges for data queried from Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFinally, to comply with the data retention policy, you can create an S3 lifecycle policy that moves data older than 6 months to Glacier Deep Archive. S3 Glacier Deep Archive, offering the lowest-cost storage among the Glacier tiers, is ideal for long-term archived data accessed less than once a year and retrieved asynchronously.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct options are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Create a job that automatically copies the last three months of data to Amazon Redshift and unloads data older than three months to Amazon S3. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to include the 6 months of data for semi-annual analysis. \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e-Implement an S3 lifecycle policy that moves data over 6 months old to S3 Glacier Deep Archive.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCreate a job that automatically copies the last three months of data to Amazon Redshift and unloads data older than three months to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to include the 6 months of data for semi-annual analysis\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because it is not possible to unload data directly from Amazon Redshift to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval. Moreover, since Redshift Spectrum cannot query data in S3 Glacier, the proposed solution approach would not work for semi-annual analysis without restoring the entire data to S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement an S3 lifecycle policy that moves data over 6 months old to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. While you can use S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval for archiving old data, Glacier Flexible Retrieval is more expensive than Deep Archive.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement Amazon QuickSight to analyze all the data stored in the S3 Data lake. Configure the QuickSight to go directly to the S3 data lake to enable real-time analytics of all the data \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Amazon QuickSight is a fast, easy-to-use tool for building visualizations and quick business insights from data. However, there might be better options for complex analytical queries, especially for large datasets like Data Lakes. There are more cost-effective ways to analyze the data than this one.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003c/strong\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/glacier/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/glacier/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-65":{"id":"p:4-65","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 65","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue crawler to run every day and discover new year, month, and day partitions in the S3 dataset\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet partition projection properties on the AWS Glue table so Athena calculates year, month, and day partitions during query planning\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to create year, month, and day partitions for the AWS Glue table whenever new files arrive\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun an Athena MSCK REPAIR TABLE command every day to discover new S3 prefixes and add the partitions to the AWS Glue table\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Crawler mỗi ngày có thể cập nhật partition, nhưng vẫn cần lượt chạy và cập nhật metadata định kỳ. Với cấu trúc ngày tháng dự đoán được, partition projection giảm công này.","Partition projection khai báo quy tắc để Athena tính các partition theo năm, tháng và ngày khi lập kế hoạch truy vấn. Không cần đăng ký từng partition mới hằng ngày; cần cấu hình phạm vi và mẫu đường dẫn S3 đúng.","DataBrew là công cụ chuẩn bị dữ liệu, không phải cơ chế suy ra partition lúc Athena truy vấn. Chạy thêm bước mỗi khi có file tăng công vận hành.","MSCK REPAIR TABLE dò các prefix kiểu Hive và thêm partition còn thiếu, nhưng phải chạy lại khi có dữ liệu mới. Partition projection loại bỏ thao tác định kỳ này cho cấu trúc có thể dự đoán."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company stores shipment tracking data in an Amazon S3 bucket and uses Amazon Athena to analyze the data. The data is organized by year, month, and day prefixes, and new data arrives every day.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer needs to configure the AWS Glue table so Athena can query daily partitions. The solution must support predictable date-based partitions with the least ongoing maintenance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:8:7":{"id":"e:8:7","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 7","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eUtilize the \u003ccode\u003eJSON SerDe\u003c/code\u003e library in Athena to parse the JSON data and create a table in Athena. Then, use SQL queries with \u003ccode\u003eCASE\u003c/code\u003e statements to pivot the data.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eLoad the JSON data from S3 into Amazon Redshift, then use the \u003ccode\u003ePIVOT\u003c/code\u003e function in Redshift to pivot the data.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eConvert the JSON data into CSV format using AWS Glue, then create a table in Athena to query the data. Use SQL queries with \u003ccode\u003eGROUP BY\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003eCASE\u003c/code\u003e statements to pivot the data.\u003c/span\u003e","Use AWS Lambda to parse the JSON data and store it in DynamoDB. Then, use Athena to query the data in DynamoDB."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA large e-commerce company has a data lake stored in Amazon S3, which contains a vast amount of customer transaction data in JSON format. The company wants to analyze this data to gain insights into customer behavior and improve its marketing strategies.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena is chosen for querying the data due to its serverless nature. However, the data engineering team is having difficulties dealing with JSON data, especially when they need to pivot it for analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow should a solution be designed to enable efficient querying of the JSON data in the S3 data lake using Amazon Athena, considering the need to pivot the data?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena is a serverless interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. It’s designed to handle large-scale datasets and can execute queries in parallel, making it a powerful tool for data analysis. In the context of the question, the company is dealing with JSON data in an S3 data lake and wants to pivot this data for analysis. Athena uses libraries called SerDes (Serializer/Deserializer) to interpret the input data format. For JSON data, Athena uses the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eorg.openx.data.jsonserde.JsonSerDe\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;library, also known as\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eJSON SerDe\u003c/code\u003e. This library allows Athena to interpret the JSON data, parse it into a structured format that can be queried using SQL.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/cda4017b02db9348cb0c201a8ac0ba477a093f8fed1e5c960cf8eb6abd6bc933.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOnce the JSON data is parsed using the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eJSON SerDe\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;library, a table can be created in Athena. This table represents the structured format of the JSON data and can be queried directly using SQL. Pivoting data is a common operation in data analysis where you transform the data from a long format to a wide format. In SQL, this can be achieved using\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eCASE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;statements. A\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eCASE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;statement lets you create new columns based on conditions in the data. This way, you can pivot the data based on your needs. This approach leverages the serverless nature of Athena, which means you don’t have to worry about managing infrastructure or scaling resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eJSON SerDe\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;library in Amazon Athena to parse the JSON data and create a table in Athena. Then, use SQL queries with\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eCASE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;statements to pivot the data.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLoad the JSON data from S3 into Amazon Redshift, then use the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ePIVOT\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;function in Redshift to pivot the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because while it’s possible to load data from S3 into Redshift and use the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ePIVOT\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;function, this would involve additional data transfer and storage costs. Moreover, it doesn’t utilize the serverless nature of Athena which can directly query data in S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eConvert the JSON data into CSV format using AWS Glue, then create a table in Athena to query the data. Use SQL queries with\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eGROUP BY\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;and\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eCASE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;statements to pivot the data\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because it involves an additional step of converting JSON data into CSV format using AWS Glue. This might not be necessary as Athena can directly query JSON data using the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eJSON SerDe\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;library.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Lambda to parse the JSON data and store it in DynamoDB. Then, use Athena to query the data in DynamoDB\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect. While this could work, loading the data in DynamoDB adds an unnecessary step in the workflow. Athena can directly query the data stored in Amazon S3 without any data movement involved.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/serde-about.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/serde-about.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/openx-json-serde.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/openx-json-serde.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:26":{"id":"e:5:26","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 26","correct":[2],"answers":["Store the JSON records in Amazon S3. Configure an S3 Event notification to invoke an AWS Glue job that uses Glue Parquet writer for converting data into Apache ORC. Load the result into an Amazon RDS PostgreSQL database. Set up a PostgreSQL Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) Driver to connect to the BI tool.","Store the JSON records in Amazon S3. Trigger an AWS Lambda function through S3 Put events to transform the data into Apache ORC. Run queries directly on S3 with Amazon Athena. Set up  Athena Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) connector to connect to the BI tool.","Use Amazon Data Firehose to stream and transform the JSON records to Apache Parquet. Deliver the results in Amazon S3. Define the schema of transformed data in an AWS Glue Data Catalog database. Run queries directly on S3 with Amazon Athena. Set up Athena Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) connector to connect to the BI tool.","Utilize Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (Amazon MSK) to ingest the streaming data. Configure an MSK consumer to process and transform the data into Apache Parquet format, subsequently storing it in Amazon Redshift. Set up a Redshift Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) Driver to connect to the BI tool."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA data engineer is designing a serverless data pipeline to process streaming JSON data and deliver it to a highly available, persistent storage. The data is being ingested into an Amazon Kinesis Data Stream. Before storage, the JSON data must be converted into a columnar format with a buffering interval of 60 seconds. The solution must enable data analysts to execute SQL queries directly on the transformed data and use existing Business Intelligence (BI) tools to visualize the results.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e Which of the following solutions meets the requirements?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Data Firehose\u003c/strong\u003e is a fully managed service that delivers real-time streaming data to destinations such as Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, Amazon OpenSearch Service, and others. It can automatically ingest and load streaming data into these data stores without any need for ETL processes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe \u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Data Catalog\u003c/strong\u003e is a centralized repository for structural and operational metadata for all of your data assets. You can save a dataset’s table definition and physical location, add business-relevant features, and track how the data has changed over time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe \u003cstrong\u003eAthena JDBC Connector\u003c/strong\u003e allows connecting BI tools, reporting tools or custom applications to Amazon Athena using standard JDBC. This enables running SQL queries and fetching results from Athena tables using common SQL tools without needing to use AWS CLI or SDKs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e8a1fdc0f0feb315eb0bb18a6a80b7a49c32477725b5f5fac2139e302ae095c2.png\" alt=\"Amazon Data Firehose\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo fulfill the requirement, the data engineer should utilize Amazon Data Firehose to stream JSON records from the Kinesis data stream to destinations like Amazon S3. This process seamlessly integrates with AWS Glue for schema definition and the transformation of data into the Parquet format without the need for custom coding, subsequently delivering the transformed data to an S3 bucket. The schema is efficiently managed in the AWS Glue Data Catalog, serving as a repository for metadata. Amazon Athena enables SQL querying of the data stored in S3, scalable for exabyte-scale analysis. Additionally, the Athena JDBC connector facilitates connectivity with existing Business Intelligence (BI) tools for visualization. This serverless approach streamlines the transformation to Parquet, effectively meeting the requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose to stream and transform the JSON records to Apache Parquet. Deliver the results in Amazon S3. Define the schema of transformed data in an AWS Glue Data Catalog database. Run queries directly on S3 with Amazon Athena. Set up Athena Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) connector to connect to the BI tool.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eStore the JSON records in Amazon S3. Trigger an AWS Lambda function through S3 Put events to transform the data into Apache ORC. Run queries directly on S3 with Amazon Athena. Set up Athena Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) connector to connect to the BI tool\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. This approach does not primarily support real-time processing as it requires data to be stored before transformation, and the asynchronous invocation of Lambda functions based on S3 events does not guarantee a fixed buffering interval.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the JSON records in Amazon S3. Configure an S3 Event notification to invoke an AWS Glue job that uses Glue Parquet writer for converting data into Apache ORC. Load the result into an Amazon RDS PostgreSQL database. Set up a PostgreSQL Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) Driver to connect to the BI tool\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. While S3 event notifications can typically publish events to AWS Lambda, Amazon SNS, and Amazon SQS, it cannot\u0026nbsp;directly trigger AWS Glue jobs.\u0026nbsp;Moreover, loading data into RDS for querying with BI tools, though feasible, deviates from a serverless approach, potentially increasing operational complexity and cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (Amazon MSK) to ingest the streaming data. Configure an MSK consumer to process and transform the data into Apache Parquet format, subsequently storing it in Amazon Redshift. Set up a Redshift Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) Driver to connect to the BI tool\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect.\u0026nbsp;This architecture introduces complexities associated with managing Kafka and Redshift, which can increase operational overhead and cost. Moreover, this solution steps away from a purely serverless approach, as Redshift requires cluster management and scaling considerations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-with-jdbc.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-with-jdbc.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/record-format-conversion.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/record-format-conversion.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue and Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:5":{"id":"e:7:5","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 5","correct":[2],"answers":["Create individual IAM roles for each application and grant the necessary permissions to access the bucket.","Implement Amazon S3 Object Lock to manage permissions more securely.","Set up dedicated Amazon S3 Access Points for each application.","Utilize Amazon S3 Lifecycle policies to manage permissions based on the data lifecycle."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company stores terabytes of data in an Amazon S3 bucket, which is accessed by multiple applications. The company uses a bucket policy to manage permissions. As application requirements evolve, the engineering team has to update the bucket policy accordingly. This process causes access issues to some applications due to misconfigurations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe company needs a way to control access for each application without disrupting the others.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3 Access Points\u003c/strong\u003e are a feature of Amazon S3 that simplifies managing data access for shared datasets. With Access Points, you can create multiple access points for a single S3 bucket, with each access point having a unique hostname and access policy tailored for a specific use case or application.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEach access point can be configured with a custom access policy granting only the necessary permissions for its intended use.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAccess points allow separating access control for different applications rather than managing everything through a single bucket policy.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNetwork controls can be applied to access points to restrict access to only requests from a specific VPC.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAccess points can be used to grant cross-account access while still retaining control over the bucket and data through the bucket policy.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAliases are automatically generated for each access point, making them interchangeable with bucket names in AWS APIs and CLI.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eCommon S3 operations can be performed by replacing the bucket name with the access point ARN or alias.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3b50e5c029d235f8ed3366ab7298d16da4dc37a9411a62210f5f989ebcc88815.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe company can meet its requirement of controlling access for each application without disrupting the others by setting up dedicated Amazon S3 Access Points for different applications to streamline access management.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA custom policy attached to each Access Point will grant only the necessary permissions for that specific application.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAs application requirements change, only the policy of that application\u0027s Access Point needs updating, avoiding disruptions to other applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis prevents issues caused by other applications from being affected when one application\u0027s permissions need to be updated. Developers no longer need to change the overall bucket policy frequently as their application ecosystem evolves.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eSet up dedicated Amazon S3 Access Points for each application.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate individual IAM roles for each application and grant the necessary permissions to access the bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This approach makes managing and auditing permissions more complicated and time-consuming because you need to grant correct permissions for each app\u0027s IAM role. It also increases security risks, as one might give applications access to more AWS services than they need.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement Amazon S3 Object Lock to manage permissions more securely\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because S3 Object Lock is designed to prevent objects from being deleted or overwritten for a fixed amount of time or indefinitely, which is not directly related to simplifying permission management or preventing disruptions due to policy misconfigurations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon S3 Lifecycle policies to manage permissions based on the data lifecycle\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because S3 Lifecycle policies are used to manage objects\u0027 lifecycle, such as transitioning objects to less expensive storage classes or automatically deleting them after a certain period, and do not directly help with permission management or preventing configuration errors.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/access-points.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/access-points.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/access-points/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/access-points/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck this Amazon S3 Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:23":{"id":"e:7:23","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 23","correct":[0],"answers":["Create an IAM role with necessary S3 permissions and assign this role to the AWS Glue service.","Create an IAM user for AWS Glue and attach policies for S3 access.","Use Amazon S3 bucket policies to grant access to AWS Glue.","Use S3 Access Control Lists (ACLs) to manage permissions."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has established a data pipeline to handle large volumes of data. The pipeline consists of an AWS Glue job that pulls data from a source S3 bucket, transforms it, and sends the result to a target S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer must configure permissions for the Glue job to access these S3 buckets, which reside in the same AWS account.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options would meet the requirements with the LEAST amount of configuration effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy to prepare and load your data for analytics. To perform its operations, AWS Glue needs to interact with other AWS services on your behalf. This is achieved by using an IAM role that has the necessary permissions. When you create an IAM role for AWS Glue, you grant your IAM role permissions that AWS Glue can assume when calling other services on your behalf. This includes access to Amazon S3 for any sources, targets, scripts, and temporary directories that you use with AWS Glue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBy creating a new IAM service role specifically for AWS Glue and attaching the necessary policies to this role, you can grant the necessary permissions to AWS Glue without affecting other services or users. This approach also aligns with the principle of least privilege, which is a best practice in IAM. This principle encourages you to grant only the permissions required to perform a task.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/863c12bae4209fa7f1592de2df2a1c0f26b29e533b57bee8c2037b5f7c3e23df.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach allows you to separate the permissions for the source and destination S3 buckets by attaching different policies to the IAM role. For example, you could attach a policy that grants read access to the source bucket and a separate policy that grants write access to the destination bucket. This way, you can meet the requirement of separating the permissions for the source and destination S3 buckets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an IAM role with necessary S3 permissions and assign this role to the AWS Glue service\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an IAM user for AWS Glue and attach policies for S3 access\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Glue jobs are designed to assume an IAM role. It\u0027s not possible to assign IAM user\u0027s credentials to AWS Glue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon S3 bucket policies to grant access to AWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;While this is a viable approach, using S3 bucket policies to grant access to the AWS Glue service requires separate bucket policies to be configured for both the source and target S3 buckets. With an IAM role, you can centralize the permission management, simplifying the configuration process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse S3 Access Control Lists (ACLs) to manage permissions\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because ACLs are less flexible and harder to manage than IAM roles. ACLs provide a broad level of access control, but they don’t provide the same level of granularity as IAM roles. This can make it difficult to adhere to the principle of least privilege, as you might end up granting more permissions than necessary. Additionally, managing ACLs can be complex and error-prone, especially if you have many S3 buckets or objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/create-an-iam-role.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/create-an-iam-role.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/set-up-iam.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/set-up-iam.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this\u0026nbsp;AWS Identity and Access Management Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-identity-and-access-management-iam/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-identity-and-access-management-iam/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:12":{"id":"e:8:12","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 12","correct":[2],"answers":["AWS Step Functions","Amazon Data Firehose","AWS Glue Workflows","Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA)"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn e-commerce company has a vast amount of data stored in Amazon S3 buckets. The data is in various formats and is updated frequently. The company needs to orchestrate an ETL pipeline to transform and load this data into Amazon Redshift for further analysis. The solution should be able to handle changes in the data schema and should be easily manageable.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich AWS service is best suited for these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eExtract, transform, and load (ETL) orchestration is a common mechanism for building big data pipelines. Orchestration for parallel ETL processing requires the use of multiple tools to perform a variety of operations. To simplify the orchestration, you can use\u0026nbsp;AWS Glue\u0026nbsp;workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/83e328a7398015d305e09a9016fce5824abfad33624b45e80d39147bfdbb3bff.png\"\u003eAWS Glue workflows\u0026nbsp;provide a visual and programmatic tool to author data pipelines by combining AWS Glue crawlers for schema discovery and AWS Glue Spark and Python shell jobs to transform the data. A workflow consists of one or more task nodes arranged as a graph. Relationships can be defined, and parameters passed between task nodes to enable you to build pipelines of varying complexity. You can trigger workflows on a schedule or on-demand. You can track the progress of each node independently or the entire workflow, making it easier to troubleshoot your pipelines.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Workflows.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Step Functions\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Step Functions is a viable option as it can use the AWS SDK to call Glue APIs. However, compared to a Glue Workflow, which is specifically designed for orchestrating Glue Crawlers and Glue Jobs, Step Functions is less straightforward. For example, when you run a crawler in Step Functions, you\u0027re responsible for monitoring its status and implementing logic to poll whether the Crawler has completed before proceeding to the next step. In contrast, Glue Workflows handle monitoring of Jobs and Crawlers automatically.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Data Firehose\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it is only designed for streaming data into destinations such as Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, and Amazon OpenSearch Service. It is not an ETL tool and does not handle data transformations or schema changes efficiently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because, while it can be used to manage complex ETL workflows, it primarily serves as a platform to programmatically author, schedule, and monitor workflows. It does not have the same level of integration with AWS Glue or the ability to handle changes in the data schema as effectively as AWS Glue workflows. It is more suited for custom workflows that require a high level of customization and flexibility.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-an-etl-pipeline-using-aws-glue-workflows-triggers-and-crawlers-with-custom-classifiers/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-an-etl-pipeline-using-aws-glue-workflows-triggers-and-crawlers-with-custom-classifiers/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:33":{"id":"e:5:33","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 33","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eParallel\u003c/code\u003e state\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003ePass\u003c/code\u003e state\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eTask\u003c/code\u003e state\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eMap\u003c/code\u003e state\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA data engineering team is developing an AWS Step Functions workflow to process daily log files stored in Amazon S3. Each log file must be parsed, validated, and transformed before being loaded into Amazon Redshift for reporting.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eThe workflow must handle a large collection of log files in parallel, applying the same transformation steps to each file without manually creating separate execution branches.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich Step Functions state will enable the team to design this type of workflow?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eWith AWS Step Functions, you can create workflows, also called\u0026nbsp;State machines, to build distributed applications, automate processes, orchestrate microservices, and create data and machine learning pipelines.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eStep Functions is based on\u0026nbsp;\u003cem\u003estate machines\u003c/em\u003e\u0026nbsp;and\u0026nbsp;\u003cem\u003etasks\u003c/em\u003e. In Step Functions, state machines are called\u0026nbsp;\u003cem\u003eworkflows\u003c/em\u003e, which are a series of event-driven steps. Each step in a workflow is called a\u0026nbsp;\u003cem\u003estate\u003c/em\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/aa7d28e0fb518d2d4b3bea2353a40c6b6350270a08a7e29d1d1307a55f60edfb.png\" alt=\"AWS Step Functions - Map state\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMap\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;state to run a set of workflow steps for each item in a dataset. The\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMap\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;state’s iterations run in parallel, which makes it possible to process a dataset quickly.\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMap\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;states can use a variety of input types, including a JSON array, a list of Amazon S3 objects, or a CSV file.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eStep Functions provides the following processing modes for the \u003ccode\u003eMap\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;state, depending on how you want to process the items in a dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Inline\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;– Limited-concurrency mode. In this mode, each iteration of the \u003ccode\u003eMap\u0026nbsp;\u003c/code\u003estate runs in the context of the workflow that contains the \u003ccode\u003eMap\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;state. Step Functions adds the execution history of these iterations to the parent workflow’s execution history. By default, \u003ccode\u003eMap\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;states run in Inline mode. In this mode, the \u003ccode\u003eMap\u003c/code\u003estate accepts only a JSON array as input. Also, this mode supports up to 40 concurrent iterations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Distributed\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;– High-concurrency mode. In this mode, the \u003ccode\u003eMap\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;state runs each iteration as a child workflow execution, which enables high concurrency of up to 10,000 parallel child workflow executions. Each child workflow execution has its own, separate execution history from that of the parent workflow. In this mode, the \u003ccode\u003eMap\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;state can accept either a JSON array or an Amazon S3 data source, such as a CSV file, as its input.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eMap\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;state.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e It allows you to iterate over a collection of items, like S3 log files, and apply the same set of steps (parse, validate, transform) to each item. It’s designed for scalable parallel or sequential processing without manually defining separate branches for each file. This makes it ideal for workflows that need to handle dynamic arrays of input data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eParallel\u0026nbsp;\u003c/code\u003estate\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it runs multiple branches of execution in parallel, but each branch must be explicitly defined. This is useful when you have a fixed number of distinct tasks, not when you need to dynamically process a large or variable number of items like log files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eTask\u003c/code\u003e state\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it only represents a single unit of work, such as invoking a Lambda function or another AWS service. While it can perform one step in the workflow, it doesn’t support iteration over multiple items unless combined with other states like \u003ccode\u003eMap\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003ePass\u003c/code\u003e state\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it simply passes its input to output without performing any action. It’s typically used for testing, placeholder logic, or reshaping data, not for executing transformations or validations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/state-map.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/state-map.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Step Functions Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:11":{"id":"e:4:11","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 11","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up an S3 Access Point to read data from the S3 bucket. Leverage the S3 Access Points policy to dynamically redact PII based on the needs of each application that accesses the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an S3 interface endpoint to read data from the S3 bucket. Leverage the S3 bucket policy to control access for each application that accesses the data only via the interface endpoint\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an S3 Object Lambda endpoint. Use the S3 Object Lambda endpoint to read data from the S3 bucket. Implement redaction logic within an S3 Object Lambda function to dynamically redact PII based on the needs of each application that accesses the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an S3 gateway endpoint to read data from the S3 bucket. Leverage the S3 bucket policy to control access for each application that accesses the data only via the gateway endpoint\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company utilizes an Amazon S3 bucket to store datasets accessed by various applications, including a financial services application that generates datasets containing personally identifiable information (PII). There is also an internal application that does not need access to this PII. To adhere to regulations, the company must avoid unnecessary sharing of PII. A data engineer is tasked with finding a solution that can dynamically redact PII depending on the specific needs of each application accessing the dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution can achieve this with minimal operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an S3 Object Lambda endpoint. Use the S3 Object Lambda endpoint to read data from the S3 bucket. Implement redaction logic within an S3 Object Lambda function to dynamically redact PII based on the needs of each application that accesses the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 Object Lambda Access Points for personally identifiable information (PII) to configure how documents are retrieved from your Amazon S3 bucket. You can control access to documents that contain PII and redact PII from documents. Amazon S3 Object Lambda Access Points use AWS Lambda functions to automatically transform the output of a standard Amazon S3 GET request. When you create an Amazon S3 Object Lambda Access Point for PII, documents are processed using Amazon Comprehend Lambda functions to control access of documents that contain PII and redact PII from documents.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4b46658006220373a3ac558c7a0b533ad5c41a7debb7f6bf2bcfd8a26149824e.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4b46658006220373a3ac558c7a0b533ad5c41a7debb7f6bf2bcfd8a26149824e.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/comprehend/latest/dg/using-access-points.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/comprehend/latest/dg/using-access-points.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an S3 Access Point to read data from the S3 bucket. Leverage the S3 Access Points policy to dynamically redact PII based on the needs of each application that accesses the data\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon S3 Access Points simplify data access for any AWS service or customer application that stores data in S3. With S3 Access Points, customers can create unique access control policies for each access point to easily control access to shared datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eEach S3 Access Point is configured with an access policy specific to a use case or application. For example, you can create an access point for your S3 bucket that grants access to groups of users or applications for your data lake. An Access Point can support a single user or application, or groups of users or applications within and across accounts, allowing separate management of each access point.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou cannot use an S3 Access Points policy to dynamically redact PII based on the needs of each application that accesses the data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/10db9efc01a5420686ababa6e77d44aaac2fb78792a2735bae794c1e371957d4.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/10db9efc01a5420686ababa6e77d44aaac2fb78792a2735bae794c1e371957d4.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/access-points/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/access-points/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/a889039365010b14ecc51a2a0e996e4db1ecbc4f563f6c3879839b52188c0db5.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/a889039365010b14ecc51a2a0e996e4db1ecbc4f563f6c3879839b52188c0db5.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/access-points/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/access-points/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an S3 interface endpoint to read data from the S3 bucket. Leverage the S3 bucket policy to dynamically redact PII for each application by allowing access only via the interface endpoint\u003c/strong\u003e - With AWS PrivateLink for Amazon S3, you can provision interface VPC endpoints (interface endpoints) in your virtual private cloud (VPC). These endpoints are directly accessible from applications that are on premises over VPN and AWS Direct Connect, or in a different AWS Region over VPC peering. You cannot use an S3 bucket policy to dynamically redact PII by allowing access only via the interface endpoint. This option acts as a distractor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an S3 gateway endpoint to read data from the S3 bucket. Leverage the S3 bucket policy to dynamically redact PII for each application by allowing access only via the gateway endpoint\u003c/strong\u003e - You can access Amazon S3 from your VPC using gateway VPC endpoints. After you create the gateway endpoint, you can add it as a target in your route table for traffic destined from your VPC to Amazon S3. With a gateway endpoint, you can access Amazon S3 from your VPC, without requiring an internet gateway or NAT device for your VPC, and with no additional cost. However, gateway endpoints do not allow access from on-premises networks, from peered VPCs in other AWS Regions, or through a transit gateway. You cannot use an S3 bucket policy to dynamically redact PII by allowing access only via the gateway endpoint. This option acts as a distractor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/comprehend/latest/dg/using-access-points.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/comprehend/latest/dg/using-access-points.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/access-points/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/access-points/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/privatelink-interface-endpoints.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/privatelink-interface-endpoints.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/vpc-endpoints-s3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/vpc-endpoints-s3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:13":{"id":"e:5:13","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 13","correct":[2],"answers":["Utilize the Advanced Parameters from the AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store to support credential rotation every 30 days.","Utilize the AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) database authentication to safely authenticate the EC2 instances to the Amazon RDS for Microsoft SQL Server database.","Utilize AWS Secrets Manager to store and automatically rotate credentials every 30 days.","Utilize the AWS IAM Identity Center to authenticate to the Amazon RDS for Microsoft SQL Server database."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is hosting its application in Amazon EC2 instances. These EC2 instances connect to an Amazon RDS for Microsoft SQL Server database. The nature of the application is that it stores and processes sensitive and confidential data in its database. They aim to minimize the possibility of exposing their database credentials.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Secrets Manager\u003c/strong\u003e offers direct integration with Amazon RDS. Not only does it store database credentials automatically, but it can also rotate these credentials automatically. This service provides a secure and centralized way to handle sensitive information like database credentials.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d8647f0c8611f6f483542a491f6a6baf252a0681058c6c963152515c4d1ec51d.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Secrets Manager\u003c/strong\u003e provides a centralized service to manage secrets like database credentials securely, allowing applications on EC2 instances to retrieve them dynamically. Its built-in support for automatic rotation of secrets ensures that database credentials are regularly updated without manual intervention, significantly reducing the potential for unauthorized access. This approach not only enhances security by automating the credential lifecycle management but also aligns with best practices for securing access to databases, like Amazon RDS for Microsoft SQL Server, by minimizing the static configuration of sensitive information on the instances and instead securely retrieving credentials as needed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize AWS Secrets Manager to store and automatically rotate credentials every 30 days.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize the AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) database authentication to safely authenticate the EC2 instances to the Amazon RDS for Microsoft SQL Server database\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Although this option seamlessly authenticates compute resources to Amazon RDS, the AWS IAM database authentication does not support Amazon RDS for Microsoft SQL Server. IAM authentication only works with MariaDB, MySQL, and PostgreSQL.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize the Advanced Parameters from the AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store to support credential rotation every 30 days\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect primarily because the AWS Systems Manager (AWS SSM) Parameter Store does not support credential rotation. Although values can be encrypted through AWS KMS, once the credentials are compromised, AWS SSM Parameter Store cannot automatically rotate these credentials.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUtilize the AWS IAM Identity Center to authenticate to the Amazon RDS for Microsoft SQL Server database\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect primarily because AWS IAM Identity Center is not used to manage database credentials or directly authenticate databases. This service is used for user identity management and access control across AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotating-secrets.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotating-secrets.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_turn-on-for-db.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_turn-on-for-db.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Secrets Manager Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-secrets-manager/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-secrets-manager/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:10":{"id":"e:5:10","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 10","correct":[2,3],"answers":["Implement a data lake architecture using AWS Lake Formation to automate data cataloging and optimize query execution.","Store the .csv files in S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval. Add randomized string as a prefix for S3 objects.","Ensure the AWS region where the S3 bucket is located is the same as the region where the Athena queries are run.","Reformat the .csv data into a more query-efficient format like Apache Parquet. Use the predicate pushdown technique for efficient data retrieval.","Apply a lifecycle policy to the S3 bucket to archive older .csv files to S3 Glacier for cost savings."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company analyzes transportation and delivery data to optimize route planning and improve delivery times. For its testing phase, the company uses Amazon S3 to store logistics metrics in .csv format and leverages Amazon Athena for data querying. The datasets are organized by date within the S3 bucket. As data volumes grow, the company seeks strategies to optimize query performance and storage efficiency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich two actions should be taken to meet these requirements most effectively? (Select TWO)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Amazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e provides an interactive query service, allowing users to examine information in Amazon S3 using conventional SQL. With Athena, you can point to your data stored in S3 and begin using standard SQL to run ad-hoc queries and get results. Athena is serverless, so you have no infrastructure to set up or manage.\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0b462a22a7964fdc26aae6da70da97402ec8937daf7e83bca6d2e6677ea3ffda.png\" alt=\"Amazon Athena - Query editor\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe two actions that should be taken to meet the requirements most effectively are: Converting CSV data into a columnar format such as Apache Parquet significantly improves performance. This conversion enables Athena to process queries more efficiently by reading only the necessary columns, thereby reducing the amount of data scanned and lowering costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSecondly, ensuring that the AWS region for the S3 data storage matches the region where Athena queries are executed minimizes network latency, leading to faster query performance. These approaches underscore the importance of strategic data organization and regional considerations in maximizing the cost-effectiveness and scalability of data analytics using AWS Athena and S3, enabling a more streamlined and effective management of large data sets and complex analytics operations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Ensure the AWS region where the S3 bucket is located is the same as the region where the Athena queries are run.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Reformat the .csv data into a more query-efficient format like Apache Parquet.\u0026nbsp;Use the predicate pushdown technique for efficient data retrieval.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement a data lake architecture using AWS Lake Formation to automate data cataloging and optimize query execution\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it only simplifies the setup and management of a secure data lake, including data cataloging and optimization of query execution; it might introduce additional complexity and overhead for an application focused on enhancing operational efficiency. Therefore, this solution does not address the specific need for query performance optimization and storage efficiency in the scenario described.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eStore the .csv files in S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval. Add randomized string as a prefix for S3 objects\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval is primarily designed for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. While it can help reduce storage costs, it incurs higher retrieval costs compared to the Standard S3 storage class. Adding a custom prefix for S3 objects can provide some level of performance improvement but not as effective as reformatting the data into a more query-efficient format like Apache Parquet.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eApply a lifecycle policy to the S3 bucket to archive older .csv files to S3 Glacier for cost savings\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Using S3 Glacier means that you would have to restore the data before it can be queried, which adds a significant delay and complexity to the process. This approach is not conducive to optimizing query performance; it’s primarily a cost-saving measure for data that is rarely accessed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/analyzing-data-in-s3-using-amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/analyzing-data-in-s3-using-amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-26":{"id":"p:1-26","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 26","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with the default retention period. Implement idempotent consumers that store checkpoints in Amazon DynamoDB. Use one shared processing path that applies conditional logic for replay events and live events\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with extended retention and create a second consumer application that starts from a timestamp inside the retention window. Use the same output tables as the live consumer and leverage the stream sequence number to prevent duplicate analytical records\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with extended retention. Implement idempotent consumers that use Amazon DynamoDB checkpointing. Use separate processing paths for replay events and live events\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with extended retention. Implement idempotent consumers that use Amazon RDS checkpointing. Use one shared processing path for replay events and live events\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Retention mặc định của Kinesis không đủ để phát lại dữ liệu 5 ngày. Dùng chung đường xử lý còn có nguy cơ để lượt phát lại ảnh hưởng dữ liệu trực tiếp.","Retention mở rộng và consumer riêng là đúng hướng, nhưng sequence number không tự đảm bảo không trùng bản ghi nghiệp vụ ở đầu ra. Cần xử lý idempotent và cô lập lượt phát lại.","Tăng retention đủ cho 5 ngày, dùng consumer idempotent và checkpoint DynamoDB. Tách đường xử lý lịch sử và trực tiếp giúp phát lại mà không gây trùng hoặc cản luồng đang chạy.","Retention mở rộng giải quyết thời gian lưu, nhưng dùng chung đường xử lý vẫn khiến phát lại có thể ảnh hưởng luồng trực tiếp. RDS checkpoint cũng tăng thành phần quản lý so với phương án được chọn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA gaming analytics company streams player interaction events from a mobile application to AWS. The company wants to prevent duplicate records in downstream engagement reports. The data engineering team must be able to replay up to 5 days of historical events after a processing defect is discovered. The replay process must not disrupt the pipeline that continues to process live events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-24":{"id":"p:1-24","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 24","correct":[1,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lifecycle Policy to transition objects to S3 One Zone IA using a prefix after 45 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lifecycle Policy to transition objects to S3 Standard IA using a prefix after 45 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lifecycle Policy to transition all objects to Glacier Deep Archive after 180 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lifecycle Policy to transition all objects to S3 Standard IA after 45 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lifecycle Policy to transition objects to Glacier Deep Archive using a prefix after 180 days\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["One Zone-IA chỉ lưu trong một AZ. Đề không nói ảnh gốc có thể tạo lại, nên Standard-IA đa AZ phù hợp hơn cho dữ liệu cần bảo vệ.","Sau 45 ngày chỉ ảnh gốc ít được đọc. Dùng bộ lọc prefix images/ để chuyển riêng nhóm này sang Standard-IA, giữ thumbnails ở lớp phù hợp với truy cập thường xuyên.","Sau 180 ngày cả ảnh và thumbnail đều cần lưu trữ lâu dài. Một quy tắc áp dụng toàn bộ đối tượng chuyển sang Deep Archive đáp ứng đúng phạm vi.","Chuyển cả bucket sau 45 ngày kéo theo thumbnails vẫn được đọc thường xuyên. Cần lọc prefix để chỉ chuyển ảnh gốc.","Bộ đề muốn lưu trữ cả hai nhóm sau 180 ngày, nên quy tắc toàn bucket là trực tiếp nhất. Lọc một prefix cụ thể có thể bỏ sót nhóm còn lại."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has an S3 bucket that contains files in two different folders - \u003ccode\u003es3://my-bucket/images\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003es3://my-bucket/thumbnails\u003c/code\u003e. When an image is first uploaded and new, it is viewed several times. Post a detailed analysis, the data analytics team has noticed that after 45 days those image files are rarely requested, but the thumbnails still are. After 180 days, the company would like to archive the image files and the thumbnails. Overall, the company would like the solution to remain highly available to prevent disasters from happening against the entire AZ.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents the best-fit solutions for an efficient cost strategy for the given S3 bucket? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:21":{"id":"e:2:21","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 21","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the default access tier\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eOpt for Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration for milliseconds latencies\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage S3 Storage Lens advanced metrics to determine when to move objects to more cost-optimized storage classes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the Archive Instant Access tier\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company stores petabytes of data in hundreds of Amazon S3 buckets in the S3 Standard storage class. The data supports several analytics, visualization, and reporting-specific workflows. The data access patterns are unpredictable and variable. Some data is not accessed for months but needs to be available in milliseconds when accessed. The company wants to optimize the costs of storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution can address these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the default access tier\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class automatically stores objects in three access tiers. One tier is optimized for frequent access, one lower-cost tier is optimized for infrequent access, and another very low-cost tier is optimized for rarely accessed data. For a low monthly object monitoring and automation charge, S3 Intelligent-Tiering monitors access patterns and automatically moves objects to the Infrequent Access tier when they haven\u0027t been accessed for 30 consecutive days. After 90 days of no access, the objects are moved to the Archive Instant Access tier without performance impact or operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eS3 Intelligent-Tiering is the recommended storage class for data with unknown, changing, or unpredictable access patterns, independent of object size or retention period, such as data lakes, data analytics, and new applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eS3 Intelligent-Tiering access tiers:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7bc764f6ecf21602c8154258ff2a6002bf53cb10c25c965697b5e6bb86f38e30.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7bc764f6ecf21602c8154258ff2a6002bf53cb10c25c965697b5e6bb86f38e30.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/intelligent-tiering-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/intelligent-tiering-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage S3 Storage Lens advanced metrics to determine when to move objects to more cost-optimized storage classes\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon S3 Storage Lens provides organization-wide visibility into object storage usage and activity trends, as well as actionable recommendations to optimize costs and apply data protection best practices. Storage Lens offers an interactive dashboard containing a single view of your object storage usage and activity across tens or hundreds of accounts in your organization, with drill-downs to generate insights at multiple aggregation levels.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eS3 Storage Lens contains more than 60 metrics, grouped into free metrics and advanced metrics (available for an additional cost). Within free metrics, you receive metrics to analyze usage (based on a daily snapshot of your objects), which are organized into the categories of cost optimization, data protection, access management, performance, and events. Within advanced metrics, you receive metrics related to the activity (such as request counts), deeper cost optimization (such as S3 Lifecycle rule counts), additional data protection (such as S3 Replication rule counts), and detailed status codes (such as 403 authorization errors).\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eOpt for Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration for milliseconds latencies\u003c/em\u003e* - Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration can speed up content transfers to and from Amazon S3 by as much as 50-500% for long-distance transfer of larger objects. Customers who have either web or mobile applications with widespread users or applications hosted far away from their S3 bucket can experience long and variable upload and download speeds over the Internet. This option acts as a distractor since S3 Transfer Acceleration will not reduce the cost of storage on Amazon S3, which is the main requirement for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the Archive Instant Access tier\u003c/strong\u003e - If an object is not accessed for 90 consecutive days, the object moves to the Archive Instant Access tier of Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering. The Archive Instant Access tier provides low latency and high-throughput performance. This is an automatic tier and one cannot opt for this tier manually.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/intelligent-tiering-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/intelligent-tiering-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:52":{"id":"e:5:52","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 52","correct":[2],"answers":["Store the custom library in an Amazon EFS volume and configure the Lambda functions to mount the EFS volume to access the custom Python library.","Package the Lambda functions together with the custom library and upload them to an Amazon S3 bucket. Update each Lambda function’s code using the S3 bucket as the source location.","Use AWS Lambda Layers to package the custom library and reference this layer in the Lambda functions.","Assign a common alias to all Lambda functions. Invoke each Lambda function by specifying the alias associated with it."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team uses Lambda functions to execute various data processing tasks. Recently, the team has developed a custom Python library that abstracts common data transformations tailored to their workload.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe team wants to implement this library into their existing Lambda functions to enhance their data processing capabilities.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution would meet the requirements in the LEAST amount of effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eWhen building serverless applications, it is quite common to have code shared across Lambda functions. This code can be your custom code used by more than one function or a standard library you add to simplify the implementation of your business logic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eInstead of having to package and deploy this shared code together with all the functions using it, you can put common components in a ZIP file and upload it as a Lambda Layer. Your function code doesn’t need to be changed and can reference the libraries in the layer as it would normally do.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eLayers can be versioned to manage updates, each version is immutable. When a\u0026nbsp;version is deleted or permissions to use it are revoked, functions that used it previously will continue to work, but you won’t be able to create new ones.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the configuration of a function, you can reference up to five layers, one of which can optionally be a runtime. When the function is invoked, layers are installed in\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003e/opt\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;in the order you provided.\u0026nbsp;Order is important because layers are all extracted under the same path, so each layer can potentially overwrite the previous one. This approach can be used to customize the environment. For example, the first layer can be a runtime, and the second layer can add specific versions of the libraries you need.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5b0767c5a85d6e5febcb4613e2427f3a1383d2db761d65275eec8ffc77247a87.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eLambda Layers allow you to package common code (such as custom Python scripts) separately from your Lambda functions.\u0026nbsp;You can share the same layer across multiple functions, reducing redundancy.\u0026nbsp;\u0026nbsp;Updates to the Python scripts can be done in one place (the layer) and automatically propagate to all functions using it.\u0026nbsp;Custom logic is centralized in the layer, promoting consistency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Lambda Layers to package the custom library and reference this layer in the Lambda functions.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePackage the Lambda functions together with the custom library and upload them to an Amazon S3 bucket. Update each Lambda function’s code using the S3 bucket as the source location\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect. While technically possible, this approach requires repackaging a Lambda function with the custom library upon any update to the library. This process is time-consuming and prone to errors, especially as the number of Lambda functions grows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the custom library in an Amazon EFS volume and configure the Lambda functions to mount the EFS volume to access the custom Python library\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect. This method involves configuring network file system access permissions and can lead to increased latency, as Lambda functions must access the library over the network. It’s an over-engineered solution for the task of library sharing, which can be more efficiently accomplished with Lambda layers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAssign a common alias to all Lambda functions. Invoke each Lambda function by specifying the alias associated with it\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Assigning aliases to Lambda functions is useful for versioning and routing traffic, but it doesn’t directly address code reusability or centralization of custom scripts. Aliases are primarily used to manage different versions of a function.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReference:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/chapter-layers.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/chapter-layers.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/new-for-aws-lambda-use-any-programming-language-and-share-common-components/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/new-for-aws-lambda-use-any-programming-language-and-share-common-components/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Lambda Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:4":{"id":"e:1:4","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 4","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLeverage Kinesis Data Analytics to detect anomalies on a data stream from Kinesis Streams by running SQL queries which compute an anomaly score for all transactions and then store all fraudulent transactions in Amazon S3. Use Amazon QuickSight to visualize the results from Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eTransform the data from JSON format to Apache Parquet format using an AWS Glue job. Configure AWS Glue crawlers to discover the schema and build the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Leverage Amazon Athena to create a table with a subset of columns. Set up Amazon QuickSight for visual analysis of the data and identify fraudulent transactions using QuickSight\u0027s built-in machine learning-powered anomaly detection\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Kinesis Data Firehose to detect anomalies on a data stream from Kinesis Streams via a Lambda function which computes an anomaly score for all transactions and stores all fraudulent transactions in Amazon RDS. Use Amazon QuickSight to visualize the results from RDS\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eTransform the data from JSON format to Apache Parquet format using an AWS Glue job. Configure AWS Glue crawlers to discover the schema and build the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Leverage Amazon SageMaker to build an anomaly detection model that can detect fraudulent transactions by ingesting data directly from Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company is looking for a solution that detects anomalies in order to identify fraudulent transactions. The company utilizes Amazon Kinesis to transfer JSON-formatted transaction records from its on-premises database to Amazon S3. The existing dataset comprises 100-column-wide records for each transaction. To identify fraudulent transactions, the solution needs to analyze just ten of these columns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate, which of the following would you suggest as the lowest-cost solution that needs the least development work and offers out-of-the-box anomaly detection functionality?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTransform the data from JSON format to Apache Parquet format using an AWS Glue job. Configure AWS Glue crawlers to discover the schema and build the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Leverage Amazon Athena to create a table with a subset of columns. Set up Amazon QuickSight for visual analysis of the data and identify fraudulent transactions using QuickSight\u0027s built-in machine learning-powered anomaly detection\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the given use case, you can use an AWS Glue job to extract, transform, and load (ETL) data from the data source (in JSON format) to the data target (in Parquet format). You can then use an AWS Glue crawler, which is a program that connects to a data store (source or target) such as Amazon S3, progresses through a prioritized list of classifiers to determine the schema for your data, and then creates metadata tables in the AWS Glue Data Catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b399e10b8388045abe6adefd91f07f2680436755049d1cde960510cadcbe3858.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b399e10b8388045abe6adefd91f07f2680436755049d1cde960510cadcbe3858.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data directly in Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) using standard SQL. Athena is serverless, so there is no infrastructure to set up or manage, and you pay only for the queries you run, thereby making this solution really low cost. You can also use Athena to build a table with only the subset of columns that are required for downstream analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFinally, you can read the data in the given Athena table via Amazon QuickSight to identify fraudulent transactions using QuickSight\u0027s built-in machine learning-powered anomaly detection. QuickSight uses proven Amazon technology to continuously run ML-powered anomaly detection across millions of metrics to discover hidden trends and outliers in your data. This anomaly detection enables you to get deep insights that are often buried in the aggregates and not scalable with manual analysis. With ML-powered anomaly detection, you can find outliers in your data without the need for manual analysis, custom development, or ML domain expertise.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage Kinesis Data Analytics to detect anomalies on a data stream from Kinesis Streams by running SQL queries which compute an anomaly score for all transactions and then store all fraudulent transactions in Amazon S3. Use Amazon QuickSight to visualize the results from Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - Using Kinesis Data Analytics involves some custom query development to analyze the incoming data to compute an anomaly score for all transactions. In addition, this solution processes all columns of the data instead of just the subset of columns required for the analysis. Therefore, this option is not the best fit for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTransform the data from JSON format to Apache Parquet format using an AWS Glue job. Configure AWS Glue crawlers to discover the schema and build the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Leverage Amazon SageMaker to build an anomaly detection model that can detect fraudulent transactions by ingesting data directly from Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon SageMaker is a fully managed service to build, train, and deploy machine learning (ML) models with fully managed infrastructure, tools, and workflows. Using SageMaker involves custom code development to build, develop, test, and deploy the anomaly detection model that is relevant to the given scenario. Instead, you can directly use QuickSight to identify fraudulent transactions using QuickSight\u0027s built-in machine learning-based anomaly detection functionality. Therefore, this option is not the right fit for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage Kinesis Data Firehose to detect anomalies on a data stream from Kinesis Streams via a Lambda function which computes an anomaly score for all transactions and stores all fraudulent transactions in Amazon RDS. Use Amazon QuickSight to visualize the results from RDS\u003c/strong\u003e - This option involves significant custom code development on a Lambda function to examine the incoming stream from Firehose and then compute an anomaly score for all transactions. In addition, the lambda looks at all the fields in the data instead of just the subset of fields required for the analysis. Therefore, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/supported-data-sources.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/supported-data-sources.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/accessing-and-visualizing-data-from-multiple-data-sources-with-amazon-athena-and-amazon-quicksight/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/accessing-and-visualizing-data-from-multiple-data-sources-with-amazon-athena-and-amazon-quicksight/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/anomaly-detection-function.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/anomaly-detection-function.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-analytics/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-analytics/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:10:3":{"id":"e:10:3","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 3","correct":[0,1,2],"answers":["Create a MySQL or PostgreSQL Aurora  database.","Modify security groups to allow JDBC connections.","Set JDBC configuration values in hive-site.xml.","Configure the AWS Glue Data Catalog.","Set up cross-account access for EMR Serverless and AWS Glue Data Catalog.","Ensure that you do not write to the same metastore table concurrently."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA large-scale data analytics company is migrating their big data platform to Amazon Web Services (AWS). They have chosen to use Amazon Elastic MapReduce (EMR) for processing large datasets and Amazon Aurora as a Hive metastore.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe company’s data engineers need to configure Amazon EMR to use Amazon Aurora as a Hive metastore. This setup will allow the metadata to persist even after the EMR cluster is terminated, enabling cost savings and improved query performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following steps need to be taken? (SELECT THREE.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon EMR uses an external database to store metadata about the tables, schema, and other Hive data structures. The company’s data engineers would need to create an Amazon Aurora database (either MySQL or PostgreSQL) that will serve as the Hive metastore. This is a crucial step because the Hive metastore is a central repository of Hive metadata. It persists metadata for Hive tables and partitions in a relational database, and provides high availability, and ease of use.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ea518a13e89bb402f9570db7c72a5b59b14a2bdc4bf585a8d0b3d560f890e110.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHive uses JDBC to connect to the metastore database. The data engineers would need to modify the security groups of both the Amazon EMR cluster and the Amazon Aurora database to allow these connections. This is important because the security group acts like a virtual firewall that controls the traffic for one or more instances. When you launch an instance, you can associate one or more security groups with the instance. You add rules to each security group that allow traffic to or from its associated instances.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSet JDBC configuration values in hive-site.xml. This involves creating a configuration file called\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ehiveConfiguration.json\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;containing edits to\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ehive-site.xml\u003c/code\u003e. This file specifies the JDBC connection parameters that Hive uses to connect to the metastore database. The data engineers would need to set the appropriate JDBC configuration values in this file to point to the Amazon Aurora database. This is necessary because the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ehive-site.xml\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;file is where Hive reads the configuration for the metastore connection, among other settings. The JDBC connection parameters that Hive uses to connect to the metastore database are defined in this file.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Create a MySQL or PostgreSQL Aurora database\u003c/strong\u003e.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Modify security groups to allow JDBC connections\u003c/strong\u003e.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Set JDBC configuration values in hive-site.xml\u003c/strong\u003e.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eConfigure the AWS Glue Data Catalog\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because it is not relevant when using Amazon Aurora as the Hive metastore. AWS Glue Data Catalog is an alternative to using an external Hive metastore, but it’s not used in conjunction with Aurora for this purpose.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up cross-account access for EMR Serverless and AWS Glue Data Catalog\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because it is also not relevant when using Amazon Aurora as the Hive metastore. This would be necessary if you were using the AWS Glue Data Catalog and needed to access it from a different AWS account, but it’s not applicable in this scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnsure that you do not write to the same metastore table concurrently\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because this is just a cautionary step to avoid data conflicts when sharing metastore information between two clusters. While it’s good practice to avoid concurrent writes, it’s not a step in the process of configuring Amazon EMR to use Amazon Aurora as a Hive metastore. It’s more of a usage guideline after the setup is complete. The data engineers would need to be aware of this guideline when using the Hive metastore, but it’s not a step they would need to take to set up the metastore.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-hive-metastore-external.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-hive-metastore-external.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-metastore-external-hive.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-metastore-external-hive.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/migrate-and-deploy-your-apache-hive-metastore-on-amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/migrate-and-deploy-your-apache-hive-metastore-on-amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these Amazon Aurora and Amazon EMR Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-aurora/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-aurora/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:22":{"id":"e:8:22","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 22","correct":[2],"answers":["AWS Step Functions state machine","AWS Batch","AWS Glue workflows","Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA)"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has a complex ETL pipeline that involves multiple AWS Glue jobs. Each job performs tasks such as data extraction, cleaning, and loading data into a Redshift data warehouse for analytics. The company wants to orchestrate these jobs into a single workflow with minimal operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich AWS resource would be the most suitable for this use case?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue workflows\u003c/strong\u003e provide a visual interface for orchestrating complex extract, transform, and load (ETL) activities involving multiple crawlers, jobs, and triggers. Each workflow manages the execution and monitoring of all its jobs and crawlers. As a workflow runs each component, it records execution progress and status. This provides you with an overview of the larger task and the details of each step.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can create a workflow from an AWS Glue blueprint or manually build a workflow a component at a time using the AWS Management Console or the AWS Glue API. Triggers within workflows can start both jobs and crawlers and can be fired when jobs or crawlers are complete. By using triggers, you can create large chains of interdependent jobs and crawlers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/79d3261f4172b9278b920867a7dc6721294434a7f1d436ffa3249079da58ce54.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn addition to triggers within a workflow that defines job and crawler dependencies, each workflow has a start trigger. There are three types of start triggers: Schedule, On-demand, and EventBridge event. With this trigger type, AWS Glue can be an event consumer in an event-driven architecture.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis would require the least operational overhead as it is fully managed and designed for this exact use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e AWS Glue workflows.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Step Functions state machine\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because this option takes more work. When a Glue job is initiated from a Step Function state machine, there isn\u0027t an immediate response indicating job completion. Instead, you\u0027d have to implement custom logic within the state machine to continuously poll the status of a job. This is not the case with AWS Glue Workflow, where you can simply use the built-in conditions to manage running jobs in sequence or parallel.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Batch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it is intended for running batch computing jobs. It is not designed for orchestrating ETL jobs or managing data workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because, while Amazon MWAA provides a managed environment for running Apache Airflow workflows, it is not primarily designed for orchestrating Glue jobs. It is more general-purpose and may require more manual configuration and maintenance for ETL workflows compared to AWS Glue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-data-pipelines-with-aws-glue-automatic-code-generation-and-workflows/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-data-pipelines-with-aws-glue-automatic-code-generation-and-workflows/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:48":{"id":"e:2:48","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 48","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon EFS to provide a fast, cost-effective, and sharable storage service\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access, to reduce the costs on Amazon S3 storage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class to optimize the Amazon S3 storage costs\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class to reduce the costs on Amazon S3 storage\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses Amazon S3 buckets to store sensitive customer data that is business-critical. The data is kept segregated and well organized to ensure low latency. Recently, the data engineering team noticed that the lifecycle policies on the Amazon S3 buckets have not been applied optimally, resulting in higher costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCan you recommend a solution to reduce storage costs on Amazon S3 while keeping the team\u0027s involvement to a minimum?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class to optimize the Amazon S3 storage costs\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class is designed to optimize costs by automatically moving data to the most cost-effective access tier, without performance impact or operational overhead. It works by storing objects in two access tiers: one tier that is optimized for frequent access and another lower-cost tier that is optimized for infrequent access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor a small monthly monitoring and automation fee per object, Amazon S3 monitors access patterns of the objects in Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering and moves the ones that have not been accessed for 30 consecutive days to the infrequent access tier. If an object in the infrequent access tier is accessed, it is automatically moved back to the frequent access tier. There are no retrieval fees when using the Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class, and no additional tiering fees when objects are moved between access tiers. It is the ideal storage class for long-lived data with access patterns that are unknown or unpredictable.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Storage Classes can be configured at the object level and a single bucket can contain objects stored in Amazon S3 Standard, Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering, Amazon S3 Standard-IA, and Amazon S3 One Zone-IA. You can upload objects directly to Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering, or use S3 Lifecycle policies to transfer objects from Amazon S3 Standard and Amazon S3 Standard-IA to Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering. You can also archive objects from Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering to Amazon S3 Glacier.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon EFS to provide a fast, cost-effective, and sharable storage service\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) provides a simple, scalable, fully managed elastic NFS file system for use with AWS Cloud services and on-premises resources. Amazon EFS offers sharable service, unlike Amazon Elastic Block Storage (EBS) which cannot be shared by instances. Amazon EFS is costlier than storing data in Amazon S3. Also, Amazon EFS needs an Amazon EC2 instance or an AWS Direct Connect network connection. Hence, this is not the correct option.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access, to reduce the costs on Amazon S3 storage\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon S3 One Zone-IA is for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. Unlike other Amazon S3 Storage Classes which store data in a minimum of three Availability Zones (AZs), Amazon S3 One Zone-IA stores data in a single AZ and costs 20% less than Amazon S3 Standard-IA. Amazon S3 One Zone-IA is ideal for customers who want a lower-cost option for infrequently accessed data but do not require the availability and resilience of Amazon S3 Standard or Amazon S3 Standard-IA. This is not the right option, since the data stored is business-critical and cannot be risked by using Amazon S3 One Zone-IA.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class to reduce the costs on Amazon S3 storage\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class has a first-byte latency of several hours, so this option is incorrect for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:19":{"id":"e:6:19","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 19","correct":[2],"answers":["Implement SageMaker Canvas for automatic data cleansing and organization. Configure a prediction model using multi-class text classification within SageMaker Canvas to label the data.","Utilize SageMaker HyperPod for data cleansing, then apply a SageMaker built-in algorithm for the creation of a binary classification model.","Implement SageMaker Canvas\u0027s automated data cleansing and preparation features for optimal data structuring. Configure to use the built-in binary classification model within SageMaker Canvas for prediction.","Deploy SageMaker Data Wrangler for data cleansing, then use SageMaker Autopilot to train a binary classification model."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA video streaming company is preparing a dataset to train a predictive model. The dataset contains null values, irrelevant data, and duplicates. The goal is to improve data quality so that the company can accurately classify subscribers into those likely to churn and those likely to stay.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution can meet the company\u0027s needs with LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon SageMaker\u003c/strong\u003e is a service that simplifies building, training, and deploying machine learning models. SageMaker is a platform that facilitates the creation of high-quality machine learning models by abstracting away the complexity typically associated with each process step. It offers an integrated Jupyter Notebook environment for data exploration and preprocessing, optimized and ready-to-use algorithms, and a fully managed deployment service. This enables practitioners to focus more on solving the problem than on the operational overhead of machine learning pipelines.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2c2ab84f34aff5fe1406be235636e1aa4a1d4b3808d5496ad7d65729cefe88f3.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSageMaker Canvas\u003c/strong\u003e is a feature of Amazon SageMaker that represents a no-code approach to machine learning. It makes machine learning accessible to users of all technical backgrounds. With a user-friendly interface, it automates data preparation, model selection, training, and deployment. This allows users to create predictive models without needing to write any code. Canvas simplifies the entire process of applying machine learning, from data cleaning to making predictions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn building a model, SageMaker Canvas offers different types of models, which are the following:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eNumeric prediction\u003c/strong\u003e (\u003cem\u003eregression\u003c/em\u003e) model is used to predict numeric data such as the price of houses based on features like square footage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCategorical prediction\u003c/strong\u003e (\u003cem\u003eclassification\u003c/em\u003e) model is mainly used in categorizing data into groups, which also have two categorical prediction model types:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e2 category prediction\u003c/strong\u003e (also known as \u003cem\u003ebinary classification\u003c/em\u003e in machine learning) is used when you have two categories you want to predict for your data, such as determining whether a customer will churn.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e3+ category prediction\u003c/strong\u003e (\u003cem\u003emulti-class classification\u003c/em\u003e) is used to predict three or more categories, such as a customer\u0027s loan status based on past payments.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTime series forecasting\u003c/strong\u003e is useful for making predictions over a period of time, such as sales predictions for the next quarter.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImage prediction\u003c/strong\u003e (\u003cem\u003esingle-label image classification\u003c/em\u003e) model can be used for image labeling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eText prediction \u003c/strong\u003e(\u003cem\u003emulti-class text classification\u003c/em\u003e) If you have a collection of customer reviews for a particular product and you want to categorize the passages of text to determine whether customers had a positive, negative, or neutral opinion about the product, you can utilize this model to assign a label to each passage of text. This approach can help you analyze the sentiment of the reviews and gain valuable insights about the product\u0027s performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement SageMaker Canvas\u0027s automated data cleansing and preparation features for optimal data structuring. Configure to use the built-in binary classification model within SageMaker Canvas for prediction.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement SageMaker Canvas for automatic data cleansing and organization. Configure a prediction model using multi-class text classification within SageMaker Canvas to label the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Although SageMaker Canvas is useful for data cleaning and organization, the primary function of multi-class text classification is to analyze labeled text data and predict labels for unlabeled text passages. This doesn\u0027t align with the company\u0027s requirement to categorize subscribers into two groups.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize SageMaker HyperPod for data cleansing, then apply a SageMaker built-in algorithm for the creation of a binary classification model\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. SageMaker HyperPod is only ideal for provisioning resilient clusters to run machine learning workloads and develop models like foundation models (FMs), large language models (LLMs), and diffusion models. Thus, it does not fulfill the company\u0027s requirement to build a predictive model that will forecast subscriber discontinuation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy SageMaker Data Wrangler for data cleansing, then use SageMaker Autopilot to train a binary classification model\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. SageMaker Data Wrangler and SageMaker Autopilot are indeed powerful tools for data preparation and model training. While these tools offer extensive functionality, they also introduce more overhead in terms of setting up, configuration, and operation. This can increase the effort and time required to develop the predictive model, unlike SageMaker Canvas, which offers a more streamlined, no-code solution for users who may not have deep technical expertise in machine learning.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/canvas-prepare-data.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/canvas-prepare-data.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/canvas.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/canvas.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/canvas-build-model.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/canvas-build-model.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon SageMaker Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:36":{"id":"e:4:36","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 36","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an OpenSearch Service domain, index every sales record by TransactionDate, and use OpenSearch queries instead of Athena to retrieve date-range records\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon RDS database, create a Sales table with an index on TransactionDate, and use Lambda to load new CSV records into the database\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConvert the CSV sales data to JSON format by using an AWS Glue job, catalog the JSON data in AWS Glue, and run Athena queries on the JSON files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse S3 prefixes to partition the sales data by TransactionDate, catalog the partitioned data in AWS Glue, add the partitions, and update Athena queries to use date filters\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA regional retail company adds new transaction records to CSV sales data in an Amazon S3 bucket every day. The dataset contains sales data from the previous 6 years and includes columns such as TransactionID, TransactionDate, ProductName, SaleAmount, SalesAssociate, and StoreLocation. The company needs to use Amazon Athena to query the CSV data and retrieve records from specific date ranges. The company wants to reduce the amount of data that Athena scans and run the queries in the most cost-effective way.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse S3 prefixes to partition the sales data by TransactionDate, catalog the partitioned data in AWS Glue, add the partitions, and update Athena queries to use date filters\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePartitioning the sales data by TransactionDate is the most cost-effective solution for Athena when users commonly query a specific time period. Athena can use partition pruning to skip S3 prefixes that do not match the date filter.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company can organize the CSV data in S3 prefixes such as TransactionDate=2026-08-22 and store the corresponding partition metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. After the partitions are added, Athena queries that filter by TransactionDate can scan only the relevant partitions instead of scanning the entire multi-year dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution directly reduces the amount of data scanned, which is important because Athena charges based on the amount of data scanned by each query. It also keeps the existing S3 and Athena architecture and avoids unnecessary databases, clusters, or search platforms.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7993a597f1af0f62fff67e94ecbab2091a8ca27d3b20d942be5ea91c67822e9c.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7993a597f1af0f62fff67e94ecbab2091a8ca27d3b20d942be5ea91c67822e9c.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an OpenSearch Service domain, index every sales record by TransactionDate, and use OpenSearch queries instead of Athena to retrieve date-range records\u003c/strong\u003e - OpenSearch Service can index documents and support fast searches by fields such as TransactionDate. It can be useful for search, log analytics, and interactive filtering use cases.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe requirement is to use Athena to query CSV data in Amazon S3. Creating and operating an OpenSearch domain adds ingestion, indexing, scaling, and maintenance work that is unnecessary for this Athena partitioning use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConvert the CSV sales data to JSON format by using an AWS Glue job, catalog the JSON data in AWS Glue, and run Athena queries on the JSON files\u003c/strong\u003e - An AWS Glue job can transform CSV data into another format and AWS Glue Data Catalog can catalog the transformed dataset. This could make the data queryable by Athena if the schema is defined correctly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, converting CSV to JSON does not reduce Athena scan cost for date-range queries in the same way that partition pruning does. JSON is still a row-oriented text format and can require Athena to scan and parse unnecessary records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon RDS database, create a Sales table with an index on TransactionDate, and use Lambda to load new CSV records into the database\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon RDS can store structured sales data, and an index on TransactionDate can support date-range lookups. Lambda can also load new records into the database when files arrive.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis design moves the workload away from Athena and the S3 data lake. It adds database provisioning, loading logic, indexing, maintenance, and operational cost when S3 partitioning can optimize the existing Athena query pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/ctas-partitioning-and-bucketing-what-is-partitioning.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/ctas-partitioning-and-bucketing-what-is-partitioning.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-data-optimization-techniques.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-data-optimization-techniques.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/tables-described.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/tables-described.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:24":{"id":"e:1:24","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 24","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure an Internet Gateway in the VPC for the AWS Glue job to access the Amazon S3 bucket via the public endpoint\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eVerify that the route table of the VPC has inbound and outbound routes for the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAttach a bucket policy to the S3 bucket that will explicitly grant access permissions to the IAM role associated with the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure private DNS options for the VPC gateway endpoint\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer has configured an AWS Glue job to read data from an Amazon S3 bucket by setting up the AWS Glue connection details and the associated IAM role. However, when the AWS Glue job is run, it fails with an error pointing to the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint that has been configured for accessing the data in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow should the data engineer troubleshoot this issue?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eVerify that the route table of the VPC has inbound and outbound routes for the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA gateway VPC endpoint enables you to create a private connection between your VPC and another AWS service. When you create a gateway endpoint, you specify the subnet route tables in your VPC that are used by the gateway endpoint. A route is automatically added to each of the route tables with a destination that specifies the prefix list ID of the service (pl-xxxxxxxx), and a target with the endpoint ID (vpce-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx). You cannot explicitly delete or modify the endpoint route, but you can change the route tables that are used by the endpoint.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA VPC endpoint for Amazon S3 enables AWS Glue to use private IP addresses to access Amazon S3 with no exposure to the public internet. AWS Glue does not require public IP addresses, and you don\u0027t need an internet gateway, a NAT device, or a virtual private gateway in your VPC. You use endpoint policies to control access to Amazon S3. Traffic between your VPC and the AWS service does not leave the Amazon network.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue with VPC endpoints:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/54f847a67019f6e2e19cbdcbe771e506b33e70f1c55d8367c8b56e5d1056d616.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/54f847a67019f6e2e19cbdcbe771e506b33e70f1c55d8367c8b56e5d1056d616.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/vpc-endpoints-s3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/vpc-endpoints-s3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAttach a bucket policy to the S3 bucket that will explicitly grant access permissions to the IAM role associated with the AWS Glue job\u003c/strong\u003e - An Amazon S3 bucket policy that does not have sufficient permissions for the IAM role associated with the AWS Glue job will not result in an error pointing to the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint. So, this option is ruled out.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure private DNS options for the VPC gateway endpoint\u003c/strong\u003e - For private connectivity, you use private DNS options for your Amazon S3 when you use an interface VPC endpoint. This configuration does not apply to the gateway endpoint.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an Internet Gateway in the VPC for the AWS Glue job to access the Amazon S3 bucket via the public endpoint\u003c/strong\u003e - The correct solution is to set up a VPC endpoint for Amazon S3 that enables AWS Glue to use private IP addresses to access Amazon S3 with no exposure to the public internet.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/route-table-options.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/route-table-options.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/vpc-endpoints-s3.htm\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/vpc-endpoints-s3.htm\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/introducing-private-dns-support-for-amazon-s3-with-aws-privatelink/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/introducing-private-dns-support-for-amazon-s3-with-aws-privatelink/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:2":{"id":"e:2:2","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 2","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable default encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) encryption (AES-256) for audit logging. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the data for monthly audits\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable default encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) encryption (AES-256) for audit logging. Copy the data into the Amazon Redshift cluster from Amazon S3 when data needs to be queried for monthly audits\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon Server-Side Encryption with KMS keys Stored in AWS Key Management Service (SSE-KMS) for audit logging. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the data for monthly audits\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon Server-Side Encryption with KMS keys Stored in AWS Key Management Service (SSE-KMS) for audit logging. Use Amazon QuickSight to query the data for monthly audits\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn Amazon Redshift cluster is used to store sensitive information of a business-critical application. The regulatory guidelines mandate tracking audit logs of the Redshift cluster. The business needs to store the audit logs securely by encrypting the logs at rest. The logs are to be stored for a year at least and audits need to be conducted on the audit logs on a monthly basis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following is a cost-effective solution that fulfills the requirement of storing the logs securely while having access to the logs for monthly audits?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable default encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) encryption (AES-256) for audit logging. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the data for monthly audits\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAudit logging is not turned on by default in Amazon Redshift. When you turn on logging on your cluster, Amazon Redshift creates and uploads logs to Amazon S3 that capture data from the time audit logging is enabled to the present time. Each logging update is a continuation of the information that was already logged.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAudit logging to Amazon S3 is an optional, manual process. When you enable logging on your cluster, you are enabling logging to Amazon S3 only. Currently, you can only use Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) encryption (AES-256) for audit logging.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift Spectrum is a feature of Amazon Redshift that lets you run queries against your data lake in Amazon S3, with no data loading or ETL required. When you issue an SQL query, it goes to the Amazon Redshift endpoint, which generates and optimizes a query plan. Amazon Redshift determines what data is local and what is in Amazon S3, generates a plan to minimize the amount of S3 data that needs to be read, and requests Amazon Redshift Spectrum workers out of a shared resource pool to read and process data from S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/77cf4bb8f4d46ffadc3ce6c624024687d2e60529003fe834c0ed0c6a27766b73.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/77cf4bb8f4d46ffadc3ce6c624024687d2e60529003fe834c0ed0c6a27766b73.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable default encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) encryption (AES-256) for audit logging. Copy the data into the Amazon Redshift cluster from Amazon S3 when data needs to be queried for monthly audits\u003c/strong\u003e - Copying data into the Redshift cluster for enabling monthly audits would turn out to be a costly solution. Using Redshift Spectrum is the right fit for the given scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon Server-Side Encryption with KMS keys Stored in AWS Key Management Service (SSE-KMS) for audit logging. Use Amazon QuickSight to query the data for monthly audits\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon Server-Side Encryption with KMS keys Stored in AWS Key Management Service (SSE-KMS) for audit logging. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the data for monthly audits\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCurrently, you can only use Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) encryption (AES-256) for audit logging. Hence, both these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:13":{"id":"e:4:13","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 13","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue to split the JSON file into separate PII and non-PII files, store each output in a different S3 bucket, and grant bucket-level access\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Macie to discover PII fields in the JSON file and configure Macie to grant query access based on each user’s authorization level\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the JSON file in Amazon S3, catalog the dataset in the AWS Glue Data Catalog, and use Lake Formation permissions to control access to sensitive and non-sensitive fields\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the JSON file in Amazon S3, create separate Athena workgroups for each team, and leverage the workgroup settings to hide sensitive JSON fields\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company stores a JSON dataset in an Amazon S3 bucket. The file contains both patient-identifying attributes and non-sensitive operational attributes. Analysts across the company need to query the non-sensitive fields, but only a small compliance team can access the patient-identifying fields.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company wants to make the data available for querying and analysis while applying fine-grained access controls with the least operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the JSON file in Amazon S3, catalog the dataset in the AWS Glue Data Catalog, and use Lake Formation permissions to control access to sensitive and non-sensitive fields\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation is the best fit because it provides fine-grained access control for data lake resources that are stored in Amazon S3 and cataloged in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. The company can catalog the JSON dataset and then grant different permissions to different users or groups.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/abd12f26838a8914b164f8ea523bcaaead18ecb1baeb8ef6be6fc47582c23ce2.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/abd12f26838a8914b164f8ea523bcaaead18ecb1baeb8ef6be6fc47582c23ce2.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach can allow broad access to non-PII fields while restricting PII fields to the limited employee group that is authorized to view them. Lake Formation permissions can be enforced for supported query engines such as Amazon Athena, which allows users to analyze the data without needing separate physical copies of the dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational overhead because it centralizes governance in Lake Formation instead of requiring the data engineer to split files, duplicate storage locations, maintain custom filtering code, or create separate databases. The company can manage permissions through the data catalog and apply access policies consistently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6c4a85da86a83037944afa0a8a823e939f767a1e05fdb953b2bb956fe4127b66.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6c4a85da86a83037944afa0a8a823e939f767a1e05fdb953b2bb956fe4127b66.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/create-a-secure-data-lake-by-masking-encrypting-data-and-enabling-fine-grained-access-with-aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/create-a-secure-data-lake-by-masking-encrypting-data-and-enabling-fine-grained-access-with-aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue to split the JSON file into separate PII and non-PII files, store each output in a different S3 bucket, and grant bucket-level access\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue can transform JSON data and write separate outputs to different Amazon S3 buckets. This design could physically separate sensitive and non-sensitive attributes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that this creates extra ETL work, duplicate data management, and ongoing maintenance when the schema changes. Lake Formation provides fine-grained access control without requiring the company to split and manage multiple physical datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Macie to discover PII fields in the JSON file and configure Macie to grant query access based on each user’s authorization level\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Macie can discover and classify sensitive data in Amazon S3, including personally identifiable information. It is useful for identifying where sensitive data exists.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option does not meet the access-control requirement because Macie does not grant or enforce query permissions for users. The company still needs a governance layer such as Lake Formation to control who can access PII and non-PII fields.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the JSON file in Amazon S3, create separate Athena workgroups for each team, and leverage the workgroup settings to hide sensitive JSON fields\u003c/strong\u003e - Athena workgroups can help separate query execution settings, costs, query history, and result locations for different teams. They are useful for operational governance of query workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because workgroups alone do not provide field-level authorization over JSON attributes. The company needs Lake Formation permissions to restrict access to sensitive fields in the cataloged data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/granting-catalog-permissions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/granting-catalog-permissions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/access-control-fine-grained.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/access-control-fine-grained.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/security-athena-lake-formation.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/security-athena-lake-formation.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/create-a-secure-data-lake-by-masking-encrypting-data-and-enabling-fine-grained-access-with-aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/create-a-secure-data-lake-by-masking-encrypting-data-and-enabling-fine-grained-access-with-aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:57":{"id":"e:5:57","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 57","correct":[1],"answers":["AWS Data Transfer Terminal","AWS DataSync","AWS Storage Gateway","AWS Glue"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA corporation stores large amounts of data in an on-premises data center. The corporation wants to have a copy of this data in Amazon S3 for further analysis. The replication process must run periodically to ensure data in the S3 bucket is up-to-date with the latest data from the on-premises data center.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich AWS service is most suitable for meeting this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAWS DataSync is a secure, online service that automates and accelerates moving data between on-premises and AWS Storage services. DataSync can copy data between Network File System (NFS) shares, Server Message Block (SMB) shares, Hadoop Distributed File Systems (HDFS), self-managed object storage, Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) buckets, Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) file systems, Amazon FSx for Windows File Server file systems, Amazon FSx for Lustre file systems, Amazon FSx for OpenZFS file systems, and Amazon FSx for NetApp ONTAP file systems.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2b4f189954a0279a640b13135c2d69f0045c0cecac2a3f879de6442ff0f45379.png\" alt=\"AWS DataSync\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWith\u0026nbsp;AWS DataSync, you can move data easily between S3 buckets for one-time migrations or periodic scheduled transfers, reducing operational overhead and infrastructure costs. DataSync is a fully managed solution that enables the transfer of data between S3 buckets with just a few clicks in the console, and without managing any additional infrastructure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS DataSync.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy to prepare and load your data for analytics. It’s not typically designed for moving data between on-premises data center and AWS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Storage Gateway\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because AWS Storage Gateway is primarily designed for hybrid cloud storage, enabling on-premises applications to seamlessly use AWS cloud storage. While it integrates well with Amazon S3, it’s better suited for continuous and real-time access to cloud storage rather than periodic replication for analysis purposes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Data Transfer Terminal\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because this service is intended for offline bulk data transfer using physical devices. It is suitable for one-time migrations but not for ongoing, periodic replication tasks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datasync/latest/userguide/what-is-datasync.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/datasync/latest/userguide/what-is-datasync.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/datasync/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/datasync/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/how-to-use-aws-datasync-to-migrate-data-between-amazon-s3-buckets/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/how-to-use-aws-datasync-to-migrate-data-between-amazon-s3-buckets/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS DataSync Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-datasync/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-datasync/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-24":{"id":"p:2-24","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 24","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena Federated Query with Athena data source connectors to join the DynamoDB, Aurora PostgreSQL, Amazon Redshift, and Amazon S3 datasets in place for the one-time report\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eBuild an AWS Glue ETL pipeline to extract data from all sources, centralize the data in Amazon S3, and run Amazon Athena queries against the consolidated dataset\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate AWS Glue crawlers for each source, export the DynamoDB, Aurora PostgreSQL, and Amazon Redshift datasets to Amazon S3, convert every exported dataset to Apache Parquet, catalog the converted files, and run the final join by using Amazon Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCapture DynamoDB changes with DynamoDB Streams, migrate Aurora PostgreSQL data with AWS DMS, export Amazon Redshift data to Amazon S3, and query the combined files with Redshift Spectrum\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Athena Federated Query với connector phù hợp đọc các nguồn tại chỗ rồi JOIN cho báo cáo một lần. Tránh xây kho hợp nhất và di chuyển dữ liệu lớn.","Glue ETL có thể tập trung dữ liệu nhưng tạo pipeline và bản sao mới không cần thiết cho một báo cáo duy nhất.","Xuất, đổi Parquet và catalog hóa mọi nguồn là nhiều bước chuẩn bị. Trái mong muốn hạn chế chuyển dữ liệu và thời gian thiết lập.","Kết hợp Streams, DMS, export và Spectrum tạo nhiều đường đồng bộ cần vận hành. Federated Query đơn giản hơn cho truy vấn một lần."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail operations company needs to create a one-time executive performance report by joining data from Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL, Amazon Redshift, and Amazon S3. The company wants to avoid building a temporary consolidated data store or moving large volumes of data between services. The reporting team also wants to minimize the time required to run the analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-53":{"id":"p:3-53","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 53","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a time-based schedule option within an AWS Glue job to invoke itself every week and run the database rollover script\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSchedule a weekly Amazon EventBridge event cron expression to invoke an AWS Lambda function that runs the database rollover job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eProvision an Amazon EC2 spot instance to run the database rollover script to be run via an OS-based weekly cron expression\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eProvision an Amazon EC2 on-demand instance to run the database rollover script to be run via an OS-based weekly cron expression\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Glue có thể chạy script nhưng nặng và chi phí hơn cần thiết cho cron Python khoảng năm phút. Lambda phù hợp tác vụ nhỏ này.","Lịch cron EventBridge gọi Lambda mỗi tuần. Hàm chạy script khoảng năm phút trong giới hạn runtime, không cần giữ máy chủ để chờ lịch.","Spot EC2 vẫn cần quản lý máy chủ và có thể bị gián đoạn. Không đáp ứng thiết kế serverless yêu cầu.","EC2 On-Demand và cron hệ điều hành phải vận hành máy chủ, trong khi công việc chỉ diễn ra vài phút mỗi tuần."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA social media startup uses AWS Cloud to manage its IT infrastructure. The data engineering team at the startup wants to perform weekly database rollovers for a MySQL database server using a serverless cron job that typically takes about 5 minutes to execute the database rollover script written in Python. The database rollover will archive the past week’s data from the production database to keep the database small while still keeping its data accessible.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you recommend as the MOST cost-efficient and reliable solution?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:51":{"id":"e:4:51","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 51","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to stream data to Amazon Redshift. Create a business intelligence dashboard by using Amazon Athena that has Amazon Redshift as a data source\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream data to Amazon S3. Create a business intelligence dashboard by using Amazon QuickSight that has Amazon Athena as a data source\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to stream data to Amazon S3. Create a business intelligence dashboard by using Amazon QuickSight that has Amazon Athena as a data source\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream data to Amazon Redshift. Create a business intelligence dashboard by using Amazon QuickSight that has Amazon Redshift as a data source\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA trading firm collects daily stock trading data from exchanges and stores it in a data warehouse. The data engineering team at the firm needs a solution that streams data directly into the data repository but should also allow SQL-based data modifications when needed. The solution should facilitate complex analytical queries that execute in the fastest possible time. The solution should also offer a business intelligence dashboard that highlights any stock price anomalies.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions represents the best fit for the given scenario?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream data to Amazon Redshift. Create a business intelligence dashboard by using Amazon QuickSight that has Amazon Redshift as a data source\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Firehose is an extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that reliably captures, transforms, and delivers streaming data to data lakes, data stores, and analytics services. It can capture, transform, and load streaming data into Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, Amazon OpenSearch Service, and Splunk, enabling near real-time analytics with existing business intelligence tools and dashboards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eKinesis Data Firehose Overview:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b58bad26f75fea37e93638ae44e062e9606bf86789742d6d37c0dd116bd7dadd.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b58bad26f75fea37e93638ae44e062e9606bf86789742d6d37c0dd116bd7dadd.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams is a massively scalable, highly durable data ingestion and processing service optimized for streaming data. Amazon Kinesis Data Streams is integrated with a number of AWS services, including Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose for near real-time transformation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eKey Concepts for Kinesis Data Streams:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/426f892154f7a54b33616f08ba7a510b8f312cbef15c10d41e32cd484bdb7260.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/426f892154f7a54b33616f08ba7a510b8f312cbef15c10d41e32cd484bdb7260.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/98dff3989547b1a58274fc2e11798966e8079a91e87209666c02c54a75d4dc5f.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/98dff3989547b1a58274fc2e11798966e8079a91e87209666c02c54a75d4dc5f.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/getting-started/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/getting-started/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the given use case, you can use Kinesis Data Firehose to stream data to Amazon Redshift. For a Redshift destination, streaming data is delivered to an S3 bucket first. Kinesis Data Firehose then issues an Amazon Redshift COPY command to load data from your S3 bucket to your Amazon Redshift cluster. If data transformation is enabled, you can optionally back up source data to another Amazon S3 bucket. Once the data is in Redshift, you can use Quicksight to create a business intelligence dashboard that has Redshift as the data source.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/561d32cc5c3f17081fb94926cd3f84a673ef023b6e1f7fde0feb5693ad005f46.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/561d32cc5c3f17081fb94926cd3f84a673ef023b6e1f7fde0feb5693ad005f46.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/what-is-this-service.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/what-is-this-service.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to stream data to Amazon Redshift. Create a business intelligence dashboard by using Amazon Athena that has Amazon Redshift as a data source\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use streaming ingestion to provide low-latency, high-speed ingestion of streaming data from Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka into an Amazon Redshift provisioned or Amazon Redshift Serverless materialized view. It lowers the time it takes to access data and it reduces storage cost. You can configure streaming ingestion for your Amazon Redshift cluster or for Amazon Redshift Serverless and create a materialized view, using SQL statements. You cannot use Amazon Athena to create a business intelligence dashboard, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream data to Amazon S3. Create a business intelligence dashboard by using Amazon QuickSight that has Amazon Athena as a data source\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to stream data to Amazon S3. Create a business intelligence dashboard by using Amazon QuickSight that has Amazon Athena as a data source\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eStoring the data in S3 and then querying the data via Athena would not facilitate the execution of complex queries in the fastest possible time since Redshift has much better performance than Athena for complex analytical queries. So both these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/getting-started/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/getting-started/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/quicksight-redshift-private-connection/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/quicksight-redshift-private-connection/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-64":{"id":"p:3-64","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 64","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure each division as a separate SageMaker Unified Studio domain, isolate administrative policies across domains, configure AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, and delegate administration at the domain level\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure each division as a domain unit, manage permissions for assets, projects, and metadata within each unit, use AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, map user profiles to the appropriate domain units, and enable cross-division sharing through access requests that domain unit owners approve or deny\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure each division as a domain unit with owner permissions, restrict projects so only owners can access assets, prevent sharing across domain units, configure AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, and map user profiles to the corresponding domain units\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate one shared project for all divisions in the SageMaker Unified Studio domain, use project membership to separate assets and metadata, configure AWS IAM Identity Center for authentication, and instruct project owners to manually track dataset access approvals outside the domain\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Tách thành nhiều domain khác với yêu cầu phân quyền theo bộ phận bên trong một domain mới. Cũng làm mô hình chia sẻ có quản trị phức tạp hơn.","Domain unit trao quyền quản trị tài sản/project cho từng bộ phận; IAM Identity Center cung cấp xác thực tập trung. Chia sẻ liên đơn vị đi qua yêu cầu và phê duyệt.","Cấm chia sẻ giữa domain units trái yêu cầu các dataset được duyệt phải có thể được khám phá và chia sẻ.","Một project chung không tạo ranh giới quản trị độc lập như domain units. Theo dõi phê duyệt bên ngoài còn mất quy trình quản trị tích hợp."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA global manufacturing company is creating a new Amazon SageMaker Unified Studio domain for its analytics and AI teams. Each division must independently manage its own projects, assets, metadata, and permissions. Some approved datasets must be discoverable and shareable with other divisions through a governed request and approval process. The company also requires centralized user authentication and mapping between identities and the appropriate organizational units.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:65":{"id":"e:2:65","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 65","correct":[0,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDefine granular IAM policies that restrict access to sensitive S3 prefixes or objects based on user roles and job functions\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDeploy Amazon Rekognition to analyze the structured financial records for PII and redact sensitive text before use in downstream services. Integrate this with a Lambda function to batch-process the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to identify and mask sensitive fields such as account numbers and national IDs using built-in transformations. Store the masked results in a new S3 bucket for downstream analytics\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable Amazon Macie on the S3 bucket to automatically discover and classify sensitive data such as PII and financial data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon Detective to investigate potential unauthorized access to sensitive fields and generate detailed visualizations of user behavior on the data\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA fintech startup processes transaction logs and customer account records that are stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. The bucket is encrypted using server-side encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS). Some of the stored data contains sensitive financial attributes such as customer bank account numbers, credit scores, and national ID numbers. To ensure data privacy and regulatory compliance, the company must automatically detect, mask, and control access to sensitive fields during the analytics workflow. Analysts need masked versions of the data for modeling, while certain pre-processing engineers require controlled access to unmasked data for enrichment tasks. The company wants a low-maintenance and scalable solution that ensures end-to-end protection of sensitive data within the pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of solutions should the company use to fulfill these requirements? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to identify and mask sensitive fields such as account numbers and national IDs using built-in transformations. Store the masked results in a new S3 bucket for downstream analytics\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that enables data analysts and engineers to clean and transform data without writing code. It includes built-in transformations for masking, redacting, or hashing sensitive fields, such as social security numbers and account details. DataBrew integrates directly with Amazon S3 and supports scheduled jobs, making it a low-maintenance and scalable solution for applying masking logic before sensitive data is analyzed. It is well-suited for structured PII transformation tasks across the data pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f925c1cb49db11b044b8024e012005a0c5576bb400071ef1e977ce289da7c55e.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f925c1cb49db11b044b8024e012005a0c5576bb400071ef1e977ce289da7c55e.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/tw/blogs/big-data/build-a-data-pipeline-to-automatically-discover-and-mask-pii-data-with-aws-glue-databrew/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/tw/blogs/big-data/build-a-data-pipeline-to-automatically-discover-and-mask-pii-data-with-aws-glue-databrew/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDefine granular IAM policies that restrict access to sensitive S3 prefixes or objects based on user roles and job functions\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS IAM is the standard and recommended approach to enforce fine-grained access control over AWS resources, including S3 buckets. In this context, IAM policies can be used to restrict access to unmasked PII data, granting permissions only to trusted users or services in the pre-processing phase. You can apply IAM conditions (e.g., restricting access via VPC endpoints, MFA, or encryption context) to ensure that only authorized identities can access or process sensitive data, aligning with compliance and least-privilege principles.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy Amazon Rekognition to analyze the structured financial records for PII and redact sensitive text before use in downstream services. Integrate this with a Lambda function to batch-process the data\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Rekognition is an AI service for image and video analysis, including facial recognition, object detection, and text detection in media. It is not designed to process structured data such as CSV or JSON containing financial records. Using Rekognition to scan S3 files with tabular PII would be impractical and misaligned with the service’s purpose.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon Detective to investigate potential unauthorized access to sensitive fields and generate detailed visualizations of user behavior on the data\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Detective is designed for security investigations and threat analysis based on AWS CloudTrail, VPC flow logs, and GuardDuty findings. It helps visualize suspicious activity, but it does not inspect or monitor data content inside S3 buckets and cannot classify or mask PII. Hence, it\u0027s not suitable for protecting or masking sensitive data in analytics pipelines.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Amazon Macie on the S3 bucket to automatically discover and classify sensitive data such as PII and financial data\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Macie uses machine learning to discover and classify sensitive data in S3, such as PII and financial records. While it helps identify where sensitive data exists, it does not provide masking or access control features. Macie is useful as a discovery and classification tool, but by itself, it cannot enforce masking or protect access to PII in the engineering pipeline. For that, other services like DataBrew and IAM are needed. In the following reference blog, AWS recommends that you use Macie to analyze the objects and identify sensitive data using machine learning (ML) and pattern matching. Then you can use AWS Glue to run a workflow to anonymize or mask the data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eData masking and granular access control using Amazon Macie and AWS Lake Formation:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/73c338d01adbffafbbee4044bacae812f483ce0189bf53ae447d0639a445335e.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/73c338d01adbffafbbee4044bacae812f483ce0189bf53ae447d0639a445335e.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/data-masking-and-granular-access-control-using-amazon-macie-and-aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/data-masking-and-granular-access-control-using-amazon-macie-and-aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/tw/blogs/big-data/build-a-data-pipeline-to-automatically-discover-and-mask-pii-data-with-aws-glue-databrew/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/tw/blogs/big-data/build-a-data-pipeline-to-automatically-discover-and-mask-pii-data-with-aws-glue-databrew/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/data-masking-and-granular-access-control-using-amazon-macie-and-aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/data-masking-and-granular-access-control-using-amazon-macie-and-aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:39":{"id":"e:1:39","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 39","correct":[1,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lifecycle Policy to transition objects to Glacier Deep Archive using a prefix after 180 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lifecycle Policy to transition all objects to Glacier Deep Archive after 180 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lifecycle Policy to transition all objects to S3 Standard IA after 45 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lifecycle Policy to transition objects to S3 Standard IA using a prefix after 45 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lifecycle Policy to transition objects to S3 One Zone IA using a prefix after 45 days\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has an S3 bucket that contains files in two different folders - \u003ccode\u003es3://my-bucket/images\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003es3://my-bucket/thumbnails\u003c/code\u003e. When an image is first uploaded and new, it is viewed several times. Post a detailed analysis, the data analytics team has noticed that after 45 days those image files are rarely requested, but the thumbnails still are. After 180 days, the company would like to archive the image files and the thumbnails. Overall, the company would like the solution to remain highly available to prevent disasters from happening against the entire AZ.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents the best-fit solutions for an efficient cost strategy for the given S3 bucket? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo manage your S3 objects, so they are stored cost-effectively throughout their lifecycle, configure their Amazon S3 Lifecycle. An S3 Lifecycle configuration is a set of rules that define actions that Amazon S3 applies to a group of objects. There are two types of actions:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTransition actions — Define when objects transition to another storage class. For example, you might choose to transition objects to the S3 Standard-IA storage class 30 days after you created them, or archive objects to the S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class one year after creating them.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eExpiration actions — Define when objects expire. Amazon S3 deletes expired objects on your behalf.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Lifecycle Policy to transition objects to S3 Standard IA using a prefix after 45 days\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eS3 Standard-IA is for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. S3 Standard-IA offers high durability, high throughput, and low latency of S3 Standard, with a low per GB storage price and per GB retrieval fee. This combination of low cost and high performance makes S3 Standard-IA ideal for long-term storage, backups, and as a data store for disaster recovery files. The minimum storage duration charge is 30 days.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs the use-case mentions, after 45 days, image files are rarely requested, but the thumbnails still are. So you need to use a prefix while configuring the Lifecycle Policy so that only objects in the \u003ccode\u003es3://my-bucket/images\u003c/code\u003e are transitioned to Standard IA and not all the objects in the bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Lifecycle Policy to transition all objects to Glacier Deep Archive after 180 days\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive is a secure, durable, and extremely low-cost Amazon S3 cloud storage class for data archiving and long-term backup. It is designed to deliver 99.999999999% durability and provide comprehensive security and compliance capabilities that can help meet even the most stringent regulatory requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Lifecycle Policy to transition all objects to S3 Standard IA after 45 days\u003c/strong\u003e - As discussed above, you need to use a prefix while configuring the Lifecycle Policy so that only objects in the \u003ccode\u003es3://my-bucket/images\u003c/code\u003e are transitioned to Standard IA and not all the objects in the bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Lifecycle Policy to transition objects to Glacier Deep Archive using a prefix after 180 days\u003c/strong\u003e - After 180 days, you can move all the objects to Glacier Deep Archive storage as per the use case. Glacier Deep Archive doesn\u0027t need prefixes for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Lifecycle Policy to transition objects to S3 One Zone IA using a prefix after 45 days\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Standard-IA is for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. S3 Standard-IA offers high durability, high throughput, and low latency of S3 Standard, with a low per GB storage price and per GB retrieval fee. This combination of low cost and high performance makes S3 Standard-IA ideal for long-term storage, backups, and as a data store for disaster recovery files. The minimum storage duration charge is 30 days.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eS3 One Zone-IA is for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. Unlike other S3 Storage Classes which store data in a minimum of three Availability Zones (AZs), S3 One Zone-IA stores data in a single AZ and costs 20% less than S3 Standard-IA. The minimum storage duration charge is 30 days.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFinally, S3 One Zone IA will not offer the required availability in case an AZ goes down.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:34":{"id":"e:6:34","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 34","correct":[0,1],"answers":["Use Amazon CloudWatch Contributor Insights to find the most accessed and throttled items in the DynamoDB table.","Redesign the DynamoDB table to use a high cardinality key to distribute the read and write operations as evenly as possible.","Use Amazon QuickSight to visualize the access patterns of the DynamoDB table.","Use Amazon Athena to analyze the access patterns of the DynamoDB table.","Increase the provisioned read and write capacity units of the DynamoDB table."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAn e-commerce company has implemented real-time inventory management using a DynamoDB table with multiple data partitions. This solution updates inventory levels as customers place orders on the website. However, during peak shopping hours, the data engineer has noticed an increase in throttled items in the DynamoDB table, which is degrading the system\u0027s performance. This causes delays in inventory updates.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eTo address these problems, the company needs to:\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eContinuously monitor and diagnose the DynamoDB table\u0027s access patterns.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAnalyze traffic trends during different times of the day.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eIdentify the most frequently accessed partition keys.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhat steps should the data engineer take to address these issues? (Select TWO.)\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon CloudWatch Contributor Insights is a diagnostic tool that provides a summarized view of your DynamoDB table’s traffic trends. It helps you identify the most frequently accessed partition keys, which can be useful in diagnosing throttling issues. By identifying the most accessed and throttled items, the data engineer can gain insights into the table\u0027s access patterns and take appropriate actions to mitigate the throttling issues.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4a0aa4e7ab08034fa033a6565d63a0099ffcc81e7fc474818fe567541fd7b85b.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn DynamoDB, data is distributed across multiple partitions based on the partition key. If a few partition keys receive a majority of the read and write traffic, this can lead to hot partitions, where these partitions receive a disproportionately high volume of traffic. This can result in throttling issues. By redesigning the table to use a high cardinality key, the read and write operations can be distributed more evenly across all the partitions, reducing the likelihood of hot partitions and throttling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e- \u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon CloudWatch Contributor Insights to find the most accessed and throttled items in the DynamoDB table.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e- \u003cstrong\u003eRedesign the DynamoDB table to use a high cardinality key to distribute the read and write operations as evenly as possible.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse Amazon QuickSight to visualize the access patterns of the DynamoDB table\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. While Amazon QuickSight is a powerful visualization tool, it does not provide the real-time diagnostic capabilities needed to identify and address throttling issues in a DynamoDB table. Its primary function is to provide a visual representation of your data rather than real-time diagnostics or performance optimization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena to analyze the access patterns of the DynamoDB table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because Amazon Athena is just a query service that makes it easy to analyze data in Amazon S3 and other data sources using standard SQL. It’s not designed to analyze access patterns of a DynamoDB table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the provisioned read and write capacity units of the DynamoDB table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because while this might help in some cases, it’s not a guaranteed solution. If the workload is unevenly distributed across the partitions (due to a low cardinality key), increasing capacity may only lead to higher costs if it\u0027s not properly scaled based on the actual traffic patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/contributorinsights_HowItWorks.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/contributorinsights_HowItWorks.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/choosing-the-right-dynamodb-partition-key/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/choosing-the-right-dynamodb-partition-key/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TroubleshootingThrottling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TroubleshootingThrottling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these Amazon DynamoDB and Amazon CloudWatch Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-cloudwatch/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-cloudwatch/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:60":{"id":"e:3:60","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 60","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate the Lambda function in the SAM template, use EventBridge Scheduler to scan the source bucket, and deploy the schedule with sam sync\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd the source S3 bucket and S3 event source to the SAM template, run sam build, and deploy by using aws cloudformation deploy\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd the source S3 bucket and S3 event source to the SAM template, run sam build, and then run sam deploy --guided\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun sam deploy with the --s3-bucket option to upload the Lambda code, and then manually add the S3 trigger in the console\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics team needs to deploy a serverless pipeline that processes inventory files. Store systems upload CSV files to a source Amazon S3 bucket, and each upload must invoke an AWS Lambda function. The Lambda function converts the CSV content to JSON and writes the transformed output to a separate destination S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer has already created an AWS Serverless Application Model template that defines the Lambda function. The data engineer wants AWS SAM to package and deploy the complete pipeline, including the S3 upload trigger.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will package and deploy this serverless data pipeline?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdd the source S3 bucket and S3 event source to the SAM template, run sam build, and then run sam deploy --guided\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS SAM can define serverless application resources in a SAM template, including an AWS Lambda function, Amazon S3 buckets, and an S3 event source that invokes the Lambda function when a new object is created. Adding both the source S3 bucket and the S3 event source to the template allows AWS SAM to deploy the pipeline as infrastructure as code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe sam build command prepares the Lambda function code and dependencies for deployment. It creates build artifacts that AWS SAM can use when deploying the application to AWS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe sam deploy --guided command is appropriate for guided deployment, especially when deploying a SAM application for the first time. This approach packages and deploys the Lambda function and related resources from the SAM template without requiring manual trigger configuration in the console.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/071009f7beb4f7819b47952768bcf516e7ec437854b5eea440edcd851dce9425.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/071009f7beb4f7819b47952768bcf516e7ec437854b5eea440edcd851dce9425.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/serverless-example-s3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/serverless-example-s3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4ace50a92a92f2f8bc0ee4ff0d96f335f3d5adda2765df76ba9bf6b0cf0f4901.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4ace50a92a92f2f8bc0ee4ff0d96f335f3d5adda2765df76ba9bf6b0cf0f4901.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/serverless-example-s3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/serverless-example-s3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun sam deploy with the --s3-bucket option to upload the Lambda code, and then manually add the S3 trigger in the console\u003c/strong\u003e - The sam deploy command can upload and deploy application artifacts when the required deployment settings are provided. The --s3-bucket option can identify an S3 bucket for deployment artifacts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that this option leaves the S3 event trigger outside the SAM template and configures it manually. That does not fully use AWS SAM to deploy the pipeline and creates configuration drift between the template and the deployed resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate the Lambda function in the SAM template, use EventBridge Scheduler to scan the source bucket, and deploy the schedule with sam sync\u003c/strong\u003e - EventBridge Scheduler can invoke targets on a schedule. This can work for time-based automation that runs at fixed intervals.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not match the event-driven requirement. The pipeline must run whenever a user uploads a CSV file to Amazon S3, so the SAM template should define an S3 event source for the Lambda function instead of a scheduled bucket scan.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdd the source S3 bucket and S3 event source to the SAM template, run sam build, and deploy by using aws cloudformation deploy\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS SAM templates are transformed into AWS CloudFormation resources during deployment. Advanced teams can sometimes use CloudFormation deployment commands as part of custom deployment workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe issue with this approach is that the recommended SAM CLI deployment workflow is to use sam deploy after sam build. The aws cloudformation deploy command does not provide the same SAM deployment experience as sam deploy --guided for packaging, parameter prompting, and SAM-managed deployment settings.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/sam-property-function-s3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/sam-property-function-s3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/serverless-example-s3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/serverless-example-s3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/using-sam-cli-build.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/using-sam-cli-build.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/using-sam-cli-deploy.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/using-sam-cli-deploy.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/serverless/sam/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/serverless/sam/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:45":{"id":"e:6:45","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 45","correct":[2],"answers":["Provision AWS Lake Formation together with EMR.","Leverage the local Hive metastore on an EMR cluster.","Use AWS Glue Data Catalog as the central metadata repository.","Deploy an external Hive metastore using an Amazon Aurora MySQL database instance."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is using a MySQL database as a central metadata store. This database holds Hive records, which serve as the schema for SQL queries run in Amazon Athena and processed on Amazon EMR. The current Hadoop-based environment also runs Apache Ranger on an Amazon EC2 instance to manage cluster policies. The data engineer wants to migrate the metadata store to a solution requiring less operational effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich would meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eThe \u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Data Catalog\u003c/strong\u003e is a central metadata repository, encompassing structural and operational metadata for data assets across various data sources. It provides a consolidated interface for maintaining and accessing information about data formats, schemas, and origins. When running an AWS Glue ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) job, this catalog is employed to understand data details, ensuring the precision of the data transformation process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/abde69738842a7c1753498f206840899d623a33298d617482940fd6f9c96ea25.png\" alt=\"AWS Glue console Setting table properties in Data Catalog\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the scenario, by using AWS Glue Data Catalog as the central metadata repository, you eliminate the need to manage separate metastores or manually configure connections, reducing operational overhead. As a managed service, AWS Glue Data Catalog provides scalability, reliability, and security out of the box, further decreasing the administrative effort required from data engineers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog as the central metadata repository.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eProvision AWS Lake Formation together with EMR\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect because AWS Lake Formation is just used to simplify and secure your data lake. While it integrates with Amazon EMR and provides capabilities for managing metadata, its primary focus is simply on setting up, securing, and managing data lakes rather than serving as a dedicated metadata repository.\u0026nbsp;It\u0027s more about data lake governance and less about being a direct solution for managing Hive metadata specifically.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage the local Hive metastore on an EMR cluster\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This approach ties the metadata management closely to the lifecycle of the EMR cluster. This would require managing the EMR cluster\u0027s scaling, security, and availability, which can increase operational effort rather than reduce it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy an external Hive metastore using an Amazon Aurora MySQL database instance\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While Amazon Aurora offers an improved, managed database solution compared to standard MySQL installations with high availability and scalability, it still requires operational effort in terms of database management, scaling, and maintenance. This option might reduce some operational efforts but does not fully leverage the serverless and integrated capabilities of AWS services designed for metadata management.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/serverless-etl-aws-glue/aws-glue-data-catalog.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/serverless-etl-aws-glue/aws-glue-data-catalog.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-5":{"id":"p:3-5","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 5","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Step Functions to orchestrate AWS Glue jobs from scheduled and event-driven starts, store processed data in Amazon S3, and use OpenSearch Service for full-text search\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Step Functions to orchestrate AWS Glue jobs from scheduled and event-driven starts, store processed data in Amazon S3, and use Amazon RDS for full-text search\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun AWS Glue jobs on a fixed 15-minute schedule for both policy documents and consultant schedules, store all processed records in Amazon S3, and use Athena SQL queries for application search\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue workflows with scheduled and event-based triggers, store processed data in DynamoDB, and build custom full-text search by using Lambda functions\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Step Functions điều phối cả luồng theo lịch và sự kiện; Glue xử lý, S3 lưu đầu ra, OpenSearch đánh chỉ mục tìm kiếm toàn văn. Phù hợp dữ liệu có nhịp cập nhật khác nhau.","RDS có một số khả năng tìm kiếm tùy engine, nhưng OpenSearch chuyên xử lý tìm kiếm tài liệu phức tạp, xếp hạng và chỉ mục trong tình huống này.","Dùng cùng lịch 15 phút cho mọi nguồn không khớp dữ liệu tài liệu cập nhật theo ngày và dữ liệu cần cập nhật nhanh. Athena không phải lớp tìm kiếm toàn văn cho ứng dụng.","DynamoDB với Lambda tự xây full-text search cần nhiều mã và vận hành. OpenSearch cung cấp trực tiếp khả năng tìm kiếm cần thiết."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare advisory company is building a data pipeline for an application that receives the most traffic during clinic business hours. The application must help users search medical policy documents and find available consultants who can answer questions. The medical policy document repository is refreshed once every night.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe application must provide complex full-text search across the policy documents and must show consultant availability from a scheduling database. Consultant availability must reflect schedule changes within 5 minutes. The company wants to use AWS Glue for ETL processing and wants the solution with the least operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-51":{"id":"p:2-51","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 51","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Aurora with a Multi-AZ deployment, add read replicas, and create indexes on SKU, Department, and Manufacturer for catalog queries\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon OpenSearch Serverless to store product documents, index the SKU and secondary attributes, and query all catalog records through search APIs\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse a DynamoDB table with on-demand capacity, store products by SKU as the primary key, and create global secondary indexes for Department and Manufacturer\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Serverless to store product records in relational tables, distribute rows by SKU, and create materialized views for Department and Manufacturer access\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Aurora có thể lưu và đánh chỉ mục nhưng cần thiết kế quan hệ, điều chỉnh khả năng mở rộng. DynamoDB phù hợp hơn kiểu truy cập khóa và thuộc tính sản phẩm biến đổi.","OpenSearch phù hợp tìm kiếm nhưng không phải lựa chọn database vận hành truy xuất item theo khóa được đề ưu tiên.","DynamoDB lưu item có thuộc tính linh hoạt; on-demand thích ứng lưu lượng khó đoán. Khóa SKU cho truy cập chính, GSI cho truy vấn theo Department và Manufacturer.","Redshift dành cho phân tích kho dữ liệu, không phải database catalog phục vụ truy xuất từng sản phẩm với độ trễ rất thấp."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn online marketplace needs to store and query catalog data for millions of products. Each product can have different attributes depending on the product type, such as size, warranty period, material, or supported device model. The application must retrieve product records by a primary identifier named SKU and must also support flexible access patterns by Department and Manufacturer. The workload has unpredictable traffic and must sustain sudden request spikes with single-digit millisecond latency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:52":{"id":"e:2:52","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 52","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Kinesis Firehose with a data transformation Lambda function to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Kinesis Streams having a consumer Lambda function to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Kinesis Streams having a consumer app running on an EC2 instance to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is tasked with managing the real-time ingestion of streaming data into AWS. The solution must have the capability to perform real-time analytics, including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes with high fault tolerance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options fulfills these criteria while minimizing the operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWith Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink, you can transform and analyze streaming data in real time with Apache Flink. Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink takes care of everything required to continuously run streaming applications and scales automatically to match the volume and throughput of your incoming data. Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink supports all operators from Apache Flink that can be used to solve a wide variety of use cases including map, KeyBy, aggregations, windows, joins, and more. Aggregations performs processing across multiple keys such as sum, min, and max. Window Join joins two data streams together on a given key and window.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c994021e76b1ecad390fff6f536e0fa5d0a77bf6e3aa0c9d351a8e0e052d81dc.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c994021e76b1ecad390fff6f536e0fa5d0a77bf6e3aa0c9d351a8e0e052d81dc.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/managed-service-apache-flink/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/managed-service-apache-flink/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the given use case, Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink is the best fit to perform real-time analytics including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes with high fault tolerance, since this solution involves minimal operation overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage Amazon Kinesis Streams having a consumer Lambda function to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes\u003c/strong\u003e - A Kinesis data stream is a set of shards. Each shard contains a sequence of data records. A consumer is an application that processes the data from a Kinesis data stream. You can map a Lambda function to a shared-throughput consumer (standard iterator), or to a dedicated-throughput consumer with enhanced fan-out. For this option, you will need to integrate a Lambda function as a consumer and develop custom code to perform time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes. This represents additional operational overhead, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage Amazon Kinesis Streams having a consumer app running on an EC2 instance to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes\u003c/strong\u003e - A Kinesis data stream is a set of shards. Each shard contains a sequence of data records. A consumer is an application that processes the data from a Kinesis data stream. You can set up a consumer app on an EC2 instance. However, you will need to develop custom code to perform time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes. In addition, you will need to monitor and maintain the EC2 instance. So, this option represents additional operational overhead and hence it is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage Amazon Kinesis Firehose with a data transformation Lambda function to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Kinesis Firehose can only be used for near real-time analysis and NOT real-time analysis, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/managed-service-apache-flink/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/managed-service-apache-flink/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kinesis.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kinesis.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/firehose/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/firehose/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:4":{"id":"e:6:4","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 4","correct":[2],"answers":["Store encryption keys in an AWS CloudHSM cluster. Create an IAM policy to limit access. Encrypt/decrypt objects using CloudHSM for S3 write operations.","Encrypt the records via Server-side Encryption with Customer-provided keys (SSE-C). Limit access to the keys responsible for encryption.","Encrypt the records via Server-side Encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS). Use KMS key policies to define the users who can access the encryption keys.","Encrypt the records via Server-side Encryption with Amazon S3 managed keys (SSE-S3). Create an IAM policy to control access to the S3-managed keys."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer manages a workflow where financial records are continuously uploaded to Amazon S3. These records contain confidential information that needs encryption measures to enhance security. Only a select group of employees must have control over the encryption keys.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow can the data engineer fulfill the requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 provides several methods to encrypt objects stored at rest:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eS3-managed encryption (SSE-S3):\u003c/strong\u003e With this method, Amazon S3 encrypts your data using keys that it manages and controls. This is the default encryption for all new objects. Amazon handles the encryption, decryption, and key management for you.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSSE-C (Server-Side Encryption with Customer-Provided Keys):\u003c/strong\u003e You manage and control the encryption keys, while Amazon S3 performs the encryption and decryption operations on your behalf. You have complete control over the keys, but Amazon handles the encryption/decryption process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eServer-Side Encryption with AWS Key Management Service (SSE-KMS):\u003c/strong\u003e Provides an additional layer of security by integrating with AWS KMS for centralized key management and access controls. KMS encrypts the data keys, which S3 then uses to encrypt the objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eClient-Side Encryption:\u003c/strong\u003e You can encrypt objects client-side before uploading to S3 using libraries like the Amazon S3 Encryption Client. This gives you complete control over the encryption process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f470b7e5c154eaeb36cd4a23645b8c6756e3b0c168edbdec8be3b779e6ceb0b3.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEncrypting files with confidential customer information in Amazon S3 requires a solution that not only secures the data but also allows for precise encryption key control. AWS Key Management Service (KMS) meets these needs by enabling users to create, manage, and control the use of encryption keys across AWS services and their applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS KMS integrates with S3 to support server-side encryption using KMS keys. When uploading data to S3, you specify the KMS key to be used for encryption; S3 then encrypts the file before storing it. KMS key policies primarily control who can access the keys and delineate the permissions regarding what actions they can perform with these keys. Through these policies, the data engineer can precisely delegate which employees can use the keys for encrypting and decrypting the financial records stored in S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eEncrypt the records via Server-side Encryption with AWS KMS keys (SSE-KMS). Use KMS key policies to define the users who can access the encryption keys.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore encryption keys in an AWS CloudHSM cluster. Create an IAM policy to limit access. Encrypt/decrypt objects using CloudHSM for S3 write operations\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;In AWS CloudHSM, you\u0027re responsible for managing the hardware security module that generates and stores your keys. This involves significant operational overhead, including hardware provisioning, scaling, and maintenance. While CloudHSM provides a high level of security, it\u0027s more complex and costly compared to managing keys with AWS KMS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEncrypt the records via Server-side Encryption with Customer-provided keys (SSE-C). Limit access to the keys responsible for encryption\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because SSE-C requires the customer to manage and secure the encryption keys outside of AWS. This increases the complexity and risk, as the customer is responsible for these keys\u0027 secure generation, storage, and rotation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEncrypt the records via Server-side Encryption with Amazon S3 managed keys (SSE-S3). Create an IAM policy to control access to the S3-managed keys\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because SSE-S3 provides a simple and managed encryption solution using Amazon S3 managed keys. However, it does not offer the same level of control or auditing over the encryption keys as SSE-KMS, making it less suitable for scenarios requiring strict access control to encryption keys.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/UsingKMSEncryption.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/UsingKMSEncryption.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/access_policies.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/access_policies.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon S3 Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:4":{"id":"e:5:4","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 4","correct":[1],"answers":["Set up an S3 bucket policy to specifically allow the AWS Glue job to access the S3 bucket.","Ensure that the VPC’s route table has routes for the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint.","Modify the AWS Glue security group to permit inbound traffic from the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint.","Configure the AWS Glue job to use a different AWS region."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is working on a project that involves extracting data from an Amazon S3 bucket, which is accessed through an S3 VPC Gateway endpoint. The engineer’s task is to process this data with an AWS Glue job and then save the processed data in an Amazon Redshift database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDespite setting up the IAM role with the right permissions for the Glue job, the engineer encounters a connection error with Amazon S3 when attempting to execute the job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow can the engineer resolve the issue?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eIn an Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC), a route table contains a set of rules, called routes, that are used to determine where network traffic is directed. Each subnet in your VPC must be associated with a route table; the table controls the traffic for the subnet. A subnet can only be associated with one route table at a time, but you can associate multiple subnets with the same route table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f67330f620c29b11badc8b1b8a32064dbbdb08be254dd02f9cd552a6463cd195.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAn Amazon S3 VPC Gateway Endpoint is a gateway that you specify as a target for a route in your route table for traffic destined to Amazon S3. When you create an endpoint, you can attach an endpoint policy to it that controls access to the service to which you’re connecting. Therefore, ensuring that the VPC’s route table has routes for the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint is crucial.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn practice, you’d have to assign the CIDR ranges for Amazon S3 as a destination in the route table of your VPC. In the AWS Management Console, these are represented by a prefix list, so you won’t have to enumerate them manually. Select the gateway endpoint as the target. This way, traffic destined for S3 will be routed directly through the gateway endpoint, enhancing both security and performance by keeping the traffic within the Amazon network and not exposing it to the public internet.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnsure that the VPC’s route table has routes for the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eModify the AWS Glue security group to permit inbound traffic from the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. AWS Glue does not directly communicate with S3 via security groups. AWS Glue and Amazon S3 communicate over the AWS network, and access is managed through IAM roles and VPC endpoint configurations, not security group settings.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an S3 bucket policy to specifically allow the AWS Glue job to access the S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. The scenario already mentions that the IAM role has the right permissions for the Glue job. This implies that the necessary permissions for accessing S3 from Glue have been granted. The issue at hand is related to the network connectivity (via VPC endpoint) rather than the absence of access permissions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure the AWS Glue job to use a different AWS region\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Changing the AWS region where the AWS Glue job runs would not inherently solve a connectivity issue caused by VPC routing or endpoint configuration mistakes. This action could introduce unnecessary complexity, including data transfer costs and latency\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/VPC_Route_Tables.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/VPC_Route_Tables.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/route-table-options.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/route-table-options.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/vpc-endpoints-s3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/vpc-endpoints-s3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these AWS Glue and Amazon VPC Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-vpc/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-vpc/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:21":{"id":"e:6:21","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 21","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eVACUUM DELETE ONLY\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eVACUUM FULL\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eVACUUM REINDEX\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eVACUUM SORT ONLY\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company needs to optimize the performance of an Amazon Redshift table containing two years of customer purchase data. Currently, the table uses an interleaved sort key on the \u003ccode\u003ecountry_id\u003c/code\u003e column. Due to weekly data additions, the distribution of data has become uneven, leading to slower queries and less available storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich \u003ccode\u003eVACUUM\u003c/code\u003e command will effectively reclaim disk space and improve query performance?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eVACUUM\u003c/strong\u003e is a command in Amazon Redshift that optimizes query performance by reclaiming disk space from deleted or updated rows, rebuilding indexes, and reorganizing tables. The main VACUUM commands are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eVACUUM FULL\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - Reclaims disk space, rebuilds indexes, and re-sorts all rows in the table. This is the default \u003ccode\u003eVACUUM\u003c/code\u003e operation in Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eVACUUM DELETE ONLY\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - Only reclaims disk space occupied by deleted rows without re-sorting data. This is faster than VACUUM FULL but does not optimize query performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eVACUUM REINDEX\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - Analyzes the distribution of values in interleaved sort key columns and performs a full VACUUM operation, including re-sorting. Used when interleaved sort keys are skewed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eVACUUM SORT ONLY\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - This command performs a sort-only operation on the table without reclaiming disk space. It is useful when rows are unsorted, but space is not an issue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift automatically performs \u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eVACUUM DELETE ONLY\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e operations in the background to reclaim space from deleted rows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0f838a54cd3e98ca262afad60a080196b2d34a646e5c4723f77e3c280721c448.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe best VACUUM command for the company to use is \u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eVACUUM REINDEX\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e. This command will re-sort the entire table based on the interleaved sort key, restoring optimal sort order and improving query performance by analyzing and rebalancing the distribution of values in the sort key columns. Since the sort key distribution is significantly uneven/skewed, using REINDEX instead of a standard VACUUM will be more effective. Additionally, VACUUM REINDEX will reclaim disk space from deleted or updated records fulfilling another of the scenario\u0027s requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eVACUUM REINDEX\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eVACUUM FULL\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because, although it reclaims disk space and might improve overall table health, it does not specifically focus on the nuanced needs of interleaved sort key optimization, nor does it offer the most effective solution for analyzing sort keys in skewed data distributions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eVACUUM DELETE ONLY\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect as it is primarily designed to free up space from deleted rows rather than focusing on optimizing sort keys or analyzing their order\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eVACUUM SORT ONLY\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This command will simply re-sort rows within each block but won\u0027t necessarily reclaim disk space.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_VACUUM_command.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_VACUUM_command.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Reclaiming_storage_space202.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Reclaiming_storage_space202.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet: \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:31":{"id":"e:8:31","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 31","correct":[0],"answers":["Use GitHub for version control and use the AWS Glue Studio’s version control feature for syncing changes.","Use GitHub for version control and AWS CodePipeline for continuous integration and delivery.","Use GitLab for version control and AWS CodeBuild for continuous integration and delivery.","Use Bitbucket and AWS CodePipeline for continuous integration and delivery."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA development team is currently managing numerous AWS Glue jobs through the AWS Glue Console. They aim to host the jobs’ source code in a repository to efficiently track code changes. Additionally, they require changes made in the repository to be automatically reflected in the Glue jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following will meet the requirements with the LEAST amount of development overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue is a serverless, scalable data integration service that simplifies the discovery, preparation, and combination of data from various sources for analytics, machine learning, and application development. It allows developers to version control their ETL scripts and workflows with its integration with Git, an open-source version control system.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis integration works for all AWS Glue job types, both visual and code-based, and offers built-in integration with GitHub. It also facilitates the use of automation tools like Jenkins and AWS CodeDeploy to deploy AWS Glue jobs. AWS CodePipeline, on the other hand, is a fully managed continuous delivery service that helps automate the release process, enabling rapid release of new features.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis combination meets all the requirements stated in the scenario, providing version control for ETL scripts and workflows, automating the detection of new code commits, running automated tests, and deploying successful builds to the AWS account.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/758469ad3fc1fe3d2e537a8a51f5f74bb10fa1114eb502cbff9b04bb1d0b5f90.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIf you have remote repositories and want to manage your AWS Glue jobs using your repositories, you can use AWS Glue Studio or the AWS CLI to sync changes to your repositories and your jobs in AWS Glue. When you sync changes this way, you\u0027re pushing the job from AWS Glue Studio to your repository or pulling from the repository to AWS Glue Studio.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWith Git integration in AWS Glue Studio, you can:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cdiv\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Integrate with Git version control systems, such as GitHub, GitLab, and Bitbucket\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Edit AWS Glue jobs in AWS Glue Studio, whether you use visual jobs or script jobs, and sync them to a repository\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Parameterize sources and targets in jobs\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Pull jobs from a repository and edit them in AWS Glue Studio\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Test jobs by pulling from branches and/or pushing to branches utilizing multi-branch workflows in AWS Glue Studio\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Download files from a repository and upload jobs into AWS Glue Studio for cross-account job creation\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Use your automation tool of choice (for example, Jenkins, AWS CodeDeploy, etc.)\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Use GitHub for version control and use the AWS Glue Studio\u0027s version control feature for syncing changes.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;Existing integration between Glue Studio and Git eliminates the need to manage multiple services or learn new tooling. This significantly reduces development time and effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Use GitHub for version control and AWS CodePipeline for continuous integration and delivery\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Although this option addresses the version control requirement with GitHub, it introduces additional layers of complexity. You\u0027d typically need to configure AWS CodePipeline, adding more steps and potential points of failure. It\u0027s more overhead compared to directly using AWS Glue Studio\u0027s built-in feature.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse GitLab for version control and AWS CodeBuild for continuous integration and delivery\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While this solution is possible, it would just require additional configuration to ensure changes made in the repository are automatically reflected in the AWS Glue jobs. This additional configuration might involve scripting or integration efforts to deploy changes to AWS Glue, leading to increased development overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Bitbucket and AWS CodePipeline for continuous integration and delivery\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Similar to the other incorrect option, while this is valid, it may require additional configuration and overhead to deploy the changes made in the repository to AWS Glue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/code-versioning-using-aws-glue-studio-and-github/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/code-versioning-using-aws-glue-studio-and-github/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/edit-job-add-source-control-integration.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/edit-job-add-source-control-integration.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:25":{"id":"e:3:25","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 25","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Athena federated queries to query the RDS for MySQL database, unload historical Redshift tables to Amazon S3 each day, and join both datasets through Athena views\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS DMS to continuously replicate the RDS for MySQL database into Amazon Redshift and run consolidated dashboard queries in Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift federated queries to directly query current-day RDS for MySQL transactions and join the results with existing Redshift historical tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eExport current-day RDS for MySQL data to Amazon S3 on a schedule, load the files into Redshift staging tables, and join the staged data with historical tables\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA grocery delivery company stores current-day order transactions in an Amazon RDS for MySQL database. The company stores historical fulfillment and sales analytics in Amazon Redshift. Reporting teams need daily dashboards that combine live order activity with historical demand patterns, delivery trends, and customer segments. The company needs near real-time insights while minimizing data transfer costs and maintenance overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift federated queries to directly query current-day RDS for MySQL transactions and join the results with existing Redshift historical tables\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift federated queries allow Redshift to query live operational data in Amazon RDS for MySQL. The reporting query can retrieve current-day transactions from the MySQL database and join those rows with historical analytics tables that already exist in Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach supports near real-time insights because the company does not need to wait for a scheduled batch extract, file export, or replication load before current-day transactions become available for reporting. The query can access the latest operational records directly from the RDS database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution also minimizes data transfer costs and maintenance overhead. The company avoids building and operating an incremental ETL pipeline, maintaining staging tables, exporting files to Amazon S3, or continuously copying all current-day transactions into Redshift only to support combined reporting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure AWS DMS to continuously replicate the RDS for MySQL database into Amazon Redshift and run consolidated dashboard queries in Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS DMS can continuously replicate data from an Amazon RDS for MySQL source into Amazon Redshift. This can be useful when the company needs a persistent analytical copy of operational data in the warehouse.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this solution introduces a replication task, target table management, change processing, monitoring, and additional data movement. The requirement emphasizes near real-time insights with minimal transfer cost and maintenance, which makes Redshift federated queries a better fit for joining live MySQL data with existing Redshift history.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Athena federated queries to query the RDS for MySQL database, unload historical Redshift tables to Amazon S3 each day, and join both datasets through Athena views\u003c/strong\u003e - Athena federated queries can query external data sources, and Athena can query data stored in Amazon S3. This can support some cross-source serverless analytics patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option requires unloading Redshift historical data to S3 each day before Athena can join it with MySQL data. That adds extra movement, scheduled data preparation, and maintenance when Redshift federated queries can perform the join directly from the Redshift environment.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eExport current-day RDS for MySQL data to Amazon S3 on a schedule, load the files into Redshift staging tables, and join the staged data with historical tables\u003c/strong\u003e - Exporting MySQL data to Amazon S3 and loading it into Redshift with COPY can support warehouse reporting. This pattern can work for batch analytics when data latency requirements are relaxed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the company needs near real-time insights. Scheduled exports and staging loads introduce delay and operational work. This option also moves data through S3 and Redshift staging tables before users can analyze it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/gsg/federated-query.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/gsg/federated-query.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/getting-started-federated-mysql.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/getting-started-federated-mysql.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/federated-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/federated-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/federated_query_example.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/federated_query_example.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-amazon-redshift-federated-querying-to-amazon-aurora-mysql-and-amazon-rds-for-mysql/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-amazon-redshift-federated-querying-to-amazon-aurora-mysql-and-amazon-rds-for-mysql/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:3":{"id":"e:8:3","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 3","correct":[0],"answers":["Incorporate the S3 bucket as a data lake location within AWS Lake Formation. Utilize the granular security features of Lake Formation, such as row-level security, to enforce the corporation’s access policies effectively.","Establish individual tables for each country’s customer data within the Amazon S3 bucket. Grant access to each data analyst specifically based on the country they are responsible for.","Use AWS Glue to catalog the data in the S3 bucket and set up separate databases for each country. Provide access to each analyst based on the country that the analyst serves.","Import the customer data into Amazon Redshift. Construct a distinct view for each country’s data. Generate separate IAM roles for each country to provide precise access to the respective country’s data. Assign the corresponding roles to the analysts based on their country of service."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multinational corporation has customer data from various countries stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. The data analysts in the team are assigned to specific countries and should only have access to the customer data from their respective countries. The corporation wants to enforce strict access policies to ensure data privacy and compliance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of these options would most efficiently and securely manage and control access to customer data while ensuring compliance with the corporation’s policies?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Lake Formation\u003c/strong\u003e is a fully managed service that helps you build, secure, and manage data lakes, and provide access control for data in the data lake. AWS Lake Formation row-level permissions allow you to provide access to specific rows in a table based on data compliance and governance policies. If you have large tables storing billions of records, you need a way to enable different users and teams to access only the data they are allowed to see. Row-level access control is a simple and performant way to protect data while giving users access to the data they need to perform their job. Lake Formation provides centralized auditing and compliance reporting by identifying which principals accessed what data, when, and through which services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7d9be3a7c775a068740a9aa0c9d13811c6cd1ef49e4ca3bb4d6a533cddfe9a53.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIf you have large fact tables storing billions of records, you need a way to enable different users and teams to access only the data they’re allowed to see. Row-level access control is a simple and performant way to protect data while giving users access to the data they need to perform their jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncorporate the S3 bucket as a data lake location within AWS Lake Formation. Utilize the granular security features of Lake Formation, such as row-level security, to enforce the corporation’s access policies effectively.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEstablish individual tables for each country’s customer data within the Amazon S3 bucket. Grant access to each data analyst specifically based on the country they are responsible for\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it only provides a rudimentary level of access control. While creating separate tables for each country’s data might seem like a good idea, it doesn’t provide the necessary granularity for access control. AWS recommends using IAM policies for access control. Sharing a table with multiple users can lead to data leaks if not managed properly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue to catalog the data in the S3 bucket and set up separate databases for each country. Provide access to each analyst based on the country that the analyst serves \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because it primarily focuses on the physical location of the data, which does not directly relate to access control or compliance with the corporation’s policies. While storing data in AWS Regions close to the customers can improve data access speed and reduce latency, it doesn’t help with access control.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImport the customer data into Amazon Redshift. Construct a distinct view for each country’s data. Generate separate IAM roles for each country to provide precise access to the respective country’s data. Assign the corresponding roles to the analysts based on their country of service\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it simply moves the data to a different service (Amazon Redshift) without providing a comprehensive solution for enforcing access policies at a granular level. While Amazon Redshift allows for the creation of views and the assignment of IAM roles, it doesn’t provide the same level of fine-grained access control as AWS Lake Formation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cbac-tutorial.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cbac-tutorial.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/part-2-effective-data-lakes-using-aws-lake-formation-secure-data-lakes-with-row-level-access-control/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/part-2-effective-data-lakes-using-aws-lake-formation-secure-data-lakes-with-row-level-access-control/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Lake Formation Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:11":{"id":"e:3:11","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 11","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to configure profile jobs, reusable recipes, and rule-based checks, then schedule the jobs for each S3 dataset\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue ETL jobs with DynamicFrames and ResolveChoice to normalize schema variations and detect all quality issues during transformation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew dashboards only, review each dataset manually, and ask analysts to record null counts and outlier patterns\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena scheduled queries to scan each S3 prefix, write SQL expressions for every expected data quality condition, and email query outputs to analysts\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media analytics company receives daily CSV and JSON files in an Amazon S3 bucket from several advertising partners. The incoming files have schema variations across partners and occasionally contain missing values, malformed fields, inconsistent date formats, and statistical outliers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer needs a repeatable process to profile the uploaded files, monitor quality metrics, and apply reusable rule-based checks across multiple datasets. The solution must minimize manual effort and avoid custom validation code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to configure profile jobs, reusable recipes, and rule-based checks, then schedule the jobs for each S3 dataset\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew is designed for visual data preparation, profiling, and repeatable data quality workflows. It can work with datasets in Amazon S3 and can profile data to produce metrics that help identify missing values, inconsistencies, distributions, and outlier patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReusable recipes and rule-based checks help the data engineer standardize quality validation across multiple datasets. This is important because the company receives both CSV and JSON files with schema variations, and the process must be repeatable instead of being recreated manually for every dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eScheduling the DataBrew jobs allows the company to run the profiling and rule-based process every day as new files arrive. This meets the requirement with minimal custom code and less manual effort than building and maintaining validation logic in Spark, Lambda, or SQL.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3617d611129ae12e0d18ad2a679ddaac86cbd797bbe6ffae0e97f2b5cf0f5878.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3617d611129ae12e0d18ad2a679ddaac86cbd797bbe6ffae0e97f2b5cf0f5878.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/setting-up-automated-data-quality-workflows-and-alerts-using-aws-glue-databrew-and-aws-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/setting-up-automated-data-quality-workflows-and-alerts-using-aws-glue-databrew-and-aws-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena scheduled queries to scan each S3 prefix, write SQL expressions for every expected data quality condition, and email query outputs to analysts\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Athena can query CSV and JSON data in Amazon S3 by using SQL. Scheduled queries can support recurring checks when data quality rules are simple and expressed cleanly in SQL.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe issue with this approach is that the team must manually write and maintain SQL validation logic for each dataset and schema variation. It does not provide the same reusable visual profiling and recipe-based data preparation experience as DataBrew.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue ETL jobs with DynamicFrames and ResolveChoice to normalize schema variations and detect all quality issues during transformation\u003c/strong\u003e - DynamicFrames and ResolveChoice can help handle schema ambiguity and type variations in AWS Glue ETL jobs. They are useful when semi-structured data contains fields that Spark DataFrames cannot immediately resolve.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option does not provide a complete repeatable quality profiling process. ResolveChoice helps align schemas, but it does not automatically provide reusable profiling metrics for null values, format inconsistencies, and outliers across multiple datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew dashboards only, review each dataset manually, and ask analysts to record null counts and outlier patterns\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue DataBrew can provide visual profiling information and dashboards that help users understand dataset quality. This can help analysts inspect data characteristics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because the process relies on manual review. The requirement asks for a repeatable rule-based process that scales across datasets with minimal manual effort, not a dashboard-only inspection workflow.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/jobs.profile.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/jobs.profile.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/jobs.recipe.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/jobs.recipe.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.configuration.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.configuration.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/enforce-customized-data-quality-rules-in-aws-glue-databrew/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/enforce-customized-data-quality-rules-in-aws-glue-databrew/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/setting-up-automated-data-quality-workflows-and-alerts-using-aws-glue-databrew-and-aws-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/setting-up-automated-data-quality-workflows-and-alerts-using-aws-glue-databrew-and-aws-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-15":{"id":"p:1-15","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 15","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to copy data from S3 bucket to Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLoad data from Amazon S3 to Amazon Redshift using AWS Glue\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon S3 Event notifications to trigger an AWS Lambda function to copy the data from the S3 bucket to Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 Replication to copy the data from the S3 bucket to Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Spectrum cho phép truy vấn dữ liệu S3 tại chỗ. Nó không phải cơ chế sao chép phần dữ liệu tăng thêm vào bảng Redshift như yêu cầu.","Glue cung cấp quy trình ETL để đọc dữ liệu xuất từ S3, biến đổi nếu cần và nạp vào Redshift. Phù hợp với tác vụ chuyển dữ liệu tăng thêm trong đề.","Tự viết Lambda để chuyển dữ liệu làm tăng công triển khai và bị giới hạn thời gian mỗi lần chạy. Theo tình huống của đề, Glue phù hợp hơn cho quy trình nạp dữ liệu.","S3 Replication sao chép đối tượng giữa các bucket S3. Nó không nạp đối tượng thành các dòng trong cơ sở dữ liệu Redshift."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has integration with a third-party service to export its data to an Amazon S3 bucket. After each export, the incremental data from the S3 bucket needs to be transferred to Amazon Redshift and this data should be available to filter on specific keys.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich AWS service/solution is a good fit to address this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:15":{"id":"e:1:15","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 15","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure a lifecycle policy to transition the objects to Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA) after 7 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure a lifecycle policy to transition the objects to Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after 30 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure a lifecycle policy to transition the objects to Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after 7 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure a lifecycle policy to transition the objects to Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA) after 30 days\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media agency stores its re-creatable assets on Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) buckets. The assets are accessed by a large number of users for the first few days and the frequency of access falls down drastically after a week. Although the assets would be accessed occasionally after the first week, they must continue to be immediately accessible when required. The cost of maintaining all the assets on Amazon S3 storage is turning out to be very expensive and the agency is looking at reducing costs as much as possible.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCan you suggest a way to lower the storage costs while fulfilling the business requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure a lifecycle policy to transition the objects to Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA) after 30 days\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 One Zone-IA is for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. Unlike other S3 Storage Classes which store data in a minimum of three Availability Zones (AZs), Amazon S3 One Zone-IA stores data in a single Availability Zone (AZ) and costs 20% less than Amazon S3 Standard-IA. Amazon S3 One Zone-IA is ideal for customers who want a lower-cost option for infrequently accessed and re-creatable data but do not require the availability and resilience of Amazon S3 Standard or Amazon S3 Standard-IA. The minimum storage duration is 30 days before you can transition objects from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 One Zone-IA.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 One Zone-IA offers the same high durability, high throughput, and low latency of Amazon S3 Standard, with a low per GB storage price and per GB retrieval fee. S3 Storage Classes can be configured at the object level, and a single bucket can contain objects stored across Amazon S3 Standard, Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering, Amazon S3 Standard-IA, and Amazon S3 One Zone-IA. You can also use S3 Lifecycle policies to automatically transition objects between storage classes without any application changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eConstraints for Lifecycle storage class transitions:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f1209c85d38e5f1b8b0761eb18853245c3f992dd591445b9899dd8259b41ef1a.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f1209c85d38e5f1b8b0761eb18853245c3f992dd591445b9899dd8259b41ef1a.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSupported Amazon S3 lifecycle transitions:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e7652e3afc14c21c6ee69edb8464b28f90d891e7d5d39ae32955fc6f1ea68e5e.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e7652e3afc14c21c6ee69edb8464b28f90d891e7d5d39ae32955fc6f1ea68e5e.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure a lifecycle policy to transition the objects to Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after 7 days\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure a lifecycle policy to transition the objects to Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA) after 7 days\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs mentioned earlier, the minimum storage duration is 30 days before you can transition objects from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 One Zone-IA or Amazon S3 Standard-IA, so both these options are added as distractors.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure a lifecycle policy to transition the objects to Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after 30 days\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon S3 Standard-IA is for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. S3 Standard-IA offers the high durability, high throughput, and low latency of Amazon S3 Standard, with a low per GB storage price and per GB retrieval fee. This combination of low cost and high performance makes Amazon S3 Standard-IA ideal for long-term storage, backups, and as a data store for disaster recovery files. However, it costs more than Amazon S3 One Zone-IA because of the redundant storage across Availability Zones (AZs). As the data is re-creatable, so you don\u0027t need to incur this additional cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:40":{"id":"e:3:40","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 40","correct":[0,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Glue ETL job to write the transformed data in the curated zone using a compressed file format\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSetup a lifecycle policy to transition the curated zone data into Glacier Deep Archive after 1 day of object creation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Glue ETL job to write the transformed data in the curated zone using CSV format\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSetup a lifecycle policy to transition the raw zone data into Glacier Deep Archive after 1 day of object creation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lambda function based job to delete the raw zone data after 1 day\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn IT company needs to set up a data lake on Amazon S3 for a healthcare client. The data lake is split into raw and curated zones. For compliance reasons, the source data needs to be kept for a minimum of 5 years. The source data arrives in the raw zone and is then processed via an AWS Glue-based ETL job into the curated zone. The data engineering team runs ad-hoc queries only on the data in the curated zone using Athena. The team is concerned about the cost of data storage in both the raw and curated zones as the data is increasing at a rate of 2 TB daily in each zone.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options would you implement together as the MOST cost-optimal solution? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSetup a lifecycle policy to transition the raw zone data into Glacier Deep Archive after 1 day of object creation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can manage your objects so that they are stored cost-effectively throughout their lifecycle by configuring their Amazon S3 Lifecycle. An S3 Lifecycle configuration is a set of rules that define actions that Amazon S3 applies to a group of objects. For example, you might choose to transition objects to the S3 Standard-IA storage class 30 days after you created them, or archive objects to the S3 Glacier storage class one year after creating them.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the given use-case, the raw zone consists of the source data, so it cannot be deleted due to compliance reasons. Therefore, you should use a lifecycle policy to transition the raw zone data into Glacier Deep Archive after 1 day of object creation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePlease read more about S3 Object Lifecycle Management:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/268818288fea47fd4f152f159efd555f1f7952018074cd7b66e4bce729eb1818.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/268818288fea47fd4f152f159efd555f1f7952018074cd7b66e4bce729eb1818.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Glue ETL job to write the transformed data in the curated zone using a compressed file format\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy for customers to prepare and load their data for analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou cannot transition the curated zone data into Glacier Deep Archive because it is used by business analysts for ad-hoc querying. Therefore, the best optimization is to have the curated zone data stored in a compressed format via the Glue job. The compressed data would reduce the storage cost incurred on the data in the curated zone.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePlease see this example for a Glue ETL Pipeline:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c9d1a7edf053b6a46c235881c47230aa1883d4161bb6773217e91cbf396c6835.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c9d1a7edf053b6a46c235881c47230aa1883d4161bb6773217e91cbf396c6835.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Lambda function-based job to delete the raw zone data after 1 day\u003c/strong\u003e - As mentioned in the use case, the source data needs to be kept for a minimum of 5 years for compliance reasons. Therefore the data in the raw zone cannot be deleted after 1 day.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSetup a lifecycle policy to transition the curated zone data into Glacier Deep Archive after 1 day of object creation\u003c/strong\u003e - You cannot transition the curated zone data into Glacier Deep Archive because it is used by the business analysts for ad-hoc querying. Hence this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Glue ETL job to write the transformed data in the curated zone using CSV format\u003c/strong\u003e - It is cost-optimal to write the data in the curated zone using a compressed format instead of CSV format. The compressed data would reduce the storage cost incurred on the data in the curated zone. So, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-31":{"id":"p:2-31","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 31","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable default encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) encryption (AES-256) for audit logging. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the data for monthly audits\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable default encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) encryption (AES-256) for audit logging. Copy the data into the Amazon Redshift cluster from Amazon S3 when data needs to be queried for monthly audits\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon Server-Side Encryption with KMS keys Stored in AWS Key Management Service (SSE-KMS) for audit logging. Use Amazon QuickSight to query the data for monthly audits\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable encryption on the Amazon S3 bucket that uses Amazon Server-Side Encryption with KMS keys Stored in AWS Key Management Service (SSE-KMS) for audit logging. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the data for monthly audits\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Theo đáp án bộ đề, lưu audit log trên S3 với SSE-S3 rồi dùng Spectrum truy vấn tại chỗ theo tháng. Tránh chi phí nạp lại dữ liệu vào bảng Redshift.","Mã hóa phù hợp nhưng COPY log vào cụm mỗi lần kiểm toán thêm lưu trữ và xử lý. Spectrum có thể truy vấn trực tiếp S3.","Bộ đề chọn SSE-S3 cho cấu hình audit log được mô tả; QuickSight cũng không thay thế đường truy vấn log bằng Spectrum. Không nên suy rộng rằng mọi cấu hình audit hiện tại đều có cùng giới hạn.","Spectrum đúng hướng, nhưng lựa chọn SSE-KMS không khớp khả năng/cấu hình xuất audit log mà lời giải gốc giả định. Đáp án bộ đề sử dụng SSE-S3."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn Amazon Redshift cluster is used to store sensitive information of a business-critical application. The regulatory guidelines mandate tracking audit logs of the Redshift cluster. The business needs to store the audit logs securely by encrypting the logs at rest. The logs are to be stored for a year at least and audits need to be conducted on the audit logs on a monthly basis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following is a cost-effective solution that fulfills the requirement of storing the logs securely while having access to the logs for monthly audits?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:8:26":{"id":"e:8:26","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 26","correct":[0],"answers":["Create a view in the SQL Server on Amazon EC2 to consolidate the required data. Set up an AWS Glue job to pull data from this view, transform it into Parquet format, and store it in an S3 bucket. Schedule this job to run daily using AWS Glue triggers.","Set up a view in the Microsoft SQL Server database using a SQL Server Agent. Initiate an AWS Glue crawler to interpret the view, followed by an AWS Glue job to fetch and relocate the data in Parquet format to S3, scheduling the job for daily execution.","Implement an AWS Lambda function that employs Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) to query the EC2-hosted SQL Server databases, extract the essential data, transform it into Parquet format, and then transfer it to an S3 bucket. Employ Amazon EventBridge to schedule the Lambda function\u0027s daily execution.","Set up AWS Step Functions to orchestrate the data extraction process. Use Amazon Athena to perform the SQL joins and convert the data into Parquet format. Schedule the Step Functions workflow to run daily, and have Athena store the output directly in an S3 bucket."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare company currently hosts its databases on Amazon EC2 Instances, which run the latest version of the Microsoft SQL Server database. The company wants to migrate its database to an Amazon RDS for Microsoft SQL Server.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDuring the course of the migration process, the data analytics team needs to export large datasets, which entail complex SQL joins across multiple tables. The exported data should be stored in Amazon S3 in Apache Parquet columnar storage format.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat should the Data Engineer use to fulfill the requirements in the MOST operationally efficient way?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy for customers to prepare and load their data for analytics. You simply point AWS Glue to your data stored on AWS, and AWS Glue discovers your data and stores the associated metadata (e.g., table definition and schema) in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Once cataloged, your data is immediately searchable, queryable, and available for ETL. AWS Glue is serverless, so there\u0027s no infrastructure to set up or manage, allowing you to focus on your data rather than the resources needed to manage it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f54e4498ed4a25efac096f87e6ae6763c7199df76b74990dcce56dfe7928ce76.png\" alt=\"\"\u003eUsing AWS Glue to extract data from a SQL Server database hosted on Amazon EC2, transform it into Apache Parquet format, and load it into Amazon S3 is an operationally efficient approach. This process leverages AWS Glue\u0027s capabilities to handle complex ETL jobs without the need for extensive infrastructure management. The transformation to Parquet format is particularly beneficial for analytics, as Parquet is optimized for query performance and cost efficiency in analytics workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a view in the SQL Server on Amazon EC2 to consolidate the required data. Set up an AWS Glue job to pull data from this view, transform it into Parquet format, and store it in an S3 bucket. Schedule this job to run daily using AWS Glue triggers.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up a view in the Microsoft SQL Server database using a SQL Server Agent. Initiate an AWS Glue crawler to interpret the view, followed by an AWS Glue job to fetch and relocate the data in Parquet format to S3, scheduling the job for daily execution\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While this option also employs AWS Glue, it introduces an additional step with the Glue crawler, which is designed to catalog data sources and infer schemas. This step could be redundant and add unnecessary complexity. Additionally, the crawling process might introduce delays and additional considerations for schema changes, which could affect the overall operational efficiency compared to the direct use of a Glue ETL job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eImplement an AWS Lambda function that employs Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) to query the EC2-hosted SQL Server databases, extract the essential data, transform it into Parquet format, and then transfer it to an S3 bucket. Employ Amazon EventBridge to schedule the Lambda function\u0027s daily execution\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. Implementing a custom solution with AWS Lambda primarily requires more development effort and introduces more points of potential failure. Lambda functions have time and resource limitations, which might not be ideal for processing large datasets. Additionally, managing the JDBC connections, handling data transformations manually, and ensuring the data is correctly written to S3 in Parquet format add layers of complexity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up AWS Step Functions to orchestrate the data extraction process. Use Amazon Athena to perform the SQL joins and convert the data into Parquet format. Schedule the Step Functions workflow to run daily, and have Athena store the output directly in an S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. This would require copying the data to S3 first, which adds unnecessary complexity and steps. Athena is more suited for querying data already in S3, not for performing joins on data in an EC2-hosted SQL Server.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheatsheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-45":{"id":"p:4-45","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 45","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eRun GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027; and run GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO NAMESPACES \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027 AND \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun GRANT SHARE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027; and run GRANT SHARE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO NAMESPACE \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027; and run GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO NAMESPACE \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Dùng GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE ... TO ACCOUNT cho từng tài khoản consumer. Chạy riêng hai lệnh cấp quyền sử dụng datashare cho hai AWS account như yêu cầu của đề.","Lựa chọn dùng NAMESPACES và AND không đúng cú pháp cho hai account ID. Chia sẻ liên tài khoản phải chỉ định ACCOUNT, không lấy account ID làm namespace ID.","SHARE và USAGE là hai quyền khác nhau. Đề cần cấp quyền sử dụng datashare cho account consumer nên phải dùng GRANT USAGE, không phải GRANT SHARE.","NAMESPACE yêu cầu định danh namespace Redshift, không phải số AWS account. Với consumer thuộc tài khoản khác như câu hỏi, dùng TO ACCOUNT."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics organization has three AWS accounts. A central data platform account owns an Amazon Redshift cluster that publishes a datashare named inventoryshare. Two separate analytics teams in two different AWS accounts need access to the same datashare. A data engineer must grant access by using the Amazon Redshift SQL interface from the producer cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich command or commands will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:8:11":{"id":"e:8:11","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 11","correct":[3],"answers":["AWS Glue","AWS Lambda","Amazon S3 Storage Lens","Amazon S3 Object Lambda"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is developing a new data analysis application. The application needs to retrieve and process data from an Amazon S3 bucket. The data access patterns are complex and vary based on the analysis being performed. The company wants to use a service that allows them to use their own code to process and transform the data as it is being retrieved from Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich AWS service should the company use to meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3 Object Lambda\u003c/strong\u003e allows you to add your own code to Amazon S3 GET, LIST, and HEAD requests to process and modify data as it is being returned to an application. This enables the use of custom code to transform the data returned by these requests in various ways, such as filtering rows, dynamically resizing and watermarking images, or redacting confidential data. This service is powered by AWS Lambda functions, which means your code runs on fully managed AWS infrastructure. This eliminates the need to create and store derivative copies of your data or to run proxies, with no changes required to your applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ce313ec97c10343e28dfc645c0f5750e08f67bb9f0855980949df734d620f748.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eS3 Object Lambda operates by using AWS Lambda functions to automatically process the output of standard S3 \u003ccode\u003eGET\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eLIST\u003c/code\u003e, or \u003ccode\u003eHEAD\u003c/code\u003e requests. You can author and run your own custom Lambda functions, allowing you to tailor the data transformation to your specific use cases. Once you configure a Lambda function, you attach it to an S3 Object Lambda service endpoint, known as an Object Lambda Access Point. When a request is sent to your Object Lambda Access Point, Amazon S3 automatically calls your Lambda function. Any data retrieved using an S3 \u003ccode\u003eGET\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eLIST\u003c/code\u003e, or \u003ccode\u003eHEAD\u003c/code\u003e request through the Object Lambda Access Point is then returned as a transformed result back to the application.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3 Object Lambda.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While AWS Glue does provide capabilities for data transformation, it does not allow for processing data as it is being retrieved from S3. It is more focused on preparing and combining data for analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAWS Lambda\u003c/span\u003e \u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. A Lambda function alone does not provide the functionality to process and transform data during its retrieval from S3 without integrating with other services like S3 Object Lambda.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3 Storage Lens\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because S3 Storage Lens is just a cloud-storage analytics feature that you can use to gain organization-wide visibility into object-storage usage and activity. It does not allow for processing data as it is being retrieved from S3. It’s more focused on providing insights about storage usage and helping you optimize costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/object-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/object-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/transforming-objects.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/transforming-objects.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-amazon-s3-object-lambda-use-your-code-to-process-data-as-it-is-being-retrieved-from-s3/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-amazon-s3-object-lambda-use-your-code-to-process-data-as-it-is-being-retrieved-from-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these Amazon S3 and AWS Lambda Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:36":{"id":"e:2:36","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 36","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure the AWS Glue ETL pipeline to use AWS Glue Data Quality rules. Write rules in Data Quality Definition Language (DQDL) to detect empty files and missing values in required fields, and conditionally write output only when all rules pass\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Step Functions to orchestrate the ETL pipeline, adding a validation state that invokes a custom AWS Lambda function to perform field-level checks before allowing the pipeline to write to the reporting bucket only when all field-level checks are good\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eTrigger an AWS Lambda function after each upload to scan the file for missing transaction records and automatically populate missing fields using historical averages\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eModify the AWS Glue ETL pipeline code in AWS Glue Studio to replace any missing transaction values with the most frequently occurring values for each field before writing output\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company processes daily sales reports uploaded to an Amazon S3 bucket by regional store managers. The company uses a daily AWS Glue ETL pipeline to standardize and aggregate each report. The output is written to a file named SalesReport.csv in a reporting S3 bucket. Occasionally, a store manager uploads an incomplete report with missing transaction records or empty files. When this happens, the company must retain the previous day\u0027s report rather than overwrite it with bad data. A data engineer must ensure the previous day\u0027s report is overwritten only when the new report passes all data quality checks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution meets this requirement with the least effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure the AWS Glue ETL pipeline to use AWS Glue Data Quality rules. Write rules in Data Quality Definition Language (DQDL) to detect empty files and missing values in required fields, and conditionally write output only when all rules pass\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Data Quality with DQDL rules is the most native, least-effort solution for validating data within an existing Glue ETL pipeline. AWS Glue Data Quality is a built-in feature that allows engineers to define validation rules directly inside the Glue pipeline without writing custom code or provisioning additional services. DQDL (Data Quality Definition Language) provides a declarative syntax for expressing rules such as IsComplete \"transaction_id\" or RowCount \u0026gt; 0, which map directly to the business requirements of detecting empty files and missing required fields. This means the engineer does not need to learn a new framework or maintain separate validation infrastructure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen Data Quality rules are configured in the Glue pipeline, the pipeline can be set to conditionally route data based on whether records pass or fail validation. Records that fail quality checks can be sent to a separate error path, while only fully validated data proceeds to overwrite the SalesReport.csv in the reporting bucket. This conditional branching is natively supported in AWS Glue Studio\u0027s visual interface, requiring minimal code changes to the existing pipeline. AWS documentation confirms that Glue Data Quality supports both row-level and dataset-level rules, making it suitable for catching both empty files and field-level missing values in a single configuration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe key advantage of this approach is that it operates entirely within the existing Glue ETL pipeline, requiring no additional AWS services, no custom Lambda code, and no changes to surrounding infrastructure. The data engineer simply adds a Data Quality node to the existing pipeline in Glue Studio and defines the DQDL rules. This satisfies the requirement of least effort while directly addressing the two failure conditions: empty files and missing required fields.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/da308d9457d08497d3cf3702f78cca63278858d7e2d4fcbcccdd6d42bcd02222.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/da308d9457d08497d3cf3702f78cca63278858d7e2d4fcbcccdd6d42bcd02222.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/22664b41dd3a28d76f36b306b21a03e68fde81994ef37b2865da686f0b2c7ba3.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/22664b41dd3a28d76f36b306b21a03e68fde81994ef37b2865da686f0b2c7ba3.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/enable-strategic-data-quality-management-with-aws-glue-dqdl-labels/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/enable-strategic-data-quality-management-with-aws-glue-dqdl-labels/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Step Functions to orchestrate the ETL pipeline, adding a validation state that invokes a custom AWS Lambda function to perform field-level checks before allowing the pipeline to write to the reporting bucket only when all field-level checks are good\u003c/strong\u003e - Orchestrating the pipeline with Step Functions adds significant architectural complexity. The engineer would need to define a new Step Functions state machine, integrate it with the existing Glue ETL job, write a custom Lambda validation function, handle retry logic and failure states, and manage IAM permissions across multiple services. While this is a valid enterprise-grade pattern for complex workflows, it represents substantial additional effort compared to simply adding a Data Quality node inside the existing Glue pipeline. Step Functions is better suited for multi-service workflows with complex branching logic, not for straightforward data validation within a single ETL job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTrigger an AWS Lambda function after each upload to scan the file for missing transaction records and automatically populate missing fields using historical averages\u003c/strong\u003e - While Lambda can be used to validate files after upload, this approach introduces significant additional effort compared to using native Glue Data Quality. The engineer would need to write, test, and deploy a custom Lambda function with logic to detect missing fields, compute historical averages for backfilling, and conditionally trigger or suppress the downstream ETL pipeline. This requires managing Lambda permissions, S3 event triggers, error handling, and potentially additional state management to track the previous day\u0027s file. None of this infrastructure exists today, making the setup overhead considerable.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eModify the AWS Glue ETL pipeline code in AWS Glue Studio to replace any missing transaction values with the most frequently occurring values for each field before writing output\u003c/strong\u003e - This option fundamentally misunderstands the requirement. The business rule states that when data is missing, the previous day\u0027s file should be preserved - not that missing values should be fabricated or imputed. Replacing missing transaction values with the most common values would corrupt the report with inaccurate data, violating data integrity. Additionally, modifying ETL transformation code to handle imputation adds development and testing effort that is unnecessary given that Glue Data Quality already provides a purpose-built validation mechanism.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/enable-strategic-data-quality-management-with-aws-glue-dqdl-labels/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/enable-strategic-data-quality-management-with-aws-glue-dqdl-labels/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:54":{"id":"e:1:54","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 54","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCheck the Step Functions TaskFailed event records in the /aws/vendedlogs/states log group\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCheck the Lambda function logs in the /aws/lambda/normalizeRecords log group\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCheck the Step Functions TaskSucceeded event records in the /aws/vendedlogs/states log group\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCheck the AWS X-Ray trace segment logs in the /aws/xray/normalizeRecords log group because X-Ray stores the Lambda runtime exception details and Step Functions state transition metadata together\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial analytics company uses AWS Step Functions to orchestrate a serverless data preparation pipeline. The company configured the Step Functions state machine to send logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs with the log level set to FATAL. The company also enabled logging for every AWS service that the pipeline invokes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA state named \"normalizeRecords\" invokes an AWS Lambda function named \"normalizeRecords\". The Lambda function validates and normalizes incoming transaction records before the workflow continues to the next state. The operations team needs to find detailed error information if an error occurs inside the Lambda function during normalization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich CloudWatch Logs log group should the operations team check to find the error details?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck the Lambda function logs in the /aws/lambda/normalizeRecords log group\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lambda automatically sends function invocation logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs when the Lambda execution role has the required logging permissions. By default, Lambda writes these logs to a log group that uses the naming pattern /aws/lambda/\u0026lt;function-name\u0026gt;. Because the function name is normalizeRecords, the relevant log group is /aws/lambda/normalizeRecords.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe detailed runtime error information for the Lambda function, such as exception messages, stack traces, custom application log statements, timeout messages, and START, END, and REPORT lines, appears in the Lambda function log stream. These details help the operations team troubleshoot the actual preprocessing or normalization failure inside the Lambda code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe Step Functions state machine can show that a task failed, but the state machine log configuration controls workflow execution history, not the full application-level logs from the invoked Lambda function. Since the state machine log level is set to FATAL, Step Functions logging is even less useful for task-level details. The team should check the Lambda log group for the function-level error details.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3cf23f4d08a65f3b39956bce43445e16afbabb9a61345582f4ae5e95e78914ab.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3cf23f4d08a65f3b39956bce43445e16afbabb9a61345582f4ae5e95e78914ab.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/operating-lambda-logging-and-custom-metrics/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/operating-lambda-logging-and-custom-metrics/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1ec0cc6167bf58ceb4cb39fc40249e38df8df9fa510600794cfe50c985672590.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1ec0cc6167bf58ceb4cb39fc40249e38df8df9fa510600794cfe50c985672590.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/operating-lambda-logging-and-custom-metrics/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/operating-lambda-logging-and-custom-metrics/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck the Step Functions TaskFailed event records in the /aws/vendedlogs/states log group\u003c/strong\u003e - The /aws/vendedlogs/states log group is commonly used for Step Functions execution history logs when CloudWatch Logs logging is configured. However, Step Functions logs provide workflow execution events, not the complete Lambda runtime logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option is also weakened by the FATAL log level. A TaskFailed event is not the right place to look for detailed Lambda exception output when the operations team needs the function-level error details.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck the AWS X-Ray trace segment logs in the /aws/xray/normalizeRecords log group because X-Ray stores the Lambda runtime exception details and Step Functions state transition metadata together\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS X-Ray can provide distributed tracing details and can help visualize service interactions and latency. However, X-Ray does not create a CloudWatch Logs log group named /aws/xray/normalizeRecords for Lambda runtime logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eX-Ray traces are useful for tracing request paths, but they are not the primary location for Lambda application logs, stack traces, and custom error output. The operations team should use the Lambda function\u0027s CloudWatch Logs log group.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck the Step Functions TaskSucceeded event records in the /aws/vendedlogs/states log group\u003c/strong\u003e - A TaskSucceeded event indicates that a Step Functions task completed successfully. It is not relevant when the team needs to troubleshoot an error during Lambda-based preprocessing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eEven if Step Functions logs include state transition events, a succeeded event would not contain the failure details. The operations team needs the Lambda function logs that capture the runtime behavior of the normalizeRecords function.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/monitoring-cloudwatchlogs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/monitoring-cloudwatchlogs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/monitoring-cloudwatchlogs-view.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/monitoring-cloudwatchlogs-view.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/cw-logs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/cw-logs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/apireference/API_LoggingConfiguration.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/apireference/API_LoggingConfiguration.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/operating-lambda-logging-and-custom-metrics/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/operating-lambda-logging-and-custom-metrics/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-62":{"id":"p:2-62","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 62","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate Amazon CloudFront distribution for the application on Amazon EC2 instances. Deploy AWS WAF on Amazon CloudFront to provide the necessary safety measures\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an Application Load Balancer (ALB) to balance the workload for all the Amazon EC2 instances. Configure Amazon CloudFront to distribute from an Application Load Balancer since AWS WAF cannot be directly configured on ALB. This configuration not only provides necessary safety but is scalable too\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS WAF can be directly configured on Amazon EC2 instances for ensuring the security of the underlying application data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS WAF can be directly configured only on an Application Load Balancer or an Amazon API Gateway. One of these two services can then be configured with Amazon EC2 to build the needed secure architecture\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["CloudFront đặt trước ứng dụng và gắn WAF để kiểm soát yêu cầu web. Đây là tích hợp hợp lệ; WAF không bảo đảm chặn mọi loại tấn công như cách diễn đạt tuyệt đối trong đề.","Lý do được đưa ra là sai: WAF có thể gắn trực tiếp với ALB được hỗ trợ. Không bắt buộc có CloudFront chỉ vì ALB không hỗ trợ WAF.","Không gắn WAF trực tiếp vào EC2 instance. Cần tài nguyên đầu vào được hỗ trợ như CloudFront hoặc ALB.","Khẳng định chỉ có ALB và API Gateway là sai vì CloudFront cũng được hỗ trợ. Vì vậy lựa chọn này bỏ sót tích hợp hợp lệ."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn application hosted on Amazon EC2 contains sensitive personal information about all its customers and needs to be protected from all types of cyber-attacks. The company is considering using the AWS Web Application Firewall (AWS WAF) to handle this requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCan you identify the correct solution leveraging the capabilities of AWS WAF?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:7:11":{"id":"e:7:11","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 11","correct":[1],"answers":["Configure AWS Data Pipeline to orchestrate data movement and processing tasks, leveraging PySpark in AWS Lambda for ETL operations.","Utilize AWS Glue crawlers for schema detection and AWS Glue Spark jobs for data transformation.","Create an Amazon Redshift cluster as a staging area. Use a stored procedure for schema identification and data transformation.","Develop a solution that incorporates Amazon Athena for schema detection, executing ETL tasks through SQL queries against S3 data."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is designing an ETL workflow that would export 5 TB of data to Amazon S3 every day. The data is sourced from a variety of platforms, including Oracle DB, Cassandra, Amazon Aurora, and Apache Kafka, with the schemas being frequently updated.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo address this, the data engineer needs a robust solution capable of schema detection for these heterogeneous data sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution has the LEAST operational overhead for this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy for customers to prepare and load data for analytics. It provides capabilities for schema detection, ETL jobs, and crawlers that make it useful for working with dynamic or changing schemas.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue crawlers\u003c/strong\u003e automatically detect schemas for data sources. Glue crawlers scan data sources, detect schemas, and store the associated metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. This enables Glue to handle changing schemas without requiring code changes. The crawlers run periodically to detect new or changed data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue jobs\u003c/strong\u003e can then be used to process the data. Glue jobs are defined using Python or Scala scripts that reference the schemas and metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. This allows Glue jobs to work with changing schemas without needing redeploys as the underlying data changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e9fd631757956c26ca9462f60441fbfaaa134b67786ddbd2493d33fc98b4473f.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eGiven the requirements for schema detection, ETL capabilities, and the need to handle dynamic schemas, AWS Glue stands out as the most suitable option. It offers a managed environment that significantly reduces the operational overhead of managing infrastructure for ETL processes. Glue Crawlers automatically detect and adapt to schema changes, ensuring that the ETL process remains robust against evolving data structures. Glue Jobs then allow for the transformation and loading of data into Amazon S3 within the required timeframe.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize AWS Glue crawlers for schema detection and AWS Glue Spark jobs for data transformation.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure AWS Data Pipeline to orchestrate data movement and processing tasks, leveraging PySpark in AWS Lambda for ETL operations\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. AWS Data Pipeline is a web service for orchestrating data movements and processing tasks. While it can integrate with AWS Lambda for custom processing logic, using PySpark within Lambda poses limitations on execution time and resource allocation, which may not be suitable for processing 5 TB of data daily.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon Redshift cluster as a staging area. Use a stored procedure for schema identification and data transformation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Using Amazon Redshift as a staging area would require significant engineering effort due to the necessity of adapting disparate data formats, schemas, and types to Redshift\u0027s structured, columnar storage system. Moreover, a stored procedure is just a set of SQL statements saved together as a single executable entity. While it can execute complex logic, it\u0027s not inherently designed for dynamic schema detection across diverse data sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDevelop a solution that incorporates Amazon Athena for schema detection, executing ETL tasks through SQL queries against S3 data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Athena is primarily a query service that does not inherently provide ETL capabilities or schema detection in the manner required for this scenario. It is designed for querying data directly in S3 using SQL, not for the ETL process itself.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-41":{"id":"p:1-41","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 41","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a CloudWatch Logs metric filter to match audit-relevant log events, configure a CloudWatch alarm on the generated metric, and use the alarm action to deliver the matching log records to the S3 bucket through Amazon Data Firehose\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a subscription filter in the CloudWatch Logs log group and configure the filter to monitor the Amazon Data Firehose delivery stream. Configure Firehose to invoke an AWS Lambda function for format conversion\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a subscription filter in the CloudWatch Logs log group that sends matching events to the Amazon Data Firehose delivery stream. Configure Firehose to invoke an AWS Lambda function to transform records before S3 delivery\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eTag the CloudWatch Logs log groups that contain audit events, configure Amazon Data Firehose to ingest only tagged log groups, and configure Firehose to write the output in the audit team\u0027s required format\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Metric filter chuyển các kết quả khớp thành số liệu, không chuyển tiếp nguyên bản log. Alarm cũng không phải đường truyền các bản ghi log qua Firehose.","Subscription filter cần gửi sự kiện từ log group tới Firehose. Mô tả dùng filter để giám sát delivery stream đã đảo sai vai trò và hướng dữ liệu.","Subscription filter chọn các log cần thiết và gửi tới Firehose. Lambda transformation chuẩn hóa từng bản ghi trước khi giao cho S3, phù hợp nhu cầu phân tích.","Tag chỉ là metadata quản lý tài nguyên, không tự thiết lập cơ chế Firehose lấy log từ các log group. Cần subscription filter để chuyển log."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company is building a centralized audit log pipeline by using Amazon Data Firehose. The pipeline must stream selected Amazon CloudWatch Logs events from application log groups to an Amazon S3 bucket. The audit team needs only security-relevant log events, and the delivered records must use a structure that its analysis tools can read reliably. The company must keep the pipeline performant as log volume grows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements and maintain reliable performance?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:63":{"id":"e:1:63","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 63","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with the default retention period. Implement idempotent consumers that store checkpoints in Amazon DynamoDB. Use one shared processing path that applies conditional logic for replay events and live events\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with extended retention. Implement idempotent consumers that use Amazon RDS checkpointing. Use one shared processing path for replay events and live events\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with extended retention and create a second consumer application that starts from a timestamp inside the retention window. Use the same output tables as the live consumer and leverage the stream sequence number to prevent duplicate analytical records\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with extended retention. Implement idempotent consumers that use Amazon DynamoDB checkpointing. Use separate processing paths for replay events and live events\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA gaming analytics company streams player interaction events from a mobile application to AWS. The company wants to prevent duplicate records in downstream engagement reports. The data engineering team must be able to replay up to 5 days of historical events after a processing defect is discovered. The replay process must not disrupt the pipeline that continues to process live events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with extended retention. Implement idempotent consumers that use Amazon DynamoDB checkpointing. Use separate processing paths for replay events and live events\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams can retain records beyond the default 24-hour period. The retention period can be increased up to 365 days, which allows consumers to reprocess records from a previous point in time when the team needs to correct downstream analytics or recover from a processing defect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe following is an example of changing the retention period using the AWS CLI:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eaws kinesis increase-stream-retention-period --stream-name retentionPeriodDemo --retention-period-hours 72\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIdempotent consumers help prevent duplicate output when the same events are processed more than once. Amazon DynamoDB is a strong fit for checkpointing and deduplication state because it provides low-latency key-value access at scale, and the Kinesis Client Library also uses DynamoDB tables to track consumer application metadata such as leases and coordination state.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSeparate processing paths for replay events and live events reduce the risk that historical reprocessing will interfere with current real-time processing. This design lets the replay workload use its own consumer application, checkpoint state, scaling configuration, and output handling while the live pipeline continues processing current events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with the default retention period. Implement idempotent consumers that store checkpoints in Amazon DynamoDB. Use one shared processing path that applies conditional logic for replay events and live events\u003c/strong\u003e - The default retention period for Kinesis Data Streams is 24 hours. A pipeline that must replay up to 5 days of historical events needs extended retention because the required replay window exceeds the default retention period.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also uses a shared processing path for live and replay traffic. Sharing the path increases the risk that a historical replay will affect current processing latency, checkpoint progress, or downstream write behavior.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with extended retention and create a second consumer application that starts from a timestamp inside the retention window. Use the same output tables as the live consumer and leverage the stream sequence number to prevent duplicate analytical records\u003c/strong\u003e - Extended retention and a second consumer application can support historical replay without blocking the live consumer. However, relying only on the Kinesis sequence number to prevent duplicate analytical records is not sufficient in most analytics pipelines.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA sequence number identifies a record within a stream shard context, but downstream duplicate prevention should use an idempotency key or durable deduplication state that aligns with the business event. This option lacks a reliable checkpointing and idempotent output design for duplicate-free reprocessing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with extended retention. Implement idempotent consumers that use Amazon RDS checkpointing. Use one shared processing path for replay events and live events\u003c/strong\u003e - Extended retention satisfies the historical replay requirement, and checkpointing can help consumers resume processing. However, using Amazon RDS for checkpointing adds more relational database management overhead than necessary for this streaming coordination pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also uses one shared processing path for replay and live data. That design can cause the replay workload to compete with real-time processing and can make duplicate prevention, checkpoint management, and operational isolation harder.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/kinesis-extended-retention.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/kinesis-extended-retention.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kinesis/latest/APIReference/API_IncreaseStreamRetentionPeriod.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/kinesis/latest/APIReference/API_IncreaseStreamRetentionPeriod.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/kcl-dynamoDB.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/kcl-dynamoDB.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/enhanced-consumers.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/enhanced-consumers.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/retaining-data-streams-up-to-one-year-with-amazon-kinesis-data-streams/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/retaining-data-streams-up-to-one-year-with-amazon-kinesis-data-streams/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:17":{"id":"e:6:17","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 17","correct":[1],"answers":["Create an Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose stream using Kinesis Data Streams as the source and Amazon Redshift as the destination.","Stream the data to the Amazon Redshift database using Amazon Redshift Streaming Ingestion.","\u003cspan\u003eIngest the data to Amazon Redshift by first staging the data in Amazon S3 and using the \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e command.\u003c/span\u003e","Ingest the data into an Amazon Aurora database and use the zero-ETL integration to sync it with the Amazon Redshift database."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA biomedical company has various medical devices that send health metrics to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe company wants to analyze current versus historical data and display the insights on a dashboard in near real-time. To achieve this, a data engineer must set up a pipeline that stores incoming data streams in an Amazon Redshift Serverless data warehouse.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will fulfill these requirements with LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams\u003c/strong\u003e is a fully managed service that can continuously capture and store terabytes of data per hour from hundreds of thousands of sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshift Streaming Ingestion\u003c/strong\u003e allows ingesting streaming data from Kinesis data streams into an Amazon Redshift data warehouse in near real-time for analytics. It can ingest hundreds of megabytes of data per second with low latency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c4bf798fd9f74674d11fae992ca8ff60da944a9dbec7c78abf6ee8bbc45c61c9.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBy utilizing Amazon Redshift\u0027s Streaming Ingestion, the company can directly ingest data from Amazon Kinesis Data Streams into an Amazon Redshift materialized view, bypassing the need for additional data processing layers or staging areas like S3 and directly ingesting data into Redshift. For the given scenario of streaming biomedical equipment data, the steps would be:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eStream the data from biomedical equipment into an Amazon Kinesis data stream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDefine an external schema in Redshift pointing to the Kinesis data stream using \u003ccode\u003eCREATE EXTERNAL SCHEMA\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eCreate a materialized view to consume the streaming data from the external schema using \u003ccode\u003eCREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePeriodically refresh the materialized view using \u003ccode\u003eREFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW\u003c/code\u003e to load new data from the stream into Redshift tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis enables near real-time analytics on the streaming biomedical data as it continuously gets ingested into Redshift with low latency using streaming ingestion. The data engineer can then run queries on both the incoming streaming data as well as historical data for analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStream the data to the Amazon Redshift database using Amazon Redshift Streaming Ingestion.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose stream using Kinesis Data Streams as the source and Amazon Redshift as the destination\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While Kinesis Data Firehose supports data streaming into Amazon Redshift, it involves staging data in Amazon S3 before loading it into Redshift, which can introduce additional latency compared to direct streaming ingestion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIngest the data to Amazon Redshift by first staging the data in Amazon S3 and using the \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e command\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This method requires data to be first stored in S3, followed by a batch loading process using the COPY command. It\u0027s less efficient for real-time data processing needs due to the delay in batch processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIngest the data into an Amazon Aurora database and use the zero-ETL integration to sync it with the Amazon Redshift database\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Although Amazon Aurora offers zero-ETL integration with Amazon Redshift, it is not directly related to streaming data ingestion from Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Moreover, this approach leads to extra operational overhead from unnecessarily managing two databases.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion-getting-started.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion-getting-started.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these Cheat Sheets for Amazon Kinesis and Amazon Redshift:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-38":{"id":"p:1-38","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 38","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSchedule an AWS Glue job to read interaction log files from Amazon S3 every 15 minutes, transform the records, and load the processed results into Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose to collect viewer events, configure buffering before delivery, transform batches with AWS Lambda, and load the data into Amazon S3 before a scheduled Amazon Redshift COPY operation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDeploy an Amazon MSK cluster, run self-managed consumer applications on Amazon EC2 to process viewer events in real time, and integrate the processed output with Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest viewer events, process the stream records with AWS Lambda functions, and store the processed results in Amazon Redshift for analytics\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Chạy ETL mỗi 15 phút là xử lý theo lô, tạo độ trễ không phù hợp phân tích tương tác gần thời gian thực.","Firehose đệm dữ liệu rồi thêm bước S3 và COPY theo lịch. Chuỗi đó tăng độ trễ so với xử lý trực tiếp luồng Kinesis.","MSK với consumer tự quản lý trên EC2 có thể xử lý thời gian thực, nhưng tốn công vận hành hơn giải pháp Kinesis và Lambda.","Kinesis lưu luồng bền vững để consumer khôi phục, Lambda xử lý sự kiện và kết quả được đưa vào Redshift. Phù hợp thiết kế có độ trễ thấp với dịch vụ được quản lý."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA sports media company is launching a real-time engagement analytics platform for its live streaming website and mobile application. The platform must ingest millions of viewer events, such as play, pause, search, and subscription actions, with minimal latency during major tournament broadcasts. The solution must scale automatically, persist event data durably so that consumers can recover from processing interruptions, and deliver processed results to Amazon Redshift for analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements in the MOST cost-effective way?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-13":{"id":"p:1-13","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 13","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eChange the data format from comma-separated text files to JSON format. Apply Snappy compression\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the data format from comma-separated text files to Apache Parquet. Compress the files using Snappy compression\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the data format from comma-separated text files to Apache ORC\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the data format from comma-separated text files to ZIP format\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["JSON không lưu theo cột. Dù nén Snappy, Athena vẫn không có lợi thế đọc riêng các cột cần thiết như với Parquet.","Parquet lưu theo cột, phù hợp với các truy vấn chỉ chọn một số cột. Nén Snappy giảm kích thước dữ liệu và hỗ trợ đọc nhanh, giải quyết cả hai vấn đề của đề.","ORC cũng là định dạng cột có thể phù hợp cho Athena. Tuy nhiên, phương án này không nêu bước nén; theo lời giải của bộ đề, Parquet kết hợp Snappy đáp ứng đầy đủ hơn.","ZIP là định dạng đóng gói/nén không được Athena hỗ trợ trực tiếp theo phương án đã nêu. Không thể chỉ đổi CSV sang ZIP để tối ưu truy vấn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer has been tasked to optimize Amazon Athena queries that are underperforming. Upon analysis, the data engineer realized that the files queried by Athena were not compressed and just stored as .csv files. The data engineer also noticed that users perform most queries by selecting a specific column.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat do you recommend to improve the query performance?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:20":{"id":"e:3:20","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 20","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eThe nightly data refreshes left the dashboard tables in need of a vacuum operation that could not be automatically performed by Amazon Redshift due to ongoing user workloads\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe Redshift cluster is undersized for the queries being executed by the dashboards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe nightly data refreshes are causing the queries to hang during the business hours\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eInefficient SQL queries are being run by the dashboards\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company recently migrated to a data lake design based on Amazon S3. Amazon Redshift and Amazon QuickSight are being used by the company\u0027s data engineering team to analyze data for better insights. To ensure access to the most up-to-date actionable data, the team has now shifted to a nightly Amazon Redshift refresh utilizing terabytes of the previous day\u0027s changes. The team has noticed that post the switchover to nightly refresh, several popular dashboards that had good performance earlier, are now seeing degraded performance during business hours as well. Amazon CloudWatch shows no notifications regarding the performance metrics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents the MOST LIKELY cause for this issue?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe nightly data refreshes left the dashboard tables in need of a vacuum operation that could not be automatically performed by Amazon Redshift due to ongoing user workloads\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe Redshift vacuum operation re-sorts rows and reclaims space in either a specified table or all tables in the current database. Amazon Redshift does not reclaim free space automatically. Such available space is created whenever you delete or update rows on a table. This process is a design choice inherited from PostgreSQL and a routine maintenance process that you need to follow for your tables to maximize the performance of your Redshift cluster. By running a vacuum command on one of your tables, you can reclaim any free space that is the result of delete and update operations. At the same time, the data of the table gets sorted. Thus, you end up with a compact and sorted table, which is useful for the performance of your cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7a346a5bbc95e673658721b269363543a7901b4d366ea38dd69b5c583b26d6c1.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7a346a5bbc95e673658721b269363543a7901b4d366ea38dd69b5c583b26d6c1.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_VACUUM_command.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_VACUUM_command.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou should note that the automatic vacuum operations can pause if the cluster experiences a period of high load. So, in case the cluster continues to see consistently high loads during the nightly refresh, the vacuum operations won\u0027t complete, thereby causing persistent performance degradation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/dbd71f0b61354371ac016dd74586fa7552eeb47ef0c2bfc4d4cbedae3d7b9e51.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/dbd71f0b61354371ac016dd74586fa7552eeb47ef0c2bfc4d4cbedae3d7b9e51.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_VACUUM_command.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_VACUUM_command.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe nightly data refreshes are causing the queries to hang during the business hours\u003c/strong\u003e - The nightly data refreshes cannot cause the queries to hang during the business hours since these refreshes are carried out via ETL steps that could use long-running queries such as a COPY command. This can only cause the queries to hang for the duration of the nightly refresh and not during business hours.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6787eaf13fe74908f1d1f3510b78e9778be35fc40a5c390d17ae9ade4105791b.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6787eaf13fe74908f1d1f3510b78e9778be35fc40a5c390d17ae9ade4105791b.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/queries-troubleshooting.html#queries-troubleshooting-query-hangs\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/queries-troubleshooting.html#queries-troubleshooting-query-hangs\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eInefficient SQL queries are being run by the dashboards\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe Redshift cluster is undersized for the queries being executed by the dashboards\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThese two options have been added as distractors. Had inefficient SQL queries or an undersized Redshift cluster been the reasons behind the cluster\u0027s performance deterioration, these would have impacted the cluster even before the switchover to the nightly refresh process. Therefore, both these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_VACUUM_command.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_VACUUM_command.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2018/12/amazon-redshift-automatic-vacuum/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2018/12/amazon-redshift-automatic-vacuum/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/queries-troubleshooting.html#queries-troubleshooting-query-hangs\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/queries-troubleshooting.html#queries-troubleshooting-query-hangs\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-3":{"id":"p:2-3","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 3","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Kinesis Streams having a consumer app running on an EC2 instance to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Kinesis Firehose with a data transformation Lambda function to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Kinesis Streams having a consumer Lambda function to conduct real-time data analysis including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Consumer trên EC2 có thể tổng hợp nhưng phải tự xây và vận hành trạng thái, checkpoint, xử lý lỗi và máy chủ.","Lambda transformation của Firehose không phải bộ xử lý cửa sổ trạng thái liên tục dài 45 phút. Cần công cụ stream processing phù hợp.","Flink hỗ trợ cửa sổ thời gian, giữ trạng thái và khôi phục bằng checkpoint. Dịch vụ được quản lý giảm công vận hành cho tổng hợp thời gian thực.","Lambda cần tự xây cơ chế giữ trạng thái qua các lần chạy cho cửa sổ dài. Không phù hợp bằng Flink khi ưu tiên ít công quản lý và chịu lỗi."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is tasked with managing the real-time ingestion of streaming data into AWS. The solution must have the capability to perform real-time analytics, including time-based aggregations over periods of up to 45 minutes with high fault tolerance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options fulfills these criteria while minimizing the operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:59":{"id":"e:5:59","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 59","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eGet the query information from the \u003ccode\u003eSTL_USAGE_CONTROL\u003c/code\u003e table view.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eGet the query information from the \u003ccode\u003eSTL_WLM_QUERY\u003c/code\u003e table view.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eGet the query information from the \u003ccode\u003eSTL_ALERT_EVENT_LOG\u003c/code\u003e table view.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eGet the query information from the \u003ccode\u003eSTL_QUERY_METRICS\u003c/code\u003e table view.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team is tasked with improving the performance and reliability of their Amazon Redshift database. During their analysis, they want to focus on identifying and resolving issues that could potentially lead to query failures, suboptimal query performance, or any other alerts that might indicate problematic database operations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following Redshift system table views would be the most suitable for the given scenario?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eRecords an alert when the query optimizer identifies conditions that might indicate performance issues. Use the STL_ALERT_EVENT_LOG view to identify opportunities to improve query performance. A query consists of multiple segments, and each segment consists of one or more steps.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d8a436ed56ad102509c09cf2b3e8483ead89f5b5c19c9fac1e20d52b4f7e16e8.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSTL_ALERT_EVENT_LOG is visible to all users. Superusers can see all rows; regular users can see only their own data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use the STL_ALERT_EVENT_LOG to identify potential issues in your queries, then follow the practices in\u0026nbsp;Tuning query performance\u0026nbsp;to optimize your database design and rewrite your queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eGet the query information from the \u003ccode\u003eSTL_ALERT_EVENT_LOG\u003c/code\u003e table view.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;Get the query information from the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eSTL_WLM_QUERY\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;table view\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because it primarily records information about query execution such as time spent on the CPU, number of rows read or written, and disk space used for spills. While it can be useful for performance tuning, it does not directly provide information about alerts or events that might indicate problematic database operations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;Get the query information from the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eSTL_USAGE_CONTROL\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;table view\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because it only logs changes in the WLM configuration and when queries are paused or canceled due to WLM rules. It can be useful for understanding how WLM configurations affect query execution, but it does not directly log alert events or issues that could lead to query failures.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Get the query information from the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eSTL_QUERY_METRICS\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;table view\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because it simply provides a snapshot of the current query queue. It can be useful for understanding the current load on the system, but it does not directly provide information about alerts or events that might indicate problematic database operations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_STL_ALERT_EVENT_LOG.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_STL_ALERT_EVENT_LOG.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-reviewing-query-alerts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-reviewing-query-alerts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:13":{"id":"e:3:13","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 13","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eIngest the sensor data in Kinesis Data Firehose, which directly writes the data into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIngest the sensor data in an Amazon SQS standard queue, which is polled by an application running on an EC2 instance, and the data is written into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIngest the sensor data in a Kinesis Data Stream, which is polled by an application running on an EC2 instance, and the data is written into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIngest the sensor data in an Amazon SQS standard queue, which is polled by a Lambda function in batches, and the data is written into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company wants to use AWS for its connected cab application that would collect sensor data from its electric cab fleet to give drivers dynamically updated map information. The company would like to build its new sensor service by leveraging fully serverless components that are provisioned and managed automatically by AWS. The development team at the company does not want an option that requires the capacity to be manually provisioned, as it does not want to respond manually to changing volumes of sensor data. The company has hired you to provide consultancy for this strategic initiative.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eGiven these constraints, which of the following solutions would you suggest as the BEST fit to develop this service?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\n\u003cstrong\u003eIngest the sensor data in an Amazon SQS standard queue, which is polled by a Lambda function in batches, and the data is written into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lambda lets you run code without provisioning or managing servers. You pay only for the compute time you consume. Amazon Simple Queue Service (SQS) is a fully managed message queuing service that enables you to decouple and scale microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications. SQS offers two types of message queues. Standard queues offer maximum throughput, best-effort ordering, and at-least-once delivery. SQS FIFO queues are designed to guarantee that messages are processed exactly once, in the exact order that they are sent.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use an AWS Lambda function to process messages in an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) queue. Lambda event source mappings support standard queues and first-in, first-out (FIFO) queues. With Amazon SQS, you can offload tasks from one component of your application by sending them to a queue and processing them asynchronously.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d0c25dd4c98f7bfc1356e4277431b5e49e9344a01cbadd05885c2db68458491e.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d0c25dd4c98f7bfc1356e4277431b5e49e9344a01cbadd05885c2db68458491e.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-sqs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-sqs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS manages all ongoing operations and underlying infrastructure needed to provide a highly available and scalable message queuing service. With SQS, there is no upfront cost, no need to acquire, install, and configure messaging software, and no time-consuming build-out and maintenance of supporting infrastructure. SQS queues are dynamically created and scale automatically so you can build and grow applications quickly and efficiently.\nAs there is no need to manually provision the capacity, this is the correct option.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIngest the sensor data in Kinesis Data Firehose, which directly writes the data into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Firehose is a fully managed service for delivering real-time streaming data to destinations such as Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3), Amazon Redshift, Amazon OpenSearch Service, Splunk, and any custom HTTP endpoint or HTTP endpoints owned by supported third-party service providers, including Datadog, Dynatrace, LogicMonitor, MongoDB, New Relic, and Sumo Logic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFirehose cannot directly write into a DynamoDB table, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIngest the sensor data in an Amazon SQS standard queue, which is polled by an application running on an EC2 instance, and the data is written into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIngest the sensor data in a Kinesis Data Stream, which is polled by an application running on an EC2 instance, and the data is written into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUsing an application on an EC2 instance is ruled out as the company wants to use fully serverless components. So both these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\n\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/sqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/sqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kinesis.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kinesis.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-sqs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-sqs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:26":{"id":"e:3:26","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 26","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eThe S3 bucket does not have versioning enabled, so Athena cannot identify the latest delivery event objects in each partition\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe AWS Glue Data Catalog does not include the latest S3 partition changes, so Athena queries do not read all current partition locations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe Athena workgroup stores query results in the same S3 bucket, so Athena replaces recent delivery records with cached query output\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe AWS Glue job schedules overlap, so Athena automatically reads older partitions until all overlapping jobs finish successfully\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company stores time-sensitive delivery event data in Amazon S3. The company partitions the dataset by event_date and event_hour, and analysts use Amazon Athena with AWS Glue Data Catalog tables to run near real-time delivery performance analysis. During an investigation, a data engineer notices that some recently arrived records do not appear in Athena query results, and some partitioned query results seem out of date. The source files exist in the correct S3 prefixes. The data engineer must identify the most likely cause of the issue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is the likely cause?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe AWS Glue Data Catalog does not include the latest S3 partition changes, so Athena queries do not read all current partition locations\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAthena uses table and partition metadata from the AWS Glue Data Catalog when it queries partitioned data in Amazon S3. If new S3 prefixes exist but the corresponding partitions are not present or updated in the Data Catalog, Athena will not automatically know to include those locations in query planning.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis explains why the source files exist in Amazon S3 but some records are missing or out of date in query results. The physical data can be present, but Athena depends on current catalog metadata to discover which partitions to scan.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineer should update the partition metadata by using an AWS Glue crawler, Athena partition repair for Hive-style partitions, partition projection, or explicit partition registration through Data Catalog APIs or DDL statements. Keeping S3 partition layout and catalog metadata synchronized is essential for time-sensitive analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe S3 bucket does not have versioning enabled, so Athena cannot identify the latest delivery event objects in each partition\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Versioning preserves multiple versions of objects in a bucket. It helps recover from accidental overwrites or deletions and can support data protection requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, Athena does not require S3 Versioning to read the latest objects from a partitioned dataset. Missing or stale Athena results are more likely caused by stale partition metadata than by the absence of versioning.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe Athena workgroup stores query results in the same S3 bucket, so Athena replaces recent delivery records with cached query output\u003c/strong\u003e - Athena workgroups can define query result locations, encryption settings, and usage controls. Query result locations should be managed carefully so they do not interfere with source datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, Athena does not replace source delivery records with cached query output just because a workgroup writes results to Amazon S3. Query result storage is separate from the partition metadata problem described in the scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe AWS Glue job schedules overlap, so Athena automatically reads older partitions until all overlapping jobs finish successfully\u003c/strong\u003e - Overlapping AWS Glue jobs can create operational issues if multiple jobs write to the same output location or update the same metadata at the same time. In some pipelines, scheduling conflicts can cause incomplete outputs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, Athena does not automatically choose older partitions because Glue job schedules overlap. The most likely issue is that the Glue Data Catalog does not reflect the latest partition changes in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/msck-repair-table.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/msck-repair-table.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/updates-and-partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/updates-and-partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/get-started-managing-partitions-for-amazon-s3-tables-backed-by-the-aws-glue-data-catalog/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/get-started-managing-partitions-for-amazon-s3-tables-backed-by-the-aws-glue-data-catalog/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-11":{"id":"p:4-11","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 11","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon SQS standard queues to collect clickstream events, invoke AWS Lambda functions to poll the queues, and write the processed records to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon MSK brokers to collect clickstream events and store the processed event data in an Amazon RDS DB instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose to ingest clickstream events and deliver the processed data directly to a provisioned Amazon Redshift cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to collect clickstream events and use Amazon Data Firehose to deliver the data to an Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["SQS và Lambda có thể gom sự kiện rồi ghi S3, nhưng bạn phải viết và duy trì logic xử lý, gom lô và lỗi. Firehose đã cung cấp đường chuyển dữ liệu được quản lý tới S3.","MSK thu nhận được sự kiện, nhưng lựa chọn ghi vào RDS thay vì S3. Nó vừa sai đích lưu trữ, vừa thêm công vận hành Kafka cho nhu cầu này.","Lựa chọn đích Redshift không đáp ứng yêu cầu lưu sự kiện ở S3 để phân tích tiếp. Ngoài ra, Firehose chuyển tới Redshift qua S3 trung gian chứ không nạp trực tiếp như mô tả.","Kinesis Data Streams tiếp nhận clickstream từ nhiều nguồn; Firehose đọc luồng, gom dữ liệu và chuyển vào S3. Hai dịch vụ được quản lý giúp giảm mã và công bảo trì."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA digital marketplace company wants to implement real-time analytics for its online shopping platform. The company must collect clickstream events from its website and mobile applications, including page views, cart updates, searches, and checkout actions. The company needs to store the events in Amazon S3 for downstream analytics and wants to minimize ongoing maintenance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST ongoing maintenance?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:5":{"id":"e:4:5","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 5","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an S3 bucket policy that grants the AWS Glue service principal full access to the S3 bucket and remove the IAM role from the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable Amazon Redshift data sharing and configure the S3 bucket as the target location for the shared dataset\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an IAM role with permissions to write to the S3 bucket and associate the IAM role with the Amazon Redshift cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the Amazon Redshift database credentials in AWS Secrets Manager and reference the secret in the AWS Glue job configuration\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics company stores partner rebate data in an Amazon Redshift cluster. The company needs to store a scheduled copy of the partner rebate data in an Amazon S3 bucket for downstream reconciliation. A data engineer creates an AWS Glue job to extract the data from Amazon Redshift and write the data to the S3 bucket on a recurring schedule. The data engineer has already configured private network connectivity among the AWS Glue job, the Amazon Redshift cluster, and Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is the next step required to meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an IAM role with permissions to write to the S3 bucket and associate the IAM role with the Amazon Redshift cluster\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue uses Amazon Redshift integration patterns that move data through Amazon S3 when reading from or writing to Redshift. For high-throughput movement, AWS Glue can issue Amazon Redshift COPY and UNLOAD commands, which require Amazon Redshift to access the relevant S3 location.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift needs an IAM role that grants the required S3 permissions, such as writing unloaded data to the target or temporary S3 path. After the role is created, the data engineer must associate the IAM role with the Amazon Redshift cluster so Redshift can assume the role during the data movement operation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis step complements the network configuration that is already complete. Private connectivity provides the route between services, but IAM permissions authorize Redshift to write to Amazon S3. Without the associated Redshift IAM role, the Glue job can still fail when Redshift attempts to unload data to the S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an S3 bucket policy that grants the AWS Glue service principal full access to the S3 bucket and remove the IAM role from the AWS Glue job\u003c/strong\u003e - An S3 bucket policy can grant access to specific IAM principals or service principals. Bucket policies are useful for controlling who can read or write objects in a bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, granting broad access to the AWS Glue service principal and removing the Glue job role is not the correct permission model. AWS Glue jobs use an IAM role, and Amazon Redshift also needs an associated IAM role to perform COPY and UNLOAD operations against S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Amazon Redshift data sharing and configure the S3 bucket as the target location for the shared dataset\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift data sharing lets Redshift share live data across Redshift clusters, workgroups, AWS accounts, or analytics consumers without copying data. It is useful for workload isolation and governed data sharing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, Redshift data sharing does not use an S3 bucket as a target location for storing a copy of the data. The company needs Redshift and AWS Glue permissions for S3-based data movement, not a Redshift data sharing configuration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the Amazon Redshift database credentials in AWS Secrets Manager and reference the secret in the AWS Glue job configuration\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Secrets Manager is a good place to store database credentials that an AWS Glue job can use to connect to Amazon Redshift. Storing credentials securely is better than hardcoding user names and passwords in scripts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the scenario states that the connection details are already configured and the remaining issue is enabling the scheduled export to Amazon S3. Secrets Manager does not give Amazon Redshift permission to write unloaded data to the S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-redshift-home.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-redshift-home.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/copy-unload-iam-role-associating-with-clusters.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/copy-unload-iam-role-associating-with-clusters.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/loading-data-access-permissions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/loading-data-access-permissions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_UNLOAD.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_UNLOAD.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-58":{"id":"p:3-58","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 58","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate Lambda functions to evaluate each data quality rule, use Step Functions to orchestrate the functions, and publish notifications when the training data fails validation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EMR provisioned cluster, install an open source Python data quality library, copy the S3 training files to the cluster, and run custom validation scripts\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Glue DataBrew project for the S3 training dataset, create a data quality ruleset, run a profile job, and use EventBridge to start the job when new data arrives\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena scheduled queries to evaluate rule conditions against the S3 training files and write failed records to a separate S3 prefix for later inspection\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Lambda và Step Functions cần tự viết logic validation và điều phối. Trái yêu cầu không viết mã kiểm tra mới.","EMR với thư viện Python cần quản lý cụm, cài thư viện và viết script. Công vận hành cao hơn DataBrew.","DataBrew project và ruleset kiểm tra dữ liệu bằng profile job. Dùng sự kiện tệp mới để khởi động theo cơ chế tích hợp phù hợp, không tự viết từng luật bằng mã.","Athena scheduled SQL cần tự viết và bảo trì logic, đồng thời chạy theo lịch thay vì phản ứng trực tiếp với dataset mới."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial technology company is building machine learning models to predict payment fraud. The company stores model training datasets as files in an Amazon S3 bucket. A data engineer must apply data quality rules to the training files when new datasets arrive. The solution must require the least operational overhead and must not require the team to write custom validation code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-43":{"id":"p:4-43","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 43","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDatasetMatch \"reference\" \"warehouse_id-\u0026gt;ref_warehouse_id, zone_id-\u0026gt;ref_zone_id\" = 1.0\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eReferentialIntegrity \"warehouse_id,zone_id\" \"reference.{ref_warehouse_id,ref_zone_id}\" = 100\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eReferentialIntegrity \"reference.{ref_warehouse_id,ref_zone_id}\" \"warehouse_id,zone_id\" = 1.0\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eReferentialIntegrity \"warehouse_id,zone_id\" \"reference.{ref_warehouse_id,ref_zone_id}\" = 1.0\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["DatasetMatch phục vụ đối chiếu mức đồng bộ giữa dataset theo ánh xạ cột. Đề chỉ cần kiểm tra mỗi cặp khóa nguồn có tồn tại ở dữ liệu tham chiếu, là nhiệm vụ của ReferentialIntegrity.","Kết quả ReferentialIntegrity là tỷ lệ từ 0 đến 1, không phải số phần trăm từ 0 đến 100. Muốn toàn bộ cặp khóa khớp phải so sánh với 1.0.","Tham số thứ nhất phải là các cột của dataset chính; tham số thứ hai chỉ dataset và cột tham chiếu. Lựa chọn này đảo ngược thứ tự nên sai cú pháp mong muốn.","Quy tắc kiểm tra cặp warehouse_id, zone_id của dataset chính có trong cặp ref_warehouse_id, ref_zone_id của reference. So sánh bằng 1.0 yêu cầu 100% cặp giá trị có đối ứng, thay vì kiểm tra hai cột độc lập."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer has two AWS Glue Data Quality datasets that contain fulfillment information for multiple warehouse locations and operating zones. One dataset is named primary, and the other dataset is named reference.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineer needs to validate that the specific paired values in the warehouse_id and zone_id columns of the primary dataset match the corresponding paired values in the ref_warehouse_id and ref_zone_id columns of the reference dataset. The engineer wants to define this validation by using Data Quality Definition Language rules in an AWS Glue Data Quality job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich rule will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:7:38":{"id":"e:7:38","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 38","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eRun the \u003ccode\u003eVACUUM DELETE\u003c/code\u003e command in Redshift to clean up and optimize the data before running the report. Afterward, execute the \u003ccode\u003eSQL COPY\u003c/code\u003e command to load the data from Amazon S3 into Redshift. Adjust the report to pull data from the latest and newly imported tables.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eRun a \u003ccode\u003eSQL JOIN\u003c/code\u003e operation with Amazon Redshift Spectrum to merge the most recent data with the S3 external table data into a new table. Adjust the report to access data from this newly formed table.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eUse the report\u0027s SQL statement to retrieve data directly from Amazon S3. Save these results in a Redshift materialized view. Adjust the report to refresh using \u003ccode\u003eSQL REFRESH\u003c/code\u003e and then extract data from the materialized view.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eRun the \u003ccode\u003eSQL UNLOAD\u003c/code\u003e command to move data from the sales table to a new external table in Amazon S3. Adjust the report to utilize Amazon Redshift Spectrum for querying the newly created table and the existing tables in Amazon S3.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA company utilizes Amazon Redshift for its primary data warehousing needs tied to an e-commerce application. Recently, they added a new SQL report for their sales data, which needs to compare specific columns from this year\u0027s sales against historical data. Data older than one year is stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. This new report has resulted in slower performance and increased query times. The company needs a solution to enhance system performance and speed up these queries.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhat is the MOST efficient solution to meet these requirements?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift Spectrum allows you to efficiently query and retrieve structured and semistructured data from files in Amazon S3 without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables. Redshift Spectrum queries employ massive parallelism to run very fast against large datasets. Much of the processing occurs in the Redshift Spectrum layer, and most of the data remains in Amazon S3. Like Amazon Athena, Redshift Spectrum is serverless, and there’s nothing to provision or manage. You just pay for the resources you consume for the duration of your Redshift Spectrum query. Like Amazon Redshift itself, you get the benefits of a sophisticated query optimizer, fast access to data on local disks, and standard SQL.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/16f0325ad19dfed538cd42a4ec50e189cca36f8f333835d5878f4e59acabd972.gif\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift offers several features to enhance query performance, such as result caching, concurrency scaling, and materialized views. Materialized views in Redshift provide a powerful way to optimize query performance for repeated and predictable query workloads. They precompute and store the results of a query, which improves the speed of data retrieval for complex queries that are run frequently. This makes them particularly useful for scenarios like the one described, where data from historical records stored in Amazon S3 is regularly queried alongside newer data. Redshift can use materialized views to serve query results from precomputed data, reducing the compute resources required and accelerating query performance, thus enabling near real-time analytics on large datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the report\u0027s SQL statement to retrieve data directly from Amazon S3. Save these results in a Redshift materialized view. Adjust the report to refresh using \u003ccode\u003eSQL REFRESH\u003c/code\u003e and then extract data from the materialized view.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun the \u003ccode\u003eVACUUM DELETE\u003c/code\u003e command in Redshift to clean up and optimize the data before running the report. Afterward, execute the \u003ccode\u003eSQL COPY\u003c/code\u003e command to load the data from Amazon S3 into Redshift. Adjust the report to pull data from the latest and newly imported tables\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Although this option suggests a good practice, optimizing data using \u003ccode\u003eVACUUM DELETE\u003c/code\u003e, copying data from Amazon S3 using the SQL COPY command is resource-extensive and may not be the most efficient approach, especially when the data is already in Amazon S3. Always remember that Amazon Redshift already has a suite of features that allows it to execute specific operations for data hosted in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eRun a \u003ccode\u003eSQL JOIN\u003c/code\u003e operation with Amazon Redshift Spectrum to merge the most recent data with the S3 external table data into a new table. Adjust the report to access data from this newly formed table\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. While Redshift Spectrum effectively allows for querying data in S3 without moving it into Redshift, this approach relies heavily on the continuous execution of SQL JOINs across Redshift and S3. This approach can introduce significant query overhead, especially when the datasets or queries are complex because it must dynamically handle large volumes of data across the two services, which can cause delays in the query execution time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun the \u003ccode\u003eSQL UNLOAD\u003c/code\u003e command to move data from the sales table to a new external table in Amazon S3. Adjust the report to utilize Amazon Redshift Spectrum for querying the newly created table and the existing tables in Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This involves loading and unloading data in and out of Amazon Redshift and Amazon S3, which consumes many unnecessary resources. If you already host your data in Amazon S3, it is best to query it directly from Amazon Redshift instead of moving data between Redshift and S3. Additionally, this option introduces complexity with multiple tables and data transfer steps.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-create-sql-command.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-create-sql-command.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-20":{"id":"p:1-20","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 20","correct":[3,4,5],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDrop and recreate the table with the PARTITIONED BY clause. Load the partitions by executing the \u003ccode\u003eALTER TABLE ADD PARTITION\u003c/code\u003e statement\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePartition the data by using a key prefix of the form year-month-day/ to the S3 objects\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the log files to Apache Avro format\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the log files to Apache Parquet format\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePartition the data by using a key prefix of the form date=year-month-day/ to the S3 objects\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDrop and recreate the table with the PARTITIONED BY clause. Load the partitions by executing the \u003ccode\u003eMSCK REPAIR TABLE\u003c/code\u003e statement\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["ALTER TABLE ADD PARTITION thêm partition theo cách chỉ định cụ thể. Dùng cấu trúc tương thích Hive và MSCK REPAIR TABLE thuận tiện hơn cho việc khám phá nhiều partition trong phương án của đề.","Prefix chỉ có ngày thiếu dạng tên_cột=giá_trị mà cách khám phá partition kiểu Hive mong đợi. Dùng date=... sẽ phù hợp hơn với MSCK REPAIR TABLE.","Avro lưu theo hàng, nên kém phù hợp hơn Parquet khi truy vấn chỉ lấy một phần cột của tập log rất lớn.","Parquet lưu theo cột và hỗ trợ nén. Athena quét ít dữ liệu hơn khi chỉ cần một số cột, từ đó giảm chi phí và tăng tốc.","Prefix dạng date=year-month-day/ tuân theo cách đặt partition kiểu Hive. Nó giúp công cụ khám phá được tên và giá trị của cột phân vùng.","Tạo bảng có PARTITIONED BY rồi chạy MSCK REPAIR TABLE để phát hiện partition theo cấu trúc Hive trên S3. Phối hợp với prefix date=... và Parquet để đạt mục tiêu của đề."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe web development team at an IT company has about 200 TB of web-log data that is stored in an Amazon S3 bucket as raw text. Each log file is identified by a key of the type year-month-day_log_HHmmss.txt where HHmmss denotes the time the log file was created. The data engineering team has created an Amazon Athena table that links to the given S3 bucket. The team executes several queries every hour against a subset of the table\u0027s columns. The company wants a Hive-metastore compatible solution that costs less and requires less maintenance to support the ongoing analytics on this log data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate, which of the following solutions would you combine to address these requirements? (Select three)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:20":{"id":"e:2:20","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 20","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Step Functions to orchestrate multiple Lambda functions, and configure the third Lambda function to run after the first two functions finish\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue workflows to run the first two AWS Glue Spark jobs in parallel, and start the third AWS Glue job after both jobs finish\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Batch to run three containerized Spark jobs on managed compute environments, and use DynamoDB to track when the first two jobs finish\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EMR to run each Spark process, send completion messages to an SQS queue, and invoke the third process with a Lambda function\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA digital media company processes 650 GB of audience engagement and campaign delivery data every day. The company stores incoming CSV files in Amazon S3, and the schemas are registered in AWS Glue Data Catalog. The company needs to convert the CSV files to Apache Parquet format and write the output to another S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe pipeline must run two long-running conversion and enrichment processes concurrently. Each process requires at least 15 GiB of memory. A third correlation process must begin only after the first two processes finish successfully. The company wants the solution with the least operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue workflows to run the first two AWS Glue Spark jobs in parallel, and start the third AWS Glue job after both jobs finish\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue workflows are designed to orchestrate multiple AWS Glue jobs, crawlers, and triggers as a single ETL pipeline. The workflow can run independent jobs in parallel and then use a conditional trigger to start a downstream job only after the required upstream jobs complete successfully.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Spark jobs can process large datasets from Amazon S3 and write transformed output back to Amazon S3. This fits the requirement to convert 650 GB of CSV files to Apache Parquet and then run a correlation process after the first two processes complete.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational overhead because AWS Glue provides both the serverless Spark processing environment and the workflow orchestration layer. The company does not need to operate Airflow, manage EMR clusters, build SQS-based completion tracking, or package containerized Spark jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Batch to run three containerized Spark jobs on managed compute environments, and use DynamoDB to track when the first two jobs finish\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Batch can run containerized batch jobs and can manage compute environments for large processing tasks. DynamoDB could track job completion state if the team builds that coordination logic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThat design adds avoidable custom operations. The company would need to build and maintain container images, Spark dependencies, completion tracking, and job coordination logic, while AWS Glue provides managed Spark jobs and workflow orchestration for this use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Step Functions to orchestrate multiple Lambda functions, and configure the third Lambda function to run after the first two functions finish\u003c/strong\u003e - Step Functions can coordinate serverless workflows and can start one task after other tasks finish. Lambda functions can process event-driven tasks and integrate with many AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe issue with this approach is that Lambda is not a good fit for long-running, memory-intensive processing of hundreds of gigabytes of data. Lambda also does not provide the Spark-based distributed processing model that AWS Glue jobs provide for this ETL workload.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon EMR to run each Spark process, send completion messages to an SQS queue, and invoke the third process with a Lambda function\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR can run Apache Spark jobs and can process large data volumes from Amazon S3. SQS and Lambda can help coordinate custom workflow signaling when one process must wait for others to complete.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis creates additional operational overhead. The team must manage EMR cluster lifecycle, Spark runtime configuration, queue messages, completion tracking, and Lambda orchestration instead of using AWS Glue workflows and Glue Spark jobs directly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/about-triggers.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/about-triggers.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/creating_running_workflows.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/creating_running_workflows.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-61":{"id":"p:3-61","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 61","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval 30 days after object creation. Delete the files 5 years after object creation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Standard-IA 30 days after object creation. Archive the files to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive 5 years after object creation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 One Zone-IA 30 days after object creation. Delete the files 5 years after object creation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 bucket lifecycle policy to move files from Amazon S3 Standard to Amazon S3 Standard-IA 30 days after object creation. Delete the files 5 years after object creation\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Glacier Flexible Retrieval cần khôi phục trước khi đọc, không đáp ứng truy cập tức thì mọi lúc.","Sau năm năm dữ liệu được phép xóa; chuyển sang Deep Archive giữ thêm dữ liệu không cần thiết. Đề yêu cầu kết thúc lưu trữ bằng expiration.","One Zone-IA chỉ lưu trong một AZ, không phù hợp dữ liệu thiết yếu không thể tái tạo theo điều kiện đề.","Standard trong 30 ngày đầu, Standard-IA cho giai đoạn ít đọc nhưng vẫn truy cập tức thì, rồi Lifecycle expiration sau năm năm. Giữ dự phòng đa AZ theo yêu cầu."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe content division at a digital media agency has an application that generates a large number of files on Amazon S3, each approximately 10 megabytes in size. The agency mandates that the files be stored for 5 years before they can be deleted. The files are frequently accessed in the first 30 days of the object creation but are rarely accessed after the first 30 days, however, immediate accessibility is always required. The files contain critical business data that is not easy to reproduce.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution is the MOST cost-effective for the given use case?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:21":{"id":"e:4:21","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 21","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate IAM roles that have different levels of granular access for different PII requirements. Assign the IAM roles to IAM groups. Use an identity-based policy to row and column level access to the IAM groups\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon QuickSight to restrict access to a dataset by configuring row-level security (RLS) on it based on the PII needs of each team. To do this, you create a query or file that has one column named \u003ccode\u003eUserName\u003c/code\u003e or \u003ccode\u003eGroupName\u003c/code\u003eand then add one column to the query or file for each field that you want to grant or restrict access to\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up AWS Lake Formation and create data filters based on the access permissions needed to each user group. Grant the data filter permissions to different IAM roles. Assign the IAM roles to users based on PII access needs. Use Athena to query the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 Access Points to create unique access control policies for each access point to easily control access to shared datasets. Create different Access Points to different user groups with different PII access requirements\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses Amazon S3 as its data lake solution, where it stores all of its data including personally identifiable information (PII). This data is used by multiple teams and user groups. As a data engineer, you have been tasked to ensure that user teams and groups can access only the PII that they require.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow will you implement a solution for this requirement with the LEAST operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up AWS Lake Formation and create data filters based on the access permissions needed for each user group. Grant the data filter permissions to different IAM roles. Assign the IAM roles to users as needed. Use Athena to query the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can implement column-level, row-level, and cell-level security by creating data filters. You select a data filter when you grant the SELECT Lake Formation permission on tables. If your table contains nested column structures, you can define a data filter by including or excluding the child columns and define row-level filter expressions on nested attributes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can grant the SELECT, DESCRIBE, and DROP Lake Formation permissions on data filters to principals.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCreating data filter from the Lake Formation console:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/da6b83b6b3715b669c7235b6a884a954a843580b75cf34809d91b9422f3890df.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/da6b83b6b3715b669c7235b6a884a954a843580b75cf34809d91b9422f3890df.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/data-filters-about.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/data-filters-about.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eGranting data filter permissions:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/24622d66a619b2e38825e56f3b9a1c14832690b1d474c7f77899b33a97e9b7bd.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/24622d66a619b2e38825e56f3b9a1c14832690b1d474c7f77899b33a97e9b7bd.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/granting-filter-perms.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/granting-filter-perms.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation allows you to define and enforce database, table, and column-level access policies when using Athena queries to read data stored in Amazon S3. Lake Formation provides an authorization and governance layer on data stored in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eLake Formation permissions apply when using Athena to query source data from Amazon S3 locations that are registered with Lake Formation. Lake Formation permissions also apply when you create databases and tables that point to registered Amazon S3 data locations. To use Athena with data registered using Lake Formation, Athena must be configured to use the AWS Glue Data Catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon QuickSight to restrict access to a dataset by configuring row-level security (RLS) on it based on the PII needs of each team. To do this, you create a query or file that has one column named \u003ccode\u003eUserName\u003c/code\u003e or \u003ccode\u003eGroupName\u003c/code\u003eand then add one column to the query or file for each field that you want to grant or restrict access to\u003c/strong\u003e - Using row-level security (RLS) with user-based rules to restrict access to a dataset is only available for Enterprise edition.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon S3 Access Points to create unique access control policies for each access point to easily control access to shared datasets. Create different Access Points for different user groups with different PII access requirements\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon S3 Access Points, a feature of S3, simplify data access for any AWS service or customer application that stores data in S3. With S3 Access Points, customers can create unique access control policies for each access point to easily control access to shared datasets. Fine-grained row-level and column-level access control are not possible with S3 Access Points.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate IAM roles that have different levels of granular access for different PII requirements. Assign the IAM roles to IAM groups. Use an identity-based policy to row and column level access to the IAM groups\u003c/strong\u003e - Identity-based IAM policies cannot provide the fine-grained access needed for this use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/data-filters-about.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/data-filters-about.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/security-athena-lake-formation.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/security-athena-lake-formation.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-18":{"id":"p:3-18","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 18","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure a read replica of the RDS database to store the last six months of data and execute more frequent queries on the read replica. Export RDS data to S3 and schedule an AWS data pipeline for an incremental copy of RDS data to S3. Configure an AWS Glue Data Catalog of the data in S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the historical data in S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS data pipeline to incrementally load the last six months of data into Amazon Redshift and execute more frequent queries on Redshift. Set up a read replica of the RDS database to run queries on the historical data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eExport RDS data to S3 and schedule an AWS data pipeline for an incremental copy of RDS data to S3. Configure an AWS Glue Data Catalog of the data in S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the entire data in S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eExport RDS data to S3 and schedule an AWS data pipeline for an incremental copy of RDS data to S3. Load and store the last six months of data from S3 in Amazon Redshift. Configure an Amazon Redshift Spectrum table to connect to all the historical data in S3\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["RDS read replica không tối ưu phân tích 50 TB như kho dữ liệu được đề yêu cầu; tách Athena còn làm đối chiếu dữ liệu phức tạp hơn.","Dữ liệu lịch sử nhiều năm ít đọc nên lưu S3 tiết kiệm hơn giữ trong RDS replica. Redshift Spectrum có thể truy vấn phần lịch sử.","Athena trên toàn bộ S3 giảm lưu trữ database nhưng bộ đề ưu tiên Redshift cho phần sáu tháng được truy vấn thường xuyên, hiệu năng cao.","Giữ dữ liệu nóng sáu tháng trong Redshift, dữ liệu lịch sử trên S3 và dùng Spectrum để truy vấn/JOIN. Tên AWS Data Pipeline là thành phần của đề gốc; ý chính là nạp tăng dần và tách nóng/lạnh."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company uses Amazon RDS to store sales data. For the analytics workloads that require high performance, only the last six months of data (approximately 50 TB) will be frequently queried. At the end of each month, the monthly sales data will be merged with the historical sales data for the last 5 years, which should also be available for analysis. The CTO at the company is looking at a cost-optimal solution that offers the best performance for this use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you select for the given requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:56":{"id":"e:3:56","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 56","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDeploy an Apache Spark Streaming application on an Amazon EMR cluster to consume the clickstream data, transform the records, and write the processed output to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with provisioned capacity and Lambda consumers to run custom filtering logic and write the processed events to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose with built-in processing capabilities to transform and filter the stream, then deliver the processed data directly to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to perform stateful processing, event filtering, and output delivery to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics company collects a large volume of clickstream events from its website and mobile application. The company needs to filter invalid events, apply simple streaming transformations, and deliver the processed data to Amazon S3 for downstream analysis. The company wants the most operationally efficient solution with minimal custom infrastructure to manage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose with built-in processing capabilities to transform and filter the stream, then deliver the processed data directly to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Data Firehose is the most operationally efficient choice when the company needs a managed streaming delivery service that can process streaming data and deliver the results to Amazon S3. Firehose manages scaling, buffering, retries, and delivery without requiring the team to manage consumers or stream processing infrastructure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe requirement focuses on filtering and transformation before storage in Amazon S3. Firehose can apply processing as part of the delivery stream and then write the processed records directly to the S3 destination.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational overhead because the company does not need to provision Kinesis consumers, operate EMR clusters, run containerized workers, or manage a separate stream processing application. Firehose provides a managed path from streaming ingestion to S3 delivery.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with provisioned capacity and Lambda consumers to run custom filtering logic and write the processed events to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - Kinesis Data Streams with Lambda consumers can process streaming records and write output to Amazon S3. This pattern can work when the team needs custom event handling logic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this design requires the team to manage stream capacity, Lambda consumer behavior, error handling, retries, and custom S3 writes. Firehose provides a more managed delivery path with less operational effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to perform stateful processing, event filtering, and output delivery to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink is well suited for complex, stateful stream processing, such as joins, windows, and rolling aggregations. It can process data from Kinesis streams and write results to downstream destinations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the requirement asks for the most operationally efficient solution for managed filtering, transformation, and S3 delivery. Firehose is simpler when the processing requirement does not require a full stream processing application.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy an Apache Spark Streaming application on an Amazon EMR cluster to consume the clickstream data, transform the records, and write the processed output to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - Apache Spark Streaming on Amazon EMR can process large streaming datasets and write transformed output to Amazon S3. This approach can support advanced processing logic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, an EMR cluster introduces cluster provisioning, scaling, patching, and monitoring responsibilities. That is more operational overhead than using a fully managed Firehose delivery stream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/what-is-this-service.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/what-is-this-service.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/data-transformation.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/data-transformation.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/create-destination.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/create-destination.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/basic-deliver.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/basic-deliver.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/firehose/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/firehose/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-48":{"id":"p:2-48","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 48","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConvert the Spark DataFrames to AWS Glue DynamicFrames and perform a DynamicFrame join so AWS Glue can avoid local shuffle storage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Glue crawler for both source datasets and update the Data Catalog before the job runs so Spark can reduce shuffle disk usage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable the AWS Glue Spark shuffle manager so the job writes shuffle files and spill data to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConvert the sort-merge join to a broadcast join so Spark sends one DataFrame to all executors before joining\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Đổi DataFrame sang DynamicFrame không loại bỏ shuffle của phép JOIN hai tập lớn. Vẫn có thể đầy đĩa cục bộ.","Crawler cập nhật metadata, không tăng dung lượng đĩa cho shuffle trong lúc Spark chạy.","Glue Spark shuffle manager cho phép ghi shuffle/spill ra S3 thay vì dựa hoàn toàn vào đĩa local. Giải quyết lỗi No space left on device của bước shuffle.","Broadcast join phù hợp khi một bên đủ nhỏ. Hai DataFrame đều lớn và cùng cỡ; đề còn yêu cầu không đổi sang tối ưu cho bảng nhỏ."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is tuning an AWS Glue Apache Spark ETL job that joins two large customer activity datasets. The job uses a sort-merge join between two equally sized Spark DataFrames.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDuring the shuffle stage, the job fails with the following error: No space left on device. The data engineer must resolve the failure without changing the join to a small-table optimization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will resolve the error?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:62":{"id":"e:3:62","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 62","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up S3 Access Point to read data from the S3 bucket. Leverage the S3 Access Points policy to dynamically filter data based on the country of the analyst making the read request\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a separate bucket for each country\u0027s customer data. Provide access to each analyst based on the country that the analyst serves\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure the S3 bucket as a data lake location in AWS Lake Formation and leverage the Lake Formation row-level security features to enforce the company\u0027s access policies\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMigrate the data to AWS Regions that are close to the countries where the customers are. Restrict access to each analyst based on the country that the analyst serves\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company stores its customer data in an Amazon S3 bucket. The data is accessed by employees from various countries for analytics purposes. The governance team needs to implement a solution ensuring that data analysts can only access customer data from their respective countries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution would satisfy these requirements with minimal operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure the S3 bucket as a data lake location in AWS Lake Formation and leverage the Lake Formation row-level security features to enforce the company\u0027s access policies\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation helps you centrally govern, secure, and globally share data for analytics and machine learning. With Lake Formation, you can manage fine-grained access control for your data lake data on Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) and its metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eLake Formation provides its own permissions model that augments the IAM permissions model. The Lake Formation permissions model enables fine-grained access to data stored in data lakes through a simple grant or revoke mechanism, much like a relational database management system (RDBMS). Lake Formation provides data filters that allow you to restrict access to a combination of columns and rows. You can use row and cell-level security to protect sensitive data like Personal Identifiable Information (PII).\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/abd2e6464f07aac8723aed0f1f3e5facf05e10e6913b5dfdce8c3f673112371a.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/abd2e6464f07aac8723aed0f1f3e5facf05e10e6913b5dfdce8c3f673112371a.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up S3 Access Point to read data from the S3 bucket. Leverage the S3 Access Points policy to dynamically filter data based on the country of the analyst making the read request\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon S3 Access Points simplify data access for any AWS service or customer application that stores data in S3. With S3 Access Points, customers can create unique access control policies for each access point to easily control access to shared datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eEach S3 Access Point is configured with an access policy specific to a use case or application. For example, you can create an access point for your S3 bucket that grants access to groups of users or applications for your data lake. An Access Point can support a single user or application, or groups of users or applications within and across accounts, allowing separate management of each access point.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou cannot use an S3 Access Points policy to dynamically filter data based on the country of the analyst making the read request.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a separate bucket for each country\u0027s customer data. Provide access to each analyst based on the country that the analyst serves\u003c/strong\u003e - Creating a separate bucket for each country\u0027s customer data is wasteful use of resources and involves significant operational effort to manage the data on an ongoing basis, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMigrate the data to AWS Regions that are close to the countries where the customers are. Restrict access to each analyst based on the country that the analyst serves\u003c/strong\u003e - This option also involves creating multiple buckets (since each S3 bucket is specific to an AWS Region), therefore it would also involve a significant operational effort to manage the data on an ongoing basis, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/register-data-lake.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/register-data-lake.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/registration-role.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/registration-role.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/access-points/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/access-points/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:17":{"id":"e:3:17","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 17","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon S3 as the data lake service. Define the Amazon S3 access point for each AWS service. Configure each access point with distinct permissions and network controls needed for each AWS service\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 for data lake storage. Use Apache Ranger through Amazon EMR to restrict data access by rows and columns. Provide data access by using Apache Pig\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon S3 as the data lake service. Configure AWS Lake Formation permissions to provide fine-grained row and column-wise access. Provide access to data for the AWS services via Lake Formation permissions only\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon S3 as the data lake service. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query data in Amazon S3 files without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables. Configure access control through the IAM roles attached to the Redshift cluster\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses several analytics services from AWS such as AWS Glue, Amazon Athena, Amazon Redshift Spectrum, and Amazon EMR to query the data. The company wants to build a data lake in AWS that can provide row-level and column-level data access to specific teams/groups that use the aforementioned AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution can address these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up Amazon S3 as the data lake service. Configure AWS Lake Formation permissions to provide fine-grained row and column-wise access. Provide access to data for the AWS services via Lake Formation permissions only\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation helps you centrally govern, secure, and globally share data for analytics and machine learning. With Lake Formation, you can manage fine-grained access control for your data lake data on Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) and its metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation permissions are enforced using granular controls at the column, row, and cell levels across AWS analytics and machine learning services, including Amazon Athena, Amazon QuickSight, Amazon Redshift Spectrum, Amazon EMR, and AWS Glue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation permissions management workflow:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5dfef0e1fb2f134c748ff7d710da0d60399cad0bd100e0db64e2dd362e349bdc.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5dfef0e1fb2f134c748ff7d710da0d60399cad0bd100e0db64e2dd362e349bdc.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up Amazon S3 as the data lake service. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query data in Amazon S3 files without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables. Configure access control through the IAM roles attached to the Redshift cluster\u003c/strong\u003e - IAM roles cannot provide granular row-level and column-level data access permissions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon S3 as the data lake service. Define the Amazon S3 access point for each AWS service. Configure each access point with distinct permissions and network controls needed for each AWS service\u003c/strong\u003e - Each access point has distinct permissions and network controls that S3 applies for any request that is made through that access point. However, fine-grained access (such as row-level and column-level data access) is not possible via S3 access points.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon S3 for data lake storage. Use Apache Ranger through Amazon EMR to restrict data access by rows and columns. Provide data access by using Apache Pig\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR natively integrates with Apache Ranger, allowing you to define, enforce, and audit fine-grained data access control. With this feature, you can define and enforce database, table, and column-level authorization policies for Apache Spark and Apache Hive users to access data through Hive Metastore. Apache Pig is a platform for analyzing large data sets that consists of a high-level language for expressing data analysis programs, coupled with infrastructure for evaluating these programs. The salient property of Pig programs is that their structure is amenable to substantial parallelization, which in turn enables them to handle very large data sets. For the given use case, configuring Amazon EMR adds a lot of operational overhead and additional costs compared to just implementing the required access through Lake Formation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/service-integrations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/service-integrations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-56":{"id":"p:4-56","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 56","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable AWS Glue Auto Scaling so the job can add and remove workers automatically while reading the historical S3 files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS Glue DynamicFrame file grouping by using groupFiles and groupSize when the job reads the small S3 files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the number of AWS Glue workers so Spark has more executors available to read the small files in parallel\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRewrite the historical pipeline to compact all files with an EMR Spark job before running the AWS Glue job for forecasting preparation\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Auto Scaling bổ sung worker nhưng không trực tiếp giảm lượng task và metadata sinh ra bởi hàng chục nghìn tệp nhỏ. Nên giảm chi phí xử lý tệp nhỏ trước.","groupFiles và groupSize cho phép DynamicFrame gom nhiều tệp nhỏ thành nhóm đầu vào khi đọc các định dạng được hỗ trợ. Ít task nhỏ hơn giúp giảm công lập lịch và áp lực lên driver mà không cần thêm pipeline.","Thêm executor không tự giảm số tệp và công lập lịch. Nút thắt do quản lý nhiều tệp nhỏ có thể vẫn tồn tại dù tăng worker.","Compaction có thể hữu ích lâu dài, nhưng thêm EMR và viết lại toàn bộ lịch sử tạo nhiều công việc. File grouping trong job hiện tại là thay đổi trực tiếp, ít vận hành hơn cho yêu cầu này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics company uses AWS Glue ETL jobs to collect and process transaction data into an Amazon S3 bucket. The ingestion pipeline writes one small file every hour, and each file is between 250 KB and 350 KB. The company now needs to train a demand forecasting model by processing 5 years of historical data that contains 45,000 small files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer creates a second AWS Glue ETL job with the smallest worker type to read the historical files from Amazon S3 and prepare the dataset for downstream analysis. The job runs slowly because Spark creates many tasks for a large number of small files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will improve the performance of the second pipeline MOST cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-5":{"id":"p:1-5","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 5","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUtilize AWS Glue to detect the schema including any ongoing changes. Extract, transform, and load the data into the S3 bucket by creating the ETL pipeline in Apache Spark\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUtilize Amazon EMR to detect the schema including any ongoing changes. Extract, transform, and load the data into the S3 bucket by creating the ETL pipeline in Apache Spark\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUtilize PySpark to detect the schema including any ongoing changes. Extract, transform, and load the data into the S3 bucket by creating the ETL pipeline in AWS Lambda\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUtilize Redshift spectrum to detect the schema including any ongoing changes. Extract, transform, and load the data into the S3 bucket by creating a stored procedure in Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Glue crawler tự khám phá schema và cập nhật thay đổi vào Data Catalog. Glue chạy ETL Spark để xử lý 2 TB dữ liệu rồi ghi S3, giảm việc tự quản lý hạ tầng và metadata.","EMR chạy được Spark, nhưng cần thêm công thiết lập, quản lý và viết logic nhận biết schema thay đổi. Glue phù hợp hơn khi ưu tiên ít vận hành.","Lambda giới hạn thời gian mỗi lần chạy và không phù hợp làm môi trường xử lý Spark cho pipeline 2 TB mỗi ngày. Đây là khối lượng ETL lớn, nên dùng Glue hoặc nền tảng xử lý dữ liệu chuyên dụng.","Spectrum là công cụ truy vấn dữ liệu S3 từ Redshift, không phải công cụ tự khám phá schema và ETL nhiều cơ sở dữ liệu như đề yêu cầu. Stored procedure không thay thế toàn bộ pipeline này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company regularly extracts about 2 TB of data daily from various data sources - including MySQL, MSSQL Server, Oracle, Vertica, and Teradata Vantage. Some of these sources feature undefined or frequently changing data schemas. A data engineer is tasked with implementing a solution that can automatically detect the schema of these data sources and perform data extraction, transformation, and loading to an Amazon S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution would meet these needs while minimizing operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:7:46":{"id":"e:7:46","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 46","correct":[0,1,2],"answers":["Use the Time to Live (TTL) feature to automatically delete items older than 60 days from the DynamoDB table.","Set up an AWS Glue job to extract the last 60 days of data from the DynamoDB table and load it into Amazon QuickSight for visualization.","Configure an Amazon S3 Lifecycle policy to transition user activity data older than 60 days to the S3 Infrequent Access (S3-IA) storage class and then permanently delete it after 2 years.","Archive older user activity data using Amazon Redshift Serverless instead of Amazon S3. Fetch data using the Amazon Athena SQL query editor.","Store all user activity data indefinitely in the DynamoDB table. Configure the table to use the DynamoDB Standard-Infrequent Access (Standard-IA) table class.","Use Amazon EMR Serverless to query the data in the S3 data lake. Set the S3 Lifecycle policy to transition the older user activity data to the S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA tech startup uses various AWS services to collect, process, and store user activity data for its mobile payment service. Its data engineering team is tasked with designing a cost-effective solution utilizing Amazon S3, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon QuickSight.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe data from the last 60 days must be fetched from the DynamoDB table and displayed on a custom QuickSight dashboard for monthly analysis. The older user activity data must be archived in a data lake for 2 years before being purged. During this period, the startup is expected to occasionally query the data using Amazon Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of steps provides the MOST cost-effective solution? (Select THREE.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon DynamoDB TTL is a feature that allows you to define a per-item timestamp to determine when an item is no longer needed. Once the timestamp expires, the item is marked for deletion from your table. This is a cost-effective way to reduce storage usage by deleting items that are no longer needed. In this scenario, setting a TTL of 60 days for items in the DynamoDB table would ensure that only the most recent 60 days of data are stored in the table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue is a fully managed ETL (extract, transform, and load) service that makes it easy to prepare and load your data for analytics. You can create and run an ETL job with a few clicks in the AWS Management Console. AWS Glue generates the ETL code that is customizable, reusable, and portable. Once your ETL job is ready, you can schedule it to run on AWS Glue’s fully managed, scale-out Apache Spark environment. AWS Glue provides a console and API operations to set up and manage your extract, transform, and load (ETL) workload. It also provides a Jupyter Notebook server you can use to develop your ETL scripts interactively.\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2c4b89ab3c5ec9ea26b03e1acd076147c35de53b57956d6eb1bd6adf737f777e.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 offers storage management features that let you organize your data and configure finely-tuned access controls to meet specific business, organizational, and compliance requirements. Amazon S3 is designed for 99.999999999% (11 9’s) of durability and stores data for millions of applications for companies worldwide. S3 Lifecycle policies make transitioning data from S3 to Archive or deleting old data versions simple and cost-effective.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Use the Time to Live (TTL) feature to automatically delete items older than 60 days from the DynamoDB table.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Set up an AWS Glue job to extract the last 60 days of data from the DynamoDB table and load it into Amazon QuickSight for visualization.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Configure an Amazon S3 Lifecycle policy to transition user activity data older than 60 days to the S3 Infrequent Access (S3-IA) storage class and then permanently delete it after 2 years.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Archive older user activity data using Amazon Redshift Serverless instead of Amazon S3. Fetch data using the Amazon Athena SQL query editor\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because Amazon Redshift is primarily a data warehousing solution designed for online analytic processing (OLAP) and not as a data lake. The scenario explicitly mentions that the older user activity data must be archived in a data lake and not in a data warehouse. Amazon S3 is cheaper than Redshift for long-term data storage, making it a more cost-effective choice for archiving older user activity data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore all user activity data indefinitely in the DynamoDB table. Configure the table to use the DynamoDB Standard-Infrequent Access (Standard-IA) table class\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because DynamoDB charges you for read and write capacity, as well as the amount of data stored; thus, storing data indefinitely will effectively increase the operating cost of the solution. Using a Standard-Infrequent Access (Standard-IA) table class is more suitable for data that is meant to be stored for a long time and not for items that are meant to expire in a period of 60 days. It’s more cost-effective to move older data to cheaper storage solutions like S3 and enable the DynamoDB table\u0027s TTL feature for automatic data deletion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Use Amazon EMR Serverless to query the data in the S3 data lake. Set the S3 Lifecycle policy to transition the older user activity data to the S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Although Amazon EMR can be used for querying data in an S3 data lake, the scenario specifies that the startup intends to use Amazon Athena for this purpose; therefore, using EMR would be unnecessary. While storing the data in S3 Glacier Deep Archive provides the cheapest storage option, it would entail a significant operational overhead since you have to restore the Glacier objects before you can access them.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TTL.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TTL.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:15":{"id":"e:2:15","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 15","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue ETL job to load the Amazon S3-based historical data into Redshift. Once the ad-hoc queries are run for the historic data, it can be removed from Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the Amazon Redshift COPY command to load the Amazon S3-based historical data into Amazon Redshift. Once the ad-hoc queries are run for the historic data, it can be removed from Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to create Amazon Redshift cluster tables pointing to the underlying historical data in Amazon S3. The analytics team can then query this historical data to cross-reference with the daily reports from Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSetup access to the historical data via Amazon Athena. The analytics team can run historical data queries on Amazon Athena and continue the daily reporting on Amazon Redshift. In case the reports need to be cross-referenced, the analytics team needs to export these in flat files and then do further analysis\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn IT company has built a custom data warehousing solution for a retail organization by using Amazon Redshift. As part of the cost optimizations, the company wants to move any historical data (any data older than a year) into Amazon S3, as the daily analytical reports consume data for just the last year. However, the analysts want to retain the ability to cross-reference this historical data along with the daily reports.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company wants to develop a solution with the LEAST amount of effort and MINIMUM cost. Which option would you recommend to facilitate this use-case?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to create Amazon Redshift cluster tables pointing to the underlying historical data in Amazon S3. The analytics team can then query this historical data to cross-reference with the daily reports from Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift is a fully-managed petabyte-scale cloud-based data warehouse product designed for large-scale data set storage and analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUsing Amazon Redshift Spectrum, you can efficiently query and retrieve structured and semistructured data from files in Amazon S3 without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift Spectrum resides on dedicated Amazon Redshift servers that are independent of your cluster. The Redshift Spectrum pushes many compute-intensive tasks, such as predicate filtering and aggregation, down to the Redshift Spectrum layer. Thus, Amazon Redshift Spectrum queries use much less of your cluster\u0027s processing capacity than other queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eRedshift Spectrum Overview:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/16f0325ad19dfed538cd42a4ec50e189cca36f8f333835d5878f4e59acabd972.gif\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/16f0325ad19dfed538cd42a4ec50e189cca36f8f333835d5878f4e59acabd972.gif\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSetup access to the historical data via Amazon Athena. The analytics team can run historical data queries on Amazon Athena and continue the daily reporting on Amazon Redshift. In case the reports need to be cross-referenced, the analytics team needs to export these in flat files and then do further analysis\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Athena is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data directly in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. Athena is serverless, so there is no infrastructure to set up or manage, and customers pay only for the queries they run. You can use Athena to process logs, perform ad-hoc analysis, and run interactive queries.\nProviding access to historical data via Athena would mean that historical data reconciliation would become difficult as the daily report would still be produced via Redshift. Such a setup is cumbersome to maintain on a day-to-day basis. Hence the option to use Athena is ruled out.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the Amazon Redshift COPY command to load the Amazon S3-based historical data into Amazon Redshift. Once the ad-hoc queries are run for the historic data, it can be removed from Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue ETL job to load the Amazon S3-based historical data into Redshift. Once the ad-hoc queries are run for the historic data, it can be removed from Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eLoading historical data into Amazon Redshift via COPY command or AWS Glue ETL job would cost heavily for a one-time ad-hoc process. The same result can be achieved more cost-efficiently by using Amazon Redshift Spectrum. Therefore both these options to load historical data into Redshift are also incorrect for the given use-case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html#c-spectrum-overviewhttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html#c-spectrum-overview\nhttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://ifg.udemy.com/course/practice-exams-aws-certified-data-engineer-associate-r/learn/#\"\u003eamazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:3":{"id":"e:1:3","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 3","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure a Spark Streaming application on an Amazon EMR cluster to poll the S3 source bucket, process uploaded files in batches, and write the three outputs to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure a Step Functions state machine as the EventBridge target, and use the state machine to start an AWS Glue workflow that runs three AWS Glue jobs in parallel\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an Amazon ECS service on AWS Fargate to watch the source bucket, start three container tasks for each uploaded file, and write the generated DataFrames to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue workflow with an EventBridge event trigger, and run three AWS Glue Spark jobs in parallel to process the file and write the outputs to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company receives a 900-GB claims extract in an Amazon S3 source bucket. When the file arrives, the company must start a data pipeline that applies business rules and creates three separate DataFrames for claims, provider summaries, and payer exceptions. The pipeline must write all three outputs to a different S3 bucket in parallel. The pipeline must be the target of an Amazon EventBridge rule that matches new file uploads to the source bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST maintenance overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue workflow with an EventBridge event trigger, and run three AWS Glue Spark jobs in parallel to process the file and write the outputs to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue is well suited for this requirement because AWS Glue Spark jobs can process large datasets and create DataFrames without requiring the company to manage a Spark cluster. The pipeline can use three AWS Glue jobs, where each job applies the required business logic and writes one output dataset to Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue workflows can orchestrate multiple AWS Glue jobs as one pipeline. The workflow can start from an EventBridge event that matches a new object upload to the source S3 bucket, which satisfies the requirement that the pipeline be the target of an EventBridge rule.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least maintenance overhead because it uses AWS Glue as the serverless ETL engine and AWS Glue workflow orchestration directly. The company does not need to manage EMR clusters, build custom container orchestration, add unnecessary Step Functions orchestration, or force Lambda functions to process a very large file.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b663d2711732a4dc6c1f7e41a4ff2373a79802947059bd709f8a78793c3d7561.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b663d2711732a4dc6c1f7e41a4ff2373a79802947059bd709f8a78793c3d7561.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-serverless-event-driven-workflow-with-aws-glue-and-amazon-eventbridge/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-serverless-event-driven-workflow-with-aws-glue-and-amazon-eventbridge/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7cbb29a71fce77b7c7106dba4010eb9d1755316d982fc454cc669dde5614f4d6.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7cbb29a71fce77b7c7106dba4010eb9d1755316d982fc454cc669dde5614f4d6.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-serverless-event-driven-workflow-with-aws-glue-and-amazon-eventbridge/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-serverless-event-driven-workflow-with-aws-glue-and-amazon-eventbridge/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure a Spark Streaming application on an Amazon EMR cluster to poll the S3 source bucket, process uploaded files in batches, and write the three outputs to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR can run Apache Spark workloads and can process large files from Amazon S3. Spark can also create DataFrames and write multiple outputs to S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option uses an EMR cluster and a Spark Streaming application for an S3 file-arrival batch workload. The company would need to manage cluster configuration, scaling, patching, and lifecycle operations, which adds more maintenance than AWS Glue workflows and serverless Glue Spark jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an Amazon ECS service on AWS Fargate to watch the source bucket, start three container tasks for each uploaded file, and write the generated DataFrames to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon ECS on AWS Fargate can run containerized processing tasks without managing EC2 instances. The company could build containers that read the uploaded file and write transformed outputs to Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option requires custom code to watch S3 events, coordinate the three tasks, manage task failures, and handle large-file processing behavior. AWS Glue provides a managed Spark-based ETL environment and workflow orchestration that better matches the requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure a Step Functions state machine as the EventBridge target, and use the state machine to start an AWS Glue workflow that runs three AWS Glue jobs in parallel\u003c/strong\u003e - Step Functions can orchestrate workflows and can start AWS Glue workflows or AWS Glue jobs. It is useful when a process needs broader state management, branching, retries, approvals, or coordination across many AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, AWS Glue workflows can already be triggered by EventBridge and can run the three Glue jobs in parallel. Adding Step Functions creates an extra orchestration layer and increases operational overhead without adding required capability.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/starting-workflow-eventbridge.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/starting-workflow-eventbridge.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-serverless-event-driven-workflow-with-aws-glue-and-amazon-eventbridge/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-serverless-event-driven-workflow-with-aws-glue-and-amazon-eventbridge/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:50":{"id":"e:4:50","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 50","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue crawlers to infer the Teradata schema, create matching Amazon Redshift tables, and run Glue jobs for the migration\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS DMS Schema Conversion to convert the Teradata schema, and use AWS DMS to migrate the warehouse data to Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS SCT to convert the Teradata schema to Amazon Redshift, and use AWS DMS to migrate the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS DMS to migrate the Teradata data first, and rely on automatic schema conversion during the Amazon Redshift load process\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics company runs a large on-premises Teradata data warehouse that stores merchandising, loyalty, and store operations data. The company wants to migrate the warehouse to Amazon Redshift so analysts can use a managed cloud data warehouse for reporting and forecasting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer must convert the Teradata schema to an Amazon Redshift-compatible schema and then migrate the warehouse data. The company wants to complete the migration with the least operational effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS SCT to convert the Teradata schema to Amazon Redshift, and use AWS DMS to migrate the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Schema Conversion Tool is the correct schema conversion tool for this scenario because the migration is from Teradata to Amazon Redshift. AWS SCT supports converting data warehouse schemas from Teradata to Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS SCT can assess the source schema, convert compatible objects, identify items that need manual action, and help create an Amazon Redshift-compatible target schema. This reduces the manual effort required compared with rewriting the Teradata schema by hand.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAfter the target schema exists, AWS DMS can migrate data to Amazon Redshift for supported migration paths. This combination uses AWS-managed migration tooling for the schema and data movement work, which provides the least operational effort among the available choices.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ac5e1666bf1fcd13841f91abed0940651e7011d4315b5057ed46d8e77f879af6.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ac5e1666bf1fcd13841f91abed0940651e7011d4315b5057ed46d8e77f879af6.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/assess-and-convert-teradata-database-objects-to-amazon-redshift-using-the-aws-schema-conversion-tool-cli/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/assess-and-convert-teradata-database-objects-to-amazon-redshift-using-the-aws-schema-conversion-tool-cli/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b4a5f571ff879cda63fe625170cb90e22f788479dd125fbecfd408f1d4f06f97.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b4a5f571ff879cda63fe625170cb90e22f788479dd125fbecfd408f1d4f06f97.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/assess-and-convert-teradata-database-objects-to-amazon-redshift-using-the-aws-schema-conversion-tool-cli/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/assess-and-convert-teradata-database-objects-to-amazon-redshift-using-the-aws-schema-conversion-tool-cli/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS DMS Schema Conversion to convert the Teradata schema, and use AWS DMS to migrate the warehouse data to Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS DMS Schema Conversion is a managed schema conversion capability within AWS DMS. It can convert supported source schemas to target engines such as Amazon Redshift for the conversion paths that DMS Schema Conversion supports.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that the current DMS Schema Conversion supported conversion paths do not list Teradata to Amazon Redshift. For a Teradata data warehouse migration to Redshift, AWS SCT is the appropriate schema conversion tool.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS DMS to migrate the Teradata data first, and rely on automatic schema conversion during the Amazon Redshift load process\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS DMS can migrate data from supported sources to supported targets, and Amazon Redshift can act as a DMS target. This is useful after the target schema is available.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because AWS DMS data migration does not replace the need to convert the Teradata schema to an Amazon Redshift-compatible schema. The target schema should be converted and created before the data migration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue crawlers to infer the Teradata schema, create matching Amazon Redshift tables, and run Glue jobs for the migration\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue crawlers can infer schemas from supported data sources and update metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. AWS Glue jobs can also transform and load data into target systems.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not provide the correct data warehouse schema conversion capability. Crawlers do not convert Teradata database objects, SQL dialects, stored procedures, or warehouse schema definitions into Amazon Redshift-compatible objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/SchemaConversionTool/latest/userguide/CHAP_Welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/SchemaConversionTool/latest/userguide/CHAP_Welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/assess-and-convert-teradata-database-objects-to-amazon-redshift-using-the-aws-schema-conversion-tool-cli/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/assess-and-convert-teradata-database-objects-to-amazon-redshift-using-the-aws-schema-conversion-tool-cli/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_SchemaConversion.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_SchemaConversion.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Target.Redshift.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Target.Redshift.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/Welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/Welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:24":{"id":"e:5:24","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 24","correct":[1],"answers":["Create an S3 Event Notifications to trigger a Lambda function for all actions on the source S3 bucket and directly log events into the destination S3 bucket.","In AWS CloudTrail, configure a trail that logs write-only data events from the source S3 bucket. Apply filters to capture events from the source bucket\u0027s Arn and direct these logs to the destination S3 bucket.","Create an S3 Event Notification that triggers a Lambda function in response to write-related activities on the source S3 bucket. Configure the Lambda function to write events to a Kinesis Data Firehose steam, which then saves logs into the designated S3 bucket.","Track write-only management events in AWS CloudTrail from the source S3 bucket. Filter them without a specific prefix and send the logs to the destination S3 bucket."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn organization uses an Amazon S3 bucket located in the \u003ccode\u003eus-east-1\u003c/code\u003e region to store legal documents. The organization wants to log all write operations performed on this bucket. These logs must be stored in a separate S3 bucket, also in the \u003ccode\u003eus-east-1\u003c/code\u003e region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow can this be achieved in the LEAST operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS CloudTrail\u003c/strong\u003e enables auditing, security monitoring, and operational troubleshooting by tracking user activity and API usage. CloudTrail logs, continuously monitors, and retains account activity related to actions across your AWS infrastructure, giving you control over storage, analysis, and remediation actions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS CloudTrail’s \u003cstrong\u003emanagement events\u003c/strong\u003e provide visibility into management or control plane operations that are performed on AWS resources, such as creating, modifying, or deleting resources. This includes API calls and console sign-ins. Management events are enabled by default when you create a trail.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS CloudTrail’s \u003cstrong\u003edata events\u003c/strong\u003e (also known as “data plane operations”) provide insights into resource or data plane operations performed within resources, such as S3 object uploads and downloads or Lambda function executions. These are often high-volume activities. Data events are disabled by default, and you need to explicitly configure the trail to record them for supported resources. Recording data events incur additional costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/cb4a44028b43efd587cf528b6fba9aed2d49042dca91fe4675275e2f699c9319.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eGiven the scenario’s requirement for minimal operational effort and direct logging of write operations to another S3 bucket in the same region, the best solution is to utilize AWS CloudTrail’s data event logging capabilities. This approach automates the logging process directly, without the need for intermediary services or additional programming, aligning perfectly with the need for operational simplicity and direct logging.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIn AWS CloudTrail, configure a trail that logs write-only data events from the source S3 bucket. Apply filters to capture events from the source bucket’s Arn and direct these logs to the destination S3 bucket.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an S3 Event Notification that triggers a Lambda function in response to write-related activities on the source S3 bucket. Configure the Lambda function to write events to a Kinesis Data Firehose steam, which then saves logs into the designated S3 bucket \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. It introduces unnecessary complexity and operational overhead in writing and maintaining the Lambda function, compared to the direct logging capability provided by CloudTrail.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTrack write-only management events in AWS CloudTrail from the source S3 bucket. Filter them without a specific prefix and send the logs to the destination S3 bucket \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because management events do not include the detailed data-level operations (such as write operations) that are captured by data events, making it less suitable for this specific logging requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an S3 Event Notifications to trigger a Lambda function for all actions on the source S3 bucket and directly log events into the destination S3 bucket \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. While operationally simple, it does not leverage the built-in capabilities of CloudTrail for direct and automated logging of S3 bucket activities, thus not fulfilling the requirement as effectively as the CloudTrail data events option.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/cloudtrail-data-management-events\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/cloudtrail-data-management-events\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these AWS CloudTrail Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-cloudtrail/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-cloudtrail/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-18":{"id":"p:4-18","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 18","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to stream data to Amazon Redshift. Create a business intelligence dashboard by using Amazon Athena that has Amazon Redshift as a data source\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream data to Amazon Redshift. Create a business intelligence dashboard by using Amazon QuickSight that has Amazon Redshift as a data source\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream data to Amazon S3. Create a business intelligence dashboard by using Amazon QuickSight that has Amazon Athena as a data source\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to stream data to Amazon S3. Create a business intelligence dashboard by using Amazon QuickSight that has Amazon Athena as a data source\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Redshift có thể nhận streaming từ Kinesis Data Streams, nhưng Athena là công cụ truy vấn chứ không phải công cụ tạo dashboard BI. Phần dashboard khiến lựa chọn này sai.","Redshift cung cấp kho dữ liệu cho SQL và truy vấn phân tích; QuickSight kết nối Redshift để làm dashboard. Firehose đảm nhiệm chuyển dữ liệu với ít vận hành. Lưu ý Firehose nạp Redshift qua S3 và COPY, không bỏ qua S3 trung gian.","S3 và Athena phù hợp phân tích data lake, nhưng đề nhấn mạnh kho dữ liệu hỗ trợ sửa dữ liệu bằng SQL và truy vấn phân tích nhanh. Trong các phương án, Redshift kết hợp QuickSight phù hợp hơn; khả năng sửa dữ liệu của Athena còn phụ thuộc định dạng bảng.","Kinesis Data Streams không tự ghi trực tiếp vào S3 nếu thiếu consumer hoặc dịch vụ chuyển dữ liệu. Lựa chọn cũng không dùng kho dữ liệu Redshift cho nhu cầu phân tích nêu trong đề."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA trading firm collects daily stock trading data from exchanges and stores it in a data warehouse. The data engineering team at the firm needs a solution that streams data directly into the data repository but should also allow SQL-based data modifications when needed. The solution should facilitate complex analytical queries that execute in the fastest possible time. The solution should also offer a business intelligence dashboard that highlights any stock price anomalies.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions represents the best fit for the given scenario?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-29":{"id":"p:1-29","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 29","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a new DynamoDB table, enable Time to Live on the new table, migrate the existing transcript analysis records to the new table, and update the reporting application to use the new table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable Time to Live on the existing DynamoDB table and write an expiration timestamp attribute to each transcript analysis item\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable DynamoDB Streams on the table, invoke an AWS Lambda function for each stream record, and delete transcript analysis items when the function determines that the item has crossed the retention window\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge scheduled rule that invokes an AWS Lambda function each day to query and delete transcript analysis records that are older than the retention window\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["TTL có thể bật ngay trên bảng hiện tại. Tạo bảng mới và di chuyển dữ liệu không cần thiết, còn thêm rủi ro chuyển đổi ứng dụng.","Bật TTL và ghi thời điểm hết hạn dạng Unix timestamp cho từng item. Ứng dụng phải tính đúng 9 ngày làm việc; TTL xóa bất đồng bộ nên không phải cơ chế xóa đúng từng giây.","Streams phản ánh thay đổi item, không phát sự kiện chỉ vì một item vừa đủ tuổi. Lambda theo Streams không thay thế được lịch hết hạn của TTL.","Lambda theo lịch có thể tìm và xóa dữ liệu cũ nhưng cần mã, quyền và tài nguyên đọc/xóa. TTL giảm các bước quản lý này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA customer experience analytics company stores sentiment scores and topic extraction results for thousands of daily support chat transcripts in an Amazon DynamoDB table. A reporting application reads only the most recent 9 business days of results. The company wants to reduce storage costs by automatically removing items that are older than 9 business days.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:4":{"id":"e:2:4","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 4","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse a Condition element with the s3:prefix condition key to restrict all object access and use aws:CurrentTime with DateGreaterThanEquals and DateLessThanEquals operators to restrict the access window\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse a Resource element with S3 object ARN patterns that include wildcards for each approved prefix and use a Condition element with aws:CurrentTime and DateGreaterThan and DateLessThan operators\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse a Condition element with the s3:ResourceAccount condition key to restrict bucket ownership and add an explicit Deny statement outside the approved business-hours window\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse a Resource element with S3 bucket ARN patterns for each classification prefix and use a Condition element with aws:RequestTag to restrict access during the approved business-hours window\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA pharmaceutical company uses AWS Glue jobs to process research datasets stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company organizes the datasets by S3 prefixes that represent sensitivity levels such as public, internal, confidential, and regulated. The security team must allow each AWS Glue job role to access only the prefixes that match the job\u0027s approved data classification. The team must also restrict access to an approved business-hours window from 8 AM to 6 PM.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich elements must the company include in a custom IAM policy to meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse a Resource element with S3 object ARN patterns that include wildcards for each approved prefix and use a Condition element with aws:CurrentTime and DateGreaterThan and DateLessThan operators\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAn IAM policy can restrict Amazon S3 object-level permissions by listing specific S3 object ARNs in the Resource element. To grant access only to approved prefixes, the policy can use object ARN patterns such as arn:aws:s3:::example-bucket/confidential/* or arn:aws:s3:::example-bucket/regulated/*.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe policy can restrict the time window by using the AWS global condition key aws:CurrentTime with date condition operators such as DateGreaterThan and DateLessThan. This allows the security team to limit when requests from the AWS Glue job role can be authorized.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution matches both requirements. The Resource element scopes S3 access to the relevant classification prefixes, and the Condition element enforces time-based access by evaluating the request time during IAM authorization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse a Condition element with the s3:ResourceAccount condition key to restrict bucket ownership and add an explicit Deny statement outside the approved business-hours window\u003c/strong\u003e - The s3:ResourceAccount condition key helps restrict access based on the AWS account that owns the S3 resource. This can be useful in some cross-account access control scenarios.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this condition key does not restrict access to specific classification prefixes inside a bucket. The company needs prefix-specific object ARNs or prefix-aware list conditions, not only an account ownership check.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse a Condition element with the s3:prefix condition key to restrict all object access and use aws:CurrentTime with DateGreaterThanEquals and DateLessThanEquals operators to restrict the access window\u003c/strong\u003e - The s3:prefix condition key can restrict prefixes for bucket listing operations such as s3:ListBucket. It is useful when a policy must control which keys a principal can list in an S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, s3:prefix does not, by itself, restrict object-level actions such as s3:GetObject or s3:PutObject. The policy still needs S3 object ARN patterns in the Resource element to restrict actual object access to the approved prefixes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse a Resource element with S3 bucket ARN patterns for each classification prefix and use a Condition element with aws:RequestTag to restrict access during the approved business-hours window\u003c/strong\u003e - The Resource element must use correct S3 ARN formats. Bucket-level ARNs such as arn:aws:s3:::example-bucket apply to bucket-level actions, while object-level actions require object ARNs such as arn:aws:s3:::example-bucket/prefix/*.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe aws:RequestTag condition key evaluates tags that a request passes to a supported service action. It does not provide a general mechanism to restrict S3 object access to business hours.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/security_iam_service-with-iam.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/security_iam_service-with-iam.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/amazon-s3-policy-keys.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/amazon-s3-policy-keys.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_resource.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_resource.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_condition.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_condition.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_condition_operators.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_condition_operators.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/techniques-for-writing-least-privilege-iam-policies/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/techniques-for-writing-least-privilege-iam-policies/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-2":{"id":"p:1-2","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 2","correct":[1,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up an AWS Glue Python shell job that uses the Athena Boto3 client start_query_execution API call to execute the Athena queries programmatically\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up a workflow in AWS Step Functions that incorporates two states. Configure the initial state prior to triggering the Lambda function. Establish the subsequent state as a Wait state, designed to periodically verify the completion status of the Athena query via the Athena Boto3 get_query_execution API call. Ensure the workflow is configured to initiate the subsequent query once the preceding one concludes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an AWS Lambda function that uses the Athena Boto3 client start_query_execution API call to execute the Athena queries programmatically\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up a workflow in AWS Step Functions that incorporates two states. Configure the initial state prior to triggering the AWS Glue Python shell job. Establish the subsequent state as a Wait state, designed to periodically verify the completion status of the Athena query via the Athena Boto3 get_query_execution API call. Ensure the workflow is configured to initiate the subsequent query once the preceding one concludes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop a Python shell script in AWS Glue to implement a sleep timer that checks every 5 minutes if the current Athena query has successfully completed. Set up the script so that it triggers the subsequent query once the current one is confirmed to have finished\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Glue Python shell có thể gọi API Athena, nhưng dùng cả một tác vụ Glue chỉ để gửi lệnh sẽ tốn chi phí hơn Lambda. Đề yêu cầu phương án tiết kiệm nhất.","Step Functions giữ trạng thái và chờ giữa các lần kiểm tra. Gọi get_query_execution để biết truy vấn đã xong rồi mới chạy câu tiếp theo; không cần giữ Lambda chạy suốt thời gian chờ.","Lambda chỉ gọi start_query_execution rồi kết thúc. Truy vấn tiếp tục chạy trong Athena, nên thời gian truy vấn vượt 15 phút không có nghĩa Lambda phải chạy quá giới hạn.","Ý tưởng điều phối bằng Step Functions phù hợp, nhưng Glue Python shell là thành phần đắt hơn cần thiết. Thay phần gửi lệnh bằng Lambda sẽ đáp ứng yêu cầu với chi phí thấp hơn.","Giữ một tác vụ Glue chạy lâu chỉ để ngủ rồi kiểm tra định kỳ làm lãng phí tài nguyên. Step Functions có trạng thái Wait phù hợp hơn cho thời gian chờ."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer needs to set up a daily execution of Amazon Athena queries, each of which may take longer than 15 minutes to run. What are the two most cost-effective steps to achieve this requirement? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:2":{"id":"e:3:2","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 2","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eProvision an Amazon EC2 on-demand instance to run the database rollover script to be run via an OS-based weekly cron expression\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a time-based schedule option within an AWS Glue job to invoke itself every week and run the database rollover script\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eProvision an Amazon EC2 spot instance to run the database rollover script to be run via an OS-based weekly cron expression\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSchedule a weekly Amazon EventBridge event cron expression to invoke an AWS Lambda function that runs the database rollover job\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA social media startup uses AWS Cloud to manage its IT infrastructure. The data engineering team at the startup wants to perform weekly database rollovers for a MySQL database server using a serverless cron job that typically takes about 5 minutes to execute the database rollover script written in Python. The database rollover will archive the past week’s data from the production database to keep the database small while still keeping its data accessible.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you recommend as the MOST cost-efficient and reliable solution?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSchedule a weekly Amazon EventBridge event cron expression to invoke an AWS Lambda function that runs the database rollover job\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lambda lets you run code without provisioning or managing servers. You pay only for the compute time you consume. AWS Lambda supports standard rate and cron expressions for frequencies of up to once per minute.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSchedule expressions using rate or cron:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/743d563de6bbf088ab89a4a0c5fcacc38382f96e3f0f04442e354fe964fdddbb.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/743d563de6bbf088ab89a4a0c5fcacc38382f96e3f0f04442e354fe964fdddbb.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a time-based schedule option within an AWS Glue job to invoke itself every week and run the database rollover script\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy for customers to prepare and load their data for analytics. AWS Glue job is meant to be used for batch ETL data processing and it\u0027s not the right fit for running a database rollover script. Although AWS Glue is also serverless, AWS Lambda is a more cost-effective option compared to AWS Glue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eProvision an Amazon EC2 spot instance to run the database rollover script to be run via an OS-based weekly cron expression\u003c/strong\u003e - A Spot Instance is an unused Amazon EC2 instance that is available for less than the On-Demand price. Because Spot Instances enable you to request unused Amazon EC2 instances at steep discounts, you can lower your Amazon EC2 costs significantly (up to 90% off the On-Demand price).\nAs the Spot Instance runs whenever capacity is available, there is no guarantee that the weekly job will be executed during the defined time window. Additionally, the given use case requires a serverless solution, therefore this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eProvision an Amazon EC2 on-demand instance to run the database rollover script to be run via an OS-based weekly cron expression\u003c/strong\u003e - An Amazon EC2 on-demand instance can certainly be used to run the database rollover script which is run via an OS-based weekly cron expression. However, the given use case requires a serverless solution, therefore this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-cloudwatchevents-expressions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-cloudwatchevents-expressions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:38":{"id":"e:2:38","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 38","correct":[1,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eIngest the dataset into Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Process the stream data using a Lambda function that masks the PII data before writing the output in Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUtilize the Detect PII transform in AWS Glue Studio to identify and mask the PII. Use an AWS Step Functions state machine to orchestrate a data pipeline to ingest the data into the S3 data lake\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIngest the dataset into Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose. Leverage a data transformation Lambda function to mask the PII data in the delivery stream and write the transformed output in Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun an AWS Glue DataBrew data profile job to identify and suggest potential PII columns present in the dataset. Execute a DataBrew job to apply the transformations to handle the sensitive columns in the entire dataset and store the masked data securely in Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUtilize the Detect PII transform in AWS Glue Studio to identify the PII. Set up a rule in AWS Glue Data Quality to mask the PII. Use an AWS Step Functions state machine to orchestrate a data pipeline to ingest the data into the S3 data lake\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is tasked with using AWS services to ingest a dataset into an Amazon S3 data lake. Upon profiling the dataset manually, the engineer finds that it contains personally identifiable information (PII). The engineer needs to devise a method to both profile the dataset and mask the PII effectively.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options can be independently used to fulfill this requirement with the least amount of operational effort? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize the Detect PII transform in AWS Glue Studio to identify and mask the PII. Use an AWS Step Functions state machine to orchestrate a data pipeline to ingest the data into the S3 data lake\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe Detect PII transform identifies Personal Identifiable Information (PII) in your data source. You choose the PII entity to identify, how you want the data to be scanned, and what to do with the PII entity that has been identified by the Detect PII transform.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe Detect PII transform provides the ability to detect, mask, or remove entities that you define, or are pre-defined by AWS. This enables you to increase compliance and reduce liability. For example, you may want to ensure that no personally identifiable information exists in your data that can be read and want to mask social security numbers with a fixed string (such as xxx-xx-xxxx), phone numbers, or addresses. You can then use an AWS Step Functions state machine to orchestrate a data pipeline to ingest the data into the S3 data lake.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2f51e227798a84a72d97c62b3e0b673c6e26cb37a0d076141bb5d5d53a6f9844.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2f51e227798a84a72d97c62b3e0b673c6e26cb37a0d076141bb5d5d53a6f9844.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun an AWS Glue DataBrew data profile job to identify and suggest potential PII columns present in the dataset. Execute a DataBrew job to apply the transformations to handle the sensitive columns in the entire dataset and store the masked data securely in Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew, a visual data preparation tool, allows users to identify and handle sensitive data by applying advanced transformations like redaction, replacement, encryption, and decryption on their personally identifiable information (PII) data, and other types of data they deem sensitive. DataBrew has PII data handling transformations, which enable you to apply data masking, encryption, decryption, and other operations on your sensitive data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can run a data profile job to identify and suggest potential PII columns present in a dataset. Then, you can target PII columns in a DataBrew project and apply various transformations to handle the sensitive columns existing in the dataset. Finally, run a DataBrew job to apply the transformations on the entire dataset and store the processed, masked, and encrypted data securely in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/27c28e33b15db5262b0a0e6e93b086591408f7c0a23f4dfec7659940eec397fc.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/27c28e33b15db5262b0a0e6e93b086591408f7c0a23f4dfec7659940eec397fc.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIngest the dataset into Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose. Leverage a data transformation Lambda function to mask the PII data in the delivery stream and write the transformed output in Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIngest the dataset into Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Process the stream data using a Lambda function that masks the PII data before writing the output in Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou will need to write custom code in the Lambda function to mask the PII data, which represents unnecessary operational effort for the given use case, so both these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize the Detect PII transform in AWS Glue Studio to identify the PII. Set up a rule in AWS Glue Data Quality to mask the PII. Use an AWS Step Functions state machine to orchestrate a data pipeline to ingest the data into the S3 data lake\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue Data Quality is a feature of AWS Glue that reduces manual data quality effort by automatically measuring and monitoring the quality of data in data lakes and pipelines. In data lakes, it then automatically recommends data quality rules. You can modify these rules, add additional rules from built-in rule types, and configure actions to alert teams when quality issues occur.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Data Quality currently supports 18 built-in rule types under four categories:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eConsistency rules check if data across different columns agrees by looking at column correlations.\nAccuracy rules check if record counts meet a set threshold and if columns are not empty, match certain patterns, have valid data types, and have valid values.\nIntegrity rules check if duplicates exist in a dataset.\nCompleteness rules check if data in your datasets do not have missing values.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Data Quality does not have any pre-built rule to mask the PII. You could create a custom rule to mask the PII, but that involves additional operational effort, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-pii-data-identification-and-handling-using-aws-glue-databrew/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-pii-data-identification-and-handling-using-aws-glue-databrew/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:41":{"id":"e:4:41","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 41","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a DynamoDB Accelerator cache for the table and configure the dashboard to retrieve completed courier deliveries from the cached base table queries\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a global secondary index that uses CourierID as the partition key and DeliveryStatus as the sort key\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a global secondary index that uses CustomerID as the partition key and DeliveryStatus as the sort key\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a local secondary index on CourierID so the courier dashboard can query completed deliveries by courier without scanning the base table\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA food delivery company stores records for all deliveries in an Amazon DynamoDB table. The table includes attributes such as DeliveryID, CustomerID, CourierID, DeliveryStatus, PickupTime, and DropoffTime.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe table currently contains several billion items. The table uses DeliveryID as the partition key and PickupTime as the sort key. The company wants to build a courier dashboard that shows each courier the deliveries that the courier has completed, based on DeliveryStatus. The solution must retrieve the required data without scanning the entire table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a global secondary index that uses CourierID as the partition key and DeliveryStatus as the sort key\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA global secondary index is the correct solution because the required access pattern does not match the base table key design. The base table is partitioned by DeliveryID and sorted by PickupTime, which is useful for retrieving a specific delivery but not for retrieving all completed deliveries for a specific courier.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA GSI can use a partition key and sort key that are different from the base table. By using CourierID as the GSI partition key, the application can query all records for one courier. By using DeliveryStatus as the GSI sort key, the application can narrow the result set to completed deliveries for that courier.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution avoids scanning the entire table, which is critical because the table contains billions of items. The dashboard can use a Query operation against the GSI and retrieve only the items that match the courier and status access pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/41c119ca154013b676f6fb3e192eba3f7a15208139413a2517efb199a7a878fc.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/41c119ca154013b676f6fb3e192eba3f7a15208139413a2517efb199a7a878fc.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/how-to-design-amazon-dynamodb-global-secondary-indexes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/how-to-design-amazon-dynamodb-global-secondary-indexes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8874acc9a25fe5f2990a5c43c352d7602e5c2c048672d63fdc9b41c357fbfc8c.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8874acc9a25fe5f2990a5c43c352d7602e5c2c048672d63fdc9b41c357fbfc8c.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/how-to-design-amazon-dynamodb-global-secondary-indexes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/how-to-design-amazon-dynamodb-global-secondary-indexes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/10fcdda5929e0f0c6e8a507197e9e998d4e6667e9d40c4d5fc2367388ac845d2.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/10fcdda5929e0f0c6e8a507197e9e998d4e6667e9d40c4d5fc2367388ac845d2.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/how-to-design-amazon-dynamodb-global-secondary-indexes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/how-to-design-amazon-dynamodb-global-secondary-indexes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a local secondary index on CourierID so the courier dashboard can query completed deliveries by courier without scanning the base table\u003c/strong\u003e - A local secondary index can provide an alternate sort key for items that share the same partition key as the base table. This can be useful when the application still queries by the base table partition key but needs a different sort order.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, an LSI cannot use CourierID as a different partition key. The base table partition key is DeliveryID, so an LSI would not support the required access pattern of querying completed deliveries by CourierID.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a global secondary index that uses CustomerID as the partition key and DeliveryStatus as the sort key\u003c/strong\u003e - A GSI that uses CustomerID and DeliveryStatus can support access patterns for customer-specific delivery history. For example, it could help an application show a customer all completed or canceled deliveries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the requirement is to show completed deliveries for each courier. CustomerID is not the correct partition key for a courier dashboard, so this index would not efficiently retrieve the required records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a DynamoDB Accelerator cache for the table and configure the dashboard to retrieve completed courier deliveries from the cached base table queries\u003c/strong\u003e - DynamoDB Accelerator can improve read performance for repeated access patterns by caching DynamoDB responses. It can be useful when applications repeatedly request the same items.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, DAX does not change the table\u0027s key design or create a new query access pattern. If the dashboard cannot efficiently query by CourierID and DeliveryStatus, caching the inefficient access pattern does not solve the root issue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/SecondaryIndexes.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/SecondaryIndexes.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/GSI.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/GSI.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/LSI.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/LSI.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Query.FilterExpression.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Query.FilterExpression.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/how-to-design-amazon-dynamodb-global-secondary-indexes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/how-to-design-amazon-dynamodb-global-secondary-indexes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:32":{"id":"e:3:32","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 32","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse an exclude pattern for the Glue crawler to filter out the unwanted files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCompressed files, like Apache Parquet, take longer to crawl. Instead, use uncompressed files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the \u003ccode\u003eScanning rate\u003c/code\u003e parameter of Glue Crawler to a higher value for the Crawler to complete the scan faster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSplit larger files into smaller ones, to reduce the overhead of reading large files for the Glue Crawler\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare company collects user health information on a daily basis and stores this data on Amazon S3 which is then queried by Amazon Athena for analysis. Any user health information older than a week is never used in the queries. The data engineering team at the company has now set up a Glue crawler to automate the process but the crawler has been running for several hours and is still unable to identify the schema of the data store.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options can be used to fix this issue?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse an exclude pattern for the Glue crawler to filter out the unwanted files\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAn exclude pattern tells the crawler to skip certain files or paths. Exclude patterns reduce the number of files that the crawler must list, making the crawler run faster. For example, use an exclude pattern to exclude metafiles and files that have already been crawled.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eMore on crawler exclude patterns:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1a543ffa2005e8584f3d8babf21c0c1f4100cfb6ffe7521342f5fff4ce8bfe8b.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1a543ffa2005e8584f3d8babf21c0c1f4100cfb6ffe7521342f5fff4ce8bfe8b.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/define-crawler.html#crawler-data-stores-exclude\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/define-crawler.html#crawler-data-stores-exclude\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCompressed files, like Apache Parquet, take longer to crawl. Instead, use uncompressed files\u003c/strong\u003e - Compressed files take longer to crawl. That\u0027s because the crawler must download the file and decompress it before reading the first megabyte or listing the file. For Apache Parquet, Apache Avro, and Apache ORC files, the crawler doesn\u0027t crawl the first megabyte. Instead, the crawler reads the metadata stored in each file. So this option is not relevant for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange the \u003ccode\u003eScanning rate\u003c/code\u003e parameter of Glue Crawler to a higher value for the Crawler to complete the scan faster\u003c/strong\u003e - Scanning rate specifies the percentage of the configured read capacity units to use by the AWS Glue crawler. This parameter is available only for DynamoDB data stores.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSplit larger files into smaller ones, to reduce the overhead of reading large files for the Glue Crawler\u003c/strong\u003e - This statement is incorrect. It takes more time to crawl a large number of small files than a small number of large files. That\u0027s because the crawler must list each file and must read the first megabyte of each new file.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/long-running-glue-crawler/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/long-running-glue-crawler/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/define-crawler.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/define-crawler.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:20":{"id":"e:7:20","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 20","correct":[0,4],"answers":["Utilize the AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT) to adapt the source schema and data definition language (DDL) script from the external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift.","Use the Amazon Athena notebook to adapt the source schema and data definition language (DDL) script from the external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift.","Use the Amazon Athena SQL query editor to transfer the data from the external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift.","\u003cspan\u003eExport the data from the external cloud data warehouse into Amazon S3 as flat delimited text files. Then, use the Amazon Redshift SQL query editor with the \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e command to transfer the data from Amazon S3 to Amazon Redshift.\u003c/span\u003e","Leverage AWS Glue Studio to transfer the data from the external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team at a healthcare startup is working on a project to analyze patient health records. They have a significant amount of patient health records stored in an external cloud data warehouse. The team’s goal is to migrate these large datasets to Amazon Redshift for advanced analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of steps would be the most efficient way to achieve this with MINIMAL effort? (Select TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS SCT is a tool that significantly reduces the effort to migrate to Amazon Redshift by automatically converting the source schema and a majority of the custom code to a format compatible with Amazon Redshift. The converted code can be in the form of views, stored procedures, and functions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn this scenario, the team needs to migrate patient health records from an external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift. The first step in this process is to ensure that the schema and DDL scripts of the external cloud data warehouse are compatible with Amazon Redshift. This is where AWS SCT comes in. By using AWS SCT, the team can automatically convert the source schema and DDL scripts to a format that Amazon Redshift can understand, thereby reducing the manual effort required in the migration process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4772ab5110b9cce2b7d202d0bc41dd23f9309483822cf0ef1de5e8aa80215d2d.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Studio is a visual interface for AWS Glue that simplifies the process of creating, running, and monitoring ETL jobs. ETL stands for Extract, Transform, and Load, which are the three main steps in data integration processes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the given scenario, the team needs to migrate patient health records from an external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift. After the schemas and DDL scripts have been converted using AWS SCT, the next step is to transfer the data itself. AWS Glue Studio can be used to create an ETL job that extracts the data from the external cloud data warehouse, transforms the data if necessary (for example, cleaning the data or converting data types), and then loads the transformed data into Amazon Redshift. This process is automated, which means the team can migrate large datasets with minimal effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Utilize the AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT) to adapt the source schema and data definition language (DDL) script from the external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Leverage AWS Glue Studio to transfer the data from the external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;Use the Amazon Athena notebook to adapt the source schema and data definition language (DDL) script from the external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because Amazon Athena is simply an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. It is not designed to convert schemas or DDL scripts from one database to another.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the Amazon Athena SQL query editor to transfer the data from the external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect. It is true that Amazon Athena can be used to query data directly in S3, but this service is not designed to transfer data from an external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift. Using AWS Glue Studio is a better option for easily transferring data to Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eExport the data from the external cloud data warehouse into Amazon S3 as flat delimited text files. Then, use the Amazon Redshift SQL query editor with the \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e command to transfer the data from Amazon S3 to Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because while this method could technically work, it would require significant manual effort and is not the most efficient way to transfer data from an external cloud data warehouse to Amazon Redshift. A more efficient way would be to use AWS Glue Studio or AWS SCT, which can automate the process and handle larger datasets more effectively.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/SchemaConversionTool/latest/userguide/CHAP_Welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/SchemaConversionTool/latest/userguide/CHAP_Welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/SchemaConversionTool/latest/userguide/CHAP_Converting.DW.html#CHAP_Converting.DW.Convert\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/SchemaConversionTool/latest/userguide/CHAP_Converting.DW.html#CHAP_Converting.DW.Convert\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-10":{"id":"p:2-10","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 10","correct":[0,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eChoose Amazon S3 as the data storage service and leverage Amazon Athena for data analysis\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS DataSync to globally share data and control access\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Glue DataBrew integration with AWS Glue Studio to orchestrate data sharing and control access\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Lake Formation to centrally govern, secure, and globally share data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Data Exchange to globally share data and control access\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["S3 làm lớp lưu trữ data lake, Athena cung cấp SQL để phân tích mà không phải quản lý cụm. Kết hợp với Glue Catalog và Lake Formation cho kiến trúc dữ liệu có quản trị.","DataSync di chuyển dữ liệu giữa hệ thống lưu trữ. Nó không phải lớp quản trị quyền chia sẻ dữ liệu của data mesh.","DataBrew và Glue Studio phục vụ chuẩn bị/ETL dữ liệu. Chúng không thay thế cơ chế cấp quyền và quản trị dữ liệu tập trung.","Lake Formation quản lý quyền chi tiết với tài nguyên data lake và chia sẻ dữ liệu theo phạm vi được hỗ trợ. Đây là lớp quản trị kết hợp Glue Catalog trong đề.","Data Exchange phục vụ trao đổi, đăng ký dữ liệu bên thứ ba. Không phải lựa chọn quản trị truy cập nội bộ data mesh ở đây."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company is planning to establish a data mesh architecture that facilitates centralized data governance, analysis, and access control. The company has opted to utilize AWS Glue for managing data catalogs and conducting extract, transform, and load (ETL) operations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat combination of AWS services would be suitable to implement this data mesh effectively? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-52":{"id":"p:4-52","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 52","correct":[0,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse the KEY distribution style for both billing tables and choose a high-cardinality join column as the distribution key\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the KEY distribution style for both billing tables and choose a low-cardinality status column as the distribution key\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Advisor to review workload-based table design recommendations and implement the relevant distribution and sort key changes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the EVEN distribution style for billing_s1 and the ALL distribution style for billing_s2 so every node stores a full copy of the smaller table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the concurrency scaling configuration so Amazon Redshift can add temporary query capacity for the slow revenue aggregation query\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Dùng cùng khóa join làm distribution key giúp các dòng cần join nằm cùng node, giảm truyền dữ liệu qua mạng. Khóa có nhiều giá trị và phân bố tương đối đều giúp tránh dồn tải vào ít slice.","Cột status ít giá trị có thể gom lượng lớn dữ liệu vào một số slice. Điều đó gây lệch tải và không giúp đặt dữ liệu cùng vị trí theo khóa join cần thiết.","Redshift Advisor phân tích workload để đề xuất điều chỉnh thiết kế bảng. Xem xét các khuyến nghị distribution key và sort key phù hợp giúp tối ưu dựa trên truy vấn thực tế.","850 triệu dòng vẫn là bảng rất lớn. ALL sao chép toàn bộ bảng sang mọi node nên tốn lưu trữ và công nạp; không nên coi mọi bảng nhỏ hơn bảng còn lại là dimension nhỏ phù hợp ALL.","Concurrency scaling chủ yếu giúp xử lý nhiều truy vấn đồng thời. Nó không tự sửa cách phân phối dữ liệu hoặc làm nhanh một phép join đang nghẽn do thiết kế bảng."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA subscription software company runs a series of SQL transformation queries against an Amazon Redshift cluster. After acquiring another business unit, the company updates a revenue aggregation query to join subscription billing tables from both business units. The first table is named billing_s1 and contains 12 billion records. The second table is named billing_s2 and contains 850 million records. After the change, the query runs much slower than before. A data engineer must improve the join and aggregation performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solutions will meet these requirements? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-1":{"id":"p:2-1","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 1","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eProcess the files using Amazon Athena and leverage the json_format function to create the new column\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eProcess the files using AWS Glue DataBrew and leverage the NEST_TO_ARRAY transformation to create the new column\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eProcess the files using AWS Glue DataBrew and leverage the NEST_TO_MAP transformation to create the new column\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eProcess the files using Amazon Athena and leverage the json_parse function to create the new column\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Athena không đọc trực tiếp workbook Excel. json_format chuyển giá trị JSON thành chuỗi, không phải phép ghép các cột Excel thành một map.","NEST_TO_ARRAY tạo mảng giá trị, còn kết quả yêu cầu có cặp tên trường–giá trị. Cần NEST_TO_MAP.","DataBrew đọc được Excel; NEST_TO_MAP gom các cột thành đối tượng với khóa là tên cột và giá trị tương ứng. Phù hợp đầu ra mẫu mà ít viết mã.","json_parse phân tích một chuỗi JSON đã có, không gom các cột Excel thành map. Athena cũng không đọc trực tiếp đầu vào .xls."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe HR department at a company wants to process employees data stored in Amazon S3 in the Microsoft Excel worksheet format. The data has the following column names: id, name, email, and phone. The department wants to create a single column to store these values in the following format:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode\u003e{\n    \"id\": 1,\n    \"name\": \"John Doe\",\n    \"email\": \"johndoe@example.com\",\n    \"phone\": \"9876543210\"\n}\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options will meet this requirement with the LEAST coding effort?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:40":{"id":"e:5:40","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 40","correct":[1],"answers":["Use Amazon Data Firehose to send sensor data from the stream to an S3 Bucket. Create an Amazon S3 event notification to invoke an AWS Lambda function upon the creation of new objects. Make use of the Lambda function to process and insert the data into Amazon Redshift. Build an Amazon QuickSight dashboard using Amazon Redshift as the source.","Utilize Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink for real-time data processing. Configure an Apache Flink connector to output data into an Amazon Timestream database, and then use this database to power an Amazon Managed Grafana dashboard for visualization.","Process the sensor data using Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink. Configure an Amazon Data Firehose stream to ingest processed data directly into Amazon Timestream. Utilize Amazon QuickSight to create dashboards drawing data from the Timestream database.","Replace the data stream with Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (MSK) and ingest the result into an Amazon S3 bucket. Build an Amazon QuickSight dashboard using Amazon S3 as the source."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn industrial enterprise uses IoT sensors to monitor operations in its factory sectors. Data from these sensors is published to a data stream managed by Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. The enterprise wants to monitor operational metrics on a dashboard in real-time. The dashboard should update in real-time to ensure minimal latency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will achieve this requirement with the least latency?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Managed Service for Apache Flink\u003c/strong\u003e is a powerful platform that enables real-time data processing. Data Engineers can use this service to analyze streaming data using Java, Scala, Python, or SQL. It simplifies the management of Apache Flink applications by handling the underlying infrastructure, such as compute resources, automatic scaling, and application backups. Moreover,\u0026nbsp;the service enables you to quickly author and run powerful SQL code against streaming sources to perform time-series analytics, feed real-time dashboards, and create real-time metrics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/420aa1912c3661014eec46f8a4c346ff73194c8b937387c77871b25b78c5543f.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo visualize and analyze processed data, it’s possible to use Amazon Timestream and Amazon Managed Grafana in conjunction. By outputting data from Apache Flink applications to Amazon Timestream, it can be viewed in real-time using Amazon Managed Grafana. This provides powerful tools for creating dynamic dashboards to monitor and analyze data. This integration allows for real-time monitoring and analysis, making it suitable for various applications, including IoT, financial analytics, and operational monitoring.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink for real-time data processing. Configure an Apache Flink connector to output data into an Amazon Timestream database, and then use this database to power an Amazon Managed Grafana dashboard for visualization.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose to send sensor data from the stream to an S3 Bucket. Create an Amazon S3 event notification to invoke an AWS Lambda function upon the creation of new objects. Make use of the Lambda function to process and insert the data into Amazon Redshift. Build an Amazon QuickSight dashboard using Amazon Redshift as the source\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. The process of storing data in S3, invoking Lambda, and then loading it into Redshift introduces latency, making it less ideal for the requirement of minimal latency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eProcess the sensor data using Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink. Configure an Amazon Data Firehose stream to ingest processed data directly into Amazon Timestream. Utilize Amazon QuickSight to create dashboards drawing data from the Timestream database\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Although Data Firehose can consume data from a data stream and deliver it to a chosen destination, it does not support Amazon Timestream as a destination.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReplace the data stream with Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (MSK) and ingest the result into an Amazon S3 bucket. Build an Amazon QuickSight dashboard using Amazon S3 as the source\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. This method introduces additional latency through the use of S3 as an intermediary storage, which is not optimal for real-time operational monitoring.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/managed-service-apache-flink/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/managed-service-apache-flink/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/grafana/latest/userguide/timestream-datasource.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/grafana/latest/userguide/timestream-datasource.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/timestream/latest/developerguide/ApacheFlink.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/timestream/latest/developerguide/ApacheFlink.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Managed Grafana\u0026nbsp;Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-managed-grafana/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-managed-grafana/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-34":{"id":"p:2-34","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 34","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAdd the target account to the default AWS KMS key. Copy the snapshot using the default AWS KMS key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd the target account to a customer-managed key. Copy the snapshot using the customer-managed key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd the target account to a customer-managed key. Copy the snapshot using the default AWS KMS key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd the target account to the default AWS KMS key. Copy the snapshot using the customer-managed key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Không thể tự sửa policy của khóa AWS managed mặc định để chia sẻ tùy ý cho tài khoản khác. Snapshot cần bản sao mã hóa bằng customer managed key.","Cấp quyền tài khoản đích trên customer managed KMS key, sao chép snapshot sang khóa đó rồi chia sẻ. Tài khoản đích sao chép snapshot được chia sẻ theo quyền đã cấp.","Dù đã cấp quyền một customer managed key, bản snapshot vẫn dùng khóa mặc định nên chưa chia sẻ được theo thiết kế này.","Cần cấp quyền trên chính customer managed key dùng mã hóa bản sao. Cấp quyền cho khóa mặc định không phải thao tác hợp lệ thay thế."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multi-national retail company uses separate AWS accounts for their business units. The finance team has an encrypted snapshot of an Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) instance that uses the default AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) key. The finance team wants to share the encrypted snapshot with their Audit team that uses another AWS account.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions would you recommend to address the given use-case?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-59":{"id":"p:2-59","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 59","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS Lake Formation permissions for the clinical research team and grant the team access to the confidential DynamoDB table through LF-Tags\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure IAM roles for EMRFS requests and map the clinical research team to a role that can read the confidential DynamoDB table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate separate EMR Studio workspaces for each analytics team and rely on workspace separation to prevent unauthorized Spark jobs from reading the confidential DynamoDB table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up runtime roles for EMR steps and allow only the clinical research team to submit steps with a runtime role that can read the confidential DynamoDB table\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Lake Formation quản trị các tài nguyên data lake tích hợp Catalog, không trực tiếp cấp quyền đọc DynamoDB cho EMR step theo cách nêu.","EMRFS là lớp truy cập S3. Role mapping cho EMRFS không thay thế quyền DynamoDB của tác vụ Spark.","Workspace tách môi trường làm việc nhưng không tự giới hạn mọi quyền AWS của Spark job trên cụm dùng chung.","Runtime role gắn quyền với từng EMR step. Chỉ đội nghiên cứu được phép dùng role có quyền đọc bảng DynamoDB nhạy cảm; các đội khác không kế thừa quyền đó."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA life sciences company runs a shared Amazon EMR cluster on Amazon EC2 instances. Several analytics teams use the cluster to run interactive analysis and batch transformations through EMR Studio workspaces and EMR steps.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe teams need to submit EMR steps that run Apache Spark jobs and read reference data from Amazon DynamoDB tables. One DynamoDB table contains confidential clinical trial data that only the clinical research team can access. A data engineer must make sure that only the clinical research team can access the confidential DynamoDB table from jobs that run on the shared EMR cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:7:19":{"id":"e:7:19","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 19","correct":[2],"answers":["Set up S3 Batch Operations for bulk data management and distribution across multiple S3 buckets.","Initially transfer the data using AWS Snowcone and implement S3 Replication Time Control (S3 RTC) to manage the replication of data across S3 buckets efficiently.","Utilize AWS DataSync for streamlined and fast data transfer to various S3 buckets.","Use Amazon Data Firehose for real-time data streaming and automatic distribution to multiple S3 buckets."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is migrating data from its various on-premises file systems into an Amazon S3 data lake managed via the AWS Lake Formation service. To enhance analytics capabilities, the data engineer is tasked with creating a data ingestion pipeline that not only transfers data efficiently but also splits the data across multiple Amazon S3 buckets in near real-time to support different analytical workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following is the MOST operationally efficient solution that the data engineer should implement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS DataSync is specifically designed to accelerate the transfer of vast volumes of data from on-premises storage systems to AWS storage services such as Amazon S3. It supports fast, secure, and reliable data migration, making it an ideal choice for projects that require the distribution of data across multiple S3 buckets in near real-time. DataSync automates the replication process, optimizes the data transfer over the network, and seamlessly integrates with Amazon S3, allowing for the direct specification of multiple target buckets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/cd86cda59cb064dbf20f07111170a6175c6310154764a4c47a4beb4ae7b7d86a.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo transfer data to or from your Amazon S3 bucket, you need to create an AWS DataSync transfer location. DataSync uses this location as a source or destination for transferring data. Keep in mind that DataSync needs access to the S3 bucket that you\u0027re transferring to or from. To do this, you must create an AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) role that DataSync assumes with the permissions required to access the bucket. You can then specify this role when creating your Amazon S3 location for DataSync.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize AWS DataSync for streamlined and fast data transfer to various S3 buckets.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up S3 Batch Operations for bulk data management and distribution across multiple S3 buckets\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect\u0026nbsp;because it is primarily\u0026nbsp;used for operations on existing S3 objects rather than the initial upload and real-time distribution of data. It does not meet the requirement for real-time data ingestion and distribution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eInitially transfer the data using AWS Snowcone and implement S3 Replication Time Control (S3 RTC) to manage the replication of data across S3 buckets efficiently\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Physically transferring data via an AWS Snowcone device is not necessary since the transfer can be fully done online. Moreover, the S3 RTC feature simply focuses on enhancing the replication speed and predictability of S3 objects within AWS, which does not directly address the need for initial data migration and near real-time distribution to multiple buckets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose for real-time data streaming and automatic distribution to multiple S3 buckets\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Although the Amazon Data Firehose service is capable of handling real-time data streaming and can automatically load streaming data into Amazon S3, this service is primarily designed for streaming use cases rather than bulk data migrations from on-premises environments. Thus, this solution does not fully meet the requirements for this specific scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datasync/latest/userguide/welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/datasync/latest/userguide/welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datasync/latest/userguide/create-object-location.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/datasync/latest/userguide/create-object-location.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS DataSync Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-datasync/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-datasync/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-27":{"id":"p:4-27","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 27","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS DMS Schema Conversion to convert the Teradata schema, and use AWS DMS to migrate the warehouse data to Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS DMS to migrate the Teradata data first, and rely on automatic schema conversion during the Amazon Redshift load process\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS SCT to convert the Teradata schema to Amazon Redshift, and use AWS DMS to migrate the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue crawlers to infer the Teradata schema, create matching Amazon Redshift tables, and run Glue jobs for the migration\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Theo phạm vi hỗ trợ mà bộ đề sử dụng, DMS Schema Conversion không cung cấp đường chuyển Teradata sang Redshift được yêu cầu. Đề chọn AWS SCT làm công cụ chuyển schema.","Di chuyển dữ liệu trước không tự chuyển được toàn bộ schema và mã SQL khác hệ quản trị. Cần chuyển và chuẩn bị schema tương thích trước khi nạp dữ liệu.","Theo đáp án bộ đề, SCT đảm nhiệm chuyển schema Teradata sang Redshift, còn DMS đảm nhiệm di chuyển dữ liệu. Khi triển khai thật, cần kiểm tra đường di chuyển nguồn Teradata và cách trích xuất được hỗ trợ; không nên hiểu rằng DMS đọc trực tiếp mọi nguồn database.","Glue crawler khám phá metadata, không thay thế công cụ chuyển schema và SQL đặc thù Teradata. Tự xây migration bằng Glue tạo nhiều mã và công kiểm tra hơn giải pháp chuyển đổi chuyên dụng."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics company runs a large on-premises Teradata data warehouse that stores merchandising, loyalty, and store operations data. The company wants to migrate the warehouse to Amazon Redshift so analysts can use a managed cloud data warehouse for reporting and forecasting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer must convert the Teradata schema to an Amazon Redshift-compatible schema and then migrate the warehouse data. The company wants to complete the migration with the least operational effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-21":{"id":"p:2-21","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 21","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into Redshift on an hourly basis and run the SQL-based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into RDS on an hourly basis and run the SQL-based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into an EMR-based Spark Cluster on an hourly basis and use SparkSQL to run the SQL-based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena to run SQL-based analytics against S3 data\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Nạp dữ liệu vào Redshift chỉ để chạy kiểm tra SQL tạo thêm pipeline và tài nguyên tính toán không cần thiết.","RDS cần sao chép dữ liệu và quản lý database. Có thể kiểm tra trực tiếp dữ liệu S3 bằng Athena.","EMR và SparkSQL làm được nhưng thêm cụm và tác vụ định kỳ. Không đơn giản bằng Athena cho SQL trên S3.","Athena chạy SQL trên dữ liệu S3 mà không phải chuyển sang database khác hoặc quản lý cụm. Phù hợp kiểm tra dữ liệu tiết kiệm và dễ vận hành."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team at a retail company has set up a workflow to ingest the clickstream data into the raw zone of the S3 data lake. The team wants to run some SQL-based data sanity checks on the raw zone of the data lake.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat AWS services would you recommend for this use-case such that the solution is cost-effective and easy to maintain?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:8":{"id":"e:4:8","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 8","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse an AWS Glue crawler to update the AWS Glue Data Catalog, create filters on the three date columns, and configure Athena to read only records that satisfy the filter conditions\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 Object Lambda to inspect each object during read requests, compare the three date values, and return only valid records to Athena while writing invalid records to a separate S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to create DATEDIFF columns for the two date gaps, inspect the calculated columns, and export records with invalid date differences to a second S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Quality to create a custom rule that evaluates the three date columns, enable row-level outcomes, and write records that fail the rule to a second S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company uses AWS Glue ETL pipelines to process order fulfillment data. The company uses Amazon Athena to analyze curated data that is stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. To improve delivery performance reporting, the company adds pickup_date and arrival_date fields to the existing order_date field. The company must validate that pickup_date is later than order_date and that arrival_date is later than pickup_date. Records that fail the validation must be written to a separate S3 bucket for review.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Quality to create a custom rule that evaluates the three date columns, enable row-level outcomes, and write records that fail the rule to a second S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Data Quality is designed to evaluate data quality rules during ETL processing. The data engineer can define a custom rule that checks the relationship across order_date, pickup_date, and arrival_date.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA custom rule is appropriate because the validation depends on multiple columns in the same record. The rule must confirm that pickup_date is later than order_date and that arrival_date is later than pickup_date. This is a cross-column consistency check, not only a single-column completeness or format check.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Data Quality for ETL jobs can produce row-level outcomes that identify which records passed or failed the rules. The ETL job can route failed records to a second S3 bucket while continuing to store valid records in the curated S3 location for Athena analysis. This uses the existing AWS Glue pipeline and avoids separate query, scan, or custom processing layers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon S3 Object Lambda to inspect each object during read requests, compare the three date values, and return only valid records to Athena while writing invalid records to a separate S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Object Lambda can transform or modify data returned from S3 GET requests. It can be useful when applications need customized object views without modifying the stored object.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option validates data at read time rather than during ETL processing. It also adds custom function logic and does not provide the same managed data quality rule evaluation and row-level failure output that AWS Glue Data Quality provides.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to create DATEDIFF columns for the two date gaps, inspect the calculated columns, and export records with invalid date differences to a second S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue DataBrew can help users prepare data visually, and DATEDIFF-style calculations can help compare dates. This can be useful for exploratory preparation or manual data cleaning workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the company already uses AWS Glue ETL pipelines. Adding DataBrew introduces another processing tool when AWS Glue Data Quality can perform the validation inside the existing ETL job and route failed records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse an AWS Glue crawler to update the AWS Glue Data Catalog, create filters on the three date columns, and configure Athena to read only records that satisfy the filter conditions\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue crawlers discover schema and update metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. This helps Athena and other query engines understand the structure and location of S3 datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, crawlers do not validate record-level business rules such as pickup_date greater than order_date and arrival_date greater than pickup_date. Metadata filters also do not create a failed-record output bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/tutorial-data-quality.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/tutorial-data-quality.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types-CustomSql.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types-CustomSql.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/getting-started-with-aws-glue-data-quality-for-etl-pipelines/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/getting-started-with-aws-glue-data-quality-for-etl-pipelines/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:22":{"id":"e:1:22","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 22","correct":[0,3,5],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eA Global Secondary Index contains a selection of attributes from the base table which are organized by a primary key that is different from that of the table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eGlobal secondary indexes support both data models - eventually consistent or strongly consistent reads\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eA Local Secondary Index maintains an alternate primary key for a given partition key value\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eApplications that need to perform many kinds of queries, using a variety of different attributes for their query criteria should use Global Secondary Indexes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFor greater query or scan flexibility, you can create up to twenty Local Secondary Indexes per table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIf the writes are throttled on the Global Secondary Indexes, then the main table will be throttled, even though the Write Capacity Units on the main tables are fine\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is working on modeling data for a DynamoDB production database. To address certain access patterns for the application, the data engineer is in the process of creating secondary indexes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options should the data engineer consider for these requirements? (Select three)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eApplications that need to perform many kinds of queries, using a variety of different attributes for their query criteria should use Global Secondary Indexes\u003c/strong\u003e - Some applications might need to perform many kinds of queries, using a variety of different attributes as query criteria. To support these requirements, you can create one or more global secondary indexes and issue Query requests against these indexes in Amazon DynamoDB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIf the writes are throttled on the Global Secondary Indexes, then the main table will be throttled, even though the Write Capacity Units on the main tables are fine\u003c/strong\u003e - For a table write to succeed, the provisioned throughput settings for the table and all of its global secondary indexes must have enough write capacity to accommodate the write. Otherwise, the write to the main table itself is throttled.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eA Global Secondary Index contains a selection of attributes from the base table which are organized by a primary key that is different from that of the table\u003c/strong\u003e - To speed up queries on non-key attributes, you can create a global secondary index. A global secondary index contains a selection of attributes from the base table, but they are organized by a primary key that is different from that of the table. The index key does not need to have any of the key attributes from the table. It doesn\u0027t even need to have the same key schema as a table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eA Local Secondary Index maintains an alternate primary key for a given partition key value\u003c/strong\u003e - This is an invalid statement. A local secondary index maintains an alternate sort key for a given partition key value. The data in a local secondary index is organized by the same partition key as the base table but with a different sort key.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eGlobal secondary indexes support both data models - eventually consistent or strongly consistent reads\u003c/strong\u003e - This statement is incorrect. Global secondary indexes support only eventually consistent data model.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFor greater query or scan flexibility, you can create up to twenty Local Secondary Indexes per table\u003c/strong\u003e - This statement is not correct. Each table in DynamoDB can have up to 20 global secondary indexes (default quota) and 5 local secondary indexes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/GSI.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/GSI.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/LSI.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/LSI.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/bp-indexes-general.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/bp-indexes-general.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:28":{"id":"e:3:28","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 28","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EMR on EKS with Kubernetes Cluster Autoscaler to scale Spark applications only from Kubernetes Metrics Server resource metrics\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EMR on EKS with Kubernetes Event-Driven Autoscaling to scale Spark streaming applications based on event-source demand\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse native Amazon EKS Horizontal Pod Autoscaler with Kubernetes Metrics Server to scale self-managed Spark driver and executor pods for streaming workloads\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun Apache Spark on Amazon EKS with AWS Auto Scaling groups and custom scaling policies based only on Kubernetes Metrics Server data\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media analytics company needs to process streaming clickstream data by using Apache Spark in a Kubernetes environment. The company already runs shared platform services on Amazon EKS and wants the new streaming workload to integrate with that existing Kubernetes infrastructure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe solution must support event-driven scaling when streaming volume increases and must reduce unused compute capacity when traffic decreases. The company wants to optimize resource utilization while minimizing the operational work required to run Spark on Kubernetes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon EMR on EKS with Kubernetes Event-Driven Autoscaling to scale Spark streaming applications based on event-source demand\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon EMR on EKS is the best fit because it lets the company run Apache Spark workloads on existing Amazon EKS infrastructure. The company can use Amazon EMR capabilities for Spark job execution instead of operating a fully self-managed Spark runtime on Kubernetes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/88aad2bf9543cfa47af3180daafd4c9bc09873af32c3fa0a3bee16e737cb3ea4.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/88aad2bf9543cfa47af3180daafd4c9bc09873af32c3fa0a3bee16e737cb3ea4.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-on-EKS-DevelopmentGuide/emr-eks.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-on-EKS-DevelopmentGuide/emr-eks.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eKubernetes Event-Driven Autoscaling, or KEDA, supports event-driven scaling for Kubernetes workloads. It can scale workloads based on event sources or custom metrics, which aligns with the requirement to increase Spark processing capacity when streaming demand rises.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option provides the least operational overhead among the choices because Amazon EMR on EKS reduces Spark platform management work, and KEDA addresses event-driven scaling. The company can integrate with its existing EKS environment while optimizing resource utilization for variable streaming traffic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ab24ad59b39237516036d554c40d6c17146b2b2fcff10bd232b87500782ae851.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ab24ad59b39237516036d554c40d6c17146b2b2fcff10bd232b87500782ae851.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/event-driven-auto-scaling-with-eks-pod-identity-and-keda.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/event-driven-auto-scaling-with-eks-pod-identity-and-keda.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f48d8863ca3a192878728d996c26e72d66b86f4e150f3b3349f27e1deadc5e5e.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f48d8863ca3a192878728d996c26e72d66b86f4e150f3b3349f27e1deadc5e5e.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/event-driven-auto-scaling-with-eks-pod-identity-and-keda.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/event-driven-auto-scaling-with-eks-pod-identity-and-keda.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse native Amazon EKS Horizontal Pod Autoscaler with Kubernetes Metrics Server to scale self-managed Spark driver and executor pods for streaming workloads\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EKS Horizontal Pod Autoscaler can scale Kubernetes pods based on observed metrics such as CPU or memory utilization. This can help with some containerized workloads that have predictable resource-based scaling behavior.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option does not meet the requirement as well as EMR on EKS with KEDA because it relies on self-managed Spark and resource-based scaling instead of event-driven scaling. The company would need to manage Spark deployment, Spark runtime operations, and scaling behavior, which adds operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun Apache Spark on Amazon EKS with AWS Auto Scaling groups and custom scaling policies based only on Kubernetes Metrics Server data\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Auto Scaling groups can add or remove EC2 capacity behind Kubernetes worker nodes. Kubernetes Metrics Server can provide resource metrics such as CPU and memory usage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not provide the best event-driven scaling model for Spark applications. It also requires custom scaling logic and does not use Amazon EMR on EKS to reduce Spark management overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon EMR on EKS with Kubernetes Cluster Autoscaler to scale Spark applications only from Kubernetes Metrics Server resource metrics\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR on EKS helps run Spark workloads on EKS, and Kubernetes Cluster Autoscaler can adjust node capacity when pods cannot be scheduled.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because Cluster Autoscaler reacts primarily to cluster scheduling needs, not directly to event-source demand. The requirement specifically calls for event-driven scaling, which KEDA addresses more directly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-on-EKS-DevelopmentGuide/emr-eks.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/EMR-on-EKS-DevelopmentGuide/emr-eks.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/emr/features/eks/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/emr/features/eks/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/event-driven-auto-scaling-with-eks-pod-identity-and-keda.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/event-driven-auto-scaling-with-eks-pod-identity-and-keda.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/solutions/event-driven-application-autoscaling-with-keda-on-amazon-eks/\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/solutions/event-driven-application-autoscaling-with-keda-on-amazon-eks/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/autoscaling-kubernetes-workloads-with-keda-using-amazon-managed-service-for-prometheus-metrics/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/autoscaling-kubernetes-workloads-with-keda-using-amazon-managed-service-for-prometheus-metrics/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:51":{"id":"e:5:51","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 51","correct":[0,2],"answers":["Arrange the data within the S3 bucket into partitions by year, month, and day.","Enhance the Glue Jobs query performance by changing the execution class to Flex.","Modify the Glue Job worker type configuration to a larger instance type.","Modify the storage class of the S3 bucket to S3 Express One Zone.","Incorporate the DynamicFrame schema class within your Apache Spark-based Glue Jobs."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAn organization collects daily user interaction and app performance logs from its mobile app. Initially, raw logs are transformed by AWS Glue for Spark Jobs and then stored in an Amazon S3 bucket.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCurrently, the data in the S3 bucket is stored without any partitioning, causing the Glue jobs to scan the entire dataset during each run.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eThe company’s Data Engineer has noticed a gradual slowdown in the Amazon QuickSight Dashboard’s query performance over time. Upon investigation, the engineer determined that the long duration of the Glue Jobs is the root cause.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhat combination of actions should the Data Engineer consider to improve query performance? (Select TWO.)\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e is a serverless data integration service that makes it easy for analytics users to discover, prepare, move, and integrate data from multiple sources. With AWS Glue, you can discover and connect to more than 70 diverse data sources and manage your data in a centralized data catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou define jobs in AWS Glue to accomplish the work that’s required to extract, transform, and load (ETL) data from a data source to a data target.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can improve performance just by reducing the amount of data loaded into your Spark cluster for each data source. Consider loading only the data that you need.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo reduce the amount of data loaded into your job when reading from Amazon S3, consider \u003cstrong\u003efile size, compression, file format, and file layout (partitions)\u003c/strong\u003e for your dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Keep the file size of inputs and outputs within a moderate range (for example, 128 MB). Files that are too small or too large can cause issues.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– If your S3 objects are in the hundreds of megabytes, consider compressing them.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Use a columnar format. Apache Parquet and Apache ORC are popular columnar data formats. Parquet and ORC store data efficiently by employing column-based compression, encoding and compressing each column based on its data type.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– By storing your S3 data to objects in different paths based on how the data will be used, you can efficiently retrieve relevant data. In this scenario, the data is currently stored without partitions, causing AWS Glue jobs to scan the entire dataset on each run. By partitioning your data, you can restrict the amount of data scanned by each downstream analytics application, improving performance and reducing cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/394ded2aeb4ef21b987ebae45e83c71dd30006921aa73c0d7320d759abb58172.png\" alt=\"Work Type instance\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIf your job is taking too much time, but executors are consuming sufficient resources, and Spark is creating a large volume of tasks relative to available cores, consider scaling cluster capacity. You can scale cluster capacity by using the following options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Enable AWS Glue Auto Scaling\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Scale-out: Increase the number of AWS Glue workers\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e– Scale up: Use a larger worker type\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Arrange the data within the S3 bucket into partitions by year, month, and day.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Modify the Glue Job worker type configuration to a larger instance type.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eModify the storage class of the S3 bucket to S3 Express One Zone\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Although using this storage class can improve latency, it will still not resolve the slow query performance. With this approach, you will deploy your Amazon S3 bucket in the same availability zone as your resources, which will improve the latency. However, it is not mentioned in the scenario if there is a latency issue. This will not resolve the slow performance issue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncorporate the DynamicFrame schema class within your Apache Spark-based Glue Jobs\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While this can potentially optimize the jobs’ performance, it is not guaranteed to solve the issue. DynamicFrames are only well-suited for working with semi-structured data formats. It can handle data that includes nested structures or varying schemas more efficiently than traditional data representations. However, the scenario did not mention the complexity of the data characteristics. If your data is already well-structured and does not contain nested structures or varying schemas, the benefits of DynamicFrames may be less pronounced. More is needed if the issue is due to resource constraints or loading a large volume of data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnhance the Glue Jobs query performance by changing the execution class to Flex\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because Flex execution is simply more suitable for non-urgent or non-time-sensitive data integration workloads, such as testing and one-time data loads. The jobs are running daily, making this solution unsuitable. With Flex, AWS Glue jobs run on spare compute capacity instead of dedicated hardware. The start and run times of jobs using Flex can vary. This solution is for optimizing cost, not for enhancing performance. It could even abruptly stop data processing when AWS takes back the resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/reduce-data-scan.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/reduce-data-scan.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/scale-cluster-capacity.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/scale-cluster-capacity.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-9":{"id":"p:2-9","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 9","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the telemetry data in CSV format, partition the dataset by reading date, and sort the records by service condition\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the telemetry data in Apache Parquet format, partition the dataset by meter type, and sort the records by reading timestamp\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the telemetry data in Apache ORC format, partition the dataset by reading date, and sort the records by meter type\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the telemetry data in JSON format, partition the dataset by facility location, and sort the records by device identifier for all downstream Athena queries\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["CSV không có ưu điểm lưu theo cột; sắp theo tình trạng dịch vụ không khớp cách lọc loại đồng hồ và thời gian.","Parquet giảm dữ liệu cột cần đọc. Partition theo loại đồng hồ giúp loại bỏ nhóm không liên quan; sắp theo timestamp hỗ trợ đọc khoảng thời gian cần phân tích.","ORC là định dạng tốt, nhưng cách phân vùng/sắp xếp này kém sát điều kiện lọc loại đồng hồ trước rồi khoảng thời gian mà đề nhấn mạnh.","JSON lưu theo bản ghi; partition theo địa điểm và sắp theo device ID không phù hợp mẫu truy vấn được mô tả."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn energy company collects hourly telemetry from thousands of smart meters into an Amazon S3 data lake. Each record includes fields such as meter type, device identifier, service condition, reading timestamp, energy zone, and facility location. The dataset grows by several million records each day. Analysts run frequent Amazon Athena queries to compare performance trends for specific meter types across time ranges.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the FASTEST query execution time?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-16":{"id":"p:1-16","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 16","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a schedule to refresh the materialized views with Amazon Redshift query editor v2\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eYou can set auto-refresh for materialized views using CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an Amazon EventBridge to trigger an AWS Lambda function based on the defined schedule. The Lambda function will call the Amazon Redshift Data API to refresh the materialized views\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMaterialized views cannot be refreshed automatically and need a manual refresh\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Query editor v2 hỗ trợ lập lịch chạy lệnh refresh materialized view. Tận dụng tính năng có sẵn đáp ứng lịch cố định mà ít phải tự xây dựng quy trình.","Auto-refresh cập nhật view dựa trên thay đổi dữ liệu và cơ chế của Redshift. Nó không tương đương với yêu cầu refresh vào các thời điểm đã định trước.","EventBridge và Lambda có thể gọi Data API theo lịch, nhưng bạn phải tự triển khai các thành phần đó. Query editor v2 đã cung cấp cách thiết lập đơn giản hơn.","Materialized view có thể được refresh tự động hoặc bằng lệnh theo lịch. Khẳng định chỉ refresh thủ công là sai."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses Amazon Redshift as its data warehouse solution. The company runs certain complex queries repeatedly over a large amount of data and hence uses Amazon Redshift materialized views. The company wants these materialized views to refresh automatically per a defined schedule.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents an optimal solution that can automate the process with the LEAST effort?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-57":{"id":"p:3-57","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 57","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS Glue ETL jobs with an Evaluate Data Quality transform, define rules inside each job, and enable data quality for an Amazon DataZone AWS Glue data source\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a DQDL ruleset for each AWS Glue table, schedule the ruleset to run, and enable data quality for an Amazon DataZone AWS Glue data source\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate DQDL rulesets for AWS Glue tables, schedule the rulesets daily, and enable data quality for an Amazon DataZone Amazon Redshift data source\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate AWS Glue crawlers for the S3 data lake, schedule crawler runs daily, and enable Amazon DataZone to infer data quality scores from crawler schema changes\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Evaluate Data Quality trong ETL kiểm tra tại bước biến đổi, nhưng đề cần điểm chất lượng gắn tài sản bảng Catalog hiển thị trong DataZone. Ruleset chạy cho bảng là đường trực tiếp hơn.","Tạo DQDL ruleset cho từng Glue table và lập lịch đánh giá. Bật data quality trên nguồn Glue của DataZone để hiển thị điểm chất lượng của tài sản tương ứng.","Quy tắc áp trên Glue tables nhưng lại bật tích hợp nguồn Redshift là sai nguồn dữ liệu. Cần nguồn AWS Glue trong DataZone.","Crawler phát hiện metadata/schema, không tự suy ra điểm chất lượng từ thay đổi schema. Cần đánh giá ruleset Data Quality."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company uses Amazon DataZone as its business catalog and governance portal. The company stores curated transaction data in an Amazon S3 data lake and catalogs the datasets in the AWS Glue Data Catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer needs to make AWS Glue Data Quality scores visible to business users in the Amazon DataZone portal. The data quality checks must run on a recurring schedule for specific AWS Glue tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:32":{"id":"e:4:32","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 32","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena to run SQL based analytics on the data in Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into DynamoDB on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into Redshift on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into RDS on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team at a company has set up a workflow to ingest the clickstream data into the raw zone of the S3 data lake. The team wants to run some SQL based data sanity checks on the raw zone of the data lake. The company has hired you as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to build a serverless solution that involves the least amount of development effort. The solution should be cost-effective and easy to maintain.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions would you build for this use-case?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena to run SQL based analytics on the data in Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data directly in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. Athena is serverless, so there is no infrastructure to set up or manage, and customers pay only for the queries they run. You can use Athena to process logs, perform ad-hoc analysis, and run interactive queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Athena Benefits:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4c19f1e2fad379ac423472029092d84b6892af3d365b13d272696f88e9e9bba9.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4c19f1e2fad379ac423472029092d84b6892af3d365b13d272696f88e9e9bba9.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into Redshift on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift is a fully-managed petabyte-scale cloud-based data warehouse product designed for large scale data set storage and analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs the team would have to maintain and monitor the Redshift cluster size and would require significant time to set up the processes to consume the data periodically, so this option is ruled out.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into DynamoDB on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon DynamoDB is a key-value and document database that delivers single-digit millisecond performance at any scale. It\u0027s a fully managed, multi-Region, multi-master, durable database with built-in security, backup and restore, and in-memory caching for internet-scale applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eLoading the incremental data into DynamoDB implies data migration jobs will have to be written via a Lambda function or an EC2 based process. This goes against the requirement that the solution should involve the least amount of development effort and ongoing maintenance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into RDS on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/strong\u003e - Loading the incremental data into RDS implies data migration jobs will have to be written via a Lambda function or an EC2 based process. This goes against the requirement that the solution should involve the least amount of development effort and ongoing maintenance. Hence this option is not correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:41":{"id":"e:3:41","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 41","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate Amazon CloudWatch alarms for separate custom metrics that represent each error category, and configure each alarm to notify a different Amazon SNS topic\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate CloudWatch Logs metric filters for each error category, publish matching counts as metrics, and configure CloudWatch alarms to notify team-specific Amazon SNS topics when thresholds are breached\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate Amazon EventBridge rules with separate event patterns for each error category, and route each matching event to a dedicated Amazon SNS topic for the responsible team\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSend processing errors to Amazon SQS queues with message attributes, and require each team to poll its assigned queue for messages that match its error category\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company runs a real-time data pipeline that processes card authorization events. The company needs an alerting mechanism that routes processing errors to different teams based on the error category. Security exceptions must notify the security response team immediately. Schema validation failures must notify the data governance team. Infrastructure failures must notify the platform operations team.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate Amazon EventBridge rules with separate event patterns for each error category, and route each matching event to a dedicated Amazon SNS topic for the responsible team\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon EventBridge supports event patterns that match specific fields in an event. The company can publish pipeline error events with an error category field, then create one rule for security exceptions, one rule for schema validation failures, and one rule for infrastructure failures.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eEach EventBridge rule can route matching events directly to a dedicated Amazon SNS topic. The security team, data governance team, and platform operations team can subscribe to their respective topics and receive only the alerts that are relevant to their responsibilities.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational overhead because it uses managed event routing and managed notification delivery. The company does not need to write Lambda routing code, operate polling consumers, build queue-processing logic, or wait for metric thresholds to trigger alarms.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate Amazon CloudWatch alarms for separate custom metrics that represent each error category, and configure each alarm to notify a different Amazon SNS topic\u003c/strong\u003e - CloudWatch alarms are useful when a metric crosses a threshold. This works well for operational conditions such as high error rates, latency, failed invocations, or resource utilization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the requirement is to notify teams immediately for each error category. Metric-based alarms can introduce delay because they depend on metric publication, evaluation periods, and threshold state changes rather than direct event routing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate CloudWatch Logs metric filters for each error category, publish matching counts as metrics, and configure CloudWatch alarms to notify team-specific Amazon SNS topics when thresholds are breached\u003c/strong\u003e - CloudWatch Logs metric filters can identify patterns in log events and turn matching log entries into CloudWatch metrics. This can help teams measure how often different error categories occur.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, metric filters do not deliver the original event immediately to the appropriate team. The design depends on metric aggregation and alarm evaluation, which is better for threshold-based monitoring than direct real-time alert routing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSend processing errors to Amazon SQS queues with message attributes, and require each team to poll its assigned queue for messages that match its error category\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon SQS can decouple producers and consumers, and message attributes can help classify messages. Separate queues can allow teams or applications to consume only relevant messages.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, SQS requires consumers to poll queues. That polling model does not satisfy the requirement for immediate notification as directly as EventBridge routing to SNS. It also shifts responsibility to each team to operate a queue consumer or notification process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-rules.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-rules.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-event-patterns.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-event-patterns.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-targets.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-targets.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/filtering-events-in-amazon-eventbridge-with-wildcard-pattern-matching/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/filtering-events-in-amazon-eventbridge-with-wildcard-pattern-matching/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:32":{"id":"e:7:32","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 32","correct":[3],"answers":["Use Amazon EMR Studio notebooks","Use Athena query editor","Use AWS Glue Studio","Use Athena Notebooks"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer needs to perform data analytics on datasets that originate from various sources. The engineer wants to use Apache Spark to process these datasets and interactively visualize the results.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement with LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Athena Notebooks\u003c/strong\u003e is an interactive coding environment where you can write and execute Apache Spark code using Python. Athena notebooks allow chaining together SQL queries, calculations, and visualizations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eApache Spark\u003c/strong\u003e is a distributed processing framework used for big data analytics. It utilizes in-memory computing techniques for fast performance on large datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eUsing \u003cstrong\u003eApache Spark in Amazon Athena Notebooks\u003c/strong\u003e allows you to run Spark interactively on data from various sources without having to provision or manage infrastructure. You can write Python code in Athena notebooks to query data, perform Spark transformations and actions, and visualize results. Athena handles the automatic scaling of resources for your Spark jobs based on requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eData can be queried directly from data lakes in Amazon S3, databases, etc., using Athena\u0027s metadata cataloging.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNotebooks provide a Jupyter-like interface to develop and run Spark code and applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eResources are serverless - you only pay for the actual notebook/query executions without any upfront costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5d447c2bf762c64585ee29ac7891241cd741ca6a942147653a57cc5dfa6c8d19.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBased on the provided requirements, the most cost-effective solution for the data engineer to implement would be Amazon Athena Notebooks. Amazon Athena is a serverless solution, eliminating the need for infrastructure provisioning and management. It enables querying data directly from various sources, eliminating the need for data migration and satisfying the requirement to analyze data from different sources. Additionally, Athena\u0027s integration with Apache Spark allows for interactive data analytics and visualization through Python notebooks, chaining together SQL queries, calculations, and visualizations for exploration purposes. Athena\u0027s automatic scaling of compute resources ensures faster notebook execution without the hassle of resource sizing. Most importantly, with Athena, the data engineer only pays for the actual queries and notebooks run, with no upfront costs or minimum commitments, making it highly cost-effective for the required interactive analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cstrong\u003e \u003cspan\u003eUse Athena Notebooks.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Use Amazon EMR Studio notebooks\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because, although EMR notebooks support Apache Spark and interactive data analysis, they require provisioning and managing an EMR cluster, which can be more costly than a serverless solution like Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Athena query editor\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because the Athena query editor is designed for running SQL queries and does not directly support interactive data visualization or analytics using Apache Spark.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Studio\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because AWS Glue Studio is primarily used for creating, running, and monitoring ETL jobs, not for interactive data analysis and visualization with Apache Spark.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/spark/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/athena/spark/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:2":{"id":"e:5:2","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 2","correct":[0],"answers":["Implement job metrics in Glue to estimate the number of data processing units (DPUs). Based on the profiled metrics, adjust the value of the maximum capacity job parameter.","Use the Glue Job Editor\u0027s Data Preview feature to analyze data transformations visually and estimate the optimal number of DPUs required for job execution.","Configure Glue job bookmarks to track and analyze data processing progress. Use this information to adjust the number of DPUs needed for future job runs.","Allocate the maximum number of DPUs."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA data engineering team at a large retail company is planning to use AWS Glue for its data pipeline. The team needs to process approximately 500GB of data daily, which is stored in Amazon S3 and partitioned by date. The team plans to run Glue jobs every hour and is considering enabling autoscaling to optimize the number of DPUs.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhat steps should the data engineering team take to estimate the right number of DPUs for Glue? \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e provides a feature called job metrics, which can be used to estimate the number of DPUs that can be used to scale out an AWS Glue job. This feature is particularly useful in understanding the resource utilization of your jobs and can help in making informed decisions about scaling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c1d59bc1fa01445d12550a5354042d02f1e56078906c8aac965f3e254706f1f9.png\" alt=\"DPUs Monitoring\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you run a job, AWS Glue provides metrics such as the total number of actively running executors, the number of completed stages, and the number of maximum needed executors. These metrics can give you insights into whether your job is under-provisioned or over-provisioned.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor example, if the number of maximum needed executors is significantly higher than the number of active executors, it indicates that the job is under-provisioned. In such a case, you can increase the maximum capacity job parameter, which effectively increases the number of DPUs allocated to the job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement job metrics in Glue to estimate the number of data processing units (DPUs). Based on the profiled metrics, adjust the value of the maximum capacity job parameter.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAllocate the maximum number of DPUs\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because\u0026nbsp;allocating the maximum number of DPUs for all jobs, regardless of their complexity or data size, can lead to unnecessary costs. It’s important to match the resources to the requirements of each job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the Glue Job Editor’s Data Preview feature to analyze data transformations visually and estimate the optimal number of DPUs required for job execution\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Data Preview is only valuable in developing and debugging ETL scripts by allowing you to see a preview of the data transformations. However, it won’t help estimate the optimal number of DPUs for job execution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Glue job bookmarks to track and analyze data processing progress. Use this information to adjust the number of DPUs needed for future job runs\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Job bookmarks is simply used for tracking data that has already been processed in a job, thereby preventing redundant processing. They don’t provide insights into the optimal number of DPUs required.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-debug-capacity.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-debug-capacity.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-glue-best-practices-build-performant-data-pipeline/building-a-cost-effective-data-pipeline.html#estimate-aws-glue-dpu\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-glue-best-practices-build-performant-data-pipeline/building-a-cost-effective-data-pipeline.html#estimate-aws-glue-dpu\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:36":{"id":"e:6:36","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 36","correct":[1],"answers":["Implement MongoDB as the data catalog. Create a Lambda function to collect and update metadata in MongoDB. Schedule the function to run at regular intervals.","Utilize the AWS Glue Data Catalog as the central metadata repository. Set up Glue crawlers on a schedule to regularly crawl the data sources and refresh the metadata catalog.","Configure AWS Glue jobs to extract schema details and store the schema data in Amazon S3.","Utilize Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility) as the data catalog and create AWS Lambda functions to collect and refresh metadata in the DocumentDB database. Set a cron job to execute the Lambda functions periodically."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team is building a central data catalog and metadata management system for various data sources on AWS. The data includes semistructured and unstructured data formats, such as CSV logs, JSON files, .xml files, and NoSQL data in Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility). The team seeks an automated solution to continuously update the data catalog and monitor changes in source metadata with the least manual intervention.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution requires the LEAST amount of operational overhead to fulfill these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eThe \u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Data Catalog\u003c/strong\u003e is a fully managed, serverless, and cloud-optimized metadata repository that integrates seamlessly with other AWS services. It provides a unified view of all your data assets, making it easier to discover, search, and manage data across various AWS data stores.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/abd8cc17cd00627f5d1062ffc56ec3c06509dd1648b882f8496acba387460bd8.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue crawlers automatically scan your data sources, extract schema information, and populate the Glue Data Catalog with metadata tables, which can be used for data discovery, extract, transform, and load (ETL) jobs, and query execution. This process significantly reduces manual efforts and ensures that your data catalog is always up-to-date with the latest schema changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUtilize the AWS Glue Data Catalog as the central metadata repository. Set up Glue crawlers on a schedule to regularly crawl the data sources and refresh the metadata catalog.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement MongoDB as the data catalog. Create a Lambda function to collect and update metadata in MongoDB. Schedule the function to run at regular intervals\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. This option\u0026nbsp;requires custom development and manual setup for metadata management, which is less efficient than using AWS Glue\u0027s automated capabilities.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure AWS Glue jobs to extract schema details and store the schema data in Amazon S3\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. While this could work, it would require you to write custom scripts. This not only adds to the development effort but also to the maintenance effort, as any changes in data formats or structures would necessitate updates to your scripts. Glue jobs is more suitable for transformation jobs rather than inferring schema.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility) as the data catalog and create AWS Lambda functions to collect and refresh metadata in the DocumentDB database. Set a cron job to execute the Lambda functions periodically\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatiblity) is primarily a document database and not specialized for metadata management across various data sources. It would require more manual configuration and lacks the seamless integration with data cataloging features provided by AWS Glue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/populate-data-catalog.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/populate-data-catalog.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/crawler-data-stores.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/crawler-data-stores.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:1":{"id":"e:7:1","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 1","correct":[1,4],"answers":["Deliver the data to an Amazon OpenSearch Service cluster encrypted with AWS KMS.","Store the data in an Amazon S3 bucket and enable security features with AWS KMS server-side encryption (SSE-KMS).","Use Amazon EMR to process and transform the data, applying custom scripts to mask PII before usage in the ML model.","Load the dataset into Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler to encode the PII, then proceed with model training.","Implement AWS Glue DataBrew to manage the data intake process and employ a Glue DataBrew recipe job to conceal the PII within the dataset."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is leveraging its extensive dataset to train a machine learning (ML) model for predictive analytics. The dataset includes sensitive personally identifiable information (PII), which should be excluded from the ML model. In compliance with security standards, the data also needs to be encrypted using AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS). A data engineer is tasked with choosing the right AWS services to prepare and provide this data to the ML model.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements in the MOST cost-effective manner? (Select TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) is a managed service that enables you to easily create and control cryptographic keys used to encrypt your data. These keys are used across a variety of AWS services to provide encryption of stored data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/859153847226f4c519487e09cea766b26308acf37a2a6d98f923e21d1aaec025.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c54b824a2333edb54f0465774b5e9efec2592ff47955ea4f90cf9146f6532068.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eServer-Side Encryption with AWS KMS (SSE-KMS) is a feature where AWS manages both the encryption process and the cryptographic keys. When you use Amazon S3 with SSE-KMS, for example, your data is automatically encrypted as it is written to S3 and decrypted when accessed, using keys managed in KMS. This process provides an additional layer of security by involving the management of encryption keys. \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThis satisfies the encryption requirement of the problem.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/79043dddc090e2729d156048e8a0929e8d01eb440460653daceb87c6afaa6d83.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that simplifies the process of cleaning and normalizing data. It features data masking capabilities, which are crucial for handling PII. DataBrew can be configured to identify and mask PII, ensuring that sensitive information is obscured or removed from datasets before they are used in machine learning models or analytics. This ensures compliance with regulations that mandate the protection of PII. This satisfies the PII masking requirement of the problem.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Store the data in an Amazon S3 bucket and enable security features with AWS KMS server-side encryption (SSE-KMS).\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Implement AWS Glue DataBrew to manage the data intake process and employ a Glue DataBrew recipe job to conceal the PII within the dataset.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eDeliver the data to an Amazon OpenSearch Service cluster encrypted with AWS KMS\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. Although Amazon OpenSearch Service with AWS KMS encryption ensures data security, it is more costly due to its search and analytics capabilities, which are not necessary for simply delivering data to an ML model.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse Amazon EMR to process and transform the data, applying custom scripts to mask PII before usage in the ML model \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. Although Amazon EMR is capable of managing large datasets and executing custom scripts for tasks like PII masking, its cost-effectiveness is diminished due to its design for more extensive big data processing, analytics, and complex ETL jobs. These advanced capabilities of Amazon EMR exceed the simple requirements of PII masking for machine learning models. As a result, it becomes a less cost-efficient choice compared to more specialized data preparation tools, such as AWS Glue DataBrew, which are tailored for simpler data processing tasks without the additional features and associated costs of handling large-scale data processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eLoad the dataset into Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler to encode the PII, then proceed with model training\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because this solution would not satisfy the requirement that the ML model should not use the PII. Encoding PII within Data Wrangler does not eliminate it. Instead, it merely alters its format, leaving the underlying sensitive information accessible and potentially usable by the ML model, which poses a risk of non-compliance with data privacy standards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/bucket-encryption.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/bucket-encryption.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch%20service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-gs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-gs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/data-wrangler.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/data-wrangler.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out the TutorialsDojo Cheat Sheets here:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-key-management-service-aws-kms/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-key-management-service-aws-kms/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:43":{"id":"e:6:43","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 43","correct":[1],"answers":["Migrate the on-premises Hive metastore to Amazon S3 using AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS).","Create AWS Data Catalog tables as an external Hive metastore for the EMR cluster.","Configure an Amazon Aurora MySQL database as an external Hive metastore for the EMR cluster.","Launch a new Hive metastore in the EMR cluster and transfer existing on-premises data to it. Configure AWS DataSync for ongoing replication of the EMR Hive metastore changes to an Amazon S3 bucket."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn organization is migrating its Apache Hadoop cluster from a data center to an Amazon EMR cluster. The organization wants to adopt a serverless architecture for managing its data catalog, which is currently managed through an Apache Hive metastore within its local Hadoop cluster. The metastore records must be stored in persistent data storage. The organization is also reviewing its existing Apache Oozie workflows as part of the broader migration from the on-premises Hadoop environment.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is the MOST cost-effective solution to fulfill these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eIn an Amazon EMR cluster, Hive records metastore information in a MySQL database on the primary node\u0027s file system by default. The metastore contains a description of the table and the underlying data on which it is built, including the partition names, data types, and so on. When a cluster terminates, all cluster nodes shut down, including the primary node. When this happens, local data is lost because node file systems use ephemeral storage. If you need the metastore to persist, you must create an\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003eexternal metastore\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;that exists outside the cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou have two options for an external metastore:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e1. AWS Glue Data Catalog\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e2. Amazon RDS or Amazon Aurora\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ea518a13e89bb402f9570db7c72a5b59b14a2bdc4bf585a8d0b3d560f890e110.png\" alt=\"Migrating Hadoop to EMR and Hive Metastore to AWS\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe AWS Glue Data Catalog provides a centralized metadata repository for clients running Apache Hive and Apache Spark. This serverless repository eliminates the need for operational tasks like scaling, patching, and managing servers, which aligns well with the organization’s goal to adopt a serverless architecture. The AWS Glue Data Catalog can seamlessly integrate with Amazon EMR, providing a managed Hive metastore service that simplifies the process of cataloging, searching, and querying metadata.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate AWS Data Catalog tables as an external Hive metastore for the EMR cluster.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMigrate the on-premises Hive metastore to Amazon S3 using AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Amazon S3 can store Hive data, but using it as a standalone metastore is not just standard practice. This is because S3, as an object storage service, lacks native support for database-like operations, schema management, and querying capabilities that are typical of a metastore. Metastores depend on structured schema information to manage and query metadata about the data stored, usually in relational databases or specialized metadata management services like the AWS Glue Data Catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an Amazon Aurora MySQL database as an external Hive metastore for the EMR cluster\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While Amazon Aurora MySQL is a valid external metastore, it\u0026nbsp;requires managing a database instance, which contradicts the requirement of a serverless solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch a new Hive metastore in the EMR cluster and transfer existing on-premises data to it. Configure AWS DataSync for ongoing replication of the EMR Hive metastore changes to an Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Local Hive metastores within EMR clusters are vulnerable to data loss during node failures, making them not a persistent storage solution. Moreover, AWS DataSync can only monitor and synchronize file changes, not database changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-hive-metastore-external.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-hive-metastore-external.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-metastore-external-hive.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-metastore-external-hive.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these Amazon Aurora and Amazon EMR Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-aurora/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-aurora/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:58":{"id":"e:3:58","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 58","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE eventtime \u0026gt;= \u00272025-01-01T00:00:00Z\u0027 GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage ORDER BY TotalEvents DESC LIMIT 10\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE errorcode IS NOT NULL AND eventtime \u0026gt;= \u00272025-01-01T00:00:00Z\u0027 GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage LIMIT 10\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE errorcode = NULL AND eventtime \u0026gt;= \u00272025-01-01T00:00:00Z\u0027 GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage ORDER BY TotalEvents DESC LIMIT 10\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE errorcode IS NOT NULL AND eventtime \u0026gt;= \u00272025-01-01T00:00:00Z\u0027 GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage ORDER BY TotalEvents DESC LIMIT 10\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer created an Amazon Athena table named audit_cloudtrail_events to query AWS CloudTrail logs for a centralized security account. The audit team wants a summary of API errors that have occurred since the beginning of 2025.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe query must include only events that contain an error code. The query must group the results by API action, error code, and error message, and must return the 10 error groups with the highest occurrence counts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich query will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE errorcode IS NOT NULL AND eventtime \u0026gt;= \u00272025-01-01T00:00:00Z\u0027 GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage ORDER BY TotalEvents DESC LIMIT 10\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis query meets all stated requirements. It filters the CloudTrail table to include only records where errorcode is not null, which means the result focuses only on events that produced an error code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe query also filters eventtime to include only events that occurred on or after the beginning of 2025. This satisfies the audit team’s requirement to analyze errors from the specified time window.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFinally, the query groups by eventname, errorcode, and errormessage, counts the events in each group, orders the groups by TotalEvents in descending order, and limits the output to 10 rows. This returns the 10 most frequent error groups.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE eventtime \u0026gt;= \u00272025-01-01T00:00:00Z\u0027 GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage ORDER BY TotalEvents DESC LIMIT 10\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - This query groups the events correctly and orders the grouped results by TotalEvents in descending order. It also filters the results to events since the beginning of 2025.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that it does not filter out records where errorcode is null. As a result, the query could include non-error events or grouped records that do not contain an actual error code, which does not meet the requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE errorcode IS NOT NULL AND eventtime \u0026gt;= \u00272025-01-01T00:00:00Z\u0027 GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage LIMIT 10\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - This query correctly filters for non-null error codes and includes only events from the required time period. It also groups the results by API action, error code, and error message.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because it does not sort the grouped results by TotalEvents in descending order. Without the ORDER BY clause, the query does not guarantee that the returned 10 rows are the error groups with the highest occurrence counts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE errorcode = NULL AND eventtime \u0026gt;= \u00272025-01-01T00:00:00Z\u0027 GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage ORDER BY TotalEvents DESC LIMIT 10\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - This query attempts to count and group error-related fields, and it orders the grouped results by TotalEvents in descending order. It also applies the required eventtime filter.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis query is incorrect because SQL comparisons with NULL require IS NULL or IS NOT NULL. The expression errorcode = NULL does not correctly identify records with missing error codes, and the requirement is to include events where an error code exists.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/query-examples-cloudtrail-logs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/query-examples-cloudtrail-logs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/cloudtrail-logs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/cloudtrail-logs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automating-aws-service-logs-table-creation-and-querying-them-with-amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automating-aws-service-logs-table-creation-and-querying-them-with-amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:15":{"id":"e:5:15","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 15","correct":[2],"answers":["Manually categorize each earthquake record based on the grid system before storing the data in the S3 bucket.","Incorporate the calculation logic directly within each SQL query in Athena","Implement an Amazon Athena User Defined Function (UDF) powered by AWS Lambda to calculate the grid areas using the geospatial indexing system.","Use Amazon Redshift to preprocess and categorize the data based on the geospatial indexing system before querying with Athena."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data analysis team is working with a large dataset of historical earthquake records stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. Their goal is to determine the distribution of earthquakes across various geographical regions. To achieve this, they need to calculate grid areas based on coordinates using a geospatial indexing system.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is the most efficient way for the team to incorporate this calculation into their Athena queries?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena now supports User Defined Functions (UDFs), allowing customers to write custom scalar functions and invoke them in SQL queries. UDFs in Athena are defined within an AWS Lambda function as methods in a Java deployment package. This feature is particularly useful for scenarios requiring custom processing, such as categorizing earthquake locations using a geospatial indexing system. UDFs enable the encapsulation of complex logic into reusable functions, thus simplifying SQL queries in Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2cd0bc1ec91fc1f74be3cff85f55d78d0758a911fc6e8b17cf19813b5e80b959.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement an Amazon Athena User Defined Function (UDF) powered by AWS Lambda to calculate the grid areas using the geospatial indexing system. \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eManually categorize each earthquake record based on the grid system before storing the data in the S3 bucket.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because this approach would require significant manual effort and would not be scalable or efficient for large datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncorporate the calculation logic directly within each SQL query in Athena\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect as it makes the query unnecessarily complex and less efficient, which is what the UDF aims to avoid. Also, built-in SQL queries in Athena may not be enough for complex calculations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift to preprocess and categorize the data based on the geospatial indexing system before querying with Athena\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because it involves an additional data processing step outside of Athena, which is not as efficient as using a UDF within Athena itself.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eResources:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/querying-udf.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/querying-udf.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/querying-udf.html#udf-write-java\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/querying-udf.html#udf-write-java\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/querying-udf.html#udf-create-deploy\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/querying-udf.html#udf-create-deploy\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:29":{"id":"e:4:29","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 29","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse the fully managed Apache Kafka cluster to capture the sensor data in near real-time. Store the data in Amazon S3 for querying\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) to capture the real-time sensor data. Define an AWS Lambda function to poll the SQS queues and process the data. Store the data in Amazon DynamoDB for querying\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose to capture the sensor data. Directly store the data in Amazon DynamoDB for querying\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to capture the sensor data. Define an AWS Lambda function to process the data and write to the DynamoDB table. Store the data in Amazon DynamoDB for querying\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn Internet-of-Things (IoT) company needs a solution that can collect near real-time data from all its devices/sensors and store them in nested JSON format. The solution must offer data persistence and support the capability to query the data with a maximum latency of 10 milliseconds.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs a data engineer, how will you implement an optimal solution such that it has the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to capture the sensor data. Define an AWS Lambda function to process the data and write to the DynamoDB table. Store the data in Amazon DynamoDB for querying\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams is a serverless streaming data service that makes it easy to capture, process, and store data streams at any scale. Kinesis Data Streams manages the infrastructure, storage, networking, and configuration needed to stream your data at the level of your data throughput. You don\u0027t have to worry about provisioning, deployment, or ongoing maintenance of hardware, software, or other services for your data streams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams is the right choice for capturing the data with the least operational overhead. AWS Lambda function can be defined to process the data and write it to Amazon DynamoDB. Amazon DynamoDB is a serverless, NoSQL, fully managed database service with single-digit millisecond response times at any scale, enabling you to develop and run modern applications while only paying for what you use. DynamoDB supports nested JSON objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the fully managed Apache Kafka cluster to capture the sensor data in near real-time. Store the data in Amazon S3 for querying\u003c/strong\u003e - Apache Kafka is a distributed data store optimized for ingesting and processing streaming data in real time. Streaming data can be defined as data that is continuously generated by thousands of data sources, which typically send the data records simultaneously. For the given use case, Kafka will not be as operationally efficient as Kinesis Data Streams. Also, storing nested JSON data in Amazon S3 will not support the requirement of a maximum allowed latency of 10 milliseconds.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose to capture the sensor data. Directly store the data in Amazon DynamoDB for querying\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Data Firehose cannot directly write to Amazon DynamoDB. Firehose currently supports Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, Amazon OpenSearch Service, Splunk, Datadog, NewRelic, Dynatrace, Sumo Logic, LogicMonitor, MongoDB, and HTTP End Point as destinations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) to capture the real-time sensor data. Define an AWS Lambda function to poll the SQS queues and process the data. Store the data in Amazon DynamoDB for querying\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon SQS offers a reliable, highly scalable hosted queue for storing messages as they travel between applications or microservices. It moves data between distributed application components and helps you decouple these components. Amazon SQS cannot be used to capture and process real-time data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/sqs/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/sqs/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:4":{"id":"e:7:4","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 4","correct":[0,1],"answers":["Data Lake Administrator","Database Creator","\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eIAMFullAccess\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e","Lake Formation Data Analyst","Lake Formation Data Catalog Full Access"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company that uses AWS Lake Formation for its data lake solution is in the process of consolidating all marketing data into a centralized data lake. During the process, the team encounters a \u003ccode\u003eNotAuthorized\u003c/code\u003e error while trying to create a new database and data catalog within Lake Formation. This error hinders the team from properly managing the data lake and creating databases.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat additional permissions, roles, or policies should the team be granted to resolve the issue? (Select TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eThe Data Lake Administrator role is an important permission in AWS Lake Formation. This permission allows a user to perform several important tasks related to managing a data lake. These tasks include registering Amazon S3 locations, accessing the Data Catalog, creating databases, creating and running workflows, granting Lake Formation permissions to other users, and viewing AWS CloudTrail logs. Without this permission, a user would not be able to perform these tasks, which are essential for managing a data lake in AWS Lake Formation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/96c3e3a708b1d5df25d044e42e673bd28400c7a8f9696c5cc8f088c49652fa55.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe database creator permission is another important permission in AWS Lake Formation. This permission specifically allows a user to create new databases within the data lake. Creating databases is a fundamental task when managing a data lake, as databases are used to organize and store the data within the lake. Without this permission, a user would not be able to create new databases, which could hinder their ability to manage the data lake effectively.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e-\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eData Lake Administrator\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDatabase Creator\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eIAMFullAccess\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While this IAM policy provides broad permissions across AWS services, it does not specifically grant the necessary permissions to manage a data lake and create databases in AWS Lake Formation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLake Formation Data Analyst\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because this role just represents a user who can run queries against the data lake such as Amazon Athena. However, this role does not necessarily have permission to manage the data lake or create databases, which are required to resolve the “NotAuthorized” error in this scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLake Formation Data Catalog Full Access\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because this permission allows a user to access the data catalog in AWS Lake Formation. However, this permission does not include the ability to create databases or manage the data lake, which is required to resolve the “NotAuthorized” error in the given scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/permissions-reference.html#persona-dl-admin\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/permissions-reference.html#persona-dl-admin\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/control-data-access-and-permissions-with-aws-lake-formation-and-amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/control-data-access-and-permissions-with-aws-lake-formation-and-amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Lake Formation Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:17":{"id":"e:4:17","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 17","correct":[1,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eRewrite the AWS Glue job as a Python shell job and process the dataset in a single-threaded script to avoid Spark shuffle spill\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable the \u003ccode\u003e--write-shuffle-files-to-s3\u003c/code\u003e job parameter and apply salting to distribute skewed keys more evenly\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Spark UI and AWS Glue metrics to inspect executor-level data skew, shuffle spill, and task distribution\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the worker type to a larger AWS Glue worker family to vertically scale disk and memory for skewed tasks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the number of AWS Glue workers horizontally so Spark automatically removes data skew from all shuffle stages\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer maintains an AWS Glue Apache Spark ETL job that transforms a large set of marketplace transaction records. The job occasionally fails during wide transformations such as joins and aggregations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe error message says, \"No space left on device.\" The data engineer needs to identify whether data skew or excessive shuffle spill is causing the issue and implement a cost-effective fix that reduces local disk pressure during the job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of steps will meet this requirement MOST cost-effectively? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Spark UI and AWS Glue metrics to inspect executor-level data skew, shuffle spill, and task distribution\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSpark UI and AWS Glue metrics help the data engineer investigate the source of the failure. A No space left on device error often occurs when Spark writes a large amount of shuffle or spill data to local executor disk.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSpark UI can show stage-level and task-level behavior, including uneven task duration, shuffle read and write sizes, and disk spill. AWS Glue metrics can also help reveal abnormalities in executor behavior and data skew.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis step satisfies the troubleshooting part of the requirement. Before changing capacity or job logic, the engineer should confirm whether skewed partitions or heavy shuffle stages are filling local disk.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable the \u003ccode\u003e--write-shuffle-files-to-s3\u003c/code\u003e job parameter and apply salting to distribute skewed keys more evenly\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003e--write-shuffle-files-to-s3\u003c/code\u003e parameter enables the AWS Glue Spark shuffle manager to write shuffle files to Amazon S3 instead of relying only on local executor disk. This directly reduces the chance that shuffle-heavy stages fail because local disk fills up.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSalting helps address skewed keys by spreading records for heavily represented keys across more partitions. This reduces the likelihood that one executor or task receives a disproportionate amount of data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis combination is cost-effective because it addresses the root causes instead of simply adding more capacity. S3-backed shuffle reduces local disk pressure, and salting reduces skew that can concentrate work on a small number of tasks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the worker type to a larger AWS Glue worker family to vertically scale disk and memory for skewed tasks\u003c/strong\u003e - Choosing a larger AWS Glue worker type can provide more memory and local disk per worker. This can sometimes help jobs that need more resources per executor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that vertical scaling does not directly solve data skew. A skewed key can still overload a small number of tasks, and larger workers can increase cost without addressing the uneven data distribution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the number of AWS Glue workers horizontally so Spark automatically removes data skew from all shuffle stages\u003c/strong\u003e - Adding more AWS Glue workers can increase the total compute capacity available to the Spark job. This can help when the job is generally under-provisioned.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because horizontal scaling alone does not eliminate data skew. If one or a few partitions contain most of the data, Spark can still place too much work on a small number of tasks and continue to spill heavily.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRewrite the AWS Glue job as a Python shell job and process the dataset in a single-threaded script to avoid Spark shuffle spill\u003c/strong\u003e - Python shell jobs can run Python scripts in AWS Glue for lightweight processing tasks. They can be useful for simple file processing or small utility workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not meet the requirement because the workload is an Apache Spark ETL job that performs large transformations. Rewriting the job as a single-threaded script would reduce scalability and would not be appropriate for large Spark-style joins or aggregations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark-shuffle-manager.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark-shuffle-manager.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/optimize-shuffles.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/optimize-shuffles.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitoring-awsglue-with-cloudwatch-metrics.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitoring-awsglue-with-cloudwatch-metrics.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-amazon-s3-shuffle-in-aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-amazon-s3-shuffle-in-aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:62":{"id":"e:4:62","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 62","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up streaming data ingestion in Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, stage the streams data into Amazon S3 and load it into the Redshift cluster using the COPY command on a real-time basis\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up streaming data ingestion in Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and create materialized views directly on top of the stream by using SQL queries. Configure the materialized view to auto-refresh\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up streaming data ingestion in Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, write the delivery streams data into Amazon S3 and load it into the Redshift cluster using the COPY command on a real-time basis\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon Redshift streaming ingestion by configuring an external schema that maps to the streaming data source in Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Create a materialized view that references the external schema. Configure the materialized view to auto-refresh\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is looking to develop real-time analytics capabilities and plans to utilize Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and Amazon Redshift to handle streaming data at rates of several gigabytes per second. The company aims to achieve near real-time insights using the existing business intelligence (BI) and analytics tools.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution would fulfill these requirements with minimal operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up Amazon Redshift streaming ingestion by configuring an external schema that maps to the streaming data source in Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Create a materialized view that references the external schema. Configure the materialized view to auto-refresh\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSetting up Amazon Redshift streaming ingestion involves creating an external schema that maps to the streaming data source and creating a materialized view that references the external schema. Amazon Redshift streaming ingestion supports Kinesis Data Streams as a source. As such, you must have a Kinesis Data Streams source available before configuring streaming ingestion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/317526c8a7bf8794da23a9333e27c174658a8709d5b7cec6a3233ca4153a022c.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/317526c8a7bf8794da23a9333e27c174658a8709d5b7cec6a3233ca4153a022c.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift streaming ingestion uses a materialized view, which is updated directly from the stream when REFRESH is run. The materialized view maps to the stream data source. You can perform filtering and aggregations on the stream data as part of the materialized view definition. Your streaming ingestion materialized view (the base materialized view) can reference only one stream, but you can create additional materialized views that join with the base materialized view and with other materialized views or tables. The materialized view is set to auto-refresh and will be refreshed as data keeps arriving in the stream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2d99657cbe32a388dd9a59911b0ad222bb3b6e56dad727eac968a2fb3460cbde.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2d99657cbe32a388dd9a59911b0ad222bb3b6e56dad727eac968a2fb3460cbde.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the given use case, you can leverage Amazon Redshift streaming ingestion for Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, which enables you to ingest data directly from the Kinesis data stream without having to stage the data in Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3). Streaming ingestion allows you to achieve low latency in the order of seconds while ingesting hundreds of megabytes of data into your Amazon Redshift cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up streaming data ingestion in Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, stage the streams data into Amazon S3 and load it into the Redshift cluster using the COPY command on a real-time basis\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift streaming ingestion allows you to connect to Kinesis Data Streams directly, without the latency and complexity associated with staging the data in Amazon S3 and loading it into the cluster. However, this option proposes staging the streaming data from Kinesis Data Streams into Amazon S3 and then loading it to the Redshift cluster, which represents an unnecessary operational overhead, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up streaming data ingestion in Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, write the delivery streams data into Amazon S3 and load it into the Redshift cluster using the COPY command on a real-time basis\u003c/strong\u003e - As mentioned above, writing the delivery streams data from Kinesis Data Firehose into Amazon S3 and then loading to the Redshift cluster represents an unnecessary operational overhead, so this option is incorrect. You should also note that Kinesis Data Firehose can only perform near real-time processing and NOT real-time data streams processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up streaming data ingestion in Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and create materialized views directly on top of the stream by using SQL queries. Configure the materialized view to auto-refresh\u003c/strong\u003e - This option acts as a distractor. You cannot create materialized views directly on top of a stream in Amazon Kinesis Data Streams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion-getting-started.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion-getting-started.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:22":{"id":"e:5:22","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 22","correct":[2],"answers":["Use Amazon QuickSight for data analysis and visualization.","Use AWS Step Functions to orchestrate data processing tasks.","Use AWS Glue DataBrew for data cleaning and normalization.","Use AWS Glue ETL jobs for data transformation."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn e-commerce company is planning to launch a new product line and wants to analyze customer reviews of similar products from their competitors. They have collected a large amount of review data. The company wants a solution that can help its data analysts, who have varying levels of technical skills, to clean, normalize, and analyze this data without worrying about infrastructure management.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich AWS service would be the most suitable for this task?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that makes it easy for data analysts and data scientists to clean and normalize data to prepare it for analytics and machine learning. It offers a wide selection of pre-built transformations to automate data preparation tasks, all without the need to write any code. You can automate filtering anomalies, converting data to standard formats, and correcting invalid values, and other tasks. After your data is ready, you can immediately use it for analytics and machine learning projects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/71d3b11f725265adfd7f3a6428f50f5cd81f5224c9babec16519d508c6650915.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSince AWS Glue DataBrew is serverless, you do not need to provision or manage any infrastructure to use it. With AWS Glue DataBrew, you can easily and quickly create and execute data preparation recipes through a visual interface. The service automatically scales up or down based on the size and complexity of your data, so you only pay for the resources you use.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTherefore, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew for data cleaning and normalization.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue ETL jobs for data transformation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because\u0026nbsp;AWS Glue ETL jobs are used for extracting, transforming, and loading data. While they can be used for data transformation, they do not provide the visual interface for data cleaning and normalization that DataBrew does.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp; Use AWS Step Functions to orchestrate data processing tasks\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While possible, this solution brings unnecessary complexity. AWS Step Functions is commonly used for coordinating multiple AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon QuickSight to visualize and clean the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Amazon QuickSight is primarily a business intelligence (BI) tool. While it does have some data preparation capabilities, it is not designed specifically for data cleaning and lacks the wide range of pre-built transformations, such as data normalization, deduplication, and data type conversion, that AWS Glue DataBrew offers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/databrew\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/databrew\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-event-driven-data-quality-pipelines-with-aws-glue-databrew/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-event-driven-data-quality-pipelines-with-aws-glue-databrew/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-38":{"id":"p:4-38","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 38","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCapture data in an Amazon EC2 instance store and then publish this data to Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose with Amazon S3 as the destination. Use Amazon Athena to query the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCapture data in an Amazon EBS volume and then publish this data to Amazon ElastiCache for Redis. Subscribe to the Redis channel to query the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCapture data in Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose with Amazon Redshift as the destination. Use Amazon Redshift to query the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCapture data in Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Use Amazon Kinesis Data Analytics to query and analyze this streaming data in real-time\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Dữ liệu chưa chuyển khỏi EC2 vẫn có nguy cơ mất khi hạ tầng hoặc tiến trình gặp sự cố. Instance store không phải tầng lưu bền vững; lưu ý bản thân reboot thông thường không đồng nghĩa dữ liệu trên instance store luôn bị xóa.","Redis Pub/Sub chuyển thông điệp cho subscriber, không cung cấp kho lịch sử bền vững để truy vấn như đề cần. Gắn thêm EBS cũng không tự biến chuỗi này thành hệ thống phân tích có khả năng mở rộng.","Firehose nhận và chuyển dữ liệu vào Redshift để lưu và truy vấn SQL, giảm phụ thuộc vào dữ liệu đang giữ trên EC2. Đây là luồng được quản lý; độ trễ chịu ảnh hưởng việc gom lô và nạp qua S3.","Kinesis Data Streams và công cụ phân tích luồng xử lý được dữ liệu đang chạy, nhưng phương án thiếu kho đích lưu dữ liệu dài hạn để truy vấn lại. Đề chọn Redshift cho cả lưu trữ lẫn phân tích."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is using a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances to ingest Internet-of-Things (IoT) data from various data sources. The data is in JSON format and ingestion rates can be as high as 1 MB/s. When an EC2 instance is restarted, the in-flight data is lost. The data engineering team at the company wants to store as well as query the ingested data in near-real-time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions provides near-real-time data querying that is scalable with minimal data loss?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:50":{"id":"e:1:50","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 50","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDecrease the Stream retention duration\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the number of shards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse batch messages\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Exponential Backoff\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eYou are a data engineer at an IT company. The company has multiple enterprise customers that manage their own mobile applications that capture and send data to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. They have been getting a \u003ccode\u003eProvisionedThroughputExceededException\u003c/code\u003e exception. Upon analysis, you notice that messages are being sent one by one at a high rate.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options will help with the exception while keeping costs at a minimum?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse batch messages\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams (KDS) is a massively scalable and durable real-time data streaming service. KDS can continuously capture gigabytes of data per second from hundreds of thousands of sources such as website clickstreams, database event streams, financial transactions, social media feeds, IT logs, and location-tracking events. The data collected is available in milliseconds to enable real-time analytics use cases such as real-time dashboards, real-time anomaly detection, dynamic pricing, and more.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams Overview:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/82bd94cc900d62610f42fd3b01a02e17d4b9fff9e0a797aefd3cdcd76aef70f6.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/82bd94cc900d62610f42fd3b01a02e17d4b9fff9e0a797aefd3cdcd76aef70f6.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen a host needs to send many records per second (RPS) to Amazon Kinesis, simply calling the basic PutRecord API action in a loop is inadequate. To reduce overhead and increase throughput, the application must batch records and implement parallel HTTP requests. This will increase the efficiency overall and ensure you are optimally using the shards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Exponential Backoff\u003c/strong\u003e - While this may help in the short term, as soon as the request rate increases, you will see the \u003ccode\u003eProvisionedThroughputExceededException\u003c/code\u003e exception again.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the number of shards\u003c/strong\u003e - Increasing shards could be a short-term fix but will substantially increase the cost, so this option is ruled out.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDecrease the Stream retention duration\u003c/strong\u003e - This operation may result in data loss and won\u0027t help with the exceptions, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/implementing-efficient-and-reliable-producers-with-the-amazon-kinesis-producer-library/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/implementing-efficient-and-reliable-producers-with-the-amazon-kinesis-producer-library/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-50":{"id":"p:4-50","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 50","correct":[2,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog to generate governed views for the Iceberg tables and require analysts to query only the generated views\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog to expire historical Iceberg snapshots so older table versions are removed from metadata and storage after the retention period\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog to generate column-level statistics for the Iceberg tables on a schedule\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog to automatically compact the Iceberg tables by rewriting small files into fewer larger files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog to automatically optimize secondary indexes for the Iceberg tables based on common player_id and session_id query filters\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Governed view giúp quản trị và đơn giản hóa quyền truy cập dữ liệu. Nó không tự gộp các tệp nhỏ hoặc tạo thống kê phục vụ bộ tối ưu truy vấn.","Hết hạn snapshot giúp dọn lịch sử và giảm dung lượng lưu trữ. Nó không trực tiếp tối ưu cách bố trí các tệp dữ liệu đang được truy vấn như compaction và thống kê cột.","Thống kê cột giúp bộ tối ưu truy vấn ước tính lượng dữ liệu và chọn kế hoạch thực thi phù hợp. Tạo thống kê theo lịch giữ thông tin này gần với dữ liệu thực tế khi bảng tăng.","Compaction viết lại nhiều tệp nhỏ thành ít tệp lớn hơn. Điều này giảm số lượt mở tệp và chi phí lập kế hoạch, đọc dữ liệu cho truy vấn Iceberg.","Iceberg không dùng secondary index kiểu database theo tính năng tự động được mô tả ở đây. Các công cụ tối ưu phù hợp trong đề là thống kê cột và compaction."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA mobile gaming company uses AWS Glue jobs to read from and write to Apache Iceberg tables that store real-time player session events. The Iceberg table data is stored in Apache Parquet format in Amazon S3. As event volume grows, analysts notice that queries against the player activity tables are becoming slower.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solutions will improve query performance? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-32":{"id":"p:1-32","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 32","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Lambda functions that run on an Amazon EventBridge schedule to read the S3 clickstream files, package the proprietary attribution libraries with the deployment bundle, transform the records in memory, and call the Amazon Redshift Data API to insert the transformed rows\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon SageMaker Processing jobs to run PySpark code with native SageMaker libraries, and configure the processing output to load transformed clickstream data into Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Studio to build and schedule serverless PySpark jobs, provide the proprietary libraries to the AWS Glue job from Amazon S3, and configure an AWS Glue connection to write the transformed data to Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EMR to run scheduled PySpark jobs, install the proprietary libraries by using bootstrap actions, and configure the Spark job to read from Amazon S3 and write to Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Lambda không phù hợp cho PySpark phức tạp trên dữ liệu hằng ngày rất lớn; có giới hạn runtime và tài nguyên. Chèn từng dòng qua Data API cũng không tối ưu cho tải lớn.","SageMaker Processing chủ yếu phục vụ quy trình xử lý cho ML. Phương án chỉ dựa vào thư viện native chưa giải quyết tốt thư viện riêng và ETL sang Redshift.","Glue Studio cung cấp tác vụ PySpark được quản lý, lập lịch và dùng thư viện riêng lưu trên S3. Kết nối Glue hỗ trợ ghi dữ liệu đã biến đổi vào Redshift.","EMR làm được nhưng cần thiết lập cụm, bootstrap thư viện và vận hành nhiều hơn. Đề ưu tiên giảm quản lý hạ tầng nên chọn Glue."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA digital advertising company stores raw impression and clickstream logs in an Amazon S3 bucket in the us-east-1 Region. The company must process the data once each night by running complex PySpark transformations that use proprietary attribution and enrichment libraries. After transformation, the company must load the curated dataset into Amazon Redshift for analytics. The solution must scale for large daily data volumes and minimize infrastructure management.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-2":{"id":"p:2-2","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 2","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ * \u0027^(John|Doe)\u0027\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ * \u0027$(John|Doe)\u0027\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ \u0027^(John|Doe)\u0027\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ \u0027$(John|Doe)\u0027\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Theo ý đồ đáp án, ^ neo ở đầu chuỗi, (John|Doe) chọn một trong hai tên và ~* biểu thị khớp không phân biệt hoa/thường. Lưu ý đề ghi MySQL nhưng dùng cú pháp kiểu PostgreSQL; không nên sao chép nguyên câu lệnh này sang MySQL.","Dấu $ neo ở cuối chuỗi, không phải đầu chuỗi. Đề cần tên bắt đầu bằng John hoặc Doe.","Dấu ^ đúng vị trí đầu chuỗi, nhưng trong cú pháp regex mà lời giải đang dùng, ~ phân biệt hoa/thường; ~* mới là không phân biệt.","Sai cả điều kiện đầu chuỗi và không phân biệt hoa/thường: dùng $ thay vì ^ và ~ thay vì ~*. Đề gốc cũng có bất nhất về tên hệ quản trị."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company stores its user information in a MySQL database table called \u003ccode\u003euser\u003c/code\u003e. The \u003ccode\u003ename\u003c/code\u003e column has the users\u0027 names stored in \u003ccode\u003efirstname lastname\u003c/code\u003e format. Due to legacy reasons, a few users have the names stored in \u003ccode\u003elastname firstname\u003c/code\u003e format. A data engineer has been tasked with developing a query that returns all records where the \u003ccode\u003ename\u003c/code\u003e column has values starting with \u003ccode\u003eJohn\u003c/code\u003e or \u003ccode\u003eDoe\u003c/code\u003e, on a case-insensitive basis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following queries represents the correct solution?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:34":{"id":"e:3:34","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 34","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eOpt for ALL distribution style for large tables. Declare primary and foreign keys for all tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpdate the distribution style for all tables to AUTO. Declare primary and foreign keys for all tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFor the rarely updated small tables, opt for ALL distribution style. Declare primary and foreign keys for all tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSwitch to Amazon Redshift Spectrum to efficiently query and retrieve data from the tables\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company\u0027s data engineer is tasked with enhancing the performance of SQL table queries on data stored in an Amazon Redshift cluster. Due to budget constraints, expanding the cluster size is not an option. The company utilizes the EVEN distribution style for loading data across multiple tables, with some tables containing hundreds of GB of data while others have less than 20 MB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution would address these needs effectively?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFor the rarely updated small tables, opt for ALL distribution style. Declare primary and foreign keys for all tables\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor ALL distribution, a copy of the entire table is distributed to every node. While EVEN distribution or KEY distribution places only a portion of a table\u0027s rows on each node, ALL distribution ensures that every row is collocated for every join that the table participates in.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eALL distribution multiplies the storage required by the number of nodes in the cluster, so it takes much longer to load, update, or insert data into multiple tables. ALL distribution is appropriate only for relatively slow-moving tables; that is, tables that are not updated frequently or extensively. Because the cost of redistributing small tables during a query is low, there isn\u0027t a significant benefit to defining small dimension tables as DISTSTYLE ALL.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHence, ALL distribution is suited for the rarely updated small tables while maintaining the EVEN distribution style for the larger tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDefine primary key and foreign key constraints between tables wherever appropriate. Even though they are informational only, the query optimizer uses those constraints to generate more efficient query plans.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eNote: Do not define primary key and foreign key constraints unless your application enforces the constraints. Amazon Redshift does not enforce unique, primary-key, or foreign-key constraints.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eOpt for ALL distribution style for large tables. Declare primary and foreign keys for all tables\u003c/strong\u003e - ALL distribution multiplies the storage required by the number of nodes in the cluster, and so it takes much longer to load, update, or insert data into multiple tables. ALL distribution is appropriate only for relatively slow-moving tables; that is, tables that are not updated frequently or extensively.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate the distribution style for all tables to AUTO. Declare primary and foreign keys for all tables\u003c/strong\u003e - If a table is largely denormalized and does not participate in joins, or if you don\u0027t have a clear choice for another distribution style, use AUTO distribution. For this use case, AUTO is not an optimal choice since tables have clearly defined access patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSwitch to Amazon Redshift Spectrum to efficiently query and retrieve data from the tables\u003c/strong\u003e - Using Amazon Redshift Spectrum, you can efficiently query and retrieve structured and semistructured data from files in Amazon S3 without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables. This option acts as a distractor since the given use case does not mention using Amazon S3 for storing data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_designating_distribution_styles.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_designating_distribution_styles.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-defining-constraints.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-defining-constraints.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-8":{"id":"p:3-8","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 8","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to retrieve and efficiently query the streaming data for analytics\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse federated queries feature of Amazon Redshift to retrieve and efficiently query the streaming data for analytics\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDirect the Kinesis Data Streams output into a Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream. Leverage the direct integration of Kinesis Data Firehose with Amazon Redshift Serverless to deliver the streaming data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the streaming ingestion feature of Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Spectrum đọc dữ liệu trên S3, không tiêu thụ trực tiếp luồng Kinesis vào Redshift.","Federated Query đọc các database ngoài được hỗ trợ, không phải cơ chế nạp luồng Kinesis.","Firehose thêm lớp delivery/buffering và staging trong đường nạp. Streaming ingestion trực tiếp ít thành phần và phù hợp độ trễ thấp hơn theo đề.","Redshift streaming ingestion đọc trực tiếp nguồn Kinesis được hỗ trợ vào materialized view. Có thể phân tích cùng dữ liệu đã lưu mà giảm pipeline trung gian."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn e-commerce company uses Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to process real-time clickstream data from its website. The company needs to perform near real-time analytics of the streaming data in conjunction with the previous day\u0027s data. The streaming data should be stored in Amazon Redshift Serverless service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:10:20":{"id":"e:10:20","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 20","correct":[0],"answers":["Execute an AWS Glue crawler to establish a data catalog for existing data and register its Amazon S3 path location on Lake Formation. For data security, allocate granular permissions to various users.","Leverage Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) to orchestrate and schedule data processing workflows. Ensure data security by assigning appropriate access controls.","Using AWS Lake Formation, register the S3 bucket where the data resides. To secure the data, assign granular permissions to different users.","Import data from Amazon S3 using an AWS Lake Formation blueprint. Subsequently, accord users granular Lake Formation permissions as needed."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is assigned to build a data lake for a company’s business data stored on Amazon S3. The data, saved in the Apache Parquet format, will be utilized for complex analytic tasks. The company anticipates that users will need granular access to the datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following will meet these requirements in the shortest amount of time and in the MOST cost-effective manner?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Lake Formation\u003c/strong\u003e is a service that makes it easy to set up a secure data lake in days. A data lake is a centralized, curated, and secured repository that stores all your data, both in its original form and prepared for analysis. A data lake enables you to break down data silos and combine different types of analytics to gain insights and guide better business decisions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 forms the storage layer for Lake Formation. If you already use S3, you typically begin by registering existing S3 buckets that contain your data. Lake Formation creates new buckets for the data lake and imports data into them. AWS always stores this data in your account, and only you have direct access to it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/88b2714812a8d8367da385e92999efc03a3553eca13595b31fc340bffae1bab3.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation is integrated with AWS Glue, which you can use to create a data catalog that describes available datasets and their appropriate business applications.\u0026nbsp;Lake Formation lets you define policies and control data access with simple \"grant and revoke permissions to data\" sets at granular levels.\u0026nbsp;You can assign permissions to IAM users, roles, groups, and Active Directory users using federation. You specify permissions on catalog objects (like tables and columns) rather than on buckets and objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation also provides blueprints that you can run for loading and cataloging data.\u0026nbsp;A blueprint allows you to\u0026nbsp;import data from MySQL, Postgres, SQL Server, MariaDB, and Oracle databases running in Amazon RDS or hosted in Amazon EC2. With Lake Formation, you can import data from MySQL, Postgres, SQL Server, MariaDB, and Oracle databases running in Amazon RDS or hosted in Amazon EC2. You can also import from on-premises databases by connecting\u0026nbsp;with Java Database Connectivity (JDBC).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIt was indicated in the scenario that data in Parquet format is already existing in Amazon S3. As a result, you don\u0027t need to utilize a Lake Formation blueprint.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eExecute an AWS Glue crawler to establish a data catalog for existing data and register its Amazon S3 path location on Lake Formation. For data security, allocate granular permissions to various users.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) to orchestrate and schedule data processing workflows. Ensure data security by assigning appropriate access controls\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because while Amazon MWAA can orchestrate and schedule data processing workflows, it does not provide a solution for creating a data lake or managing granular access to datasets. These tasks would need to be handled separately, which could increase complexity and cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUsing AWS Lake Formation, register the S3 bucket where the data resides. To secure the data, assign granular permissions to different users\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. You should register the Amazon S3 path of the bucket where your data is stored and not the bucket name.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImport data from Amazon S3 using an AWS Lake Formation blueprint. Subsequently, accord users granular Lake Formation permissions as needed\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because Amazon S3 is not a valid import source in a Lake Formation blueprint.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/building-securing-and-managing-data-lakes-with-aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/building-securing-and-managing-data-lakes-with-aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Lake Formation Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-6":{"id":"p:1-6","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 6","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAccess and integrate third-party datasets available through AWS DataSync\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAccess and integrate third-party datasets available through AWS Data Exchange\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAccess and integrate third-party datasets available through AWS Marketplace\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAccess and integrate third-party datasets from AWS CodeCommit repositories\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["DataSync tập trung vào chuyển dữ liệu giữa các hệ thống lưu trữ. Nó không cung cấp sẵn cơ chế khám phá, đăng ký và tích hợp bộ dữ liệu bên thứ ba như Data Exchange.","AWS Data Exchange chuyên hỗ trợ tìm kiếm, đăng ký và sử dụng dữ liệu bên thứ ba. Đây là lựa chọn trực tiếp để bổ sung dữ liệu cho hệ thống phân tích với ít công tích hợp.","Marketplace là nơi mua phần mềm, dịch vụ và các sản phẩm khác. Trong bộ đề, Data Exchange là dịch vụ chuyên biệt cho việc tiếp nhận và sử dụng bộ dữ liệu bên thứ ba.","CodeCommit là kho quản lý phiên bản mã nguồn Git. Nó không phải dịch vụ phân phối và tích hợp dữ liệu thương mại của bên thứ ba."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn e-commerce company is looking to enhance its product recommendation system which relies on user behavior and preferences. The company wants to achieve this by integrating insights from third-party datasets into its existing analytics platform. The company aims to minimize the effort and time involved in this integration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution would achieve this with the least operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-20":{"id":"p:3-20","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 20","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eInvoke the AWS Glue crawler via an AWS Lambda function that is triggered by an S3:ObjectCreated:* event notification on the S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon CloudWatch Events with the rate (5 minutes) expression to execute the AWS Glue crawler every 5 minutes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena to directly analyze the current data in Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eModify the execution schedule of the AWS Glue crawler from 4 hours to 1 minute\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Tệp mới trên S3 kích hoạt Lambda gọi crawler, giảm thời gian metadata bị cũ so với chờ lịch bốn giờ. Khi triển khai cần xử lý trùng sự kiện và crawler đang chạy.","Lựa chọn chưa có cơ chế gọi crawler hợp lệ từ rule theo mô tả của bộ đề. Lambda làm cầu nối gọi API là phương án được chọn.","Đổi công cụ sang Athena không giữ yêu cầu Spark SQL trên EMR. Catalog dùng chung vẫn phải được cập nhật đúng.","Lịch crawler một phút không phù hợp độ phân giải lịch được đề nêu. Dùng sự kiện tệp mới để phản ứng theo dữ liệu."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media company uses an ad-hoc Kinesis Firehose-based solution to ingest raw data in JSON format and then deliver it to an Amazon S3 bucket. The data engineering team at the company uses Apache Spark SQL to analyze this data via Amazon EMR, which is configured to use AWS Glue Data Catalog as the metastore. An AWS Glue crawler runs every four hours to update the schema of the data catalog. The team has noticed that it sometimes obtains outdated data. You have been hired by the company as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to build a solution for ensuring that the team always has access to the current data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents the best solution to meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:56":{"id":"e:1:56","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 56","correct":[0,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDevelop the leaderboard using DynamoDB with DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) as it meets the in-memory, high availability, and low latency requirements\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop the leaderboard using AWS Neptune as it meets the in-memory, high availability, low latency requirements\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop the leaderboard using DynamoDB as it meets the in-memory, high availability, low latency requirements\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop the leaderboard using RDS Aurora as it meets the in-memory, high availability, low latency requirements\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop the leaderboard using ElastiCache Redis as it meets the in-memory, high availability, low latency requirements\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company runs multiple gaming platforms that need to store game state, player data, session history, and leaderboards. The company is looking to move to AWS Cloud to scale reliably to millions of concurrent users and requests while ensuring consistently low latency measured in single-digit milliseconds. The data engineering team at the company is evaluating multiple in-memory data stores with the ability to power its on-demand, live leaderboard. The company\u0027s leaderboard requires high availability, low latency, and real-time processing to deliver customizable user data for the community of its users.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions can be used to address the given requirements? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDevelop the leaderboard using ElastiCache Redis as it meets the in-memory, high availability, and low latency requirements\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon ElastiCache for Redis is a blazing fast in-memory data store that provides sub-millisecond latency to power internet-scale real-time applications. Amazon ElastiCache for Redis is a great choice for real-time transactional and analytical processing use cases such as caching, chat/messaging, gaming leaderboards, geospatial, machine learning, media streaming, queues, real-time analytics, and session store.\nElastiCache for Redis can be used to power the live leaderboard, so this option is correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eElastiCache for Redis Overview:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/139489941d5c99822b7b4d15c1614d5c42e906d6f5e19035cb6855bc976682ea.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/139489941d5c99822b7b4d15c1614d5c42e906d6f5e19035cb6855bc976682ea.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDevelop the leaderboard using DynamoDB with DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) as it meets the in-memory, high availability, low latency requirements\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon DynamoDB is a key-value and document database that delivers single-digit millisecond performance at any scale. It\u0027s a fully managed, multi-Region, multi-master, durable database with built-in security, backup and restore, and in-memory caching for internet-scale applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDAX is a DynamoDB-compatible caching service that enables you to benefit from fast in-memory performance for demanding applications. So DynamoDB with DAX can be used to power the live leaderboard.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDAX Overview:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e66e0a55be25e24ed3968b752057dc74152b833614c72deb3df8d73f4dd9286c.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e66e0a55be25e24ed3968b752057dc74152b833614c72deb3df8d73f4dd9286c.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/DAX.concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/DAX.concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDevelop the leaderboard using Amazon Redshift as it meets the in-memory, high availability, low latency requirements\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift is a fully managed, scalable data warehouse that enables secure analytics at scale. Redshift is not an in-memory database, so this option is not correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDevelop the leaderboard using DynamoDB as it meets the in-memory, high availability, low latency requirements\u003c/strong\u003e - DynamoDB is not an in-memory database, so this option is not correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDevelop the leaderboard using RDS Aurora as it meets the in-memory, high availability, low latency requirements\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Aurora is a MySQL and PostgreSQL-compatible relational database built for the cloud, that combines the performance and availability of traditional enterprise databases with the simplicity and cost-effectiveness of open source databases. Amazon Aurora features a distributed, fault-tolerant, self-healing storage system that auto-scales up to 64TB per database instance. Aurora is not an in-memory database, so this option is not correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/elasticache/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/elasticache/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/elasticache/redis/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/elasticache/redis/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/dax/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/dax/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-62":{"id":"p:4-62","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 62","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an OpenSearch Service domain, index every sales record by TransactionDate, and use OpenSearch queries instead of Athena to retrieve date-range records\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConvert the CSV sales data to JSON format by using an AWS Glue job, catalog the JSON data in AWS Glue, and run Athena queries on the JSON files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse S3 prefixes to partition the sales data by TransactionDate, catalog the partitioned data in AWS Glue, add the partitions, and update Athena queries to use date filters\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon RDS database, create a Sales table with an index on TransactionDate, and use Lambda to load new CSV records into the database\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["OpenSearch có thể tìm theo ngày nhưng không đáp ứng điều kiện tiếp tục dùng Athena trên CSV S3. Nó còn thêm việc lập chỉ mục và vận hành một hệ thống mới.","Đổi CSV sang JSON không tạo lợi ích loại bỏ partition theo ngày. Cả hai vẫn là dạng văn bản theo hàng; cần tổ chức dữ liệu theo cột lọc để giảm quét.","Tổ chức prefix theo TransactionDate, khai báo partition trong Glue và lọc bằng cột partition trong Athena. Athena có thể bỏ qua các ngày ngoài phạm vi, giảm dữ liệu đọc và chi phí.","RDS cùng Lambda tạo thêm database và pipeline nạp riêng. Đề cần tối ưu Athena trên dữ liệu đã ở S3 nên không cần di chuyển sang hệ thống khác."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA regional retail company adds new transaction records to CSV sales data in an Amazon S3 bucket every day. The dataset contains sales data from the previous 6 years and includes columns such as TransactionID, TransactionDate, ProductName, SaleAmount, SalesAssociate, and StoreLocation. The company needs to use Amazon Athena to query the CSV data and retrieve records from specific date ranges. The company wants to reduce the amount of data that Athena scans and run the queries in the most cost-effective way.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:17":{"id":"e:1:17","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 17","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Glue DataBrew to analyze the data stored on Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Athena to analyze the data stored on Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Glue Data Catalog to analyze the data stored on Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Redshift Spectrum to analyze the data stored on Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial analytics company wants to gather insights from personal finance data stored on Amazon S3 in the Microsoft Excel workbook format.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents a serverless solution to interactively discover, clean and transform this raw data for performing this analysis?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage AWS Glue DataBrew to analyze the data stored on Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that enables users to clean and normalize data. AWS Glue DataBrew is a serverless solution to get insights from raw data. You can interactively discover, visualize, clean, and transform raw data. DataBrew makes smart suggestions to help you identify data quality issues that can be difficult to find and time-consuming to fix. To prepare the data, you can choose from more than 250 point-and-click transformations. These include removing nulls, replacing missing values, fixing schema inconsistencies, creating columns based on functions, and many more.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/254878d07e18a0bc4d60b4333d3bfe02d72fd6dc8ffbff59df9c6d8630a5288c.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/254878d07e18a0bc4d60b4333d3bfe02d72fd6dc8ffbff59df9c6d8630a5288c.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eRegarding any files stored in Amazon S3 or any files that you upload from a local drive, DataBrew supports the following file formats: comma-separated value (CSV), Microsoft Excel, JSON, ORC, and Parquet.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage Amazon Athena to analyze the data stored on Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Athena is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data directly in Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) using standard SQL. Athena supports creating tables and querying data from CSV, TSV, custom-delimited, and JSON formats; data from Hadoop-related formats: ORC, Apache Avro and Parquet; logs from Logstash, AWS CloudTrail logs, and Apache WebServer logs. Athena does not support querying data from files stored in the Microsoft Excel workbook format, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage Amazon Redshift Spectrum to analyze the data stored on Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - Using Amazon Redshift Spectrum, you can efficiently query and retrieve structured and semistructured data from files in Amazon S3 without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables. Redshift does not support reading Excel files directly, as it can only support CSV, AVRO, JSON, PARQUET and ORC formats.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage Amazon Glue Data Catalog to analyze the data stored on Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - The AWS Glue Data Catalog contains references to data that is used as sources and targets of your extract, transform, and load (ETL) jobs in AWS Glue. To create your data warehouse or data lake, you must catalog this data. The AWS Glue Data Catalog is an index to the location, schema, and runtime metrics of your data. The Amazon Glue Data Catalog itself cannot be used to analyze the data stored on Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Data Catalog:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5593d4981c62cbfd5954eea27348213acc753100206c8802e936769d031b3bc2.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5593d4981c62cbfd5954eea27348213acc753100206c8802e936769d031b3bc2.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/supported-data-file-sources.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/supported-data-file-sources.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/supported-serdes.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/supported-serdes.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/copy-parameters-data-format.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/copy-parameters-data-format.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-59":{"id":"p:1-59","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 59","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eBatch process the raw transactions data into Amazon S3 flat files. Use S3 events to trigger an AWS Lambda function to remove sensitive data from the raw transactions in the flat file and then store the cleansed transactions in Amazon DynamoDB. Leverage DynamoDB Streams to share the transaction data with the internal applications\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFeed the streaming transactions into Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Leverage AWS Lambda integration to remove sensitive data from every transaction and then store the cleansed transactions in Amazon DynamoDB. The internal applications can consume the raw transactions off the Amazon Kinesis Data Stream\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFeed the streaming transactions into Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose. Leverage AWS Lambda integration to remove sensitive data from every transaction and then store the cleansed transactions in Amazon DynamoDB. The internal applications can consume the raw transactions off the Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePersist the raw transactions into Amazon DynamoDB. Configure a rule in Amazon DynamoDB to update the transaction by removing sensitive data whenever any new raw transaction is written. Leverage Amazon DynamoDB Streams to share the transaction data with the internal applications\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Gom giao dịch thành tệp theo lô làm tăng độ trễ trước khi làm sạch. Không phù hợp yêu cầu gần thời gian thực.","Kinesis cho nhiều consumer đọc luồng; Lambda loại dữ liệu nhạy cảm trước khi lưu vào DynamoDB. Tách tiếp nhận luồng, biến đổi và lưu trữ theo đúng yêu cầu.","Firehose là dịch vụ giao dữ liệu tới đích, không phải luồng để nhiều ứng dụng tùy ý đọc như Kinesis Data Streams. DynamoDB cũng không phải đích giao trực tiếp theo mô tả này.","Phương án lưu dữ liệu nhạy cảm trước rồi mới xóa trái yêu cầu làm sạch trước khi lưu. DynamoDB cũng không có loại quy tắc cập nhật tự động như lựa chọn mô tả."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company runs its flagship web application on AWS. The application serves thousands of users during peak hours. The company needs a scalable near-real-time solution to share hundreds of thousands of financial transactions with multiple internal applications. The solution should also remove sensitive details from the transactions before storing the cleansed transactions in a document database for low-latency retrieval.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you recommend?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:7:17":{"id":"e:7:17","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 17","correct":[0,1,3],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eUpdate the AWS Glue crawler\u0027s configuration in the \u003ccode\u003eTutorial\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account to target the S3 bucket containing the VPC flow logs in the \u003ccode\u003eDojo\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eImplement a bucket policy on the S3 bucket in the \u003ccode\u003eDojo\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account that explicitly permits the AWS Glue crawler\u0027s IAM role in the \u003ccode\u003eTutorial\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account access to the bucket.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eUtilize AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM) in the \u003ccode\u003eDojo\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account to share the S3 bucket with the \u003ccode\u003eTutorial\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account, facilitating cross-account access.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eConfigure an IAM role in the \u003ccode\u003eTutorial\u003c/code\u003e’s account with permissions to access the S3 bucket in the \u003ccode\u003eDojo\u003c/code\u003e’s account and associate this role with the AWS Glue crawler.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eCreate a VPC Endpoint for S3 in \u003ccode\u003eDojo\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account to streamline access between the AWS Glue crawler in the \u003ccode\u003eTutorial\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account and the S3 bucket.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eEstablish a Direct Connect link between the \u003ccode\u003eTutorials\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003eDojo\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s accounts to ensure private connectivity for the AWS Glue crawler to access the S3 bucket.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team configures an AWS Glue crawler in the \u003ccode\u003eTutorial\u0027s\u003c/code\u003e AWS account to catalog VPC flow logs stored in an Amazon S3 bucket within the \u003ccode\u003eDojo\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account. The goal is to ensure the AWS Glue crawler in the \u003ccode\u003eTutorial\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account can access and catalog data seamlessly, focusing on integrating AWS services for cross-account resource access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of actions should be taken to fulfill these requirements? (Select THREE.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue is a completely managed service for ETL (extract, transform, load), simplifying the process for users to prepare and import their data for analytical purposes. The AWS Glue crawler can be configured to access data stores in different AWS accounts by using appropriate permissions and configurations. Cross-account access in AWS involves configuring resource-based policies and IAM roles to grant the necessary permissions across accounts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d277dd91559959aa7a73fc11fd8bdb72707e068d23a6fb2f4f2d620c80927d2d.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe administrator of Account A assigns a bucket policy that allows cross-account access, enabling Account B to execute certain operations on the bucket. It is important to recognize that the administrator of Account B gains these permissions by default. Subsequently, the administrator of Account B applies a user policy to an account user, passing on the rights obtained from Account A. Following this, the user in Account B tests the permissions by accessing a file in the bucket that is controlled by Account A.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e-\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate the AWS Glue crawler\u0027s configuration in the \u003ccode\u003eTutorial\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account to target the S3 bucket containing the VPC flow logs in the \u003ccode\u003eDojo\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e-\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003eImplement a bucket policy on the S3 bucket in the \u003ccode\u003eDojo\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account that explicitly permits the AWS Glue crawler\u0027s IAM role in the \u003ccode\u003eTutorial\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account access to the bucket.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e-\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an IAM role in the \u003ccode\u003eTutorial\u003c/code\u003e’s account with permissions to access the S3 bucket in the \u003ccode\u003eDojo\u003c/code\u003e’s account and associate this role with the AWS Glue crawler.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM) in the \u003ccode\u003eDojo\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account to share the S3 bucket with the \u003ccode\u003eTutorial\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account, facilitating cross-account access\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect\u0026nbsp;because the AWS RAM service is primarily used for sharing AWS resources across accounts, but it does not directly facilitate access for AWS Glue crawlers to S3 buckets in another account.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCreate a VPC Endpoint for S3 in \u003ccode\u003eDojo\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account to streamline access between the AWS Glue crawler in the \u003ccode\u003eTutorial\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s account and the S3 bucket\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect\u0026nbsp;because VPC Endpoints simply facilitate private connections between VPCs and AWS services within the same account and do not directly address cross-account access for AWS Glue crawlers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEstablish a Direct Connect link between the \u003ccode\u003eTutorials\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003eDojo\u003c/code\u003e\u0027s accounts to ensure private connectivity for the AWS Glue crawler to access the S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because, in this scenario, Direct Connect is only used for establishing a dedicated network connection from your on-premises data center to AWS and does not directly facilitate cross-account access for AWS Glue crawlers to S3 buckets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-crawler.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-crawler.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/example-walkthroughs-managing-access-example2.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/example-walkthroughs-managing-access-example2.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:44":{"id":"e:2:44","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 44","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the telemetry data in Apache Parquet format, partition the dataset by meter type, and sort the records by reading timestamp\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the telemetry data in JSON format, partition the dataset by facility location, and sort the records by device identifier for all downstream Athena queries\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the telemetry data in Apache ORC format, partition the dataset by reading date, and sort the records by meter type\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the telemetry data in CSV format, partition the dataset by reading date, and sort the records by service condition\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn energy company collects hourly telemetry from thousands of smart meters into an Amazon S3 data lake. Each record includes fields such as meter type, device identifier, service condition, reading timestamp, energy zone, and facility location. The dataset grows by several million records each day. Analysts run frequent Amazon Athena queries to compare performance trends for specific meter types across time ranges.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the FASTEST query execution time?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the telemetry data in Apache Parquet format, partition the dataset by meter type, and sort the records by reading timestamp\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eApache Parquet is a columnar storage format that improves Athena query performance by allowing Athena to read only the columns required by a query. This reduces the volume of data scanned from Amazon S3 compared with row-based formats such as CSV or JSON.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePartitioning the dataset by meter type aligns with the query pattern because analysts frequently run queries for specific meter types. When the query includes the meter type partition column in the WHERE clause, Athena can prune irrelevant partitions and scan only the relevant subset of S3 objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSorting the Parquet data by reading timestamp further improves query efficiency for time-range analysis. Columnar formats such as Parquet store statistics that can help query engines skip unnecessary data blocks, so sorting by a commonly filtered range column can reduce scan work and improve execution time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4ec01f8798952144ec6e16642f20e6df70601b413aaceb977b3bb71de999d026.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4ec01f8798952144ec6e16642f20e6df70601b413aaceb977b3bb71de999d026.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the telemetry data in CSV format, partition the dataset by reading date, and sort the records by service condition\u003c/strong\u003e - CSV is a row-based text format and is less efficient for Athena analytical queries than Parquet or ORC. Athena often needs to scan more data from S3 when querying CSV because the format does not provide the same column pruning benefits.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePartitioning by reading date can help date-filtered queries, but the primary query pattern focuses on specific meter types. Sorting by service condition also does not align with the stated analytics pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the telemetry data in Apache ORC format, partition the dataset by reading date, and sort the records by meter type\u003c/strong\u003e - Apache ORC is a columnar format and can perform well with Athena. Sorting by meter type can also help when queries filter or group by meter type.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, partitioning by reading date is less aligned with the stated requirement than partitioning by meter type. Since analysts frequently query performance trends for specific meter types, partitioning on meter type allows Athena to eliminate more irrelevant data early in the query plan.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the telemetry data in JSON format, partition the dataset by facility location, and sort the records by device identifier for all downstream Athena queries\u003c/strong\u003e - JSON is a flexible text format, but it is not optimized for high-performance Athena analytics at large scale. It typically scans more data than columnar formats and can increase query latency and cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePartitioning by facility location does not match the query pattern that focuses on meter type. Sorting by device identifier also helps individual-device lookups more than meter-type performance analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-s3-throttling-optimizing-your-tables.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-s3-throttling-optimizing-your-tables.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-data-optimization-techniques.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-data-optimization-techniques.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-33":{"id":"p:3-33","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 33","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eS3 table location should be similar to the following - \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/table1.csv\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/table2.csv\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eS3 table location path should be similar to this: \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/myprefix//input//\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate individual S3 prefixes for each table like so - \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/table1/table1.csv\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/table2/table2.csv\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eS3 file names should be similar to the following - \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/athena/inputdata/_file1\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["LOCATION của bảng nên trỏ prefix chứa dữ liệu, không thiết kế mỗi table location là tên tệp đơn như ví dụ. Tách prefix riêng cho từng bảng.","Đường dẫn chứa dấu gạch chéo kép bất thường có thể không khớp prefix thực tế. LOCATION cần trỏ đúng thư mục dữ liệu S3.","Mỗi bảng có prefix riêng chứa tệp của bảng đó; LOCATION trỏ tới prefix tương ứng. Tránh trộn nhiều bảng hoặc trỏ nhầm file.","Tệp bắt đầu bằng dấu gạch dưới thường bị Athena bỏ qua khi đọc dữ liệu. Đổi cách đặt tên và LOCATION phù hợp."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is configuring Amazon Athena to create a table for each file stored under the same prefix in Amazon S3. After running CREATE TABLE statement in Athena with expected columns and their data types, the engineer has issued a SELECT query. However, the query has returned zero records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following is the right way to configure the Amazon S3 location path?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-45":{"id":"p:1-45","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 45","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS CloudTrail to stream event data to Amazon Kinesis. Use Amazon Kinesis stream-level metrics in the Amazon CloudWatch to trigger an AWS Lambda function that will trigger an error workflow\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun Amazon Athena SQL queries against AWS CloudTrail log files stored in Amazon S3 buckets. Use Amazon QuickSight to generate reports for managerial dashboards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Trusted Advisor publishes metrics about check results to Amazon CloudWatch. Create an alarm to track status changes for checks in the Service Limits category for the APIs. The alarm will then notify when the service quota is reached or exceeded\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon CloudWatch metric filter that processes AWS CloudTrail logs having API call details and looks at any errors by factoring in all the error codes that need to be tracked. Create an alarm based on this metric\u0027s rate to send an Amazon SNS notification to the required team\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Metric cấp luồng Kinesis không tự biểu thị mã lỗi API trong bản ghi. Phương án cũng không nêu đường đưa CloudTrail vào Kinesis hợp lệ và bộ lọc lỗi cần thiết.","Athena và QuickSight phù hợp phân tích, báo cáo lịch sử. Chúng không tự tạo cảnh báo gần thời gian thực như yêu cầu.","Trusted Advisor và cảnh báo quota không tương đương phát hiện cuộc gọi API bị từ chối hoặc mã lỗi bảo mật. Cần theo dõi nội dung CloudTrail.","Đưa CloudTrail vào CloudWatch Logs, dùng metric filter khớp các mã lỗi cần theo dõi rồi đặt alarm theo tần suất. SNS gửi cảnh báo khi hoạt động bất thường tăng."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eWhile consolidating logs for the weekly reporting, a development team at an e-commerce company noticed that an unusually large number of illegal AWS application programming interface (API) queries were made sometime during the week. Due to the off-season, there was no visible impact on the systems. However, this event led the management team to seek an automated solution that can trigger near-real-time warnings in case such an event recurs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents the best solution for the given scenario?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:6:15":{"id":"e:6:15","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 15","correct":[3],"answers":["Create a new workflow in Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) and set up the Salesforce solution to forward the data to MWAA via the Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) connector in Redshift. Use an AWS-managed key for encrypting connection data.","Set up an AWS Glue Workflow to orchestrate a Crawler and ETL Job between the Salesforce application and Redshift. Configure a rule using EventBridge and define the event pattern that will trigger the Glue Workflow as the target.","\u003cspan\u003eLaunch a user-defined function (UDF) in Redshift with a \u003ccode\u003eRun on demand\u003c/code\u003e flow trigger. Configure the UDF to synchronize the data exchange between the Salesforce solution and the Redshift data warehouse. \u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eUse an Amazon AppFlow flow with a \u003ccode\u003eRun on event\u003c/code\u003e trigger. Configure it to ingest data from the Salesforce solution to the Redshift data warehouse. Use customer-managed key for encrypting connection data.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA startup has a Salesforce-powered SaaS application with a data warehouse running in Amazon Redshift. As part of its workflow, the company needs to set up an automated solution to continuously send encrypted data from its SaaS solution to its Redshift data warehouse. This process is required to meet the company\u0027s data policy. Additionally, the data engineer must ensure that the company retains full control over its encrypted data. The team considered using Amazon EventBridge Scheduler to trigger a custom ingestion job but found that a direct connector would reduce operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following meets the requirements with the LEAST amount of operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon AppFlow is a fully managed integration service that enables you to securely exchange data between software as a service (SaaS) applications, such as Salesforce, and AWS services, such as Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) and Amazon Redshift. For example, you can ingest contact records from Salesforce to Amazon Redshift or pull support tickets from Zendesk to an Amazon S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b721dcd19152e1135746a96b610b7255b8d3e5efd5c511818b9ccae77046b735.png\" alt=\"Configure flow page in Amazon AppFlow setting Salesforce as the source and Amazon Redshift as the destination.\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAppFlow relieves you from investing significant time and highly skilled developer staff to build and maintain custom API connectors for AWS services to exchange data with SaaS applications. Systems Administrators and business analysts can quickly implement most of the integrations they need without waiting months for IT to finish integration projects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA trigger determines how a flow runs. The following are the supported flow trigger types:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun on demand\u003c/strong\u003e — Users manually run the flow as needed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun on event\u003c/strong\u003e — Amazon AppFlow runs the flow in response to an event from a SaaS application.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun on schedule\u003c/strong\u003e — Amazon AppFlow runs the flow on a recurring schedule.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon AppFlow provides both AWS-managed and customer-managed keys for encrypting connection data and data stored in Amazon S3 when it is a destination. We recommend that you use customer-managed keys, as it puts you in full control over your encrypted data. When you choose customer-managed keys, Amazon AppFlow attaches a resource policy to the KMS key that grants it access to the KMS key.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse an Amazon AppFlow flow with a \u003ccode\u003eRun on event\u003c/code\u003e trigger. Configure it to ingest data from the Salesforce solution to the Redshift data warehouse. Use customer-managed key for encrypting connection data.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch a user-defined function (UDF) in Redshift with a \u003ccode\u003eRun on demand\u003c/code\u003e flow trigger. Configure the UDF to synchronize the data exchange between the Salesforce solution and the Redshift data warehouse\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because UDFs simply have certain limitations in terms of accessing external systems or services that are outside of AWS, such as a custom SaaS solution. In addition, a UDF is not capable of continuously sending data from the SaaS application to the Redshift data warehouse. It\u0027s also important to note that the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eRun on demand\u003c/code\u003e flow trigger is commonly used in Amazon AppFlow and not in UDF.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a new workflow in Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) and set up the Salesforce solution to forward the data to MWAA via the Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) connector in Redshift. Use an AWS-managed key for encrypting connection data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Although using Amazon MWAA can satisfy the given requirement of continuously sending data from the SaaS application to Amazon Redshift, it still entails a lot of operational overhead to establish the end-to-end connection. Take note that the scenario explicitly asks for the least amount of overhead. Moreover, the use of AWS-managed keys does not fulfill the goal of providing the company with full control over its encrypted data. A customer-managed key should be used in this scenario instead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eSet up an AWS Glue Workflow to orchestrate a Crawler and ETL Job between the Salesforce application and Redshift. Configure a rule using EventBridge and define the event pattern that will trigger the Glue Workflow as the target\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because AWS Glue Workflows is primarily used to create and visualize complex ETL activities. It is not for integrating an external SaaS solution with an AWS Service. Using Eventbridge requires an intermediary service to write the output data to Amazon Redshift. This solution would not continuously send data from the SaaS application to Amazon Redshift with the LEAST operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/what-is-appflow.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/what-is-appflow.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/appflow/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/appflow/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon AppFlow Cheat Sheet\u003c/strong\u003e:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-appflow/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-appflow/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:3":{"id":"e:4:3","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 3","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDevelop an AWS Glue extract, transform, and load (ETL) job. Import the Python Pandas library and leverage the DataFrame.dropDuplicates method for data deduplication\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop a custom extract, transform, and load (ETL) job in Python using AWS Lambda. Set up the Python dedupe library as a Lambda layer and then leverage the dedupe library to perform data deduplication\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop an AWS Glue extract, transform, and load (ETL) job. Convert the Glue DynamicFrame into an Apache Spark DataFrame and leverage the DataFrame.dropDuplicates method for data deduplication\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop an AWS Glue extract, transform, and load (ETL) job and leverage the FindMatches machine learning (ML) transform for data deduplication\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is transitioning a legacy application to a data lake on Amazon S3. During the course of the review, a data engineer discovered duplicates in the legacy data. What is the most efficient way for the data engineer to eliminate these duplicates from the legacy application data with minimal operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDevelop an AWS Glue extract, transform, and load (ETL) job and leverage the FindMatches machine learning (ML) transform for data deduplication\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use AWS Glue to orchestrate your ETL (extract, transform, and load) jobs to build data warehouses and data lakes and generate output streams. AWS Glue calls API operations to transform your data, create runtime logs, store your job logic, and create notifications to help you monitor your job runs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e85836b758f1ff06c7388515c9be6adb3e87cbb13e750188ee2d3c02fe1fdb19.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e85836b758f1ff06c7388515c9be6adb3e87cbb13e750188ee2d3c02fe1fdb19.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can create machine learning transforms to cleanse your data. You can call these transforms from your ETL script. Your data passes from transform to transform in a data structure called a DynamicFrame, which is an extension to an Apache Spark SQL DataFrame. The DynamicFrame contains your data, and you reference its schema to process your data. The FindMatches transform enables you to identify duplicate or matching records in your dataset, even when the records do not have a common unique identifier and no fields match exactly. This will not require writing any code or knowing how machine learning works. You teach this machine learning transform by labeling example datasets to indicate which rows match. The machine learning transform learns which rows should be matched the more you teach it with example labeled data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option is the best fit for the given use case as it involves minimal operational effort to eliminate duplicates since it leverages a built-in ML transform that is tailored for deduplication.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDevelop a custom extract, transform, and load (ETL) job in Python using AWS Lambda. Set up the Python dedupe library as a Lambda layer and then leverage the dedupe library to perform data deduplication\u003c/strong\u003e - This option involves additional overhead of configuring the requisite libraries using an AWS Lambda function layers, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDevelop an AWS Glue extract, transform, and load (ETL) job. Import the Python Pandas library and leverage the DataFrame.dropDuplicates method for data deduplication\u003c/strong\u003e - This option involves additional overhead of importing the requisite libraries and developing custom code for data deduplication, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDevelop an AWS Glue extract, transform, and load (ETL) job. Convert the Glue DynamicFrame into an Apache Spark DataFrame and leverage the DataFrame.dropDuplicates method for data deduplication\u003c/strong\u003e - This option involves additional overhead of converting the data into an Apache Spark DataFrame and developing custom code for data deduplication, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-intro-tutorial.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-intro-tutorial.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/machine-learning.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/machine-learning.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:28":{"id":"e:1:28","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 28","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAccess the data from SageMaker Notebook by reading it using the boto3 client. Initialize a DynamoDB Client and do a \u003ccode\u003eScan\u003c/code\u003e which will return all the data you need\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDirectly export your DynamoDB table into a .csv file and upload this file to Amazon S3. Access the S3 data from Sagemaker\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Data Pipeline console to export the DynamoDB table to Amazon S3. Exported JSON files are converted to comma-separated value (CSV) format to use as a data source for Amazon SageMaker\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue to transfer your table from DynamoDB to Amazon S3. Access the S3 data from Sagemaker\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company stores its data in Amazon DynamoDb. The company needs to access this data in Amazon DynamoDb from an Amazon Sagemaker notebook for running machine learning models.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents a solution to address this requirement with the LEAST operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAccess the data from SageMaker Notebook by reading it using the boto3 client. Initialize a DynamoDB Client and do a \u003ccode\u003eScan\u003c/code\u003e which will return all the data you need\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can access the data in your SageMaker Notebook by reading it using the boto3 client. Initialize a DynamoDB Client and do a \u003ccode\u003eScan\u003c/code\u003e which will return all the data that you require.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eNote that this will be in JSON format and you would need to convert that to a format that is useful for the rest of your notebook, such as converting to a pandas dataframe.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe Scan operation returns one or more items and item attributes by accessing every item in a table or a secondary index. To have DynamoDB return fewer items, you can provide a FilterExpression operation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the AWS Data Pipeline console to export the DynamoDB table to Amazon S3. Exported JSON files are converted to comma-separated value (CSV) format to use as a data source for Amazon SageMaker\u003c/strong\u003e - Console-based access to the AWS Data Pipeline service has been deprecated since 2023. Continued access to AWS Data Pipeline is through the command line interface and API. Hence, this option is irrelevant for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue to transfer your table from DynamoDB to Amazon S3. Access the S3 data from Sagemaker\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue will need a Glue Crawler to populate the AWS Glue Data Catalog with tables. Also, using Amazon S3 is unnecessary for the solution, since Sagemaker can directly read from DynamoDB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDirectly export your DynamoDB table into a .csv file and upload this file to Amazon S3. Access the S3 data from Sagemaker\u003c/strong\u003e - Transferring data to S3 is an unnecessary step that increases execution time as well as cost. Reading directly from DynamoDB into SageMaker is the straightforward, cost-effective way of implementing the solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/questions/QUowJvrZpiRjOS5v0OhDkEXQ/accessing-dynamodb-data-from-sagemaker\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/questions/QUowJvrZpiRjOS5v0OhDkEXQ/accessing-dynamodb-data-from-sagemaker\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-15":{"id":"p:2-15","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 15","correct":[0,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) to set up a task that runs periodically to migrate data older than 3 months from your RDS instance to an S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eWrite an AWS Lambda function that identifies and exports old data from RDS to S3. Trigger the Lambda function through Amazon CloudWatch RDS Events\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS Step Functions to create an automation workflow. The workflow will call DataSync to sync the database files to the Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFor cost optimization, configure S3 lifecycle rules to transition archived data to Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFor cost optimization, configure S3 lifecycle rules to transition archived data to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Theo đáp án bộ đề, DMS xuất các dòng lịch sử cần thiết sang S3. Cần thiết lập bộ lọc và lịch chạy phù hợp; DMS không tự hiểu quy tắc tuổi ba tháng nếu chưa cấu hình.","RDS Events phản ánh sự kiện vận hành database, không phát sự kiện khi một dòng vừa quá ba tháng. Trigger được nêu không đáp ứng quy tắc tuổi dữ liệu.","DataSync đồng bộ tệp, không trực tiếp đọc và lọc các dòng SQL Server trong RDS theo tuổi như yêu cầu.","Intelligent-Tiering dành cho mẫu truy cập khó dự đoán. Dữ liệu đã xác định lưu trữ lịch sử lâu dài có thể dùng Deep Archive tiết kiệm hơn theo đề.","S3 Lifecycle chuyển dữ liệu lịch sử đã xuất sang Deep Archive để giảm chi phí lưu lâu dài. Phù hợp khi chấp nhận thời gian khôi phục của lớp này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA large SQL Server database on RDS contains historical transaction data. A process is needed to automate the following requirements: regularly identify data older than 3 months, and export the old data to an S3 bucket for long-term, cost-effective storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options can be used to automate this export from SQL Server to S3 and perform lifecycle management in Amazon S3 to build a solution with the least operational overhead? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:46":{"id":"e:5:46","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 46","correct":[3],"answers":["Use CloudWatch Logs insights to review the log group associated with the Glue job. Query the logs for \"DPU\u0027 to optimize the appropriate number of DPUs.","Visualize the Glue job to determine its appropriate number of DPUs in the Glue console\u0027s Extract, Transform, and Load (ETL) section.","Use a job bookmark to track the DPU consumed by the Glue job.","\u003cspan\u003eExamine the past job runs in the \u003ccode\u003eJob run monitoring\u003c/code\u003e section of the Glue console. Visualize the Glue job’s profiled metrics to determine the appropriate DPU capacity.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses AWS Glue to create a data pipeline for processing medical records in JSON format. The Glue job was initially configured with 20 Data Processing Units (DPUs), yet the execution time is still quite slow. The data engineering team already uses Amazon CloudWatch Application Insights to monitor the overall health of its applications, but it needs a more precise way to analyze the Glue job’s resource utilization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow can a data engineer determine the optimal DPU capacity for the Glue job?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eWith AWS Glue, you only pay for the time your ETL job takes to run. You are charged an hourly rate based on the number of Data Processing Units (DPUs) to run your ETL job. A Data Processing Unit can also be referred to as a worker, which is the processing power of your ETL job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b93f7447904e33198e45bce0d197ec054b88fe6d50ca768394bd3ddf933bf310.png\" alt=\"AWS Glue console: Job execution metric chart showing active and allocated Spark executors\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe graph illustrates the maximum number of allocated executors, represented by a horizontal red line, which correlates with the number of Data Processing Units (DPUs) designated for the job; in this scenario, 10 DPUs are allocated. The AWS Glue console presents job metrics, including executor allocation based on DPUs for each job run. For in-depth analysis, select a job run and opt for ‘View run metrics’ to examine detailed performance data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use the Job Run Monitoring section in the AWS Glue console to determine the appropriate DPU capacity needed. The job monitoring section of the AWS Glue console uses the results of previous job runs to specify the proper DPU capacity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eExamine the past job runs in the \u003ccode\u003eJob run monitoring\u003c/code\u003e section of the Glue console. Visualize the Glue job’s profiled metrics to determine the appropriate DPU capacity.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse CloudWatch Logs insights to review the log group associated with the Glue job. Query the logs for “DPU’ to optimize the appropriate number of DPUs\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. You can use CloudWatch to monitor the AWS Glue performance. However, CloudWatch logs might not indicate the optimal number of DPUs needed, as they will inspect the aggregate job details and not help determine the appropriate DPU capacity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eVisualize the Glue job to determine its appropriate number of DPUs in the Glue console’s Extract, Transform, and Load (ETL) section\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While inspecting it can be helpful for a high-level overview of job configurations and operations, it does not simply offer in-depth analysis or visualization that can help to optimize DPU usage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse a job bookmark to track the DPU consumed by the Glue job \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because job bookmarks in AWS Glue do not serve the purpose of tracking DPU consumption. Instead, job bookmarks are used to enable Glue jobs to process only new or updated data in subsequent runs by maintaining state information about the data previously processed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-debug-capacity.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-debug-capacity.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-continuous-logging.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-continuous-logging.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:36":{"id":"e:5:36","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 36","correct":[1],"answers":["Load the data into Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the data.","Query the database using the Amazon Redshift Data API.","Configure an Amazon Redshift JDBC driver to connect to the database and use an open-source library for querying.","Load the data into Amazon S3 and use Amazon Redshift Spectrum for querying."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn e-commerce company stores terabytes of data in Amazon Redshift. The company must run SQL queries from web applications without managing database connections. The solution should be scalable, secure, and not require a persistent connection to the database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution would meet the requirements with the LEAST amount of effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshift Data API\u003c/strong\u003e is an Amazon Redshift feature that simplifies access to your Amazon Redshift data warehouse by removing the need to manage database drivers, connections, network configurations, data buffering, credentials, and more. You can run SQL statements using the AWS Software Development Kit (AWS SDK), which supports different languages such as C++, Go, Java, JavaScript, .Net, Node.js, PHP, Python, and Ruby.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWith the Data API, you can programmatically access data in your Amazon Redshift cluster from different AWS services such as AWS Lambda, Amazon SageMaker notebooks, and also your on-premises applications using the AWS SDK. This allows you to build cloud-native, containerized, serverless, web-based, and event-driven applications on the AWS Cloud.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3d8fe0b6a740a26139e225944ff6ab15261c10cfff749b7082dea433a85051dc.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe Data API also enables you to run analytical queries on Amazon Redshift’s native tables, external tables in your data lake via Amazon Redshift Spectrum, and also across Amazon Redshift clusters, which is known as data sharing. You can also perform federated queries with external data sources like Amazon Aurora.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the given scenario, the company needs to run SQL queries from applications without managing database connections, which is exactly what the Data API offers. The Data API handles the management of connections and scalability for you, making it an ideal choice for web applications that need to interact with databases.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eQuery the database using the Amazon Redshift Data API.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eConfigure an Amazon Redshift JDBC driver to connect to the database and use an open-source library for querying\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. This approach primarily requires managing database connections directly within the application, which contradicts the requirement to avoid managing connections.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLoad the data into Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it requires loading the data from Amazon Redshift into Amazon S3, which is an additional step. Additionally, Athena is primarily designed to query data stored in S3, not to directly query Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eLoad the data into Amazon S3 and use Amazon Redshift Spectrum for querying\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. This option is typically more suited for scenarios where data resides in S3 and occasional complex queries are needed rather than for applications requiring frequent, direct interactions with a Redshift database. It also does not eliminate the need to manage Redshift connections.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/data-api.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/data-api.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/get-started-with-the-amazon-redshift-data-api/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/get-started-with-the-amazon-redshift-data-api/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:18":{"id":"e:1:18","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 18","correct":[2,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eRemove partitions and perform \u003ccode\u003edata bucketing\u003c/code\u003e on the S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eTransform .csv files to JSON format by fetching the required key-value pairs only\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe S3 bucket should be configured in the same AWS Region where the Athena queries are being run\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe S3 bucket should be configured in the same Availability Zone where the Athena queries are being run\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eTransform .csv files to Parquet format by fetching only the data fields required for predicates\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA university has tie-ups with local hospitals to share anonymized health statistics of people. The data is stored in Amazon S3 as .csv files. Amazon Athena is used to run extensive analytics on the data for finding correlations between different parameters in the data. The university is facing high costs and performance-related issues as the volume of data is growing rapidly. The data in the S3 bucket is already partitioned by date and the university does not want to change this partition scheme.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs a data engineer, how can you further improve query performance? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe S3 bucket should be configured in the same AWS Region where the Athena queries are being run\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAthena supports the ability to query Amazon S3 data in an AWS Region that is different from the Region in which you are using Athena. Querying across Regions can be an option when moving the data is not practical or permissible, or if you want to query data across multiple regions. Even if Athena is not available in a particular Region, data from that Region can be queried from another Region in which Athena is available.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, cross-Region access to Athena has several limitations. Most importantly, running a cross-region query can result in more data transferred than the size of the dataset. In addition, there are cross-Region data transfer charges also involved. Therefore, for further optimizations, the S3 bucket should be configured in the same AWS Region where the Athena queries are being run.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d77d2722e4689e69d9dd32fc8a4482f3cf5dbd29587633b1272bda2cd36bb3db.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d77d2722e4689e69d9dd32fc8a4482f3cf5dbd29587633b1272bda2cd36bb3db.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/querying-across-regions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/querying-across-regions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTransform .csv files to Parquet format by fetching only the data fields required for predicates\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eApache Parquet and Apache ORC are popular file formats for analytics workloads. They are often described as columnar file formats because they store data not by row, but by column. They also have features that allow query engines to reduce the amount of data that needs to be loaded in different ways. For example, by storing and compressing columns separately, you can achieve higher compression ratios and only the columns referenced in a query need to be read.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/fb530a9fe6a3a02985692b156b5021946c6884158eb7b69a0c6714a2d0c23dea.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/fb530a9fe6a3a02985692b156b5021946c6884158eb7b69a0c6714a2d0c23dea.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTransform .csv files to JSON format by fetching the required key-value pairs only\u003c/strong\u003e - As mentioned above, Apache Parquet and ORC are columnar storage formats that are optimized for fast retrieval of data and used in AWS analytical applications. JSON format does not offer any such advantages, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe S3 bucket should be configured in the same Availability Zone where the Athena queries are being run\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Athena as well as Amazon S3 manage data at the AWS Region level, rather than the Availability Zone (AZ) level. Configuring an S3 bucket at the AZ level is not possible. This option has been added as a distractor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRemove partitions and perform \u003ccode\u003edata bucketing\u003c/code\u003e on the S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e - Bucketing is a way to organize the records of a dataset into categories called buckets. This meaning of bucket and bucketing is different from, and should not be confused with Amazon S3 buckets. In data bucketing, records that have the same value for a property go into the same bucket. Records are distributed as evenly as possible among buckets so that each bucket has roughly the same amount of data. In practice, the buckets are files, and a hash function determines the bucket that a record goes into. A bucketed dataset will have one or more files per bucket per partition. The bucket that a file belongs to is encoded in the file name. Bucketing is useful when a dataset is bucketed by a certain property and you want to retrieve records in which that property has a certain value. Because the data is bucketed, Athena can use the value to determine which files to look at. For example, suppose a dataset is bucketed by customer_id and you want to find all records for a specific customer. Athena determines the bucket that contains those records and only reads the files in that bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eGood candidates for bucketing occur when you have columns that have high cardinality (that is, have many distinct values), are uniformly distributed, and that you frequently query for specific values.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSince the use case clearly states that you should improve query performance within the constraints of maintaining the partition scheme, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/querying-across-regions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/querying-across-regions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/ctas-partitioning-and-bucketing.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/ctas-partitioning-and-bucketing.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:61":{"id":"e:2:61","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 61","correct":[2,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEstablish a process to get managerial approval for deleting Amazon S3 objects\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an event trigger on deleting any Amazon S3 object. The event invokes an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) notification via email to the IT manager\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable multi-factor authentication (MFA) delete on the Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the configuration on the Amazon S3 console so that the user needs to provide additional confirmation while deleting any Amazon S3 object\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable versioning on the Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare startup needs to enforce compliance and regulatory guidelines for objects stored in Amazon S3. One of the key requirements is to provide adequate protection against accidental deletion of objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat are your recommendations to address these guidelines? (Select two) ?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable versioning on the Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eVersioning is a means of keeping multiple variants of an object in the same bucket. You can use versioning to preserve, retrieve, and restore every version of every object stored in your Amazon S3 bucket.\nVersioning-enabled buckets enable you to recover objects from accidental deletion or overwrite.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor example:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIf you overwrite an object, it results in a new object version in the bucket. You can always restore the previous version.\nIf you delete an object, instead of removing it permanently, Amazon S3 inserts a delete marker, which becomes the current object version. You can always restore the previous version. Hence, this is the correct option.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eVersioning Overview:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b55f2798b482bff78730025acd0e36ca814819d23f0b1ac93b8e22d4d98a6c32.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b55f2798b482bff78730025acd0e36ca814819d23f0b1ac93b8e22d4d98a6c32.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/Versioning.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/Versioning.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable multi-factor authentication (MFA) delete on the Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo provide additional protection, multi-factor authentication (MFA) delete can be enabled. MFA delete requires secondary authentication to take place before objects can be permanently deleted from an Amazon S3 bucket. Hence, this is the correct option.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an event trigger on deleting any Amazon S3 object. The event invokes an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) notification via email to the IT manager\u003c/strong\u003e - Sending an event trigger after object deletion does not meet the objective of preventing object deletion by mistake because the object has already been deleted. So, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEstablish a process to get managerial approval for deleting Amazon S3 objects\u003c/strong\u003e - This option for getting managerial approval is just a distractor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange the configuration on the Amazon S3 console so that the user needs to provide additional confirmation while deleting any Amazon S3 object\u003c/strong\u003e - There is no provision to set up Amazon S3 configuration to ask for additional confirmation before deleting an object. This option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/Versioning.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/Versioning.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/UsingMFADelete.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/UsingMFADelete.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:7":{"id":"e:4:7","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 7","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003ePush score updates to an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic, subscribe an AWS Lambda function to this Amazon SNS topic to process the updates and then store these processed updates in a SQL database running on Amazon EC2 instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePush score updates to an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) queue which uses a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances (with Auto Scaling) to process these updates in the Amazon SQS queue and then store these processed updates in an Amazon RDS MySQL database\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePush score updates to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams which uses an AWS Lambda function to process these updates and then store these processed updates in Amazon DynamoDB\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePush score updates to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams which uses a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances (with Auto Scaling) to process the updates in Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and then store these processed updates in Amazon DynamoDB\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA gaming company is developing a mobile game that streams score updates to a backend processor and then publishes results on a leaderboard. The company has hired you as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to design a solution that can handle major traffic spikes, process the mobile game updates in the order of receipt, and store the processed updates in a highly available database. The company wants to minimize the management overhead required to maintain the solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following will you recommend to meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePush score updates to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams which uses an AWS Lambda function to process these updates and then store these processed updates in Amazon DynamoDB\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo help ingest real-time data or streaming data at large scales, you can use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams (KDS). KDS can continuously capture gigabytes of data per second from hundreds of thousands of sources. The data collected is available in milliseconds, enabling real-time analytics. KDS provides ordering of records, as well as the ability to read and/or replay records in the same order to multiple Amazon Kinesis Applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lambda integrates natively with Kinesis Data Streams. The polling, checkpointing, and error-handling complexities are abstracted when you use this native integration. The processed data can then be configured to be saved in Amazon DynamoDB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePush score updates to an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) queue which uses a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances (with Auto Scaling) to process these updates in the Amazon SQS queue and then store these processed updates in an Amazon RDS MySQL database\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePush score updates to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams which uses a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances (with Auto Scaling) to process the updates in Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and then store these processed updates in Amazon DynamoDB\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePush score updates to an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic, subscribe an AWS Lambda function to this Amazon SNS topic to process the updates and then store these processed updates in a SQL database running on Amazon EC2 instance\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThese three options use Amazon EC2 instances as part of the solution architecture. The use case seeks to minimize the management overhead required to maintain the solution. However, Amazon EC2 instances involve several maintenance activities such as managing the guest operating system and software deployed to the guest operating system, including updates and security patches, etc. Hence these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/best-practices-for-consuming-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-using-aws-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/best-practices-for-consuming-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-using-aws-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:49":{"id":"e:3:49","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 49","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain, index every activity record as a document, configure dedicated master and data nodes, and use search APIs for all high-concurrency record reads and writes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon Redshift table with the EVEN distribution style and use a JDBC connection to perform all read and write operations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon Redshift table with the KEY distribution style and use the Amazon Redshift Data API to perform all read and write operations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon DynamoDB table with provisioned capacity to support the required read and write throughput, and use DynamoDB APIs for all record access\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA gaming company is designing an event log table for a player activity service. The service continuously ingests activity records and other internal applications must retrieve specific records through dependable API calls. The table must support more than 5,000 concurrent write operations and 7,000 read operations each second.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon DynamoDB table with provisioned capacity to support the required read and write throughput, and use DynamoDB APIs for all record access\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon DynamoDB is the best fit for this workload because it provides managed NoSQL storage with API-based access to specific items. Applications can use DynamoDB APIs such as GetItem, PutItem, UpdateItem, and Query to read or write individual records without opening database connections or scanning files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe workload requires thousands of concurrent writes and reads each second. DynamoDB provisioned capacity allows the data engineer to configure read capacity units and write capacity units to meet known throughput requirements. The table design should use a partition key that distributes activity records evenly so the workload does not create hot partitions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution meets the requirement because it supports continuous ingestion, high request rates, low-latency item access, and dependable API-based operations. It avoids using analytical systems such as Amazon Redshift, Amazon Athena, or Redshift Spectrum for a high-concurrency operational access pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e6d8c303ad6f6a4a506b66f961373412e1886c24b21ebea785e34046bec563c0.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e6d8c303ad6f6a4a506b66f961373412e1886c24b21ebea785e34046bec563c0.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/choose-the-right-throughput-strategy-for-amazon-dynamodb-applications/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/choose-the-right-throughput-strategy-for-amazon-dynamodb-applications/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon Redshift table with the KEY distribution style and use the Amazon Redshift Data API to perform all read and write operations\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift is a data warehouse that supports analytical workloads across large datasets. The KEY distribution style can help colocate rows for join performance when the distribution key aligns with query patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, Redshift is not the best choice for high-concurrency operational writes and API-based access to individual records. The Redshift Data API is useful for running SQL statements without managing persistent connections, but it does not turn Redshift into a low-latency operational key-value store.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain, index every activity record as a document, configure dedicated master and data nodes, and use search APIs for all high-concurrency record reads and writes\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon OpenSearch Service can index documents and provide low-latency search and analytics over log-style data. It is useful when applications need full-text search, filtering, aggregations, or observability dashboards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this requirement focuses on dependable API-based access to specific records and sustained high write and read operations. DynamoDB is a better fit for key-based operational access, while OpenSearch adds index management, shard sizing, and cluster tuning overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon Redshift table with the EVEN distribution style and use a JDBC connection to perform all read and write operations\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift with EVEN distribution can distribute rows evenly across slices when there is no clear distribution key. JDBC connections can support SQL access from applications and data tools.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, Redshift remains an analytical data warehouse, not an operational log table for very high concurrent reads and writes. Using JDBC for all record operations can create connection management and concurrency challenges for this application pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Introduction.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Introduction.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/choose-the-right-throughput-strategy-for-amazon-dynamodb-applications/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/choose-the-right-throughput-strategy-for-amazon-dynamodb-applications/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-17":{"id":"p:1-17","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 17","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCopy the data from all the sources into a single S3 bucket. Use Athena queries on the saved S3 data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena Federated Query to join the data from all data sources\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop an AWS Glue job using Apache Spark to join the data from all the sources\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eProvision an EMR cluster to join the data from all the sources. Configure Spark for Athena to run the data analysis job\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Sao chép tất cả dữ liệu sang S3 tạo chi phí lưu trữ và chuyển dữ liệu bổ sung. Nếu chỉ cần truy vấn liên nguồn thì bước sao chép này không cần thiết.","Athena Federated Query dùng connector để truy vấn nhiều nguồn và JOIN kết quả. Không cần tập trung toàn bộ dữ liệu vào một kho mới, phù hợp với yêu cầu tiết kiệm của đề.","Glue Spark có thể JOIN các nguồn nhưng cần thêm một tác vụ xử lý dữ liệu. Với bài toán truy vấn Athena này, Federated Query trực tiếp hơn.","Tự tạo và quản lý EMR để JOIN dữ liệu tốn công và chi phí hơn. Đề không yêu cầu một cụm Spark riêng."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data analytics job requires data from multiple sources like Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon RDS, and Amazon Redshift. The job is run on Amazon Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following is the MOST cost-effective way to join data from these sources?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-3":{"id":"p:3-3","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 3","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eRun ALTER TABLE SET TBLPROPERTIES to update the table properties so Athena ignores the empty status=pending S3 prefix\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun MSCK REPAIR TABLE to remove the stale status=pending partition from the AWS Glue Data Catalog metadata\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun ALTER TABLE ADD PARTITION for the status=completed prefix and leverage Athena to automatically remove the empty status=pending partition\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun ALTER TABLE DROP PARTITION to remove the stale status=pending partition from the AWS Glue Data Catalog metadata\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["TBLPROPERTIES sửa thuộc tính bảng, không xóa mục partition cụ thể đã lỗi thời trong Catalog.","MSCK REPAIR TABLE bổ sung partition được phát hiện, không tự xóa các partition cũ không còn dữ liệu.","Thêm partition completed không tự loại bỏ pending. Metadata partition cũ vẫn cần được xóa riêng.","ALTER TABLE DROP PARTITION xóa mục partition pending khỏi metadata. Đối tượng đã được chuyển trên S3; thao tác này sửa Catalog để phản ánh trạng thái mới."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company stores delivery status data in an Amazon S3 bucket and uses AWS Glue Data Catalog tables so Amazon Athena can query the data. The data is partitioned by status code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer manually moves all objects from the partition path status=pending to the partition path status=completed. The status=pending S3 prefix is now empty, but the status=pending partition still appears in the AWS Glue Data Catalog metadata. The data engineer must correct the metadata discrepancy in Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich Athena command should the data engineer run?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:9":{"id":"e:1:9","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 9","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon S3 Event Notification that sends a message to an Amazon SNS topic. Subscribe the Amazon EC2 instances to the Amazon SNS topic\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge event that reacts to object uploads in Amazon S3 and invokes one of the Amazon EC2 instances\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSubscribe the Amazon EC2 instances to Amazon S3 Analytics - storage class analysis\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon S3 Event Notification that sends a message to an Amazon SQS queue. Make the Amazon EC2 instances read from the Amazon SQS queue\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA photo-sharing company is storing user profile pictures in an Amazon S3 bucket and an image analysis application is deployed on four Amazon EC2 instances. A data engineer would like to trigger an image analysis procedure only on one of the four Amazon EC2 instances for each photo uploaded.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat do you recommend?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon S3 Event Notification that sends a message to an Amazon SQS queue. Make the Amazon EC2 instances read from the Amazon SQS queue\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe Amazon S3 event notification feature enables you to receive notifications when certain events happen in your bucket. To enable notifications, you must first add a notification configuration that identifies the events you want Amazon S3 to publish and the destinations where you want Amazon S3 to send the notifications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 supports the following destinations where it can publish events:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) queue\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lambda\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Simple Queue Service (SQS) is a fully managed message queuing service that enables you to decouple and scale microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications. SQS offers two types of message queues. Standard queues offer maximum throughput, best-effort ordering, and at-least-once delivery. SQS FIFO queues are designed to guarantee that messages are processed exactly once, in the exact order that they are sent.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHere we have to use Amazon S3 Event Notifications (which can send a message to either AWS Lambda, Amazon SNS, or Amazon SQS) to send a message to the Amazon SQS queue. By using Amazon SQS, we know only one Amazon EC2 instance among the four will pick up a message and process it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSubscribe the Amazon EC2 instances to Amazon S3 Analytics - storage class analysis\u003c/strong\u003e - By using the Amazon S3 analytics storage class analysis tool, you can analyze storage access patterns to help you decide when to transition the right data to the right storage class. Storage class analysis observes data access patterns to help you determine when to transition less frequently accessed STANDARD storage to the STANDARD_IA (IA, for infrequent access) storage class. This option acts as a distractor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge event that reacts to object uploads in Amazon S3 and invokes one of the Amazon EC2 instances\u003c/strong\u003e- Amazon EventBridge events cannot invoke applications on Amazon EC2 instances, so we have to rule out that answer.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon S3 Event Notification that sends a message to an Amazon SNS topic. Subscribe the Amazon EC2 instances to the Amazon SNS topic\u003c/strong\u003e- Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) is a highly available, durable, secure, fully managed pub/sub messaging service that enables you to decouple microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications. Using Amazon SNS would send a message to each Amazon EC2 instance via the Amazon SNS topic, therefore making all of them work for each upload. This is not the intended behavior.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/NotificationHowTo.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/dev/NotificationHowTo.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/sqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/sqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:10:6":{"id":"e:10:6","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 6","correct":[0,3],"answers":["Identify and optimize high CPU usage queries using Amazon RDS Performance Insights.","Schedule weekly manual reboots of the RDS DB instance.","Add more tables and indexes by altering the database structure.","Transition to a DB instance with greater CPU and memory resources.","Cache the database queries to reduce loading time."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn e-commerce company is experiencing a significant increase in sales during the holiday season. Due to this surge, database queries processing customer orders are taking longer than usual. This is causing delays in the checkout process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe company’s primary database is hosted on Amazon RDS for SQL server, and the workload is write-intensive. To provide customers with a better checkout experience, the company needs to address the high CPU utilization of their RDS instance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich possible solutions can help solve the issue effectively? (Select TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon RDS Performance Insights\u003c/strong\u003e is a useful feature that gathers and analyzes important performance metrics from an Amazon RDS database instance, such as database load and SQL queries executed. It presents these metrics in an easy-to-understand visual format over a period of time to help you analyze your database load and identify any potential bottlenecks. To use Performance Insights, you must enable it when creating an RDS instance. By default, it provides seven days of metric history.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b4030e9436233b722131c44b55a188b9f4970979be244fbd20a60e5212751a1a.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAn instance upgrade in RDS is the process of upgrading the underlying database engine or version of an existing RDS database instance. It is important to upgrade instances for the following reasons:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNewer versions provide bug fixes, security enhancements, performance improvements, and new features introduced by the database vendor. This helps keep the database secure and optimized over time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eUpgrading helps maintain support status as database vendors stop supporting older versions after a certain time period.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFuture-proofing applications by upgrading to a version compatible with planned features in newer RDS instances or the database engine.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/93ac5fa7263998c4449d32e3f353187c2b64d27ba4dc1a748de5066b8f0544d1.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe company should first optimize the problematic queries identified using Performance Insights. However, since this is a write-intensive workload during peak season, upgrading the RDS instance to a larger size would also be needed to scale up the database capacity and meet the demands of processing higher order volumes. Together, these solutions can help improve database performance and provide customers with a seamless checkout experience.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Identify and optimize high CPU usage queries using Amazon RDS Performance Insights.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e– Transition to a DB instance with greater CPU and memory resources.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdd more tables and indexes by altering the database structure\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While indexing can help improve read query performance, it’s not beneficial for write-intensive workloads. In fact, excessive indexing can lead to increased overhead during insert, update, and delete operations, potentially aggravating CPU and write latency issues.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSchedule weekly manual reboots of the RDS DB instance\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Regularly rebooting the database instance does not directly impact long-term CPU utilization trends. While it might temporarily alleviate some issues, it does not address the root cause of high CPU usage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCache the database queries to reduce loading time\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Caching is generally more beneficial for read-heavy workloads. Since the database in question is write-intensive, caching would have a negligible impact on reducing CPU utilization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PerfInsights.UsingDashboard.AnalyzeDBLoad.AdditionalMetrics.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PerfInsights.UsingDashboard.AnalyzeDBLoad.AdditionalMetrics.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Overview.DBInstance.Modifying.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Overview.DBInstance.Modifying.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/rds-aurora-postgresql-high-cpu\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/rds-aurora-postgresql-high-cpu\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon RDS Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-relational-database-service-amazon-rds/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-relational-database-service-amazon-rds/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:8":{"id":"e:6:8","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 8","correct":[2],"answers":["Use AWS CloudTrail\u0027s Data Events to monitor and log data access and API activity for the Glue ETL jobs.","Query the Glue Data Catalog using Amazon Athena for real-time monitoring of Glue job metrics and performance analysis.","Profile and monitor Glue operations using Glue Monitoring and the Glue Job profiler through Amazon CloudWatch.","Use AWS CloudFormation templates to review and manage the configuration of the Glue ETL job resources."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data analytics company is using AWS Glue for its ETL jobs to process and transform large-scale data sets. The team’s Glue ETL jobs are built using PySpark scripts to perform distributed data processing and transformations across large-scale datasets. The team is experiencing irregularities and inefficiencies in its job execution times and is seeking to optimize it. To solve this, the team wants to accurately and effectively identify the root causes of these Glue jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich method should the team use to gather the MOST detailed data and analyses for these operations?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue is a managed ETL service that facilitates the extraction, transformation, and loading of data for analysis. It automates and simplifies data integration processes, making it easier to prepare and consolidate data for analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f73bc9b7b375cf33ec66532d0b97bb3015d0d6e138729026c76bc2a966b052a3.png\" alt=\"AWS Glue job run monitoring dashboard\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue offers a Job run monitoring feature that gives a quick overview of job runs, including status, timestamps, and other key details. For deeper insights, the AWS Glue Job Profiler goes a step further, offering detailed metrics such as execution time, data processing rates, and memory usage. These metrics are crucial for pinpointing performance bottlenecks and fine-tuning resource utilization in ETL jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen these features are integrated with Amazon CloudWatch, they create dedicated log groups for each Glue job. These logs in CloudWatch provide a granular view of job performance. For example, by analyzing log data, teams can track how long specific data transformation tasks take or identify recurring errors in job runs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/11710a779a4faeeff902d5508af117fc4df306236ccd5dd747baa8fc97cac799.png\" alt=\"Amazon CloudWatch log group details and active log streams for AWS Glue jobs\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;Profile and monitor Glue operations using Glue Monitoring and the Glue Job profiler through Amazon CloudWatch.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS CloudTrail\u0027s Data Events to monitor and log data access and API activity for the Glue ETL jobs\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because AWS CloudTrail\u0027s Data Events are primarily used for recording data plane activities of some AWS resources, such as object uploads to Amazon S3, Lambda Function invocations, or item deletions in a DynamoDB table. While they can log some API activity for AWS Glue, they are not designed for in-depth performance monitoring or analysis of Glue jobs, especially in comparison to the detailed metrics available through the AWS Glue job profiler and CloudWatch.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eQuery the Glue Data Catalog using Amazon Athena for real-time monitoring of Glue job metrics and performance analysis\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it is inefficient for real-time performance monitoring. Querying the AWS Glue Data Catalog with Athena provides access to metadata but does not offer the same level of real-time, detailed job execution metrics and analysis capabilities as the AWS Glue job profiler and CloudWatch.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS CloudFormation templates to review and manage the configuration of the Glue ETL job resources\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because AWS CloudFormation is used for infrastructure deployment and resource management, not for analyzing AWS Glue job execution performance or identifying the root causes of ETL job inefficiencies.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-profile-glue-job-cloudwatch-metrics.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-profile-glue-job-cloudwatch-metrics.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/glue-athena.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/glue-athena.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/xray/latest/devguide/security-logging-monitoring.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/xray/latest/devguide/security-logging-monitoring.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:54":{"id":"e:3:54","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 54","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog views for shared domain views, manage access with Lake Formation, audit access with CloudTrail, and use security definer views to mask PII\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate Amazon Redshift materialized views for each domain, publish Redshift datashares to the other teams, and use Redshift permissions for all cross-domain analysis\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate separate S3 prefixes for each domain, use IAM policies to control access to each prefix, and require Athena and Redshift users to query only their assigned prefixes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate standard Athena views for each domain, use those Athena views from Amazon Redshift for cross-domain reporting, enable CloudTrail auditing, and use Lake Formation table permissions\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company is implementing a data mesh architecture on AWS. The market analytics, credit risk, and regulatory reporting domains each own their own datasets and must share only approved views with other domains. The company has more than 1,200 tables across 60 databases in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. All domains use Amazon Athena for ad hoc analysis and Amazon Redshift for complex reporting. The regulatory reporting team must audit all data access, and personally identifiable information must be hidden from teams that do not have approval to view it. The company needs a scalable solution that supports cross-domain analysis across both query engines.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog views for shared domain views, manage access with Lake Formation, audit access with CloudTrail, and use security definer views to mask PII\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Data Catalog views provide a scalable way to define shared views over data that is registered in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. The company can create approved views for each domain and expose only the columns and rows that other teams are allowed to query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eData Catalog views support a common view object that users can query from services such as Amazon Athena and Amazon Redshift. This is important because the company needs both ad hoc analysis in Athena and complex reporting in Redshift without creating separate, inconsistent view definitions for each engine.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eLake Formation can manage access to the views and the underlying governed data, while CloudTrail can support auditing of data access. Security definer view semantics help protect underlying tables and allow the company to mask or omit PII fields in shared views while still enabling cross-domain analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/680ed3a4bdd49e30da5efdc7d69614d9e0aa78b47c1d28bd894dad50660e8186.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/680ed3a4bdd49e30da5efdc7d69614d9e0aa78b47c1d28bd894dad50660e8186.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automate-creating-aws-glue-data-catalog-views-with-aws-sdk-for-data-mesh-use-case/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automate-creating-aws-glue-data-catalog-views-with-aws-sdk-for-data-mesh-use-case/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate standard Athena views for each domain, use those Athena views from Amazon Redshift for cross-domain reporting, enable CloudTrail auditing, and use Lake Formation table permissions\u003c/strong\u003e - Athena views can support on-demand analysis in Athena, and Lake Formation can help govern access to cataloged data. CloudTrail can also support auditing for governance and compliance workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, standard Athena views are not the best scalable shared view layer for both Athena and Amazon Redshift. The requirement needs a common cross-engine approach for many databases and tables, which AWS Glue Data Catalog views are designed to provide.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate separate S3 prefixes for each domain, use IAM policies to control access to each prefix, and require Athena and Redshift users to query only their assigned prefixes\u003c/strong\u003e - IAM policies and S3 prefixes can restrict access to data at the storage layer. This approach can work for simple data lake layouts where access boundaries align cleanly with object paths.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, prefix-level access is too coarse for sharing specific views, masking PII, and supporting fine-grained cross-domain analytics across many tables and databases. It also does not provide a consistent view layer for Athena and Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate Amazon Redshift materialized views for each domain, publish Redshift datashares to the other teams, and use Redshift permissions for all cross-domain analysis\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift materialized views and datashares can support reporting and controlled data sharing in Redshift-centered architectures. They can be useful when the primary analytics access pattern is inside Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option does not provide a shared governance layer for Athena and Redshift together. It also makes Redshift the center of the data mesh access model, while the requirement includes Athena users and AWS Glue Data Catalog resources across many domains.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-views.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-views.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/views-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/views-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/data-catalog-views-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/data-catalog-views-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-aws-glue-data-catalog-views-using-amazon-athena-and-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-aws-glue-data-catalog-views-using-amazon-athena-and-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automate-creating-aws-glue-data-catalog-views-with-aws-sdk-for-data-mesh-use-case/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/automate-creating-aws-glue-data-catalog-views-with-aws-sdk-for-data-mesh-use-case/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-37":{"id":"p:1-37","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 37","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse RDS MySQL as the database with ElastiCache\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse DynamoDB as the database with DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Aurora MySQL as the database with Aurora cluster cache\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse DocumentDB as the database with API Gateway\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["RDS với ElastiCache cần thiết kế dữ liệu quan hệ và tự tích hợp cache. Với dữ liệu IoT linh hoạt, phương án này thêm công phát triển.","DynamoDB phù hợp với dữ liệu key-value/document linh hoạt; DAX tăng tốc truy cập đọc bằng cache tương thích DynamoDB. Các dịch vụ được quản lý giảm vận hành.","Aurora là database quan hệ; cơ chế cache của cụm không thay thế lớp DAX đáp ứng trực tiếp thiết kế được đề yêu cầu.","DocumentDB là database tài liệu, còn API Gateway không phải bộ nhớ đệm database tương đương DAX. Cặp dịch vụ này không đáp ứng yêu cầu như phương án được chọn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn IT company wants to process IoT data from the field devices of an agricultural sciences company. The data engineering team at the company is designing a new database infrastructure for capturing this IoT data. The database should be resilient with minimal operational overhead and require the least development effort. The application includes a device tracking system that stores the GPS data for all devices. Real-time IoT data, as well as metadata lookups, must be performed with high throughput and microsecond latency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options would you recommend as the MOST efficient solution for these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:52":{"id":"e:3:52","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 52","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS KMS to generate encryption keys with automatic key rotation, encrypt the database credentials, and store the encrypted values in an Amazon DynamoDB table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the database credentials in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store as secure strings, configure an EventBridge scheduled rule to invoke a custom Lambda function every 45 days, and update the application configuration file after each successful rotation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Secrets Manager to store the database credentials, configure automatic rotation with a Lambda rotation function, and update the application to retrieve the current credentials through Secrets Manager API calls\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the database credentials in an encrypted S3 bucket, rotate the credentials every 45 days by using an S3 Lifecycle rule, and use bucket policies to control access\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA medical research organization stores clinical trial records in an on-premises MySQL database. The organization has built an application that connects to the database to retrieve and update patient study data. The security team requires database credentials to rotate every 45 days to reduce the risk of unauthorized access. The organization wants to avoid application code changes whenever the credentials rotate.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Secrets Manager to store the database credentials, configure automatic rotation with a Lambda rotation function, and update the application to retrieve the current credentials through Secrets Manager API calls\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Secrets Manager is designed to store and manage sensitive values such as database credentials. The application can call the Secrets Manager API to retrieve the current credential at runtime instead of storing a static user name and password in code or configuration files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSecrets Manager supports automatic rotation by invoking a Lambda rotation function on a schedule. For a database credential, the rotation function updates the secret value and updates the credential in the target database or service. This lets the organization maintain its 45-day rotation requirement without manually changing application code for each rotation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational overhead because Secrets Manager provides purpose-built secret storage, access control through IAM, API-based retrieval, and managed rotation orchestration. For an on-premises MySQL database, the Lambda rotation function must have network access to the database, but the application does not need code changes each time the password changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6d9c24228f2890a0a431f2820bed4915e69644a9c6e9d3ec3e0f1d56cd853414.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6d9c24228f2890a0a431f2820bed4915e69644a9c6e9d3ec3e0f1d56cd853414.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/how-to-securely-provide-database-credentials-to-lambda-functions-by-using-aws-secrets-manager/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/how-to-securely-provide-database-credentials-to-lambda-functions-by-using-aws-secrets-manager/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the database credentials in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store as secure strings, configure an EventBridge scheduled rule to invoke a custom Lambda function every 45 days, and update the application configuration file after each successful rotation\u003c/strong\u003e - Systems Manager Parameter Store can store secure string parameters and applications can retrieve parameter values. It can be useful for configuration values and some secrets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, automatic credential rotation is not built into Parameter Store in the same way as Secrets Manager. The scheduled Lambda function and application configuration update process add custom operational work and increase the risk of stale credentials.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS KMS to generate encryption keys with automatic key rotation, encrypt the database credentials, and store the encrypted values in an Amazon DynamoDB table\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS KMS can encrypt and protect cryptographic keys, and automatic KMS key rotation can rotate key material for eligible KMS keys. KMS is important for encryption, but it does not automatically rotate database user passwords.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option would still require custom logic to generate a new database password, update the MySQL database, update the stored credential, and coordinate application retrieval. That creates more operational overhead than Secrets Manager rotation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the database credentials in an encrypted S3 bucket, rotate the credentials every 45 days by using an S3 Lifecycle rule, and use bucket policies to control access\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon S3 can store encrypted objects, and bucket policies can control access to those objects. However, S3 Lifecycle rules manage object transitions and expiration, not database password rotation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis design does not update the on-premises MySQL database credential. It also forces the company to build custom rotation and retrieval behavior rather than using a managed secrets service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotating-secrets.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotating-secrets.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_lambda.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_lambda.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_lambda-functions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_lambda-functions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/hardcoded-db-creds.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/hardcoded-db-creds.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/how-to-securely-provide-database-credentials-to-lambda-functions-by-using-aws-secrets-manager/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/how-to-securely-provide-database-credentials-to-lambda-functions-by-using-aws-secrets-manager/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:64":{"id":"e:3:64","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 64","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEXPLAIN ANALYZE\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eOPTIMIZE\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEXPLAIN\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePREPARE\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses Amazon Athena for ad-hoc analysis of customer data in its data lake hosted on Amazon S3. A data engineer at the company wants to understand the detailed breakdown for each of the SQL query’s run plan and the computational cost of each operation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following Amazon Athena statements can be used to accomplish this task?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEXPLAIN ANALYZE\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe EXPLAIN ANALYZE statement shows both the distributed execution plan of a specified SQL statement and the computational cost of each operation in a SQL query. You can output the results in text or JSON format. Administrators can benefit from using EXPLAIN ANALYZE because it provides a scanned data count, which helps you reduce financial impact due to user queries and apply optimizations for better cost control.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c205e18b3ce1f5dd428940ffb3f7197f540e24f1e9467910671ce290ffd14efe.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c205e18b3ce1f5dd428940ffb3f7197f540e24f1e9467910671ce290ffd14efe.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEXPLAIN\u003c/strong\u003e - The EXPLAIN statement only provides a detailed breakdown of a query’s run plan. You can analyze the plan to identify and reduce query complexity and improve its runtime. You can also use EXPLAIN to validate SQL syntax prior to running the query. Doing so helps prevent errors that would have occurred while running the query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePREPARE\u003c/strong\u003e - Creates an SQL statement with the name statement_name to be run at a later time. The statement can include parameters represented by question marks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eOPTIMIZE\u003c/strong\u003e - Optimizes rows in an Apache Iceberg table by rewriting data files into a more optimized layout based on their size and the number of associated delete files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/optimize-federated-query-performance-using-explain-and-explain-analyze-in-amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/optimize-federated-query-performance-using-explain-and-explain-analyze-in-amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/athena-explain-statement.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/athena-explain-statement.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:15":{"id":"e:3:15","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 15","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the images using the Amazon S3 Standard-IA storage class\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a data monitoring application on an Amazon EC2 instance in the same region as the bucket storing the images. The application is invoked daily via Amazon CloudWatch and it changes the storage class of infrequently accessed objects to Amazon S3 Standard-IA and the frequently accessed objects are migrated to Amazon S3 Standard class\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the images using the Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a data monitoring application on an Amazon EC2 instance in the same region as the bucket storing the images. The application is invoked daily via Amazon CloudWatch and it changes the storage class of infrequently accessed objects to Amazon S3 One Zone-IA and the frequently accessed objects are migrated to Amazon S3 Standard class\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team at a social media company has noticed that while some of the images stored in Amazon S3 are frequently accessed, others sit idle for a considerable time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is your recommendation to build the MOST cost-effective solution?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the images using the Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class is designed to optimize costs by automatically moving data to the most cost-effective access tier, without performance impact or operational overhead. It works by storing objects in two access tiers: one tier that is optimized for frequent access and another lower-cost tier that is optimized for infrequent access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor a small monthly monitoring and automation fee per object, Amazon S3 monitors access patterns of the objects in S3 Intelligent-Tiering and moves the ones that have not been accessed for 30 consecutive days to the infrequent access tier. If an object in the infrequent access tier is accessed, it is automatically moved back to the frequent access tier. Therefore using the Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class is the correct solution for the given problem statement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Storage Classes Overview:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8e60dd73fc8b86832996d65cb20dc7a959db149930599272f5f638957925920d.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8e60dd73fc8b86832996d65cb20dc7a959db149930599272f5f638957925920d.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the images using the Amazon S3 Standard-IA storage class\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Standard-IA is for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. Amazon S3 Standard-IA offers high durability, high throughput, and low latency of Amazon S3 Standard, with a low per GB storage price and per GB retrieval fee. This combination of low cost and high performance makes Amazon S3 Standard-IA ideal for long-term storage, backups, and as a data store for disaster recovery files. The minimum storage duration charge is 30 days. As some of the objects are frequently accessed, the per GB retrieval fee for Amazon S3 Standard-IA can cause the costs to shoot up, hence this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a data monitoring application on an Amazon EC2 instance in the same region as the bucket storing the images. The application is invoked daily via Amazon CloudWatch and it changes the storage class of infrequently accessed objects to Amazon S3 One Zone-IA and the frequently accessed objects are migrated to Amazon S3 Standard class\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a data monitoring application on an Amazon EC2 instance in the same region as the bucket storing the images. The application is invoked daily via Amazon CloudWatch and it changes the storage class of infrequently accessed objects to Amazon S3 Standard-IA and the frequently accessed objects are migrated to Amazon S3 Standard class\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCreating a data monitoring application on an Amazon EC2 instance for managing the desired Amazon S3 storage class entails significant development cost as well as infrastructure maintenance effort. The Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class does the job cost-effectively. Therefore both these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:34":{"id":"e:4:34","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 34","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Step Functions to orchestrate a parallel workflow state that starts the SNS notification workflow and the log retrieval workflow at the same time\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lambda function that reads CloudWatch metrics every minute, increases the AWS Glue worker count during high disk usage, and decreases the worker count when disk usage drops\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the number of DPUs that each AWS Glue ETL job uses so the jobs have more compute, memory, and disk resources during peak periods\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable Auto Scaling for the AWS Glue ETL jobs so AWS Glue can add and remove workers dynamically as stream processing demand changes\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company uses Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to collect transaction events from several payment systems. An AWS Glue ETL pipeline analyzes the stream to detect suspicious outliers. When the pipeline detects an outlier, it publishes a notification to an Amazon SNS topic. The SNS topic starts a second workflow that retrieves related audit logs and stores the logs in an Amazon S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDuring peak transaction periods, the company notices delays before notifications reach the SNS topic. A data engineer reviews Amazon CloudWatch metrics and observes a high value for the glue.driver.BlockManager.disk.diskSpaceUsed_MB metric during the same periods. The company must resolve the issue with the least operational effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Auto Scaling for the AWS Glue ETL jobs so AWS Glue can add and remove workers dynamically as stream processing demand changes\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Auto Scaling allows AWS Glue to automatically add and remove workers during a job run based on the parallelism and resource needs of the workload. This is useful for streaming ETL jobs that experience changing traffic patterns during the day.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe high glue.driver.BlockManager.disk.diskSpaceUsed_MB metric indicates that Spark is using significant disk space for cached blocks, shuffle outputs, broadcasts, or spilling. During peak periods, this can cause slower processing and delay the outlier notifications that the job sends to Amazon SNS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational effort because the data engineer does not need to manually test and maintain a fixed DPU setting for every traffic pattern. AWS Glue can scale workers up when the streaming workload requires more resources and scale workers down when demand decreases.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/542749dcc152e4f40a422b8f144c34e0c7e06b31429e495ea511fae841ff8fd5.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/542749dcc152e4f40a422b8f144c34e0c7e06b31429e495ea511fae841ff8fd5.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-auto-scaling-automatically-resize-serverless-computing-resources-for-lower-cost-with-optimized-apache-spark/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-auto-scaling-automatically-resize-serverless-computing-resources-for-lower-cost-with-optimized-apache-spark/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the number of DPUs that each AWS Glue ETL job uses so the jobs have more compute, memory, and disk resources during peak periods\u003c/strong\u003e - Increasing DPUs can help because each additional worker adds compute, memory, and disk resources. More resources can reduce the pressure that causes heavy Spark disk usage during shuffle or spill-heavy processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this approach requires manual capacity tuning. The team must estimate peak demand, adjust worker counts, and potentially overprovision resources for periods when traffic is lower. Auto Scaling provides a lower-maintenance way to address changing workload demand.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Step Functions to orchestrate a parallel workflow state that starts the SNS notification workflow and the log retrieval workflow at the same time\u003c/strong\u003e - Step Functions can orchestrate workflows and can run branches in parallel. It is useful when a process needs explicit state management, branching, retries, or coordination across multiple AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the delay is caused by resource pressure in the AWS Glue Spark job, not by a missing orchestration pattern. Running workflow branches in parallel does not reduce Spark disk usage or improve the ETL job\u0027s ability to process high-volume stream data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Lambda function that reads CloudWatch metrics every minute, increases the AWS Glue worker count during high disk usage, and decreases the worker count when disk usage drops\u003c/strong\u003e - A Lambda function could inspect CloudWatch metrics and call AWS APIs to adjust job settings if the company builds the required automation. This design could react to high disk usage signals.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option creates custom scaling logic that the team must write, test, monitor, and maintain. AWS Glue Auto Scaling already provides managed worker scaling without requiring a custom metrics-driven controller.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/auto-scaling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/auto-scaling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-streaming-auto-scaling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-streaming-auto-scaling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitoring-awsglue-with-cloudwatch-metrics.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitoring-awsglue-with-cloudwatch-metrics.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-auto-scaling-automatically-resize-serverless-computing-resources-for-lower-cost-with-optimized-apache-spark/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-auto-scaling-automatically-resize-serverless-computing-resources-for-lower-cost-with-optimized-apache-spark/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:50":{"id":"e:5:50","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 50","correct":[2],"answers":["Implement AWS Lambda functions to transfer data from Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, DynamoDB, and CloudWatch to S3, and then analyze using Amazon Athena.","Configure Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query data in DynamoDB, Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, and S3, with a separate process for CloudWatch Logs analysis.","Utilize Amazon Athena Federated Query to join and analyze data across Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, DynamoDB, S3, and CloudWatch Logs.","Deploy Amazon EMR with Apache Hadoop to process data from DynamoDB, Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, and S3, and use Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights for log analysis."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team needs an efficient method to analyze historical data for a market research project. The data resides in various AWS services, including Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon S3, along with logs stored in Amazon CloudWatch. The team plans to execute a one-time query that requires joining this data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is the MOST cost-effective solution that the data engineering team can implement to meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Athena Federated Query\u003c/strong\u003e enables running SQL queries across data stored in multiple data sources without extracting or loading the data into Amazon S3. This feature allows querying data directly from its original location, whether it resides in databases, data warehouses, data lakes, or custom data sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/a74702e6384e082aea1ecf4285046b55a3753201cee1bc30afbfd48915aaa391.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eLeveraging Athena’s Federated Query feature to access and join data from multiple sources directly from Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, DynamoDB,\u0026nbsp;S3, and CloudWatch Logs minimizes the cost by avoiding unnecessary data movement. It simplifies the architecture by leveraging a single query interface for diverse data sources, meeting efficient and cost-effective analysis requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon Athena Federated Query to join and analyze data across Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, DynamoDB, S3, and CloudWatch Logs.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy Amazon EMR with Apache Hadoop to process data from DynamoDB, Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, and S3, and use Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights for log analysis\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This approach involves provisioning and managing an EMR cluster, which can be more expensive and has greater overhead compared to using Athena Federated Query, especially for a one-time job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement AWS Lambda functions to transfer data from Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, DynamoDB, and CloudWatch to S3, and then analyze using Amazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it introduces unnecessary complexity and potential costs by requiring all data to be moved to S3 before analysis, which Athena Federated Query can do without.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query data in DynamoDB, Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL, and S3, with a separate process for CloudWatch Logs analysis\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because Redshift Spectrum can only query data in S3 and does not support direct querying of CloudWatch Logs. Moreover, having a separate process for CloudWatch Logs analysis would complicate the overall solution and increase costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-a-data-source.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-a-data-source.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheatsheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:44":{"id":"e:7:44","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 44","correct":[2],"answers":["Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to run the SQL statements in parallel.","Use Amazon Redshift User-defined functions to encapsulate the SQL statements.","Use Amazon Redshift Stored Procedures to encapsulate the SQL statements.","Use Amazon Redshift ML to predict the results of the SQL statements."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team uses a custom-built application to generate sales reports for a company. The data is hosted in Amazon Redshift, and the application logic executes 30 SQL statements sequentially when generating the report. The team wants to simplify the operation and improve the network traffic between the application and the Redshift database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would be the MOST effective solution?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eIn Amazon Redshift, stored procedures serve as a mechanism to encapsulate logic for various operations, such as data transformation and validation, as well as business-specific logic. This encapsulation allows for a more streamlined and efficient process, particularly when dealing with multiple SQL statements. By bundling these statements into a single stored procedure, the number of round trips between the custom-built application and the database can be significantly reduced, thereby simplifying the operation and enhancing network traffic efficiency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eStored procedures are stored directly in the database, making them accessible to any user with the appropriate privileges. They offer a level of flexibility not found in user-defined functions (UDFs), as they can incorporate not only SELECT queries but also data definition language (DDL) and data manipulation language (DML). Unlike UDFs, stored procedures do not necessarily need to return a value.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/34891ff4086fcab102cbc094d0f0206cd845ce68e259b3da675752eba82f3ed2.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eStored procedures in Amazon Redshift are used to encapsulate logic for data transformation, data validation, and business-specific logic. By combining multiple SQL steps into a stored procedure, you can reduce round trips between your custom-built application and the database. This would simplify the operation and improve the network traffic between the custom-built application and Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Stored Procedures to encapsulate the SQL statements.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to run the SQL statements in parallel\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because Amazon Redshift Spectrum is only useful when querying data directly against Amazon S3. Moreover, the idea of running SQL statements in parallel is more about optimizing query performance across distributed data rather than simplifying operational processes or reducing the application-database interaction overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift User-defined functions to encapsulate the SQL statements\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. UDFs are typically used to encapsulate frequently used calculations or transformations within SQL queries, not for managing a sequence of data retrieval tasks. UDFs execute within the context of a single SQL query and cannot manage the flow control of multiple sequential operations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift ML to predict the results of the SQL statements\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because Amazon Redshift ML is simply a feature for training and applying machine learning models using familiar SQL commands. Predicting the outcome of a sequence of SQL statements isn\u0027t within its scope. Additionally,\u0026nbsp;when generating reports with SQL queries, the aim is to absolute values from the actual database data, not to predict outcomes as with machine learning models.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/stored-procedure-create.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/stored-procedure-create.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/stored-procedure-enhancements-in-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/stored-procedure-enhancements-in-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:13":{"id":"e:2:13","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 13","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an interface VPC endpoint for AWS Glue, enable private DNS for the endpoint, and attach the interface endpoint to the same subnet that the AWS Glue job uses\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eVerify that the route table associated with the AWS Glue job subnet includes a route to the Amazon S3 prefix list that targets the S3 gateway endpoint\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpdate the AWS Glue job code so the S3 connection details use a fully qualified domain name for the bucket endpoint\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpdate the AWS Glue security group to allow inbound traffic from the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial analytics company is configuring an AWS Glue job to read encrypted data files from an Amazon S3 bucket. The data engineer has configured an AWS Glue connection that uses a private subnet and has attached an IAM role with the required S3 permissions. When the data engineer runs the job, AWS Glue returns an error that indicates a problem with the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint for the selected subnet.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineer must resolve the connectivity issue so that the AWS Glue job can access the S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eVerify that the route table associated with the AWS Glue job subnet includes a route to the Amazon S3 prefix list that targets the S3 gateway endpoint\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAn Amazon S3 gateway endpoint works by adding a route to the route table that is associated with the subnet. The route uses the Amazon S3 prefix list as the destination and the S3 gateway endpoint as the target.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen an AWS Glue job uses a connection that places the job in a VPC subnet, the subnet must have a valid network path to Amazon S3. If the job cannot validate or use the S3 gateway endpoint, the data engineer should verify that the route table for the selected subnet is associated with the correct S3 gateway endpoint route.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution directly addresses the reported endpoint issue. IAM permissions and bucket policies control authorization, but the error indicates that AWS Glue cannot reach Amazon S3 through the VPC gateway endpoint path. Correcting the subnet route table fixes the network path that the job needs to access the S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate the AWS Glue security group to allow inbound traffic from the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint\u003c/strong\u003e - Security groups control traffic to and from elastic network interfaces. Amazon S3 gateway endpoints do not use endpoint network interfaces in the same way that interface endpoints do.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBecause an S3 gateway endpoint is a route table target, adding inbound security group rules from the endpoint does not resolve the routing problem. The data engineer must verify the subnet route table instead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an interface VPC endpoint for AWS Glue, enable private DNS for the endpoint, and attach the interface endpoint to the same subnet that the AWS Glue job uses\u003c/strong\u003e - An interface endpoint for AWS Glue provides private connectivity to AWS Glue APIs from a VPC. It does not provide private connectivity from an AWS Glue job to Amazon S3 objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe requirement involves an Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint. Creating an AWS Glue interface endpoint does not add the required S3 route to the subnet route table and does not fix the S3 data access path.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate the AWS Glue job code so the S3 connection details use a fully qualified domain name for the bucket endpoint\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue jobs can read S3 paths without requiring the job code to include a fully qualified S3 service domain name. The application code is not the main issue when the failure identifies the S3 VPC gateway endpoint.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option does not address the subnet routing configuration. The data engineer needs to ensure that the VPC subnet used by the AWS Glue connection has a route to Amazon S3 through the gateway endpoint.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/vpc-endpoints-s3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/vpc-endpoints-s3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/connection-S3-VPC.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/connection-S3-VPC.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/start-connecting.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/start-connecting.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/vpc-endpoints-s3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/vpc-endpoints-s3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/gateway-endpoints.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/privatelink/gateway-endpoints.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:4":{"id":"e:3:4","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 4","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUpdate the sort key to equal distribution key\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the distribution style to ALL for the cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe distribution key should be changed to the table column that has the largest dimension\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure the sort key to be the column most used in JOIN of tables\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster as its database service and the cluster uses key distribution. While running the maintenance tasks on the cluster, a data engineer noticed that of the four ra3 cluster nodes, one node had a CPU load of over 90% most of the time, while the other three nodes were averaging around 10%. The company has tasked the data engineer to balance the load more evenly across all four compute nodes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat changes are needed if provisioning additional nodes is not an option?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe distribution key should be changed to the table column that has the largest dimension\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn key distribution, the rows are distributed according to the values in one column. The leader node places matching values on the same node slice. If you distribute a pair of tables on the joining keys, the leader node collocates the rows on the slices according to the values in the joining columns. This way, matching values from the common columns are physically stored together.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eChoosing the right distribution key that matches the data characteristics and the patterns in which queries are made can lead to a more balanced distribution of data and workload. This approach helps avoid scenarios where one node is overloaded while others remain underutilized.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow to choose the correct key:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4b66d6439cccd521166f844e8a5a06b082dbabca92f2cffb991dc33fa92ec7b4.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4b66d6439cccd521166f844e8a5a06b082dbabca92f2cffb991dc33fa92ec7b4.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_Distribution_examples.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_Distribution_examples.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAn example showcasing how the key can cause distribution skew:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/01133cfeae8e83044a7e46a6cbe3623b1655aacaad8c0caed1ad892702fea9a0.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/01133cfeae8e83044a7e46a6cbe3623b1655aacaad8c0caed1ad892702fea9a0.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_Distribution_examples.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_Distribution_examples.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure the sort key to be the column most used in JOIN of tables\u003c/strong\u003e - When you create a table, you can alternatively define one or more of its columns as sort keys. When data is initially loaded into the empty table, the rows are stored on disk in sorted order. Information about sort key columns is passed to the query planner, and the planner uses this information to construct plans that exploit the way that the data is sorted. The general rule is to Set the DISTKEY to the column most commonly used in JOIN and Set the SORTKEY to the column most used in WHERE.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange the distribution style to ALL for the cluster\u003c/strong\u003e - In ALL distribution, a copy of the entire table is distributed to every node. Where EVEN distribution or KEY distribution places only a portion of a table\u0027s rows on each node, ALL distribution ensures that every row is collocated for every join that the table participates in. ALL distribution multiplies the storage required by the number of nodes in the cluster, so it takes much longer to load, update, or insert data into multiple tables. ALL distribution is appropriate only for relatively slow-moving tables; that is, tables that are not updated frequently or extensively. This would necessitate provisioning additional nodes, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate the sort key to equal distribution key\u003c/strong\u003e - Updating the sort key will not have an impact on the distribution skew seen in the use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_choosing_dist_sort.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_choosing_dist_sort.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/viewing-distribution-styles.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/viewing-distribution-styles.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_Distribution_examples.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_Distribution_examples.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Sorting_data.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Sorting_data.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:45":{"id":"e:1:45","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 45","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConstruct a view within the SQL Server databases on the EC2 instances that includes the essential data elements. Then, set up an AWS Glue job to fetch the data directly from this view and transfer it to an S3 bucket in Parquet format. Ensure this AWS Glue job is scheduled to execute daily\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop an AWS Lambda function that utilizes Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) to query databases hosted on EC2 instances. Set up this Lambda function to fetch the necessary data, convert it into Parquet format, and upload it to an S3 bucket. Set up a daily schedule via Amazon EventBridge to trigger the Lambda function\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up the SQL Server Agent to execute a daily SQL query on the SQL Server databases hosted on the EC2 instances, extracting the specified data elements. Configure the query to output the results as .csv files directly into an S3 bucket. Additionally, establish an S3 event trigger to activate an AWS Lambda function, which will convert these .csv files into the Parquet format\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a SQL query on the SQL Server database hosted on the EC2 instances to establish a view containing the necessary data elements. Then, configure an AWS Glue crawler to access and read this view. Set up an AWS Glue job to extract the data and convert it into Parquet format before transferring it to an S3 bucket. Configure this AWS Glue job to execute daily\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is transitioning its database servers from Amazon EC2 instances running Microsoft SQL Server to Amazon RDS for Microsoft SQL Server DB instances. During this migration, the data engineering team needs to export this data, which is derived from SQL joins across multiple tables, using a daily schedule. The migrated data should be stored in Apache Parquet format on Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is the most operationally efficient solution to meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a SQL query on the SQL Server database hosted on the EC2 instances to establish a view containing the necessary data elements. Then, configure an AWS Glue crawler to access and read this view. Set up an AWS Glue job to extract the data and convert it into Parquet format before transferring it to an S3 bucket. Configure this AWS Glue job to execute daily\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou need to set up a view containing the necessary data elements by using a SQL query on the SQL Server database, like so:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/bcd03572c1210b8197669b9ea41bbcdafeabde2dda51b21ce9882ff71dd51063.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/bcd03572c1210b8197669b9ea41bbcdafeabde2dda51b21ce9882ff71dd51063.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/extracting-multidimensional-data-from-microsoft-sql-server-analysis-services-using-aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/extracting-multidimensional-data-from-microsoft-sql-server-analysis-services-using-aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThen, you can configure an AWS Glue crawler to access and read this view. Run the crawler to hydrate the AWS Glue Data Catalog table, which is subsequently used in the AWS Glue job as the source table for extracting data from SQL Server.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5a4c8b8cdbeae31c681bcc415e77375b37b36b337b7673deb898de2c25f1352f.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5a4c8b8cdbeae31c681bcc415e77375b37b36b337b7673deb898de2c25f1352f.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/extracting-multidimensional-data-from-microsoft-sql-server-analysis-services-using-aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/extracting-multidimensional-data-from-microsoft-sql-server-analysis-services-using-aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFinally, you can create an AWS Glue job, that runs on a daily schedule, to extract the data from this view (accessible via the AWS Glue Catalog) and convert it into Parquet format while writing the output to an S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConstruct a view within the SQL Server databases on the EC2 instances that includes the essential data elements. Then, set up an AWS Glue job to fetch the data directly from this view and transfer it to an S3 bucket in Parquet format. Ensure this AWS Glue job is scheduled to execute daily\u003c/strong\u003e - This option does not use a Glue Crawler access and read the view data. Using a Glue crawler avoids the need to manage the view\u0027s metadata manually on each run. So, this option is operationally inefficient.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up the SQL Server Agent to execute a daily SQL query on the SQL Server databases hosted on the EC2 instances, extracting the specified data elements. Configure the query to output the results as .csv files directly into an S3 bucket. Additionally, establish an S3 event trigger to activate an AWS Lambda function, which will convert these .csv files into the Parquet format\u003c/strong\u003e - This option is operationally inefficient as it adopts a two-step approach, by initially creating CSV files and later re-writing to Parquet format via a Lambda function.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDevelop an AWS Lambda function that utilizes Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) to query databases hosted on EC2 instances. Set up this Lambda function to fetch the necessary data, convert it into Parquet format, and upload it to an S3 bucket. Set up a daily schedule via Amazon EventBridge to trigger the Lambda function\u003c/strong\u003e - This option requires significant coding effort to fetch the necessary data via the Lambda function, so it is operationally inefficient.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/extracting-multidimensional-data-from-microsoft-sql-server-analysis-services-using-aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/extracting-multidimensional-data-from-microsoft-sql-server-analysis-services-using-aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:34":{"id":"e:1:34","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 34","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAdd a created_at attribute in the table and then use a CloudWatch Events rule to invoke a Lambda function daily. The Lambda function deletes items older than a week on the basis of this attribute\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd a created_at attribute in the table and then use a cron job on EC2 instance to invoke a Python script daily. The script deletes items older than a week on the basis of this attribute\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd a new attribute in the table to track the expiration time and enable time to live (TTL) on the table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd a new attribute in the table to track the expiration time and set up a Glue job to delete items that are more than a week old\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA gaming company maintains a staging environment for its flagship application which uses a DynamoDB table to keep track of the gaming history of the players. This data needs to be kept for only a week and then it can be deleted. The IT manager has noticed that the table has several months of data in the table. The company wants to implement a cost-effective solution to keep only the latest week\u0027s data in the table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions requires the MINIMUM development effort and ongoing maintenance?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdd a new attribute in the table to track the expiration time and enable time to live (TTL) on the table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use DynamoDB Time to Live (TTL) to determine when an item is no longer needed. TTL uses a per-item timestamp to determine items for expiration. Shortly after the date and time of the specified timestamp, DynamoDB deletes the item from your table without consuming any write throughput. After you enable TTL on a table, a per-partition scanner background process automatically and continuously evaluates the expiry status of items in the table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c997f5d03e129121ad8d30207b54395d2dc00173aa86478962a413eace3f1cbf.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c997f5d03e129121ad8d30207b54395d2dc00173aa86478962a413eace3f1cbf.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TTL.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TTL.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/71a0a7ef58f0a5d9547431452d46737cdbd8202af2647932fecd6261ff02c40b.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/71a0a7ef58f0a5d9547431452d46737cdbd8202af2647932fecd6261ff02c40b.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/howitworks-ttl.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/howitworks-ttl.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdd a new attribute in the table to track the expiration time and set up a Glue job to delete items that are more than a week old\u003c/strong\u003e - Although this solution is feasible, it would require a significant development effort to build and maintain a Glue job to trim the DynamoDB table. There is an additional cost of running the Glue job on a daily basis as well.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdd a created_at attribute in the table and then use a cron job on the EC2 instance to invoke a Python script daily. The script deletes items older than a week on the basis of this attribute\u003c/strong\u003e - Provisioning an EC2 instance and developing Python script involves significant development effort, maintenance, and additional cost. So this option is not correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdd a created_at attribute in the table and then use a CloudWatch Events rule to invoke a Lambda function daily. The Lambda function deletes items older than a week on the basis of this attribute\u003c/strong\u003e - Custom code needs to be written for the Lambda function to implement the functionality to trim the DynamoDB table. The lambda function execution is also charged separately. So this option is not correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TTL.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TTL.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/howitworks-ttl.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/howitworks-ttl.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-24":{"id":"p:4-24","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 24","correct":[1,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eWhen loading multiple files into a single table, use a single S3DistCp command\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eWhen loading multiple files into a single table, use a single COPY command\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage temporary staging tables during the data loading process\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eWhen loading multiple files into a single table, use multiple COPY commands\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to upload data from multiple files in Amazon S3 into a single Amazon Redshift table\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["S3DistCp là công cụ sao chép dữ liệu thường dùng với EMR và HDFS. Nó không phải lệnh nạp dữ liệu hàng loạt vào Redshift.","Một lệnh COPY có thể đọc nhiều tệp và phân phối công việc song song cho các phần xử lý của Redshift. Đây là cách nạp nhiều tệp vào cùng bảng được ưu tiên trong đề.","Staging table giữ dữ liệu mới để kiểm tra, biến đổi hoặc hợp nhất trước khi cập nhật bảng đích. Nó giúp tổ chức luồng nạp hiệu quả và hạn chế tác động lên bảng đang phục vụ truy vấn.","Chạy nhiều COPY đồng thời vào cùng bảng có thể tạo cạnh tranh tài nguyên và làm giảm hiệu quả. Một COPY đọc nhiều tệp tận dụng cơ chế nạp song song sẵn có tốt hơn.","Spectrum truy vấn dữ liệu S3 thông qua external table. Nó không phải công cụ upload nhiều tệp vào bảng Redshift như lựa chọn mô tả; COPY phục vụ việc nạp đó."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn IT company is revamping its ETL process and wants to transfer data from Amazon S3 to an Amazon Redshift cluster. The company wants to load the data in bulk onto Amazon Redshift using the best possible high-performance solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate, which of the following steps would you recommend for this requirement? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:65":{"id":"e:5:65","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 65","correct":[3],"answers":["Utilize API calls for the retrieval and integration of third-party datasets from AWS DataSync.","Use Amazon Glue to crawl the datasets and store the metadata in Amazon Athena for querying.","Use Amazon Kinesis to stream the datasets in real-time into the data pipeline.","\u003cspan\u003eShare the third-party datasets through AWS Data Exchange and access them using the \u003ccode\u003eGetDataSet\u003c/code\u003e API.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is working on a project that references third-party datasets from various business partners. The datasets are hosted in Amazon S3 buckets. The company’s data engineering team must recommend a method for accessing and integrating these datasets into its existing data pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following methods meets the requirements for the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAWS Data Exchange is a service that helps AWS customers easily find, subscribe to, and use third-party data in the cloud.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/27ea70da27b787fd4768901fc4239720c57ee3d340dc3829c41903f7f4e5018b.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you have access to an AWS Data Exchange dataset, you can use compatible AWS or partner analytics and machine learning services to extract insights from it. This makes AWS Data Exchange a powerful tool for integrating diverse datasets into an existing data pipeline for comprehensive analysis and insights, which is exactly what the data engineering team in question needs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMoreover, AWS Data Exchange provides options for both subscribers and data providers. It eliminates the need for data providers to build and maintain any data delivery and entitlement infrastructure. Anyone with an AWS account can create and send data grants to data receivers. Therefore, utilizing API calls for the retrieval and integration of third-party datasets from AWS Data Exchange is the most direct and efficient method for the team’s needs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eShare the third-party datasets through AWS Data Exchange and access them using the \u003ccode\u003eGetDataSet\u003c/code\u003e API. \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse Amazon Glue to crawl the datasets and store the metadata in Amazon Athena for querying\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because while Amazon Glue can indeed crawl datasets and store metadata, it’s primarily used for preparing and loading data for analytics. In the context of the question, the team needs to access and integrate third-party datasets from AWS Data Exchange. Amazon Glue does not provide a direct method for this. It would require additional steps to export the data from AWS Data Exchange and import it into Amazon Glue, which is not the most efficient or direct method for the team’s needs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis to stream the datasets in real-time into the data pipeline\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because Amazon Kinesis is designed for real-time streaming data. It’s an excellent service when you need to collect, process, and analyze real-time data to get timely insights and react quickly to new information. However, in this scenario, the team needs to access and integrate diverse datasets that are hosted on various platforms within the AWS ecosystem, not stream real-time data. Therefore, using Amazon Kinesis would not meet the team’s requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize API calls for the retrieval and integration of third-party datasets from AWS DataSync\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because AWS DataSync is just a data transfer service that simplifies, automates, and accelerates moving data between on-premises storage systems and AWS Storage services, as well as between AWS Storage services. While it’s useful for transferring large amounts of data over the internet or AWS Direct Connect, it does not provide a direct method for accessing and integrating third-party datasets from AWS Data Exchange. The team would need to first transfer the data from AWS Data Exchange to an AWS Storage service and then use AWS DataSync to move the data, which is not the most direct or efficient method for their needs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Data Exchange Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-data-exchange/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-data-exchange/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:43":{"id":"e:4:43","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 43","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse the CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS) and INSERT INTO statements in Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the SELECT command in Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the UNLOAD statement in Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS) query in Athena\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn application needs the output of SELECT query from Amazon Athena to be stored in an Amazon S3 bucket in Apache Parquet format. The data engineer has been advised against creating any additional tables in Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow can this be achieved with the LEAST possible effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the UNLOAD statement in Athena\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena automatically stores query results and metadata information for each query that runs in a query result location that you can specify in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWrites query results from a SELECT statement to the specified data format. Supported formats for UNLOAD include Apache Parquet, ORC, Apache Avro, and JSON. CSV is the only output format supported by the Athena SELECT command, but you can use the UNLOAD command, which supports a variety of output formats, to enclose your SELECT query and rewrite its output to one of the formats that UNLOAD supports.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe UNLOAD statement is useful when you want to output the results of a SELECT query in a non-CSV format but do not require the associated table. For example, a downstream application might require the results of a SELECT query to be in JSON format, and Parquet or ORC might provide a performance advantage over CSV if you intend to use the results of the SELECT query for additional analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS) query in Athena\u003c/strong\u003e - A CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS) query creates a new table in Athena from the results of a SELECT statement from another query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the SELECT command in Athena\u003c/strong\u003e - CSV is the only output format supported by the Athena SELECT command, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS) and INSERT INTO statements in Athena\u003c/strong\u003e - You can use Create Table as Select (CTAS) and INSERT INTO statements in Athena to extract, transform, and load (ETL) data into Amazon S3 for data processing. A new table will still be created in Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/unload.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/unload.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/querying.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/querying.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-17":{"id":"p:3-17","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 17","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate Amazon CloudWatch alarms for separate custom metrics that represent each error category, and configure each alarm to notify a different Amazon SNS topic\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate CloudWatch Logs metric filters for each error category, publish matching counts as metrics, and configure CloudWatch alarms to notify team-specific Amazon SNS topics when thresholds are breached\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate Amazon EventBridge rules with separate event patterns for each error category, and route each matching event to a dedicated Amazon SNS topic for the responsible team\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSend processing errors to Amazon SQS queues with message attributes, and require each team to poll its assigned queue for messages that match its error category\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Alarm phụ thuộc chu kỳ đánh giá và chuyển trạng thái metric, không phải cách trực tiếp gửi từng sự kiện lỗi đến đúng đội.","Metric filter rồi alarm tạo thêm bước tổng hợp và ngưỡng. Đề muốn định tuyến theo loại sự kiện ngay khi phát sinh.","EventBridge pattern phân loại lỗi rồi gửi từng loại tới SNS topic của đội phụ trách. Không cần gom thành metric để quyết định người nhận.","SQS yêu cầu consumer lấy thông điệp; không tự gửi thông báo chủ động ngay cho các đội. EventBridge với SNS phù hợp hơn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company runs a real-time data pipeline that processes card authorization events. The company needs an alerting mechanism that routes processing errors to different teams based on the error category. Security exceptions must notify the security response team immediately. Schema validation failures must notify the data governance team. Infrastructure failures must notify the platform operations team.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:8:35":{"id":"e:8:35","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 35","correct":[0],"answers":["Utilize Elastic Map Reduce File System (EMRFS) as the storage and Graviton instances for computing.","Utilize the local file system as the storage and spot instances for computing.","Utilize Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS) as the storage and x86 instances for computing.","Utilize Amazon Elastic File System (EFS) as the storage and spot instances for computing."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is preparing to deploy an Amazon EMR cluster, utilizing Apache Spark for data extraction, transformation, and loading (data ETL). The cluster must sustain the company\u0027s performance under enduring workloads, and high reliability is of the utmost importance. The cluster must store data persistently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich storage and computing will satisfy the requirements MOST cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon EMR\u003c/strong\u003e (previously called Amazon Elastic MapReduce) is a managed cluster platform that simplifies running big data frameworks, such as Apache Hadoop and Apache Spark, on AWS to process and analyze vast amounts of data. Using these frameworks and related open-source projects, you can process data for analytics purposes and business intelligence workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBased on the EMR Workflow, planning the storage is an essential first step in configuring Amazon EMR.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/bf21d4551b6e6167bb214b0d0b774e6b28c954dbca1ae5f7b4dcda8b9abc004e.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can choose from a variety of file systems to store input data, output data, and log files:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHadoop Distributed File System (HDFS)\u003c/strong\u003e - HDFS is a distributed, scalable, and portable file system for Hadoop. HDFS distributes the data it stores across instances in the cluster, storing multiple copies of data on different instances to ensure that no data is lost if an individual instance fails. One advantage is that it\u0027s fast; a disadvantage is that it\u0027s ephemeral storage which is reclaimed when the cluster ends. It\u0027s best used for caching the results produced by intermediate job-flow steps.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEMR File System (EMRFS)\u003c/strong\u003e - EMRFS is an implementation of the Hadoop file system used for reading and writing regular files from Amazon EMR directly to Amazon S3. EMRFS provides the convenience of storing persistent data in Amazon S3 for use with Hadoop.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLocal file system\u003c/strong\u003e - The local file system refers to a locally connected disk. When you create a Hadoop cluster, each node is created from an Amazon EC2 instance that comes with a preconfigured block of pre-attached disk storage called an instance store. Data on instance store volumes persists only during the lifecycle of its Amazon EC2 instance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor computing,\u003cstrong\u003e AWS Graviton\u003c/strong\u003e processors are custom-designed by AWS to enable the best price performance for workloads in Amazon EC2. Amazon EC2 instances powered by AWS Graviton2 processors provide up to 40% better price performance over comparable fifth-generation x86-based instances for a wide variety of workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Elastic Map Reduce File System (EMRFS) as the storage and Graviton instances for computing.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e EMRFS is the most suitable file system for storing persistent data. At the same time, Graviton instances are the best choice in terms of cost-effectiveness while not sacrificing performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS) as the storage and x86 instances for computing\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While HDFS is fast and can perform better, it is unsuitable for storing persistent data. HDFS uses temporary storage, available only during the cluster\u0027s life cycle. x86 instances are inferior to Graviton as Amazon EC2 instances powered by AWS Graviton2 processors provide up to 40% better price performance over comparable fifth-generation x86-based instances for various workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize the local file system as the storage and spot instances for computing\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because of unreliability. Data on a local file system only persists at the lifecycle of the EC2 instance and will be deleted after EC2 instance termination. The company will also run enduring workloads that make spot instances unsuitable because spot instances can be interrupted when EC2 reclaims the instance to repurpose capacity. While this option can be cheaper, there are better options than this one, considering high reliability and performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon Elastic File System (EFS) as the storage and spot instances for computing\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While EFS is suitable for many use cases, it is generally more expensive than S3 for data-intensive applications like Spark on EMR. Also, the use of spot instances, although cost-effective, could pose a risk of interruption, which may affect the reliability of the system.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/graviton/getting-started/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/ec2/graviton/getting-started/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-file-systems.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-file-systems.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-gs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-gs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon EMR Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:57":{"id":"e:2:57","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 57","correct":[1,2,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eThe security group of Amazon RDS should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon EC2 instances in the Auto Scaling group on port 80\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe security group of Amazon RDS should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon EC2 instances in the Auto Scaling group on port 5432\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe security group of the Amazon EC2 instances should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Application Load Balancer on port 80\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe security group of the Application Load Balancer should have an inbound rule from anywhere on port 443\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe security group of the Application Load Balancer should have an inbound rule from anywhere on port 80\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe security group of the Amazon EC2 instances should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon RDS database on port 5432\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn HTTP application is deployed on an Auto Scaling Group, is accessible from an Application Load Balancer (ALB) that provides HTTPS termination and accesses a PostgreSQL database managed by Amazon RDS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow should you configure the security groups? (Select three)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe security group of Amazon RDS should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon EC2 instances in the Auto Scaling group on port 5432\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe security group of the Amazon EC2 instances should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Application Load Balancer on port 80\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe security group of the Application Load Balancer should have an inbound rule from anywhere on port 443\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA security group acts as a virtual firewall that controls the traffic for one or more instances. When you launch an instance, you can specify one or more security groups; otherwise, we use the default security group. You can add rules to each security group that allows traffic to or from its associated instances. You can modify the rules for a security group at any time; the new rules are automatically applied to all instances that are associated with the security group. When we decide whether to allow traffic to reach an instance, we evaluate all the rules from all the security groups that are associated with the instance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe following are the characteristics of security group rules:\n1. By default, security groups allow all outbound traffic.\n2. Security group rules are always permissive; you can\u0027t create rules that deny access.\n3. Security groups are stateful\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePostgreSQL port = 5432\nHTTP port = 80\nHTTPS port = 443\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe traffic goes like this :\nThe client sends an HTTPS request to ALB on port 443. This is handled by the rule - \"The security group of the Application Load Balancer should have an inbound rule from anywhere on port 443\"\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe Application Load Balancer then forwards the request to one of the Amazon EC2 instances. This is handled by the rule - \"The security group of the Amazon EC2 instances should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Application Load Balancer on port 80\"\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe Amazon EC2 instance further accesses the PostgreSQL database managed by Amazon RDS on port 5432. This is handled by the rule - \"The security group of Amazon RDS should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon EC2 instances in the Auto Scaling group on port 5432\"\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe security group of the Application Load Balancer should have an inbound rule from anywhere on port 80\u003c/strong\u003e - The client sends an HTTPS request to ALB on port 443 and not on port 80, so this is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe security group of the Amazon EC2 instances should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon RDS database on port 5432\u003c/strong\u003e - The security group of the Amazon EC2 instances should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Application Load Balancer and not from the security group of the Amazon RDS database, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe security group of Amazon RDS should have an inbound rule from the security group of the Amazon EC2 instances in the Auto Scaling group on port 80\u003c/strong\u003e - The Amazon EC2 instance further accesses the PostgreSQL database managed by Amazon RDS on port 5432 and not on port 80, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ec2-security-groups.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ec2-security-groups.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-15":{"id":"p:3-15","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 15","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a trail to log data events using the AWS CloudTrail console. Configure the trail to receive data events from the Orders bucket by specifying an empty prefix and the option to log \u003ccode\u003eWrite\u003c/code\u003e data events. Configure the Audit bucket as the destination bucket for the trail\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon S3 Event notification to trigger an AWS Lambda function on the \u003ccode\u003eNew object created\u003c/code\u003e event for all the objects in the Orders bucket. The Lambda function will write the data event to the Audit bucket. The IAM role assigned to the Lambda function should have full access privileges on both the S3 buckets\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a trail to log data events using the AWS CloudTrail console. Configure the trail to receive data events from the Orders bucket by specifying the prefix as \u003ccode\u003eAll-objects\u003c/code\u003e and the option to log \u003ccode\u003eWrite\u003c/code\u003e data events. Configure the Audit bucket as the destination bucket for the trail\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon S3 Event notification to trigger Amazon EventBridge. The EventBridge event will trigger an AWS Lambda function to copy the Orders bucket object metadata to the Audit bucket. The EventBridge event will then log the event into AWS CloudTrail logs\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["CloudTrail data events ghi thao tác object. Chọn Write và prefix rỗng để bao phủ mọi object trong Orders, rồi xuất log sang Audit bucket.","Lambda tự sao chép thông tin sự kiện thêm mã và cấp full access quá rộng. CloudTrail cung cấp audit trail được quản lý.","All-objects bị hiểu là prefix có tên đó, không phải từ khóa đại diện mọi object. Muốn áp dụng toàn bucket cần để prefix rỗng theo cấu hình đề.","EventBridge/Lambda sao chép metadata không tự tạo audit trail CloudTrail như mô tả. Dùng CloudTrail data events trực tiếp đơn giản hơn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn e-commerce company stores a copy of its order details in an Amazon S3 bucket (Orders bucket). The company wants to log all writes to the Orders bucket into another Amazon S3 bucket (Audit bucket) that is in the same AWS Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will address this requirement with the LEAST operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:6:24":{"id":"e:6:24","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 24","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eExecute SQL queries via Amazon Athena using the \u003ccode\u003eREGEXP_LIKE\u003c/code\u003e function to search for keywords.  \u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eImplement a \u003ccode\u003eFindMatches\u003c/code\u003e transform in AWS Glue to identify potentially fraudulent user accounts.\u003c/span\u003e","Create an AWS Glue PySpark job. Use PySpark’s regular expression function to compare new accounts against the list of flagged accounts.","\u003cspan\u003eUse Redshift Spectrum with the \u003ccode\u003eSQL LIKE\u003c/code\u003e operator to search for similarities in data.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn e-commerce company has observed new accounts being created with minor alterations in names and emails based on a list of flagged accounts. Their existing system cannot detect these subtle variations. The updated account records are stored in an Amazon S3 bucket for inspection.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer must develop a solution for identifying accounts that could match a known suspicious account.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution would meet the requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue FindMatches Machine Learning (ML) Transform enables you to identify duplicate or matching records in your dataset, even when the records do not have a common unique identifier and no fields match exactly. This will not require writing any code or knowing how machine learning works.\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9e39481d3df05e7cb101d52cea586b5a2cd05ee51b80e01c63a7520e429efafe.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFindMatches can be useful in many different problems, such as:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Matching customers\u003c/strong\u003e: Linking customer records across different customer databases, even when many customer fields do not match exactly across the databases (e.g., different name spelling, address differences, missing or inaccurate data, etc).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Matching products\u003c/strong\u003e: Matching products in your catalog against other product sources, such as product catalog against a competitor\u0027s catalog, where entries are structured differently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Improving fraud detection\u003c/strong\u003e: Identifying duplicate customer accounts, determining when a newly created account is (or might be) a match for a previously known fraudulent user.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Other matching problems\u003c/strong\u003e: Match addresses, movies, parts lists, etc. In general, if a human being could look at your database rows and determine that they were a match, there is a really good chance that the FindMatches transform can help you.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the scenario where there\u0027s a need to detect subtle variations in names and emails from flagged data, using AWS Glue FindMatches Transform offers a direct solution.\u0026nbsp;This would allow the company to spot discrepancies without the intricacies of setting up custom models or understanding how Machine Learning works. Custom machine learning can be expensive and tricky. With FindMatches, you avoid the time and cost of setting up and tuning your own model.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement a \u003ccode\u003eFindMatches\u003c/code\u003e transform in AWS Glue to identify potentially fraudulent user accounts. \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eExecute SQL queries via Amazon Athena using the \u003ccode\u003eREGEXP_LIKE\u003c/code\u003e function to search for keywords\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While \u003ccode\u003eREGEXP_LIKE\u003c/code\u003e allows for pattern matching, it requires precise regex patterns to be effective. This approach could miss variations that don\u0027t fit the predefined patterns and might not scale well for complex variations without extensive manual effort to update and maintain these patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Glue PySpark job. Use PySpark’s regular expression function to compare new accounts against the list of flagged accounts \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Regular expressions work for pattern matching but might not be the best tool for catching subtle variations in data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse Redshift Spectrum with the \u003ccode\u003eSQL LIKE\u003c/code\u003e operator to search for similarities in data \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. The \u003ccode\u003eLIKE\u003c/code\u003e operator works on patterns and is typically used for exact substrings or basic wildcard matching. When it comes to variations in names and emails, a pattern might not always capture all discrepancies. Thus, this approach might miss subtle variations and generate false negatives.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2022/03/aws-glue-fuzzy-findmatches/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2022/03/aws-glue-fuzzy-findmatches/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/matching-patient-records-with-the-aws-lake-formation-findmatches-transform/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/matching-patient-records-with-the-aws-lake-formation-findmatches-transform/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:19":{"id":"e:1:19","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 19","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure a dead-letter queue for the EventBridge Scheduler schedules, store failed invocation events in the queue, and analyze the returned order failure details\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse EventBridge Scheduler archive and replay to replay returned order events and compare successful workflow runs with failed workflow runs\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a CloudWatch alarm on the FailedInvocations metric and configure the alarm to invoke a Lambda function that restarts the returned order workflow\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eReduce the maximum retry attempts value in the EventBridge Scheduler retry policy so failed returned order invocations fail faster\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company uses AWS Glue ETL workflows to process ecommerce order events. The company processes submitted, packed, shipped, and returned orders through separate AWS Glue workflows because each order status requires different transformation logic. The order management application sends events to Amazon EventBridge Scheduler configurations that invoke the appropriate AWS Glue workflow for each order status. The data engineering team reviews CloudWatch metrics and notices that the FailedInvocations metric is high for returned order schedules. The team must identify the cause of the failed invocations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure a dead-letter queue for the EventBridge Scheduler schedules, store failed invocation events in the queue, and analyze the returned order failure details\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eEventBridge Scheduler supports dead-letter queues for schedules. A dead-letter queue stores events that EventBridge Scheduler cannot deliver successfully to the configured target after the retry policy is exhausted.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe dead-letter event includes details that help the data engineering team troubleshoot the failure. These details can include the target ARN, error code, error message, and the input payload associated with the failed schedule invocation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution directly addresses the requirement to determine the cause of the failed invocations. By analyzing the messages in the DLQ for returned order schedules, the team can identify whether the failures are related to permissions, invalid target parameters, unsupported API configuration, throttling, or another target invocation issue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/06b7dfebe924fc467912b17bcbeefd24f12d9293fd62bf803b78a5e69875e961.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/06b7dfebe924fc467912b17bcbeefd24f12d9293fd62bf803b78a5e69875e961.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/introducing-amazon-eventbridge-scheduler/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/introducing-amazon-eventbridge-scheduler/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse EventBridge Scheduler archive and replay to replay returned order events and compare successful workflow runs with failed workflow runs\u003c/strong\u003e - EventBridge event buses support archive and replay for events that flow through an event bus. Archive and replay can help teams test consumers or recover event processing for event bus patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, EventBridge Scheduler does not use archive and replay as the troubleshooting mechanism for failed schedule target invocations. A DLQ is the appropriate feature for capturing failed invocation details from schedules.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a CloudWatch alarm on the FailedInvocations metric and configure the alarm to invoke a Lambda function that restarts the returned order workflow\u003c/strong\u003e - A CloudWatch alarm can notify the team or start automated remediation when the FailedInvocations metric exceeds a threshold. This can be useful for operational monitoring.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the alarm itself does not capture the failed event payload or target error details. Restarting the workflow without understanding the failure can hide the symptom without solving the cause.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReduce the maximum retry attempts value in the EventBridge Scheduler retry policy so failed returned order invocations fail faster\u003c/strong\u003e - Reducing maximum retry attempts can make failed events reach a terminal state sooner. This might reduce delayed retry traffic to a target.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, reducing retries does not help the team determine why the invocations failed. It can also increase the number of dropped events if the schedule does not have a DLQ configured.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/scheduler/latest/UserGuide/configuring-schedule-dlq.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/scheduler/latest/UserGuide/configuring-schedule-dlq.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/scheduler/latest/UserGuide/troubleshooting.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/scheduler/latest/UserGuide/troubleshooting.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/scheduler/latest/UserGuide/managing-schedule.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/scheduler/latest/UserGuide/managing-schedule.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/introducing-amazon-eventbridge-scheduler/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/introducing-amazon-eventbridge-scheduler/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:48":{"id":"e:5:48","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 48","correct":[2],"answers":["Write a PySpark script for counting distinct entries and run it in an Amazon EMR Serverless application.","Use AWS Glue Crawlers to infer the schema of source data. Write an AWS Glue Spark job to perform the unique customer count.","\u003cspan\u003eConstruct a recipe with AWS Glue DataBrew to calculate the distinct count of customers by using the \u003ccode\u003eCOUNT_DISTINCT\u003c/code\u003e function.\u003c/span\u003e","Configure an AWS Lambda function to execute a Python script that reads the S3 file, processes the concatenation, and uses a distinct count algorithm."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company that provides a geolocation service gathers large datasets of longitude and latitude coordinates representing customer interactions. These datasets are saved in a CSV document, which can reach sizes of up to 3GB. The document is then uploaded to Amazon S3 for storage and analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team needs to merge the latitude and longitude fields and then analyze the data to find the total number of unique customer entries in these daily records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow can the team satisfy the requirements in the LEAST amount of development effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew\u003c/strong\u003e is a user-friendly tool that simplifies data preparation by allowing users to clean, transform, and profile data without writing any code. With DataBrew, users can easily visualize, clean, and normalize data directly from data stores like Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, and Amazon RDS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/402141c5f6d16748dccd20571cd76158d42f51134f6a8dac3b7e1a1af2f8a67d.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew makes it easier for data engineering teams to perform tasks like merging fields and aggregating counts. With the \u003ccode\u003eCOUNT_DISTINCT\u003c/code\u003e function, identifying unique customers is just a few clicks away, eliminating the need for custom code or scripts. The recipe and results can be saved and reused for ongoing analysis of daily records with minimal effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eConstruct a recipe with AWS Glue DataBrew to calculate the distinct count of customers by using the \u003ccode\u003eCOUNT_DISTINCT\u003c/code\u003e function.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eWrite a PySpark script for counting distinct entries and run it in an Amazon EMR Serverless application\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Although EMR Serverless involves less overhead in managing an EMR cluster, you’d still need to write the code compared to using AWS Glue DataBrew, which offers a more straightforward, no-code approach to accomplish the task.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Crawlers to infer the schema of source data. Write an AWS Glue Spark job to perform the unique customer count\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. AWS Glue Crawlers are used to discover and profile data, create metadata, and make the data searchable in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. AWS Glue DataBrew allows direct connection to Amazon S3 without needing to infer the schema using Glue crawlers. Also, this option entails more development effort due to having to code a Spark job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an AWS Lambda function to execute a Python script that reads the S3 file, processes the concatenation, and uses a distinct count algorithm\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. AWS Lambda allows running code in response to triggers without provisioning or managing servers. However, processing large files (up to 3GB, as mentioned) directly within Lambda may be challenging due to its limitations in execution time and memory. It would require careful management of resources and potentially splitting the file into smaller chunks, increasing the complexity and operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/databrew/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/databrew/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.functions.COUNT_DISTINCT.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.functions.COUNT_DISTINCT.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipes.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipes.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:4":{"id":"e:8:4","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 4","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eCreate an IAM service role with the \u003ccode\u003eAWSGlueServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e managed policy and attach this role to the crawler. Set up the Glue Crawler to run daily and specify the database in the Glue Data Catalog.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eCreate an IAM service role with the \u003ccode\u003eAmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess\u003c/code\u003e policy and attach this role to the crawler. Set up the Glue Crawler to run daily and specify a different database. Run the crawler to catalog the data.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eCreate an IAM service role with the \u003ccode\u003eAWSGlueServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e policy and attach this role to the crawler. Set up the Glue Crawler to run on-demand and specify the database in the Glue Data Catalog.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eCreate an IAM service role with the \u003ccode\u003eAmazonS3FullAccess\u003c/code\u003e policy and attach this role to the crawler. Set up the Glue Crawler to run daily and specify a new S3 path as the target location. Run the crawler to catalog the data.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA company ingests hundreds of unstructured files in Amazon S3 every day. The data must be cataloged using an AWS Glue crawler, and the results must be output to an Glue Data Catalog database.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich of the following would meet this requirement in the LEAST amount of effort?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy to prepare and load your data for analytics. An AWS Glue crawler is a component of AWS Glue that automates the process of discovering, classifying, and cataloging data stored in various data stores. The crawler scans your data sources, identifies data formats, infers schemas, and then creates or updates metadata tables in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. This catalog acts as a central metadata repository that AWS Glue and other Amazon services use for ETL, data querying, and data discovery tasks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo perform its operations, a crawler needs to interact with other AWS services on your behalf. This is achieved by using an IAM role that has the necessary permissions. When you create an IAM role for AWS Glue, you grant your IAM role permissions that AWS Glue can assume when calling other services on your behalf. This includes access to Amazon S3 for any sources, targets, scripts, and temporary directories that you use with AWS Glue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/76d3c851606f5602efae4982d0301c961825b8c19d655013b00f3378b68fcfcf.png\" alt=\"AWSGlueServiceRole\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eAWSGlueServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;managed policy provides the necessary permissions for AWS Glue crawler to access the resources it needs for reading data from Amazon S3 and writing metadata to AWS Glue Data Catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8f5d46649e1fd1d7b236832cb6ae02d3c00703cadd447658e122661e44ad67bb.png\" alt=\"Crawler Sschedule\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFinally, you configure the AWS Glue Crawler to run daily and write the output to a specified database in AWS Glue Data Catalog. This meets the requirement of cataloging the data every day.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an IAM service role with the \u003ccode\u003eAWSGlueServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e managed policy and attach this role to the crawler. Set up the Glue Crawler to run daily and specify the database in the Glue Data Catalog.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an IAM service role with the \u003ccode\u003eAmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess\u003c/code\u003e policy and attach this role to the crawler. Set up the Glue Crawler to run daily and specify a different database. Run the crawler to catalog the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. The\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eAmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;policy only provides read access to S3 resources and is not sufficient for\u0026nbsp;the crawler\u0027s tasks, such as writing metadata to the AWS Glue Data Catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an IAM service role with the \u003ccode\u003eAWSGlueServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e policy and attach this role to the crawler. Set up the Glue Crawler to run on-demand and specify the database in the Glue Data Catalog\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Setting up the crawler to run on-demand instead of on a regular schedule typically requires manual intervention each time the data needs to be cataloged. This approach increases operational effort, especially given the daily ingestion of hundreds of files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an IAM service role with the \u003ccode\u003eAmazonS3FullAccess\u003c/code\u003e policy and attach this role to the crawler. Set up the Glue Crawler to run daily and specify a new S3 path as the target location. Run the crawler to catalog the data\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. This policy grants broader S3 permissions than necessary for the crawler\u0027s purpose. Similar to the other incorrect option, this policy does not contain the relevant Glue permissions for the crawler. Also, the main task of a Glue crawler is to catalog data into AWS Glue Data Catalog.\u0026nbsp; The target for a crawler\u0027s output should be a Data Catalog database where the table definitions are stored, not another S3 path location.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/create-an-iam-role.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/create-an-iam-role.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/set-up-iam.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/set-up-iam.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this\u0026nbsp;AWS Identity and Access Management Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-identity-and-access-management-iam/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-identity-and-access-management-iam/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:42":{"id":"e:2:42","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 42","correct":[1,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable the \u003ccode\u003e--job-bookmark-option\u003c/code\u003e parameter with job-bookmark-enable so AWS Glue tracks Iceberg table commits and enables Iceberg support\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet the \u003ccode\u003e--enable-auto-scaling\u003c/code\u003e AWS Glue job parameter to true\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet iceberg as the value for the \u003ccode\u003e--datalake-formats\u003c/code\u003e AWS Glue job parameter\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a DynamoDB table for Iceberg metadata tracking and configure the AWS Glue job to use DynamoDB as the primary Iceberg catalog\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an empty \u003ccode\u003e--conf\u003c/code\u003e key for the AWS Glue job and rely on AWS Glue to infer all Apache Iceberg Spark settings automatically\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is building a 140-TB transactional data lake in Amazon S3 by using Apache Iceberg tables. The data engineer wants to run AWS Glue Apache Spark jobs that use the Iceberg framework to process the data lake. The jobs must support large-scale processing while reducing the need to manually tune worker capacity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet iceberg as the value for the \u003ccode\u003e--datalake-formats\u003c/code\u003e AWS Glue job parameter\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue provides native support for Apache Iceberg as a data lake framework. To enable that native support in an AWS Glue Apache Spark job, the data engineer must set the \u003ccode\u003e--datalake-formats\u003c/code\u003e job parameter to iceberg.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis parameter tells AWS Glue to include the supported Iceberg libraries in the job runtime. The Spark job can then use the Iceberg framework to read from and write to Iceberg tables in the data lake.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis step is required because the job must use the Iceberg framework. Without enabling the Iceberg data lake format, the AWS Glue Spark job does not automatically include the native Iceberg integration that the workload needs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/08138511cf7a23772cfab0b23bebf7131fabe5dc8d82cc877367a9821a9db07d.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/08138511cf7a23772cfab0b23bebf7131fabe5dc8d82cc877367a9821a9db07d.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-format-iceberg.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-format-iceberg.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet the \u003ccode\u003e--enable-auto-scaling\u003c/code\u003e AWS Glue job parameter to true\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Auto Scaling helps large Spark jobs adjust worker capacity based on workload demand. For a 140-TB data lake, processing requirements can vary significantly across tables, partitions, and job stages.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSetting \u003ccode\u003e--enable-auto-scaling\u003c/code\u003e to true allows AWS Glue to scale the job resources instead of requiring the data engineer to manually size the job for every run. This helps reduce manual tuning while supporting large-scale Spark processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis step complements the Iceberg configuration. The \u003ccode\u003e--datalake-formats\u003c/code\u003e parameter enables the Iceberg framework, and Auto Scaling helps the Glue job process the large data lake more efficiently with less manual capacity management.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an empty \u003ccode\u003e--conf\u003c/code\u003e key for the AWS Glue job and rely on AWS Glue to infer all Apache Iceberg Spark settings automatically\u003c/strong\u003e - The \u003ccode\u003e--conf\u003c/code\u003e parameter can configure Spark settings for AWS Glue jobs. In Iceberg workloads, Spark configuration can be useful when the job needs explicit catalog or extension settings.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe issue with this option is that creating an empty \u003ccode\u003e--conf\u003c/code\u003e key does not configure anything. It does not enable the Iceberg framework, and it does not replace the required \u003ccode\u003e--datalake-formats\u003c/code\u003e iceberg setting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a DynamoDB table for Iceberg metadata tracking and configure the AWS Glue job to use DynamoDB as the primary Iceberg catalog\u003c/strong\u003e - DynamoDB can support scalable key-value access patterns and can be used in some data lake architectures for state or locking patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because DynamoDB is not the required step to enable Apache Iceberg in AWS Glue Spark jobs. The required steps are to enable the Iceberg data lake format and enable Auto Scaling for large-scale processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable the \u003ccode\u003e--job-bookmark-option\u003c/code\u003e parameter with job-bookmark-enable so AWS Glue tracks Iceberg table commits and enables Iceberg support\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue job bookmarks can track previously processed data for supported sources. They are useful for incremental ETL workflows that need to avoid reprocessing the same input files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not enable Iceberg support. Job bookmarks do not add the Iceberg framework to the Spark runtime, and they do not provide the capacity scaling required for a 140-TB data lake workload.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-format-iceberg.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-format-iceberg.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/apache-iceberg-on-aws/iceberg-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/apache-iceberg-on-aws/iceberg-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-glue-arguments.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-glue-arguments.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/auto-scaling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/auto-scaling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-auto-scaling-automatically-resize-serverless-computing-resources-for-lower-cost-with-optimized-apache-spark/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-auto-scaling-automatically-resize-serverless-computing-resources-for-lower-cost-with-optimized-apache-spark/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:13":{"id":"e:6:13","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 13","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM products WHERE product_name LIKE \u0027TAD%\u0027 OR product_name LIKE \u0027BAG%\u0027;\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM products WHERE product_name ~ \u0027^TAD|BAG\u0027;\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM products WHERE product_name SIMILAR TO \u0027(TAD|BAG)%\u0027;\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM products WHERE product_name !~ \u0027^TAD|BAG\u0027;\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company handles a large amount of data in their Amazon Redshift. The company wants to find all the product names that start with “TAD” or “BAG.” Additionally, they want to process the data, which will be used for creating reports in the fastest way possible.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;What query would meet the requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eA pattern-matching operator searches a string for a pattern specified in the conditional expression and returns true or false depending on whether it finds a match. Amazon Redshift uses three methods for pattern matching:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cdiv\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eLIKE\u003c/code\u003e Operator where it compares a string expression, and its pattern-matching covers the entire string.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSIMILAR TO\u003c/code\u003e Operator where it matches a string expression with a SQL standard regular expression pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003ePOSIX\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;Operator that provides a more powerful means for pattern matching since it can match any portion of the string and performs a case-sensitive match.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/228befb8442b1cbe75b192fa5dfe6afea0c2e63ed02018d4537322a4786bf4f2.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eUsing \u003ccode\u003eSIMILAR TO\u003c/code\u003e or \u003ccode\u003ePOSIX\u003c/code\u003e operators are computationally expensive when doing regular expression matching. Furthermore, \u003ccode\u003eLIKE\u003c/code\u003e the command performs way better compared to the other operators as it does not use \u003ccode\u003eREGEX\u003c/code\u003e, which causes potential drawbacks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, The correct option is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM products WHERE product_name LIKE \u0027TAD%\u0027 OR product_name LIKE \u0027BAG%\u0027;\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003e\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\nThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e \u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM products WHERE product_name ~ \u0027^TAD|BAG\u0027;\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This query uses \u003ccode\u003ePOSIX\u003c/code\u003e regex with the \u003ccode\u003e~\u003c/code\u003e operator to match product names. While this archives the same result as the \u003ccode\u003eLIKE\u003c/code\u003e operator, using \u003ccode\u003eREGEX\u003c/code\u003e is generally slower and computationally expensive.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e \u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM products WHERE product_name SIMILAR TO \u0027(TAD|BAG)%\u0027;\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This query uses the \u003ccode\u003eSIMILAR TO\u003c/code\u003e operator, an SQL standard, for pattern matching. However, this is less efficient and computationally expensive as this operator internally translates to a \u003ccode\u003eREGEX\u003c/code\u003e, causing performance drawbacks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM products WHERE product_name !~ \u0027^TAD|BAG\u0027;\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e \u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. This query uses the \u003ccode\u003ePOSIX\u003c/code\u003e regex operator for negation pattern matching. This operator will find product names that do not match the specific pattern, making it incorrect to find patterns. Additionally, \u003ccode\u003eREGEX\u003c/code\u003e operators are less efficient for pattern matching than the \u003ccode\u003eLIKE\u003c/code\u003e operator.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/pattern-matching-conditions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/pattern-matching-conditions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/pattern-matching-conditions-posix.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/pattern-matching-conditions-posix.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e"},"e:9:1":{"id":"e:9:1","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 9 Â· CÃ¢u 1","correct":[2],"answers":["Enable Amazon Athena\u0027s Reuse query results feature for commonly executed queries.","Employ a Federated Query in Amazon Athena to concurrently query data from multiple disparate data sources.","Execute a CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS) query in Amazon Athena to convert query results into efficient storage formats like Parquet or ORC.","Frequently update the indexing of data in Amazon S3 to enhance data retrieval speeds for Amazon Athena queries."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team at a large e-commerce company is working to improve the performance of their data analytics workloads. They extensively use Amazon Athena to query a wide range of records, which are sourced from various platforms and consolidated in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe team has noticed a decline in query performance due to the growing size and complexity of the datasets. They are seeking a solution to improve the efficiency and performance of their queries in Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following methods would be the MOST effective for optimizing query performance for their specific needs?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data directly in Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) using standard SQL. This feature is highly effective in converting query results into more efficient storage formats, such as Parquet or ORC, which are open source columnar formats. These formats are optimized for Athena, leading to reduced data scanning per query, thereby enhancing query performance and lowering costs. Athena does not support transaction-based operations on table data, ensuring that operations like CREATE, UPDATE, or DELETE are ACID-compliant. When using CTAS, the \u003ccode\u003eformat\u003c/code\u003e property can specify storage formats like \u003ccode\u003eORC\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003ePARQUET\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eAVRO\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eJSON\u003c/code\u003e, or \u003ccode\u003eTEXTFILE\u003c/code\u003e, with the default being \u003ccode\u003ePARQUET\u003c/code\u003e. Additionally, for formats like \u003ccode\u003ePARQUET\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003eORC\u003c/code\u003e, compression formats like \u003ccode\u003eSNAPPY\u003c/code\u003e can be specified using the \u003ccode\u003ewrite_compression\u003c/code\u003e property. This approach of transforming data into efficient formats directly caters to the need for improved query performance due to increased data volume and complexity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/fc9be0c5dde1a782172f5523931947bf1e1312fbd2cb861c9f6e027e3bd15c9e.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eExecute a CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS) query in Amazon Athena to convert query results into efficient storage formats like Parquet or ORC.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEmploy a Federated Query in Amazon Athena to concurrently query data from multiple disparate data sources\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Federated Query is a powerful feature for querying across multiple data sources, but it does not inherently improve the performance of queries on existing datasets in S3. It’s more suited for scenarios requiring data consolidation from various sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Amazon Athena’s Reuse query results feature for commonly executed queries\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This feature only retrieves results from a previously executed query and does not enhance the actual query execution or address issues with large data volumes and complex queries. This feature is particularly helpful when the results of the query won’t change for a given time frame.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFrequently update the indexing of data in Amazon S3 to enhance data retrieval speeds for Amazon Athena queries\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Amazon S3 does not support traditional data indexing like a database. Athena queries data directly in S3 and relies on table formats and partitions defined in the AWS Glue Data Catalog for performance optimization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eResources:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/ctas.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/ctas.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/using-athena-sql.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/using-athena-sql.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:29":{"id":"e:7:29","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 29","correct":[0,1],"answers":["Use AWS Lambda to execute the Python code.","Use Amazon EventBridge to execute the Python code every Monday at 9:00 AM UTC.","Use AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK) to define cloud infrastructure in code and provision it through AWS CloudFormation.","Use AWS Cloud9 to write, run, and debug the Python code.","Use AWS CloudShell to execute the Python code."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is developing a data processing pipeline for their e-commerce platform. The pipeline requires the execution of Python code every Monday at 9:00 AM UTC to analyze customer behavior data stored in S3 buckets that serve as a data lake. This data includes information such as page views, product clicks, and purchase history. The solution should prioritize operational efficiency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options would be most suitable for this scenario? (Select TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Lambda is a serverless computing service that lets you run your code without provisioning or managing servers. In the given scenario, you can upload your Python code to AWS Lambda, which takes care of all the capacity, scaling, patching, and administration of the infrastructure to run your code. This allows you to focus on analyzing customer behavior data rather than managing infrastructure, which aligns with the requirement to prioritize operational efficiency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/da0fad61a33a04df8d1673d7c89ef751d002dfa304ed67c1955a590c0354d995.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon EventBridge is a serverless event bus service that makes it easy to connect applications together using data from your own applications, Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) applications, and AWS services. You can set up a rule in EventBridge to trigger your AWS Lambda function every Monday at 9:00 AM UTC. This way, your Python code gets executed automatically at the specified time each week.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e- Use AWS Lambda to execute the Python code.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e- Use Amazon EventBridge to execute the Python code every Monday at 9:00 AM UTC.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;Use AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK) to define cloud infrastructure in code and provision it through AWS CloudFormation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect. While CDK can be used to create the infrastructure needed for the pipeline (including Lambda functions and EventBridge rules), it doesn\u0027t directly execute Python code as part of a data processing pipeline. It\u0027s more about setting up the infrastructure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Cloud9 to write, run, and debug the Python code\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because AWS Cloud9 is just a cloud-based integrated development environment (IDE) that lets you write, run, and debug your code with just a browser. It can\u0027t be used to execute Python code on schedule as part of a data processing pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS CloudShell to execute the Python code\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because AWS CloudShell is simply a browser-based shell\u0026nbsp;intended for interactive use and does not support scheduling or automation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/lambda/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Lambda Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:11":{"id":"e:5:11","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 11","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eAdd the \u003ccode\u003eapache-airflow-providers-snowflake\u003c/code\u003e Python dependency on the web server and place it in the \u003ccode\u003edags\u003c/code\u003e directory.\u003c/span\u003e","Install the Apache Airflow base package and generate an Apache Airflow AWS connection URI string.","\u003cspan\u003eInstall the \u003ccode\u003eapache-airflow-providers-ssh\u003c/code\u003e package on the web server via the \u003ccode\u003erequirements.txt\u003c/code\u003e file.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eInstall the \u003ccode\u003eapache-airflow-providers-ssh\u003c/code\u003e package on the web server by configuring the \u003ccode\u003eauthorized_keys\u003c/code\u003e file.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is setting up a secure and highly available managed workflow orchestration using Apache Airflow in AWS. The company chose Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) for its data workflow orchestration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer needs to establish an SSH connection to a remote Amazon EC2 instance from the company’s MWAA environment using the \u003ccode\u003eSSHOperator\u003c/code\u003e in a directed acyclic graph (DAG). The networking between the MWAA environment and the EC2 instance has already been configured.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat should the data engineer do to meet the above requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAmazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow is a managed orchestration service for Apache Airflow that you can use to set up and operate data pipelines in the cloud at scale. Apache Airflow is an open-source tool used to programmatically author, schedule, and monitor sequences of processes and tasks referred to as workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWith Amazon MWAA, you can use Apache Airflow and Python to create workflows without having to manage the underlying infrastructure for scalability, availability, and security. Amazon MWAA automatically scales its workflow execution capacity to meet your needs. It also integrates with AWS security services to help provide you with fast and secure access to your data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b1897f88e4fa9d7f9f8d98e847aec5d1dc4dd710149aa45c01ac6f87d0c56847.png\" alt=\"Specify details page for creating an Amazon MWAA environment alongside a requirements.txt file setup\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use the SSHOperator in a directed acyclic graph (DAG) to connect to a remote Amazon EC2 instance from your Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow environment. You can also use a similar approach to connect to any remote instance with SSH access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn order to do this, you have to upload an SSH secret key (.pem) first to your environment’s dags directory on Amazon S3. Then, you install the necessary dependencies using requirements.txt and create a new Apache Airflow connection in the UI. Finally, you write a DAG that creates an SSH connection to the remote instance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou must add the following parameter to requirements.txt to install the apache-airflow-providers-ssh package on the web server.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003e-c https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/airflow/constraints-Airflow-version/constraints-Python-version.txt apache-airflow-providers-ssh\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOnce your environment updates and Amazon MWAA successfully installs the dependency, you will see a new SSH connection type in the UI.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eInstall the \u003ccode\u003eapache-airflow-providers-ssh\u003c/code\u003e package on the web server via the \u003ccode\u003erequirements.txt\u003c/code\u003e file:\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdd the \u003ccode\u003eapache-airflow-providers-snowflake\u003c/code\u003e Python dependency on the web server and place it in the \u003ccode\u003edags\u003c/code\u003e directory\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. You actually have to install the \u003ccode\u003eapache-airflow-providers-ssh\u003c/code\u003e package and not the \u003ccode\u003eapache-airflow-providers-snowflake\u003c/code\u003e Python dependency since this one is primarily used in AWS Secrets Manager when setting up an Apache Airflow Snowflake connection.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eInstall the \u003ccode\u003eapache-airflow-providers-ssh\u003c/code\u003e package on the web server by configuring the \u003ccode\u003eauthorized_keys\u003c/code\u003e file\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. While using the \u003ccode\u003eapache-airflow-providers-ssh\u003c/code\u003e package is right, configuring the \u003ccode\u003eauthorized_keys\u003c/code\u003e file is wrong since this only contains the SSH-RSA public keys. The \u003ccode\u003eapache-airflow-providers-ssh\u003c/code\u003e package must be added to the requirements.txt file instead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Install the Apache Airflow base package and generate an Apache Airflow AWS connection URI string\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because this is only needed if you are configuring an Apache Airflow connection using an AWS Secrets Manager secret.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/samples-ssh.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/samples-ssh.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/what-is-mwaa.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/what-is-mwaa.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/connections-secrets-manager.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/connections-secrets-manager.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this\u0026nbsp;Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-managed-workflows-for-apache-airflow/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-managed-workflows-for-apache-airflow/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:30":{"id":"e:6:30","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 30","correct":[3],"answers":["Run Apache Oozie on Amazon EC2 to process the analytics workload.","Use Amazon Athena to process the analytics workload.","Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to run the analytics workload.","Use Amazon Redshift Serverless to run the analytics workload."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company runs a complex analytics workload every week on an Amazon Redshift cluster. The workload includes the following tasks:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eLaunching a new Redshift cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePerforming processing and loading data to an Amazon S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTerminating the Redshift cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe company wants to minimize operational overhead and ensure that all these tasks are handled automatically. The solution must also be able to handle the complexity of the analytics functions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution offers the LEAST operational overhead while meeting these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift Serverless is a fully managed data warehouse service that automatically scales compute resources up or down to meet the demands of your workload. This means that it can handle the processing of large amounts of data without requiring manual intervention to adjust compute capacity. This automatic scaling and task management significantly reduce operational overhead, making it an ideal solution for the company\u0027s needs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e2f5fb13caf8e60c45008ee4727f18ec37ff50885420098241fe09f4cae49a32.png\" alt=\"Amazon Redshift Serverless\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFurthermore, Amazon Redshift Serverless is designed to handle complex analytics functions. It uses machine learning to deliver consistently fast performance, even for complex queries and large datasets. This means that it can efficiently manage the company’s complex analytics workload.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;Use Amazon Redshift Serverless to run the analytics workload.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun Apache Oozie on Amazon EC2 to process the analytics workload\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because Apache Oozie is a workflow scheduler primarily designed for Hadoop ecosystems. Running Oozie on EC2 requires complex setup, including infrastructure provisioning, configuration of dependencies, and ongoing instance management. This results in high operational overhead, which does not meet the company\u0027s goal to reduce manual operations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena to process the analytics workload\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;While Athena is serverless and has low operational overhead, its use of pooled resources can lead to variable performance, especially for complex analytic workloads that demand consistent and high-performance computing resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to run the analytics workload\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Redshift Spectrum is simply an extension of a Redshift cluster that allows you to directly query data on S3. Amazon Redshift Spectrum still needs an existing Redshift cluster to function and does not operate on its own.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/redshift-serverless/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/redshift/redshift-serverless/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/gsg/new-user-serverless.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/gsg/new-user-serverless.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-amazon-redshift-serverless-run-analytics-at-any-scale-without-having-to-manage-infrastructure/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/introducing-amazon-redshift-serverless-run-analytics-at-any-scale-without-having-to-manage-infrastructure/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-61":{"id":"p:4-61","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 61","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 standard storage for maximum performance, Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering for intelligent, durable storage, and Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive for archival storage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon EC2 instance store for maximum performance, Amazon S3 for durable data storage, and Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive for archival storage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon EBS for maximum performance, Amazon S3 for durable data storage, and Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive for archival storage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon EC2 instance store for maximum performance, AWS DataSync for durable data storage and Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive for archival storage\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["S3 là object storage qua mạng, không thay thế ổ cục bộ cho xử lý video cần I/O có độ trễ thấp nhất. Hai lớp S3 còn lại có thể lưu dữ liệu nhưng phần xử lý tốc độ cao chưa phù hợp.","Instance store phù hợp dữ liệu xử lý tạm cần I/O cục bộ cao; S3 giữ nội dung bền vững; Deep Archive giữ lịch sử ít truy cập với chi phí thấp. Dữ liệu quan trọng phải được lưu ngoài instance store vì ổ này không bền vững theo vòng đời instance.","EBS cung cấp block storage bền vững và có loại hiệu năng cao. Tuy nhiên bộ đề ưu tiên instance store cho phần dữ liệu tạm cần I/O cục bộ tối đa; lựa chọn thực tế còn phụ thuộc loại volume và instance.","Instance store và Deep Archive đúng vai trò được nêu, nhưng DataSync là dịch vụ chuyển dữ liệu. Nó không phải kho lưu trữ bền vững cho 450 TB nội dung."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media company is evaluating the possibility of moving its IT infrastructure to the AWS Cloud. The company needs at least 10 terabytes of storage with the maximum possible I/O performance for processing certain video files on its servers. The company also needs close to 450 terabytes of very durable storage for storing media content and almost double of it, i.e. 900 terabytes for archival of legacy data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich set of services will you recommend to meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:9":{"id":"e:4:9","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 9","correct":[0,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an IAM role with necessary permissions to access the Secrets Manager and attach the role to the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a configuration file on Amazon S3 and access the file using the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the credentials in the AWS Glue job parameters that will be accessed dynamically when the job is started\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an IAM role with necessary permissions to access the Secrets Manager and attach the role to the Redshift cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the credentials in AWS Secrets Manager\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eDuring a code review it was discovered that the credentials to access an Amazon Redshift cluster were hard-coded into an AWS Glue job script. As a data engineer, you have been tasked with remediating the security vulnerability in the script and suggesting a way to securely access the needed credentials by the AWS Glue job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich options would you recommend for this remediation task? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the credentials in AWS Secrets Manager\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an IAM role with the necessary permissions to access the Secrets Manager and attach the role to the AWS Glue job\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS recommends that you use AWS Secrets Manager to supply connection credentials for your data store. Using Secrets Manager this way lets AWS Glue access your secret at runtime for ETL jobs and crawler runs, and helps keep your credentials secure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo use Secrets Manager with AWS Glue, you must attach an IAM role with the necessary permissions to the AWS Glue job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCreating secret for AWS Glue:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8f81bed14f156874270fd46f57042b88dfdbbb918ffe833bf929698c824c92d6.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8f81bed14f156874270fd46f57042b88dfdbbb918ffe833bf929698c824c92d6.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/connection-properties-secrets-manager.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/connection-properties-secrets-manager.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a configuration file on Amazon S3 and access the file using the AWS Glue job\u003c/strong\u003e - The configuration file can be read by any other user or program and hence is not secure enough to store Redshift credentials.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an IAM role with necessary permissions to access the Secrets Manager and attach the role to the Redshift cluster\u003c/strong\u003e - Attaching an IAM role to Redshift will not provide the necessary access to the AWS Glue job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the credentials in the AWS Glue job parameters that will be accessed dynamically when the job is started\u003c/strong\u003e - When creating an AWS Glue job, you can provide additional configuration information through the Argument fields (Job Parameters in the console). When writing AWS Glue scripts, you may want to access job parameter values to alter the behavior of your code. AWS Glue has predefined argument names that it recognizes and can be used to set up the script environment for your jobs and job runs. You should never store passwords or other access credentials in AWS Glue job parameters, as these can be compromised.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/connection-properties-secrets-manager.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/connection-properties-secrets-manager.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:47":{"id":"e:3:47","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 47","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate AWS Glue streaming jobs that poll each Aurora MySQL database for changed rows, write intermediate files to Amazon S3, and load the files into Amazon Redshift Serverless on a frequent schedule\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS DMS to configure binary log replication from Aurora MySQL to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, transform schemas, and load the changes into Amazon Redshift Serverless\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate AWS DMS tasks that perform schema conversion for each Aurora MySQL database and load the converted changes into Amazon Redshift Serverless\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate Aurora zero-ETL integrations with Amazon Redshift Serverless for the Aurora MySQL databases to make ongoing changes available in Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail marketplace company needs to capture ongoing data changes from several Amazon Aurora MySQL databases that support orders, payments, and fulfillment services. The company must make the updates available for analytics in Amazon Redshift Serverless in near real time. The solution must be serverless and must minimize ongoing maintenance for the data engineering team.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate Aurora zero-ETL integrations with Amazon Redshift Serverless for the Aurora MySQL databases to make ongoing changes available in Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAurora zero-ETL integration with Amazon Redshift is designed to make transactional data from Aurora available in Amazon Redshift for analytics without building and maintaining custom ETL pipelines. The company can create integrations from the Aurora MySQL sources to Amazon Redshift Serverless so ongoing database changes become available for analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/dc3ff833baf39687821d1226445320f75840ee2b9a973d5eb24e80f25a3f716a.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/dc3ff833baf39687821d1226445320f75840ee2b9a973d5eb24e80f25a3f716a.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution meets the near real-time requirement because zero-ETL integrations continuously replicate changes from Aurora to Redshift. The analytics teams can query the data in Redshift Serverless without waiting for hourly batch jobs, custom change data capture jobs, or file-based loads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also provides the least operational overhead because AWS manages the integration workflow. The company does not need to manage Kafka connectors, DMS replication tasks, schema conversion jobs, Glue polling logic, or S3-based change file pipelines.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8170d82be7b45db0587cf17320cabc22e0024f7600caf764f4dcc4365907863c.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8170d82be7b45db0587cf17320cabc22e0024f7600caf764f4dcc4365907863c.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-zero-etl-integrations-with-aws-databases-and-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-zero-etl-integrations-with-aws-databases-and-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9454a548477f6a2d537dfe7d58a44539a15fad91374eba8f6b9f5b1c656c1566.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9454a548477f6a2d537dfe7d58a44539a15fad91374eba8f6b9f5b1c656c1566.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-zero-etl-integrations-with-aws-databases-and-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-zero-etl-integrations-with-aws-databases-and-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate AWS Glue streaming jobs that poll each Aurora MySQL database for changed rows, write intermediate files to Amazon S3, and load the files into Amazon Redshift Serverless on a frequent schedule\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue jobs can read from JDBC sources and write to Amazon S3 or Amazon Redshift. A Glue-based pipeline could periodically extract changed rows and load them into Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, polling each Aurora MySQL database and staging data in S3 adds custom logic and scheduling overhead. It also increases latency compared with a managed zero-ETL integration that continuously replicates source changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS DMS to configure binary log replication from Aurora MySQL to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, transform schemas, and load the changes into Amazon Redshift Serverless\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS DMS can use MySQL binary logs to capture ongoing changes, and Kinesis Data Streams can receive streaming data. This architecture can support a custom near real-time ingestion pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the company would need to manage DMS configuration, stream capacity, schema handling, transformations, and Redshift loading. That creates more operational overhead than using Aurora zero-ETL integrations directly with Amazon Redshift Serverless.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate AWS DMS tasks that perform schema conversion for each Aurora MySQL database and load the converted changes into Amazon Redshift Serverless\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS DMS can migrate or replicate data between supported sources and targets, and schema conversion can help with migrations between heterogeneous database engines. This can be useful for database migration projects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this workload needs near real-time analytics from Aurora MySQL into Redshift Serverless, not a schema conversion-heavy migration workflow. DMS tasks require more setup and monitoring than the managed zero-ETL integration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.creating.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.creating.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.filtering.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.filtering.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/zero-etl-using.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/zero-etl-using.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-zero-etl-integrations-with-aws-databases-and-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/announcing-zero-etl-integrations-with-aws-databases-and-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:34":{"id":"e:7:34","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 34","correct":[1],"answers":["Apache Spark error logs, CPU usage, and Data Processing Unit (DPU) usage.","Apache Spark job progress bar, Apache Spark job logs, driver logs, and executor logs.","Quantum Processing unit (QPU), system logs, and CPU usage.","Apache Spark error logs, driver logs, and network usage."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team is using AWS Glue for their ETL jobs as part of a larger data analytics solution. They are leveraging CloudWatch Logs Insights to analyze various types of logs from their AWS Glue jobs to optimize job performance and troubleshoot issues.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following combinations of metrics and logs can be directly monitored and analyzed in CloudWatch Logs Insights?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue provides real-time, continuous logging for AWS Glue jobs. You can view real-time \u003cstrong\u003eApache Spark job logs\u003c/strong\u003e in Amazon CloudWatch, including \u003cstrong\u003edriver logs, executor logs, and an Apache Spark job progress ba\u003c/strong\u003er. Viewing real-time logs provides you with a better perspective on the running job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c945b3744a9191051a267bf4eae45f84e18dc55619e0822dc226e9482c0317d1.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you start an AWS Glue job, it sends the real-time logging information to CloudWatch (every 5 seconds and before each executor termination) after the Spark application starts running. You can view the logs on the AWS Glue console or the CloudWatch console dashboard.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe continuous logging feature includes the following capabilities:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cdiv\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Continuous logging with a default filter to reduce high verbosity in the logs\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Continuous logging with no filter\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- A custom script logger to log application-specific messages\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- A console progress bar to track the running status of the current AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eApache Spark job progress bar, Apache Spark job logs, driver logs, and executor logs.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eApache Spark error logs, CPU usage, and Data Processing Unit (DPU) usage\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. CPU and Data Processing Unit (DPU) usage are metrics that cannot be directly monitored in CloudWatch Logs Insights. DPUs are a measure of computation power used by AWS Glue and do not produce logs that can be analyzed by CloudWatch Logs Insights.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eQuantum Processing unit (QPU), system logs, and CPU usage\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. QPU refers to computing resources, specifically within the context of Amazon Braket, which is a service for exploring and experimenting with quantum computing. QPUs are not relevant to the log analysis of AWS Glue jobs. Moreover, CPU usage is monitored through CloudWatch Metrics rather than Logs Insights, which focuses on the analysis of log data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eApache Spark error logs, driver logs, and network usage\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While driver logs can be monitored and analyzed in CloudWatch Logs Insights, Apache Spark error logs and network usage can\u0027t be directly monitored in CloudWatch Logs Insights. Error logs provide information about errors that occurred during the execution of the Spark job, but they are not specific to AWS Glue and are not directly monitored by CloudWatch Logs Insights. Network usage is a system-level metric that is not directly related to the execution of the AWS Glue job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-continuous-logging.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-continuous-logging.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/new-amazon-cloudwatch-log-class-to-cost-effectively-scale-your-aws-glue-workloads/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/new-amazon-cloudwatch-log-class-to-cost-effectively-scale-your-aws-glue-workloads/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-43":{"id":"p:3-43","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 43","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Secrets Manager to store the database credentials, configure automatic rotation with a Lambda rotation function, and update the application to retrieve the current credentials through Secrets Manager API calls\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the database credentials in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store as secure strings, configure an EventBridge scheduled rule to invoke a custom Lambda function every 45 days, and update the application configuration file after each successful rotation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS KMS to generate encryption keys with automatic key rotation, encrypt the database credentials, and store the encrypted values in an Amazon DynamoDB table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the database credentials in an encrypted S3 bucket, rotate the credentials every 45 days by using an S3 Lifecycle rule, and use bucket policies to control access\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Secrets Manager quản lý secret và gọi Lambda rotation để đổi mật khẩu database, kể cả nguồn tại chỗ có kết nối mạng phù hợp. Ứng dụng lấy giá trị hiện tại qua API nên không sửa mã mỗi lần đổi.","Parameter Store kết hợp lịch và cập nhật tệp cấu hình phải tự xây nhiều bước, dễ giữ mật khẩu cũ. Secrets Manager có quy trình rotation chuyên dụng.","Xoay khóa KMS không đồng nghĩa đổi mật khẩu MySQL. Mật khẩu database vẫn như cũ dù khóa mã hóa thay đổi.","S3 Lifecycle chuyển lớp hoặc hết hạn object, không đăng nhập MySQL để xoay mật khẩu. Đây là nhầm giữa vòng đời lưu trữ và bí mật ứng dụng."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA medical research organization stores clinical trial records in an on-premises MySQL database. The organization has built an application that connects to the database to retrieve and update patient study data. The security team requires database credentials to rotate every 45 days to reduce the risk of unauthorized access. The organization wants to avoid application code changes whenever the credentials rotate.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-58":{"id":"p:4-58","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 58","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a daily AWS Glue job that exports the DynamoDB table to Amazon S3, filters metadata records by ingestion timestamp, and writes only active records back into DynamoDB\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd a lifecycle configuration to the DynamoDB table that removes items when the ingestion timestamp is older than the retention period\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable DynamoDB TTL on the table and update the pipeline code to write the correct expiration timestamp into the configured TTL attribute\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a DynamoDB stream and configure a Lambda function to evaluate item changes and delete records that exceed the retention period\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Xuất toàn bảng, lọc rồi ghi lại là một pipeline ETL nặng cho nhu cầu xóa dữ liệu hết hạn. TTL giải quyết trực tiếp mà không cần tái tạo dataset.","DynamoDB không dùng lifecycle configuration kiểu S3 để xóa item theo ingestion timestamp. Cơ chế đúng là TTL với thuộc tính thời điểm hết hạn.","Bật TTL cho một thuộc tính và ghi thời điểm hết hạn dạng số Unix epoch giây vào mỗi item. DynamoDB tự xóa bất đồng bộ các item hết hạn, nên không cần job dọn theo lịch.","Streams chỉ phát sự kiện khi item thay đổi; việc thời gian trôi qua không tự tạo sự kiện mới. Vì vậy Lambda đọc stream không phải cơ chế tự phát hiện mọi item đến hạn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media analytics company is building a new data pipeline that writes job metadata to an Amazon DynamoDB table. Each metadata item includes information such as source file name, ingestion timestamp, processing status, and output location. The data engineer must ensure that metadata items older than a configured retention period are automatically removed from the table with the least configuration effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:6:38":{"id":"e:6:38","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 38","correct":[2],"answers":["Set up an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster. Leverage Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query data in S3, and handle data ingestion via Redshift stored procedures.","Set up an Amazon Athena workgroup. Leverage Apache Spark through Athena for data exploration in S3, granting the team access to workgroup schemas and tables.","Set up an AWS Glue Data Catalog. Define a schema using AWS Glue Schema Registry, and orchestrate data ingestion into Amazon Redshift Serverless via AWS Glue workflows.","Set up AWS Glue Data Catalog, Schema Registry, and Lambda functions with Redshift Data API. Manage data ingestion into Amazon Redshift Serverless via AWS Step Functions."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company\u0027s data engineering team needs to process and analyze terabytes of IoT data stored in Amazon S3. The structure of the data changes periodically as often as the IoT device gets upgraded.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe team is required to find a solution that includes data cataloging and schema management to ensure consistent access and analysis capabilities.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich option satisfies the mentioned requirements in the MOST cost-effective manner?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e is a service that facilitates the extraction, transformation, and loading (ETL) of data to prepare it for analytics. It is fully managed and offers visual workflows along with a Python library to easily perform data transformations at scale.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe \u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Data Catalog\u003c/strong\u003e is a centralized metadata repository that stores table definitions, physical locations of data, and business attributes and tracks change over time. It provides a unified interface to query metadata across data sources. When AWS Glue ETL jobs run, they use the data catalog to understand data formats and ensure correct transformations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe \u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Schema Registry\u003c/strong\u003e tracks schema changes to tables in the data catalog. It allows schemas to evolve over time while maintaining compatibility with existing analytics, queries, and applications. This enables schema-on-read for analytics tools like Athena, EMR, and Redshift Spectrum.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3c3c3d7f0fbc04dd47e19feba0b79f38397c5efc94d5a3a53fd16a1a0dc8784c.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshift Serverless\u003c/strong\u003e is a cloud-based, fully managed data warehouse service that allows you to easily analyze your data using standard SQL and existing BI tools. It\u0027s designed to be fast and cost-effective, making it ideal for petabyte-scale data analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo meet the requirements for cost-effective data cataloging, schema management, and analytics, the recommended solution involves utilizing the AWS Glue Data Catalog for organizing and querying vast amounts of dynamic IoT data stored in S3, ensuring consistent access for analysis. The AWS Glue Schema Registry is employed to manage evolving schemas of IoT data in a version-controlled manner during device upgrades. AWS Glue workflows orchestrate the periodic ingestion of data from S3 into Amazon Redshift Serverless, enabling scalable analytics capabilities without the need for provisioning and managing dedicated Redshift clusters. With Redshift Serverless, customers benefit from a pay-for-what-you-use model, which is more cost-effective for handling large and fluctuating IoT datasets. This entire serverless solution leverages native AWS services, minimizing both cost and complexity by eliminating the need for additional infrastructure setup and maintenance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an AWS Glue Data Catalog. Define a schema using AWS Glue Schema Registry, and orchestrate data ingestion into Amazon Redshift Serverless via AWS Glue workflows.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster. Leverage Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query data in S3, and handle data ingestion via Redshift stored procedures\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This option involves unnecessary complexity and higher costs associated with managing a provisioned cluster and does not leverage the serverless capabilities that can lead to cost savings.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an Amazon Athena workgroup. Leverage Apache Spark through Athena for data exploration in S3, granting the team access to workgroup schemas and tables\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While Athena provides serverless querying capabilities, this option lacks the comprehensive data management and schema consistency features provided by AWS Glue Schema Registry and Data Catalog, which are essential for handling evolving data structures efficiently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up AWS Glue Data Catalog, Schema Registry, and Lambda functions with Redshift Data API. Manage data ingestion into Amazon Redshift Serverless via AWS Step Functions\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This option introduces unnecessary components like AWS Lambda functions for data ingestion into Amazon Redshift Serverless and AWS Step Functions for orchestration. While these services can be used, they require custom code development, maintenance, and incur additional costs for execution and data transfer.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/query-editor-v2-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/query-editor-v2-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/creating-redshift-connection.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/creating-redshift-connection.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue and Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-11":{"id":"p:3-11","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 11","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eMove objects to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Flexible Archive after three years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMove objects to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after three years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMove objects to S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA) after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after three years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMove objects to S3 Intelligent-Tiering after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after three years\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Glacier Flexible Retrieval có thể truy xuất nhanh hơn, nhưng đề không yêu cầu tốc độ khôi phục cao cho dữ liệu rất cũ. Deep Archive kinh tế hơn theo lời giải.","Giữ Standard khi đọc nhiều, chuyển Standard-IA sau sáu tháng khi ít đọc, rồi Deep Archive sau ba năm khi gần như lưu trữ. Phù hợp mẫu truy cập đã biết.","One Zone-IA đánh đổi dự phòng đa AZ. Đề không cho phép giảm độ bền vùng sẵn sàng của dữ liệu như phương án này.","Intelligent-Tiering hữu ích khi truy cập khó đoán. Ở đây các giai đoạn đã rõ, Lifecycle theo lớp cố định là phương án bộ đề ưu tiên."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses the S3 Standard storage class for all its data storage needs on Amazon S3. A data engineer analyzed the data access patterns and discovered that for the first six months, data files are accessed multiple times daily. From six months to three years, the data files are accessed about once or twice a month. After three years, each file is accessed only once or twice annually. The data engineer is tasked with creating S3 Lifecycle policies to develop new data storage rules, ensuring high availability while seeking cost-effectiveness.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution will best meet these needs?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:26":{"id":"e:2:26","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 26","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the original files in S3 Standard-Infrequent Access immediately after upload, keep the files for 75 days, and configure an S3 Lifecycle rule to expire the files when the retention period ends\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the original files in S3 Standard for 75 days, transition the files to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and then configure expiration after archival storage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the original files in S3 Intelligent-Tiering for 75 days, enable bucket versioning, and configure an S3 Lifecycle rule to expire the files after the retention period\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the original files in S3 Standard for 75 days and configure an S3 Lifecycle rule to expire the current versions of the files after the retention period\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA video production company uploads large raw footage files to an Amazon S3 bucket in the us-west-2 Region for transcoding and quality checks. After the transcoding workflow finishes, the company must retain the original raw files for 75 days in case editors need to reprocess any footage. After 75 days, the company no longer needs the originals and wants to delete them to reduce storage costs. The processed videos are stored in a separate S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich S3 Lifecycle configuration will meet these requirements for the original files MOST cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the original files in S3 Standard for 75 days and configure an S3 Lifecycle rule to expire the current versions of the files after the retention period\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Lifecycle can expire current object versions after a specified number of days from object creation. The company can create a Lifecycle rule for the raw footage bucket or for a specific prefix that stores original files and set the expiration action to 75 days.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution keeps the original files available in S3 Standard throughout the reprocessing window. S3 Standard is appropriate when the company might need immediate access to the originals during the first 75 days after upload.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option is the most cost-effective fit because the company does not need the original files after the retention period. Expiring the files directly avoids unnecessary transition requests, minimum storage duration charges, version storage, Object Lock configuration, or long-term archival storage for files that the company plans to delete.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the original files in S3 Intelligent-Tiering for 75 days, enable bucket versioning, and configure an S3 Lifecycle rule to expire the files after the retention period\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Intelligent-Tiering can optimize storage costs when access patterns are unknown or change over time. However, it also has monitoring and automation considerations, and the company has a clear fixed retention period for the original files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eEnabling versioning also can increase storage consumption because noncurrent versions may remain after updates or deletes unless the Lifecycle configuration also manages noncurrent versions. The requirement does not need version history for the raw originals.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the original files in S3 Standard-Infrequent Access immediately after upload, keep the files for 75 days, and configure an S3 Lifecycle rule to expire the files when the retention period ends\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Standard-Infrequent Access can reduce storage cost for infrequently accessed objects, but it has a minimum storage duration of 30 days and retrieval charges. It is best when objects remain stored long enough and are accessed infrequently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option transitions the files immediately even though editors might need to reprocess them during the retention period. For a short, fixed retention window with possible immediate reprocessing needs, direct expiration from S3 Standard is the simpler and safer choice.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the original files in S3 Standard for 75 days, transition the files to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and then configure expiration after archival storage\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval is designed for archive data that does not require immediate access. It also has a minimum storage duration of 90 days, so transitioning the objects around the end of a 75-day retention window can introduce unnecessary minimum-duration charges.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company does not need long-term storage after the retention period. It should expire the original files directly instead of moving them to an archive class before deletion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-expire-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-expire-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/how-to-set-lifecycle-configuration-intro.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/how-to-set-lifecycle-configuration-intro.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/intro-lifecycle-rules.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/intro-lifecycle-rules.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/glacier-storage-classes.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/glacier-storage-classes.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/expiring-amazon-s3-objects-based-on-last-accessed-date-to-decrease-costs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/expiring-amazon-s3-objects-based-on-last-accessed-date-to-decrease-costs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-9":{"id":"p:3-9","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 9","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eChange the distribution style to ALL for the cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpdate the sort key to equal distribution key\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure the sort key to be the column most used in JOIN of tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe distribution key should be changed to the table column that has the largest dimension\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["ALL sao chép toàn bảng tới các node, tốn lưu trữ và không phải cách sửa phân phối khóa lệch cho bảng lớn.","Sort key quyết định thứ tự lưu trong node, không tự phân phối lại dữ liệu giữa các node.","Đổi sort key theo cột JOIN chưa sửa nguyên nhân một node chứa phần lớn dữ liệu. Cần điều chỉnh distribution key.","Ý chính là chọn distribution key phân phối dữ liệu đều hơn, thường có đủ nhiều giá trị phân biệt và tránh giá trị nóng. Cụm từ largest dimension trong đáp án gốc khá mơ hồ; không nên hiểu là chỉ chọn cột có kích thước byte lớn nhất."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster as its database service and the cluster uses key distribution. While running the maintenance tasks on the cluster, a data engineer noticed that of the four ra3 cluster nodes, one node had a CPU load of over 90% most of the time, while the other three nodes were averaging around 10%. The company has tasked the data engineer to balance the load more evenly across all four compute nodes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat changes are needed if provisioning additional nodes is not an option?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:64":{"id":"e:1:64","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 64","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUtilize Amazon EMR to detect the schema including any ongoing changes. Extract, transform, and load the data into the S3 bucket by creating the ETL pipeline in Apache Spark\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUtilize PySpark to detect the schema including any ongoing changes. Extract, transform, and load the data into the S3 bucket by creating the ETL pipeline in AWS Lambda\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUtilize AWS Glue to detect the schema including any ongoing changes. Extract, transform, and load the data into the S3 bucket by creating the ETL pipeline in Apache Spark\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUtilize Redshift spectrum to detect the schema including any ongoing changes. Extract, transform, and load the data into the S3 bucket by creating a stored procedure in Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company regularly extracts about 2 TB of data daily from various data sources - including MySQL, MSSQL Server, Oracle, Vertica, and Teradata Vantage. Some of these sources feature undefined or frequently changing data schemas. A data engineer is tasked with implementing a solution that can automatically detect the schema of these data sources and perform data extraction, transformation, and loading to an Amazon S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution would meet these needs while minimizing operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize AWS Glue to detect the schema including any ongoing changes. Extract, transform, and load the data into the S3 bucket by creating the ETL pipeline in Apache Spark\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn many use cases, the data teams responsible for building the data pipeline don’t have any control of the source schema, and they need to build a solution to identify changes in the source schema in order to be able to build the process or automation around it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor example, assume you’re receiving claim files from different external partners in the form of flat files, and you’ve built a solution to process claims based on these files. However, because these files were sent by external partners, you don’t have much control over the schema and data format. For example, columns such as customer_id and claim_id were changed to customerid and claimid by one partner, and another partner added new columns such as customer_age and earning and kept the rest of the columns the same. You need to identify such changes in advance so you can edit the ETL job to accommodate the changes, such as changing the column name or adding new columns to process the claims.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can capture these schema changes in your data source using an AWS Glue crawler. You can use an AWS Glue crawler to extract the metadata from data in an S3 bucket. Then you can use an AWS Glue ETL job to extract the changes in the schema to the AWS Glue Data Catalog. You can develop the code for the AWS Glue ETL job using Apache Spark.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/bee038cca2ecef41a33885462df2f0f9a3c743d130c227bb67d7188f4d4c274d.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/bee038cca2ecef41a33885462df2f0f9a3c743d130c227bb67d7188f4d4c274d.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/identify-source-schema-changes-using-aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/identify-source-schema-changes-using-aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/355f1463518937d9f3b150ead496b3a33980343b834452ffe5225cc411a4a279.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/355f1463518937d9f3b150ead496b3a33980343b834452ffe5225cc411a4a279.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/identify-source-schema-changes-using-aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/identify-source-schema-changes-using-aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon EMR to detect the schema including any ongoing changes. Extract, transform, and load the data into the S3 bucket by creating the ETL pipeline in Apache Spark\u003c/strong\u003e - You will have to write significant code using Apache Spark in Amazon EMR to be able to detect the schema including any ongoing changes. So, this option is not the best fit for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize PySpark to detect the schema including any ongoing changes. Extract, transform, and load the data into the S3 bucket by creating the ETL pipeline in AWS Lambda\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Lambda has a maximum execution time (timeout) of 15 minutes which is not sufficient to run an ETL pipeline to process 2 TB of data daily. As such, AWS Lambda is not designed to run big data ETL pipelines. So, this option just acts as a distractor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Redshift spectrum to detect the schema including any ongoing changes. Extract, transform, and load the data into the S3 bucket by creating a stored procedure in Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - You can define an Amazon Redshift stored procedure using the PostgreSQL procedural language PL/pgSQL to perform a set of SQL queries and logical operations. The procedure is stored in the database and available for any user with sufficient database privileges. You cannot use a stored procedure in Amazon Redshift to perform ETL operations for the given use case. This option just acts as a distractor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/identify-source-schema-changes-using-aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/identify-source-schema-changes-using-aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/stored-procedure-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/stored-procedure-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-7":{"id":"p:3-7","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 7","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse native Amazon EKS Horizontal Pod Autoscaler with Kubernetes Metrics Server to scale self-managed Spark driver and executor pods for streaming workloads\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun Apache Spark on Amazon EKS with AWS Auto Scaling groups and custom scaling policies based only on Kubernetes Metrics Server data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EMR on EKS with Kubernetes Event-Driven Autoscaling to scale Spark streaming applications based on event-source demand\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EMR on EKS with Kubernetes Cluster Autoscaler to scale Spark applications only from Kubernetes Metrics Server resource metrics\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["HPA theo CPU/RAM và Spark tự quản lý chưa đáp ứng rõ việc mở rộng theo sự kiện nguồn, đồng thời tăng công quản lý Spark.","Auto Scaling dựa trên metric tài nguyên không trực tiếp phản ánh nhu cầu luồng như backlog. Còn phải vận hành Spark riêng.","EMR on EKS tận dụng Kubernetes hiện có; KEDA mở rộng theo tín hiệu nguồn sự kiện. Phù hợp tăng/giảm tài nguyên theo tải streaming.","Cluster Autoscaler mở rộng node theo nhu cầu pod, không tự mở rộng ứng dụng Spark theo lượng sự kiện nguồn như KEDA."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media analytics company needs to process streaming clickstream data by using Apache Spark in a Kubernetes environment. The company already runs shared platform services on Amazon EKS and wants the new streaming workload to integrate with that existing Kubernetes infrastructure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe solution must support event-driven scaling when streaming volume increases and must reduce unused compute capacity when traffic decreases. The company wants to optimize resource utilization while minimizing the operational work required to run Spark on Kubernetes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-28":{"id":"p:3-28","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 28","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003ePartition the data by pet type\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePartition the data by customer\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMigrate the data to Redshift tables for a more cost-efficient analysis\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePartition the data by store region\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Phân tích tập trung vào từng loại thú cưng. Partition theo pet type cho Athena bỏ qua dữ liệu các loại không được hỏi, giảm byte quét.","Partition theo khách hàng không khớp điều kiện lọc chính là loại thú cưng và dễ tạo quá nhiều partition nhỏ.","Di chuyển sang Redshift thêm pipeline và lưu trữ/tính toán. Chưa cần thiết khi có thể tối ưu trực tiếp layout S3 cho Athena.","Region cửa hàng không phải chiều lọc được đề nhấn mạnh. Không giúp giảm lượng quét tốt bằng phân vùng theo pet type."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company operates a chain of pet-care shops and it stores all of its data in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company uses Amazon Athena to analyze customer preferences to launch new products for the different types of pets. The company\u0027s data is of the order of tens of terabytes and the company is looking at reducing the costs of running these queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat do you recommend?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-53":{"id":"p:2-53","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 53","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Config to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon CloudWatch to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS CloudTrail to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Systems Manager to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["AWS Config ghi cấu hình và lịch sử thay đổi tài nguyên, đồng thời đánh giá theo quy tắc tuân thủ. Đúng trọng tâm cấu hình của đề.","CloudWatch tập trung log, metric, alarm và sức khỏe vận hành. Không thay thế lịch sử cấu hình tài nguyên của AWS Config.","CloudTrail trả lời ai đã gọi API gì, khi nào. Config theo dõi trạng thái/cấu hình tài nguyên và việc đáp ứng quy tắc; hai dịch vụ bổ sung nhau.","Systems Manager quản lý vận hành nhiều loại tài nguyên, nhưng không phải dịch vụ chuyên ghi lịch sử và đánh giá cấu hình mà câu hỏi hướng tới."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company has recently migrated from on-premises infrastructure to AWS Cloud. The data engineering team wants to implement a solution that allows all resource configurations to be reviewed and make sure that they meet compliance guidelines. Also, the solution should be able to offer the capability to look into the resource configuration history across the application stack.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions would you recommend to the team?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-21":{"id":"p:4-21","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 21","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EFS file system, enable audit logging for the Redshift cluster, and configure Redshift to write audit logs to the file system\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon S3 bucket, enable Redshift database audit logging and user activity logging, and configure the cluster to deliver logs to the bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Config rules to evaluate Redshift cluster configuration changes, and store compliance evaluation results as the complete database audit trail\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EBS volume, enable audit logging for the Redshift cluster, and configure Redshift to store connection and user activity logs on the volume\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["EFS là hệ thống tệp dùng chung nhưng không phải đích được hỗ trợ cho Redshift audit logging như mô tả. Không thể cấu hình cluster xuất audit log trực tiếp vào EFS.","Với Redshift provisioned, bật database audit logging, bật user activity logging và cấu hình ghi log vào S3. Như vậy có nhật ký ngoài kho dữ liệu để lưu lâu dài và phục vụ kiểm toán.","AWS Config chỉ đánh giá thay đổi cấu hình và tuân thủ tài nguyên. Kết quả đó không bao gồm đầy đủ kết nối database, thay đổi người dùng và SQL activity.","Bạn không gắn EBS riêng vào Redshift để làm đích audit log. S3 là đích phù hợp trong các lựa chọn và tách được log khỏi data warehouse."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company runs an Amazon Redshift provisioned data warehouse for regulated reporting. Auditors require the company to retain records of database connection attempts, user changes, and SQL activity that users perform in the warehouse.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer must configure the Redshift environment so the audit logs are stored outside the data warehouse for compliance review and long-term retention.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-19":{"id":"p:1-19","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 19","correct":[1,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDevelop the leaderboard using DynamoDB as it meets the in-memory, high availability, low latency requirements\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop the leaderboard using ElastiCache Redis as it meets the in-memory, high availability, low latency requirements\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop the leaderboard using DynamoDB with DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) as it meets the in-memory, high availability, and low latency requirements\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop the leaderboard using AWS Neptune as it meets the in-memory, high availability, low latency requirements\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop the leaderboard using RDS Aurora as it meets the in-memory, high availability, low latency requirements\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["DynamoDB có độ trễ thấp nhưng bản thân nó không phải kho dữ liệu in-memory. Điều kiện quyết định ở đây là cần lớp lưu trữ hoặc đệm trong bộ nhớ.","Redis trong ElastiCache là kho in-memory, phù hợp với leaderboard có truy cập nhanh và cập nhật thời gian thực. Có thể triển khai tính sẵn sàng cao theo cấu hình phù hợp.","DAX bổ sung bộ nhớ đệm in-memory cho DynamoDB để tăng tốc các lần đọc phù hợp. Kết hợp này đáp ứng yêu cầu có lớp dữ liệu trong bộ nhớ.","Neptune là cơ sở dữ liệu đồ thị. Nó không phải giải pháp in-memory leaderboard được yêu cầu trong câu này.","Aurora là cơ sở dữ liệu quan hệ có lưu trữ bền vững. Nó không phải kho in-memory chuyên cho trường hợp leaderboard này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company runs multiple gaming platforms that need to store game state, player data, session history, and leaderboards. The company is looking to move to AWS Cloud to scale reliably to millions of concurrent users and requests while ensuring consistently low latency measured in single-digit milliseconds. The data engineering team at the company is evaluating multiple in-memory data stores with the ability to power its on-demand, live leaderboard. The company\u0027s leaderboard requires high availability, low latency, and real-time processing to deliver customizable user data for the community of its users.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions can be used to address the given requirements? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:19":{"id":"e:4:19","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 19","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure the Lambda functions to run in the company\u0027s VPC so the functions can use private networking for S3 and AWS Glue access\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the memory allocation of the Lambda functions to 512 MB so the functions receive more proportional CPU capacity\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a second set of Lambda functions with the same 128 MB memory setting and distribute S3 events across both function groups by using separate event notification prefixes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the Maximum Retry Attempts setting for the Lambda functions so failed invocations have more opportunities to complete successfully\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media streaming company stores subscriber activity files in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company runs an analysis pipeline whenever a subscriber cancels a premium plan. The pipeline uses Lambda functions that respond to S3 events, clean the activity data, and start an AWS Glue workflow for downstream processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe Lambda functions are configured with 128 MB of memory, 512 MB of ephemeral storage, and a timeout of 20 seconds. The functions complete successfully, but monitoring shows that CPU usage is frequently close to 100 percent, which slows the pipeline. The company wants to improve function performance and reduce total pipeline runtime.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the memory allocation of the Lambda functions to 512 MB so the functions receive more proportional CPU capacity\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lambda allocates CPU power in proportion to the amount of memory configured for a function. When a function is CPU-bound and CPU usage is frequently near 100 percent, increasing memory gives the function more compute capacity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn this scenario, the Lambda functions are already completing successfully. The problem is slow performance caused by CPU saturation, not failures, lack of timeout, or missing network access. Increasing memory from 128 MB to 512 MB can reduce execution duration because the functions receive more CPU resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution directly addresses the bottleneck and can reduce the total runtime of the pipeline. The company should test the new memory setting and monitor duration, cost, and performance, but memory tuning is the correct Lambda configuration change for CPU-bound workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a second set of Lambda functions with the same 128 MB memory setting and distribute S3 events across both function groups by using separate event notification prefixes\u003c/strong\u003e - Splitting events across multiple function groups can increase concurrency or separate processing paths when the workload is limited by event distribution. This can be useful for some high-throughput event designs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, each individual function would still run with only 128 MB of memory and the same limited proportional CPU capacity. The per-invocation CPU bottleneck remains, so this option does not directly improve the slow data-cleaning logic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure the Lambda functions to run in the company\u0027s VPC so the functions can use private networking for S3 and AWS Glue access\u003c/strong\u003e - Configuring Lambda functions to run in a VPC can be necessary when functions must access private resources such as databases or internal services. It can also support private connectivity patterns through VPC endpoints.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, VPC configuration does not increase CPU allocation for the functions. The bottleneck is CPU saturation during processing, so moving the functions into a VPC does not solve the performance problem.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the Maximum Retry Attempts setting for the Lambda functions so failed invocations have more opportunities to complete successfully\u003c/strong\u003e - Increasing Maximum Retry Attempts can help when asynchronous invocations fail intermittently. Retries can give failed events another chance to process successfully.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the functions are not failing. They complete successfully but slowly. Adding retries does not improve CPU capacity and can add more duplicate work if errors occur later.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-memory.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-memory.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/troubleshooting-configuration.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/troubleshooting-configuration.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/gettingstarted-limits.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/gettingstarted-limits.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/operating-lambda-performance-optimization-part-2/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/operating-lambda-performance-optimization-part-2/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/understanding-techniques-to-reduce-aws-lambda-costs-in-serverless-applications/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/understanding-techniques-to-reduce-aws-lambda-costs-in-serverless-applications/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:19":{"id":"e:2:19","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 19","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lambda function for the transformation, configure S3 Event Notifications to invoke the function on object creation, and publish the output to an SNS topic with an email subscription\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDeploy an EMR cluster with EMRFS to read the S3 files, schedule transformation steps, write output to another S3 bucket, and configure S3 notifications to an SNS topic\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Glue job that runs every hour, scans the S3 bucket for new report files, transforms matching files, and writes email-ready output to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun an ECS task that polls the S3 bucket for new report files, executes the transformation code on a schedule, and sends email messages through operating system commands\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer needs to run a lightweight transformation whenever a user uploads a report file to an Amazon S3 bucket. Each transformation completes in less than 45 seconds. After the transformation finishes, the data engineer must receive the output in an email message. Users upload approximately one report file every hour throughout the day.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements in the MOST operationally efficient way?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Lambda function for the transformation, configure S3 Event Notifications to invoke the function on object creation, and publish the output to an SNS topic with an email subscription\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lambda is the best fit because the transformation runs for less than 45 seconds and occurs only about once per hour. Lambda can run the transformation code without requiring the company to provision or manage servers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Event Notifications can invoke the Lambda function automatically when a new object is created in the bucket. This event-driven approach avoids polling and starts the transformation only when a user uploads a file.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon SNS can deliver messages to email subscribers. The Lambda function can publish the transformed output to an SNS standard topic, and the data engineer can subscribe an email address to receive the result. This creates a simple serverless workflow with minimal operational effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun an ECS task that polls the S3 bucket for new report files, executes the transformation code on a schedule, and sends email messages through operating system commands\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon ECS can run containerized transformation code, and scheduled tasks can process files in Amazon S3. This can be useful when workloads need custom runtime packaging or long-running containers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe issue with this approach is that polling adds unnecessary work for a low-volume event-driven workload. The team must manage task definitions, scheduling, container images, polling logic, and email commands when S3 can invoke Lambda directly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Glue job that runs every hour, scans the S3 bucket for new report files, transforms matching files, and writes email-ready output to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue can run ETL jobs over S3 data and can be appropriate for larger transformations, data preparation, and scheduled processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that the job would scan for new files on a schedule instead of reacting to each upload event. For a sub-minute transformation, Lambda with S3 Event Notifications is simpler and more operationally efficient.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy an EMR cluster with EMRFS to read the S3 files, schedule transformation steps, write output to another S3 bucket, and configure S3 notifications to an SNS topic\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR can process large-scale distributed workloads and can access S3 data through EMRFS. It is useful for Spark, Hive, Presto, and other big data frameworks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not match the workload size or duration. A short transformation that runs once per hour does not justify provisioning and operating an EMR cluster, and S3 notifications to SNS alone would not run the transformation code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-s3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-s3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventNotifications.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventNotifications.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-event-types-and-destinations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-event-types-and-destinations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-email-notifications.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-email-notifications.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/s3-event-notification/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/s3-event-notification/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:1":{"id":"e:2:1","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 1","correct":[2,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Data Exchange to globally share data and control access\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Glue DataBrew integration with AWS Glue Studio to orchestrate data sharing and control access\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChoose Amazon S3 as the data storage service and leverage Amazon Athena for data analysis\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS DataSync to globally share data and control access\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Lake Formation to centrally govern, secure, and globally share data\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company is planning to establish a data mesh architecture that facilitates centralized data governance, analysis, and access control. The company has opted to utilize AWS Glue for managing data catalogs and conducting extract, transform, and load (ETL) operations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat combination of AWS services would be suitable to implement this data mesh effectively? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChoose Amazon S3 as the data storage service and leverage Amazon Athena for data analysis\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage AWS Lake Formation to centrally govern, secure, and globally share data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data mesh architecture empowers business units (organized into domains) to have high ownership and autonomy for the technologies they use while providing technology that enforces data security policies both within and between domains through data sharing. Data consumers request access to these data products, which are approved by producer owners within a framework that provides decentralized governance, but centralized monitoring and auditing of the data sharing process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation helps you centrally govern, secure, and globally share data for analytics and machine learning. With Lake Formation, you can manage fine-grained access control for your data lake data on Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) and its metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eLake Formation tag-based access control (TBAC) solves the role explosion problem by allowing data stewards to create LF-tags (based on their business needs) that are attached to resources. You can create policies on a smaller number of logical tags instead of specifying policies on named resources. LF-tags enable you to categorize and explore data based on taxonomies, which reduces policy complexity and scales permissions management. You can create and manage policies with tens of logical tags instead of thousands of resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eLake Formation TBAC decouples policy creation from resource creation, which helps data stewards manage permissions on many databases, tables, and columns by removing the need to update policies every time a new resource is added to the data lake. Finally, TBAC allows you to create policies even before the resources come into existence. All you have to do is tag the resource with the right LF-tag to make sure existing policies manage it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHere is a deep-dive into data mesh implementation using Lake Formation with a case study:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage AWS DataSync to globally share data and control access\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS DataSync is an online data movement and discovery service that simplifies and accelerates data migrations to AWS as well as moving data to and from on-premises storage, edge locations, other cloud providers, and AWS Storage services. This option acts as a distractor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage AWS Data Exchange to globally share data and control access\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Data Exchange is a service that makes it easy for customers to find, subscribe to, and use third-party data in the AWS Cloud. This option acts as a distractor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage AWS Glue DataBrew integration with AWS Glue Studio to orchestrate data sharing and control access\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue DataBrew is a fully managed visual data preparation service for cleaning, normalizing, and transforming data. AWS Glue DataBrew is integrated with AWS Glue Studio, so you can orchestrate DataBrew recipes within your AWS Glue ETL jobs and workflows. You cannot use these services to implement a data mesh.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/what-is/data-mesh/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/what-is/data-mesh/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-22":{"id":"p:1-22","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 22","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable management events for the Amazon SNS topics in CloudTrail and use CloudTrail Lake SQL queries to analyze the audit records\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable data events for the Amazon SNS topics in the existing CloudTrail Lake event data store and use CloudTrail Lake SQL queries to analyze the audit records\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable data events for the Amazon SNS topics in CloudTrail, create an AWS Glue Data Catalog table for the event data, and query the records by using Amazon Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon SNS delivery status logging for all topics, send the delivery logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, create a CloudWatch Logs Insights query to search for Publish and PublishBatch activity, and share the saved query with the application team and the security team\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Management events ghi hoạt động quản lý tài nguyên. Publish và PublishBatch là thao tác dữ liệu của SNS, nên chọn loại sự kiện này sẽ thiếu nhật ký cần kiểm toán.","Bật SNS data events trong event data store CloudTrail Lake hiện có để ghi hoạt động Publish/PublishBatch. Dùng SQL của CloudTrail Lake truy vấn trực tiếp, không cần thêm hệ thống phân tích.","Data events là đúng loại, nhưng thêm Glue Catalog và Athena tạo công cấu hình không cần thiết khi CloudTrail Lake đã có sẵn khả năng truy vấn.","Delivery status logging phản ánh kết quả gửi thông điệp tới đích. Nó không thay thế bản ghi CloudTrail về người gọi và các hành động API Publish/PublishBatch."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media technology company runs several applications on AWS. The applications publish user activity messages to multiple Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topics in the ca-central-1 Region. The security team must audit every Publish and PublishBatch API action across all SNS topics. The application team and the security team also need to query the audit records. The company already has an AWS CloudTrail Lake event data store for centralized event analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-17":{"id":"p:4-17","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 17","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUpdate Athena query settings to enable Apache Spark\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eActivate Apache Spark in the underlying Amazon Athena Engine version\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Athena Query Editor to access Apache Spark from Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Spark-enabled Athena workgroup\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Thiết lập cho một truy vấn SQL không chuyển workgroup sang Spark. Muốn dùng Spark trong Athena, cần tạo workgroup có loại xử lý phù hợp.","Chọn phiên bản SQL engine không đồng nghĩa bật Spark. Spark được cấu hình khi tạo workgroup dành cho Spark.","Athena Query Editor phục vụ truy vấn SQL. Spark dùng môi trường notebook trong workgroup hỗ trợ Spark, nên mở Query Editor thôi là chưa đủ.","Tạo workgroup hỗ trợ Apache Spark, sau đó dùng notebook để chạy mã Spark. Workgroup này cung cấp môi trường xử lý phù hợp thay cho luồng SQL CTAS trước đây."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has developed Athena SQL queries for Extract, Transform, and Load (ETL) tasks by using Create Table As Select (CTAS). The company now needs to replace SQL with Apache Spark for its new analytics solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich configuration changes are needed to begin using Apache Spark on Amazon Athena ?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:23":{"id":"e:3:23","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 23","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the telemetry data in an Amazon S3 bucket in Apache Parquet format and configure query access by using Amazon Redshift Spectrum\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the telemetry data in an Amazon S3 bucket in JSON format and configure auto-copy ingestion from Amazon S3 into the Redshift cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the telemetry data directly in Amazon Redshift managed storage, compress the tables with automatic compression encodings, and resize the cluster as historical data volume increases\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the telemetry data in an Amazon S3 bucket in Apache Parquet format and configure auto-copy ingestion from Amazon S3 into the Redshift cluster\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA utilities company collects a large volume of smart meter telemetry from thousands of field devices. The company must retain the telemetry data indefinitely for historical analysis and regulatory reporting. Analysts use an Amazon Redshift cluster to analyze the data, and the company wants the most cost-effective storage and query architecture.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the telemetry data in an Amazon S3 bucket in Apache Parquet format and configure query access by using Amazon Redshift Spectrum\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift Spectrum allows Amazon Redshift users to query data directly from Amazon S3. This lets the company keep indefinitely retained IoT telemetry in Amazon S3 instead of loading all historical data into Redshift managed storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eApache Parquet is a columnar storage format. When analytical queries read only selected columns, Parquet can reduce the amount of data that Redshift Spectrum scans. This improves query efficiency and helps lower query cost compared with row-based formats such as JSON or CSV.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution is the most cost-effective fit because it combines low-cost, durable Amazon S3 storage with Redshift-based analytics access. The company can retain data indefinitely in S3 and use Redshift Spectrum to query the data only when analysts need it, instead of continuously loading and storing all data inside the Redshift cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the telemetry data directly in Amazon Redshift managed storage, compress the tables with automatic compression encodings, and resize the cluster as historical data volume increases\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift managed storage can store and analyze large datasets, and compression encodings can reduce table storage requirements. Resizing can also help the cluster handle larger workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, storing all indefinite historical telemetry directly in Redshift can be more expensive than retaining the data in Amazon S3. This option also ties long-term storage growth to the Redshift environment instead of separating low-cost storage from query compute.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the telemetry data in an Amazon S3 bucket in Apache Parquet format and configure auto-copy ingestion from Amazon S3 into the Redshift cluster\u003c/strong\u003e - Apache Parquet is an efficient format for analytical workloads, and Amazon Redshift auto-copy can automate loading files from Amazon S3 into Redshift. However, this option loads the telemetry data into the Redshift cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company needs to retain a large amount of IoT sensor data indefinitely. Loading all historical data into Redshift managed storage can increase storage costs compared with keeping the data in Amazon S3 and querying it through Redshift Spectrum.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the telemetry data in an Amazon S3 bucket in JSON format and configure auto-copy ingestion from Amazon S3 into the Redshift cluster\u003c/strong\u003e - Auto-copy ingestion can help automate data loading from Amazon S3 into Amazon Redshift. This can be useful when the data must become part of local Redshift tables for frequent, low-latency analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option uses JSON and loads the data into the cluster. It combines a less efficient analytical file format with higher long-term Redshift storage usage, which is not the most cost-effective approach for indefinite retention.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-spectrum-data-files.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-spectrum-data-files.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-spectrum-external-performance.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-spectrum-external-performance.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/gsg/data-lake.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/gsg/data-lake.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/10-best-practices-for-amazon-redshift-spectrum/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/10-best-practices-for-amazon-redshift-spectrum/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:45":{"id":"e:4:45","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 45","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eReferentialIntegrity \"warehouse_id,zone_id\" \"reference.{ref_warehouse_id,ref_zone_id}\" = 1.0\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDatasetMatch \"reference\" \"warehouse_id-\u0026gt;ref_warehouse_id, zone_id-\u0026gt;ref_zone_id\" = 1.0\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eReferentialIntegrity \"warehouse_id,zone_id\" \"reference.{ref_warehouse_id,ref_zone_id}\" = 100\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eReferentialIntegrity \"reference.{ref_warehouse_id,ref_zone_id}\" \"warehouse_id,zone_id\" = 1.0\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer has two AWS Glue Data Quality datasets that contain fulfillment information for multiple warehouse locations and operating zones. One dataset is named primary, and the other dataset is named reference.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineer needs to validate that the specific paired values in the warehouse_id and zone_id columns of the primary dataset match the corresponding paired values in the ref_warehouse_id and ref_zone_id columns of the reference dataset. The engineer wants to define this validation by using Data Quality Definition Language rules in an AWS Glue Data Quality job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich rule will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferentialIntegrity \"warehouse_id,zone_id\" \"reference.{ref_warehouse_id,ref_zone_id}\" = 1.0\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe ReferentialIntegrity rule is the correct DQDL rule type when the data engineer needs to compare values in one or more columns from a primary dataset against corresponding values in a reference dataset. In this scenario, the validation must compare the paired warehouse_id and zone_id values from the primary dataset with the paired ref_warehouse_id and ref_zone_id values from the reference dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe syntax places the primary dataset columns first. The reference dataset columns appear second in the format reference_dataset_alias.{reference_column_1,reference_column_2}. This structure tells AWS Glue Data Quality which columns from the primary dataset must be validated against which columns in the reference dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe expression = 1.0 requires a complete match according to the rule response. This means the validation succeeds only when all relevant paired values in the primary dataset satisfy the referential integrity condition against the reference dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0a6870b5feb706d0351451ad035dd701f1ca02377b23b5a1ba588d2cae62f8ca.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0a6870b5feb706d0351451ad035dd701f1ca02377b23b5a1ba588d2cae62f8ca.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types-ReferentialIntegrity.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types-ReferentialIntegrity.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDatasetMatch \"reference\" \"warehouse_id-\u0026gt;ref_warehouse_id, zone_id-\u0026gt;ref_zone_id\" = 1.0\u003c/strong\u003e - DatasetMatch compares two datasets to determine whether the datasets are synchronized based on mapped columns. It can be useful when the goal is to compare records across two datasets more broadly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that this question asks for referential integrity between a specific set of paired column values in the primary dataset and the corresponding values in the reference dataset. The AWS Glue DQDL rule that directly represents this relationship is ReferentialIntegrity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferentialIntegrity \"warehouse_id,zone_id\" \"reference.{ref_warehouse_id,ref_zone_id}\" = 100\u003c/strong\u003e - This option uses the ReferentialIntegrity rule type and correctly identifies the primary columns and reference dataset columns. The column mapping structure is aligned with the intended validation pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option fails because the expression uses = 100. DQDL referential integrity results are evaluated as a ratio, so a complete match should use = 1.0 rather than = 100.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferentialIntegrity \"reference.{ref_warehouse_id,ref_zone_id}\" \"warehouse_id,zone_id\" = 1.0\u003c/strong\u003e - This option uses ReferentialIntegrity and includes both the primary columns and the reference columns. It also uses the correct 1.0 expression for a complete match.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option reverses the required argument order. DQDL syntax expects the primary dataset columns first and the reference dataset alias with reference columns second, so the rule does not correctly express the validation from primary to reference.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types-ReferentialIntegrity.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types-ReferentialIntegrity.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-getting-started.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-getting-started.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/set-up-advanced-rules-to-validate-quality-of-multiple-datasets-with-aws-glue-data-quality/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/set-up-advanced-rules-to-validate-quality-of-multiple-datasets-with-aws-glue-data-quality/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:36":{"id":"e:3:36","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 36","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EMR provisioned cluster, install an open source Python data quality library, copy the S3 training files to the cluster, and run custom validation scripts\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Glue DataBrew project for the S3 training dataset, create a data quality ruleset, run a profile job, and use EventBridge to start the job when new data arrives\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate Lambda functions to evaluate each data quality rule, use Step Functions to orchestrate the functions, and publish notifications when the training data fails validation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena scheduled queries to evaluate rule conditions against the S3 training files and write failed records to a separate S3 prefix for later inspection\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial technology company is building machine learning models to predict payment fraud. The company stores model training datasets as files in an Amazon S3 bucket. A data engineer must apply data quality rules to the training files when new datasets arrive. The solution must require the least operational overhead and must not require the team to write custom validation code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Glue DataBrew project for the S3 training dataset, create a data quality ruleset, run a profile job, and use EventBridge to start the job when new data arrives\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew is designed for visual data preparation and data profiling without requiring users to write code. A data engineer can create a DataBrew project for the training dataset that is stored in Amazon S3 and define the required data quality rules in a ruleset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA DataBrew profile job can run the ruleset against the dataset and generate data quality validation results. This is well suited for ML training data because the company can validate completeness, valid values, ranges, patterns, uniqueness, and other quality expectations before the data enters model development workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eEventBridge can trigger an automated workflow when new data arrives in the S3 bucket. This keeps the process automated while still avoiding custom validation code, custom orchestration, or cluster management, which makes it the lowest operational overhead option among the choices.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9d04c770a2cd485d584316562781774cccf72e71b484b72d9c2e9856021d43de.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9d04c770a2cd485d584316562781774cccf72e71b484b72d9c2e9856021d43de.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-event-driven-data-quality-pipelines-with-aws-glue-databrew/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-event-driven-data-quality-pipelines-with-aws-glue-databrew/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate Lambda functions to evaluate each data quality rule, use Step Functions to orchestrate the functions, and publish notifications when the training data fails validation\u003c/strong\u003e - Lambda and Step Functions can build a flexible validation workflow. The team could create one function per rule or combine several rules into a larger validation function.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this approach requires custom code for the validation logic, workflow design, error handling, retries, and notifications. The requirement asks for a solution that does not require new code and has the least operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon EMR provisioned cluster, install an open source Python data quality library, copy the S3 training files to the cluster, and run custom validation scripts\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR can run distributed data processing workloads, and open source Python packages can validate data quality when engineers write the required logic. This approach can be powerful for highly customized validation requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option requires the company to manage a provisioned cluster, install and maintain libraries, copy or read data from S3, and write validation scripts. That is significantly more operational work than using DataBrew rulesets and profile jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena scheduled queries to evaluate rule conditions against the S3 training files and write failed records to a separate S3 prefix for later inspection\u003c/strong\u003e - Athena can query files in Amazon S3 and can evaluate rule conditions by using SQL. This can work for teams that already express validation logic as SQL queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, scheduled queries require the team to write and maintain SQL for every validation rule and manage failed-record outputs. This adds more operational effort than configuring DataBrew rulesets and running profile jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-rules.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-rules.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-rules-create.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-rules-create.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-ruleset-create-a-profile-job.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/profile.data-quality-ruleset-create-a-profile-job.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/jobs.profile.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/jobs.profile.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-event-driven-data-quality-pipelines-with-aws-glue-databrew/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-event-driven-data-quality-pipelines-with-aws-glue-databrew/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-62":{"id":"p:1-62","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 62","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse batch messages\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDecrease the Stream retention duration\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the number of shards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Exponential Backoff\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Gom bản ghi gửi theo batch giảm số request so với gửi từng bản ghi. Đây là cách tối ưu chi phí mà bộ đề chọn; vẫn phải tôn trọng hạn mức byte và bản ghi của shard.","Retention quyết định thời gian giữ dữ liệu, không tăng năng lực ghi mỗi giây. Giảm retention không sửa throttling và có thể làm mất cửa sổ phát lại cần thiết.","Thêm shard tăng năng lực nhưng cũng tăng chi phí. Đề ưu tiên tối ưu cách gửi batch trước.","Exponential backoff hữu ích khi retry sau throttling, nhưng không loại bỏ nguyên nhân gửi quá nhiều yêu cầu riêng lẻ hoặc vượt năng lực bền vững."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eYou are a data engineer at an IT company. The company has multiple enterprise customers that manage their own mobile applications that capture and send data to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. They have been getting a \u003ccode\u003eProvisionedThroughputExceededException\u003c/code\u003e exception. Upon analysis, you notice that messages are being sent one by one at a high rate.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options will help with the exception while keeping costs at a minimum?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:10:10":{"id":"e:10:10","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 10","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSend application logs to CloudWatch Logs log groups, stream the log events to OpenSearch Service with subscription filters, and create OpenSearch dashboards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse CloudWatch Logs anomaly detection to identify unusual log patterns and use those anomaly results as the primary query and visualization layer\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eExport CloudWatch Logs log groups to Amazon S3 on a schedule by using Lambda, then query the exported log files with Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eExport CloudWatch Logs to Amazon S3 every few minutes with a scheduled Lambda function, catalog the exported files with AWS Glue, and build Athena queries with QuickSight dashboards\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company runs several customer-facing applications on AWS. Each application already writes operational and application logs, and the operations team wants to analyze errors, latency patterns, and user activity across the applications. The team must query and visualize the logs in near real time so engineers can troubleshoot issues quickly from dashboards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSend application logs to CloudWatch Logs log groups, stream the log events to OpenSearch Service with subscription filters, and create OpenSearch dashboards\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon CloudWatch Logs can collect application logs in log groups. CloudWatch Logs subscription filters can stream matching log events to supported destinations, including Amazon OpenSearch Service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon OpenSearch Service is designed for search and log analytics use cases. After the log events are indexed in OpenSearch Service, the operations team can create queries, visualizations, and dashboards in OpenSearch Dashboards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution meets the near real time requirement because logs can stream from CloudWatch Logs to OpenSearch Service soon after ingestion. It also gives engineers a managed search and visualization layer for troubleshooting application behavior across multiple workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eExport CloudWatch Logs log groups to Amazon S3 on a schedule by using Lambda, then query the exported log files with Athena\u003c/strong\u003e - Exporting CloudWatch Logs data to Amazon S3 and querying the files with Athena can support historical log analysis. Athena is useful when teams need serverless SQL over archived logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that scheduled exports and Athena queries do not provide a near real time log search and dashboard experience. This approach introduces delay and is better suited for periodic analysis than rapid troubleshooting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse CloudWatch Logs anomaly detection to identify unusual log patterns and use those anomaly results as the primary query and visualization layer\u003c/strong\u003e - CloudWatch Logs anomaly detection can help identify unusual patterns in log events. It can be useful for surfacing unexpected behavior without writing every detection rule manually.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option does not provide the complete query and visualization solution required by the company. Anomaly detection identifies patterns, but OpenSearch Service with dashboards is a better fit for interactive near real time log analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eExport CloudWatch Logs to Amazon S3 every few minutes with a scheduled Lambda function, catalog the exported files with AWS Glue, and build Athena queries with QuickSight dashboards\u003c/strong\u003e - This option uses several AWS analytics services that can support log analysis over data stored in Amazon S3. Athena can query log files, AWS Glue can catalog the data, and QuickSight can visualize prepared datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option is incorrect because it creates a batch-oriented pipeline with multiple moving parts and export delays. The requirement is near real-time querying and visualization of application logs, which is better addressed by streaming CloudWatch Logs to Amazon OpenSearch Service with subscription filters.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/CWL_OpenSearch_Stream.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/CWL_OpenSearch_Stream.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/Subscriptions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/Subscriptions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/SubscriptionFilters.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/SubscriptionFilters.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/dashboards.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/dashboards.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/create-an-opensearch-dashboard-with-amazon-opensearch-service/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/create-an-opensearch-dashboard-with-amazon-opensearch-service/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:58":{"id":"e:1:58","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 58","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Step Functions state machine that starts the AWS Glue job, invoke the three Lambda functions from the AWS Glue job code before the job exits, and view the pipeline flow in the AWS Glue console\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Step Functions workflow that starts the AWS Glue job and invokes the three Lambda functions after the job succeeds, and view the workflow graph and execution path in Step Functions Workflow Studio\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eBuild the orchestration logic inside the AWS Glue job, invoke the three Lambda functions from the job script after the transformations finish, and inspect the pipeline flow by using Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow environment, build a DAG that starts the AWS Glue job and invokes the three Lambda functions after the job succeeds, configure CloudWatch Logs for task history, and use Airflow plus CloudWatch dashboards to reconstruct the full pipeline execution path\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA digital advertising company is building a serverless data pipeline in AWS. The pipeline must start an AWS Glue ETL job to prepare campaign performance data. After the AWS Glue job completes successfully, the pipeline must invoke three AWS Lambda functions to validate metrics, update reporting metadata, and notify downstream systems. A data engineer needs to view the full workflow sequence and execution path in one interface.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Step Functions workflow that starts the AWS Glue job and invokes the three Lambda functions after the job succeeds, and view the workflow graph and execution path in Step Functions Workflow Studio\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Step Functions is a serverless orchestration service that can coordinate multiple AWS services as steps in a workflow. The workflow can use a Task state to start the AWS Glue job and then continue only after the job completes successfully.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eStep Functions also integrates directly with AWS Lambda. The data engineer can model the three Lambda invocations as subsequent workflow states after the successful AWS Glue job state. This approach keeps the AWS Glue job focused on ETL processing while Step Functions handles sequencing, dependencies, retries, and failure handling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution also satisfies the visibility requirement. Step Functions provides a visual representation of the state machine and execution flow, and Workflow Studio provides a visual interface for building and reviewing the workflow. The data engineer can see the complete pipeline sequence in one place instead of reconstructing the flow from separate service logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4a2931961af7c87a9e73ffc5f025772b6394860626e2d5c0ea475744839e2275.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4a2931961af7c87a9e73ffc5f025772b6394860626e2d5c0ea475744839e2275.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/workflow-studio.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/workflow-studio.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d43fab098e5dd5f60b9522613fdc5437366550d388b4e489a88afead1d2511bc.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d43fab098e5dd5f60b9522613fdc5437366550d388b4e489a88afead1d2511bc.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/workflow-studio.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/workflow-studio.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eBuild the orchestration logic inside the AWS Glue job, invoke the three Lambda functions from the job script after the transformations finish, and inspect the pipeline flow by using Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights\u003c/strong\u003e - This option places orchestration logic inside the AWS Glue job script. That design tightly couples ETL processing with downstream Lambda invocation logic and makes the pipeline harder to modify, test, and operate.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCloudWatch Logs Insights can query logs, but it does not provide a single visual workflow interface for the entire pipeline lineage. The team would need to infer the execution path from log records rather than viewing a managed workflow graph.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow environment, build a DAG that starts the AWS Glue job and invokes the three Lambda functions after the job succeeds, configure CloudWatch Logs for task history, and use Airflow plus CloudWatch dashboards to reconstruct the full pipeline execution path\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow can reduce the burden of operating Apache Airflow infrastructure, and Airflow DAGs can represent pipeline dependencies. However, this option still introduces an Airflow environment, DAG management, Airflow task operations, and additional CloudWatch dashboard work.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor this requirement, Step Functions provides a more direct AWS-native serverless workflow. It can invoke AWS Glue and Lambda with managed service integrations and show the workflow graph and execution path in one Step Functions interface.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Step Functions state machine that starts the AWS Glue job, invoke the three Lambda functions from the AWS Glue job code before the job exits, and view the pipeline flow in the AWS Glue console\u003c/strong\u003e - This option uses Step Functions only for the AWS Glue job and then moves Lambda invocation logic into the AWS Glue job code. That split prevents Step Functions from representing the full pipeline sequence as separate workflow states.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe AWS Glue console can show AWS Glue job details, but it does not provide the complete workflow graph for the Glue job and all three Lambda functions. The data engineer would not get the full end-to-end execution path in one orchestration interface.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-step-functions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-step-functions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/workflow-studio.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/workflow-studio.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-statemachines.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-statemachines.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/new-aws-step-functions-workflow-studio-a-low-code-visual-tool-for-building-state-machines/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/new-aws-step-functions-workflow-studio-a-low-code-visual-tool-for-building-state-machines/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:2":{"id":"e:7:2","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 2","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eReplace the Action field with \u003ccode\u003e\"Action\": [\"s3:PutObject\"]\u003c/code\u003e and the Resource field with \u003ccode\u003e\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*\"]\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eAdd \u003ccode\u003e\"Action\": [\"s3:ListBucket\"]\u003c/code\u003e to the Action field. Replace the Resource field with \u003ccode\u003e\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name\"]\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eReplace the Action field with \u003ccode\u003e\"Action\": [\"s3:GetObject\"]\u003c/code\u003e and the Resource field with \u003ccode\u003e\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*\"]\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eAdd the \u003ccode\u003eAction\": [\"s3:GetObjectVersion\"]\u003c/code\u003e to the Action field. Replace the Resource field with \u003ccode\u003e\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*\"]\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses a Lambda function to retrieve objects from an S3 bucket in the same AWS account. The current IAM policy for the Lambda execution role is as follows:\u003ccode\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\n{\u003cbr\u003e\n\"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\u003cbr\u003e\n\"Statement\": [\u003cbr\u003e\n{\u003cbr\u003e\n\"Effect\": \"Allow\",\u003cbr\u003e\n\"Action\": [\u003cbr\u003e\n\"s3:Get*\",\u003cbr\u003e\n\"s3:Put*\"\u003cbr\u003e\n],\u003cbr\u003e\n\"Resource\": \"*\"\u003cbr\u003e\n}\u003cbr\u003e\n]\u003cbr\u003e\n}\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis policy is overly permissive and does not adhere to the principle of least privilege.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe company needs to modify the IAM policy to ensure that the Lambda function has only the necessary permissions to interact with the S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following modifications would be the most appropriate?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) is a web service that helps you securely control access to AWS resources. When a\u0026nbsp;principal\u0026nbsp;makes a request in AWS, the AWS enforcement code checks whether the principal is authenticated (signed in) and authorized (has permissions). You manage access in AWS by creating policies and attaching them to IAM identities or AWS resources. Policies are JSON documents in AWS that, when attached to an identity or resource, define their permissions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f63f490b2330db9728393df9506be45e3b9542714e207f2c1679d15be1f5d0d3.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003es3:GetObject\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;action grants permission to retrieve objects from an S3 bucket. This action adheres to the principle of least privilege, which is a security practice that restricts access rights for users to the bare minimum permissions they need to perform their work.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you specify the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003es3:GetObject\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;action in the IAM policy, the Lambda function is allowed to retrieve objects from the specified S3 bucket. The\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003e\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*\"]\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;part of the policy specifies the bucket and objects the policy applies to.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis policy is appropriate for a Lambda function that needs to retrieve objects from an S3 bucket, as it grants only the necessary permissions and no more. This is in line with the principle of least privilege.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReplace the Action field with \u003ccode\u003e\"Action\": [\"s3:GetObject\"]\u003c/code\u003e and the Resource field with \u003ccode\u003e\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*\"]\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReplace the Action field with \u003ccode\u003e\"Action\": [\"s3:PutObject\"]\u003c/code\u003e and the Resource field with \u003ccode\u003e\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*\"]\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect because the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003es3:PutObject\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;action allows only for writing or uploading objects to the S3 bucket, which is unnecessary for the Lambda function’s purpose of retrieving objects from the S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdd \u003ccode\u003e\"Action\": [\"s3:ListBucket\"]\u003c/code\u003e to the Action field. Replace the Resource field with \u003ccode\u003e\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name\"]\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect. While this is a step towards least privilege, it does not specifically allow retrieving objects with \u003ccode\u003es3:GetObject\u003c/code\u003e, which is required for the Lambda function to perform its task. Moreover, \u003ccode\u003es3:ListBucket\u003c/code\u003e is applied at the bucket level, not the object level, and does not alone fulfill the requirement to retrieve objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdd the \u003ccode\u003eAction\": [\"s3:GetObjectVersion\"]\u003c/code\u003e to the Action field. Replace the Resource field with \u003ccode\u003e\"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*\"]\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003ccode\u003e\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. This policy would simply allow the function to retrieve different versions of an object in a versioned bucket. While this can be necessary for some use cases, it is not mentioned as a requirement in the scenario. Furthermore, the modification does not restrict actions to only those needed and includes additional permissions not specified as necessary.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/iam/resources/best-practices/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/iam/resources/best-practices/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this\u0026nbsp;AWS Identity and Access Management Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-identity-and-access-management-iam/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-identity-and-access-management-iam/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:11":{"id":"e:1:11","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 11","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure an Amazon EventBridge to trigger an AWS Lambda function based on the defined schedule. The Lambda function will call the Amazon Redshift Data API to refresh the materialized views\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eYou can set auto-refresh for materialized views using CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMaterialized views cannot be refreshed automatically and need a manual refresh\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a schedule to refresh the materialized views with Amazon Redshift query editor v2\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses Amazon Redshift as its data warehouse solution. The company runs certain complex queries repeatedly over a large amount of data and hence uses Amazon Redshift materialized views. The company wants these materialized views to refresh automatically per a defined schedule.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents an optimal solution that can automate the process with the LEAST effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a schedule to refresh the materialized views with Amazon Redshift query editor v2\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn a data warehouse environment, applications often must perform complex queries on large tables. An example is SELECT statements that perform multi-table joins and aggregations on tables that contain billions of rows. Processing these queries can be expensive, in terms of system resources and the time it takes to compute the results.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eMaterialized views in Amazon Redshift provide a way to address these issues. A materialized view contains a precomputed result set, based on an SQL query over one or more base tables. You can issue SELECT statements to query a materialized view, in the same way that you can query other tables or views in the database. Amazon Redshift returns the precomputed results from the materialized view, without having to access the base tables at all. From the user standpoint, the query results are returned much faster compared to when retrieving the same data from the base tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eMaterialized views in Amazon Redshift:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ad9c1ac30239981d1b59c72384abcfa18aa36ead6ba1e429744c6b3583fbd24d.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ad9c1ac30239981d1b59c72384abcfa18aa36ead6ba1e429744c6b3583fbd24d.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can create a schedule to run an SQL statement to refresh a materialized view with Amazon Redshift query editor v2. You create a schedule to run your SQL statement at the time intervals that match your business needs. When it\u0027s time for the scheduled query to run, the query is started by Amazon EventBridge and uses the Amazon Redshift Data API.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo schedule queries, the AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) user defining the schedule and the IAM role associated with the schedule must be configured with the IAM permissions to use Amazon EventBridge and Amazon Redshift Data API.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eYou can set auto-refresh for materialized views using CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift can automatically refresh materialized views with up-to-date data from its base tables when materialized views are created or altered by enabling the autorefresh option. Amazon Redshift auto-refreshes materialized views as soon as possible after base tables changes. But, we want the materialized view to be refreshed as per a schedule and not whenever the base table changes. Hence, this option is not correct for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an Amazon EventBridge to trigger an AWS Lambda function based on the defined schedule. The Lambda function will call the Amazon Redshift Data API to refresh the materialized views\u003c/strong\u003e - These steps are internally run by Amazon Redshift query editor v2 when you schedule a query. Operationally it is more efficient to leverage the Redshift query editor v2 option.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMaterialized views cannot be refreshed automatically and need a manual refresh\u003c/strong\u003e - This statement is incorrect and given only as a distractor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/query-editor-v2-schedule-query.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/query-editor-v2-schedule-query.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-create-sql-command.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-create-sql-command.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:60":{"id":"e:2:60","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 60","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eProcess the files using AWS Glue DataBrew and leverage the NEST_TO_ARRAY transformation to create the new column\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eProcess the files using AWS Glue DataBrew and leverage the NEST_TO_MAP transformation to create the new column\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eProcess the files using Amazon Athena and leverage the json_parse function to create the new column\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eProcess the files using Amazon Athena and leverage the json_format function to create the new column\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe HR department at a company wants to process employees data stored in Amazon S3 in the Microsoft Excel worksheet format. The data has the following column names: id, name, email, and phone. The department wants to create a single column to store these values in the following format:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode\u003e{\n    \"id\": 1,\n    \"name\": \"John Doe\",\n    \"email\": \"johndoe@example.com\",\n    \"phone\": \"9876543210\"\n}\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options will meet this requirement with the LEAST coding effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eProcess the files using AWS Glue DataBrew and leverage the NEST_TO_MAP transformation to create the new column\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn DataBrew, a recipe step is an action that transforms your raw data into a form that is ready to be consumed by your data pipeline. A DataBrew function is a special kind of recipe step that performs a computation based on parameters.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use the NEST_TO_MAP recipe step to convert user-selected columns into key-value pairs, each with a key representing the column name and a value representing the row value. The order of the selected column is not maintained while creating the resultant map.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eExample:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode\u003e{\n    \"RecipeAction\": {\n        \"Operation\": \"NEST_TO_MAP\",\n        \"Parameters\": {\n            \"sourceColumns\": \"[\"age\",\"weight_kg\",\"height_cm\"]\",\n            \"targetColumn\": \"columnName\",\n            \"removeSourceColumns\": \"true\"\n        }\n    }\n}\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eProcess the files using Amazon Athena and leverage the json_format function to create the new column\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eProcess the files using Amazon Athena and leverage the json_parse function to create the new column\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe function json_format returns the JSON text serialized from the input JSON value.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe function json_parse returns the JSON value deserialized from the input JSON text.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/009bcc11e9a7fcd8f753cc4836ca146d5090a8ec2e605cf5d34a670d9f3d6b48.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/009bcc11e9a7fcd8f753cc4836ca146d5090a8ec2e605cf5d34a670d9f3d6b48.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://prestodb.io/docs/current/functions/json.html\"\u003ehttps://prestodb.io/docs/current/functions/json.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAthena supports creating tables and querying data from CSV, TSV, custom-delimited, and JSON formats; data from Hadoop-related formats: ORC, Apache Avro, and Parquet; logs from Logstash, AWS CloudTrail logs, and Apache WebServer logs. Athena does not support querying data from files stored in the Microsoft Excel workbook format, so both these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eProcess the files using AWS Glue DataBrew and leverage the NEST_TO_ARRAY transformation to create the new column\u003c/strong\u003e - You can use the NEST_TO_ARRAY recipe step to convert user-selected columns into array values. The order of the selected columns is maintained while creating the resultant array. The different column data types are typecast to a common type that supports the data types of all columns. Since the output is an array and not a JSON, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/supported-data-file-sources.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/supported-data-file-sources.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/supported-serdes.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/supported-serdes.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.NEST_TO_MAP.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.NEST_TO_MAP.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.NEST_TO_ARRAY.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.NEST_TO_ARRAY.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://prestodb.io/docs/current/functions/json.html\"\u003ehttps://prestodb.io/docs/current/functions/json.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:32":{"id":"e:8:32","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 32","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM data_table WHERE REGEXP_COUNT(category, \u0027^TAD\u0027) OR REGEXP_COUNT(category, \u0027^AGO\u0027);\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM table WHERE category = \u0027^TAD|GO\u0027;\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM data_table WHERE REGEXP_INSTR(category, \u0027^TAD\u0027) OR REGEXP_INSTR(category, \u0027^AGO\u0027);\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM table WHERE category IN (\u0027TAD%\u0027, \u0027AGO%\u0027);\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team at a company is using Amazon Redshift for its data warehousing needs. There is a table with a column named \u003ccode\u003ecategory\u003c/code\u003e. The team needs to create a query that returns a list of data where the \u003ccode\u003ecategory\u003c/code\u003e field starts with either “TAD” or “AGO”.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following Redshift SQL queries would correctly fulfill the team’s requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eREGEXP_COUNT\u0026nbsp;\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003efunction searches a string for a regular expression pattern and returns an integer that indicates the number of times the specified pattern occurs in the string. If no match is found, then the function returns 0.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8e9e78cd09903bffe5a0580d9898234baf4e33907104629a47a50c8cd322b245.png\" alt=\"Amazon Redshift - REGEXP_COUNT function\"\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\nThe command above uses the\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eREGEXP_COUNT\u0026nbsp;\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003efunction, which counts the number of matches of the given regular expression pattern in the category field. The pattern \u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003e^TAD\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e ensures that the match happens at the start of the string, and similarly, \u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003e^AGO\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e checks for matches at the start of the string with \"AGO\".\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM data_table WHERE REGEXP_COUNT(category, \u0027^TAD\u0027) OR REGEXP_COUNT(category, \u0027^AGO\u0027);\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe command below is incorrect because the = operator is primarily used for exact string matching, and \u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003e\u0027^TAD|GO\u0027\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is being treated as a literal string. The pattern \u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003e\u0027^TAD|GO\u0027\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e would match exact categories that are exactly equal to ^TAD or GO, which is not the intended behavior. This does not apply regular expression logic as expected for matching categories starting with \"TAD\" or \"AGO\".\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003e\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM table WHERE category = \u0027^TAD|GO\u0027;\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe command below is incorrect because \u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eREGEXP_INSTR\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e typically returns the position of the first match of the regular expression in the string, rather than counting matches. In this case, checking the position is not the most efficient or straightforward way to filter rows where the category starts with \"TAD\" or \"AGO\". Using \u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eREGEXP_COUNT\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e would be more suitable because it directly counts matches and avoids checking the position.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM data_table WHERE REGEXP_INSTR(category, \u0027^TAD\u0027) OR REGEXP_INSTR(category, \u0027^AGO\u0027);\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe command below is incorrect because the IN operator is used for exact matches, not for pattern matching. While the % symbol is used for wildcard matching, the IN operator does not interpret the % as a pattern but as a literal string. This makes the query incorrect, as IN cannot be used with patterns like TAD% and AGO%.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM table WHERE category IN (\u0027TAD%\u0027, \u0027AGO%\u0027);\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/REGEXP_COUNT.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/REGEXP_COUNT.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/doc-history.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/doc-history.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:15":{"id":"e:7:15","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 15","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eUtilize pushdown predicates to filter partitions upfront. Minimize data load by applying filters to the partition metadata within \u003ccode\u003eDynamicFrame\u003c/code\u003e.\u003cspan\u003e\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eTransform the \u003ccode\u003eDynamicFrame\u003c/code\u003e into a Spark SQL DataFrame for efficient partitioned writing into Amazon S3.\u003c/span\u003e","\u0026lt;span\u0026gt;Optimize the process using server-side filtering with the catalog partition predicates during \u0026lt;code\u0026gt;DynamicFrame\u0026lt;/code\u0026gt; creation.\u0026lt;/span\u0026gt;","Aggregate all input files from the source table into a unified in-memory partition before executing queries to simplify the data processing workflow."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team has a task to configure an ETL job using AWS Glue \u003ccode\u003eDynamicFrame\u003c/code\u003e to manage data in a partitioned Glue table. The table is optimized with partition indexes to enhance query efficiency. The team aims to refine the ETL operation by selectively processing necessary data within a \u003ccode\u003eDynamicFrame\u003c/code\u003e, using partition filtering and partition indexes to boost query performance and minimize costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following is the MOST cost-effective solution that fulfills these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is a powerful ETL service that easily moves data between different data stores. By using AWS Glue, you can easily create and manage ETL jobs to transfer data from various sources, such as Amazon S3, Amazon RDS, and Amazon Redshift. Additionally, AWS Glue enables you to transform your data as needed to fit your specific needs. One of the key advantages of AWS Glue is its automatic schema discovery and mapping, which allows you to map data from different sources with different schemas easily.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/18e0c0355167f7d0fee8cc999690dcb52088e50656c1865da6ea1fa31c8f3d54.png\" alt=\"DynamicFrame\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue\u0027s support for server-side filtering with catalog partition predicates directly during the creation of \u003ccode\u003eDynamicFrame\u003c/code\u003e is a powerful feature for optimizing ETL processes. This capability allows the ETL job to selectively process only the necessary data by utilizing the metadata catalog\u0027s partition indexes. Doing so significantly reduces the volume of data that needs to be read and processed, leading to reductions in execution time and cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eServer-side filtering applies filter predicates against the partition metadata stored in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. This means that before the data is even loaded into the \u003ccode\u003eDynamicFrame\u003c/code\u003e, AWS Glue can narrow down the data to just the relevant partitions based on the criteria specified by the ETL job. This approach is more efficient than client-side filtering, where data is first loaded into memory before being filtered, and it leverages the AWS Glue catalog\u0027s capabilities to minimize data scan and processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eOptimize the process using server-side filtering with the catalog partition predicates during \u003ccode\u003eDynamicFrame\u003c/code\u003e creation.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize pushdown predicates to filter partitions upfront. Minimize data load by applying filters to the partition metadata within \u003ccode\u003eDynamicFrame\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Pushdown predicates are less efficient than catalog partition predicates when used directly during \u003ccode\u003eDynamicFrame\u003c/code\u003e creation. This is because pushdown predicates apply filters after the \u003ccode\u003eDynamicFrame\u003c/code\u003e has been created, leading to higher initial data loads before the filter is applied.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTransform the \u003ccode\u003eDynamicFrame\u003c/code\u003e into a Spark SQL DataFrame for efficient partitioned writing into Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect\u0026nbsp;because this approach simply focuses on the writing process rather than optimizing the reading process by filtering unnecessary data early on.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAggregate all input files from the source table into a unified in-memory partition before executing queries to simplify the data processing workflow\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect.\u0026nbsp;This approach would typically increase costs and processing time due to the large volume of data being loaded into memory, contrary to the goal of minimizing costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-glue-best-practices-build-performant-data-pipeline/building-a-performance-efficient-data-pipeline.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-glue-best-practices-build-performant-data-pipeline/building-a-performance-efficient-data-pipeline.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:30":{"id":"e:4:30","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 30","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Glue ETL job that reads the Parquet file, writes custom PySpark code to calculate column-level statistics, and stores the profiling output in Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIngest the dataset into Amazon Redshift Spectrum and run SQL queries to calculate profile statistics for the Parquet file\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStream the dataset into Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink, run SQL queries against the stream, and use the Apache Flink dashboard to profile the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Glue DataBrew profile job for the dataset and review the generated profile results\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company stores a 150 MB patient appointment dataset in an Amazon S3 bucket as an Apache Parquet file. A data engineer needs to profile the dataset to understand column statistics, missing values, and data distributions before creating data preparation steps.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement in the MOST operationally efficient way?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Glue DataBrew profile job for the dataset and review the generated profile results\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew profile jobs are designed to evaluate datasets and generate profile results. A profile job can collect information such as column statistics, value distributions, missing values, and other characteristics that help a data engineer understand the dataset before preparing it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe dataset is stored in Amazon S3 as an Apache Parquet file, which DataBrew supports as an input file format. The data engineer can create a DataBrew dataset that references the S3 file, run a profile job, and review the results without writing custom code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option is the most operationally efficient because it uses a managed data profiling capability for the exact task. The data engineer does not need to create custom Spark logic, load data into a data warehouse, build dashboards, or manually write many SQL profiling queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Glue ETL job that reads the Parquet file, writes custom PySpark code to calculate column-level statistics, and stores the profiling output in Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue ETL jobs can read Parquet files from Amazon S3 and run PySpark transformations. A data engineer could write custom logic to calculate column statistics and store profiling output.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option requires custom code and job maintenance for a standard profiling task. DataBrew profile jobs are purpose-built to generate profiling results with less operational effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIngest the dataset into Amazon Redshift Spectrum and run SQL queries to calculate profile statistics for the Parquet file\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift Spectrum can query data stored in Amazon S3 through external tables. A data engineer could run SQL queries to calculate profiling statistics from the Parquet file.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this approach requires the engineer to define external metadata and write SQL for the desired profiling checks. AWS Glue DataBrew profile jobs provide a managed profiling workflow with less manual effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStream the dataset into Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink, run SQL queries against the stream, and use the Apache Flink dashboard to profile the data\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink is designed for real-time stream processing. It can run continuous SQL or application logic over streaming data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe source dataset is a static 150 MB Parquet file in Amazon S3. Streaming the file into Flink just to profile it adds unnecessary ingestion and stream-processing complexity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/jobs.profile.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/jobs.profile.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/APIReference/API_JobSample.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/APIReference/API_JobSample.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/getting-started.05.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/getting-started.05.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/announcing-aws-glue-databrew-a-visual-data-preparation-tool-that-helps-you-clean-and-normalize-data-faster/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/announcing-aws-glue-databrew-a-visual-data-preparation-tool-that-helps-you-clean-and-normalize-data-faster/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-12":{"id":"p:2-12","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 12","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eWrite an AWS Glue PySpark job. Use Apache Spark to transform the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun the transformations on the data using AWS Glue DataBrew\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue Python shell job. Use the pre-loaded Pandas library to run transformations on the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun the transformations on the data using AWS Glue Data Studio\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Spark có thể làm được nhưng nguồn CSV khoảng 100 MB không nhất thiết cần xử lý phân tán. Chi phí cao hơn Python shell cho bài toán nhỏ này.","DataBrew phù hợp chuẩn bị dữ liệu trực quan, nhưng đề chọn Python shell với Pandas vì kinh tế hơn cho ETL nhỏ đã mô tả.","Glue Python shell với Pandas xử lý được tệp CSV vừa phải mà không khởi động môi trường Spark phân tán. Đây là phương án tiết kiệm theo bộ đề.","Glue Studio là giao diện tạo và quản lý tác vụ, không tự xác định một mô hình thực thi rẻ hơn Python shell. Tên Glue Data Studio trong lựa chọn cũng không phải tên dịch vụ chuẩn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn Extract, Transform, and Load (ETL) job needs to be implemented which will process data uploaded to an Amazon S3 bucket daily. The uploaded data is in the form of .csv files, each of which is around 100 MB in size.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents the most cost-effective solution for the ETL job?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:8:27":{"id":"e:8:27","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 27","correct":[3,4],"answers":["Use AWS Batch to manage the Athena queries.","\u003cspan\u003eUtilize AWS Glue Python shell job with client \u003ccode\u003estart_query_execution\u003c/code\u003e API to run the Athena Queries.\u003c/span\u003e","Employ Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) to orchestrate the Athena queries.","\u003cspan\u003eImplement AWS Lambda to run the Athena queries using the \u003ccode\u003estart_query_execution\u003c/code\u003e API.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eUse AWS Step Functions workflow to orchestrate the Athena queries. Use the \u003ccode\u003eget_query_execution\u003c/code\u003e API to check the status of the queries. Implement a Wait state in the workflow to check if a query has been completed before running the next query.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has a data pipeline that uses Lambda functions to handle complex logic, including executing Amazon Athena queries. These queries must be run in a specific order as part of the Lambda functions. The results of these queries are used in real-time decision-making, so it’s important that the pipeline can handle failures gracefully. The company wants to orchestrate this process in a way that allows for easy monitoring and error handling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options would be the most efficient and reliable way to orchestrate this process? (SELECT TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that lets you run your code without provisioning or managing servers. You can use AWS Lambda to run the Athena queries using the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003estart_query_execution\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;API. In a complex data pipeline where queries are dependent on each other and need to be run in a specific order, AWS Lambda’s event-driven nature is beneficial. It can be configured to trigger based on specific events, ensuring the correct sequence of query execution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/66bf31c8ecbc9af2bce2fe9774ce340c5b80c795299060d0c0aee72dcee51750.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Step Functions is a serverless workflow service that lets you coordinate the components of distributed applications as a series of steps in a visual workflow. You can use AWS Step Functions to orchestrate the Athena queries and ensure they are run in the correct order. The\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eget_query_execution\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;API can be used to check the status of the queries. A wait state can be implemented in the Step Functions state machine to ensure that queries are run in the correct order.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Implement AWS Lambda to run the Athena queries using the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003estart_query_execution\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;API.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Use AWS Step Functions workflow to orchestrate the Athena queries. Use the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eget_query_execution\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;API to check the status of the queries. Implement a wait state in the Step Functions state machine to ensure that queries are run in the correct order.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Batch to manage the Athena queries\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because AWS Batch is used for batch computing workloads, not for running Athena queries. It does not provide the same level of control over the execution order of the queries as AWS Lambda or AWS Step Functions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize AWS Glue Python shell job with client\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003estart_query_execution\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;API to run the Athena Queries\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because while AWS Glue can be used to run Athena queries, it is primarily used for ETL jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEmploy Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) to orchestrate the Athena queries\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because while Amazon MWAA can be used to orchestrate workflows, it is more complex to set up and manage compared to AWS Lambda or AWS Step Functions. It may not be the most efficient choice for this specific use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these AWS Lambda and AWS Step Functions Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:26":{"id":"e:1:26","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 26","correct":[2,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up a workflow in AWS Step Functions that incorporates two states. Configure the initial state prior to triggering the AWS Glue Python shell job. Establish the subsequent state as a Wait state, designed to periodically verify the completion status of the Athena query via the Athena Boto3 get_query_execution API call. Ensure the workflow is configured to initiate the subsequent query once the preceding one concludes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop a Python shell script in AWS Glue to implement a sleep timer that checks every 5 minutes if the current Athena query has successfully completed. Set up the script so that it triggers the subsequent query once the current one is confirmed to have finished\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up a workflow in AWS Step Functions that incorporates two states. Configure the initial state prior to triggering the Lambda function. Establish the subsequent state as a Wait state, designed to periodically verify the completion status of the Athena query via the Athena Boto3 get_query_execution API call. Ensure the workflow is configured to initiate the subsequent query once the preceding one concludes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an AWS Lambda function that uses the Athena Boto3 client start_query_execution API call to execute the Athena queries programmatically\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an AWS Glue Python shell job that uses the Athena Boto3 client start_query_execution API call to execute the Athena queries programmatically\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer needs to set up a daily execution of Amazon Athena queries, each of which may take longer than 15 minutes to run. What are the two most cost-effective steps to achieve this requirement? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an AWS Lambda function that uses the Athena Boto3 client start_query_execution API call to execute the Athena queries programmatically\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can an AWS Lambda function to execute Athena queries programmatically by using the Athena Boto3 client start_query_execution API call, like so:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/92e006d960dcf405da3e68b4ec79e69a7af28a76c099586e6a4255bfb536af88.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/92e006d960dcf405da3e68b4ec79e69a7af28a76c099586e6a4255bfb536af88.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/schedule-query-athena\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/schedule-query-athena\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up a workflow in AWS Step Functions that incorporates two states. Configure the initial state prior to triggering the Lambda function. Establish the subsequent state as a Wait state, designed to periodically verify the completion status of the Athena query via the Athena Boto3 get_query_execution API call. Ensure the workflow is configured to initiate the subsequent query once the preceding one concludes\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Step Functions is a serverless orchestration service. It is based on state machines and tasks. In Step Functions, a workflow is called a state machine, which is a series of event-driven steps. Each step in a workflow is called a state. A Task state represents a unit of work that another AWS service, such as AWS Lambda, performs. A Task state can call any AWS service or API.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eStates are elements in your state machine. A state is referred to by its name, which can be any string, but must be unique within the scope of the entire state machine.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eStates can perform a variety of functions in your state machine:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDo some work in your state machine (a Task state)\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eMake a choice between branches of execution (a Choice state)\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eStop an execution with a failure or success (a Fail or Succeed state)\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePass its input to its output, or inject some fixed data into the workflow (a Pass state)\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eProvide a delay for a certain amount of time or until a specified date and time (a Wait state)\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBegin parallel branches of execution (a Parallel state)\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDynamically iterate steps (a Map state)\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe AWS Step Functions service integration with Amazon Athena enables you to use Step Functions to start and stop query execution and get query results. Using Step Functions, you can run ad-hoc or scheduled data queries, and retrieve results targeting your S3 data lakes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d70d8d4374263190dc6efa82654097a39d52e47554e9f10cc753d75f55d4789c.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d70d8d4374263190dc6efa82654097a39d52e47554e9f10cc753d75f55d4789c.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-athena.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-athena.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the given use case, incorporating a Wait state into the workflow allows for regular monitoring of the Athena query\u0027s status, advancing to the subsequent step only after the query is finalized. Step Functions are better suited for managing long-running processes and maintaining the status across different stages of the workflow.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an AWS Glue Python shell job that uses the Athena Boto3 client start_query_execution API call to execute the Athena queries programmatically\u003c/strong\u003e - Although you could certainly use an AWS Glue Python shell job to invoke the start_query_execution API call via the Athena Boto3 client, however, it turns out to be costlier than using a Lambda function to invoke the call. So, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up a workflow in AWS Step Functions that incorporates two states. Configure the initial state prior to triggering the AWS Glue Python shell job. Establish the subsequent state as a Wait state, designed to periodically verify the completion status of the Athena query via the Athena Boto3 get_query_execution API call. Ensure the workflow is configured to initiate the subsequent query once the preceding one concludes\u003c/strong\u003e - As mentioned earlier, for the given use case - leveraging an AWS Glue Python shell job instead of a Lambda function is a costlier proposition, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDevelop a Python shell script in AWS Glue to implement a sleep timer that checks every 5 minutes if the current Athena query has successfully completed. Set up the script so that it triggers the subsequent query once the current one is confirmed to have finished\u003c/strong\u003e - It is costlier as well as resource-inefficient to check for the status of Athena query execution every 5 minutes via a long-running Glue job. So, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/schedule-query-athena\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/schedule-query-athena\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-athena.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-athena.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-30":{"id":"p:3-30","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 30","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse an exclude pattern for the Glue crawler to filter out the unwanted files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the \u003ccode\u003eScanning rate\u003c/code\u003e parameter of Glue Crawler to a higher value for the Crawler to complete the scan faster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSplit larger files into smaller ones, to reduce the overhead of reading large files for the Glue Crawler\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCompressed files, like Apache Parquet, take longer to crawl. Instead, use uncompressed files\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Exclude pattern loại các đường dẫn/tệp không cần crawl, giảm khối lượng khám phá metadata. Cần cập nhật pattern phù hợp cách tổ chức dữ liệu cũ.","Scanning rate được nói tới cho việc dùng năng lực đọc nguồn như DynamoDB, không phải nút tăng tốc crawler S3 trong câu này.","Tách thêm tệp không chắc làm nhanh hơn và có thể tăng số object phải liệt kê. Nguyên nhân chính là crawl cả dữ liệu không còn dùng.","Parquet có metadata schema trong tệp; không nên kết luận mọi tệp nén đều cần giải nén toàn bộ để crawl. Đổi sang không nén không sửa phạm vi crawl dư thừa."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare company collects user health information on a daily basis and stores this data on Amazon S3 which is then queried by Amazon Athena for analysis. Any user health information older than a week is never used in the queries. The data engineering team at the company has now set up a Glue crawler to automate the process but the crawler has been running for several hours and is still unable to identify the schema of the data store.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options can be used to fix this issue?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:34":{"id":"e:5:34","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 34","correct":[1],"answers":["Configure an IAM policy to limit user access permissions during Redshift data insertion activities.","\u003cspan\u003eUse a \u003ccode\u003eLOCK\u003c/code\u003e command at the beginning of a transaction before inserting data.\u003c/span\u003e","Enable the Serializable Isolation level to manage consistency for insert transactions.","Create an independent schema in Redshift specifically for handling data ingestion and loading processes."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA bank is currently using an Amazon Redshift provisioned data warehouse environment. It is used to support its analytical workloads and transaction-based data operations. The IT Lead wants the Data Warehouse to restrict read and write operations during the data insertion process in Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow can the Data Engineer accomplish this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eLock\u003c/code\u003e command restricts access to the database table. This command enables the acquisition of a table-level lock in “ACCESS EXCLUSIVE” mode. It waits, if necessary, for any conflicting locks to be released. Explicitly locking a table in this way causes other transactions or sessions to wait when attempting to read or write to the table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis ensures that the data insertion process can be completed without other transactions simultaneously reading from or writing to the table, preventing concurrent operations from interfering with the data-loading process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4b914299839d64259cd71abbc680301dad882219632d4bfd3081f5ec08e2f061.png\" alt=\"Executing a SERIALIZABLE transaction with a table lock and insert command\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse a \u003ccode\u003eLOCK\u003c/code\u003e command at the beginning of a transaction before inserting data.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an IAM policy to limit user access permissions during Redshift data insertion activities\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. While setting up an IAM Policy for Redshift may restrict user access to the Redshift cluster, it does not explicitly target the prevention of read and write access during data insertion. IAM policies focus more on controlling who can access the Redshift cluster rather than managing access at the operational level during data insertion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable the Serializable Isolation level to manage consistency for insert transactions\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Setting the transaction isolation level to \u003ccode\u003eSERIALIZABLE\u003c/code\u003e provide the highest isolation level by ensuring that transactions are entirely isolated. However, this does not explicitly lock tables for other users. It only guarantees that concurrent transactions are executed in a way as if they were serialized.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an independent schema in Redshift specifically for handling data ingestion and loading processes\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While you can control access to the schema very tightly, this may just increase the operational overhead instead of using the LOCK command in Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_LOCK.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_LOCK.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_serial_isolation.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_serial_isolation.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:63":{"id":"e:2:63","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 63","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Inventory and run a daily Lambda function to transition objects between storage classes and delete objects after 400 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the Archive Access tier enabled and configure an S3 bucket policy to delete objects that are older than 400 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects from S3 Standard to S3 One Zone-IA after 45 days, transition them to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 150 days, and expire them after 400 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects from S3 Standard to S3 Standard-IA after 45 days, transition them to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 150 days, and expire them after 400 days\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media analytics company needs to reduce storage costs for an Amazon S3 bucket that receives 8 million event objects every day. The objects range from 4 KB to 6 MB. The objects must remain immediately accessible for the first 45 days. Users access the objects infrequently from day 46 through day 150. From day 151 through day 400, the objects must remain retrievable within an hour. The company can delete the objects after 400 days.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects from S3 Standard to S3 Standard-IA after 45 days, transition them to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 150 days, and expire them after 400 days\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eS3 Lifecycle policies are designed to transition objects between storage classes and expire objects based on object age. This makes Lifecycle the most operationally efficient mechanism for a predictable retention and access pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe objects need immediate access for the first 45 days, so S3 Standard is appropriate during the initial active-access period. From day 46 through day 150, users access the objects infrequently, so S3 Standard-IA matches the requirement for immediate access with lower storage cost for infrequently accessed data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFrom day 151 through day 400, the objects must be retrievable within an hour. S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval supports archive storage with retrieval options that can meet this requirement. The same S3 Lifecycle rule can expire the objects after 400 days, which avoids custom deletion automation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/126c73f6693ff877a637379e29032e2760bf90cddbfe0cad32de3c9cc8ae0291.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/126c73f6693ff877a637379e29032e2760bf90cddbfe0cad32de3c9cc8ae0291.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0a0d47bb5d14db39cd7ab5e483692577fef89e496ce6dcaec710851d61484957.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0a0d47bb5d14db39cd7ab5e483692577fef89e496ce6dcaec710851d61484957.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the Archive Access tier enabled and configure an S3 bucket policy to delete objects that are older than 400 days\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Intelligent-Tiering can optimize storage costs when access patterns are unknown or changing. It can also use optional archive access tiers for objects that become rarely accessed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this scenario has a known access pattern with specific day ranges. Also, an S3 bucket policy does not expire objects based on age. S3 Lifecycle expiration is the correct mechanism for deleting objects after the retention period.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable S3 Inventory and run a daily Lambda function to transition objects between storage classes and delete objects after 400 days\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Inventory can list objects and a Lambda function can use that information to move or delete objects. This design could be built to follow the required timing rules.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, it requires custom code, daily scheduling, retry handling, and operational monitoring. S3 Lifecycle provides native object transition and expiration controls with much less maintenance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects from S3 Standard to S3 One Zone-IA after 45 days, transition them to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 150 days, and expire them after 400 days\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 One Zone-IA can reduce storage cost for infrequently accessed data that does not require storage across multiple Availability Zones. S3 Glacier Deep Archive can reduce archive storage cost further.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, S3 One Zone-IA is not the best fit when the company needs resilient storage for large operational datasets. S3 Glacier Deep Archive also does not meet the requirement to retrieve objects within an hour.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lifecycle-mgmt.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-expire-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-expire-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/restoring-objects-retrieval-options.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/restoring-objects-retrieval-options.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:38":{"id":"e:4:38","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 38","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate individual IAM policies for each data engineer, allow access only to one AWS Region, and require the identity broker to select the engineer\u0027s assigned role\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate one IAM role for pipeline access, create Region-specific IAM policies with aws:RequestedRegion conditions, and pass the correct policy as a session policy during AssumeRole\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate one IAM role with administrator permissions, configure the pipeline code to set the AWS Region from the engineer\u0027s profile, and rely on SDK configuration to enforce locality\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate separate IAM roles for data engineers in each AWS Region and require each engineer to assume the Region-specific role that matches the engineer\u0027s work location\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multinational financial services company must comply with data residency regulations that require each Region\u0027s data to remain accessible only from that same AWS Region. A data engineer is building a pipeline that creates analytics resources in the AWS Region where the engineer works. The pipeline must access data only from that Region. The company uses Active Directory for identity and authentication. A custom identity broker verifies that employees are signed in to Active Directory and then calls the AssumeRole API operation to obtain temporary AWS credentials for each employee.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet the locality requirements with the LEAST administrative effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate one IAM role for pipeline access, create Region-specific IAM policies with aws:RequestedRegion conditions, and pass the correct policy as a session policy during AssumeRole\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company already uses a custom identity broker that calls AssumeRole to issue temporary credentials. The most scalable approach is to create one IAM role that contains the broad permissions the pipeline might need, then use a session policy to restrict each session to the engineer\u0027s authorized AWS Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA session policy can limit the effective permissions of the temporary credentials that AssumeRole returns. The permissions for the session become the intersection of the IAM role permissions and the session policy, so the session policy can reduce access without requiring a separate IAM role for every Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe Region-specific policy can use the aws:RequestedRegion condition key to allow resource creation and data access only in the engineer\u0027s approved Region. The identity broker can select the appropriate session policy based on the engineer\u0027s Active Directory attributes, which reduces administrative overhead compared with managing many Region-specific roles or individual policies.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/16662a1975391720e94261c4457c6f726300c04df687cddb26a436cd4208666b.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/16662a1975391720e94261c4457c6f726300c04df687cddb26a436cd4208666b.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/create-fine-grained-session-permissions-using-iam-managed-policies/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/create-fine-grained-session-permissions-using-iam-managed-policies/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/26aee859b1e6934c10ae98c4f648776f521303b772e34fd9e15ad105b24b1dfd.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/26aee859b1e6934c10ae98c4f648776f521303b772e34fd9e15ad105b24b1dfd.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/create-fine-grained-session-permissions-using-iam-managed-policies/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/create-fine-grained-session-permissions-using-iam-managed-policies/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate separate IAM roles for data engineers in each AWS Region and require each engineer to assume the Region-specific role that matches the engineer\u0027s work location\u003c/strong\u003e - Creating a separate IAM role for each Region can enforce Region-specific access if every role has the correct policy conditions. Engineers could assume only the role that matches their assigned Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this approach increases administrative overhead as the number of Regions and teams grows. The company must create, update, audit, and troubleshoot many roles instead of using one role with session policies that the identity broker applies dynamically.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate one IAM role with administrator permissions, configure the pipeline code to set the AWS Region from the engineer\u0027s profile, and rely on SDK configuration to enforce locality\u003c/strong\u003e - SDK Region configuration can direct API calls to a chosen AWS Region. This helps applications select service endpoints and can reduce accidental use of the wrong Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, SDK configuration is not an authorization control. A user could change the Region configuration or make calls outside the intended Region unless IAM policies or session policies enforce the restriction.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate individual IAM policies for each data engineer, allow access only to one AWS Region, and require the identity broker to select the engineer\u0027s assigned role\u003c/strong\u003e - Individual IAM policies can restrict each engineer to a specific Region. This can technically enforce locality if the policies are carefully written and attached to the correct principals.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, assigning individual policies and roles to each data engineer creates significant administrative overhead. The identity broker can apply Region restrictions more efficiently by passing the appropriate session policy when it calls AssumeRole.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/STS/latest/APIReference/API_AssumeRole.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/STS/latest/APIReference/API_AssumeRole.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_credentials_temp_request.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_credentials_temp_request.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/access_policies.html#policies_session\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/access_policies.html#policies_session\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_condition-keys.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_condition-keys.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/easier-way-to-control-access-to-aws-regions-using-iam-policies/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/easier-way-to-control-access-to-aws-regions-using-iam-policies/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/create-fine-grained-session-permissions-using-iam-managed-policies/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/create-fine-grained-session-permissions-using-iam-managed-policies/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:2":{"id":"e:6:2","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 2","correct":[2],"answers":["Update the security group configurations associated with each Elastic Network Interface (ENI) of the Kafka brokers within the cluster. Add a security group rule that allows specific microservices to access their designated brokers.","Modify the partitioning key in the producer application to prevent data intended for feedback collection from being written to the content recommendation topic.","Set up Amazon MSK to utilize Kafka access control lists (ACLs). Restrict the permissions of each microservice to only its respective Amazon MSK topic.","Deploy individual source connectors for each microservice using Kafka Connect and modify the connector configurations to link each to its specific Amazon MSK topic."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company that provides streaming services operates several microservices. These handle various tasks such as recommending content, managing user profiles, and collecting feedback. Each microservice consumes TLS-encrypted streaming data from a specific Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (Amazon MSK) topic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, after the deployment of a new recommendation algorithm, the microservice for content recommendation started receiving data meant for feedback collection. This has led to inconsistencies in the user experience and skewed analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution would ensure that each microservice can only access data from its designated topic?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (Amazon MSK\u003c/strong\u003e) is a fully managed service that enables you to build and run applications that use Apache Kafka to process streaming data. Amazon MSK provides the control-plane operations, such as those for creating, updating, and deleting clusters. It lets you use Apache Kafka data-plane operations, such as those for producing and consuming data. It runs open-source versions of Apache Kafka. This means existing applications, tooling, and plugins from partners and the Apache Kafka community are supported without requiring changes to application code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/103456a9049a79f869de1a254dc0e320ac3d0cae979a310aae7e63a467803567.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eApache Kafka\u0027s Access Control Lists (ACLs) offer a highly granular approach to permissions management. They provide a mechanism to regulate which applications can read or write to a certain topic.\u0026nbsp;They follow the format \"Principal P is [Allowed/Denied] Operation O From Host H on any Resource R matching ResourcePattern RP\". If RP doesn\u0027t match a specific resource R, then R has no associated ACLs, and therefore, no one other than super users is allowed to access R. To change this Apache Kafka behavior, you set the property\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eallow.everyone.if.no.acl.found\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;to true. Amazon MSK sets it to true by default. This means that with Amazon MSK clusters, if you don\u0027t explicitly set ACLs on a resource, all principals can access this resource. If you enable ACLs on a resource, only the authorized principals can access it. If you want to restrict access to a topic and authorize a client using TLS mutual authentication, add ACLs using the Apache Kafka authorizer CLI.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the scenario, after deploying a new recommendation algorithm, the content recommendation microservice began receiving data intended for feedback collection. This led to inconsistencies in user experience and skewed analytics. The solution to guarantee that each microservice can only access data from its assigned topic is to configure Amazon MSK to use Kafka access control lists (ACLs). By limiting the permissions of each microservice to its respective Amazon MSK topic only, the company can ensure that each microservice only accesses the data it is supposed to, thereby preventing any cross-contamination of data across different microservices. This approach provides a robust and secure method for managing access to data in a microservices architecture.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTherefore, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up Amazon MSK to utilize Kafka access control lists (ACLs). Restrict the permissions of each microservice to only its respective Amazon MSK topic.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate the security group configurations associated with each Elastic Network Interface (ENI) of the Kafka brokers within the cluster. Add a security group rule that allows specific microservices to access their designated brokers\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Security groups in AWS are used to control inbound and outbound network traffic to AWS resources, and they operate at the level of network interfaces. While they can restrict which IP addresses can connect to the Kafka brokers, they can’t control access at the level of individual topics. Therefore, this wouldn’t prevent a misconfigured microservice from consuming data from the wrong topic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eModify the partitioning key in the producer application to prevent data intended for feedback collection from being written to the content recommendation topic\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. The partitioning key determines how messages are distributed among the various partitions within a Kafka topic. Modifying the partitioning key in the producer application would change the distribution of the messages, but it would not alter or restrict which microservices can access which topics\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy individual source connectors for each microservice using Kafka Connect and modify the connector configurations to link each to its specific Amazon MSK topic\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Kafka Connect is designed to bridge Kafka with other systems, such as databases or search systems. Its main purpose is to enable data flow between Kafka and other platforms, not to enforce data access controls\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-acls.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/msk-acls.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/what-is-msk.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/msk/latest/developerguide/what-is-msk.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon MKS Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-managed-streaming-for-apache-kafka-amazon-msk/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-managed-streaming-for-apache-kafka-amazon-msk/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-64":{"id":"p:4-64","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 64","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS DataSync to transfer files from the on-premises servers to Amazon EFS, enable automatic backups with AWS Backup, and configure Athena to query the EFS files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse an on-premises cron job to compress the files and upload them directly to Amazon S3 by using the AWS CLI, then enable S3 Versioning\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the Kinesis Agent on the on-premises servers to send the files to Amazon Data Firehose, and deliver the data to a versioning-enabled S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse a self-managed Kafka agent on the on-premises servers to stream the files to Amazon MSK, and deliver the data to a versioning-enabled S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["DataSync và EFS phục vụ truyền và lưu tệp, nhưng Athena không truy vấn trực tiếp EFS như data lake S3 trong đề. Kiến trúc còn thêm lớp backup không cần thiết cho yêu cầu đã nêu.","Cron và AWS CLI có thể upload cùng S3 Versioning để phục hồi xóa nhầm, nhưng bạn phải tự quản lý lịch, retry và theo dõi. Đề muốn ứng dụng nhỏ gửi vào dịch vụ ingestion được quản lý.","Kinesis Agent theo dõi và gửi bản ghi từ tệp phù hợp tới Firehose, rồi Firehose chuyển vào S3. Bật Versioning giữ các phiên bản để khôi phục khi xóa nhầm thông thường; vẫn cần quản lý quyền xóa vĩnh viễn version.","Kafka agent, MSK và connector đích tạo thêm thành phần cần cấu hình, theo dõi. Firehose cung cấp đường chuyển dữ liệu vào S3 đơn giản hơn cho nhu cầu này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company receives daily occupancy and revenue files from each branch location. The company wants to upload the files to AWS and use Amazon Athena to query the data from Amazon S3. The company must protect the uploaded files from accidental deletion. The company will run a small application on its on-premises servers to forward the files to a fully managed AWS ingestion service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-41":{"id":"p:4-41","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 41","correct":[0,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a KMS key in the destination Region and create a Redshift snapshot copy grant that uses that destination KMS key\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a KMS key in the source Region and create a Redshift snapshot copy grant that uses the source Region key\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable snapshot copying in the source Region and specify the snapshot copy grant name that exists in the destination Region\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an S3 bucket in the destination Region, export the encrypted Redshift snapshot to the bucket, and configure S3 replication\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable snapshot copying in the destination Region and specify the snapshot copy grant name that exists in the source Region\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Snapshot sao chép cần khóa KMS hợp lệ ở Region đích. Tạo khóa và snapshot copy grant tại đích để Redshift được phép dùng khóa đó khi mã hóa bản sao.","Khóa chỉ ở Region nguồn không đáp ứng yêu cầu mã hóa bản sao bằng khóa hợp lệ ở Region đích. Snapshot copy grant cần tham chiếu khóa phía đích.","Bật snapshot copy trên cluster ở Region nguồn và cung cấp tên grant đã tạo tại Region đích. Hai bước này kết hợp việc khởi động sao chép với quyền mã hóa ở nơi nhận.","Redshift snapshot không được xuất thành object S3 thông thường để dùng S3 replication theo mô tả. Cần cơ chế cross-Region snapshot copy của Redshift.","Thao tác bật copy phải thực hiện cho cluster nguồn, dùng grant tại Region đích. Lựa chọn này đảo ngược cả nơi bật sao chép lẫn nơi đặt grant."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company encrypts its Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster by using AWS Key Management Service. The company is implementing a disaster recovery strategy that requires automated snapshot copies to a different AWS Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer must configure cross-Region snapshot copy by using the AWS CLI. The copied snapshots must remain encrypted with a KMS key that is valid in the destination Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:62":{"id":"e:1:62","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 62","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable DynamoDB Streams on the table, invoke an AWS Lambda function for each stream record, and delete transcript analysis items when the function determines that the item has crossed the retention window\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a new DynamoDB table, enable Time to Live on the new table, migrate the existing transcript analysis records to the new table, and update the reporting application to use the new table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable Time to Live on the existing DynamoDB table and write an expiration timestamp attribute to each transcript analysis item\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge scheduled rule that invokes an AWS Lambda function each day to query and delete transcript analysis records that are older than the retention window\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA customer experience analytics company stores sentiment scores and topic extraction results for thousands of daily support chat transcripts in an Amazon DynamoDB table. A reporting application reads only the most recent 9 business days of results. The company wants to reduce storage costs by automatically removing items that are older than 9 business days.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Time to Live on the existing DynamoDB table and write an expiration timestamp attribute to each transcript analysis item\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon DynamoDB Time to Live is designed to remove items that are no longer needed. The table can define a TTL attribute, and each item can store an expiration time as an epoch timestamp. For this scenario, the application or ingestion process can calculate the timestamp that represents the end of the 9-business-day retention period and write that value to each item.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAfter TTL is enabled, DynamoDB automatically deletes expired items in the background. This removes the need to run daily scans, maintain deletion code, or provision a separate batch cleanup process. TTL deletion does not consume write throughput in the source Region, which helps reduce operational effort and cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution uses the existing DynamoDB table and applies a native lifecycle capability directly to the stored items. It is the least operationally intensive option because the company only needs to enable TTL and ensure that new records include the correct expiration timestamp.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a new DynamoDB table, enable Time to Live on the new table, migrate the existing transcript analysis records to the new table, and update the reporting application to use the new table\u003c/strong\u003e - Creating a new DynamoDB table with TTL can technically support item expiration, but the existing table can enable TTL directly. The company does not need to migrate data or point the application to a new table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option adds unnecessary migration effort, application changes, testing, and cutover risk. It does not provide a meaningful benefit over enabling TTL on the existing table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable DynamoDB Streams on the table, invoke an AWS Lambda function for each stream record, and delete transcript analysis items when the function determines that the item has crossed the retention window\u003c/strong\u003e - DynamoDB Streams captures item-level changes, but it does not automatically emit a stream record at the moment an item becomes older than the retention window. A Lambda function that reacts only to stream records would not reliably delete items simply because time has passed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach also adds unnecessary event processing and custom logic. TTL is the appropriate feature for time-based item expiration in a DynamoDB table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge scheduled rule that invokes an AWS Lambda function each day to query and delete transcript analysis records that are older than the retention window\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EventBridge and AWS Lambda can implement a scheduled cleanup process. However, the function would need logic to identify expired records, handle pagination, delete items, manage throttling, and recover from failures.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option adds custom operational responsibility for a capability that DynamoDB already provides natively. It is less efficient and has more moving parts than TTL.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TTL.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/TTL.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/time-to-live-ttl-how-to.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/time-to-live-ttl-how-to.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/ttl-expired-items.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/ttl-expired-items.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-51":{"id":"p:1-51","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 51","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAttach a security group to the S3 bucket that permits inbound HTTPS connections only from the CIDR range of the analytics VPC\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an S3 bucket policy that allows access only when requests originate from the company VPC or the approved S3 VPC endpoint\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Block Public Access on the bucket and rely on the setting to restrict object access only to workloads deployed in the VPC\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure network ACLs on the VPC subnets to allow selected workloads to reach the S3 bucket and deny all other S3 traffic\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Không gắn security group trực tiếp vào bucket S3. Security group áp dụng cho tài nguyên mạng hỗ trợ nó, không phải bucket.","Bucket policy có thể dùng điều kiện aws:SourceVpc hoặc aws:SourceVpce để giới hạn nguồn truy cập. Cần cấu hình Allow/Deny phù hợp để chặn các nguồn ngoài phạm vi được phép.","Block Public Access ngăn truy cập công khai, không giới hạn mọi truy cập có xác thực chỉ từ một VPC. Hai yêu cầu này khác nhau.","NACL kiểm soát lưu lượng subnet, không áp đặt điều kiện truy cập lên bucket đối với tất cả nguồn bên ngoài VPC."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media analytics company stores time-series playback metrics from streaming applications in an Amazon S3 bucket. Several analytics workloads that run in the company\u0027s VPC need to read the metrics, but the security team must prevent access from workloads that run outside that VPC. A data engineer must enforce this restriction at the S3 bucket level.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:10:8":{"id":"e:10:8","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 8","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue workflows to run a crawler every hour against Amazon RDS, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon OpenSearch Service, then start AWS Glue ETL jobs to process all records that the crawlers discover\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge Scheduler to invoke a Lambda function every hour to check each data store, send processing messages to Amazon SQS when the function finds new data, and use a second Lambda function to process the messages with retries and a dead-letter queue\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun an Apache Spark application on Amazon EMR every hour to check Amazon RDS, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon OpenSearch Service for new records, process the results, and handle errors in the Spark code\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to create a DAG that runs every hour, checks each data store for new records, processes the records, and handles errors in Python operators\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company needs a fully automated solution to check for new records in several operational data stores and process any records that the solution finds. The solution must run every hour and must work with Amazon RDS, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon OpenSearch Service. Each processing run will handle up to 8 MB of data at a time. The company wants to minimize cost and operational overhead while using reliable error handling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge Scheduler to invoke a Lambda function every hour to check each data store, send processing messages to Amazon SQS when the function finds new data, and use a second Lambda function to process the messages with retries and a dead-letter queue\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon EventBridge Scheduler can invoke an AWS Lambda function on an hourly schedule. The first Lambda function can connect to Amazon RDS, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon OpenSearch Service to check for new data and create processing messages when it finds records that need work.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon SQS decouples the discovery step from the processing step. The message can contain metadata, identifiers, or pointers to the records rather than the entire dataset. A second Lambda function can read messages from the SQS queue, retrieve the relevant data from the source service, and process up to the required 8 MB working set.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has low operational overhead because it uses managed serverless services. It also has robust error handling because Lambda integrations with SQS support retries, visibility timeout behavior, and dead-letter queues for messages that fail processing repeatedly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0b3a96697cca0461fc4289924d3aeea0788359fe06a002554b37bcf01b507b93.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0b3a96697cca0461fc4289924d3aeea0788359fe06a002554b37bcf01b507b93.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-eventbridge-scheduler.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-eventbridge-scheduler.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to create a DAG that runs every hour, checks each data store for new records, processes the records, and handles errors in Python operators\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon MWAA can schedule workflows and implement dependency management through Apache Airflow DAGs. It can also run Python operators that check multiple data stores and process results.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, Amazon MWAA introduces an Airflow environment, DAG operations, worker configuration, monitoring, and platform-level administration. For an hourly process that handles only a small amount of data, Lambda with EventBridge Scheduler and SQS is more cost-effective and operationally simpler.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun an Apache Spark application on Amazon EMR every hour to check Amazon RDS, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon OpenSearch Service for new records, process the results, and handle errors in the Spark code\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR with Apache Spark can process large-scale datasets and connect to multiple data sources. Spark also allows developers to implement custom error handling in the application code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, EMR is unnecessarily heavy for a workload that runs hourly and processes only up to 8 MB of data at a time. The company would pay for and manage a big data processing environment when a serverless Lambda-based design is sufficient.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue workflows to run a crawler every hour against Amazon RDS, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon OpenSearch Service, then start AWS Glue ETL jobs to process all records that the crawlers discover\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue crawlers help discover schemas and update the AWS Glue Data Catalog. AWS Glue workflows can orchestrate Glue jobs, crawlers, and triggers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, crawlers are not the correct mechanism to check Amazon OpenSearch Service for new operational records every hour, and they do not provide a lightweight record-processing workflow for small data volumes. This design adds catalog and ETL overhead that the requirement does not need.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-eventbridge-scheduler.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-eventbridge-scheduler.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/gettingstarted-limits.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/gettingstarted-limits.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-sqs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-sqs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-sqs-configure.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-sqs-configure.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-sqs-errorhandling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-sqs-errorhandling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/lambda-rds-connect.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/lambda-rds-connect.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-50":{"id":"p:3-50","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 50","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eThe S3 bucket does not have versioning enabled, so Athena cannot identify the latest delivery event objects in each partition\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe AWS Glue Data Catalog does not include the latest S3 partition changes, so Athena queries do not read all current partition locations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe Athena workgroup stores query results in the same S3 bucket, so Athena replaces recent delivery records with cached query output\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe AWS Glue job schedules overlap, so Athena automatically reads older partitions until all overlapping jobs finish successfully\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Athena không yêu cầu bật S3 Versioning để đọc object hiện tại. Thiếu versioning không giải thích việc bỏ sót partition mới.","Catalog chưa cập nhật partition ngày/giờ mới khiến Athena không biết cần đọc các vị trí đó. Cập nhật partition, crawler hoặc cấu hình projection phù hợp để phản ánh dữ liệu mới.","Vị trí ghi kết quả truy vấn không tự khiến Athena ghi đè bản ghi nguồn hoặc thay chúng bằng cache. Đây không phải cơ chế mặc định như lựa chọn mô tả.","Glue job chạy chồng nhau không làm Athena tự chọn partition cũ cho tới khi mọi job xong. Cần kiểm tra metadata và dữ liệu thực tế."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company stores time-sensitive delivery event data in Amazon S3. The company partitions the dataset by event_date and event_hour, and analysts use Amazon Athena with AWS Glue Data Catalog tables to run near real-time delivery performance analysis. During an investigation, a data engineer notices that some recently arrived records do not appear in Athena query results, and some partitioned query results seem out of date. The source files exist in the correct S3 prefixes. The data engineer must identify the most likely cause of the issue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is the likely cause?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-14":{"id":"p:3-14","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 14","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon S3 as the data lake service. Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query data in Amazon S3 files without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables. Configure access control through the IAM roles attached to the Redshift cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon S3 as the data lake service. Configure AWS Lake Formation permissions to provide fine-grained row and column-wise access. Provide access to data for the AWS services via Lake Formation permissions only\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon S3 as the data lake service. Define the Amazon S3 access point for each AWS service. Configure each access point with distinct permissions and network controls needed for each AWS service\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 for data lake storage. Use Apache Ranger through Amazon EMR to restrict data access by rows and columns. Provide data access by using Apache Pig\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["IAM role của cụm giới hạn tài nguyên truy cập, không tự áp dụng bộ lọc hàng/cột cho mọi nhóm trên toàn bộ dịch vụ phân tích.","S3 lưu dữ liệu; Lake Formation áp quyền hàng/cột qua các tích hợp được hỗ trợ. Cần tránh cấp đường truy cập S3 trực tiếp có thể bỏ qua kiểm soát này.","S3 Access Point quản lý quyền với đối tượng và mạng, không trực tiếp lọc từng hàng hoặc cột trong dataset.","Tự vận hành Ranger và thiết kế riêng cho EMR/Pig thêm công và chưa thống nhất mọi dịch vụ. Lake Formation phù hợp quản trị tập trung hơn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses several analytics services from AWS such as AWS Glue, Amazon Athena, Amazon Redshift Spectrum, and Amazon EMR to query the data. The company wants to build a data lake in AWS that can provide row-level and column-level data access to specific teams/groups that use the aforementioned AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution can address these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-5":{"id":"p:2-5","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 5","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize Amazon Redshift Spectrum for querying the data, employing standard SQL for structured data sources and PartiQL for handling data stored in JSON format\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize AWS Glue jobs to transform the JSON data to .csv format and query the resultant data in .csv format using Amazon Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize Amazon Athena for querying the data, employing standard SQL for structured data sources and PartiQL for handling data stored in JSON format\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize AWS Glue jobs to transform both the JSON and .csv format data to parquet format and query the resultant data using Amazon Athena\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Spectrum là đường truy vấn dữ liệu S3 từ Redshift và cần thêm cấu hình liên quan cụm. Bộ đề chọn Athena để giảm công vận hành truy vấn đa nguồn.","Chuyển JSON sang CSV là bước ETL không bắt buộc để truy vấn dữ liệu JSON đã được mô tả schema. Nó tạo thêm mã và tác vụ.","Ý chính của đáp án là dùng Glue Catalog quản lý metadata và Athena truy vấn, với connector phù hợp cho các nguồn ngoài S3. Phần đề mô tả PartiQL cần đối chiếu theo engine/connector thực tế, không coi đó là cú pháp chung thay thế Athena SQL.","Parquet có thể tối ưu phân tích, nhưng chuyển tất cả định dạng trước khi truy vấn làm tăng công ETL. Đề ưu tiên ít vận hành chứ chưa yêu cầu tối ưu layout."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company maintains its datasets in JSON and .csv formats in an Amazon S3 bucket and utilizes Amazon RDS for Microsoft SQL Server, Amazon DynamoDB (in provisioned capacity mode), and an Amazon Redshift cluster. The data engineering team is tasked with creating a solution that enables data scientists to query all these data sources using an SQL-like syntax.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution would fulfill these requirements while incurring the least operational overhead and minimizing the costs?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-31":{"id":"p:3-31","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 31","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the data in compressed .csv, partitioned by date and sorted by the status of the device\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the data in compressed .csv, partitioned by date and sorted by device type\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the data in Apache Parquet, partitioned by device type and sorted by date\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the data in Apache ORC, partitioned by date and sorted by device type of the device\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["CSV vẫn theo hàng; sắp theo trạng thái không khớp mẫu phân tích theo loại thiết bị. Không tối ưu bằng định dạng cột.","Partition/sort đúng hướng hơn nhưng CSV không có ưu điểm đọc theo cột cho truy vấn phân tích lớn.","Parquet là định dạng phù hợp, nhưng bộ đề yêu cầu tổ chức chính theo ngày. Phương án đã đảo vai trò partition và sort.","ORC giảm đọc cột không cần thiết; partition theo ngày phù hợp truy vấn hằng ngày và sort theo device type hỗ trợ lọc/nhóm thiết bị."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn Internet-of-Things (IoT) devices company uses Amazon S3 as the data lake to store the input data that is ingested from the field devices on an hourly basis. The ingested data has attributes such as the device type, ID of the device, the status of the device, the timestamp of the event, the source IP address, etc. The data runs into millions of records per day and the company wants to run complex analytical queries on this data on a daily basis for product improvements for each device type.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich is the most optimal way to save this data to get the best performance from the millions of data points saved on a daily basis?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:7":{"id":"e:1:7","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 7","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse RDS MySQL as the database with ElastiCache\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Aurora MySQL as the database with Aurora cluster cache\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse DynamoDB as the database with DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse DocumentDB as the database with API Gateway\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn IT company wants to process IoT data from the field devices of an agricultural sciences company. The data engineering team at the company is designing a new database infrastructure for capturing this IoT data. The database should be resilient with minimal operational overhead and require the least development effort. The application includes a device tracking system that stores the GPS data for all devices. Real-time IoT data, as well as metadata lookups, must be performed with high throughput and microsecond latency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options would you recommend as the MOST efficient solution for these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse DynamoDB as the database with DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon DynamoDB is a key-value and document database that delivers single-digit millisecond performance at any scale. It\u0027s a fully managed, multi-region, multi-master, durable NoSQL database with built-in security, backup and restore, and in-memory caching for internet-scale applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) is a fully managed, highly available, in-memory cache for DynamoDB that delivers up to a 10x performance improvement – from milliseconds to microseconds – even at millions of requests per second. DAX does all the heavy lifting required to add in-memory acceleration to your DynamoDB tables, without requiring developers to manage cache invalidation, data population, or cluster management.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDAX Overview:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e66e0a55be25e24ed3968b752057dc74152b833614c72deb3df8d73f4dd9286c.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e66e0a55be25e24ed3968b752057dc74152b833614c72deb3df8d73f4dd9286c.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/DAX.concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/DAX.concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTypically, DynamoDB response times can be measured in single-digit milliseconds. Certain use cases require response times in microseconds. For these use cases, DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) delivers fast response times for accessing eventually consistent data. Additionally, DAX reduces operational and application complexity by providing a managed service that is API-compatible with DynamoDB. Therefore, it requires only minimal functional changes to use with an existing application. Therefore, this is the correct option.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2417fd6b5cb84c10b0d53f3e3b9106b77473c83377184f35afec803bf010130b.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2417fd6b5cb84c10b0d53f3e3b9106b77473c83377184f35afec803bf010130b.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.amazonaws.cn/en_us/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/DAX.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.amazonaws.cn/en_us/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/DAX.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse RDS MySQL as the database with ElastiCache\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe given use-case deals with IoT data which has a variable underlying data structure by its nature. Relational databases are not a good fit for capturing such data. Also integrating ElastiCache with RDS involves custom code at the application level as well as provisioning and maintaining a separate ElastiCache cluster. This operational overhead goes against the specified requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Aurora MySQL as the database with Aurora cluster cache\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe given use-case deals with IoT data which has a variable underlying data structure by its nature. Relational databases are not a good fit for capturing such data. In addition, the main benefit of the cluster cache feature is to improve the performance of the new primary/writer instance after failover occurs. So Aurora with cluster cache is not the right fit to handle IoT data with microsecond latency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/be1eb39c4858120958129991a67e485eaba07f993e600b4c406cb3f569df7cf7.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/be1eb39c4858120958129991a67e485eaba07f993e600b4c406cb3f569df7cf7.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/introduction-to-aurora-postgresql-cluster-cache-management/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/introduction-to-aurora-postgresql-cluster-cache-management/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse DocumentDB as the database with API Gateway\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility) is a fast, reliable, and fully managed database service. Amazon DocumentDB makes it easy to set up, operate, and scale MongoDB-compatible databases in the cloud.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon API Gateway is a fully managed service that makes it easy for developers to create, publish, maintain, monitor, and secure APIs at any scale. APIs act as the front door for applications to access data, business logic, or functionality from your backend services. Using API Gateway, you can create RESTful APIs and WebSocket APIs that enable real-time two-way communication applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option has been added as a distractor as API Gateway cannot be used to optimize the database infrastructure for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow API Gateway Works:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/92f57b54f2085988d419fb2ef1bb4643e1183e3eb33fd65388162f85db455f89.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/92f57b54f2085988d419fb2ef1bb4643e1183e3eb33fd65388162f85db455f89.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/api-gateway/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/api-gateway/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.amazonaws.cn/en_us/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/DAX.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.amazonaws.cn/en_us/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/DAX.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/introduction-to-aurora-postgresql-cluster-cache-management/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/introduction-to-aurora-postgresql-cluster-cache-management/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/DAX.concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/DAX.concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/api-gateway/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/api-gateway/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:44":{"id":"e:5:44","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 44","correct":[0],"answers":["Create an Intelligent-Tiering Archive configuration and choose the default access tier.","Create an Intelligent-Tiering Archive configuration, and choose the Deep Archive Access tier.","\u003cspan\u003eUse S3 Storage Lens to monitor the \u003ccode\u003eNumberOfObjects\u003c/code\u003e metric and transition data to S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access if the number of objects in a bucket exceeds a certain threshold.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eUse S3 Storage Lens to monitor the \u003ccode\u003eBucketSizeBytes\u003c/code\u003e metric and transition data to S3 Glacier Deep Archive if the total size of a bucket exceeds a certain threshold.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA corporation has a vast amount of data stored in Amazon S3 buckets. The access patterns for this data are both unpredictable and frequently change. The corporation wants to optimize storage costs while ensuring that data retrieval times remain within milliseconds.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following strategies would be the most suitable for this scenario with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering is a storage class designed to optimize costs by automatically moving data between two access tiers (frequent and infrequent) based on changing access patterns. When you upload data to S3 Intelligent-Tiering, it is automatically stored in the Frequent Access tier, which is optimized for data that is accessed frequently. If an object hasn’t been accessed for 30 consecutive days, S3 Intelligent-Tiering moves it to the Infrequent Access tier, which is less expensive. If an object in the Infrequent Access tier is accessed later, it is automatically moved back to the Frequent Access tier. This automatic tiering allows you to optimize storage costs without impacting data retrieval times or requiring operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5feab252e2d6493bfc69ec9934bafbb95680fc1ab32946cfec83ea31506b0edf.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the context of the question, the corporation’s data access patterns are unpredictable and variable, which makes S3 Intelligent-Tiering an ideal choice. Regardless of how frequently or infrequently the data is accessed, S3 Intelligent-Tiering ensures that all data can be retrieved within milliseconds, meeting the corporation’s requirements. Furthermore, because S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically moves data between access tiers based on access patterns, it requires the least operational overhead compared to other options that involve manual intervention or monitoring with S3 Storage Lens.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Intelligent-Tiering Archive configuration and choose the default access tier.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Intelligent-Tiering Archive configuration, and choose the Deep Archive Access tier\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because the Deep Archive Access tier is not designed for data that needs to be retrieved within milliseconds. It is meant for long-term archiving of data that is rarely accessed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse S3 Storage Lens to monitor the \u003ccode\u003eNumberOfObjects\u003c/code\u003e metric and transition data to S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access if the number of objects in a bucket exceeds a certain threshold\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it suggests transitioning data based on the number of objects in a bucket, not on access patterns. S3 Storage Lens is used here to monitor the \u003ccode\u003eNumberOfObjects\u003c/code\u003e metric, which provides the total number of objects stored in a bucket. However, this does not take into account the access patterns of the data, which are unpredictable and variable in this scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse S3 Storage Lens to monitor the \u003ccode\u003eBucketSizeBytes\u003c/code\u003e metric and transition data to S3 Glacier Deep Archive if the total size of a bucket exceeds a certain threshold\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect because it suggests transitioning data based on the total size of a bucket, not on access patterns. S3 Storage Lens is used here to monitor the \u003ccode\u003eBucketSizeBytes\u003c/code\u003e metric, which provides the total size of a bucket in bytes. However, this does not take into account the access patterns of the data, which are unpredictable and variable in this scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/intelligent-tiering-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/intelligent-tiering-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/intelligent-tiering/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/intelligent-tiering/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon S3 Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-9":{"id":"p:1-9","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 9","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Glue DataBrew to create a recipe that uses the COUNT_DISTINCT aggregate function to determine the number of distinct customers\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eQuery the customer data file in .xls format stored in Amazon S3 using SQL commands via Amazon Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Glue DataBrew to create a recipe that uses the FLAG_DUPLICATE_ROWS function to determine the number of distinct customers\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop an Apache Spark job in an AWS Glue notebook to read the file in Amazon S3 and determine the number of distinct customers\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["DataBrew đọc được .xls, ghép cột họ và tên bằng recipe rồi dùng COUNT_DISTINCT để đếm số giá trị khác nhau. Không phải tự viết Spark, nên ít công thực hiện.","Athena không truy vấn trực tiếp tệp .xls. Phải chuyển định dạng trước, nên phương án này chưa đáp ứng đầu vào đã cho.","FLAG_DUPLICATE_ROWS đánh dấu các dòng trùng lặp, không trực tiếp trả về số khách hàng khác nhau. Yêu cầu đếm cần COUNT_DISTINCT sau bước ghép tên.","Spark có thể đọc và xử lý dữ liệu rồi đếm distinct, nhưng phải tự viết và duy trì mã. DataBrew có sẵn các phép biến đổi cần thiết nên đơn giản hơn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA nightly cron job generates a customer data file of 1 GB size in .xls format and stores it in an Amazon S3 bucket. A data engineer is tasked with concatenating the column in the file that contains customer first names with the column that contains customer last names and then calculating the number of distinct customers in the file.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following will you suggest to address this requirement with the least operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-64":{"id":"p:2-64","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 64","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eRun containerized scripts on Amazon ECS tasks, store intermediate files in Amazon S3, and use Step Functions to coordinate the pipeline stages\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to visually build the pipeline and use EventBridge Scheduler to run each preparation job on a schedule\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDeploy AWS Glue jobs for the script-based stages and use AWS Glue workflows to schedule and orchestrate the pipeline\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to build the pipeline and operate Airflow DAGs that run the scripts on a schedule\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Container ECS và Step Functions làm được nhưng cần đóng gói runtime, image, network và task definition. Lựa chọn cũng không chỉ rõ Fargate để bảo đảm serverless.","DataBrew thiên về phép chuẩn bị trực quan; đề yêu cầu các script nhiều bước và phụ thuộc trong một workflow. Glue jobs/workflows phù hợp trực tiếp hơn.","Glue jobs thực thi script và Glue workflows lập lịch, phối hợp các bước. Đây là giải pháp ETL được quản lý, không cần tự vận hành máy chủ.","MWAA quản lý Airflow nhưng vẫn có môi trường và DAG cần vận hành. Không phải lựa chọn serverless trực tiếp mà bộ đề ưu tiên."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company needs to deploy a multi-stage data processing pipeline for claims and provider data. Each stage of the pipeline must run transformation scripts, and the data engineering team needs to schedule and coordinate the stages as one workflow. The company must use only fully managed and serverless services for the pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-6":{"id":"p:2-6","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 6","correct":[0,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUtilize the Detect PII transform in AWS Glue Studio to identify and mask the PII. Use an AWS Step Functions state machine to orchestrate a data pipeline to ingest the data into the S3 data lake\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUtilize the Detect PII transform in AWS Glue Studio to identify the PII. Set up a rule in AWS Glue Data Quality to mask the PII. Use an AWS Step Functions state machine to orchestrate a data pipeline to ingest the data into the S3 data lake\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun an AWS Glue DataBrew data profile job to identify and suggest potential PII columns present in the dataset. Execute a DataBrew job to apply the transformations to handle the sensitive columns in the entire dataset and store the masked data securely in Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIngest the dataset into Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose. Leverage a data transformation Lambda function to mask the PII data in the delivery stream and write the transformed output in Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIngest the dataset into Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Process the stream data using a Lambda function that masks the PII data before writing the output in Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Detect PII trong Glue Studio nhận diện và áp dụng xử lý che dữ liệu nhạy cảm. Step Functions điều phối pipeline ghi dữ liệu đã xử lý vào S3.","Glue Data Quality kiểm tra chất lượng bằng quy tắc; quy tắc chất lượng không phải phép biến đổi che PII. Cần dùng đúng transform xử lý dữ liệu nhạy cảm.","Profile job DataBrew giúp xác định cột có PII; recipe/job áp dụng phép che trên dataset rồi ghi S3. Đây là phương án ít mã khác có thể dùng độc lập.","Firehose với Lambda đòi hỏi tự viết mã phát hiện, che và bảo trì logic PII. Không ít công bằng các tính năng Glue/DataBrew có sẵn.","Kinesis với Lambda cũng cần tự lập trình logic che dữ liệu. Đề không yêu cầu thêm nền tảng streaming và muốn giảm công thực hiện."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is tasked with using AWS services to ingest a dataset into an Amazon S3 data lake. Upon profiling the dataset manually, the engineer finds that it contains personally identifiable information (PII). The engineer needs to devise a method to both profile the dataset and mask the PII effectively.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options can be independently used to fulfill this requirement with the least amount of operational effort? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-34":{"id":"p:4-34","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 34","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse the table metrics of the DMS task to verify the statistics for tables being migrated including the DDL statements completed\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure DMS data validation on the migration task so it can compare the source and target data for the DMS task and report any mismatches\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure DMS premigration assessment on the migration task so the assessment can compare the source and target data and report any mismatches\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Schema Conversion Tool for the migration task so it can compare the source and target data and report any mismatches\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Table metrics cho biết số thao tác insert, update, delete hoặc DDL đã xử lý. Những thống kê này không so sánh giá trị từng bản ghi giữa nguồn và đích nên không chứng minh dữ liệu khớp.","DMS data validation so sánh dữ liệu nguồn và đích rồi báo các khác biệt. Đây là tính năng cần để kiểm tra trước cutover; việc xác thực vẫn tạo thêm tải đọc nên cần cấu hình và theo dõi tác động lên nguồn.","Premigration assessment kiểm tra những vấn đề có thể cản trở di chuyển, như kiểu dữ liệu hoặc cấu hình. Nó không xác minh rằng dữ liệu đã nạp ở đích khớp nguồn.","SCT chuyển schema và mã database giữa các hệ quản trị. Nó không thay thế chức năng đối chiếu dữ liệu nguồn–đích của DMS data validation."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multi-national retail company wants to migrate its on-premises Oracle database to Aurora MySQL. The company has hired you as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to carry out the migration with minimal downtime using AWS DMS. The company has mandated that the migration must have minimal impact on the performance of the source database and you must validate that the data was migrated accurately from the source to the target before the cutover.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions will MOST effectively address this use-case?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-40":{"id":"p:2-40","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 40","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue jobs to transform the payment activity data into Apache Parquet files in Amazon S3, catalog the files in AWS Glue Data Catalog, and assign each data team a separate Athena workgroup for analysis\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon OpenSearch Service domains with indexes to search and analyze the data, and assign each data team a separate domain to configure storage and query performance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to process the data, and assign each data team a separate stream to consume\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Aurora clusters with Aurora I/O-Optimized configuration to store the data, and assign each data team a separate Aurora cluster\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Athena trên tệp S3 phù hợp phân tích, không tối ưu cho ứng dụng tìm kiếm tần suất cao và độ trễ thấp như đề.","OpenSearch dùng index cho tìm kiếm và phân tích gần thời gian thực. Tách domain giúp mỗi đội điều chỉnh năng lực, lưu trữ và hiệu năng theo workload riêng.","Kinesis và Flink tiếp nhận/xử lý luồng, không tự cung cấp lớp index và API truy vấn tìm kiếm cho người dùng cuối.","Aurora phù hợp database quan hệ; I/O-Optimized không biến nó thành công cụ tìm kiếm/index chuyên dụng như yêu cầu."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial technology company is building an application that must search and analyze payment activity data in near real time. The application must support up to 1,200 search and analytics requests each second while keeping query latency low. The company wants each data product team to own its environment and tune storage, indexing, and query performance based on that team\u0027s budget and workload requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-27":{"id":"p:1-27","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 27","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003ePush score updates to an SQS queue which uses a fleet of EC2 instances (with Auto Scaling) to process these updates in the SQS queue and then store these processed updates in an RDS MySQL database\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePush score updates to Kinesis Data Streams which uses a Lambda function to process these updates and then store these processed updates in DynamoDB\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePush score updates to Kinesis Data Streams which uses a fleet of EC2 instances (with Auto Scaling) to process the updates in Kinesis Data Streams and then store these processed updates in DynamoDB\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePush score updates to an SNS topic, subscribe a Lambda function to this SNS topic to process the updates, and then store these processed updates in a SQL database running on Amazon EC2\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Cần quản lý đội EC2 và database quan hệ; hàng đợi SQS không được nêu là FIFO. Chưa phù hợp tiêu chí ít quản lý và xử lý có thứ tự.","Kinesis tiếp nhận luồng, Lambda xử lý và DynamoDB lưu kết quả với hạ tầng được quản lý. Thứ tự cần được bảo đảm trong phạm vi shard/partition key, không phải thứ tự toàn cục của mọi sự kiện.","Kinesis và DynamoDB phù hợp, nhưng EC2 consumer đòi hỏi quản lý máy chủ và mở rộng. Lambda giảm công vận hành hơn.","SNS và database SQL trên EC2 không cung cấp thiết kế streaming có thứ tự như lựa chọn Kinesis. Database tự quản lý còn tăng vận hành."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA gaming company is developing a mobile game that streams score updates to a backend processor and then publishes results on a leaderboard. The company has hired you as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to design a solution that can handle major traffic spikes, process the mobile game updates in the order of receipt, and store the processed updates in a highly available database. The company wants to minimize the management overhead required to maintain the solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions will you suggest to address these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:47":{"id":"e:1:47","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 47","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) with Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn Internet of Things (IoT) company would like to have a streaming system that performs real-time analytics on the ingested IoT data. Once the analytics is done, the company would like to send notifications back to the mobile applications of the IoT device owners.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following AWS technologies would you recommend to send these notifications to the mobile applications?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams makes it easy to collect, process, and analyze real-time, streaming data so you can get timely insights and react quickly to new information. Amazon Kinesis Data Streams offers key capabilities to cost-effectively process streaming data at any scale, along with the flexibility to choose the tools that best suit the requirements of your application.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWith Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, you can ingest real-time data such as video, audio, application logs, website clickstreams, and IoT telemetry data for machine learning, analytics, and other applications. Amazon Kinesis Data Streams enables you to process and analyze data as it arrives and responds instantly instead of having to wait until all your data is collected before the processing can begin.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams will be great for event streaming from IoT devices, but not for sending notifications as it doesn\u0027t have such a feature.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) is a highly available, durable, secure, fully managed pub/sub messaging service that enables you to decouple microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications. Amazon SNS provides topics for high-throughput, push-based, many-to-many messaging. Amazon SNS is a notification service and will be perfect for this use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eStreaming data with Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and using Amazon SNS to send the response notifications is the optimal solution for the given scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) with Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Simple Queue Service (SQS) is a fully managed message queuing service that enables you to decouple and scale microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications. Amazon SQS eliminates the complexity and overhead associated with managing and operating message-oriented middleware and empowers developers to focus on differentiating work. Using SQS, you can send, store, and receive messages between software components at any volume, without losing messages or requiring other services to be available. Kinesis is better for streaming data since queues aren\u0027t meant for real-time streaming of data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES) is a cloud-based email-sending service designed to help digital marketers and application developers send marketing, notification, and transactional emails. It is a reliable, cost-effective service for businesses of all sizes that use email to keep in contact with their customers. It is an email service and not a notification service as is the requirement in the current use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)\u003c/strong\u003e - As explained above, Amazon Kinesis Data Streams works well for streaming real-time data. Amazon SQS is a queuing service that helps decouple system architecture by offering flexibility and ease of maintenance. It cannot send notifications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/sns/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/sns/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/ses/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/ses/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/sqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/sqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-48":{"id":"p:1-48","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 48","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) with Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Kinesis phục vụ tiếp nhận và xử lý dữ liệu luồng; SNS hỗ trợ thông báo tới các ứng dụng di động. Mỗi dịch vụ đảm nhiệm đúng một phần yêu cầu.","SNS phù hợp thông báo, nhưng SQS là hàng đợi công việc, không phải lựa chọn luồng dữ liệu phân tích mà đề đang hướng tới.","SES chuyên gửi email, không phải dịch vụ push notification tới ứng dụng di động trong trường hợp này.","SQS cho các thành phần phần mềm lấy thông điệp từ hàng đợi. Nó không thay thế SNS để gửi push notification cho ứng dụng."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn Internet of Things (IoT) company would like to have a streaming system that performs real-time analytics on the ingested IoT data. Once the analytics is done, the company would like to send notifications back to the mobile applications of the IoT device owners.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following AWS technologies would you recommend to send these notifications to the mobile applications?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:32":{"id":"e:1:32","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 32","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge to decouple the system architecture\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) for effective decoupling of system architecture\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) to decouple the architecture\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) to communicate between systems and decouple the architecture\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA CRM company has a software as a service (SaaS) application that feeds updates to other in-house and third-party applications. The SaaS application and the in-house applications are being migrated to use AWS services for this inter-application communication.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you suggest to asynchronously decouple the architecture?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge to decouple the system architecture\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBoth Amazon EventBridge and Amazon SNS can be used to develop event-driven applications, but for this use case, EventBridge is the right fit.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon EventBridge is recommended when you want to build an application that reacts to events from SaaS applications and/or AWS services. Amazon EventBridge is the only event-based service that integrates directly with third-party SaaS partners. Amazon EventBridge also automatically ingests events from over 90 AWS services without requiring developers to create any resources in their accounts. Further, Amazon EventBridge uses a defined JSON-based structure for events and allows you to create rules that are applied across the entire event body to select events to forward to a target. Amazon EventBridge currently supports over 15 AWS services as targets, including AWS Lambda, Amazon SQS, Amazon SNS, Amazon Kinesis Streams, and Firehose, among others. At launch, Amazon EventBridge has limited throughput (see Service Limits) which can be increased upon request, and typical latency of around half a second.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow Amazon EventBridge works:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/74bbd4ef40bc0965581e60bbc38aa958d507d41fcf37411f240c4d8255e4b218.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/74bbd4ef40bc0965581e60bbc38aa958d507d41fcf37411f240c4d8255e4b218.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) to communicate between systems and decouple the architecture\u003c/strong\u003e - As discussed above, Amazon SNS can be used for event-based services. However, the given use case needs integration with third-party SaaS services, hence Amazon EventBridge is the right choice, as Amazon SNS does not support third-party services integration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) to decouple the architecture\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon SQS is a message queuing service from Amazon and works well for decoupling applications. It does not directly integrate with third-party SaaS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) for effective decoupling of system architecture\u003c/strong\u003e - Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) offers a synchronous decoupling of applications, which is not the right fit for the current use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/sns/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/sns/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:40":{"id":"e:6:40","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 40","correct":[2],"answers":["Set the provisioned capacity to match the highest observed demand during peak periods.","Update the table’s class to DynamoDB Standard Infrequent Access (DynamoDB Standard-IA).","Update the table’s capacity to use AWS Application Auto Scaling based on minimum and maximum utilization.","Switch to an on-demand capacity mode for the table."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team manages an Amazon DynamoDB table configured with provisioned capacity mode. The table uses a primary key to uniquely identify the items stored by the application. The table experiences a highly predictable traffic pattern, with peaks occurring at midnight every day. These peaks are followed by a period of low activity in the morning, which gradually transitions to normal levels for the remainder of the day.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team wants to maintain the application\u0027s performance during peak times and save costs during low usage periods.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution meets this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon DynamoDB\u003c/strong\u003e allows for two capacity modes for managing throughput costs: provisioned and on-demand. In provisioned capacity mode, the user specifies the number of reads and writes per second as required by the application. This mode is cost-effective for predictable workloads where the throughput does not frequently change. On-demand capacity mode, on the other hand, offers flexible billing and automatically accommodates the table\u0027s workload without requiring manual scaling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Application Auto Scaling\u003c/strong\u003e enables automatic adjustment of resources to maintain consistent, predictable performance at the lowest possible cost. It supports scheduling, which allows for capacity planning based on known usage patterns. This feature is particularly useful for applications with predictable load patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/895a982dbd4b418dc1aed971dc09ca501d759fa9cb2a5863bc49abe835291edf.png\" alt=\"Table capacity configuration for Amazon DynamoDB auto scaling settings\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eImplementing \u003cstrong\u003eAWS Application Auto Scaling for DynamoDB\u003c/strong\u003e allows for the dynamic adjustment of provisioned capacity to align with the application’s workload patterns, ensuring high performance during peak times and cost savings during off-peak times. This approach leverages the predictable nature of the workload, enhancing resource utilization efficiency without the need for manual intervention or the costs associated with maintaining excessive provisioned capacity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate the table’s capacity to use AWS Application Auto Scaling based on minimum and maximum utilization.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet the provisioned capacity to match the highest observed demand during peak periods\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;In a provisioned capacity mode, you pay for the specified capacity regardless of actual usage. Therefore, matching capacity to peak demand without adjusting for periods of low activity only results in paying for unneeded capacity during those lower periods, which is not cost-effective.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate the table’s class to DynamoDB Standard Infrequent Access (DynamoDB Standard-IA)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;DynamoDB Standard-IA is primarily used in scenarios where data is accessed less frequently, offering lower storage costs while maintaining the same performance levels as the standard storage class. However, reading from and writing to these tables costs more than the Standard class, making it pricier for tables that are frequently used, such as the one in the scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSwitch to an on-demand capacity mode for the table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. The on-demand capacity mode charges for each read or write, unlike the provisioned mode, where you pay for a set capacity. For tables with predictable traffic, using provisioned capacity often works out cheaper. This is because you can set your capacity to cover the expected throughput, avoiding the costs tied to the per-action pricing of the on-demand mode.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/AutoScaling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/AutoScaling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/autoscaling/application/userguide/what-is-application-auto-scaling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/autoscaling/application/userguide/what-is-application-auto-scaling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon DynamoDB Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-30":{"id":"p:1-30","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 30","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate Amazon Redshift row-level security policies that use multi-condition SQL predicates for division membership and data sensitivity, attach the policies to the shared tables, and grant each division role access through the appropriate policies\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eApply Amazon Redshift dynamic data masking policies to the PII columns in the clinical study tables, grant all research divisions SELECT access to the shared base tables, and leverage the masking policies to prevent cross-division access to records\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate one Amazon Redshift datashare for each research division and use cross-Region data sharing to provide each division with a copy of the full clinical study dataset\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate Amazon Redshift row-level security policies that use simple division filters, attach the policies to the shared tables, and grant EXPLAIN RLS permission to the researchers\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["RLS với điều kiện kết hợp đơn vị và độ nhạy dữ liệu giới hạn những hàng mỗi vai trò được thấy ngay trên bảng dùng chung. Không cần nhân bản dataset.","Dynamic data masking che giá trị cột, không tự chặn truy cập vào các hàng của đơn vị khác. Cần row-level security để lọc bản ghi.","Datashare không tự thay thế điều kiện lọc từng hàng theo đơn vị. Chia sẻ toàn bộ tập dữ liệu không đáp ứng giới hạn bản ghi được xem.","Chỉ lọc đơn vị chưa thể hiện điều kiện về độ nhạy. EXPLAIN RLS liên quan việc xem kế hoạch có thông tin RLS, không bổ sung chính sách bảo vệ dữ liệu."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA biomedical research organization stores clinical study analysis data in an Amazon Redshift cluster in the us-east-1 Region. Several research divisions need to query the same dataset for regulatory reporting and exploratory analysis. The organization must avoid creating duplicate copies of the dataset. Researchers must see only the records that belong to their own divisions, and the organization must protect personally identifiable information (PII) from users who do not have authorization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:63":{"id":"e:5:63","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 63","correct":[2],"answers":["Use AWS Glue to transfer data from each database to Amazon Redshift and query the data using SQL.","Run custom code using an AWS Lambda function to extract data from databases. Store the extracted data in Amazon DynamoDB. Use Amazon Athena for querying.","Leverage Amazon Athena Federated Query to deploy and configure the appropriate data source connectors for each database engine and then run queries using Amazon Athena.","Use Amazon EMR to run big data frameworks like Apache Spark or Hadoop over the data in these databases."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA global e-commerce company is experiencing rapid growth and expanding its operations to multiple regions worldwide. The company’s data is stored in various database engines, including MySQL, PostgreSQL, and MongoDB. The data engineering team is tasked with creating a unified view of all these data sources for analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution would meet this requirement with the LEAST operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena Federated Query is a feature of Amazon Athena, AWS’s interactive query service, that makes it easy to analyze data in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. Federated Query extends Athena’s capabilities by allowing users to perform SQL queries across multiple data sources beyond Amazon S3. This includes a wide range of databases and services, both relational and non-relational, such as MySQL, PostgreSQL, Amazon DynamoDB, and others, without having to move the data into a single repository.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/47d3399c1fb03470c05e9b170c31c24bc804ae679f27e16a345cd145b1dab82f.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eConnectors must be created using AWS Lambda functions to enable Federated Query in Amazon Athena. These connectors serve as intermediaries, allowing Athena to execute SQL queries on a variety of data sources by translating the queries into the target data source’s native query language. AWS offers built-in connectors for several common databases, simplifying integration. For data sources without a pre-built connector, custom connectors can be developed. This approach allows querying nearly any data store directly from Athena, enabling complex analysis across various datasets without data movement or duplication.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage Amazon Athena Federated Query to deploy and configure the appropriate data source connectors for each database engine and then run queries using Amazon Athena.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue to transfer data from each database to Amazon Redshift and query the data using SQL\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Transferring data to Amazon Redshift requires additional steps for data loading and maintenance, making it more complex than using Athena Federated Query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun custom code using an AWS Lambda function to extract data from databases. Store the extracted data in Amazon DynamoDB. Use Amazon Athena for querying\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Although AWS Lambda functions can be typically used to extract data, storing it in DynamoDB and then querying with Athena adds unnecessary complexity. This approach does not leverage the direct querying capability of Athena Federated Query, which is designed to query data directly from various data sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon EMR to run big data frameworks like Apache Spark or Hadoop over the data in these databases\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because Amazon EMR is primarily used for processing large amounts of data using popular big data frameworks. This approach would allow for complex data processing and analysis across multiple sources. However, it’s more complex and resource-intensive than necessary for simply creating a unified view of existing databases.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-a-data-source.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-a-data-source.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:9:2":{"id":"e:9:2","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 9 Â· CÃ¢u 2","correct":[0],"answers":["Enable the \"Reuse Query Results\" feature for each query and set a maximum age of 7 days for reusing query results","Modify the query slightly every time to ensure fresh results are obtained for accurate weekly sales reports","Set a maximum per-query data scan limit of 1GB in Amazon Athena","Use Amazon Redshift to store the weekly sales data and run queries from there"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data analytics team in a large retail company frequently runs complex queries on Amazon Athena to generate weekly sales reports. These reports are essential for their marketing and stock management strategies. The data relevant to these reports does not change during the report generation period. The team is exploring methods to optimize their query performance and reduce costs while maintaining the accuracy and timeliness of the reports.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich approach should the team adopt to achieve these objectives while querying their sales data in Amazon Athena?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena’s “Reuse Query Results” feature allows users to reuse the last stored query result when re-running a query, enhancing performance and reducing costs. This is especially beneficial when the underlying data does not change within a certain time frame, like in weekly sales report generation. By enabling this feature and setting a maximum reuse age of 7 days, the team can efficiently utilize the previously stored results without re-running the query, ensuring timely and cost-effective report generation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5d2ec1a9c6c1c0a01814e6e64f280bf46e1ef0446ec626262a5e89aa7466c786.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable the “Reuse Query Results” feature for each query and set a maximum age of 7 days for reusing query results.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift to store the weekly sales data and run queries from there\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While Amazon Redshift is a powerful data warehousing solution, it does not address the specific need to optimize query performance using the existing Athena setup and its ‘Reuse Query Results’ feature.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eModify the query slightly every time to ensure fresh results are obtained for accurate weekly sales reports\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Modifying the query prevents the reuse of previous results, thereby not leveraging the ‘Reuse Query Results’ feature’s benefits in scenarios where the underlying data remains unchanged.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet a maximum per-query data scan limit of 1GB in Amazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Imposing a strict data scan limit could lead to query failures, especially for complex queries that require scanning data beyond the set limit. This approach is primarily beneficial for cost control, not for optimizing query performance in scenarios like generating weekly sales reports where data completeness and accuracy are critical.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eResources:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/reusing-query-results.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/reusing-query-results.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"http://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/reduce-cost-and-improve-query-performance-with-amazon-athena-query-result-reuse/\"\u003ehttp://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/reduce-cost-and-improve-query-performance-with-amazon-athena-query-result-reuse/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:57":{"id":"e:3:57","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 57","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDefine the target column with the SUPER data type and load the semi-structured JSON values into the Redshift table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the JSON files in Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the nested attributes separately from the Redshift warehouse\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue to flatten every JSON attribute into separate relational columns before loading the data into the Redshift target table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConvert the JSON attribute into a VARCHAR column, store the raw JSON string, and parse every nested field manually in each downstream SQL query\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA marketing analytics company receives customer enrichment records from an external data provider and loads the records into an Amazon Redshift data warehouse. The same warehouse already stores campaign data and purchase history. Analysts want to combine these datasets to identify customers who are likely to respond to future offers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDuring ingestion, a data engineer discovers that one attribute in the third-party feed contains nested JSON values with varying structures. The company wants to load this field into Amazon Redshift with the least transformation effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow should the data engineer load the JSON field into the data warehouse?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDefine the target column with the SUPER data type and load the semi-structured JSON values into the Redshift table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe SUPER data type is the best fit because Amazon Redshift supports semi-structured data such as JSON through SUPER columns. The data engineer can store nested and schema-flexible JSON values in the same Redshift table as structured customer attributes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach minimizes transformation work. The engineer does not need to flatten every nested JSON key before loading the data, which is useful when the third-party field has varying structures across records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAfter loading the JSON values into a SUPER column, analysts can query the semi-structured attributes together with structured warehouse tables such as purchases, products, and campaigns. This meets the requirement to combine the third-party data with existing Redshift data while using the least effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/19bc1b73273cc695da60e62a136b5340d25c195548e9768a42e7d8fb9e3826fe.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/19bc1b73273cc695da60e62a136b5340d25c195548e9768a42e7d8fb9e3826fe.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/work-with-semistructured-data-using-amazon-redshift-super/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/work-with-semistructured-data-using-amazon-redshift-super/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5b869f6c4c9f74c8c682e8e7d1c5c8993ec2ccbfaf87c2b7e176611e67db097f.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5b869f6c4c9f74c8c682e8e7d1c5c8993ec2ccbfaf87c2b7e176611e67db097f.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/work-with-semistructured-data-using-amazon-redshift-super/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/work-with-semistructured-data-using-amazon-redshift-super/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the JSON files in Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the nested attributes separately from the Redshift warehouse\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Athena can query data directly in Amazon S3 and can work well for ad hoc analysis over data lake files. This can be useful when teams do not need to load all data into a warehouse.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option does not meet the requirement because the company needs to load the customer data into Amazon Redshift and combine it with existing warehouse tables. Keeping the JSON data outside Redshift adds extra query coordination and does not provide the least-effort warehouse loading path.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue to flatten every JSON attribute into separate relational columns before loading the data into the Redshift target table\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue can transform JSON data and flatten nested fields before loading the results into Amazon Redshift. This approach can work when the target schema is stable and analysts need fully relational columns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that flattening all JSON attributes increases ETL development and maintenance. Because the JSON field has varying structures, using a SUPER column avoids unnecessary schema redesign and custom transformation logic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConvert the JSON attribute into a VARCHAR column, store the raw JSON string, and parse every nested field manually in each downstream SQL query\u003c/strong\u003e - A VARCHAR column can store raw JSON text, and Redshift provides functions that can extract values from JSON strings. This might work for simple JSON fields with predictable structures.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not provide the least-effort or best-supported approach for nested semi-structured data. AWS recommends using SUPER for semi-structured data instead of storing JSON as plain text and repeatedly parsing it in downstream queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/super-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/super-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/query-super.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/query-super.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/work-with-semistructured-data-using-amazon-redshift-super/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/work-with-semistructured-data-using-amazon-redshift-super/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:55":{"id":"e:2:55","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 55","correct":[1,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS CodePipeline to coordinate the ETL workflow and infrastructure deployments, and define the AWS Glue ETL job dependencies as pipeline stages\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue workflows to create a visual graph of the ETL jobs, triggers, and dependencies that run the multi-step data processing pipeline\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Step Functions to coordinate the AWS Glue ETL jobs, load stages, and dependency logic, and configure retry and error handling behavior in the state machine\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to create a DAG that coordinates the AWS Glue ETL jobs and Redshift load tasks on a managed Airflow environment\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge rules to start AWS Glue ETL jobs when new S3 objects arrive, chain additional EventBridge rules for later stages, and manage complex dependencies across rules\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company is building a serverless, multi-stage data processing pipeline in AWS. The pipeline reads raw claims data from an Amazon S3 data lake, transforms the data by using several AWS Glue ETL jobs, and loads the curated results into an Amazon Redshift database. A data engineer must orchestrate the workflow, manage dependencies between stages, and keep the pipeline serverless.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solutions will meet these requirements? (Choose TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Step Functions to coordinate the AWS Glue ETL jobs, load stages, and dependency logic, and configure retry and error handling behavior in the state machine\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Step Functions is a serverless orchestration service that can coordinate AWS Glue jobs as part of a state machine. The data engineer can model each pipeline stage as a state, invoke AWS Glue ETL jobs, and move to the next stage only when the previous step completes successfully.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eStep Functions also supports workflow-level error handling. The data engineer can define Retry and Catch behavior to handle job failures, transient service errors, and fallback paths without writing a custom orchestration framework.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution fits the requirement because the pipeline is multi-step and serverless. Step Functions keeps orchestration separate from ETL code, which makes the dependencies, retries, failure handling, and Redshift load sequence easier to manage and monitor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue workflows to create a visual graph of the ETL jobs, triggers, and dependencies that run the multi-step data processing pipeline\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue workflows are designed to orchestrate complex ETL activities that involve AWS Glue jobs, crawlers, and triggers. A workflow can represent the relationships between these components as a graph, which helps the data engineer visualize dependencies across the pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eGlue workflows can run jobs on demand, on a schedule, or through triggers. This makes them suitable for coordinating multiple Glue ETL jobs that extract data from Amazon S3, transform the data, and prepare downstream loads into Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution also remains serverless because AWS Glue manages the ETL execution environment and workflow orchestration components. The data engineer does not need to provision orchestration servers or build a custom dependency engine.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c5977a6d0a5eb6e96de0c1325bc4d08549ff77908523cffadacf65137bd6ea13.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c5977a6d0a5eb6e96de0c1325bc4d08549ff77908523cffadacf65137bd6ea13.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f531e622ef07640fffc44bdd4cbca5ab03b855be1dc6a3cf9cae87656af1ed49.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f531e622ef07640fffc44bdd4cbca5ab03b855be1dc6a3cf9cae87656af1ed49.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge rules to start AWS Glue ETL jobs when new S3 objects arrive, chain additional EventBridge rules for later stages, and manage complex dependencies across rules\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EventBridge can respond to S3 object creation events and can invoke AWS Glue workflows or jobs. It is useful for event-driven pipeline starts and service event routing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, chaining many EventBridge rules to manage complex ETL dependencies is not the best orchestration pattern. EventBridge rules do not provide the same end-to-end workflow graph, dependency management, and coordinated retry structure that Step Functions or AWS Glue workflows provide.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS CodePipeline to coordinate the ETL workflow and infrastructure deployments, and define the AWS Glue ETL job dependencies as pipeline stages\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS CodePipeline orchestrates software release workflows, such as source, build, test, and deployment stages. It can coordinate infrastructure and application delivery activities.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis ETL requirement focuses on data pipeline orchestration, not continuous delivery of application or infrastructure changes. CodePipeline is not the right service to manage recurring data transformation dependencies across Glue jobs and Redshift loads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to create a DAG that coordinates the AWS Glue ETL jobs and Redshift load tasks on a managed Airflow environment\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow can orchestrate data pipelines and can coordinate AWS Glue jobs. It is a managed service for Apache Airflow and can be appropriate for teams that already use Airflow DAGs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the question asks for serverless ETL orchestration with the least direct fit among the given choices. Step Functions and AWS Glue workflows provide AWS-native serverless orchestration for Glue-centric pipelines with less platform complexity than maintaining Airflow DAGs and an MWAA environment.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-error-handling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-error-handling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-amazon-redshift-based-etl-workflows-with-aws-step-functions-and-aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-amazon-redshift-based-etl-workflows-with-aws-step-functions-and-aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:65":{"id":"e:1:65","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 65","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eFeed the streaming transactions into Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Leverage AWS Lambda integration to remove sensitive data from every transaction and then store the cleansed transactions in Amazon DynamoDB. The internal applications can consume the raw transactions off the Amazon Kinesis Data Stream\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFeed the streaming transactions into Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose. Leverage AWS Lambda integration to remove sensitive data from every transaction and then store the cleansed transactions in Amazon DynamoDB. The internal applications can consume the raw transactions off the Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePersist the raw transactions into Amazon DynamoDB. Configure a rule in Amazon DynamoDB to update the transaction by removing sensitive data whenever any new raw transaction is written. Leverage Amazon DynamoDB Streams to share the transaction data with the internal applications\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eBatch process the raw transactions data into Amazon S3 flat files. Use S3 events to trigger an AWS Lambda function to remove sensitive data from the raw transactions in the flat file and then store the cleansed transactions in Amazon DynamoDB. Leverage DynamoDB Streams to share the transaction data with the internal applications\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company runs its flagship web application on AWS. The application serves thousands of users during peak hours. The company needs a scalable near-real-time solution to share hundreds of thousands of financial transactions with multiple internal applications. The solution should also remove sensitive details from the transactions before storing the cleansed transactions in a document database for low-latency retrieval.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you recommend?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFeed the streaming transactions into Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Leverage AWS Lambda integration to remove sensitive data from every transaction and then store the cleansed transactions in Amazon DynamoDB. The internal applications can consume the raw transactions off the Amazon Kinesis Data Stream\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to build custom applications that process or analyze streaming data for specialized needs.\nAmazon Kinesis Data Streams manages the infrastructure, storage, networking, and configuration needed to stream your data at the level of your data throughput. You don\u0027t have to worry about provisioning, deployment, or ongoing maintenance of hardware, software, or other services for your data streams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow Amazon Kinesis Data Streams Work:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d25a0d11ef63c99886c7296c83480acafa4c5b61e4c92655fce197e621ce183d.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d25a0d11ef63c99886c7296c83480acafa4c5b61e4c92655fce197e621ce183d.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams Key Concepts:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/89068c2aff8f2555ab2ad59db2fed635c43b91ddff3a471e3f4f55651094821a.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/89068c2aff8f2555ab2ad59db2fed635c43b91ddff3a471e3f4f55651094821a.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/33cd7371a04c5dc2dbbc9396c9ee8ec7b9ede872f25a488e3978c5b7ce907dea.jpg\"\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/key-concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/key-concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the given use case, you can stream the raw financial transactions into Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, which in turn, are processed by the AWS Lambda function that is set up as one of the consumers of the data stream. The Lambda would remove sensitive data from every transaction and then store the cleansed transactions in Amazon DynamoDB. The internal applications can be configured as the other consumers of the data stream and ingest the raw transactions\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eBatch process the raw transactions data into Amazon S3 flat files. Use S3 events to trigger an AWS Lambda function to remove sensitive data from the raw transactions in the flat file and then store the cleansed transactions in Amazon DynamoDB. Leverage DynamoDB Streams to share the transaction data with the internal applications\u003c/strong\u003e- The use case requires a near-real-time solution for cleansing, processing, and storing the transactions, so using a batch process would be incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFeed the streaming transactions into Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose. Leverage AWS Lambda integration to remove sensitive data from every transaction and then store the cleansed transactions in Amazon DynamoDB. The internal applications can consume the raw transactions off the Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose is an extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that reliably captures, transforms, and delivers streaming data to data lakes, data stores, and analytics services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b58bad26f75fea37e93638ae44e062e9606bf86789742d6d37c0dd116bd7dadd.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b58bad26f75fea37e93638ae44e062e9606bf86789742d6d37c0dd116bd7dadd.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou cannot set up multiple consumers for Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose delivery streams as it can dump data in a single data repository at a time, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePersist the raw transactions into Amazon DynamoDB. Configure a rule in Amazon DynamoDB to update the transaction by removing sensitive data whenever any new raw transaction is written. Leverage Amazon DynamoDB Streams to share the transaction data with the internal applications\u003c/strong\u003e - There is no such rule within Amazon DynamoDB that can auto-update every time a new item is written in a DynamoDB table. You would need to use an Amazon DynamoDB trigger to invoke an external service like a Lambda function on every new write, which can then cleanse and update the item. In addition, this process introduces inefficiency in the workflow as the same item is written and then updated for cleansing purposes. Therefore this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/key-concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/key-concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:26":{"id":"e:6:26","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 26","correct":[1],"answers":["Implement AWS Kinesis Data Firehose for streaming S3 data lake contents to an Amazon OpenSearch cluster. Use OpenSearch Dashboards for visualizing data quality metrics.","Connect the S3 data lake as a data set in AWS Glue DataBrew and create a profiling job for evaluating data quality metrics.","Develop AWS Glue ETL jobs using the Deequ Spark library for daily data validation. Store ETL outputs in S3 and instruct analysts to use Amazon Athena for querying and visualizing data quality metrics.","Set up an Amazon EMR cluster with Apache Spark and Apache Zeppelin. Conduct a daily transfer of S3 data to the cluster\u0027s Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS). Use Spark MLlib for quality checks and Zeppelin notebooks for result visualization."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA marketing agency runs daily sales campaigns and stores the results in their Amazon S3 data lake. They want to implement a new platform that allows analysts to visually verify data quality against set standards before any transformation or analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich set of actions can fulfill the requirements with the least amount of management overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that helps data analysts with data preparation tasks such as data profiling, cleaning, and normalizing all without the need to write any code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can create your own data quality rules to validate business requirements for specific datasets. These rules enable you to check for issues such as duplicate values in columns or to ensure uniqueness between columns. You can manage these rules by grouping them into rule sets and applying them during profile jobs. The system displays the results on a dashboard, offering a clear and concise view of how well the data complies with the set business rules.\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7524687b59974ce9a6a082a22426eef0ef3c6fabdb26b4bfed61a98ee685d2ad.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003eSince AWS Glue DataBrew is serverless, you do not need to provision or manage any infrastructure to use it. With AWS Glue DataBrew, you can easily and quickly create and execute data preparation recipes through a visual interface. The service automatically scales up or down based on the size and complexity of your data, so you only pay for the resources you use.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTherefore, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConnect the S3 data lake as a data set in AWS Glue DataBrew and create a profiling job for evaluating data quality metrics.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement AWS Kinesis Data Firehose for streaming S3 data lake contents to an Amazon OpenSearch cluster. Use OpenSearch Dashboards for visualizing data quality metrics\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. This approach involves the unnecessary complexity of streaming data from S3 using Kinesis Data Firehose when the data is already stored in the S3 data lake. Moreover, OpenSearch is more oriented towards real-time data streaming and visualization, making it an overkill solution for the scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;Develop AWS Glue ETL jobs using the Deequ Spark library for daily data validation. Store ETL outputs in S3 and instruct analysts to use Amazon Athena for querying and visualizing data quality metrics\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Deequ is an open-source library built on top of Apache Spark to verify data quality at scale.\u0026nbsp;While it excels at unit testing data for quality constraints, it\u0027s not primarily tailored for visual data profiling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an Amazon EMR cluster with Apache Spark and Apache Zeppelin. Conduct a daily transfer of S3 data to the cluster\u0027s Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS). Use Spark MLlib for quality checks and Zeppelin notebooks for result visualization\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Deploying and managing an Amazon EMR cluster typically involves more administrative overhead. Additionally, using Spark\u0027s MLlib for data quality assessments in this scenario introduces complexity that may be unnecessary, as the requirement is for visual data profiling and not machine learning.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/databrew\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/databrew\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-event-driven-data-quality-pipelines-with-aws-glue-databrew/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-event-driven-data-quality-pipelines-with-aws-glue-databrew/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-55":{"id":"p:1-55","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 55","correct":[1,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eTerminated Amazon EC2 instances can be recovered if they are configured at the launch of instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eA recovered instance is identical to the original instance, including the instance ID, private IP addresses, Elastic IP addresses, and all instance metadata\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIf your instance has a public IPv4 address, it retains the public IPv4 address after recovery\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDuring instance recovery, the instance is migrated during an instance reboot, and any data that is in memory is retained\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIf your instance has a public IPv4 address, it does not retain the public IPv4 address after recovery\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Instance đã terminated không thể được phục hồi bằng cơ chế EC2 recovery này. Recovery xử lý sự cố của instance còn tồn tại.","Theo cơ chế recovery được đề hỏi, instance giữ định danh, private IP, Elastic IP và metadata khi được phục hồi trên hạ tầng khác.","Public IPv4 được giữ lại sau recovery trong tình huống này. Đừng nhầm recovery với thao tác stop/start thông thường.","Recovery có quá trình khởi động lại, dữ liệu trong RAM không được giữ. Chỉ nói instance giữ danh tính không có nghĩa giữ bộ nhớ đang chạy.","Khẳng định mất public IPv4 trái với hành vi recovery được nêu trong lời giải. Đáp án đúng là địa chỉ đó được giữ."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team at a social media company wants to use Amazon CloudWatch alarms to automatically recover Amazon EC2 instances if they become impaired. The team has hired you to provide subject matter expertise.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following statements would you identify as CORRECT regarding this automatic recovery process? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-60":{"id":"p:4-60","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 60","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an on-demand AWS Glue trigger, run a Lambda function every 10 minutes to check the S3 bucket, and start the workflow when the file exists\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a scheduled AWS Glue trigger that starts the workflow every 10 minutes, and configure the first job to stop when the daily file is missing\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an event-based AWS Glue trigger, log S3 PutObject events to CloudTrail, and use EventBridge to forward matching events to the AWS Glue workflow\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure S3 Event Notifications to invoke a Lambda function for each upload, and have the function start only the first AWS Glue job in the workflow\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Polling mỗi 10 phút có thể phát hiện file nhưng tạo độ trễ tối đa gần một chu kỳ kiểm tra. Cần xử lý ngay khi file đến nên trigger theo sự kiện phù hợp hơn.","Khởi chạy workflow liên tục khi chưa có file lãng phí tài nguyên, đặc biệt với nhiều worker. Lịch cố định không khớp thời điểm upload thay đổi từng ngày.","Ghi S3 PutObject dưới dạng CloudTrail data event, dùng EventBridge khớp sự kiện và gửi tới Glue workflow có event trigger. Luồng bắt đầu theo lúc file đến thay vì chạy thăm dò định kỳ.","Gọi riêng job đầu bằng Lambda không tương đương khởi động workflow và chuỗi phụ thuộc của nó. Nếu dùng Lambda thì phải điều phối đúng workflow; lựa chọn này chỉ nêu chạy một job."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team runs an AWS Glue workflow that processes a large supplier inventory file and loads the transformed records into Amazon Redshift. The file arrives in an Amazon S3 bucket once each day, but the upload time changes because the supplier generates the file after its own reconciliation process finishes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe AWS Glue workflow uses many workers because the company wants the file processed as soon as possible after arrival. The team needs to avoid delays from periodic polling or scheduled checks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:6":{"id":"e:2:6","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 6","correct":[1,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a new security group that allows traffic from the same IPs as specified in the current Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new security group with the Amazon CloudFront distribution\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an origin access control (OAC) and associate it with the Amazon CloudFront distribution. Set up the permissions in the Amazon S3 bucket policy so that only the OAC can read the objects\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS WAF ACL and use an IP match condition to allow traffic only from those IPs that are allowed in the Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new AWS WAF ACL with the Amazon CloudFront distribution\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a new NACL that allows traffic from the same IPs as specified in the current Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new NACL with the Amazon CloudFront distribution\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Web Application Firewall (AWS WAF) ACL and use an IP match condition to allow traffic only from those IPs that are allowed in the Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new AWS WAF ACL with the Amazon S3 bucket policy\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team at a company is moving the static content from the company\u0027s logistics website hosted on Amazon EC2 instances to an Amazon S3 bucket. The team wants to use an Amazon CloudFront distribution to deliver the static content. The security group used by the Amazon EC2 instances allows the website to be accessed by a limited set of IP ranges from the company\u0027s suppliers. Post-migration to Amazon CloudFront, access to the static content should only be allowed from the aforementioned IP addresses.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich options would you combine to build a solution to meet these requirements? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an origin access control (OAC) and associate it with the Amazon CloudFront distribution. Set up the permissions in the Amazon S3 bucket policy so that only the OAC can read the objects\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you use Amazon CloudFront with an Amazon S3 bucket as the origin, you can configure Amazon CloudFront and Amazon S3 in a way that provides the following benefits:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eRestricts access to the Amazon S3 bucket so that it\u0027s not publicly accessible\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eMakes sure that viewers (users) can access the content in the bucket only through the specified Amazon CloudFront distribution—that is, prevents them from accessing the content directly from the bucket, or through an unintended CloudFront distribution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo do this, configure Amazon CloudFront to send authenticated requests to Amazon S3, and configure Amazon S3 to only allow access to authenticated requests from Amazon CloudFront. Amazon CloudFront provides two ways to send authenticated requests to an Amazon S3 origin via origin access control (OAC).\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eExam Alert:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou should note that AWS recommends using OAC because it supports:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAll Amazon S3 buckets in all AWS Regions, including opt-in Regions launched after December 2022\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 server-side encryption with AWS KMS (SSE-KMS)\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDynamic requests (POST, PUT, etc.) to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS WAF ACL and use an IP match condition to allow traffic only from those IPs that are allowed in the Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new AWS WAF ACL with the Amazon CloudFront distribution\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS WAF is a web application firewall that lets you monitor the HTTP and HTTPS requests that are forwarded to your protected web application resources. You can protect the following resource types:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon CloudFront distribution\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon API Gateway REST API\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eApplication Load Balancer\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS AppSync GraphQL API\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Cognito user pool\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS WAF also lets you control access to your content. Based on conditions that you specify, such as the IP addresses that requests originate from or the values of query strings, your protected resource responds to requests either with the requested content, with an HTTP 403 status code (Forbidden), or with a custom response.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIf you want to allow or block web requests based on the IP addresses that the requests originate from, create one or more IP match conditions via your AWS WAF. An IP match condition lists up to 10,000 IP addresses or IP address ranges that your requests originate from.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the given use case, you should add those IP addresses that are allowed in the Amazon EC2 security group into the IP match condition.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Web Application Firewall (AWS WAF) ACL and use an IP match condition to allow traffic only from those IPs that are allowed in the Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new AWS WAF ACL with the Amazon S3 bucket policy\u003c/strong\u003e - You cannot associate an AWS WAF ACL with an Amazon S3 bucket policy.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a new NACL that allows traffic from the same IPs as specified in the current Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new NACL with the Amazon CloudFront distribution\u003c/strong\u003e - NACL is associated with a subnet within a VPC. Amazon CloudFront delivers your content through a worldwide network of data centers called edge locations. So a NACL cannot be associated with an Amazon CloudFront distribution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a new security group that allows traffic from the same IPs as specified in the current Amazon EC2 security group. Associate this new security group with the Amazon CloudFront distribution\u003c/strong\u003e - A security group acts as a virtual firewall for your Amazon EC2 instances to control incoming and outgoing traffic. Inbound rules control the incoming traffic to your instance, and outbound rules control the outgoing traffic from your instance. Amazon CloudFront delivers your content through a worldwide network of data centers called edge locations. So a security group cannot be associated with Amazon CloudFront distribution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/private-content-restricting-access-to-s3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/private-content-restricting-access-to-s3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/what-is-aws-waf.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/what-is-aws-waf.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/classic-web-acl-ip-conditions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/classic-web-acl-ip-conditions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-57":{"id":"p:1-57","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 57","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure a Spark Streaming application on an Amazon EMR cluster to poll the S3 source bucket, process uploaded files in batches, and write the three outputs to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue workflow with an EventBridge event trigger, and run three AWS Glue Spark jobs in parallel to process the file and write the outputs to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an Amazon ECS service on AWS Fargate to watch the source bucket, start three container tasks for each uploaded file, and write the generated DataFrames to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure a Step Functions state machine as the EventBridge target, and use the state machine to start an AWS Glue workflow that runs three AWS Glue jobs in parallel\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Polling S3 bằng ứng dụng Spark trên EMR thêm cụm cần quản lý và không tận dụng trực tiếp sự kiện tệp mới.","EventBridge kích hoạt Glue workflow; ba Glue Spark job chạy song song để tạo và ghi ba đầu ra. Phù hợp tệp lớn và giảm thành phần điều phối tự quản lý.","Fargate giảm quản lý máy chủ nhưng vẫn cần tự xây container, theo dõi bucket và điều phối ba tác vụ. Glue workflow hỗ trợ sẵn mô hình xử lý này.","Thêm Step Functions chỉ để khởi động một Glue workflow tạo tầng điều phối dư thừa. EventBridge có thể khởi động workflow theo cấu hình của đề."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company receives a 900-GB claims extract in an Amazon S3 source bucket. When the file arrives, the company must start a data pipeline that applies business rules and creates three separate DataFrames for claims, provider summaries, and payer exceptions. The pipeline must write all three outputs to a different S3 bucket in parallel. The pipeline must be the target of an Amazon EventBridge rule that matches new file uploads to the source bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST maintenance overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:58":{"id":"e:2:58","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 58","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eRun an AWS Batch array job that divides the CSV file into chunks, validates the chunks in parallel, and uses custom code to track failed records across all child jobs\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse an AWS Step Functions Distributed Map state to process the CSV records in parallel and set ToleratedFailurePercentage to 12\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDeploy an Amazon EMR cluster with Apache Spark to process the CSV file in parallel and configure custom Spark logic to stop the job when failed validations exceed 12%\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Glue job that reads the CSV file from Amazon S3, repartitions the data in Spark, validates the records, and uses a custom accumulator to stop processing when the failed-record percentage exceeds 12%\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA payment processing company receives a daily CSV file in an Amazon S3 bucket. The file contains several million settlement records that must be validated before the company updates a transaction database. The data engineering team needs a serverless solution that processes the records in parallel. The workflow must stop the entire processing run if more than 12% of the records fail validation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse an AWS Step Functions Distributed Map state to process the CSV records in parallel and set ToleratedFailurePercentage to 12\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Step Functions Distributed Map is designed for large-scale parallel workloads. It can read a CSV file from Amazon S3 by using an ItemReader and can process each row through child workflow executions, which fits the requirement to validate millions of transaction records in parallel.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2188722553d1cbd5aa61cd55c67a5f64eddc2c365a165f1b1f212a206afa9814.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2188722553d1cbd5aa61cd55c67a5f64eddc2c365a165f1b1f212a206afa9814.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/state-map-distributed.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/state-map-distributed.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDistributed Map supports failure thresholds by count or percentage. The data engineer can set ToleratedFailurePercentage to 12 so the Map Run fails automatically when failed item processing exceeds the allowed threshold. This directly satisfies the requirement to stop the entire process when more than 12% of records fail validation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational overhead because Step Functions provides managed orchestration, parallelization, execution tracking, and failure-threshold handling. The company does not need to run clusters, build a custom distributed counter, manage queue fan-out logic, or maintain custom job-control code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/fd720b511c1ac07aa8ec9f63a2fa723f2ee83c39a267c94f54c8b68e9376acdf.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/fd720b511c1ac07aa8ec9f63a2fa723f2ee83c39a267c94f54c8b68e9376acdf.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/step-functions-distributed-map-a-serverless-solution-for-large-scale-parallel-data-processing/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/step-functions-distributed-map-a-serverless-solution-for-large-scale-parallel-data-processing/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d5f249ee36522f511fd7644b3ec5e0603979eaa569b318fd9469d347a8eac5ab.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d5f249ee36522f511fd7644b3ec5e0603979eaa569b318fd9469d347a8eac5ab.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/step-functions-distributed-map-a-serverless-solution-for-large-scale-parallel-data-processing/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/step-functions-distributed-map-a-serverless-solution-for-large-scale-parallel-data-processing/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun an AWS Batch array job that divides the CSV file into chunks, validates the chunks in parallel, and uses custom code to track failed records across all child jobs\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Batch can run parallel workloads, and an array job can process chunks of a large file. However, the team must build and maintain chunking logic, job coordination, error aggregation, and the global failure-threshold mechanism.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option adds more operational overhead than Step Functions Distributed Map. The requirement is serverless-style orchestration with built-in failure threshold handling, which Distributed Map provides directly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy an Amazon EMR cluster with Apache Spark to process the CSV file in parallel and configure custom Spark logic to stop the job when failed validations exceed 12%\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR with Apache Spark can process large CSV files in parallel. Spark can validate records and can implement custom logic to calculate the failure percentage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option requires the company to configure, scale, monitor, and manage an EMR cluster or EMR job runtime. It also requires custom failure-threshold logic, which adds overhead compared with using a managed Distributed Map field.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Glue job that reads the CSV file from Amazon S3, repartitions the data in Spark, validates the records, and uses a custom accumulator to stop processing when the failed-record percentage exceeds 12%\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue can run Spark ETL jobs and can process large files from Amazon S3. It can also repartition data to improve parallel processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the failure-threshold behavior would require custom Spark logic and careful job control. Step Functions Distributed Map provides the parallel record processing and tolerated failure percentage setting with less custom code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/state-map-distributed.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/state-map-distributed.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/input-output-itemreader.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/input-output-itemreader.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-error-handling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-error-handling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/step-functions-distributed-map-a-serverless-solution-for-large-scale-parallel-data-processing/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/step-functions-distributed-map-a-serverless-solution-for-large-scale-parallel-data-processing/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:13":{"id":"e:7:13","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 13","correct":[0,1],"answers":["Turn on the full transaction log settings on the on-premises SQL Server.","\u003cspan\u003eCreate a DMS task in Full load plus \u003ccode\u003eCHANGE DATA CAPTURE (CDC)\u003c/code\u003e mode.\u003c/span\u003e","Create an AWS Datasync task to run one-time transfers for migration of the on-premises SQL Server.","Use the AWS Schema Conversion Tool (SCT) to convert the source database schema to be compatible with the target database.","\u003cspan\u003eCreate a DMS task in \u003ccode\u003eCHANGE DATA CAPTURE (CDC) only\u003c/code\u003e mode.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA data engineering team is tasked with migrating and continuously replicating data from an on-premises Microsoft SQL Server database to Amazon \u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003eRDS\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e for SQL Server. The team intends to use AWS Database Migration Service (AWS \u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003eDMS\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e) with a \u003cspan\u003eDMS\u003c/span\u003e replication instance for both initial migration and subsequent data change synchronization. The objective is to implement a solution that requires minimal manual effort.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich combination of actions should the team take to fulfill these requirements? (Select TWO.)\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)\u003c/strong\u003e allows you to migrate databases to AWS easily and securely. It supports migrations between on-premises and AWS databases and between different AWS database services. The source or target of the migration must be on AWS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAn AWS DMS task can be created to capture ongoing changes from the source data store during the data migration process. Additionally, a task can be configured to capture ongoing changes after completing the initial full-load migration to a supported target data store. This process, known as ongoing replication or change data capture (CDC), is employed by AWS DMS when replicating ongoing changes from a source data store. The CDC process works by collecting changes to the database logs using the database engine\u0027s native API.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThere are two types of ongoing replication tasks available in AWS DMS:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFull load plus Change data capture (CDC)\u003c/strong\u003e – The task migrates existing data and then updates the target database based on changes to the source database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange data capture (CDC) only\u003c/strong\u003e – The task migrates ongoing changes after you have data on your target database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/aacbd939d2845b3486f2fb943c48ce52bb78d2135209f27a167c3ef59ca12287.png\" alt=\"AWS DMS\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThere are \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Source.SQLServer.html\"\u003edifferent prerequisites\u003c/a\u003e that must be configured in the source database to allow Change Data Capture (CDC) to take place. Most commonly, transaction logs need to be enabled, which depends on the specific database being used. For example, with Microsoft SQL Server as the source, full transaction logging must be turned on to enable CDC. On the other hand, if the source is a MySQL database, binary logging must be enabled first before CDC can be utilized.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the scenario, since the requirement is to conduct an initial migration from the on-premises Microsoft SQL Server database to Amazon RDS for SQL Server, followed by capturing ongoing changes thereafter, an AWS DMS task that runs in the Full load plus CDC (Change Data Capture) mode must be used. This mode ensures that the initial full load migration is performed, and subsequent data changes are continuously replicated to the target database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Turn on the full transaction log settings on the on-premises SQL Server.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Create a DMS task in Full load plus \u003ccode\u003eCHANGE DATA CAPTURE (CDC)\u003c/code\u003e mode.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Datasync task to run one-time transfers for migration of the on-premises SQL Server\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. AWS DataSync is primarily used for transferring files and objects between on-premises storage and AWS storage services, not for migrating databases. Also, one-time transfers would not fulfill the requirement for subsequent data change synchronization after the initial migration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the AWS Schema Conversion Tool (SCT) to convert the source database schema to be compatible with the target database\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. AWS SCT is not required in this case since the source and target databases are both SQL Server instances, and no schema conversion is necessary.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a DMS task in \u003ccode\u003eCHANGE DATA CAPTURE (CDC) only\u003c/code\u003e mode\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. This option can only work if the initial full load migration has already been completed by another method. However, the scenario requires a solution for both initial migration and subsequent data change synchronization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-on-premises-microsoft-sql-server-database-to-amazon-rds-for-sql-server.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/migrate-an-on-premises-microsoft-sql-server-database-to-amazon-rds-for-sql-server.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Source.SQLServer.html#CHAP_Source.SQLServer.Prerequisites\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Source.SQLServer.html#CHAP_Source.SQLServer.Prerequisites\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS DMS Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-database-migration-service/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-database-migration-service/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:6":{"id":"e:8:6","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 6","correct":[0],"answers":["Deploy the Lambda in the same private subnet. Allow traffic from Lambda\u0027s security group through the 5432 port in the RDS database instance\u0027s security group.","Migrate the RDS database instance to a public subnet. Configure the Lambda to connect to RDS with credentials stored in Secrets Manager.","Enable IAM DB Authentication in the DB instance. Create an IAM role that allows Amazon RDS access and attach it to the Lambda.","Deploy the Lambda in a different VPC. Allow traffic from Lambda\u0027s security group through the 5432 port in the RDS database instance\u0027s security group."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA developer wants to utilize AWS Lambda to manipulate data in their existing RDS database instance. The database instance is in a private subnet with the PosgestSQL database engine. The developer must connect to the database at the default port (5432) and run queries from the lambda without traversing the public internet.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet the requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eYou can use the \u003cstrong\u003eAmazon RDS\u003c/strong\u003e console to simplify setting up a connection between a Lambda function and a DB instance. Often, your DB instance is in a private subnet within a VPC. The Lambda function can be used by applications to access your private DB instance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1d0305108960bc3f83dee9a5fe24466c0bec6ec7f3d62fb0fd68aceeb585334d.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen connecting a Lambda function to an RDS instance in the same VPC, use the following networking configurations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eNote\u003c/strong\u003e: By default, all subnets within a VPC contain a local route. The destination is the VPC\u0027s Classless Inter-Domain Routing (CIDR), and the target is local.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor \u003cstrong\u003eSecurity Groups\u003c/strong\u003e:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor instances that are attached to the same security group, make the security group the source for the inbound rule. Make the security group the destination for the outbound rule.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor example, if the Lambda function and RDS instance are both in security group sg-abcd1234, then each instance has the following inbound and outbound rules.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6c26e55ed1fea8b6d1758a726f635b4fcafbdb59735daeb82af63c2a53dc06fe.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor instance, in different security groups, make sure that both security groups allow access to each other.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor example, if the Lambda function is in security group sg-1234 and the RDS instance is in sg-abcd, then each group has the following rules:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/bd2e47adec27f5c3c94e9c40dddeb511ee70beee8e5a72896c0cd78d894f88be.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSince you\u0027re gonna use the PostgreSQL engine, use the 5432 port in your security group configuration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy the Lambda in the same private subnet. Allow traffic from Lambda\u0027s security group through the 5432 port in the RDS database instance\u0027s security group.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMigrate the RDS Database instance to a public subnet. Configure the Lambda to connect to RDS with credentials stored in Secrets Manager\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Although this solution will allow Lambda to connect, it is unrecommended to deploy the database instance in a public subnet. It will open the database to unwanted connections. The Lambda will connect to the database through the internet, which is undesirable. For security reasons, this solution is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable IAM DB Authentication in the DB instance. Create an IAM role that allows Amazon RDS access and attach it to the Lambda\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While this solution can connect the Lambda to the RDS database instance, it still cannot satisfy the requirement not to use the internet. You can deploy the Lambda in the same private subnet and use IAM db authentication to connect to the database without a password. However, to use the IAM authentication, you must ensure that the Lambda can connect to the internet. Hence, the subnet route table needs to include a NAT gateway. The traffic will have to traverse the internet, which is undesirable.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy the Lambda in a different VPC.\u0026nbsp;Allow traffic from Lambda\u0027s security group through the 5432 port in the RDS database instance\u0027s security group\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. This option makes the network configuration more complicated without any clear benefits. Since it involves a different VPC, you would need to set up VPC peering or transit gateways to facilitate communication between the two VPCs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/connect-lambda-to-an-rds-instance\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/connect-lambda-to-an-rds-instance\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/lambda-rds-connect.html#lambda-rds-connect-connecting\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/lambda-rds-connect.html#lambda-rds-connect-connecting\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-rds.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-rds.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Lambda Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:42":{"id":"e:7:42","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 42","correct":[2],"answers":["Online","Offline","Online and Offline","Batch"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is working on a machine learning project that requires real-time feature access for model predictions and batch feature access for training models. The engineer decides to use Amazon SageMaker Feature Store for managing the features. The broader application also uses Amazon Cognito to manage user authentication for applications that consume the model\u0027s predictions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich mode of SageMaker Feature Store should the engineer choose to meet the requirements of both real-time feature access and batch feature access?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eIn Feature Store, features are stored in a collection called a\u0026nbsp;\u003cem\u003efeature group\u003c/em\u003e.\u0026nbsp;You can visualize a feature group as a table in which each column is a feature, with a unique identifier for each row. In principle, a feature group is composed of features and values specific to each feature. A\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eRecord\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;is a collection of values for features that correspond to a unique\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eRecordIdentifier\u003c/code\u003e. Altogether, a\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eFeatureGroup\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;is a group of features defined in your\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eFeatureStore\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;to describe a\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eRecord\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use Feature Store in\u0026nbsp;the following modes:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cdiv\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eOnline\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;– In online mode, features are read with low latency (milliseconds) reads and used for high throughput predictions. This mode requires a feature group to be stored in an online store.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eOffline\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;– In offline mode, large streams of data are\u0026nbsp;fed to an offline store, which can be used for training and batch inference. This mode requires a feature group to be stored in an offline store. The offline store uses your S3 bucket for storage and can also fetch data using Athena queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eOnline and Offline\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;– This includes both online and offline modes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8922c56fadcb3c2a7a23bd8e629a8753576605351f8675c221bb63d0a872bf15.png\" alt=\"SageMaker Feature Store workflow for online inference and offline model training\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can ingest data into feature groups in Feature Store in two ways: streaming or in batches. When you ingest data through streaming, a collection of records are pushed to the Feature Store by calling a synchronous\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ePutRecord\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;API call. This API enables you to maintain the latest feature values in the Feature Store and to push new feature values as soon as an update is detected.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAlternatively, Feature Store can process and ingest data in batches. You can author features using Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler, create feature groups in Feature Store, and ingest features in batches using a SageMaker Processing job with a notebook exported from Data Wrangler. This mode allows for batch ingestion into the offline store. It also supports ingestion into the online store if the feature group is configured for both online and offline use.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eOnline and Offline.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eOnline\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because it only provides low-latency real-time access to features, which is suitable for model predictions. However, it does not support batch feature access, which is needed for training models.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eOffline\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because it only supports batch feature access, which is suitable for training models. However, it does not provide real-time feature access, which is needed for model predictions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eBatch\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because there is no “Batch” mode in the Amazon SageMaker Feature Store. The Offline store provides batch feature access, but it does not provide real-time feature access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/feature-store.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/feature-store.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/simplify-iterative-machine-learning-model-development-by-adding-features-to-existing-feature-groups-in-amazon-sagemaker-feature-store/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/simplify-iterative-machine-learning-model-development-by-adding-features-to-existing-feature-groups-in-amazon-sagemaker-feature-store/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon SageMaker AI Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:30":{"id":"e:7:30","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 30","correct":[3],"answers":["Use the built-in Aggregate View.","Set up a Custom scalar user-defined function (UDF) using either a SQL SELECT clause or a Python program.","Integrate Amazon ElastiCache to cache query results.","Implement a Materialized View in Amazon Redshift."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company that provides a popular music streaming service is experiencing slow query performance when its data team tries to analyze user listening habits. The data team needs to perform complex queries on large tables containing billions of rows of data, which include user demographics, song metadata, and user activity logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe current infrastructure is struggling to handle the load, causing delays in generating insights. The data team routinely executes a \u003ccode\u003eVACUUM FULL\u003c/code\u003e operation to maintain table health, but this has not resolved the performance bottlenecks for these repeatedly executed complex queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would meet the requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMaterialized views\u003c/strong\u003e in Amazon Redshift provide a way to handle complex queries on large datasets. A materialized view contains a precomputed result set, based on an SQL query over one or more base tables. This means that the results of the query are calculated and stored when the view is created, rather than being calculated at query time. This can significantly speed up query performance on large datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMaterialized views are especially useful for speeding up queries that are predictable and repeated. Instead of performing resource-intensive queries against large tables (such as aggregates or multiple joins), applications can query a materialized view and retrieve a precomputed result set. For example, consider the scenario where a set of queries is used to populate dashboards, such as Amazon QuickSight. This use case is ideal for a materialized view because the queries are predictable and repeated over and over again.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ad9c1ac30239981d1b59c72384abcfa18aa36ead6ba1e429744c6b3583fbd24d.png\" alt=\"Materialized view aggregating total sales by event from source tables\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn addition, Amazon Redshift can automatically rewrite these queries to use materialized views, even when the query doesn’t explicitly reference a materialized view. This improves query performance because many computation steps can be skipped and the precomputed results returned directly. Unlike a simple cache, many materialized views can be incrementally refreshed when DML changes are applied on the underlying (base) tables and can be used by other similar queries, not just the query used to create the materialized view.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eImplement a Materialized View in Amazon Redshift.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e Use the built-in Aggregate View\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. Aggregate view is simply a feature in Amazon DynamoDB, not Amazon Redshift. They represent all the tables in a data model, including table column names, sample data, and all global secondary indexes associated with the table. This does not inherently improve the performance of complex queries on large datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e Set up a Custom scalar user-defined function (UDF) using either a SQL SELECT clause or a Python program\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. While user-defined functions in Amazon Redshift can be used to encapsulate complex operations, they do not inherently improve the performance of querying large datasets. A UDF executes on a single row and then returns a single value. It doesn’t benefit from parallel execution and can’t return a table or a result set.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e Integrate Amazon ElastiCache to cache query results\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. Amazon ElastiCache is an in-memory key-value store that sits between your application and the data store (database) that it accesses. It can be used for caching to improve application and database speed. However, it is not designed to handle complex queries on large datasets like the ones mentioned in the scenario. It is more suited only for scenarios where the same data is being read repeatedly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/optimize-your-amazon-redshift-query-performance-with-automated-materialized-views/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/optimize-your-amazon-redshift-query-performance-with-automated-materialized-views/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:9:3":{"id":"e:9:3","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 9 Â· CÃ¢u 3","correct":[1],"answers":["Use the CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS) command in Athena to periodically replicate data into new tables with updated partitions.","\u003cspan\u003eRun the \u003ccode\u003eMSCK REPAIR TABLE\u003c/code\u003e command in Athena to automatically discover and add the new Hive-compatible partitions from the S3 data lake.\u003c/span\u003e","Use an AWS Glue Crawler scheduled to run daily, which will automatically detect and update Athena with the new partitions in the S3 data lake.","Manually update the Athena table metadata for each new partition added to the S3 data lake."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company is managing an Amazon S3 data lake. This data lake is regularly updated with new shipment tracking information, stored in a Hive-compatible partitioned format. The company uses Amazon Athena to analyze this data. However, the data engineering team has recently encountered an issue where Athena queries fail to return results from newly added data partitions in the S3 data lake.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow can the team ensure Athena returns newly inserted data and the latest partitions with minimal development effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003eMSCK REPAIR TABLE\u003c/code\u003e command in Amazon Athena is specifically designed to address the issue of Athena not returning results from newly added data partitions. This command works by scanning the specified directory in Amazon S3, which is linked to the Athena table, to discover and add any new Hive-compatible partitions. This automatic update of the table metadata in Athena is essential for ensuring that the query engine recognizes and includes newly added data partitions in its query results. The command is particularly beneficial in environments where data partitions in S3 are frequently updated or added, as it streamlines the synchronization process between the data storage in S3 and the metadata in Athena, thereby facilitating accurate and up-to-date query results.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/47af3e63e9acb041fe53b064517dbf78c9f860a2e3b625226ccf88403840a0b5.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun the MSCK REPAIR TABLE command in Athena to automatically discover and add the new Hive-compatible partitions from the S3 data lake.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eManually update the Athena table metadata for each new partition added to the S3 data lake\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Manually updating the metadata for each new partition is a labor-intensive process and can be error-prone, especially in a dynamic environment where partitions are frequently added.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS) command in Athena to periodically replicate data into new tables with updated partitions\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This method does not directly address the issue of integrating new partitions into existing tables. Instead, it creates additional tables based on query results, which could lead to unnecessary data duplication and increased management overhead without solving the partition discovery problem efficiently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse an AWS Glue Crawler scheduled to run daily, which will automatically detect and update Athena with the new partitions in the S3 data lake\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. AWS Glue Crawlers are more suited for broader data cataloging tasks and may not provide the immediacy and specificity required for updating Athena’s metadata as efficiently as the MSCK REPAIR TABLE command.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eResources:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/msck-repair-table.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/msck-repair-table.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/athena-create-use-partitioned-tables\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/athena-create-use-partitioned-tables\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:50":{"id":"e:7:50","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 50","correct":[0],"answers":["Create a stored procedure or native function that invokes an AWS Lambda function upon loan status updates. Configure the Lambda function to forward the approval event to an Amazon SQS queue for subsequent processing actions of the systems.","Set up an Amazon RDS event subscription to trigger an AWS Lambda function upon loan status update. Configure the Lambda function to send the approval event to an Amazon SQS queue. Update the systems to read from the queue.","Create an Amazon EventBridge rule to detect changes in loan status within the database. Configure the rule to trigger an AWS Lambda function to send approval events to an Amazon SQS queue. Update the systems to read from the queue.","Configure the Aurora to publish messages directly on an Amazon SNS topic upon loan approval. Subscribe an AWS Lambda function to this topic. Configure the function to forward messages to an Amazon SQS queue for processing by the systems."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA financial company uses an Amazon Aurora database with Database Activity Streams enabled for its loan approval system. When a loan application\u0027s status is updated in the database, different processing systems must be triggered to execute various tasks.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich option would best meet the requirements?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eYou can leverage AWS Lambda functions to manage data changes. When a loan application is approved, the corresponding data needs to be removed from the current applications and forwarded to a distributed processing system. This can be achieved by invoking an AWS Lambda function from an Amazon Aurora MySQL-Compatible Edition DB cluster using a native function or a stored procedure. This approach is particularly useful when you want to integrate your Aurora MySQL database with other AWS services. For instance, you might want to capture data changes whenever a row in a table is modified in your database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b70b2a14ad630d108b7811e3cf408d09c00bc1093bb91f0ee450ee042b02a879.png\" alt=\"Aurora, Lambda, and SQS\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn this scenario, you can set up a trigger that activates a Lambda function whenever a loan is approved. The Lambda function can then be programmed to remove the approved loan data from the current applications in the Aurora database and send the removed loan data to an Amazon Simple Queue Service (SQS) queue. The distributed processing system can then consume the loan data from the SQS queue at its own pace, effectively achieving the requirement of forwarding approved loan data from the Aurora database to the processing system.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a stored procedure or native function that invokes an AWS Lambda function upon loan status updates. Configure the Lambda function to forward the approval event to an Amazon SQS queue for subsequent processing actions of the systems.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an Amazon RDS event subscription to trigger an AWS Lambda function upon loan status update. Configure the Lambda function to send the approval event to an Amazon SQS queue. Update the systems to read from the queue\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. RDS Event Subscriptions can only notify you of operational events such as instance status changes. They can\u0027t monitor table-level events like inserts or updates, which the scenario requires.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge rule to detect changes in loan status within the database. Configure the rule to trigger an AWS Lambda function to send approval events to an Amazon SQS queue. Update the systems to read from the queue\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. EventBridge doesn\u0027t directly monitor changes at the data level within Aurora databases. It would typically require some mechanism to emit those changes Lambda as events, which isn\u0027t natively supported in Amazon Aurora.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure the Aurora to publish messages directly on an Amazon SNS topic upon loan approval. Subscribe an AWS Lambda function to this topic. Configure the function to forward messages to an Amazon SQS queue for processing by the systems\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While Amazon Aurora supports invoking AWS Lambda functions based on database activity, it\u0027s not capable of directly publishing events to SNS topics without a Lambda function acting as an intermediary.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/AuroraMySQL.Integrating.Lambda.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/AuroraMySQL.Integrating.Lambda.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/capturing-data-changes-in-amazon-aurora-using-aws-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/capturing-data-changes-in-amazon-aurora-using-aws-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Aurora Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-aurora/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-aurora/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:46":{"id":"e:2:46","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 46","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUpdate the existing code that writes data to Amazon S3, so that it also invokes the Boto3 AWS Glue create_partition API call\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue crawler to run on a daily schedule\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSchedule an AWS Lambda function to run the AWS Glue CreatePartition API twice each day\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eManually execute the MSCK REPAIR TABLE command from the AWS Glue console on demand\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company intends to organize its Amazon S3 storage used for a data lake by implementing partitioning that has the S3 object keys in the format: s3://bucket/prefix/year=2023/month=01/day=01. A data engineer is tasked with ensuring that the AWS Glue Data Catalog remains updated in sync with any new partitions added to the S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution would best meet these requirements while minimizing latency?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate the existing code that writes data to Amazon S3, so that it also invokes the Boto3 AWS Glue create_partition API call\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePartitioning is an important technique for organizing datasets so they can be queried efficiently. It organizes data in a hierarchical directory structure based on the distinct values of one or more columns. For example, you might decide to partition your application logs in Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) by date, broken down by year, month, and day. Files that correspond to a single day\u0027s worth of data are then placed under a prefix such as s3://my_bucket/logs/year=2018/month=01/day=23/.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePartitioning means organizing data into directories (or \"prefixes\") on Amazon S3 based on a particular property of the data. Such properties are called partition keys. A common partition key is the date or some other unit of time such as the year or month. However, a dataset can be partitioned by more than one key. For example, data about product sales might be partitioned by date, product category, and market.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the given use case, you need to update the existing code that writes data to Amazon S3, so that it also invokes the Boto3 AWS Glue create_partition API call.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue crawler to run on a daily schedule\u003c/strong\u003e - A daily schedule to invoke the Glue Crawler invites severe latency into the solution. So, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eManually execute the MSCK REPAIR TABLE command from the AWS Glue console on demand\u003c/strong\u003e - This option has the worst outcome in terms of latency, so this is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSchedule an AWS Lambda function to run the AWS Glue CreatePartition API twice each day\u003c/strong\u003e - A schedule that invokes a Lambda function twice a day run the AWS Glue CreatePartition API will have siginificant latency to sync any new partitions. So, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://boto3.amazonaws.com/v1/documentation/api/latest/reference/services/glue/client/create_partition.html\"\u003ehttps://boto3.amazonaws.com/v1/documentation/api/latest/reference/services/glue/client/create_partition.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/alter-table-add-partition.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/alter-table-add-partition.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/get-started-managing-partitions-for-amazon-s3-tables-backed-by-the-aws-glue-data-catalog/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/get-started-managing-partitions-for-amazon-s3-tables-backed-by-the-aws-glue-data-catalog/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-10":{"id":"p:4-10","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 10","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 Object Lambda to inspect each object during read requests, compare the three date values, and return only valid records to Athena while writing invalid records to a separate S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Quality to create a custom rule that evaluates the three date columns, enable row-level outcomes, and write records that fail the rule to a second S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to create DATEDIFF columns for the two date gaps, inspect the calculated columns, and export records with invalid date differences to a second S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse an AWS Glue crawler to update the AWS Glue Data Catalog, create filters on the three date columns, and configure Athena to read only records that satisfy the filter conditions\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Object Lambda kiểm tra lúc đọc, trong khi yêu cầu là kiểm tra chất lượng trong pipeline ETL và tách bản ghi lỗi. Cách này cần thêm mã tùy chỉnh và không tận dụng cơ chế kết quả theo từng dòng của Glue Data Quality.","Dùng quy tắc tùy chỉnh của Glue Data Quality để kiểm tra order_date \u003c pickup_date \u003c arrival_date. Bật kết quả theo từng dòng giúp xác định bản ghi vi phạm và ghi chúng sang bucket khác để xử lý riêng.","Tính DATEDIFF giúp tìm khoảng cách giữa các ngày, nhưng lựa chọn còn cần thao tác kiểm tra và tách lỗi riêng. Glue Data Quality tích hợp quy tắc kiểm tra và kết quả từng dòng vào pipeline phù hợp hơn.","Crawler khám phá schema và cập nhật Data Catalog, không thực thi quy tắc chất lượng cho từng dòng. Lọc khi truy vấn cũng không tự tạo luồng lưu riêng những bản ghi lỗi theo yêu cầu."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company uses AWS Glue ETL pipelines to process order fulfillment data. The company uses Amazon Athena to analyze curated data that is stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. To improve delivery performance reporting, the company adds pickup_date and arrival_date fields to the existing order_date field. The company must validate that pickup_date is later than order_date and that arrival_date is later than pickup_date. Records that fail the validation must be written to a separate S3 bucket for review.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:32":{"id":"e:5:32","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 32","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eNEST_TO_ARRAY\u003c/code\u003e transformation.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eUNNEST_MAP\u003c/code\u003e transformation.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eNEST_TO_MAP\u003c/code\u003e transformation.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eUNNEST_ARRAY\u003c/code\u003e transformation.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company gathers observations on different animal species and compiles them into a CSV file with attributes such as \u003ccode\u003eAnimal_ID\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eAnimal_Name\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eScientific_Name\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eStatus\u003c/code\u003e and many others.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe company aims to transform this data into a flat JSON format using AWS Glue DataBrew, where each key represents a column name and its corresponding value from the rows. In the output, the original order of the columns from the CSV does not matter.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following can meet the requirements with the LEAST amount of coding effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew\u003c/strong\u003e is a visual data preparation tool that makes it easy to clean and normalize data for analysis and reporting without the need for writing code. It provides a broad set of transformations that can be applied to data sets to prepare them for analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA \u003cstrong\u003eDataBrew recipe\u003c/strong\u003e is a set of data transformation steps that are applied sequentially to clean, normalize, and prepare data. Here are some of those recipe steps:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eNEST_TO_ARRAY\u003c/strong\u003e converts selected columns into an array while maintaining their order and typecasting them into a common data type.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eNEST_TO_MAP\u003c/strong\u003e converts user-selected columns into key-value pairs, with each key representing the column name and the corresponding row value as the value. Column order is not preserved, and different column data types are typecast to a common type.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUNNEST_MAP\u003c/strong\u003e separates a map column into key-value pairs, generating a row for each pair. It only works for one map column level.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUNNEST_ARRAY\u003c/strong\u003e function extracts array elements into separate rows, allowing only one level of an array column to be unnested.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/bd4e44236904a5bcf47680223aa9fa727067b7844cbb179d121b00d1235a7e3b.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003eNEST_TO_MAP\u003c/code\u003e transformation takes each row of the DataFrame and nests the column values into a map using the column names as keys. This converts the data into \u003ccode\u003eJSON\u003c/code\u003e format where each row becomes a JSON object, meeting the needs described in the question.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e Use the \u003ccode\u003eNEST_TO_MAP\u003c/code\u003e transformation.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eNEST_TO_ARRAY\u003c/code\u003e transformation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because this transformation simply converts data into an array, not into a key-value pair structure required for \u003ccode\u003eJSON\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eUNNEST_MAP\u003c/code\u003e transformation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect as UNNEST_MAP is more suitable for expanding map data into separate columns, not for creating nested JSON structures from flat data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eUNNEST_ARRAY\u003c/code\u003e transformation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because this transformation is primarily used to expand arrays into separate rows or columns, which does not align with the goal of converting to a \u003ccode\u003eJSON\u003c/code\u003e structure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.data-structure.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.data-structure.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/transformations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/transformations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:12":{"id":"e:6:12","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 12","correct":[0],"answers":["Amazon AppFlow","Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA)","AWS Step Functions","Amazon EventBridge"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn organization plans to ingest data from external software-as-a-service (SaaS) applications and store it in an Amazon S3 bucket. The organization will then analyze the data using Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich AWS service would meet this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon AppFlow\u003c/strong\u003e is a fully managed integration service that enables secure, private data transfer between AWS services and SaaS applications with minimal operational overhead. It allows for the direct integration of SaaS applications like Salesforce, ServiceNow, Slack, and many others with AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b7e1361174f3ea9b8065737e40dbf85c912517b1497ce47599186f2c6b3a1ef5.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon AppFlow simplifies the process of securely transferring data between SaaS applications and AWS services, thereby significantly reducing the operational overhead associated with data integration tasks. It supports various data transformation capabilities, which means that data can be formatted and transformed as needed during the transfer process, ensuring that the data stored in Amazon S3 is ready for analysis in Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon AppFlow.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because Amazon MWAA is a managed service for orchestrating complex workflows using Apache Airflow. It requires more operational overhead compared to Amazon AppFlow for the use case of simply transferring data from SaaS applications to AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Step Functions\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because AWS Step Functions is primarily used for coordinating multiple AWS services into serverless workflows. While it can handle complex workflows involving AWS services and external systems, it introduces unnecessary complexity and operational overhead for the specific task of transferring data from SaaS applications to Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon EventBridge\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because Amazon EventBridge is a serverless event bus service designed for application integration and event-driven computing. Although it can route data from various sources to AWS services, it is not specifically optimized for the direct transfer of data from SaaS applications to AWS services like Amazon AppFlow is.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/what-is-appflow.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/what-is-appflow.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/appflow/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/appflow/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon AppFlow Cheat Sheet\u003c/strong\u003e:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-appflow/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-appflow/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-9":{"id":"p:4-9","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 9","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUpdate the capacity mode from provisioned to on-demand so that the table\u0027s capacity automatically adjusts to the traffic variations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eImplement AWS Application Auto Scaling to adjust provisioned capacity according to usage patterns. Set higher capacity during anticipated peak times and reduce it during periods of low activity\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd Global Secondary Index as well as Local Secondary Index to the DynamoDB table to ensure maximum performance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eImplement AWS Application Auto Scaling to adjust provisioned capacity to meet the maximum demand seen in the last 12 months\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["On-demand tiện cho nhu cầu khó đoán. Ở đây giờ cao điểm đã biết trước, nên bộ đề ưu tiên provisioned kết hợp điều chỉnh theo lịch để chủ động tài nguyên và chi phí.","Application Auto Scaling có thể tăng provisioned capacity trước sáng thứ Hai và giảm vào cuối tuần theo lịch. Cách này chuẩn bị sẵn tài nguyên cho đợt tăng đã dự đoán, đồng thời tránh duy trì công suất cao cả tuần.","GSI và LSI tạo thêm cách truy vấn dữ liệu, không tự giải quyết thiếu năng lực đọc/ghi. Thêm index không cần thiết còn tăng chi phí và công ghi dữ liệu.","Giữ công suất bằng mức cao nhất trong 12 tháng gây lãng phí khi nhu cầu thấp. Đề yêu cầu thay đổi năng lực theo các giai đoạn sử dụng đã biết."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses an Amazon DynamoDB table in provisioned capacity mode to store data for an application that experiences predictable demand. Activity spikes sharply every Monday morning, while weekends see very low usage. The company needs a solution that ensures consistent application performance during these peak periods in a cost-effective manner.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is the best solution to meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:45":{"id":"e:3:45","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 45","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eModify the execution schedule of the AWS Glue crawler from 4 hours to 1 minute\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon CloudWatch Events with the rate (5 minutes) expression to execute the AWS Glue crawler every 5 minutes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena to directly analyze the current data in Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eInvoke the AWS Glue crawler via an AWS Lambda function that is triggered by an S3:ObjectCreated:* event notification on the S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media company uses an ad-hoc Kinesis Firehose-based solution to ingest raw data in JSON format and then deliver it to an Amazon S3 bucket. The data engineering team at the company uses Apache Spark SQL to analyze this data via Amazon EMR, which is configured to use AWS Glue Data Catalog as the metastore. An AWS Glue crawler runs every four hours to update the schema of the data catalog. The team has noticed that it sometimes obtains outdated data. You have been hired by the company as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to build a solution for ensuring that the team always has access to the current data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents the best solution to meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eInvoke the AWS Glue crawler via an AWS Lambda function that is triggered by an S3:ObjectCreated:* event notification on the S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b399e10b8388045abe6adefd91f07f2680436755049d1cde960510cadcbe3858.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b399e10b8388045abe6adefd91f07f2680436755049d1cde960510cadcbe3858.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAn AWS Glue crawler is a program that connects to a data store (source or target) such as Amazon S3, progresses through a prioritized list of classifiers to determine the schema for your data, and then creates metadata tables in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. You can use a Lambda function that is triggered by an S3:ObjectCreated:* event notification on the S3 bucket to invoke the AWS Glue crawler on-demand. This obviates the need to periodically run the crawler on a schedule to update the new data in the existing data catalog tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eData Lake Architecture:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e61ad343ef727be9750880df512235eb793d802eee1667eaa6492dea698244fe.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e61ad343ef727be9750880df512235eb793d802eee1667eaa6492dea698244fe.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-and-automate-a-serverless-data-lake-using-an-aws-glue-trigger-for-the-data-catalog-and-etl-jobs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-and-automate-a-serverless-data-lake-using-an-aws-glue-trigger-for-the-data-catalog-and-etl-jobs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon CloudWatch Events with the rate (5 minutes) expression to execute the AWS Glue crawler every 5 minutes\u003c/strong\u003e - CloudWatch Events do not support AWS Glue crawler as a destination type, so this option just acts as a distractor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eModify the execution schedule of the AWS Glue crawler from 4 hours to 1 minute\u003c/strong\u003e - For AWS Glue crawlers, the minimum precision for a schedule is 5 minutes. So this option just acts as a distractor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena to directly analyze the current data in Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - The use case mentions that the analysis must be done by using Apache Spark SQL on Amazon EMR, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-data-warehouse-schedule.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-data-warehouse-schedule.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/components-key-concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-and-automate-a-serverless-data-lake-using-an-aws-glue-trigger-for-the-data-catalog-and-etl-jobs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-and-automate-a-serverless-data-lake-using-an-aws-glue-trigger-for-the-data-catalog-and-etl-jobs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-50":{"id":"p:1-50","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 50","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) to decouple the architecture\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge to decouple the system architecture\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) to communicate between systems and decouple the architecture\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) for effective decoupling of system architecture\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["SQS tách rời các thành phần bằng hàng đợi, nhưng không có mô hình tích hợp sự kiện SaaS đối tác trực tiếp phù hợp như EventBridge trong đề.","EventBridge là event bus để nhận, lọc và định tuyến sự kiện từ ứng dụng, dịch vụ AWS và nguồn SaaS được hỗ trợ. Phù hợp giao tiếp bất đồng bộ giữa các hệ thống.","SNS phù hợp phát thông báo fan-out. Trong tình huống nhấn mạnh tích hợp nguồn SaaS, EventBridge cung cấp cơ chế kết nối và định tuyến phù hợp hơn.","ELB phân phối lưu lượng yêu cầu đến các máy chủ. Nó không phải event bus hay hàng đợi để tách rời giao tiếp bất đồng bộ."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA CRM company has a software as a service (SaaS) application that feeds updates to other in-house and third-party applications. The SaaS application and the in-house applications are being migrated to use AWS services for this inter-application communication.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you suggest to asynchronously decouple the architecture?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-14":{"id":"p:1-14","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 14","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue to transfer your table from DynamoDB to Amazon S3. Access the S3 data from Sagemaker\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAccess the data from SageMaker Notebook by reading it using the boto3 client. Initialize a DynamoDB Client and do a \u003ccode\u003eScan\u003c/code\u003e which will return all the data you need\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDirectly export your DynamoDB table into a .csv file and upload this file to Amazon S3. Access the S3 data from Sagemaker\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Data Pipeline console to export the DynamoDB table to Amazon S3. Exported JSON files are converted to comma-separated value (CSV) format to use as a data source for Amazon SageMaker\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Glue rồi S3 tạo thêm một pipeline và bản sao dữ liệu không cần thiết nếu notebook có thể đọc DynamoDB trực tiếp. Do đó không ít công vận hành nhất.","Notebook có thể dùng boto3 để gọi DynamoDB Scan và lấy dữ liệu trực tiếp. Trong thực tế cần xử lý phân trang khi kết quả lớn; ý chính là không cần chuyển qua S3 trước.","Xuất CSV rồi tải lên S3 là bước trung gian làm tăng thời gian và công việc. Trường hợp này đã có thể đọc dữ liệu qua DynamoDB API.","Lời giải gốc loại phương án dùng giao diện AWS Data Pipeline và chuyển JSON sang CSV vì thêm các bước không cần thiết. Đọc trực tiếp từ notebook đơn giản hơn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company stores its data in Amazon DynamoDb. The company needs to access this data in Amazon DynamoDb from an Amazon Sagemaker notebook for running machine learning models.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents a solution to address this requirement with the LEAST operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:6:41":{"id":"e:6:41","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 41","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eCreate a Lambda function to read S3 data and format it in a pandas dataframe. Use Python to run a \u003ccode\u003eSQL SELECT\u003c/code\u003e statement on the dataframe to query specific columns.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003e\nUtilize Amazon Athena to perform \u003ccode\u003eSQL SELECT\u003c/code\u003e queries to fetch specific columns.\u003c/span\u003e","Configure an AWS Glue DataBrew project to ingest Apache Parquet files from the S3 bucket and perform column-specific queries.","Configure an S3 Object Lambda access point that filters and retrieves only the necessary columns from the Parquet files."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA data engineer needs to perform a one-time, ad-hoc query to retrieve specific columns from a large dataset stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. The dataset contains 25 columns in Apache Parquet format and has been registered in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. The solution must be efficient, serverless, and avoid introducing operational overhead or requiring infrastructure management. \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich solution will best meet these requirements?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e is a user-friendly query service that allows users to analyze data stored in Amazon S3 by using familiar SQL queries. It operates in a serverless environment, which means there\u0027s no need to manage any infrastructure, and users only pay for the queries they execute. This makes Athena a highly efficient and cost-effective solution for ad-hoc queries or one-time operations, as it automatically scales resources based on demand.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/70c00cdaad24a2bff3f7c221e72a554b34595eb14d63ff6510b348a138d21449.png\" alt=\"Athena SELECT Column Query\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBy utilizing Amazon Athena, a data engineer can efficiently retrieve specific columns from a large Apache Parquet dataset stored in Amazon S3 without introducing additional operational overhead. This is particularly beneficial since Athena can query data directly from S3, eliminating the need for data loading or complex ETL processes. This feature significantly reduces the effort and time typically associated with moving or copying data to another storage or processing system.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMoreover, Athena\u0027s native support for the Apache Parquet columnar format makes it an ideal choice for analytical workloads. Parquet is optimized for retrieving specific columns, allowing data engineers to access the information they need quickly and efficiently. Additionally, since Athena uses standard SQL syntax, data engineers and analysts who are already familiar with SQL can easily query and analyze the data without having to learn a new query language or framework.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon Athena to perform \u003ccode\u003eSQL SELECT\u003c/code\u003e queries to fetch specific columns.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCreate an AWS Lambda function to read S3 data and format it in a pandas dataframe. Use Python to run a \u003ccode\u003eSQL SELECT\u003c/code\u003e statement on the dataframe to query specific columns\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. This method requires setting up a Lambda function and writing code, which introduces operational overhead and may not be suitable for a one-time query operation. Additionally, processing large datasets within a Lambda function may not be efficient due to the typically limited resources and execution time constraints of Lambda functions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure a Glue DataBrew project to ingest Apache Parquet files from the S3 bucket and perform column-specific queries\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While DataBrew can ingest Parquet files from S3 and perform column-specific queries, configuring a project involves multiple steps and generating recipes. For a one-time column retrieval from Parquet in S3, DataBrew\u0027s capabilities will only introduce unnecessary complexity and overhead compared to more direct solutions like Athena SQL queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an S3 Object Lambda access point that filters and retrieves only the necessary columns from the Parquet files\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. While S3 Object Lambda could retrieve specific columns, it typically serves for data transformation rather than querying specific columns directly and is not the most straightforward approach for ad-hoc querying.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/Welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/Welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/how-to-optimize-querying-your-data-in-amazon-s3/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/how-to-optimize-querying-your-data-in-amazon-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these Amazon S3 and Amazon Athena following Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:36":{"id":"e:7:36","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 36","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eFail\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eParallel\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eChoice\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSucceed\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is looking to improve the efficiency of their data processing workflows on AWS, aiming to decrease the overall processing time. The solution involves executing multiple tasks concurrently within their AWS Step Functions managed workflow.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich AWS Step Functions state should the data engineer utilize to meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eIn AWS Step Functions, the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eParallel\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;state is used to create parallel branches of execution in your state machine. AWS Step Functions automatically transitions to the next state after all branches of the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eParallel\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;state complete their tasks. This means that the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eParallel\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;state is the one to check to ensure all parallel processes have been completed successfully before moving on.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/a24820582364c76da0ffc04232ffbddb7bd6d1dbbc9c980c94e9fae1443d03d2.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eParallel\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;state causes AWS Step Functions to execute each branch, starting with the state named in that branch\u0027s\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eStartAt\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;field, as concurrently as possible and wait until all branches terminate (reach a terminal state) before processing the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eParallel\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;state\u0027s\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eNext\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;field.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eParallel.\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFail\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eFail\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;state in AWS Step Functions is used to terminate the state machine with a failure. It does not have the functionality to check the completion of parallel tasks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChoice\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eChoice\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;state simply allows for branching logic based on input data. While it can direct the workflow to different execution paths based on specified conditions, it does not support the concurrent execution of tasks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSucceed\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. This state is just\u0026nbsp;used to terminate the execution of a state machine successfully. It does not have the functionality to check the completion of parallel tasks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-states.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-states.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/amazon-states-language-parallel-state.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/amazon-states-language-parallel-state.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Step Functions Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-37":{"id":"p:2-37","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 37","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUpdate the AWS Glue security group to allow inbound traffic from the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eVerify that the route table associated with the AWS Glue job subnet includes a route to the Amazon S3 prefix list that targets the S3 gateway endpoint\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an interface VPC endpoint for AWS Glue, enable private DNS for the endpoint, and attach the interface endpoint to the same subnet that the AWS Glue job uses\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpdate the AWS Glue job code so the S3 connection details use a fully qualified domain name for the bucket endpoint\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Gateway endpoint không có ENI/security group như interface endpoint. Thêm rule inbound từ endpoint không sửa tuyến mạng tới S3.","Route table của subnet Glue cần tuyến tới S3 prefix list qua S3 gateway endpoint. Kiểm tra đúng subnet/route association thay vì chỉ xem IAM.","Glue interface endpoint phục vụ truy cập API Glue. Nó không thay thế tuyến mạng từ job tới dữ liệu S3.","Đổi mã sang tên miền đầy đủ không bổ sung tuyến bị thiếu trong route table. Nguyên nhân nằm ở cấu hình mạng."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial analytics company is configuring an AWS Glue job to read encrypted data files from an Amazon S3 bucket. The data engineer has configured an AWS Glue connection that uses a private subnet and has attached an IAM role with the required S3 permissions. When the data engineer runs the job, AWS Glue returns an error that indicates a problem with the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint for the selected subnet.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineer must resolve the connectivity issue so that the AWS Glue job can access the S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:5":{"id":"e:1:5","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 5","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDevelop an AWS Glue job using Apache Spark to join the data from all the sources\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCopy the data from all the sources into a single S3 bucket. Use Athena queries on the saved S3 data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena Federated Query to join the data from all data sources\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eProvision an EMR cluster to join the data from all the sources. Configure Spark for Athena to run the data analysis job\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data analytics job requires data from multiple sources like Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon RDS, and Amazon Redshift. The job is run on Amazon Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following is the MOST cost-effective way to join data from these sources?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena Federated Query to join the data from all data sources\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIf you have data in sources other than Amazon S3, you can use Athena Federated Query to query the data in place or build pipelines that extract data from multiple data sources and store them in Amazon S3. With Athena Federated Query, you can run SQL queries across data stored in relational, non-relational, object, and custom data sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAthena uses data source connectors that run on AWS Lambda to run federated queries. A data source connector is a piece of code that can translate between your target data source and Athena. You can think of a connector as an extension of Athena\u0027s query engine. Prebuilt Athena data source connectors exist for data sources like Amazon CloudWatch Logs, Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon DocumentDB, and Amazon RDS, and JDBC-compliant relational data sources such MySQL, and PostgreSQL\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCopy the data from all the sources into a single S3 bucket. Use Athena queries on the saved S3 data\u003c/strong\u003e - Copying data from all sources into Amazon S3 results in unnecessary storage costs on S3, therefore, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDevelop an AWS Glue job using Apache Spark to join the data from all the sources\u003c/strong\u003e - You can certainly leverage an AWS Glue job using Apache Spark to perform data analytics on these sources, however, this solution is not cost-effective.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eProvision an EMR cluster to join the data from all the sources. Configure Spark for Athena to run the data analysis job\u003c/strong\u003e - Provisioning and setting up an EMR cluster is time-consuming and it is also not cost-effective for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-a-data-source.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-a-data-source.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:10:2":{"id":"e:10:2","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 2","correct":[0],"answers":["Set up a VPC connection in QuickSight. Create inbound and outbound rules in the security groups for both services to allow traffic between them.","Create an outbound rule in the security group of the Redshift cluster to allow traffic to Quicksight’s IP address. Establish a VPN connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight and modify the network ACLs to allow traffic between them.","Create an inbound rule in the security group of the Redshift cluster to allow traffic from Quicksight’s IP address. Establish a Direct Connect connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight and modify the route tables to allow traffic between them.","Set up a VPC connection in QuickSight. Establish a VPC peering connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight’s VPC. Create inbound and outbound rules in the security groups for both services to allow traffic between them."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAn e-commerce company stores customer data in an Amazon Redshift cluster hosted in a private VPC. To safeguard sensitive data such as customer contact details, Amazon QuickSight enforces column-level security (CLS) during report generation.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eThe company aims to create visual charts of sales rankings by product category and customer demographics. To achieve this, the company\u0027s data engineer must configure QuickSight to connect to the Redshift cluster securely.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eHow can the data engineer meet the requirements in the LEAST amount of effort?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eYou can create a VPC connection in Amazon QuickSight to securely access private databases, such as an Amazon Redshift cluster, directly within your VPC. This setup is ideal for scenarios where your databases must be accessible only from certain IP addresses or within a secure environment, ensuring your data remains isolated from the public internet. When QuickSight connects to your VPC, it creates elastic network interfaces (ENIs) to facilitate a secure channel of communication directly to your resources, like a Redshift cluster, without traversing the public internet.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can connect Amazon QuickSight to the same VPC where your Amazon Redshift cluster is located. Once connected, you\u0027ll need to configure the security groups associated with both QuickSight and the Amazon Redshift cluster. The security groups act as virtual firewalls, controlling the traffic that can enter and leave the network interfaces.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the Redshift security group\u0027s configuration, the inbound rule must allow TCP traffic on port 5439 (the default port number of Redshift) from the QuickSight security group, enabling QuickSight to initiate connections to the Redshift cluster. The outbound rule should permit all TCP traffic to the QuickSight security group, allowing Redshift to send responses back to QuickSight.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c5b3cd0611879e227eb94eb58ce4877b7642d1617642e7162ff74abcae20851a.png\" alt=\"Amazon Redshift Security Group\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor QuickSight\u0027s security group, the inbound rules need to allow incoming responses from Redshift’s port range, and the outbound rules must permit initiating queries to the Redshift cluster on port 5439.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/08c668c073ee98f2a0349dee97348afac4fd638d73df55c65600e9efef1ce3ff.png\" alt=\"Amazon QuickSight Security Group\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up a VPC connection in QuickSight. Create inbound and outbound rules in the security groups for both services to allow traffic between them.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an outbound rule in the security group of the Redshift cluster to allow traffic to Quicksight’s IP address. Establish a VPN connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight and modify the network ACLs to allow traffic between them\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Establishing a VPN connection is not necessary in this scenario. The use of VPNs is more applicable to scenarios where you are connecting on-premise resources to AWS resources, not for connecting AWS services within the same region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an inbound rule in the security group of the Redshift cluster to allow traffic from Quicksight’s IP address. Establish a Direct Connect connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight and modify the route tables to allow traffic between them\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Similar to the other incorrect option,\u0026nbsp;this only introduces complexity that isn\u0027t necessary for connecting QuickSight to Redshift. Direct Connect is primarily used to create a dedicated, private connection between your on-premises data center and AWS. QuickSight, being a fully managed AWS service, does not require Direct Connect to access Redshift within AWS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up a VPC connection in QuickSight. Establish a networking VPC peering connection between the Redshift cluster and Quicksight’s VPC. Create inbound and outbound rules in the security groups for both services to allow traffic between them\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While this method is technically viable, VPC peering just adds unnecessary complexity to the given requirement. Since QuickSight can connect to Redshift through a simpler VPC connection setup, introducing VPC peering is not the most straightforward approach. It would be useful in scenarios where a Redshift cluster and QuickSight need to communicate across different VPCs\u0026nbsp;without a shared VPC connection or when cross-account VPC communication is required.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/working-with-aws-vpc.html#private-connection-to-vpc-using-quicksight\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/working-with-aws-vpc.html#private-connection-to-vpc-using-quicksight\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/quicksight-redshift-private-connection\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/quicksight-redshift-private-connection\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-quicksight-deployment-models-for-cross-account-and-cross-region-access-to-amazon-redshift-and-amazon-rds/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-quicksight-deployment-models-for-cross-account-and-cross-region-access-to-amazon-redshift-and-amazon-rds/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon QuickSight Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-quicksight/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-quicksight/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:19":{"id":"e:5:19","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 19","correct":[2],"answers":["Directly use Amazon RDS for complex querying and analysis. Implement RDS access controls to limit access.","Migrate the data to an Amazon S3 bucket and use AWS IAM for access control. Use Amazon Athena for querying and analysis.","Leverage data lake via AWS Lake Formation. Set up a JDBC connection to Amazon RDS using AWS Glue. Register your S3 bucket within Lake Formation. Implement the access controls provided by Lake Formation to limit access.","Transfer the data to Amazon Redshift for analysis. Use AWS Security Groups for access control."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company possesses a large volume of purchase data stored in an Amazon RDS instance. This data is sensitive, necessitating strict access control, including fine-grained access control across accounts. However, the company also wishes to facilitate complex querying and analysis of this data for business intelligence purposes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following meets the requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation is a service that makes it easy to set up, secure, and manage data lakes. It provides a centralized, curated, and secured repository that stores all your data, both in its original form and prepared for analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2e37e00f9f1db72c7e86c5786ff10dd9ddbf4a585cf8bcc18e31c031b5859a64.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy to prepare and load your data for analytics. It can be used to set up a JDBC connection to Amazon RDS, enabling seamless data integration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 provides scalable and secure storage for your data lake. Registering your S3 bucket within Lake Formation allows the data to be cataloged and prepared for analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eLake Formation also provides fine-grained access control to data in the lake, ensuring sensitive data is protected. This means you can control who has access to your data, what actions they can take, and which data they can access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Leverage data lake via AWS Lake Formation. Set up a JDBC connection to Amazon RDS using AWS Glue. Register your S3 bucket within Lake Formation. Implement the access controls provided by Lake Formation to limit access.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e Directly use Amazon RDS for complex querying and analysis. Implement RDS access controls to limit access\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because\u0026nbsp;While Amazon RDS is a cloud-based platform that makes it easy to set up and operate a relational database in the cloud, it is not designed for complex querying and analysis. It provides high availability, automated scaling, backup, maintenance, and updates, and offers a number of security features. However, for complex querying and analysis, additional tools like Amazon Redshift or Athena might be needed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eMigrate the data to an Amazon S3 bucket and use AWS IAM for access control. Use Amazon Athena for querying and analysis\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. While this option is possible, managing fine-grained access control with IAM policies and S3 buckets can be complex and may increase operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cstrong\u003e \u003cspan\u003eTransfer the data to Amazon Redshift for analysis. Use AWS Security Groups for access control\u003c/span\u003e \u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect.\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eAmazon Redshift is a fully managed, petabyte-scale data warehouse service in the cloud. It can be used for complex querying and analysis. AWS Security Groups can be used to grant other users inbound access to an Amazon Redshift cluster. However, transferring data to Amazon Redshift requires additional steps and resources, which could increase operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/getting-started-tutorial-jdbc.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/getting-started-tutorial-jdbc.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/integrating-aws-lake-formation-with-amazon-rds-for-sql-server/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/integrating-aws-lake-formation-with-amazon-rds-for-sql-server/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Lake Formation Cheat Sheet:\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:19":{"id":"e:3:19","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 19","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eRun ALTER TABLE ADD PARTITION for the status=completed prefix and leverage Athena to automatically remove the empty status=pending partition\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun ALTER TABLE DROP PARTITION to remove the stale status=pending partition from the AWS Glue Data Catalog metadata\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun MSCK REPAIR TABLE to remove the stale status=pending partition from the AWS Glue Data Catalog metadata\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun ALTER TABLE SET TBLPROPERTIES to update the table properties so Athena ignores the empty status=pending S3 prefix\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company stores delivery status data in an Amazon S3 bucket and uses AWS Glue Data Catalog tables so Amazon Athena can query the data. The data is partitioned by status code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer manually moves all objects from the partition path status=pending to the partition path status=completed. The status=pending S3 prefix is now empty, but the status=pending partition still appears in the AWS Glue Data Catalog metadata. The data engineer must correct the metadata discrepancy in Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich Athena command should the data engineer run?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun ALTER TABLE DROP PARTITION to remove the stale status=pending partition from the AWS Glue Data Catalog metadata\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eALTER TABLE DROP PARTITION is the correct Athena command for removing a stale partition from table metadata. In this scenario, the data engineer moved the objects from status=pending to status=completed, so the old partition location is empty but still registered in the AWS Glue Data Catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAthena uses the partition metadata in the Data Catalog to decide which S3 locations to scan. If an old partition remains registered after the S3 data is moved, Athena can still show that partition even though the underlying prefix no longer contains data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDropping the stale partition removes the incorrect metadata entry without changing the objects in Amazon S3. This directly resolves the mismatch between the S3 layout and the Glue Data Catalog partition metadata.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun ALTER TABLE SET TBLPROPERTIES to update the table properties so Athena ignores the empty status=pending S3 prefix\u003c/strong\u003e - ALTER TABLE SET TBLPROPERTIES can update table-level properties. This is useful for settings such as classification, projection configuration, or other metadata properties.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option does not remove a specific stale partition from the Data Catalog. The problem is a partition metadata entry that still points to an empty S3 prefix, so the data engineer must drop that partition.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun MSCK REPAIR TABLE to remove the stale status=pending partition from the AWS Glue Data Catalog metadata\u003c/strong\u003e - MSCK REPAIR TABLE can discover Hive-style partitions that exist in Amazon S3 and add missing partition metadata to the Data Catalog. It is commonly used when new partition folders are added outside Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that MSCK REPAIR TABLE does not remove stale partitions after data is deleted or moved from S3. It is not the right command for cleaning up a partition that still exists in metadata but no longer has data in its S3 location.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun ALTER TABLE ADD PARTITION for the status=completed prefix and leverage Athena to automatically remove the empty status=pending partition\u003c/strong\u003e - ALTER TABLE ADD PARTITION can register a new partition location in the Data Catalog. This is useful when a new S3 prefix exists and Athena needs metadata for that new partition.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAdding the status=completed partition does not remove the stale status=pending partition. Athena will not automatically delete the old partition metadata simply because another partition was added.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/alter-table-drop-partition.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/alter-table-drop-partition.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/troubleshooting-athena.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/troubleshooting-athena.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/msck-repair-table.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/msck-repair-table.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/alter-table-add-partition.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/alter-table-add-partition.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:30":{"id":"e:3:30","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 30","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up CloudWatch Logs streams in each application AWS account to forward events to CloudWatch Logs in the centralized logging AWS account. In the centralized logging AWS account, subscribe a Kinesis Data Firehose stream to Amazon CloudWatch Events and further use the Firehose stream to store the log data in S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up a new IAM role in each application AWS account with permissions to view CloudWatch Logs. Create a Lambda function to assume this new role and perform an hourly export of each AWS account\u0027s CloudWatch Logs data to an S3 bucket in the centralized logging AWS account\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up CloudWatch Logs agents to publish data to a Kinesis Data Firehose stream in the centralized logging AWS account. Create a Lambda function to read messages from the stream and push messages to Kinesis Data Firehose and then store the data in S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Kinesis Data Firehose in the logging account and then subscribe the delivery stream to CloudWatch Logs streams in each application AWS account via subscription filters. Persist the log data in an Amazon S3 bucket inside the logging AWS account\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company has multiple AWS accounts hosting its portfolio of IT applications that serve the company\u0027s retail and enterprise customers. A CloudWatch Logs agent is installed on each of the EC2 instances running these IT applications. The company wants to aggregate all security events in a centralized AWS account dedicated to log storage. The centralized operations team at the company needs to perform near-real-time gathering and collating events across multiple AWS accounts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions would you recommend to address these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up Kinesis Data Firehose in the logging account and then subscribe the delivery stream to CloudWatch Logs streams in each application AWS account via subscription filters. Persist the log data in an Amazon S3 bucket inside the logging AWS account\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can configure Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to aggregate and collate CloudWatch Logs from different AWS accounts and receive their log events in a centralized logging AWS Account (this is known as cross-account data sharing) by using a CloudWatch Logs destination and then creating a Subscription Filter. This log event data can be read from a centralized Amazon Kinesis Firehose delivery stream to perform downstream processing and analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can collaborate with an owner of a different AWS account and receive their log events on your AWS resources, such as an Amazon Kinesis or Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose stream (this is known as cross-account data sharing). You can use a subscription filter with Kinesis Streams, Lambda, or Kinesis Data Firehose. Logs that are sent to a receiving service through a subscription filter are Base64 encoded and compressed with the gzip format.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up a new IAM role in each application AWS account with permissions to view CloudWatch Logs. Create a Lambda function to assume this new role and perform an hourly export of each AWS account\u0027s CloudWatch Logs data to an S3 bucket in the centralized logging AWS account\u003c/strong\u003e - As the Lambda function is performing an hourly export, it\u0027s not a near-real-time solution. In addition, Lambda is not the right choice to build a high volume and high-velocity streaming solution which is better handled by using the Kinesis Family of services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up CloudWatch Logs agents to publish data to a Kinesis Data Firehose stream in the centralized logging AWS account. Create a Lambda function to read messages from the stream and push messages to Kinesis Data Firehose and then store the data in S3\u003c/strong\u003e - The CloudWatch Logs agent (on the path to deprecation) supports the collection of logs from only servers running Linux. It is recommended to use the unified CloudWatch agent. It enables you to collect both logs and advanced metrics with one agent. It offers support across operating systems, including servers running Windows Server. This agent also provides better performance. CloudWatch Logs agent cannot publish data to a Kinesis Data Firehose stream, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up CloudWatch Logs streams in each application AWS account to forward events to CloudWatch Logs in the centralized logging AWS account. In the centralized logging AWS account, subscribe a Kinesis Data Firehose stream to Amazon CloudWatch Events and further use the Firehose stream to store the log data in S3\u003c/strong\u003e - You can use a subscription filter with Kinesis Streams, Lambda, or Kinesis Data Firehose. So you cannot just forward events directly to CloudWatch Logs in another account. In addition, the Kinesis Data Firehose stream cannot subscribe to CloudWatch Events, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/CrossAccountSubscriptions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/CrossAccountSubscriptions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/SubscriptionFilters.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/SubscriptionFilters.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/stream-amazon-cloudwatch-logs-to-a-centralized-account-for-audit-and-analysis/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/stream-amazon-cloudwatch-logs-to-a-centralized-account-for-audit-and-analysis/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:48":{"id":"e:7:48","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 48","correct":[1],"answers":["Store the index in an OR1 storage","Add UltraWarm nodes on the cluster and move the index to it.","Move the index in a Cold storage.","Use Index State Management (ISM) to schedule the deletion of the index data."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses an Amazon OpenSearch Service cluster for log analytics. Due to regulatory compliance, the company must store a large amount of index data for an indefinite period of time. The data is accessed infrequently, but when accessed, it needs to be available immediately. The company also wants to minimize costs as much as possible.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution would meet the requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eUltraWarm nodes in Amazon OpenSearch Service utilize Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) along with associated caching solutions to enhance performance. UltraWarm provides a significantly lower cost per GiB for read-only data that is queried less frequently and doesn’t require the same performance level as hot storage. Although data in UltraWarm can’t be modified, it can be moved to the hot storage tier for modifications before being moved back.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/38364f7ef9e2d6fe9c4cc454fff592bfbbdefb889c3f1dc7bfa4886100a57b2e.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen determining UltraWarm storage needs, only the size of the primary shards is considered. When querying for the list of shards in UltraWarm, both primary and replicas are listed. However, both shards are placeholders for the same, single copy of the data, which resides in Amazon S3. The durability of data in Amazon S3 eliminates the need for replicas, and Amazon S3 abstracts away any operating system or service considerations. In the hot tier, accounting for one replica, 20 GB of index uses 40 GB of storage. In the UltraWarm tier, it’s billed at 20 GB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u0026nbsp;Add UltraWarm nodes on the cluster and move the index to it.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eStore the index in an OR1 storage\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because. OR1 instances keep a copy of the data in both local (Amazon EBS) and remote storage (Amazon S3). This contrasts with UltraWarm nodes, which are optimized to store data primarily in remote storage to reduce costs. This makes OR1 instances more expensive compared to UltraWarm.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMove the index in a Cold storage\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Although Cold storage is the most cost-effective for long-term storage of rarely accessed data, it requires a reattachment process to the cluster for the data to become accessible.\u0026nbsp;This introduces delays in data availability, contradicting the requirement for data to be available immediately when accessed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Index State Management (ISM) to schedule the deletion of the index data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. ISM is a feature in Amazon OpenSearch Service that automates routine index management tasks. It can be used to move data between different storage classes (hot, UltraWarm, and cold) based on a schedule or delete stale data.\u0026nbsp;Since there is no definite period for when to delete or continue storing the data, applying ISM isn\u0027t the right solution for this scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/ultrawarm.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/ultrawarm.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/choose-the-right-storage-tier-for-your-needs-in-amazon-opensearch-service/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/choose-the-right-storage-tier-for-your-needs-in-amazon-opensearch-service/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon OpenSearch Service Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-opensearch-service/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-opensearch-service/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-44":{"id":"p:1-44","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 44","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse a provisioned Amazon EMR cluster running Spark to ingest and process the compressed files from S3. Trigger workflows using Amazon EventBridge rules and Step Functions. Store processed data in Amazon RDS and use Amazon Managed Grafana to visualize the vehicle inventory dashboards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream incoming vehicle log files from S3 and transform them on-the-fly with AWS Lambda. Store transformed data in Amazon Redshift. Use Redshift Query Editor V2 for ad hoc queries and Amazon QuickSight for reporting dashboards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue to process the uploaded S3 data files. Configure S3 Event Notifications to trigger AWS Lambda for near real-time orchestration. Use Amazon Athena for on-demand querying of transformed data stored in S3. Use Amazon QuickSight to visualize the results through an interactive dashboard\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon OpenSearch Ingestion Pipelines to pull data from S3, process the log files, and index them into Amazon OpenSearch Service. Use OpenSearch Dashboards for real-time visualization. Enable scheduled queries with AWS Glue for historical analysis and reporting\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Cụm EMR provisioned và RDS tạo chi phí cố định, thêm quản lý cho nhiều tệp rất nhỏ. Không phù hợp tiêu chí ít vận hành của đề.","Firehose không tự đóng vai trò kéo mọi tệp vừa có trên S3 như mô tả. Thiết kế còn thêm Redshift khi phân tích trực tiếp trên S3 đã đủ.","Sự kiện S3 kích hoạt Lambda điều phối, Glue xử lý dữ liệu, Athena truy vấn và QuickSight hiển thị. Đây là phương án bộ đề chọn để tận dụng dịch vụ được quản lý; độ tươi dashboard vẫn phụ thuộc cấu hình làm mới.","OpenSearch có thể phục vụ tìm kiếm và dashboard, nhưng tạo thêm chỉ mục, hệ thống lưu trữ và quy trình phân tích lịch sử. Bộ đề ưu tiên chuỗi Glue–Athena–QuickSight trên S3."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company operates a near real-time inventory tracking system for vehicle depots across multiple geographic regions. Third-party vendors upload multiple logs of vehicle arrivals and departures in the form of small compressed files (less than 10 KB) to a central Amazon S3 bucket. The company needs to immediately process new uploads to keep a dashboard up to date. The dashboard must be refreshed near real-time to reflect the latest vehicle inventory across regions. A data engineer is tasked with designing a cost-effective, low-latency, and scalable solution that automates the processing and transformation of the uploaded data, enables ad hoc querying for business analysts, and supports visual reporting through dashboards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will best meet these requirements in the most cost-effective and scalable manner?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:56":{"id":"e:5:56","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 56","correct":[3],"answers":["Amazon Athena Capacity Reservations","Amazon Athena Query editor","Amazon Athena Workgroups","Amazon Athena Federated Query"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer wants to analyze terabytes of data stored in an Amazon Redshift cluster. The data engineer must be able to run queries directly on the cluster without the need for complex ETL jobs or setting up additional data infrastructure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich feature of Amazon Athena will allow this direct access to the data source?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Athena Federated Query\u003c/strong\u003e allows you to run SQL queries across data stored in relational, non-relational, object, and custom data sources. Athena uses data source connectors that run on AWS Lambda to run federated queries. A data source connector is a piece of code that can translate between your target data source and Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/47d3399c1fb03470c05e9b170c31c24bc804ae679f27e16a345cd145b1dab82f.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon CloudWatch Logs, Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon DocumentDB, and Amazon RDS, and JDBC-compliant relational data sources such MySQL, and PostgreSQL under the Apache 2.0 license. You can also use the Athena Query Federation SDK to write custom connectors.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use the Amazon Athena Console or the AWS Serverless Application Repository to choose, configure, and deploy a data source connector to your account. After you deploy data source connectors, they are associated with a catalog you can specify in SQL queries. You can combine SQL statements from multiple catalogs and span multiple data sources with a single query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Athena Federated Query.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Athena Query editor\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. This is simply the web interface provided by Athena for running queries. While it’s essential for interacting with Athena, the query editor itself doesn’t provide the functionality to query external data sources like Redshift without ETL processes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Capacity Reservations\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. The main purpose of provisioned capacity is to give a predictable query performance, not to extend Athena’s data access capabilities across different storage systems.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Athena Workgroups\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because workgroups in Amazon Athena are primarily used to separate query execution between teams, workloads, applications, or users. They help manage query-related resources but do not enable querying across different data sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-a-data-source.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-a-data-source.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:1":{"id":"e:1:1","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 1","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon SNS delivery status logging for all topics, send the delivery logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, create a CloudWatch Logs Insights query to search for Publish and PublishBatch activity, and share the saved query with the application team and the security team\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable data events for the Amazon SNS topics in CloudTrail, create an AWS Glue Data Catalog table for the event data, and query the records by using Amazon Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable management events for the Amazon SNS topics in CloudTrail and use CloudTrail Lake SQL queries to analyze the audit records\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable data events for the Amazon SNS topics in the existing CloudTrail Lake event data store and use CloudTrail Lake SQL queries to analyze the audit records\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media technology company runs several applications on AWS. The applications publish user activity messages to multiple Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topics in the ca-central-1 Region. The security team must audit every Publish and PublishBatch API action across all SNS topics. The application team and the security team also need to query the audit records. The company already has an AWS CloudTrail Lake event data store for centralized event analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable data events for the Amazon SNS topics in the existing CloudTrail Lake event data store and use CloudTrail Lake SQL queries to analyze the audit records\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon SNS supports CloudTrail data event logging for the Publish and PublishBatch API actions. These API calls represent data plane activity against SNS topics, so the company must enable data events for Amazon SNS topics rather than relying only on management events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCloudTrail Lake is designed to collect, store, and query CloudTrail events by using SQL. Because the company already has an event data store, enabling the required SNS data events in that event data store allows the application team and security team to query the audit data directly in CloudTrail Lake.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational overhead because it avoids a separate S3-based log pipeline, an AWS Glue Data Catalog table, Amazon Athena query setup, custom Lambda processing, or duplicate audit storage. It uses the native CloudTrail Lake query capability for the audit data that the company needs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUsing AWS CloudTrail data events to audit your Amazon SNS workloads:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f6a01249e2267bdb447aad00828d2997c3a0f1abffb3b748c513153cf1c859e9.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f6a01249e2267bdb447aad00828d2997c3a0f1abffb3b748c513153cf1c859e9.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/using-aws-cloudtrail-data-events-to-audit-your-amazon-sns-and-amazon-sqs-workloads/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/using-aws-cloudtrail-data-events-to-audit-your-amazon-sns-and-amazon-sqs-workloads/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/49881b0b3df8e6a554b3cc630db65dec62418edb10253dc663d0c7aa8ca17cd9.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/49881b0b3df8e6a554b3cc630db65dec62418edb10253dc663d0c7aa8ca17cd9.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/using-aws-cloudtrail-data-events-to-audit-your-amazon-sns-and-amazon-sqs-workloads/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/using-aws-cloudtrail-data-events-to-audit-your-amazon-sns-and-amazon-sqs-workloads/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable management events for the Amazon SNS topics in CloudTrail and use CloudTrail Lake SQL queries to analyze the audit records\u003c/strong\u003e - CloudTrail management events record control plane activity, such as creating, updating, or deleting AWS resources. Publish and PublishBatch are Amazon SNS data plane operations, so management events do not provide the required audit coverage for message publishing activity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option uses CloudTrail Lake for querying, which is appropriate for the existing event data store. However, it enables the wrong event category and therefore would not meet the requirement to audit all Publish and PublishBatch API actions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable data events for the Amazon SNS topics in CloudTrail, create an AWS Glue Data Catalog table for the event data, and query the records by using Amazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e - Enabling data events for Amazon SNS topics captures the correct API activity. However, this option adds AWS Glue Data Catalog and Amazon Athena even though the company already has a CloudTrail Lake event data store for collecting and querying events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe requirement asks for the least operational overhead. Creating and maintaining an external table and Athena query path is unnecessary when CloudTrail Lake can query the event data directly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon SNS delivery status logging for all topics, send the delivery logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, create a CloudWatch Logs Insights query to search for Publish and PublishBatch activity, and share the saved query with the application team and the security team\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon SNS delivery status logging helps monitor message delivery outcomes to supported endpoints. Delivery logging does not provide a complete CloudTrail audit trail of who called Publish or PublishBatch across all SNS topics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCloudWatch Logs Insights can query log data, but this design uses the wrong source for API audit records. The security team needs CloudTrail data events for SNS topics, not delivery status logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/logging-using-cloudtrail.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/logging-using-cloudtrail.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-lake.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-lake.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/query-event-data-store.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/query-event-data-store.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2023/09/amazon-sns-aws-cloudtrail-data-event-logging-publish-publishbatch-api-actions/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2023/09/amazon-sns-aws-cloudtrail-data-event-logging-publish-publishbatch-api-actions/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/using-aws-cloudtrail-data-events-to-audit-your-amazon-sns-and-amazon-sqs-workloads/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/using-aws-cloudtrail-data-events-to-audit-your-amazon-sns-and-amazon-sqs-workloads/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:52":{"id":"e:4:52","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 52","correct":[1,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eWrite the transformed data in Avro format and use AWS Glue Data Catalog to track schema metadata for Athena queries\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eWrite the transformed data in ORC format and use AWS Glue Data Catalog to track schema metadata for Athena queries\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eWrite the transformed data in Apache Parquet format and use AWS Glue Data Catalog to track schema metadata for Athena queries\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eWrite the transformed data in ORC format and store schema definitions in separate JSON files in Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eWrite the transformed data in Apache Parquet format and use an external DynamoDB table to track schema changes for Athena queries\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media analytics company runs Apache Spark jobs on an Amazon EMR cluster to process streaming playback events from connected devices. The Spark jobs transform the data and write the curated output to an Amazon S3 bucket. Analysts use Amazon Athena to query the curated data for viewing trends, device usage, and content engagement patterns. The company needs to choose data formats and metadata management approaches that provide the shortest Athena query times.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solutions will meet these requirements? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eWrite the transformed data in ORC format and use AWS Glue Data Catalog to track schema metadata for Athena queries\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eORC is a columnar storage format that is optimized for analytical queries. When Athena queries only selected columns, ORC allows the query engine to read less data than row-oriented formats such as JSON or CSV.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUsing AWS Glue Data Catalog gives Athena the table and schema metadata that it needs to query the ORC files in Amazon S3. The cataloged schema helps analysts query the curated streaming output without manually defining metadata for each query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution helps produce shorter query times because it combines a columnar format with a managed metadata catalog. The EMR Spark jobs can write ORC output, and Athena can use the Glue catalog table to query that data efficiently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eWrite the transformed data in Apache Parquet format and use AWS Glue Data Catalog to track schema metadata for Athena queries\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eApache Parquet is also a columnar storage format that is widely used for analytics on Amazon S3. Parquet can reduce the amount of data Athena scans when queries access only a subset of columns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Data Catalog is the correct metadata layer for Athena. The company can catalog the Parquet data, track table definitions, and allow Athena to use the catalog when running SQL queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis combination meets the requirement for the shortest query times because Parquet is optimized for columnar reads, compression, and predicate pushdown. It is a strong fit for transformed streaming data that analysts query through Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c9fc148bfeb672dafe6ea4c98f11d941f9c11526630f35516479552ed0d396ad.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c9fc148bfeb672dafe6ea4c98f11d941f9c11526630f35516479552ed0d396ad.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eWrite the transformed data in Avro format and use AWS Glue Data Catalog to track schema metadata for Athena queries\u003c/strong\u003e - Avro is a row-oriented format that supports schema evolution and is useful for data serialization. It can be appropriate for streaming pipelines and message-oriented workloads that need compact encoded records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short for Athena analytical query performance because Avro does not provide the same columnar read benefits as ORC or Parquet. When analysts query only selected columns, Athena can scan less data with columnar formats.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eWrite the transformed data in ORC format and store schema definitions in separate JSON files in Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - ORC is a valid columnar file format for Athena and can improve query performance compared with row-based formats. The EMR Spark jobs could write curated output in ORC format.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option does not meet the metadata management requirement because separate schema files in Amazon S3 are not the normal catalog that Athena uses for table metadata. Using AWS Glue Data Catalog provides the managed schema layer required for Athena queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eWrite the transformed data in Apache Parquet format and use an external DynamoDB table to track schema changes for Athena queries\u003c/strong\u003e - Apache Parquet is an excellent format for Athena analytics because it is columnar and efficient for selected-column queries. The data format portion of this option is valid.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe metadata approach is the problem. Athena does not use an external DynamoDB table as the standard schema catalog for S3 datasets. AWS Glue Data Catalog is the managed catalog that Athena uses for table definitions and schema metadata.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-data-optimization-techniques.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-data-optimization-techniques.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-s3-throttling-optimizing-your-tables.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-s3-throttling-optimizing-your-tables.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/understanding-tables-databases-and-the-data-catalog.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/understanding-tables-databases-and-the-data-catalog.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:3":{"id":"e:7:3","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 3","correct":[2],"answers":["Use Amazon S3 bucket policies to control access to AWS Glue resources.","Use AWS Glue Data Catalog encryption settings to control access to databases and ETL jobs.","Use AWS Glue Data Catalog resource policies to manage access at a granular level.","Use Amazon Athena workgroups to control access to AWS Glue resources."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is using AWS Glue for ETL jobs and Athena for querying data stored in S3. The corporation has strict security policies and requires fine-grained access control over AWS Glue resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThere are two departments in the corporation, Finance and HR. Each department has its own set of ETL jobs and databases in AWS Glue. The requirement is that the Finance department should only have access to run its own ETL jobs and query its own databases. The same applies to the HR department.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following approaches would be the most appropriate to implement this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Catalog resource policies allow you to manage access at a granular level. You can specify permissions at the database, table, and column level. This is particularly useful in scenarios where you need to restrict access to specific databases or tables within AWS Glue, such as the scenario described in the question.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the given scenario, the corporation has two departments, Finance and HR, each with its own set of ETL jobs and databases in AWS Glue. The requirement is that each department should only have access to run its own ETL jobs and query its own databases. This level of access control can be achieved using AWS Glue Catalog resource policies.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/330d02045808e2d4ea6b80d95b4a9a2637f62523aefda8ed8862539ba00a6a8a.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWith AWS Glue Catalog resource policies, you can define fine-grained access to the AWS Glue data catalog by using resource-level permissions in IAM policies. These policies can restrict access to different portions of the catalog based on users, roles, or applied at a resource level. This allows you to provide granular control over which users can access the various metadata definitions in your data lake.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn addition, it’s important to note that the AWS Glue Data Catalog policies define the access to the metadata, and the S3 policies define the access to the content itself. You can restrict which metadata operations can be performed, such as GetDatabases, GetTables, CreateTable, and others, using identity-based policies (IAM). You can also restrict which data catalog objects those operations are performed on.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog resource policies to manage access at a granular level.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon S3 bucket policies to control access to AWS Glue resources\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because Amazon S3 bucket policies are used to manage access to S3 buckets and objects, not AWS Glue resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog encryption settings to control access to databases and ETL jobs \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because encryption settings are used for data security, not for managing access to AWS Glue resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e Use Amazon Athena workgroups to control access to AWS Glue resources\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect because Amazon Athena workgroups are used to control access to Athena queries, not to control access to AWS Glue resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/fine-grained-access-to-glue-resources.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/fine-grained-access-to-glue-resources.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/restrict-access-to-your-aws-glue-data-catalog-with-resource-level-iam-permissions-and-resource-based-policies/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/restrict-access-to-your-aws-glue-data-catalog-with-resource-level-iam-permissions-and-resource-based-policies/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-34":{"id":"p:3-34","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 34","correct":[0,1,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an IAM role for Amazon Redshift which has a policy to allow Redshift to access AWS Lake Formation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an external schema in Amazon Redshift by using the Amazon Redshift IAM role\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eGrant permissions in Lake Formation to allow the Amazon Redshift IAM role to access the specific columns of the historical trades table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an internal schema in Amazon Redshift by using the Amazon Redshift IAM role\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eGrant permissions in the S3 bucket policy to allow the Amazon Redshift IAM role to access the specific columns of the historical trades table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an IAM role for Amazon Redshift which has a policy to allow Redshift to access the S3 bucket having data for the historical trades table\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Role của Redshift cần quyền tích hợp Lake Formation để dùng cơ chế quản trị dữ liệu của lake thay vì bỏ qua nó.","External schema tham chiếu Catalog/dữ liệu ngoài S3 để Spectrum truy vấn. Gắn role được cấp quyền thích hợp.","Lake Formation cấp SELECT trên những cột được phép cho role Redshift. Đây là nơi áp quyền chi tiết mà bucket policy không thực hiện được.","Internal schema chứa các bảng nội bộ Redshift, không phải định nghĩa đường truy vấn external table S3 bằng Spectrum.","Bucket policy kiểm soát object, không chọn được cột bên trong Parquet. Cần Lake Formation column permissions.","Chỉ cấp role đọc S3 trực tiếp không thực thi giới hạn cột như yêu cầu. Phải đi qua quyền Lake Formation trong thiết kế được chọn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA stock trading company uses Amazon Redshift to power the Business Intelligence (BI) specific queries which are run on Redshift. The data engineering team at the company needs to provide the sales team access to a historical trades table whose data is stored in Apache Parquet format in an S3 bucket of the company\u0027s data lake. The data engineering team should provide access to only a few specific columns in the historical trades table so that the access does not violate the compliance regulations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options should be combined together to build a solution for the given use case? (Select three)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:20":{"id":"e:4:20","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 20","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up access to the historical data via Athena. The analytics team can run historical data queries on Athena and continue the daily reporting on Redshift. In case the reports need to be cross-referenced, the analytics team needs to export these in flat files and then do further analysis\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the Redshift COPY command to load the S3-based historical data into Redshift. Once the ad-hoc queries are run for the historic data, it can be removed from Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Redshift Spectrum to create Redshift cluster tables pointing to the underlying historical data in S3. The analytics team can then query this historical data to cross-reference with the daily reports from Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Glue ETL job to load the S3-based historical data into Redshift. Once the ad-hoc queries are run for the historic data, it can be removed from Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn IT company has built a custom data warehousing solution for a large shipping company by using Amazon Redshift. The solution helps the shipping company to analyze the international/domestic cargo transportation details and operational records for the ships. As part of the cost optimizations, the shipping company now wants to move any historical data (any data older than a year) into Amazon S3, as the daily analytical reports consume data for just the last year. However, the data engineers at multiple divisions of the shipping company want to retain the ability to cross-reference this historical data along with the daily reports. The shipping company wants to develop a solution with the LEAST amount of effort and MINIMUM cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich option would you recommend for this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Redshift Spectrum to create Redshift cluster tables pointing to the underlying historical data in S3. The analytics team can then query this historical data to cross-reference with the daily reports from Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift is a fully-managed petabyte-scale cloud-based data warehouse product designed for large-scale data set storage and analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUsing Amazon Redshift Spectrum, you can efficiently query and retrieve structured and semistructured data from files in Amazon S3 without having to load the data into Amazon Redshift tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift Spectrum resides on dedicated Amazon Redshift servers that are independent of your cluster. The Redshift Spectrum pushes many compute-intensive tasks, such as predicate filtering and aggregation, down to the Redshift Spectrum layer. Thus, Redshift Spectrum queries use less of your cluster\u0027s processing capacity than other queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eRedshift Spectrum Overview\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/16f0325ad19dfed538cd42a4ec50e189cca36f8f333835d5878f4e59acabd972.gif\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/16f0325ad19dfed538cd42a4ec50e189cca36f8f333835d5878f4e59acabd972.gif\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSetup access to the historical data via Athena. The analytics team can run historical data queries on Athena and continue the daily reporting on Redshift. In case the reports need to be cross-referenced, the analytics team needs to export these in flat files and then do further analysis\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Athena is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data directly in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. Athena is serverless, so there is no infrastructure to set up or manage, and customers pay only for the queries they run. You can use Athena to process logs, perform ad-hoc analysis, and run interactive queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eProviding access to historical data via Athena would mean that historical data reconciliation would become difficult as the daily report would still be produced via Redshift. Such a setup is cumbersome to maintain on a day-to-day basis. Hence the option to use Athena is ruled out.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the Redshift COPY command to load the S3-based historical data into Redshift. Once the ad-hoc queries are run for the historic data, it can be removed from Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Glue ETL job to load the S3-based historical data into Redshift. Once the ad-hoc queries are run for the historic data, it can be removed from Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eLoading historical data into Redshift via COPY command or via Glue ETL job would be cost-heavy for a one-time ad-hoc process. The same result can be achieved more cost-efficiently by using Redshift Spectrum. Therefore both these options to load historical data into Redshift are also incorrect for the given use-case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/10-best-practices-for-amazon-redshift-spectrum/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/10-best-practices-for-amazon-redshift-spectrum/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:41":{"id":"e:1:41","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 41","correct":[1,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up AWS Lambda with AWS Step Functions to process the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eProvision Amazon EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group to process the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up AWS Fargate with Amazon ECS to process the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) to ingest the data\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company\u0027s real-time streaming application is running on AWS. As the data is ingested, a job runs on the data and takes 30 minutes to complete. The workload frequently experiences high latency due to the large volume of incoming data. A data engineer needs to design a scalable and serverless solution to enhance performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of steps do you recommend? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up AWS Fargate with Amazon ECS to process the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams (KDS) is a massively scalable and durable real-time data streaming service. KDS can continuously capture gigabytes of data per second from hundreds of thousands of sources such as website clickstreams, database event streams, financial transactions, social media feeds, IT logs, and location-tracking events. The data collected is available in milliseconds to enable real-time analytics use cases such as real-time dashboards, real-time anomaly detection, dynamic pricing, and more.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers that works with both Amazon Elastic Container Service (ECS) and Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS). Fargate makes it easy for you to focus on building your applications. Fargate removes the need to provision and manage servers, lets you specify and pay for resources per application, and improves security through application isolation by design.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the given use case, we can use Kinesis Data Streams as the ingestion layer and the containerized ECS application on AWS Fargate as the processing layer. Both these components are serverless and can scale to offer the desired performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) to ingest the data\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Database Migration Service helps you migrate databases to AWS quickly and securely. DMS cannot be used for real-time data ingestion. Hence, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up AWS Lambda with AWS Step Functions to process the data\u003c/strong\u003e - The maximum timeout value for any AWS Lambda function is 15 minutes. When the specified timeout is reached, AWS Lambda terminates the execution of your Lambda function. Since the use case talks about a job that runs for 30 minutes, AWS Lambda is not the right fit.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eProvision Amazon EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group to process the data\u003c/strong\u003e - The given requirement is for a serverless solution to process the data. Hence, provisioning an Amazon EC2 instance is clearly not the right solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/building-a-scalable-streaming-data-processor-with-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-on-aws-fargate/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/building-a-scalable-streaming-data-processor-with-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-on-aws-fargate/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:38":{"id":"e:1:38","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 38","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAccess and integrate third-party datasets available through AWS DataSync\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAccess and integrate third-party datasets available through AWS Marketplace\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAccess and integrate third-party datasets from AWS CodeCommit repositories\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAccess and integrate third-party datasets available through AWS Data Exchange\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn e-commerce company is looking to enhance its product recommendation system which relies on user behavior and preferences. The company wants to achieve this by integrating insights from third-party datasets into its existing analytics platform. The company aims to minimize the effort and time involved in this integration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution would achieve this with the least operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAccess and integrate third-party datasets available through AWS Data Exchange\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Data Exchange is a service that helps AWS easily share and manage data entitlements from other organizations at scale.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs a data receiver, you can track and manage all of your data grants and AWS Marketplace data subscriptions in one place. When you have access to an AWS Data Exchange data set, you can use compatible AWS or partner analytics and machine learning to extract insights from it. You can also discover and subscribe to new third-party data sets available through AWS Data Exchange from the AWS Marketplace catalog\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor data senders, AWS Data Exchange eliminates the need to build and maintain any data delivery and entitlement infrastructure. Anyone with an AWS account can create and send data grants to data receivers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAccess and integrate third-party datasets available through AWS DataSync\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS DataSync is an online data movement and discovery service that simplifies and accelerates data migrations to AWS as well as moving data to and from on-premises storage, edge locations, other cloud providers, and AWS Storage services. Although you could access third-party datasets, it would require a custom integration effort to consume the data within AWS services. So, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAccess and integrate third-party datasets available through AWS Marketplace\u003c/strong\u003e - You can use AWS Marketplace to find third-party software, data, and services that run on AWS and manage from a centralized location. AWS Marketplace includes thousands of software listings and simplifies software licensing and procurement with flexible pricing options and multiple deployment methods. Although you could access third-party datasets, it would require a custom integration effort to consume the data within AWS services. So, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAccess and integrate third-party datasets from AWS CodeCommit repositories\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS CodeCommit is a secure, highly scalable, managed source control service that makes it easier for teams to collaborate on code. It is not meant to provide access to third-party datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/data-exchange/latest/userguide/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/datasync/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/datasync/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/mp/marketplace-service/overview/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/mp/marketplace-service/overview/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/codecommit/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/codecommit/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:10:14":{"id":"e:10:14","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 14","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable AWS Glue Auto Scaling so the job can add and remove workers automatically while reading the historical S3 files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRewrite the historical pipeline to compact all files with an EMR Spark job before running the AWS Glue job for forecasting preparation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the number of AWS Glue workers so Spark has more executors available to read the small files in parallel\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS Glue DynamicFrame file grouping by using groupFiles and groupSize when the job reads the small S3 files\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics company uses AWS Glue ETL jobs to collect and process transaction data into an Amazon S3 bucket. The ingestion pipeline writes one small file every hour, and each file is between 250 KB and 350 KB. The company now needs to train a demand forecasting model by processing 5 years of historical data that contains 45,000 small files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer creates a second AWS Glue ETL job with the smallest worker type to read the historical files from Amazon S3 and prepare the dataset for downstream analysis. The job runs slowly because Spark creates many tasks for a large number of small files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will improve the performance of the second pipeline MOST cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure AWS Glue DynamicFrame file grouping by using groupFiles and groupSize when the job reads the small S3 files\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DynamicFrame file grouping is the most cost-effective solution for this scenario. The performance issue comes from processing a large number of very small files, which can cause Spark to create excessive tasks and add scheduling overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe groupFiles and groupSize options allow AWS Glue to group multiple small S3 files into larger logical units when reading the data. This reduces the number of Spark tasks that the job needs to manage and improves throughput without requiring more worker capacity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach directly addresses the small-file problem. Because the input contains 45,000 files, explicit grouping is useful because automatic grouping is enabled only when the input contains more than 50,000 files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ee3b5693346871e0c9c8ff530dba7b099cd597faabf8c0e77ac1e8f701052e11.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ee3b5693346871e0c9c8ff530dba7b099cd597faabf8c0e77ac1e8f701052e11.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/best-practices-to-scale-apache-spark-jobs-and-partition-data-with-aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/best-practices-to-scale-apache-spark-jobs-and-partition-data-with-aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable AWS Glue Auto Scaling so the job can add and remove workers automatically while reading the historical S3 files\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue Auto Scaling can help jobs adjust worker capacity based on workload demand. It can reduce the need to manually choose a fixed number of workers for some Spark workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that Auto Scaling does not directly solve excessive task creation caused by many small input files. File grouping targets the root cause more directly and avoids adding capacity before reducing Spark scheduling overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the number of AWS Glue workers so Spark has more executors available to read the small files in parallel\u003c/strong\u003e - Increasing the number of workers can add more Spark executors and can help when a job is compute-bound or needs more parallel processing capacity. This can improve some large ETL workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because the problem is not simply a lack of workers. The job is inefficient because it reads many small files, so adding workers can increase cost without reducing the task overhead caused by the small-file layout.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRewrite the historical pipeline to compact all files with an EMR Spark job before running the AWS Glue job for forecasting preparation\u003c/strong\u003e - Compacting small files into larger files can improve long-term analytics performance. Amazon EMR Spark jobs can perform large-scale compaction when a team needs a separate processing environment.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis creates additional operational overhead and cost. The requirement asks to improve the second AWS Glue pipeline, and AWS Glue already provides DynamicFrame file grouping to reduce small-file read overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/grouping-input-files.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/grouping-input-files.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-s3-home.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-s3-home.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/parallelize-tasks.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/parallelize-tasks.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/best-practices-to-scale-apache-spark-jobs-and-partition-data-with-aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/best-practices-to-scale-apache-spark-jobs-and-partition-data-with-aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:35":{"id":"e:6:35","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 35","correct":[0],"answers":["Use AWS Glue to crawl the data sources and store the metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. With Amazon Athena, use SQL and PartiQL to query structured and semi-structured data respectively.","Utilize AWS Glue to crawl data sources, saving metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Run SQL queries using Redshift spectrum.","Utilize AWS Glue to crawl data sources, with metadata stored in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Transform JSON format data to Apache Parquet or .csv using AWS Glue jobs, storing transformed data in S3. Query both original and transformed data using Amazon Athena.","Configure Amazon RDS to directly query data using federated queries for RDS, DynamoDB, and S3. Store JSON and .csv data in S3 and use SQL Server-linked servers for cross-database queries."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn enterprise exports JSON and CSV files from a Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) application to an Amazon S3 bucket. Additionally, they have various internal services that store data in Amazon RDS for MySQL, SQL Server, and Amazon DynamoDB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering division must create a system that allows data scientists to execute queries across all these data storage options using an SQL-like syntax.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will satisfy these prerequisites while minimizing operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue crawlers are designed to discover and catalog data from various data sources automatically, creating and updating tables in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. This catalog serves as a central metadata repository, which data engineers and scientists can use to understand the structure and schema of their data across multiple data stores. The process of using crawlers simplifies the discovery of data, as it automatically infers the schema of the data and categorizes it accordingly, which is crucial for efficiently managing and querying large datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4e1bf2d783e751dd83f24ae392a24b61137276c045b8edd15874638912af7764.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBy leveraging Amazon Athena, which supports both SQL and PartiQL, queries can be executed across structured and unstructured data, respectively. PartiQL, an extension of SQL, simplifies querying unstructured data like JSON by offering built-in functions for easy navigation and parsing. Additionally, you can continue using native SQL from structured data sources such as relational database systems, ensuring compatibility and ease of use across the entire dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue to crawl the data sources and store the metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. With Amazon Athena, use SQL and PartiQL to query structured and semi-structured data respectively.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUtilize AWS Glue to crawl data sources, saving metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Run SQL queries using Redshift spectrum\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect because it simply introduces more complexity and cost compared to Athena for querying across diverse data sources without the need for data transformation. Although Redshift Spectrum allows querying data in S3, it does not meet the requirements given in the scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize AWS Glue to crawl data sources, with metadata stored in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Transform JSON format data to Apache Parquet or .csv using AWS Glue jobs, storing transformed data in S3. Query both original and transformed data using Amazon Athena \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because this option primarily involves additional operational overhead for data transformation. It requires configuration for AWS Glue Jobs and handling data formats, which is unnecessary for the requirement to query data in its original format.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eConfigure Amazon RDS to directly query data using federated queries for RDS, DynamoDB, and S3. Store JSON and .csv data in S3 and use SQL Server-linked servers for cross-database queries\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect because Amazon RDS federated queries simply do not natively support querying across DynamoDB and S3 in the manner described. This option introduces significant complexity and misrepresents the capabilities of federated queries and SQL Server-linked servers in the context of AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-crawler.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-crawler.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheatsheet:\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:40":{"id":"e:4:40","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 40","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAvro\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eORC\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eParquet\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eXML\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team at an e-commerce company is looking at setting up a data lake on Amazon S3 that uses a columnar storage format that is optimized for fast retrieval of data. The team wants to ensure that the underlying data storage format also supports complex data types.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following data file formats would you recommend for the given use case?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eORC\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eApache Parquet and ORC are columnar storage formats that are optimized for fast retrieval of data and used in AWS analytical applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eColumnar storage formats have the following characteristics that make them suitable for use with Athena:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCompression by column, with compression algorithm selected for the column data type to save storage space in Amazon S3 and reduce disk space and I/O during query processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePredicate pushdown in Parquet and ORC enables Athena queries to fetch only the needed blocks, improving query performance. When an Athena query obtains specific column values from your data, it uses statistics from data block predicates, such as max/min values, to determine whether to read or skip the block.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSplitting of data in Parquet and ORC allows Athena to split the reading of data to multiple readers and increase parallelism during its query processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eORC (Optimized Row Columnar) format also provides an efficient way to store Hive data. ORC files are often smaller than Parquet files, and ORC indexes can make querying faster. In addition, ORC supports complex types such as structs, maps, and lists.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5998de2816d9d1d55b1b2e2ac8d69a3f3011f8c35d33fbfa4a10f161783750d9.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5998de2816d9d1d55b1b2e2ac8d69a3f3011f8c35d33fbfa4a10f161783750d9.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eParquet\u003c/strong\u003e - Apache Parquet provides efficient data compression and encoding schemes and is ideal for running complex queries and processing large amounts of data. As mentioned earlier, for complex data types, ORC might be a better choice as it supports a wider range of complex data types.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAvro\u003c/strong\u003e - Avro is an open-source object container file format. It features row-based storage. Avro stores data definition in JSON so data can be easily read and interpreted. It uses the JSON file format for defining the data types, and protocols and serializes the data in a compact binary format, making for efficient, resource-sparing storage. A great feature of Avro is Schema evolution which supports data schemas that undergo changes over a period. It deals with schema changes such as missing fields, added fields, and changed fields.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eXML\u003c/strong\u003e - The XML format does not represent a columnar storage format that is optimized for fast retrieval of data. So, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/serde-about.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/serde-about.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://bryteflow.com/how-to-choose-between-parquet-orc-and-avro/\"\u003ehttps://bryteflow.com/how-to-choose-between-parquet-orc-and-avro/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-14":{"id":"p:4-14","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 14","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse the fully managed Apache Kafka cluster to capture the sensor data in near real-time. Store the data in Amazon S3 for querying\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose to capture the sensor data. Directly store the data in Amazon DynamoDB for querying\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) to capture the real-time sensor data. Define an AWS Lambda function to poll the SQS queues and process the data. Store the data in Amazon DynamoDB for querying\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to capture the sensor data. Define an AWS Lambda function to process the data and write to the DynamoDB table. Store the data in Amazon DynamoDB for querying\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["MSK có thể thu nhận luồng, nhưng S3 không phải database đáp ứng truy vấn từng bản ghi JSON với độ trễ mục tiêu 10 ms. Kiến trúc này cũng cần nhiều quản lý hơn phương án Kinesis và DynamoDB.","Firehose không có đích ghi DynamoDB trực tiếp như lựa chọn mô tả. Cần tầng xử lý ghi bảng; Kinesis Data Streams kết hợp Lambda phù hợp hơn.","SQS và Lambda xử lý được thông điệp nhưng không tối ưu cho bài toán luồng cảm biến có khả năng lưu giữ và đọc lại theo mô hình streaming. Bộ đề ưu tiên Kinesis Data Streams làm tầng thu nhận.","Kinesis Data Streams thu dữ liệu cảm biến; Lambda xử lý rồi ghi vào DynamoDB. DynamoDB lưu được cấu trúc lồng nhau và phù hợp truy cập khóa với độ trễ mili giây; cần thiết kế khóa và năng lực đúng để đạt mục tiêu của ứng dụng."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn Internet-of-Things (IoT) company needs a solution that can collect near real-time data from all its devices/sensors and store them in nested JSON format. The solution must offer data persistence and support the capability to query the data with a maximum latency of 10 milliseconds.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs a data engineer, how will you implement an optimal solution such that it has the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:47":{"id":"e:4:47","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 47","correct":[1,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCheck the IAM permissions on the service role created for the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePartition the data in the S3 bucket based on year, month, and day\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCross-job data access could be the reason for slow-running queries. Use a separate AWS Glue Spark cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSetup AWS Glue Streaming to efficiently handle streaming data in near real-time\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure larger Glue job workers by changing the instance type of the workers\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company stores its data in a single Amazon S3 bucket that is updated daily. An AWS Glue job processes this data which is consumed by Amazon QuickSight to generate an interactive dashboard. Of late, the dashboard queries have been getting slower and an initial inspection has revealed that AWS Glue jobs are running longer than expected.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs a data engineer, which of the following actions will you take to improve the performance of the AWS Glue job? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePartition the data in the S3 bucket based on year, month, and day\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePartition columns are usually designed by a common query pattern in your use case. For example, a common practice is to partition the data based on year, month, and day because many queries tend to run time series analyses in typical use cases. This often leads to a multi-level partitioning scheme. Data is organized in a hierarchical directory structure based on the distinct values of one or more columns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eOverview of partitions for Amazon S3 bucket:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9ce20623fb3e14181e6d0dce9d24e29994d711b7e58d5f6fad7f20beda7d1ee7.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9ce20623fb3e14181e6d0dce9d24e29994d711b7e58d5f6fad7f20beda7d1ee7.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/get-started-managing-partitions-for-amazon-s3-tables-backed-by-the-aws-glue-data-catalog\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/get-started-managing-partitions-for-amazon-s3-tables-backed-by-the-aws-glue-data-catalog\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure larger Glue job workers by changing the instance type of the workers\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can manually change the instance type of your AWS Glue workers to use workers with more cores, memory, and storage. Larger worker types make it possible for you to vertically scale and run intensive data integration jobs, such as memory-intensive data transforms, skewed aggregations, and entity-detection checks involving petabytes of data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eScaling up also assists in cases where the Spark driver needs larger capacity—for instance, because the job query plan is quite large. Using larger workers can also reduce the total number of workers needed, which increases performance by reducing shuffles in intensive operations such as a join.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCross-job data access could be the reason for slow-running queries. Use a separate AWS Glue Spark cluster\u003c/strong\u003e - Cross-job data access is possible if you have two AWS Glue Spark jobs in a single AWS Account, each running in a separate AWS Glue Spark cluster. This option acts as a distractor, as the given use case talks about a single Glue job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSetup AWS Glue Streaming to efficiently handle streaming data in near real-time\u003c/strong\u003e - Glue Streaming is for efficiently handling streaming data in near real-time. The given scenario revolves around slow queries, therefore the use case of streaming is not relevant here.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck the IAM permissions on the service role created for the AWS Glue job\u003c/strong\u003e - IAM permissions are irrelevant here since access is not an issue, rather performance is.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/get-started-managing-partitions-for-amazon-s3-tables-backed-by-the-aws-glue-data-catalog\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/get-started-managing-partitions-for-amazon-s3-tables-backed-by-the-aws-glue-data-catalog\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/scale-cluster-capacity.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/scale-cluster-capacity.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-18":{"id":"p:1-18","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 18","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams in each Region to ingest trading events, use Amazon Data Firehose to deliver records into regional Amazon MSK clusters, and configure AWS KMS encryption with IAM roles for access control\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDeploy Apache Kafka MirrorMaker 2.0 in each Region, configure custom replication flows between all regional Kafka clusters, and enable the SSL security protocol for encrypted cross-Region traffic\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Database Migration Service with Amazon MSK endpoints in each Region, create replication tasks for the trading topics, configure TLS for the endpoints, and run the tasks continuously to synchronize streaming data between the regional clusters\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon MSK Replicator to configure fully connected replication relationships among the Amazon MSK clusters in all three Regions, enable TLS encryption, and use IAM authentication for cluster access\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Kinesis và Firehose không phải dịch vụ sao chép hai chiều giữa các cụm Kafka. Thêm đường chuyển đổi này làm phức tạp hệ thống và không tự bảo toàn ngữ nghĩa topic/partition của Kafka.","MirrorMaker 2.0 có thể sao chép Kafka nhưng phải tự triển khai, mở rộng, giám sát và bảo trì ở nhiều Region. Không đáp ứng tiêu chí ít công vận hành nhất.","DMS phục vụ di chuyển và đồng bộ các nguồn dữ liệu được hỗ trợ. Nó không phải lựa chọn chuyên biệt cho sao chép Kafka-to-Kafka nhiều vùng như MSK Replicator.","MSK Replicator cung cấp sao chép được quản lý giữa các cụm MSK. Cấu hình quan hệ giữa các vùng, TLS và IAM phù hợp với thiết kế yêu cầu. Mốc độ trễ trong đề là mục tiêu vận hành, không nên hiểu là bảo đảm cố định của dịch vụ."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multinational brokerage firm runs a streaming platform for equity order events in the us-west-2 Region, the eu-central-1 Region, and the ap-southeast-1 Region. The firm needs near real-time bidirectional synchronization of trading events across all three Regions. The solution must encrypt data in transit, preserve event ordering for each Kafka partition, support multi-Region disaster recovery, and keep replication latency under 500 milliseconds during normal operating conditions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:30":{"id":"e:2:30","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 30","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena Federated Query with Athena data source connectors to join the DynamoDB, Aurora PostgreSQL, Amazon Redshift, and Amazon S3 datasets in place for the one-time report\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eBuild an AWS Glue ETL pipeline to extract data from all sources, centralize the data in Amazon S3, and run Amazon Athena queries against the consolidated dataset\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate AWS Glue crawlers for each source, export the DynamoDB, Aurora PostgreSQL, and Amazon Redshift datasets to Amazon S3, convert every exported dataset to Apache Parquet, catalog the converted files, and run the final join by using Amazon Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCapture DynamoDB changes with DynamoDB Streams, migrate Aurora PostgreSQL data with AWS DMS, export Amazon Redshift data to Amazon S3, and query the combined files with Redshift Spectrum\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail operations company needs to create a one-time executive performance report by joining data from Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL, Amazon Redshift, and Amazon S3. The company wants to avoid building a temporary consolidated data store or moving large volumes of data between services. The reporting team also wants to minimize the time required to run the analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena Federated Query with Athena data source connectors to join the DynamoDB, Aurora PostgreSQL, Amazon Redshift, and Amazon S3 datasets in place for the one-time report\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena Federated Query allows Athena to run SQL queries across data stored in sources other than Amazon S3 by using data source connectors. Athena supports connectors for sources such as Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon Redshift, and JDBC-compatible relational databases, while Athena can also query data stored in Amazon S3 directly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution meets the one-time reporting requirement because the company can query the data in place instead of building an ETL pipeline or creating a temporary consolidated copy of every dataset. Avoiding unnecessary data movement reduces setup effort and helps the reporting team produce the analysis more quickly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAthena Federated Query is also suitable when teams need to join data across multiple systems for ad hoc analysis. The company can define the required connectors, run the federated SQL query, and produce the report without operating an EMR cluster, managing a large migration workflow, or duplicating data into a new analytics store.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c81456d768bcee1e5b87982f99e811f5e1ba143e71ea532454107d06d24e782f.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c81456d768bcee1e5b87982f99e811f5e1ba143e71ea532454107d06d24e782f.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/82b9940d6c775343e6c8ea63d1d1de1fa9b80330ce6af2bba0c0b8987b3fd0ed.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/82b9940d6c775343e6c8ea63d1d1de1fa9b80330ce6af2bba0c0b8987b3fd0ed.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eBuild an AWS Glue ETL pipeline to extract data from all sources, centralize the data in Amazon S3, and run Amazon Athena queries against the consolidated dataset\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue can extract, transform, and load data from multiple sources into Amazon S3. Amazon Athena can then query the centralized dataset after the ETL pipeline finishes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option moves and centralizes data even though the company needs only a one-time report. Building and running the ETL pipeline increases setup time and creates unnecessary data movement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate AWS Glue crawlers for each source, export the DynamoDB, Aurora PostgreSQL, and Amazon Redshift datasets to Amazon S3, convert every exported dataset to Apache Parquet, catalog the converted files, and run the final join by using Amazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue crawlers can catalog datasets, and converting files to Apache Parquet can improve query performance for repeated analytical workloads. Athena can then query the optimized data in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option exports and converts every dataset before the report can run. The approach is wordier, slower to prepare, and more operationally heavy than querying the sources in place with Athena Federated Query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCapture DynamoDB changes with DynamoDB Streams, migrate Aurora PostgreSQL data with AWS DMS, export Amazon Redshift data to Amazon S3, and query the combined files with Redshift Spectrum\u003c/strong\u003e - DynamoDB Streams, AWS DMS, Amazon Redshift exports, and Redshift Spectrum can support data movement and data lake query patterns. This design could eventually create a consolidated analytical dataset in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option is unnecessarily complex for a one-time report. It requires several movement mechanisms and does not minimize query preparation time compared with Athena Federated Query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/federated-queries.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/federated-queries.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connectors-dynamodb.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connectors-dynamodb.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connectors-redshift.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connectors-redshift.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/query-any-data-source-with-amazon-athenas-new-federated-query/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/improve-federated-queries-with-predicate-pushdown-in-amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/improve-federated-queries-with-predicate-pushdown-in-amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-19":{"id":"p:3-19","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 19","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eIngest the sensor data in an Amazon SQS standard queue, which is polled by a Lambda function in batches, and the data is written into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIngest the sensor data in an Amazon SQS standard queue, which is polled by an application running on an EC2 instance, and the data is written into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIngest the sensor data in Kinesis Data Firehose, which directly writes the data into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIngest the sensor data in a Kinesis Data Stream, which is polled by an application running on an EC2 instance, and the data is written into an auto-scaled DynamoDB table for downstream processing\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["SQS đệm sự kiện, Lambda lấy batch xử lý và ghi DynamoDB tự mở rộng. Không cần quản lý EC2 consumer; vẫn thiết kế xử lý lặp an toàn.","Consumer chạy trên EC2 không đáp ứng yêu cầu các thành phần hoàn toàn không cần quản lý máy chủ.","Firehose không có đích DynamoDB trực tiếp theo mô tả. Cần đường xử lý khác nếu muốn ghi item DynamoDB.","Dù Kinesis phù hợp streaming, ứng dụng consumer trên EC2 vẫn cần quản lý máy chủ, trái tiêu chí đề."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company wants to use AWS for its connected cab application that would collect sensor data from its electric cab fleet to give drivers dynamically updated map information. The company would like to build its new sensor service by leveraging fully serverless components that are provisioned and managed automatically by AWS. The development team at the company does not want an option that requires the capacity to be manually provisioned, as it does not want to respond manually to changing volumes of sensor data. The company has hired you to provide consultancy for this strategic initiative.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eGiven these constraints, which of the following solutions would you suggest as the BEST fit to develop this service?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:53":{"id":"e:2:53","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 53","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eGrant the finance team (defined as a group) permission to read from the table. Use the GRANT SQL command to allow read-only access to a subset of columns having non-sensitive data to the other users\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eGrant the finance group permission to read from the table. Create a view of the new table with only those columns having non-sensitive data. Grant the other users read-only permissions to this view\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eGrant all users read-only permissions to the non-sensitive columns. Add the finance team to the administrator group so they have complete access to the table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eGrant the finance team (defined as a group) permission to read from the table. Create a second table having data only for columns with non-sensitive data. Grant read-only permissions to the second table for the rest of the users\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA business is moving their data to Amazon Redshift. A core table with billions of rows needs to be moved to Redshift. This table contains certain columns that have sensitive data that can only be accessed by the finance team. Once the data is moved to Redshift, queries will be run on this table by multiple teams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow will you configure a solution for this requirement such that the columns holding sensitive data are only accessible to members of the finance team?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eGrant the finance team (defined as a group) permission to read from the table. Use the GRANT SQL command to allow read-only access to a subset of columns having non-sensitive data to the other users\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSince March 2020, Amazon Redshift supports column-level access control for data in Redshift. Customers can use column-level GRANT and REVOKE statements to help meet their security and compliance needs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eRedshift\u0027s table-level access controls for the data in Redshift are already in use by many customers, but they also want the ability to control access in more detail. You can now control access to columns without having to implement view-based access control or use another system. Column-level access control is available in all Amazon Redshift regions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eGRANT command defines access privileges for a user or user group. Privileges include access options such as being able to read data in tables and views, write data, create tables, and drop tables. Use this command to give specific privileges for a table, database, schema, function, procedure, language, or column.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe syntax for column-level privileges on Amazon Redshift tables and views looks like the below:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode\u003eGRANT { { SELECT | UPDATE } ( column_name [, ...] ) [, ...] | ALL [ PRIVILEGES ] ( column_name [,...] ) }\n    ON { [ TABLE ] table_name [, ...] }\n    TO { username | GROUP group_name | PUBLIC } [, ...]\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eGrant all users read-only permissions to the non-sensitive columns. Add the finance team to the administrator group so they have complete access to the table\u003c/strong\u003e - This is a security concern as the finance team do not need access privileges as an administrator.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eGrant the finance team (defined as a group) permission to read from the table. Create a second table having data only for columns with non-sensitive data. Grant read-only permissions to the second table for the rest of the users\u003c/strong\u003e - This is not an efficient solution as the storage space would be wasted to create the second table. It is better to use column-level access controls on the original table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eGrant the finance group permission to read from the table. Create a view of the new table with only those columns having non-sensitive data. Grant the other users read-only permissions to this view\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS recommends that you should use column-level access control, instead of views, to manage access to sensitive columns within a table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_GRANT.html#r_GRANT-usage-notes-clp\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_GRANT.html#r_GRANT-usage-notes-clp\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2020/03/announcing-column-level-access-control-for-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2020/03/announcing-column-level-access-control-for-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:5":{"id":"e:5:5","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 5","correct":[1,3],"answers":["Convert the CSV records into a Delta Lake format. Create a Delta Lake table using Amazon Athena.","Create an Apache Iceberg table using Amazon Athena and insert the current table into it.","Convert the CSV records into an Apache Hudi format. Run an AWS Glue crawler to generate a Hudi table.","Use Amazon Athena for inserting and deleting records.","Use Amazon Redshift Serverless for inserting and deleting records."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company stores CSV records in an Amazon S3 bucket. These records are queried using the Amazon Athena query editor. The company wants to be able to run SQL queries to add and delete data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of steps would meet the requirements with the LEAST development overhead? (Select TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eApache Iceberg is an open table format for very large analytic datasets. Iceberg manages large collections of files as tables, and it supports modern analytical data lake operations such as record-level insert, update, delete, and time travel queries. The Iceberg specification allows seamless table evolution, such as schema and partition evolution, and is designed for optimized usage on Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. Athena supports read, time travel, write, and DDL queries for Apache Iceberg tables that use the Apache Parquet format for data and the AWS Glue catalog for their metastore.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/507f8dfcc2c70bcc4b31bfa57ca08385ed7c05d83d3dc73d1bb6ec208c6169b4.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThere are different ways of creating an Iceberg table, such as directly defining the table schema with a CREATE TABLE statement, then populating it with existing data using the INSERT INTO statement. Alternatively, you can also have a Glue Crawler to crawl your data and automatically create the table for you. Once established, Iceberg table data can be directly managed in Amazon Athena through INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE queries. These operations on Iceberg tables produce new snapshots with each transaction, enabling querying of table states at different points in time through Iceberg’s time travel capability.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e– \u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Apache Iceberg table using Amazon Athena and insert the current table into it.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e– \u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena for inserting and deleting records.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Serverless for inserting and deleting records\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While Amazon Redshift Serverless allows you to run SQL queries without managing the underlying infrastructure, it involves more development effort due to moving or copying data from Amazon S3 into Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConvert the CSV records into a Delta Lake format. Create a Delta Lake table using Amazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Delta Lake provides ACID transactions, scalable metadata handling, and unifies streaming and batch data processing. However, Athena only supports read queries against Delta Lake tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConvert the CSV records into an Apache Hudi format. Run an AWS Glue crawler to generate a Hudi table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Although Hudi tables are capable of handling upserts, deletes, and incremental data processing, Athena lacks support for the insertion of new Hudi data into a Hudi table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/what-is/apache-iceberg/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/what-is/apache-iceberg/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:2":{"id":"e:8:2","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 2","correct":[1,4],"answers":["Grant the Lambda function’s execution role with full RDS permissions.","Connect the Lambda function to the same VPC as the database.","Create a new VPC and connect it to the Lambda function. Enable VPC peering with the database’s VPC.","Grant the Lambda function\u0027s execution role read and write permissions for RDS.","Attach the same security group to the Lambda function and RDS database, allowing inbound and outbound traffic on port 3306."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer has created an AWS Lambda function that will read and write data to an Amazon RDS for MySQL database. The database is located in a private subnet of a VPC. The Lambda function’s execution role has already been granted the \u003ccode\u003eAWSLambdaVPCAccessExecutionRole\u003c/code\u003e managed policy.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat set of steps must be done next to enable database access for the function? (Select TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Lambda functions do not reside within a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) by default. They are executed in a managed environment by AWS, which is outside of your VPC. This setup means that if your Lambda function needs to interact with resources located in a private subnet of a VPC, such as an Amazon RDS for MySQL database, special configurations are required to enable this connectivity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen connecting a Lambda function to private resources like a database in a private subnet, the first step is to configure the function to access the VPC. This involves specifying the VPC ID, subnet IDs, and security group IDs that the Lambda function will use.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/21507ad33427b4e92204201902779ab860cc21c091ac5dc37fc3f5df63eedf27.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo perform this configuration:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConnect the Lambda Function to the VPC:\u003c/strong\u003e This is done by selecting the VPC and the specific private subnets within the VPC where the database resides. This step ensures that the Lambda function can operate within the network that houses the database.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSecurity Group Configuration:\u003c/strong\u003e Attach a security group to the Lambda function that allows it to communicate with the RDS instance. Typically, this involves allowing inbound and outbound traffic on port 3306 (the default port for MySQL databases) between the Lambda function and the RDS database.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAn important part of this configuration involves assigning the correct permissions to the Lambda function\u0027s execution role. AWS provides the \u003ccode\u003eAWSLambdaVPCAccessExecutionRole\u003c/code\u003e managed policy, which grants the Lambda function the necessary permissions to create, configure, and manage network interfaces within the VPC. This managed policy includes permissions for actions like \u003ccode\u003eec2:CreateNetworkInterface\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eec2:DescribeNetworkInterfaces\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eec2:DeleteNetworkInterface\u003c/code\u003e, etc., which are essential for the Lambda function to interact with VPC resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the scenario, you can deploy a Lambda function in the same VPC and use the same self-referencing security group for both RDS and Lambda. For the RDS instance, an inbound rule allowing traffic on port 3306 is necessary to accept traffic from the Lambda function. On the other hand, configuring the security group to allow outbound traffic on port 3306 allows the Lambda function to make outbound calls to the RDS database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e- \u003cstrong\u003eConnect the Lambda function to the same VPC as the database.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e- \u003cstrong\u003eAttach the same security group to the Lambda function and RDS database, allowing inbound and outbound traffic on port 3306.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eGrant the Lambda function’s execution role with full RDS permissions \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Merely granting IAM permissions does not address the network connectivity requirement between the Lambda function and the RDS database in a private subnet.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCreate a new VPC and connect it to the Lambda function. Enable VPC peering with the database’s VPC \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. This approach introduces unnecessary complexity and is not required for the Lambda function to access the RDS database within the same VPC.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eGrant the Lambda function\u0027s execution role read and write permissions for RDS \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. Just like the other incorrect option, IAM permissions related to RDS generally pertain to management operations, such as creating and deleting an RDS instance, not to how the Lambda function writes and reads data to the actual database. These permissions do not affect the Lambda function\u0027s ability to interact with the data within the RDS database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/connect-lambda-to-an-rds-instance\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/connect-lambda-to-an-rds-instance\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-vpc.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-vpc.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Lambda Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-4":{"id":"p:1-4","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 4","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConstruct a view within the SQL Server databases on the EC2 instances that includes the essential data elements. Then, set up an AWS Glue job to fetch the data directly from this view and transfer it to an S3 bucket in Parquet format. Ensure this AWS Glue job is scheduled to execute daily\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a SQL query on the SQL Server database hosted on the EC2 instances to establish a view containing the necessary data elements. Then, configure an AWS Glue crawler to access and read this view. Set up an AWS Glue job to extract the data and convert it into Parquet format before transferring it to an S3 bucket. Configure this AWS Glue job to execute daily\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up the SQL Server Agent to execute a daily SQL query on the SQL Server databases hosted on the EC2 instances, extracting the specified data elements. Configure the query to output the results as .csv files directly into an S3 bucket. Additionally, establish an S3 event trigger to activate an AWS Lambda function, which will convert these .csv files into the Parquet format\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop an AWS Lambda function that utilizes Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) to query databases hosted on EC2 instances. Set up this Lambda function to fetch the necessary data, convert it into Parquet format, and upload it to an S3 bucket. Set up a daily schedule via Amazon EventBridge to trigger the Lambda function\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Phương án đọc view bằng Glue có thể thực hiện được, nhưng theo tiêu chí của bộ đề, thiếu crawler khiến nhóm phải tự quản lý metadata nhiều hơn. Đáp án được chọn bổ sung crawler.","View gom logic JOIN tại SQL Server; crawler khám phá metadata; Glue ETL đọc dữ liệu, chuyển sang Parquet và ghi S3 theo lịch hằng ngày. Các bước phân công rõ ràng, dễ tự động hóa.","Xuất CSV rồi kích hoạt Lambda để đổi sang Parquet tạo thêm một bước trung gian, lưu trữ tạm và thành phần cần quản lý. Glue có thể chuyển và ghi Parquet trong cùng quy trình.","Lambda với JDBC đòi hỏi tự viết mã kết nối, đọc, biến đổi, ghi dữ liệu và xử lý lỗi. Với yêu cầu ETL theo lịch, Glue giảm công triển khai hơn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is transitioning its database servers from Amazon EC2 instances running Microsoft SQL Server to Amazon RDS for Microsoft SQL Server DB instances. During this migration, the data engineering team needs to export this data, which is derived from SQL joins across multiple tables, using a daily schedule. The migrated data should be stored in Apache Parquet format on Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is the most operationally efficient solution to meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:10":{"id":"e:2:10","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 10","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConcurrent state\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eParallel state\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChoice state\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMap state\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is configuring an AWS Step Function for a banking system workflow. The workflow must process large amounts of data files in parallel and it should simultaneously apply transformations for each file.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich Step Functions state is the right fit for this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMap state\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use the Map state to run a set of workflow steps for each item in a dataset. The Map state\u0027s iterations run in parallel, which makes it possible to process a dataset quickly. Map states can use a variety of input types, including a JSON array, a list of Amazon S3 objects, or a CSV file.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eStep Functions provides two types of processing modes for using the Map state in your workflows: inline mode and distributed mode.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBy default, Map states run in inline mode. In inline mode, the Map state accepts only a JSON array as input. In this mode, the Map state supports up to 40 concurrent iterations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn distributed mode, the Map state allows high-concurrency processing. In distributed mode, the Map state processes the items in the dataset in iterations called child workflow executions. You can specify the number of child workflow executions that can run in parallel. Each child workflow execution has its own, separate execution history from that of the parent workflow. If you don\u0027t specify, Step Functions runs 10,000 parallel child workflow executions in parallel.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eParallel state\u003c/strong\u003e - The Parallel state (\"Type\": \"Parallel\") can be used to add separate branches of execution in your state machine. A \u003ccode\u003eParallel\u003c/code\u003e state causes AWS Step Functions to execute each branch, starting with the state named in that branch\u0027s \u003ccode\u003eStartAt\u003c/code\u003e field, as concurrently as possible, and wait until all branches terminate (reach a terminal state) before processing the \u003ccode\u003eParallel\u003c/code\u003e state\u0027s \u003ccode\u003eNext\u003c/code\u003e field.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChoice state\u003c/strong\u003e - A Choice state (\"Type\": \"Choice\") adds conditional logic to a state machine. This state contains an array of Choice Rules that determine which state the state machine transitions to next. You use a comparison operator in a Choice Rule to compare an input variable with a specific value. For example, using Choice Rules you can compare if an input variable is greater than or less than 100.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConcurrent state\u003c/strong\u003e - This state does not exist. This is a made-up option that acts as a distractor.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/amazon-states-language-map-state.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/amazon-states-language-map-state.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:13":{"id":"e:1:13","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 13","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003ePush score updates to Kinesis Data Streams which uses a fleet of EC2 instances (with Auto Scaling) to process the updates in Kinesis Data Streams and then store these processed updates in DynamoDB\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePush score updates to an SNS topic, subscribe a Lambda function to this SNS topic to process the updates, and then store these processed updates in a SQL database running on Amazon EC2\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePush score updates to Kinesis Data Streams which uses a Lambda function to process these updates and then store these processed updates in DynamoDB\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePush score updates to an SQS queue which uses a fleet of EC2 instances (with Auto Scaling) to process these updates in the SQS queue and then store these processed updates in an RDS MySQL database\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA gaming company is developing a mobile game that streams score updates to a backend processor and then publishes results on a leaderboard. The company has hired you as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to design a solution that can handle major traffic spikes, process the mobile game updates in the order of receipt, and store the processed updates in a highly available database. The company wants to minimize the management overhead required to maintain the solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions will you suggest to address these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePush score updates to Kinesis Data Streams which uses a Lambda function to process these updates and then store these processed updates in DynamoDB\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo help ingest real-time data or streaming data at large scales, you can use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams (KDS). KDS can continuously capture gigabytes of data per second from hundreds of thousands of sources. The data collected is available in milliseconds, enabling real-time analytics. KDS provides ordering of records, as well as the ability to read and/or replay records in the same order to multiple Amazon Kinesis Applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eLambda integrates natively with Kinesis Data Streams. The polling, checkpointing, and error-handling complexities are abstracted when you use this native integration. The processed data can then be configured to be saved in DynamoDB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePush score updates to an SQS queue which uses a fleet of EC2 instances (with Auto Scaling) to process these updates in the SQS queue and then store these processed updates in an RDS MySQL database\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePush score updates to Kinesis Data Streams which uses a fleet of EC2 instances (with Auto Scaling) to process the updates in Kinesis Data Streams and then store these processed updates in DynamoDB\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePush score updates to an SNS topic, subscribe a Lambda function to this SNS topic to process the updates, and then store these processed updates in an SQL database running on Amazon EC2\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThese three options use EC2 instances as part of the solution architecture. The use case seeks to minimize the management overhead required to maintain the solution. However, EC2 instances involve several maintenance activities such as managing the guest operating system and software deployed to the guest operating system, including updates and security patches, etc. Hence these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/best-practices-for-consuming-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-using-aws-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/best-practices-for-consuming-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-using-aws-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:42":{"id":"e:5:42","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 42","correct":[3],"answers":["Configure Glue Data Quality to analyze patterns in patient records by setting rules that identify potential matches. Compare identifiable fields and flag records with high similarity scores.","Create a custom Glue script using Python that iteratively compares patient records based on predefined RegEx patterns to identify matches across the dataset.","Implement a filtering process using the Glue PySpark filter class that iterates through patient data based on predefined criteria to group records that share common attributes.","\u003cspan\u003eSet up the Glue \u003ccode\u003eFindMatches\u003c/code\u003e ML transform by adding it to the Glue job and using a labeling file to guide matching. Use the labeled data to iteratively improve the transform’s ability to identify and group similar patient records.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA healthcare organization is aggregating patient data from various sources into a staging bucket on Amazon S3. This data is then processed by an AWS Glue ETL job\u003c/span\u003e\u003cem\u003e\u003cspan\u003e, \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/em\u003e\u003cspan\u003ewith Glue job run insights used to monitor performance and execution metrics, and is subsequently stored in a data lake on S3. The stored data is accessible for querying through Amazon Athena. \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eThe organization wants to retrieve all medical records for each patient. However, the records don’t have a common unique identifier. \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich solution would enable the organization to match patient records with the LEAST development overhead?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe AWS Glue \u003ccode\u003eFindMatches\u003c/code\u003e ML transform, part of the AWS Glue service, is a machine learning tool adept at identifying and associating records that refer to the same entity, especially when a common unique identifier is absent. This process involves a “teaching” approach where users label example records as matches or non-matches. AWS Glue leverages these labeled examples to understand patterns in data, enabling it to recognize similar records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the context of matching patient records, the AWS Glue \u003ccode\u003eFindMatches\u003c/code\u003e ML transform is particularly beneficial. It can analyze complex attributes in patient data, such as demographics or medical histories, and identify correlations. For example, it may match patients by finding similarities in treatment histories or other shared characteristics, without relying on unique identifiers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe solution integrates seamlessly with the healthcare organization’s AWS infrastructure, including S3 and AWS Glue ETL. As data flows into the S3 bucket and undergoes the ETL process, \u003ccode\u003eFindMatches\u003c/code\u003e ML continuously and automatically groups similar records. This process maintains data consistency and accuracy, efficiently handling the organization’s large and complex datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5438fc38a10f389e3030079714b848236bbf400959951963216ee5568575fe35.png\" alt=\"FindMatches\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up the Glue \u003ccode\u003eFindMatches\u003c/code\u003e ML transform by adding it to the Glue job and using a labeling file to guide matching. Use the labeled data to iteratively improve the transform’s ability to identify and group similar patient records.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eConfigure Glue Data Quality to analyze patterns in patient records by setting rules that identify potential matches. Compare identifiable fields and flag records with high similarity scores\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because AWS Glue Data Quality primarily focuses on ensuring the overall quality of data, such as its accuracy, completeness, and consistency. While it can analyze patterns and compare fields, it is not specifically designed for the complex task of matching disparate records based on similarity. It lacks the advanced machine learning capabilities of \u003ccode\u003eFindMatches\u003c/code\u003e ML, which are necessary for accurately deduplicating and matching patient records in a large, diverse dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCreate a custom Glue script using Python that iteratively compares patient records based on predefined RegEx patterns to identify matches across the dataset\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect, as this method typically requires significant manual effort to develop and maintain. Writing a custom script for matching records can be time-consuming and may not scale well with large datasets. Additionally, without the aid of machine learning, the script might not effectively handle the nuances and complexities of patient data, leading to less accurate matching compared to a specialized ML solution like \u003ccode\u003eFindMatches\u003c/code\u003e ML.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eImplement a filtering process using the Glue PySpark filter class that iterates through patient data based on predefined criteria to group records that share common attributes\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because while AWS Glue PySpark can filter and process data based on specified criteria, this approach is more suited for straightforward data transformations and filtering tasks. It does not inherently possess the sophisticated pattern recognition and learning capabilities required for effectively matching patient records, especially when the records lack a common identifier. This method may result in incomplete or inaccurate matching compared to the machine learning-driven approach of \u003ccode\u003eFindMatches\u003c/code\u003e ML.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-api-crawler-pyspark-transforms-findmatches.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-api-crawler-pyspark-transforms-findmatches.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-api-crawler-pyspark-transforms-filter.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-api-crawler-pyspark-transforms-filter.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out the TutorialsDojo Cheat Sheets here:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:8":{"id":"e:7:8","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 8","correct":[0],"answers":["Create a User-Defined Function (UDF) directly in Redshift.","Integrate Amazon Athena with Redshift using a custom UDF via the Athena Query Federation SDK.","Use Amazon EMR to process the data and then import the results into Redshift.","Create a custom function using AWS Glue, register it in the Glue Data Catalog, and then access it from Redshift."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA data analyst is working with a large dataset stored in Amazon Redshift. The analyst needs to perform a complex data processing task that requires a custom function. The function will be used as part of a SQL query. The analyst wants to accomplish this with the least amount of effort and overhead.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich of the following options should the analyst choose? \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift, a fully managed, petabyte-scale data warehouse service, allows you to create custom scalar UDFs using either a SQL SELECT clause or a Python program. Once created, the UDF is stored in the database and is available for any user with sufficient privileges to run.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eRunning a custom scalar UDF in Amazon Redshift is as straightforward as running existing Amazon Redshift functions. This means you don’t need to learn new ways of invoking functions, saving you time and effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c727355caa34901cd1f08deb960b6940e6dffe7d487556ede59a14975b6b76e6.png\" alt=\"Redshift User-Define Functions\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor Python UDFs, you can even import your own custom Python modules. This allows you to leverage pre-existing code and libraries, further enhancing the power and flexibility of your UDFs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/82d7f87a030a2fbd83dd09765f332e6f19d552de935dd81ded89201ad2cd81f3.png\" alt=\"Redshift User-Defined Functions\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMoreover, UDFs in Redshift provide more control and flexibility over data processing workflows and potentially improve performance by reducing the amount of data transferred between the database and client application. This is because the computations are done within the database itself, rather than transferring data to an external location for processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a User-Defined Function (UDF) directly in Redshift.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eIntegrate Amazon Athena with Redshift using a custom UDF via the Athena Query Federation SDK\u003c/span\u003e \u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. While it is possible to create custom UDFs in Amazon Athena using the Athena Query Federation SDK, integrating these UDFs with Amazon Redshift would require additional effort and overhead. You would need to write the UDF in Java, package it into a JAR file, deploy it using AWS Lambda, and then manage the integration between Amazon Athena and Amazon Redshift. This process primarily involves multiple steps and services, making it more complex and time-consuming compared to creating a UDF directly in Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon EMR to process the data and then import the results into Redshift \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Amazon EMR is just a cloud-native big data platform that allows you to process large amounts of data quickly and cost-effectively. However, using Amazon EMR to process the data and then importing the results into Amazon Redshift would require additional effort and overhead. You would need to set up an EMR cluster, write the processing code, run the job, and then import the results into Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCreate a custom function using AWS Glue, register it in the Glue Data Catalog, and then access it from Redshift\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. While AWS Glue allows you to create custom functions and register them in the AWS Glue Data Catalog, accessing these functions from Amazon Redshift would require additional steps and overhead. You would need to write the function code, register it in the AWS Glue Data Catalog using the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eaws glue create-user-defined-function\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;command in the AWS CLI, and then configure Amazon Redshift to access the function.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/user-defined-functions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/user-defined-functions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/user-defined-functions-for-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/user-defined-functions-for-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-42":{"id":"p:4-42","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 42","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003ePartition the data by ingestion date and use Amazon EMR Spark jobs to query the weekly order metrics from Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore all order files in a single S3 prefix and use Athena queries with WHERE clauses to filter by order date at runtime\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePartition the data by order date and query the matching partitions by using Amazon Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePartition the data by order date and load all records into Amazon Redshift before running the weekly analytics queries\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Truy vấn lọc theo ngày đặt hàng, không phải ngày dữ liệu được nạp. Phân vùng theo ingestion date có thể không loại được các phần dữ liệu cần bỏ qua; EMR cũng thêm công vận hành cho truy vấn mỗi tuần.","WHERE lọc kết quả nhưng nếu không có cách tổ chức phù hợp thì vẫn có thể phải đọc rất nhiều dữ liệu. Phân vùng theo cột lọc giúp Athena bỏ qua các vùng ngoài khoảng ngày cần truy vấn.","Phân vùng theo order date khớp trực tiếp các điều kiện lọc theo tuần, tháng hoặc đợt khuyến mãi. Athena chỉ đọc partition phù hợp, giảm dữ liệu quét và chi phí khi tập dữ liệu tăng.","Phân vùng đúng nhưng nạp toàn bộ dữ liệu vào Redshift trước mỗi lượt phân tích tạo thêm tài nguyên và bước vận hành. Athena đủ phù hợp cho nhu cầu truy vấn trực tiếp S3 hằng tuần."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company ingests daily order records into an Amazon S3 data lake. A data engineer runs analytics queries once each week to calculate order metrics for multiple order-date ranges, such as week-to-date, month-to-date, and custom promotional periods.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe order volume is expected to increase significantly over the next year. The data engineer needs a cost-effective optimization that keeps query performance stable as the dataset grows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement MOST cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:30":{"id":"e:5:30","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 30","correct":[1],"answers":["Use an AWS Glue for Spark to extract data from Amazon Aurora into Amazon Redshift for analysis.","Use the zero-ETL integration feature of Amazon Aurora with Amazon Redshift for analysis.","Use Amazon Data Firehose to stream data from Amazon Aurora to Amazon Redshift for analysis.","Export the data from Amazon Aurora to Amazon S3, then use Amazon Athena for analysis."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multinational e-commerce company is ingesting data from various sources into an Amazon Aurora database. The database currently stores 15 GB of data and is growing daily by 100 GB. The company’s data engineering team is tasked with performing near real-time predictive analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options would be the most efficient and require the least operational effort for analyzing the data?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eLeveraging Amazon Aurora’s zero-ETL integration with Amazon Redshift allows direct analysis of the data without the need for ETL jobs. This feature enables near real-time analytics and machine learning on petabytes of transactional data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWith zero-ETL integration, transactional data from Aurora is made available in Amazon Redshift within seconds of being written into Aurora. This enables near real-time analytics on large volumes of data.\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0d657ff287e83878a09823b88ec1e8fcc4e32f02f6b33141779249b6cf95f561.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis integration removes the need to build and manage complex data pipelines that perform extract, transform, and load (ETL) operations. This significantly reduces the operational effort required to analyze the data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe zero-ETL integration allows data from multiple Aurora database clusters to be replicated to a single Amazon Redshift data warehouse. This enables unified analytics across multiple applications and data sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen using both Amazon Aurora Serverless v2 and Amazon Redshift Serverless, there’s no need to manage any infrastructure. This further reduces the operational burden.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWith the Aurora zero-ETL integration with Amazon Redshift, you can use Amazon Redshift’s comprehensive analytical capabilities, such as built-in machine learning, materialized views, data sharing, and federated access to multiple data stores and data lakes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse the zero-ETL integration feature of Amazon Aurora with Amazon Redshift for analysis.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eExport the data from Amazon Aurora to Amazon S3, then use Amazon Athena for analysis\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because it involves additional steps of exporting data to S3 and then using Athena for analysis. This process would be slower and time-consuming than using zero-ETL integration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse an AWS Glue for Spark to extract data from Amazon Aurora into Amazon Redshift for analysis\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. While AWS Glue for Spark can write directly to Amazon Redshift, the process of moving data to Redshift involves staging the data into Amazon S3 first. This additional step isn’t near real-time and is slower than zero-ETL integration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose to stream data from Amazon Aurora to Amazon Redshift for analysis\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because Amazon Aurora is not a valid source of Data Firehose. This means that you cannot directly transfer newly inserted data from an Aurora database to Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/zero-etl/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/zero-etl/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/zero-etl.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/getting-started-guide-for-near-real-time-operational-analytics-using-amazon-aurora-zero-etl-integration-with-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/getting-started-guide-for-near-real-time-operational-analytics-using-amazon-aurora-zero-etl-integration-with-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these Amazon Aurora and Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-aurora/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-aurora/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-20":{"id":"p:4-20","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 20","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable audit trail for Amazon Redshift on Amazon CloudTrail\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable audit logging for Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable and download audit reports from AWS Config\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable audit metrics on Amazon CloudWatch\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["CloudTrail ghi các thao tác API của dịch vụ Redshift, chẳng hạn tạo hoặc xóa cluster. Nó không thay thế nhật ký kết nối database và các câu SQL do người dùng thực thi.","Bật Redshift database audit logging để ghi kết nối và thay đổi người dùng. Để ghi hoạt động SQL, cần bật thêm user activity logging qua tham số enable_user_activity_logging; đây là phần cấu hình cần thiết để đáp ứng đầy đủ đề.","AWS Config theo dõi cấu hình tài nguyên và mức tuân thủ quy tắc. Nó không ghi toàn bộ phiên kết nối hoặc câu truy vấn SQL trong database.","CloudWatch metrics biểu diễn số liệu giám sát, không phải bản ghi chi tiết từng SQL và từng lần đăng nhập. Redshift audit logging mới cung cấp loại dữ liệu kiểm toán được yêu cầu."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare company uses an Amazon Redshift database cluster to store sensitive user data. The regulatory guidelines mandate logging so that any database authentication attempts as well as the connections/disconnections are recorded. Also, the logs must include a record of each query executed against the database along with the database user who executed that query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options represents the best solution for these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-44":{"id":"p:4-44","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 44","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003ePush score updates to an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) queue which uses a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances (with Auto Scaling) to process these updates in the Amazon SQS queue and then store these processed updates in an Amazon RDS MySQL database\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePush score updates to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams which uses an AWS Lambda function to process these updates and then store these processed updates in Amazon DynamoDB\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePush score updates to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams which uses a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances (with Auto Scaling) to process the updates in Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and then store these processed updates in Amazon DynamoDB\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePush score updates to an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic, subscribe an AWS Lambda function to this Amazon SNS topic to process the updates and then store these processed updates in a SQL database running on Amazon EC2 instance\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["SQS không ghi rõ FIFO nên không bảo đảm thứ tự như yêu cầu. Fleet EC2 còn cần quản lý máy chủ, và RDS thêm khâu quản lý năng lực so với phương án serverless được chọn.","Kinesis tiếp nhận cập nhật, Lambda xử lý và DynamoDB lưu kết quả với ít hạ tầng phải quản lý. Cần dùng partition key phù hợp, chẳng hạn người chơi, để giữ thứ tự trong từng luồng khóa; không hiểu là có thứ tự toàn cục giữa mọi shard.","Kiến trúc xử lý được dữ liệu nhưng fleet EC2 vẫn cần vận hành và cấu hình mở rộng. Lambda giảm công quản lý consumer trong tình huống này.","SNS topic thông thường không bảo đảm thứ tự như đề cần. Database SQL tự vận hành trên EC2 cũng tăng đáng kể công quản lý và thiết kế khả năng sẵn sàng cao."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA gaming company is developing a mobile game that streams score updates to a backend processor and then publishes results on a leaderboard. The company has hired you as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to design a solution that can handle major traffic spikes, process the mobile game updates in the order of receipt, and store the processed updates in a highly available database. The company wants to minimize the management overhead required to maintain the solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following will you recommend to meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-52":{"id":"p:1-52","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 52","correct":[1,2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAWS Snowball Edge\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eBasic Schema Copy\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Snowball chuyển lượng dữ liệu lớn bằng thiết bị. Nó không chuyển đổi schema, khóa ngoại, chỉ mục hay stored procedure giữa các hệ quản trị.","SCT đánh giá và chuyển đổi schema cùng các đối tượng cơ sở dữ liệu được hỗ trợ. Kết hợp DMS để xử lý cả cấu trúc lẫn dữ liệu khi đổi hệ quản trị.","DMS chuyển dữ liệu và có thể đồng bộ thay đổi cho quá trình di chuyển. Dùng SCT cho cấu trúc phức tạp và DMS cho dữ liệu.","Glue là dịch vụ ETL, không phải công cụ chuyên chuyển đổi toàn bộ đối tượng database phức tạp giữa các engine như SCT.","Basic Schema Copy chỉ xử lý cấu trúc cơ bản. Không đáp ứng đầy đủ secondary index, foreign key và stored procedure mà đề nhấn mạnh."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has a license-based, expensive, legacy commercial database solution deployed at its on-premises data center. The company wants to migrate this database to a more efficient, open-source, and cost-effective option on AWS Cloud. The CTO at the company wants a solution that can handle complex database configurations such as secondary indexes, foreign keys, and stored procedures.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following AWS services should be combined to handle this use case? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:7:25":{"id":"e:7:25","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 25","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eTemporarily store the reference dataset in the Lambda function\u0027s \u003ccode\u003e/tmp\u003c/code\u003e directory.\u003c/span\u003e","Transfer the reference dataset to an Amazon S3 bucket and set up a Lambda function for data interaction using Amazon S3 API.","Launch a new file system in the Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) console and upload the reference data. Create an access point and configure the Lambda function for data access.","Implement a cluster and domain in Amazon OpenSearch Service and integrate data using Logstash. Modify the Lambda function to communicate with this environment for data access and manipulation."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA software company is rehosting a high-volume data-processing application running on-premises to AWS Lambda. Currently, the application has access to 200 GB of data stored on a local disk for its operations. The company prefers to migrate the application with minimal changes to the code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich approach would best meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS)\u003c/strong\u003e is an easy-to-use and scalable file storage solution for AWS Cloud services and on-premises resources. It can scale up to petabytes of data without affecting your applications and automatically adjusts its size as files are added or removed. This makes it a highly reliable and available storage option for a wide range of workloads and applications, including AWS Lambda functions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAn\u003cstrong\u003e Amazon EFS access point\u003c/strong\u003e is a way to manage application access to shared datasets within an EFS file system. With access points, you can configure a user, group, and root directory for all file system requests made through that access point. This ensures that access control is enforced at the file system level.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Lambda\u003c/strong\u003e enables serverless execution of code, scaling automatically to meet demand from a few requests to thousands per second.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2b14cec07f3a534b9e9e33587f128225f3a16c79e70fb5d6f129df55d4125387.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eLambda functions can seamlessly mount an EFS file system to the function\u0027s file system namespace using an access point. This setup allows Lambda functions to read and write files just as if they were on a local file system but with EFS\u0027s scalability, flexibility, and performance. This method requires minimal changes to existing code that interacts with mounted volumes, making it the most suitable option for migrating data-intensive workloads with minimal code refactoring.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cstrong\u003e \u003cspan\u003eLaunch a new file system in the Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) console and upload the reference data. Create an access point and configure the Lambda function for data access.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTemporarily store the reference dataset in the Lambda function\u0027s \u003ccode\u003e/tmp\u003c/code\u003e directory\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. The \u003ccode\u003e/tmp\u003c/code\u003e directory in AWS Lambda provides a temporary storage space for the function\u0027s execution environment. Data in this directory exists only for the duration of the function\u0027s runtime and is then lost. Additionally, with its limited size (512 MB to 10 GB), it\u0027s unsuitable for workloads that require access to 200 GB of data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTransfer the reference dataset to an Amazon S3 bucket and set up a Lambda function for data interaction using Amazon S3 API\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While a Lambda function can read and write data to and from an S3 bucket, implementing this solution would require significant modifications to the existing code that accesses data through a mounted volume. Specifically, you would need to modify the code to use the S3 data API with the AWS SDK, which would not meet the requirement to minimize the number of code modifications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement a cluster and domain in Amazon OpenSearch Service and integrate data using Logstash. Modify the Lambda function to communicate with this environment for data access and manipulation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Amazon OpenSearch Service (successor to Amazon Elasticsearch Service) offers powerful search capabilities over large volumes of data. While it\u0027s an excellent service for search and analytics, using it for data-intensive workloads that originally rely on mounted data volumes would necessitate substantial changes to the application logic, particularly for workloads not primarily focused on search or analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-filesystem.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-filesystem.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/efs/latest/ug/efs-access-points.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/efs/latest/ug/efs-access-points.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these Amazon EFS and AWS Lambda Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-efs/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-efs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:25":{"id":"e:8:25","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 25","correct":[2],"answers":["The data queried by Athena is not in a columnar format.","The EMR cluster is located in a region different from Athena.","The S3 bucket where the data is stored is in a region different from the EMR cluster.","The Athena query execution limit has been reached."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team is working on a project that uses Amazon Athena for ad-hoc querying and Amazon EMR for big data processing. They have set up a multi-step pipeline where raw data is processed by EMR, stored in an S3 bucket, and then queried using Athena. However, they are encountering delays in their pipeline due to high latencies in data processing with EMR. They have checked the configuration of the EMR cluster and found no issues.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat could be the MOST likely reason behind this issue?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon Web Services runs on servers in data centers around the world, which are grouped by geographical Region. When you launch an Amazon EMR cluster, you must specify a Region. You might choose a particular Region to reduce latency, minimize costs, or address regulatory requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9c7b2fd856d0cb7bac71dbad0d0f92ae72748585c390dac7533b2919193d1a05.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor best performance, it’s recommended that the cluster be launched in the same region as your data. For instance, if the Amazon S3 bucket stores your input data in the US West (Oregon) Region, you should launch your cluster in the US West (Oregon) Region to avoid cross-region data transfer fees. If you use an Amazon S3 bucket to receive the output of the cluster, you would also want to create it in the US West (Oregon) Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe S3 bucket where the data is stored is in a region different from the EMR cluster.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe data queried by Athena is not in a columnar format\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because the format of the data queried by Athena primarily affects the performance of Athena queries, not the latency in accessing EMR\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe EMR cluster is located in a different region than Athena\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. The location of the Athena service itself does not directly impact the performance of EMR, which is responsible for processing the data before storing it in the S3 bucket. As long as the EMR cluster and the S3 bucket are in the same region, the location of Athena should not be a major factor contributing to the EMR processing delays.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eThe Athena query execution limit has been reached\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because\u0026nbsp;these limits are primarily related to Athena\u0027s query performance and would not directly impact the performance of EMR.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReference:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-region.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-region.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this\u0026nbsp;Amazon EMR Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-32":{"id":"p:3-32","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 32","correct":[3,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eChoose a column with low cardinality in the filtered result set\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eA fact table with multiple distribution keys is useful when multiple dimension tables have to be joined to it\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSmall Dimension tables should be marked to use KEY distribution style, which will cause them to be replicated to each physical node in the cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eData should be distributed in such a way that the rows that participate in joins are already collocated on the nodes with their joining rows in other tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIf a dimension table cannot be collocated with the fact table or other important joining tables, use ALL distribution style for such tables\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Cardinality thấp trong kết quả lọc dễ dồn công việc vào ít slice. Cần xem phân phối của dữ liệu sau lọc, không chỉ toàn bảng.","Một bảng Redshift có một distribution key trong mô hình KEY, không có nhiều distkey cho từng bảng JOIN khác nhau.","KEY phân phối theo giá trị khóa; ALL mới sao chép toàn bảng tới các node. Lựa chọn nhầm hai khái niệm.","Đặt các hàng sẽ JOIN trên cùng node giảm chuyển dữ liệu qua mạng. Đây là mục tiêu quan trọng khi chọn distribution key.","Với dimension nhỏ phù hợp mà không thể đồng vị trí bằng key, ALL đặt bản sao ở mỗi node để tránh redistribution khi JOIN. Cân nhắc chi phí nếu bảng lớn hoặc cập nhật nhiều."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn e-commerce company performs analytics on the company\u0027s data using the Amazon Redshift cluster. The Redshift cluster has two important tables: the orders table and the product table which have millions of rows each. A few small tables with supporting data are also present. The team is looking for the right distribution patterns for the tables, to optimize query speed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following are the key points to consider while planning for the best distribution style for your data? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:37":{"id":"e:2:37","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 37","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to build the pipeline and operate Airflow DAGs that run the scripts on a schedule\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDeploy AWS Glue jobs for the script-based stages and use AWS Glue workflows to schedule and orchestrate the pipeline\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to visually build the pipeline and use EventBridge Scheduler to run each preparation job on a schedule\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun containerized scripts on Amazon ECS tasks, store intermediate files in Amazon S3, and use Step Functions to coordinate the pipeline stages\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company needs to deploy a multi-stage data processing pipeline for claims and provider data. Each stage of the pipeline must run transformation scripts, and the data engineering team needs to schedule and coordinate the stages as one workflow. The company must use only fully managed and serverless services for the pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy AWS Glue jobs for the script-based stages and use AWS Glue workflows to schedule and orchestrate the pipeline\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue jobs are designed to run ETL scripts without requiring the company to manage servers. A data engineer can write or generate PySpark, Spark, Python shell, or other supported job scripts depending on the processing requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue workflows can coordinate multiple AWS Glue jobs, crawlers, and triggers as one ETL pipeline. The workflow can run jobs in a defined order, run independent stages in parallel, and start the pipeline on a schedule.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution meets the requirement because it uses fully managed and serverless AWS Glue capabilities for both script execution and pipeline orchestration. The company does not need to manage EC2 instances, EMR clusters, Airflow environments, or custom container orchestration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6648124feeae0dfdb9a2d102d9c82fa75edf8cf05ba4ae6b7da08eb841a02222.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6648124feeae0dfdb9a2d102d9c82fa75edf8cf05ba4ae6b7da08eb841a02222.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/88662d2a9220109611a6448fe5153fd10b42000230bff03f6c98b60519a221ba.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/88662d2a9220109611a6448fe5153fd10b42000230bff03f6c98b60519a221ba.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun containerized scripts on Amazon ECS tasks, store intermediate files in Amazon S3, and use Step Functions to coordinate the pipeline stages\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon ECS tasks can run containerized scripts, and Step Functions can coordinate multi-stage workflows. This pattern can be valid for custom application workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this design requires the company to build and maintain container images, task definitions, networking configuration, and custom runtime packaging. AWS Glue jobs and workflows provide a more direct fully managed serverless ETL solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to visually build the pipeline and use EventBridge Scheduler to run each preparation job on a schedule\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue DataBrew provides a visual data preparation experience and can run DataBrew jobs with low coding effort. It can be useful when users need interactive profiling and recipe-based transformations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, DataBrew is not the best fit when each pipeline stage must run a script. AWS Glue jobs and workflows provide a better serverless ETL pattern for scripted, multi-stage pipelines.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to build the pipeline and operate Airflow DAGs that run the scripts on a schedule\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow is a managed orchestration service for Apache Airflow. It can build and schedule complex workflows by using Airflow DAGs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option does not provide the simplest serverless ETL execution pattern for script-based stages. AWS Glue jobs directly run ETL scripts in a serverless environment, and AWS Glue workflows can orchestrate those jobs without adding an Airflow layer.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/author-job-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/author-job-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:43":{"id":"e:3:43","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 43","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003ePartition the data by store region\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePartition the data by pet type\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePartition the data by customer\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMigrate the data to Redshift tables for a more cost-efficient analysis\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company operates a chain of pet-care shops and it stores all of its data in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company uses Amazon Athena to analyze customer preferences to launch new products for the different types of pets. The company\u0027s data is of the order of tens of terabytes and the company is looking at reducing the costs of running these queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat do you recommend?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePartition the data by pet type\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBy partitioning your data, you can restrict the amount of data scanned by each query, thus improving performance and reducing cost. You can partition your data by any key. A common practice is to partition the data based on time, often leading to a multi-level partitioning scheme. For example, a customer who has data coming in every hour might decide to partition by year, month, date, and hour. Another customer, who has data coming from many different sources but that is loaded only once per day, might partition by a data source identifier and date.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhile analyzing the new product launches for the different types of pets, most of the queries will be related to the pet type, so partitioning data by pet type will reduce the amount of data scanned by Athena and hence reduce the associated costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePartitioning data in Athena:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/fe813c88fdc66366a616f0153e7ea8f271fd35b3ec023f597ac3528cdcdb1b71.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/fe813c88fdc66366a616f0153e7ea8f271fd35b3ec023f597ac3528cdcdb1b71.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003e*Migrate the data to Redshift tables for a more cost-efficient analysis\u003c/em\u003e - Migrating data to Redshift will increase the costs compared to directly querying data on S3 via Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePartition the data by customer\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePartition the data by store region\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePartitioning data by customers or by store region will not help in optimizing the queries relevant to the given use case, so both these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-3":{"id":"p:4-3","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 3","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon Redshift streaming ingestion by configuring an external schema that maps to the streaming data source in Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Create a materialized view that references the external schema. Configure the materialized view to auto-refresh\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up streaming data ingestion in Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, stage the streams data into Amazon S3 and load it into the Redshift cluster using the COPY command on a real-time basis\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up streaming data ingestion in Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose, write the delivery streams data into Amazon S3 and load it into the Redshift cluster using the COPY command on a real-time basis\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up streaming data ingestion in Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and create materialized views directly on top of the stream by using SQL queries. Configure the materialized view to auto-refresh\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Redshift streaming ingestion đọc trực tiếp từ Kinesis Data Streams thông qua external schema. Materialized view tham chiếu schema đó và tự làm mới giúp dữ liệu sẵn sàng cho phân tích gần thời gian thực, không cần tầng S3 trung gian.","Đưa dữ liệu qua S3 rồi chạy COPY thêm bước trung gian và độ trễ. COPY nạp theo lô, nên gọi cách này là nạp thời gian thực không giải quyết được yêu cầu hiệu quả bằng streaming ingestion.","Firehose chuyển dữ liệu tới Redshift qua S3 và COPY. Cơ chế gom lô này có độ trễ và không tối ưu bằng kết nối streaming trực tiếp cho yêu cầu của đề.","Materialized view được tạo trong Redshift, không phải trực tiếp trong Kinesis Data Streams. Cần tạo external schema ánh xạ tới nguồn stream trước khi tạo view đọc dữ liệu."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is looking to develop real-time analytics capabilities and plans to utilize Amazon Kinesis Data Streams and Amazon Redshift to handle streaming data at rates of several gigabytes per second. The company aims to achieve near real-time insights using the existing business intelligence (BI) and analytics tools.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution would fulfill these requirements with minimal operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-60":{"id":"p:1-60","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 60","correct":[0,2,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eData at rest inside the volume is encrypted\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eData moving between the volume and the instance is NOT encrypted\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAny snapshot created from the volume is encrypted\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAny snapshot created from the volume is NOT encrypted\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eData moving between the volume and the instance is encrypted\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eData at rest inside the volume is NOT encrypted\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["EBS encryption mã hóa dữ liệu lưu trong volume. Đây là một trong các phạm vi bảo vệ được đề hỏi.","Khẳng định không mã hóa dữ liệu giữa volume và instance là sai trong cơ chế EBS encryption được mô tả.","Snapshot tạo từ EBS volume đã mã hóa cũng được mã hóa, giúp duy trì bảo vệ dữ liệu trong bản sao.","Snapshot từ volume đã mã hóa không trở thành bản không mã hóa. Lựa chọn này trái cơ chế kế thừa mã hóa.","EBS encryption bảo vệ cả dữ liệu di chuyển giữa volume và instance theo cơ chế của dịch vụ, ngoài dữ liệu lưu trên volume.","Dữ liệu nằm trong volume đã bật EBS encryption được mã hóa. Khẳng định ngược lại là sai."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA pharmaceutical company is considering moving to AWS Cloud to accelerate the research and development process. Most of the daily workflows would be centered around running batch jobs on Amazon EC2 instances with storage on Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS) volumes. The CTO is concerned about meeting HIPAA compliance norms for sensitive data stored on Amazon EBS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options represent the correct capabilities of an encrypted Amazon EBS volume? (Select three)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-22":{"id":"p:2-22","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 22","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the original files in S3 Intelligent-Tiering for 75 days, enable bucket versioning, and configure an S3 Lifecycle rule to expire the files after the retention period\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the original files in S3 Standard for 75 days and configure an S3 Lifecycle rule to expire the current versions of the files after the retention period\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the original files in S3 Standard-Infrequent Access immediately after upload, keep the files for 75 days, and configure an S3 Lifecycle rule to expire the files when the retention period ends\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the original files in S3 Standard for 75 days, transition the files to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and then configure expiration after archival storage\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Versioning có thể giữ noncurrent versions sau khi hết hạn bản hiện tại, phát sinh lưu trữ không cần thiết. Đề chỉ yêu cầu giữ bản gốc 75 ngày rồi xóa.","Giữ tệp trong S3 Standard để sẵn sàng xử lý lại, rồi Lifecycle expiration sau 75 ngày. Thiết kế đơn giản cho thời hạn lưu cố định của đề.","Standard-IA có phí truy xuất và chưa tối ưu cho tệp còn có thể được dùng lại trong quy trình xử lý. Bộ đề chọn Standard trong giai đoạn giữ ngắn này.","Sau 75 ngày không còn cần dữ liệu, nên đưa tiếp vào Glacier làm thêm chi phí và thời gian lưu không cần thiết. Cần xóa chứ không lưu trữ tiếp."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA video production company uploads large raw footage files to an Amazon S3 bucket in the us-west-2 Region for transcoding and quality checks. After the transcoding workflow finishes, the company must retain the original raw files for 75 days in case editors need to reprocess any footage. After 75 days, the company no longer needs the originals and wants to delete them to reduce storage costs. The processed videos are stored in a separate S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich S3 Lifecycle configuration will meet these requirements for the original files MOST cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:6:14":{"id":"e:6:14","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 14","correct":[0,1],"answers":["Use AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) to migrate the database to S3 with Full Load + change data capture (CDC).","Use Apache Parquet format when writing the data into the S3 bucket.","Use AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) to migrate the database to S3 with change data capture (CDC) Only.","Use CSV format when writing the data into the S3 bucket.","Use AWS Lambda to trigger data replication on a daily basis from the MySQL database to the S3 bucket."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company operates a MySQL database in a data center. Recently, they have decided to replicate their data into an Amazon S3 bucket for analysis. The company must ensure that ongoing changes in its database are reflected in the target S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;Which solution can satisfy the requirements? (Select TWO).\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Database Migration Service(AWS DMS) is a cloud service that makes it possible to migrate relational database, data warehouses, NoSQL databases, and other data stores.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use AWS DMS to transfer data to Amazon S3 from any database source. When Amazon S3 is employed as a target in an AWS DMS task, both full load and change data capture(CDC) data are written to a comma-separated value (.csv) format by default. However, to ensure more compact storage and faster query performance, you may opt to have the data written in Apache Parquet(.parquet) format.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/398e4a7b657a7e2dfb9b0251d60b19936a7d36648d5749eea0076b71cd6af088.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen using Full load + CDC in AWS DMZ Task, the task migrates existing data and then updates the target database based on changes to the source database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct options are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Use AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) to migrate the database to S3 with Full Load + CDC.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Use Apache Parquet format when writing the data into the S3 bucket.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse AWS Database Migration Service (DMS) to migrate the database to S3 with CDC Only\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect it is because, by default, When using Amazon S3 as a target in an AWS DMS task, both full load and CDC data are used. However, choosing the CDC needs to pay more attention to the initial full load of the existing data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse CSV format when writing the data into the S3 bucket\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. While you can use CSV format when writing the data, there are better choices for optimal query performance, especially when dealing with extensive data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Lambda to trigger data replication on a daily basis from the MySQL database to the S3 bucket \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. While AWS Lambda can be used for data movement tasks, its batch-processing nature could lead to significant delays in reflecting the latest data changes. Additionally, the manual effort required to manage and scale the replication gives an additional amount of operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Task.CDC.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Task.CDC.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Target.S3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Target.S3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Database Migration Service Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-database-migration-service/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-database-migration-service/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-3":{"id":"p:1-3","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 3","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge rule that matches failed AWS Glue job state change events. Configure the rule to invoke an AWS Lambda function that parses the event and publishes a notification to an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge rule that matches failed AWS Glue job state change events. Use the EventBridge rule metric in Amazon CloudWatch to create an alarm, and configure the alarm to notify an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eModify every AWS Glue ETL script to publish a custom Amazon CloudWatch metric after each transformation stage, create a CloudWatch alarm for the custom metric, and configure the alarm to notify an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic when the metric value indicates a failed pipeline run\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon CloudWatch Logs for the AWS Glue jobs. Create an Amazon EventBridge rule that detects new log entries, invokes an AWS Lambda function to inspect the logs, and publishes a message to an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic when failure patterns appear\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Bắt sự kiện Glue thất bại là đúng hướng, nhưng thêm Lambda để chuyển thông báo sang SNS tạo thêm mã nguồn, quyền, triển khai và bảo trì không cần thiết.","Glue phát sự kiện thay đổi trạng thái tới EventBridge. Lọc trạng thái thất bại, dùng metric của rule để tạo CloudWatch alarm rồi gửi SNS: tận dụng dịch vụ có sẵn, ít công vận hành.","Sửa cả tám ETL script để phát metric riêng tốn công và có thể bỏ sót lỗi xảy ra trước đoạn ghi metric. Sự kiện trạng thái Glue đã cung cấp tín hiệu cần thiết.","Phân tích chuỗi log bằng Lambda cần tự quản lý mẫu lỗi và mã xử lý. Sự kiện thất bại của Glue trực tiếp và đơn giản hơn việc dò lỗi trong log."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics company runs eight AWS Glue ETL jobs in the eu-west-1 Region to transform point-of-sale data for executive dashboards. The dashboards are business critical, and the data engineering team must notify the data platform stakeholders whenever any of the AWS Glue pipeline jobs fails.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:15":{"id":"e:4:15","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 15","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, and send the telemetry data with a Partition ID that uses the value of the Desktop ID\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) standard queue, and send the telemetry data as is\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) FIFO (First-In-First-Out) queue, and send the telemetry data as is\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) FIFO (First-In-First-Out) queue, and make sure the telemetry data is sent with a Group ID attribute representing the value of the Desktop ID\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eYou are establishing a monitoring solution for desktop systems, that will be sending telemetry data into AWS every 1 minute. Data for each system must be processed in order, independently, and you would like to scale the number of consumers to be possibly equal to the number of desktop systems that are being monitored.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat do you recommend?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) FIFO (First-In-First-Out) queue, and make sure the telemetry data is sent with a Group ID attribute representing the value of the Desktop ID\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Simple Queue Service (SQS) is a fully managed message queuing service that enables you to decouple and scale microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications. SQS offers two types of message queues. Standard queues offer maximum throughput, best-effort ordering, and at-least-once delivery. SQS FIFO queues are designed to guarantee that messages are processed exactly once, in the exact order that they are sent.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWe, therefore, need to use an SQS FIFO queue. If we don\u0027t specify a GroupID, then all the messages are in absolute order, but we can only have 1 consumer at most.\nTo allow for multiple consumers to read data for each Desktop application, and to scale the number of consumers, we should use the \"Group ID\" attribute. So this is the correct option.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) FIFO (First-In-First-Out) queue, and send the telemetry data as is\u003c/strong\u003e - This is incorrect because if we send the telemetry data as is then we will not be able to scale the number of consumers to be equal to the number of desktop systems. In this case, each message will have its consumer. So we should use the \"Group ID\" attribute so that multiple consumers can read data for each Desktop application.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) standard queue, and send the telemetry data as is\u003c/strong\u003e - An Amazon SQS standard queue has no ordering capability so that\u0027s ruled out.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, and send the telemetry data with a Partition ID that uses the value of the Desktop ID\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Kinesis Data Streams (KDS) is a massively scalable and durable real-time data streaming service. KDS can continuously capture gigabytes of data per second from hundreds of thousands of sources such as website clickstreams, database event streams, financial transactions, social media feeds, IT logs, and location-tracking events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eKinesis Data Streams would work and would give us the data for each desktop application within shards, but we can only have as many consumers as shards in Kinesis (which is in practice, much less than the number of producers).\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/solving-complex-ordering-challenges-with-amazon-sqs-fifo-queues/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/solving-complex-ordering-challenges-with-amazon-sqs-fifo-queues/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/sqs/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/sqs/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:58":{"id":"e:5:58","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 58","correct":[0],"answers":["AWS Glue Interactive Sessions","AWS Glue DataBrew","AWS Glue Data Catalog","AWS Glue Streaming ETL service"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team is collecting log data from their website and storing it in Amazon S3 in JSON format. They need to transform and analyze this data to understand user behavior on the site. Some transformations needed are calculating daily active users, average session duration, and error rates. The processed data needs to be loaded into Amazon Redshift every day for analysis. The team has been using AWS Glue for their ETL jobs, but they are facing challenges with the development and testing of ETL scripts, which is slowing down their process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following can the team leverage to improve the development and testing aspect of their ETL process?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Interactive Sessions\u003c/strong\u003e is a feature that allows developers to rapidly build, test, and run data preparation and analytics applications. It provides a programmatic and visual interface for building and testing Extract, Transform, and Load (ETL) scripts for data preparation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eInteractive sessions run Apache Spark analytics applications and provide on-demand access to a remote Spark runtime environment. AWS Glue transparently manages serverless Spark for these interactive sessions. This means developers can focus more on writing the transformation logic than managing the infrastructure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/df8172bd19faf7c52d82e97f5d0d715168554745768568db09bd08d3350c5093.png\" alt=\"\"\u003eInteractive sessions are flexible, allowing developers to build and test applications from the environment of their choice. Developers can create and work with interactive sessions through the AWS Command Line Interface and the API.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor visual authoring and testing of notebook scripts, developers can use Jupyter-compatible notebooks. Interactive sessions provide an open-source Jupyter kernel that integrates almost anywhere that Jupyter does, including integrating with IDEs such as PyCharm, IntelliJ, and VS Code. This enables developers to author code in their local environment and run it seamlessly on the interactive sessions’ backend.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eUsing the interactive sessions API, customers can programmatically run applications that use Apache Spark analytics without having to manage Spark infrastructure. Developers can run one or more Spark statements within a single interactive session.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTherefore, AWS Glue Interactive Sessions provides a faster, cheaper, and more flexible way to build and run data preparation and analytics applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Interactive Sessions.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Data Catalog\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because it primarily serves as a persistent metadata store, but it does not directly aid in the development and testing of ETL scripts, which is the team’s main challenge.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Streaming ETL service\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because it is mainly used for real-time data preparation and loading. While it can be useful for continuous ingestion pipelines, it doesn’t directly address the challenge of developing and testing ETL scripts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because it is primarily a visual data preparation tool that enables users to clean and normalize data without writing any code. While it can help with data cleaning and normalization, it doesn’t directly address the development and testing of ETL scripts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/interactive-sessions-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/interactive-sessions-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/interactive-sessions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/interactive-sessions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:64":{"id":"e:4:64","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 64","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EFS file system, enable audit logging for the Redshift cluster, and configure Redshift to write audit logs to the file system\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EBS volume, enable audit logging for the Redshift cluster, and configure Redshift to store connection and user activity logs on the volume\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon S3 bucket, enable Redshift database audit logging and user activity logging, and configure the cluster to deliver logs to the bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Config rules to evaluate Redshift cluster configuration changes, and store compliance evaluation results as the complete database audit trail\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company runs an Amazon Redshift provisioned data warehouse for regulated reporting. Auditors require the company to retain records of database connection attempts, user changes, and SQL activity that users perform in the warehouse.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer must configure the Redshift environment so the audit logs are stored outside the data warehouse for compliance review and long-term retention.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon S3 bucket, enable Redshift database audit logging and user activity logging, and configure the cluster to deliver logs to the bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift database audit logging can record database-level activity for compliance and security monitoring. The audit logs include connection logs, user logs, and user activity logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster, the company can configure audit logging to deliver log files to an Amazon S3 bucket. Storing the logs in S3 keeps the audit records outside the data warehouse and supports long-term retention and independent review.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe user activity log requires the associated database parameter to be enabled. Therefore, enabling Redshift audit logging, enabling user activity logging, and choosing an S3 bucket as the destination meets the requirement to store user activity and connection activity logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon EFS file system, enable audit logging for the Redshift cluster, and configure Redshift to write audit logs to the file system\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EFS provides shared file storage that can be mounted by supported compute services. It is useful for workloads that need a managed network file system.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option does not meet the requirement because Amazon Redshift audit logging does not write audit logs to an EFS file system. Redshift supports audit log delivery to destinations such as Amazon S3 for provisioned clusters.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Config rules to evaluate Redshift cluster configuration changes, and store compliance evaluation results as the complete database audit trail\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Config can evaluate resource configuration against rules and record configuration changes. It is useful for checking whether Redshift clusters meet governance requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not provide a complete audit trail of database connections and user SQL activity. AWS Config evaluates configuration compliance, while Redshift database audit logging captures the required user and connection activity logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon EBS volume, enable audit logging for the Redshift cluster, and configure Redshift to store connection and user activity logs on the volume\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EBS provides block storage for EC2 instances. EBS volumes can support applications that run on EC2 and need persistent local block storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because Amazon Redshift is a managed data warehouse service. The data engineer does not attach an EBS volume to a Redshift cluster to store audit logs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/redshift-audit-logging\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/redshift-audit-logging\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-30":{"id":"p:4-30","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 30","correct":[0,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a zero-ETL integration from the Amazon Aurora MySQL cluster to Amazon Redshift and configure data filters to include only the required reporting tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS DMS replication task from Amazon Aurora MySQL to Amazon Redshift and configure table mappings to replicate only the required reporting tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift query editor v2 to query the Amazon Aurora MySQL database directly from the reporting dashboards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate materialized views in Amazon Redshift for all 90 Aurora MySQL source tables and refresh the materialized views on a fixed schedule before each reporting cycle\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a destination database in Amazon Redshift from the zero-ETL integration so analysts can query the replicated Aurora data\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Zero-ETL từ Aurora MySQL sang Redshift tự quản lý đồng bộ cho phân tích. Dùng data filters chỉ chọn 24 bảng cần báo cáo giúp tránh sao chép cả 90 bảng.","DMS có thể chọn bảng để sao chép nhưng cần thêm cấu hình và quản lý replication. Đề ưu tiên ít vận hành nhất nên chọn tích hợp zero-ETL được quản lý.","Query editor v2 là giao diện chạy truy vấn, không tự tạo cơ chế truy vấn Aurora trực tiếp từ dashboard. Chỉ mở công cụ này không thay thế luồng đồng bộ dữ liệu.","Tạo materialized view cho cả 90 bảng vẫn xử lý dữ liệu dư thừa và phụ thuộc lịch làm mới. Nó không giải quyết trực tiếp việc chỉ cần 24 bảng với ít công vận hành.","Sau khi tạo integration, cần tạo database đích trong Redshift từ integration đó. Database này cung cấp các bảng Aurora được đồng bộ để nhóm phân tích truy vấn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company generates operational reports from 24 tables in an Amazon Redshift data warehouse. The source data resides in an Amazon Aurora MySQL database that contains 90 tables. Currently, the company refreshes the entire Aurora dataset into Amazon Redshift every 30 minutes, which delays reporting jobs and consumes unnecessary resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:54":{"id":"e:4:54","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 54","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003ePartition the data by order date and load all records into Amazon Redshift before running the weekly analytics queries\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePartition the data by ingestion date and use Amazon EMR Spark jobs to query the weekly order metrics from Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePartition the data by order date and query the matching partitions by using Amazon Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore all order files in a single S3 prefix and use Athena queries with WHERE clauses to filter by order date at runtime\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company ingests daily order records into an Amazon S3 data lake. A data engineer runs analytics queries once each week to calculate order metrics for multiple order-date ranges, such as week-to-date, month-to-date, and custom promotional periods.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe order volume is expected to increase significantly over the next year. The data engineer needs a cost-effective optimization that keeps query performance stable as the dataset grows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement MOST cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePartition the data by order date and query the matching partitions by using Amazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePartitioning the data by order date aligns the physical S3 layout with the most common query pattern. Because the weekly queries filter metrics by order-date ranges, Athena can scan only the partitions that match the requested dates.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach helps query performance remain more stable as data volume increases. Without partition pruning, Athena would need to scan more historical order data as the dataset grows, which increases both query runtime and query cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAthena is also cost-effective for this pattern because the queries run only once each week and the data already resides in Amazon S3. The company avoids provisioning or operating a separate compute cluster or data warehouse for an intermittent analytics workload.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePartition the data by ingestion date and use Amazon EMR Spark jobs to query the weekly order metrics from Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - Partitioning by ingestion date can help when queries filter data by the date the files arrived in the data lake. Amazon EMR can also process large datasets with Apache Spark.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that the query pattern uses order date, not ingestion date. EMR also adds cluster management and compute overhead for a weekly query workload that Athena can handle directly over S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore all order files in a single S3 prefix and use Athena queries with WHERE clauses to filter by order date at runtime\u003c/strong\u003e - Athena can query files in Amazon S3 and can apply WHERE clauses to filter query results by columns such as order date.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe issue with this approach is that storing all data in a single unpartitioned prefix prevents Athena from pruning S3 locations effectively. As the dataset grows, queries will scan more data and performance can degrade.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePartition the data by order date and load all records into Amazon Redshift before running the weekly analytics queries\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift is a powerful data warehouse for frequent, high-performance analytics over structured data. Partitioning by order date before loading can help organize data for some downstream analytics patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because the workload runs only once each week. Loading all records into Redshift adds warehouse cost and operational steps when Athena can query partitioned S3 data more directly and cost-effectively.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-data-optimization-techniques.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-data-optimization-techniques.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-18":{"id":"p:2-18","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 18","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eIncrease the number of shards in Kinesis Data Streams to support throughput from both vehicles\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the partition key to use the sensor ID instead of the name of the vehicle\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up another Kinesis Data Stream for Vehicle A with twenty shards and then direct Vehicle A sensor data to this new Kinesis Data Stream\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up another Kinesis Data Stream for Vehicle B with ten shards and then direct Vehicle B sensor data to this new Kinesis Data Stream\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Tổng năng lực hai shard đã đủ theo phân tích đề; vấn đề là phân phối không đều. Thêm shard có thể tốn tiền mà chưa sửa được khóa gây điểm nóng.","Dùng sensor ID tăng số giá trị partition key và phân phối dữ liệu đều hơn giữa các shard. Dùng tên xe gom nhiều cảm biến vào cùng khóa, dễ gây hot shard.","Tạo thêm stream 20 shard là cấp dư tài nguyên. Cần sửa cách phân phối khóa trước.","Một stream 10 shard khác cũng thêm chi phí không cần thiết. Đổi partition key giải quyết nguyên nhân mất cân bằng hiện tại."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA research agency has deployed two autonomous underwater vehicles in the ocean to track parameters such as salinity, temperature, speed and direction of currents, etc. Vehicle A has twenty sensors whereas Vehicle B has ten sensors. Each sensor is identified by a unique ID. Amazon Kinesis Data Streams is being used to gather data from each sensor. A single Amazon Kinesis Data Stream with two shards is configured based on the total incoming and outgoing data throughput. Two partition keys are generated based on the name of the vehicles. During initial testing, data from Vehicle A experiences a bottleneck whereas data from Vehicle B does not. The overall stream throughput has been validated to be less than the assigned Kinesis Data Streams throughput.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions would you use to address this bottleneck without increasing the total cost and complexity of the system?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-51":{"id":"p:4-51","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 51","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003ePlace each department’s tables and views in separate databases and manage permissions at the database level for each department\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRename every table and view to include the department name and manage permissions by following the naming convention manually\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePlace each department’s tables and views in dedicated schemas and manage permissions at the schema level\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore department data in separate S3 prefixes, create external schemas for each prefix, and manage all access through bucket policies\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Database riêng có thể tách dữ liệu, và Redshift có hỗ trợ truy vấn liên database ở cấu hình phù hợp. Tuy nhiên cách này phức tạp hơn schema khi các bộ phận thường xuyên join dữ liệu cùng kho.","Tên bảng chỉ là quy ước, không phải ranh giới phân quyền. Dựa vào tên và cấp quyền thủ công cho từng object làm tăng công quản lý và nguy cơ bỏ sót.","Schema nhóm các bảng và view của từng bộ phận trong cùng database, thuận tiện quản lý quyền và join chéo bằng tên schema.object. Cần cấp quyền schema cùng quyền object hoặc default privileges phù hợp.","Chuyển dữ liệu ra S3 thay đổi kiến trúc không cần thiết. Bucket policy không thay thế toàn bộ quyền SQL đối với bảng và view trong Redshift."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA manufacturing company uses a shared Amazon Redshift cluster as its central analytics warehouse. The engineering department and the finance department each maintain their own tables and views in the same warehouse.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company’s security policy requires each department to have separate permissions for its own analytics objects. Analysts also frequently run SQL queries that join engineering objects with finance objects in the same query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:8":{"id":"e:3:8","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 8","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse an AWS Glue Flex job after business hours to process the previous day\u0027s files, apply the transformations, and load the curated data into Amazon Redshift Serverless\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse an AWS Glue Flex job after business hours to process the previous day\u0027s files, apply the transformations, and load the curated data into an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse a provisioned Amazon EMR cluster after business hours to process the previous day\u0027s files, apply the transformations, and load the curated data into Amazon Redshift Serverless\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EMR Serverless after business hours to transform the previous day\u0027s files, store the curated output in Amazon S3, and require partners to query the files directly with Athena\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn insurance company uses an Amazon S3 bucket as a central landing zone for files from several policy administration systems. The company must clean, standardize, and transform the previous day\u0027s data before making the curated data available to external reporting partners. The reporting partners need to run SQL queries against the prepared data during normal business hours. The company wants the most cost-effective solution for the overnight transformations and the daytime SQL query workload.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse an AWS Glue Flex job after business hours to process the previous day\u0027s files, apply the transformations, and load the curated data into Amazon Redshift Serverless\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Flex jobs are designed for non-urgent data integration workloads. Because the company processes the previous day\u0027s data after normal business hours, the job does not need the fastest startup time or dedicated execution capacity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift Serverless is a good fit for the partner query workload because the partners run SQL queries during business hours. Redshift Serverless can automatically provision and scale capacity for the query workload without requiring the company to operate a provisioned cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution is the most cost-effective option because it combines lower-cost overnight ETL with a serverless SQL analytics layer. The company avoids paying for a continuously running EMR cluster or Redshift provisioned cluster when the workload pattern is scheduled and time-bound.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/30da45056fab13ff95593f530fb89af279031b5c1271d565913f97720f0c6358.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/30da45056fab13ff95593f530fb89af279031b5c1271d565913f97720f0c6358.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-flex-jobs-cost-savings-on-etl-workloads/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-flex-jobs-cost-savings-on-etl-workloads/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse a provisioned Amazon EMR cluster after business hours to process the previous day\u0027s files, apply the transformations, and load the curated data into Amazon Redshift Serverless\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR can run distributed data processing jobs and can process large datasets from Amazon S3. It can also write curated results into Amazon Redshift Serverless for partner queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, a provisioned EMR cluster requires cluster lifecycle management, capacity planning, patching, and monitoring. For scheduled overnight processing, AWS Glue Flex provides a lower-maintenance and more cost-effective ETL option.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon EMR Serverless after business hours to transform the previous day\u0027s files, store the curated output in Amazon S3, and require partners to query the files directly with Athena\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR Serverless can run analytics applications without managing EMR clusters. Athena can also query curated data directly in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the requirement asks for multiple partners to run SQL queries on the prepared data, and the source options center on loading the prepared data into Amazon Redshift for partner access. This option changes the query layer and does not use the lower-cost AWS Glue Flex job class for the overnight transformation workload.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse an AWS Glue Flex job after business hours to process the previous day\u0027s files, apply the transformations, and load the curated data into an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue Flex is appropriate for the overnight transformation work. It can process the previous day\u0027s data with lower cost for non-urgent jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option loads the output into an Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster. A provisioned cluster can continue to incur cost even outside the partner query window, while Redshift Serverless better matches a business-hours query pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/glue/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-flex-jobs-cost-savings-on-etl-workloads/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-flex-jobs-cost-savings-on-etl-workloads/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/serverless-whatis.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/serverless-whatis.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/serverless-billing-on-demand.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/serverless-billing-on-demand.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:9:5":{"id":"e:9:5","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 9 Â· CÃ¢u 5","correct":[1],"answers":["Deploy AWS Glue Data Quality to manage access controls through validation rules to dictate permissions for Amazon S3 and the AWS Glue Data Catalog","Use AWS Lake Formation permissions to manage access policies for both Amazon S3 and AWS Glue Data Catalog","Utilize the job scheduling feature of AWS Glue DataBrew to align access rights between Amazon S3 and AWS Glue Data Catalog","Apply S3 bucket policies for integrated permission control across both AWS Glue and Amazon S3"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA digital marketing firm operates a data lake on Amazon S3. The firm utilizes AWS Glue Data Catalog for metadata management. Over the years, the data lake has grown significantly due to the addition of various data sources and metadata.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe administrator in charge of the data lake wants to establish a method to efficiently manage permissions, ensuring synchronization between Amazon S3 and the Data Catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will allow the administrator to handle permissions management with the LEAST amount of development overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation is a service that simplifies the management of security and governance for data lakes. It offers centralized, granular access control to data across multiple AWS services like Amazon S3 and AWS Glue Data Catalog. By using Lake Formation, the administrator can manage permissions in a unified way, which is beneficial for large data lakes with numerous data sources and metadata.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3365184b30b797919379cb8aa6dc7be6c0d5c0384a75427ec6763a804bb1e166.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/a0bb6b875eb3367c3b26a6b1047388385bf307ca315c2b243128be00c0473fb9.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is to \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Lake Formation permissions to manage access policies for both Amazon S3 and AWS Glue Data Catalog.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eDeploy AWS Glue Data Quality to manage access controls through validation rules to dictate permissions for Amazon S3 and the AWS Glue Data Catalog \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect as AWS Glue Data Quality is designed for data validation and quality checks, not for managing access permissions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eApply S3 bucket policies for permission control across both AWS Glue and Amazon S3\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. Although possible, this solution would entail significant management overhead, and not observe the “least amount of development overhead”.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUtilize the job scheduling feature of AWS Glue DataBrew to align access rights between Amazon S3 and AWS Glue Data Catalog\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because job scheduling is more intended for data preparation and transformation tasks rather than permission management.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/serverless-etl-aws-glue/aws-glue-data-catalog.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/serverless-etl-aws-glue/aws-glue-data-catalog.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/access-policy-language-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/access-policy-language-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out the TutorialsDojo Cheat Sheets here:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-40":{"id":"p:3-40","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 40","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate standard Athena views for each domain, use those Athena views from Amazon Redshift for cross-domain reporting, enable CloudTrail auditing, and use Lake Formation table permissions\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate separate S3 prefixes for each domain, use IAM policies to control access to each prefix, and require Athena and Redshift users to query only their assigned prefixes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog views for shared domain views, manage access with Lake Formation, audit access with CloudTrail, and use security definer views to mask PII\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate Amazon Redshift materialized views for each domain, publish Redshift datashares to the other teams, and use Redshift permissions for all cross-domain analysis\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["View Athena thông thường không phải lớp view dùng chung đa engine theo thiết kế yêu cầu. Cần Data Catalog views cho khả năng tương tác phù hợp.","IAM prefix chỉ giới hạn object, không biểu diễn view đã phê duyệt hay che PII ở cấp cột.","Glue Data Catalog views tạo lớp view dùng chung; Lake Formation quản lý quyền, CloudTrail phục vụ audit. Security definer view có thể trình bày dữ liệu đã lọc/che theo định nghĩa view và quyền được cấp.","Redshift materialized view/datashare tập trung vào hệ Redshift, chưa đáp ứng lớp truy cập view chung cho cả Athena và các domain như đề yêu cầu."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company is implementing a data mesh architecture on AWS. The market analytics, credit risk, and regulatory reporting domains each own their own datasets and must share only approved views with other domains. The company has more than 1,200 tables across 60 databases in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. All domains use Amazon Athena for ad hoc analysis and Amazon Redshift for complex reporting. The regulatory reporting team must audit all data access, and personally identifiable information must be hidden from teams that do not have approval to view it. The company needs a scalable solution that supports cross-domain analysis across both query engines.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:49":{"id":"e:1:49","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 49","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Lake Formation to define access policies for each of the teams separately to restrict access to query history for the respective teams and control costs\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Athena workgroup for each team and apply tags. Use these tags in a new IAM policy to configure appropriate permissions to the workgroups\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an IAM group for each team. Assign appropriate permissions to each of the IAM groups by associating custom IAM policies that restrict access to the query history and control costs\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage S3 Access Points to control access to query history of the teams by creating unique access control policies for each access point\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eMultiple teams within a company use Amazon Athena to run ad-hoc queries on its data stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. The usage costs for Athena have been running high and the company wants to set a limit on the amount of data that can be scanned by each team. Also, the teams should not have access to queries, query results, or query history of other teams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs a data engineer, how will you implement the requirement with the least operational and cost overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Athena workgroup for each team and apply tags. Use these tags in a new IAM policy to configure appropriate permissions to the workgroups\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUse workgroups to separate users, teams, applications, or workloads, to set limits on the amount of data each query or the entire workgroup can process, and to track costs. Because workgroups act as resources, you can use resource-level identity-based policies to control access to a specific workgroup. You can also view query-related metrics in Amazon CloudWatch, control costs by configuring limits on the amount of data scanned, create thresholds, and trigger actions, such as Amazon SNS, when these thresholds are breached.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo further control costs, you can create capacity reservations with the number of data processing units that you specify and add one or more workgroups to the reservation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBenefits of using workgroups:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e00f915c48fd6a4f2d320ec0de03e6932f03c8d0fd56dd0fed6a13525b152ae4.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e00f915c48fd6a4f2d320ec0de03e6932f03c8d0fd56dd0fed6a13525b152ae4.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/workgroups-benefits.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/workgroups-benefits.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Lake Formation to define access policies for each of the teams separately to restrict access to query history for the respective teams and control costs\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Lake Formation allows you to define and enforce database, table, and column-level access policies when using Athena queries to read data stored in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAthena query results locations in Amazon S3 cannot be registered with Lake Formation and IAM permissions policies for Amazon S3 control access. In addition, Lake Formation permissions do not apply to Athena query history. You need to use Athena workgroups to control access to query history.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an IAM group for each team. Assign appropriate permissions to each of the IAM groups by associating custom IAM policies that restrict access to the query history and control costs\u003c/strong\u003e - You cannot use IAM policies to restrict access to query history for different groups of Athena users. So, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage S3 Access Points to control access to query history of the teams by creating unique access control policies for each access point\u003c/strong\u003e - With S3 Access Points, customers can create unique access control policies for each access point to easily control access to shared datasets. Customers with shared datasets including data lakes, media archives, and user-generated content can easily scale access for hundreds of applications by creating individualized access points with names and permissions customized for each application. Any access point can be restricted to a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) to firewall S3 data access within customers’ private networks, and AWS Service Control Policies can be used to ensure all access points are VPC restricted. You cannot use access control policies to control access to Athena query history, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/manage-queries-control-costs-with-workgroups.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/manage-queries-control-costs-with-workgroups.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/access-points/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/access-points/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:20":{"id":"e:6:20","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 20","correct":[2],"answers":["Configure a scheduled AWS Glue crawler that runs every day.","\u003cspan\u003eSet the \u003ccode\u003eenableUpdateCatalog\u003c/code\u003e option to \u003ccode\u003etrue\u003c/code\u003e within the Glue job’s script.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eImplement a code within the Lambda function to automatically invoke the Boto3 \u003ccode\u003ecreate_partition\u003c/code\u003e API in AWS Glue.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eRun the \u003ccode\u003eMSCK REPAIR TABLE\u003c/code\u003e command directly from the Amazon Athena query editor after new data is added.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer executes a weekly AWS Glue job, sourcing data from an Amazon S3 bucket. This bucket receives regular data uploads from a Lambda function, organized in a path format by year, month, and day.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineer must create a solution to update the AWS Glue Data Catalog as soon as new partitions are added to the bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will achieve this objective?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e is a managed ETL service that simplifies data preparation and analytics. The AWS Glue Data Catalog is a central repository for metadata regarding data utilized in ETL jobs within AWS Glue. This catalog is critical for managing the schema and structure of data in various storage mediums, including Amazon S3. Crawlers within AWS Glue are designed to scan data stores, identify data schema, and populate the Data Catalog with metadata tables describing the data, facilitating seamless ETL processes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/04e81e7faba69a1d568f53c8e339759d82ebc90576560453ac89d3124ca4af09.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBoto3, AWS\u0027s SDK for Python, offers various methods to interact with AWS services programmatically, including AWS Glue. Through the Boto3 AWS Glue client, developers can directly invoke operations such as \u003ccode\u003ecreate_partition\u003c/code\u003e , allowing for real-time updates to the AWS Glue Data Catalog as soon as data is written to S3, without the need for manual intervention or waiting for scheduled crawlers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Implement a code within the Lambda function to automatically invoke the Boto3 \u003ccode\u003ecreate_partition\u003c/code\u003e API in AWS Glue.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure a scheduled AWS Glue crawler that runs every day\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This approach involves updating the AWS Glue Data Catalog at scheduled intervals, which may not meet the real-time needs after an ETL job. Although AWS Glue crawlers can effectively discover new and existing data in S3 and create or update the Data Catalog, the once-a-week schedule may cause a delay in reflecting new data partitions. This delay can affect the availability of data for analysis and querying.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet the \u003ccode\u003eenableUpdateCatalog\u003c/code\u003e option to \u003ccode\u003etrue\u003c/code\u003e within the Glue job’s script\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This option is only helpful if you want to update the Data Catalog with the output data from the job. It won\u0027t automatically detect and add new partitions from the input data source.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun the \u003ccode\u003eMSCK REPAIR TABLE\u003c/code\u003e command directly from the Amazon Athena query editor after new data is added \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. While this command can repair and add new partitions to the Data Catalog, it is not an automated solution. Running this command manually from the Athena query editor requires manual intervention and may miss new partitions if not executed promptly after data is added to the S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-api-catalog-partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-api-catalog-partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://boto3.amazonaws.com/v1/documentation/api/latest/reference/services/glue/client/create_partition.html#\"\u003ehttps://boto3.amazonaws.com/v1/documentation/api/latest/reference/services/glue/client/create_partition.html#\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-21":{"id":"p:3-21","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 21","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCalculate a SHA-256 checksum for each document before upload and pass the checksum value to the AWS SDK in each Amazon S3 upload request\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Object Lock in governance mode on the bucket so uploaded patient billing documents cannot be accidentally overwritten or deleted\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the bucket default encryption to server-side encryption with customer-provided keys and use S3 Bucket Keys to validate uploaded objects\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDownload every uploaded patient billing document from Amazon S3 after upload and run a custom program to compare the files with source copies\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Tính SHA-256 từ tệp nguồn và gửi cùng upload để S3 kiểm tra checksum của dữ liệu nhận. Xác nhận toàn vẹn ngay trong quá trình tải lên.","Object Lock bảo vệ dữ liệu sau khi lưu, không xác nhận tệp lưu trùng với nguồn ban đầu.","Mã hóa bảo vệ tính bí mật, không thay thế checksum; S3 Bucket Keys liên quan SSE-KMS và chi phí KMS, không kiểm tra toàn vẹn theo mô tả này.","Tải lại rồi so sánh có thể kiểm tra nhưng thêm lưu lượng và mã, lại không kiểm tra ngay trong request upload. Checksum tích hợp trực tiếp hơn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare company uploads confidential patient billing documents to an Amazon S3 bucket each night. The data engineering team must independently confirm that each uploaded object is identical to the source file and that no corruption occurred during the transfer. The solution must validate object integrity as part of the upload process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:7:43":{"id":"e:7:43","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 43","correct":[2],"answers":["Create separate views in Amazon Athena for each table involved in the queries.","Enable the Query Result Reuse feature in Amazon Athena.","\u003cspan\u003eCreate a view in Amazon Athena for each \u003ccode\u003eSELECT\u003c/code\u003e query.\u003c/span\u003e","Use the Athena workgroup settings for query execution management"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer uses Amazon Athena to run nested \u003ccode\u003eSELECT\u003c/code\u003e queries for joining multiple tables. The data engineer wants to run additional queries on these results but finds the process challenging and time-consuming.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would simplify the querying process for the team?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eIn Amazon Athena, a view is a logical, not a physical table. It’s essentially a saved query that you can reference, just like a table. This is particularly useful when dealing with complex queries. In the given scenario, the data engineer has already prepared complex \u003ccode\u003eSELECT\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;queries that join multiple tables for data aggregation. These queries are likely intricate and time-consuming to write and execute. By creating a view for each of these\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eSELECT\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;queries, the team can save the definitions of these complex queries. This means that they don’t have to write the entire query each time they want to use it; they can simply reference the view. This significantly simplifies the querying process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ddcd68f6cb8517357b13f9161dfe78a990448b33d2ae75702f939529b03a0add.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFurthermore, the team can run further queries against these views. In this context, the views act as virtual tables or composed queries on the underlying data. This is a more efficient approach as it reduces the time and computational resources required to run complex queries repeatedly. It also allows the team to focus on data analysis rather than data management.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a view in Amazon Athena for each \u003ccode\u003eSELECT\u003c/code\u003e query.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable the Query Result Reuse feature in Amazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. This feature would only\u0026nbsp;speed up queries by caching the results of previous queries. It won\u0027t inherently simplify the process of writing or managing complex queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate separate views in Amazon Athena for each table involved in the queries\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;This method would be more helpful if simplifying access to individual tables is the goal. However, the challenge is to simplify running queries against the results of a single complex query. This approach would still require complex joins and logic to be applied every time the data is queried.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the Athena workgroup settings for query execution management\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Workgroup is just a way for you to control query execution and access, set data usage limits, and configure query result locations. It won\u0027t help in simplifying.\u0026nbsp;While it\u0027s an essential feature for managing Athena usage at scale, it doesn\u0027t directly simplify the writing or structuring of complex queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/views.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/views.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/views.html#creating-views\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/views.html#creating-views\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:61":{"id":"e:5:61","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 61","correct":[1,4],"answers":["Use Amazon EventBridge Scheduler to run the Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs) every day.","Create an AWS Step Functions workflow with task states for running the Spark job in an Amazon EMR Serverless application, initiating an AWS Glue crawler, and publishing an Amazon SNS message to inform the operations team.","Create a Lambda function that imports the unprocessed data into an Amazon Redshift cluster. Create a second Lambda function to execute the Spark job using the Amazon Redshift integration for Apache Spark. Orchestrate the Lambda functions in an AWS Step Functions workflow.","Set up a cron job in Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (MWAA) with Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs) to execute the Spark job, initiate an AWS Glue crawler, and send an alert to the operations team through Amazon SNS.","Create an Amazon EventBridge scheduled rule to trigger the AWS Step Functions workflow every day."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics firm analyzes its shipment data daily. The firm plans to execute a data processing job using Apache Spark to calculate delivery performance and subsequently notify its operations team. The unprocessed data is stored on Amazon S3, and the computed statistics, which will be analyzed using SQL, will also be stored on Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich steps should the team take to fulfill these requirements while minimizing operational overhead? (SELECT TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAmazon EventBridge is a serverless event bus service that enables you to easily connect applications together using data from your applications, integrated Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) applications, and AWS services. It allows you to route real-time data streams to different endpoints and trigger workflows in a decoupled fashion. When integrated with AWS Step Functions, it can trigger complex workflows on a schedule, such as daily data processing jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/002abf5587c0362bb3fcef408936d902214b55d3a3782bf6aa00d59ced4ad34f.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn this scenario, a scheduled EventBridge rule initiates a workflow in AWS Step Functions, orchestrating a series of tasks. First, it runs an Apache Spark job on an EMR Serverless application, processing data without requiring server management. Next, AWS Glue crawls the processed data, registering its schema in the AWS Glue Data Catalog for SQL querying with services like Athena. Finally, upon successful completion, Amazon SNS notifies the operations team. This approach utilizes serverless services to reduce operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e–\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge scheduled rule to trigger the AWS Step Functions workflow every day.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e–\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Step Functions workflow with task states for running the Spark job in an Amazon EMR Serverless application, initiating an AWS Glue crawler, and publishing an Amazon SNS message to inform the operations team.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCreate a Lambda function that imports the unprocessed data into an Amazon Redshift cluster. Create a second Lambda function to execute the Spark job using the Amazon Redshift integration for Apache Spark. Orchestrate the Lambda functions in an AWS Step Functions workflow\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect. While Redshift is a powerful data warehousing solution, it’s not designed to execute Spark jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up a cron job in Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (MWAA) with Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs) to execute the Spark job, initiate an AWS Glue crawler, and send an alert to the operations team through Amazon SNS\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because while Amazon MWAA can schedule and orchestrate workflows, it’s more complex and requires more operational overhead than AWS Step Functions. The question asks for a solution that minimizes operational overhead, making this option less suitable.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge Scheduler to run the Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs) every day\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect. While Amazon EventBridge Scheduler is capable of triggering various AWS resources such as Lambda functions, it does not directly manage or execute Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs) associated with Apache Airflow workflows.\u0026nbsp;Apache Airflow uses its scheduler to manage DAG execution within its environment.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/step-functions/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/step-functions/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Step Functions Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-28":{"id":"p:4-28","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 28","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue streaming ETL to store the data into Amazon S3. Use S3 Analytics for analyzing the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (MSK) to read the data in near-real-time. Develop a custom application for the dashboard by using D3.js\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to push data into Amazon S3. Use Amazon QuickSight to build the dashboards from S3 data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to push the data into an Amazon OpenSearch Service. Visualize the data by using OpenSearch (Kibana) dashboards\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["S3 Analytics phân tích kiểu truy cập để hỗ trợ chọn lớp lưu trữ, không phân tích clickstream và tạo dashboard nghiệp vụ. Đây là nhầm lẫn giữa phân tích lưu trữ với phân tích dữ liệu.","Chuyển sang MSK rồi tự viết dashboard D3.js làm tăng đáng kể mã và thời gian triển khai. Đề cần một giải pháp có thể đưa vào dùng nhanh.","Đường Firehose tới S3 và dashboard QuickSight không phải lựa chọn phù hợp cho yêu cầu làm mới dữ liệu liên tục mỗi 10 giây trong đề. Việc gom lô và cập nhật dataset có thể thêm độ trễ.","Firehose có thể chuyển dữ liệu tới OpenSearch, còn OpenSearch Dashboards cung cấp trực quan hóa và tự làm mới. Có thể đặt dashboard làm mới mỗi 10 giây; cần phân biệt chu kỳ làm mới màn hình với độ trễ nạp dữ liệu đầu cuối."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn e-commerce company wants to develop a click analytics dashboard to see near-real-time user click patterns. The clicks are currently ingested from various devices through Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. The dashboard must be refreshed automatically every ten seconds to display the most updated data. The company is looking for an easy-to-implement solution that can be put into production as soon as possible.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution would you recommend for the given requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-12":{"id":"p:3-12","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 12","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon DynamoDB table with provisioned capacity to support the required read and write throughput, and use DynamoDB APIs for all record access\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon Redshift table with the KEY distribution style and use the Amazon Redshift Data API to perform all read and write operations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain, index every activity record as a document, configure dedicated master and data nodes, and use search APIs for all high-concurrency record reads and writes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon Redshift table with the EVEN distribution style and use a JDBC connection to perform all read and write operations\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["DynamoDB phù hợp truy cập item qua API với lưu lượng đọc/ghi lớn. Provisioned capacity phải được tính theo kích thước item, loại đọc và số thao tác, không chỉ đếm request.","Redshift tối ưu phân tích, không phải database vận hành cho rất nhiều yêu cầu đọc/ghi từng bản ghi. Data API không thay đổi bản chất đó.","OpenSearch phù hợp tìm kiếm nhưng thêm cấu hình index và cụm; DynamoDB trực tiếp hơn cho truy cập item bằng API trong đề.","Đổi sang EVEN và JDBC không biến Redshift thành kho key-value có độ trễ thấp cho nhiều thao tác từng item."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA gaming company is designing an event log table for a player activity service. The service continuously ingests activity records and other internal applications must retrieve specific records through dependable API calls. The table must support more than 5,000 concurrent write operations and 7,000 read operations each second.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-20":{"id":"p:2-20","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 20","correct":[0,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003ePartitioning CTAS query results works well when the number of partitions you plan to have is limited and the partitioned columns have low cardinality\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePartitioning CTAS query results works well when the number of partitions you plan to have is limited and the partitioned columns have high cardinality\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eBucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has low cardinality and evenly distributed values\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eBucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has high cardinality and sparsely distributed values\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eBucketing CTAS query results works well when you bucket data by the column that has high cardinality and evenly distributed values\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Partition phù hợp khi số giá trị phân vùng được kiểm soát và không quá lớn. Bộ đề nhấn mạnh cột cardinality thấp để tránh tạo quá nhiều partition nhỏ.","Cardinality cao dễ sinh quá nhiều partition, tăng chi phí quản lý và tạo tệp nhỏ. Không khớp mục tiêu số partition hạn chế.","Cột ít giá trị phân biệt không tận dụng tốt bucketing và dễ phân phối lệch. Bộ đề ưu tiên cardinality cao.","Nhiều giá trị nhưng phân phối thưa/lệch không bảo đảm các bucket cân bằng. Cần phân phối đều để phát huy hiệu quả.","Bucketing dùng hàm băm để phân phối giá trị vào số bucket xác định. Cột cardinality cao, phân phối đều và thường được lọc theo giá trị là ứng viên phù hợp."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company wants to store data from Athena CTAS (\u003ccode\u003eCREATE TABLE AS SELECT\u003c/code\u003e) query results in Amazon S3. A data engineer wants to understand the distinction between partitioning vs bucketing for storing data via such CTAS queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate, which of the following options would you identify as the right fit for choosing bucketing vs partitioning? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-26":{"id":"p:4-26","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 26","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an S3 bucket policy that grants the AWS Glue service principal full access to the S3 bucket and remove the IAM role from the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable Amazon Redshift data sharing and configure the S3 bucket as the target location for the shared dataset\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the Amazon Redshift database credentials in AWS Secrets Manager and reference the secret in the AWS Glue job configuration\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an IAM role with permissions to write to the S3 bucket and associate the IAM role with the Amazon Redshift cluster\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Glue job cần execution role; bỏ role và cấp toàn quyền cho service principal không phải mô hình quyền đúng. Ngoài quyền của Glue, Redshift còn cần quyền truy cập S3 khi thực hiện UNLOAD.","Redshift data sharing chia sẻ dữ liệu cho consumer được hỗ trợ, không xuất một bản sao định kỳ vào S3 bằng cách đặt bucket làm đích datashare.","Secrets Manager giúp bảo vệ thông tin đăng nhập database, nhưng không cấp quyền ghi S3 cho Redshift. Bước còn thiếu trong tình huống này là role mà Redshift dùng khi xuất dữ liệu.","Khi connector Glue đọc Redshift qua UNLOAD, chính Redshift cần ghi dữ liệu tạm ra S3. Tạo role có quyền ghi bucket cần thiết và liên kết với cluster để cấp quyền cho thao tác này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics company stores partner rebate data in an Amazon Redshift cluster. The company needs to store a scheduled copy of the partner rebate data in an Amazon S3 bucket for downstream reconciliation. A data engineer creates an AWS Glue job to extract the data from Amazon Redshift and write the data to the S3 bucket on a recurring schedule. The data engineer has already configured private network connectivity among the AWS Glue job, the Amazon Redshift cluster, and Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is the next step required to meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:55":{"id":"e:3:55","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 55","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eRun AWS Glue jobs on a fixed 15-minute schedule for both policy documents and consultant schedules, store all processed records in Amazon S3, and use Athena SQL queries for application search\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Step Functions to orchestrate AWS Glue jobs from scheduled and event-driven starts, store processed data in Amazon S3, and use OpenSearch Service for full-text search\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Step Functions to orchestrate AWS Glue jobs from scheduled and event-driven starts, store processed data in Amazon S3, and use Amazon RDS for full-text search\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue workflows with scheduled and event-based triggers, store processed data in DynamoDB, and build custom full-text search by using Lambda functions\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare advisory company is building a data pipeline for an application that receives the most traffic during clinic business hours. The application must help users search medical policy documents and find available consultants who can answer questions. The medical policy document repository is refreshed once every night.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe application must provide complex full-text search across the policy documents and must show consultant availability from a scheduling database. Consultant availability must reflect schedule changes within 5 minutes. The company wants to use AWS Glue for ETL processing and wants the solution with the least operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Step Functions to orchestrate AWS Glue jobs from scheduled and event-driven starts, store processed data in Amazon S3, and use OpenSearch Service for full-text search\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Step Functions can orchestrate AWS Glue jobs as part of a managed workflow. This allows the company to run the nightly document processing flow on a schedule and also start the consultant availability flow from event-driven changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 is a suitable landing and processed-data store for the pipeline outputs. The document repository changes only once each day, while consultant availability must refresh within 5 minutes, so the workflow needs both scheduled and event-driven processing patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon OpenSearch Service is the right fit for complex full-text search over legal or policy documents. This solution uses managed AWS services for orchestration, ETL, storage, and search, which reduces the need to build and operate custom scheduling, indexing, and search components.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Step Functions to orchestrate AWS Glue jobs from scheduled and event-driven starts, store processed data in Amazon S3, and use Amazon RDS for full-text search\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon RDS can store structured relational data and can support some text-search capabilities depending on the selected database engine. It is useful for transactional workloads and relational application data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that the requirement calls for complex full-text search across legal or policy documents. Amazon OpenSearch Service is more appropriate for rich document search, indexing, relevance scoring, and search-oriented query behavior.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun AWS Glue jobs on a fixed 15-minute schedule for both policy documents and consultant schedules, store all processed records in Amazon S3, and use Athena SQL queries for application search\u003c/strong\u003e - Athena can query data in Amazon S3 and is useful for interactive analytics over data lake files. Scheduled Glue jobs can also process data at regular intervals.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not meet the application search requirement well. Athena is not designed to power complex full-text application search, and a fixed 15-minute schedule does not meet a 5-minute freshness requirement for consultant availability changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue workflows with scheduled and event-based triggers, store processed data in DynamoDB, and build custom full-text search by using Lambda functions\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue workflows can coordinate Glue jobs and can use scheduled or event-based triggers. DynamoDB can support low-latency key-value access for application data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe issue with this approach is the custom search solution. Building complex full-text search with Lambda functions adds operational overhead and search-engine development work, while OpenSearch Service provides managed full-text search capabilities.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/about-triggers.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/about-triggers.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/searching.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/searching.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-50":{"id":"p:2-50","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 50","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eQuery the AWS Glue Data Catalog tables that the job reads and compare table metadata changes with the failed stage execution\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eReview Amazon CloudWatch metrics for the AWS Glue job and use the Glue namespace to inspect every individual Spark stage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eOpen the AWS Glue Spark UI for the job run and review the job, stage, task, DAG, and execution details\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the AWS Glue job run insights feature to examine every Spark stage metric and task timeline for the failed run\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Data Catalog chứa schema, bảng và partition, không lưu task timeline hoặc thông số runtime từng Spark stage.","CloudWatch có metric Glue hữu ích, nhưng không thay thế toàn bộ màn hình chi tiết job/stage/task của Spark UI.","Spark UI cho thấy DAG, stage, task, thời gian chạy và shuffle. Đây là công cụ trực tiếp để điều tra bước Spark bị lỗi.","Job run insights hỗ trợ phân tích lỗi và gợi ý, nhưng không cung cấp mọi metric/timeline từng task như Spark UI."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer supports several AWS Glue Apache Spark ETL jobs that transform daily advertising and clickstream datasets. The company has enabled monitoring and logging for all AWS Glue jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eOne Spark job starts failing during a transformation stage. The data engineer wants to investigate the failure by reviewing detailed metrics for each individual Spark stage, including task progress, stage timing, and shuffle behavior.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow can the data engineer access the stage-level metrics?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:6:28":{"id":"e:6:28","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 28","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eAppend \u003ccode\u003eHAVING SUM(clicks) \u0026gt; 100\u003c/code\u003e following the \u003ccode\u003eGROUP BY\u003c/code\u003e clause.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eInclude a \u003ccode\u003eWHERE orders \u0026gt; 100\u003c/code\u003e condition before the \u003ccode\u003eGROUP BY\u003c/code\u003e clause.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eInsert \u003ccode\u003eLIMIT 100\u003c/code\u003e at the end of the query to limit the number of results.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eAppend \u003ccode\u003eHAVING SUM(orders) \u0026gt; 100\u003c/code\u003e followed by \u003ccode\u003eORDER BY total_orders DESC;\u003c/code\u003e after the GROUP BY clause.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA marketing analyst is utilizing Amazon Athena to analyze campaign performance data hosted on an S3 bucket. The analyst has created an external table in Athena to aggregate the data, containing metrics like campaign names, impressions, clicks, and orders. The aim is to pinpoint which marketing campaigns yielded the highest order counts and filter out any campaigns that generated less than 100 orders.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo achieve this objective, the analyst drafts the following initial query:\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003ccode\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\nSELECT campaign, SUM(orders) AS total_orders\u003cbr\u003e\nFROM campaign_performance\u003cbr\u003e\nGROUP BY campaign\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe analyst must ensure that only the top-performing campaigns are included in the final report.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich adjustment should the analyst make to the query?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eData distribution in Amazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e pertains to the organization of data stored in Amazon S3 when running interactive queries using standard SQL. Unlike traditional databases, Athena allows for the analysis of vast datasets without the need for database loading or server maintenance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003eHAVING\u003c/code\u003e clause, when used in conjunction with the \u003ccode\u003eGROUP BY\u003c/code\u003e clause allows for the filtering of groups based on specific conditions applied to aggregate functions like \u003ccode\u003eSUM\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eCOUNT\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eAVG\u003c/code\u003e, etc. This clause is applied after groups are formed, enabling the filtering of groups based on aggregated values. It\u0027s important to note that any column referenced in the \u003ccode\u003eHAVING\u003c/code\u003e clause must be part of the \u003ccode\u003eGROUP BY\u003c/code\u003e clause or an aggregate function.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/80ba254e0af5a81084c71e39a703b7eb2a9e92a18be33961a253ec76690b92a2.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe correct strategy for refining the query to identify top-performing campaigns involves using the \u003ccode\u003eHAVING\u003c/code\u003e clause to filter grouped results based on the aggregate function \u003ccode\u003eSUM(orders)\u003c/code\u003e. The addition of \u003ccode\u003eORDER BY total_orders DESC;\u003c/code\u003e ensures the presentation of results in descending order of total orders, facilitating a quick assessment of the highest-performing campaigns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAppend \u003ccode\u003eHAVING SUM(orders) \u0026gt; 100\u003c/code\u003e followed by \u003ccode\u003eORDER BY total_orders DESC;\u003c/code\u003e after the \u003ccode\u003eGROUP BY\u003c/code\u003e clause.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAppend \u003ccode\u003eHAVING SUM(clicks) \u0026gt; 100\u003c/code\u003e following the \u003ccode\u003eGROUP BY\u003c/code\u003e clause\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect as it filters campaigns based on the number of clicks instead of orders, which does not align with the goal of measuring campaign success by orders generated.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eInclude a \u003ccode\u003eWHERE orders \u0026gt; 100\u003c/code\u003e condition before the \u003ccode\u003eGROUP BY\u003c/code\u003e clause\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because the \u003ccode\u003eWHERE\u003c/code\u003e clause filters individual rows before aggregation, which is not suitable when filtering based on the sum of orders per campaign.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eInsert \u003ccode\u003eLIMIT 100\u003c/code\u003e at the end of the query to limit the number of results\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect as this approach just limits the number of campaigns returned rather than filtering campaigns based on the sum of orders generated.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/select.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/select.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/clean-rooms/latest/sql-reference/r_HAVING_clause.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/clean-rooms/latest/sql-reference/r_HAVING_clause.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-8":{"id":"p:2-8","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 8","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena federated queries to read outdated records from the Amazon Redshift sales_orders table, delete those records, and insert the latest rows from the daily_updates table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon Redshift stored procedure that deletes matching stale records from the sales_orders table and inserts the latest records from the daily_updates staging table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to delete outdated records from the sales_orders table and insert the latest records from the daily_updates table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon Redshift materialized view that joins the sales_orders table with the daily_updates table and configure downstream applications to query the materialized view instead of refreshing the base table\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Athena Federated Query dùng để đọc/truy vấn liên nguồn, không phải công cụ cập nhật bảng Redshift bằng DELETE và INSERT.","Stored procedure thực hiện xóa các bản cũ khớp khóa rồi chèn bản mới từ bảng staging ngay trong Redshift. Có thể quản lý logic và tính nhất quán của cập nhật tại database.","Spectrum đọc dữ liệu ngoài Redshift trên S3. Không phải cơ chế sửa dữ liệu giữa hai bảng nội bộ của Redshift.","Materialized view không xóa bản lỗi thời và chèn bản mới vào bảng gốc như yêu cầu. Chuyển ứng dụng sang view là thay đổi khác bài toán."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn online grocery company stores same-day purchase transactions in Amazon Redshift. The company has a sales_orders table that contains the existing order records. The company also has a daily_updates staging table that contains newly created orders and changed order records from the last processing window.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company must remove outdated records from the sales_orders table and load the latest versions from the daily_updates table. Several reporting applications query the sales_orders table throughout the day and must display current order information.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:12":{"id":"e:2:12","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 12","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up runtime roles for EMR steps and allow only the clinical research team to submit steps with a runtime role that can read the confidential DynamoDB table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure IAM roles for EMRFS requests and map the clinical research team to a role that can read the confidential DynamoDB table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate separate EMR Studio workspaces for each analytics team and rely on workspace separation to prevent unauthorized Spark jobs from reading the confidential DynamoDB table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS Lake Formation permissions for the clinical research team and grant the team access to the confidential DynamoDB table through LF-Tags\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA life sciences company runs a shared Amazon EMR cluster on Amazon EC2 instances. Several analytics teams use the cluster to run interactive analysis and batch transformations through EMR Studio workspaces and EMR steps.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe teams need to submit EMR steps that run Apache Spark jobs and read reference data from Amazon DynamoDB tables. One DynamoDB table contains confidential clinical trial data that only the clinical research team can access. A data engineer must make sure that only the clinical research team can access the confidential DynamoDB table from jobs that run on the shared EMR cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up runtime roles for EMR steps and allow only the clinical research team to submit steps with a runtime role that can read the confidential DynamoDB table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eRuntime roles for EMR steps are designed for job-scoped access control on Amazon EMR clusters. A data engineer can define an IAM runtime role that has permissions to read the confidential DynamoDB table and allow only the clinical research team to submit EMR steps with that role.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach is important in a multi-tenant EMR cluster. Without runtime roles, jobs can inherit broad permissions from the cluster instance profile, which can allow teams to access AWS resources that they should not use. Runtime roles narrow the permissions to the job or step that is running.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution meets the requirement because the confidential DynamoDB access is tied to the team-authorized runtime role, not to the entire shared cluster. Other teams can continue to use EMR Studio workspaces and EMR steps, but their steps run with roles that do not include permissions for the confidential table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure AWS Lake Formation permissions for the clinical research team and grant the team access to the confidential DynamoDB table through LF-Tags\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Lake Formation can provide fine-grained permissions for data lake resources that use the AWS Glue Data Catalog, such as catalog databases, tables, columns, and supported S3-backed datasets. It can also integrate with some Amazon EMR access control patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the confidential data is in a DynamoDB table, and the teams need EMR steps to run Spark jobs that fetch data from DynamoDB. Lake Formation permissions are not the direct control for DynamoDB table access in this scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure IAM roles for EMRFS requests and map the clinical research team to a role that can read the confidential DynamoDB table\u003c/strong\u003e - IAM roles for EMRFS requests can help isolate Amazon S3 access by mapping users or groups to IAM roles for EMRFS operations. This is useful when teams need different permissions for S3 data accessed through EMRFS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, EMRFS role mapping is specific to EMRFS access patterns and does not control Spark access to DynamoDB tables. The requirement is to restrict DynamoDB access for EMR steps, so runtime roles are the appropriate job-scoped control.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate separate EMR Studio workspaces for each analytics team and rely on workspace separation to prevent unauthorized Spark jobs from reading the confidential DynamoDB table\u003c/strong\u003e - EMR Studio workspaces can separate user experiences and provide managed notebook environments for teams. This can improve organization and usability for interactive analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, workspace separation alone does not enforce DynamoDB table permissions for Spark jobs that run on the shared cluster. The job needs a scoped IAM role that determines which AWS resources the job can access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-steps-runtime-roles.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-steps-runtime-roles.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-studio-runtime.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-studio-runtime.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-access-iam.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-access-iam.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-iam-roles.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-iam-roles.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-runtime-roles-for-amazon-emr-steps-use-iam-roles-and-aws-lake-formation-for-access-control-with-amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-runtime-roles-for-amazon-emr-steps-use-iam-roles-and-aws-lake-formation-for-access-control-with-amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-56":{"id":"p:3-56","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 56","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into Amazon Redshift on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into Amazon RDS on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into an Amazon EMR-based Spark Cluster on an hourly basis and use SparkSQL to run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena to run SQL based analytics against Amazon S3 data\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Nạp sang Redshift theo giờ chỉ để kiểm tra SQL thêm pipeline, lưu trữ và quản lý không cần thiết.","RDS cần bản sao dữ liệu và tài nguyên database riêng. Athena truy vấn trực tiếp raw zone trên S3 đơn giản hơn.","EMR/SparkSQL làm được nhưng thêm cụm hoặc môi trường xử lý và tác vụ nhập dữ liệu. Quá nhiều thành phần cho yêu cầu này.","Athena chạy SQL trực tiếp trên dữ liệu S3 đã mô tả schema. Không phải quản lý cụm hoặc di chuyển raw data sang database khác."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team at an e-commerce company has set up a workflow to ingest the clickstream data into the raw zone of the Amazon S3 data lake. The team wants to run some SQL-based data sanity checks on the raw zone of the data lake.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat AWS services would you recommend for this use-case such that the solution is cost-effective and easy to maintain?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-39":{"id":"p:3-39","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 39","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDeploy an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database with the aws_s3 extension, use AWS Step Functions to invoke Lambda functions that synchronize department-specific S3 data into tables, and grant database permissions by department\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up AWS Lake Formation, assign LF-Tags to AWS Glue Data Catalog resources, and enable Lake Formation tag-based access control for department-specific permissions\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse S3 Access Points for each department, configure one access point policy per department, and require data engineers to update the access point policies whenever datasets, columns, or department permissions change\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift and Amazon Redshift Spectrum as the only query layer for the data lake, and configure one Redshift IAM role with access to the S3 datasets\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Sao chép data lake vào RDS và tự đồng bộ thêm lưu trữ, mã và vận hành. Không cung cấp quản trị thống nhất tại nguồn.","LF-Tags gắn phân loại lên tài nguyên Catalog; Lake Formation tag-based access control cấp quyền theo đặc điểm dữ liệu. Giảm việc cập nhật policy thủ công cho từng bảng/cột.","Access Point kiểm soát object và đường truy cập, không tự áp quyền từng cột. Cập nhật policy thủ công cũng không tối ưu khi dataset thay đổi.","Một role đọc S3 chung qua Redshift không phân biệt đầy đủ quyền hàng/cột giữa các phòng ban. Lake Formation cung cấp cơ chế phù hợp hơn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company maintains an Amazon S3 based data lake that contains datasets for clinical operations, finance, research, and compliance teams. The data lake receives several million patient interaction records each day. A data engineer must design an access control solution that allows each department to access only the datasets and columns that the department is authorized to use. The solution must enforce least privilege and minimize ongoing permission management effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:8:8":{"id":"e:8:8","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 8","correct":[3],"answers":["Amazon EMR","AWS Glue Data Catalog","AWS Glue Crawlers","AWS Glue Studio Job Notebooks"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is working on a project that involves processing and transforming large amounts of data. The data is stored in various formats and locations. The engineer needs a solution that allows for interactive development and debugging, supports real-time data processing, and doesn’t require provisioning or managing clusters. The solution should also integrate well with other AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following AWS services would best meet the requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Studio Notebook\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;provides an interactive development environment that allows data engineers to write, edit, and test their ETL scripts. The notebooks are based on Jupyter Notebooks, a popular open-source web application that allows you to create and share documents that contain live code, equations, visualizations, and narrative text. This makes it easier for data engineers to develop and debug their ETL scripts interactively, reducing the overall development timeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/72f01c7cb5d57fd4857e236ffcd38474893e528e72fcad4f9116cbbf674f8045.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Studio Notebook also support real-time data processing. This is particularly useful when data is sourced from IoT devices, change data capture (CDC) services, and streaming services such as Amazon Kinesis and Apache Kafka.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Studio Job Notebooks.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon EMR\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it typically requires cluster provisioning and management, which doesn\u0027t align with the engineer\u0027s requirement for a solution that doesn\u0027t require managing clusters.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Data Catalog\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because the AWS Glue Data Catalog is just a managed service that lets you store, annotate, and share metadata in the AWS Cloud, but it does not provide an interactive development environment or real-time data processing capabilities.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Crawlers\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because while AWS Glue Crawlers can automatically discover new data and extract metadata, they do not provide an interactive development environment or real-time data processing capabilities.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/notebook-getting-started.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/notebook-getting-started.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/interactively-develop-your-aws-glue-streaming-etl-jobs-using-aws-glue-studio-notebooks/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/interactively-develop-your-aws-glue-streaming-etl-jobs-using-aws-glue-studio-notebooks/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-data-integration-jobs-with-ai-companion-on-aws-glue-studio-notebook-powered-by-amazon-codewhisperer/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-data-integration-jobs-with-ai-companion-on-aws-glue-studio-notebook-powered-by-amazon-codewhisperer/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:25":{"id":"e:5:25","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 25","correct":[3],"answers":["Use Redshift Event Notifications to monitor the table activities.","Set up Amazon CloudWatch to monitor the Redshift cluster and configure an alarm that triggers an AWS Lambda function to write the statuses of table loads into the DynamoDB table.","Enable AWS CloudTrail management events for Redshift and use these events to trigger an AWS Lambda function that writes the table load statuses to the DynamoDB table.","Configure AWS CloudTrail to capture Redshift API calls and deliver them as CloudTrail logs. Create an Amazon EventBridge rule to match Redshift events from CloudTrail Events and invoke an AWS Lambda function that updates the DynamoDB table with the load statuses."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAn organization uses Amazon Redshift to store daily transaction data. To ensure compliance, the organization needs to track and log the daily load status of Redshift tables to determine if data has been successfully loaded. A data engineer is tasked with updating an Amazon DynamoDB table with these load statuses for auditing purposes.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich of the following solutions should the data engineer implement to meet these requirements?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon CloudTrail\u003c/strong\u003e is a service that helps you enable governance, compliance, operational auditing, and risk auditing of your AWS account. It provides a comprehensive log of actions taken within your AWS environment, including API calls made through the AWS Management Console, AWS SDKs, Command-line tools, and other AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAn \u003cstrong\u003eAmazon EventBridge rule\u003c/strong\u003e is a set of instructions that define how to process and route events in your AWS environment. Rules match incoming events and send them to targets for processing. You can create rules that respond to events recorded by CloudTrail, allowing you to automate responses to specific API calls or changes in your AWS resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAn \u003cstrong\u003eAWS Lambda function\u003c/strong\u003e is a of code that runs in response to events and automatically manages the underlying compute resources. It allows you to run code without provisioning or managing servers. Various AWS services, like EventBridge, can trigger Lambda functions, enabling you to build serverless applications and automate processes in response to events in your AWS environment.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7d87bc57a5f1f08b1458a0269a1e31148881f47dc76068fcb85b7331a912eec4.png\" alt=\"Amazon EventBridge Event pattern\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo monitor specific table activities or load statuses within Amazon Redshift, \u003cstrong\u003eEventBridge\u003c/strong\u003e is effective for capturing API call events from CloudTrail and triggering downstream actions. \u003cstrong\u003eCloudTrail\u003c/strong\u003e records Redshift API calls, which can then be leveraged by \u003cstrong\u003eEventBridge\u003c/strong\u003e rules to match and trigger a Lambda function. This Lambda function can subsequently update DynamoDB with real-time load statuses.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eCloudTrail’s integration with EventBridge provides the ability to capture Redshift-specific events, allowing the data engineer to build an automated pipeline for updating the DynamoDB table with relevant load statuses. The use of EventBridge ensures that only Redshift-related CloudTrail events trigger the Lambda function, making this setup both efficient and targeted.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure AWS CloudTrail to capture Redshift API calls and deliver them as CloudTrail logs. Create an Amazon EventBridge rule to match Redshift events from CloudTrail Events and invoke an AWS Lambda function that updates the DynamoDB table with the load statuses.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable AWS CloudTrail management events for Redshift and use these events to trigger an AWS Lambda function that writes the table load statuses to the DynamoDB table \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because while CloudTrail typically logs Redshift API calls, without EventBridge, it lacks a filtering mechanism that can match specific events to trigger Lambda functions effectively.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse Redshift Event Notifications to monitor the table activities\u003c/span\u003e \u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because Redshift Event Notifications are primarily designed for cluster-level alerts and events, such as failures or performance issues, making them insufficient for the compliance tracking of specific table load statuses.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cstrong\u003e \u003cspan\u003eSet up Amazon CloudWatch to monitor the Redshift cluster and configure an alarm that triggers an AWS Lambda function to write the statuses of table loads into the DynamoDB table\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect because CloudWatch monitors Redshift cluster metrics rather than specific API activities. CloudWatch alarms would not directly track specific table load statuses in the context required.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out the following Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-cloudtrail/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-cloudtrail/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-52":{"id":"p:2-52","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 52","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure CloudWatch alarms for key Kinesis Data Streams and Data Firehose metrics, including IncomingBytes, OutgoingBytes, and DeliveryToS3.Success\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lambda function that runs every day, calls AWS APIs to inspect the Kinesis stream and Firehose delivery stream, and sends SNS notifications\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDeploy Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to analyze the telemetry stream, detect unusual sensor patterns, and invoke alerts when machine readings deviate from expected ranges\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable AWS CloudTrail data events for the S3 bucket and review object-level API activity to determine whether Data Firehose delivered telemetry files successfully\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["CloudWatch có metric tích hợp về dữ liệu vào/ra Kinesis và tỷ lệ Firehose giao S3 thành công. Alarm phát hiện bất thường vận hành mà không tự viết tác vụ polling.","Kiểm tra mỗi ngày phản ứng chậm và cần mã riêng. Metric tích hợp và alarm phù hợp giám sát liên tục hơn.","Flink phát hiện bất thường trong giá trị cảm biến là chất lượng/ngữ nghĩa dữ liệu, khác giám sát sức khỏe tiếp nhận và giao dữ liệu của pipeline.","CloudTrail ghi thao tác API đối tượng S3, nhưng không cung cấp đầy đủ metric throughput và tiêu thụ Kinesis mà đề cần."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA smart factory company runs a streaming data pipeline on AWS to process telemetry from industrial IoT devices. The pipeline uses Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest machine readings and Amazon Data Firehose to deliver the processed records to an Amazon S3 bucket. A data engineer must monitor the health of the pipeline and receive alerts when ingestion throughput, downstream consumption, or delivery to Amazon S3 shows abnormal behavior. The solution must require the least operational effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:1":{"id":"e:4:1","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 1","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EMR Spark Streaming to consume records from Amazon MSK, write curated files to Amazon S3, and refresh external Redshift tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an external schema for the streaming data source and create a regular Amazon Redshift table that directly references the external schema\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an external schema that maps to the Amazon MSK cluster and create a materialized view that consumes records from the MSK topic\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSend Amazon MSK records to Amazon S3 and use a Lambda function to copy each batch into a new Amazon Redshift table\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA gaming analytics company wants to ingest player activity events from an Amazon MSK cluster into an Amazon Redshift data warehouse. The analytics team needs fast access to the incoming events for near-real-time dashboards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company also wants to avoid storing multiple intermediate copies of the streaming data and wants the solution with the least operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an external schema that maps to the Amazon MSK cluster and create a materialized view that consumes records from the MSK topic\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift streaming ingestion is the correct fit for ingesting streaming records from Amazon MSK with low latency. The data engineer creates an external schema that maps Amazon Redshift to the MSK cluster and topic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineer then creates a materialized view that references the external schema. Amazon Redshift can ingest data from the MSK topic into the materialized view, which makes the streaming data available for analytics without building a separate ETL pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational overhead because it uses native Redshift streaming ingestion instead of adding Glue jobs, Lambda functions, EMR clusters, intermediate S3 storage, or scheduled load jobs. It also optimizes storage cost by avoiding unnecessary duplicate landing and staging copies before the data becomes queryable in Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1f7ee55106db778eb85e5586f027c697f027e4af6586d87f6b2f4a7eac1ca423.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1f7ee55106db778eb85e5586f027c697f027e4af6586d87f6b2f4a7eac1ca423.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-data-streaming-ingestion-for-analytics-using-amazon-msk-and-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-data-streaming-ingestion-for-analytics-using-amazon-msk-and-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b0f3c74b47b6641203453e74bbd3e10d9ce23515bc0feb171fc653fffc6d121e.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b0f3c74b47b6641203453e74bbd3e10d9ce23515bc0feb171fc653fffc6d121e.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-data-streaming-ingestion-for-analytics-using-amazon-msk-and-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-data-streaming-ingestion-for-analytics-using-amazon-msk-and-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon EMR Spark Streaming to consume records from Amazon MSK, write curated files to Amazon S3, and refresh external Redshift tables\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR Spark Streaming can process data from Kafka-compatible sources, including Amazon MSK. It can be useful for complex streaming transformations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis creates more operational overhead than required. The company would need to run and manage EMR streaming workloads and external table refresh behavior, while Redshift streaming ingestion can consume MSK data directly into a materialized view.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an external schema for the streaming data source and create a regular Amazon Redshift table that directly references the external schema\u003c/strong\u003e - An external schema is part of the correct Redshift streaming ingestion setup. It maps Amazon Redshift to the streaming source so Redshift can reference the MSK topic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because a regular Redshift table does not directly reference and consume streaming records from the external schema. Redshift streaming ingestion uses a materialized view as the ingestion object.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSend Amazon MSK records to Amazon S3 and use a Lambda function to copy each batch into a new Amazon Redshift table\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon S3 and Lambda can support event-driven processing patterns. A Lambda function can transform or load small batches when objects arrive in a bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe issue with this approach is that it introduces custom loading logic and an intermediate S3 layer. It also requires the company to manage batching, retry handling, load ordering, and error handling instead of using native Redshift streaming ingestion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion-getting-started-MSK.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion-getting-started-MSK.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-data-streaming-ingestion-for-analytics-using-amazon-msk-and-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-data-streaming-ingestion-for-analytics-using-amazon-msk-and-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-27":{"id":"p:2-27","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 27","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge rule to monitor Amazon RDS events, invoke an AWS Lambda function to parse the events, and store the parsed records in Amazon OpenSearch Service\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable native Amazon RDS for MySQL database audit logging, publish the audit logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, configure CloudWatch metric filters and alarms, and enable AWS CloudTrail logging for RDS API activity\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable Amazon CloudWatch Logs for the DB instance, create metric filters for database modifications and instance changes, and configure notifications for suspicious database activity\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon RDS Performance Insights to capture SQL activity, enable enhanced monitoring for operating system metrics, store the monitoring output in CloudWatch Logs, and use CloudWatch dashboards to identify users who changed transaction rows and RDS instance settings\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["RDS Events ghi sự kiện vận hành như bảo trì và failover, không phải lịch sử từng người dùng sửa dữ liệu trong MySQL.","Audit log MySQL theo dõi hành động bên trong database; CloudTrail ghi thao tác quản trị RDS qua API. Đưa log vào CloudWatch để truy vấn, tạo metric và cảnh báo khi cần.","Chỉ bật nơi nhận CloudWatch Logs không tự tạo đầy đủ audit database và CloudTrail API. Cần bật đúng hai nguồn nhật ký.","Performance Insights và Enhanced Monitoring giúp phân tích tải/OS. Chúng không thay thế audit log để xác định đầy đủ ai sửa dòng dữ liệu và ai đổi cấu hình RDS."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company runs an Amazon RDS for MySQL database that stores customer transaction records. The compliance team must collect audit logs that identify database users who modify transaction data. The team must also track users or roles that make administrative changes to the RDS DB instance. The audit records must be available for review and investigation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:3":{"id":"e:3:3","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 3","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable Amazon Macie for the S3 bucket, configure a sensitive data discovery job, and use an AWS Glue job to load the discovered files into Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue job to read the S3 files, detect and permanently obfuscate sensitive values, and load only masked data into Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue job to load the S3 files into Amazon Redshift, identify sensitive columns, create Redshift masking policies, and apply dynamic data masking\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena views to hide sensitive columns in the S3 files, and configure Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the views instead of loading data into Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company receives daily account activity files in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company must automatically load the files into an Amazon Redshift table every night. The files include sensitive fields such as tax identifiers, email addresses, and phone numbers. Users must be able to query the Redshift table, but unauthorized users must not be able to view the sensitive fields as plaintext.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue job to load the S3 files into Amazon Redshift, identify sensitive columns, create Redshift masking policies, and apply dynamic data masking\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift dynamic data masking is the correct feature because the requirement says users must not view sensitive data as plaintext in Amazon Redshift. Dynamic data masking applies masking rules at query time, so different users or roles can see different representations of the same underlying column values.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe AWS Glue job can handle the scheduled ingestion from Amazon S3 into Amazon Redshift. After the data is available in Redshift, the company can create masking policies for sensitive columns and attach those policies to the appropriate users or roles.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational overhead because it uses native Redshift security controls instead of building a custom masking pipeline. The company can protect sensitive values in Redshift without permanently changing the stored data and without coordinating Macie findings, Lambda scanners, or separate masked copies.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/15772ec6290720aad21774e2bb26467d1290dfad86e2f7236cb7625c869e8240.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/15772ec6290720aad21774e2bb26467d1290dfad86e2f7236cb7625c869e8240.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/how-dynamic-data-masking-support-in-amazon-redshift-helps-achieve-data-privacy-and-compliance/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/how-dynamic-data-masking-support-in-amazon-redshift-helps-achieve-data-privacy-and-compliance/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/86de92958e4bfe2d55cf1fc76a09f71b28717528fb9342f22f6f1e23a3a4e0de.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/86de92958e4bfe2d55cf1fc76a09f71b28717528fb9342f22f6f1e23a3a4e0de.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/how-dynamic-data-masking-support-in-amazon-redshift-helps-achieve-data-privacy-and-compliance/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/how-dynamic-data-masking-support-in-amazon-redshift-helps-achieve-data-privacy-and-compliance/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Amazon Macie for the S3 bucket, configure a sensitive data discovery job, and use an AWS Glue job to load the discovered files into Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Macie can discover sensitive data in Amazon S3 and generate findings. This can help the company identify whether files contain sensitive values before or after ingestion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option only discovers sensitive data and loads the files into Amazon Redshift. It does not apply masking policies in Redshift, so unauthorized users could still query plaintext sensitive values.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue job to read the S3 files, detect and permanently obfuscate sensitive values, and load only masked data into Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue Sensitive Data Detection can identify and mask sensitive values during ETL processing. This can be useful when the company wants to permanently obfuscate sensitive values before storing them in a target system.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the original solution pattern requires obfuscation in Amazon Redshift. Permanent masking during ETL removes the ability to apply role-specific Redshift access behavior, such as allowing approved users to see plaintext while masking values for other users.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena views to hide sensitive columns in the S3 files, and configure Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the views instead of loading data into Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - Athena views can hide selected columns from query results, and Redshift Spectrum can query external data in Amazon S3. This can help in some lakehouse access patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the requirement says the company must load data into an Amazon Redshift table. This option avoids the Redshift table ingestion requirement and does not use Redshift dynamic data masking.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_ddm.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_ddm.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_CREATE_MASKING_POLICY.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_CREATE_MASKING_POLICY.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_ddm-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_ddm-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-redshift-home.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-redshift-home.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/how-dynamic-data-masking-support-in-amazon-redshift-helps-achieve-data-privacy-and-compliance/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/how-dynamic-data-masking-support-in-amazon-redshift-helps-achieve-data-privacy-and-compliance/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:39":{"id":"e:2:39","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 39","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Versioning and S3 MFA Delete on the bucket, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and use Lifecycle expiration after 6 years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Versioning on the bucket, use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new documents, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and expire versions after 6 years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Object Lock on the bucket, use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new documents, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and expire versions after 6 years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Object Lock on the bucket, use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new documents, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Deep Archive, and expire versions after 6 years\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA legal services company stores signed case documents in an Amazon S3 bucket. Every object version must remain immutable for 6 years and must follow a write-once-read-many retention model. The company also needs to reduce storage cost for older documents. Any object version that is older than 15 months must be retrievable within 5 hours, and the company can permanently delete object versions after the 6-year retention period ends.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable S3 Object Lock on the bucket, use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new documents, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and expire versions after 6 years\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eS3 Object Lock is required because the documents must use a write-once-read-many model and all object versions must remain immutable for the retention period. Object Lock prevents protected object versions from being overwritten or deleted until the retention period or legal hold no longer applies.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eS3 Object Lock works with S3 Versioning, so each version can be retained independently. The company can configure retention for object versions and use Lifecycle rules to manage storage transitions and expiration after the retention period has ended.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eS3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval is the correct archive storage class for the older document versions because the requirement says objects older than 15 months must be retrievable within 5 hours. S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval supports retrieval options that can meet this access requirement, while still reducing storage cost for older data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable S3 Versioning on the bucket, use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new documents, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and expire versions after 6 years\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Versioning preserves multiple versions of objects in a bucket. This helps recover from accidental overwrites or deletions and is an important foundation for version-level retention.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, versioning by itself does not enforce a WORM model. Users or roles with sufficient permissions could still delete versions unless Object Lock retention or legal holds protect them.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable S3 Object Lock on the bucket, use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new documents, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Deep Archive, and expire versions after 6 years\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Object Lock correctly addresses the WORM and immutability requirement. S3 Intelligent-Tiering can also help optimize storage costs when access patterns change.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option transitions older object versions to S3 Glacier Deep Archive. Deep Archive is designed for long-term archival data with longer restore times, so it does not satisfy the requirement that older documents be accessible within 5 hours.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable S3 Versioning and S3 MFA Delete on the bucket, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and use Lifecycle expiration after 6 years\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 MFA Delete adds protection for version deletion operations by requiring multi-factor authentication for certain delete actions. S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval also meets the archive retrieval timing requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, MFA Delete is not the same as S3 Object Lock. The requirement explicitly calls for all versions to be immutable and use a WORM model, so Object Lock is the required control.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lock.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lock.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lock-managing.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lock-managing.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/restoring-objects-retrieval-options.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/restoring-objects-retrieval-options.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:10":{"id":"e:3:10","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 10","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eS3 table location path should be similar to this: \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/myprefix//input//\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eS3 table location should be similar to the following - \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/table1.csv\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/table2.csv\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate individual S3 prefixes for each table like so - \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/table1/table1.csv\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/table2/table2.csv\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eS3 file names should be similar to the following - \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/athena/inputdata/_file1\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is configuring Amazon Athena to create a table for each file stored under the same prefix in Amazon S3. After running CREATE TABLE statement in Athena with expected columns and their data types, the engineer has issued a SELECT query. However, the query has returned zero records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following is the right way to configure the Amazon S3 location path?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate individual S3 prefixes for each table like so - \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/table1/table1.csv\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/table2/table2.csv\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - Athena query returns zero records if your table location is similar to the following:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/table1.csv\ns3://doc-example-bucket/table2.csv\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo resolve this issue, create individual S3 prefixes for each table similar to the following:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/table1/table1.csv\ns3://doc-example-bucket/table2/table2.csv\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/fe600b361d192dab4fc3ac569b8586376099736f2d9433338f6bc6741564d5c4.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/fe600b361d192dab4fc3ac569b8586376099736f2d9433338f6bc6741564d5c4.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/athena-empty-results/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/athena-empty-results/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eS3 table location should be similar to the following - \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/table1.csv\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/table2.csv\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - Glue crawlers create separate tables for data that is stored in the same S3 prefix. However, when you query those tables in Athena, you get zero records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor example, your Athena query returns zero records if your table location is similar to the following:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/table1.csv\ns3://doc-example-bucket/table2.csv\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo resolve this issue, create individual S3 prefixes for each table similar to the following:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/table1/table1.csv\ns3://doc-example-bucket/table2/table2.csv\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eS3 table location path should be similar to this: \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/myprefix//input//\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - Athena doesn\u0027t support table location paths that include a double slash (//). For example, the following LOCATION path returns empty results:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/myprefix//input//\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo resolve this issue, copy the files to a location that doesn\u0027t have double slashes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eS3 file names should be similar to the following - \u003ccode\u003es3://doc-example-bucket/athena/inputdata/_file1\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - If the files in your S3 path have names that start with an underscore or a dot, then Athena considers these files as placeholders. Athena ignores these files when processing a query. If all the files in your S3 path have names that start with an underscore or a dot, then you get zero records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/athena-empty-results/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/athena-empty-results/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-47":{"id":"p:1-47","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 47","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eExpire old Iceberg snapshots for the streaming event tables to reduce historical metadata and remove previous table versions that analysts no longer need\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConvert the Iceberg table metadata files to JSON compression and increase the query engine split size so each query scans fewer metadata files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun compaction on the streaming Iceberg tables to rewrite many small data files into fewer larger files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eArchive historical Iceberg snapshots to a lower-cost Amazon S3 storage class so the table keeps only recent snapshot metadata in active storage\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Hết hạn snapshot giảm lịch sử và metadata cũ, nhưng không gộp các tệp dữ liệu nhỏ đang được bảng sử dụng.","Đổi nén metadata hoặc split size không viết lại các tệp nhỏ. Chưa xử lý nguyên nhân chính gây chậm.","Compaction gộp nhiều tệp dữ liệu nhỏ thành ít tệp lớn hơn. Nhờ đó giảm số lần mở tệp và chi phí lập kế hoạch, đọc dữ liệu của bảng Iceberg.","Chuyển snapshot lịch sử sang lớp rẻ hơn có thể giảm chi phí, nhưng không cải thiện bố cục các tệp dữ liệu đang hoạt động."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA digital advertising company maintains a self-managed transactional data lake in an Amazon S3 Standard bucket. The data lake uses Apache Iceberg tables and receives campaign events through both streaming ingestion jobs and scheduled batch pipelines.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAnalysts report that queries against the streaming event tables are becoming slower. A data engineer reviews the Iceberg table layout and finds that the streaming tables contain a large number of small data files. The data engineer must improve query performance for these real-time Iceberg tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:51":{"id":"e:1:51","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 51","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eQuery the customer data file in .xls format stored in Amazon S3 using SQL commands via Amazon Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop an Apache Spark job in an AWS Glue notebook to read the file in Amazon S3 and determine the number of distinct customers\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Glue DataBrew to create a recipe that uses the COUNT_DISTINCT aggregate function to determine the number of distinct customers\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Glue DataBrew to create a recipe that uses the FLAG_DUPLICATE_ROWS function to determine the number of distinct customers\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA nightly cron job generates a customer data file of 1 GB size in .xls format and stores it in an Amazon S3 bucket. A data engineer is tasked with concatenating the column in the file that contains customer first names with the column that contains customer last names and then calculating the number of distinct customers in the file.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following will you suggest to address this requirement with the least operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage AWS Glue DataBrew to create a recipe that uses the COUNT_DISTINCT aggregate function to determine the number of distinct customers\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that enables users to clean and normalize data without writing any code. Using DataBrew helps reduce the time it takes to prepare data for analytics and machine learning (ML) by up to 80 percent, compared to custom-developed data preparation. You can choose from over 250 ready-made transformations to automate data preparation tasks, such as filtering anomalies, converting data to standard formats, and correcting invalid values.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo prepare the data, you can choose from more than 250 point-and-click transformations. These include removing nulls, replacing missing values, fixing schema inconsistencies, creating columns based on functions, and many more. You can also use transformations to apply natural language processing (NLP) techniques to split sentences into phrases. Immediate previews show a portion of your data before and after transformation, so you can modify your recipe before applying it to the entire dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the given use case, you can use the COUNT_DISTINCT aggregate function to determine the number of distinct customers\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCOUNT_DISTINCT - Returns the total number of distinct values from the selected source columns in a new column. Empty and null values are ignored.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d30f92299afbecd95cc1d69ffcaac40f79014b6dfa5538911de3e9c2e677df7b.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d30f92299afbecd95cc1d69ffcaac40f79014b6dfa5538911de3e9c2e677df7b.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.functions.COUNT_DISTINCT.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.functions.COUNT_DISTINCT.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eQuery the customer data file in .xls format stored in Amazon S3 using SQL commands via Amazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e - Athena supports creating tables and querying data from CSV, TSV, custom-delimited, and JSON formats; data from Hadoop-related formats: ORC, Apache Avro and Parquet; logs from Logstash, AWS CloudTrail logs, and Apache WebServer logs. You cannot query files stored in Amazon S3 in the .xls format via Amazon Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDevelop an Apache Spark job in an AWS Glue notebook to read the file in Amazon S3 and determine the number of distinct customers\u003c/strong\u003e - It is certainly possible to develop an Apache Spark job in an AWS Glue notebook to read the file in Amazon S3 and determine the number of distinct customers by using countDistinct Function. However, this option involves significant operational overhead to develop the script, so it is ruled out.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage AWS Glue DataBrew to create a recipe that uses the FLAG_DUPLICATE_ROWS function to determine the number of distinct customers\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFLAG_DUPLICATE_ROWS - Returns a new column with a specified value in each row that indicates whether that row is an exact match of an earlier row in the dataset. When matches are found, they are flagged as duplicates. The initial occurrence is not flagged, because it doesn\u0027t match an earlier row. So, this option is not relevant for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.functions.COUNT_DISTINCT.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.functions.COUNT_DISTINCT.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.FLAG_DUPLICATE_ROWS.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/recipe-actions.FLAG_DUPLICATE_ROWS.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/supported-serdes.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/supported-serdes.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-34":{"id":"p:1-34","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 34","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAdd a new attribute in the table to track the expiration time and set up a Glue job to delete items that are more than a week old\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd a created_at attribute in the table and then use a cron job on EC2 instance to invoke a Python script daily. The script deletes items older than a week on the basis of this attribute\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd a created_at attribute in the table and then use a CloudWatch Events rule to invoke a Lambda function daily. The Lambda function deletes items older than a week on the basis of this attribute\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd a new attribute in the table to track the expiration time and enable time to live (TTL) on the table\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Glue có thể xóa item cũ nhưng phải tự xây và duy trì tác vụ chạy mỗi ngày. DynamoDB đã có TTL cho nhu cầu hết hạn item.","EC2 và cron thêm máy chủ, mã Python và công vận hành chỉ để dọn dữ liệu. Không tiết kiệm bằng tính năng TTL có sẵn.","Lambda theo lịch giảm quản lý máy chủ nhưng vẫn cần tự viết mã tìm và xóa item. TTL thực hiện việc hết hạn với ít công hơn.","Thêm thuộc tính thời điểm hết hạn và bật TTL để DynamoDB tự dọn item cũ. Cần ghi thời điểm hết hạn cho cả dữ liệu hiện có; TTL xóa bất đồng bộ."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA gaming company maintains a staging environment for its flagship application which uses a DynamoDB table to keep track of the gaming history of the players. This data needs to be kept for only a week and then it can be deleted. The IT manager has noticed that the table has several months of data in the table. The company wants to implement a cost-effective solution to keep only the latest week\u0027s data in the table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions requires the MINIMUM development effort and ongoing maintenance?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:60":{"id":"e:1:60","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 60","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eChange the data format from comma-separated text files to Apache ORC\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the data format from comma-separated text files to JSON format. Apply Snappy compression\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the data format from comma-separated text files to Apache Parquet. Compress the files using Snappy compression\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the data format from comma-separated text files to ZIP format\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer has been tasked to optimize Amazon Athena queries that are underperforming. Upon analysis, the data engineer realized that the files queried by Athena were not compressed and just stored as .csv files. The data engineer also noticed that users perform most queries by selecting a specific column.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat do you recommend to improve the query performance?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange the data format from comma-separated text files to Apache Parquet. Compress the files using Snappy compression\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena query performance improves if you convert your data into open-source columnar formats, such as Apache parquet or ORC.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhy choose Columnar storage formats:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f61b8e579ca17f2ea74eea8e642c76b8033eb927fcd977b37ea0964045bb10fd.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f61b8e579ca17f2ea74eea8e642c76b8033eb927fcd977b37ea0964045bb10fd.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo convert your existing raw data from other storage formats to Parquet or ORC, you can run CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS) queries in Athena and specify a data storage format as Parquet or ORC, or use the AWS Glue Crawler.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCompressing your data can speed up your queries significantly. The smaller data sizes reduce the data scanned from Amazon S3, resulting in lower costs of running queries. It also reduces the network traffic from Amazon S3 to Athena. Athena supports a variety of compression formats, including common formats like gzip, Snappy, and zstd.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSNAPPY – Compression algorithm that is part of the Lempel-Ziv 77 (LZ7) family. Snappy focuses on high compression and decompression speed rather than the maximum compression of data. This will improve the performance of the queries for the current use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange the data format from comma-separated text files to JSON format. Apply Snappy compression\u003c/strong\u003e - JSON is not a columnar format and is not as well optimized as Apache Parquet for Athena queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange the data format from comma-separated text files to ORC\u003c/strong\u003e - ORC is also a columnar format and provides an efficient way to store Hive data. ORC files are often smaller than Parquet files, and ORC indexes can make querying faster. We still need compression to speed up the Athena queries, so this option is not the best fit.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange the data format from comma-separated text files to ZIP format\u003c/strong\u003e - The ZIP file format is not supported by Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/compression-formats.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/compression-formats.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-54":{"id":"p:4-54","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 54","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eIncrease the number of DPUs that each AWS Glue ETL job uses so the jobs have more compute, memory, and disk resources during peak periods\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable Auto Scaling for the AWS Glue ETL jobs so AWS Glue can add and remove workers dynamically as stream processing demand changes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Step Functions to orchestrate a parallel workflow state that starts the SNS notification workflow and the log retrieval workflow at the same time\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Lambda function that reads CloudWatch metrics every minute, increases the AWS Glue worker count during high disk usage, and decreases the worker count when disk usage drops\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Tăng cố định DPU có thể giảm thiếu tài nguyên nhưng phải tự ước lượng và chỉnh theo giờ cao điểm. Tài nguyên dư lúc thấp tải làm cách này kém linh hoạt hơn Auto Scaling.","Glue Auto Scaling điều chỉnh số worker theo nhu cầu thực thi trong giới hạn cấu hình. Nó giảm công chỉnh năng lực thủ công khi lưu lượng thay đổi; cần giám sát các nút thắt và chọn giới hạn phù hợp.","Chạy hai nhánh song song không giải quyết thiếu tài nguyên trong job phân tích stream. Luồng lấy log còn phụ thuộc kết quả phát hiện bất thường nên không thể tùy tiện khởi động độc lập.","Tự viết Lambda theo dõi metric và điều chỉnh worker tạo cơ chế mở rộng phải duy trì. Glue Auto Scaling đã cung cấp chức năng quản lý phù hợp hơn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company uses Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to collect transaction events from several payment systems. An AWS Glue ETL pipeline analyzes the stream to detect suspicious outliers. When the pipeline detects an outlier, it publishes a notification to an Amazon SNS topic. The SNS topic starts a second workflow that retrieves related audit logs and stores the logs in an Amazon S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDuring peak transaction periods, the company notices delays before notifications reach the SNS topic. A data engineer reviews Amazon CloudWatch metrics and observes a high value for the glue.driver.BlockManager.disk.diskSpaceUsed_MB metric during the same periods. The company must resolve the issue with the least operational effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-39":{"id":"p:4-39","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 39","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the JSON file in Amazon S3, catalog the dataset in the AWS Glue Data Catalog, and use Lake Formation permissions to control access to sensitive and non-sensitive fields\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue to split the JSON file into separate PII and non-PII files, store each output in a different S3 bucket, and grant bucket-level access\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Macie to discover PII fields in the JSON file and configure Macie to grant query access based on each user’s authorization level\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the JSON file in Amazon S3, create separate Athena workgroups for each team, and leverage the workgroup settings to hide sensitive JSON fields\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Đăng ký metadata trong Glue Data Catalog và dùng Lake Formation để phân quyền chi tiết cho công cụ truy vấn tích hợp. Nhờ đó có thể giới hạn các trường nhạy cảm cho nhóm được phép mà không phải tách nhiều bản dữ liệu.","Tách vật lý PII và dữ liệu thường có thể đáp ứng phân quyền bucket, nhưng cần thêm ETL và quản lý khi schema thay đổi. Lake Formation giảm công duy trì các bản dữ liệu riêng.","Macie giúp phát hiện và phân loại dữ liệu nhạy cảm. Nó không phải bộ máy cấp quyền truy vấn theo cột cho từng người dùng.","Athena workgroup quản lý môi trường truy vấn, kết quả và mức sử dụng. Workgroup không tự che các trường JSON; quyền dữ liệu chi tiết cần cơ chế như Lake Formation."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company stores a JSON dataset in an Amazon S3 bucket. The file contains both patient-identifying attributes and non-sensitive operational attributes. Analysts across the company need to query the non-sensitive fields, but only a small compliance team can access the patient-identifying fields.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company wants to make the data available for querying and analysis while applying fine-grained access controls with the least operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:22":{"id":"e:2:22","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 22","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAdd the target account to a customer-managed key. Copy the snapshot using the default AWS KMS key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd the target account to the default AWS KMS key. Copy the snapshot using the default AWS KMS key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd the target account to the default AWS KMS key. Copy the snapshot using the customer-managed key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAdd the target account to a customer-managed key. Copy the snapshot using the customer-managed key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multi-national retail company uses separate AWS accounts for their business units. The finance team has an encrypted snapshot of an Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) instance that uses the default AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) key. The finance team wants to share the encrypted snapshot with their Audit team that uses another AWS account.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions would you recommend to address the given use-case?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdd the target account to a customer-managed key. Copy the snapshot using the customer-managed key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account\u003c/strong\u003e - You can\u0027t share a snapshot that\u0027s encrypted using the default AWS KMS encryption key.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eTo share an encrypted Amazon RDS DB snapshot:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAdd the target account to a custom (non-default) KMS key.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCopy the snapshot using the customer-managed key, and then share the snapshot with the target account.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCopy the shared DB snapshot from the target account.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDetailed steps to implement this solution:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5be6f102a9b0024228d348f9eed94d16b7650bed0816e7deae14f73d5dbda93f.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5be6f102a9b0024228d348f9eed94d16b7650bed0816e7deae14f73d5dbda93f.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/share-encrypted-rds-snapshot-kms-key/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/share-encrypted-rds-snapshot-kms-key/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\"Add the target account to the default AWS KMS key. Copy the snapshot using the default AWS KMS key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account\"\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\"Add the target account to a customer-managed key. Copy the snapshot using the default AWS KMS key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account\"\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\"Add the target account to the default AWS KMS key. Copy the snapshot using the customer-managed key, and then share the snapshot with the target account. Copy the shared DB snapshot from the target account\"\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThese three options contradict the explanation provided above, so these are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/share-encrypted-rds-snapshot-kms-key/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/share-encrypted-rds-snapshot-kms-key/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:9":{"id":"e:2:9","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 9","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a Standard workflow in Step Functions and implement a Wait for Callback pattern to wait for each validation stage to finish\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Lambda functions to implement the workflow and use EventBridge rules to invoke each validation stage after the preceding stage finishes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Express workflow in Step Functions and implement a Wait for Callback pattern so each external validation step can return its result\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Express workflow in Step Functions and split each transaction into multiple executions so no single execution exceeds the workflow duration limit\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company needs to implement a transaction review workflow. Each payment request must pass through several dependent validation stages, including fraud review, balance verification, compliance screening, and final approval. Some validations call external systems and can take several hours to return a result.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company must approve or reject every payment request within 18 hours. The complete workflow must run for less than 18 hours and must wait for each validation result before moving to the next stage. The company wants the solution with the least operational cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Standard workflow in Step Functions and implement a Wait for Callback pattern to wait for each validation stage to finish\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Step Functions Standard workflows are designed for durable, auditable workflows that can run for long periods. A Standard workflow can run for up to one year, so an 18-hour transaction validation process fits within the supported duration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe Wait for Callback pattern is appropriate when a workflow must pause until an external validation system returns a result. Step Functions can pass a task token to the validation process and wait until the external system calls back with success or failure before the workflow continues to the next dependent stage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational cost and overhead for the stated requirement because Step Functions manages state, sequencing, retries, timeouts, and workflow history. The company does not need to build custom state tracking, callback handling, or orchestration logic across multiple services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2c458c42e396f94a92b2b448fc614b3d8f91a24b84897315c36b2a661cbe8e8b.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2c458c42e396f94a92b2b448fc614b3d8f91a24b84897315c36b2a661cbe8e8b.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/choosing-workflow-type.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/choosing-workflow-type.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Express workflow in Step Functions and implement a Wait for Callback pattern so each external validation step can return its result\u003c/strong\u003e - Express workflows are optimized for high-volume, short-duration workflows. They can be cost-effective for brief event-processing workloads that do not require long execution time or callback integration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option does not meet the requirement because Express workflows can run for only up to 5 minutes and do not support the \u003ccode\u003e.waitForTaskToken\u003c/code\u003e callback integration pattern. The transaction workflow can last many hours and must wait for external validation callbacks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Lambda functions to implement the workflow and use EventBridge rules to invoke each validation stage after the preceding stage finishes\u003c/strong\u003e - Lambda and EventBridge can coordinate simple event-driven processing. A validation function could publish an event that starts the next validation stage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe issue with this approach is that the company would need to build custom workflow state management, timeout handling, retries, ordering guarantees, and final approval or rejection logic. Step Functions Standard provides these orchestration capabilities as a managed service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Express workflow in Step Functions and split each transaction into multiple executions so no single execution exceeds the workflow duration limit\u003c/strong\u003e - Splitting work across multiple Express workflow executions can sometimes help with short-lived high-volume tasks. A parent or external coordinator can start new executions for different workflow stages.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because it creates custom coordination to work around Express workflow limits. It also does not provide native callback waiting for long-running external validations, so it is less suitable than one Standard workflow using callback tasks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/choosing-workflow-type.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/choosing-workflow-type.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-to-resource.html#connect-wait-token\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-to-resource.html#connect-wait-token\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/callback-task-sample-sqs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/callback-task-sample-sqs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-error-handling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/concepts-error-handling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/building-cost-effective-aws-step-functions-workflows/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/building-cost-effective-aws-step-functions-workflows/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-63":{"id":"p:3-63","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 63","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to create Apache Airflow DAGs that orchestrate the AWS Glue jobs, Amazon EMR processing steps, Amazon Redshift loads, Amazon S3 outputs, dependencies, retries, and monitoring\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Step Functions Express Workflows to invoke the AWS Glue jobs and Amazon EMR processing steps, and configure each workflow execution to coordinate all transformation and loading tasks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate AWS Lambda functions for every ETL step, configure Amazon EventBridge to invoke AWS Glue jobs, and use the Lambda functions to move data through the pipeline\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDeploy Apache Airflow on Amazon EC2 instances, install the AWS providers package manually, create DAGs for the AWS Glue jobs and Amazon EMR steps, and configure custom scripts for retries, monitoring, and worker scaling\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["MWAA cung cấp Airflow được quản lý; DAG biểu diễn phụ thuộc, retry và các bước Glue, EMR, Redshift, S3. Phù hợp workflow ETL phức tạp theo mô hình Airflow.","Express workflow có thời gian chạy ngắn, không phù hợp điều phối cả chuỗi tác vụ big data dài. Không nên nhầm Express với Standard.","Tự nối từng bước bằng Lambda/EventBridge phân tán logic trạng thái và phụ thuộc. Khó vận hành hơn DAG tập trung cho pipeline lớn.","Airflow tự cài trên EC2 phải quản lý worker, nâng cấp, mở rộng và giám sát. MWAA giảm phần hạ tầng vận hành này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA global retail company processes checkout transactions, catalog changes, warehouse events, and clickstream logs across several AWS services. The company needs a scalable, fully managed orchestration solution to coordinate complex ETL workflows. The workflows must use AWS Glue and Amazon EMR for data processing, store curated outputs in Amazon Redshift and Amazon S3, and support task dependencies, automated retries, operational monitoring, and event-driven execution patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:10:4":{"id":"e:10:4","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 4","correct":[0,1],"answers":["Increase the shard count of the Kinesis data stream.","Implement exponential backoff and retry logic in the application.","Activate the enhanced monitoring feature for the stream.","Enable the enhanced fan-out functionality on the stream.","Use a larger EC2 instance type for the Kinesis Data Streams application."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses an Amazon Kinesis data stream to manage its real-time data processing workloads. Recently, their consumer application on an EC2 instance started encountering \u003ccode\u003eProvisionedThroughputExceededException\u003c/code\u003e errors.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe company must resolve this issue to ensure that the read data calls to the stream consistently succeed and that their real-time data processing is not disrupted.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich actions could be taken to resolve the error? (Select TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams\u003c/strong\u003e is a real-time data streaming service offered by Amazon Web Services (AWS). It enables the collection, processing, and analysis of large streams of data records in real-time. A Kinesis data stream is a collection of data records that are continually and sequentially added to over time, much like a stream. Each data record in the stream is composed of a sequence number, a partition key, and a data blob, which is an immutable sequence of bytes. It is capable of handling real-time data from hundreds of thousands of sources at a massive scale. It’s commonly used for scenarios such as collecting and processing log and event data, real-time analytics, and real-time metrics and reporting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c59e1d966f1ed91fe21ba9dcf469b48032c30e88b1c7421ac56a648947158db5.png\" alt=\"\"\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams uses shards, which are essentially independent streams of data within a Kinesis data stream. Each shard has a fixed rate of data it can handle — 1 MB/sec data input and 2 MB/sec data output. If the data rate exceeds the capacity of the current shards, you will receive a\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eProvisionedThroughputExceededException\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;error. Therefore, increasing the number of shards in your data stream can increase the throughput of your data stream and resolve this error. This is a process known as resharding, which involves splitting or merging shards to adjust the total capacity of the stream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen a\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eProvisionedThroughputExceededException\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;error occurs, it means that the application is reading data too quickly from the stream. Implementing a backoff strategy, where the application waits for a short period before trying the request again, can help manage the rate of data retrieval and avoid exceeding the read-throughput limit. This is a common strategy for handling rate-limited APIs and services, and it’s particularly important when dealing with real-time data processing workloads where maintaining a steady flow of data is crucial.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e-\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the shard count of the Kinesis data stream.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e-\u0026nbsp;Implement exponential backoff and retry logic in the application.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eActivate the enhanced monitoring feature for the stream\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because while enhanced monitoring provides additional metrics for the stream, it does not directly resolve the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eProvisionedThroughputExceededException\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;error. This error is related to the stream’s capacity, not its monitoring.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable the enhanced fan-out functionality on the stream\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;While enhanced fan-out can improve the consumer-side experience by providing dedicated throughput per consumer, it does not directly address the overall capacity quotas of the data stream. It\u0027s more about optimizing the consumption pattern rather than increasing the stream\u0027s capacity to accept writes or aggregate reads without exceeding provisioned throughput.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse a larger EC2 instance type for the Kinesis Data Streams application\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because the size of the EC2 instance does not directly affect the capacity of the Kinesis data stream. The\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eProvisionedThroughputExceededException\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;error is related to the stream’s capacity, not the size of the EC2 instance running the application.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/kinesis-using-sdk-java-resharding.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/kinesis-using-sdk-java-resharding.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/troubleshooting-consumers.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/troubleshooting-consumers.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Kinesis Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:42":{"id":"e:3:42","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 42","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eMove objects to S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA) after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after three years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMove objects to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Flexible Archive after three years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMove objects to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after three years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMove objects to S3 Intelligent-Tiering after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after three years\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses the S3 Standard storage class for all its data storage needs on Amazon S3. A data engineer analyzed the data access patterns and discovered that for the first six months, data files are accessed multiple times daily. From six months to three years, the data files are accessed about once or twice a month. After three years, each file is accessed only once or twice annually. The data engineer is tasked with creating S3 Lifecycle policies to develop new data storage rules, ensuring high availability while seeking cost-effectiveness.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution will best meet these needs?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMove objects to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after three years\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe S3 Standard-IA storage class is designed for long-lived and infrequently accessed data. (IA stands for infrequent access.) S3 Standard-IA and S3 One Zone-IA objects are available for millisecond access (similar to the S3 Standard storage class). Amazon S3 charges a retrieval fee for these objects, so they are most suitable for infrequently accessed data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUse S3 Glacier Deep Archive for archiving data that rarely need to be accessed. Data stored in the S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class has a minimum storage duration period of 180 days and a default retrieval time of 12 hours. It is the lowest-cost storage option in AWS. Storage costs for the S3 Glacier Deep Archive are less expensive than using the S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval storage class.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSince the use case demands high availability while maintaining cost-effectiveness, so both S3 Standard-IA and S3 Glacier Deep Archive offer the best fit.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/a3754edfc0a91e2986ca896b05377f7a3be8306edc78b94c3b2d5e59d9a7e249.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/a3754edfc0a91e2986ca896b05377f7a3be8306edc78b94c3b2d5e59d9a7e249.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMove data objects to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after 30 days. Transfer the data objects to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 1 year\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval is used for archives where portions of the data might need to be retrieved in minutes. Data stored in the S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval storage class has a minimum storage duration period of 90 days and can be accessed in as little as 1-5 minutes by using expedited retrieval. Storage costs for the S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval are more than the S3 Glacier Deep Archive. Since the use case poses no constraints on the first-byte latency, using S3 Glacier Deep Archive is more cost-effective while still maintaining high availability. So, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMove objects to S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA) after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after three years\u003c/strong\u003e - Using S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA) is ruled out as it does not guarantee high availability. So, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMove objects to S3 Intelligent-Tiering after six months. Transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after three years\u003c/strong\u003e - For a small monthly object monitoring and automation charge, S3 Intelligent-Tiering monitors access patterns and automatically moves objects that have not been accessed to lower-cost access tiers. S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically stores objects in three access tiers: one tier that is optimized for frequent access, a 40% lower-cost tier that is optimized for infrequent access, and a 68% lower-cost tier optimized for rarely accessed data. Since access patterns are very clear, Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering is not needed for this use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-40":{"id":"p:1-40","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 40","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the database access credentials in an SSE-S3 encrypted text file on S3. Configure the application web servers to retrieve the credentials from S3 on system boot. Write custom code to change the database access credentials stored on the encrypted file after 90 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the database access credentials as the EC2 instance user data. Configure the application web servers to retrieve the credentials from the user data while bootstrapping. Write custom code to change the database access credentials stored in the user data after 90 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Secrets Manager to store the database access credentials with the rotation interval configured to 90 days. Set up the application web servers to retrieve the credentials from the Secrets Manager\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the database access credentials in a KMS encrypted text file on EFS. Configure the application web servers to retrieve the credentials from EFS on system boot. Write custom code to change the database access credentials stored on the encrypted file after 90 days\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Mã hóa tệp trên S3 không tự quản lý vòng đời thông tin đăng nhập. Phương án vẫn phải viết mã đổi mật khẩu 90 ngày một lần.","User data không phải kho bí mật chuyên dụng và việc xoay mật khẩu vẫn cần tự lập trình. Không phù hợp yêu cầu bảo mật, tự động hóa có sẵn.","Secrets Manager lưu thông tin đăng nhập và hỗ trợ cấu hình rotation. Ứng dụng lấy secret qua API và IAM thay vì nhúng mật khẩu vào máy chủ.","EFS với tệp được mã hóa chỉ giải quyết lưu trữ. Vẫn cần tự đồng bộ và xoay mật khẩu, nhiều công hơn Secrets Manager."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company is migrating its infrastructure from the on-premises data center to AWS Cloud. The company wants to deploy its two-tier application with the EC2 instance-based web servers in a public subnet and PostgreSQL RDS-based database layer in a private subnet. The company wants to ensure that the database access credentials used by the web servers are handled securely as well as these credentials are changed every 90 days in an automated way using a built-in integration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions would you recommend for the given use case?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-24":{"id":"p:3-24","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 24","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eInefficient SQL queries are being run by the dashboards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe Redshift cluster is undersized for the queries being executed by the dashboards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe nightly data refreshes left the dashboard tables in need of a vacuum operation that could not be automatically performed by Amazon Redshift due to ongoing user workloads\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eThe nightly data refreshes are causing the queries to hang during the business hours\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Đề gắn việc chậm với thay đổi sang cập nhật dữ liệu lớn mỗi đêm, không cung cấp dấu hiệu SQL dashboard vừa thay đổi. Cần kiểm tra bảo trì bảng trước.","Chưa có bằng chứng cụm thiếu năng lực; nguyên nhân bộ đề đưa ra là bảng cần VACUUM sau cập nhật lớn.","Các thay đổi lớn có thể tạo dòng đã xóa và dữ liệu chưa được sắp xếp tối ưu. Nếu automatic vacuum chưa thực hiện được do tải, cần xem xét VACUUM phù hợp để khôi phục hiệu năng.","Một tác vụ chỉ chạy ban đêm không tự gây khóa đang hoạt động suốt giờ làm việc sau khi đã kết thúc. Vấn đề được đề nhắm tới là trạng thái bảng sau cập nhật."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company recently migrated to a data lake design based on Amazon S3. Amazon Redshift and Amazon QuickSight are being used by the company\u0027s data engineering team to analyze data for better insights. To ensure access to the most up-to-date actionable data, the team has now shifted to a nightly Amazon Redshift refresh utilizing terabytes of the previous day\u0027s changes. The team has noticed that post the switchover to nightly refresh, several popular dashboards that had good performance earlier, are now seeing degraded performance during business hours as well. Amazon CloudWatch shows no notifications regarding the performance metrics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents the MOST LIKELY cause for this issue?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:23":{"id":"e:1:23","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 23","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon CloudFront with Amazon S3 as the storage solution for the static assets\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS Lambda with an Amazon RDS database to provide a serverless architecture\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Lambda with Amazon ElastiCache and Amazon RDS for serving static assets at high speed and low latency\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon CloudFront with Amazon DynamoDB for greater speed and low latency access to static assets\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn online gaming application has a large chunk of its traffic coming from users who download static assets such as historic leaderboard reports and the game tactics for various games. The current infrastructure and design are unable to handle the traffic and application freezes on most of the pages.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following is a cost-optimal solution that requires the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon CloudFront with Amazon S3 as the storage solution for the static assets\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you put your content in an Amazon S3 bucket in the cloud, a lot of things become much easier. First, you don’t need to plan for and allocate a specific amount of storage space because Amazon S3 buckets scale automatically. As Amazon S3 is a serverless service, you don’t need to manage or patch servers that store files yourself; you just put and get your content. Finally, even if you require a server for your application (for example, because you have a dynamic application), the server can be smaller because it doesn’t have to handle requests for static content.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon CloudFront is a content delivery network (CDN) service that delivers static and dynamic web content, video streams, and APIs around the world, securely and at scale. By design, delivering data out of Amazon CloudFront can be more cost-effective than delivering it from Amazon S3 directly to your users. Amazon CloudFront serves content through a worldwide network of data centers called Edge Locations. Using edge servers to cache and serve content improves performance by providing content closer to where viewers are located.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen a user requests content that you serve with Amazon CloudFront, their request is routed to a nearby Edge Location. If Amazon CloudFront has a cached copy of the requested file, CloudFront delivers it to the user, providing a fast (low-latency) response. If the file they’ve requested isn’t yet cached, CloudFront retrieves it from your origin – for example, the Amazon S3 bucket where you’ve stored your content. Then, for the next local request for the same content, it’s already cached nearby and can be served immediately.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBy caching your content in Edge Locations, Amazon CloudFront reduces the load on your Amazon S3 bucket and helps ensure a faster response for your users when they request content. Also, data transfer out for content by using Amazon CloudFront is often more cost-effective than serving files directly from Amazon S3, and there is no data transfer fee from Amazon S3 to Amazon CloudFront. You only pay for what is delivered to the internet from Amazon CloudFront, plus request fees.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure AWS Lambda with an Amazon RDS database to provide a serverless architecture\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon RDS is not the right choice for the given scenario because of the overhead of a database management system, as the given use-case can be addressed by using the Amazon S3 storage solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon CloudFront with Amazon DynamoDB for greater speed and low latency access to static assets\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon DynamoDB is a key-value and document database that delivers single-digit millisecond performance at any scale. However, Amazon DynamoDB is overkill for the given use-case and will prove to be a very costly solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Lambda with Amazon ElastiCache and Amazon RDS for serving static assets at high speed and low latency\u003c/strong\u003e - As discussed above, Amazon RDS is not needed for this use case where the web application needs to display static pages and facilitate downloads of historic data. Amazon S3 is much better suited for this requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/networking-and-content-delivery/amazon-s3-amazon-cloudfront-a-match-made-in-the-cloud/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/networking-and-content-delivery/amazon-s3-amazon-cloudfront-a-match-made-in-the-cloud/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-26":{"id":"p:3-26","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 26","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up a new IAM role in each application AWS account with permissions to view CloudWatch Logs. Create a Lambda function to assume this new role and perform an hourly export of each AWS account\u0027s CloudWatch Logs data to an S3 bucket in the centralized logging AWS account\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up CloudWatch Logs agents to publish data to a Kinesis Data Firehose stream in the centralized logging AWS account. Create a Lambda function to read messages from the stream and push messages to Kinesis Data Firehose and then store the data in S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Kinesis Data Firehose in the logging account and then subscribe the delivery stream to CloudWatch Logs streams in each application AWS account via subscription filters. Persist the log data in an Amazon S3 bucket inside the logging AWS account\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up CloudWatch Logs streams in each application AWS account to forward events to CloudWatch Logs in the centralized logging AWS account. In the centralized logging AWS account, subscribe a Kinesis Data Firehose stream to Amazon CloudWatch Events and further use the Firehose stream to store the log data in S3\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Export theo giờ không đạt gần thời gian thực và cần tự viết cơ chế assume role, xuất và xử lý lỗi nhiều tài khoản.","CloudWatch agent không trực tiếp tạo đường Firehose như mô tả; Firehose cũng không phải stream để Lambda tùy ý đọc rồi gửi ngược vào chính nó.","Thiết lập đích và quyền subscription liên tài khoản để chuyển CloudWatch Logs sang Firehose tại tài khoản logging, rồi giao S3. Đây là pipeline được quản lý cho tập trung log.","CloudWatch Events khác CloudWatch Logs. Thiết kế đã nhầm nguồn sự kiện với luồng log; cần Logs subscription tới đích được hỗ trợ."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company has multiple AWS accounts hosting its portfolio of IT applications that serve the company\u0027s retail and enterprise customers. A CloudWatch Logs agent is installed on each of the EC2 instances running these IT applications. The company wants to aggregate all security events in a centralized AWS account dedicated to log storage. The centralized operations team at the company needs to perform near-real-time gathering and collating events across multiple AWS accounts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions would you recommend to address these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:29":{"id":"e:3:29","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 29","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUpdate the Athena workgroup settings for the given query, so it has access to the most stable version of Athena engine\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpdate the Athena workgroup settings for the given query, so it has access to the latest version of Athena engine\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSpecify the larger table on the left side of the join and the smaller table on the right side\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSpecify the smaller table on the left side of the join and the larger table on the right side\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn Amazon Athena query is lagging in performance. The query has a join on two tables using the equality condition. You have been hired as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to optimize the query. Upon analysis, you have noticed that one of the tables is large with millions of rows of data and the other table is small.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat do you recommend to address the given requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSpecify the larger table on the left side of the join and the smaller table on the right side\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eChoosing the right join order is critical for better query performance. When you join two tables using the equality condition, specify the larger table on the left side of the join and the smaller table on the right side. For the most common type of joins that use equality conditions, Athena builds a lookup table from the table on the right and distributes it to the worker nodes. It then streams the table on the left, joining rows by looking up matching values in the lookup table. This is called a distributed hash join. Because the lookup table built from the table on the right side is kept in memory, the smaller that table is, the less memory will be used, and the faster the join will run.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e34ab0d49a2028eb84a0646f6fad1e6f4a0c694becb521e92c72f006cd51c023.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e34ab0d49a2028eb84a0646f6fad1e6f4a0c694becb521e92c72f006cd51c023.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate the Athena workgroup settings for the given query, so it has access to the latest version of Athena engine\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate the Athena workgroup settings for the given query, so it has access to the most stable version of Athena engine\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use workgroups to separate users, teams, applications, or workloads, to set limits on the amount of data each query or the entire workgroup can process, and to track costs. Because workgroups act as resources, you can use resource-level identity-based policies to control access to a specific workgroup. You can also view query-related metrics in Amazon CloudWatch, control costs by configuring limits on the amount of data scanned, create thresholds, and trigger actions, such as Amazon SNS, when these thresholds are breached.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAthena occasionally releases a new engine version to provide improved performance, functionality, and code fixes. When a new engine version is available, Athena notifies you in the console. You can choose to let Athena decide when to upgrade, or manually specify an Athena engine version per workgroup.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThese two options have been added as distractors since the version of the Athena engine used for the query is not relevant to the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSpecify the smaller table on the left side of the join and the larger table on the right side\u003c/strong\u003e - This violates the aforementioned recommendation for optimizing join queries having two tables of asymmetric sizes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-7":{"id":"p:1-7","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 7","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAttach a bucket policy to the S3 bucket that will explicitly grant access permissions to the IAM role associated with the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eVerify that the route table of the VPC has inbound and outbound routes for the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure private DNS options for the VPC gateway endpoint\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an Internet Gateway in the VPC for the AWS Glue job to access the Amazon S3 bucket via the public endpoint\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Bucket policy giải quyết quyền truy cập. Dấu hiệu lỗi trong đề trỏ vào đường kết nối qua S3 gateway endpoint, nên cần kiểm tra định tuyến trước thay vì chỉ thêm quyền.","Kiểm tra route table gắn với subnet chạy Glue: phải có tuyến tới prefix list của S3 qua gateway endpoint. Có quyền IAM mà thiếu đường mạng phù hợp thì tác vụ vẫn không truy cập được S3.","Private DNS là cấu hình liên quan tới interface endpoint trong tình huống này. S3 gateway endpoint dùng route table, nên chỉnh private DNS không sửa được lỗi định tuyến đó.","Internet Gateway đưa kết nối theo đường công khai, trong khi thiết kế cần truy cập S3 qua endpoint riêng. Cần sửa đường qua gateway endpoint thay vì thay đổi mô hình kết nối."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer has configured an AWS Glue job to read data from an Amazon S3 bucket by setting up the AWS Glue connection details and the associated IAM role. However, when the AWS Glue job is run, it fails with an error pointing to the Amazon S3 VPC gateway endpoint that has been configured for accessing the data in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow should the data engineer troubleshoot this issue?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-23":{"id":"p:4-23","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 23","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eChange the application architecture to create a new S3 bucket for each customer and then upload each customer\u0027s files directly under the respective buckets\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the application architecture to create a new S3 bucket for each day\u0027s data and then upload the daily files directly under that day\u0027s bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the application architecture to use the S3 Glacier Deep Archive storage class\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the application architecture to create customer-specific custom prefixes within the single bucket and then upload the daily files into those prefixed locations\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Tạo một bucket cho mỗi khách hàng làm tăng số tài nguyên và chính sách phải quản lý. Có thể phân tán tải bằng nhiều prefix ngay trong một bucket thay vì thay đổi kiến trúc theo cách này.","Tách bucket theo ngày không cần thiết và vẫn tập trung các lượt ghi hiện tại vào bucket của ngày đó. Prefix theo khách hàng giúp phân tán yêu cầu ghi phù hợp hơn.","Glacier Deep Archive cần khôi phục trước khi đọc nên không đáp ứng yêu cầu xem tệp ngay sau khi tải lên. Đổi storage class cũng không phải cách chính để phân tán tải yêu cầu.","Phân bố object vào các prefix theo khách hàng giúp S3 mở rộng thông lượng trên nhiều prefix. Đây là cách ít thay đổi tài nguyên hơn tạo nhiều bucket; vẫn cần xử lý retry khi tải tăng nhanh."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data-storage service uses Amazon S3 under the hood to power its storage offerings which allow the customers to upload and view the files immediately. Currently, all the customer files are uploaded directly under a single S3 bucket. The data analytics team has started seeing scalability issues where customer file uploads are failing during peak access hours with more than 5000 requests per second.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents the MOST resource-efficient and cost-optimal way of resolving this issue?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:6":{"id":"e:3:6","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 6","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eIncrease the concurrency scaling quota for the Amazon Redshift cluster so more reporting queries can run at the same time\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate Redshift Spectrum external tables over the Redshift warehouse tables and rewrite the complex transformation queries to reference the external tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate Amazon Redshift materialized views for the frequently queried transformation patterns and refresh the views after the daily data loads finish\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate additional Amazon Redshift read-only clusters, copy the transformed tables to each cluster after the daily load, and route each reporting team to a separate cluster endpoint\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company maintains a central Amazon Redshift data warehouse that receives daily sales, inventory, and customer activity data from Amazon RDS for SQL Server and Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL databases. Several reporting teams run the same complex transformation queries each morning to calculate margin trends, regional demand forecasts, and customer segment metrics. A data engineer observes that these queries take several hours to complete and must reduce execution time as much as possible.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate Amazon Redshift materialized views for the frequently queried transformation patterns and refresh the views after the daily data loads finish\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift materialized views are designed to improve performance for repeated and predictable analytical workloads. A materialized view stores a precomputed result set from a SQL query, so later queries can reuse the stored result instead of recomputing every join, aggregation, and transformation from the base tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach fits the scenario because the reporting teams run the same complex transformation patterns each morning after the daily data load. The data engineer can create materialized views for those patterns and refresh the views after the load completes, so reports can read precomputed results.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution reduces query execution time as much as possible among the options because it directly avoids repeated execution of expensive transformation logic. Increasing concurrency, exporting data, or moving queries to external tables does not remove the heavy compute work from the repeated queries in the same way.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/00406bbb7faad08e9600aa7629b8544162978f7bf25743d853b5034b525f95c0.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/00406bbb7faad08e9600aa7629b8544162978f7bf25743d853b5034b525f95c0.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f5b347e91167ee317951ce14a343b8183ff7dd613a22fe1718f8e7cebe13f520.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f5b347e91167ee317951ce14a343b8183ff7dd613a22fe1718f8e7cebe13f520.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the concurrency scaling quota for the Amazon Redshift cluster so more reporting queries can run at the same time\u003c/strong\u003e - Concurrency scaling can help Amazon Redshift support more simultaneous queries when the cluster has queueing pressure. It can improve throughput when many users submit queries at the same time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the problem is that individual complex transformation queries take hours to finish. Increasing a concurrency scaling quota does not precompute results or substantially reduce the work required by each expensive query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate additional Amazon Redshift read-only clusters, copy the transformed tables to each cluster after the daily load, and route each reporting team to a separate cluster endpoint\u003c/strong\u003e - Separate read-only clusters can reduce workload contention between teams. This can help when many teams compete for the same Redshift cluster resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, copying transformed tables to multiple clusters adds data movement, synchronization, cost, and operational overhead. It also does not address the root problem as efficiently as storing frequently used complex results in materialized views.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate Redshift Spectrum external tables over the Redshift warehouse tables and rewrite the complex transformation queries to reference the external tables\u003c/strong\u003e - Redshift Spectrum can query external tables over data that resides in Amazon S3. It is useful when Redshift users need to query data lake files without loading all the data into local Redshift tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option incorrectly uses Spectrum external tables as a performance layer over Redshift warehouse tables. Spectrum is not intended to create external tables based on Redshift tables, and it would not precompute the complex transformations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-auto-mv.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-auto-mv.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-query-performance.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-query-performance.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/optimize-your-amazon-redshift-query-performance-with-automated-materialized-views/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/optimize-your-amazon-redshift-query-performance-with-automated-materialized-views/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:35":{"id":"e:7:35","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 35","correct":[0],"answers":["Collect metrics and logs from the microservices using Amazon CloudWatch Container Insights. Configure a Data Firehose subscription filter to stream the logs to an Amazon OpenSearch Service cluster.","Configure an Amazon Kinesis Agent to channel the logs from the microservices to Kinesis Data Streams. Relay the logs to Amazon DynamoDB with AWS Lambda for storage and analysis.","Use Amazon CloudWatch Application Insights to aggregate metrics and logs from the microservices. Set up an AWS Lambda subscription filter to push the log data to an Amazon OpenSearch Service cluster.","Channel logs from microservices to Data Firehose, then relay the logs to OpenSearch Service for enhanced search and analysis."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team is enhancing the observability of an application deployed in an Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe team wants to centralize the collection of low-level system metrics and logs from various microservices and enable near real-time visualization of these data for better insight and quicker response times.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution will satisfy these requirements with the LEAST amount of operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon CloudWatch Container Insights\u003c/strong\u003e is a service that monitors, troubleshoots, and alarms on containerized applications and microservices. It collects metrics and logs from EKS, ECS, and Kubernetes clusters and provides automatic dashboards in the CloudWatch console for end-to-end visibility.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eData Firehose\u003c/strong\u003e is a fully managed service that delivers real-time streaming data to destinations like S3, Redshift, and OpenSearch Service. A subscription filter in Firehose allows you to selectively filter logs from CloudWatch Logs and deliver them to the configured destination.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe\u003cstrong\u003e Amazon OpenSearch Service\u003c/strong\u003e is a fully managed service that makes it easy to deploy, operate, and scale OpenSearch implementations. It provides enhanced security, availability, integration with other AWS services, and lower costs compared to managing your own OpenSearch cluster. Furthermore, it enables the visualization of log data through Kibana dashboards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c6040b1a6b71867578086fa089a8ed487d274fc595a11492ac7dcf100d89d5e1.jpg\" alt=\"Diagram of the given correct solution.\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team can use Amazon CloudWatch Container Insights to collect metrics and logs from the microservices running in the various EKS clusters. They can configure a Firehose stream to transport the logs from Container Insights to Amazon OpenSearch Service. OpenSearch Service is a fully managed service that makes it easy to deploy, operate, and scale for log analytics. It allows indexing and visualizing the logs from Container Insights for near real-time search and analysis. This removes the operational overhead of managing the infrastructure. The team can use OpenSearch Dashboards or Kibana for visualization and Grafana for dashboards and alerts, providing a single pane of glass for monitoring across all their clusters and enabling improved troubleshooting capabilities.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCollect metrics and logs from the microservices using Amazon CloudWatch Container Insights. Configure a Data Firehose subscription filter to stream the logs to an Amazon OpenSearch Service cluster.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an Amazon Kinesis Agent to channel the logs from the microservices to Kinesis Data Streams. Relay the logs to Amazon DynamoDB with AWS Lambda for storage and analysis\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. This option does not provide an integrated solution for real-time visualization of query results. While it captures logs and uses AWS Lambda for processing, DynamoDB is primarily a NoSQL database suited for storage and not for the real-time visualization of logs and metrics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon CloudWatch Application Insights to aggregate metrics and logs from the microservices. Set up an AWS Lambda subscription filter to push the log data to an Amazon OpenSearch Service cluster\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. CloudWatch Application Insights is primarily designed for monitoring applications rather than being a direct solution for aggregating metrics and logs from microservices. It doesn\u0027t offer a built-in way to collect and visualize Kubernetes metrics or logs specifically. Furthermore, using AWS Lambda as an intermediary to deliver logs to Amazon OpenSearch introduces additional latency and complexity. AWS Lambda functions add an extra processing step, which can result in delays, especially under high throughput or when the function needs to scale.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChannel logs from microservices to Data Firehose, then relay the logs to OpenSearch Service for enhanced search and analysis\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While this option involves Amazon OpenSearch Service for search and analysis, it misses the integration with Amazon CloudWatch Container Insights, designed explicitly for monitoring containerized applications and provides essential metrics and logs crucial for observability.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/Subscriptions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/Subscriptions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/ContainerInsights.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/monitoring/ContainerInsights.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/opensearch-service/latest/developerguide/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these Tutorials Dojo Cheat Sheets: \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-cloudwatch/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-cloudwatch/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-opensearch-service/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-opensearch-service/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:43":{"id":"e:1:43","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 43","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a CloudWatch Logs metric filter to match audit-relevant log events, configure a CloudWatch alarm on the generated metric, and use the alarm action to deliver the matching log records to the S3 bucket through Amazon Data Firehose\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a subscription filter in the CloudWatch Logs log group that sends matching events to the Amazon Data Firehose delivery stream. Configure Firehose to invoke an AWS Lambda function to transform records before S3 delivery\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a subscription filter in the CloudWatch Logs log group and configure the filter to monitor the Amazon Data Firehose delivery stream. Configure Firehose to invoke an AWS Lambda function for format conversion\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eTag the CloudWatch Logs log groups that contain audit events, configure Amazon Data Firehose to ingest only tagged log groups, and configure Firehose to write the output in the audit team\u0027s required format\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company is building a centralized audit log pipeline by using Amazon Data Firehose. The pipeline must stream selected Amazon CloudWatch Logs events from application log groups to an Amazon S3 bucket. The audit team needs only security-relevant log events, and the delivered records must use a structure that its analysis tools can read reliably. The company must keep the pipeline performant as log volume grows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements and maintain reliable performance?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a subscription filter in the CloudWatch Logs log group that sends matching events to the Amazon Data Firehose delivery stream. Configure Firehose to invoke an AWS Lambda function to transform records before S3 delivery\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCloudWatch Logs subscription filters provide a real-time feed of log events from a log group to supported destinations such as Amazon Data Firehose. The filter pattern allows the company to send only matching security-relevant events instead of forwarding every log event to Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Data Firehose can invoke an AWS Lambda function to transform incoming records before delivery. This transformation step can reshape, enrich, or convert the selected CloudWatch Logs records into the format that the audit team\u0027s analysis tools require.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution maintains reliable performance because CloudWatch Logs sends matching events directly to Firehose, and Firehose manages buffering, delivery, retries, and integration with Amazon S3. The company should size and configure the Firehose stream for the expected log volume, but it does not need to build a custom polling, export, or post-delivery processing workflow.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f06dc7aa0490f9a85cf45538689dedbe0bee7f4b6dbba2eeeaa7e2a7a179cb69.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/f06dc7aa0490f9a85cf45538689dedbe0bee7f4b6dbba2eeeaa7e2a7a179cb69.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/infrastructure-and-automation/how-to-automatically-subscribe-to-amazon-cloudwatch-logs-groups/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/infrastructure-and-automation/how-to-automatically-subscribe-to-amazon-cloudwatch-logs-groups/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b1dd241eacd35394a202d314ef1cb44d3add2833edc0c60c03e10025df1c0ca9.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b1dd241eacd35394a202d314ef1cb44d3add2833edc0c60c03e10025df1c0ca9.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/infrastructure-and-automation/how-to-automatically-subscribe-to-amazon-cloudwatch-logs-groups/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/infrastructure-and-automation/how-to-automatically-subscribe-to-amazon-cloudwatch-logs-groups/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a CloudWatch Logs metric filter to match audit-relevant log events, configure a CloudWatch alarm on the generated metric, and use the alarm action to deliver the matching log records to the S3 bucket through Amazon Data Firehose\u003c/strong\u003e - CloudWatch Logs metric filters can search log events for matching patterns and convert those matches into CloudWatch metrics. They are useful for dashboards, alarms, and operational monitoring when teams need to count or measure patterns in log data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, metric filters do not route the original log events to Amazon Data Firehose, transform log records into an analysis-compatible format, or deliver selected records to Amazon S3. The pipeline needs a CloudWatch Logs subscription filter to send matching log events to Firehose, and Firehose can then use Lambda transformation before S3 delivery.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a subscription filter in the CloudWatch Logs log group and configure the filter to monitor the Amazon Data Firehose delivery stream. Configure Firehose to invoke an AWS Lambda function for format conversion\u003c/strong\u003e - A CloudWatch Logs subscription filter does not monitor a Firehose delivery stream. The subscription filter belongs to the CloudWatch Logs log group and uses Firehose as the destination for matching log events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option reverses the relationship between the services. Firehose can receive events from CloudWatch Logs and invoke Lambda for transformation, but the filter must evaluate log events in the log group.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTag the CloudWatch Logs log groups that contain audit events, configure Amazon Data Firehose to ingest only tagged log groups, and configure Firehose to write the output in the audit team\u0027s required format\u003c/strong\u003e - Tags can help organize resources and support access control or cost allocation patterns. However, Amazon Data Firehose does not automatically ingest CloudWatch Logs events based only on log group tags.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also lacks a CloudWatch Logs subscription filter pattern to select only relevant audit events. The company needs a log-level filter and a Firehose destination, not a tag-based ingestion mechanism.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/Subscriptions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/Subscriptions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/SubscriptionFilters.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/SubscriptionFilters.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/subscription-concepts.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudWatch/latest/logs/subscription-concepts.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/infrastructure-and-automation/how-to-automatically-subscribe-to-amazon-cloudwatch-logs-groups/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/infrastructure-and-automation/how-to-automatically-subscribe-to-amazon-cloudwatch-logs-groups/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:36":{"id":"e:1:36","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 36","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EMR to run scheduled PySpark jobs, install the proprietary libraries by using bootstrap actions, and configure the Spark job to read from Amazon S3 and write to Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon SageMaker Processing jobs to run PySpark code with native SageMaker libraries, and configure the processing output to load transformed clickstream data into Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Studio to build and schedule serverless PySpark jobs, provide the proprietary libraries to the AWS Glue job from Amazon S3, and configure an AWS Glue connection to write the transformed data to Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Lambda functions that run on an Amazon EventBridge schedule to read the S3 clickstream files, package the proprietary attribution libraries with the deployment bundle, transform the records in memory, and call the Amazon Redshift Data API to insert the transformed rows\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA digital advertising company stores raw impression and clickstream logs in an Amazon S3 bucket in the us-east-1 Region. The company must process the data once each night by running complex PySpark transformations that use proprietary attribution and enrichment libraries. After transformation, the company must load the curated dataset into Amazon Redshift for analytics. The solution must scale for large daily data volumes and minimize infrastructure management.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Studio to build and schedule serverless PySpark jobs, provide the proprietary libraries to the AWS Glue job from Amazon S3, and configure an AWS Glue connection to write the transformed data to Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue is the best fit because it provides a serverless Apache Spark environment for extract, transform, and load workloads. The company can use AWS Glue Studio to author, manage, run, and monitor PySpark jobs without provisioning or managing Spark clusters.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue supports custom Python dependencies for ETL jobs. The company can package proprietary libraries and provide them to the AWS Glue job by using job parameters such as --additional-python-modules or --extra-py-files, with libraries stored in Amazon S3 when required. This satisfies the requirement to run complex PySpark transformations that depend on internal libraries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue also supports Amazon Redshift as a target for ETL workloads. The company can configure an AWS Glue connection to Amazon Redshift and schedule the job to run daily. This approach provides high scalability for large data workloads while minimizing operational overhead compared with managing EC2, EMR clusters, ECS containers, or custom scheduling infrastructure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b0d5ec4d1df48b7f81aa4a22d92b216f511e2bede4fff7df8db30deb6ad66693.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b0d5ec4d1df48b7f81aa4a22d92b216f511e2bede4fff7df8db30deb6ad66693.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/process-data-with-varying-data-ingestion-frequencies-using-aws-glue-job-bookmarks/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/process-data-with-varying-data-ingestion-frequencies-using-aws-glue-job-bookmarks/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9c599d2890658bdab2331a034312986b0a87b0649865175d5d640673fe3d93e3.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9c599d2890658bdab2331a034312986b0a87b0649865175d5d640673fe3d93e3.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/process-data-with-varying-data-ingestion-frequencies-using-aws-glue-job-bookmarks/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/process-data-with-varying-data-ingestion-frequencies-using-aws-glue-job-bookmarks/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Lambda functions that run on an Amazon EventBridge schedule to read the S3 clickstream files, package the proprietary attribution libraries with the deployment bundle, transform the records in memory, and call the Amazon Redshift Data API to insert the transformed rows\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Lambda is useful for event-driven processing and lightweight transformations. However, complex PySpark transformations over large daily clickstream datasets are not a good fit for Lambda because Lambda has execution duration, memory, package, and runtime constraints.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also inserts rows into Amazon Redshift through the Redshift Data API, which is inefficient for large-scale analytical loads. A Spark ETL service such as AWS Glue can process large files in parallel and load transformed data into Redshift more appropriately.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon SageMaker Processing jobs to run PySpark code with native SageMaker libraries, and configure the processing output to load transformed clickstream data into Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon SageMaker Processing jobs can run data processing workloads and can support Spark-based processing in some ML-oriented pipelines. However, SageMaker Processing is primarily positioned for machine learning preprocessing, postprocessing, and evaluation workflows rather than general daily ETL into Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also mentions native SageMaker libraries, which does not address the requirement to use proprietary PySpark libraries. AWS Glue is a more direct fit for scheduled S3-to-Redshift ETL with custom PySpark transformations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon EMR to run scheduled PySpark jobs, install the proprietary libraries by using bootstrap actions, and configure the Spark job to read from Amazon S3 and write to Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR is a strong platform for running Spark and PySpark at scale. Bootstrap actions can install custom libraries on EMR cluster nodes before jobs run, so this option can technically satisfy the transformation requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, EMR requires more operational management than AWS Glue for this use case. The company must manage cluster configuration, bootstrap actions, scaling behavior, cluster lifecycle, and operational monitoring. AWS Glue provides a lower-overhead serverless Spark ETL service for scheduled batch transformations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-python-libraries.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-python-libraries.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-redshift-home.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-redshift-home.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-data-warehouse-schedule.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-data-warehouse-schedule.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/process-data-with-varying-data-ingestion-frequencies-using-aws-glue-job-bookmarks/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/process-data-with-varying-data-ingestion-frequencies-using-aws-glue-job-bookmarks/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:3":{"id":"e:5:3","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 3","correct":[1,4],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eDecrease the number of shards in the Kinesis stream to reduce complexity using the \u003ccode\u003eMergeShard\u003c/code\u003e command.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eIncrease the number of shards in the Kinesis stream to handle the increased data volume using the \u003ccode\u003eSplitShard\u003c/code\u003e command.\u003c/span\u003e","Replace the data stream with Amazon Data Firehose instead.","Implement Step Scaling to the Kinesis Data Stream.","Enable Kinesis Data Streams Enhanced Fan-Out and HTTP/2 data retrieval to reduce latency."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA large e-commerce company is leveraging an Amazon Kinesis stream to ingest real-time data from its website, such as user clicks and page views. A major sale event has been launched recently, which significantly increased the volume of data flowing into the Kinesis stream. The stream is unable to handle the increased data volume, causing data loss and increased latency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following actions should be taken to handle the increased data volume and ensure that the Kinesis stream can ingest the data in real-time? (SELECT TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams\u003c/strong\u003e supports\u0026nbsp;\u003cem\u003eresharding\u003c/em\u003e, which lets you adjust the number of shards in your stream to adapt to changes in the rate of data flow through the stream. Resharding is considered an advanced operation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThere are two types of resharding operations: shard split and shard merge. In a shard split, you divide a single shard into two shards. In a shard merge, you combine two shards into a single shard. Resharding is always\u0026nbsp;\u003cem\u003epairwise\u003c/em\u003e\u0026nbsp;in the sense that you cannot split into more than two shards in a single operation, and you cannot merge more than two shards in a single operation. The shard or pair of shards that the resharding operation acts on are referred to as\u0026nbsp;\u003cem\u003eparent\u003c/em\u003e\u0026nbsp;shards. The shard or pair of shards that result from the resharding operation are referred to as\u0026nbsp;\u003cem\u003echild\u0026nbsp;\u003c/em\u003eshards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eSplitShard\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;command is used to increase the number of shards in a Kinesis data stream, which can help handle increased data volume. Each shard in a Kinesis data stream provides a fixed amount of capacity, so adding more shards increases the stream’s capacity to ingest and transport data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0f2085cf47bf929811acd47a26e6072bc176215b2693cd86897cda5012a147ee.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEnhanced Fan-Out is a feature in Kinesis Data Streams that provides each consumer with its own 2 MB per second of dedicated bandwidth per shard, which can help handle increased data volume. Additionally, the HTTP/2 data retrieval API can reduce latency, making data delivery from producers to consumers faster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/72c1ee4584659e05a2873b2abacfca92d44c80e5dc1e9be067767c900d26f95d.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e–\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the number of shards in the Kinesis stream to handle the increased data volume using the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eSplitShard\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;command.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e–\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Kinesis Data Streams Enhanced Fan-Out and HTTP/2 data retrieval to reduce latency.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDecrease the number of shards in the Kinesis stream to reduce complexity using the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMergeShard\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;command\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect. The\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMergeShard\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;command is used to reduce the number of shards in a Kinesis data stream. Reducing the number of shards would decrease the stream’s capacity to ingest and transport data, which is not suitable when the data volume has increased.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eImplement Step Scaling to the Kinesis Data Stream\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis\u0026nbsp;incorrect. While step scaling is a concept in AWS for adjusting capacity based on demand, it is not directly applicable to Kinesis Data Streams. Kinesis Data Streams are scaled using shards, and the number of shards can be adjusted using the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eSplitShard\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;and\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMergeShard\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;commands.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003eReplace the data stream with Amazon Data Firehose instead\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because the throughput of Data Firehose is not exceptionally higher than Kinesis Data Streams.\u0026nbsp;In fact, the throughput of an Amazon Kinesis data stream is designed to scale without limits by increasing the number of shards within a data stream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/kds-enhanced-fanout/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/kinesis/split-shard.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/kinesis/split-shard.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/kinesis-using-sdk-java-resharding.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/kinesis-using-sdk-java-resharding.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck Out this Kinesis Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:37":{"id":"e:6:37","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 37","correct":[3],"answers":["Utilize Amazon S3 for the data lake\u0027s storage and implement S3 access policies for row and column access restrictions. Facilitate data access directly via Amazon S3.","Utilize Apache Ranger through Amazon Elastic MapReduce (EMR) to manage access at the row and column level. Integrate Amazon S3 for data lake storage and provide data access via Apache Pig.","Utilize Amazon Redshift for data lake storage with Redshift policies for access control. Access data via Apache Spark and Amazon Athena federated queries.","Utilize Amazon S3 for data lake storage and leverage AWS Lake Formation for row and column-level access control. Enable data access via AWS Lake Formation."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial company is developing a data lake using Amazon S3. The company plans to utilize services such as Amazon Athena, Amazon Elastic MapReduce (EMR), and Amazon Redshift Spectrum to meet its data analysis needs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe company has several departments that need access to the data lake. Each department should only access parts of the data relevant to their work, with permissions set at both the column and row levels to meet their specific needs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow can the company meet this requirement in the LEAST amount of operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAWS Lake Formation\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e is a central hub on Amazon S3 for storing structured and unstructured data until needed. It manages permissions and access for data stored in S3. Lake Formation provides granular control over data access and a unified Storage API for secure data operations. Key features include encryption, private endpoints, and audit logs for enhanced security and isolation of the data lake within S3.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8edbe54def93332c386526d8c54857c292f04df6de424de2e2cbe756efa4b58f.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eLake Formation also offers centralized data governance through the AWS Glue Data Catalog, employing row and column-level access controls for precise access management. This ensures fine-grained control over data accessibility. Amazon Athena and Redshift Spectrum can then query S3 data while adhering to Lake Formation\u0027s permissions, thereby maintaining proper access control. Apache Pig on Amazon ElasticMap Reduce can also leverage Lake Formation permissions to securely access data from the S3 data lake. Lastly, integration with Apache Ranger enables centralized access management, where Ranger policies are applied to data access via Apache Pig, and other tools.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon S3 for data lake storage and leverage AWS Lake Formation for row and column-level access control. Enable data access via AWS Lake Formation.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon S3 for the data lake\u0027s storage and implement S3 access policies for row and column access restrictions. Facilitate data access directly via Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because S3 access policies alone cannot provide the granular row-level and column-level access control required by the scenario. While S3 offers robust data storage capabilities, it lacks the finer access controls available through AWS Lake Formation, which are essential for detailed data access management as described.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Apache Ranger through Amazon Elastic MapReduce (EMR) to manage access at the row and column level. Integrate Amazon S3 for data lake storage and provide data access via Apache Pig\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Apache Ranger manages access in Hadoop environments but doesn\u0027t fully utilize AWS\u0027s capabilities for managing data lake security. AWS Lake Formation provides centralized access control across multiple services, making it a better choice.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize Amazon Redshift for data lake storage with Redshift policies for access control. Access data via Apache Spark and Amazon Athena federated queries\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This option is not aligned with the scenario\u0027s focus on building a data lake with Amazon S3 and managing access with AWS Lake Formation. Although Amazon Redshift is a powerful data warehousing solution, it is not intended to serve as the primary storage for a data lake. Furthermore, using Redshift for data lake storage would not provide the same level of granular access control or the ease of management offered by AWS Lake Formation when used in conjunction with Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cbac-tutorial.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/cbac-tutorial.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/control-data-access-and-permissions-with-aws-lake-formation-and-amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/control-data-access-and-permissions-with-aws-lake-formation-and-amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Lake Formation Cheat Sheet: \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-58":{"id":"p:1-58","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 58","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDistribute the static content through Amazon EFS\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDistribute the static content through Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDistribute the dynamic content through Amazon EFS\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDistribute the dynamic content through Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["EFS chỉ đổi nơi chứa tệp; ECS vẫn phải phục vụ nội dung cho người dùng. Vì vậy chưa giảm nguồn lưu lượng chính qua ECS.","Đưa nội dung tĩnh lên S3 để người dùng tải trực tiếp giúp giảm tải mạng cho ECS. Giữ phần xử lý động trong ứng dụng.","Vấn đề nằm ở 90% lưu lượng nội dung tĩnh. Chuyển chỗ lưu nội dung động sang EFS không giải quyết đúng nguyên nhân.","S3 không chạy mã xử lý động phía máy chủ như ứng dụng trong ECS. Chỉ nên chuyển tệp tĩnh trong tình huống này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team has deployed a microservice to the Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS). The application layer is in a Docker container that provides both static and dynamic content through an Application Load Balancer. With increasing load, the Amazon ECS cluster is experiencing higher network usage. The team has looked into the network usage and found that 90% of it is due to distributing static content of the application.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat do you recommend to improve the application\u0027s network usage and decrease costs?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:6:42":{"id":"e:6:42","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 42","correct":[2],"answers":["Ingest event data to a self-managed Apache Kafka hosted in Amazon EC2 instances. Analyze the data with Amazon Redshift.","Publish the data to an Amazon Aurora Serverless database and use native SQL queries.","Ingest event data to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Create an application for consuming data and loading results in Amazon DynamoDB for querying.","Publish game event data to an Amazon SQS queue. Create an AWS Lambda function to process data in batches."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA gaming company seeks a system to monitor player activities and interactions across various online platforms in real-time. The desired system must be capable of ingesting streaming data from game events formatted as nested JSON. The system must also efficiently handle a high volume of I/O requests generated by continuous player activity and game events. It must also keep the query latency under single-digit milliseconds to provide quick updates for game dashboards and analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow can the company minimize operational overhead while meeting these requirements effectively?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data\u003c/strong\u003e Streams is a service that continuously captures and stores streaming data records in real-time. It can ingest massive amounts of data from hundreds of sources, such as application logs, social media feeds, website clickstreams, etc.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Producers like applications, servers, devices, etc., can continuously send data records to a data stream in Kinesis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Consumers, like custom applications, can then process/analyze the streaming data by reading data records from the stream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- Applications can use the Kinesis Client Library for reading data from streams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eProcessed records can then be stored in other AWS services like DynamoDB, Redshift, S3, etc.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon DynamoDB\u003c/strong\u003e is a fully managed NoSQL database that offers single-digit millisecond performance at any scale. It is a document and key-value database that provides predictable performance with seamless scalability.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e- It allows data to be stored and queried in JSON format, which makes it suitable for storing processed records from Kinesis streams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0537ad1242f42db026dbd12ea851c3949faccd08090514a97f914a7834d4ebcb.png\" alt=\"Data stream analytics with Managed Apache Flink\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo best fulfill the gaming company\u0027s requirements, it\u0027s recommended that Amazon Kinesis Data Streams be utilized to ingest game event data. With its ability to handle streaming data from numerous sources at high throughput, Kinesis Data Streams is an ideal choice for this task. Subsequently, storing the results in Amazon DynamoDB allows for efficient querying. DynamoDB\u0027s capability to store data in nested JSON format aligns well with the needs of the project, while its fast and predictable performance ensures swift queries. With DynamoDB being a fully managed NoSQL database service, latency for queries can be maintained under 10 milliseconds, meeting the necessary performance criteria.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eIngest event data to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Create an application for consuming data and loading results in Amazon DynamoDB for querying.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIngest event data to a self-managed Apache Kafka hosted in Amazon EC2 instances. Analyze the data with Amazon Redshift\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect.\u0026nbsp;This solution would simply require managing Kafka clusters on EC2 instances, which adds operational overhead. Additionally, Redshift is better suited for batch analytics rather than low-latency queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePublish the data to an Amazon Aurora Serverless database and use native SQL queries\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While Amazon Aurora Serverless can automatically scale, it is primarily a relational database service, which makes it less suitable for handling nested JSON data and low-latency queries at scale.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePublish game event data to an Amazon SQS queue. Create an AWS Lambda function to process data in batches\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Although SQS queues are great for decoupling components, they introduce latency that may prevent the single-digit millisecond response times from being achieved. Moreover, processing data in batches could further delay the availability of data for real-time analysis and dashboard updates.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/introduction.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/introduction.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/kds.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/kds.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/what-is/real-time-data-streaming/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/what-is/real-time-data-streaming/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these Amazon Kinesis and Amazon DynamoDB Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-kinesis/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:53":{"id":"e:3:53","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 53","correct":[0,1,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eGrant permissions in Lake Formation to allow the Amazon Redshift IAM role to access the specific columns of the historical trades table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an IAM role for Amazon Redshift which has a policy to allow Redshift to access AWS Lake Formation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an internal schema in Amazon Redshift by using the Amazon Redshift IAM role\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eGrant permissions in the S3 bucket policy to allow the Amazon Redshift IAM role to access the specific columns of the historical trades table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an external schema in Amazon Redshift by using the Amazon Redshift IAM role\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an IAM role for Amazon Redshift which has a policy to allow Redshift to access the S3 bucket having data for the historical trades table\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA stock trading company uses Amazon Redshift to power the Business Intelligence (BI) specific queries which are run on Redshift. The data engineering team at the company needs to provide the sales team access to a historical trades table whose data is stored in Apache Parquet format in an S3 bucket of the company\u0027s data lake. The data engineering team should provide access to only a few specific columns in the historical trades table so that the access does not violate the compliance regulations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options should be combined together to build a solution for the given use case? (Select three)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an IAM role for Amazon Redshift which has a policy to allow Redshift to access AWS Lake Formation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an external schema in Amazon Redshift by using the Amazon Redshift IAM role\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eGrant permissions in Lake Formation to allow the Amazon Redshift IAM role to access the specific columns of the historical trades table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift is a fully managed, scalable data warehouse that enables secure analytics at scale. Using Redshift Spectrum, Amazon Redshift customers can easily query their data in Amazon S3. Redshift Spectrum is a built-in feature of Amazon Redshift, and your existing queries and BI tools will continue to work seamlessly. Under the hood, AWS manages a fleet of thousands of Redshift Spectrum nodes spread across multiple Availability Zones. These are transparently scaled and allocated to your queries based on the data that you need to process, with no provisioning or commitments. Redshift Spectrum is also highly concurrent—you can access your Amazon S3 data from any number of Amazon Redshift clusters. To access this data on S3 via Redshift Spectrum, you need to create an external schema in Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift Spectrum supports column level access control for data stored in Amazon S3 and managed by AWS Lake Formation. Column-level access control can be used to limit access to only the specific columns of a table rather than allowing access to all columns of a table. To use this feature, an administrator needs to create an IAM role for Amazon Redshift and create the policy to allow Redshift to access AWS Lake Formation. The administrator can then use the Lake Formation console to specify the tables and columns that the role is allowed access to. The column-level access control policies can also be created and managed by the SQL grant statements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an internal schema in Amazon Redshift by using the Amazon Redshift IAM role\u003c/strong\u003e - As mentioned in the explanation above, you need to create an external schema in Amazon Redshift to access the data on S3 via Redshift spectrum.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eGrant permissions in the S3 bucket policy to allow the Amazon Redshift IAM role to access the specific columns of the historical trades table\u003c/strong\u003e - You need to grant permissions in Lake Formation to allow the Amazon Redshift IAM role to access the specific columns of the historical trades table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an IAM role for Amazon Redshift which has a policy to allow Redshift to access the S3 bucket having data for the historical trades table\u003c/strong\u003e - You need to create an IAM role for Amazon Redshift which has a policy to allow Redshift to access AWS Lake Formation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2019/08/amazon-redshift-spectrum-now-supports-column-level-access-control-with-aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2019/08/amazon-redshift-spectrum-now-supports-column-level-access-control-with-aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c-using-spectrum.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/achieve-finer-grained-data-security-with-column-level-access-control-in-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/achieve-finer-grained-data-security-with-column-level-access-control-in-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-60":{"id":"p:3-60","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 60","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with provisioned capacity and Lambda consumers to run custom filtering logic and write the processed events to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink to perform stateful processing, event filtering, and output delivery to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose with built-in processing capabilities to transform and filter the stream, then deliver the processed data directly to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDeploy an Apache Spark Streaming application on an Amazon EMR cluster to consume the clickstream data, transform the records, and write the processed output to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Kinesis provisioned và Lambda cần quản lý năng lực, consumer, lỗi và mã ghi S3. Nhiều công hơn đường delivery được quản lý khi biến đổi chỉ đơn giản.","Flink mạnh về trạng thái và phép xử lý phức tạp, nhưng thêm ứng dụng xử lý không cần thiết cho lọc/biến đổi đơn giản rồi ghi S3.","Firehose cung cấp đường tiếp nhận, đệm, xử lý được hỗ trợ và giao dữ liệu tới S3. Cấu hình phép biến đổi/lọc phù hợp giúp giảm hạ tầng tự quản lý.","EMR với Spark Streaming cần cụm và ứng dụng riêng. Không ít vận hành nhất cho yêu cầu đơn giản này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics company collects a large volume of clickstream events from its website and mobile application. The company needs to filter invalid events, apply simple streaming transformations, and deliver the processed data to Amazon S3 for downstream analysis. The company wants the most operationally efficient solution with minimal custom infrastructure to manage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:51":{"id":"e:2:51","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 51","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eQuery the AWS Glue Data Catalog tables that the job reads and compare table metadata changes with the failed stage execution\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eOpen the AWS Glue Spark UI for the job run and review the job, stage, task, DAG, and execution details\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the AWS Glue job run insights feature to examine every Spark stage metric and task timeline for the failed run\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eReview Amazon CloudWatch metrics for the AWS Glue job and use the Glue namespace to inspect every individual Spark stage\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer supports several AWS Glue Apache Spark ETL jobs that transform daily advertising and clickstream datasets. The company has enabled monitoring and logging for all AWS Glue jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eOne Spark job starts failing during a transformation stage. The data engineer wants to investigate the failure by reviewing detailed metrics for each individual Spark stage, including task progress, stage timing, and shuffle behavior.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow can the data engineer access the stage-level metrics?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eOpen the AWS Glue Spark UI for the job run and review the job, stage, task, DAG, and execution details\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe AWS Glue Spark UI is the correct place to inspect detailed Spark execution information for AWS Glue Apache Spark jobs. It provides visibility into Spark jobs, stages, tasks, directed acyclic graphs, and execution timelines.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis is useful when a job fails in a specific transformation stage. The data engineer can use the Spark UI to identify slow stages, failed tasks, shuffle behavior, skew, input and output sizes, and other details that help explain why the Spark job failed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution directly meets the requirement because the engineer needs metrics for individual stages within the job. AWS Glue monitoring and CloudWatch metrics provide useful operational visibility, but Spark UI provides the detailed stage-level Spark execution view.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eQuery the AWS Glue Data Catalog tables that the job reads and compare table metadata changes with the failed stage execution\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue Data Catalog stores metadata about databases, tables, partitions, and schemas. Reviewing catalog metadata can help identify schema or partition issues that might affect a job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe issue with this approach is that catalog metadata does not contain Spark runtime stage metrics. It cannot show task progress, failed Spark stages, shuffle details, or execution timelines for a job run.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReview Amazon CloudWatch metrics for the AWS Glue job and use the Glue namespace to inspect every individual Spark stage\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon CloudWatch can show AWS Glue job metrics and observability metrics. These metrics are helpful for monitoring job health, resource usage, failures, and operational trends.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that CloudWatch does not provide the full Spark UI view of every individual Spark stage and task. For detailed stage inspection, the engineer should use the AWS Glue Spark UI.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the AWS Glue job run insights feature to examine every Spark stage metric and task timeline for the failed run\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue job run insights can help diagnose job failures and provide summarized information to speed up troubleshooting. It is useful for identifying common failure patterns and probable causes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because run insights is not the primary interface for detailed Spark stage metrics and task timelines. The Spark UI is the purpose-built tool for reviewing individual Spark stages.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark-ui.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark-ui.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark-ui-jobs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark-ui-jobs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-spark.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-job-insights.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/monitor-job-insights.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/investigate.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/investigate.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-studio-serverless-spark-ui-for-better-monitoring-and-troubleshooting/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-studio-serverless-spark-ui-for-better-monitoring-and-troubleshooting/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:28":{"id":"e:4:28","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 28","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse the Kinesis Agent on the on-premises servers to send the files to Amazon Data Firehose, and deliver the data to a versioning-enabled S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse an on-premises cron job to compress the files and upload them directly to Amazon S3 by using the AWS CLI, then enable S3 Versioning\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS DataSync to transfer files from the on-premises servers to Amazon EFS, enable automatic backups with AWS Backup, and configure Athena to query the EFS files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse a self-managed Kafka agent on the on-premises servers to stream the files to Amazon MSK, and deliver the data to a versioning-enabled S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company receives daily occupancy and revenue files from each branch location. The company wants to upload the files to AWS and use Amazon Athena to query the data from Amazon S3. The company must protect the uploaded files from accidental deletion. The company will run a small application on its on-premises servers to forward the files to a fully managed AWS ingestion service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the Kinesis Agent on the on-premises servers to send the files to Amazon Data Firehose, and deliver the data to a versioning-enabled S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Data Firehose is a fully managed service that can deliver streaming data to destinations such as Amazon S3. The Kinesis Agent can run on the company\u0027s on-premises servers and send file or log records to a Firehose delivery stream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 is the correct storage layer because Athena can query data stored in S3. By enabling S3 Versioning on the destination bucket, the company can preserve object versions and recover from accidental deletions or overwrites.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational overhead because the ingestion target is fully managed and the storage layer is serverless. The company does not need to manage file servers, databases, EMR clusters, Kafka brokers, or custom ingestion scheduling for the daily hotel files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS DataSync to transfer files from the on-premises servers to Amazon EFS, enable automatic backups with AWS Backup, and configure Athena to query the EFS files\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS DataSync can transfer data from on-premises storage to AWS storage services, and AWS Backup can protect Amazon EFS file systems. This can be useful for file migration or hybrid file synchronization workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because Athena is designed to query data in Amazon S3 and supported data sources, not files stored directly in Amazon EFS as the primary data lake target. It also introduces EFS and backup management when the requirement points to S3-based analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse an on-premises cron job to compress the files and upload them directly to Amazon S3 by using the AWS CLI, then enable S3 Versioning\u003c/strong\u003e - Direct uploads to Amazon S3 with the AWS CLI can store the files in a location that Athena can query. Enabling S3 Versioning would also help protect files from accidental deletion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not meet the fully managed ingestion service requirement. The company would need to manage custom cron scheduling, retry logic, local failure handling, credential rotation, monitoring, and upload validation on the on-premises servers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse a self-managed Kafka agent on the on-premises servers to stream the files to Amazon MSK, and deliver the data to a versioning-enabled S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon MSK can provide a managed Apache Kafka cluster for streaming workloads, and Kafka-based pipelines can deliver data to Amazon S3 if the company configures the required connectors or consumers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis creates more operational overhead than necessary. The option uses a self-managed Kafka agent and an MSK cluster when Amazon Data Firehose can directly deliver the forwarded records to Amazon S3 as a fully managed ingestion service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/what-is-this-service.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/what-is-this-service.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/writing-with-agents.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/writing-with-agents.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/create-destination.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/create-destination.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/Versioning.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/Versioning.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/unify-log-aggregation-and-analytics-across-compute-platforms/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/unify-log-aggregation-and-analytics-across-compute-platforms/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:7":{"id":"e:5:7","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 7","correct":[1],"answers":["Modify the existing Redshift cluster to enable encryption using the default Redshift KMS key and perform periodic key rotation through the Redshift console.","Update the current Redshift cluster configuration to enable AWS KMS encryption by specifying a customer-managed KMS key.","Recreate the cluster using dedicated HSM-backed encryption to meet compliance requirements.","Launch a replacement Redshift cluster configured with the AWS-owned KMS key and restore data from a snapshot of the current cluster."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA financial services organization operates an Amazon Redshift cluster that was originally deployed without encryption. A recent compliance audit mandates the following:\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAll data at rest in the cluster must be encrypted using AWS Key Management Service (AWS \u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003eKMS\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e).\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eThe encryption key must support periodic rotation in accordance with internal key management policies.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich solution will satisfy these requirements with the LEAST administrative effort?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eYou can modify an unencrypted cluster to enable AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) encryption, using either an AWS-owned key or a customer-managed key. When you enable AWS KMS encryption on your cluster, Amazon Redshift automatically migrates your data to a new encrypted cluster. Additionally, you can migrate an encrypted cluster to an unencrypted cluster by using the AWS Command Line Interface (CLI), although this cannot be done through the AWS Management Console.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe KMS keys that you create are customer-managed keys. Customer-managed keys are KMS keys in your AWS account that you create, own, and manage. You have full control over these KMS keys, including establishing and maintaining their key policies, IAM policies, and grants, enabling and disabling them, rotating their cryptographic material, adding tags, creating aliases that refer to the KMS keys, and scheduling the KMS keys for deletion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/cc2308841ef67892f42be1d303166799861baa58c18ae9665d3bd6053c9311b9.png\" alt=\"AWS KMS Customer managed keys\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAutomatic key rotation is optional for customer-managed keys and can be enabled or disabled at any time. The rotation date is determined by when automatic key rotation was last enabled. If you change the rotation period for a key that already has automatic key rotation enabled, the rotation date may be adjusted accordingly. As a result, the rotation date can change multiple times throughout the key’s lifespan.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate the current Redshift cluster configuration to enable AWS KMS encryption by specifying a customer-managed KMS key.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;Customer-managed keys allow you to enable automatic annual rotation or perform manual rotation whenever required. Additionally,\u0026nbsp;you don’t need to recreate the cluster; you simply update the configuration to use your own KMS key, and AWS handles the encryption transparently. This satisfies both compliance and minimizes administrative overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch a replacement Redshift cluster configured with the AWS-owned KMS key and restore data from a snapshot of the current cluster\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because AWS-owned keys are fully managed by AWS and do not primarily support customer-controlled rotation. Since the compliance requirement mandates periodic rotation aligned with internal policies, this option fails to meet the requirement. Additionally,\u0026nbsp;this approach also requires creating a new cluster and restoring from snapshots, which is disruptive and adds significant administrative effort compared to simply updating the existing cluster\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eModify the existing Redshift cluster to enable encryption using the default Redshift KMS key and perform periodic key rotation through the Redshift console\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because the default Redshift-managed KMS key does not support customer-managed rotation. Rotation is not configurable by the customer, so this fails the requirement for internal key management policies.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRecreate the cluster using dedicated HSM-backed encryption to meet compliance requirements\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because while HSM-backed encryption typically provides strong security, it introduces significant administrative overhead and complexity. Unless compliance explicitly mandates dedicated HSMs, this approach is unnecessary and not the least-effort solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/changing-cluster-encryption.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/changing-cluster-encryption.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/rotate-keys.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/rotate-keys.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html#customer-mgn-key\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html#customer-mgn-key\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS KMS Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-key-management-service-aws-kms/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-key-management-service-aws-kms/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:22":{"id":"e:6:22","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 22","correct":[3],"answers":["Implement an AWS Step Function workflow to orchestrate the automation of materialized view updates.","Configure an AWS Glue workflow specifically to refresh the materialized views.","Launch an AWS Lambda user-defined function (UDF) to trigger updates within Redshift for the materialized views.","Execute scheduled refresh commands using the query editor v2 in Redshift."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA manufacturing company uses a multi-node Amazon Redshift cluster for its operational database and data warehousing. The company aims to streamline the process of updating its Redshift materialized views routinely through automation.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich solution requires the MINIMAL operational overhead to fulfill this requirement?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshift\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis a managed data warehousing service that allows easy data analysis using SQL and existing BI tools. Amazon Redshift enables you to process massive amounts of structured data with advanced query optimization, columnar storage, and parallel processing, making it easy to run complex analytic queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAdditionally, Amazon Redshift service allows users to create and manage materialized views, which are stored or cached views of a query\u0027s result set for faster access. Redshift supports various automation options, including AWS services and external tools, to automate the updates of these views.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/986855c867f924b8dace526da6e1a5120541f7099623b94adcfb65e56ea5ec60.png\" alt=\"Amazon Redshift\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe query editor v2 in Amazon Redshift can execute SQL commands directly within the Redshift console, including commands to refresh materialized views. These commands can be scheduled to execute automatically and can be set up directly in the Redshift environment. This provides a straightforward method to automate materialized view updates without additional infrastructure or services, thereby minimizing operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eExecute scheduled refresh commands using the query editor v2 in Redshift.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement an AWS Step Function workflow to orchestrate the automation of materialized view updates\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. AWS Step Functions is a service that is primarily used for coordinating complex workflows involving multiple tasks and services, but it is not well-suited for running simple operations that occur entirely within Amazon Redshift. In this case, refreshing materialized views is a self-contained task within the Redshift environment, and using Step Functions would be an unnecessary overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue workflow specifically to refresh the materialized views\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While Glue can connect to Redshift as a data source or target, using it solely for refreshing materialized views would require additional coding, job setup, and potentially moving data in and out of Redshift, which adds operational complexity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch an AWS Lambda user-defined function (UDF) to trigger updates within Redshift for the materialized views\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Using AWS Lambda to refresh materialized views in Amazon Redshift adds complexity and typically requires custom code development and maintenance. It is less operationally efficient than Redshift\u0027s native scheduling capabilities.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/query-editor-v2-schedule-query.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/query-editor-v2-schedule-query.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshift Overview:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003ciframe src=\"./📊 Your Progress - Tutorials Dojo_files/jlLERNzhHOg.html\"\u003e\u003c/iframe\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-43":{"id":"p:2-43","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 43","correct":[2,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEstablish a process to get managerial approval for deleting Amazon S3 objects\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an event trigger on deleting any Amazon S3 object. The event invokes an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) notification via email to the IT manager\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable versioning on the Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the configuration on the Amazon S3 console so that the user needs to provide additional confirmation while deleting any Amazon S3 object\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable multi-factor authentication (MFA) delete on the Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Quy trình phê duyệt hành chính không phải cơ chế kỹ thuật giữ lại object hoặc ngăn xóa version vĩnh viễn.","Email sau khi xóa chỉ thông báo sự việc, không ngăn mất dữ liệu hoặc lưu lại phiên bản đã xóa.","Versioning giữ các phiên bản để khôi phục khi có xóa hoặc ghi đè ngoài ý muốn. Kết hợp MFA Delete để tăng bảo vệ xóa vĩnh viễn.","Không có tùy chọn cấu hình bucket theo mô tả để ép mọi thao tác xóa qua mọi công cụ đều có thêm hộp xác nhận. Cần versioning và MFA Delete.","MFA Delete yêu cầu xác thực bổ sung cho các thao tác nhạy cảm như xóa version vĩnh viễn. Nó bổ sung bảo vệ bên cạnh versioning."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare startup needs to enforce compliance and regulatory guidelines for objects stored in Amazon S3. One of the key requirements is to provide adequate protection against accidental deletion of objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat are your recommendations to address these guidelines? (Select two) ?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:18":{"id":"e:2:18","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 18","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ * \u0027$(John|Doe)\u0027\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ \u0027^(John|Doe)\u0027\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ * \u0027^(John|Doe)\u0027\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ \u0027$(John|Doe)\u0027\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company stores its user information in a MySQL database table called \u003ccode\u003euser\u003c/code\u003e. The \u003ccode\u003ename\u003c/code\u003e column has the users\u0027 names stored in \u003ccode\u003efirstname lastname\u003c/code\u003e format. Due to legacy reasons, a few users have the names stored in \u003ccode\u003elastname firstname\u003c/code\u003e format. A data engineer has been tasked with developing a query that returns all records where the \u003ccode\u003ename\u003c/code\u003e column has values starting with \u003ccode\u003eJohn\u003c/code\u003e or \u003ccode\u003eDoe\u003c/code\u003e, on a case-insensitive basis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following queries represents the correct solution?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ * \u0027^(John|Doe)\u0027\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn SQL, \u003ccode\u003e~\u003c/code\u003e is the regular expression operator. You can use \u003ccode\u003e~*\u003c/code\u003e to make the query case-insensitive. The \u003ccode\u003e^\u003c/code\u003e operator matches a pattern at the start of a string. For the given use case, you can use the \u003ccode\u003e^\u003c/code\u003e operator to find all records where the \u003ccode\u003ename\u003c/code\u003e column has values starting with \u003ccode\u003eJohn\u003c/code\u003e or \u003ccode\u003eDoe\u003c/code\u003e on a case-insensitive basis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ * \u0027$(John|Doe)\u0027\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ \u0027$(John|Doe)\u0027\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003e$\u003c/code\u003e operator matches a pattern at the end of a string. So, both these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT * FROM user WHERE name ~ \u0027^(John|Doe)\u0027\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e - Without the \u003ccode\u003e~ *\u003c/code\u003e characters combination in the WHERE clause, the query would return the results on a case-sensitive basis, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://www.atlassian.com/data/sql/how-regex-works-in-sql\"\u003ehttps://www.atlassian.com/data/sql/how-regex-works-in-sql\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/regexp.html\"\u003ehttps://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/regexp.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:27":{"id":"e:7:27","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 27","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge rule with an event pattern that matches the \u003ccode\u003eSendMessage\u003c/code\u003e SQS actions. Select the Step Functions workflow as the target. Implement a filtering logic in the Step functions workflow.\u003c/span\u003e","Filter orders based on the SQS queue length, considering longer queues to have more relevant orders.","Create an AWS Lambda function with an event source mapping for SQS. Configure an event pattern that filters non-PH orders. Write the function’s code to invoke the Step Functions workflow.","Create a pipe in Amazon EventBridge Pipes with the SQS queue as the source. Configure an event pattern that filters non-PH orders. Select the Step Functions workflow as the target."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA Philippines-based company ships orders globally. These orders are placed in an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) queue. The company has an AWS Step Functions workflow that exclusively processes international orders. The workflow currently uses a \u003ccode\u003eChoice\u003c/code\u003e state to route international orders, but it still starts for every message, incurring unnecessary cost for domestic (\u0027PH\u0027) orders. This workflow should only be executed when an order\u0027s data does not contain the \u0027PH\u0027 country code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options would satisfy the requirements in the LEAST amount of development effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon EventBridge Pipes connects sources to targets. Pipes are intended for point-to-point integrations between supported sources and targets, with support for advanced transformations and enrichment. It reduces the need for specialized knowledge and integration code when developing event-driven architectures, fostering consistency across your company’s applications. To set up a pipe, you choose the source, add optional filtering, define optional enrichment, and choose the target for the event data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/650af49b6e2b401d8e32338bd4e536ddd54c1fa60c2a9ec6fb0d362239739ffb.jpg\" alt=\"Event filtering pattern matching SQS events to a Step Function target\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn this scenario, the goal is to only run the Step Functions workflow for orders not marked with the \u0027PH\u0027 country code, avoiding unnecessary executions. By using Amazon EventBridge Pipes with an \"anything-but\" event filter, we can directly target only those orders. This setup filters out orders before they trigger the workflow, ensuring that only international orders proceed for processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Create a pipe in Amazon EventBridge Pipes with the SQS queue as the source. Configure an event pattern that filters non-PH orders. Select the Step Functions workflow as the target.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge rule with an event pattern that matches the \u003ccode\u003eSendMessage\u003c/code\u003e SQS actions. Select the Step Functions workflow as the target. Implement a filtering logic in the Step functions workflow\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. With this approach, the workflow would be triggered for each order, regardless of the country code, leading to unnecessary executions of the workflow. Moreover, relying on the \u003ccode\u003eSendMessage\u003c/code\u003e action in Amazon EventBridge for processing orders may not be simply reliable due to potential delays or the asynchronous nature of the message delivery system in Amazon SQS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFilter orders based on the SQS queue length, considering longer queues to have more relevant orders\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because\u0026nbsp;the length of the SQS queue (the number of messages in the queue) is just not a reliable indicator of an order\u0027s relevance to the workflow. Also, this approach will not filter orders containing non-PH country codes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Lambda function with an event source mapping for SQS. Configure an event pattern that filters non-PH orders. Write the function’s code to invoke the Step Functions workflow.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While feasible, this solution demands extra development effort because it involves using a Lambda function as an intermediary to invoke the Step Functions. This is in contrast to using EventBridge Pipes, which provides a more straightforward approach.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-pipes.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-pipes.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon EventBridge Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-eventbridge/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-eventbridge/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-42":{"id":"p:1-42","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 42","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure a lifecycle policy to transition the objects to Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after 30 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure a lifecycle policy to transition the objects to Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after 7 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure a lifecycle policy to transition the objects to Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA) after 30 days\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure a lifecycle policy to transition the objects to Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA) after 7 days\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Standard-IA hỗ trợ truy cập nhanh nhưng đắt hơn One Zone-IA. Đề nói tài sản có thể tạo lại, nên có thể chấp nhận lưu một AZ để giảm chi phí.","Chuyển từ Standard sang Standard-IA bằng Lifecycle sau chỉ 7 ngày không phù hợp điều kiện thời gian chuyển lớp được kiểm tra trong đề.","One Zone-IA phù hợp dữ liệu ít đọc nhưng cần truy cập ngay và có thể tạo lại nếu mất AZ. Lifecycle chuyển sau 30 ngày đáp ứng điều kiện thời gian.","Lớp One Zone-IA phù hợp loại dữ liệu, nhưng mốc chuyển 7 ngày không hợp lệ theo điều kiện Lifecycle từ Standard của đề."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media agency stores its re-creatable assets on Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) buckets. The assets are accessed by a large number of users for the first few days and the frequency of access falls down drastically after a week. Although the assets would be accessed occasionally after the first week, they must continue to be immediately accessible when required. The cost of maintaining all the assets on Amazon S3 storage is turning out to be very expensive and the agency is looking at reducing costs as much as possible.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCan you suggest a way to lower the storage costs while fulfilling the business requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-16":{"id":"p:4-16","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 16","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure the S3 bucket to use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new tax summary documents and expire the objects after 6 years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an S3 Lifecycle rule to transition objects to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval after 10 days and expire the objects after 6 years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an S3 Lifecycle rule to transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 10 days and expire the objects after 6 years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore new tax summary documents in S3 Standard for 10 days, copy the objects to a second S3 bucket that uses S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access, and use an EventBridge scheduled Lambda function to delete both copies after 6 years\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Intelligent-Tiering hữu ích khi khó đoán tần suất truy cập. Trong đề, thời điểm giảm truy cập đã rõ sau 10 ngày, nên lifecycle sang Glacier Instant Retrieval phù hợp hơn với yêu cầu tối ưu chi phí.","Glacier Instant Retrieval dành cho dữ liệu ít đọc nhưng vẫn cần truy cập ngay khi yêu cầu. Lifecycle tự chuyển sau 10 ngày và xóa sau 6 năm, khớp chu kỳ sử dụng và lưu giữ đã nêu.","Glacier Deep Archive cần khôi phục trước khi đọc và không cung cấp truy cập tức thì. Nó không đáp ứng yêu cầu tài liệu luôn sẵn sàng ngay cho khách hàng.","Sao chép sang bucket khác, giữ hai bản và dùng Lambda xóa theo lịch làm tăng công vận hành. One Zone-IA cũng chỉ lưu trong một Availability Zone; lifecycle trực tiếp sang Glacier Instant Retrieval phù hợp hơn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company generates annual tax summary documents for customers and stores the documents in an Amazon S3 bucket. Customers commonly download the documents during the first 10 days after the company publishes them. After that period, customers access the documents rarely. The company must retain the documents for 6 years, and the documents must remain immediately available when customers request them.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements in the MOST cost-effective way?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:7:6":{"id":"e:7:6","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 6","correct":[2],"answers":["AWS Glue Standard","AWS Glue Development Endpoint","AWS Glue Flex","AWS Glue Streaming ETL"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team in a large organization is looking for a cost-effective solution to handle their non-urgent data integration workloads. These workloads include tasks such as testing and one-time data loads, which do not require fast job startup times or consistent runtimes. They are considering a solution that runs on spare compute capacity instead of dedicated hardware, which can lead to variable start and run times.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would be the most suitable for this scenario?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Flex\u003c/strong\u003e is designed for non-urgent data integration workloads that do not require fast job startup times or consistent runtimes. It runs on spare compute capacity instead of dedicated hardware, which can lead to variable start and runtimes but is more cost-effective.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ecdca33bb35099139fb1ff7fadd11e891888c6d71427e56a47bfd3f8a30850f4.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWith AWS Glue Flex, jobs run on spare compute capacity in AWS instead of dedicated hardware. This means that the start and runtimes of jobs using Flex can vary because spare compute resources aren’t always readily available and can be reclaimed during the run of a job. However, this approach is more cost-effective for non-urgent workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eRegardless of the execution option used, AWS Glue jobs have the same capabilities, including access to custom connectors, visual authoring interface, job scheduling, and Glue Auto Scaling. The Flex execution class is available for AWS Glue 3.0 Spark jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Flex.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Standard\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it is designed for time-sensitive tasks that require fast job startup times and consistent runtimes, achieved through the allocation of dedicated resources, making it more expensive and less flexible.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAWS Glue Development Endpoint\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect because it is specifically built for active development and testing of ETL scripts, not for running production workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAWS Glue Streaming ETL\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect because this service is primarily used for streaming ETL jobs, which require real-time processing. It’s not designed for non-urgent workloads that can tolerate variable start and runtimes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-flex-jobs-cost-savings-on-etl-workloads/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-flex-jobs-cost-savings-on-etl-workloads/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:21":{"id":"e:8:21","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 21","correct":[0,1],"answers":["Create a Lambda function that leverages Amazon Redshift API to query the status of each table’s data load in the Redshift cluster. Load the status recording in the Amazon DynamoDB table.","Configure an Amazon EventBridge rule to trigger the Lambda function at a specific time each day.","Enable Redshift\u0027s built-in integration with DynamoDB to automatically sync the table statuses without the need for additional AWS services or scripting.","Set up a direct connection between Redshift and DynamoDB using a VPC peering connection to sync table statuses automatically.","Utilize an EC2 instance to run a cron job that checks the Redshift table statuses and updates the DynamoDB table accordingly."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company uses an Amazon Redshift cluster to store daily sales data. Several tables within this cluster are updated daily with new sales records. The company wants to automate the monitoring process, which verifies whether the tables are successfully updated daily or if any updates are still pending. Additionally, it should be accurately reflected in an Amazon DynamoDB table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options would be the most appropriate way to load the status of Redshift tables automatically? (SELECT TWO)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Lambda is a serverless computing service that runs code in response to events and automatically manages the computing resources required by that code. It is ideal for executing small pieces of code (functions) in response to events. Amazon Redshift is a fully managed, petabyte-scale data warehouse service in the cloud. It allows users to run fast, complex queries against vast amounts of data. Amazon DynamoDB is a key-value and document database that delivers single-digit millisecond performance at any scale, making it a great choice for keeping real-time status updates. Lastly, Amazon EventBridge is a serverless event bus that makes it easy to connect applications together using data from your apps, integrated Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) applications, and AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4c741ad819b076683e89bb2ff25b449a8ba17ef87939ba759aa35b7b1a455dbf.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lambda can interact with Amazon Redshift to check the status of the tables and then update the Amazon DynamoDB table accordingly. This eliminates the need for managing servers or runtime environments, focusing solely on the code that monitors and updates the table statuses. Amazon EventBridge can be used to trigger this Lambda function at regular intervals, ensuring the process is automated and consistent.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e- \u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Lambda function that leverages Amazon Redshift API to query the status of each table’s data load in the Redshift cluster. Load the status recording in the Amazon DynamoDB table.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e- \u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an Amazon EventBridge to trigger the Lambda function at a specific time each day.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Redshift\u0027s built-in integration with DynamoDB to automatically sync the table statuses without the need for additional AWS services or scripting \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because there is no such built-in feature or direct integration that allows Amazon Redshift to automatically sync its table statuses with DynamoDB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up a direct connection between Redshift and DynamoDB using a VPC peering connection to sync table statuses automatically\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While VPC peering can facilitate communication between two services within AWS, it does not inherently provide a method for data synchronization or the automatic updating of table statuses. The syncing of data would still require a custom implementation, such as a script or an application, to handle the logic and data transfer between Redshift and DynamoDB. Thus, simply setting up a VPC peering connection does not address the automation or the logic required to sync the table statuses.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize an EC2 instance to run a cron job that checks the Redshift table statuses and updates the DynamoDB table accordingly\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While you can use EC2 instance to run a cron job, it introduces additional complexity and management overhead. An Amazon EC2 instance would need to be provisioned, configured, and maintained. The cron job would require a script to interact with both Redshift and DynamoDB, necessitating ongoing maintenance and monitoring to ensure its continuous operation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/data-api.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/data-api.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/redshift-lambda-function-queries\"\u003ehttps://repost.aws/knowledge-center/redshift-lambda-function-queries\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Lambda Cheatsheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:33":{"id":"e:7:33","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 33","correct":[0],"answers":["Detach the security group from all associated VPC resources and delete any security group rules within the VPC that reference the security group.","Reconfigure the VPC to utilize a new security group for DataBrew connectivity before deleting the old one.","Disable deletion protection on the security group in AWS Security Hub to allow its removal.","Delete the Elastic Network Interface (ENI) associated with AWS Glue DataBrew before removing the security group."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is moving its AWS Glue DataBrew project to a new AWS account managed by a new data engineering team. This requires stopping DataBrew activities in the existing account and removing certain configurations that enabled Glue DataBrew to connect to the company’s Amazon RDS databases, EC2 instances, and other VPC resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe new data engineering team is responsible for cutting off the previous DataBrew connection. Moreover, the team must thoroughly understand the appropriate procedures to ensure a seamless and secure transition.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following should the data engineering team implement to satisfy the given requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSecurity groups\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eare crucial to Amazon Web Services (AWS) network security, especially for resources deployed in a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC). It acts as a virtual firewall for instances, allowing control of inbound and outbound traffic at the instance level. Unlike network access control lists (ACLs), which operate at the subnet level, security groups are directly associated with individual resources such as Amazon EC2 instances and AWS Glue DataBrew services that work within a VPC.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA security group\u0027s rules are responsible for the traffic allowed to and from resources associated with it. When a new instance or service is launched within a VPC, it can be related to one or more security groups, which then govern traffic flow to and from the resource. Managing these security groups effectively is crucial for maintaining the security and integrity of the resources within a VPC.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/404cd30bfe98dec4655f583d729e67bf70c50665fff13980f6975d7237dd5135.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor the scenario described, the task is removing a security group associated explicitly with AWS Glue DataBrew operations as part of a migration to a new AWS account. This process involves a few critical steps to ensure the removal does not disrupt existing operations or compromise security. First, detaching the security group from any resources within the VPC is necessary. This step ensures that no active resources are left unprotected or inadvertently exposed due to the security group\u0027s removal. Following this, it\u0027s also essential to delete any rules within the security group that reference other security groups or resources within the VPC, ensuring a clean and orderly removal process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBy integrating AWS Glue DataBrew with a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC), you can securely access and process data from VPC\u0027s protected environment. This requires configuring security groups and VPC endpoints to control traffic flow and facilitate private connections. Proper network configuration is essential for maintaining secure and efficient workflows using DataBrew\u0027s visual interface for data cleaning and normalization tasks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDetach the security group from all associated VPC resources and delete any security group rules within the VPC that reference the security group.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReconfigure the VPC to utilize a new security group for DataBrew connectivity before deleting the old one\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because reconfiguring to a new security group does not fulfill the requirement to properly detach and manage dependencies of the existing security group before its deletion.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDisable deletion protection on the security group in AWS Security Hub to allow its removal\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Deletion protection is a feature that applies only to AWS resources, such as EC2 instances and RDS databases, to prevent accidental deletion. Security groups, in general, don\u0027t have deletion protection and can be deleted once they are no longer associated with any resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDelete the Elastic Network Interface (ENI) associated with AWS Glue DataBrew before removing the security group\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Deleting an ENI is not enough to remove the associated security group. Additionally, deleting an ENI could disrupt network connectivity for services that rely on it, resulting in unintended consequences beyond the scope of securely managing security groups.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/what-is-amazon-vpc.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/what-is-amazon-vpc.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/databrew-with-vpc.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/databrew-with-vpc.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/working-with-security-groups.html#deleting-security-group\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/working-with-security-groups.html#deleting-security-group\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon VPC Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-vpc/\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-vpc/\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-17":{"id":"p:2-17","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 17","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse S3 bucket ACLs to restrict access to the analytics files, configure SSE-KMS encryption for the bucket, and store the database credentials in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store with rotation performed by a scheduled Lambda function\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon CloudFront signed URLs to restrict access to the S3 objects, configure SSE-KMS with a customer managed KMS key, store database credentials in AWS Secrets Manager, and require the application team to rotate the secret manually after each maintenance window\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse IAM policies to restrict access to the S3 data, configure SSE-KMS with the AWS managed key for Amazon S3, and store the database credentials in AWS Secrets Manager with automatic rotation by a Lambda rotation function\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse IAM policies to restrict access to the S3 data, configure SSE-S3 encryption for the bucket, and store the database credentials in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store with a scheduled Lambda function that rotates the credentials\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["ACL không phải cách quản trị quyền phù hợp nhất ở đây. Parameter Store với Lambda tự viết để rotation thêm công so với quy trình Secrets Manager.","Customer managed key không khớp yêu cầu AWS managed key; xoay secret thủ công cũng trái điều kiện tự động.","IAM giới hạn quyền S3; SSE-KMS dùng khóa AWS managed cho S3; Secrets Manager với Lambda rotation đổi thông tin đăng nhập tự động. Đáp ứng đủ ba phần yêu cầu.","SSE-S3 dùng khóa do S3 quản lý, khác loại AWS managed KMS key mà lời giải yêu cầu. Rotation tự xây quanh Parameter Store cũng thêm quản lý."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare technology company stores protected analytics files in an Amazon S3 bucket in the eu-central-1 Region. The company must restrict access to the S3 objects and encrypt the objects by using AWS managed keys. A serverless application uses an AWS Lambda function to connect to a reporting database, and the company must store and rotate the database credentials automatically.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-25":{"id":"p:4-25","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 25","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Glue ETL job that reads the Parquet file, writes custom PySpark code to calculate column-level statistics, and stores the profiling output in Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIngest the dataset into Amazon Redshift Spectrum and run SQL queries to calculate profile statistics for the Parquet file\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Glue DataBrew profile job for the dataset and review the generated profile results\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStream the dataset into Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink, run SQL queries against the stream, and use the Apache Flink dashboard to profile the data\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["PySpark có thể tính thống kê, nhưng bạn phải viết và duy trì logic cho một nhu cầu profiling tiêu chuẩn. DataBrew đã có profile job chuyên dụng.","Spectrum đọc dữ liệu S3 tại chỗ, không phải bước ingest như mô tả. Tự viết nhiều truy vấn thống kê cũng tốn công hơn chạy profile job có sẵn.","DataBrew profile job tự tạo thống kê cột, số giá trị thiếu và phân bố dữ liệu. Đây là cách trực tiếp để hiểu tập Parquet trước khi thiết kế bước làm sạch.","Flink dành cho xử lý luồng. Chuyển một tệp Parquet tĩnh thành stream và dùng dashboard vận hành Flink không phải cách profiling dataset đơn giản."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company stores a 150 MB patient appointment dataset in an Amazon S3 bucket as an Apache Parquet file. A data engineer needs to profile the dataset to understand column statistics, missing values, and data distributions before creating data preparation steps.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement in the MOST operationally efficient way?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:60":{"id":"e:5:60","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 60","correct":[1],"answers":["AWS Glue","Amazon Elastic MapReduce (Amazon EMR)","Amazon Athena","Amazon Redshift"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company plans to process large amounts of data stored in Amazon S3. The data is in various formats, such as CSV, JSON, and Parquet. The company needs a solution that can handle the complexity of processing vast amounts of data and provide the flexibility to use popular distributed frameworks such as Apache Spark, Hadoop, Presto, and Flink.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat service would be best suited to fulfill the requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAmazon EMR is a managed cluster platform that simplifies running big data frameworks, such as\u0026nbsp;Apache Hadoop\u0026nbsp;and\u0026nbsp;Apache Spark, on AWS to process and analyze vast amounts of data. Using these frameworks and related open-source projects, you can process data for analytics purposes and business intelligence workloads. Amazon EMR also lets you transform and move large amounts of data into and out of other AWS data stores and databases, such as Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) and Amazon DynamoDB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d06c827b457c4292069360a044e72b0bd6557acb7c8575a454f77414bafb12aa.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cspan\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Elastic MapReduce (Amazon EMR)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is a fast, fully managed, petabyte-scale data warehouse service that makes it simple and cost-effective to analyze all your data using your existing business intelligence tools. However, it is not designed to process large amounts of data stored in various formats such as CSV, JSON, and Parquet, nor does it provide the flexibility to use popular distributed frameworks such as Apache Spark, Hadoop, Presto, and Flink.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service that makes it easy for users to prepare and load their data for analytics. While AWS Glue can technically run Spark jobs and process data in various formats, it’s less suitable for big data processing than Amazon EMR, which provides built-in support for popular distributed frameworks like Apache Spark, Hadoop, Presto, and Flink.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAmazon Athena\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is simply an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. It’s not designed for big data processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/emr/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-what-is-emr.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-what-is-emr.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon EMR Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-12":{"id":"p:1-12","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 12","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eRun an Amazon Macie sensitive data discovery job to identify PII and configure Macie to redact matching values from the CloudWatch Logs log group\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Lambda subscription filter on the CloudWatch Logs log group to read every log event, detect PII with custom regular expressions, replace the sensitive values, and write the sanitized events to a separate log group\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCall AWS Glue sensitive data detection APIs from the ETL job and use the detected entities to mask values before writing log entries\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a CloudWatch Logs data protection policy that uses managed data identifiers for PII and attach the policy to the CloudWatch Logs log group\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Macie phát hiện dữ liệu nhạy cảm trong S3. Nó không gắn vào CloudWatch Logs để trực tiếp che PII trong log group.","Lambda có thể tạo bản log đã làm sạch ở nơi khác, nhưng cần mã phát hiện PII và không che trực tiếp log gốc. Đề muốn xử lý ngay tại log group mà không sửa ứng dụng.","Gọi API phát hiện dữ liệu nhạy cảm bên trong ETL đòi hỏi sửa mã tác vụ, trái yêu cầu. Đây cũng không phải chính sách tập trung tại CloudWatch Logs.","Gắn data protection policy vào log group và dùng managed data identifiers để nhận diện PII. CloudWatch Logs hỗ trợ che dữ liệu khi hiển thị theo quyền, không cần sửa mã ETL."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company runs an AWS Glue ETL job in the ap-southeast-2 Region to process patient appointment records. The job writes operational messages and error details to an Amazon CloudWatch Logs log group. Some log entries can contain personally identifiable information (PII), and a data engineer must mask the PII in the log group without modifying the ETL application code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:8:15":{"id":"e:8:15","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 15","correct":[3,4],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eUse S3 Event Notifications to trigger the Lambda function when the \u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectTagging:*\u003c/code\u003e event type occurs. Specify the Lambda function as the destination for publishing the event.\u003c/span\u003e","Configure S3 Event Notifications to send a message to an Amazon SQS queue when a new CSV file is uploaded. Then, configure the Lambda function to be directly triggered by this SQS queue.","\u003cspan\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge to trigger the Lambda function when the \u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated:*\u003c/code\u003e event type occurs. Implement a code in the function that filters out files not ending with \u003ccode\u003e.csv\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eUse S3 Event Notifications to trigger the Lambda function when the \u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated:*\u003c/code\u003e event type occurs. Specify the Lambda function as the destination for publishing the event.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eCreate an S3 Event Notifications with a filter rule to send notifications when the uploaded file ends with \u003ccode\u003e.csv\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA data engineering team must perform data transformation of CSV files uploaded to an Amazon S3 bucket. An AWS Lambda function is ready to process these files. The team wants to invoke the Lambda function automatically whenever a new CSV file is uploaded to the S3 bucket.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich action would meet the requirements in the LEAST amount of development effort? (Select TWO.)\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eThe Amazon S3 Event Notifications functionality allows you to be alerted when specific occurrences take place in your S3 bucket. By setting up a notification configuration, you can specify which events should trigger Amazon S3 to send out notifications.\u0026nbsp;Additionally, you need to designate where these notifications should be delivered.\u0026nbsp;This configuration is stored in the\u0026nbsp;\u003cem\u003enotification\u003c/em\u003e\u0026nbsp;subresource that\u0027s associated with a bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2cf83ab5bc2b2c2482bdae7173cda36507af22e0d11146f6c066c54c209e5452.png\" alt=\"Amazon S3 Event notification\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated:*\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;event type includes all object creation events, such as uploading a new CSV file. When this event occurs, the S3 Event Notification feature triggers the specified AWS Lambda function. The Lambda function can then process the CSV file as needed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNotifications can be configured to trigger based on the prefix and suffix of an object\u0027s key name. For instance, you can set up a rule to receive notifications only when image files with a \".jpg\" extension are added to a bucket. Alternatively, you can configure notifications to be sent to an Amazon SNS topic when objects with the prefix \"images/\" are added, while directing notifications for objects prefixed with \"logs/\" to an AWS Lambda function within the same bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e-\u003c/strong\u003e \u003cstrong\u003eUse S3 Event Notifications to trigger the Lambda function when the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated:*\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;event type occurs. Specify the Lambda function as the destination for publishing the event.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eThis is the most straightforward solution and requires minimal development effort. Configuring Amazon S3 Event Notifications with the \u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated:*\u003c/code\u003e event type directly triggers the Lambda function whenever an object is uploaded to the S3 bucket. There’s no need to build complex filtering logic, as the event will be published directly to Lambda, eliminating any additional overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Create an S3 Event Notifications with a filter rule to send notifications when the uploaded file ends with \u003ccode\u003e.csv.\u0026nbsp;\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eThis option adds a filter to ensure that only CSV files trigger the notification. This solution directly meets the requirement of triggering the Lambda function for CSV files, without the need for additional filtering logic in the Lambda function.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e Use S3 Event Notifications to trigger the Lambda function when the \u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectTagging:*\u003c/code\u003e event type occurs. Specify the Lambda function as the destination for publishing the event\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectTagging:*\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;event type is primarily triggered when an object’s tags are created, deleted, or updated, not when a new object is created.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure S3 Event Notifications to send a message to an Amazon SQS queue when a new CSV file is uploaded. Then, configure the Lambda function to be directly triggered by this SQS queue\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because while this approach would work, it typically involves more development effort than necessary because it introduces an additional component (SQS) into the architecture.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge to trigger the Lambda function when the \u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated:*\u003c/code\u003e event type occurs. Implement a code in the function that filters out files not ending with \u003ccode\u003e.csv\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis\u0026nbsp;incorrect.\u0026nbsp;This adds development effort because you need to manually code and maintain the filtering logic for \u003ccode\u003e.csv\u003c/code\u003e files, whereas S3 Event Notifications can be configured to filter events without code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-s3-example.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-s3-example.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventNotifications.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventNotifications.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-filtering.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-filtering.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon S3 Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:31":{"id":"e:2:31","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 31","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure an Application Load Balancer (ALB) to balance the workload for all the Amazon EC2 instances. Configure Amazon CloudFront to distribute from an Application Load Balancer since AWS WAF cannot be directly configured on ALB. This configuration not only provides necessary safety but is scalable too\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate Amazon CloudFront distribution for the application on Amazon EC2 instances. Deploy AWS WAF on Amazon CloudFront to provide the necessary safety measures\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS WAF can be directly configured on Amazon EC2 instances for ensuring the security of the underlying application data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS WAF can be directly configured only on an Application Load Balancer or an Amazon API Gateway. One of these two services can then be configured with Amazon EC2 to build the needed secure architecture\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn application hosted on Amazon EC2 contains sensitive personal information about all its customers and needs to be protected from all types of cyber-attacks. The company is considering using the AWS Web Application Firewall (AWS WAF) to handle this requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCan you identify the correct solution leveraging the capabilities of AWS WAF?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate Amazon CloudFront distribution for the application on Amazon EC2 instances. Deploy AWS WAF on Amazon CloudFront to provide the necessary safety measures\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you use AWS WAF with Amazon CloudFront, you can protect your applications running on any HTTP web server, whether it\u0027s a webserver that\u0027s running in Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) or a web server that you manage privately. You can also configure Amazon CloudFront to require HTTPS between CloudFront and your own web server, as well as between viewers and Amazon CloudFront.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS WAF is tightly integrated with Amazon CloudFront and the Application Load Balancer (ALB), services that AWS customers commonly use to deliver content for their websites and applications. When you use AWS WAF on Amazon CloudFront, your rules run in all AWS Edge Locations, located around the world close to your end-users. This means security doesn’t come at the expense of performance. Blocked requests are stopped before they reach your web servers. When you use AWS WAF on Application Load Balancer, your rules run in the region and can be used to protect internet-facing as well as internal load balancers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an Application Load Balancer (ALB) to balance the workload for all the Amazon EC2 instances. Configure Amazon CloudFront to distribute from an Application Load Balancer since AWS WAF cannot be directly configured on ALB. This configuration not only provides necessary safety but is scalable too\u003c/strong\u003e - This statement is wrong. You can configure AWS WAF on Application Load Balancers (ALB).\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS WAF can be directly configured on Amazon EC2 instances for ensuring the security of the underlying application data\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS WAF can be deployed on Amazon CloudFront, the Application Load Balancer (ALB), and Amazon API Gateway. It cannot be configured directly on an Amazon EC2 instance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS WAF can be directly configured only on an Application Load Balancer or an Amazon API Gateway. One of these two services can then be configured with Amazon EC2 to build the needed secure architecture\u003c/strong\u003e - This statement is only partially correct. AWS WAF can also be deployed on Amazon CloudFront service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/waf/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/waf/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/cloudfront-features.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/cloudfront-features.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-19":{"id":"p:2-19","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 19","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Comprehend to identify the essay language and topics, use Amazon Kendra to search course materials, and use an AWS Lambda function to assemble feedback with references\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eHost a custom fine-tuned large language model on Amazon SageMaker AI, train the model on the institute\u0027s course books, and generate feedback with section references\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon OpenSearch Service domain for keyword search across course materials, run Amazon Comprehend entity detection on each essay, pass matching textbook paragraphs to an Amazon Bedrock model, and use custom prompt templates to generate multilingual feedback and manually formatted citations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Base from the institute\u0027s course materials and configure a multilingual foundation model to generate feedback with source citations\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Comprehend phân tích ngôn ngữ và Kendra tìm tài liệu, nhưng vẫn phải tự xây tạo phản hồi, trích dẫn và điều phối bằng Lambda.","Fine-tune và host mô hình riêng tốn công huấn luyện, vận hành; cũng không tự đảm bảo trích dẫn chính xác tới đoạn tài liệu nguồn.","Thiết kế này có thể tạo RAG nhưng phải tự nối tìm kiếm, entity detection, prompt và trích dẫn. Knowledge Bases cung cấp quy trình được quản lý hơn.","Bedrock Knowledge Bases truy xuất tài liệu khóa học để làm căn cứ cho phản hồi và cung cấp trích dẫn nguồn. Kết hợp foundation model đa ngôn ngữ phù hợp ngôn ngữ người học."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA language learning institute is building an AI tutoring application that reviews student writing assignments. The application must provide individualized feedback that is grounded in the institute\u0027s approved course books and study guides. The application must support submissions in several languages, return feedback in the learner\u0027s selected language, and include citations that point to the relevant sections of the course materials.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-32":{"id":"p:2-32","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 32","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eGrant all users read-only permissions to the non-sensitive columns. Add the finance team to the administrator group so they have complete access to the table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eGrant the finance team (defined as a group) permission to read from the table. Create a second table having data only for columns with non-sensitive data. Grant read-only permissions to the second table for the rest of the users\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eGrant the finance team (defined as a group) permission to read from the table. Use the GRANT SQL command to allow read-only access to a subset of columns having non-sensitive data to the other users\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eGrant the finance group permission to read from the table. Create a view of the new table with only those columns having non-sensitive data. Grant the other users read-only permissions to this view\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Đội tài chính chỉ cần quyền đọc dữ liệu nhạy cảm, không cần quyền quản trị. Cấp admin vi phạm nguyên tắc quyền tối thiểu.","Tạo bảng thứ hai cho hàng tỷ dòng làm nhân bản dữ liệu và phát sinh đồng bộ. Quyền cấp cột trên bảng gốc tiết kiệm hơn.","Cấp đội tài chính quyền đọc bảng; dùng GRANT SELECT trên các cột không nhạy cảm cho nhóm khác. Đáp ứng phân quyền mà không sao chép dữ liệu.","View có thể giới hạn cột trong một số thiết kế, nhưng cần thêm đối tượng để quản lý. Quyền cấp cột trực tiếp là phương án bộ đề chọn với ít công hơn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA business is moving their data to Amazon Redshift. A core table with billions of rows needs to be moved to Redshift. This table contains certain columns that have sensitive data that can only be accessed by the finance team. Once the data is moved to Redshift, queries will be run on this table by multiple teams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow will you configure a solution for this requirement such that the columns holding sensitive data are only accessible to members of the finance team?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-22":{"id":"p:3-22","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 22","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSwap out Kinesis Data Streams with SQS Standard queues to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSwap out Kinesis Data Streams with SQS FIFO queues to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Enhanced Fanout feature of Kinesis Data Streams to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSwap out Kinesis Data Streams with Kinesis Data Firehose to support the desired read throughput for the downstream applications\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["SQS Standard phân phối công việc cho consumer, không thay thế luồng để mỗi ứng dụng độc lập đọc cùng dữ liệu và phát lại.","SQS FIFO bảo đảm thứ tự trong message group, nhưng vẫn là hàng đợi công việc, không xử lý vấn đề băng thông đọc chia sẻ của các consumer Kinesis.","Enhanced fan-out cấp thông lượng đọc riêng cho consumer đã đăng ký theo shard. Giảm việc các ứng dụng tranh nhau năng lực đọc dùng chung.","Firehose là đường giao dữ liệu tới đích, không cho nhiều ứng dụng tự đọc stream như Kinesis Data Streams."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has developed an end-to-end AWS cloud-based Internet-of-Things (IoT) solution that provides customers with integrated IoT functionality in devices including baby monitors, security cameras, and entertainment systems. The company is using Kinesis Data Streams (KDS) to process IoT data from these devices. Multiple consumer applications are using the incoming data streams and the data engineers have noticed a performance lag in the data delivery speed between producers and consumers of the data streams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you suggest to improve the performance for the given use case?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-55":{"id":"p:3-55","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 55","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the images using the Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the images using the Amazon S3 Standard-IA storage class\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a data monitoring application on an Amazon EC2 instance in the same region as the bucket storing the images. The application is invoked daily via Amazon CloudWatch and it changes the storage class of infrequently accessed objects to Amazon S3 One Zone-IA and the frequently accessed objects are migrated to Amazon S3 Standard class\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a data monitoring application on an Amazon EC2 instance in the same region as the bucket storing the images. The application is invoked daily via Amazon CloudWatch and it changes the storage class of infrequently accessed objects to Amazon S3 Standard-IA and the frequently accessed objects are migrated to Amazon S3 Standard class\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Intelligent-Tiering tự chuyển tầng theo tần suất truy cập, phù hợp bộ ảnh có mẫu truy cập không đồng nhất mà giảm việc quản lý thủ công.","Đưa mọi ảnh vào Standard-IA khiến ảnh thường xuyên đọc phát sinh phí truy xuất. Không tối ưu cho tập dữ liệu có cả ảnh nóng và lạnh.","Tự xây ứng dụng EC2 quản lý lớp lưu trữ thêm công; One Zone-IA còn đánh đổi dự phòng đa AZ. Intelligent-Tiering làm tự động hơn.","EC2 và mã theo dõi/chuyển lớp vẫn tạo chi phí vận hành. Dịch vụ Intelligent-Tiering đã cung cấp cơ chế tương ứng."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team at a social media company has noticed that while some of the images stored in Amazon S3 are frequently accessed, others sit idle for a considerable time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is your recommendation to build the MOST cost-effective solution?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:59":{"id":"e:1:59","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 59","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eMigrate away from DynamoDB\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Local Secondary Index (LSI)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a Global Secondary Index (GSI)\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate DynamoDB Streams\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eYou are a data engineer at an IT company that recently moved its production application to AWS and migrated data from PostgreSQL to AWS DynamoDB. You are adding new tables to AWS DynamoDB and need to allow your application to query your data by the primary key and an alternate key. This option must be added at the outset when you are first creating tables, otherwise, changes cannot be done once the table is created.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following actions would you suggest?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Local Secondary Index (LSI)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSome applications only need to query data using the base table\u0027s primary key; however, there may be situations where an alternate sort key would be helpful. To give your application a choice of sort keys, you can create one or more local secondary indexes on a table and issue Query or Scan requests against these indexes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eLocal secondary indexes are created at the same time that you create a table. You cannot add a local secondary index to an existing table, nor can you delete any local secondary indexes that currently exist.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDifferences between GSI and LSI:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/fa187c7297f2ad0cdde0645710d2d4a30a629e479e0a84e00dbbee9333fb4001.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/fa187c7297f2ad0cdde0645710d2d4a30a629e479e0a84e00dbbee9333fb4001.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/SecondaryIndexes.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/SecondaryIndexes.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate DynamoDB Streams\u003c/strong\u003e - DynamoDB Streams captures a time-ordered sequence of item-level modifications in any DynamoDB table and stores this information in a log for up to 24 hours. Applications can access this log and view the data items as they appeared before and after they were modified, in near real-time. This option is not relevant to the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a Global Secondary Index (GSI)\u003c/strong\u003e - GSI is an index with a partition key and a sort key that can be different from those on the base table. Global secondary indexes can be created at the same time that you create a table. You can also add a new global secondary index to an existing table, or delete an existing global secondary index.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMigrate away from DynamoDB\u003c/strong\u003e - Migrating to another database that is not NoSQL may cause you to make changes that require substantial code development.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/SecondaryIndexes.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/SecondaryIndexes.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-13":{"id":"p:3-13","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 13","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eOpt for ALL distribution style for large tables. Declare primary and foreign keys for all tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFor the rarely updated small tables, opt for ALL distribution style. Declare primary and foreign keys for all tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpdate the distribution style for all tables to AUTO. Declare primary and foreign keys for all tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSwitch to Amazon Redshift Spectrum to efficiently query and retrieve data from the tables\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["ALL nhân bản bảng trên các node, rất tốn lưu trữ và cập nhật khi bảng lớn hàng trăm GB.","Dùng ALL cho bảng nhỏ ít thay đổi giúp JOIN tại chỗ. Khai báo khóa chính/ngoại đúng dữ liệu giúp optimizer; cần tự bảo đảm tính hợp lệ vì các ràng buộc này không được Redshift cưỡng chế như OLTP.","AUTO hữu ích trong nhiều trường hợp, nhưng bộ đề chọn tối ưu rõ ràng cho bảng nhỏ ít đổi bằng ALL. Không thể suy rằng AUTO luôn kém hơn.","Spectrum truy vấn dữ liệu ngoài trên S3, không tự tối ưu phân phối các bảng đang nằm trong cụm Redshift."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company\u0027s data engineer is tasked with enhancing the performance of SQL table queries on data stored in an Amazon Redshift cluster. Due to budget constraints, expanding the cluster size is not an option. The company utilizes the EVEN distribution style for loading data across multiple tables, with some tables containing hundreds of GB of data while others have less than 20 MB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution would address these needs effectively?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:9:6":{"id":"e:9:6","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 9 Â· CÃ¢u 6","correct":[2],"answers":["Implement Amazon Athena for querying and analyzing data within the Amazon S3 data lake.","Use Amazon Redshift to load and transform data. Run validation queries to check for format correctness and duplication.","Use AWS Glue Data Quality to automatically monitor and validate data quality within the ETL pipeline. Set up validation rules and metrics in AWS Glue to check for format correctness, duplication, and integrity.","Create data processing workflows in Amazon EMR to process and validate data quality using custom scripts."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team is consolidating patient records into an Amazon S3 data lake. Some of the records were handwritten and digitized through Optical Character Recognition (OCR). The team wants to ensure the quality of data before integrating it into their ETL pipeline. Important checks such as data format validation and detection of typographical errors must be done.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich AWS service will provide the least development overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Data Quality, as a component of AWS Glue, automates the identification of data quality issues. It scans the data for common problems such as inconsistencies, missing values, or format errors. This feature is crucial in healthcare scenarios where data must be accurate and consistent. For example, in the case of patient records, AWS Glue Data Quality can automatically detect irregularities like mismatched date formats or incomplete patient entries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/72cee905926b62c89aa284e97972b86bddbae6a9c350f14f499dc1e5a01807f6.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis service also recommends data quality rules based on its initial analysis. These rules are directly applicable for data validation, ensuring adherence to required quality standards. In the context of an ETL pipeline, it can provide real-time validation and monitoring of data as it is ingested and transformed. This integration is particularly beneficial in maintaining the integrity of data in an S3 data lake. For instance, as patient data is centralized, AWS Glue Data Quality can continuously monitor and validate the data for accuracy, flagging any discrepancies for review or correction, thereby upholding the data’s reliability and compliance with healthcare standards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eGiven these features, the correct answer is to \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Quality to automatically monitor and validate data quality within the ETL pipeline. Set up validation rules and metrics in AWS Glue to check for format correctness, duplication, and integrity.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cstrong\u003e \u003cspan\u003eUse Amazon Redshift to load and transform data. Run validation queries to check for format correctness and duplication\u003c/span\u003e \u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because Amazon Redshift is primarily used as a data warehouse and is not specifically designed for automated data quality checks. It would require significant development effort to build custom validation queries and processes, leading to higher development overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eImplement Amazon Athena for querying and analyzing data within the Amazon S3 data lake\u003c/span\u003e \u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because Amazon Athena is a query service that makes it easy to analyze data in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. While Athena can be typically used to run ad-hoc queries for integrity checks and to find duplicate records, it is not primarily a data quality service. Using Athena for continuous data quality monitoring would require significant manual effort and does not provide the automated, comprehensive data quality checks that a service like AWS Glue Data Quality offers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCreate data processing workflows in Amazon EMR to process and validate data quality with custom scripts\u003c/span\u003e \u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because Amazon EMR (Elastic MapReduce) is a cloud-native big data platform, used for processing large datasets using popular distributed frameworks such as Apache Spark and Hadoop. While it is powerful for data processing tasks, using EMR for data quality checks would be overkill and require significant development effort. EMR is more suited for complex data processing tasks rather than automated data quality monitoring and validation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out the Tutorials Dojo Cheat Sheets here:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:24":{"id":"e:2:24","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 24","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse IAM policies to restrict access to the S3 data, configure SSE-KMS with the AWS managed key for Amazon S3, and store the database credentials in AWS Secrets Manager with automatic rotation by a Lambda rotation function\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse S3 bucket ACLs to restrict access to the analytics files, configure SSE-KMS encryption for the bucket, and store the database credentials in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store with rotation performed by a scheduled Lambda function\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon CloudFront signed URLs to restrict access to the S3 objects, configure SSE-KMS with a customer managed KMS key, store database credentials in AWS Secrets Manager, and require the application team to rotate the secret manually after each maintenance window\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse IAM policies to restrict access to the S3 data, configure SSE-S3 encryption for the bucket, and store the database credentials in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store with a scheduled Lambda function that rotates the credentials\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare technology company stores protected analytics files in an Amazon S3 bucket in the eu-central-1 Region. The company must restrict access to the S3 objects and encrypt the objects by using AWS managed keys. A serverless application uses an AWS Lambda function to connect to a reporting database, and the company must store and rotate the database credentials automatically.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse IAM policies to restrict access to the S3 data, configure SSE-KMS with the AWS managed key for Amazon S3, and store the database credentials in AWS Secrets Manager with automatic rotation by a Lambda rotation function\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIAM policies can restrict which principals can access Amazon S3 buckets and objects. The company can grant the Lambda function and authorized users only the required S3 actions on the required bucket or object prefixes, which supports least privilege access to the protected analytics files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSSE-KMS can encrypt Amazon S3 objects by using AWS KMS keys. When Amazon S3 uses SSE-KMS and the request or bucket configuration does not specify a KMS key ID, Amazon S3 uses the AWS managed key for Amazon S3, which is \u003ccode\u003eaws/s3\u003c/code\u003e. This satisfies the requirement to encrypt the data by using AWS managed keys while still using AWS KMS integration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Secrets Manager is the correct service for storing and automatically rotating database credentials. Secrets Manager can invoke a Lambda rotation function according to a configured rotation schedule. The application Lambda function can retrieve the current credential from Secrets Manager at runtime instead of storing static credentials in configuration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse S3 bucket ACLs to restrict access to the analytics files, configure SSE-KMS encryption for the bucket, and store the database credentials in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store with rotation performed by a scheduled Lambda function\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 ACLs are a legacy access control mechanism and are not the preferred way to manage access for modern S3 security designs. AWS recommends using IAM policies and bucket policies for most access control requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Systems Manager Parameter Store can store secure string values, but it does not provide the same managed database credential rotation workflow as Secrets Manager. A custom scheduled Lambda rotation process adds operational work and is less suitable than Secrets Manager automatic rotation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon CloudFront signed URLs to restrict access to the S3 objects, configure SSE-KMS with a customer managed KMS key, store database credentials in AWS Secrets Manager, and require the application team to rotate the secret manually after each maintenance window\u003c/strong\u003e - CloudFront signed URLs can restrict access to content delivered through CloudFront. However, this requirement concerns S3 data access controls and database credentials for a Lambda function, not content distribution through CloudFront.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also uses a customer managed KMS key instead of an AWS managed key and requires manual secret rotation. Manual rotation does not meet the requirement to rotate the database credentials automatically.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse IAM policies to restrict access to the S3 data, configure SSE-S3 encryption for the bucket, and store the database credentials in AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store with a scheduled Lambda function that rotates the credentials\u003c/strong\u003e - IAM policies can restrict access to Amazon S3 data, and SSE-S3 provides server-side encryption with Amazon S3 managed keys. However, this option stores credentials in Systems Manager Parameter Store and uses a scheduled Lambda function to rotate them.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThat rotation model requires the company to build, test, monitor, and maintain custom rotation logic. Secrets Manager provides a purpose-built managed workflow for automatic secret rotation with Lambda rotation functions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/security-iam.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/security-iam.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/specifying-kms-encryption.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/specifying-kms-encryption.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/bucket-encryption.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/bucket-encryption.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_lambda.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_lambda.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_turn-on-for-db.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/rotate-secrets_turn-on-for-db.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/rotate-amazon-rds-database-credentials-automatically-with-aws-secrets-manager/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/rotate-amazon-rds-database-credentials-automatically-with-aws-secrets-manager/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:7":{"id":"e:2:7","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 7","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Config to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon CloudWatch to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Systems Manager to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS CloudTrail to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company has recently migrated from on-premises infrastructure to AWS Cloud. The data engineering team wants to implement a solution that allows all resource configurations to be reviewed and make sure that they meet compliance guidelines. Also, the solution should be able to offer the capability to look into the resource configuration history across the application stack.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions would you recommend to the team?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Config to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Config is a service that enables you to assess, audit, and evaluate the configurations of your AWS resources. With Config, you can review changes in configurations and relationships between AWS resources, dive into detailed resource configuration histories, and determine your overall compliance against the configurations specified in your internal guidelines. You can use Config to answer questions such as - “What did my AWS resource look like at xyz point in time?”\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow AWS Config Works:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/44f6c566f85b623c587a8bc0acc92a8a1385a1dcd860bb7f90ec76712da22519.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/44f6c566f85b623c587a8bc0acc92a8a1385a1dcd860bb7f90ec76712da22519.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/config/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/config/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon CloudWatch to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS CloudWatch provides you with data and actionable insights to monitor your applications, respond to system-wide performance changes, optimize resource utilization, and get a unified view of operational health. You cannot use Amazon CloudWatch to maintain a history of resource configuration changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS CloudTrail to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes\u003c/strong\u003e - With AWS CloudTrail, you can log, continuously monitor and retain account activity related to actions across your AWS infrastructure. You can use AWS CloudTrail to answer questions such as - “Who made an API call to modify this resource?”. AWS CloudTrail provides an event history of your AWS account activity thereby enabling governance, compliance, operational auditing, and risk auditing of your AWS account. You cannot use AWS CloudTrail to maintain a history of resource configuration changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Systems Manager to review resource configurations to meet compliance guidelines and maintain a history of resource configuration changes\u003c/strong\u003e - Using AWS Systems Manager, you can group resources, like Amazon EC2 instances, Amazon S3 buckets, or Amazon RDS instances, by application, view operational data for monitoring and troubleshooting, and take action on your groups of resources. You cannot use AWS Systems Manager to maintain a history of resource configuration changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eExam Alert:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou may see scenario-based questions asking you to select one of Amazon CloudWatch vs AWS CloudTrail vs AWS Config. Just remember the following -\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThink resource performance monitoring, events, and alerts; think Amazon CloudWatch.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThink account-specific activity and audit; think AWS CloudTrail.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThink resource-specific history, audit, and compliance; think AWS Config.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/config/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/config/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/cloudtrail/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/cloudtrail/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:16":{"id":"e:7:16","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 16","correct":[3],"answers":["Migrate to Amazon EMR and centralize data storage on Amazon S3. Utilize persistent EMR clusters for job execution.","Transition to Amazon EMR with a complete data migration to Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS). Expand the cluster with additional core nodes as storage demands increase.","Move into Amazon EMR. Configure to store the active data within HDFS and archival data in Amazon S3. Utilize Apache Hive for table integration across S3 and HDFS.","Transition the on-premises Apache Hadoop cluster to Amazon EMR. Centralize the data storage on Amazon S3 and initiate transient EMR clusters for task execution."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company runs an on-premises data processing system that uses an Apache Hadoop cluster to execute various batch processing jobs written in several open-source frameworks. These frameworks include Apache Spark, Apache MXNet, Apache HBase, Apache Hudi, Presto, et cetera. Due to an anticipated surge in data storage needs, the corporation is considering migrating to AWS to leverage enhanced storage scalability and durability while continuing to utilize its existing job scripts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will effectively fulfill these criteria at the LOWEST cost?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon EMR\u003c/strong\u003e is a managed service that allows you to process big data workloads using popular frameworks such as Apache Hadoop, Spark, HBase, and others. By migrating your on-premises Apache Hadoop cluster to Amazon EMR and storing your data centrally on Amazon S3, you can take advantage of AWS services\u0027 scalability, durability, and cost-effectiveness. Amazon S3 is a highly durable and scalable storage service that provides a cost-effective solution for storing large amounts of data. Utilizing Amazon EMR allows you to execute existing job scripts with minimal modifications while leveraging the managed scaling and cluster management features. Additionally, Amazon EMR supports transient clusters that can be created on demand for specific jobs and terminated once the job is completed. This feature can offer significant cost savings over persistent clusters that run continuously.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7fa7d39e80fb936ae52fcd75d0989987dd0bdb75b81055012c4a400611de52e1.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eInitiating transient EMR clusters for task execution aligns to minimize costs while meeting the surge in data storage and processing requirements. Transient clusters are created for the duration of specific tasks and terminated afterward, eliminating the cost of running idle resources. This approach, combined with storing data in Amazon S3, takes full advantage of AWS\u0027s pay-as-you-go pricing model, ensuring that storage is scalable and cost-efficient.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eCentralizing data in Amazon S3 also simplifies data management and enhances data durability and availability. This solution meets the company\u0027s immediate needs for scalability and durability. It does so in the most cost-effective manner possible, leveraging the best of what AWS offers for big data processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTransition the on-premises Apache Hadoop cluster to Amazon EMR. Centralize the data storage on Amazon S3 and initiate transient EMR clusters for task execution.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMigrate to Amazon EMR and centralize data storage on Amazon S3. Utilize persistent EMR clusters for job execution\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Using persistent EMR clusters for job execution is more expensive than using transient clusters that are terminated after the jobs are completed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTransition to Amazon EMR with a complete data migration to Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS). Expand the cluster with additional core nodes as storage demands increase\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This approach involves higher costs due to the need for additional core nodes to expand the cluster, and it doesn\u0027t utilize the cost-effectiveness and scalability of Amazon S3 for storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMove into Amazon EMR. Configure to store the active data within HDFS and archival data in Amazon S3. Utilize Apache Hive for table integration across S3 and HDFS\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Although Amazon S3 is used for archival storage, Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS) requires EMR cluster resources to maintain active data; thus, it leads to higher costs compared to a solution that fully utilizes Amazon S3 for storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-instances-guidelines.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ManagementGuide/emr-plan-instances-guidelines.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/emr/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/aws/aws-emr-best-practices/blob/main/content/reliability/best_practices.md\"\u003ehttps://github.com/aws/aws-emr-best-practices/blob/main/content/reliability/best_practices.md\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon EMR Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-45":{"id":"p:2-45","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 45","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUpload the compressed file in a single operation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpload the compressed file using multipart upload\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFTP the compressed file into an Amazon EC2 instance that runs in the same region as the Amazon S3 bucket. Then transfer the file from the Amazon EC2 instance into the Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpload the compressed file using multipart upload with Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration (Amazon S3TA)\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Gửi một phần duy nhất không tận dụng tải song song và phải truyền lại nhiều dữ liệu hơn nếu lỗi. Tệp 2 GB phù hợp multipart.","Multipart tăng tốc bằng cách tải các phần song song. Bộ đề chọn bổ sung Transfer Acceleration để tối ưu đường truyền từ xa hơn nữa.","Gửi vòng qua EC2 tạo thêm bước sao chép, máy chủ và điểm lỗi. Không nhanh, gọn bằng tải trực tiếp tới S3 bằng multipart.","Multipart truyền các phần song song; Transfer Acceleration tận dụng mạng biên để hỗ trợ đường truyền xa. Đây là kết hợp bộ đề chọn; lợi ích thực tế phụ thuộc vị trí và mạng."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data analytics company measures what the consumers watch and what advertising they’re exposed to. This real-time data is ingested into its on-premises data center and subsequently, the daily data feed is compressed into a single file and uploaded on Amazon S3 for backup. The typical compressed file size is around 2 gigabytes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following is the fastest way to upload the daily compressed file into Amazon S3?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-48":{"id":"p:4-48","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 48","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to schedule DAGs that invoke Spark-based ETL jobs across the data platform\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Step Functions and EventBridge to orchestrate AWS Glue jobs and Amazon EMR jobs across the existing ETL workflow\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMigrate the ETL jobs to AWS Glue Spark jobs and use AWS Glue workflows to orchestrate and automate the pipeline\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePackage the Spark jobs in containers, run them as Amazon ECS tasks on Fargate, and use Step Functions to coordinate the containerized stages\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["MWAA điều phối công việc nhưng bản thân không cung cấp môi trường Spark serverless để thay thế cụm xử lý. Chỉ đổi scheduler chưa đạt mục tiêu chuyển toàn bộ ETL sang serverless.","Step Functions và EventBridge điều phối được, nhưng phương án vẫn giữ các EMR job trên hạ tầng hiện tại. Nó chưa nêu chuyển phần chạy Spark sang môi trường serverless.","Glue Spark jobs cung cấp môi trường Spark được quản lý, còn Glue workflows điều phối job và trigger trong pipeline. Chuyển các bước về Glue đáp ứng cả xử lý Spark lẫn tự động hóa serverless trong các lựa chọn.","Fargate chạy container nhưng bạn vẫn phải đóng gói và quản lý cách thực thi Spark phân tán. Công việc này phức tạp hơn dùng môi trường Spark có sẵn của Glue."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media analytics company is modernizing its extract, transform, and load workflows. The current architecture uses Amazon EMR with Apache Spark for high-volume audience analytics transformations and AWS Glue for smaller ETL jobs. The workflows write curated datasets to an Amazon S3 based data lake. The company wants to move to a fully managed serverless architecture that can continue to use Spark for data processing. The new solution must orchestrate multiple ETL jobs, automate execution, and reduce manual operational work.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:29":{"id":"e:1:29","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 29","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eArchive historical Iceberg snapshots to a lower-cost Amazon S3 storage class so the table keeps only recent snapshot metadata in active storage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConvert the Iceberg table metadata files to JSON compression and increase the query engine split size so each query scans fewer metadata files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun compaction on the streaming Iceberg tables to rewrite many small data files into fewer larger files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eExpire old Iceberg snapshots for the streaming event tables to reduce historical metadata and remove previous table versions that analysts no longer need\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA digital advertising company maintains a self-managed transactional data lake in an Amazon S3 Standard bucket. The data lake uses Apache Iceberg tables and receives campaign events through both streaming ingestion jobs and scheduled batch pipelines.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAnalysts report that queries against the streaming event tables are becoming slower. A data engineer reviews the Iceberg table layout and finds that the streaming tables contain a large number of small data files. The data engineer must improve query performance for these real-time Iceberg tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun compaction on the streaming Iceberg tables to rewrite many small data files into fewer larger files\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCompaction is the correct solution because the performance problem comes from many small files in the real-time Apache Iceberg tables. Small files increase planning overhead and require query engines to open and process many objects during reads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCompaction rewrites many small data files into fewer larger files. This improves read performance because query engines such as Athena, Amazon EMR, AWS Glue ETL, and other Iceberg-compatible engines can process fewer files with less metadata and object access overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution directly addresses the root cause that the data engineer observed. The issue is not old snapshots, orphaned objects, or storage class placement. The table needs data file layout optimization, and compaction is the Iceberg maintenance operation that solves the small-files problem.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c486ffbd2724c6540168a157198ed8e4a3cd32a1ac3b1ce37981217203fa87df.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c486ffbd2724c6540168a157198ed8e4a3cd32a1ac3b1ce37981217203fa87df.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/apache-iceberg-on-aws/best-practices-compaction.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/apache-iceberg-on-aws/best-practices-compaction.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eExpire old Iceberg snapshots for the streaming event tables to reduce historical metadata and remove previous table versions that analysts no longer need\u003c/strong\u003e - Expiring old snapshots is an Apache Iceberg maintenance activity that can remove obsolete table snapshots and reduce metadata associated with historical table versions. It can help control metadata growth and storage usage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the reported performance issue is caused by many small active data files in the real-time tables. Snapshot expiration does not rewrite those small data files into larger files, so it does not directly solve the slow query problem.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConvert the Iceberg table metadata files to JSON compression and increase the query engine split size so each query scans fewer metadata files\u003c/strong\u003e - Metadata format and query engine split settings can influence planning or scan behavior in some environments. Tuning these settings might help specific workloads after the file layout is healthy.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option does not address the main problem: many small active data files. Compaction is the correct maintenance action because it rewrites the data files into a more efficient layout for reads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eArchive historical Iceberg snapshots to a lower-cost Amazon S3 storage class so the table keeps only recent snapshot metadata in active storage\u003c/strong\u003e - Moving historical snapshots to a lower-cost storage class might reduce storage cost for old table versions if the implementation supports the desired archival pattern. It can be related to retention and cost optimization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, archiving snapshots does not improve the physical layout of current data files. The queries remain slow because the real-time tables still contain many small files that the query engine must plan and read.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/apache-iceberg-on-aws/best-practices-compaction.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/apache-iceberg-on-aws/best-practices-compaction.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/apache-iceberg-optimization-solving-the-small-files-problem-in-amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/apache-iceberg-optimization-solving-the-small-files-problem-in-amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:3":{"id":"e:2:3","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 3","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up another Kinesis Data Stream for Vehicle A with twenty shards and then direct Vehicle A sensor data to this new Kinesis Data Stream\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the number of shards in Kinesis Data Streams to support throughput from both vehicles\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the partition key to use the sensor ID instead of the name of the vehicle\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up another Kinesis Data Stream for Vehicle B with ten shards and then direct Vehicle B sensor data to this new Kinesis Data Stream\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA research agency has deployed two autonomous underwater vehicles in the ocean to track parameters such as salinity, temperature, speed and direction of currents, etc. Vehicle A has twenty sensors whereas Vehicle B has ten sensors. Each sensor is identified by a unique ID. Amazon Kinesis Data Streams is being used to gather data from each sensor. A single Amazon Kinesis Data Stream with two shards is configured based on the total incoming and outgoing data throughput. Two partition keys are generated based on the name of the vehicles. During initial testing, data from Vehicle A experiences a bottleneck whereas data from Vehicle B does not. The overall stream throughput has been validated to be less than the assigned Kinesis Data Streams throughput.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions would you use to address this bottleneck without increasing the total cost and complexity of the system?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange the partition key to use the sensor ID instead of the name of the vehicle\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams is a fully managed, serverless streaming data service that makes it easy to elastically ingest and store logs, events, clickstreams, and other forms of streaming data in real time. Kinesis Data Streams has two capacity modes: on-demand and provisioned, and both come with specific billing options.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIn the on-demand mode, pricing is based on the volume of data ingested and retrieved along with a per-hour charge for each data stream in your account.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWith provisioned capacity mode, you specify the number of shards necessary for your application based on its write and read request rate. A shard is a unit of capacity that provides 1 MB/second of write and 2 MB/second of read throughout. A record is the data that your data producer adds to your Amazon Kinesis data stream. A PUT Payload Unit is counted in 25 KB payload “chunks” that comprise a record. For example, a 5 KB record contains one PUT Payload Unit, a 45 KB record contains two PUT Payload Units, and a 1 MB record contains 40 PUT Payload Units. PUT Payload Unit is charged a per-million PUT Payload Units rate. In the provisioned mode (applicable for the given scenario), you pay by the shard hour (1MB/second ingress, 2MB/second egress) and PUT Payload Units, per 1,000,000 units.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the existing use case, the Kinesis Data Stream has been partitioned into two shards, one for each of the two vehicles. Therefore, data from the 20 sensors of Vehicle A is going to the first shard and the data from the 10 sensors of Vehicle B is going to the other shard, causing an imbalance and thereby resulting in a throughput bottleneck for Vehicle A. The correct solution is to partition the shards on the basis of the sensor ID which will result in an even distribution across the two shards. The use-case already states that the overall stream throughput is less than the assigned Kinesis Data Streams throughput, so there is no need to provision extra shards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the number of shards in Kinesis Data Streams to support throughput from both vehicles\u003c/strong\u003e - As mentioned in the explanation above, there is no need to provision extra shards as it would result in additional costs. The right solution is to partition the shards on the basis of the sensor ID which will result in an even distribution across the two shards already provisioned.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up another Kinesis Data Stream for Vehicle A with twenty shards and then direct Vehicle A sensor data to this new Kinesis Data Stream\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up another Kinesis Data Stream for Vehicle B with ten shards and then direct Vehicle B sensor data to this new Kinesis Data Stream\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBoth these options have been added as distractors. There is no need to provision extra shards for any of the vehicles, as it would result in additional costs. The right solution is to partition the shards on the basis of the sensor ID which will result in an even distribution across the two shards already provisioned.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/pricing/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/pricing/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-16":{"id":"p:3-16","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 16","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to configure profile jobs, reusable recipes, and rule-based checks, then schedule the jobs for each S3 dataset\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena scheduled queries to scan each S3 prefix, write SQL expressions for every expected data quality condition, and email query outputs to analysts\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue ETL jobs with DynamicFrames and ResolveChoice to normalize schema variations and detect all quality issues during transformation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew dashboards only, review each dataset manually, and ask analysts to record null counts and outlier patterns\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["DataBrew profile jobs cung cấp thống kê dữ liệu, recipe tái sử dụng và kiểm tra theo quy tắc. Lập lịch cho từng dataset giúp quy trình lặp lại được.","Athena SQL có thể kiểm tra nhưng cần viết và duy trì nhiều quy tắc, schema và cơ chế gửi kết quả riêng.","ResolveChoice xử lý tình huống kiểu dữ liệu không nhất quán; không tự phát hiện mọi lỗi thiếu dữ liệu, định dạng và outlier.","Chỉ xem dashboard rồi ghi chép bằng tay không đáp ứng quy trình tự động, có thể tái sử dụng và giám sát chất lượng."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media analytics company receives daily CSV and JSON files in an Amazon S3 bucket from several advertising partners. The incoming files have schema variations across partners and occasionally contain missing values, malformed fields, inconsistent date formats, and statistical outliers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer needs a repeatable process to profile the uploaded files, monitor quality metrics, and apply reusable rule-based checks across multiple datasets. The solution must minimize manual effort and avoid custom validation code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:56":{"id":"e:4:56","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 56","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up an AWS Step Functions state machine to run the Lambda function and then the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an AWS Glue workflow to run the Lambda function and then the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to set up an Apache Airflow workflow that is deployed on an Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) cluster. The workflow definition should have the first task as the Lambda function and the second task as the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to set up an Apache Airflow workflow that is deployed on an Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) cluster. The workflow definition should have the first task as the Lambda function and the second task as the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company needs to set up a data pipeline that includes an AWS Lambda function and an AWS Glue job. You have been hired as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to build a solution that has the least management overhead and is fully integrated with AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat do you recommend?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an AWS Step Functions state machine to run the Lambda function and then the AWS Glue job\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Step Functions is a serverless orchestration service that lets you integrate with AWS Lambda functions and other AWS services to build business-critical applications. Step Functions is based on state machines and tasks. In Step Functions, a workflow is called a state machine, which is a series of event-driven steps. Each step in a workflow is called a state. A Task state represents a unit of work that another AWS service, such as AWS Lambda, performs. A Task state can call any AWS service or API.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWith Step Functions\u0027 built-in controls, you examine the state of each step in your workflow to make sure that your application runs in order and as expected. Depending on your use case, you can have Step Functions call AWS services, such as Lambda, to perform tasks. You can create workflows that process and publish machine learning models. You can have Step Functions control AWS services, such as AWS Glue, to create extract, transform, and load (ETL) workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Step Functions state machine represents a simple way to orchestrate a workflow on AWS, as it has minimal management overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to set up an Apache Airflow workflow that is deployed on an Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) cluster. The workflow definition should have the first task as the Lambda function and the second task as the AWS Glue job\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to set up an Apache Airflow workflow that is deployed on an Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) cluster. The workflow definition should have the first task as the Lambda function and the second task as the AWS Glue job\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow is a managed orchestration service for Apache Airflow that you can use to set up and operate data pipelines in the cloud at scale. Apache Airflow is an open-source tool used to programmatically author, schedule, and monitor sequences of processes and tasks referred to as workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon MWAA offers support for using containers to scale the worker fleet on demand and reduce scheduler outages using Amazon ECS on AWS Fargate. Operators that invoke tasks on Amazon ECS containers, and Kubernetes operators that create and run pods on a Kubernetes cluster are supported.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUsing Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to set up an Apache Airflow workflow that is deployed on an Amazon ECS cluster or Amazon EKS cluster represents a solution with a very high management overhead. So, both these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an AWS Glue workflow to run the Lambda function and then the AWS Glue job\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue workflow is only for orchestrating complex ETL operations using AWS Glue, so you cannot have a Lambda function as a component of the workflow. So, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/workflows_overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/what-is-mwaa.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/what-is-mwaa.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-38":{"id":"p:3-38","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 38","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eScan and delete items using batch mode\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eScan and delete items individually\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDelete then re-create the table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the purge table option\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Scan rồi batch delete vẫn tiêu thụ thao tác đọc/ghi trên rất nhiều item. Không kinh tế nếu được phép thay cả bảng staging.","Xóa từng item còn nhiều request hơn và rất chậm cho hàng triệu bản ghi.","Với bảng staging được phép thay mới, xóa rồi tạo lại tránh xóa từng item. Cần tái tạo đúng khóa, index, quyền và cấu hình liên quan trước khi nạp lại.","DynamoDB không có thao tác purge table theo cách mô tả. Đây là lựa chọn gây nhiễu."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA sports analytics firm uses AWS DynamoDB to store information about user\u0027s favorite sports teams and allows the information to be searchable from their home page. The data engineering team at the firm has a requirement wherein all 1 million records in a DynamoDB staging table should be deleted and then re-loaded at 3:00 AM each night.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich option is an efficient way to delete with minimal costs?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:40":{"id":"e:2:40","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 40","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into RDS on an hourly basis and run the SQL-based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into an EMR-based Spark Cluster on an hourly basis and use SparkSQL to run the SQL-based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into Redshift on an hourly basis and run the SQL-based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena to run SQL-based analytics against S3 data\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team at a retail company has set up a workflow to ingest the clickstream data into the raw zone of the S3 data lake. The team wants to run some SQL-based data sanity checks on the raw zone of the data lake.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat AWS services would you recommend for this use-case such that the solution is cost-effective and easy to maintain?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena to run SQL-based analytics against S3 data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data directly in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. Athena is serverless, so there is no infrastructure to set up or manage, and customers pay only for the queries they run. You can use Athena to process logs, perform ad-hoc analysis, and run interactive queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Athena Benefits:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4c19f1e2fad379ac423472029092d84b6892af3d365b13d272696f88e9e9bba9.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4c19f1e2fad379ac423472029092d84b6892af3d365b13d272696f88e9e9bba9.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into Redshift on an hourly basis and run the SQL-based sanity checks\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift is a fully managed petabyte-scale cloud-based data warehouse product designed for large-scale data set storage and analysis.\nAs the development team would have to maintain and monitor the Redshift cluster size and would require significant development time to set up the processes to consume the data periodically, so this option is ruled out.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into an EMR-based Spark Cluster on an hourly basis and use SparkSQL to run the SQL-based sanity checks\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR is the industry-leading cloud big data platform for processing vast amounts of data using open source tools such as Apache Spark, Apache Hive, Apache HBase, Apache Flink, Apache Hudi, and Presto. Amazon EMR uses Hadoop, an open-source framework, to distribute your data and processing across a resizable cluster of Amazon EC2 instances.\nUsing an EMR cluster would imply managing the underlying infrastructure so it’s ruled out because the correct solution for the given use case should require the least amount of development effort and ongoing maintenance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into RDS on an hourly basis and run the SQL-based sanity checks\u003c/strong\u003e - Loading the incremental data into RDS implies data migration jobs will have to be written via a Lambda function or an EC2-based process. This goes against the requirement that the solution should involve the least amount of development effort and ongoing maintenance. Hence this option is not correct.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:6":{"id":"e:4:6","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 6","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore new tax summary documents in S3 Standard for 10 days, copy the objects to a second S3 bucket that uses S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access, and use an EventBridge scheduled Lambda function to delete both copies after 6 years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure the S3 bucket to use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new tax summary documents and expire the objects after 6 years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an S3 Lifecycle rule to transition objects to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval after 10 days and expire the objects after 6 years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an S3 Lifecycle rule to transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 10 days and expire the objects after 6 years\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company generates annual tax summary documents for customers and stores the documents in an Amazon S3 bucket. Customers commonly download the documents during the first 10 days after the company publishes them. After that period, customers access the documents rarely. The company must retain the documents for 6 years, and the documents must remain immediately available when customers request them.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements in the MOST cost-effective way?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an S3 Lifecycle rule to transition objects to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval after 10 days and expire the objects after 6 years\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eS3 Glacier Instant Retrieval is designed for archive data that is rarely accessed but still needs milliseconds retrieval performance. This fits the requirement because customers rarely access the tax summary documents after the first 10 days, but the documents must remain immediately available when requested.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAn S3 Lifecycle rule can transition objects from S3 Standard to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval after the initial active-access period. The same Lifecycle configuration can expire the objects after 6 years to satisfy the retention requirement and avoid keeping documents longer than required.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution is cost-effective because the company stores objects in S3 Standard only while access is more likely, then moves them to a lower-cost storage class that still supports immediate retrieval. It avoids the retrieval delays of deep archive storage and avoids custom deletion automation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/aa03c5c02d5c1bd1580e31a5c521d7ee1003e7e88f1ff851b79a86cd3bdd2665.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/aa03c5c02d5c1bd1580e31a5c521d7ee1003e7e88f1ff851b79a86cd3bdd2665.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure the S3 bucket to use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new tax summary documents and expire the objects after 6 years\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Intelligent-Tiering can reduce storage costs when access patterns are unknown or change over time. It automatically moves objects between access tiers based on observed access patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the access pattern is known: the documents are commonly accessed for about 10 days and then rarely accessed for years. A Lifecycle transition to S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval after 10 days is a more targeted and cost-effective fit for this predictable pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an S3 Lifecycle rule to transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 10 days and expire the objects after 6 years\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Glacier Deep Archive provides very low-cost long-term archival storage. It can be appropriate for data that users almost never need to access quickly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the requirement says the documents must remain readily accessible to customers. Deep Archive retrieval is not immediate, so this option does not meet the customer access requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore new tax summary documents in S3 Standard for 10 days, copy the objects to a second S3 bucket that uses S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access, and use an EventBridge scheduled Lambda function to delete both copies after 6 years\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access can reduce storage cost for infrequently accessed data that does not require multiple Availability Zone resilience. It can be useful for reproducible data or secondary copies.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, customer financial documents require durable long-term retention, and storing the only archive copy in a single Availability Zone storage class is not the best fit. This design also creates duplicate storage, cross-bucket copy logic, and custom Lambda deletion logic instead of using a simple Lifecycle configuration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-transition-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-expire-general-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-expire-general-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/storage-class-intro.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/glacier/instant-retrieval/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/glacier/instant-retrieval/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:10:18":{"id":"e:10:18","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 18","correct":[2],"answers":["Set the provisioned capacity to match the highest observed demand during peak periods.","Update the table’s class to DynamoDB Standard Infrequent Access (DynamoDB Standard-IA).","Update the table’s capacity to use AWS Application Auto Scaling based on minimum and maximum utilization.","Switch to an on-demand capacity mode for the table."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team manages an Amazon DynamoDB table configured with provisioned capacity mode. The table uses a primary key to uniquely identify the items stored by the application. The table experiences a highly predictable traffic pattern, with peaks occurring at midnight every day. These peaks are followed by a period of low activity in the morning, which gradually transitions to normal levels for the remainder of the day.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team wants to maintain the application\u0027s performance during peak times and save costs during low usage periods.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution meets this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon DynamoDB\u003c/strong\u003e allows for two capacity modes for managing throughput costs: provisioned and on-demand. In provisioned capacity mode, the user specifies the number of reads and writes per second as required by the application. This mode is cost-effective for predictable workloads where the throughput does not frequently change. On-demand capacity mode, on the other hand, offers flexible billing and automatically accommodates the table\u0027s workload without requiring manual scaling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Application Auto Scaling\u003c/strong\u003e enables automatic adjustment of resources to maintain consistent, predictable performance at the lowest possible cost. It supports scheduling, which allows for capacity planning based on known usage patterns. This feature is particularly useful for applications with predictable load patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/895a982dbd4b418dc1aed971dc09ca501d759fa9cb2a5863bc49abe835291edf.png\" alt=\"Table capacity configuration for Amazon DynamoDB auto scaling settings\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eImplementing \u003cstrong\u003eAWS Application Auto Scaling for DynamoDB\u003c/strong\u003e allows for the dynamic adjustment of provisioned capacity to align with the application’s workload patterns, ensuring high performance during peak times and cost savings during off-peak times. This approach leverages the predictable nature of the workload, enhancing resource utilization efficiency without the need for manual intervention or the costs associated with maintaining excessive provisioned capacity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate the table’s capacity to use AWS Application Auto Scaling based on minimum and maximum utilization.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet the provisioned capacity to match the highest observed demand during peak periods\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;In a provisioned capacity mode, you pay for the specified capacity regardless of actual usage. Therefore, matching capacity to peak demand without adjusting for periods of low activity only results in paying for unneeded capacity during those lower periods, which is not cost-effective.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate the table’s class to DynamoDB Standard Infrequent Access (DynamoDB Standard-IA)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;DynamoDB Standard-IA is primarily used in scenarios where data is accessed less frequently, offering lower storage costs while maintaining the same performance levels as the standard storage class. However, reading from and writing to these tables costs more than the Standard class, making it pricier for tables that are frequently used, such as the one in the scenario.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSwitch to an on-demand capacity mode for the table\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. The on-demand capacity mode charges for each read or write, unlike the provisioned mode, where you pay for a set capacity. For tables with predictable traffic, using provisioned capacity often works out cheaper. This is because you can set your capacity to cover the expected throughput, avoiding the costs tied to the per-action pricing of the on-demand mode.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/AutoScaling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/AutoScaling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/autoscaling/application/userguide/what-is-application-auto-scaling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/autoscaling/application/userguide/what-is-application-auto-scaling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon DynamoDB Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-dynamodb/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-36":{"id":"p:4-36","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 36","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a Read Replica in the same Region as the Master database and point the analytics workload there\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eMigrate the analytics application to AWS Lambda\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable Multi-AZ for the RDS database and run the analytics workload on the standby database\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFor Disaster Recovery purposes, create a Read Replica in another Region as the Master database and point the analytics workload there\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Read replica trong cùng Region tách truy vấn phân tích khỏi database phục vụ CRM. Nó giảm tải đọc cho nguồn và tránh chi phí, độ trễ liên vùng; cần chấp nhận khả năng trễ sao chép.","Chuyển mã phân tích sang Lambda không giảm các truy vấn mà database vẫn phải xử lý. Nút thắt tài nguyên database vẫn tồn tại.","Trong mô hình RDS Multi-AZ DB instance mà lời giải đề sử dụng, standby dành cho failover và không phục vụ truy vấn đọc. Đừng nhầm mô hình này với RDS Multi-AZ DB cluster có reader riêng.","Read replica liên Region có thể tách tải nhưng tăng độ trễ và chi phí truyền dữ liệu. Đề chỉ cần giảm ảnh hưởng của phân tích lên CRM, nên replica cùng Region phù hợp hơn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA global CRM company has recently migrated its technology infrastructure from its on-premises data center to AWS Cloud. The data engineering team has provisioned an RDS PostgreSQL DB cluster for the company\u0027s flagship CRM application. An analytics workload also runs on the same database which publishes near real-time reports for the senior management of the company. When the analytics workload runs, it slows down the CRM application as well, resulting in a bad user experience.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you recommend as the MOST cost-optimal solution to fix this issue?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:21":{"id":"e:3:21","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 21","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eExport RDS data to S3 and schedule an AWS data pipeline for an incremental copy of RDS data to S3. Load and store the last six months of data from S3 in Amazon Redshift. Configure an Amazon Redshift Spectrum table to connect to all the historical data in S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS data pipeline to incrementally load the last six months of data into Amazon Redshift and execute more frequent queries on Redshift. Set up a read replica of the RDS database to run queries on the historical data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure a read replica of the RDS database to store the last six months of data and execute more frequent queries on the read replica. Export RDS data to S3 and schedule an AWS data pipeline for an incremental copy of RDS data to S3. Configure an AWS Glue Data Catalog of the data in S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the historical data in S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eExport RDS data to S3 and schedule an AWS data pipeline for an incremental copy of RDS data to S3. Configure an AWS Glue Data Catalog of the data in S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the entire data in S3\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company uses Amazon RDS to store sales data. For the analytics workloads that require high performance, only the last six months of data (approximately 50 TB) will be frequently queried. At the end of each month, the monthly sales data will be merged with the historical sales data for the last 5 years, which should also be available for analysis. The CTO at the company is looking at a cost-optimal solution that offers the best performance for this use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you select for the given requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eExport RDS data to S3 and schedule an AWS data pipeline for an incremental copy of RDS data to S3. Load and store the last six months of data from S3 in Amazon Redshift. Configure an Amazon Redshift Spectrum table to connect to all the historical data in S3\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use the AWS data pipeline to automate the movement and transformation of data. It allows you to quickly define a dependent chain of data sources, destinations, and predefined or custom data processing activities called a pipeline. Based on a schedule you define, your pipeline regularly performs processing activities such as distributed data copy, SQL transforms, MapReduce applications, or custom scripts against destinations such as Amazon S3, Amazon RDS, Amazon Redshift or Amazon DynamoDB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use the AWS data pipeline to set up a one-time export of RDS data to S3 as well as an ongoing incremental copy of RDS data to S3. You can also use the AWS data pipeline to load data from S3 to Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8a19ffcd234507dc3ed34c18184ecc7bc2c469fc6fc763aaaf99f4bcab2f744e.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8a19ffcd234507dc3ed34c18184ecc7bc2c469fc6fc763aaaf99f4bcab2f744e.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/what-is-datapipeline.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/what-is-datapipeline.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift is a fully managed, scalable data warehouse that enables secure analytics at scale. For the given use case, you can use the AWS data pipeline to load data from S3 to Redshift and then use Redshift to query the last six months of data to ensure high performance. Further, you can use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the entire historical data in S3 which would be a cost-effective solution to facilitate analysis of the historical data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUsing Redshift Spectrum, Amazon Redshift customers can easily query their data in Amazon S3. Redshift Spectrum is a built-in feature of Amazon Redshift, and your existing queries and BI tools will continue to work seamlessly. Under the hood, AWS manages a fleet of thousands of Redshift Spectrum nodes spread across multiple Availability Zones. These are transparently scaled and allocated to your queries based on the data that you need to process, with no provisioning or commitments. Redshift Spectrum is also highly concurrent—you can access your Amazon S3 data from any number of Amazon Redshift clusters.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/77cf4bb8f4d46ffadc3ce6c624024687d2e60529003fe834c0ed0c6a27766b73.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/77cf4bb8f4d46ffadc3ce6c624024687d2e60529003fe834c0ed0c6a27766b73.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure a read replica of the RDS database to store the last six months of data and execute more frequent queries on the read replica. Export RDS data to S3 and schedule an AWS data pipeline for an incremental copy of RDS data to S3. Configure an AWS Glue Data Catalog of the data in S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the historical data in S3\u003c/strong\u003e - You cannot use RDS to facilitate queries on the last six months of data as it would not match the high performance offered by Redshift, which is a better option for the given requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS data pipeline to incrementally load the last six months of data into Amazon Redshift and execute more frequent queries on Redshift. Set up a read replica of the RDS database to run queries on the historical data\u003c/strong\u003e - Using a read replica on RDS to store and analyze the entire historical data would turn out to be costlier than storing the data on S3 and enable analysis using Redshift spectrum. So this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eExport RDS data to S3 and schedule an AWS data pipeline for an incremental copy of RDS data to S3. Configure an AWS Glue Data Catalog of the data in S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the entire data in S3\u003c/strong\u003e - It is certainly possible to query the entire data in S3 using Athena, however, it will not be able to match the high performance offered by Redshift to query the last six months of data. So this option is not the best fit for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/what-is-datapipeline.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/what-is-datapipeline.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/dp-template-incrementalcopyrdstos3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/dp-template-incrementalcopyrdstos3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/dp-template-redshiftrdsincremental.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/dp-template-redshiftrdsincremental.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/dp-copydata-redshift-create.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/datapipeline/latest/DeveloperGuide/dp-copydata-redshift-create.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/amazon-redshift-spectrum-extends-data-warehousing-out-to-exabytes-no-loading-required/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-53":{"id":"p:4-53","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 53","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EMR Spark Streaming to consume records from Amazon MSK, write curated files to Amazon S3, and refresh external Redshift tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an external schema for the streaming data source and create a regular Amazon Redshift table that directly references the external schema\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an external schema that maps to the Amazon MSK cluster and create a materialized view that consumes records from the MSK topic\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSend Amazon MSK records to Amazon S3 and use a Lambda function to copy each batch into a new Amazon Redshift table\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["EMR Streaming cộng bước ghi S3 tạo bản sao trung gian và thêm công quản lý xử lý luồng. Đề muốn dữ liệu vào Redshift nhanh với ít thành phần nhất.","External schema là bước cần thiết nhưng streaming ingestion đọc vào materialized view, không phải một bảng thường tự tham chiếu stream như mô tả.","Tạo external schema ánh xạ MSK và materialized view đọc topic tương ứng. Redshift streaming ingestion đưa dữ liệu trực tiếp vào view, tránh các bản sao S3 trung gian.","Ghi S3 rồi dùng Lambda COPY từng lô thêm độ trễ và mã vận hành. Tạo bảng mới mỗi lô còn làm phức tạp cấu trúc truy vấn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA gaming analytics company wants to ingest player activity events from an Amazon MSK cluster into an Amazon Redshift data warehouse. The analytics team needs fast access to the incoming events for near-real-time dashboards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company also wants to avoid storing multiple intermediate copies of the streaming data and wants the solution with the least operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:7:10":{"id":"e:7:10","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 10","correct":[1],"answers":["Implement AWS Budgets to set custom cost and usage budgets that alert when thresholds are exceeded.","Utilize AWS Cost Explorer to create and retrieve customizable reports that analyze AWS costs and usage data.","Deploy Amazon QuickSight for visualizing and analyzing AWS costs and usage data through dashboards.","Configure Amazon CloudWatch to monitor and alert on AWS service usage and associated costs."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team has recently implemented an ETL workload that leverages various AWS services. Given the complexity and scale of their operations, the team now seeks a solution that will enable them to generate custom reports of their costs and usage across these services. The goal is to analyze spending habits and find areas to cut costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Cost Explorer\u003c/strong\u003e is a tool designed to provide a comprehensive view of your AWS costs and usage over time, helping you better manage your AWS resources. It provides the following benefits:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCustom reporting\u003c/strong\u003e - You can create custom reports to analyze your cost and usage data at different levels of granularity, like total costs across all accounts or specific costs by service, region, etc. This allows you to gain insights into your spending patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCost optimization\u003c/strong\u003e - Cost Explorer provides visibility into your usage at different levels, which helps you identify opportunities to optimize costs, like reducing the usage of underutilized resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTrend analysis\u003c/strong\u003e - With Cost Explorer, you can analyze cost and usage trends over multiple years at monthly granularity. This helps understand fluctuations and plan budgets accordingly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eResource level data\u003c/strong\u003e - It provides cost attribution down to the individual resource level, like EC2 instances, allowing you to pinpoint expensive resources consuming the most costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5eef491e8d08815e8472d4cd75145848954c78fb54872ac425f7c425809c27fe.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eCost Explorer would be a good fit for the data engineering team as it would allow them to generate customized reports on costs and usage of various AWS services used in their ETL workload. This will help them gain insights into spending patterns and identify opportunities to optimize costs, like rightsizing resources or reducing the usage of underutilized ones.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize AWS Cost Explorer to create and retrieve customizable reports that analyze AWS costs and usage data.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement AWS Budgets to set custom cost and usage budgets that alert when thresholds are exceeded\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because AWS Budgets primarily focuses on monitoring and controlling AWS costs by setting budgets and alerts rather than generating detailed reports on costs and usage patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy Amazon QuickSight for visualizing and analyzing AWS costs and usage data through dashboards\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because, although Amazon QuickSight can be used for visualization and analysis, it would require the data to be imported and processed, making it a more complex solution compared to directly using AWS Cost Explorer for cost analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon CloudWatch to monitor and alert on AWS service usage and associated costs\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because Amazon CloudWatch is primarily a monitoring service for AWS cloud resources and applications, not tailored for detailed cost and usage reporting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cost-management/latest/userguide/ce-what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/cost-management/latest/userguide/ce-what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws-cloud-financial-management/extended-history-and-more-granular-data-available-within-aws-cost-explorer/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws-cloud-financial-management/extended-history-and-more-granular-data-available-within-aws-cost-explorer/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Billing and Cost Management Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-billing-and-cost-management/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-billing-and-cost-management/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-46":{"id":"p:3-46","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 46","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE eventtime \u0026gt;= \u00272025-01-01T00:00:00Z\u0027 GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage ORDER BY TotalEvents DESC LIMIT 10\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE errorcode IS NOT NULL AND eventtime \u0026gt;= \u00272025-01-01T00:00:00Z\u0027 GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage LIMIT 10\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE errorcode IS NOT NULL AND eventtime \u0026gt;= \u00272025-01-01T00:00:00Z\u0027 GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage ORDER BY TotalEvents DESC LIMIT 10\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSELECT COUNT(*) AS TotalEvents, eventname, errorcode, errormessage FROM audit_cloudtrail_events WHERE errorcode = NULL AND eventtime \u0026gt;= \u00272025-01-01T00:00:00Z\u0027 GROUP BY eventname, errorcode, errormessage ORDER BY TotalEvents DESC LIMIT 10\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Thiếu điều kiện errorcode IS NOT NULL nên có thể tính cả sự kiện thành công. Không đúng yêu cầu chỉ thống kê lỗi.","Có lọc và nhóm nhưng thiếu ORDER BY TotalEvents DESC. LIMIT 10 không tự chọn mười nhóm xuất hiện nhiều nhất.","IS NOT NULL chọn sự kiện lỗi; điều kiện thời gian lấy từ đầu 2025; GROUP BY gom đúng ba trường; ORDER BY giảm dần và LIMIT 10 lấy mười nhóm lỗi phổ biến nhất.","So sánh = NULL không kiểm tra null đúng trong SQL; hơn nữa cần lấy mã lỗi có giá trị. Dùng IS NOT NULL."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer created an Amazon Athena table named audit_cloudtrail_events to query AWS CloudTrail logs for a centralized security account. The audit team wants a summary of API errors that have occurred since the beginning of 2025.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe query must include only events that contain an error code. The query must group the results by API action, error code, and error message, and must return the 10 error groups with the highest occurrence counts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich query will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:6:7":{"id":"e:6:7","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 7","correct":[0],"answers":["Utilize an ephemeral volume provided by the node\u0027s RAM for the containers.","Configure the containers so that the application code connects directly to Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX).","\u003cspan\u003eHave containers use a \u003ccode\u003ePersistentVolume\u003c/code\u003e backed by Amazon EFS, managed through AWS Controllers for Kubernetes (ACK).\u003c/span\u003e","Configure the containers so that the application code connects directly to Amazon MemoryDB for Redis."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is developing a containerized application to transform data using containers orchestrated by Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS), operating on Amazon EC2 nodes. The containers run as Pods on the EC2 nodes. In this setup, a data engineer faces the task of selecting an appropriate storage solution for the data before its transformation. Each container will process distinct datasets and subsequently store the transformed data in a data lake. Data sharing among the containers is prohibited.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich storage solution offers the LOWEST latency for this scenario?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eUsing an ephemeral volume provided by the node\u0027s RAM in Amazon EKS is the most efficient approach for achieving the lowest latency. Amazon EKS, a managed Kubernetes service, orchestrates container deployment on nodes, which can be EC2 instances or managed through AWS Fargate. Ephemeral volumes, tied to the pod\u0027s lifecycle, can swiftly access the node\u0027s local resources like drives or RAM. Since the requirement does not involve data sharing between containers and focuses on low-latency access, local storage like node RAM is ideal.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e760e6d58d5665a009780c6e0eaaeb2ed55972643b707f9783857e5016ec56df.jpg\" alt=\"ephemeral volume\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize an ephemeral volume provided by the node\u0027s RAM for the containers.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure the containers so that the application code connects directly to Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Although DAX, a write-through cache for DynamoDB, offers low-latency access to stored items, it typically requires network calls for storage access. This external storage setup introduces more latency compared to local node storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHave containers use a \u003ccode\u003ePersistentVolume\u003c/code\u003e backed by Amazon EFS, managed through AWS Controllers for Kubernetes (ACK)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While AWS Controllers for Kubernetes (ACK) allow for efficient management of AWS services within the Kubernetes environment, using a PersistentVolume backed by Amazon EFS introduces higher latency. This is primarily due to the network calls required for accessing EFS, making it less suitable for scenarios where low latency is crucial.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure the containers so that the application code connects directly to Amazon MemoryDB for Redis\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While MemoryDB provides low-latency in-memory database services, it requires network connectivity for data access. Like DAX, being external to the node means increased latency compared to local storage options.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/storage.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/storage.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/persistent-storage-for-kubernetes/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/storage/persistent-storage-for-kubernetes/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/eks-compute.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/eks-compute.html\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-elastic-kubernetes-service-eks/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-elastic-kubernetes-service-eks/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:5:9":{"id":"e:5:9","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 9","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eCreate an ETL job written in Python. Import the Python \u003ccode\u003erecordlinkage\u003c/code\u003e library and use it for data deduplication.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eCreate an ETL job written in AWS Glue. Utilize \u003ccode\u003eFindMatches\u003c/code\u003e machine learning transform for data deduplication.\u003c/span\u003e","Create an ETL job written AWS Glue. Import the Python dedupe library and use it for data deduplication.","\u003cspan\u003eCreate an ETL job written in Python. Import the Pandas library and use \u003ccode\u003eDataFrame.drop_duplicates()\u003c/code\u003e function for data deduplication.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses Amazon S3 as a data lake to store data from its application. However, upon reviewing the data, there is a lot of duplicate information, such as customers who have signed up more than once, products that have accidentally added more than once, and so forth. The data engineer must find a way to detect and eliminate these duplicate information.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution must the engineer implement to satisfy the requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e is a serverless data integration service that makes it easy for analytics users to discover, prepare, move, and integrate data from multiple sources. It can be used for analytics, machine learning, and application development. AWS Glue consolidates major data integration capabilities into a single service. These include data discovery, modern ETL, cleansing, transforming, and centralized cataloging.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use AWS Glue to find matching records across a dataset (including ones without identifiers) by using the new \u003cstrong\u003eFindMatches ML Transform\u003c/strong\u003e, a custom machine learning transformation that helps you identify matching records.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe FindMatches transform enables you to identify duplicate or matching records in your dataset, even when the records do not have a common unique identifier and no fields match exactly. This will not require writing any code or knowing how machine learning works. FindMatches can be useful in many different problems, such as:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMatching customers\u003c/strong\u003e: Linking customer records across different customer databases, even when many customer fields do not match exactly across the databases (e.g., different name spelling, address differences, missing or inaccurate data, etc).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMatching products\u003c/strong\u003e: Matching products in your catalog against other product sources, such as product catalog against a competitor’s catalog, where entries are structured differently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImproving fraud detection\u003c/strong\u003e: Identifying duplicate customer accounts, determining when a newly created account is (or might be) a match for a previously known fraudulent user.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eOther matching problems\u003c/strong\u003e: Match addresses, movies, parts lists, etc. In general, if a human being could look at your database rows and determine that they were a match, there is a really good chance that the FindMatches transform can help you.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEach FindMatches transform must be taught what should be considered a match and what should not be considered a match. You teach your transform by adding labels to a file and uploading your choices to AWS Glue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/7117b4a679955514f3cad1a464f5eb82e3ed1f98faf581c6f4d11482eee9e47d.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an ETL job written in AWS Glue. Utilize \u003ccode\u003eFindMatches\u003c/code\u003e machine learning transform for data deduplication.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an ETL job written in Python. Import the Python \u003ccode\u003erecordlinkage\u003c/code\u003e library and use it for data deduplication\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Although this solution can perform data deduplication, it still requires significant operational overhead, as you would need to create custom code written in Python. The Python Record Linkage Toolkit is a library to link records in or between data sources. The toolkit provides most of the tools needed for record linkage and deduplication. It can resolve the issue, but complexity will arise in coding and when you’re going to deal with unstructured data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an ETL job written in Python. Import the Pandas library and use \u003ccode\u003eDataFrame.drop_duplicates()\u003c/code\u003e function for data deduplication.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Even though this solution can remove duplicate information, it still remains inadequate as it cannot really distinguish the names or products the same way humans differentiate them. It can only completely delete duplicate rows of data or information with the same values. For example, it will detect John James and Johnny James as different names, even though they usually belong to the same person.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an ETL job written AWS Glue. Import the Python dedupe library and use it for data deduplication\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because while it can satisfy the requirement, it will still require a significant amount of custom coding. The Find Matches machine learning Transform will not require writing code or knowing how machine learning works. You only must edit a CSV file to identify which matches or does not (labeling) from a sample record. Hence, considering the operational overhead, Find Matches is the best answer.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/machine-learning.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/machine-learning.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2019/08/aws-glue-provides-findmatches-ml-transform-to-deduplicate/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2019/08/aws-glue-provides-findmatches-ml-transform-to-deduplicate/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/harmonize-data-using-aws-glue-and-aws-lake-formation-findmatches-ml-to-build-a-customer-360-view/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/harmonize-data-using-aws-glue-and-aws-lake-formation-findmatches-ml-to-build-a-customer-360-view/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-44":{"id":"p:3-44","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 44","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate AWS Glue streaming jobs that poll each Aurora MySQL database for changed rows, write intermediate files to Amazon S3, and load the files into Amazon Redshift Serverless on a frequent schedule\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate Aurora zero-ETL integrations with Amazon Redshift Serverless for the Aurora MySQL databases to make ongoing changes available in Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS DMS to configure binary log replication from Aurora MySQL to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, transform schemas, and load the changes into Amazon Redshift Serverless\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate AWS DMS tasks that perform schema conversion for each Aurora MySQL database and load the converted changes into Amazon Redshift Serverless\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Polling Aurora, ghi tệp trung gian và nạp theo lịch cần logic theo dõi thay đổi riêng, đồng thời tăng độ trễ.","Aurora zero-ETL integration sao chép các thay đổi nguồn được hỗ trợ sang Redshift Serverless theo cơ chế quản lý sẵn. Giảm việc tự xây và duy trì pipeline CDC.","DMS–Kinesis–biến đổi–Redshift tạo nhiều tầng vận hành hơn tích hợp zero-ETL trực tiếp cho trường hợp đã hỗ trợ.","Tác vụ DMS không tự thực hiện đầy đủ schema conversion theo cách lựa chọn mô tả. Bộ đề có tích hợp zero-ETL trực tiếp phù hợp hơn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail marketplace company needs to capture ongoing data changes from several Amazon Aurora MySQL databases that support orders, payments, and fulfillment services. The company must make the updates available for analytics in Amazon Redshift Serverless in near real time. The solution must be serverless and must minimize ongoing maintenance for the data engineering team.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:12":{"id":"e:4:12","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 12","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to create year, month, and day partitions for the AWS Glue table whenever new files arrive\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue crawler to run every day and discover new year, month, and day partitions in the S3 dataset\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun an Athena MSCK REPAIR TABLE command every day to discover new S3 prefixes and add the partitions to the AWS Glue table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet partition projection properties on the AWS Glue table so Athena calculates year, month, and day partitions during query planning\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company stores shipment tracking data in an Amazon S3 bucket and uses Amazon Athena to analyze the data. The data is organized by year, month, and day prefixes, and new data arrives every day.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer needs to configure the AWS Glue table so Athena can query daily partitions. The solution must support predictable date-based partitions with the least ongoing maintenance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet partition projection properties on the AWS Glue table so Athena calculates year, month, and day partitions during query planning\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAthena partition projection is the correct solution for predictable partition values such as year, month, and day. With partition projection, Athena calculates partition values and S3 locations from table properties that are configured on the AWS Glue table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach avoids the need to create new partition entries in the AWS Glue Data Catalog every day. When queries include date filters, Athena can project the relevant partition locations during query planning instead of retrieving partition metadata from the catalog.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least ongoing maintenance because the data engineer configures the projection rules once for the date ranges and S3 path format. The company does not need to run a daily crawler, Lambda function, or repair command just to keep date partitions current.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/a45d5e7cd7f8d0b57714ced10fafcbddd6a3da9cd918eccbc4d890dc65de1428.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/a45d5e7cd7f8d0b57714ced10fafcbddd6a3da9cd918eccbc4d890dc65de1428.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/speed-up-your-amazon-athena-queries-using-partition-projection/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/speed-up-your-amazon-athena-queries-using-partition-projection/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue crawler to run every day and discover new year, month, and day partitions in the S3 dataset\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue crawlers can discover S3 data, infer schemas, and add partitions to the AWS Glue Data Catalog. A daily crawler can keep metadata updated when new partition folders arrive.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that the crawler still creates and updates partition metadata as a recurring process. Partition projection is more operationally efficient for predictable date-based partitions because Athena can calculate the partitions without daily catalog updates.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to create year, month, and day partitions for the AWS Glue table whenever new files arrive\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue DataBrew is useful for visual data preparation, profiling, and data quality activities. It can help analysts clean and standardize datasets without writing code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not meet the partition management requirement. DataBrew is not the Athena partition projection feature and is not the managed mechanism for calculating year, month, and day partition locations during Athena query planning.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun an Athena MSCK REPAIR TABLE command every day to discover new S3 prefixes and add the partitions to the AWS Glue table\u003c/strong\u003e - The MSCK REPAIR TABLE command can discover Hive-style partitions in Amazon S3 and add them to the catalog. It can be useful for some manually managed partitioned datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option still requires a recurring metadata update process. It also can become inefficient for large partitioned tables, while partition projection is specifically designed to avoid catalog lookups and partition registration for predictable partition structures.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection-setting-up.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partition-projection-setting-up.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-data-optimization-techniques.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-data-optimization-techniques.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/speed-up-your-amazon-athena-queries-using-partition-projection/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/speed-up-your-amazon-athena-queries-using-partition-projection/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:65":{"id":"e:3:65","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 65","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eBuild a custom Amazon Bedrock application that converts natural language prompts into SQL queries, runs the generated SQL against Amazon Redshift, and renders the results in a custom dashboard interface\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable Amazon Q in Amazon Quick Suite and generate Amazon Quick Suite dashboards and reports by using natural language prompts\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Quick Suite dashboards with federated query access to Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Quick Suite dashboards with zero-ETL access to Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multinational retail company stores sales and inventory data in Amazon Redshift. The company uses Amazon Quick Suite to build operational dashboards and management reports. Business analysts in different regions have different levels of technical expertise. Several analysts do not know SQL, but they frequently need to create new reports and dashboards for business stakeholders. The company wants analysts to use natural language prompts to create dashboards and reports more efficiently with the least operational effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Amazon Q in Amazon Quick Suite and generate Amazon Quick Suite dashboards and reports by using natural language prompts\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Q in Amazon Quick Suite is designed for generative business intelligence. It helps business users and analysts build and consume insights by using natural language instead of relying only on manual dashboard design or SQL authoring.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution directly addresses the analysts who lack SQL knowledge. Analysts can describe the dashboards, reports, metrics, and visuals they need in natural language, and Amazon Q can help create analyses, refine visuals, generate insights, and accelerate report creation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also has the least operational effort because it uses a managed capability inside the existing Amazon Quick Suite environment. The company does not need to build a custom natural language-to-SQL system, operate a separate BI tool, or create custom Lambda-based report generation workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Quick Suite dashboards with federated query access to Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - Federated query access can help a BI tool query data from a source system. However, it does not provide the natural language dashboard and report generation capability that the analysts need.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option focuses on connectivity to Amazon Redshift rather than generative BI. Analysts who lack SQL knowledge would still need help authoring reports, designing visuals, and translating business questions into analytical logic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Quick Suite dashboards with zero-ETL access to Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - Zero-ETL integrations can reduce data movement between services and simplify some analytics architectures. However, zero-ETL access does not by itself let analysts create dashboards and reports from natural language prompts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe requirement is not primarily about reducing data movement from Amazon Redshift. The requirement is to make report creation easier for analysts with varying technical skills, which Amazon Q in Amazon Quick Suite addresses more directly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eBuild a custom Amazon Bedrock application that converts natural language prompts into SQL queries, runs the generated SQL against Amazon Redshift, and renders the results in a custom dashboard interface\u003c/strong\u003e - A custom Amazon Bedrock application could translate natural language questions into SQL and query Amazon Redshift. However, this approach requires the company to build and maintain prompt logic, SQL validation, authorization controls, semantic mappings, dashboard rendering, and operational monitoring.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option is much more complex than enabling a managed generative BI capability in Amazon Quick Suite. It also creates additional risks around incorrect SQL generation, data governance, and report consistency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/generate-dashboards-from-natural-language-prompts-in-amazon-quick/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/generate-dashboards-from-natural-language-prompts-in-amazon-quick/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/quick-bi.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/quick/latest/userguide/quick-bi.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:59":{"id":"e:2:59","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 59","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eRun the transformations on the data using AWS Glue DataBrew\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eWrite an AWS Glue PySpark job. Use Apache Spark to transform the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue Python shell job. Use the pre-loaded Pandas library to run transformations on the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun the transformations on the data using AWS Glue Data Studio\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn Extract, Transform, and Load (ETL) job needs to be implemented which will process data uploaded to an Amazon S3 bucket daily. The uploaded data is in the form of .csv files, each of which is around 100 MB in size.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents the most cost-effective solution for the ETL job?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue Python shell job. Use the pre-loaded Pandas library to run transformations on the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue ETL supports running plain non-distributed Python scripts as a shell script to run small to medium-sized generic tasks that are often part of an ETL workflow. For example, to submit SQL queries to services such as Amazon Redshift, Amazon Athena, or Amazon EMR, or run machine learning (ML) and scientific analyses.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can run Python shell jobs using one Data Processing Unit (DPU) or 0.0625 DPU (which is 1/16 DPU), allowing you to run cost-effective small to medium jobs that do not require Spark runtime.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCompared to AWS Lambda, which has a strict 15-minute maximum timeout, AWS Glue Python Shell can be configured with a much longer timeout and higher memory, often required for data engineering jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePython shell jobs in AWS Glue come pre-loaded with libraries such as Boto3, NumPy, SciPy, Pandas, and others.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eWrite an AWS Glue PySpark job. Use Apache Spark to transform the data\u003c/strong\u003e - The AWS Glue PySpark job can certainly be used for the ETL job, however, it is a costlier solution than just using the AWS Glue Python shell job (with Pandas library) for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun the transformations on the data using AWS Glue DataBrew\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that makes it easier for data analysts and data scientists to clean and normalize data to prepare it for analytics and machine learning (ML). AWS Glue DataBrew is not an ETL solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun the transformations on the data using AWS Glue Data Studio\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue Studio is a graphical interface that makes it easy to create, run, and monitor data integration jobs in AWS Glue. You can visually compose data transformation workflows and seamlessly run them on the Apache Spark-based serverless ETL engine in AWS Glue. Glue Data Studio is not an ETL solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job-python.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/add-job-python.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-glue-best-practices-build-performant-data-pipeline/additional-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-glue-best-practices-build-performant-data-pipeline/additional-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:14":{"id":"e:3:14","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 14","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift row-level security policies to filter table rows for each user based on department, region, and assigned business unit\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Lake Formation LF-Tags to manage Redshift database object permissions for all internal users who query native Redshift tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift role-based access control to grant database privileges to roles and assign the roles to users\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift column-level privileges to grant or deny access to sensitive columns for each user and reporting group\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial analytics company runs an Amazon Redshift data warehouse that more than 150 employees access each day. Users connect by assuming different IAM roles that represent departments such as finance, risk, operations, and executive reporting.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company needs to manage access to schemas, tables, and other database objects based on each user’s job function, assigned permissions, and the sensitivity of the underlying data. The security team wants a scalable permission model that avoids managing privileges separately for every individual user.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift role-based access control to grant database privileges to roles and assign the roles to users\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift role-based access control is the correct solution because the company needs to control access to database objects for many users based on job role, permissions, and data sensitivity. RBAC lets administrators create database roles, grant privileges to those roles, and assign the roles to users.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis model scales better than managing privileges user by user. For example, the company can create roles for finance analysts, risk reviewers, operations users, and executives, then grant each role the appropriate access to schemas, tables, views, or other database objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eRBAC directly matches the requirement because it provides a broad permission-management framework for many Redshift users. Row-level security, column-level security, and dynamic data masking can provide additional fine-grained controls, but RBAC is the primary feature for managing database access by role.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2120b06ac8886b1aba64874eacb40954ee2a1e0c08163b05e78ae60fffd4b566.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2120b06ac8886b1aba64874eacb40954ee2a1e0c08163b05e78ae60fffd4b566.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-management-of-database-privileges-in-amazon-redshift-using-role-based-access-control/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-management-of-database-privileges-in-amazon-redshift-using-role-based-access-control/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/292cb0efcbbdada35bbaef57617ea7bc93a721d8fb6419798a11a3b1d33439ee.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/292cb0efcbbdada35bbaef57617ea7bc93a721d8fb6419798a11a3b1d33439ee.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-management-of-database-privileges-in-amazon-redshift-using-role-based-access-control/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-management-of-database-privileges-in-amazon-redshift-using-role-based-access-control/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift row-level security policies to filter table rows for each user based on department, region, and assigned business unit\u003c/strong\u003e - Row-level security can restrict which rows a user or role can access within a table. It is useful when different users should see different records from the same table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option is too narrow for the requirement. The company needs to manage access to database objects based on job roles and permissions across more than 100 users, not only filter rows inside selected tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Lake Formation LF-Tags to manage Redshift database object permissions for all internal users who query native Redshift tables\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Lake Formation LF-Tags can help manage access to data lake resources and can support tag-based governance for cataloged data. This is useful for centralized governance of data lake tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not match the requirement because the company needs to control user access to native objects in an Amazon Redshift data warehouse. Amazon Redshift RBAC is the appropriate permission model for Redshift database users and objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift column-level privileges to grant or deny access to sensitive columns for each user and reporting group\u003c/strong\u003e - Column-level security can restrict access to specific columns in a table. This can help when users may access a table but should not see certain sensitive attributes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because the requirement is broader than column access. The company needs scalable access control based on job role and permissions across Redshift objects, which is the purpose of RBAC.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Roles.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Roles.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_roles-managing.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_roles-managing.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_tutorial-RBAC.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_tutorial-RBAC.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_Database_objects.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_Database_objects.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-management-of-database-privileges-in-amazon-redshift-using-role-based-access-control/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/simplify-management-of-database-privileges-in-amazon-redshift-using-role-based-access-control/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:45":{"id":"e:7:45","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 45","correct":[0],"answers":["Utilize AWS Step Functions for workflow orchestration and Amazon EventBridge for the weekly scheduling of tasks.","Implement Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (MWAA) for both orchestration and weekly task scheduling.","Configure Amazon S3 Events to trigger an AWS Lambda function, which in turn schedules an AWS Glue job weekly.","Use AWS CodePipeline for orchestration tasks, complemented by Amazon EventBridge for the pipeline\u0027s weekly scheduling."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company needs to periodically assess air quality changes in a particular city. The company recently launched a serverless data pipeline on AWS that feeds data into an Amazon QuickSight dashboard.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAfter a month, the data engineering team noticed that the dashboard data was obsolete and did not refresh due to the data pipeline\u0027s failure to execute every week. To solve this issue, the team must put in place a system to orchestrate the data workflow properly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution can fulfill these requirements with the LEAST operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Step Functions\u003c/strong\u003e serves as a serverless function orchestrator, enabling the design and execution of complex workflows across AWS services. It simplifies the automation of data pipelines by managing tasks, ensuring they execute in order, handling errors, and retrying as needed. This service is instrumental in orchestrating tasks that transform, analyze, and store data, providing visibility and control over each execution step.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3a3ef8e5d1b2305384b0b83557f8e96d33264e42873a2ba91f0794bbd8bfd0b8.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon EventBridge\u003c/strong\u003e offers a serverless event bus that connects applications with data from various sources. It schedules and triggers workflows automatically, such as those orchestrated by AWS Step Functions, based on a predefined schedule using cron or rate expressions. EventBridge facilitates the automation of the data pipeline\u0027s execution on a weekly basis without the need for manual intervention, aligning with the requirement for operational simplicity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;Utilize AWS Step Functions for workflow orchestration and Amazon EventBridge for the weekly scheduling of tasks.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (MWAA) for both orchestration and weekly task scheduling\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect\u0026nbsp;because\u0026nbsp;it simply introduces a higher operational and maintenance overhead compared to the serverless solutions provided by AWS Step Functions and Amazon EventBridge, making it less ideal for the specified requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eConfigure Amazon S3 Events to trigger an AWS Lambda function, which in turn schedules an AWS Glue job weekly\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect\u0026nbsp;because it only complicates the\u0026nbsp;workflow with unnecessary intermediaries and manual setup steps, deviating from the goal of operational simplicity and automation. Using a combination of AWS Step Functions and Amazon EventBridge should suffice.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS CodePipeline for orchestration tasks, complemented by Amazon EventBridge for the pipeline\u0027s weekly scheduling\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because the AWS CodePipeline service is primarily tailored more towards continuous integration and delivery rather than data pipeline orchestration. This makes it less suitable for the task compared to the more tailored and integrated capabilities of AWS Step Functions and EventBridge for data workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\"\u003e https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/cw-events.html#cw-events-execution-started\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/cw-events.html#cw-events-execution-started\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Step Functions Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-step-functions/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:34":{"id":"e:8:34","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 34","correct":[3],"answers":["Athena Data Catalog","Athena data source","Athena query settings","Athena workgroup"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA tech startup is currently utilizing Amazon Athena for their data analytics operations, using SQL queries for ETL tasks via the Create Table As Select (CTAS) feature. They have recently decided to transition to Apache Spark due to its capabilities in handling large datasets across distributed clusters.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich feature of Amazon Athena would allow the startup to use Apache Spark for their data analytics operations?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAn Athena workgroup is a feature of Amazon Athena that allows for the grouping of queries. By default, each account has a primary workgroup, and the default permissions allow all authenticated users access to this workgroup. Each workgroup that you create shows saved queries and query history only for queries that ran in it. This separates your queries from other queries within an account and makes it more efficient for you to locate your own saved queries and queries in history.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8517c43c90657d6e15da76b79e804afc0fef68ada1d6f4dacd83883fd9950766.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen a workgroup is enabled with Apache Spark, it allows the use of Apache Spark with Amazon Athena for data analytics operations. This is particularly useful for organizations that need to process large datasets across distributed clusters, as it allows them to leverage the advanced data processing capabilities of Apache Spark within the Amazon Athena environment. You can set up workgroup-wide settings and enforce their usage by all queries that run in a workgroup. The settings include query results location in Amazon S3, expected bucket owner, encryption, and control of objects written to the query results bucket. When you enforce workgroup-wide settings, all queries run in this workgroup use workgroup settings. This happens even if their client-side settings may differ from workgroup settings.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e Athena workgroup.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAthena Data Catalog\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because Athena Data Catalog is just a central location where you can store and retrieve table metadata across all your data stored in Amazon S3. It does not provide the capability to use Apache Spark with Amazon Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAthena data source\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because Athena data sources refer to the databases and tables that Athena uses to run queries. While they are an important part of using Athena, they do not enable the use of Apache Spark with Amazon Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAthena query settings\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because Athena query settings allow you to manage query-related configurations in Amazon Athena. They do not enable the use of Apache Spark with Amazon Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/user-created-workgroups.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/user-created-workgroups.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/notebooks-spark-getting-started.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/notebooks-spark-getting-started.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Athena Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-56":{"id":"p:2-56","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 56","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSetup access to the historical data via Amazon Athena. The analytics team can run historical data queries on Amazon Athena and continue the daily reporting on Amazon Redshift. In case the reports need to be cross-referenced, the analytics team needs to export these in flat files and then do further analysis\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the Amazon Redshift COPY command to load the Amazon S3-based historical data into Amazon Redshift. Once the ad-hoc queries are run for the historic data, it can be removed from Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum to create Amazon Redshift cluster tables pointing to the underlying historical data in Amazon S3. The analytics team can then query this historical data to cross-reference with the daily reports from Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue ETL job to load the Amazon S3-based historical data into Redshift. Once the ad-hoc queries are run for the historic data, it can be removed from Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Tách Athena và Redshift rồi xuất kết quả để đối chiếu tạo thao tác thủ công. Spectrum cho phép kết hợp trực tiếp trong SQL Redshift.","COPY dữ liệu lịch sử vào rồi xóa sau truy vấn tốn thời gian và dung lượng tạm. Không cần nạp lại nếu dùng Spectrum.","Tạo external schema/table tham chiếu dữ liệu S3 để Redshift Spectrum đọc tại chỗ. Có thể JOIN với bảng nội bộ phục vụ báo cáo hiện tại.","Glue ETL nạp lại lịch sử có cùng bất lợi sao chép và quản lý dữ liệu tạm. Spectrum tránh bước nhập lại này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn IT company has built a custom data warehousing solution for a retail organization by using Amazon Redshift. As part of the cost optimizations, the company wants to move any historical data (any data older than a year) into Amazon S3, as the daily analytical reports consume data for just the last year. However, the analysts want to retain the ability to cross-reference this historical data along with the daily reports.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company wants to develop a solution with the LEAST amount of effort and MINIMUM cost. Which option would you recommend to facilitate this use-case?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-51":{"id":"p:3-51","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 51","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon DynamoDB table in the provisioned capacity mode with auto-scaling enabled\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon DynamoDB table in the on-demand capacity mode\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon DynamoDB table with a global secondary index\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon DynamoDB global table in the provisioned capacity mode\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Provisioned auto scaling phù hợp tải có thể dự báo hơn và cần thời gian phản ứng. Có thể không tối ưu cho đột biến nhanh và thời gian dài không dùng.","On-demand tính theo yêu cầu và giảm việc dự báo RCU/WCU cho lưu lượng khó đoán. Phù hợp lúc không dùng ban đêm và tăng nhanh ban ngày, trong phạm vi hạn mức dịch vụ.","GSI tạo thêm kiểu truy cập dữ liệu, không quyết định mô hình năng lực cho tải biến động.","Global table phục vụ nhiều Region; không giải quyết trực tiếp việc chọn chế độ năng lực cho lưu lượng không dự báo được."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA ride-sharing company wants to use an Amazon DynamoDB table for data storage. The table will not be used during the night hours whereas the read and write traffic will often be unpredictable during day hours. When traffic spikes occur they will happen very quickly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following will you recommend as the best-fit solution?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-30":{"id":"p:2-30","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 30","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse the federated queries feature of Amazon Athena to join the different data sources and visualize the data using Amazon QuickSight\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Lake Formation to migrate the data sources into Amazon S3. Configure AWS Glue Data Catalog to connect the S3 data to Amazon Athena for further analysis and visualizations. Use a Glue Crawler to automate the process\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon QuickSight to connect to the data sources and generate the visualizations needed for the dashboard\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Lake Formation to migrate the data sources into Amazon S3. Use Amazon QuickSight to generate the visualizations needed for the dashboards\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Athena federated query có thể tạo lớp JOIN trung gian, nhưng thêm connector và SQL cần phát triển khi QuickSight đã kết nối được các nguồn phù hợp.","Lake Formation không phải công cụ chuyển mọi nguồn vào S3 theo cách mô tả. Thiết kế còn thiếu công cụ trực quan hóa trực tiếp và thêm bước không cần thiết.","Dùng connector QuickSight cho các nguồn được hỗ trợ để dựng dataset và dashboard. Cấu hình mạng/quyền cần thiết cho nguồn tại chỗ; không bắt buộc di chuyển toàn bộ dữ liệu trước.","Tập trung tất cả dữ liệu qua Lake Formation không phải bước bắt buộc để dựng dashboard. Kết nối QuickSight trực tiếp giảm thời gian triển khai trong tình huống này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses AWS services such as Amazon Redshift and Amazon S3 as well as their on-premises SQL Server database to store the consumer data. The company also uses Salesforce as its SaaS application. The company wants to build a dashboard that will help the managers visualize the data points from all these systems.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents a simple and easy way to build the dashboard in the least possible time?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-54":{"id":"p:2-54","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 54","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) for data ingestion and configure AWS Lambda to trigger logic for downstream processing\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) for data ingestion and configure AWS Lambda to trigger logic for downstream processing\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon API Gateway with the existing REST-based interface to create a high-performing architecture\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest the data, process it using AWS Lambda, or run analytics using Amazon Kinesis Data Analytics\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["SNS là dịch vụ pub/sub thông báo. Dù có retry tùy loại đích, nó không thay thế luồng có retention và khả năng consumer đọc lại như Kinesis trong bài toán này.","SQS có retry và tách rời công việc tốt, nhưng đề nhấn mạnh phân tích dữ liệu luồng liên tục. Kinesis phù hợp hơn cho nhiều consumer và phát lại luồng.","API Gateway cải thiện lớp tiếp nhận API nhưng không tự cung cấp bộ đệm, lưu luồng và khả năng xử lý lại khi downstream quá tải.","Kinesis tách tiếp nhận khỏi xử lý, giữ dữ liệu trong retention để consumer khôi phục/đọc lại. Lambda hoặc công cụ phân tích luồng xử lý downstream; tên Kinesis Data Analytics trong đề là tên lịch sử."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA health care application processes the real-time health data of the patients into an analytics workflow. With a sharp increase in the number of users, the system has become slow and sometimes even unresponsive as it does not have a retry mechanism. The startup is looking at a scalable solution that has minimal implementation overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you recommend as a scalable alternative to the current solution?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:4-59":{"id":"p:4-59","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 59","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eExport CloudWatch Logs log groups to Amazon S3 on a schedule by using Lambda, then query the exported log files with Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse CloudWatch Logs anomaly detection to identify unusual log patterns and use those anomaly results as the primary query and visualization layer\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSend application logs to CloudWatch Logs log groups, stream the log events to OpenSearch Service with subscription filters, and create OpenSearch dashboards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eExport CloudWatch Logs to Amazon S3 every few minutes with a scheduled Lambda function, catalog the exported files with AWS Glue, and build Athena queries with QuickSight dashboards\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Xuất S3 theo lịch phù hợp phân tích lịch sử nhưng thêm độ trễ và không cung cấp trải nghiệm tìm log liên tục như yêu cầu. Athena không tự tạo dashboard gần thời gian thực từ chuỗi này.","Anomaly detection phát hiện mẫu log bất thường, nhưng kết quả bất thường không thay thế công cụ tìm kiếm và trực quan hóa toàn bộ log theo yêu cầu.","CloudWatch Logs subscription filters chuyển sự kiện mới tới OpenSearch qua tích hợp phù hợp. OpenSearch lập chỉ mục log và Dashboards giúp tìm kiếm, phân tích lỗi và độ trễ gần thời gian thực.","Chuỗi xuất S3, cập nhật catalog rồi truy vấn và làm dashboard có nhiều bước chờ. Nó phù hợp báo cáo định kỳ hơn là xử lý sự cố nhanh từ log mới."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company runs several customer-facing applications on AWS. Each application already writes operational and application logs, and the operations team wants to analyze errors, latency patterns, and user activity across the applications. The team must query and visualize the logs in near real time so engineers can troubleshoot issues quickly from dashboards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:7:21":{"id":"e:7:21","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 21","correct":[2],"answers":["Parse Amazon CloudWatch Logs from the Redshift cluster using an AWS Lambda function. Store logs in Amazon OpenSearch Service for detailed analysis.","Integrate Amazon CloudWatch Logs with Amazon Redshift to collect Redshift SQL query logs. Utilize Amazon CloudWatch alarms and Amazon SNS to alert on any anomalies.","Enable Amazon Redshift\u0027s built-in audit logging feature to capture detailed database transactions, including SQL query executions and user activities. Integrate it with an AWS CloudTrail to enhance tracking of Redshift API calls.","Utilize the AWS Config for continuous monitoring of Amazon Redshift clusters\u0027 logging features to maintain compliance with regulations."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA manufacturing company is in the process of establishing a cloud-based data analytics solution using an Amazon Redshift data warehouse. This new setup will empower the company to conduct an in-depth bi-weekly analysis of its production efficiency and inventory levels.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDue to strict regulatory requirements, the company seeks an effective method to oversee and document all activities within its Amazon Redshift resource. The aim is to systematically record SQL queries, user actions, and alterations to databases and database objects for audit and traceability purposes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution would satisfy these requirements with the LEAST amount of operational overhead involved?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e keeps a record of all connections and user activities in your database, which is commonly known as database auditing. These logs are essential for security and troubleshooting purposes. They are stored in Amazon S3 buckets, which provide easy access and data security features for those who monitor database activities.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift creates three types of log files containing different information:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Connection log\u003c/strong\u003e - records all authentication attempts, connections, and disconnections.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUser log\u003c/strong\u003e - keeps track of changes to database user definitions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUser activity log\u003c/strong\u003e - records each query before it is run on the database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe user activity log is handy for troubleshooting since it provides information about the types of queries the users and the system perform in the database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/de3092182a5c40e6395cf413e500b788d877a3f2080912fddade5d8630c86c2d.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhile the connection and user log contain the same information as the system tables, the log files provide an easier way to retrieve and review the information. You need database permissions to query the system tables, while the log files rely on Amazon S3 permissions. Viewing the information in log files instead of querying system tables also helps reduce the impact of interacting with the database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS CloudTrail and Amazon Redshift integration offers detailed records of Redshift API calls, including API caller identity, time, source IP address, request parameters, and response elements. This integration provides a comprehensive audit trail that includes the actions taken within the Redshift cluster and API interactions that affect the cluster\u0027s configuration or data query. The built-in audit logging and CloudTrail integration create a robust monitoring solution that secures and audits a Redshift environment, ensuring compliance obligations are met, and data is safeguarded. This dual approach simplifies audit log management and enhances the visibility of operations within and around the Redshift clusters, making it easier to take a proactive stance on security and compliance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Amazon Redshift\u0027s built-in audit logging feature to capture detailed database transactions, including SQL query executions and user activities. Integrate it with an AWS CloudTrail to enhance tracking of Redshift API calls.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eParse Amazon CloudWatch Logs from the Redshift cluster using an AWS Lambda function. Store logs in Amazon OpenSearch Service for detailed analysis\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because using AWS Lambda and integrating it with Amazon OpenSearch Service for parsing and storing logs can be complex and require custom development and maintenance. Moreover, CloudWatch Log entries for Redshift do not entirely capture SQL query executions, user activities, and other things that are fully covered by the built-in Audit Logging feature.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIntegrate Amazon CloudWatch Logs with Amazon Redshift to collect Redshift SQL query logs. Utilize Amazon CloudWatch alarms and Amazon SNS to alert on any anomalies\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This approach mainly focuses on collecting SQL query logs and creating alerts for anomalies, which covers only a part of the monitoring requirements. However, it doesn\u0027t fulfill the need to document all user activities and changes made to databases and their objects in a way that is easy to audit for compliance purposes. Using the built-in Audit Logging feature is still the best option than using a combination of CloudWatch Logs and Alarms.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize the AWS Config for continuous monitoring of Amazon Redshift clusters\u0027 logging features to maintain compliance with regulations\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. AWS Config is a service for assessing and monitoring AWS resource configurations over time, but it doesn\u0027t capture detailed transactional audit logs in Redshift. It may not be suitable for the company\u0027s need to analyze production efficiency and inventory levels.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/logging-with-cloudtrail.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/logging-with-cloudtrail.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out our Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:39":{"id":"e:3:39","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 39","correct":[2,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSmall Dimension tables should be marked to use KEY distribution style, which will cause them to be replicated to each physical node in the cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eA fact table with multiple distribution keys is useful when multiple dimension tables have to be joined to it\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIf a dimension table cannot be collocated with the fact table or other important joining tables, use ALL distribution style for such tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChoose a column with low cardinality in the filtered result set\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eData should be distributed in such a way that the rows that participate in joins are already collocated on the nodes with their joining rows in other tables\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn e-commerce company performs analytics on the company\u0027s data using the Amazon Redshift cluster. The Redshift cluster has two important tables: the orders table and the product table which have millions of rows each. A few small tables with supporting data are also present. The team is looking for the right distribution patterns for the tables, to optimize query speed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following are the key points to consider while planning for the best distribution style for your data? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eData should be distributed in such a way that the rows that participate in joins are already collocated on the nodes with their joining rows in other tables\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA prime goal of data distribution is to minimize data movement when queries run. Therefore, if the rows that participate in joins or aggregates are already collocated on the nodes with their joining rows in other tables, the optimizer doesn\u0027t need to redistribute as much data when queries run.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIf a dimension table cannot be collocated with the fact table or other important joining tables, use ALL distribution style for such tables\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you execute a query, the query optimizer redistributes the rows to the compute nodes as needed to perform any joins and aggregations. The goal in selecting a table distribution style is to minimize the impact of the redistribution step by locating the data where it needs to be before the query is run.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eOne of the best approaches is to change some dimension tables to use ALL distribution. If a dimension table cannot be collocated with the fact table or other important joining tables, you can improve query performance significantly by distributing the entire table to all of the nodes. Using ALL distribution multiplies storage space requirements and increases load times and maintenance operations, so you should weigh all factors before choosing ALL distribution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eChoosing the best distribution style:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/fbc160165902dc2a1ca30a75373ca24c493974a14868000f89344adb8f50abcd.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/fbc160165902dc2a1ca30a75373ca24c493974a14868000f89344adb8f50abcd.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-best-dist-key.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-best-dist-key.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChoose a column with low cardinality in the filtered result set\u003c/strong\u003e - This is incorrect. A column with high cardinality should be considered in the filtered result set. If you distribute a sales table on a date column, for example, you should probably get fairly even data distribution, unless most of your sales are seasonal. However, if you commonly use a range-restricted predicate to filter for a narrow date period, most of the filtered rows occur on a limited set of slices and the query workload is skewed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eA fact table with multiple distribution keys is useful when multiple dimension tables have to be joined to it\u003c/strong\u003e - Your fact table can have only one distribution key. Any tables that join on another key aren\u0027t collocated with the fact table. Choose one dimension to collocate based on how frequently it is joined and the size of the joining rows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSmall Dimension tables should be marked to use KEY distribution style, which will cause them to be replicated to each physical node in the cluster\u003c/strong\u003e - In a KEY distribution style, the rows are distributed according to the values in one column. The leader node places matching values on the same node slice. If you distribute a pair of tables on the joining keys, the leader node collocates the rows on the slices according to the values in the joining columns. This way, matching values from the common columns are physically stored together.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA copy of the entire table is distributed to every node in ALL distribution styles.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_choosing_dist_sort.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_choosing_dist_sort.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-best-dist-key.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/c_best-practices-best-dist-key.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:1":{"id":"e:3:1","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 1","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon AppFlow to extract Salesforce opportunities, use AWS Glue to extract MySQL records and correlate the datasets, and use Step Functions to orchestrate the process\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon AppFlow to extract Salesforce opportunities, use AWS Glue to extract MySQL records and correlate the datasets, and use Amazon MWAA to orchestrate the process\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Database Migration Service to continuously stream MySQL changes to Amazon S3, export Salesforce data manually each night, and correlate the files with Athena scheduled queries\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon AppFlow to extract Salesforce opportunities, use Kinesis Data Streams to extract MySQL records, and use Managed Service for Apache Flink to correlate the datasets\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA software company stores daily subscription purchase records in a MySQL database. The sales team also manages a large set of qualified opportunities in Salesforce. The analytics team wants to correlate the two datasets to identify accounts that are likely to convert into new customers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company receives 3 GB of new purchase records each day and maintains 120 GB of Salesforce opportunity data. A data engineer must create a nightly process to extract both datasets, correlate the records, and produce the analysis with the least operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon AppFlow to extract Salesforce opportunities, use AWS Glue to extract MySQL records and correlate the datasets, and use Step Functions to orchestrate the process\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon AppFlow is a managed integration service that can transfer data from SaaS applications such as Salesforce to AWS destinations. This makes it a good fit for extracting the Salesforce opportunity dataset without building and maintaining custom Salesforce API ingestion code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue can connect to the MySQL database by using JDBC and can run the nightly ETL logic that reads the daily purchase records, joins them with the Salesforce opportunity data, and produces the correlated output. The 3 GB daily sales records and 120 GB opportunity dataset are suitable for a batch ETL process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Step Functions can orchestrate the end-to-end workflow with managed state transitions. This keeps the orchestration serverless and avoids the additional operational work of maintaining an Airflow environment when the requirement is a once-per-night workflow with a few managed service steps.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/34ba96a2605cb94f504d2426da0159e94b927e9c0b561c8278e2e08296c5e109.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/34ba96a2605cb94f504d2426da0159e94b927e9c0b561c8278e2e08296c5e109.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/what-is-appflow.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/what-is-appflow.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Database Migration Service to continuously stream MySQL changes to Amazon S3, export Salesforce data manually each night, and correlate the files with Athena scheduled queries\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Database Migration Service can replicate database changes to AWS targets, and Athena can query files in Amazon S3. This can be useful for data lake ingestion and SQL-based analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because it introduces continuous replication for a nightly workload and relies on a manual Salesforce export. The requirement asks for least operational overhead, so a managed Salesforce connector through AppFlow is a better fit.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon AppFlow to extract Salesforce opportunities, use AWS Glue to extract MySQL records and correlate the datasets, and use Amazon MWAA to orchestrate the process\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon AppFlow and AWS Glue are appropriate services for the Salesforce and MySQL portions of the workflow. The extraction and correlation components in this option align with the data sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is the orchestration choice. Amazon MWAA requires an Airflow environment and more operational management than Step Functions. For a nightly managed workflow, Step Functions provides simpler orchestration with less overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon AppFlow to extract Salesforce opportunities, use Kinesis Data Streams to extract MySQL records, and use Managed Service for Apache Flink to correlate the datasets\u003c/strong\u003e - Kinesis Data Streams and Managed Service for Apache Flink can process streaming data. This architecture is useful when events must be analyzed continuously with low latency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not match the requirement because the process runs once each night. Streaming MySQL records into Kinesis and correlating them with Flink adds unnecessary complexity for a scheduled batch analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/salesforce.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/salesforce.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/what-is-appflow.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/appflow/latest/userguide/what-is-appflow.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-jdbc-home.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-jdbc-home.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/connectors-chapter.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/connectors-chapter.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/step-functions/latest/dg/connect-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:42":{"id":"e:4:42","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 42","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue workflows to run a crawler every hour against Amazon RDS, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon OpenSearch Service, then start AWS Glue ETL jobs to process all records that the crawlers discover\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge Scheduler to invoke a Lambda function every hour to check each data store, send processing messages to Amazon SQS when the function finds new data, and use a second Lambda function to process the messages with retries and a dead-letter queue\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun an Apache Spark application on Amazon EMR every hour to check Amazon RDS, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon OpenSearch Service for new records, process the results, and handle errors in the Spark code\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to create a DAG that runs every hour, checks each data store for new records, processes the records, and handles errors in Python operators\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA logistics company needs a fully automated solution to check for new records in several operational data stores and process any records that the solution finds. The solution must run every hour and must work with Amazon RDS, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon OpenSearch Service. Each processing run will handle up to 8 MB of data at a time. The company wants to minimize cost and operational overhead while using reliable error handling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge Scheduler to invoke a Lambda function every hour to check each data store, send processing messages to Amazon SQS when the function finds new data, and use a second Lambda function to process the messages with retries and a dead-letter queue\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon EventBridge Scheduler can invoke an AWS Lambda function on an hourly schedule. The first Lambda function can connect to Amazon RDS, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon OpenSearch Service to check for new data and create processing messages when it finds records that need work.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon SQS decouples the discovery step from the processing step. The message can contain metadata, identifiers, or pointers to the records rather than the entire dataset. A second Lambda function can read messages from the SQS queue, retrieve the relevant data from the source service, and process up to the required 8 MB working set.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has low operational overhead because it uses managed serverless services. It also has robust error handling because Lambda integrations with SQS support retries, visibility timeout behavior, and dead-letter queues for messages that fail processing repeatedly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0b3a96697cca0461fc4289924d3aeea0788359fe06a002554b37bcf01b507b93.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0b3a96697cca0461fc4289924d3aeea0788359fe06a002554b37bcf01b507b93.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-eventbridge-scheduler.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-eventbridge-scheduler.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to create a DAG that runs every hour, checks each data store for new records, processes the records, and handles errors in Python operators\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon MWAA can schedule workflows and implement dependency management through Apache Airflow DAGs. It can also run Python operators that check multiple data stores and process results.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, Amazon MWAA introduces an Airflow environment, DAG operations, worker configuration, monitoring, and platform-level administration. For an hourly process that handles only a small amount of data, Lambda with EventBridge Scheduler and SQS is more cost-effective and operationally simpler.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun an Apache Spark application on Amazon EMR every hour to check Amazon RDS, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon OpenSearch Service for new records, process the results, and handle errors in the Spark code\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EMR with Apache Spark can process large-scale datasets and connect to multiple data sources. Spark also allows developers to implement custom error handling in the application code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, EMR is unnecessarily heavy for a workload that runs hourly and processes only up to 8 MB of data at a time. The company would pay for and manage a big data processing environment when a serverless Lambda-based design is sufficient.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue workflows to run a crawler every hour against Amazon RDS, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon OpenSearch Service, then start AWS Glue ETL jobs to process all records that the crawlers discover\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue crawlers help discover schemas and update the AWS Glue Data Catalog. AWS Glue workflows can orchestrate Glue jobs, crawlers, and triggers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, crawlers are not the correct mechanism to check Amazon OpenSearch Service for new operational records every hour, and they do not provide a lightweight record-processing workflow for small data volumes. This design adds catalog and ETL overhead that the requirement does not need.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-eventbridge-scheduler.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-eventbridge-scheduler.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/gettingstarted-limits.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/gettingstarted-limits.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-sqs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-sqs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-sqs-configure.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-sqs-configure.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-sqs-errorhandling.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/services-sqs-errorhandling.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/lambda-rds-connect.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/lambda-rds-connect.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-29":{"id":"p:3-29","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 29","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka. Create a topic for the initial raw data. Use a Kafka producer to write data on this topic. Use the Apache Kafka consumer API to create a consumer application (that can be hosted on Amazon EC2 instance) that reads data from this topic, transforms the data as needed, and writes it to Amazon S3 for final storage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to ingest data. Configure an AWS Lambda function to cleanse/transform the data written into the Firehose delivery stream which is then delivered to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) to ingest incoming data. Configure an AWS Lambda function to read events from the SQS queue and upload the events to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Configure a stream for incoming raw data. Kinesis Agent can be used to write data to the stream. Configure an Amazon Kinesis Data Analytics application to read the raw data and transform it to the necessary format before writing it to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Kafka/MSK có thể cấu hình giới hạn thông điệp phù hợp bản ghi tới 10 MB. Consumer chuẩn hóa các cột rồi ghi S3; cần đồng bộ giới hạn producer, broker và consumer.","Theo hạn mức bản ghi mà đề sử dụng, Firehose không nhận trực tiếp bản ghi tới 10 MB theo phương án này. Lambda transformation không loại bỏ giới hạn đầu vào.","Bản ghi tới 10 MB vượt kích thước thông điệp trực tiếp của SQS trong đề. Không có bước lưu payload ngoài và gửi con trỏ để giải quyết.","Theo hạn mức Kinesis Data Streams mà đề giả định, bản ghi lớn tới 10 MB không phù hợp. Bộ đề chọn MSK có cấu hình kích thước thông điệp."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company wants to store all of its consumer data on Amazon S3. Before storing the data, the company must clean it by standardizing the formats of a few of the data columns. A single data record might range in size from 500 KB to 10 MB.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of these options represents the right solution?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:53":{"id":"e:1:53","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 53","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate Amazon Redshift row-level security policies that use simple division filters, attach the policies to the shared tables, and grant EXPLAIN RLS permission to the researchers\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eApply Amazon Redshift dynamic data masking policies to the PII columns in the clinical study tables, grant all research divisions SELECT access to the shared base tables, and leverage the masking policies to prevent cross-division access to records\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate one Amazon Redshift datashare for each research division and use cross-Region data sharing to provide each division with a copy of the full clinical study dataset\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate Amazon Redshift row-level security policies that use multi-condition SQL predicates for division membership and data sensitivity, attach the policies to the shared tables, and grant each division role access through the appropriate policies\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA biomedical research organization stores clinical study analysis data in an Amazon Redshift cluster in the us-east-1 Region. Several research divisions need to query the same dataset for regulatory reporting and exploratory analysis. The organization must avoid creating duplicate copies of the dataset. Researchers must see only the records that belong to their own divisions, and the organization must protect personally identifiable information (PII) from users who do not have authorization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate Amazon Redshift row-level security policies that use multi-condition SQL predicates for division membership and data sensitivity, attach the policies to the shared tables, and grant each division role access through the appropriate policies\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift row-level security (RLS) provides granular access control over records in tables and schemas. RLS policies can use SQL predicates to determine which rows a user or role can access. In this scenario, the organization can define predicates that evaluate division membership and other sensitivity attributes so researchers see only the records that match their authorized division.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach avoids creating multiple copies of the dataset. The organization can keep a single shared set of Redshift tables and enforce access at query time through security policies attached to those tables. This design is better than copying data into separate clusters, schemas, or external datasets because it centralizes the governed source of truth.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor PII protection, the organization can use the same security model to restrict rows that contain sensitive records to authorized roles. If the organization also needs column-level PII obfuscation for authorized row access patterns, Amazon Redshift dynamic data masking or column-level privileges can complement RLS. The core requirement for division-specific record access is best met by multi-condition RLS policies attached directly to the shared tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/85f2588d7c2a07cad7256fb8c8db7c135843eaecf5b6de2482ff36df395c4c81.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/85f2588d7c2a07cad7256fb8c8db7c135843eaecf5b6de2482ff36df395c4c81.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/achieve-fine-grained-data-security-with-row-level-access-control-in-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/achieve-fine-grained-data-security-with-row-level-access-control-in-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/cd0ad21457fb409b36e87658e5b46efe10c587c026c2d3e37cb13e880fb6bec6.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/cd0ad21457fb409b36e87658e5b46efe10c587c026c2d3e37cb13e880fb6bec6.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/achieve-fine-grained-data-security-with-row-level-access-control-in-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/achieve-fine-grained-data-security-with-row-level-access-control-in-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eApply Amazon Redshift dynamic data masking policies to the PII columns in the clinical study tables, grant all research divisions SELECT access to the shared base tables, and leverage the masking policies to prevent cross-division access to records\u003c/strong\u003e - Dynamic data masking can help protect sensitive PII columns by changing how values appear at query time for different users or roles. This is useful for privacy controls, especially when some users need access to records but should not see raw sensitive values.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, masking alone does not restrict researchers to records from their own divisions. If all divisions receive SELECT access to the base tables, users could still query rows from other divisions, even if selected PII columns are masked. The solution must enforce row-level access, not only column-level obfuscation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate one Amazon Redshift datashare for each research division and use cross-Region data sharing to provide each division with a copy of the full clinical study dataset\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift data sharing can provide read access to live data without copying or moving it. However, this option says each division receives a copy of the full dataset, which conflicts with the requirement to avoid multiple dataset copies.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also does not enforce division-specific record access. Sharing the entire dataset with each division would expose records across departments unless additional row-level controls are configured.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate Amazon Redshift row-level security policies that use simple division filters, attach the policies to the shared tables, and grant EXPLAIN RLS permission to the researchers\u003c/strong\u003e - Basic SQL filters based only on division are weaker than multi-condition RLS predicates when the data includes sensitive records and compliance requirements. The organization needs a policy model that can account for division membership and data sensitivity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eGranting EXPLAIN RLS is not required for ordinary researchers to query protected data. That permission can expose policy-related details for troubleshooting and administration, but it does not provide the access control mechanism needed to protect PII or enforce division-specific visibility.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_rls.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_rls.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_CREATE_RLS_POLICY.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_CREATE_RLS_POLICY.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_ddm.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_ddm.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/datashare-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/datashare-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/achieve-fine-grained-data-security-with-row-level-access-control-in-amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/achieve-fine-grained-data-security-with-row-level-access-control-in-amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/how-dynamic-data-masking-support-in-amazon-redshift-helps-achieve-data-privacy-and-compliance/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/how-dynamic-data-masking-support-in-amazon-redshift-helps-achieve-data-privacy-and-compliance/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-5":{"id":"p:4-5","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 5","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to set up an Apache Airflow workflow that is deployed on an Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) cluster. The workflow definition should have the first task as the Lambda function and the second task as the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to set up an Apache Airflow workflow that is deployed on an Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) cluster. The workflow definition should have the first task as the Lambda function and the second task as the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an AWS Glue workflow to run the Lambda function and then the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an AWS Step Functions state machine to run the Lambda function and then the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["MWAA là dịch vụ Airflow được AWS quản lý; mô tả triển khai MWAA lên cụm ECS do bạn chọn không đúng mô hình sử dụng dịch vụ. Với chuỗi Lambda rồi Glue đơn giản, Airflow cũng tạo thêm công quản lý không cần thiết.","MWAA không phải một workflow đem triển khai trực tiếp lên cụm EKS của bạn như lựa chọn mô tả. Step Functions tích hợp sẵn Lambda và Glue, phù hợp hơn với yêu cầu ít quản lý.","Glue workflows điều phối các thành phần Glue như job, crawler và trigger. Nó không phải lựa chọn tích hợp trực tiếp thuận tiện cho một bước Lambda rồi một bước Glue như Step Functions.","Step Functions có tích hợp sẵn với Lambda và Glue. State machine có thể gọi Lambda, chạy Glue job, chờ kết quả và xử lý lỗi mà không cần vận hành máy chủ điều phối."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company needs to set up a data pipeline that includes an AWS Lambda function and an AWS Glue job. You have been hired as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to build a solution that has the least management overhead and is fully integrated with AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat do you recommend?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-39":{"id":"p:2-39","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 39","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSELECT * NOT IN customer_account_id, source_system_key FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history;\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSELECT * EXCEPT customer_account_id, source_system_key FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history;\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSELECT * FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history WHERE column_name NOT IN (\u0027customer_account_id\u0027, \u0027source_system_key\u0027);\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSELECT * EXCLUDE customer_account_id, source_system_key FROM analytics.fulfilled_order_history;\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["NOT IN là điều kiện so sánh giá trị, không phải cú pháp loại cột khỏi SELECT * trong Redshift.","EXCEPT thường là toán tử hiệu hai tập kết quả; đây không phải cú pháp Redshift mà đề yêu cầu để bỏ cột khỏi dấu *.","WHERE lọc dòng dựa trên giá trị, không loại tên cột khỏi danh sách SELECT. column_name ở đây cũng không tự đại diện metadata của mọi cột.","SELECT * EXCLUDE liệt kê các cột không muốn trả về trong cú pháp Redshift được đề sử dụng. Tránh phải viết thủ công 118 cột còn lại."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses Amazon Redshift as its enterprise data warehouse. A data engineer must query a table named analytics.fulfilled_order_history, which contains 120 columns. The query must return all columns except the columns named customer_account_id and source_system_key.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich Amazon Redshift SQL statement will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-1":{"id":"p:3-1","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 1","correct":[3,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eTransform the data in each partition to Apache ORC format\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCompress the files in gzip format to improve query performance against the partitions\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePerform bucketing on the data in each partition\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an AWS Glue partition index and leverage partition filtering via the GetPartitions call\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Athena partition projection based on the S3 bucket prefix\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["ORC giảm lượng dữ liệu phải đọc nhưng không giải quyết thời gian lập kế hoạch do phải liệt kê quá nhiều partition.","Nén gzip xử lý kích thước tệp, không giảm công tra cứu metadata partition đang là điểm nghẽn.","Bucketing bên trong partition không loại bỏ số lượng partition cần tìm trong Catalog.","Partition index và lọc khi GetPartitions giúp tìm metadata partition cần thiết mà không phải quét toàn bộ danh sách.","Partition projection cho Athena tính các vị trí partition theo quy tắc thay vì liệt kê chúng từ Catalog. Phù hợp cấu trúc prefix có quy luật."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is encountering slow query performance while executing Amazon Athena queries on datasets stored in an Amazon S3 bucket, with AWS Glue Data Catalog serving as the metadata repository. The data engineer has identified the root cause of the sluggish performance as the excessive number of partitions in the S3 bucket, leading to increased Athena query planning times.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat are the two possible approaches to mitigate this issue and enhance query efficiency (Select two)?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:41":{"id":"e:2:41","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 41","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eColumnValues \"ContactEmail\" matches \"[%@%.%]\" with threshold \u0026gt; 0.1\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eColumnValues \"ContactEmail\" matches \"[%@%.%]\" with threshold \u0026gt; 0.9\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUniqueness \"ContactEmail\" matches \"[%@%.%]\" with threshold \u0026gt; 0.9\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCompleteness \"ContactEmail\" \u0026gt; 0.9\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA regional insurance company stores historical policyholder records in an Amazon Redshift table. A column named ContactEmail contains null values, malformed strings, and some valid email addresses. Several downstream notification and compliance workflows depend on the quality of the ContactEmail column. A data engineer must define an AWS Glue Data Quality rule that fails when fewer than 90% of the values in the ContactEmail column are valid email addresses.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich component of an AWS Glue Data Quality rule will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eColumnValues \"ContactEmail\" matches \"[%@%.%]\" with threshold \u0026gt; 0.9\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe ColumnValues rule type evaluates whether values in a specified column satisfy a condition. In this case, the rule checks whether values in the ContactEmail column match the email-style pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe with threshold clause specifies the percentage of rows that must satisfy the rule condition. Setting the threshold to \u0026gt; 0.9 means the rule passes only when more than 90% of the values in the ContactEmail column match the pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis rule directly meets the requirement because the company needs to validate the percentage of acceptable values in one column. Null entries and malformed values will not satisfy the matching condition, so they reduce the valid percentage and can cause the rule to fail when the valid percentage drops below the required level.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/aae4f5a94ee4a8630e3dfd094e8b90faff08cb8204983087ea45b22bce18d837.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/aae4f5a94ee4a8630e3dfd094e8b90faff08cb8204983087ea45b22bce18d837.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/aws-glue-data-quality-is-generally-available/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/aws-glue-data-quality-is-generally-available/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUniqueness \"ContactEmail\" matches \"[%@%.%]\" with threshold \u0026gt; 0.9\u003c/strong\u003e - The Uniqueness rule type evaluates whether values in a column are unique. It is useful when a column should not contain duplicate values, such as identifiers or keys.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option does not validate whether values are email addresses. A column can contain many unique invalid strings and still pass a uniqueness-focused rule, so this option does not meet the requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eColumnValues \"ContactEmail\" matches \"[%@%.%]\" with threshold \u0026gt; 0.1\u003c/strong\u003e - This option uses the correct ColumnValues rule type and applies a matches condition to the ContactEmail column. However, the threshold is too low for the stated requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA threshold of \u0026gt; 0.1 means the rule can pass when only slightly more than 10% of values match the pattern. The company needs the rule to fail when fewer than 90% of values are valid, so the threshold must be greater than 0.9.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCompleteness \"ContactEmail\" \u0026gt; 0.9\u003c/strong\u003e - The Completeness rule type checks whether values are present and not null. It can help detect missing values in the ContactEmail column.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, completeness alone does not determine whether non-null values are valid email addresses. A malformed string can be complete but still fail the email-format requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types-ColumnValues.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types-ColumnValues.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-gs-studio.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-gs-studio.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/aws-glue-data-quality-is-generally-available/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/aws-glue-data-quality-is-generally-available/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-25":{"id":"p:1-25","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 25","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon EventBridge to detect new objects in the S3 data lake and start an AWS Glue workflow. Use AWS Glue DataBrew to identify and mask sensitive data, and write the prepared output to a curated S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon S3 Event Notifications to invoke an AWS Glue job when new files arrive. Use Amazon Macie to detect and mask sensitive data, and load the processed output into Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest the customer files. Use Amazon Comprehend to detect and mask sensitive values, and write the processed records to Amazon DynamoDB tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure an AWS Lambda function to poll the S3 bucket every 15 minutes, use custom regular expressions to identify sensitive fields, mask matching values in the function code, and write the transformed data to a separate S3 prefix\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["EventBridge nhận sự kiện tệp mới và khởi động Glue workflow; DataBrew nhận diện, che dữ liệu nhạy cảm rồi ghi S3 đã xử lý. Không cần tự viết cơ chế thăm dò và che dữ liệu.","Macie phát hiện dữ liệu nhạy cảm nhưng không phải công cụ biến đổi để trực tiếp che rồi xuất dữ liệu. Đích Redshift cũng không đúng yêu cầu giữ kết quả trên S3.","Tệp đến vài lần mỗi tuần không cần một hệ thống streaming mới. Phương án còn chuyển đầu ra sang DynamoDB, lệch yêu cầu phân tích từ S3.","Polling mỗi 15 phút và tự viết biểu thức phát hiện PII tạo mã cần bảo trì, đồng thời có độ trễ. Cơ chế sự kiện và DataBrew giảm công vận hành hơn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multinational online marketplace receives customer profile files a few times each week in an Amazon S3 data lake in the us-west-2 Region. The analytics team must use the files only after the company automatically detects new arrivals and masks sensitive customer attributes. The company wants to keep the processed data in Amazon S3 for downstream analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-4":{"id":"p:2-4","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 4","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue crawler to run on a daily schedule\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eManually execute the MSCK REPAIR TABLE command from the AWS Glue console on demand\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSchedule an AWS Lambda function to run the AWS Glue CreatePartition API twice each day\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpdate the existing code that writes data to Amazon S3, so that it also invokes the Boto3 AWS Glue create_partition API call\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Crawler chỉ chạy hằng ngày có thể khiến partition mới chưa xuất hiện trong Catalog nhiều giờ. Không đạt độ trễ thấp nhất.","Sửa bảng thủ công phụ thuộc người thực hiện và chậm cập nhật. Đây không phải phương án tự động đồng bộ ngay khi ghi dữ liệu.","Gọi API chỉ hai lần/ngày vẫn để metadata chậm nhiều giờ so với dữ liệu S3.","Ngay trong luồng ghi dữ liệu, gọi Glue create_partition để đăng ký partition mới. Không chờ lịch crawler, nên giảm độ trễ cập nhật Catalog."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company intends to organize its Amazon S3 storage used for a data lake by implementing partitioning that has the S3 object keys in the format: s3://bucket/prefix/year=2023/month=01/day=01. A data engineer is tasked with ensuring that the AWS Glue Data Catalog remains updated in sync with any new partitions added to the S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution would best meet these requirements while minimizing latency?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:1:21":{"id":"e:1:21","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 21","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConvert the CSV logs to one Apache Parquet file per day, partition the data by transaction date in Amazon S3, and use predicate pushdown filters\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConvert the CSV logs into multiple ORC files, partition the data by transaction date in Amazon S3, and use columnar pushdown filters in Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConvert the CSV logs to Avro files, partition the files by transaction date in Amazon S3, and use Athena partition projection for the daily partitions\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eKeep the CSV logs in Amazon S3, compress each daily file with gzip, partition the files by transaction date, and query the compressed files with Athena\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn online grocery company stores daily checkout activity logs in CSV format in Amazon S3. Analysts use Amazon Athena to query only a small subset of columns from the logs on the same day that each log arrives. As order volume grows, Athena query runtimes are increasing, and the data engineering team needs to optimize the dataset for the shortest query times.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConvert the CSV logs into multiple ORC files, partition the data by transaction date in Amazon S3, and use columnar pushdown filters in Athena\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eORC is a columnar file format that is well suited for analytical queries in Athena. Because the analysts query only a subset of attributes, Athena can read the relevant columns instead of scanning every column in row-based CSV files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePartitioning the data by transaction date also helps because the company analyzes logs on the same day the logs arrive. When queries include the date partition, Athena can skip unrelated partitions and read less data from Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCreating multiple ORC files improves parallelism because Athena can split the work across multiple readers. This combination of columnar storage, date partitioning, columnar pushdown, and parallel file processing provides the shortest query times among the available options.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5d455148e0f4a00825541cde30099a27ce50aee7f36a5375dff467df0da1f2f3.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5d455148e0f4a00825541cde30099a27ce50aee7f36a5375dff467df0da1f2f3.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eKeep the CSV logs in Amazon S3, compress each daily file with gzip, partition the files by transaction date, and query the compressed files with Athena\u003c/strong\u003e - Compressing CSV files can reduce storage size and may reduce the amount of data transferred from Amazon S3. Date partitioning can also help Athena avoid scanning unrelated days.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, compressed CSV remains a row-based text format. Athena cannot take full advantage of columnar reads for queries that use only a small subset of attributes, so this option is not as fast as ORC with columnar pushdown.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConvert the CSV logs to Avro files, partition the files by transaction date in Amazon S3, and use Athena partition projection for the daily partitions\u003c/strong\u003e - Avro is a compact row-oriented format that supports schema evolution. Partition projection can also reduce partition metadata lookup overhead when a table has many predictable partitions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, Avro does not provide the same columnar scan benefits as ORC for queries that select only a subset of attributes. The workload is primarily optimized by converting from CSV to a columnar format and using date partitions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConvert the CSV logs to one Apache Parquet file per day, partition the data by transaction date in Amazon S3, and use predicate pushdown filters\u003c/strong\u003e - Apache Parquet is a columnar format and can improve Athena performance compared with CSV. Partitioning by transaction date and using predicate pushdown are also appropriate optimization techniques.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option creates a single Parquet file per day. The correct option uses multiple ORC files to improve parallelism, which better matches the requirement for the shortest query times as transaction volume grows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/columnar-storage.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-s3-throttling-optimizing-your-tables.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-s3-throttling-optimizing-your-tables.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-data-optimization-techniques.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/performance-tuning-data-optimization-techniques.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:58":{"id":"e:4:58","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 58","correct":[0,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable snapshot copying in the source Region and specify the snapshot copy grant name that exists in the destination Region\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a KMS key in the source Region and create a Redshift snapshot copy grant that uses the source Region key\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an S3 bucket in the destination Region, export the encrypted Redshift snapshot to the bucket, and configure S3 replication\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable snapshot copying in the destination Region and specify the snapshot copy grant name that exists in the source Region\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a KMS key in the destination Region and create a Redshift snapshot copy grant that uses that destination KMS key\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company encrypts its Amazon Redshift provisioned cluster by using AWS Key Management Service. The company is implementing a disaster recovery strategy that requires automated snapshot copies to a different AWS Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer must configure cross-Region snapshot copy by using the AWS CLI. The copied snapshots must remain encrypted with a KMS key that is valid in the destination Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a KMS key in the destination Region and create a Redshift snapshot copy grant that uses that destination KMS key\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift requires a snapshot copy grant when copying snapshots of an AWS KMS-encrypted cluster to another Region. The grant permits Amazon Redshift to use a KMS key to encrypt the copied snapshots.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe KMS key must be valid in the destination Region because AWS KMS keys are Regional resources. Creating the KMS key and the snapshot copy grant in the destination Region allows Redshift to encrypt the copied snapshots correctly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis step is necessary for disaster recovery because the company wants the cross-Region snapshot copies to remain encrypted. Without the destination Region KMS key and snapshot copy grant, Redshift cannot use the correct encryption configuration for the copied snapshots.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable snapshot copying in the source Region and specify the snapshot copy grant name that exists in the destination Region\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAfter the destination Region snapshot copy grant exists, the data engineer must enable snapshot copying for the Redshift cluster from the source Region. The enable operation identifies the destination Region for copied snapshots.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor an encrypted Redshift cluster, the enable-snapshot-copy configuration must reference the snapshot copy grant that was created for the destination Region. This tells Redshift which grant to use when encrypting copied snapshots in the target Region.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis step completes the cross-Region copy setup. The destination Region grant handles encryption permissions, and the source Region snapshot copy setting starts the automated copy behavior for disaster recovery.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/beb4d7d3160999c8173aafa5830e816db9de1b760c23791a4a6e9a7d338936a3.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/beb4d7d3160999c8173aafa5830e816db9de1b760c23791a4a6e9a7d338936a3.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/migrate-your-amazon-redshift-cluster-to-another-aws-region/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/migrate-your-amazon-redshift-cluster-to-another-aws-region/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a KMS key in the source Region and create a Redshift snapshot copy grant that uses the source Region key\u003c/strong\u003e - Creating a KMS key in the source Region can support encryption of the source Redshift cluster. The source cluster might already use a KMS key for encryption at rest.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that copied snapshots in another Region must use a KMS key that is valid in the destination Region. A snapshot copy grant tied only to a source Region key does not satisfy the destination Region encryption requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an S3 bucket in the destination Region, export the encrypted Redshift snapshot to the bucket, and configure S3 replication\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon S3 replication can copy S3 objects across Regions. Exporting data to S3 can be useful for some analytics, archival, or lake-based recovery patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because Redshift cross-Region snapshot copy does not require exporting encrypted snapshots to S3. The managed Redshift snapshot copy feature uses a snapshot copy grant and destination Region KMS key.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable snapshot copying in the destination Region and specify the snapshot copy grant name that exists in the source Region\u003c/strong\u003e - Enabling snapshot copy involves configuring the Redshift cluster to copy snapshots to another Region. The snapshot copy grant name is part of the encrypted snapshot copy configuration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option reverses the required direction. The engineer enables snapshot copying from the source Region and references the grant created for the destination Region, not the other way around.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/xregioncopy-kms-encrypted-snapshot.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/xregioncopy-kms-encrypted-snapshot.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/redshift/create-snapshot-copy-grant.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/redshift/create-snapshot-copy-grant.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/redshift/enable-snapshot-copy.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/redshift/enable-snapshot-copy.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/APIReference/API_CreateSnapshotCopyGrant.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/APIReference/API_CreateSnapshotCopyGrant.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/migrate-your-amazon-redshift-cluster-to-another-aws-region/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/migrate-your-amazon-redshift-cluster-to-another-aws-region/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:9":{"id":"e:6:9","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 9","correct":[0],"answers":["Substitution","Decryption","Probabilistic encryption","Nulling out or deletion"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare company has recently started using Amazon Glue DataBrew for data preparation tasks, moving away from previous pipelines that relied on an AWS Lambda user-defined function for custom data masking. A dataset containing patient information, which includes Personally Identifiable Information (PII), is given for processing. To comply with data privacy regulations, the PII data needs to be masked before further processing. The technique chosen for this task should replace the actual PII data with other authentic-looking values to maintain the overall structure and statistical distribution of the data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following data-masking techniques should be used?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSubstitution\u003c/strong\u003e is a data-masking technique that involves replacing actual PII data with other authentic-looking values. This technique is useful in maintaining the overall structure and statistical distribution of the data, which is important for testing or development purposes. For example, a patient’s real name in a healthcare dataset could be replaced with a fictitious but real-sounding name. This way, the sensitive information is protected, but the data remains useful for analysis or testing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/628dfe583a2d0808f4b08b15c75dcc947054415cb8dbe803fe8f5f60bbb74b28.jpg\" alt=\"AWS Glue DataBrew job run history for a PII masking recipe\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSubstitution.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDecryption\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is a process used to convert encrypted data back into its original form. It requires a specific key, which is used to decrypt the encrypted data. In the context of data privacy, decryption would be used to access the original data, not to mask or protect it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eProbabilistic encryption\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is a type of encryption that produces different ciphertext each time it’s applied, even for the same plaintext. This means that if you encrypt the same data twice, you’ll get two different ciphertexts. While this adds an extra layer of security, it’s not the same as replacing PII data with other authentic-looking values. It’s a way to protect data, but it doesn’t maintain the same structure or statistical distribution of the data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eNulling out or deletion\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is a data-masking technique where a particular field is replaced with a null value or the column is deleted entirely. While this does protect sensitive information, it doesn’t replace the PII data with other authentic-looking values. Instead, it removes the data entirely, which can impact the utility of the dataset for testing or development purposes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/personal-information-protection.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/databrew/latest/dg/personal-information-protection.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/what-is/data-masking/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/what-is/data-masking/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-63":{"id":"p:1-63","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 63","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon S3 Event Notification that sends a message to an Amazon SQS queue. Make the Amazon EC2 instances read from the Amazon SQS queue\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon S3 Event Notification that sends a message to an Amazon SNS topic. Subscribe the Amazon EC2 instances to the Amazon SNS topic\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge event that reacts to object uploads in Amazon S3 and invokes one of the Amazon EC2 instances\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSubscribe the Amazon EC2 instances to Amazon S3 Analytics - storage class analysis\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["S3 gửi sự kiện vào SQS, các EC2 cùng lấy việc từ hàng đợi để phân phối xử lý. Với SQS Standard vẫn cần idempotency vì thông điệp có thể được giao lại.","SNS phát fan-out tới các subscriber, khiến nhiều consumer nhận cùng sự kiện. Không phù hợp mục tiêu phân một ảnh cho một worker xử lý.","EventBridge không tự chọn rồi gọi tiến trình ứng dụng bất kỳ trên một EC2 như mô tả. Cần cơ chế phân phối công việc rõ ràng, như SQS.","S3 Analytics phân tích mẫu truy cập để tối ưu lớp lưu trữ. Nó không tạo cơ chế kích hoạt xử lý từng ảnh mới."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA photo-sharing company is storing user profile pictures in an Amazon S3 bucket and an image analysis application is deployed on four Amazon EC2 instances. A data engineer would like to trigger an image analysis procedure only on one of the four Amazon EC2 instances for each photo uploaded.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat do you recommend?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-65":{"id":"p:2-65","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 65","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Versioning on the bucket, use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new documents, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and expire versions after 6 years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Object Lock on the bucket, use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new documents, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Deep Archive, and expire versions after 6 years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Versioning and S3 MFA Delete on the bucket, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and use Lifecycle expiration after 6 years\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Object Lock on the bucket, use S3 Intelligent-Tiering for new documents, transition object versions older than 15 months to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and expire versions after 6 years\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Versioning giữ lịch sử nhưng người có quyền vẫn có thể xóa version vĩnh viễn. Nó không tự thực thi WORM sáu năm.","Object Lock đúng cho WORM, nhưng Deep Archive không đáp ứng thời gian khôi phục trong 5 giờ.","MFA Delete tăng xác thực khi xóa nhưng không khóa bất biến theo thời hạn như Object Lock. Không thay thế yêu cầu WORM.","Object Lock bảo vệ từng version trong thời gian retention; Glacier Flexible Retrieval đáp ứng mục tiêu khôi phục theo chế độ phù hợp. Lifecycle chỉ xóa vĩnh viễn khi đã hết bảo vệ sáu năm, cần quản lý cả current/noncurrent versions."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA legal services company stores signed case documents in an Amazon S3 bucket. Every object version must remain immutable for 6 years and must follow a write-once-read-many retention model. The company also needs to reduce storage cost for older documents. Any object version that is older than 15 months must be retrievable within 5 hours, and the company can permanently delete object versions after the 6-year retention period ends.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-61":{"id":"p:1-61","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 61","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse EventBridge Scheduler archive and replay to replay returned order events and compare successful workflow runs with failed workflow runs\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate a CloudWatch alarm on the FailedInvocations metric and configure the alarm to invoke a Lambda function that restarts the returned order workflow\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure a dead-letter queue for the EventBridge Scheduler schedules, store failed invocation events in the queue, and analyze the returned order failure details\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eReduce the maximum retry attempts value in the EventBridge Scheduler retry policy so failed returned order invocations fail faster\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Archive/replay là tính năng của EventBridge event bus, không phải cơ chế tương ứng của Scheduler để thu thập chi tiết gọi đích thất bại.","Alarm có thể cảnh báo số lỗi, nhưng tự khởi động lại không cung cấp bản sự kiện và thông tin thất bại để điều tra đầy đủ.","DLQ của Scheduler giữ các sự kiện gọi đích không thành công sau xử lý retry. Từ đó đọc chi tiết để tìm nguyên nhân workflow cho đơn hàng trả lại bị lỗi.","Giảm số lần retry chỉ làm dừng thử sớm hơn. Nó không thu thập thông tin cần thiết để phân tích nguyên nhân."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company uses AWS Glue ETL workflows to process ecommerce order events. The company processes submitted, packed, shipped, and returned orders through separate AWS Glue workflows because each order status requires different transformation logic. The order management application sends events to Amazon EventBridge Scheduler configurations that invoke the appropriate AWS Glue workflow for each order status. The data engineering team reviews CloudWatch metrics and notices that the FailedInvocations metric is high for returned order schedules. The team must identify the cause of the failed invocations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:44":{"id":"e:3:44","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 44","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable CloudTrail data events for the S3 bucket, send the events to CloudWatch Logs, and create CloudWatch alarms for policy-violation patterns\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse S3 server access logging, deliver logs to another S3 bucket, and manually query requester fields when the security team investigates violations\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Macie to classify sensitive objects in the S3 bucket and send findings to the security team when new sensitive files appear\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Config managed rules to evaluate S3 bucket compliance, create compliance alarms, and notify the security team when the bucket becomes noncompliant\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA pharmaceutical research company stores processed clinical analytics data in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company uses IAM roles to give research, compliance, and analytics teams different levels of access to the bucket. The company has a strict data access policy that defines which users and roles can read, write, or delete objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe security team wants to receive a notification whenever a user violates the data access policy. Each notification must include the identity of the user who performed the unauthorized S3 object-level action.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable CloudTrail data events for the S3 bucket, send the events to CloudWatch Logs, and create CloudWatch alarms for policy-violation patterns\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS CloudTrail data events can record object-level activity for Amazon S3 buckets. These events can capture actions such as object reads, writes, and deletes, which makes CloudTrail the right source for detecting violations of an S3 object access policy.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eCloudTrail event records include identity information about the principal that made the request. This helps the company include the user or role identity in the notification when a policy-violation pattern is detected.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSending CloudTrail events to CloudWatch Logs allows the team to create metric filters and alarms for specific access patterns. This solution aligns with the requirement to monitor S3 object-level access and notify the company when a user violates the data access policy.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Config managed rules to evaluate S3 bucket compliance, create compliance alarms, and notify the security team when the bucket becomes noncompliant\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Config can evaluate resource configurations against rules. It is useful for detecting configuration drift, such as whether a bucket has public access settings or encryption enabled.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that AWS Config does not track every S3 object-level access attempt by user. The company needs event-level audit data that includes the identity of the user who performed the action.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse S3 server access logging, deliver logs to another S3 bucket, and manually query requester fields when the security team investigates violations\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 server access logs can record requests made to a bucket and can include requester information. They can help with retrospective access analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because the workflow described relies on manual investigation instead of automated notification. CloudTrail data events integrated with CloudWatch Logs provide a more direct monitoring and alerting path for policy-violation patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Macie to classify sensitive objects in the S3 bucket and send findings to the security team when new sensitive files appear\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Macie can discover and classify sensitive data in Amazon S3. It is useful for identifying sensitive objects and generating findings about data exposure risks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis does not meet the requirement because the company needs notifications for user access-policy violations. Classifying sensitive files does not identify each unauthorized object-level action with the username that performed it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/logging-data-events-with-cloudtrail.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-logging.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-logging.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/send-cloudtrail-events-to-cloudwatch-logs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/send-cloudtrail-events-to-cloudwatch-logs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/ServerLogs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/ServerLogs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-31":{"id":"p:1-31","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 31","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCheck the Step Functions TaskFailed event records in the /aws/vendedlogs/states log group\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCheck the Lambda function logs in the /aws/lambda/normalizeRecords log group\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCheck the AWS X-Ray trace segment logs in the /aws/xray/normalizeRecords log group because X-Ray stores the Lambda runtime exception details and Step Functions state transition metadata together\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCheck the Step Functions TaskSucceeded event records in the /aws/vendedlogs/states log group\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Log Step Functions thể hiện sự kiện của workflow, không chứa đầy đủ lỗi runtime bên trong Lambda. Mức FATAL còn hạn chế những sự kiện được ghi.","Lỗi thực thi và stack trace của hàm nằm trong CloudWatch log group /aws/lambda/normalizeRecords khi logging đã bật. Đây là nơi trực tiếp để tìm nguyên nhân bên trong hàm.","X-Ray hỗ trợ tracing, không tự tạo log group theo mẫu được nêu để gộp toàn bộ exception Lambda và lịch sử Step Functions. Cần đọc log của hàm.","TaskSucceeded mô tả bước thành công, không phải nơi điều tra lỗi thực thi của Lambda. Tìm log /aws/lambda/normalizeRecords."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial analytics company uses AWS Step Functions to orchestrate a serverless data preparation pipeline. The company configured the Step Functions state machine to send logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs with the log level set to FATAL. The company also enabled logging for every AWS service that the pipeline invokes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA state named \"normalizeRecords\" invokes an AWS Lambda function named \"normalizeRecords\". The Lambda function validates and normalizes incoming transaction records before the workflow continues to the next state. The operations team needs to find detailed error information if an error occurs inside the Lambda function during normalization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich CloudWatch Logs log group should the operations team check to find the error details?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:7:37":{"id":"e:7:37","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 37","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eUNLOAD\u003c/code\u003e command to export data from Amazon Redshift to Amazon S3 in Parquet format.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e command to export data from Amazon Redshift to Amazon S3.\u003c/span\u003e","Store infrequently accessed data in Amazon Redshift.","\u003cspan\u003eCreate an external table in Amazon Redshift Spectrum and load the data from Amazon Redshift using the \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e command.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is using Amazon Redshift for its data warehousing needs. The company noticed that over 5 TB of its stored content is infrequently accessed. The company wants to optimize costs and storage space for this data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options should the company choose to meet its requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003eUNLOAD\u003c/code\u003e command in Amazon Redshift allows you to unload the result of a query to Amazon S3. This command is particularly useful for exporting data that is not frequently accessed, thereby saving storage space in Redshift and reducing costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003eUNLOAD\u003c/code\u003e command supports exporting data in various formats, including CSV, JSON, and Parquet. Parquet is an efficient open columnar storage format for analytics. It is up to 2x faster to unload and consumes up to 6x less storage in Amazon S3 compared to text formats. This makes Parquet a good choice for storing large volumes of infrequently accessed data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/a9068ce28f6c24a15d6677532f03e568996facab16c2f91cc7cb828aa7c3fa4c.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 is a cost-effective storage service. The cost of storing data in S3 depends on the amount of data stored, the duration of storage, and the storage class. By exporting infrequently accessed data from Redshift to S3, the company can optimize its storage costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Use the \u003ccode\u003eUNLOAD\u003c/code\u003e command to export data from Amazon Redshift to Amazon S3 in Parquet format.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse the \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e command to export data from Amazon Redshift to Amazon S3 \u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because the \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e command is used to load data into Amazon Redshift from data files from an Amazon DynamoDB table. It’s not used to export data from Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore infrequently accessed data in Amazon Redshift \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because storing infrequently accessed data in Amazon Redshift can be expensive as Redshift is designed for high-performance analysis and not for storing infrequently accessed data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an external table in Amazon Redshift Spectrum and load the data from Amazon Redshift using the \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e command \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Redshift Spectrum is simply a feature that allows you to query data directly in S3 using Redshift SQL queries without having to load the data into Redshift. You would typically use Redshift Spectrum to query data already in S3. Once the data is in S3, you can create external tables that reference this data. Redshift Spectrum does not have its own built-in storage; rather, it serves as a bridge for querying external data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReference:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_UNLOAD.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_UNLOAD.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:39":{"id":"e:6:39","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 39","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eAttach the \u003ccode\u003eAmazonSageMakerFullAccess\u003c/code\u003e managed policy to the SageMaker’s execution role.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eAttach the \u003ccode\u003eAwsGlueSessionUserRestrictedServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e managed policy to the SageMaker’s execution role.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eEnsure the data engineer\u0027s IAM user has permission to perform the \u003ccode\u003ests:AssumeRole\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003esagemaker:AddAssociation\u003c/code\u003e actions.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eAdd the \u003ccode\u003eAWSGlueServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e managed policy to the IAM user of the data engineer.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is trying to use AWS Glue Interactive Sessions in Amazon SageMaker Studio for data preparation tasks. The data engineer has successfully created a SageMaker IAM role with trust policies for both SageMaker and Glue services. The interactive session is being incorporated into an ML workflow that prepares data for subsequent analytical tasks. Despite this setup, the data engineer is being denied access when attempting to initiate an Glue Interactive Session.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHow can the data engineer resolve the issue?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon SageMaker Studio is an integrated development environment (IDE) for machine learning that provides every tool needed to build, train, and deploy machine learning models. SageMaker offers a user-friendly interface for executing ML workflows, making its tools accessible across various integrated development environments (IDEs). Additionally, It conducts operations on your behalf by leveraging other AWS services. You need to assign permissions to enable SageMaker to utilize these services and access the corresponding resources. This permission is achieved through an AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) execution role, granting SageMaker the necessary permissions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5f9a876af9a6e759a735c721d871167ff49a330cf58f56bb6561f9c382016743.png\" alt=\"Amazon SageMaker console Setting up an IAM role using Role Manager\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Interactive Sessions provides a programmatic and visual interface for building and testing ETL scripts for data preparation. To use it in Amazon SageMaker Studio, the SageMaker execution role needs to have the necessary permissions for AWS Glue. This can be achieved by attaching the \u003ccode\u003eAwsGlueSessionUserRestrictedServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e managed policy to the SageMaker execution role.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe \u003ccode\u003eAwsGlueSessionUserRestrictedServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e policy grants the required permissions for creating and managing AWS Glue Interactive Sessions, as well as accessing the AWS Glue Data Catalog and other related resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAttach the \u003ccode\u003eAwsGlueSessionUserRestrictedServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e managed policy to the SageMaker’s execution role.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Attach the \u003ccode\u003eAmazonSageMakerFullAccess\u003c/code\u003e managed policy to the SageMaker’s execution role.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;This policy just grants all actions across Amazon SageMaker, which is not required for the specific task of using AWS Glue Interactive Sessions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnsure the data engineer\u0027s IAM user has permission to perform the \u003ccode\u003ests:AssumeRole\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003esagemaker:AddAssociation\u003c/code\u003e actions\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ests:AssumeRole\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;is required to assume an IAM role, this permission is not specifically needed for using AWS Glue Interactive Sessions. Additionally, the \u003ccode\u003esagemaker:AddAssociation\u003c/code\u003e permission is only used for associating resources with a SageMaker notebook instance or studio, but it\u0027s not directly related to AWS Glue Interactive Sessions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eAdd the \u003ccode\u003eAWSGlueServiceRole\u003c/code\u003e managed policy to the IAM user of the data engineer\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect because this policy is typically used to grant AWS Glue the permissions it needs to execute jobs and does not apply to the SageMaker execution role\u0027s permissions for AWS Glue Interactive Sessions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/getting-started-glue-sm.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/getting-started-glue-sm.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/sagemaker-roles.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/sagemaker-roles.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these AWS Glue and Amazon SageMaker AI Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-37":{"id":"p:3-37","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 37","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the JSON files in Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the nested attributes separately from the Redshift warehouse\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue to flatten every JSON attribute into separate relational columns before loading the data into the Redshift target table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDefine the target column with the SUPER data type and load the semi-structured JSON values into the Redshift table\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConvert the JSON attribute into a VARCHAR column, store the raw JSON string, and parse every nested field manually in each downstream SQL query\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Đưa JSON sang Athena riêng khiến phân tích với dữ liệu Redshift phải phối hợp thêm hệ thống. Đề muốn nạp thuộc tính này vào kho hiện tại.","Flatten toàn bộ JSON cần ETL và thay đổi schema khi cấu trúc biến động. SUPER giữ cấu trúc lồng nhau với ít công hơn.","Kiểu SUPER của Redshift lưu dữ liệu bán cấu trúc như JSON lồng nhau. Cho phép truy vấn thuộc tính và kết hợp với bảng trong kho mà không cần làm phẳng toàn bộ trước.","VARCHAR giữ chuỗi JSON nhưng phải parse nhiều lần và tự xử lý các trường. SUPER được thiết kế trực tiếp cho dữ liệu bán cấu trúc."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA marketing analytics company receives customer enrichment records from an external data provider and loads the records into an Amazon Redshift data warehouse. The same warehouse already stores campaign data and purchase history. Analysts want to combine these datasets to identify customers who are likely to respond to future offers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDuring ingestion, a data engineer discovers that one attribute in the third-party feed contains nested JSON values with varying structures. The company wants to load this field into Amazon Redshift with the least transformation effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow should the data engineer load the JSON field into the data warehouse?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:16":{"id":"e:5:16","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 16","correct":[3],"answers":["Utilize AWS CloudFormation to define and deploy serverless resources through infrastructure such as Code (IaC).","Leverage AWS CodeBuild for compiling source code, running tests, and producing software packages ready to deploy.","Configure AWS Systems Manager (SSM) to automate the management and deployment of serverless components.","Use AWS Serverless Application Model (SAM) to define, package, and deploy serverless applications and resources."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is building a serverless data pipeline to process and analyze streaming data with AWS. The pipeline mainly consists of several Lambda functions orchestrated by AWS Step Functions. The company seeks a way to create, manage, and deploy its pipeline with minimal overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution meets these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe \u003cstrong\u003eAWS Serverless Application Model (AWS SAM)\u003c/strong\u003e is an essential framework that simplifies the process of developing serverless applications. It is an open-source tool that uses a concise syntax to represent the fundamental components of serverless architectures, such as functions, APIs, databases, and event source mappings. With AWS SAM, developers can define their serverless applications using a minimal amount of code by using YAML for the modeling process. The integration of AWS SAM with AWS CloudFormation provides an effortless way of defining crucial elements of serverless applications, including Amazon API Gateway APIs, AWS Lambda functions, and Amazon DynamoDB tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003ccenter\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/53159d3c3969db9d76dabbcf54d309857f56ab60596a44f0d71731fb22d317bc.png\"\u003e\u003c/center\u003eIn the scenario, using SAM leads to writing more concise code for serverless resources like AWS Lambda and AWS Step Functions. This conciseness simplifies infrastructure management and deployment. Additionally, SAM enables local testing of Lambda functions, which speeds up development by allowing for fast iterations and immediate feedback without deploying to AWS. This set of features streamlines the development and deployment process of serverless data pipelines.\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Serverless Application Model (SAM) to define, package, and deploy serverless applications and resources.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize AWS CloudFormation for defining and deploying serverless resources through Infrastructure as Code (IaC)\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While it’s a valid solution writing native CloudFormation resources requires more verbose and detailed definitions compared to AWS SAM, especially when it comes to serverless resources like Lambda functions and Step Functions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure AWS Systems Manager (SSM) to automate the management and deployment of serverless components\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;AWS Systems Manager (SSM) is just a management service that helps you automatically apply operational actions across your AWS resources. This service primarily focuses on operational insights, management, and resource automation once deployed and not on\u0026nbsp;deploying serverless applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage AWS CodeBuild for compiling source code, running tests, and producing software packages ready to deploy\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. AWS CodeBuild focuses on handling the building stage of your application lifecycle rather than defining, packaging, and deploying serverless resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/serverless/sam/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/serverless/sam/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/what-is-sam.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/serverless-application-model/latest/developerguide/what-is-sam.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Serverless Application Model Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-serverless-application-model-sam/\"\u003e\u003cspan\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-serverless-application-model-sam/\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-33":{"id":"p:4-33","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 33","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse SageMaker Data Wrangler to generate a Data Quality and Insights report, and review the report before writing each dataset to storage\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue ETL jobs with SQL transforms to check required columns for null values and run separate SQL queries for referential integrity\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Lake Formation permissions to restrict access to incomplete records and apply LF-Tags to separate valid records from invalid records\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue ETL jobs with an Evaluate Data Quality transform, IsComplete rules for required columns, and ReferentialIntegrity rules for related datasets\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Báo cáo của Data Wrangler hữu ích khi khảo sát dữ liệu, nhưng việc đọc báo cáo không phải kiểm tra tự động lặp lại trong Glue ETL. Đề cần bước xác thực ngay trước khi lưu đầu ra.","SQL tùy chỉnh có thể phát hiện null và khóa không khớp, nhưng cần tự viết, duy trì và tổng hợp kết quả. Glue Data Quality có quy tắc chuyên dụng nên ít công hơn.","Lake Formation quản lý quyền truy cập; LF-Tags phân loại tài nguyên để phân quyền. Chúng không thực hiện kiểm tra null hoặc tính toàn vẹn tham chiếu của bản ghi.","Evaluate Data Quality tích hợp kiểm tra ngay trong Glue ETL. IsComplete phát hiện giá trị thiếu ở cột bắt buộc; ReferentialIntegrity kiểm tra giá trị có tồn tại trong dữ liệu tham chiếu hay không."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is building a new data pipeline that prepares customer activity data for executive dashboards. Business users report that some dashboard records are incomplete because required fields are missing and some records do not match the related reference data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer must add data quality checks before the pipeline writes the curated output to storage. The checks must detect null values in required columns and validate referential integrity against related datasets with the least operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:3:51":{"id":"e:3:51","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 51","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eChange the bucket default encryption to server-side encryption with customer-provided keys and use S3 Bucket Keys to validate uploaded objects\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDownload every uploaded patient billing document from Amazon S3 after upload and run a custom program to compare the files with source copies\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable S3 Object Lock in governance mode on the bucket so uploaded patient billing documents cannot be accidentally overwritten or deleted\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCalculate a SHA-256 checksum for each document before upload and pass the checksum value to the AWS SDK in each Amazon S3 upload request\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare company uploads confidential patient billing documents to an Amazon S3 bucket each night. The data engineering team must independently confirm that each uploaded object is identical to the source file and that no corruption occurred during the transfer. The solution must validate object integrity as part of the upload process.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCalculate a SHA-256 checksum for each document before upload and pass the checksum value to the AWS SDK in each Amazon S3 upload request\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 supports checksum-based integrity validation for object uploads. The data engineering team can calculate a SHA-256 checksum for each document before upload and include the checksum value in the upload request through the AWS SDK.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 validates the uploaded object against the checksum value that the client provides. If the checksum does not match, the upload fails instead of storing a corrupted object. This helps confirm that the uploaded object is identical to the data that the client intended to send.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution meets the requirement because it independently verifies data integrity during the transfer process. It avoids manual review, avoids downloading all objects after upload, and uses native S3 checksum support instead of building a separate validation workflow.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable S3 Object Lock in governance mode on the bucket so uploaded patient billing documents cannot be accidentally overwritten or deleted\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Object Lock in governance mode can help prevent protected objects from being overwritten or deleted unless a user has special bypass permissions. This is useful for retention and immutability use cases.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, Object Lock does not verify that the upload completed without corruption. It can preserve an object after upload, but it cannot prove that the stored object matches the original source file.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange the bucket default encryption to server-side encryption with customer-provided keys and use S3 Bucket Keys to validate uploaded objects\u003c/strong\u003e - Server-side encryption with customer-provided keys can encrypt objects at rest by using keys supplied by the customer. Encryption protects confidentiality of stored data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, encryption settings do not independently validate that the uploaded data matches the source file. S3 Bucket Keys are also associated with reducing request costs for SSE-KMS, not validating object integrity for SSE-C uploads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDownload every uploaded patient billing document from Amazon S3 after upload and run a custom program to compare the files with source copies\u003c/strong\u003e - Downloading every object after upload and comparing it with the source file could validate integrity if implemented correctly. A programmatic comparison is more complete than a manual sample review.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this approach requires a second full data transfer, custom comparison code, extra processing, and ongoing operational maintenance. Passing checksums during upload validates integrity more directly and efficiently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/checking-object-integrity.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/checking-object-integrity.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/checking-object-integrity-upload.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/checking-object-integrity-upload.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdkref/latest/guide/feature-dataintegrity.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sdkref/latest/guide/feature-dataintegrity.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-31":{"id":"p:4-31","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 31","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure multiple per-workgroup limits by utilizing the workgroup-wide data usage control limit on Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure multiple per-query limits by utilizing the per-query cost control limit feature on Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure a single per-query limit by configuring all the mentioned thresholds into a single limit configuration\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure a single per-workgroup limit by configuring all the mentioned thresholds into a single limit configuration\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Thiết lập nhiều ngưỡng tổng dữ liệu quét cho workgroup theo các khoảng giờ hoặc ngày để theo dõi sử dụng. Các ngưỡng này dùng để cảnh báo; không nên nhầm với giới hạn từng truy vấn tự hủy query khi vượt mức.","Per-query limit áp dụng cho dữ liệu quét của từng truy vấn, không cộng dồn theo giờ hoặc ngày. Một workgroup chỉ có một giới hạn loại này theo cơ chế nêu trong đề.","Gộp nhiều ngưỡng tổng theo thời gian thành một per-query limit không đúng loại kiểm soát. Đề quan tâm tổng sử dụng của workgroup, không phải kích thước một query.","Một ngưỡng workgroup không biểu diễn đồng thời nhiều mốc cảnh báo với các khoảng thời gian khác nhau. Cần cấu hình nhiều ngưỡng như lựa chọn đúng."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA consulting firm uses Amazon Athena to analyze data in Amazon S3 using SQL. A rapid expansion plan has resulted in the company doubling its data engineers in a year resulting in high usage costs for Athena. Preliminary investigation suggests that most of the day-to-day queries run for only a few seconds fetching limited data. The firm wants to define different thresholds on hourly or daily aggregates on data scanned by the queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate, how will you configure a solution for this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-13":{"id":"p:2-13","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 13","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDeploy separate Amazon Redshift clusters for ETL and analytics workloads, use Amazon Redshift data sharing to expose live data to the analytics cluster, and use cross-database queries when users need consistent access across shared databases\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Spectrum for all analytical queries against data in Amazon S3, and continue running ETL updates on the Amazon Redshift dimension tables with transaction isolation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the workload management concurrency slots on the existing Amazon Redshift cluster, assign ETL queries and reporting queries to separate queues, and keep both workloads running against the same updated dimension tables\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon Redshift federated queries to access operational source databases directly, create read replicas for the source systems, and route analytical queries to the read replicas instead of Redshift tables\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Tách tài nguyên tính toán ETL và phân tích, dùng Redshift data sharing để truy cập dữ liệu dùng chung. Theo lời giải, cách này giảm cạnh tranh workload và hỗ trợ truy cập nhất quán mà không nhân bản bằng ETL riêng.","Spectrum truy vấn S3 nhưng chưa giải quyết trực tiếp việc cập nhật các bảng Redshift và yêu cầu dữ liệu báo cáo nhất quán. Còn cần đồng bộ thêm một bản trên S3.","Thêm concurrency slot và tách queue không loại bỏ tranh chấp trên cùng dữ liệu đang cập nhật. Có thể làm cạnh tranh tài nguyên tăng thêm.","Đọc source database qua federated query và replica thay đổi đường dữ liệu. Không giải quyết trực tiếp bài toán báo cáo trên các bảng Redshift hiện có."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics company runs several concurrent reporting queries on Amazon Redshift sales and inventory tables throughout the day. Business users require consistent reporting snapshots four times each day. The company also runs ETL jobs that update product, customer, and store dimension tables while analysts run dashboard and ad hoc queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company has found that concurrent reporting workloads create lock contention during ETL updates. During peak business hours, dashboards experience query timeouts and some ETL transactions encounter deadlocks. The company needs to isolate the reporting workload from the ETL workload while maintaining consistent access to current data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will fix this issue?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-11":{"id":"p:2-11","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 11","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as \u003ccode\u003es3:*\u003c/code\u003e. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the destination as Amazon EventBridge and trigger the Lambda function from EventBridge\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as \u003ccode\u003es3:*\u003c/code\u003e. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the ARN of the Lambda function as the destination for the event notification\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as \u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated:*\u003c/code\u003e. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the ARN of the Lambda function as the destination for the event notification\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as \u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated:*\u003c/code\u003e. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the destination as Amazon EventBridge and trigger the Lambda function from EventBridge\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["s3:* không phải cấu hình sự kiện tạo đối tượng cụ thể cần dùng. Thêm EventBridge cũng không cần thiết cho một Lambda chuyển định dạng đơn giản.","Cần giới hạn sự kiện tạo đối tượng bằng s3:ObjectCreated:*. Khai báo s3:* như lựa chọn không thể hiện đúng điều kiện kích hoạt.","Lọc sự kiện s3:ObjectCreated:* và suffix .csv, rồi gọi Lambda trực tiếp. Chỉ xử lý tệp CSV mới và không thêm tầng điều phối.","Dùng EventBridge có thể thiết kế được theo cơ chế riêng, nhưng cấu hình S3 notification tới Lambda trực tiếp đơn giản hơn cho yêu cầu này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uploads all of its data to an Amazon S3 bucket. While multiple formats of data can be uploaded to the bucket, the downstream applications need to consume data only the .csv files are uploaded. These .csv files have to be transformed into Apache Parquet format for downstream consumption.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:16":{"id":"e:2:16","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 16","correct":[1,3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena to analyze the SQL statements being run on the RDS instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eActivate Enhanced Monitoring to view CPU utilization information for the RDS MySQL DB instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eActivate Amazon CloudWatch Events and review the event data that has the SQL statements behind the CPU spikes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable RDS Performance Insights and review the appropriate dashboard to visualize the database load and filter the load by waits, SQL statements, hosts, or users\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eImplement ElastiCache in front of the RDS DB instance to reduce the CPU load on the RDS instance\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team at an e-commerce company is doing a root-cause analysis for a recent spike in CPU utilization for an RDS MySQL DB instance that caused the application to perform poorly. The standard metrics available in Amazon CloudWatch are not enough to guide the investigation. The company has hired you as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to determine what caused the CPU spike.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following steps would you recommend to provide more details about the underlying processes and queries resulting in an increase in the CPU load? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eMonitoring is an important part of maintaining the reliability, availability, and performance of Amazon RDS. You can monitor network throughput, client connections, I/O for read, write, or metadata operations, and burst credit balances for your DB instances. AWS provides various tools that you can use to monitor Amazon RDS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8b84d4243c70b1012263f33862f64b8164be4553649bda45a86c48e10d5462ad.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/8b84d4243c70b1012263f33862f64b8164be4553649bda45a86c48e10d5462ad.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/MonitoringOverview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/MonitoringOverview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3919fc78ca9b19bef939984bbc43d2913d4dfcdf8611120b4ce4f8d02b0469d6.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3919fc78ca9b19bef939984bbc43d2913d4dfcdf8611120b4ce4f8d02b0469d6.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/MonitoringOverview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/MonitoringOverview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eActivate Enhanced Monitoring to view CPU utilization information for the RDS MySQL DB instance\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use Enhanced Monitoring to capture metrics in real-time for the operating system (OS) that your DB instance runs on. Enhanced Monitoring metrics are useful for the given use case as it allows you to see how the different processes or threads on a DB instance use the CPU. By default, Enhanced Monitoring metrics are stored for 30 days in the CloudWatch Logs, which are different from typical CloudWatch metrics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/53221a8ba628577928440f0d6625ae698f2df2d065bc5453ef463ed009c40589.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/53221a8ba628577928440f0d6625ae698f2df2d065bc5453ef463ed009c40589.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_Monitoring.OS.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_Monitoring.OS.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable RDS Performance Insights and review the appropriate dashboard to visualize the database load and filter the load by waits, SQL statements, hosts, or users\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePerformance Insights allows you to understand your database\u0027s performance and help you analyze any issues that affect it. With the Performance Insights dashboard, you can visualize the database load and filter the load by waits, SQL statements, hosts, or users.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use Performance Insights for the given use case as it allows you to see the underlying SQL statements causing an increase in the database load.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe central metric for Performance Insights is DB Load, which represents the average number of active sessions for the DB engine. The DB Load metric is collected every second. An active session is a connection that has submitted work to the DB engine and is waiting for a response. For example, if you submit a SQL query to the DB engine, the database session is active while the DB engine is processing the query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/14cb7e1fbee4594c29c95a73903c695ae432ff0bc2465f31fc6db79fcf9fee9b.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/14cb7e1fbee4594c29c95a73903c695ae432ff0bc2465f31fc6db79fcf9fee9b.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PerfInsights.UsingDashboard.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PerfInsights.UsingDashboard.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement ElastiCache in front of the RDS DB instance to reduce the CPU load on the RDS instance\u003c/strong\u003e - You can apply a band-aid on the underlying issue by using ElastiCache as it can potentially decrease the CPU load on the RDS DB instance, however, it will present no insights about the underlying processes and queries resulting in an increase in the CPU load.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Athena to analyze the SQL statements being run on the RDS instance\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Athena is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data directly in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. Athena is serverless, so there is no infrastructure to set up or manage, and customers pay only for the queries they run. You can use Athena to process logs, perform ad-hoc analysis, and run interactive queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eRecently Athena has introduced federated queries that can be used to quickly analyze records from different sources such as RDS MySQL. However, you still cannot use it to analyze the SQL statements being run on the RDS instance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eActivate Amazon CloudWatch Events and review the event data that has the SQL statements behind the CPU spikes\u003c/strong\u003e - This is a made-up option as CloudWatch Events do not contain SQL statements in the event data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/MonitoringOverview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/MonitoringOverview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_Monitoring.OS.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_Monitoring.OS.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PerfInsights.UsingDashboard.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PerfInsights.UsingDashboard.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-65":{"id":"p:3-65","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 65","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Quick Suite dashboards with federated query access to Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Quick Suite dashboards with zero-ETL access to Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eBuild a custom Amazon Bedrock application that converts natural language prompts into SQL queries, runs the generated SQL against Amazon Redshift, and renders the results in a custom dashboard interface\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable Amazon Q in Amazon Quick Suite and generate Amazon Quick Suite dashboards and reports by using natural language prompts\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Kết nối truy vấn liên nguồn chỉ giải quyết truy cập dữ liệu, không tạo báo cáo/dashboard bằng ngôn ngữ tự nhiên.","Zero-ETL giải quyết di chuyển dữ liệu, không tự cung cấp khả năng tạo biểu đồ bằng lời nói cho analyst không biết SQL.","Tự xây ứng dụng Bedrock cần quản lý chuyển câu hỏi thành SQL, bảo vệ truy vấn và giao diện báo cáo. Công phát triển lớn hơn tính năng có sẵn.","Amazon Q trong Quick Suite theo tên gọi của bộ đề hỗ trợ tạo nội dung BI bằng mô tả ngôn ngữ tự nhiên. Analyst có thể xây báo cáo/dashboard mà giảm nhu cầu tự viết SQL."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multinational retail company stores sales and inventory data in Amazon Redshift. The company uses Amazon Quick Suite to build operational dashboards and management reports. Business analysts in different regions have different levels of technical expertise. Several analysts do not know SQL, but they frequently need to create new reports and dashboards for business stakeholders. The company wants analysts to use natural language prompts to create dashboards and reports more efficiently with the least operational effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:49":{"id":"e:4:49","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 49","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon SQS standard queues to collect clickstream events, invoke AWS Lambda functions to poll the queues, and write the processed records to Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon MSK brokers to collect clickstream events and store the processed event data in an Amazon RDS DB instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to collect clickstream events and use Amazon Data Firehose to deliver the data to an Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose to ingest clickstream events and deliver the processed data directly to a provisioned Amazon Redshift cluster\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA digital marketplace company wants to implement real-time analytics for its online shopping platform. The company must collect clickstream events from its website and mobile applications, including page views, cart updates, searches, and checkout actions. The company needs to store the events in Amazon S3 for downstream analytics and wants to minimize ongoing maintenance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST ongoing maintenance?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to collect clickstream events and use Amazon Data Firehose to deliver the data to an Amazon S3 bucket\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams is designed to collect real-time streaming data from sources such as websites, mobile applications, and application event producers. It provides a scalable ingestion layer for clickstream events that the company can use as the source of the analytics pipeline.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Data Firehose can use a Kinesis data stream as a source and deliver streaming data to Amazon S3. Firehose manages buffering, delivery, retries, and integration with the S3 destination. This removes the need to build custom upload workers or maintain file delivery scripts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution provides low ongoing maintenance because it uses managed streaming ingestion and managed delivery to Amazon S3. The company can store the clickstream data in S3 for downstream analytics while avoiding self-managed Kafka brokers, EC2 collectors, containerized upload services, and custom queue consumers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon SQS standard queues to collect clickstream events, invoke AWS Lambda functions to poll the queues, and write the processed records to Amazon S3\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon SQS can buffer messages and decouple producers from consumers. Lambda can poll SQS queues and process messages before writing results to Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this design requires custom Lambda processing logic, batching behavior, error handling, and S3 write handling. Kinesis Data Streams with Data Firehose provides a more direct managed streaming path to S3 with less custom code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon MSK brokers to collect clickstream events and store the processed event data in an Amazon RDS DB instance\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon MSK can collect streaming events through Apache Kafka, and it can support high-throughput streaming architectures. However, using MSK brokers adds Kafka cluster administration, topic configuration, capacity planning, and connector or consumer management.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon RDS is also not the best storage destination for high-volume clickstream analytics data. Storing the events in Amazon S3 provides a more suitable data lake foundation for downstream analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose to ingest clickstream events and deliver the processed data directly to a provisioned Amazon Redshift cluster\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Data Firehose can deliver streaming data to Amazon Redshift. This can be useful when the company needs to load data into Redshift tables for warehouse analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the requirement is to store the clickstream data for analytics, and the source pattern uses Amazon S3 as the storage destination. A provisioned Redshift cluster adds cluster management and warehouse cost that the company does not need for simple event storage in S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/introduction.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/streams/latest/dev/introduction.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/what-is-this-service.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/what-is-this-service.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/writing-with-kinesis-streams.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/writing-with-kinesis-streams.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/create-destination.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/firehose/latest/dev/create-destination.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-8":{"id":"p:1-8","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 8","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLeverage custom parameter groups for the Amazon Redshift cluster to turn on the concurrency scaling of the cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable Short query acceleration (SQA) to concurrently run queries and thereby improve the concurrency scaling of the cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRe-configure the cluster to DC2 nodes and enable workload manager (WLM) queue as a concurrency scaling queue\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable workload manager (WLM) queue as a concurrency scaling queue. Set the \u003ccode\u003eConcurrency Scaling mode\u003c/code\u003e value to \u003ccode\u003eauto\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Điểm cần cấu hình là chế độ concurrency scaling của hàng đợi WLM. Chỉ nói dùng custom parameter group mà không bật chế độ này cho hàng đợi chưa thực hiện đúng yêu cầu.","SQA ưu tiên các truy vấn ngắn để chúng không phải chờ truy vấn dài. Nó không thay thế concurrency scaling cho cả tải đọc và ghi.","Theo lời giải của đề, concurrency scaling cho thao tác ghi cần RA3. Chuyển cụm đang dùng RA3 sang DC2 làm mất khả năng cần thiết này.","Bật concurrency scaling tại hàng đợi WLM và đặt chế độ thành auto. Các truy vấn đủ điều kiện có thể dùng thêm năng lực xử lý khi nhu cầu tăng; RA3 đáp ứng yêu cầu tải ghi của đề."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses Amazon Redshift as their data warehouse service in the cloud. The performance of the Redshift cluster needs improvement and the company decided to scale read and write capacity to meet the user demand. The cluster runs on RA3 nodes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs a data engineer, which solution will you use to turn on the concurrency scaling of the cluster?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:3-41":{"id":"p:3-41","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 41","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Database Migration Service to continuously stream MySQL changes to Amazon S3, export Salesforce data manually each night, and correlate the files with Athena scheduled queries\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon AppFlow to extract Salesforce opportunities, use AWS Glue to extract MySQL records and correlate the datasets, and use Step Functions to orchestrate the process\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon AppFlow to extract Salesforce opportunities, use AWS Glue to extract MySQL records and correlate the datasets, and use Amazon MWAA to orchestrate the process\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon AppFlow to extract Salesforce opportunities, use Kinesis Data Streams to extract MySQL records, and use Managed Service for Apache Flink to correlate the datasets\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Xuất Salesforce thủ công không đạt tự động hóa ít vận hành. CDC liên tục từ MySQL cũng không cần thiết chỉ để chạy theo đêm.","AppFlow có connector Salesforce, Glue lấy MySQL và kết hợp dữ liệu, Step Functions điều phối các bước. Phù hợp xử lý theo đêm mà ít quản lý riêng.","Các bước lấy dữ liệu phù hợp nhưng MWAA thêm môi trường Airflow cần quản lý. Với pipeline này Step Functions đơn giản hơn theo đề.","Kinesis không tự trích xuất MySQL theo cách nêu. Xây cả pipeline streaming/Flink quá phức tạp cho đối chiếu theo lô hằng đêm."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA software company stores daily subscription purchase records in a MySQL database. The sales team also manages a large set of qualified opportunities in Salesforce. The analytics team wants to correlate the two datasets to identify accounts that are likely to convert into new customers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe company receives 3 GB of new purchase records each day and maintains 120 GB of Salesforce opportunity data. A data engineer must create a nightly process to extract both datasets, correlate the records, and produce the analysis with the least operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:21":{"id":"e:5:21","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 21","correct":[2],"answers":["Deploy Amazon Athena for centralized logging across all accounts.","Manually establish a user-managed, centralized data lake using Amazon S3 for each account.","Use AWS CloudTrail Lake for centralized logging across all accounts.","Configure AWS Lake Formation for centralized logging across all accounts."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is utilizing multiple AWS accounts for various departments. The corporation wants to streamline its logging queries across all accounts with minimal setup and management overhead. The team currently uses Amazon CloudWatch Log Insights to analyze logs within individual accounts, but a centralized solution is needed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would provide the most efficient solution for the corporation’s requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS CloudTrail Lake\u003c/strong\u003e simplifies activity log analysis by integrating collection, storage, optimization, and query in the same product. By consolidating these features into one environment, CloudTrail Lake eliminates the need for separate data processing pipelines that span across teams and products.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4df11fef64078c642ddf5bbcfc6d1a8376fd2e23a85f06006ab8c946874a92d4.png\" alt=\"AWS CloudTrail Lake query editor running a SQL query to count S3 bucket creation events\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS CloudTrail Lake provides an SQL-based query interface, allowing you to easily analyze your logs using standard SQL queries. This is particularly useful for auditing, security investigation, and operational troubleshooting purposes. CloudTrail Lake also features sample queries to help you get started with writing queries for common scenarios, such as collecting a user’s activity, that can accelerate security investigations. With immutable event data storage and a default retention period of seven years, CloudTrail Lake can meet the compliance requirements of most users. CloudTrail Lake can also collect events from multiple AWS regions and accounts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Use AWS CloudTrail Lake for centralized logging across all accounts.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Configure AWS Lake Formation for centralized logging across all accounts\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because AWS Lake Formation is primarily used for managing, securing, and sharing data for analytics and machine learning. It is not specifically designed for centralized logging across multiple AWS accounts. It would require more setup and management overhead compared to AWS CloudTrail Lake.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003e Manually establish a user-managed, centralized data lake using Amazon S3 for each account\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because manually establishing a user-managed, centralized data lake using Amazon S3 for each account would require significant effort and time. It would involve setting up data replication from multiple AWS accounts, which is not as efficient as using AWS CloudTrail Lake.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Deploy Amazon Athena for centralized logging across all accounts\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect\u0026nbsp;because Amazon Athena, while it can be used to query logs, is not just a centralized logging solution. You would still need a way to aggregate logs from multiple AWS accounts into a centralized location, which Amazon Athena does not provide by itself. Therefore, it’s not the most efficient solution for the corporation’s requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-lake.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-lake.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/cloudtrail-logs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/cloudtrail-logs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2022/01/aws-cloudtrail-lake-audit-security/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2022/01/aws-cloudtrail-lake-audit-security/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS CloudTrail Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-cloudtrail/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-cloudtrail/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:14":{"id":"e:8:14","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 14","correct":[1,4],"answers":["Implement an Amazon Data Firehose stream with AWS Lambda for PII redaction.","Create a Glue job in AWS Glue Studio and use the Detect PII transform to identify and redact columns containing PII.","Create a rule in AWS Glue Data Quality to determine and redact the PII.","Use AWS Macie for PII identification and data redaction.","Leverage AWS Step Functions to orchestrate the ingestion and processing workflow of the dataset."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA team of data engineers is developing an ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) pipeline that will use an Amazon S3-based data lake as its source. Sensitive information has been discovered during data exploration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo ensure data privacy and protection, the data engineering team must implement a mechanism that can identify and redact the Personally Identifiable Information (PII) before the data is ingested.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of steps should be implemented to meet these requirements with the LEAST amount of development effort? (Select TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Studio\u003c/strong\u003e offers a no-code visual interface for data integration tasks, including the detection and redaction of PII from datasets before they are ingested into a data lake. This approach minimizes the operational effort as it leverages AWS\u0027s managed services to handle both the detection of sensitive information and the orchestration of data workflows. AWS Glue\u0027s PII transform feature automatically identifies and masks or redacts sensitive data, ensuring that any PII present in the source data is appropriately handled before it reaches the S3 data lake.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Step Functions\u003c/strong\u003e can then be used to orchestrate the workflow, controlling the flow of data from its source through the AWS Glue Studio for PII redaction and finally into the Amazon S3 data lake. This service provides a robust mechanism for managing complex data-processing workflows with minimal manual intervention, reducing operational burden.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0b0874b7bf4f6bb2bffe6c2fefef51fa43ff113174c9fb4c94d9ae881ab60003.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn this scenario, the company can use AWS Glue Studio to visually create a Glue job to identify and redact PII from datasets. AWS Glue Studio includes a built-in Detect PII transform that the company can use right away. Glue Studio will automatically generate the necessary code for the job. Once done, you can include the Glue job as part of the pipeline orchestrated by an AWS Step Functions workflow.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Create a Glue job in AWS Glue Studio and use the Detect PII transform to identify and redact columns containing PII.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e- Leverage AWS Step Functions to orchestrate the ingestion and processing workflow of the dataset.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement an Amazon Data Firehose stream with AWS Lambda for PII redaction\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While feasible, this approach requires custom development and management of a Lambda function for PII detection and redaction, potentially increasing operational effort and complexity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a rule in AWS Glue Data Quality to determine and redact the PII\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because AWS Glue Data Quality primarily focuses on monitoring data quality over time rather than performing data transformation tasks like PII redaction.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Macie for PII identification and data redaction\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because AWS Macie is primarily a data security and data privacy service focused on identifying and protecting sensitive data in AWS. While it can identify PII, it doesn\u0027t directly support redaction or data transformation as part of an ETL process like AWS Glue Studio.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/detect-PII.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-data-pipeline-to-automatically-discover-and-mask-pii-data-with-aws-glue-databrew/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-data-pipeline-to-automatically-discover-and-mask-pii-data-with-aws-glue-databrew/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-65":{"id":"p:1-65","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 65","correct":[2,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) to ingest the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up AWS Lambda with AWS Step Functions to process the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eProvision Amazon EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group to process the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up AWS Fargate with Amazon ECS to process the data\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["DMS chuyên di chuyển/CDC database. Đề không mô tả nguồn database cần di chuyển, mà cần nền tảng tiếp nhận luồng ứng dụng nói chung.","Step Functions không kéo dài giới hạn 15 phút của một lần chạy Lambda. Tác vụ liền mạch 30 phút phù hợp container hơn.","Kinesis Data Streams tiếp nhận luồng và tách producer khỏi consumer. Có thể tăng năng lực theo lưu lượng và cho bộ xử lý lấy dữ liệu độc lập.","EC2 Auto Scaling vẫn đòi hỏi quản lý máy chủ. Không đáp ứng yêu cầu serverless.","ECS trên Fargate chạy container mà không cần quản lý EC2, phù hợp tác vụ xử lý dài 30 phút. Kết hợp Kinesis cho phần tiếp nhận dữ liệu."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company\u0027s real-time streaming application is running on AWS. As the data is ingested, a job runs on the data and takes 30 minutes to complete. The workload frequently experiences high latency due to the large volume of incoming data. A data engineer needs to design a scalable and serverless solution to enhance performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich combination of steps do you recommend? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:44":{"id":"e:4:44","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 44","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse the table metrics of the DMS task to verify the statistics for tables being migrated including the DDL statements completed\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure DMS premigration assessment on the migration task so the assessment can compare the source and target data and report any mismatches\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure DMS data validation on the migration task so it can compare the source and target data for the DMS task and report any mismatches\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Schema Conversion Tool for the migration task so it can compare the source and target data and report any mismatches\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multi-national retail company wants to migrate its on-premises Oracle database to Aurora MySQL. The company has hired you as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to carry out the migration with minimal downtime using AWS DMS. The company has mandated that the migration must have minimal impact on the performance of the source database and you must validate that the data was migrated accurately from the source to the target before the cutover.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions will MOST effectively address this use-case?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure DMS data validation on the migration task so it can compare the source and target data for the DMS task and report any mismatches\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use AWS DMS data validation to ensure that your data has migrated accurately from the source to the target. DMS compares the source and target records and then reports any mismatches. In addition, for a CDC-enabled task, AWS DMS compares the incremental changes and reports any mismatches. As part of data validation, DMS compares each row in the source with its corresponding row at the target and verifies that those rows contain the same data. For this comparison, DMS issues appropriate queries to retrieve the data. These queries consume additional resources at the source and the target as well as additional network resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDMS data validation overview:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2c0a0a2f1f2cbedc8e07ec20454a1176f21f1c4c3e5f51339c9aa795d769c4bf.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2c0a0a2f1f2cbedc8e07ec20454a1176f21f1c4c3e5f51339c9aa795d769c4bf.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Validating.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Validating.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the table metrics of the DMS task to verify the statistics for tables being migrated including the DDL statements completed\u003c/strong\u003e - You can use table metrics to capture statistics such as insert, update, delete, and DDL statements completed for the tables being migrated. This option will not help you compare the source and target data for the DMS task and report any mismatches.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b530a764b56b17286f5cf1529f323aacf25b852c1be4ec201acbc2eee9c7c898.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b530a764b56b17286f5cf1529f323aacf25b852c1be4ec201acbc2eee9c7c898.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Monitoring.html#CHAP_Monitoring.Metrics\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Monitoring.html#CHAP_Monitoring.Metrics\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure DMS premigration assessment on the migration task so the assessment can compare the source and target data and report any mismatches\u003c/strong\u003e - A premigration assessment evaluates specified components of a database migration task to help identify any problems that might prevent a migration task from running as expected. This assessment gives you a chance to identify issues before you run a new or modified task. You can then fix problems before they occur while running the migration task itself. This can avoid delays in completing a given database migration needed to repair data and your database environment. This option will not help you compare the source and target data for the DMS task and report any mismatches.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Schema Conversion Tool for the migration task so it can compare the source and target data and report any mismatches\u003c/strong\u003e - You can use the AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT) to convert your existing database schema from one database engine to another. You can convert relational OLTP schema or data warehouse schema. Your converted schema is suitable for an Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) MySQL, MariaDB, Oracle, SQL Server, PostgreSQL DB, an Amazon Aurora DB cluster, or an Amazon Redshift cluster. This option will not help you compare the source and target data for the DMS task and report any mismatches.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Validating.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Validating.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Monitoring.html#CHAP_Monitoring.Metrics\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/dms/latest/userguide/CHAP_Monitoring.html#CHAP_Monitoring.Metrics\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:31":{"id":"e:3:31","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 31","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the data in compressed .csv, partitioned by date and sorted by the status of the device\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the data in Apache ORC, partitioned by date and sorted by device type of the device\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the data in compressed .csv, partitioned by date and sorted by device type\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the data in Apache Parquet, partitioned by device type and sorted by date\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn Internet-of-Things (IoT) devices company uses Amazon S3 as the data lake to store the input data that is ingested from the field devices on an hourly basis. The ingested data has attributes such as the device type, ID of the device, the status of the device, the timestamp of the event, the source IP address, etc. The data runs into millions of records per day and the company wants to run complex analytical queries on this data on a daily basis for product improvements for each device type.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich is the most optimal way to save this data to get the best performance from the millions of data points saved on a daily basis?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the data in Apache ORC, partitioned by date and sorted by device type of the device\u003c/strong\u003e - Apache Parquet and ORC are columnar storage formats that are optimized for fast retrieval of data and used in AWS analytical applications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBy partitioning your data, you can restrict the amount of data scanned by each query, thus improving performance and reducing cost. You can partition your data by any key. A common practice is to partition the data based on time, often leading to a multi-level partitioning scheme. For example, a customer who has data coming in every hour might decide to partition by year, month, date, and hour. Another customer, who has data coming from many different sources but that is loaded only once per day, might partition by a data source identifier and date.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the given use case, as the company does daily analysis, so it only needs to look at the data generated for a given date. Hence partitioning by date offers significant performance and cost advantages. Since the company also wants to do analysis for product improvements for each device type, it is better to keep the data sorted by device type, so it allows faster query execution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the data in Apache Parquet, partitioned by device type and sorted by date\u003c/strong\u003e -  Apache Parquet is a columnar storage format that is optimized for fast retrieval of data and used in AWS analytical applications. However, partitioning by device type is incorrect for this use case, and partitioning by date is optimal.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the data in compressed .csv, partitioned by date and sorted by the status of the device\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the data in compressed .csv, partitioned by date and sorted by device type\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBoth the above options are not columnar storage formats, they are row-based formats that are not optimal for big data retrievals for complex analytical queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:27":{"id":"e:4:27","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 27","correct":[3,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog to expire historical Iceberg snapshots so older table versions are removed from metadata and storage after the retention period\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog to generate governed views for the Iceberg tables and require analysts to query only the generated views\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog to automatically optimize secondary indexes for the Iceberg tables based on common player_id and session_id query filters\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog to automatically compact the Iceberg tables by rewriting small files into fewer larger files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog to generate column-level statistics for the Iceberg tables on a schedule\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA mobile gaming company uses AWS Glue jobs to read from and write to Apache Iceberg tables that store real-time player session events. The Iceberg table data is stored in Apache Parquet format in Amazon S3. As event volume grows, analysts notice that queries against the player activity tables are becoming slower.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solutions will improve query performance? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog to generate column-level statistics for the Iceberg tables on a schedule\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Data Catalog can generate column-level statistics for Apache Iceberg tables. These statistics help supported query engines understand table characteristics such as distinct values and other column-level properties.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eQuery engines can use these statistics to create better query execution plans. For example, better statistics can help the optimizer make more efficient decisions for joins, filtering, and scan planning.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis improves query performance without requiring the company to redesign the table or manually tune every query. Scheduling statistics generation helps keep optimizer metadata current as real-time gaming data continues to arrive.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4a6d90f9997105a11d2cf32bc89741004a108e55ce2ebccc073336f195156a15.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4a6d90f9997105a11d2cf32bc89741004a108e55ce2ebccc073336f195156a15.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/accelerate-query-performance-with-apache-iceberg-statistics-on-the-aws-glue-data-catalog/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/accelerate-query-performance-with-apache-iceberg-statistics-on-the-aws-glue-data-catalog/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c823957702225da63feb27fc612387cd4da2b149a60591eea028288f5f8f4612.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/c823957702225da63feb27fc612387cd4da2b149a60591eea028288f5f8f4612.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/aws-glue-data-catalog-now-supports-automatic-compaction-of-apache-iceberg-tables/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/aws-glue-data-catalog-now-supports-automatic-compaction-of-apache-iceberg-tables/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog to automatically compact the Iceberg tables by rewriting small files into fewer larger files\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Data Catalog can automatically compact Apache Iceberg tables. Compaction rewrites many small files into fewer larger files, which reduces the overhead of planning and scanning large numbers of small objects in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis is especially important for real-time streaming tables because streaming writes often create many small Parquet files. As the number of files grows, query engines spend more time opening files, reading metadata, and planning scans.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAutomatic compaction directly addresses this physical layout problem. It improves read performance for the Iceberg tables while reducing the manual work required to run custom compaction jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog to generate governed views for the Iceberg tables and require analysts to query only the generated views\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue Data Catalog views can provide a governed view layer over cataloged data. Views can help teams expose selected rows or columns and simplify user access patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, views do not automatically optimize the physical layout of Iceberg table files or generate optimizer statistics. They can improve governance and usability, but they do not directly solve slow query performance caused by Iceberg table layout or missing column statistics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog to expire historical Iceberg snapshots so older table versions are removed from metadata and storage after the retention period\u003c/strong\u003e - Expiring historical Iceberg snapshots can reduce metadata growth and remove older table versions that are no longer needed. This can help control storage usage and manage time travel retention.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, snapshot expiration does not rewrite the active data files that current queries scan. If slow performance comes from many small Parquet files or missing optimizer statistics, compaction and column statistics are the more direct solutions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Catalog to automatically optimize secondary indexes for the Iceberg tables based on common player_id and session_id query filters\u003c/strong\u003e - Secondary indexes can improve query performance in some database systems when queries filter on indexed columns. A gaming workload might commonly filter on player_id, session_id, or event_time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, AWS Glue Data Catalog does not provide an automatic secondary index optimizer for Apache Iceberg tables in the way this option describes. Iceberg query optimization in this context relies on table statistics, partition pruning, file metadata, and table maintenance operations such as compaction.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/column-statistics.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/column-statistics.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/iceberg-column-statistics.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/iceberg-column-statistics.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/iceberg-generate-column-stats.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/iceberg-generate-column-stats.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/table-optimizers.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/table-optimizers.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/compaction-management.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/compaction-management.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/accelerate-query-performance-with-apache-iceberg-statistics-on-the-aws-glue-data-catalog/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/accelerate-query-performance-with-apache-iceberg-statistics-on-the-aws-glue-data-catalog/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/aws-glue-data-catalog-now-supports-automatic-compaction-of-apache-iceberg-tables/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/aws-glue-data-catalog-now-supports-automatic-compaction-of-apache-iceberg-tables/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:17":{"id":"e:8:17","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 17","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cspan\u003eConfigure Kinesis Data Streams as the source and Amazon S3 as the destination. Load the data from Amazon S3 to Amazon Redshift by running the \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e command.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eConfigure the Kinesis Data Streams as the source and the Kinesis Firehose as the destination. Deliver data with Kinesis Firehose to an Amazon S3 bucket and issue an Amazon Redshift \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e command.\u003c/span\u003e","Configure streaming ingestion by creating an external schema mapping the data stream. Then, create a materialized view referencing the external schema with auto refresh.","\u003cspan\u003eConfigure Kinesis Data Streams to load data using SQL queries. Create materialized views with auto refresh set to \u003ccode\u003eYES\u003c/code\u003e using stream data.\u003c/span\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses an Amazon Redshift cluster to store and analyze weekly sales data. To enhance its analytics, the company plans to perform SQL joins between streaming transaction data and the data within the Redshift cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis transaction data is ingested into an Amazon Kinesis Data Stream at a rate of several megabytes per second. The company aims to visualize these joined datasets in Amazon QuickSight to derive insights in near real-time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich set of steps can satisfy the requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eYou can use \u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams\u003c/strong\u003e to collect and process large streams of data records in real-time. You can send the processed records to dashboards, use them to generate alerts, dynamically change pricing and advertising strategies, or send data to a variety of other AWS services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e is a fully managed, petabyte-scale data warehouse service in the cloud. You can load data and start querying right away in the Amazon Redshift query editor v2 or in your favorite business intelligence (BI) tool. Enjoy the best price performance and familiar SQL features in an easy-to-use, zero administration environment.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift supports streaming ingestion from Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Streaming ingestion provides low-latency, high-speed ingestion of stream data from Amazon Kinesis Data Streams into an Amazon Redshift provisioned or Amazon Redshift Serverless materialized view. It lowers the time it takes to access data, and it reduces storage cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0b758707e518813fa73165234627c86f8976a003ad4e929cd585aae0c1859108.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003cem\u003eSample Architecture of Streaming Ingestion with Amazon Kinesis Data Stream and Amazon Redshift. The source for this architecture is coming from DynamoDB with data streaming turned on.\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThese are the steps in configuring a streaming ingestion, assuming you already have an existing Kinesis Data Stream:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e1. In Amazon Redshift, create an external schema to map the data from Kinesis to a schema.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode\u003eCREATE EXTERNAL SCHEMA kds\nFROM KINESIS\nREGION \u0027us-west-2\u0027\nIAM_ROLE { default | \u0027iam-role-arn\u0027 };\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e2. Create a materialized view to consume the stream data. The following example defines a materialized view with JSON source data. \u003cstrong\u003eNote:\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eTo turn on auto-refresh, use\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eAUTO REFRESH YES\u003c/code\u003e. The default behavior is manual refresh.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode\u003eCREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW my_view AUTO REFRESH YES AS\nSELECT approximate_arrival_timestamp,\nJSON_PARSE(kinesis_data) as Data\nFROM kds.my_stream_name\nWHERE CAN_JSON_PARSE(kinesis_data);\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e3. Refresh the view, which invokes Redshift to read from the stream and load data into the materialized view.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode\u003eREFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW my_view;\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e4. Query data in the materialized view.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBy following the steps above, you can directly ingest the data from Kinesis Data Stream to Amazon Redshift without the need to stage data. This will satisfy the requirements for adding real-time capabilities to the company\u0027s setup.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure streaming ingestion by creating an external schema mapping the data stream. Then, create a materialized view referencing the external schema with auto refresh.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Kinesis Data Streams as the source and Amazon S3 as the destination. Load the data from Amazon S3 to Amazon Redshift by running the \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e command\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. The Kinesis Data Stream does not have built-in support for directly loading data into Amazon S3. This approach is only technically possible using other AWS services with Kinesis Data Stream, such as AWS Lambda or Kinesis Data Firehose. While loading data using the COPY command can deliver data to Amazon Redshift, it is primarily used for bulk data loading, not for real-time applications. Hence, this solution cannot satisfy the requirements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure the Kinesis Data Streams as the source and the Kinesis Firehose as the destination. Deliver data with Kinesis Firehose to an Amazon S3 bucket and issue an Amazon Redshift \u003ccode\u003eCOPY\u003c/code\u003e command\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. While it is a viable approach, this method introduces delays since the data is first staged in Amazon S3 and then loaded into the Amazon Redshift cluster. This additional step largely impacts the goal of achieving near real-time analytics due to the time required to move data through these stages.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Kinesis Data Streams to load data using SQL queries. Create materialized views with auto refresh set to \u003ccode\u003eYES\u003c/code\u003e using stream data\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because running SQL queries directly in the Kinesis Data Streams is impossible. Kinesis Data Streams is a managed service provided by AWS that allows you to build custom applications that process or analyze streaming data in real time. It does not support running SQL queries directly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/near-real-time-analytics-using-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion-with-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-and-amazon-dynamodb/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/near-real-time-analytics-using-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion-with-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-and-amazon-dynamodb/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion-getting-started.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion-getting-started.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/materialized-view-streaming-ingestion.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:33":{"id":"e:2:33","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 33","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) for data ingestion and configure AWS Lambda to trigger logic for downstream processing\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon API Gateway with the existing REST-based interface to create a high-performing architecture\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) for data ingestion and configure AWS Lambda to trigger logic for downstream processing\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest the data, process it using AWS Lambda, or run analytics using Amazon Kinesis Data Analytics\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA health care application processes the real-time health data of the patients into an analytics workflow. With a sharp increase in the number of users, the system has become slow and sometimes even unresponsive as it does not have a retry mechanism. The startup is looking at a scalable solution that has minimal implementation overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following would you recommend as a scalable alternative to the current solution?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest the data, process it using AWS Lambda, or run analytics using Amazon Kinesis Data Analytics\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams (KDS) is a massively scalable and durable real-time data streaming service with support for a retry mechanism. KDS can continuously capture gigabytes of data per second from hundreds of thousands of sources such as website clickstreams, database event streams, financial transactions, social media feeds, IT logs, and location-tracking events. The data collected is available in milliseconds to enable real-time analytics use cases such as real-time dashboards, real-time anomaly detection, dynamic pricing, and more.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eKDS makes sure your streaming data is available to multiple real-time analytics applications, to Amazon S3, or AWS Lambda within 70 milliseconds of the data being collected. Amazon Kinesis data streams scale from megabytes to terabytes per hour and scale from thousands to millions of PUT records per second. You can dynamically adjust the throughput of your stream at any time based on the volume of your input data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHow Data Streams Work:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/165666b0785d6f6c80178f86b2f78e5004b43597eaec81582bcd6b93aece8c73.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/165666b0785d6f6c80178f86b2f78e5004b43597eaec81582bcd6b93aece8c73.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/?nc=sn\u0026amp;loc=2\u0026amp;dn=2\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/?nc=sn\u0026amp;loc=2\u0026amp;dn=2\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) for data ingestion and configure AWS Lambda to trigger logic for downstream processing\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) is a fully managed messaging service for both application-to-application (A2A) and application-to-person (A2P) communication. Amazon SNS is a push mechanism that does not support robust retry mechanisms, as is needed in the current use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) for data ingestion and configure AWS Lambda to trigger logic for downstream processing\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) is a messaging service that helps in decoupling systems and reducing the complexity of architecture. Amazon SQS can still work but Amazon Kinesis Data streams is custom-made for streaming real-time data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon API Gateway with the existing REST-based interface to create a high-performing architecture\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon API Gateway is not meant for processing real-time streaming data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/?nc=sn\u0026amp;loc=2\u0026amp;dn=2\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/?nc=sn\u0026amp;loc=2\u0026amp;dn=2\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:10:5":{"id":"e:10:5","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 5","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse a Condition element with the s3:prefix condition key to restrict all object access and use aws:CurrentTime with DateGreaterThanEquals and DateLessThanEquals operators to restrict the access window\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse a Resource element with S3 object ARN patterns that include wildcards for each approved prefix and use a Condition element with aws:CurrentTime and DateGreaterThan and DateLessThan operators\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse a Condition element with the s3:ResourceAccount condition key to restrict bucket ownership and add an explicit Deny statement outside the approved business-hours window\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse a Resource element with S3 bucket ARN patterns for each classification prefix and use a Condition element with aws:RequestTag to restrict access during the approved business-hours window\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA pharmaceutical company uses AWS Glue jobs to process research datasets stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company organizes the datasets by S3 prefixes that represent sensitivity levels such as public, internal, confidential, and regulated. The security team must allow each AWS Glue job role to access only the prefixes that match the job\u0027s approved data classification. The team must also restrict access to an approved business-hours window from 8 AM to 6 PM.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich elements must the company include in a custom IAM policy to meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse a Resource element with S3 object ARN patterns that include wildcards for each approved prefix and use a Condition element with aws:CurrentTime and DateGreaterThan and DateLessThan operators\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAn IAM policy can restrict Amazon S3 object-level permissions by listing specific S3 object ARNs in the Resource element. To grant access only to approved prefixes, the policy can use object ARN patterns such as arn:aws:s3:::example-bucket/confidential/* or arn:aws:s3:::example-bucket/regulated/*.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe policy can restrict the time window by using the AWS global condition key aws:CurrentTime with date condition operators such as DateGreaterThan and DateLessThan. This allows the security team to limit when requests from the AWS Glue job role can be authorized.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution matches both requirements. The Resource element scopes S3 access to the relevant classification prefixes, and the Condition element enforces time-based access by evaluating the request time during IAM authorization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse a Condition element with the s3:ResourceAccount condition key to restrict bucket ownership and add an explicit Deny statement outside the approved business-hours window\u003c/strong\u003e - The s3:ResourceAccount condition key helps restrict access based on the AWS account that owns the S3 resource. This can be useful in some cross-account access control scenarios.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this condition key does not restrict access to specific classification prefixes inside a bucket. The company needs prefix-specific object ARNs or prefix-aware list conditions, not only an account ownership check.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse a Condition element with the s3:prefix condition key to restrict all object access and use aws:CurrentTime with DateGreaterThanEquals and DateLessThanEquals operators to restrict the access window\u003c/strong\u003e - The s3:prefix condition key can restrict prefixes for bucket listing operations such as s3:ListBucket. It is useful when a policy must control which keys a principal can list in an S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, s3:prefix does not, by itself, restrict object-level actions such as s3:GetObject or s3:PutObject. The policy still needs S3 object ARN patterns in the Resource element to restrict actual object access to the approved prefixes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse a Resource element with S3 bucket ARN patterns for each classification prefix and use a Condition element with aws:RequestTag to restrict access during the approved business-hours window\u003c/strong\u003e - The Resource element must use correct S3 ARN formats. Bucket-level ARNs such as arn:aws:s3:::example-bucket apply to bucket-level actions, while object-level actions require object ARNs such as arn:aws:s3:::example-bucket/prefix/*.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe aws:RequestTag condition key evaluates tags that a request passes to a supported service action. It does not provide a general mechanism to restrict S3 object access to business hours.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/security_iam_service-with-iam.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/security_iam_service-with-iam.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/amazon-s3-policy-keys.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/amazon-s3-policy-keys.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_resource.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_resource.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_condition.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_condition.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_condition_operators.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_condition_operators.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/techniques-for-writing-least-privilege-iam-policies/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/security/techniques-for-writing-least-privilege-iam-policies/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:10":{"id":"e:6:10","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 10","correct":[0],"answers":["Execute an AWS Glue crawler to establish a data catalog for existing data and register its Amazon S3 path location on Lake Formation. For data security, allocate granular permissions to various users.","Leverage Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) to orchestrate and schedule data processing workflows. Ensure data security by assigning appropriate access controls.","Using AWS Lake Formation, register the S3 bucket where the data resides. To secure the data, assign granular permissions to different users.","Import data from Amazon S3 using an AWS Lake Formation blueprint. Subsequently, accord users granular Lake Formation permissions as needed."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is assigned to build a data lake for a company’s business data stored on Amazon S3. The data, saved in the Apache Parquet format, will be utilized for complex analytic tasks. The company anticipates that users will need granular access to the datasets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following will meet these requirements in the shortest amount of time and in the MOST cost-effective manner?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Lake Formation\u003c/strong\u003e is a service that makes it easy to set up a secure data lake in days. A data lake is a centralized, curated, and secured repository that stores all your data, both in its original form and prepared for analysis. A data lake enables you to break down data silos and combine different types of analytics to gain insights and guide better business decisions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 forms the storage layer for Lake Formation. If you already use S3, you typically begin by registering existing S3 buckets that contain your data. Lake Formation creates new buckets for the data lake and imports data into them. AWS always stores this data in your account, and only you have direct access to it.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/88b2714812a8d8367da385e92999efc03a3553eca13595b31fc340bffae1bab3.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation is integrated with AWS Glue, which you can use to create a data catalog that describes available datasets and their appropriate business applications.\u0026nbsp;Lake Formation lets you define policies and control data access with simple \"grant and revoke permissions to data\" sets at granular levels.\u0026nbsp;You can assign permissions to IAM users, roles, groups, and Active Directory users using federation. You specify permissions on catalog objects (like tables and columns) rather than on buckets and objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation also provides blueprints that you can run for loading and cataloging data.\u0026nbsp;A blueprint allows you to\u0026nbsp;import data from MySQL, Postgres, SQL Server, MariaDB, and Oracle databases running in Amazon RDS or hosted in Amazon EC2. With Lake Formation, you can import data from MySQL, Postgres, SQL Server, MariaDB, and Oracle databases running in Amazon RDS or hosted in Amazon EC2. You can also import from on-premises databases by connecting\u0026nbsp;with Java Database Connectivity (JDBC).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIt was indicated in the scenario that data in Parquet format is already existing in Amazon S3. As a result, you don\u0027t need to utilize a Lake Formation blueprint.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eExecute an AWS Glue crawler to establish a data catalog for existing data and register its Amazon S3 path location on Lake Formation. For data security, allocate granular permissions to various users.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow (Amazon MWAA) to orchestrate and schedule data processing workflows. Ensure data security by assigning appropriate access controls\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because while Amazon MWAA can orchestrate and schedule data processing workflows, it does not provide a solution for creating a data lake or managing granular access to datasets. These tasks would need to be handled separately, which could increase complexity and cost.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUsing AWS Lake Formation, register the S3 bucket where the data resides. To secure the data, assign granular permissions to different users\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. You should register the Amazon S3 path of the bucket where your data is stored and not the bucket name.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImport data from Amazon S3 using an AWS Lake Formation blueprint. Subsequently, accord users granular Lake Formation permissions as needed\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because Amazon S3 is not a valid import source in a Lake Formation blueprint.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/building-securing-and-managing-data-lakes-with-aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/building-securing-and-managing-data-lakes-with-aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/how-it-works.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Lake Formation Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-35":{"id":"p:4-35","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 35","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into Redshift on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into RDS on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLoad the incremental raw zone data into DynamoDB on an hourly basis and run the SQL based sanity checks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Athena to run SQL based analytics on the data in Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Nạp dữ liệu vào Redshift mỗi giờ tạo thêm pipeline và tài nguyên để quản lý. Với kiểm tra SQL trực tiếp trên vùng raw S3, bước sao chép này không cần thiết.","Nạp dữ liệu vào RDS đòi hỏi database, schema và quy trình cập nhật riêng. Athena đọc ngay dữ liệu S3 với ít thành phần hơn.","DynamoDB là database khóa–giá trị và tài liệu, không phải công cụ phù hợp cho các kiểm tra SQL tùy ý trên raw data lake. Sao chép dữ liệu vào đó còn tăng công phát triển.","Athena chạy SQL trực tiếp trên dữ liệu S3 sau khi khai báo metadata phù hợp. Không cần quản lý server hoặc nạp sang database khác, nên hợp yêu cầu serverless và ít phát triển."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe data engineering team at a company has set up a workflow to ingest the clickstream data into the raw zone of the S3 data lake. The team wants to run some SQL based data sanity checks on the raw zone of the data lake. The company has hired you as an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate to build a serverless solution that involves the least amount of development effort. The solution should be cost-effective and easy to maintain.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions would you build for this use-case?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:41":{"id":"e:5:41","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 41","correct":[2],"answers":["Restart the EMR cluster.","\u003cspan\u003eRun \u003ccode\u003eALTER TABLE sales_data DROP PARTITION (year=\u00272024\u0027, month=\u002701\u0027, day=\u002702\u0027)\u003c/code\u003e, then recreate the partition.\u003c/span\u003e","\u003cspan\u003eRun \u003ccode\u003eMSCK REPAIR TABLE sales_data\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/span\u003e","Delete and re-upload the data to HDFS."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer is working with a company that heavily relies on big data analytics. The company uses an Amazon EMR cluster running Apache Hive to query data stored on Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS). The data on HDFS is stored in a partitioned format in a table named \u003ccode\u003esales_data\u003c/code\u003e, partitioned by date (e.g., year=2024/month=01/day=01).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen a new batch of data for the date 2024/01/02 is added directly to HDFS using the \u003ccode\u003ehadoop fs -put\u003c/code\u003e command, it is noticed that the new partition is not visible through Hive queries on the \u003ccode\u003esales_data\u003c/code\u003e table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat action can be taken to resolve this issue and make the new partition visible in Hive queries?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThe\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMSCK REPAIR TABLE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;command is used to synchronize the metadata of a table with the actual data layout in the file system. When new data is added directly to HDFS, Hive is not aware of the new partitions. This is because Hive requires metadata about partitions to be updated in its metastore, which doesn’t happen when data is added directly to HDFS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eRunning\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMSCK REPAIR TABLE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;scans the file system for new partitions that were added after the table was created. It then compares the partitions in the table metadata and the partitions in the file system. If new partitions are present in the file system that are not in the table metadata, it adds those partitions to the metadata and to the Hive table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/59ef558ada57c88d6359d8408541ca1976d11f298c62d6e870c7695be116ee4e.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen you add physical partitions, the metadata in the catalog becomes inconsistent with the layout of the data in the file system, and information about the new partitions needs to be added to the catalog. To update the metadata, you run\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMSCK REPAIR TABLE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;so that you can query the data in the new partitions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIt’s important to note that\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMSCK REPAIR TABLE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;only adds partitions to metadata; it does not remove them. To remove partitions from metadata after the partitions have been manually deleted in the file system, you would run the command\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eALTER TABLE table-name DROP PARTITION\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn the scenario, the data engineer has added a new batch of data for the date 2024/01/02 directly to HDFS using the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003ehadoop fs -put\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;command. However, the new partition is not visible through Hive queries. By running the\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003eMSCK REPAIR TABLE\u003c/code\u003e\u0026nbsp;command, the data engineer can update the Hive metastore with the metadata of the new partition, making it visible in Hive queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun \u003ccode\u003eMSCK REPAIR TABLE sales_data\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun \u003ccode\u003eALTER TABLE sales_data DROP PARTITION (year=\u00272024\u0027, month=\u002701\u0027, day=\u002702\u0027)\u003c/code\u003e, then recreate the partition\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because dropping and recreating the partition would mean deleting and recreating the data, which could be time-consuming and resource-intensive. Moreover, this method does not address the root cause of the issue, which is that Hive is not aware of partitions if they are directly added to HDFS. Therefore, even if the partition is dropped and recreated, the same issue could occur in the future when new data is added directly to HDFS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDelete and re-upload the data to HDFS\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because\u0026nbsp;this action does not solve the issue as Hive still needs the metadata of the new partitions to be updated in its metastore. Simply deleting and re-uploading the data does not update the metadata in Hive’s metastore. Therefore, even after re-uploading the data, the new partition would still not be visible in Hive queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRestart the EMR cluster\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Restarting the EMR cluster might seem like a potential solution to some issues, but in this case, it does not address the root cause of the problem. The issue lies in the fact that Hive is not aware of the new partitions added directly to HDFS, and restarting the EMR cluster does not make Hive aware of these new partitions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-msck-optimization.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/emr/latest/ReleaseGuide/emr-msck-optimization.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/msck-repair-table.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/msck-repair-table.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/get-started-managing-partitions-for-amazon-s3-tables-backed-by-the-aws-glue-data-catalog/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/get-started-managing-partitions-for-amazon-s3-tables-backed-by-the-aws-glue-data-catalog/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon EMR Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:5":{"id":"e:2:5","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 5","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable Amazon CloudWatch Logs for the DB instance, create metric filters for database modifications and instance changes, and configure notifications for suspicious database activity\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon RDS Performance Insights to capture SQL activity, enable enhanced monitoring for operating system metrics, store the monitoring output in CloudWatch Logs, and use CloudWatch dashboards to identify users who changed transaction rows and RDS instance settings\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge rule to monitor Amazon RDS events, invoke an AWS Lambda function to parse the events, and store the parsed records in Amazon OpenSearch Service\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable native Amazon RDS for MySQL database audit logging, publish the audit logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, configure CloudWatch metric filters and alarms, and enable AWS CloudTrail logging for RDS API activity\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial services company runs an Amazon RDS for MySQL database that stores customer transaction records. The compliance team must collect audit logs that identify database users who modify transaction data. The team must also track users or roles that make administrative changes to the RDS DB instance. The audit records must be available for review and investigation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable native Amazon RDS for MySQL database audit logging, publish the audit logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, configure CloudWatch metric filters and alarms, and enable AWS CloudTrail logging for RDS API activity\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon RDS for MySQL supports database audit logging by using the MariaDB Audit Plugin. The audit plugin can record database-level activity, including the database user and the operations that the user performs. This meets the requirement to track each user who modifies data in the database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePublishing the database audit logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs makes the logs available for centralized retention, search, monitoring, and audit review. CloudWatch metric filters and alarms can help the company identify important audit patterns, such as unexpected write activity or sensitive table changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS CloudTrail complements the database audit logs by recording Amazon RDS API calls. CloudTrail helps identify users, roles, timestamps, and API actions for administrative changes to the DB instance, such as modifying DB settings, changing logging configuration, or updating instance properties. Together, native database audit logs and CloudTrail cover both database data modifications and RDS instance-level changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an Amazon EventBridge rule to monitor Amazon RDS events, invoke an AWS Lambda function to parse the events, and store the parsed records in Amazon OpenSearch Service\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EventBridge can receive Amazon RDS events and route those events to targets such as Lambda. RDS events can help monitor operational events, such as failovers, backups, maintenance, or configuration-related notifications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, RDS events do not provide a complete database audit trail that identifies each database user who modifies data inside MySQL tables. This option also does not use native MySQL audit logging, which is required for database-level user activity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable Amazon CloudWatch Logs for the DB instance, create metric filters for database modifications and instance changes, and configure notifications for suspicious database activity\u003c/strong\u003e - CloudWatch Logs can store logs that Amazon RDS publishes, and metric filters can detect patterns in log streams. Notifications can help alert operations teams when suspicious patterns appear.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, simply enabling CloudWatch Logs and metric filters does not create the required audit source. The company must first enable native RDS for MySQL database audit logging to capture database user activity, and it must use CloudTrail to audit RDS instance-level API changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon RDS Performance Insights to capture SQL activity, enable enhanced monitoring for operating system metrics, store the monitoring output in CloudWatch Logs, and use CloudWatch dashboards to identify users who changed transaction rows and RDS instance settings\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon RDS Performance Insights helps analyze database performance and SQL load. Enhanced Monitoring provides operating system metrics for the DB instance, such as CPU, memory, process, and file system activity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThese tools do not provide a compliance audit trail of each database user who modifies data, and they do not capture complete RDS administrative API activity. The company needs native database audit logging for MySQL activity and CloudTrail for RDS instance changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Appendix.MySQL.Options.AuditPlugin.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Appendix.MySQL.Options.AuditPlugin.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_LogAccess.MySQLDB.PublishtoCloudWatchLogs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_LogAccess.MySQLDB.PublishtoCloudWatchLogs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/logging-using-cloudtrail.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/logging-using-cloudtrail.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/amazon-rds-monitoring-alerting/audit-trails.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/amazon-rds-monitoring-alerting/audit-trails.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/monitor-amazon-rds-for-mysql-and-mariadb-logs-with-amazon-cloudwatch/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/monitor-amazon-rds-for-mysql-and-mariadb-logs-with-amazon-cloudwatch/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-1":{"id":"p:4-1","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 1","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDevelop a custom extract, transform, and load (ETL) job in Python using AWS Lambda. Set up the Python dedupe library as a Lambda layer and then leverage the dedupe library to perform data deduplication\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop an AWS Glue extract, transform, and load (ETL) job. Import the Python Pandas library and leverage the DataFrame.dropDuplicates method for data deduplication\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop an AWS Glue extract, transform, and load (ETL) job and leverage the FindMatches machine learning (ML) transform for data deduplication\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDevelop an AWS Glue extract, transform, and load (ETL) job. Convert the Glue DynamicFrame into an Apache Spark DataFrame and leverage the DataFrame.dropDuplicates method for data deduplication\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Tự viết Lambda và đóng gói thư viện dedupe có thể xử lý trùng lặp, nhưng cần quản lý mã, thư viện và giới hạn chạy của Lambda. Đề ưu tiên ít công vận hành nên cách này kém phù hợp hơn tính năng có sẵn của Glue.","Lựa chọn này vừa cần mã tùy chỉnh, vừa nhầm tên hàm: pandas dùng drop_duplicates, còn dropDuplicates thuộc Spark. Nó cũng không thay thế khả năng nhận diện các bản ghi gần giống nhau của FindMatches.","FindMatches của AWS Glue dùng học máy để nhận diện các bản ghi trùng hoặc gần giống nhau, kể cả khi chúng không khớp hoàn toàn từng ký tự. Đây là tính năng tích hợp phù hợp để làm sạch dữ liệu cũ với ít mã tự viết.","Spark dropDuplicates phù hợp khi các giá trị cần so sánh khớp chính xác. FindMatches phù hợp hơn với dữ liệu cũ có những bản ghi cùng đối tượng nhưng cách ghi khác nhau; đó là lý do bộ đề chọn FindMatches."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is transitioning a legacy application to a data lake on Amazon S3. During the course of the review, a data engineer discovered duplicates in the legacy data. What is the most efficient way for the data engineer to eliminate these duplicates from the legacy application data with minimal operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:7:14":{"id":"e:7:14","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 14","correct":[2],"answers":["Use AWS Glue for Ray to write Python scripts for data transformation.","Utilize AWS Glue on Apache Spark, using PySpark for data transformation.","Use an AWS Glue job using Python Shell and pandas for data transformation.","Utilize AWS Glue on Apache Spark, writing the logic in Scala for data transformation."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team is automating the process to extract, transform, and load (ETL) gzip-compressed JSON files from an Amazon S3 bucket. The gzip files are uploaded daily, with each having a total size of less than 30 MB. As part of the transformation logic, the team needs to remove duplicate records using \u003ccode\u003eDataFrame.drop_duplicates()\u003c/code\u003e before loading the processed data. The goal is to implement the ETL pipeline in the most cost-effective way.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich action should be taken to meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e is a serverless data integration service that makes it easier to discover, prepare, and combine data for analytics, machine learning (ML), and application development. AWS Glue provides all the capabilities needed for data integration, so you can start analyzing your data and putting it to use in minutes instead of months. AWS Glue provides both visual and code-based interfaces to make data integration easier. Users can more easily find and access data using the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Data engineers and ETL (extract, transform, and load) developers can visually create, run, and monitor ETL workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue offers different engines on which you can run jobs, providing flexibility for various data processing needs:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSpark:\u003c/strong\u003e AWS Glue on Apache Spark is designed for complex ETL jobs that require processing large volumes of data. Spark\u0027s distributed computing model enables high-performance data transformation and analysis across clusters of computers. It\u0027s ideal for tasks that need to handle big data sets, perform extensive data manipulation, or require the use of Spark\u0027s rich ecosystem of libraries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRay:\u003c/strong\u003e AWS Glue for Ray is optimized for highly parallel and compute-intensive tasks. Ray is a framework that allows for easy scaling of applications from a laptop to a cluster, making it suitable for machine learning workflows, real-time data processing, and tasks that require concurrency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePython Shell:\u003c/strong\u003e AWS Glue\u0027s Python Shell is best suited for light to medium data transformation tasks that do not necessitate the distributed computing capabilities of Spark or Ray. It allows for simpler, script-based ETL jobs that can be executed without the overhead of setting up a Spark or Ray environment.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/bf2df14672760afc4537ab798effc0fe0cd199fb5d4709dad2e30e1ca6885c1f.png\" alt=\"AWS Glue Studio console Selecting the script engine (Python shell, Ray, or Spark)\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWith AWS Glue, you only pay for the time that your ETL job takes to run. There are no resources to manage, no upfront costs, and you are not charged for startup or shutdown time. AWS charges an hourly rate based on the number of data processing units (DPUs) used to run your ETL job. A single standard DPU provides 4 vCPU and 16 GB of memory, whereas a high-memory DPU (M-DPU) provides 4 vCPU and 32 GB of memory.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn simple ETL tasks, such as the given scenario of processing small gzip files, using AWS Glue\u0027s Python Shell proves highly cost-effective. The data engineering team can opt to allocate only 1/16th of a DPU for the ETL job. This approach allows them to execute their tasks economically, paying only for the minimal computing power required and avoiding the higher expenses associated with more resource-intensive options like Spark or Ray.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse an AWS Glue job using Python Shell and pandas for data transformation.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue for Ray to write Python scripts for data transformation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because Ray is primarily used for large-scale parallel data processing, which might be overkill for processing files under 30 MB. Moreover, jobs that use AWS Glue for Ray are more expensive to run than those using Python Shell.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize AWS Glue on Apache Spark, using PySpark for data transformation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. AWS Glue with Apache Spark using PySpark is simply a powerful option, but it\u0027s not the most cost-effective for small files under 30MB. Spark is more suited for handling large-scale data processing, and the overhead it introduces might not be necessary for smaller files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize AWS Glue on Apache Spark, writing the logic in Scala for data transformation\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Similar to the other incorrect option,\u0026nbsp;this solution runs on the Spark engine, which might be more than necessary for small file processing and can increase costs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-glue-best-practices-build-performant-data-pipeline/additional-considerations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-glue-best-practices-build-performant-data-pipeline/additional-considerations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/aws-glue-python-shell-now-supports-python-3-9-with-a-flexible-pre-loaded-environment-and-support-to-install-additional-libraries/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/aws-glue-python-shell-now-supports-python-3-9-with-a-flexible-pre-loaded-environment-and-support-to-install-additional-libraries/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:49":{"id":"e:2:49","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 49","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Lake Formation to migrate the data sources into Amazon S3. Configure AWS Glue Data Catalog to connect the S3 data to Amazon Athena for further analysis and visualizations. Use a Glue Crawler to automate the process\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Lake Formation to migrate the data sources into Amazon S3. Use Amazon QuickSight to generate the visualizations needed for the dashboards\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure Amazon QuickSight to connect to the data sources and generate the visualizations needed for the dashboard\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse the federated queries feature of Amazon Athena to join the different data sources and visualize the data using Amazon QuickSight\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uses AWS services such as Amazon Redshift and Amazon S3 as well as their on-premises SQL Server database to store the consumer data. The company also uses Salesforce as its SaaS application. The company wants to build a dashboard that will help the managers visualize the data points from all these systems.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents a simple and easy way to build the dashboard in the least possible time?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure Amazon QuickSight to connect to the data sources and generate the visualizations needed for the dashboard\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon QuickSight is a very fast, easy-to-use, cloud-powered business analytics service that makes it easy for all employees within an organization to build visualizations, perform ad-hoc analysis, and quickly get business insights from their data, anytime, on any device.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eQuickSight can connect to AWS data sources including Amazon RDS, Amazon Aurora, Amazon Redshift, Amazon Athena, and Amazon S3. You can also upload Excel spreadsheets or flat files (CSV, TSV, CLF, and ELF), connect to on-premises databases like SQL Server, MySQL, and PostgreSQL, and import data from SaaS applications like Salesforce.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eQuickSight Key Features:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0c7dc34728a33d844935dc64b4ac31f7c1c4abfc1461f4a6cce6dd9c65e4f00c.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0c7dc34728a33d844935dc64b4ac31f7c1c4abfc1461f4a6cce6dd9c65e4f00c.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/quicksight/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/quicksight/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse the federated queries feature of Amazon Athena to join the different data sources and visualize the data using Amazon QuickSight\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Athena supports federated query, a feature that allows you to query data in sources other than Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3). You can use federated queries in Athena to query the data in place or build pipelines that extract data from multiple data sources and store them in Amazon S3.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can use different connectors to run federated queries with complex joins across different data sources with Athena and visualize the data with Amazon QuickSight. This option requires significant query development effort to join the data sources, so it\u0027s not the right fit for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Lake Formation to migrate the data sources into Amazon S3. Use Amazon QuickSight to generate the visualizations needed for the dashboards\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Lake Formation to migrate the data sources into Amazon S3. Configure AWS Glue Data Catalog to connect the S3 data to Amazon Athena for further analysis and visualizations. Use a Glue Crawler to automate the process\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBoth these options require a lot of heavy lifting to migrate data from the different sources into S3. These options are unnecessarily complex and hence not the best fit for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/quicksight/resources/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/quicksight/resources/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/data-sources-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/data-sources-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-45":{"id":"p:3-45","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 45","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eIncrease the concurrency scaling quota for the Amazon Redshift cluster so more reporting queries can run at the same time\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate Amazon Redshift materialized views for the frequently queried transformation patterns and refresh the views after the daily data loads finish\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate additional Amazon Redshift read-only clusters, copy the transformed tables to each cluster after the daily load, and route each reporting team to a separate cluster endpoint\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate Redshift Spectrum external tables over the Redshift warehouse tables and rewrite the complex transformation queries to reference the external tables\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Concurrency scaling tăng số truy vấn đồng thời, không tự tính trước phần biến đổi rất đắt mà mỗi truy vấn đều lặp lại.","Materialized view lưu kết quả các phép biến đổi dùng nhiều lần. Refresh sau khi nạp dữ liệu hằng ngày để các báo cáo đọc kết quả đã tính thay vì chạy lại toàn bộ.","Nhân cụm và sao chép bảng thêm chi phí và đồng bộ. Chưa tối ưu nguồn gốc là các phép tính giống nhau lặp lại.","Spectrum external table trỏ dữ liệu ngoài trên S3, không phải lớp bọc để biến bảng nội bộ Redshift thành truy vấn nhanh hơn."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company maintains a central Amazon Redshift data warehouse that receives daily sales, inventory, and customer activity data from Amazon RDS for SQL Server and Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL databases. Several reporting teams run the same complex transformation queries each morning to calculate margin trends, regional demand forecasts, and customer segment metrics. A data engineer observes that these queries take several hours to complete and must reduce execution time as much as possible.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:6:18":{"id":"e:6:18","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 18","correct":[2],"answers":["Ensure that primary and foreign keys are defined for each table while maintaining an EVEN distribution style across all tables.","Change the distribution style of larger tables to ALL distribution and set primary and foreign keys across all tables.","Change the distribution style of small tables to ALL distribution and set primary and foreign keys across all tables.","Change the distribution style of small tables to KEY distribution and set primary and foreign keys across all tables"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company needs to improve SQL query efficiency in its data warehouse, which uses Amazon Redshift. It cannot expand the cluster due to cost constraints. The data engineering team reviews \u003ccode\u003eSTL_QUERY_METRICS\u003c/code\u003e to monitor query performance and identify workloads that may benefit from improved table design.\u003cbr\u003e\nThe company\u0027s data is in various tables, with some having thousands of megabytes of data, while others are smaller and updated infrequently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich strategy is the most effective for meeting these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eData distribution in Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e refers to how data is stored across the nodes and slices within a cluster. When you load data into a Redshift table, the rows are distributed and stored across these slices according to the table\u0027s distribution style.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe different distribution styles in \u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEVEN distribution\u003c/strong\u003e - The rows are distributed evenly across all nodes and slices in a round-robin fashion, regardless of the values in any particular column. This is used when the table doesn\u0027t participate in joins or when there is no clear choice of key distribution column.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eKEY distribution\u003c/strong\u003e - The rows are distributed based on the values in a specified distribution key column. Rows with the same value are stored together physically. This helps optimize queries that filter or join on the distribution key column.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eALL distribution\u003c/strong\u003e - A copy of the entire table is stored on each node, so every row is collocated for any join query involving that table. However, it requires more storage and load time compared to EVEN or KEY. Used for relatively static tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAUTO distribution\u003c/strong\u003e - Redshift automatically assigns the optimal distribution style based on table size and characteristics. It initially uses ALL and may later change to KEY or EVEN, depending on how the table grows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/5c45822348caa14c54f7cd339e3a2cdc7982da85c985cdfbc49890d6fb8275c0.jpg\" alt=\"Amazon Redshift DISTSTYLE ALL copying the full table across all compute nodes\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eConsidering the described scenario, the solution that best meets the requirement involves using the ALL distribution style for the small, infrequently updated table. Changing the smaller dimension tables to ALL distribution would replicate these tables to all nodes, allowing them to be colocated with larger fact tables during joins. Furthermore, setting the primary keys on dimension tables and referencing these as foreign keys in fact tables allows Redshift to leverage these constraints for query optimization.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eUsing ALL distribution for relevant small tables improves query performance without requiring the company to expand its cluster due to cost constraints. Lastly, ALL distribution is well-suited for smaller tables that see infrequent changes, as it avoids data redistribution during updates.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange the distribution style of small tables to ALL distribution and set primary and foreign keys across all tables.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cstrong\u003e \u003cspan\u003eEnsure that primary and foreign keys are defined for each table while maintaining an EVEN distribution style across all tables\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect because the EVEN distribution is just not optimal for small, infrequently updated tables that are frequently joined with large tables. It can lead to unnecessary data redistribution during joins, which can degrade performance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eChange the distribution style of larger tables to ALL distribution and set primary and foreign keys across all tables\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. Distributing large tables with an ALL distribution style would significantly increase storage requirements and could slow down query performance due to the additional data being processed on each node.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eChange the distribution style of small tables to KEY distribution and set primary and foreign keys across all tables\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. While KEY distribution can be effective for distributing data based on the values of a specific column (usually a join key), using ALL for small tables is simply more efficient as it removes the need for redistribution during joins, leading to faster query execution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Distributing_data.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Distributing_data.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Defining_constraints.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/t_Defining_constraints.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-redshift/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:7:24":{"id":"e:7:24","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 7 Â· CÃ¢u 24","correct":[0,1],"answers":["Leverage Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler to query the necessary information from the Redshift cluster. After the exploratory data analysis (EDA) is complete, export the results to Amazon S3 for further processing.","\u003cspan\u003eConfigure AWS Secrets Manager to store the connection details of the Redshift cluster. Use the \u003ccode\u003eAWS SDK for pandas\u003c/code\u003e to retrieve the secret values and connect to the Redshift cluster.\u003c/span\u003e","Use AWS Lambda to run the EDA queries on the Redshift cluster. Store the results in DynamoDB for further processing.","Configure AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store to store the connection details of the Redshift cluster. Use Amazon Redshift Data API to connect to the Redshift cluster.","Launch an Amazon EMR cluster and run an Apache Spark job to query relevant information from the Redshift cluster. Export the results to Amazon S3 for further processing."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is leveraging Amazon Redshift for its data warehousing needs. The data engineering team is tasked with running an exploratory data analysis (EDA) to prepare the data for a predictive model. The team needs to query relevant information from the Redshift cluster and export the results for further processing. The connection details for the Redshift cluster must be securely stored and retrieved.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following approaches would meet the requirements? (SelectTWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon SageMaker Data Wrangler\u003c/strong\u003e reduces the time it takes to aggregate and prepare data for machine learning (ML) from weeks to minutes. With SageMaker Data Wrangler, you can simplify the process of data preparation and feature engineering, and complete each step of the data preparation workflow, including data selection, cleansing, exploration, and visualization from a single visual interface. After the exploratory data analysis (EDA) is complete, exporting the results to Amazon S3 for further processing aligns with the requirements of securely storing and retrieving data for predictive modeling. Amazon S3 has a simple web services interface that you can use to store and retrieve any amount of data at any time from anywhere on the web.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/24f0181ef7a422564f0b52039c384533fc25682e257ffa7637b4dcb3d21991a1.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Secrets Manager protects access to your applications, services, and IT resources without the upfront investment and ongoing maintenance costs of operating your own infrastructure. This service enables you to easily rotate, manage, and retrieve database credentials, API keys, and other secrets throughout their lifecycle.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS SDK\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003efor\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003epandas\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;(previously known as \u003cstrong\u003eawswrangler\u003c/strong\u003e) is an open-source Python project that bridges the pandas library with AWS data and analytics services, effectively expanding pandas\u0027 capabilities into the AWS cloud ecosystem. This tool makes it straightforward to work with various AWS services (e.g, Athena, Glue, Redshift, Secrets Manager, S3) directly from pandas DataFrames. This includes support for a range of file formats such as Parquet, CSV, JSON, and EXCEL.\u0026nbsp;By leveraging the AWS SDK for pandas, developers can write more efficient and streamlined code for a wide range of tasks, such as exploratory data analysis (EDA), data transformation, and loading (ETL) processes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e-\u0026nbsp;Leverage Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler to query the necessary information from the Redshift cluster. After the exploratory data analysis (EDA) is complete, export the results to Amazon S3 for further processing.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e-\u0026nbsp;Configure AWS Secrets Manager to store the connection details of the Redshift cluster. Use the \u003ccode\u003eAWS SDK for pandas\u003c/code\u003e to retrieve the secret values and connect to the Redshift cluster.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Lambda to run the EDA queries on the Redshift cluster. Store the results in DynamoDB for further processing\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because DynamoDB is a NoSQL database service, which is not suitable for storing EDA results that are typically structured and relational in nature.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store to store the connection details of the Redshift cluster. Use Amazon Redshift Data API to connect to the Redshift cluster\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect. While you can use the AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store to store sensitive data like database credentials, it does not have a native integration with Redshift Data API, unlike Secrets Manager. In AWS, Secrets Manager is more preferable for storing database credentials due to its automated secrets rotation and tight integration with AWS database services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch an Amazon EMR cluster and run an Apache Spark job to query relevant information from the Redshift cluster. Export the results to Amazon S3 for further processing\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. While EMR and Spark are powerful tools for big data processing, they are not designed to interact directly with Amazon Redshift for the purpose of running EDA.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/data-wrangler.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/data-wrangler.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/import-data-from-cross-account-amazon-redshift-in-amazon-sagemaker-data-wrangler-for-exploratory-data-analysis-and-data-preparation/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/import-data-from-cross-account-amazon-redshift-in-amazon-sagemaker-data-wrangler-for-exploratory-data-analysis-and-data-preparation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/using-the-amazon-redshift-data-api-to-interact-with-amazon-redshift-clusters/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/using-the-amazon-redshift-data-api-to-interact-with-amazon-redshift-clusters/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these Amazon SageMaker and AWS Secrets Manager Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-sagemaker/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-secrets-manager/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-secrets-manager/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:27":{"id":"e:1:27","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 27","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eFor S3 path s3://INPUT-FOLDER2, the crawler creates one table with columns of both the schemas. And for S3 path s3://INPUT-FOLDER1, the crawler creates two tables, each table having columns of one schema respectively\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFor both the S3 paths s3://INPUT-FOLDER1 and s3://INPUT-FOLDER2, the crawler creates two tables each, each table having columns of one schema respectively\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFor both the S3 paths s3://INPUT-FOLDER1 and s3://INPUT-FOLDER2, the crawler creates one table with columns of both the schemas\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eFor the S3 path s3://INPUT-FOLDER1, the crawler creates one table with columns of both the schemas. For the S3 path s3://INPUT-FOLDER2, the crawler creates two tables, each table having columns of one schema respectively\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eConsider the following scenario on Amazon S3: A folder INPUT-FOLDER1 has 10 files, 8 files with schema SCH_A and 2 files with schema SCH_B, and another folder INPUT-FOLDER2 has 10 files, 7 files with the schema SCH_A and 3 files with the schema SCH_B. The schemas are defined as follows:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSCH_A:\n{ \"id\": 1, \"first_name\": \"John\", \"last_name\": \"Doe\"}\n{ \"id\": 2, \"first_name\": \"Li\", \"last_name\": \"Juan\"}\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode\u003eSCH_B:\n{\"city\":\"Dublin\",\"country\":\"Ireland\"}\n{\"city\":\"Paris\",\"country\":\"France\"}\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is the outcome, when the crawler crawls the Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) path s3://INPUT-FOLDER1 and s3://INPUT-FOLDER2 separately?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFor the S3 path s3://INPUT-FOLDER1, the crawler creates one table with columns of both schemas. For the S3 path s3://INPUT-FOLDER2, the crawler creates two tables, each table having columns of one schema respectively\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor schemas to be considered similar, the following conditions must be true:\n1. The partition threshold is higher than 0.7 (70%).\n2. The maximum number of different schemas (also referred to as \"clusters\" in this context) doesn\u0027t exceed 5.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe crawler infers the schema at the folder level and compares the schemas across all folders. If the schemas that are compared match, that is, if the partition threshold is higher than 70%, then the schemas are denoted as partitions of a table. If they don’t match, then the crawler creates a table for each folder, resulting in a higher number of tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSuppose that the folder DOC-EXAMPLE-FOLDER1 has 10 files, 8 files with schema SCH_A and 2 files with SCH_B.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSuppose that the files with the schema SHC_A are similar to the following:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode\u003e{ \"id\": 1, \"first_name\": \"John\", \"last_name\": \"Doe\"}\n{ \"id\": 2, \"first_name\": \"Li\", \"last_name\": \"Juan\"}\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSuppose that the files with the schema SCH_B are similar to the following:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode\u003e{\"city\":\"Dublin\",\"country\":\"Ireland\"}\n{\"city\":\"Paris\",\"country\":\"France\"}\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen the crawler crawls the Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) path s3://DOC-EXAMPLE-FOLDER1, the crawler creates one table. The table comprises columns of both schemas SCH_A and SCH_B. This is because 80% of the files in the path belong to the SCH_A schema, and 20% of the files belong to the SCH_B schema. Therefore, the partition threshold value is met. Also, the number of different schemas hasn\u0027t exceeded the number of clusters, and the cluster size limit isn\u0027t exceeded.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSuppose that the folder DOC-EXAMPLE-FOLDER2 has 10 files, 7 files with the schema SCH_A and 3 files with the schema SCH_B.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhen the crawler crawls the Amazon S3 path s3://DOC-EXAMPLE-FOLDER2, the crawler creates one table for each file. This is because 70% of the files belong to the schema SCH_A and 30% of the files belong to the schema SCH_B. This means that the partition threshold isn\u0027t met. You can check the crawler logs in Amazon CloudWatch to get information on the created tables.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFor both the S3 paths s3://INPUT-FOLDER1 and s3://INPUT-FOLDER2, the crawler creates two tables each, each table having columns of one schema respectively\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFor both the S3 paths s3://INPUT-FOLDER1 and s3://INPUT-FOLDER2, the crawler creates one table with columns of both the schemas\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFor S3 path s3://INPUT-FOLDER2, the crawler creates one table with columns of both schemas. For S3 path s3://INPUT-FOLDER1, the crawler creates two tables, each table having columns of one schema respectively\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThese three options contradict the explanation provided above, so these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/glue-crawler-detect-schema/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/premiumsupport/knowledge-center/glue-crawler-detect-schema/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-1":{"id":"p:1-1","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 1","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Glue DataBrew to analyze the data stored on Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Athena to analyze the data stored on Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Redshift Spectrum to analyze the data stored on Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage Amazon Glue Data Catalog to analyze the data stored on Amazon S3\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["DataBrew đọc được tệp Excel trên S3 và cho phép khám phá, làm sạch, biến đổi dữ liệu bằng giao diện trực quan. Không cần quản lý máy chủ hoặc tự viết quy trình ETL, nên phù hợp nhất với yêu cầu.","Athena dùng SQL để truy vấn các định dạng như CSV, JSON, Parquet và ORC. Nó không đọc trực tiếp workbook Excel trong tình huống này; cần chuyển định dạng trước.","Redshift Spectrum truy vấn dữ liệu trên S3 mà không cần nạp vào Redshift, nhưng không đọc trực tiếp tệp Excel. Vì vậy chưa giải quyết được dữ liệu đầu vào của đề.","Glue Data Catalog lưu thông tin về vị trí, cấu trúc và metadata của dữ liệu. Nó là danh mục dữ liệu, không phải công cụ trực tiếp làm sạch hoặc phân tích workbook Excel."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial analytics company wants to gather insights from personal finance data stored on Amazon S3 in the Microsoft Excel workbook format.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following represents a serverless solution to interactively discover, clean and transform this raw data for performing this analysis?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-16":{"id":"p:2-16","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 16","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the reservation session data in an Amazon DynamoDB table and configure DynamoDB Accelerator to cache frequently accessed items\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the reservation session data in an Amazon ElastiCache for Redis cluster, configure the application to write all transactional records directly to the cache, and create daily snapshots for durability\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the reservation session data in an Amazon RDS for MySQL cluster and configure RDS Optimized Reads for the database instances\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the reservation session data in an Amazon S3 Apache Iceberg table and enable S3 Transfer Acceleration for low-latency object access\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["DynamoDB lưu dữ liệu phiên bán cấu trúc; DAX cache các item được đọc thường xuyên. Tách dữ liệu bền vững và cache để giảm độ trễ với ít quản trị.","Dùng Redis cache làm nơi duy nhất giữ dữ liệu giao dịch và chỉ snapshot mỗi ngày tạo bài toán độ bền, khôi phục và eviction. DynamoDB phù hợp làm database chính hơn.","RDS MySQL theo mô tả cần quản lý instance và mô hình quan hệ; Optimized Reads không thay thế cache item cho workload đã nêu.","Iceberg trên S3 phù hợp phân tích dữ liệu, không phải đọc/ghi item phiên với độ trễ thấp. Transfer Acceleration không biến S3 thành database giao dịch."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA travel booking company needs to store semi-structured reservation session data for a customer-facing application. The application writes session records only when customers create or modify itineraries, but it reads the records frequently during search, checkout, and support workflows. The database layer must be serverless, require minimal administration, and return frequently accessed records within milliseconds.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-14":{"id":"p:2-14","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 14","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the default access tier\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eOpt for Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration for milliseconds latencies\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage S3 Storage Lens advanced metrics to determine when to move objects to more cost-optimized storage classes\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering with the Archive Instant Access tier\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Intelligent-Tiering tự chuyển giữa các tầng truy cập phù hợp khi cách dùng khó dự đoán. Các tầng truy cập mặc định giữ khả năng truy cập nhanh; không bật tầng lưu trữ cần khôi phục nếu yêu cầu luôn tính bằng mili giây.","Transfer Acceleration tăng hiệu quả truyền dữ liệu đường dài, không tự tối ưu lớp và chi phí lưu trữ dữ liệu ít đọc.","Storage Lens cung cấp số liệu và gợi ý, không tự thực hiện việc chuyển tầng theo truy cập cho từng đối tượng như Intelligent-Tiering.","Archive Instant Access là tầng được tự động sử dụng bên trong Intelligent-Tiering theo tuổi truy cập, không phải cách chọn riêng để đưa mọi đối tượng mới vào ngay. Bộ đề ưu tiên cấu hình Intelligent-Tiering mặc định."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company stores petabytes of data in hundreds of Amazon S3 buckets in the S3 Standard storage class. The data supports several analytics, visualization, and reporting-specific workflows. The data access patterns are unpredictable and variable. Some data is not accessed for months but needs to be available in milliseconds when accessed. The company wants to optimize the costs of storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution can address these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:62":{"id":"e:2:62","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 62","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize AWS Glue jobs to transform the JSON data to .csv format and query the resultant data in .csv format using Amazon Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize AWS Glue jobs to transform both the JSON and .csv format data to parquet format and query the resultant data using Amazon Athena\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize Amazon Athena for querying the data, employing standard SQL for structured data sources and PartiQL for handling data stored in JSON format\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize Amazon Redshift Spectrum for querying the data, employing standard SQL for structured data sources and PartiQL for handling data stored in JSON format\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company maintains its datasets in JSON and .csv formats in an Amazon S3 bucket and utilizes Amazon RDS for Microsoft SQL Server, Amazon DynamoDB (in provisioned capacity mode), and an Amazon Redshift cluster. The data engineering team is tasked with creating a solution that enables data scientists to query all these data sources using an SQL-like syntax.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat solution would fulfill these requirements while incurring the least operational overhead and minimizing the costs?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize Amazon Athena for querying the data, employing standard SQL for structured data sources and PartiQL for handling data stored in JSON format\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe AWS Glue Data Catalog contains references to data that is used as sources and targets of your extract, transform, and load (ETL) jobs in AWS Glue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/35dff286e1196603004856680134250535b74ddde449855025ae4b76b7de6279.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/35dff286e1196603004856680134250535b74ddde449855025ae4b76b7de6279.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIf you have data in sources other than Amazon S3, you can use Athena Federated Query to query the data in place or build pipelines that extract data from multiple data sources and store them in Amazon S3. With Athena Federated Query, you can run SQL queries across data stored in relational, non-relational, object, and custom data sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAthena uses data source connectors that run on AWS Lambda to run federated queries. A data source connector is a piece of code that can translate between your target data source and Athena. You can think of a connector as an extension of Athena\u0027s query engine. Prebuilt Athena data source connectors exist for data sources like Amazon CloudWatch Logs, Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon DocumentDB, and Amazon RDS, and JDBC-compliant relational data sources such MySQL, and PostgreSQL under the Apache 2.0 license.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena also allows federated query pass-through, which allows entire queries to be executed directly on the underlying data source. Federated query pass-through allows you to take advantage of the unique functions, query language, and performance capabilities of different data sources, and can result in faster query execution and less data processed by Athena. You can run Athena queries on DynamoDB using the PartiQL language. Federated query pass-through is also useful when you want to run SELECT queries that aggregate, join, or invoke functions of your data source that are not available in Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize Amazon Redshift Spectrum for querying the data, employing standard SQL for structured data sources and PartiQL for handling data stored in JSON format\u003c/strong\u003e - Using Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the data would involve additional overhead of setting up Redshift spectrum on the existing Redshift cluster. Redshift Spectrum would also turn out to be costlier than Amazon Athena, as it leverages the resources of the Redshift cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize AWS Glue jobs to transform the JSON data to .csv format and query the resultant data in .csv format using Amazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e - Using AWS Glue jobs to transform the JSON data to .csv format represents unnecessary work to build a solution, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLeverage AWS Glue to crawl the various data sources and store the resultant metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Utilize AWS Glue jobs to transform both the JSON and .csv format data to parquet format and query the resultant data using Amazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e - Using AWS Glue jobs to transform both the JSON and .csv format data to parquet format represents unnecessary work to build a solution, so this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-a-data-source.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-a-data-source.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2024/04/amazon-athena-federated-query-pass-through/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2024/04/amazon-athena-federated-query-pass-through/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/catalog-and-crawler.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:39":{"id":"e:4:39","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 39","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCapture data in an Amazon EC2 instance store and then publish this data to Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose with Amazon S3 as the destination. Use Amazon Athena to query the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCapture data in Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Use Amazon Kinesis Data Analytics to query and analyze this streaming data in real-time\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCapture data in Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose with Amazon Redshift as the destination. Use Amazon Redshift to query the data\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCapture data in an Amazon EBS volume and then publish this data to Amazon ElastiCache for Redis. Subscribe to the Redis channel to query the data\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is using a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances to ingest Internet-of-Things (IoT) data from various data sources. The data is in JSON format and ingestion rates can be as high as 1 MB/s. When an EC2 instance is restarted, the in-flight data is lost. The data engineering team at the company wants to store as well as query the ingested data in near-real-time.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following solutions provides near-real-time data querying that is scalable with minimal data loss?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCapture data in Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose with Amazon Redshift as the destination. Use Amazon Redshift to query the data\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Firehose is the easiest way to reliably load streaming data into data lakes, data stores, and analytics services. It can capture, transform, and deliver streaming data to Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, Amazon Elasticsearch Service, generic HTTP endpoints, and service providers like Datadog, New Relic, MongoDB, and Splunk.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Firehose is the easiest way to capture, transform, and load streaming data into Redshift for near real-time analytics. It is also an auto-scaling solution as there is no need to provision any shards like Kinesis Data Streams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift allows you to run complex analytic queries against petabytes of structured data, using sophisticated query optimization, columnar storage on high-performance local disks, and massively parallel query execution. Most results come back in seconds.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCapture data in an Amazon EC2 instance store and then publish this data to Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose with Amazon S3 as the destination. Use Amazon Athena to query the data\u003c/strong\u003e - Instance store is a temporary storage available on Amazon EC2 instances. The in-flight data (that is, data arriving from the source) being processed by a specific Amazon EC2 instance will be lost in case that instance is restarted. Hence, this cannot be the option for the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCapture data in an Amazon EBS volume and then publish this data to Amazon ElastiCache for Redis. Subscribe to the Redis channel to query the data\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon EBS volumes cannot be used to store high volume data. Amazon EBS can be used to store cache data if a database is hosted on an Amazon EC2 instance. However, Amazon EBS cannot be used in place of a database. Amazon ElastiCache is a caching service. It is not relevant to the given use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCapture data in Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Use Amazon Kinesis Data Analytics to query and analyze this streaming data in real-time\u003c/strong\u003e - For Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, you have to manually allocate the shards for scaling the data ingestion process. Amazon Kinesis Data Streams (KDS) and Amazon Kinesis Data Analytics are for real-time processing of data and cannot provide long-term storage of data unlike a database or a data warehouse. So, this option is not right for the current use case.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/redshift/features/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/redshift/features/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-firehose/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-analytics/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-analytics/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/faqs/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:1":{"id":"e:6:1","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 1","correct":[2],"answers":["Implement Amazon RDS with IAM database authentication to manage fine-grained access at various levels.","Implement an Amazon Aurora database as a catalog and control access using SQL GRANTs at different levels.","Establish a data lake and a data catalog using AWS Lake Formation, controlling access via Lake Formation data filters.","Deploy a Hive metastore using Amazon EMR and manage user access through HiveQL data definition language statements."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer at a multinational corporation is in charge of setting up a system for managing extensive organizational data. The system must be capable of granular access control at multiple levels: database, table, column, row, and cell. The solution must be implemented with minimal operational overhead. The company already uses Database audit logging to track access to sensitive data, but a more comprehensive access control mechanism is required.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich option meets these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation is the optimal choice for setting up a centralized metadata storage solution that demands fine-grained access control. Utilizing Lake Formation data filters, a data engineer can enforce security measures at the database, table, column, row, and cell levels. This capability, coupled with its scalability and reliability, makes Lake Formation the ideal solution with minimal operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/81d1d3a867ae85f70961f120ee01e0ef6d496787cd1a7e843c30a3d627a90eae.jpg\" alt=\"Registering an Amazon S3 location in AWS Lake Formation\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEstablish a data lake and a data catalog using AWS Lake Formation, controlling access via Lake Formation data filters.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement Amazon RDS with IAM database authentication to manage fine-grained access at various levels\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Although Amazon RDS supports IAM database authentication for access management, it does not inherently provide the level of detailed control (specifically at the row and cell levels) required in this scenario. This limitation just makes it less suitable than Lake Formation for managing intricate permissions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eImplement an Amazon Aurora database as a catalog and control access using SQL GRANTs at different levels\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. While Amazon Aurora allows extensive access control through SQL GRANTs, it lacks the native capability to manage permissions at the row and cell levels without additional tools or configurations. Furthermore, this approach involves significant operational management, especially if integrating with a Hive metastore.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy a Hive metastore using Amazon EMR and manage user access through HiveQL data definition language statements\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. Deploying a Hive metastore on Amazon EMR does not simply offer the necessary granularity for permissions control at the row and cell levels. Moreover, using HiveQL for access management does not align with the requirement for minimal operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/data-filters-about.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/data-filters-about.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/managing-dl-tutorial.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/managing-dl-tutorial.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Redshift Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-54":{"id":"p:1-54","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 54","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS Lambda with an Amazon RDS database to provide a serverless architecture\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon CloudFront with Amazon DynamoDB for greater speed and low latency access to static assets\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Lambda with Amazon ElastiCache and Amazon RDS for serving static assets at high speed and low latency\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon CloudFront with Amazon S3 as the storage solution for the static assets\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Tệp tĩnh không cần Lambda và database quan hệ để phân phối. Các thành phần này làm tăng công và chi phí.","DynamoDB phù hợp key-value/document, không phải kho phân phối tệp tĩnh kinh tế như S3. CloudFront nên dùng S3 làm origin trong trường hợp này.","Lambda, ElastiCache và RDS tạo nhiều tầng không cần thiết cho tệp tĩnh. S3 kết hợp CDN giải quyết trực tiếp hơn.","S3 lưu tệp tĩnh, CloudFront lưu đệm và phân phối gần người dùng. Giảm tải hệ thống ứng dụng với ít quản lý máy chủ."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn online gaming application has a large chunk of its traffic coming from users who download static assets such as historic leaderboard reports and the game tactics for various games. The current infrastructure and design are unable to handle the traffic and application freezes on most of the pages.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following is a cost-optimal solution that requires the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:6:33":{"id":"e:6:33","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 33","correct":[0],"answers":["Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and use S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR) to replicate the data to buckets in each region.","Use Amazon S3 Express One Zone storage class for the bucket and use S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR) to replicate the data to buckets in each region.","Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and use AWS Direct Connect to establish a dedicated network connection from each region to AWS.","Use Amazon S3 Express One Zone storage class for the bucket and use AWS Direct Connect to establish a dedicated network connection from each region to AWS."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA financial company has an Amazon S3 bucket that is used as a data lake for various cryptocurrency data sent from crypto exchanges across the globe. The DevOps team notices inconsistencies in data upload times across different regions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe company aims to guarantee high availability and fast access to the S3 bucket data across all regions as well as ensure data sovereignty. The challenge lies in accomplishing these objectives with minimal management overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich set of actions can fulfill the requirements with the LEAST amount of management overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3 Transfer Acceleration\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is a bucket-level feature that enables fast, easy, and secure transfers of files over long distances between your client and your Amazon S3 bucket. It takes advantage of Amazon CloudFront’s globally distributed edge locations. As the data arrives at an edge location, data is routed to Amazon S3 over an optimized network path. This results in faster data transfers and reduced latency, which is crucial for the scenario’s requirement of fast access retrieval times.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/634c54b34080e185b6978e899070b3d3ac6188739d636e42d5bf3caa6fcc4da8.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR)\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is a feature that automatically replicates data between buckets across different AWS Regions. This helps to reduce latency by allowing users to access a copy of the data that is closer to them. It also addresses the requirement of data sovereignty, as data is stored within the specific regions where the company operates.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0024cfca85c0bebc231b10f4105fe54cec8494b1ddb324b8d1b722b0b4911a2b.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBoth S3 Transfer Acceleration and S3 Cross-Region Replication are fully managed services, which means that AWS handles all the heavy lifting, and you don’t have to worry about the operational burden of scaling, patching, and maintaining servers or software. This allows you to focus on your core business and applications, thereby fulfilling the requirement of the least amount of management overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and use S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR) to replicate the data to buckets in each region.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon S3 Express One Zone storage class for the bucket and use S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR) to replicate the data to buckets in each region\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. Amazon S3 Express One Zone is a high-performance, single-Availability Zone storage class that\u0027s purpose-built to deliver consistent single-digit millisecond data access for one specific Availability Zone only. This type of storage class won\u0027t suit the scenario since the data sources are sending data from all over the globe. While S3 CRR can help with data sovereignty and availability, the use of S3 Express One Zone does not meet the requirement for fast transfer times from a group of global sources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and use AWS Direct Connect to establish a dedicated network connection from each region to AWS\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because while S3 Transfer Acceleration can improve access times, AWS Direct Connect does not address the requirement of data sovereignty as it does not replicate the data to different regions. AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated network connection from your premises to AWS, but it does not automatically replicate data across regions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon S3 Express One Zone storage class for the bucket and use AWS Direct Connect to establish a dedicated network connection from each region to AWS\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003ean Amazon S3 Express One Zone is not suitable for a solution where the data producers are sending files from different parts of the world. Take note that this particular storage class is only fast in a given Availability Zone and AWS Direct Connect does not address the requirement of data sovereignty as it does not replicate the data to different regions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/transfer-acceleration/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/s3/transfer-acceleration/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://medium.com/cloud-techies/s3-same-region-replication-srr-and-cross-region-replication-crr-34d446806bab\"\u003ehttps://medium.com/cloud-techies/s3-same-region-replication-srr-and-cross-region-replication-crr-34d446806bab\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon S3 Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:46":{"id":"e:6:46","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 46","correct":[1,3],"answers":["Initiate an AWS Glue job every 20 minutes via Amazon EventBridge Schedule. Process the data with an Apache Spark DataFrame and load it into Amazon Redshift tables.","Initiate an AWS Glue workflow job every 20 minutes via Amazon EventBridge rules. Use a Glue crawler to detect schema modifications and then process and load data into Amazon Redshift with Apache Spark DataFrame after schema validation.","Create two AWS Lambda functions: one to trigger a Glue crawler upon new S3 file uploads and another to execute a Glue job processing data with Apache Spark DataFrame and loading it into Amazon Redshift. The second function is coordinated by an Amazon EventBridge rule after the crawler\u0027s successful execution.","Initiate a Glue workflow through an S3 event notification with an AWS Lambda function. Trigger an on-demand Glue crawler followed by a Glue job using Apache Spark DataFrame to process and load data into Amazon Redshift upon schema detection.","Use an Amazon EMR cluster with Apache Hive to process the data every 20 minutes and load it into Amazon Redshift tables."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team needs to develop an ETL(Extract, Transform, and Load) pipeline to handle data inflow from ten distinct source systems. These systems generate files in CSV, JSON, and Apache Parquet formats. These files, varying in size from 5 MB to 15 GB, are stored in a single Amazon S3 bucket every 20 minutes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe pipeline must dynamically adjust to schema changes while processing and loading the data into ten specific tables in an Amazon Redshift database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solutions will MOST effectively meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue workflows\u003c/strong\u003e enable the orchestration of complex ETL tasks by linking multiple jobs and crawlers, allowing for automated data processing sequences. These workflows can be initiated based on predefined schedules or in response to specific events, facilitating flexible and efficient data management strategies.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon EventBridge\u003c/strong\u003e (formerly CloudWatch Events) is a serverless event bus service that connects AWS services, applications, and devices. Using simple rules, events in one service can be used to trigger actions in another.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eApache Spark DataFrames\u003c/strong\u003e are distributed data collections arranged into named columns that enable parallel SQL operations on massive datasets. Glue jobs can use Spark DataFrames to process and convert data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eS3 event notifications\u003c/strong\u003e allow you to execute a Lambda function anytime an object is created or updated in an S3 bucket. Once new data arrives in S3, this can be used to start a Glue process for near-real-time ETL.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/1efa1848d968b8d58dcb803ebd806b3f92fdd444323dcacb5f79eea4c9e2641c.png\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue is a managed ETL service that facilitates the preparation and loading of data for analytics. It can automatically discover the schema of the data through Glue crawlers, making it ideal for environments where the data schema can change over time. The integration with Amazon EventBridge or S3 event notifications allows for automating these ETL jobs in response to data arriving in S3, adhering to the requirement of handling files every 20 minutes. Processing data with Apache Spark DataFrame before loading it into Amazon Redshift is an efficient method for handling large datasets and transforming them as needed.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answers are:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e- \u003cstrong\u003eInitiate an AWS Glue workflow job every 20 minutes via Amazon EventBridge rules. Use a Glue crawler to detect schema modifications and then process and load data into Amazon Redshift with Apache Spark DataFrame after schema validation.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003e- \u003cstrong\u003eInitiate a Glue workflow through an S3 event notification with an AWS Lambda function. Trigger an on-demand Glue crawler followed by a Glue job using Apache Spark DataFrame to process and load data into Amazon Redshift upon schema detection.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eInitiate an AWS Glue job every 20 minutes via Amazon EventBridge Schedule. Process the data with an Apache Spark DataFrame and load it into Amazon Redshift tables \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because it primarily lacks dynamic schema adaptation. While leveraging AWS Glue and Amazon EventBridge for periodic data processing and loading, this option does not include a mechanism like a Glue crawler for detecting and adapting to schema changes in the source data. Schema changes are critical in this scenario, and by handling them dynamically, the ETL process could succeed when unexpected changes occur.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e Create two AWS Lambda functions: one to trigger a Glue crawler upon new S3 file uploads and another to execute a Glue job processing data with Apache Spark DataFrame and loading it into Amazon Redshift. The second function is coordinated by an Amazon EventBridge rule after the crawler\u0027s successful execution \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because it only introduces unnecessary complexity and potential points of failure. While this solution addresses schema changes using a Glue crawler, coordinating multiple AWS Lambda functions adds complexity and could lead to inefficiencies. The process might be more streamlined and reliable than directly using Glue workflows, which are designed to handle such ETL tasks more elegantly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse an Amazon EMR cluster with Apache Hive to process the data every 20 minutes and load it into Amazon Redshift tables\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect because it does not efficiently address dynamic schema changes and involves more complex setup and maintenance compared to AWS Glue-based solutions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventNotifications.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Tutorials Dojo Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:1-56":{"id":"p:1-56","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 56","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eTransfer the on-premises data into multiple AWS Snowball Edge Storage Optimized devices. Copy the AWS Snowball Edge data into Amazon S3 Glacier\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up AWS direct connect between the on-premises data center and AWS Cloud. Use this connection to transfer the data into Amazon S3 Glacier\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection between the on-premises data center and AWS Cloud. Use this connection to transfer the data into Amazon S3 Glacier\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eTransfer the on-premises data into multiple AWS Snowball Edge Storage Optimized devices. Copy the AWS Snowball Edge data into Amazon S3 and create a lifecycle policy to transition the data into Amazon S3 Glacier\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Theo quy trình trong bộ đề, dữ liệu nhập từ Snowball vào S3 trước, không nhập trực tiếp vào Glacier. Cần thêm Lifecycle để chuyển lớp lưu trữ.","Thiết lập Direct Connect để di chuyển một lần 5 PB có chi phí và thời gian truyền lớn. Bộ đề ưu tiên vận chuyển dữ liệu bằng thiết bị.","VPN qua mạng không phù hợp nhất về thời gian và hiệu quả cho một lần chuyển 5 PB. Thiết bị nhập dữ liệu vật lý là phương án bộ đề chọn.","Dùng nhiều Snowball Edge chuyển dữ liệu từ trung tâm dữ liệu vào S3, sau đó Lifecycle chuyển sang Glacier để lưu lâu dài. Hai bước nhập dữ liệu và chuyển lớp cần tách rõ."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media company wants to get out of the business of owning and maintaining its own IT infrastructure. As part of this digital transformation, the media company wants to archive about 5 petabytes of data in its on-premises data center for durable long-term storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is your recommendation to migrate this data in the MOST cost-optimal way?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:1-11":{"id":"p:1-11","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 11","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Lake Formation to define access policies for each of the teams separately to restrict access to query history for the respective teams and control costs\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an IAM group for each team. Assign appropriate permissions to each of the IAM groups by associating custom IAM policies that restrict access to the query history and control costs\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eLeverage S3 Access Points to control access to query history of the teams by creating unique access control policies for each access point\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an Athena workgroup for each team and apply tags. Use these tags in a new IAM policy to configure appropriate permissions to the workgroups\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Lake Formation quản lý quyền với dữ liệu và metadata, không phải cơ chế tách lịch sử truy vấn Athena và đặt giới hạn quét cho từng nhóm.","Chỉ tạo nhóm IAM không tạo ra ranh giới quản lý truy vấn và mức sử dụng dữ liệu cần thiết. Cần workgroup, rồi dùng IAM để giới hạn quyền với từng workgroup.","S3 Access Points kiểm soát truy cập đối tượng S3. Chúng không trực tiếp quản lý lịch sử truy vấn hay giới hạn dữ liệu quét của Athena.","Mỗi đội dùng một Athena workgroup để tách hoạt động truy vấn và cấu hình kiểm soát dữ liệu quét. Kết hợp tags và IAM để giới hạn quyền; dữ liệu kết quả trên S3 cũng cần quyền phù hợp."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eMultiple teams within a company use Amazon Athena to run ad-hoc queries on its data stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. The usage costs for Athena have been running high and the company wants to set a limit on the amount of data that can be scanned by each team. Also, the teams should not have access to queries, query results, or query history of other teams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs a data engineer, how will you implement the requirement with the least operational and cost overhead?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:4:24":{"id":"e:4:24","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 24","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable AWS Glue Auto Scaling so the job can add and remove workers automatically while reading the historical S3 files\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRewrite the historical pipeline to compact all files with an EMR Spark job before running the AWS Glue job for forecasting preparation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eIncrease the number of AWS Glue workers so Spark has more executors available to read the small files in parallel\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eConfigure AWS Glue DynamicFrame file grouping by using groupFiles and groupSize when the job reads the small S3 files\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics company uses AWS Glue ETL jobs to collect and process transaction data into an Amazon S3 bucket. The ingestion pipeline writes one small file every hour, and each file is between 250 KB and 350 KB. The company now needs to train a demand forecasting model by processing 5 years of historical data that contains 45,000 small files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eA data engineer creates a second AWS Glue ETL job with the smallest worker type to read the historical files from Amazon S3 and prepare the dataset for downstream analysis. The job runs slowly because Spark creates many tasks for a large number of small files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will improve the performance of the second pipeline MOST cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eConfigure AWS Glue DynamicFrame file grouping by using groupFiles and groupSize when the job reads the small S3 files\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DynamicFrame file grouping is the most cost-effective solution for this scenario. The performance issue comes from processing a large number of very small files, which can cause Spark to create excessive tasks and add scheduling overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe groupFiles and groupSize options allow AWS Glue to group multiple small S3 files into larger logical units when reading the data. This reduces the number of Spark tasks that the job needs to manage and improves throughput without requiring more worker capacity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis approach directly addresses the small-file problem. Because the input contains 45,000 files, explicit grouping is useful because automatic grouping is enabled only when the input contains more than 50,000 files.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ee3b5693346871e0c9c8ff530dba7b099cd597faabf8c0e77ac1e8f701052e11.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/ee3b5693346871e0c9c8ff530dba7b099cd597faabf8c0e77ac1e8f701052e11.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/best-practices-to-scale-apache-spark-jobs-and-partition-data-with-aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/best-practices-to-scale-apache-spark-jobs-and-partition-data-with-aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable AWS Glue Auto Scaling so the job can add and remove workers automatically while reading the historical S3 files\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue Auto Scaling can help jobs adjust worker capacity based on workload demand. It can reduce the need to manually choose a fixed number of workers for some Spark workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that Auto Scaling does not directly solve excessive task creation caused by many small input files. File grouping targets the root cause more directly and avoids adding capacity before reducing Spark scheduling overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eIncrease the number of AWS Glue workers so Spark has more executors available to read the small files in parallel\u003c/strong\u003e - Increasing the number of workers can add more Spark executors and can help when a job is compute-bound or needs more parallel processing capacity. This can improve some large ETL workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option falls short because the problem is not simply a lack of workers. The job is inefficient because it reads many small files, so adding workers can increase cost without reducing the task overhead caused by the small-file layout.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRewrite the historical pipeline to compact all files with an EMR Spark job before running the AWS Glue job for forecasting preparation\u003c/strong\u003e - Compacting small files into larger files can improve long-term analytics performance. Amazon EMR Spark jobs can perform large-scale compaction when a team needs a separate processing environment.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis creates additional operational overhead and cost. The requirement asks to improve the second AWS Glue pipeline, and AWS Glue already provides DynamicFrame file grouping to reduce small-file read overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/grouping-input-files.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/grouping-input-files.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-s3-home.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-connect-s3-home.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/parallelize-tasks.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/tuning-aws-glue-for-apache-spark/parallelize-tasks.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/best-practices-to-scale-apache-spark-jobs-and-partition-data-with-aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/best-practices-to-scale-apache-spark-jobs-and-partition-data-with-aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:5":{"id":"e:6:5","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 5","correct":[0],"answers":["Register the Amazon S3 location in AWS Lake Formation. Utilize Lake Formation\u0027s data filters to manage access for different users. Query data using Amazon Athena.","Use AWS Glue to create ETL jobs that transform and move the data into Amazon Redshift. Apply row-level security in Redshift for different user groups.","Create a query interface using Amazon Athena to execute backend data queries. Create user groups in Amazon Cognito and assign permission levels based on user-specific PII access requirements.","Provision IAM roles with granular access permissions and associate these roles with IAM user groups. Attach an IAM policy to the group that restricts access at the column level."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has centralized its data storage in an Amazon S3 bucket. Within this repository, specific datasets include personally identifiable information (PII). Given the diversity of user groups needing access to raw data, a data engineer must ensure that each group can only access the PII relevant to them.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution would satisfy the requirement in the LEAST amount of operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Lake Formation\u003c/strong\u003e streamlines the process of setting up a secure data lake in AWS. It provides fine-grained access control to sensitive data stored in Amazon S3 through a simple request or revoke process, allowing organizations to define who has access to specific aspects of their data. When integrated with \u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Athena\u003c/strong\u003e, a serverless interactive query service, Lake Formation enables column-level security (CLS) by leveraging data filters. This approach significantly reduces the effort required to manage access to PII data, as it directly associates user access levels with their IAM roles, eliminating the need for complex custom solutions or manual intervention.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/17d0d6e4012bd290002af76c4b6a9613d522b87d43f4755e254fa1adeb57a9da.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRegister the Amazon S3 location in AWS Lake Formation. Utilize Lake Formation\u0027s data filters to manage access for different users. Query data using Amazon Athena.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue to create ETL jobs that transform and move the data into Amazon Redshift. Apply row-level security in Redshift for different user groups\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e is incorrect. This approach introduces additional complexity, such as the need for ETL jobs and data transfer into Redshift. Implementing row-level security in Redshift adds overhead in terms of managing the data pipeline, handling transformations, and dealing with ongoing data movement. It’s not as streamlined as using Lake Formation and Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate a query interface using Amazon Athena to execute backend data queries. Create user groups in Amazon Cognito and assign permission levels based on user-specific PII access requirements \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because it mainly suggests building a custom solution, which inherently requires more effort than using AWS\u0027s managed services designed for security and access control. While integrating Athena and Cognito is feasible, the development, maintenance, and scalability of custom UIs typically demand significantly more resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eProvision IAM roles with granular access permissions and associate these roles with IAM user groups. Attach an IAM policy to the group that restricts access at the column level \u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect because it primarily uses IAM roles and identity-based policies for column-level access, which isn\u0027t directly supported by IAM for S3 resources.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/what-is.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/welcome.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/welcome.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/what-is-amazon-cognito.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/cognito/latest/developerguide/what-is-amazon-cognito.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Lake Formation Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:59":{"id":"e:3:59","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 59","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up AWS Lake Formation, assign LF-Tags to AWS Glue Data Catalog resources, and enable Lake Formation tag-based access control for department-specific permissions\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift and Amazon Redshift Spectrum as the only query layer for the data lake, and configure one Redshift IAM role with access to the S3 datasets\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDeploy an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database with the aws_s3 extension, use AWS Step Functions to invoke Lambda functions that synchronize department-specific S3 data into tables, and grant database permissions by department\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse S3 Access Points for each department, configure one access point policy per department, and require data engineers to update the access point policies whenever datasets, columns, or department permissions change\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare analytics company maintains an Amazon S3 based data lake that contains datasets for clinical operations, finance, research, and compliance teams. The data lake receives several million patient interaction records each day. A data engineer must design an access control solution that allows each department to access only the datasets and columns that the department is authorized to use. The solution must enforce least privilege and minimize ongoing permission management effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST operational effort?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up AWS Lake Formation, assign LF-Tags to AWS Glue Data Catalog resources, and enable Lake Formation tag-based access control for department-specific permissions\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation provides centralized governance for data lakes that use Amazon S3 and the AWS Glue Data Catalog. Lake Formation permissions can control access to databases, tables, columns, and underlying S3 data for supported analytics services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eLake Formation tag-based access control, or LF-TBAC, lets the company assign LF-Tags to Data Catalog resources and grant permissions based on tag expressions. For example, the company can tag datasets by department, sensitivity level, region, or data domain, then grant each department access only to resources that match approved tag values.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution has the least operational effort for a large, multi-department data lake because it avoids maintaining large numbers of resource-specific IAM policies. As new datasets arrive, data stewards can classify the resources with LF-Tags, and the existing tag-based permissions can continue to enforce least privilege at scale.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6ced6f50eed2a279096f723c5487a9e8a7ad1d3d6a1e94e6ccd01a81bb6771a2.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6ced6f50eed2a279096f723c5487a9e8a7ad1d3d6a1e94e6ccd01a81bb6771a2.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9d68cf008a6eb971f1374964fd86ceec08daf6abf7134b87b8733a41b8755bb1.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9d68cf008a6eb971f1374964fd86ceec08daf6abf7134b87b8733a41b8755bb1.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database with the aws_s3 extension, use AWS Step Functions to invoke Lambda functions that synchronize department-specific S3 data into tables, and grant database permissions by department\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL with the aws_s3 extension can import or export data between PostgreSQL and Amazon S3. This can support database-centric workflows that need to move files into or out of PostgreSQL.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option creates a separate database copy of data lake content and requires custom synchronization logic. It does not provide centralized lake governance across S3 datasets, Glue Data Catalog metadata, table permissions, and column-level access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse S3 Access Points for each department, configure one access point policy per department, and require data engineers to update the access point policies whenever datasets, columns, or department permissions change\u003c/strong\u003e - S3 Access Points can simplify access management for shared datasets by creating named access points with distinct policies. They can be useful when multiple applications or teams need different access paths to the same bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option still requires data engineers to manage access point policies as datasets and department permissions change. It also does not provide the same fine-grained Data Catalog permissions, column-level controls, and tag-based governance model that Lake Formation LF-TBAC provides.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift and Amazon Redshift Spectrum as the only query layer for the data lake, and configure one Redshift IAM role with access to the S3 datasets\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift Spectrum can query data stored in Amazon S3, and Redshift can assume an IAM role to access external data. This approach can provide a controlled SQL entry point for some analytics workloads.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, a single Redshift IAM role with broad S3 access does not enforce department-specific least privilege across the data lake. It also makes Redshift the primary access layer instead of using Lake Formation to govern access centrally across supported analytics services.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/tag-based-access-control.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/tag-based-access-control.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/managing-tags.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/managing-tags.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/TBAC-assigning-tags.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/TBAC-assigning-tags.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/granting-catalog-perms-TBAC.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/granting-catalog-perms-TBAC.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/easily-manage-your-data-lake-at-scale-using-tag-based-access-control-in-aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/easily-manage-your-data-lake-at-scale-using-tag-based-access-control-in-aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/build-a-modern-data-architecture-and-data-mesh-pattern-at-scale-using-aws-lake-formation-tag-based-access-control/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:14":{"id":"e:4:14","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 14","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Studio\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue workflows\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Step Functions\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has established an ETL (extract, transform, and load) data pipeline using AWS Glue. A data engineer is tasked with crawling a table from Microsoft SQL Server and must manage the pipeline to extract, transform, and load the crawled data into an Amazon S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich AWS service or feature would facilitate these tasks most cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue workflows\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eExtract, transform, and load (ETL) orchestration is a common mechanism for building big data pipelines. Orchestration for parallel ETL processing requires the use of multiple tools to perform a variety of operations. AWS Glue workflows provide a visual and programmatic tool to author data pipelines by combining AWS Glue crawlers for schema discovery and AWS Glue Spark and Python shell jobs to transform the data. A workflow consists of one of more task nodes arranged as a graph. Relationships can be defined and parameters passed between task nodes to enable you to build pipelines of varying complexity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9f2657b4d7b675d7889905840bb4f30ee03d2f30978cd4d0013e275480d94993.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9f2657b4d7b675d7889905840bb4f30ee03d2f30978cd4d0013e275480d94993.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-an-etl-pipeline-using-aws-glue-workflows-triggers-and-crawlers-with-custom-classifiers/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-an-etl-pipeline-using-aws-glue-workflows-triggers-and-crawlers-with-custom-classifiers/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUsing AWS Glue workflows, you can design a complex multi-job, multi-crawler ETL process that AWS Glue can run and track as a single entity. After you create a workflow and specify the jobs, crawlers, and triggers in the workflow, you can run the workflow on demand or on a schedule.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Step Functions\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Step Functions is a generic serverless orchestration service offered by AWS. The given use case requires a feature that facilitates complex multi-job, multi-crawler ETL processes that AWS Glue can run and track as a single entity. Therefore, AWS Glue workflows is a much better fit for the given requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue Studio\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue Studio is a graphical interface that makes it easy to create, run, and monitor data integration jobs in AWS Glue. You can visually compose data transformation workflows and seamlessly run them on the Apache Spark-based serverless ETL engine in AWS Glue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool that makes it easier for data analysts and data scientists to clean and normalize data to prepare it for analytics and machine learning (ML). You can choose from over 250 prebuilt transformations to automate data preparation tasks, all without the need to write any code.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-an-etl-pipeline-using-aws-glue-workflows-triggers-and-crawlers-with-custom-classifiers/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-an-etl-pipeline-using-aws-glue-workflows-triggers-and-crawlers-with-custom-classifiers/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/orchestrate-using-workflows.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/orchestrate-using-workflows.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/what-is-glue.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/databrew/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/glue/features/databrew/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:40":{"id":"e:1:40","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 40","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up AWS direct connect between the on-premises data center and AWS Cloud. Use this connection to transfer the data into Amazon S3 Glacier\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection between the on-premises data center and AWS Cloud. Use this connection to transfer the data into Amazon S3 Glacier\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eTransfer the on-premises data into multiple AWS Snowball Edge Storage Optimized devices. Copy the AWS Snowball Edge data into Amazon S3 and create a lifecycle policy to transition the data into Amazon S3 Glacier\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eTransfer the on-premises data into multiple AWS Snowball Edge Storage Optimized devices. Copy the AWS Snowball Edge data into Amazon S3 Glacier\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA media company wants to get out of the business of owning and maintaining its own IT infrastructure. As part of this digital transformation, the media company wants to archive about 5 petabytes of data in its on-premises data center for durable long-term storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhat is your recommendation to migrate this data in the MOST cost-optimal way?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTransfer the on-premises data into multiple AWS Snowball Edge Storage Optimized devices. Copy the AWS Snowball Edge data into Amazon S3 and create a lifecycle policy to transition the data into Amazon S3 Glacier\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Snowball Edge Storage Optimized is the optimal choice if you need to securely and quickly transfer dozens of terabytes to petabytes of data to AWS. It provides up to 80 TB of usable HDD storage, 40 vCPUs, 1 TB of SATA SSD storage, and up to 40 Gb network connectivity to address large-scale data transfer and pre-processing use cases.\nThe data stored on the AWS Snowball Edge device can be copied into the Amazon S3 bucket and later transitioned into Amazon S3 Glacier via a lifecycle policy. You can\u0027t directly copy data from AWS Snowball Edge devices into Amazon S3 Glacier.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTransfer the on-premises data into multiple AWS Snowball Edge Storage Optimized devices. Copy the AWS Snowball Edge data into Amazon S3 Glacier\u003c/strong\u003e - As mentioned earlier, you can\u0027t directly copy data from AWS Snowball Edge devices into Amazon S3 Glacier. Hence, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up AWS direct connect between the on-premises data center and AWS Cloud. Use this connection to transfer the data into Amazon S3 Glacier\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Direct Connect lets you establish a dedicated network connection between your network and one of the AWS Direct Connect locations. Using industry-standard 802.1q VLANs, this dedicated connection can be partitioned into multiple virtual interfaces. Direct Connect involves significant monetary investment and takes more than a month to set up, therefore it\u0027s not the correct fit for this use case where just a one-time data transfer has to be done.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection between the on-premises data center and AWS Cloud. Use this connection to transfer the data into Amazon S3 Glacier\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Site-to-Site VPN enables you to securely connect your on-premises network or branch office site to your Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC). VPN Connections are a good solution if you have an immediate need, and have low to modest bandwidth requirements. Because of the high data volume for the given use case, Site-to-Site VPN is not the correct choice.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/snowball/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/snowball/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:65":{"id":"e:4:65","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 65","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eEnable and download audit reports from AWS Config\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable audit metrics on Amazon CloudWatch\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable audit trail for Amazon Redshift on Amazon CloudTrail\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eEnable audit logging for Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA healthcare company uses an Amazon Redshift database cluster to store sensitive user data. The regulatory guidelines mandate logging so that any database authentication attempts as well as the connections/disconnections are recorded. Also, the logs must include a record of each query executed against the database along with the database user who executed that query.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options represents the best solution for these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable audit logging for Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift logs information about connections and user activities in your database. These logs help you monitor the database for security and troubleshooting purposes, a process called database auditing. The logs are stored in Amazon S3 buckets.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/98eecb7cbf59c60157c1a5fb54c38c9200190ad423f808675475a2e1a7ab826f.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/98eecb7cbf59c60157c1a5fb54c38c9200190ad423f808675475a2e1a7ab826f.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAudit logging is not turned on by default in Amazon Redshift. When you turn on logging on your cluster, Amazon Redshift creates and uploads logs to Amazon S3 that capture data from the time audit logging is enabled to the present time. Each logging update is a continuation of the information that was already logged.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable audit trail for Amazon Redshift on Amazon CloudTrail\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift is integrated with AWS CloudTrail, a service that provides a record of actions taken by a user, role, or an AWS service in Amazon Redshift. CloudTrail captures all API calls for Amazon Redshift as events. These include calls from the Amazon Redshift console and from code calls to the Amazon Redshift API operations. For example, you can have a CloudTrail log entry for a CreateCluster call or a DeleteCluster call. CloudTrail cannot capture details such as database authentication attempts, any connections/disconnections to the database, or capturing the queries executed by Redshift users. So this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable and download audit reports from AWS Config\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Config helps with auditing and recording compliance of your AWS resources. You cannot use AWS Config for capturing Redshift details such as database authentication attempts, any connections/disconnections to the database or capturing the queries executed by Redshift users. So this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEnable audit metrics on Amazon CloudWatch\u003c/strong\u003e - You can use CloudWatch to monitor the performance of your Redshift cluster and not for audit purposes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/db-auditing.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/metrics-listing.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/mgmt/metrics-listing.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:20":{"id":"e:1:20","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 20","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Quality in AWS Glue jobs to define rules for known issues and enable anomaly detection for unexpected quality patterns\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue jobs to run SQL statements that validate known data issues and write failed records to a separate Amazon S3 location\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon CloudWatch anomaly detection on AWS Glue job metrics and create alarms to identify unusual record counts and failed validation runs\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to profile the processed datasets and manually configure quality rules from the profile results after each schema change\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA subscription analytics company runs a data platform on AWS. The platform uses AWS Glue Data Catalog for metadata management and AWS Glue ETL jobs to process customer usage data before analytics teams consume the results. The company has identified recurring data quality issues, including missing account identifiers and invalid usage totals. The company also wants the validation process to detect unexpected quality issues, such as unusual changes in record counts or abnormal column statistics. The solution must minimize operational overhead.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue Data Quality in AWS Glue jobs to define rules for known issues and enable anomaly detection for unexpected quality patterns\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Data Quality is the best fit because the company already uses AWS Glue for its data platform. The data engineering team can add data quality validations directly to AWS Glue ETL jobs instead of creating a separate validation framework.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor known issues, the team can define rule-based checks by using AWS Glue Data Quality and Data Quality Definition Language. Examples include rules for required account identifiers, valid numeric usage totals, row counts, uniqueness, completeness, and other expected conditions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor unexpected issues, AWS Glue Data Quality anomaly detection can use historical statistics to identify unusual patterns that static rules might miss. This meets both requirements with the least operational overhead because the solution uses native AWS Glue capabilities rather than custom code, third-party frameworks, or separate profiling workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3f2aaaf4edc4075512d0ce37a0c4ffbf901a9d0b05198f5b3f402ca6631d6b00.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/3f2aaaf4edc4075512d0ce37a0c4ffbf901a9d0b05198f5b3f402ca6631d6b00.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-anomaly-detection.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-anomaly-detection.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/09efefc6da7512482e01fec4ba1d434323ce9f27ef0ab8afa820a76e4e176aca.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/09efefc6da7512482e01fec4ba1d434323ce9f27ef0ab8afa820a76e4e176aca.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-anomaly-detection.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-anomaly-detection.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue jobs to run SQL statements that validate known data issues and write failed records to a separate Amazon S3 location\u003c/strong\u003e - SQL statements in AWS Glue jobs can validate some known data quality issues. For example, SQL logic can find null values, invalid ranges, or duplicate records if the engineering team writes the required queries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, SQL statements alone do not provide a managed anomaly detection capability for unexpected quality problems. The company would need to build and maintain historical baselines, statistical comparisons, exception handling, and reporting logic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue DataBrew to profile the processed datasets and manually configure quality rules from the profile results after each schema change\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue DataBrew can profile datasets and help users define data quality rules with limited coding. It is useful for visual data preparation, exploration, and rule creation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the platform already uses AWS Glue jobs for processing. This option also relies on manual rule updates after profiling and does not provide the same integrated AWS Glue ETL job approach with rule-based checks and anomaly detection.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon CloudWatch anomaly detection on AWS Glue job metrics and create alarms to identify unusual record counts and failed validation runs\u003c/strong\u003e - CloudWatch anomaly detection can identify unusual behavior in metrics such as job duration, error counts, or custom published metrics. It can help with operational monitoring of AWS Glue jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, CloudWatch anomaly detection does not directly validate data records, columns, completeness, uniqueness, or business rules inside the dataset. It monitors metrics, while the requirement is to implement data quality validations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/glue-data-quality.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-anomaly-detection.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-anomaly-detection.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-configuring-anomaly-detection-etl-jobs.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-configuring-anomaly-detection-etl-jobs.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-data-quality-anomaly-detection/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/introducing-aws-glue-data-quality-anomaly-detection/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:3:12":{"id":"e:3:12","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 12","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eDeploy Apache Airflow on Amazon EC2 instances, install the AWS providers package manually, create DAGs for the AWS Glue jobs and Amazon EMR steps, and configure custom scripts for retries, monitoring, and worker scaling\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate AWS Lambda functions for every ETL step, configure Amazon EventBridge to invoke AWS Glue jobs, and use the Lambda functions to move data through the pipeline\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Step Functions Express Workflows to invoke the AWS Glue jobs and Amazon EMR processing steps, and configure each workflow execution to coordinate all transformation and loading tasks\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to create Apache Airflow DAGs that orchestrate the AWS Glue jobs, Amazon EMR processing steps, Amazon Redshift loads, Amazon S3 outputs, dependencies, retries, and monitoring\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA global retail company processes checkout transactions, catalog changes, warehouse events, and clickstream logs across several AWS services. The company needs a scalable, fully managed orchestration solution to coordinate complex ETL workflows. The workflows must use AWS Glue and Amazon EMR for data processing, store curated outputs in Amazon Redshift and Amazon S3, and support task dependencies, automated retries, operational monitoring, and event-driven execution patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow to create Apache Airflow DAGs that orchestrate the AWS Glue jobs, Amazon EMR processing steps, Amazon Redshift loads, Amazon S3 outputs, dependencies, retries, and monitoring\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Managed Workflows for Apache Airflow provides a managed Apache Airflow environment. The company can define Directed Acyclic Graphs, or DAGs, to represent task dependencies across AWS Glue jobs, Amazon EMR processing steps, Amazon Redshift loading tasks, and Amazon S3 output operations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eApache Airflow DAGs support dependency management, retries, scheduling, sensors, and task-level monitoring patterns. Amazon MWAA also integrates with Amazon CloudWatch for environment metrics and Airflow logs, which helps the operations team monitor pipeline health and troubleshoot failed ETL steps.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution fits the requirement because the company needs a fully managed orchestration layer for complex data workflows that span multiple AWS analytics services. Amazon MWAA removes the need to self-manage Airflow infrastructure while preserving Airflow\u0027s DAG-based orchestration model for complex ETL pipelines.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/be1e5efe187c0ef3e5c839a142794fbc504e8ae2463d22dd466040d5079e4bbc.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/be1e5efe187c0ef3e5c839a142794fbc504e8ae2463d22dd466040d5079e4bbc.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/building-complex-workflows-with-amazon-mwaa-aws-step-functions-aws-glue-and-amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/building-complex-workflows-with-amazon-mwaa-aws-step-functions-aws-glue-and-amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Step Functions Express Workflows to invoke the AWS Glue jobs and Amazon EMR processing steps, and configure each workflow execution to coordinate all transformation and loading tasks\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Step Functions can orchestrate AWS services and can invoke AWS Glue and Amazon EMR tasks. However, the option specifies Express Workflows, which are designed for high-event-rate workloads and short-duration executions rather than long-running, complex ETL orchestration across big data processing jobs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also does not provide the same DAG-centric data engineering interface that Apache Airflow provides for complex ETL dependency modeling. For this source pattern, Amazon MWAA is the better fit for coordinating Glue, EMR, Redshift, and S3 pipeline activities with managed Airflow capabilities.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate AWS Lambda functions for every ETL step, configure Amazon EventBridge to invoke AWS Glue jobs, and use the Lambda functions to move data through the pipeline\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Lambda and Amazon EventBridge can support event-driven processing. Lambda functions can also call AWS service APIs to start AWS Glue jobs or coordinate lightweight pipeline steps.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option builds custom orchestration logic across Lambda functions and EventBridge rules. The company would need to implement dependency handling, retries, state tracking, and monitoring logic manually, which increases operational overhead for complex ETL workflows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy Apache Airflow on Amazon EC2 instances, install the AWS providers package manually, create DAGs for the AWS Glue jobs and Amazon EMR steps, and configure custom scripts for retries, monitoring, and worker scaling\u003c/strong\u003e - Self-managed Apache Airflow on Amazon EC2 can orchestrate AWS Glue, Amazon EMR, Amazon Redshift, and Amazon S3 tasks by using Airflow operators. DAGs can also represent dependencies and retries.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option is not fully managed. The company would need to manage EC2 instances, Airflow installation, upgrades, plugins, worker scaling, scheduler availability, metadata database operations, security patches, and monitoring. Amazon MWAA provides the Airflow orchestration model with less operational effort.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/what-is-mwaa.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/what-is-mwaa.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/monitoring-airflow.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/monitoring-airflow.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/configuring-dag-folder.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/mwaa/latest/userguide/configuring-dag-folder.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/building-complex-workflows-with-amazon-mwaa-aws-step-functions-aws-glue-and-amazon-emr/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/building-complex-workflows-with-amazon-mwaa-aws-step-functions-aws-glue-and-amazon-emr/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-an-end-to-end-etl-pipeline-using-amazon-s3-aws-glue-and-amazon-redshift-serverless-with-amazon-mwaa/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/orchestrate-an-end-to-end-etl-pipeline-using-amazon-s3-aws-glue-and-amazon-redshift-serverless-with-amazon-mwaa/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-6":{"id":"p:4-6","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 6","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue workflows\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Step Functions\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Studio\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue DataBrew\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Glue workflows gom crawler, job và trigger thành một luồng ETL có thể theo dõi chung. Vì các bước ở đây đều thuộc Glue, đây là cách điều phối phù hợp và ít thành phần bổ sung.","Step Functions có thể điều phối nhiều dịch vụ, nhưng bài này chỉ cần chuỗi crawler và ETL của Glue. Glue workflows đáp ứng trực tiếp nhu cầu đó mà không cần thêm lớp điều phối bên ngoài.","Glue Studio là giao diện trực quan để thiết kế, chạy và theo dõi job. Câu hỏi cần điều phối quan hệ phụ thuộc giữa crawler và job, là vai trò của Glue workflows.","DataBrew giúp chuẩn bị và làm sạch dữ liệu bằng thao tác trực quan. Nó không phải công cụ điều phối toàn bộ chuỗi crawler và Glue ETL trong tình huống này."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company has established an ETL (extract, transform, and load) data pipeline using AWS Glue. A data engineer is tasked with crawling a table from Microsoft SQL Server and must manage the pipeline to extract, transform, and load the crawled data into an Amazon S3 bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich AWS service or feature would facilitate these tasks most cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"p:2-29":{"id":"p:2-29","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 29","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eColumnValues \"ContactEmail\" matches \"[%@%.%]\" with threshold \u0026gt; 0.9\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUniqueness \"ContactEmail\" matches \"[%@%.%]\" with threshold \u0026gt; 0.9\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eColumnValues \"ContactEmail\" matches \"[%@%.%]\" with threshold \u0026gt; 0.1\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCompleteness \"ContactEmail\" \u0026gt; 0.9\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Ý đồ lời giải là dùng ColumnValues để kiểm tra giá trị khớp mẫu và ngưỡng khoảng 90%. Cần lưu ý mẫu [%@%.%] và dấu \u003e trong đề nên được kiểm tra lại trước khi áp dụng thực tế; chúng không mặc nhiên là bộ kiểm tra email đầy đủ.","Uniqueness đo tỷ lệ giá trị duy nhất, không xác nhận định dạng email. Các chuỗi sai vẫn có thể đều khác nhau.","Ngưỡng 0.1 tương ứng khoảng 10%, quá thấp so với yêu cầu 90%. Dùng đúng loại quy tắc nhưng sai mức đạt.","Completeness kiểm tra dữ liệu không thiếu/null, không kiểm tra email có định dạng hợp lệ. Chuỗi bất kỳ có thể vẫn được tính là đầy đủ."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA regional insurance company stores historical policyholder records in an Amazon Redshift table. A column named ContactEmail contains null values, malformed strings, and some valid email addresses. Several downstream notification and compliance workflows depend on the quality of the ContactEmail column. A data engineer must define an AWS Glue Data Quality rule that fails when fewer than 90% of the values in the ContactEmail column are valid email addresses.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich component of an AWS Glue Data Quality rule will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:8:10":{"id":"e:8:10","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 10","correct":[3],"answers":["Implement an AWS Glue PySpark job to process and transform data from CSV to JSON format.","\u003cspan\u003eUtilize the \u003ccode\u003eNEST_TO_MAP\u003c/code\u003e transform in AWS Glue DataBrew to convert CSV files to JSON format before storing them in the data lake.\u003c/span\u003e","Configure an AWS Glue PySpark job to process the source data and ingest it into the S3 data lake in Apache Avro format.","Launch an AWS Glue extract, transform, and load (ETL) job to transform the CSV data into Apache Parquet format."],"question":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eA finance company stores transaction records in \u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003eCSV\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e format within an Amazon S3 bucket, protected using dual-layer server-side encryption with AWS \u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003e\u003cspan\u003eKMS\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/span\u003e keys (DSSE-\u003cspan\u003eKMS\u003c/span\u003e). The data analytics team rarely queries an entire record, typically requiring only 1-3 columns for analysis. The company wants to minimize the costs related to its query operations.\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan\u003eWhich solution would meet the requirement?\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAWS Glue\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is a powerful ETL service that easily moves data between different data stores. By using AWS Glue, you can easily create and manage ETL jobs to transfer data from various sources, such as Amazon S3, Amazon RDS, and Amazon Redshift. Additionally, AWS Glue enables you to transform your data as needed to fit your specific needs. One of the key advantages of using AWS Glue is that data engineers can automate the time-consuming steps of data preparation for analytics and data storage, such as loading data from various sources, cleaning, normalizing data, and combining it into a data lake or a relational database for analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/eea57a805f71d4d36a080098419482dd7db72940773575d04a5cbba405c3025d.png\" alt=\"AWS Glue\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen working with big data processing, it is often necessary to transform data from one format to another to optimize processing efficiency. Apache Parquet is a columnar storage format designed to provide higher efficiency and performance for big data processing. By storing and processing large amounts of data with high compression rates and faster query times, Parquet can offer significant benefits to the company. Fortunately, Apache Parquet is compatible with many data processing frameworks such as Spark, Hive, and Hadoop, making it a versatile format for big data processing. Moreover, by using AWS Glue and other AWS services, companies can easily convert their .csv files to the more efficient Apache Parquet format and store the output files in an S3 bucket, making it easy to access and process large amounts of data. This efficiency reduces storage costs and improves performance when querying the data, making it an ideal choice for analytical queries that do not need to scan the entire dataset, such as those typically performed with Amazon Athena.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLaunch an AWS Glue extract, transform, and load (ETL) job to transform the CSV data into Apache Parquet format.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cspan\u003e \u003cstrong\u003eImplement an AWS Glue PySpark job to process and transform data from CSV to JSON format\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Transforming data from CSV to JSON would not meet the requirement of minimizing query costs. JSON is a row-oriented format, which means querying a few columns out of many would still require processing the entire dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUtilize the \u003ccode\u003eNEST_TO_MAP\u003c/code\u003e transform in AWS Glue DataBrew to convert CSV files to JSON format before storing them in the data lake\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect. Similar to the other incorrect option, JSON, being row-oriented, is not suitable for scenarios where only a small subset of columns is queried frequently.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eConfigure an AWS Glue PySpark job to process the source data and ingest it into the S3 data lake in Apache Avro format\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect.\u0026nbsp;Apache Avro is a file format primarily designed to store data in rows and support schema evolution. This row-based nature of Apache Avro means that more data needs to be scanned during a query compared to columnar formats. This feature makes it an excellent choice for data likely to undergo schema changes over time. However, when it comes to running analytical queries that focus on a small set of columns from a large dataset, Apache Avro may not be as efficient as columnar formats like Apache Parquet.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-format-parquet-home.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/aws-glue-programming-etl-format-parquet-home.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/glue-athena.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/glue-athena.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/three-aws-glue-etl-job-types-for-converting-data-to-apache-parquet.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/patterns/three-aws-glue-etl-job-types-for-converting-data-to-apache-parquet.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:4-8":{"id":"p:4-8","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 8","correct":[1,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eCreate a configuration file on Amazon S3 and access the file using the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the credentials in AWS Secrets Manager\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an IAM role with necessary permissions to access the Secrets Manager and attach the role to the Redshift cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the credentials in the AWS Glue job parameters that will be accessed dynamically when the job is started\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an IAM role with necessary permissions to access the Secrets Manager and attach the role to the AWS Glue job\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Đưa mật khẩu sang tệp S3 chỉ chuyển vị trí lưu, chưa cung cấp cơ chế quản lý và xoay vòng bí mật chuyên dụng. Secrets Manager phù hợp hơn; vẫn cần bỏ thông tin đã ghi cứng trong mã.","Secrets Manager lưu thông tin đăng nhập dưới dạng bí mật và hỗ trợ kiểm soát truy cập, xoay vòng. Glue job lấy thông tin khi chạy thay vì ghi cứng mật khẩu trong script.","Glue job là thành phần cần đọc bí mật, nên quyền phải nằm trên role của Glue. Gắn role vào Redshift không cấp thêm quyền cho mã đang chạy trong Glue.","Job parameters không phải kho bí mật; giá trị có thể xuất hiện trong cấu hình hoặc log. Không nên dùng chúng để thay thế Secrets Manager cho mật khẩu database.","Gắn quyền đọc bí mật cần thiết vào IAM role thực thi Glue job. Khi chạy, job dùng role này để lấy thông tin từ Secrets Manager; đây là bước đi cùng việc lưu bí mật vào dịch vụ."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eDuring a code review it was discovered that the credentials to access an Amazon Redshift cluster were hard-coded into an AWS Glue job script. As a data engineer, you have been tasked with remediating the security vulnerability in the script and suggesting a way to securely access the needed credentials by the AWS Glue job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich options would you recommend for this remediation task? (Select two)\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:2:28":{"id":"e:2:28","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 28","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as \u003ccode\u003es3:*\u003c/code\u003e. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the destination as Amazon EventBridge and trigger the Lambda function from EventBridge\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as \u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated:*\u003c/code\u003e. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the ARN of the Lambda function as the destination for the event notification\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as \u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated:*\u003c/code\u003e. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the destination as Amazon EventBridge and trigger the Lambda function from EventBridge\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as \u003ccode\u003es3:*\u003c/code\u003e. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the ARN of the Lambda function as the destination for the event notification\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company uploads all of its data to an Amazon S3 bucket. While multiple formats of data can be uploaded to the bucket, the downstream applications need to consume data only the .csv files are uploaded. These .csv files have to be transformed into Apache Parquet format for downstream consumption.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as \u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated:*\u003c/code\u003e. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the ARN of the Lambda function as the destination for the event notification\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003es3:ObjectCreated:* - This event type will publish the following events : s3:ObjectCreated:Put, s3:ObjectCreated:Post, s3:ObjectCreated:Copy, s3:ObjectCreated:CompleteMultipartUpload.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 API operations such as PUT, POST, and COPY can create an object. With these event types, you can enable notifications when an object is created using a specific API operation. Alternatively, you can use the s3:ObjectCreated:* event type to request notification regardless of the API that was used to create an object.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003es3:ObjectCreated:CompleteMultipartUpload includes objects that are created using UploadPartCopy for Copy operations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis is the correct option since we want to process the data only when .csv files are uploaded.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eYou can configure notifications to be filtered by the prefix and suffix of the key name of objects. For example, you can set up a configuration where you\u0027re sent a notification only when image files with a \".jpg\" file name extension are added to a bucket.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eExample notification configuration for multiple non-overlapping suffixes:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/aab9a324cc2f7f4d99c49cb064ac94c2b75b5b96f7eb6a1a45a3054f3d19dcbb.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/aab9a324cc2f7f4d99c49cb064ac94c2b75b5b96f7eb6a1a45a3054f3d19dcbb.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-filtering.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-filtering.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as \u003ccode\u003es3:*\u003c/code\u003e. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the ARN of the Lambda function as the destination for the event notification\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as \u003ccode\u003es3:*\u003c/code\u003e. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the ARN of the Lambda function as the destination for the event notification\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBoth these options have the event type as \u003ccode\u003es3:*\u003c/code\u003e, which means ALL S3 event types will trigger a notification for downstream processing, which is unnecessary. The given use case states that you need to process only when .csv files are uploaded (that is, you need to set up processing only for \u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated:*\u003c/code\u003e type of events that have a suffix of .csv). So, both these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up an Amazon S3 event notification with event type as \u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated:*\u003c/code\u003e. Create a suffix filter for the notification configuration to generate notifications only when the suffix includes .csv. Set the destination as Amazon EventBridge and trigger the Lambda function from EventBridge\u003c/strong\u003e - Unlike other destinations, you can either enable or disable events to be delivered to EventBridge for a bucket. If you enable delivery, all events are sent to EventBridge.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eUsing Eventbridge to process S3 event notifications turns out to be a roundabout way of doing things for the given use case. There is no need to invoke the Lambda function via Eventbridge when you can configure Amazon S3 to directly trigger the Lambda function using the S3 event notifications. So, this option is incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-event-types-and-destinations.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-event-types-and-destinations.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-filtering.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/notification-how-to-filtering.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/new-use-amazon-s3-event-notifications-with-amazon-eventbridge/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws/new-use-amazon-s3-event-notifications-with-amazon-eventbridge/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:3-35":{"id":"p:3-35","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 3 Â· CÃ¢u 35","correct":[3],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eStore the telemetry data directly in Amazon Redshift managed storage, compress the tables with automatic compression encodings, and resize the cluster as historical data volume increases\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the telemetry data in an Amazon S3 bucket in Apache Parquet format and configure auto-copy ingestion from Amazon S3 into the Redshift cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the telemetry data in an Amazon S3 bucket in JSON format and configure auto-copy ingestion from Amazon S3 into the Redshift cluster\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eStore the telemetry data in an Amazon S3 bucket in Apache Parquet format and configure query access by using Amazon Redshift Spectrum\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Giữ toàn bộ lịch sử vô hạn trong Redshift làm tăng chi phí lưu trữ kho dữ liệu. S3 phù hợp lớp lưu dài hạn hơn theo đề.","Parquet tốt nhưng auto-copy vẫn nạp thêm bản dữ liệu vào Redshift. Spectrum tránh bản sao đó khi chỉ cần truy vấn tại chỗ.","JSON lưu theo bản ghi và phương án vẫn COPY vào Redshift, làm tăng cả chi phí quét/biến đổi và lưu trữ.","S3 lưu lịch sử với chi phí phù hợp; Parquet giảm dữ liệu cột cần đọc; Spectrum cho Redshift truy vấn trực tiếp và kết hợp với dữ liệu khác."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA utilities company collects a large volume of smart meter telemetry from thousands of field devices. The company must retain the telemetry data indefinitely for historical analysis and regulatory reporting. Analysts use an Amazon Redshift cluster to analyze the data, and the company wants the most cost-effective storage and query architecture.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements MOST cost-effectively?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:29":{"id":"e:5:29","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 29","correct":[1],"answers":["Attach an IAM policy to each HR department’s unique IAM role that only allows access to the employee records within their respective Regions.","Use AWS Lake Formation to implement fine-grained access control. Register the Amazon S3 location with AWS Lake Formation and create data filters that restrict access based on the AWS Region.","Split the employee records across individual S3 buckets in every region. Set up a policy to grant access and limit it based on the specific region.","Adjust the departments’ roles to incorporate a data filter tailored to each department’s region."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company runs its workloads across five distinct AWS Regions, each hosting its own human resources (HR) department. These departments use IAM roles tailored to their operations. The company’s employee data is centralized within an Amazon S3-based data lake. To streamline data management and enhance security, the company’s data engineering team must implement a strategy restricting HR departments from accessing only employee records within their respective Regions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich strategy should the data engineering team utilize to restrict access with the LEAST operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAWS Lake Formation\u0026nbsp;is a fully managed service that helps you build, secure, and manage data lakes, and provide access control for data in the data lake. Customers across lines of business (LOBs) need a way to manage granular access permissions for different users at the table and column level. Lake Formation helps you manage fine-grained access for internal and external customers from a centralized location and in a scalable way.\u0026nbsp;With a data lake, the goal is to have fine-grained access control to data. In Lake Formation, this means fine-grained access control to Data Catalog resources and Amazon S3 locations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/d0dcee4369188e3a500a4ffcbfe46d528acef37452c1d13becf3179460313728.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYou must specify an AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) role when you register an Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) location. Lake Formation assumes that role when it grants temporary credentials to integrated AWS services that access the data in that location.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9fa5f798fb40921723a38a432d86585c0cc11cde41713151f6e7d82f5b480a79.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Lake Formation to implement fine-grained access control. Register the Amazon S3 location with AWS Lake Formation and create data filters that restrict access based on the AWS Region.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAttach an IAM policy to each HR department’s unique IAM role that only allows access to the employee records within their respective Regions\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis\u0026nbsp;incorrect. Managing and updating multiple IAM policies, which can become cumbersome as the number of departments and regions grows.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSplit the employee records across individual S3 buckets in every region. Set up a policy to grant access and limit it based on the specific region\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect. This approach can make data management and querying more difficult, as you would need to manage access policies for each bucket separately.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAdjust the departments’ roles to incorporate a data filter tailored to each department’s region\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is\u0026nbsp;incorrect.\u0026nbsp;You cannot filter data access at the data level using IAM roles alone. IAM roles and policies provide access control at the resource level (e.g., S3 buckets, S3 objects, databases, tables), but they do not allow for fine-grained access control within data objects or specific rows/columns of a table.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/register-location.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/register-location.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/manage-fine-grained-access-control-using-aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/manage-fine-grained-access-control-using-aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/access-control-fine-grained.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/access-control-fine-grained.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this AWS Lake Formation Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lake-formation/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:48":{"id":"e:1:48","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 48","correct":[1,3,4],"answers":["\u003cp\u003ePartition the data by using a key prefix of the form year-month-day/ to the S3 objects\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003ePartition the data by using a key prefix of the form date=year-month-day/ to the S3 objects\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDrop and recreate the table with the PARTITIONED BY clause. Load the partitions by executing the \u003ccode\u003eALTER TABLE ADD PARTITION\u003c/code\u003e statement\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDrop and recreate the table with the PARTITIONED BY clause. Load the partitions by executing the \u003ccode\u003eMSCK REPAIR TABLE\u003c/code\u003e statement\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the log files to Apache Parquet format\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eChange the log files to Apache Avro format\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eThe web development team at an IT company has about 200 TB of web-log data that is stored in an Amazon S3 bucket as raw text. Each log file is identified by a key of the type year-month-day_log_HHmmss.txt where HHmmss denotes the time the log file was created. The data engineering team has created an Amazon Athena table that links to the given S3 bucket. The team executes several queries every hour against a subset of the table\u0027s columns. The company wants a Hive-metastore compatible solution that costs less and requires less maintenance to support the ongoing analytics on this log data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAs an AWS Certified Data Engineer Associate, which of the following solutions would you combine to address these requirements? (Select three)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange the log files to Apache Parquet format\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePartition the data by using a key prefix of the form date=year-month-day/ to the S3 objects\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDrop and recreate the table with the PARTITIONED BY clause. Load the partitions by executing the \u003ccode\u003eMSCK REPAIR TABLE\u003c/code\u003e statement\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Athena is an interactive query service that makes it easy to analyze data stored in Amazon S3 using standard SQL. Athena is serverless, so there is no infrastructure to manage, and you pay only for the queries that you run.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eBy partitioning your data, you can restrict the amount of data scanned by each query, thus improving performance and reducing cost. You can partition your data by any key. A common practice is to partition the data based on time, often leading to a multi-level partitioning scheme.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAthena can use Apache Hive style partitions, whose data paths contain key-value pairs connected by equal signs (for example, country=us/... or year=2021/month=01/day=26/...). Thus, the paths include both the names of the partition keys and the values that each path represents.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAthena can also use non-Hive style partitioning schemes. For example, CloudTrail logs and Kinesis Data Firehose delivery streams use separate path components for date parts such as data/2021/01/26/us/6fc7845e.json. For such non-Hive compatible data, you use ALTER TABLE ADD PARTITION to add the partitions manually.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eSince the given use case needs a hive-metastore compatible solution, you can use a key prefix of the form date=year-month-day/ for partitioning data and use \u003ccode\u003eMSCK REPAIR TABLE\u003c/code\u003e statement to load the partitions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eConsiderations and Limitations for Athena:\n\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9069e22b1a19de7d4ce167ecc02a125930b01042b3feb59ce172c2367b853dce.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/9069e22b1a19de7d4ce167ecc02a125930b01042b3feb59ce172c2367b853dce.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAvro is a row-based storage format whereas Parquet is a columnar-based storage format. Writing operations in Avro are more efficient than Parquet whereas Parquet is much better for analytical operations since the reads and querying are much more efficient than writing. Parquet is better suited for querying a subset of columns in a multi-column table whereas Avro is better suited for ETL operations where we need to query all the columns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eFor the given use case, several queries are executed every hour, so Parquet is a better format than Avro.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHighly recommend the following blog on the top performance tuning tips for Amazon Athena:\n\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDrop and recreate the table with the PARTITIONED BY clause. Load the partitions by executing the \u003ccode\u003eALTER TABLE ADD PARTITION\u003c/code\u003e statement\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ePartition the data by using a key prefix of the form year-month-day/ to the S3 objects\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eChange the log files to Apache Avro format\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003ePer the explanation provided above, these three options do not meet the requirements for the given use case, so these options are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://www.clairvoyant.ai/blog/big-data-file-formats\"\u003ehttps://www.clairvoyant.ai/blog/big-data-file-formats\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/partitions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/top-10-performance-tuning-tips-for-amazon-athena/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-data-source-hive.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/connect-to-data-source-hive.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:31":{"id":"e:1:31","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 31","correct":[1,4,5],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eData at rest inside the volume is NOT encrypted\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eData moving between the volume and the instance is encrypted\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eData moving between the volume and the instance is NOT encrypted\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAny snapshot created from the volume is NOT encrypted\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eData at rest inside the volume is encrypted\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eAny snapshot created from the volume is encrypted\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA pharmaceutical company is considering moving to AWS Cloud to accelerate the research and development process. Most of the daily workflows would be centered around running batch jobs on Amazon EC2 instances with storage on Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS) volumes. The CTO is concerned about meeting HIPAA compliance norms for sensitive data stored on Amazon EBS.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options represent the correct capabilities of an encrypted Amazon EBS volume? (Select three)\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eData at rest inside the volume is encrypted\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAny snapshot created from the volume is encrypted\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eData moving between the volume and the instance is encrypted\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS) provides block-level storage volumes for use with Amazon EC2 instances. When you create an encrypted Amazon EBS volume and attach it to a supported instance type, data stored at rest on the volume, data moving between the volume and the instance, snapshots created from the volume, and volumes created from those snapshots are all encrypted. It uses AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) customer master keys (CMK) when creating encrypted volumes and snapshots. Encryption operations occur on the servers that host Amazon EC2 instances, ensuring the security of both data-at-rest and data-in-transit between an instance and its attached Amazon EBS storage.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eData moving between the volume and the instance is NOT encrypted\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAny snapshot created from the volume is NOT encrypted\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eData at rest inside the volume is NOT encrypted\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThese three options contradict the explanation provided above, so these are incorrect.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReference:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/EBSEncryption.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/EBSEncryption.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:10:13":{"id":"e:10:13","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 13","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eReferentialIntegrity \"warehouse_id,zone_id\" \"reference.{ref_warehouse_id,ref_zone_id}\" = 1.0\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDatasetMatch \"reference\" \"warehouse_id-\u0026gt;ref_warehouse_id, zone_id-\u0026gt;ref_zone_id\" = 1.0\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eReferentialIntegrity \"warehouse_id,zone_id\" \"reference.{ref_warehouse_id,ref_zone_id}\" = 100\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eReferentialIntegrity \"reference.{ref_warehouse_id,ref_zone_id}\" \"warehouse_id,zone_id\" = 1.0\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineer has two AWS Glue Data Quality datasets that contain fulfillment information for multiple warehouse locations and operating zones. One dataset is named primary, and the other dataset is named reference.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineer needs to validate that the specific paired values in the warehouse_id and zone_id columns of the primary dataset match the corresponding paired values in the ref_warehouse_id and ref_zone_id columns of the reference dataset. The engineer wants to define this validation by using Data Quality Definition Language rules in an AWS Glue Data Quality job.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich rule will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferentialIntegrity \"warehouse_id,zone_id\" \"reference.{ref_warehouse_id,ref_zone_id}\" = 1.0\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe ReferentialIntegrity rule is the correct DQDL rule type when the data engineer needs to compare values in one or more columns from a primary dataset against corresponding values in a reference dataset. In this scenario, the validation must compare the paired warehouse_id and zone_id values from the primary dataset with the paired ref_warehouse_id and ref_zone_id values from the reference dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe syntax places the primary dataset columns first. The reference dataset columns appear second in the format reference_dataset_alias.{reference_column_1,reference_column_2}. This structure tells AWS Glue Data Quality which columns from the primary dataset must be validated against which columns in the reference dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe expression = 1.0 requires a complete match according to the rule response. This means the validation succeeds only when all relevant paired values in the primary dataset satisfy the referential integrity condition against the reference dataset.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0a6870b5feb706d0351451ad035dd701f1ca02377b23b5a1ba588d2cae62f8ca.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0a6870b5feb706d0351451ad035dd701f1ca02377b23b5a1ba588d2cae62f8ca.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types-ReferentialIntegrity.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types-ReferentialIntegrity.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDatasetMatch \"reference\" \"warehouse_id-\u0026gt;ref_warehouse_id, zone_id-\u0026gt;ref_zone_id\" = 1.0\u003c/strong\u003e - DatasetMatch compares two datasets to determine whether the datasets are synchronized based on mapped columns. It can be useful when the goal is to compare records across two datasets more broadly.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe limitation is that this question asks for referential integrity between a specific set of paired column values in the primary dataset and the corresponding values in the reference dataset. The AWS Glue DQDL rule that directly represents this relationship is ReferentialIntegrity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferentialIntegrity \"warehouse_id,zone_id\" \"reference.{ref_warehouse_id,ref_zone_id}\" = 100\u003c/strong\u003e - This option uses the ReferentialIntegrity rule type and correctly identifies the primary columns and reference dataset columns. The column mapping structure is aligned with the intended validation pattern.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option fails because the expression uses = 100. DQDL referential integrity results are evaluated as a ratio, so a complete match should use = 1.0 rather than = 100.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferentialIntegrity \"reference.{ref_warehouse_id,ref_zone_id}\" \"warehouse_id,zone_id\" = 1.0\u003c/strong\u003e - This option uses ReferentialIntegrity and includes both the primary columns and the reference columns. It also uses the correct 1.0 expression for a complete match.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option reverses the required argument order. DQDL syntax expects the primary dataset columns first and the reference dataset alias with reference columns second, so the rule does not correctly express the validation from primary to reference.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types-ReferentialIntegrity.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types-ReferentialIntegrity.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/dqdl-rule-types.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-getting-started.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/data-quality-getting-started.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/set-up-advanced-rules-to-validate-quality-of-multiple-datasets-with-aws-glue-data-quality/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/set-up-advanced-rules-to-validate-quality-of-multiple-datasets-with-aws-glue-data-quality/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:8:19":{"id":"e:8:19","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 8 Â· CÃ¢u 19","correct":[1],"answers":["Configure Amazon S3 to publish an event with the video\u0027s S3 path to an Amazon SQS queue when a new file is uploaded. Then, have the transcoding service poll the queue.","Set up S3 Event Notifications to send a message to an AWS Lambda function whenever a new object is created in the bucket, and then have the Lambda function trigger the transcoding service.","Use AWS Lambda to continuously poll the S3 bucket for new files and trigger the transcoding service when a new file is found.","Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to stream the data from the S3 bucket to AWS Lambda, and then have Lambda trigger the transcoding service when a new file is detected."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA data engineering team at a media company is developing a new feature for their application that allows users to upload videos. Once a video is uploaded to their Amazon S3 bucket, a process must be triggered immediately to transcode it for different devices. The team has decided to use the \u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated\u003c/code\u003e action as the event trigger.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich of the following options is the most efficient way to set up this architecture?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eAmazon S3 provides a useful feature called \u0027Event Notifications\u0027, which enables you to receive notifications when specific events occur in your S3 bucket. To use this feature, you need to create a notification configuration that specifies the events you want to track and the destinations where you want to receive the notifications.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/73fe4ef0a3a70ad8150db51eb850ba66439982075f5608e75b55e43dae1c1334.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lambda can be used to process event notifications from Amazon Simple Storage Service. By configuring notification settings on a bucket, Amazon S3 can send an event to a Lambda function when an object is created or deleted. To enable this functionality, you must grant Amazon S3 permission to invoke a function using the function\u0027s resource-based permissions policy.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/93df42455c606dd452507951b6cf80345e2cf83b43b2e1dee93a38aa8cc13dd4.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eUsing S3 Event notifications to trigger a Lambda function eliminates the need to continuously poll the S3 bucket for new files or involve additional services. The \u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated\u003c/code\u003e event ensures that the Lambda function is triggered immediately when a new file is uploaded. The Lambda function then invokes or initiates the transcoding service, leading to prompt video processing.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up S3 Event Notifications to send a message to an AWS Lambda function whenever a new object is created in the bucket, and then have the Lambda function trigger the transcoding service.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eConfigure Amazon S3 to publish an event with the video\u0027s S3 path to an Amazon SQS queue when a new file is uploaded. Then, have the transcoding service poll the queue\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. While possible,\u0026nbsp;this requires implementing additional polling logic within the transcoding service to continuously check the queue for new messages.\u0026nbsp;With AWS Lambda, you can directly handle the S3 event without the need for an intermediary queue.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUse AWS Lambda to continuously poll the S3 bucket for new files and trigger the transcoding service when a new file is found\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because it’s inefficient. Continuously polling the S3 bucket would consume unnecessary resources and might not trigger the transcoding service as quickly as the \u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated\u003c/code\u003e event.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to stream the data from the S3 bucket to AWS Lambda, and then have Lambda trigger the transcoding service when a new file is detected\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because it’s overkill for this scenario. While Kinesis Data Streams can be used with S3 and Lambda for real-time data processing, it’s not necessary for this use case. The\u0026nbsp;\u003ccode\u003es3:ObjectCreated\u003c/code\u003e event can directly trigger a Lambda function, which is a straightforward and more efficient solution.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventNotifications.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventNotifications.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-s3.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-s3.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out these Amazon S3 and AWS Lambda Cheat Sheets:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-lambda/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-s3/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:2:35":{"id":"e:2:35","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 35","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon OpenSearch Serverless to store product documents, index the SKU and secondary attributes, and query all catalog records through search APIs\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Aurora with a Multi-AZ deployment, add read replicas, and create indexes on SKU, Department, and Manufacturer for catalog queries\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse a DynamoDB table with on-demand capacity, store products by SKU as the primary key, and create global secondary indexes for Department and Manufacturer\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Serverless to store product records in relational tables, distribute rows by SKU, and create materialized views for Department and Manufacturer access\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eAn online marketplace needs to store and query catalog data for millions of products. Each product can have different attributes depending on the product type, such as size, warranty period, material, or supported device model. The application must retrieve product records by a primary identifier named SKU and must also support flexible access patterns by Department and Manufacturer. The workload has unpredictable traffic and must sustain sudden request spikes with single-digit millisecond latency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse a DynamoDB table with on-demand capacity, store products by SKU as the primary key, and create global secondary indexes for Department and Manufacturer\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon DynamoDB is a strong fit for product catalog data that has variable attributes. DynamoDB stores items in a flexible schema model, so each product item can include only the attributes that apply to that product type.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe application must retrieve items by SKU and also support access by Department and Manufacturer. The table can use SKU as the primary key for direct item lookups. Global secondary indexes can support additional query patterns that use Department and Manufacturer as index keys.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eDynamoDB on-demand capacity is appropriate because the traffic is unpredictable and can spike suddenly. On-demand mode automatically adapts to request volume and supports single-digit millisecond performance without requiring the company to provision read and write capacity in advance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0308ab5c28959092a3c1373d7c7e2cfe7fe020d772b90f7458e6a5f9fa3e7552.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0308ab5c28959092a3c1373d7c7e2cfe7fe020d772b90f7458e6a5f9fa3e7552.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/demystifying-amazon-dynamodb-on-demand-capacity-mode/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/demystifying-amazon-dynamodb-on-demand-capacity-mode/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/35cb5b5dfe0f8df69831840a79ae128138d6095ff240ff5f8850cfbec7164b68.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/35cb5b5dfe0f8df69831840a79ae128138d6095ff240ff5f8850cfbec7164b68.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/demystifying-amazon-dynamodb-on-demand-capacity-mode/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/demystifying-amazon-dynamodb-on-demand-capacity-mode/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0a46939d68d7d6c1412f11818b1e8abf7bceb1e6723eb18df579ed8d58da6fd2.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/0a46939d68d7d6c1412f11818b1e8abf7bceb1e6723eb18df579ed8d58da6fd2.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/demystifying-amazon-dynamodb-on-demand-capacity-mode/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/demystifying-amazon-dynamodb-on-demand-capacity-mode/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Aurora with a Multi-AZ deployment, add read replicas, and create indexes on SKU, Department, and Manufacturer for catalog queries\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Aurora can store product data in relational tables, and indexes can support lookups by SKU, Department, and Manufacturer. Multi-AZ deployments and read replicas can also improve availability and read scaling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the requirement calls for variable product attributes and unpredictable high-volume access with single-digit millisecond latency during traffic spikes. DynamoDB on-demand capacity is a better fit for a flexible, key-based operational workload that needs automatic scaling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon OpenSearch Serverless to store product documents, index the SKU and secondary attributes, and query all catalog records through search APIs\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon OpenSearch Serverless can index product documents and support search use cases across many attributes. It can be useful when the application needs relevance ranking, full-text search, faceted navigation, or complex search expressions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, the core access pattern requires dependable primary-key retrieval by SKU and flexible key-based queries by secondary attributes. DynamoDB with GSIs is better suited for high-volume operational item access with single-digit millisecond latency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Redshift Serverless to store product records in relational tables, distribute rows by SKU, and create materialized views for Department and Manufacturer access\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift Serverless can store and analyze large datasets without managing a provisioned cluster. Materialized views can accelerate repeated analytical query patterns.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, Redshift is an analytical warehouse, not the best operational store for high-volume product lookups during sudden spikes. It also does not provide the same flexible schema and on-demand request scaling model that DynamoDB provides for catalog access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Introduction.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/Introduction.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/on-demand-capacity-mode.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/on-demand-capacity-mode.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/HowItWorks.CoreComponents.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/HowItWorks.CoreComponents.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/GSI.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/GSI.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/demystifying-amazon-dynamodb-on-demand-capacity-mode/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/demystifying-amazon-dynamodb-on-demand-capacity-mode/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"p:2-25":{"id":"p:2-25","label":"Luyá»‡n táº­p Â· Äá» 2 Â· CÃ¢u 25","correct":[0],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eConfigure the AWS Glue ETL pipeline to use AWS Glue Data Quality rules. Write rules in Data Quality Definition Language (DQDL) to detect empty files and missing values in required fields, and conditionally write output only when all rules pass\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse AWS Step Functions to orchestrate the ETL pipeline, adding a validation state that invokes a custom AWS Lambda function to perform field-level checks before allowing the pipeline to write to the reporting bucket only when all field-level checks are good\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eTrigger an AWS Lambda function after each upload to scan the file for missing transaction records and automatically populate missing fields using historical averages\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eModify the AWS Glue ETL pipeline code in AWS Glue Studio to replace any missing transaction values with the most frequently occurring values for each field before writing output\u003c/p\u003e"],"vi":["Dùng Glue Data Quality/DQDL kiểm tra dữ liệu rỗng và trường bắt buộc. Chỉ ghi đè SalesReport.csv khi kiểm tra đạt, nhờ vậy giữ báo cáo cũ nếu dữ liệu mới không hợp lệ.","Step Functions và Lambda có thể làm được nhưng thêm state machine, mã kiểm tra, quyền và xử lý lỗi. Pipeline Glue hiện tại có sẵn khả năng kiểm tra phù hợp hơn.","Tự điền bằng trung bình lịch sử tạo dữ liệu không có trong giao dịch gốc. Không đáp ứng yêu cầu giữ báo cáo cũ khi đầu vào thiếu.","Điền giá trị phổ biến rồi ghi đè che giấu lỗi dữ liệu thay vì từ chối báo cáo chưa hợp lệ. Cần kiểm tra và chặn ghi đầu ra."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail company processes daily sales reports uploaded to an Amazon S3 bucket by regional store managers. The company uses a daily AWS Glue ETL pipeline to standardize and aggregate each report. The output is written to a file named SalesReport.csv in a reporting S3 bucket. Occasionally, a store manager uploads an incomplete report with missing transaction records or empty files. When this happens, the company must retain the previous day\u0027s report rather than overwrite it with bad data. A data engineer must ensure the previous day\u0027s report is overwritten only when the new report passes all data quality checks.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution meets this requirement with the least effort?\u003c/p\u003e\n"},"e:5:54":{"id":"e:5:54","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 5 Â· CÃ¢u 54","correct":[1],"answers":["Store the schema definition in Amazon S3 and use AWS Lambda to validate records against it.","Set up both producers and auditors to validate records against a schema definition stored in AWS Glue Schema Registry.","Use AWS Glue ETL jobs to transform the data stream according to the schema changes.","Apply Kinesis Producer Library (KPL) to verify entries against an Amazon DynamoDB table\u0027s schema description."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA multinational corporation streams real-time financial transaction data to a Kinesis data stream for third-party financial auditors to analyze. The schema and format of the data received by each auditor may vary due to their individual auditing standards and requirements. The corporation wants to efficiently manage and control schema changes across its entire data stream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich option would be most effective in fulfilling these requirements?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eAWS Glue Schema Registry is a central repository for storing and managing schema definitions for your data. It helps ensure that all records in your data stream conform to a defined format and structure.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo use the schema registry, you first define the schema for your data. Once the schema is defined, you can configure your producers and consumers to validate records against the schema definition using AWS Glue Schema Registry APIs or through the Kinesis Producer Library (KPL) and Kinesis Client Library (KCL) libraries. This means that any time a new record is added to the data stream, it will be checked against the schema to ensure it conforms to the defined format and structure. If the record does not meet the schema requirements, it can be rejected or transformed to conform to the schema before it is added to the data stream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/6381b793f422f0c7393c9ed6fabff8907eea925498732bf8c5d2c900fd74be0e.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBy using AWS Glue Schema Registry, you can streamline the process of managing and controlling schema changes for your data. This can help you avoid the need for defensive coding and coordination between teams, improve the quality of your data, reduce the risk of downstream application failures, and take advantage of integration with multiple AWS services. Additionally, the schema registry allows you to version each schema, giving you the freedom to evolve your schemas while maintaining reliability.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSet up both producers and auditors to validate records against a schema definition stored in AWS Glue Schema Registry.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eApply Kinesis Producer Library (KPL) to verify entries against an Amazon DynamoDB table’s schema description\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003eis incorrect. Kinesis Producer Library (KPL) is used to efficiently send data to a Kinesis data stream. While KPL does aggregate and compress data before sending it to Kinesis Data Streams, it does not inherently validate records against a schema definition. Moreover, storing the schema definition in a DynamoDB table would not provide the centralized schema management and versioning capabilities that AWS Glue Schema Registry offers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eStore the schema definition in Amazon S3 and use AWS Lambda to validate records against it\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because although this approach is technically feasible, it would not be efficient. It would require triggering a Lambda function for every record in the data stream, which could be costly and may not scale well with high-volume data streams. Furthermore, this approach does not provide a built-in way to manage and control schema changes.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse AWS Glue ETL jobs to transform the data stream according to the schema changes\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;is incorrect because AWS Glue ETL jobs are used to prepare and load data for analytics, not for real-time schema validation or management in a data stream. While Glue ETL jobs can transform data based on a defined schema, they do not provide real-time schema validation for producers and consumers of a Kinesis data stream.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/integrate-aws-glue-schema-registry-with-the-aws-glue-data-catalog-to-enable-effective-schema-enforcement-in-streaming-analytics-use-cases/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/integrate-aws-glue-schema-registry-with-the-aws-glue-data-catalog-to-enable-effective-schema-enforcement-in-streaming-analytics-use-cases/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/validate-evolve-and-control-schemas-in-amazon-msk-and-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-with-aws-glue-schema-registry/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/validate-evolve-and-control-schemas-in-amazon-msk-and-amazon-kinesis-data-streams-with-aws-glue-schema-registry/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/schema-registry-works.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/latest/dg/schema-registry-works.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this\u0026nbsp;AWS Glue Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/aws-glue/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:4:31":{"id":"e:4:31","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 4 Â· CÃ¢u 31","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eModify the IAM role that is associated with the RDS DB instance to allow network connections from the EC2 instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUpdate the RDS DB instance security group to allow inbound MySQL traffic from the security group that is attached to the EC2 instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eModify an ec2_authorized_hosts parameter in the RDS DB parameter group to include the EC2 instance private IP address and restart the DB instance\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eCreate an AWS Systems Manager Session Manager document that forwards JDBC traffic from the EC2 instance to the RDS DB instance and attach the document to the RDS DB parameter group\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company is building a non-production reporting application that runs on an Amazon EC2 instance. The application must connect to an Amazon RDS for MySQL DB instance by using Java Database Connectivity (JDBC). The EC2 instance and the RDS DB instance are in the same VPC and use subnets from the same private subnet tier.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUpdate the RDS DB instance security group to allow inbound MySQL traffic from the security group that is attached to the EC2 instance\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon RDS DB instances in a VPC use VPC security groups to control inbound network access. To allow an application on Amazon EC2 to connect to an RDS for MySQL DB instance, the RDS security group must allow inbound TCP traffic on the MySQL port from the EC2 instance or, preferably, from the security group that is attached to the EC2 instance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe fact that the EC2 instance and RDS DB instance are in the same subnet or subnet tier does not automatically allow JDBC traffic. Security groups still enforce traffic permissions. The database security group must explicitly allow the application traffic path.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution follows a least-privilege network access pattern. Referencing the EC2 security group as the source allows only resources associated with that security group to connect to the RDS DB instance on the database port, instead of opening the database to a broad CIDR range.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/42d1d3b59e2571346494078f8ca8ec998049c8016448dcd2ae852b3be4cc3d54.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/42d1d3b59e2571346494078f8ca8ec998049c8016448dcd2ae852b3be4cc3d54.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Overview.RDSSecurityGroups.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Overview.RDSSecurityGroups.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b66509e636efbc451e11a6f5d05910056ee0930e68110b2d8210d10faa684538.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/b66509e636efbc451e11a6f5d05910056ee0930e68110b2d8210d10faa684538.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Overview.RDSSecurityGroups.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Overview.RDSSecurityGroups.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eModify the IAM role that is associated with the RDS DB instance to allow network connections from the EC2 instance\u003c/strong\u003e - IAM roles control AWS API permissions for AWS resources and workloads. An IAM role attached to an RDS DB instance does not authorize TCP network connectivity from an EC2 instance to the database listener.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe application still needs a valid network path and the correct security group rules. JDBC connectivity to an RDS for MySQL instance depends on endpoint, port, database credentials, and network access controls, not an IAM role that permits connections from EC2.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eModify an ec2_authorized_hosts parameter in the RDS DB parameter group to include the EC2 instance private IP address and restart the DB instance\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon RDS DB parameter groups manage database engine configuration parameters. They do not include an ec2_authorized_hosts parameter that authorizes EC2 clients for JDBC connectivity.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAllowing a client host to connect to an RDS database is a network security group task. Restarting the DB instance after modifying a nonexistent parameter would not solve the connection requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCreate an AWS Systems Manager Session Manager document that forwards JDBC traffic from the EC2 instance to the RDS DB instance and attach the document to the RDS DB parameter group\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Systems Manager Session Manager can provide secure interactive access to managed instances and can support port forwarding scenarios for administrative access. However, it is not the normal way for an application running on EC2 to connect to a colocated RDS DB instance.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe option also incorrectly attaches a Session Manager document to an RDS DB parameter group. The required fix is to allow the application traffic in the RDS security group.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/ec2-rds-connect.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/ec2-rds-connect.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Overview.RDSSecurityGroups.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Overview.RDSSecurityGroups.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/tutorial-connect-ec2-instance-to-rds-database.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/tutorial-connect-ec2-instance-to-rds-database.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/gettingstartedguide/security-groups.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/gettingstartedguide/security-groups.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/data-api.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/data-api.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:1:55":{"id":"e:1:55","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 1 Â· CÃ¢u 55","correct":[2],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose to collect viewer events, configure buffering before delivery, transform batches with AWS Lambda, and load the data into Amazon S3 before a scheduled Amazon Redshift COPY operation\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eSchedule an AWS Glue job to read interaction log files from Amazon S3 every 15 minutes, transform the records, and load the processed results into Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest viewer events, process the stream records with AWS Lambda functions, and store the processed results in Amazon Redshift for analytics\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eDeploy an Amazon MSK cluster, run self-managed consumer applications on Amazon EC2 to process viewer events in real time, and integrate the processed output with Amazon Redshift\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA sports media company is launching a real-time engagement analytics platform for its live streaming website and mobile application. The platform must ingest millions of viewer events, such as play, pause, search, and subscription actions, with minimal latency during major tournament broadcasts. The solution must scale automatically, persist event data durably so that consumers can recover from processing interruptions, and deliver processed results to Amazon Redshift for analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution will meet these requirements in the MOST cost-effective way?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest viewer events, process the stream records with AWS Lambda functions, and store the processed results in Amazon Redshift for analytics\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Kinesis Data Streams is designed for real-time streaming data ingestion. It can collect high-volume event streams from sources such as websites, mobile applications, application logs, and clickstreams. The company can scale the stream to handle millions of viewer activity events during peak broadcast periods.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAWS Lambda integrates natively with Kinesis Data Streams. Lambda can poll the stream, invoke functions with batches of records, and process events with low operational overhead. This serverless processing model helps the company avoid managing consumer fleets while still supporting near real-time event transformation.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eKinesis Data Streams also stores records durably across multiple Availability Zones in an AWS Region. This durability helps protect against data loss and allows consumers to recover from interruptions by reading records that remain within the stream retention period. Compared with operating Apache Kafka clusters or EC2-based ingestion fleets, Kinesis Data Streams with Lambda provides a scalable and cost-effective managed architecture for this requirement.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e258265fbd7b01472d594ce5478aaf7fd5d2c7e650dedd41229e1bbea0520f22.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e258265fbd7b01472d594ce5478aaf7fd5d2c7e650dedd41229e1bbea0520f22.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4a127e7e2169ec36de2bdd2e2a3b2a0817541fd28d43d93b450b20212353a162.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/4a127e7e2169ec36de2bdd2e2a3b2a0817541fd28d43d93b450b20212353a162.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSchedule an AWS Glue job to read interaction log files from Amazon S3 every 15 minutes, transform the records, and load the processed results into Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - AWS Glue can run scalable ETL jobs and can load transformed data into Amazon Redshift. However, a job that runs every 15 minutes is a batch-oriented design and does not provide minimum-latency processing for real-time viewer activity events.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also requires events to land in Amazon S3 before processing begins. That staging step delays analytics and does not match the requirement to ingest millions of events with minimal latency.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eUse Amazon Data Firehose to collect viewer events, configure buffering before delivery, transform batches with AWS Lambda, and load the data into Amazon S3 before a scheduled Amazon Redshift COPY operation\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Data Firehose can collect streaming data, transform records with Lambda, and deliver data to supported destinations. However, Firehose uses buffering before delivery, which can add latency compared with direct stream processing from Kinesis Data Streams.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis option also introduces an S3 staging and scheduled Redshift COPY process. That design is better for near-real-time or batch delivery, but it does not provide the minimum-latency processing path required for live engagement analytics.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeploy an Amazon MSK cluster, run self-managed consumer applications on Amazon EC2 to process viewer events in real time, and integrate the processed output with Amazon Redshift\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon MSK can ingest and process real-time streaming data by using Apache Kafka. It can support high-throughput event workloads and can integrate with analytics targets such as Amazon Redshift.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eHowever, this option requires the company to operate an MSK cluster and manage self-managed consumer applications on Amazon EC2. For this requirement, Kinesis Data Streams with Lambda provides a more cost-effective and lower-overhead managed approach.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/features/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/kinesis/data-streams/features/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/big-data-analytics-options/amazon-kinesis.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/big-data-analytics-options/amazon-kinesis.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kinesis.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/with-kinesis.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/real-time-analytics-with-amazon-redshift-streaming-ingestion/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:10:12":{"id":"e:10:12","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 10 Â· CÃ¢u 12","correct":[1],"answers":["\u003cp\u003eRun GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO NAMESPACES \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027 AND \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027; and run GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun GRANT SHARE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027; and run GRANT SHARE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/p\u003e","\u003cp\u003eRun GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO NAMESPACE \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027; and run GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO NAMESPACE \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/p\u003e"],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA retail analytics organization has three AWS accounts. A central data platform account owns an Amazon Redshift cluster that publishes a datashare named inventoryshare. Two separate analytics teams in two different AWS accounts need access to the same datashare. A data engineer must grant access by using the Amazon Redshift SQL interface from the producer cluster.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eWhich command or commands will meet this requirement?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003eCorrect option:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027; and run GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eAmazon Redshift data sharing lets a producer cluster grant datashare access to consumers in other AWS accounts. For cross-account sharing, the producer grants USAGE on the datashare to each consumer AWS account ID.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe correct SQL pattern uses the ACCOUNT keyword with each consumer account number. Because two separate AWS accounts need access, the data engineer can run one GRANT USAGE statement for the first consumer account and another GRANT USAGE statement for the second consumer account.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThis solution matches the producer-side SQL interface requirement. After the producer grants access, each consumer account can associate with the datashare and create a database from the datashare according to its consumer-side configuration.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/567ab29ac38fec85b01207bc0ae457b7fd577410c102db07d16ce8a57d37983d.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/567ab29ac38fec85b01207bc0ae457b7fd577410c102db07d16ce8a57d37983d.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/security-considerations-for-amazon-redshift-cross-account-data-sharing/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/security-considerations-for-amazon-redshift-cross-account-data-sharing/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/30b3a2a7035dc55ec02776a898267b1d112b466877cba2aae21ffc53e0afa18e.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/30b3a2a7035dc55ec02776a898267b1d112b466877cba2aae21ffc53e0afa18e.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/security-considerations-for-amazon-redshift-cross-account-data-sharing/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/security-considerations-for-amazon-redshift-cross-account-data-sharing/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2bd4c8f63ccc77df605d76c5cd605d74949c8deedec63a8646985cef011ddc69.jpg\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/2bd4c8f63ccc77df605d76c5cd605d74949c8deedec63a8646985cef011ddc69.jpg\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\nvia - \u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/security-considerations-for-amazon-redshift-cross-account-data-sharing/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/security-considerations-for-amazon-redshift-cross-account-data-sharing/\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIncorrect options:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO NAMESPACES \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027 AND \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/strong\u003e - Namespaces are valid consumers in specific datashare scenarios, such as granting access to an Amazon Redshift namespace. However, this option uses NAMESPACES with AWS account IDs and combines the values with AND.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe requirement says the two teams use separate AWS accounts. Cross-account authorization should use ACCOUNT with each consumer account number, not NAMESPACE with account IDs.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun GRANT SHARE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027; and run GRANT SHARE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO ACCOUNT \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/strong\u003e - Amazon Redshift supports the SHARE permission on a datashare for users or roles that need to grant usage to a consumer. That is different from granting a consumer account access to use the datashare.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eThe consumer AWS accounts need USAGE on the datashare. Granting SHARE to ACCOUNT is not the correct producer-side SQL command for cross-account consumer access.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eRun GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO NAMESPACE \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-1\u0026gt;\u0027; and run GRANT USAGE ON DATASHARE inventoryshare TO NAMESPACE \u0027\u0026lt;consumer-account-2\u0026gt;\u0027;\u003c/strong\u003e - The NAMESPACE keyword identifies a Redshift namespace GUID, not a general AWS account ID. This option incorrectly places consumer AWS account IDs in the namespace position.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eIf the producer needs to share with separate AWS accounts, the command must use ACCOUNT. Using NAMESPACE with account IDs will not correctly authorize the two external accounts.\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003eReferences:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_GRANT.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_GRANT.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/datashare-overview.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/datashare-overview.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/writes-adding-data-consumer.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/writes-adding-data-consumer.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/writes-managing-permissions.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/writes-managing-permissions.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/security-considerations-for-amazon-redshift-cross-account-data-sharing/\"\u003ehttps://aws.amazon.com/blogs/big-data/security-considerations-for-amazon-redshift-cross-account-data-sharing/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"},"e:6:29":{"id":"e:6:29","label":"PhÃ²ng thi Â· Äá» 6 Â· CÃ¢u 29","correct":[2],"answers":["Activate Amazon Inspector to regularly assess the S3 data lake for compliance with data protection standards, including PII detection. Use Amazon EventBridge to trigger the masking function based on Inspector findings.","Implement S3 Event notifications to trigger an AWS Lambda function that analyzes objects for personally identifiable information (PII) using custom scripts. Upon detecting PII, the Lambda function then invokes the existing S3 Object Lambda masking function","Activate Amazon Macie within the AWS account for continuous monitoring and identification of personally identifiable information (PII) within the S3 data lake. Configure the Amazon EventBridge rule to capture Macie\u0027s findings and trigger the masking function as the designated target for this rule.","Utilize AWS Lake Formation to set up data access rules that automatically scan for personally identifiable information (PII). Trigger an AWS Lambda function to invoke the existing S3 Object Lambda masking function upon detection of sensitive data."],"question":"\u003cp\u003eA company gathers a large amount of data from user interactions via its user registration system, which is then stored in an Amazon S3 data lake. Some of this data contains personally identifiable information (PII). The company wants to automate the process of identifying new and existing data that contains sensitive personal information and mask it before any analysis.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe data engineer has been tasked to redact the PII automatically through a masking S3 Object Lambda function that’s powered by a prebuilt \u003ccode\u003eComprehendPiiRedactionS3ObjectLambda\u003c/code\u003e Lambda function. In addition, the solution must also be capable of automatically triggering a notification when a piece of personally identifiable information is detected from the data lake and provide a detailed report on the identified data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhich solution effectively meets these requirements with MINIMAL operational overhead?\u003c/p\u003e","explanation":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eAmazon Macie\u003c/strong\u003e is a security service that utilizes machine learning algorithms to automatically identify, classify, and secure sensitive data in AWS. Amazon Macie protects sensitive data, such as personally identifiable information (PII) (e.g., names, addresses, credit card numbers, and intellectual property), from unauthorized access. After identifying sensitive data, Macie generates detailed reports and alerts, which can be integrated with Amazon EventBridge for automated response actions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cimg src=\"exams/assets/e5c55e99cad624971c9c1321fbaf667331f118c7d79ba4caee2609f3664194b0.png\" alt=\"\"\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eUsing Amazon Macie in conjunction with Amazon EventBridge allows for the automation of the process to detect PII and trigger the masking application with minimal operational overhead. Macie\u0027s continuous monitoring capability ensures both new and existing data in the S3 data lake are analyzed for PII, aligning perfectly with the corporation\u0027s requirements for automating the identification and masking of personal information before analysis. Furthermore, the integration of Amazon Macie for PII detection and Amazon EventBridge for event-driven triggering of the masking application provides a seamless and automated workflow. This minimizes operational overhead while ensuring sensitive data is automatically masked, adhering to data protection standards.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHence, the correct answer is: \u003cspan\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eActivate Amazon Macie within the AWS account for continuous monitoring and identification of personally identifiable information (PII) within the S3 data lake. Configure the Amazon EventBridge rule to capture Macie\u0027s findings and trigger the masking function as the designated target for this rule.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eActivate Amazon Inspector to regularly assess the S3 data lake for compliance with data protection standards, including PII detection. Use Amazon EventBridge to trigger the masking application based on Inspector findings\u0026nbsp;\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect because Amazon Inspector is primarily used for security assessments and vulnerability management of EC2 instances and applications, not for detecting PII in S3 objects.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eImplement S3 Event notifications to trigger an AWS Lambda function that analyzes objects for personally identifiable information (PII) using custom scripts. Upon detecting PII, the Lambda function then invokes the existing S3 Object Lambda masking function\u003c/span\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003eis incorrect. This option\u0026nbsp;involves significant operational overhead to develop, maintain, and scale custom scripts for\u0026nbsp;personally identifiable information (PII) detection.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe option that says:\u0026nbsp;\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cspan\u003eUtilize AWS Lake Formation to set up data access rules that automatically scan for personally identifiable information (PII). Trigger an AWS Lambda function to invoke the existing S3 Object Lambda masking function upon detection of sensitive data\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e is incorrect. AWS Lake Formation is primarily used for managing secure data lake access and does not inherently provide automated PII detection capabilities like\u0026nbsp;Amazon Macie.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReferences:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventBridge.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/EventBridge.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/macie/latest/user/findings-types.html#findings-sensitive-data-types\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/macie/latest/user/findings-types.html#findings-sensitive-data-types\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/macie/latest/user/findings-publish-event-schemas.html\"\u003ehttps://docs.aws.amazon.com/macie/latest/user/findings-publish-event-schemas.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCheck out this Amazon Macie Cheat Sheet:\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-macie/\"\u003ehttps://tutorialsdojo.com/amazon-macie/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"}};